window.LESSON_DATA = {"devotions":{"label":"Term","children":{"Term 1":{"label":"Week","children":{"Week 1: The Arrival of Jesus and John":{"label":"Day","children":{"Week Overview":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Luke 1—2:38</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Jesus is God's powerful Son who fulfils God's promises.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Source</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Connect B1 Upper Primary — Lesson 1</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Monday: Meeting Zechariah — an impossible promise":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">10–15 minutes</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Luke 1:5–22</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Meeting Zechariah — an impossible promise</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Jesus is God's powerful Son who fulfils God's promises.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning that God keeps his promises, even ones that seem impossible.</li><li>We are learning who Zechariah and Elizabeth were, and the promise the angel Gabriel brought them.</li><li>I can explain why believing an impossible promise can be hard — even with proof right in front of you.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Listen for participation in the opening belief question. Note which students can recall Zechariah's name, his situation, and connect it back to the 'would you believe a superpower claim' opener by the end of the session.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Screen/device to display the comic panels (already embedded in the app)</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"2\">1</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Welcome &amp; hook</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">1–2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Welcome the class back warmly — this is the first day of a brand-new term and a brand-new Bible story. “Welcome back, everyone! This term we’re going to meet someone amazing and important. He’s a Super Saviour!” Build a little excitement and mystery — don’t reveal who yet.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">2</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Opening question — would you believe it?</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2–3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">This question is the setup for the whole story, so don't rush it. Push gently on &quot;what would it take to convince you&quot; — most students will land on &quot;I'd need to see it for myself.&quot; Hold onto that thought out loud: &quot;Keep that in mind, because in today's story, someone gets exactly that kind of proof — and still has a hard time believing it.&quot;</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">If someone told you they had a real superpower, would you believe them? What would it take to convince you?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Take a few quick responses. Push gently: most kids will say they'd need to SEE proof. Hold onto this — it's exactly the position Zechariah is about to be in, except he gets proof from an actual angel and still struggles to believe it.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"3\">3</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Introducing the comic &amp; Zechariah</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3–4 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">“This term we’ll be reading our very own Bible comic. It begins with a man named Zechariah.” Show the first comic panel. Explain who Zechariah and Elizabeth were — an older married couple who were never able to have children, which in their culture and time carried real sadness.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Show comic panel 01-zechariah-elizabeth-intro on screen as you introduce them.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"5\">4</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Gabriel’s promise &amp; the silence</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">4–5 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">“One day, as Zechariah was serving God in the temple, and all the people were praying outside, the angel Gabriel appeared to him!” Show panel 02 — the angel’s promise. Then show panel 03 — the crowd’s confusion as Zechariah comes out unable to speak. Callback to the opening question: Zechariah got the most convincing proof possible — an actual angel, face to face — and still doubted. Build suspense; don’t explain yet exactly what he said or did wrong (revealed Day 2 — he doubted the angel).</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Show comic panels 02-gabriel-in-temple and 03-crowd-cant-speak in sequence.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Zechariah saw an actual angel and heard the promise himself — so why do you think he still struggled to believe it, and what do you think happened to him inside the temple that made him unable to speak?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Accept any responses — we're building anticipation, not confirming the answer yet. The full reveal comes tomorrow (he doubted the angel's promise about John, even after seeing him face to face).</td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Day 2 reveals why Zechariah lost his voice, and introduces Mary and the angel’s announcement about Jesus.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Tuesday: Two surprising babies":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">10–15 minutes</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Luke 1:24–38</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Two surprising babies</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Jesus is God's powerful Son who fulfils God's promises.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning what happened to Zechariah's voice, and why.</li><li>We are learning that God announced both John and Jesus before they were even born.</li><li>I can name John and Jesus and begin to wonder who they are and why their births mattered.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Check that students can recall why John's birth was extraordinary and can name both babies by the end of the session.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Screen/device to display the comic panels (already embedded in the app)</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"3\">1</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Recap &amp; reveal</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2–3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">“Last time, Zechariah had just seen an angel — and now he couldn’t speak! The angel had told him that he and his wife Elizabeth, who were very old and had never been able to have children, would have a son. They were to name him John.” Show panel 04. Note for yourself (not necessarily stated outright unless asked): Zechariah lost his voice because he doubted the angel’s promise — you can confirm this if students ask directly.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Show comic panel 04-elizabeth-pregnant.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">2</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Why John's birth mattered</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2–3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Pause on the wonder of this moment for Elizabeth before moving on to Mary's story.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Why was John's birth so extraordinary?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Guide toward: his parents were very old and had never been able to have children before — this was humanly impossible.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"3\">3</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Gabriel visits Mary</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">4–5 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">“About one month later, God sent his angel Gabriel to visit a young woman named Mary.” Show panels 05–07 in sequence, reading each line aloud with feeling — these are the angel’s actual words to Mary.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Show comic panels 05-gabriel-visits-mary, 06-dont-be-afraid-jesus, and 07-jesus-will-be-great in sequence.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">4</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Naming the babies</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2–3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Check the class has caught both names before moving into the open wonder question.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">What were the names of these two important babies?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">John and Jesus.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">5</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Open wonder question</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2–3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Close today’s session by opening the big question without answering it — this is meant to stay unresolved until later in the week.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Who do you think John is? Who do you think Jesus is?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Accept any guesses — this is genuinely open at this point in the story; we’ll build the answer across the week.</td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Day 3 reveals more of what the angel said about Jesus becoming King, and shows his birth.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Wednesday: A promised King is born":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">10–15 minutes</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Luke 1:30–35; 2:1–7</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">A promised King is born</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Jesus is God's powerful Son who fulfils God's promises.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning that the angel announced Jesus would be an eternal King.</li><li>We are learning that Mary trusted God even though she didn't understand how it would happen.</li><li>I can say this week's memory verse and explain what it means in my own words.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Listen for whether students can repeat the memory verse with reasonable accuracy by the end of the activity, and whether they can articulate what made John's and Jesus's births unusual.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Screen/device to display the comic panels (already embedded in the app)</li><li>Memory verse displayed on screen (built into the app's verse slide)</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"3\">1</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Recap &amp; the King promise</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3–4 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">“An angel told Mary she would have a very special son, named Jesus. Let’s hear what else the angel said about him.” Show panel 08, reading the angel’s words aloud with weight — this is the heart of the promise: an eternal kingdom.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Show comic panel 08-kingdom-never-end.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"3\">2</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Mary's question &amp; the angel's answer</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2–3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Show panel 09. Point out that Mary asked a very reasonable question (“How will this happen?”) and that the angel's answer pointed to God's power, not a normal explanation.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Show comic panel 09-how-will-this-happen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"3\">3</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Jesus is born</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2–3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">“Mary believed the angel and trusted God. Some time later, baby Jesus was born.” Show panels 10 and 11. Land on the line “Nothing is impossible for God!” as the takeaway image/moment.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Show comic panels 10-nativity and 11-baby-jesus.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"3\">4</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Memory verse</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3–4 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Introduce the verse slide (Luke 1:32–33, CEV). Read it together as a class two or three times, then invite the class to try saying it once without looking at the screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Use the built-in verse block, then run the say-it-together activity that follows it in the app.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">5</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Closing question</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Close by drawing the comparison between an ordinary birth and what made John's and Jesus's so different.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">What's normally needed for a baby to be born — and what was different about John and Jesus?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Guide toward: babies normally just come from a mum and dad; these two were announced in advance, by an angel, and given specific important jobs before they were even born.</td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Day 4 brings it all together — who John and Jesus are, and what each was born to do.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Memory Verse</div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">“He will be great and will be called the Son of God Most High. The Lord God will make him king, as his ancestor David was. He will rule the people of Israel forever, and his kingdom will never end.” — <em>Luke 1:32–33 (CEV)</em></blockquote></div>"},"Thursday: Why John and Jesus matter":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">10–15 minutes</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Luke 1 (review)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Why John and Jesus matter</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Jesus is God's powerful Son who fulfils God's promises.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are bringing together everything we've learned this week about John and Jesus.</li><li>I can explain, in my own words, who John is and what he was born to do.</li><li>I can explain, in my own words, who Jesus is and what he was born to do.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">This is the week's wrap-up — use the four review questions as an informal check of understanding before moving on. Listen for whether students connect John to 'getting people ready' and Jesus to 'the promised King.'</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Memory verse displayed on screen (built into the app's verse slide)</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"2\">1</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Recap intro</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">1–2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">“This week we’ve met two very special babies — John and Jesus. Let’s see what we’ve figured out about why they’re so important.” Frame this session as pulling together the whole week's story rather than introducing anything new.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">2</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Who is Jesus? Who is John?</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">4–5 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Work through these two questions one at a time, letting students draw on everything seen across the week. Don't rush — this is the moment the whole week's build-up pays off.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Who is Jesus? · Who is John?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Jesus: guide toward the Son of God, the promised King. John: guide toward he goes ahead of Jesus to get people ready.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">3</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>What were they born to do?</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3–4 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Push one level deeper from identity into purpose/mission for each of them.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">What do you think John was born to do? · What do you think Jesus was born to do?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">John: he would help turn people back to God and get them ready for the Lord to come. Jesus: he was born to be King forever — and his kingdom would never end.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"3\">4</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Memory verse from memory</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2–3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Bring back the verse slide one more time. Challenge the class to recite it together fully from memory this time, with the screen as a backup only.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Use the built-in verse block, then the say-it-from-memory activity that follows it in the app.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"3\">5</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Closing prayer</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">1–2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Close the week together with the prayer in the app, said as a class.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Use the built-in prayer block to close.</td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Week 2 moves on to the next chapter of the Connect B1 Upper Primary manual.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Memory Verse</div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">“He will be great and will be called the Son of God Most High. The Lord God will make him king, as his ancestor David was. He will rule the people of Israel forever, and his kingdom will never end.” — <em>Luke 1:32–33 (CEV)</em></blockquote></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Closing Prayer</div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Dear God, thank you for the births of John and Jesus. Please help us understand how important their jobs were. And please help us find out more about them as this term goes on. Amen.</blockquote></div>"}}},"Week 2: Ready ... set ...":{"label":"Day","children":{"Week Overview":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Luke 3:1—4:13</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Jesus is God's powerful Son who fulfils God's promises.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Source</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Connect B1 Upper Primary — Lesson 2</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Monday: Getting ready for someone important":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">10–15 minutes</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Luke 3:1–6</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Getting ready for someone important</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Jesus is God's powerful Son who fulfils God's promises.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning that John's job was to get people ready for Jesus' arrival.</li><li>We are learning this week's memory verse and what it means to 'turn back to God.'</li><li>I can explain why John's message of repentance and baptism mattered.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Listen for whether students can explain, in their own words, what job John had — note who can connect 'getting ready' to John preparing people for Jesus by the end of the session.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Screen/device to display the comic panels (already embedded in the app)</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">1</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Dinner-guest hook</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2–3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Open with the imagined scenario — really let students picture it. &quot;Imagine someone really important was suddenly coming to your house for dinner tonight.&quot; Push for specifics: would they clean their room? Change clothes? The point to land softly (don't over-explain yet) is that getting ready properly takes real action, not just good intentions.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Imagine someone really important was suddenly coming to your house for dinner tonight. What would you do to get ready?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Accept any responses — cleaning up, tidying their room, putting on best clothes, etc. The point is that getting ready takes real effort and action, not just words.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"3\">2</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Reintroducing John</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2–3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">&quot;Last term we met John — the baby whose birth was announced by an angel before he was even born. Now John is grown up, and he has a very important job: getting people ready for someone far more important than a dinner guest.&quot; This is a deliberate callback to Week 1 — give students a moment to remember John if they need it.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Show comic image wk2-01-john-in-desert.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"3\">3</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>John's message</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3–4 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Show the next panel and read John's words aloud with some weight — this is the heart of his preaching. Introduce this week's memory verse here directly from the panel's words.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Show comic image wk2-02-john-preaching-river, then introduce the verse slide (Luke 3:3, CEV).</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"3\">4</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Isaiah's prophecy</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2–3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Show the Isaiah panel. Point out that this prophecy was written 700 years before John was even born — God had a plan for John's job long, long in advance.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Show comic image wk2-03-isaiah-prophecy.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">5</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Closing question</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Close by drawing the link explicit between the opening hook and John's message — getting ready for God isn't just saying sorry, it's a real change. Don't fully resolve this; it's the bridge into tomorrow's lesson on 'producing fruit.'</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">John's message wasn't just words — people had to actually do something. Why do you think 'getting ready' for God needed more than just saying sorry?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Guide toward: real change shows up in what we do, not just what we say. This sets up tomorrow's 'fruit' teaching directly.</td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Day 2 unpacks what 'getting ready' actually looks like in action — through John's teaching about fruit trees and his specific instructions to the crowds.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Memory Verse</div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">“Turn back to God and be baptised! Then your sins will be forgiven.” — <em>Luke 3:3 (CEV)</em></blockquote></div>"},"Tuesday: What 'getting ready' looks like":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">10–15 minutes</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Luke 3:7–18</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">What 'getting ready' looks like</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Jesus is God's powerful Son who fulfils God's promises.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning that John used the picture of fruit trees to teach what real repentance looks like.</li><li>We are learning that John gave specific, practical instructions to different groups of people.</li><li>I can name one specific action that would show real change in my own life.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Listen for whether students can explain the fruit-tree metaphor in their own words, and whether they can name a concrete (not vague) example of 'producing fruit' during the activity.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Screen/device to display the comic panels (already embedded in the app)</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"3\">1</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Recap &amp; fruit trees</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2–3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">&quot;Yesterday, John told everyone to turn back to God. But what does that actually look like? John had an answer.&quot; Show the fruit-tree panel and let the metaphor land before asking the question.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Show comic image wk2-04-fruit-trees-teaching.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">2</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Unpacking the metaphor</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2–3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">This question does the real teaching work — don't rush past it. Make sure the class actually arrives at 'you'd judge by what it produces' before moving on.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">If a tree never grew any fruit, how would you know just by looking at it whether it was healthy or not?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Guide toward: you'd know by what it actually produces, not by what it looks like on the outside. That's John's point — real change in us shows up in what we actually do.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"3\">3</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>John's specific instructions</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3–4 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Show both panels in sequence. Point out that John didn't give the same instruction to everyone — he gave specific, practical advice depending on each group's actual situation and temptations.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Show comic images wk2-05-what-should-we-do and wk2-06-tax-collectors-soldiers in sequence.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"3\">4</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Personal application activity</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2–3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">This is the activity where the lesson becomes personal. Push for something concrete and specific rather than a vague good intention — &quot;be nicer&quot; doesn't count, &quot;share my lunch with someone who doesn't have one&quot; does.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">John gave different people different instructions, but the idea was the same each time. Invite a few students to share their specific 'fruit' idea for the week.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"5\">5</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Someone more powerful is coming</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Show the final panel of the day. Let the question hang — this is meant to make students do the connecting work themselves rather than telling them outright.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Show comic image wk2-07-could-john-be-king.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">John just said someone more powerful than him is coming. After everything we know from last term, who do you think that is?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Jesus — this is a deliberate callback. Let students make the connection themselves before confirming.</td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Day 3 shows Jesus himself arriving at the river to be baptised by John.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Wednesday: Jesus is baptised":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">10–15 minutes</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Luke 3:21–22</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Jesus is baptised</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Jesus is God's powerful Son who fulfils God's promises.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning that Jesus came to the same river where John was baptising people.</li><li>We are learning what happened immediately after Jesus was baptised — the Holy Spirit and the voice from heaven.</li><li>I can say this week's memory verse and explain what it means in my own words.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Listen for whether students can describe what happened at Jesus' baptism (the dove, the voice) and whether they can repeat the memory verse with reasonable accuracy.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Screen/device to display the comic panels (already embedded in the app)</li><li>Memory verse displayed on screen (built into the app's verse slide)</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"3\">1</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Jesus arrives</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2–3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">&quot;John told everyone someone more powerful than him was coming. One day, that someone arrived — at the very same river.&quot; Show the arrival panel and let the moment build a little before revealing what happens.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Show comic image wk2-08-jesus-arrives-at-river.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"5\">2</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Jesus is baptised</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3–4 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Show the baptism panel, then ask the puzzle question. This is meant to provoke real thinking — Jesus didn't need forgiveness the way everyone else did, so why be baptised? Don't over-resolve it; a short class discussion is enough.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Show comic image wk2-09-jesus-baptised.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">John had been telling everyone to turn back to God and be baptised because of their sins. So why do you think Jesus — who hadn't done anything wrong — wanted to be baptised too?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">This is genuinely open and a bit puzzling on purpose. Accept any thoughtful guesses; the main point for today is what happens next, not fully resolving this question.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"3\">3</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>The Holy Spirit and the voice</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3–4 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Show the dove/voice panel and read the heavenly voice's words aloud with weight — this is one of the most important moments in the whole Gospel of Luke, confirming exactly who Jesus is.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Show comic image wk2-10-holy-spirit-descends.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"3\">4</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Memory verse</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2–3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Bring back this week's verse slide (Luke 3:3). Read it together two or three times, then invite the class to try it once without looking.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Use the built-in verse block, then run the say-it-together activity that follows it in the app.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">5</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Closing check</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">1–2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Quick recall question to close — make sure the key detail (whose voice, what it said) has landed before moving to tomorrow's wrap-up.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Whose voice spoke from heaven, and what did the voice call Jesus?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">God's voice — calling Jesus his Son, whom he loves.</td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Day 4 brings the week together — what John's job was, and what Jesus' baptism revealed about who he is.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Memory Verse</div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">“Turn back to God and be baptised! Then your sins will be forgiven.” — <em>Luke 3:3 (CEV)</em></blockquote></div>"},"Thursday: Bringing it together":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">10–15 minutes</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Luke 3:1—4:13 (review)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Bringing it together</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Jesus is God's powerful Son who fulfils God's promises.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are bringing together everything we've learned this week about John and Jesus' baptism.</li><li>I can explain, in my own words, what John's job was and what it means to 'produce fruit.'</li><li>I can explain what happened at Jesus' baptism and what it revealed about who he is.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">This is the week's wrap-up — use the three review questions as an informal check of understanding. Listen for whether students connect 'fruit' to real action, and whether they can recall the dove and the voice from heaven.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Memory verse displayed on screen (built into the app's verse slide)</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"3\">1</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Recap intro</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">1–2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">&quot;This week we watched John get everyone ready, and then watched the very person he was preparing the way for finally arrive.&quot; Show the wide baptism scene as a visual anchor for the whole week's story before moving into review questions.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Show comic image wk2-11-baptism-wide-scene.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">2</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>John's job</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Quick recall — keep this moving, it's the simplest of the three review questions.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">What was John's job, in your own words?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Guide toward: getting people ready for Jesus, by telling them to turn back to God and show real change.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">3</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Producing fruit</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2–3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Try to get specific recall here — if students remember their own Tuesday example, that's a good sign the lesson stuck. Affirm whatever they share.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">What does it mean to 'produce fruit' — and can you remember the example you picked on Tuesday?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Showing real change through actions, not just words. Let students recall and share their own specific example from Day 2 if they can.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">4</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Jesus' baptism</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2–3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">This is the theological high point of the week — make sure it lands clearly before moving to the verse.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">What happened right after Jesus was baptised, and what did it tell us about who he is?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">The Holy Spirit came down like a dove, and God's voice called Jesus his Son, whom he loves — confirming Jesus is exactly who the angel said he'd be.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"3\">5</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Memory verse from memory</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Bring back the verse slide one more time. Challenge the class to recite it fully from memory, with the screen as backup only.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Use the built-in verse block, then the say-it-from-memory activity that follows it in the app.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"3\">6</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Closing prayer</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">1–2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Close the week together with the prayer in the app, said as a class.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Use the built-in prayer block to close.</td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Week 3 moves on to the next chapter of the Connect B1 Upper Primary manual — Jesus returning to Nazareth and claiming to be the promised rescuer.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Memory Verse</div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">“Turn back to God and be baptised! Then your sins will be forgiven.” — <em>Luke 3:3 (CEV)</em></blockquote></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Closing Prayer</div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Dear God, thank you for John, who got people ready for Jesus. Thank you for Jesus, your Son, whom you love. Please help us turn back to you this week, not just in what we say, but in what we do. Amen.</blockquote></div>"}}},"Week 3: The one you've all been waiting for":{"label":"Day","children":{"Week Overview":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Luke 4:14—30</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Jesus is God's powerful Son who fulfils God's promises.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Source</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Connect B1 Upper Primary — Lesson 3</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Monday: Jesus says who he is":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">10–15 minutes</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Luke 4:14–21</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Jesus says who he is</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Jesus is God's powerful Son who fulfils God's promises.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning that Jesus went back to his hometown synagogue and read a promise from Isaiah about a coming rescuer.</li><li>We are learning that Jesus directly claimed this promise was about himself.</li><li>I can explain what kind of people Jesus said he came for.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Listen for whether students can explain, in their own words, that Jesus stood up and claimed the Isaiah promise was about himself — note who can name at least two groups of people the passage says he came for.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Screen/device to display the comic panels (already embedded in the app)</li><li>Memory verse displayed on screen (built into the app's verse slide)</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">1</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>The long wait</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2–3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Open with the personal question — let a few students share something they waited a long time for. Don't rush this; the feeling of anticipation finally paying off is the emotional anchor for the whole week.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Has there ever been something you waited for ages to happen — and then it actually did? What was that like?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Accept any answer — a birthday, a holiday, a long-awaited event. The point is the feeling of a long wait finally being over, which is exactly the feeling we're building toward today.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"3\">2</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Recap and set-up</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Briefly recap the story so far across the term: John got everyone ready, Jesus was baptised, God called him his Son. Today we find out what Jesus himself has to say about who he is.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Show comic image wk3-01-jesus-walks-to-nazareth.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"5\">3</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Jesus reads the scroll</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3–4 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Show the synagogue panel, then bring up the verse slide and read it together slowly — this is a dense, important passage. Take time over it rather than rushing through.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Show comic image wk3-02-reads-isaiah-scroll, then the verse slide (Luke 4:18–19, CEV).</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Jesus reads a list of things this promised person was supposed to do. What kinds of people does the list say he's come for?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">The poor, prisoners, the blind, everyone who suffers — people who are struggling or stuck. Jesus is describing someone who rescues and restores.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"5\">4</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Today, in front of you</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3–4 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Show the final panel and read Jesus' line aloud with weight: &quot;What you have heard me read has come true today.&quot; Make sure students grasp the size of the claim — Jesus isn't just teaching about an old prophecy, he's saying it's about him, right now, in that room.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Show comic image wk3-03-fulfilled-today.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Jesus didn't just read an old promise — he said it about himself, right there, out loud. What is Jesus actually claiming about who he is?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Jesus is claiming to be the promised rescuer-King the Scriptures were talking about — God's chosen one, sent to save and restore people. This is a huge, direct claim.</td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Day 2 shows how the crowd in his own hometown actually responds to this huge claim — and it isn't what you'd expect.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Memory Verse</div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">“The Lord’s Spirit has come to me, because he has chosen me to tell the good news to the poor. The Lord has sent me to announce freedom for prisoners, to give sight to the blind, to free everyone who suffers, and to say, ‘This is the year the Lord has chosen.’” — <em>Luke 4:18–19 (CEV)</em></blockquote></div>"},"Tuesday: The hometown crowd turns":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">10–15 minutes</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Luke 4:22–24</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">The hometown crowd turns</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Jesus is God's powerful Son who fulfils God's promises.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning how the people of Nazareth responded to Jesus' claim about himself.</li><li>We are learning that familiarity with Jesus led to doubt rather than belief for many in his hometown.</li><li>I can explain why Jesus didn't perform miracles in Nazareth.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Listen for whether students can explain that the crowd's familiarity with Jesus — knowing him as 'just' Joseph's son — made them doubt rather than believe his claim.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Screen/device to display the comic panels (already embedded in the app)</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"2\">1</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Recap yesterday's claim</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">1–2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Quickly remind the class of yesterday's big claim — Jesus said the Isaiah promise was about him. Today we see how people actually responded to that.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"5\">2</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Amazed, then doubtful</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3–4 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Show the panel and read the crowd's question aloud. Let the discomfort of the question sit for a moment before discussing it — this is the turning point of the whole story.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Show comic image wk3-04-josephs-son.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">&quot;Isn't this Joseph's son?&quot; — why do you think knowing Jesus since he was a kid made it harder for these people to accept his huge claim, rather than easier?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Guide toward: when you've known someone your whole life as 'just' the carpenter's son, it's hard to suddenly see them as God's promised rescuer. Familiarity made them doubt rather than believe.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"5\">3</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>No miracles at home</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3–4 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Show both panels in sequence — first the healing happening elsewhere, then the contrast of nothing happening in Nazareth. Make the contrast clear and let students draw the conclusion themselves with the question that follows.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Show comic images wk3-05-healing-other-towns and wk3-06-no-miracles-nazareth in sequence.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Jesus had done amazing things in other towns, but not in his own hometown. What does that tell us about how the people of Nazareth were responding to him?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">They weren't willing to trust and believe in him the way people in other towns had. Their familiarity with Jesus had turned into doubt and rejection, not faith.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"5\">4</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Personal reflection</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2–3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Bring the lesson home with a personal connection — invite a few students to share without putting anyone on the spot. The aim is to help them feel, not just know, why familiarity can get in the way of really seeing someone.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Run the reflection activity as a quick share-around or pair discussion.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Think of a time it was hard to take someone seriously just because you knew them really well — a sibling, a classmate, a friend. What made it hard to see them differently?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Accept any honest answer — the point is simply naming that familiarity can make it harder, not easier, to see someone clearly.</td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Day 3 shows the crowd's doubt turning into something far more dangerous — and how Jesus responds to it.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Wednesday: From doubt to danger":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">10–15 minutes</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Luke 4:28–30</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">From doubt to danger</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Jesus is God's powerful Son who fulfils God's promises.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning that the crowd in Nazareth moved from doubting Jesus to trying to physically harm him.</li><li>We are learning that Jesus walked away from the danger completely unharmed.</li><li>I can say this week's memory verse and explain what it means in my own words.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Listen for whether students can describe how the crowd's response escalated, and whether they can offer a thoughtful idea about what Jesus calmly walking away reveals about him.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Screen/device to display the comic panels (already embedded in the app)</li><li>Memory verse displayed on screen (built into the app's verse slide)</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"5\">1</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>From doubt to anger</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3–4 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Show the panel and read the caption with appropriate seriousness — this is intense content, so keep the tone calm and matter-of-fact rather than dramatic. Acknowledge it's a strong reaction before moving to the discussion question.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Show comic image wk3-07-crowd-drives-to-cliff.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">The crowd's reaction goes from doubt all the way to wanting to hurt Jesus. Why do you think people can react so strongly against someone who challenges what they thought they already knew?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Accept thoughtful guesses — being told you're wrong about someone, or that the truth is bigger than you assumed, can feel threatening, and people sometimes respond to that feeling with anger rather than reflection.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"5\">2</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Jesus walks away</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3–4 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Show the final panel. Let students notice how calm Jesus looks compared to the chaos behind him — that contrast is the point.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Show comic image wk3-08-jesus-slips-away.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Jesus calmly walks away from a crowd that just tried to throw him off a cliff. What does that tell us about him?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Guide toward: Jesus wasn't in danger the way an ordinary person would be — his time hadn't come yet, and nothing could happen to him outside of God's plan. It's another clue about just how powerful he really is.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"3\">3</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Memory verse</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2–3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Bring back this term's verse slide (Luke 3:3b). Read it together two or three times, then invite the class to try it once without looking.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Use the built-in verse block, then run a say-it-together activity.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"2\">4</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Closing thought</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">1–2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Close by naming plainly that today we've seen two very different responses to Jesus already forming — rejection in Nazareth. Tomorrow we'll pull together what we've learned about who Jesus actually is.</td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Day 4 brings the week together — what Jesus claimed about himself, and the very different ways people respond to him.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Memory Verse</div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">“Turn back to God and be baptised! Then your sins will be forgiven.” — <em>Luke 3:3b (CEV)</em></blockquote></div>"},"Thursday: Who is Jesus, and how will you respond?":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">10–15 minutes</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Luke 4:14–30 (review)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Who is Jesus, and how will you respond?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Jesus is God's powerful Son who fulfils God's promises.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are bringing together everything we've learned this week about Jesus' claim and how people responded to it.</li><li>I can explain in my own words what Jesus claimed about himself.</li><li>I can say this week's memory verse and explain what it means in my own words.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">This is the week's wrap-up — use the two review questions as an informal check of understanding. Listen for whether students can state Jesus' claim clearly and offer a thoughtful idea about why responses to him differed so sharply.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Screen/device to display the comic panels (already embedded in the app)</li><li>Memory verse displayed on screen (built into the app's verse slide)</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"3\">1</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Recap the week</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2–3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">&quot;This week we watched Jesus stand up in his hometown synagogue and say, out loud, that he was the promised rescuer everyone had been waiting for. Let's pull together everything we've seen.&quot; Frame this session as pulling together the whole week rather than introducing anything new.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Show comic image wk3-03-fulfilled-today.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">2</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>What did Jesus claim?</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2–3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Let students put the claim in their own words before you confirm it — this is a good check of real understanding rather than just recall.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">In your own words, what did Jesus claim about himself this week?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">That he is the promised rescuer-King the Scriptures spoke about — sent by God to bring good news, freedom, and healing to people who are struggling.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"5\">3</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Two responses</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3–4 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Show the cliff-edge panel again as a visual reminder of how seriously the rejection escalated, then work through the question. This is the moment the whole week's build-up pays off — don't rush it.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Show comic image wk3-07-crowd-drives-to-cliff.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">This week we saw two very different responses to Jesus: amazement, and then anger and rejection. Why do you think the very same claim can make one person amazed and another person furious?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Accept thoughtful answers — it often comes down to whether someone is willing to let go of what they thought they already knew and really listen, or whether they hold onto their assumptions instead.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"3\">4</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Memory verse from memory</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Bring back the verse slide one more time. Challenge the class to recite it fully from memory, with the screen as backup only.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Use the built-in verse block, then the say-it-from-memory activity that follows it in the app.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"3\">5</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Closing prayer</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">1–2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Close the week together with the prayer in the app, said as a class.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Use the built-in prayer block to close.</td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Week 4 moves on to the next chapter of the Connect B1 Upper Primary manual — Jesus calling his first disciples.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Memory Verse</div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">“Turn back to God and be baptised! Then your sins will be forgiven.” — <em>Luke 3:3b (CEV)</em></blockquote></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Closing Prayer</div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Dear God, thank you for sending Jesus, the one we've all been waiting for. Thank you that he came with good news for people who are struggling. Help us to really listen to him, instead of just thinking we already know who he is. Amen.</blockquote></div>"}}},"Week 4: How perfect are you?":{"label":"Day","children":{"Week Overview":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Luke 5:1–11</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Jesus is God's powerful Son who fulfils God's promises.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Source</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Connect B1 Upper Primary — Lesson 4</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Monday: A bad night, and a strange instruction":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">10–15 minutes</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Luke 5:1–5</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">A bad night, and a strange instruction</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Jesus is God's powerful Son who fulfils God's promises.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning that Peter and his friends had fished all night and caught nothing.</li><li>We are learning that Jesus told Peter to try again, in the daytime, in the deep water — against all of Peter's fishing experience.</li><li>I can explain why Peter's choice to obey anyway showed he was starting to trust Jesus.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Listen for whether students can explain that Peter chose to obey Jesus' instruction even though it went against his own experience and expertise as a fisherman.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Screen/device to display the comic panels (already embedded in the app)</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"3\">1</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>A rough night</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2–3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Set the scene with the opening story, then show the map and washing-nets panels. Make sure students understand that fishing was Peter's actual job — his livelihood depended on nights like this going well.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Show comic images wk4-00-galilee-map and wk4-01-fishermen-washing-nets.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"5\">2</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Caught nothing</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2–3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Show the panel and let the disappointment of the line land before discussing it.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Show comic image wk4-02-caught-nothing.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Imagine you'd worked all night at something hard and had absolutely nothing to show for it. How do you think Peter was feeling right at this moment?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Accept any reasonable answer — tired, frustrated, discouraged, maybe even a bit embarrassed in front of the watching crowd. The point is to notice Peter isn't in a great mood when Jesus arrives.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"5\">3</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Jesus teaches, then asks again</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3–4 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Show the next three panels in sequence — Jesus borrowing the boat to teach, then his surprising instruction to go back out, and Peter's tired-but-willing reply. Read Peter's line aloud slowly; it's the turning point of the day.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Show comic images wk4-03-row-out-a-little, wk4-04-teaches-from-boat, and wk4-05-let-down-nets in sequence.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Peter's a professional fisherman — he knows this isn't a good time of day to fish, and he's exhausted. But he does it anyway because Jesus told him to. What does that tell us about how Peter was starting to see Jesus?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Even though it didn't make sense based on his own experience and expertise, Peter chose to trust and obey Jesus' instruction. He's starting to recognise that Jesus is worth listening to, even when it's hard or seems pointless.</td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Day 2 shows exactly what happens when those tired, doubtful nets actually go down.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Tuesday: More fish than the boats can hold":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">10–15 minutes</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Luke 5:6–7</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">More fish than the boats can hold</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Jesus is God's powerful Son who fulfils God's promises.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning that the catch of fish was so large it nearly sank two boats.</li><li>We are learning that the scale of this miracle points to Jesus having power over creation itself.</li><li>I can explain why the size of the catch matters to the story, not just the fact that there was a catch.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Listen for whether students can explain that the overwhelming scale of the catch is what reveals Jesus' power, rather than treating it as just a lucky fishing story.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Screen/device to display the comic panels (already embedded in the app)</li><li>Memory verse displayed on screen (built into the app's verse slide)</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"3\">1</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Recap and the catch</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Quickly recap yesterday's tired, reluctant obedience, then show the first panel of today's miracle.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Show comic image wk4-06-nets-fill-with-fish.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"5\">2</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Nets breaking, boats sinking</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3–4 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Show both panels in sequence and read the dialogue with real energy — this is meant to feel like chaos and astonishment, not a calm fishing trip.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Show comic images wk4-07-help-nets-breaking and wk4-08-two-boats-sinking in sequence.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">This isn't just 'quite a good catch' — it's so many fish that two boats nearly sink. Why do you think the story makes such a big deal of exactly how many fish there were?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Guide toward: the sheer scale makes it obvious this wasn't luck or skill — it's something only God's power could do. The size of the miracle is the point; it shows us something about who Jesus really is.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"3\">3</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Memory verse</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Bring back this term's verse slide and read it together.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Use the built-in verse block.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"3\">4</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Picture the scene</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3–4 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Run the activity as a quick acting-out moment or a vivid group description — the goal is to help the chaos and scale of the miracle really land before moving into tomorrow's quieter, more serious response.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Run as a brief group activity — acting out or describing the scene.</td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Day 3 shows how Peter himself reacts to standing in the middle of all this — and it's not what you might expect.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Memory Verse</div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">“Turn back to God and be baptised! Then your sins will be forgiven.” — <em>Luke 3:3b (CEV)</em></blockquote></div>"},"Wednesday: \"I am a sinner\"":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">10–15 minutes</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Luke 5:8–10</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">&quot;I am a sinner&quot;</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Jesus is God's powerful Son who fulfils God's promises.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning that Peter responded to the miracle by recognising his own sinfulness in front of Jesus.</li><li>We are learning that no-one is perfect except God (Romans 3:23).</li><li>I can explain why Jesus' power led Peter to think about his own sin, and how Jesus responded to that.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Listen for whether students can connect Jesus' display of power to Peter's sudden awareness of his own sinfulness, and whether they can state in their own words that no-one is perfect except God.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Screen/device to display the comic panels (already embedded in the app)</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"5\">1</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Peter's surprising reaction</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3–4 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Show the panel and read both lines of dialogue clearly — Peter's fear and confession, then Jesus' calm, kind response. Let the contrast between the two reactions sit for a moment.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Show comic image wk4-09-peter-im-a-sinner.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">You'd think Peter would be thrilled — the biggest catch of his life! But instead, he falls down and says, &quot;Don't come near me, I am a sinner.&quot; Why do you think seeing Jesus' power made Peter think about his own sin, of all things?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Guide toward: seeing this much power up close made Peter realise exactly who he was standing in front of — not just a clever teacher, but someone with power that belongs to God alone. That realisation made Peter suddenly very aware of how imperfect he was by comparison.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"5\">2</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Where do we really stand?</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3–4 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Run this as a quick, honest discussion rather than a game — the comparison isn't between students, it's between any of us and God's perfection. Bring in the verse once the discussion has had a chance to land.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Run the 1–10 scale discussion, then show the Romans 3:23 verse.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">If you imagine a scale from 1 to 10, where 1 is perfectly good and 10 is the worst possible, where would most people place themselves? Now where does the Bible say God belongs on that scale?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Most people will place themselves somewhere in the middle, often closer to 'pretty good.' But Romans 3:23 says all of us have sinned and fallen short of God's glory — the real comparison isn't between people, it's between us and God's perfection, and next to that, no-one is actually close.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">3</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Jesus' response</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2–3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Close by drawing attention to what Jesus does next — not rejection, but a calling. This sets up tomorrow's final response from Peter and his friends.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Jesus' response to Peter wasn't to agree and walk away — it was to give him a job. What does that tell us about how Jesus treats people who recognise their own sin?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Jesus doesn't reject Peter for admitting he's a sinner — instead, he calls him into something new. Recognising our own imperfection isn't the end of the story with Jesus; it's actually the starting point for being used by him.</td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Day 4 brings the week to a close with how Peter and his friends actually respond to that calling.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Memory Verse</div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">“All of us have sinned and fallen short of God's glory.” — <em>Romans 3:23 (CEV)</em></blockquote></div>"},"Thursday: Leaving it all behind":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">10–15 minutes</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Luke 5:11 (review)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Leaving it all behind</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Jesus is God's powerful Son who fulfils God's promises.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are bringing together everything we've learned this week about Jesus' power and Peter's response to it.</li><li>I can explain why Peter and his friends chose to leave everything to follow Jesus.</li><li>I can say what this week's events showed us about who Jesus is.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">This is the week's wrap-up — use the two review questions as an informal check of understanding. Listen for whether students can name both the power and the holiness themes from this week, and whether they can offer a thoughtful idea about why the size of the miracle made following Jesus an easier choice.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Screen/device to display the comic panels (already embedded in the app)</li><li>Memory verse displayed on screen (built into the app's verse slide)</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"5\">1</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Recap the week</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2–3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">&quot;This week we've watched Peter go from an exhausting, fruitless night of fishing, to witnessing the biggest catch of his life, to realising exactly who he was standing in front of. Today we see how the story ends.&quot; Show the wide scene as a visual recap of the miracle itself.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Show comic image wk4-11-wide-boat-scene.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Quick recap: what two things did we learn about Jesus this week from this one event?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Jesus has power over creation itself (the size of the catch), and Jesus is so perfectly holy that being near him makes ordinary people aware of their own sin (Peter's reaction).</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"5\">2</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Leaving it all behind</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3–4 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Show the final panel and give the decision its proper weight — this wasn't a small choice. Let students wrestle with the question before offering the guidance.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Show comic image wk4-10-leave-everything-follow.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Peter and his friends just had the best catch of their entire careers — and then they walked away from all of it, boats and fish included, to follow Jesus. Why do you think the size of the miracle actually made that decision easier, not harder?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Accept thoughtful answers — having just seen exactly how much power and provision Jesus has, it made more sense to trust him with their whole future than to stay focused on the fish in front of them.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"3\">3</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Memory verse</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">1–2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Bring back the verse slide one more time and read it together as a class.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Use the built-in verse block.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"3\">4</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Closing prayer</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">1–2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Close the week together with the prayer in the app, said as a class.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Use the built-in prayer block to close.</td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Week 5 moves on to the next chapter of the Connect B1 Upper Primary manual — Jesus showing his authority to forgive sins and heal.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Memory Verse</div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">“Turn back to God and be baptised! Then your sins will be forgiven.” — <em>Luke 3:3b (CEV)</em></blockquote></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Closing Prayer</div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Dear God, thank you for being more powerful than anything we could imagine, and thank you that you are perfect when we are not. Help us, like Peter, to be honest about the ways we fall short — and to trust you enough to follow you with our whole lives. Amen.</blockquote></div>"}}},"Week 5: Who do you think Jesus is?":{"label":"Day","children":{"Week Overview":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Luke 5:17–26</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Jesus is God's powerful Son who fulfils God's promises.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Source</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Connect B1 Upper Primary — Lesson 5</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Monday: Four friends and a problem":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">10–15 minutes</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Luke 5:17–19</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Four friends and a problem</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Jesus is God's powerful Son who fulfils God's promises.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning that four friends carried a man who couldn't walk to see Jesus, hoping he could heal him.</li><li>We are learning that when the crowd blocked their way, the friends found another way in — lowering their friend through the roof.</li><li>I can explain what the friends' determination shows us about their faith in Jesus.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Listen for whether students can describe the obstacle the friends faced (the crowded house) and explain that their unusual solution shows real faith that Jesus could help.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Screen/device to display the comic panels (already embedded in the app)</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">1</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Going to extreme lengths for a friend</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Open with the warm-up question before introducing today's story — it primes students to notice the friends' effort once the comic begins.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Think of a friend who really needed your help with something. How far would you actually go to get them what they needed — would you climb something, carry something heavy, or push through a big crowd?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Take a few quick responses — we're building towards a story about four friends who go to genuinely extreme lengths for someone they care about. There's no wrong answer here; we just want students thinking about effort and persistence before we meet our four friends.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"3\">2</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>A big crowd, an important audience</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2–3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Set the scene with the first panel. Make sure students notice that Pharisees and experts in the Law of Moses are present — this detail matters later in the week.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Show comic image wk5-01-jesus-teaches-crowd.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"5\">3</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Carried, but blocked</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3–4 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Show the next two panels in sequence. Let the frustration of being blocked land before discussing it.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Show comic images wk5-02-man-carried-to-jesus and wk5-03-too-crowded in sequence.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">The friends have already carried their friend all this way — and now the door is literally blocked. Most people would give up here. What does it tell you about these four friends that they didn't?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Guide toward: they clearly cared enough about their friend to keep looking for another way, rather than turning back. Their persistence is the whole reason the rest of the story is even possible.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"5\">4</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Through the roof</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3–4 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Show the climbing panel, then give the dramatic roof-breaking panel real time and attention — it's the visual highlight of the week. Read the action slowly.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Show comic images wk5-04-friends-go-upstairs and wk5-05-lowered-through-roof.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Breaking a hole in someone else's roof to lower your friend down through it is a pretty wild plan! Why do you think Luke includes this much detail about exactly how hard the friends worked to reach Jesus?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Accept thoughtful answers — the level of detail makes clear this wasn't a casual visit. It shows real, determined faith that Jesus could do something about this man's condition, and sets up tomorrow's question of what Jesus actually does in response.</td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Day 2 reveals exactly what Jesus says to the man on the mat — and why it causes such a stir.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Tuesday: \"Your sins are forgiven\"":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">10–15 minutes</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Luke 5:20–21</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">&quot;Your sins are forgiven&quot;</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Jesus is God's powerful Son who fulfils God's promises.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning that Jesus' first response to the man was to forgive his sins, not to heal him.</li><li>We are learning that the Pharisees and teachers of the Law reacted strongly because, in their understanding, only God can forgive sins.</li><li>I can explain why Jesus' words shocked the religious leaders watching.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Listen for whether students can explain that the Pharisees' shock came from their correct belief that only God can forgive sins, and that Jesus' words were therefore a claim about his own identity.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Screen/device to display the comic panels (already embedded in the app)</li><li>Memory verse displayed on screen (built into the app's verse slide)</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"5\">1</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>An unexpected first response</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3–4 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Recap yesterday's roof scene in a sentence, then show today's panel and let the line of dialogue sit — it's deliberately surprising.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Show comic image wk5-06-your-sins-are-forgiven.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">The man came to be healed of not being able to walk — but Jesus' first words are about forgiveness, not walking. Why do you think Jesus chose to deal with that first?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Accept thoughtful answers — Jesus is showing that the deeper problem every person has isn't physical, it's sin separating us from God. He's treating that as the more important issue, even though it's not the one anyone in the room was expecting him to mention.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"5\">2</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>The religious leaders react</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3–4 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Show the Pharisees' panel and read all three speech bubbles with real shock and outrage in your voice — this is meant to feel like a scandal in the room.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Show comic image wk5-07-pharisees-only-god-can-forgive.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">The Pharisees and teachers of the Law knew their Scriptures extremely well, and they immediately understood the size of what Jesus just claimed. According to them, who alone has the right to forgive sins?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Only God. That's exactly the Pharisees' objection — by saying 'your sins are forgiven' as if it were his own decision to make, Jesus was making a claim about himself that, if untrue, would be deeply blasphemous. They're not wrong that only God can forgive sins — they're wrong about who's standing in front of them.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"3\">3</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Memory verse</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Bring back this term's verse slide and read it together — point out that it's the same idea of forgiveness we've just seen Jesus claim the authority to give.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Use the built-in verse block.</td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Day 3 shows how Jesus answers the Pharisees' objection — with a question, and then with undeniable proof.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Memory Verse</div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">“Turn back to God and be baptised! Then your sins will be forgiven.” — <em>Luke 3:3b (CEV)</em></blockquote></div>"},"Wednesday: Proving the harder claim":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">10–15 minutes</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Luke 5:22–24</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Proving the harder claim</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Jesus is God's powerful Son who fulfils God's promises.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning that Jesus answered the Pharisees' objection with a question about which claim is easier to prove.</li><li>We are learning that Jesus healed the man visibly in order to prove his invisible authority to forgive sins.</li><li>I can explain why the healing miracle works as proof for the forgiveness claim.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Listen for whether students can explain, in their own words, that healing is the visible miracle that proves the invisible claim of forgiveness — not two separate, unrelated acts.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Screen/device to display the comic panels (already embedded in the app)</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"5\">1</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Jesus' counter-question</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3–4 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Show the panel and read Jesus' question slowly — give students real time to think it through before offering the guidance.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Show comic image wk5-08-jesus-easier-question.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Think about Jesus' question carefully: which of those two things — forgiving sins, or healing a paralysed man — is actually easier to prove happened, just by looking?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Healing is the easier one to prove — if the man gets up and walks, everyone can see it happened. Forgiving sins is invisible; you can't actually check whether it really happened just by looking. Jesus is about to use the visible, provable miracle to back up the invisible claim nobody could otherwise check.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"5\">2</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>The proof</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">4–5 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Show the authority panel and read Jesus' command with real weight. This is the turning point of the whole passage — give it space.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Show comic image wk5-09-authority-get-up-walk.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Jesus deliberately does the miracle everyone can see, specifically to prove the claim nobody could see. What does that tell us about why Jesus healed this man — was it only about kindness, or was there something bigger going on?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Guide toward: it was an act of kindness, but it was also evidence. Jesus is using a visible miracle to prove an invisible truth — that he really does have the authority of God himself to forgive sins. The healing answers the Pharisees' exact objection.</td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Day 4 brings the week to a close with how the man, and the crowd, respond to what they've just witnessed.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Thursday: \"We have seen a great miracle today\"":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">10–15 minutes</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Luke 5:25–26 (review)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">&quot;We have seen a great miracle today&quot;</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Jesus is God's powerful Son who fulfils God's promises.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are bringing together everything we've learned this week about Jesus' authority to forgive and to heal.</li><li>I can explain how the man and the crowd responded to what Jesus did.</li><li>I can say what this week's events showed us about who Jesus is.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">This is the week's wrap-up — use the two review questions as an informal check of understanding. Listen for whether students can restate the link between the healing and the forgiveness claim, and offer a thoughtful idea about the man's instinct to thank God first.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Screen/device to display the comic panels (already embedded in the app)</li><li>Memory verse displayed on screen (built into the app's verse slide)</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"5\">1</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Recap the week</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2–3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">&quot;This week we've watched four friends go to extraordinary lengths to bring their friend to Jesus, watched Jesus make a claim that shocked the religious experts in the room, and watched him prove it in front of everyone. Today we see how it all ends.&quot; Show the roof scene again as a visual recap of how the story began.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Show comic image wk5-05-lowered-through-roof.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Quick recap: what was the big claim Jesus made this week, and what did he do to prove it?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Jesus claimed he has the authority on earth to forgive sins — something the Pharisees said only God could do. He proved it by healing the man instantly and visibly, since that's the part of the claim everyone could actually check.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"5\">2</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Thank you God</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3–4 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Show both final panels in sequence and let the man's reaction and the crowd's amazement land before discussing it.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Show comic images wk5-10-man-thanks-god and wk5-11-crowd-amazed in sequence.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">The man's very first reaction, the moment he could move again, was to thank God — not to celebrate being able to walk, or to thank his friends, even though they'd worked so hard for him. What does that tell us about what he understood had really just happened to him?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Accept thoughtful answers — he seems to understand that the real gift wasn't just his legs working again, but God's forgiveness and power at work through Jesus. His instinct is gratitude towards God himself, which suggests he grasped the deeper meaning behind the miracle, not just the physical result.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"3\">3</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Memory verse</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">1–2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Bring back the verse slide one more time and read it together as a class.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Use the built-in verse block.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"3\">4</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Closing prayer</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">1–2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Close the week together with the prayer in the app, said as a class.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Use the built-in prayer block to close.</td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Week 6 moves on to the next chapter of the Connect B1 Upper Primary manual.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Memory Verse</div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">“Turn back to God and be baptised! Then your sins will be forgiven.” — <em>Luke 3:3b (CEV)</em></blockquote></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Closing Prayer</div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Dear God, thank you that Jesus has the authority to forgive sins and to heal. Thank you that, like the four friends, we can bring the people we care about to Jesus — even when it's hard. Help us to respond like the man on the mat did, with real gratitude to you. Amen.</blockquote></div>"}}},"Week 6: More powerful than the raging sea":{"label":"Day","children":{"Week Overview":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Luke 8:22–25</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Jesus is God's powerful Son who fulfils God's promises.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Source</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Connect B1 Upper Primary — Lesson 6</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Monday: A storm swallows the boat":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">10–15 minutes</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Luke 8:22–23</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">A storm swallows the boat</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Jesus is God's powerful Son who fulfils God's promises.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning that Jesus and his disciples set sail across the Sea of Galilee, and Jesus fell asleep during the journey.</li><li>We are learning that a sudden, violent storm struck the lake while Jesus slept.</li><li>I can explain why the disciples' fishing experience makes their terror in this storm meaningful.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Listen for whether students can describe the two key details of today's passage — Jesus asleep, and a storm powerful enough to frighten experienced fishermen — and connect them to build anticipation for tomorrow.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Screen/device to display the comic panels (already embedded in the app)</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">1</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Wildest storms</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">1–2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Open with the warm-up question before introducing today's story — it primes students to think about how genuinely uncontrollable storms are, before we meet someone who controls one.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Think of the wildest storm you've ever seen or heard about — howling wind, pouring rain, huge waves. What would it actually take to make a storm like that just stop, right in the middle of it?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Take a few quick responses — most students will say 'nothing' or 'you just have to wait it out.' That's exactly the point: no person can just make a storm stop. Hold onto that thought — we're about to meet someone who does exactly that.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"3\">2</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Setting sail</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Show the first two panels in sequence, setting the scene calmly before the storm hits.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Show comic images wk6-01-lets-go-other-side and wk6-02-jesus-fallen-asleep.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"5\">3</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>The storm hits</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3–4 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Show the storm panel and let it land before asking the question — emphasise that these were professional fishermen on their own lake.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Show comic image wk6-03-strong-wind-struck.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">At least four of Jesus' disciples were professional fishermen who worked on the Sea of Galilee almost every day — they knew its storms better than anyone. If experienced fishermen like these were terrified by this particular storm, what does that tell you about how powerful it really was?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Guide toward: this wasn't ordinary bad weather that scared some inexperienced travellers — it frightened people who'd faced storms on this exact lake many times before. That's a strong clue this was a genuinely massive, life-threatening storm.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"5\">4</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Asleep in the storm</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2–3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Show the dramatic sleeping panel and give it real time — it's the visual highlight of today. Read the cliffhanger question slowly.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Show comic image wk6-04-jesus-asleep-in-storm.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">The storm is raging, water is pouring into the boat, and the disciples are terrified — but Jesus is still fast asleep. What do you think is going through the disciples' minds as they look over at him right now?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Accept any responses — we're building tension, not confirming an answer yet. Most students will guess disbelief, frustration, or panic that he isn't reacting. Tomorrow we find out exactly what they do about it.</td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Day 2 reveals what the terrified disciples do — and what happens the moment they wake Jesus up.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Tuesday: \"Master, Master! We're going to drown!\"":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">10–15 minutes</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Luke 8:24</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">&quot;Master, Master! We're going to drown!&quot;</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Jesus is God's powerful Son who fulfils God's promises.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning that the terrified disciples woke Jesus and begged him to save them.</li><li>We are learning that Jesus commanded the wind and waves to stop, and they obeyed him instantly.</li><li>I can connect the storm obeying Jesus' spoken word with God speaking creation into existence in Genesis.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Listen for whether students can describe what Jesus did (commanded the storm, rather than praying about it or waiting) and explain why instant obedience from wind and waves is significant.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Screen/device to display the comic panels (already embedded in the app)</li><li>Memory verse displayed on screen (built into the app's verse slide)</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"5\">1</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Waking Jesus up</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2–3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Recap yesterday's cliffhanger in a sentence, then show today's panel and read the disciples' cry with real urgency.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Show comic image wk6-05-master-were-going-to-drown.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">The disciples wake Jesus up in a total panic. What do you think they actually expected him to do about a storm this size — and why wake him up at all, if there's nothing he can do?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Accept thoughtful answers — there's no wrong answer here. Many students will say they just wanted company, or hoped he could at least help bail water. None of them are expecting what happens next.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"5\">2</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>One command, instant calm</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3–4 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Show the panel of Jesus commanding the storm and give it real weight — this is the turning point of the whole passage.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Show comic image wk6-06-jesus-orders-storm-stop.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Jesus didn't pray about it, wait for it to pass, or ask anyone for help — he just spoke, and the wind and waves obeyed him immediately. Where else in the Bible do we hear about something instantly obeying God's spoken word?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Right back in Genesis 1 — God speaks, and creation simply obeys ('Let there be light,' and there was light). Jesus commanding the storm the exact same way is a huge clue about who he really is.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"3\">3</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Memory verse</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Bring back this unit's verse slide and read it together. Point out that the same Jesus who gives eternal life, as our memory verse says, is the one we've just watched command a storm with a single word — he has power over creation itself, and over life and death.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Use the built-in verse block.</td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Day 3 looks at how the disciples react once the danger is gone — and the big question they're left asking.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Memory Verse</div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">“God's gift is eternal life given by Jesus Christ our Lord.” — <em>Romans 6:23b (CEV)</em></blockquote></div>"},"Wednesday: \"Who is this?\"":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">10–15 minutes</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Luke 8:25</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">&quot;Who is this?&quot;</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Jesus is God's powerful Son who fulfils God's promises.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning that once the storm stopped, the disciples' fear of drowning turned into awe and fear of Jesus himself.</li><li>We are learning that Psalm 65:5–7 describes silencing the roaring sea as something only God can do.</li><li>I can explain why the disciples' question 'Who is this?' is really a question about whether Jesus is God.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Listen for whether students can explain the shift in the disciples' fear (from the storm to Jesus' power) and connect the Psalm 65 passage to the disciples' question about Jesus' identity.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Screen/device to display the comic panels (already embedded in the app)</li><li>A CEV Bible or the built-in verse slide for Psalm 65:5–7</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"5\">1</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Completely still</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2–3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Show the calm-sea panel and let the contrast with yesterday's chaos sink in before asking the question.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Show comic image wk6-07-sea-completely-still.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">A minute ago the disciples were terrified they were about to drown. Now the danger is completely gone — and yet the comic shows them looking just as scared as before. If it isn't the storm frightening them anymore, what do you think it is?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Accept thoughtful answers — guide toward: they're now afraid of Jesus himself, or more precisely, of how much power he clearly has. Fear of the storm has turned into awe (and a bit of fear) at what Jesus just did.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"3\">2</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>&quot;Who is this?&quot;</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2–3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Show the final comic panel and read both lines of dialogue with real wonder in your voice.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Show comic image wk6-08-who-is-this.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"5\">3</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Only God silences the sea</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">4–5 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Read Psalm 65:5–7 slowly — either from the built-in verse slide or a CEV Bible. Give students a moment to notice the specific line about silencing the roaring waves before asking the final question.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Use the built-in Psalm 65:5–7 verse block.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">In the Old Testament, the Psalm we just read says that only God himself has the power to silence the roaring waves and put the mountains in place. So when the disciples ask, 'Who is this?' — what do you think they're really starting to wonder about Jesus?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Guide toward: they're starting to wonder if Jesus might actually be God. The Psalm describes something only God can do, and the disciples have just watched Jesus do exactly that.</td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Day 4 brings the week together and asks what it actually looks like to trust Jesus, even when we're still afraid.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Memory Verse</div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">“Our God, you save us, and your fearsome deeds answer our prayers for justice! You give hope to people everywhere on earth, even those across the sea. You are strong, and your mighty power put the mountains in place. You silence the roaring waves and the noisy shouts of the nations.” — <em>Psalm 65:5–7 (CEV)</em></blockquote></div>"},"Thursday: More powerful than the raging sea":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">10–15 minutes</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Luke 8:22–25 (review)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">More powerful than the raging sea</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Jesus is God's powerful Son who fulfils God's promises.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are bringing together everything we've learned this week about Jesus calming the storm.</li><li>I can explain what the disciples' question 'Who is this?' points to, and how this week's events answer it.</li><li>I can describe what it looks like to trust Jesus even when we're still afraid.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">This is the week's wrap-up — use the two review questions as an informal check of understanding. Listen for whether students can restate that Jesus' power over the storm points to his identity as God's Son, and offer a thoughtful idea about trusting Jesus in the midst of fear rather than instead of it.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Screen/device to display the comic panels (already embedded in the app)</li><li>Memory verse displayed on screen (built into the app's verse slide)</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"5\">1</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Recap the week</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2–3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">&quot;This week we watched a storm so fierce it terrified experienced fishermen, watched Jesus sleep right through it, and then watched him stand up and command it to stop — instantly.&quot; Show the panel of Jesus commanding the storm again as a visual recap.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Show comic image wk6-06-jesus-orders-storm-stop.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Quick recap: what was the big question the disciples were left asking by the end of this story, and what have we learned this week points toward the answer?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">The big question was 'Who is this? Even the winds and the waves obey him!' This week has pointed us toward the answer: Jesus has the same power over creation that only God has, according to Psalm 65 — which strongly suggests Jesus is God's own Son.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"5\">2</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Trusting Jesus in the storm</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3–4 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Show the wide feature panel and let it land as the visual high point of the whole week before asking the personal application question.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Show comic image wk6-09-jesus-calms-storm-wide.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">The disciples were in the boat right next to Jesus and they were still absolutely terrified during the storm. Do you think trusting Jesus means we'll never feel scared, or is it something else?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Guide toward: trusting Jesus doesn't mean fear disappears — the disciples were genuinely afraid, and that's okay. The real question is who you turn to in the middle of that fear. The disciples turned to Jesus, even before they fully understood who he was.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"3\">3</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Memory verse</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">1–2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Bring back the verse slide one more time and read it together as a class.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Use the built-in verse block.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"3\">4</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Closing prayer</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">1–2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Close the week together with the prayer in the app, said as a class.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Use the built-in prayer block to close.</td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Week 7 moves on to the next chapter of the Connect B1 Upper Primary manual.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Memory Verse</div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">“God's gift is eternal life given by Jesus Christ our Lord.” — <em>Romans 6:23b (CEV)</em></blockquote></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Closing Prayer</div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Dear God, thank you that Jesus is so powerful that even the wind and the waves obey him. Thank you that we can trust him, even in the middle of our own storms and fears. Help us to remember today what we've learned about who Jesus is. Amen.</blockquote></div>"}}},"Week 7: Faith is ...":{"label":"Day","children":{"Week Overview":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Luke 8:40–56</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Faith is believing Jesus and acting on it — and it's not about how much faith you have, but who you put it in.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Source</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Connect B1 Upper Primary — Lesson 7</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Monday: A leader begs, and a stranger's story breaks in":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">10–15 minutes</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Luke 8:40–44</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">A leader begs, and a stranger's story breaks in</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Faith is believing Jesus and acting on it — and it's not about how much faith you have, but who you put it in.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning that Jairus, a respected synagogue leader, publicly begged Jesus to heal his dying daughter.</li><li>We are learning that a sick woman in the crowd believed that simply touching Jesus' clothing would heal her.</li><li>I can explain why both Jairus' status and the woman's 12-year illness make their approach to Jesus significant.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Listen for whether students can describe what each person believed about Jesus and connect it to what it cost them (Jairus' pride, the woman's quiet risk) to act on it.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Screen/device to display the comic panels (already embedded in the app)</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">1</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Begging for help</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">1–2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Open with the warm-up question before introducing today's story — it primes students to think about what it takes for a respected person to publicly ask for help.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Think of someone whose job means people usually come to THEM for help — a doctor, a teacher, a leader of some kind. What would it take for that person to publicly drop to their knees and beg someone else for help?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Accept thoughtful answers — most students will say something like a genuine emergency, or something they truly couldn't fix themselves. Hold onto that thought: today we meet an important leader who does exactly that.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"5\">2</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>A crowd, and a desperate leader</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3–4 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Show the first two panels in sequence. Give the second panel real weight — an important, respected man falling at Jesus' feet in public.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Show comic images wk7-01-huge-crowd-welcomed-him and wk7-02-jairus-daughter-dying.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Jairus was an important, respected leader — he had a reputation to protect. Given that, why do you think he was willing to fall at Jesus' feet, right there in front of the whole crowd, and beg?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Guide toward: his love for his daughter and his desperate need mattered more to him than his pride or his position. Whatever people thought of him didn't matter anymore — only Jesus could help, so that's exactly what he did.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"5\">3</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>A second, quieter kind of desperation</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3–4 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Show the woman's panel and let the contrast with Jairus land — a public leader begging out loud, and a sick woman planning to act in complete secrecy.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Show comic image wk7-03-if-i-touch-jesus.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Luke tells us this woman had been sick for 12 years, and no doctor could cure her. She didn't ask Jesus to stop, or even to notice her — she just planned to secretly touch his clothing. What does that plan tell you about how much she trusted Jesus' power, even without him knowing she was there?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Guide toward: she had complete confidence in Jesus — so much that she believed just touching the edge of his robe, without him even realising, would be enough to heal her. She trusted that his power was real, whether or not he was paying attention to her specifically.</td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Day 2 finds out what happens the moment the woman reaches out — and the news that interrupts everything right afterward.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Tuesday: \"You're now well because of your faith\"":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">10–15 minutes</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Luke 8:45–48</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">&quot;You're now well because of your faith&quot;</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Faith is believing Jesus and acting on it — and it's not about how much faith you have, but who you put it in.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning that the woman was healed the instant she touched Jesus' clothing.</li><li>We are learning that Jesus drew her forward publicly to name her faith, rather than letting her leave unnoticed.</li><li>I can explain why Jesus' public response to the woman mattered as much as her physical healing.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Listen for whether students can explain why Jesus asked 'who touched me?' despite already knowing, and can describe what public restoration meant for someone who'd likely been avoided for 12 years.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Screen/device to display the comic panels (already embedded in the app)</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"5\">1</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Healed instantly</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2–3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Recap yesterday's cliffhanger in a sentence, then show today's panel and ask why Jesus stopped to ask a question he already knew the answer to.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Show comic image wk7-04-woman-was-healed.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">The moment she touched him, before Jesus even said a word, she was healed. Then Jesus stopped and asked, ‘Who touched me?’ — even though he already knew exactly what had happened. Why do you think Jesus wanted to find out, in front of everyone, who it was?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Guide toward: Jesus wasn't confused — he wanted this quiet, hidden act of faith brought out into the open, not to embarrass her, but so she (and everyone watching) could hear him say out loud what had really healed her: her faith, not magic or coincidence.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"3\">2</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>“Not me”</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Show the panel of the crowd denying it — build a little suspense before revealing that the woman is about to step forward herself.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Show comic image wk7-05-who-touched-me.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"5\">3</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>“You're now well because of your faith”</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3–4 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Show the panel and read both speech bubbles slowly — this is the emotional high point of the woman's story.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Show comic image wk7-06-you-are-well-because-of-faith.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Jesus called her and told her she was healed because of her faith, then blessed her with peace — all out loud, in front of the crowd that had probably avoided her for years. What do you think it meant for her to be spoken to like that, publicly, after 12 years of being overlooked?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Guide toward: this wasn't just physical healing — it was public restoration. Jesus gave her back her dignity and her place in the community, not just her health, by naming her faith in front of everyone instead of letting her slip away unseen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"2\">4</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Interrupted</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">1–2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Read the final cliffhanger line slowly and let it sit — don't reveal what the news is yet.</td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Day 3 reveals the news that arrives for Jairus — and what Jesus says in response.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Wednesday: \"Don't worry! Have faith\"":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">10–15 minutes</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Luke 8:49–53</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">&quot;Don't worry! Have faith&quot;</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Faith is believing Jesus and acting on it — and it's not about how much faith you have, but who you put it in.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning that Jairus received news that his daughter had died before Jesus could reach the house.</li><li>We are learning that Jesus told Jairus to keep having faith despite the news, and that mourners mocked Jesus when he arrived.</li><li>I can explain why Jesus asking for faith in a hopeless-looking situation reveals what real faith is.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Listen for whether students can articulate that faith is tested most when circumstances look hopeless, and can describe why public mockery makes that faith harder to hold onto.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Screen/device to display the comic panels (already embedded in the app)</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">1</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Too late?</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">1–2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Open with the warm-up question before revealing the news — it primes students to feel the weight of what Jairus is about to hear.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Imagine you'd already asked someone for help, and while you were still waiting, you got news that made it seem too late for that help to matter anymore. What would you be tempted to think in that moment?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Accept thoughtful answers — most students will say something like 'there's no point anymore' or 'it's hopeless now.' Hold onto that thought: today Jairus gets exactly that kind of news.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"5\">2</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Devastating news, and a strange response</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">4–5 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Show the panel and read both speech bubbles with real weight — the servant's blunt news, then Jesus' surprising instruction straight after.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Show comic image wk7-07-daughter-has-died.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Jesus told Jairus, ‘Don't worry! Have faith and your daughter will get well’ — right after Jairus had just been told it was too late. What does Jesus asking for faith at this exact moment, when the situation looked completely hopeless, tell you about what real faith actually is?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Guide toward: faith isn't just trusting Jesus when things look promising or easy — it's trusting him even when circumstances look hopeless. Jesus wasn't asking Jairus to believe something easy; he was asking him to keep trusting when there seemed to be no reason left to.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"5\">3</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Laughed at</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3–4 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Show the panel of the mourners laughing and let the social pressure of the moment land before asking the question.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Show comic image wk7-08-child-just-asleep.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">The mourners had already made up their minds about what was true, and they laughed at Jesus for saying something different. Why do you think it might be hard to keep trusting Jesus when everyone else around you is completely certain he's wrong?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Guide toward: it takes real courage to keep trusting Jesus when the people around you are confident, certain, and even mocking. That's exactly the kind of faith Jairus is being asked to hold onto right now — faith that doesn't depend on everyone else agreeing.</td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Day 4 finds out what happens next — and brings both of this week's stories together.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Thursday: Faith is ...":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">10–15 minutes</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Luke 8:54–56 (review)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Faith is ...</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Faith is believing Jesus and acting on it — and it's not about how much faith you have, but who you put it in.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are bringing together everything we've learned this week about Jairus' daughter and the sick woman.</li><li>I can explain what Jairus and the woman had in common, despite how different their situations were.</li><li>I can connect Jesus' power over death in this story to the promise of eternal life in our memory verse.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">This is the week's wrap-up — use the two review questions as an informal check of understanding. Listen for whether students can name that faith is believing AND doing, and that it's about who you trust rather than how much you trust.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Screen/device to display the comic panels (already embedded in the app)</li><li>Memory verse displayed on screen (built into the app's verse slide)</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"5\">1</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Child, get up!</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3–4 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Recap the week in a sentence, then show today's panel and read Jesus' two words slowly — let the simplicity of the command land.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Show comic image wk7-09-child-get-up.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Jesus took the girl's hand and said just two words: ‘Child, get up!’ — and she did, instantly. Given everything we've watched Jesus do this week already, does it surprise you that his words alone could bring someone back from death? Why or why not?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Guide toward: by this point in the week, students should connect the pattern — if Jesus could heal a 12-year illness with just a touch of his robe, it makes sense that his power isn't limited by how serious, how hopeless, or even how dead a situation looks. His voice alone was enough.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"5\">2</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Two very different people, one thing in common</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3–4 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Show the final panel, then ask students to think back across the whole week before answering — this is the big connecting question for the unit.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Show comic image wk7-10-daughter-back-to-life.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Think back over the whole week: Jairus, a powerful, respected leader, and the sick woman, someone many people had probably stopped noticing — they were about as different as two people could be. What was the one thing they both did that made the real difference?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Guide toward the manual's key point: faith is about believing AND doing — it isn't about how much faith you have, but who you put it in. Jairus believed and acted (falling at Jesus' feet, then continuing on despite the worst possible news). The woman believed and acted (reaching out to touch his robe). Both trusted Jesus specifically — not just believed something vague.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"5\">3</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Memory verse</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2–3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Bring up the verse slide and read it together. Point out that the same Jesus who gives eternal life, as the verse says, is the one we've just watched reverse death itself with two words.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Use the built-in verse block.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Our memory verse says God's gift is eternal life, given through Jesus. Today we watched Jesus give a 12-year-old girl her life back with just two words. What do you think this shows us about the eternal life Jesus promises?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Guide toward: if Jesus has the power to reverse death on the spot for one girl, that's exactly what makes his promise of eternal life credible — it isn't just a nice idea, it's backed up by the same power we've watched him use all week.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"3\">4</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Closing prayer</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">1–2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Close the week together with the prayer in the app, said as a class.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Use the built-in prayer block to close.</td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Week 8 moves on to the next chapter of the Connect B1 Upper Primary manual.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Memory Verse</div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">“God's gift is eternal life given by Jesus Christ our Lord.” — <em>Romans 6:23b (CEV)</em></blockquote></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Closing Prayer</div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Dear God, thank you that anyone can put their faith in Jesus — whether we feel important or overlooked, whether things look hopeful or hopeless. Thank you that Jesus has power even over death. Help us to believe and act on that faith this week. Amen.</blockquote></div>"}}},"Week 8: The signs of life":{"label":"Day","children":{"Week Overview":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Luke 9:18–22, 44b–45, 51</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Jesus' miracles are signs that point to who he is and what he came to do — die and rise again to save people from their sin.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Source</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Connect B1 Upper Primary — Lesson 8</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Monday: \"But who do you say I am?\"":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">10–15 minutes</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Luke 9:18–20</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">&quot;But who do you say I am?&quot;</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Jesus' miracles are signs that point to who he is and what he came to do — die and rise again to save people from their sin.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning that Jesus asked his disciples what people were saying about him, then asked them directly who THEY said he was.</li><li>We are learning that Peter answered that Jesus is the Messiah, God's promised King — the one true answer among many guesses.</li><li>I can explain the difference between being impressed by Jesus and actually recognising who he is.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Listen for whether students can explain that lots of people had theories about Jesus, but only Peter's answer was actually true — and can connect this term's miracles to that same recognition.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Screen/device to display the comic panels (already embedded in the app)</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">1</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>What has this term shown us?</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Open with the warm-up question before introducing today's story — it primes students to connect this week to everything they've already seen Jesus do.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Think about everything we've watched Jesus do this term so far — calming a raging storm, healing a woman who'd been sick for 12 years, raising Jairus's daughter back to life. What do you think all of that adds up to? What is it starting to show you about who Jesus really is?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Take a few responses — there's no single 'right' wording yet, and that's fine. Hold onto their answers: today's story is the moment in Luke's Gospel where someone finally puts it into words out loud.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"5\">2</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>What do people say?</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3–4 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Show the first two panels in sequence, then ask the question — let students notice that all the guesses, while impressive, were wrong.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Show comic images wk8-01-jesus-praying-with-disciples and wk8-02-what-do-people-say.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">People's guesses were John the Baptist, Elijah, or a prophet come back to life — all seriously significant figures in Israel's history. What do you think it shows that people reached for names like that, even though every single guess turned out to be wrong?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Guide toward: people could tell Jesus was someone extraordinary and powerful — his miracles and teaching clearly pointed to something big happening. But pointing in the right direction isn't the same as arriving at the truth. Impressive guesses can still be wrong guesses.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"5\">3</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Peter's answer</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">4–5 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Show the panel and read both lines slowly — give real weight to Peter's answer as the turning point of the passage.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Show comic image wk8-03-you-are-the-messiah.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Out of everyone's guesses that day, Peter's answer was the only one that was actually true. What's the difference between knowing lots of impressive facts about Jesus and being able to say, like Peter did, exactly who Jesus is?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Guide toward the manual's key idea: Jesus' miracles are signs that point to who he is — but a sign only does its job if you follow it all the way to what it's pointing at. Peter didn't just admire Jesus' power; he named him as the Messiah, God's promised King. That's the difference between being impressed by Jesus and actually recognising him.</td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Day 2 finds out what being the Messiah is actually going to cost Jesus — and it isn't what the disciples were expecting.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Tuesday: A King who chooses to suffer":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">10–15 minutes</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Luke 9:21–22</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">A King who chooses to suffer</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Jesus' miracles are signs that point to who he is and what he came to do — die and rise again to save people from their sin.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning that Jesus told his disciples the Messiah must suffer, be rejected, be killed, and rise to life on the third day.</li><li>We are learning that Mark 10:45 explains this suffering as Jesus giving his life to rescue many people.</li><li>I can explain why Jesus choosing to suffer is part of God's rescue plan, not a plan gone wrong.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Listen for whether students can state that Jesus predicted his own suffering, death, and resurrection in advance, and can connect this to Mark 10:45's picture of Jesus giving his life to rescue people.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Screen/device to display the comic panels (already embedded in the app)</li><li>Mark 10:45 displayed on screen (built into the app's verse slide)</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"5\">1</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>A shocking prediction</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">4–5 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Recap Peter's confession from yesterday in a sentence, then show today's panel and read the speech bubble slowly — let the weight of 'suffer... rejected... killed' land before moving to the question.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Show comic image wk8-04-messiah-must-suffer.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">If you were one of the disciples expecting Jesus to be a powerful King, how do you think it would feel to hear him say he was going to suffer, be rejected, and be killed?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Guide toward: this would be shocking and confusing — many people at the time expected the promised King to defeat their enemies and rule as an earthly king, not suffer and die. Jesus is describing a completely different kind of King than they were picturing.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">2</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>But three days later...</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3–4 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Point back to the same panel and re-read just the last line about rising to life — make sure students notice Jesus told them the ending in advance.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Jesus said he would be killed, 'but three days later he will rise to life!' Why do you think it matters that Jesus told them the ending before it even happened?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Guide toward: Jesus wasn't a victim of circumstances he couldn't see coming — he knew exactly what was going to happen, including that it wouldn't be the end. Suffering and death were part of the plan, not a plan that failed.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"5\">3</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Why suffer at all?</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3–4 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Bring up the Mark 10:45 verse slide and read it together, then connect it directly back to today's panel.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Use the built-in verse block.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">This verse says the Son of Man — that's Jesus' own name for himself — came to 'give his life to rescue many people.' How does that help explain WHY Jesus, God's promised King, would choose to suffer and die?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Guide toward: Jesus wasn't trapped into suffering — giving his life was the rescue itself. Being a slave master would mean using power to serve himself; being a slave who gives his life is choosing to use his power to save others instead, at the greatest possible cost to himself.</td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Day 3 shows how the disciples actually reacted to hearing all this — and it isn't with understanding.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Memory Verse</div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">“The Son of Man did not come to be a slave master, but a slave who would give his life to rescue many people.” — <em>Mark 10:45 (CEV)</em></blockquote></div>"},"Wednesday: Too confused, too scared to ask":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">10–15 minutes</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Luke 9:43b–45</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Too confused, too scared to ask</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Jesus' miracles are signs that point to who he is and what he came to do — die and rise again to save people from their sin.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning that the disciples did not understand Jesus' prediction about suffering, death, and resurrection, even after hearing it twice.</li><li>We are learning that the disciples were too afraid to ask Jesus what he meant.</li><li>I can explain why true information can still be hard to accept when it doesn't match what you expected.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Listen for whether students can explain that the disciples' confusion came from Jesus' words not matching their expectations of a Messiah, not from doubting his power, and can relate this to their own experiences of being afraid to ask questions.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Screen/device to display the comic panels (already embedded in the app)</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"5\">1</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>&quot;I don't understand&quot;</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3–4 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Show the panel and read both lines — let the disciples' confusion feel genuine, not silly.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Show comic image wk8-05-i-dont-understand.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">The disciples had just watched Jesus heal the sick, calm a storm, and raise a girl from the dead this term — yet this news about suffering and dying completely stumped them. Why do you think this particular part of what Jesus said was so hard to understand?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Guide toward: it wasn't that they doubted Jesus' power — they'd just watched proof of it all term. It's that a King who chooses to suffer and die didn't fit any picture they already had of what 'Messiah' meant. New, true information can still be hard to accept when it doesn't match what you expected.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"3\">2</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Said again, a week later</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2–3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Show the panel and note how deliberately Jesus repeats himself — this wasn't a throwaway comment.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Show comic image wk8-06-pay-close-attention.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"5\">3</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Still confused, too scared to ask</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">4–5 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Show the final panel and let the honesty of 'neither do I' land before asking the question — this is a great moment to connect to students' own experience.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Show comic image wk8-07-still-dont-understand.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Luke tells us the disciples didn't understand — and they were too scared to ask Jesus what he meant. Have you ever not understood something important, but felt too nervous to ask a question about it? What do you think held the disciples back?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Guide toward: maybe they were afraid of what the answer might mean, or afraid of looking foolish, or maybe the news was just too big and sad to want more details right away. Being confused AND afraid to ask is a very human response, even for people who had already put their trust in Jesus.</td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Day 4 brings the week together and asks what all of Jesus' signs this term have really been pointing to.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Thursday: The signs of life":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">10–15 minutes</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Luke 9:51 (review)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">The signs of life</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Jesus' miracles are signs that point to who he is and what he came to do — die and rise again to save people from their sin.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are bringing together everything we've learned this week about Peter's confession and Jesus' prediction.</li><li>I can explain that Jesus deliberately chose to go to Jerusalem, knowing what awaited him there.</li><li>I can connect this week's events to the promise of eternal life in our memory verse.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">This is the week's wrap-up — use the two review questions as an informal check of understanding. Listen for whether students can state that Jesus' miracles are signs pointing to him as the Messiah who came to die and rise again, and can connect that to the memory verse's promise of eternal life.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Screen/device to display the comic panels (already embedded in the app)</li><li>Memory verse displayed on screen (built into the app's verse slide)</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"5\">1</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Made up his mind</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3–4 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Recap the week in a sentence, then show today's panel and read it slowly — emphasise that Jesus chose this path deliberately.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Show comic image wk8-08-heads-to-jerusalem.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Jesus knew exactly what was waiting for him in Jerusalem — suffering and death — and he made up his mind to go anyway. What does that tell you about Jesus?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Guide toward: Jesus wasn't forced into this or caught by surprise — he deliberately chose to walk toward it because it was the only way to rescue people from their sin. That's courage and love, not someone resigned to a fate they couldn't avoid.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">2</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>What have the signs been pointing to?</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3–4 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Ask students to think back across the whole term before answering — this is the big connecting question for the unit.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Think back over the whole term: a storm calmed, a sick woman healed, a dead girl raised to life, and now Jesus himself explaining he would suffer, die, and rise again. Putting all of that together, what would you say Jesus came to do?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Guide toward: every sign this term has been building toward this answer — Jesus is God's promised King who came not to rule by force, but to suffer, die, and rise again so that people's sins could be forgiven. Guide students back to Peter's confession on Monday: recognising this is exactly what it means to say Jesus is the Messiah.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"5\">3</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Memory verse</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Bring up the verse slide and read it together. Point out that the eternal life promised in the verse only became possible because of the events Jesus predicted this week.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Use the built-in verse block.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Our memory verse says God's gift is eternal life, given through Jesus. How does everything we've watched Jesus predict this week — his suffering, death, and rising to life — connect to that gift becoming possible?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Guide toward: the gift of eternal life isn't just a nice idea — it's only possible because Jesus actually went through with what he predicted this week. His death paid for sin, and his resurrection proves death doesn't get the final word. That's exactly what makes the memory verse true.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"3\">4</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Closing prayer</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">1–2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Close the week together with the prayer in the app, said as a class.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Use the built-in prayer block to close.</td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Week 9 moves on to the next chapter of the Connect B1 Upper Primary manual.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Memory Verse</div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">“God's gift is eternal life given by Jesus Christ our Lord.” — <em>Romans 6:23b (CEV)</em></blockquote></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Closing Prayer</div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Dear God, thank you that Jesus is the Messiah, your promised King. Thank you that he chose to go toward suffering and death because he loves us, and that his rising to life three days later proves your promise of eternal life is real. Help us to be like Peter, and to say, out loud, who we believe Jesus is. Amen.</blockquote></div>"}}},"Week 9: To die or not to die?":{"label":"Day","children":{"Week Overview":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Luke 22:39–53</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">The Bible explains that Jesus chose to die on the cross and rise again because he loves us.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Source</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Connect B1 Upper Primary — Lesson 9</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Monday: “Not what I want, but what you want”":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">10–15 minutes</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Luke 22:39–42</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">“Not what I want, but what you want”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">The Bible explains that Jesus chose to die on the cross and rise again because he loves us.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning that Jesus prayed in the Garden of Gethsemane, asking his Father if there was another way, but choosing his Father's will over his own.</li><li>We are learning that the 'cup' Jesus prayed about represents the death that sin deserves.</li><li>I can explain that Jesus chose obedience to his Father even though it meant real suffering for him.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Listen for whether students can explain that Jesus asked God if the suffering could be avoided, but chose to go through with his Father's will anyway — and can connect the 'cup' to the punishment sin deserves.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Screen/device to display the comic panels (already embedded in the app)</li><li>Romans 6:23a displayed on screen (built into the app's verse slide)</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">1</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Facing something hard</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2–3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Open with the warm-up question before introducing today's story — it primes students to think about what it feels like to know pain is coming.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Think of the last time you knew something hard or painful was coming — a tricky conversation, a scary appointment, something you'd been putting off. Did you want to face it head-on, or avoid it for as long as you could?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Take a few honest responses — most people, understandably, want to avoid pain and put off hard things. Hold onto this: today we're going to watch Jesus face the hardest, most painful night of his life, and choose not to avoid it.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"5\">2</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Into the garden</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Set the scene briefly, then show the first panel and read the speech bubble.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Show comic image wk9-01-pray-not-be-tested.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Jesus told his disciples to pray that they wouldn't be tested — right before facing the hardest night of his own life. Why do you think he was thinking about their struggle, even in the middle of facing his own?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Guide toward: Jesus was compassionate and others-focused even while dreading what was ahead for himself — a preview of the way he'll respond with compassion even to the soldiers who arrest him later this week.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"5\">3</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>The cup</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">4–5 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Show the second panel and read it slowly, then bring up the Romans 6:23a verse slide to explain what the 'cup' represents before asking the question.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Show comic image wk9-02-father-if-you-will, then use the built-in verse block for Romans 6:23a.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Jesus asked if there was another way to avoid the cup — the death sin deserves — but what did he decide in the end?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Guide toward: Jesus was completely honest about not wanting the suffering ahead — he genuinely didn't want to face it. But he chose to submit to what his Father wanted, not what he wanted. He was about to take, in our place, the death that sin deserves. That choice — obedience even when it costs everything — is the turning point of the whole passage.</td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Day 2 shows just how much that choice cost Jesus physically and emotionally — and how his own disciples responded.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Memory Verse</div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">“Sin pays off with death.” — <em>Romans 6:23a (CEV)</em></blockquote></div>"},"Tuesday: Sweat like drops of blood":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">10–15 minutes</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Luke 22:43–46</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Sweat like drops of blood</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">The Bible explains that Jesus chose to die on the cross and rise again because he loves us.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning that Jesus was in such anguish in the garden that Luke describes his sweat as being like drops of blood.</li><li>We are learning that Jesus' disciples fell asleep instead of praying with him, even after he asked them to stay awake.</li><li>I can explain that Jesus was fully human and truly suffered, even while remaining obedient to his Father.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Listen for whether students can describe the physical and emotional weight of Jesus' anguish in the garden, and can articulate how the disciples' failure to stay awake added to that loneliness.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Screen/device to display the comic panels (already embedded in the app)</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"5\">1</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Real anguish</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">4–5 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Recap yesterday in a sentence, then show the angel panel and describe the sweat-like-blood detail from the Bible passage before asking the question.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Show comic image wk9-03-angel-from-heaven.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Luke tells us Jesus was in so much anguish that night that his sweat fell to the ground like drops of blood, and God sent an angel to strengthen him. What does this tell you about how real this moment was for Jesus — even though he is God's own Son?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Guide toward: Jesus was completely human as well as completely God. This wasn't easy for him or something he was just pretending to struggle with — the suffering ahead genuinely terrified him. Yet even in his anguish, he kept praying and stayed obedient.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"5\">2</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Asleep when it mattered most</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">4–5 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Show the second panel and read it, then ask the question — let students sit with how isolating this moment was for Jesus.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Show comic image wk9-04-why-are-you-asleep.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">The disciples couldn't manage to stay awake, even for one hour, on the night Jesus needed them most. How do you think it felt for Jesus to face this alone, when the people closest to him couldn't stay awake with him?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Accept responses like lonely, disappointed, sad. Guide toward: even the people who loved Jesus most let him down that night — yet he didn't give up on his mission or turn away from what he'd chosen to do.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"3\">3</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>A moment of silence</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Close today's session with the short silent reflection — keep it brief and genuinely quiet, no talking.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Use the built-in activity block: sit in silence for fifteen seconds.</td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Day 3 shows who actually arrives in the garden that night — and how Jesus responds even to the person betraying him.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Wednesday: Betrayed with a kiss":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">10–15 minutes</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Luke 22:47–51</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Betrayed with a kiss</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">The Bible explains that Jesus chose to die on the cross and rise again because he loves us.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning that Judas betrayed Jesus with a kiss, and that Jesus was arrested by an armed crowd.</li><li>We are learning that Jesus healed the ear of one of the men arresting him, even in the middle of his own betrayal.</li><li>I can explain, using Matthew 26:53–54, that Jesus had the power to escape but chose not to use it.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Listen for whether students can explain that Jesus responded to betrayal and violence with compassion, and can connect Matthew 26:53–54 to the idea that Jesus was in control the whole time, not a helpless victim.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Screen/device to display the comic panels (already embedded in the app)</li><li>Matthew 26:53–54 displayed on screen (built into the app's verse slide)</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"5\">1</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>A kiss that betrays</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3–4 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Show the panel and read the speech bubble, then ask the question — give students a moment to sit with how personal this betrayal was.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Show comic image wk9-05-judas-betraying-with-kiss.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Judas had been one of Jesus' own twelve disciples — someone who had walked with him for years. He used a kiss, a normal sign of friendship, as the signal to betray Jesus to the soldiers. Why do you think this detail makes the betrayal even harder to hear?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Guide toward: a kiss is normally a sign of love and loyalty. Judas twisted something good into the very method of betrayal. It shows just how far Jesus' own friend was willing to go against him.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"5\">2</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Enough of that</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">4–5 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Show both panels in sequence — the sword question, then the healing — and read them together before asking the question.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Show comic images wk9-06-should-we-attack and wk9-07-enough-of-that.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Jesus was about to be unfairly arrested by these very men, yet the first thing he did was heal one of them. What does this tell you about the kind of King Jesus really is?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Guide toward: Jesus responded to violence and betrayal with compassion, even toward someone actively working against him. This is exactly the kind of King the disciples didn't expect — one who heals his enemies rather than destroying them.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"5\">3</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>12 armies of angels</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3–4 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Bring up the verse slide and read it together, then connect it back to Monday's prayer before asking the final question.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Use the built-in verse block for Matthew 26:53–54.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Jesus points out that he could have called down more than 12 armies of angels to rescue himself — he had all the power he needed to walk away completely unharmed. Why do you think he chose not to use that power?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Guide toward: Jesus wasn't weak or powerless in this moment — he was in complete control the whole time. He chose not to use his power to save himself because rescuing us required him not to rescue himself. This connects directly to Monday's prayer: 'not what I want, but what you want.'</td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Day 4 brings the week together and asks the biggest question of all: why did Jesus go through with any of this?</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Memory Verse</div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">“Don't you know that I could ask my Father, and right away he would send me more than 12 armies of angels? But then, how could the words of the Scriptures come true, which say that this must happen?” — <em>Matthew 26:53–54 (CEV)</em></blockquote></div>"},"Thursday: Why Jesus chose this":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">10–15 minutes</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Luke 22:51–53 (review)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Why Jesus chose this</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">The Bible explains that Jesus chose to die on the cross and rise again because he loves us.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are bringing together everything we've learned this week about Jesus' prayer, betrayal, and arrest.</li><li>I can explain that Jesus chose to go through all of this because he loves us, not because he was forced into it.</li><li>I can connect this week's events to the promise of eternal life in our memory verse.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">This is the week's wrap-up — use the review questions as an informal check of understanding. Listen for whether students can state that Jesus chose to suffer, be betrayed, and be arrested out of love, and can connect that choice to the promise of eternal life in the memory verse.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Screen/device to display the comic panel and feature image (already embedded in the app)</li><li>Memory verse displayed on screen (built into the app's verse slide)</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"5\">1</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Darkness is in control</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3–4 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Recap the week in a sentence, then show today's panel and read Jesus' words slowly before asking the question.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Show comic image wk9-08-why-treat-me-like-criminal.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Jesus points out that the crowd could have arrested him any day, out in the open at the temple — but they came at night, in secret, with weapons. What do you think Jesus meant when he said 'darkness is in control'?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Guide toward: this wasn't really about swords and clubs — it was spiritual darkness and opposition to Jesus finally acting against him. Yet even this dark moment was still part of God's plan, not a plan going wrong.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"5\">2</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Why did Jesus go through with it?</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3–4 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Display the wide garden image and ask students to think back across the whole week before answering — this is the big connecting question for the week.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Show feature image wk9-09-gethsemane-wide-shot.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Think back over everything we've watched this week: Jesus praying in agony, being betrayed by a friend, being arrested by an armed crowd — and choosing not to use his power to escape any of it. Why do you think Jesus went through with all of this?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Take a few responses, then guide toward the week's big idea: Jesus didn't have to go through any of this. He chose to, because he loves us.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"5\">3</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>The greatest way to show love</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">4–5 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Bring up the John 15:12–14 verse slide and read it together, then connect it directly back to the week's events before moving to the final two verses.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Use the built-in verse blocks for John 15:12–14, John 3:16–17, and Romans 6:23b in sequence.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">This verse says the greatest way to show love for friends is to die for them. How does everything we watched this week — the prayer, the betrayal, the arrest — show that this is exactly what Jesus was doing?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Guide toward: Jesus wasn't a victim of bad luck. Every choice he made this week, starting with 'not what I want but what you want,' was Jesus deliberately loving us enough to go through with the suffering ahead, all the way to the cross.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"3\">4</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Memory verse from memory</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2–3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Challenge the class to recite the memory verse together fully from memory, with the screen as a backup only.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Use the say-it-from-memory activity block.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"3\">5</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Closing prayer</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">1–2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Close the week together with the prayer in the app, said as a class.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Use the built-in prayer block to close.</td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Week 10 moves on to the next chapter of the Connect B1 Upper Primary manual.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Memory Verse</div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">“Now I tell you to love each other, as I have loved you. The greatest way to show love for friends is to die for them. And you are my friends, if you obey me.” — <em>John 15:12–14 (CEV)</em><br>“God loved the people of this world so much that he gave his only Son, so that everyone who has faith in him will have eternal life and never really die. God did not send his Son into the world to condemn its people. He sent him to save them!” — <em>John 3:16–17 (CEV)</em><br>“God's gift is eternal life given by Jesus Christ our Lord.” — <em>Romans 6:23b (CEV)</em></blockquote></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Closing Prayer</div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Dear God, thank you that Jesus chose to go through the garden, the betrayal, and the arrest, because he loves us. Thank you that even in his most painful moments he stayed obedient to you and full of compassion toward others. Help us to trust how much he loves us. Amen.</blockquote></div>"}}},"Week 10: Even stronger than death!":{"label":"Day","children":{"Week Overview":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Luke 24; 1 Corinthians 15</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Jesus is more powerful than death — he rose from the dead, proving he is God's Son, and now offers eternal life to everyone who trusts him.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Source</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Connect B1 Upper Primary — Lesson 10</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Monday: Death couldn't hold him down":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">10–15 minutes</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Luke 23:44–56; 24:1–3</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Death couldn't hold him down</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Jesus is more powerful than death — he rose from the dead, proving he is God's Son, and now offers eternal life to everyone who trusts him.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning that Jesus died on the cross and was buried in a tomb with a stone rolled in front of it.</li><li>We are learning that some women went to the tomb on the third day and found it empty.</li><li>I can explain that the disciples did not expect or understand that Jesus would rise from the dead.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Listen for whether students can recall that Jesus' body was placed in a sealed tomb, and can articulate that the women were confused and afraid when they found it empty rather than expecting a resurrection.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Screen/device to display the comic panels (already embedded in the app)</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">1</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>If someone came back</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2–3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Open with the warm-up question before introducing today's story — it primes students for the shock the characters in the story are about to feel.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Last week we left Jesus being arrested in the garden, with darkness ‘in control.’ If somebody you loved died, and then a few days later you heard they were alive again, what would you think when you first heard that news?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Take a few honest responses — most kids will land on disbelief, thinking it's a trick, or wanting proof for themselves. Hold onto that reaction, because it's exactly how this week's story unfolds.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"3\">2</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>The cross and the tomb</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3–4 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Briefly explain that after being arrested, Jesus was put on trial and crucified. Show both panels in sequence and read the captions.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Show comic images wk10-01-crucifixion and wk10-02-body-in-tomb in sequence.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">3</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Did they believe him?</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Remind students of Lesson 8's comic, where the disciples said ‘I don't understand’ when Jesus told them he would die and rise again. Ask the question and let students reason through it.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Jesus had told his disciples again and again that he would die and then rise again on the third day — we even saw them say ‘I don't understand’ to that news a couple of weeks ago. Do you think the disciples believed him at this point, or were they expecting Jesus to actually come back?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Guide toward: no — the disciples didn't understand or believe Jesus' own warnings about his death and resurrection, so when it actually happened, they were devastated and grieving, not hopeful or expectant. That's important context for what happens next.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"5\">4</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>An empty tomb</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3–4 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Set the scene — three days later, early on Sunday morning, some women went to the tomb. Show both panels and read the speech bubbles, then ask the closing question.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Show comic images wk10-03-women-who-rolled-stone and wk10-04-tomb-is-empty in sequence.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">The women expected to find Jesus' dead body in the tomb — but it wasn't there. What do you think might have run through their minds in that moment?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Accept any responses — confusion, fear, maybe they thought someone had stolen the body. This is genuinely open at this point in the story; we find out what's really going on tomorrow.</td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Day 2 reveals who the women meet at the tomb, and what happens when they run to tell the disciples.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Tuesday: He's not here — he's alive!":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">10–15 minutes</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Luke 24:4–12</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">He's not here — he's alive!</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Jesus is more powerful than death — he rose from the dead, proving he is God's Son, and now offers eternal life to everyone who trusts him.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning that two men in bright clothes told the women Jesus had risen, just as he said he would.</li><li>We are learning that the women told the disciples, but the disciples didn't believe them at first.</li><li>I can explain that even Peter, after seeing the empty tomb himself, was still confused rather than convinced.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Listen for whether students can explain that the two men's message was a fulfilled promise, not a surprise twist, and can describe how the disciples and Peter responded with doubt rather than instant belief.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Screen/device to display the comic panels (already embedded in the app)</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"5\">1</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Recap &amp; two men appear</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Recap yesterday in a sentence, then show the panel and read the speech bubble before asking the question.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Show comic image wk10-05-two-men-appear.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">The two men said Jesus had been ‘raised from death’ — not stolen, not moved, but alive again. How is that different from what the women were probably expecting to hear?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Guide toward: the women were likely expecting either a dead body, or an empty tomb meaning someone had stolen it. The idea that a dead man was now truly alive again was something else entirely — not just surprising, but seemingly impossible.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"5\">2</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Remember what Jesus said</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3–4 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Show the panel and read it, then connect back to Week 1's Zechariah before asking the question.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Show comic image wk10-06-remember-what-jesus-said.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">The two men reminded the women of something Jesus had already told them would happen. Why do you think it can still be hard to believe a promise, even when someone told you exactly what would happen beforehand?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Accept any responses. Guide toward: this is the same pattern we've seen all term — back in Week 1, Zechariah struggled to believe an angel's promise even face to face. Believing a promise before you see it come true is genuinely hard, even with the clearest warning.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"5\">3</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Peter runs to the tomb</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">4–5 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Show all three panels in sequence and read them, then ask the closing question.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Show comic images wk10-07-women-tell-disciples, wk10-08-peter-runs-to-tomb, and wk10-09-strips-of-linen in sequence.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Peter saw the empty tomb with his own eyes, exactly as the women had said. Do you think Peter was convinced yet that Jesus was really alive?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Guide toward: Luke tells us Peter went away ‘wondering what had happened’ — even seeing the evidence himself, he was still confused, not yet convinced. It will take actually seeing Jesus in person to change that, which is where tomorrow's story picks up.</td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Day 3 shows what happens when Jesus himself finally stands in front of his disciples.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Wednesday: Touch me and see":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">10–15 minutes</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Luke 24:36–43</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Touch me and see</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Jesus is more powerful than death — he rose from the dead, proving he is God's Son, and now offers eternal life to everyone who trusts him.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning that Jesus appeared in person to his disciples about a week after the resurrection.</li><li>We are learning that Jesus proved he had a real physical body by inviting the disciples to touch him and by eating in front of them.</li><li>I can explain why physical proof mattered for convincing the disciples — and for us today.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Listen for whether students can describe at least two ways Jesus proved his resurrection was physical and real (being touched, eating food), and can explain why that evidence matters.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Screen/device to display the comic panels (already embedded in the app)</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"5\">1</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Peace be with you</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Recap Tuesday in a sentence, then show the panel and read the speech bubble before asking the question.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Show comic image wk10-10-peace-be-with-you.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Jesus didn't just appear as a memory or an idea — he stood right there in the room with his disciples. How do you think they felt in that moment?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Accept responses like shocked, terrified, confused. Guide toward: Luke tells us they thought he was a ghost — which sets up exactly what Jesus does next.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"5\">2</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Look at my hands and feet</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3–4 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Show the panel and read it slowly, then ask the question.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Show comic image wk10-11-look-at-my-hands-and-feet.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Jesus told his disciples to look at his hands and feet and even touch him. Why do you think he wanted them to physically see and touch him, rather than just telling them ‘it's really me’?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Guide toward: Jesus wanted to prove, beyond any doubt, that this wasn't a ghost or their imagination playing tricks on them. He had a real physical body, truly raised from death — not just a spirit or a vision.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"5\">3</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Have you got something to eat?</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3–4 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Show the panel and read it, then ask the closing question and connect it to why this evidence still matters today.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Show comic image wk10-12-have-you-got-something-to-eat.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Jesus even asked for food and ate it in front of everyone — ghosts don't eat! Why do you think a detail like this matters for us today, thousands of years later?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Guide toward: it's real, physical, historical evidence that convinced eyewitnesses at the time. It's part of why we can trust the resurrection actually happened, rather than being just a story or a feeling someone had.</td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Day 4 brings the week to a close, showing where Jesus went next and why his resurrection is such good news for us.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Thursday: Jesus is alive — and so can we be":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">10–15 minutes</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Luke 24:44–53; Acts 2:23–24; 1 Corinthians 15:3–8a</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Jesus is alive — and so can we be</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Jesus is more powerful than death — he rose from the dead, proving he is God's Son, and now offers eternal life to everyone who trusts him.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are bringing together everything we've learned this week about the resurrection, ascension, and what it all means.</li><li>I can explain why Jesus needed to both die and rise again to deal with sin and death.</li><li>I can connect the eyewitness evidence for the resurrection to the promise of eternal life in our memory verse.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">This is the week's wrap-up — use the review questions as an informal check of understanding. Listen for whether students can explain why the resurrection matters (not just that it happened), and can connect it to the promise of eternal life in the memory verse.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Screen/device to display the comic panels (already embedded in the app)</li><li>Memory verse and supporting verses displayed on screen (built into the app's verse slides)</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"5\">1</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Why die AND rise again?</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3–4 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Recap the week in a sentence, then show today's first panel and read it slowly before asking the question.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Show comic image wk10-13-jesus-explains-scriptures.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Jesus said all of this — his death and his resurrection — happened so people could repent and be forgiven. Why do you think Jesus needed to do both — die AND rise again — rather than just one or the other?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Guide toward: his death paid the punishment for sin, but if he'd stayed dead, that would mean death and sin had won after all. Because he rose again, we know death has been defeated, and his followers can share in that same new life.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"5\">2</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Taken up to heaven</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3–4 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Show the ascension panel, then bring up the John 14:1–3 verse slide and read it together before asking the question.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Show comic image wk10-14-taken-up-to-heaven, then use the built-in verse block for John 14:1–3.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Jesus promised he's preparing a place in heaven and will come back for his followers. How does knowing Jesus is alive right now, doing exactly that, change how his followers can think about their own future?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Accept any responses — hope, confidence, something to look forward to rather than fear. There's no single right answer; encourage students to put it in their own words.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"5\">3</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Death could not hold him</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3–4 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Show the final comic panel, then bring up the Acts 2:23–24 verse slide and read it together before asking the question.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Show comic image wk10-15-return-to-jerusalem-praising, then use the built-in verse block for Acts 2:23–24.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">This verse says death ‘could not hold him in its power.’ Why is Jesus being more powerful than death such good news for anyone who trusts in him?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Guide toward: because Jesus conquered death, everyone who trusts him can share in that same victory — death is not the final word for those who belong to him.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"5\">4</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Eyewitnesses</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3–4 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Bring up the 1 Corinthians 15:3–8a verse slide and read it together, then ask the question.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Use the built-in verse block for 1 Corinthians 15:3–8a.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">This verse lists specific eyewitnesses — Peter, the twelve, over five hundred people, James, and all the apostles. Why do you think Paul was so careful to list exactly who saw Jesus alive?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Guide toward: Paul is presenting evidence, not just retelling a nice story. Many of those eyewitnesses were still alive when he wrote this and could be asked directly. This is part of why we can be confident today that the resurrection is a real historical event, not a legend.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"5\">5</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>God's gift is eternal life</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3–4 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Bring up the Romans 6:23b memory verse slide and read it together, then ask the final connecting question.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Use the built-in verse block for Romans 6:23b.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Our memory verse says God's gift is eternal life, given through Jesus. How does everything we've seen this week — the empty tomb, Jesus eating and being touched, his promise to prepare a place for us — make that gift feel real, rather than just a nice idea?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Guide toward: because Jesus really did rise physically and is alive right now, his promise of eternal life for those who trust him isn't wishful thinking — it's backed up by something that actually happened in history.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"3\">6</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Memory verse from memory</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">1–2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Challenge the class to recite the memory verse together fully from memory, with the screen as a backup only.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Use the say-it-from-memory activity block.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"3\">7</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Closing prayer</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">1–2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Close the week — and the term's unit on Luke's Gospel — together with the prayer in the app, said as a class.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Use the built-in prayer block to close.</td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 11 begins a new term and a new unit for the B1 cycle.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Memory Verse</div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">“Don't be worried! Have faith in God and have faith in me. There are many rooms in my Father's house. I wouldn't tell you this, unless it was true. I am going there to prepare a place for each of you. After I have done this, I will come back and take you with me. Then we will be together.” — <em>John 14:1–3 (CEV)</em><br>“God had already planned and decided that Jesus would be handed over to you. So you took him and had evil men put him to death on a cross. But God set him free from death and raised him to life. Death could not hold him in its power.” — <em>Acts 2:23–24 (CEV)</em><br>“Christ died for our sins, as the Scriptures say. He was buried, and three days later he was raised to life, as the Scriptures say. Christ appeared to Peter, then to the twelve. After this, he appeared to more than five hundred other followers — most of them are still alive, but some have died. He also appeared to James, and then to all of the apostles. Finally, he appeared to me.” — <em>1 Corinthians 15:3–8a (CEV)</em><br>“God's gift is eternal life given by Jesus Christ our Lord.” — <em>Romans 6:23b (CEV)</em></blockquote></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Closing Prayer</div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Dear God, thank you that Jesus is more powerful than death, and that he rose again just as he promised. Thank you that his resurrection means we can trust every one of his promises, including the promise of eternal life. Help us to live with hope because Jesus is alive. Amen.</blockquote></div>"}}}}},"Term 2":{"label":"Week","children":{"Week 1: Let the exploring begin!":{"label":"Day","children":{"Week Overview":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Genesis 1–11</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Right from the very beginning, people keep breaking their relationship with God through sin — but every single time, God responds with grace instead of giving up on them. Genesis 1–11 sets the stage for the biggest act of grace yet: God's promises to Abraham.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Source</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Connect B1 Upper Primary — Lesson 11</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Monday: In the beginning...":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">10–15 minutes</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Genesis 1–2</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">In the beginning...</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Right from the very beginning, people keep breaking their relationship with God through sin — but every single time, God responds with grace instead of giving up on them. Genesis 1–11 sets the stage for the biggest act of grace yet: God's promises to Abraham.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning that God created everything, including people, and that all of it was very good.</li><li>We are learning that Adam and Eve lived in a perfect relationship with God, each other, and creation.</li><li>I can say this term's new memory verse, Genesis 1:1.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Listen for whether students can describe creation as ‘very good’ in their own words, and can explain what made Eden's relationships between God, people, and creation perfect.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>CEV Bible (optional, for reading Genesis 1:1 aloud)</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">1</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Exploring, starting today</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2–3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Open by framing the whole term as an exploring theme leading to Abraham next week, then ask the warm-up question.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">This term we're going exploring — all the way back to the very start of the Bible, to a story that leads up to a man named Abraham, who we'll meet next week. If you were exploring a brand new place, what's the first thing you'd want to work out?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Accept responses like a map, landmarks, or a starting point. Guide toward: good explorers look for patterns, so they know what's coming next. That's exactly what we're doing today — looking for a pattern right from the first page of the Bible.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"2\">2</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Everything, and it was very good</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3–4 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Read through the two creation story blocks together, keeping the tone celebratory — this is the high point before things go wrong tomorrow.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">3</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Your best day ever</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2–3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Ask the question and let students share freely — there's no wrong answer here.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Think about one of the best days you've ever had. What made it so good?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Accept any answers. Use this to help students imagine just how good ‘very good’ must have felt for Adam and Eve — no sadness, no arguments, nothing broken between them and God.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"3\">4</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>This term's memory verse</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Introduce Genesis 1:1 as the new memory verse for this term's unit and say it together a few times.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Use the built-in verse block for Genesis 1:1, then the say-it-together activity block.</td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Day 2 shows what happens when that perfect relationship breaks down for the first time.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Memory Verse</div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">“In the beginning God created the heavens and the earth.” — <em>Genesis 1:1 (CEV)</em></blockquote></div>"},"Tuesday: The first cycle":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">10–15 minutes</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Genesis 3</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">The first cycle</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Right from the very beginning, people keep breaking their relationship with God through sin — but every single time, God responds with grace instead of giving up on them. Genesis 1–11 sets the stage for the biggest act of grace yet: God's promises to Abraham.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning that Adam and Eve chose to disobey God even though he had already given them everything they needed.</li><li>We are learning that God's response to their sin included real consequences, but also real grace.</li><li>I can describe the pattern SIN → CONSEQUENCES → GRACE in my own words.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Listen for whether students can retell what Adam and Eve did wrong, name a consequence they faced, and identify the clothing God made them as an act of grace.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>CEV Bible (optional, for reading Genesis 3:21 aloud)</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"2\">1</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Recap: a perfect start</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">1–2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Briefly recap yesterday's perfect relationship between Adam, Eve, and God before introducing the turn.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">2</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>The one thing they wanted</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3–4 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Tell the story of the one forbidden tree and the decision to disobey, then ask the question.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Adam and Eve already had a perfect world — but they still wanted the one thing they weren't allowed to have. Why do you think it can be so tempting to want the one thing you can't have, even when you're surrounded by good things already?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Accept honest responses — there's no single right answer here. It's a very human pattern: focusing on the one limit rather than everything we already have.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">3</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Consequences, then grace</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">4–5 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Explain the consequences of the sin, then reveal that God still made them clothes before sending them out, and ask the question.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">God could have simply given up on Adam and Eve after this. Instead, before sending them out of the garden, he made them clothes with his own hands. What does that tell you about how God felt toward them, even right after they had sinned against him?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Guide toward: it shows God still cared for them practically, and that being sent out was part of a plan to eventually save them — not to abandon them for good.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"2\">4</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Naming the pattern</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">1–2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Say the SIN → CONSEQUENCES → GRACE pattern out loud together so it sticks before the memory verse.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"3\">5</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Memory verse</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">1–2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Say this week's memory verse together, trying it once without looking at the screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Use the built-in verse block for Genesis 1:1, then the say-it-together activity block.</td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Day 3 shows the same pattern play out again — this time for the whole world.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Memory Verse</div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">“In the beginning God created the heavens and the earth.” — <em>Genesis 1:1 (CEV)</em></blockquote></div>"},"Wednesday: The cycle again":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">10–15 minutes</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Genesis 6–9</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">The cycle again</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Right from the very beginning, people keep breaking their relationship with God through sin — but every single time, God responds with grace instead of giving up on them. Genesis 1–11 sets the stage for the biggest act of grace yet: God's promises to Abraham.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning that sin spread across the whole world as more people filled the earth.</li><li>We are learning that God judged the world's sin with a flood, but showed grace to Noah's family and made a covenant with a rainbow as its sign.</li><li>I can spot the SIN → CONSEQUENCES → GRACE pattern for a second time in this unit.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Listen for whether students can identify the flood as the consequence and the rainbow covenant as the grace in this second cycle, without needing it spelled out for them.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"2\">1</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Same pattern, bigger scale</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">1–2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Frame today as the same pattern from yesterday, now playing out for the whole world rather than just two people.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">2</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Sin spreads</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Describe how sin spread as the population grew, then ask the question.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">That's a pretty extreme description — hearts that were only evil, all the time. Why do you think sin tends to spread and grow worse over time, instead of staying small and contained?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Accept responses. Guide toward: sin doesn't stay isolated — it spreads through choices, relationships, and generations, a bit like the plague described earlier in the manual's Bible background.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"2\">3</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>The flood and the ark</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Tell the flood and ark story briefly, keeping the focus on judgement plus rescue, not on frightening detail.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">4</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>A promise with a rainbow</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3–4 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Reveal the rainbow covenant, then ask the closing question.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Once again we see SIN, then CONSEQUENCES... and then GRACE — an undeserved promise, with a rainbow as its sign. Why do you think God chose to give people a visible reminder of his promise, rather than just making the promise and leaving it there?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Guide toward: physical reminders help us remember and trust promises we can't always feel or see day to day — it's a pattern God uses more than once in the Bible.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"3\">5</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Memory verse</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">1–2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Try the memory verse together, aiming for fully from memory this time.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Use the built-in verse block for Genesis 1:1, then the say-it-from-memory activity block.</td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Day 4 shows the pattern one more time — but with a piece missing.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Memory Verse</div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">“In the beginning God created the heavens and the earth.” — <em>Genesis 1:1 (CEV)</em></blockquote></div>"},"Thursday: A cycle with a piece missing":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">10–15 minutes</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Genesis 11:1–9</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">A cycle with a piece missing</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Right from the very beginning, people keep breaking their relationship with God through sin — but every single time, God responds with grace instead of giving up on them. Genesis 1–11 sets the stage for the biggest act of grace yet: God's promises to Abraham.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning that the people at Babel sinned through pride, trying to make a name for themselves instead of trusting God's plan.</li><li>We are learning that this third cycle ends with consequences but no visible grace — an unfinished pattern.</li><li>I can explain why the missing grace at Babel sets up next week's introduction to Abraham.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Listen for whether students can identify that grace is missing from this third cycle, and can hold the tension of ‘has God stopped loving people?’ without needing it resolved today.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"2\">1</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Recap: two cycles so far</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">1–2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Recap the two completed cycles from earlier in the week before introducing Babel.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">2</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>A tower to the sky</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Tell the Tower of Babel story, then ask the pride question.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">The people wanted to ‘make a name for themselves’ instead of trusting God's plan for them. What's the difference between working hard at something, and trying to make yourself great instead of trusting God?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Accept responses. Guide toward: the problem isn't hard work or ambition itself — it's pride that rejects God's plan and tries to put ourselves at the centre instead of him.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">3</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Scattered — and something's missing</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3–4 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Describe the scattering and confusion of languages, then ask students to spot what's missing from the pattern.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">SIN, then CONSEQUENCES... but think back to the pattern from the last two days. What's missing this time?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Guide toward: there's no GRACE step this time — the cycle looks broken and unfinished, as if God has stopped being kind to his people.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">4</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Has God stopped loving people?</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2–3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Ask the harder question and let it sit — don't over-resolve it, since next week's lesson is the answer.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Does that mean God has really stopped loving the people he made?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Guide toward: no — the Bible tells us God never stops loving the people he made. It's fine to leave this question sitting unresolved for now; the class finds out how God answers it next week.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"3\">5</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Memory verse and closing prayer</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Finish the week's memory verse fully from memory, then close together with the prayer in the app.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Use the say-it-from-memory activity block, then the built-in prayer block to close.</td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 12 introduces Abraham, and how God's promises to him begin to answer the missing grace from this week.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Memory Verse</div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">“In the beginning God created the heavens and the earth.” — <em>Genesis 1:1 (CEV)</em></blockquote></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Closing Prayer</div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Dear God, thank you that you are loving and kind, and that you keep showing us grace even when we don't deserve it. Please help us trust that you haven't stopped loving your people, even when we can't yet see how. Help us learn all about your plan for Abraham this term. Amen.</blockquote></div>"}}},"Week 2: Promises, promises, promises":{"label":"Day","children":{"Week Overview":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Genesis 12:1–9</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Last week's pattern seemed to break at Babel, with no grace in sight. This week we find out God never stopped loving his people — he had a plan all along, starting with an ordinary man named Abraham. Through Abraham, and the promises God makes to him, the whole world will be blessed.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Source</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Connect B1 Upper Primary — Lesson 12</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Monday: Meet the explorer":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">10–15 minutes</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Genesis 12:1–7</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Meet the explorer</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Last week's pattern seemed to break at Babel, with no grace in sight. This week we find out God never stopped loving his people — he had a plan all along, starting with an ordinary man named Abraham. Through Abraham, and the promises God makes to him, the whole world will be blessed.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning that God called an ordinary man named Abraham to leave his home and go to a land God would show him.</li><li>We are learning that Abraham trusted and obeyed God, even without knowing exactly where he was going.</li><li>I can say this term's new memory verse, Numbers 23:19, and explain what it means for God to ‘keep’ a promise.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Listen for whether students can retell who Abraham was and what God asked him to do, and can describe Abraham's response as trust rather than certainty.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>CEV Bible (optional, for reading Genesis 12:1–7 aloud)</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">1</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Exploring without a map</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2–3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Open with the warm-up question about exploring somewhere new with no map, then bridge into meeting Abraham.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">If you were about to go exploring somewhere brand new, with no map and no idea exactly where you'd end up, what's one thing that would make it hard to say yes?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Accept responses like fear of the unknown, leaving family/friends behind, or not knowing what to pack. Use this to set up how big a deal Abraham's obedience is about to be.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"2\">2</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>An ordinary man</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">1–2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Introduce Abraham as an ordinary descendant of Noah, living his everyday life in Haran with Sarah and Lot, before God spoke to him.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"3\">3</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Acting out God's call</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">4–5 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Choose four volunteers for Abraham, Sarah, Lot, and God, and run the built-in script as a fullscreen presenter for the whole class to watch.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Use the built-in roleplay block — tap 'Start the script on screen' to open the fullscreen presenter for your four volunteers.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">4</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Could you trust that much?</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2–3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Ask the question and let students be honest about how hard that would be.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Abraham left his home country without knowing exactly where he'd end up. Do you think you could trust someone enough to do that — pack up and go, just because they told you to?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Accept honest responses. Guide toward: it's a huge ask, which is exactly why the Bible holds Abraham up as such a striking example of trust in God.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"3\">5</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>This term's memory verse</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Introduce Numbers 23:19 as the new memory verse for this unit and say it together for the first time.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Use the built-in verse block for Numbers 23:19, then the say-it-together activity block.</td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Day 2 unpacks the three specific promises God made to Abraham in that call.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Memory Verse</div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">“God is no mere human! He doesn’t tell lies or change his mind. God always keeps his promises.” — <em>Numbers 23:19 (CEV)</em></blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Roleplay: Lesson 12 script: God calls Abraham</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-amber-soft\">Characters: Abraham, Sarah, Lot, God</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Abraham</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Hi, my name is Abraham. I live in the land of Haran with my wife Sarah and my nephew Lot, and all our extended family.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>(stage direction)</em> (Sarah and Lot enter.)</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Abraham</th><td class=\"lt-td\">One day, I was going about my business as usual when God spoke to me. He said...</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">God</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Leave your country, your family, and your relatives, and go to the land that I will show you. I will bless you and make your descendants into a great nation. You will become famous and be a blessing to others. I will bless anyone who blesses you, but I will put a curse on anyone who puts a curse on you. Everyone on earth will be blessed because of you.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Abraham</th><td class=\"lt-td\">It all seemed pretty strange to me. I was 75 years old when the LORD told me to leave Haran. Sarah was only a little younger than me, and we didn't have any children. But I obeyed and left. I took my wife Sarah, Lot, and all the possessions and servants I had collected while in Haran.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>(stage direction)</em> (Abraham, Sarah, and Lot act out packing their bags and begin walking on the spot.)</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Abraham</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We walked and walked for ages until finally we came to the land of Canaan. We went as far as the sacred tree of Moreh, in a place called Shechem, and stopped there.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>(stage direction)</em> (Abraham, Sarah, and Lot stop walking on the spot.)</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Abraham</th><td class=\"lt-td\">The Canaanites were still living in the land at that time, but the LORD appeared and spoke to me again, and promised...</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">God</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I will give this land to your family forever.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Abraham</th><td class=\"lt-td\">This was a pretty amazing promise! So I built an altar there for the LORD and thanked him for the promises he made to me. I knew that God would keep the promises he made. So I trusted him!</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>(stage direction)</em> (Abraham acts out building a stone mound.)</p></div></div>"},"Tuesday: Three big promises":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">10–15 minutes</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Genesis 12:1–3</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Three big promises</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Last week's pattern seemed to break at Babel, with no grace in sight. This week we find out God never stopped loving his people — he had a plan all along, starting with an ordinary man named Abraham. Through Abraham, and the promises God makes to him, the whole world will be blessed.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning the three specific promises God made to Abraham: land, a great nation, and blessing to the whole world.</li><li>We are learning that these promises would have seemed almost impossible to Abraham and Sarah at the time.</li><li>I can name all three promises God made to Abraham.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Listen for whether students can list all three promises (land, great nation, blessings to the world) without prompting.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"2\">1</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Recap: God calls Abraham</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">1–2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Briefly recap yesterday's roleplay before unpacking the promises in detail.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"2\">2</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Land, nation, blessing</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3–4 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Introduce each of the three promises one at a time, giving students a moment to picture what each one meant.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">3</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>The hardest promise to believe</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2–3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Ask which promise would have felt hardest to believe, given Abraham and Sarah's circumstances.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">At the time, Abraham and Sarah didn't own any land, and didn't have any children at all. Which of those three promises do you think would have felt hardest for them to believe?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Accept responses — there's no single right answer. Guide toward: all three would have seemed almost impossible at the time, which is exactly why trusting God's promise mattered so much.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">4</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Promises for us too?</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2–3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Plant the idea that these promises reach beyond Abraham to us today, without fully resolving it yet.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">The Bible says these promises weren't just for Abraham back then — they matter for people today too, including us. Why do you think a promise made to one man, thousands of years ago, could still matter for us now?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Guide toward: the third promise — blessing the whole world — is the big clue. It's fine if students can't fully answer this yet; it's something we'll keep discovering this term.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"3\">5</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Memory verse</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">1–2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Try the memory verse together, once without looking at the screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Use the built-in verse block for Numbers 23:19, then the say-it-together activity block.</td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Day 3 looks at how Abraham actually responded to these promises — by trusting and obeying, one step at a time.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Memory Verse</div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">“God is no mere human! He doesn’t tell lies or change his mind. God always keeps his promises.” — <em>Numbers 23:19 (CEV)</em></blockquote></div>"},"Wednesday: Obeying without knowing":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">10–15 minutes</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Genesis 12:4–7</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Obeying without knowing</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Last week's pattern seemed to break at Babel, with no grace in sight. This week we find out God never stopped loving his people — he had a plan all along, starting with an ordinary man named Abraham. Through Abraham, and the promises God makes to him, the whole world will be blessed.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning that Abraham obeyed God immediately, packing up his whole household and setting off without knowing exactly where he'd end up.</li><li>We are learning that when God repeated his promise at Shechem, Abraham responded by building an altar and worshipping God publicly.</li><li>I can describe Abraham's obedience as trusting one step at a time, rather than seeing the whole plan in advance.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Listen for whether students can explain that Abraham obeyed without knowing the destination, and can identify the altar at Shechem as a public act of worship and trust.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>CEV Bible (optional, for reading Genesis 12:4–7 aloud)</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"2\">1</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>From promise to action</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">1–2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Bridge from yesterday's three promises into how Abraham actually responded to them.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">2</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Packing up at 75</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2–3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Tell how Abraham obeyed immediately and set off not knowing his destination, then ask the trust question.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">What's the difference between trusting a plan when you can see every single step in advance, and trusting a plan when you can only see the very next step?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Guide toward: real trust is shown most clearly when you can't see the whole picture — that's exactly the kind of trust Abraham showed.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">3</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>An altar at Shechem</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Tell how God repeated the land promise at Shechem and Abraham responded by building an altar, then ask the worship question.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Abraham didn't just say a quiet 'thank you' in his head — he built an altar and worshipped God out in the open, where anyone could see. Why do you think he responded that publicly, rather than keeping it to himself?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Guide toward: worship isn't just private gratitude — it's a way of publicly declaring that we trust and honour God, and it can encourage others around us too.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"3\">4</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Memory verse</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">1–2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Try the memory verse together, aiming for fully from memory this time.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Use the built-in verse block for Numbers 23:19, then the say-it-from-memory activity block.</td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Day 4 brings the three promises back together and asks the big question: is God really a promise keeper?</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Memory Verse</div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">“God is no mere human! He doesn’t tell lies or change his mind. God always keeps his promises.” — <em>Numbers 23:19 (CEV)</em></blockquote></div>"},"Thursday: Is God a promise keeper?":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">10–15 minutes</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Genesis 12:1–9 (recap)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Is God a promise keeper?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Last week's pattern seemed to break at Babel, with no grace in sight. This week we find out God never stopped loving his people — he had a plan all along, starting with an ordinary man named Abraham. Through Abraham, and the promises God makes to him, the whole world will be blessed.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning that Abraham's story answers last week's unresolved question — God never stopped loving the people he made.</li><li>We are learning that God's promises to Abraham were his plan to bless the whole world, undoing the scattering at Babel.</li><li>I can explain, in my own words, why the Bible calls God a promise keeper.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Listen for whether students can connect Abraham's story back to the missing grace from last week's Babel cycle, and can explain in their own words why God is described as a promise keeper.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"2\">1</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Recap the week</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">1–2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Recap Abraham, the three promises, and his obedience before asking the big question.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">2</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Promise keeper or promise breaker?</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2–3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Ask the central question of the week and guide students firmly toward the answer.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">So here's the big question this whole week has been building to: based on what we've seen so far, do you think God is a promise keeper, or a promise breaker?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Accept responses, but guide firmly toward: God is a promise keeper — that's exactly what this term's memory verse tells us, and it's what we'll keep seeing all term.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"2\">3</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>The missing grace, found</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2–3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Connect Abraham's call directly back to last week's unfinished Babel cycle — the grace was on its way all along.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">4</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>What this means for us</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2–3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Ask what it means personally that God keeps his promises, letting students make it their own.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">The Bible tells us these promises weren't only for Abraham — they're for people today too, including us. What do you think it means for you personally that God keeps his promises?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Accept honest responses. Guide toward: if God always keeps his word, that means we can trust him with the big things in our own lives too, not just admire Abraham's story from a distance.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"3\">5</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Memory verse and closing prayer</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Finish the week's memory verse fully from memory, then close together with the prayer in the app.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Use the say-it-from-memory activity block, then the built-in prayer block to close.</td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 13 shows that even though God keeps every promise, Abraham has to learn to wait a long time for this one to come true.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Memory Verse</div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">“God is no mere human! He doesn’t tell lies or change his mind. God always keeps his promises.” — <em>Numbers 23:19 (CEV)</em></blockquote></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Closing Prayer</div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Dear God, thank you for the promises you made to Abraham. Thank you that you are a God who keeps every promise you make, even when we can't see how yet. Please help us trust you the way Abraham did, one step at a time. Amen.</blockquote></div>"}}},"Week 3: Waiting, waiting, waiting":{"label":"Day","children":{"Week Overview":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Genesis 15:1–18:15; 21:1–7</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">God kept every promise he made to Abraham and Sarah — but it took twenty-five long years. Waiting is hard, and Abraham and Sarah sometimes struggled to trust God's timing along the way. But God never forgot, and he never gave up. Christians believe God can always be trusted to keep his promises, even if he takes far longer than we expect.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Source</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Connect B1 Upper Primary — Lesson 13</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Monday: Beginning to wonder":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">10–15 minutes</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Genesis 15:1–6</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Beginning to wonder</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">God kept every promise he made to Abraham and Sarah — but it took twenty-five long years. Waiting is hard, and Abraham and Sarah sometimes struggled to trust God's timing along the way. But God never forgot, and he never gave up. Christians believe God can always be trusted to keep his promises, even if he takes far longer than we expect.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning that years passed after God's promises to Abraham with no child being born, and that Abraham began to doubt.</li><li>We are learning that God reassured Abraham with the promise of descendants as countless as the stars.</li><li>I can explain that Abraham trusted God even though he couldn't see how the promise would come true.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Listen for whether students can explain why the passing of time (rather than the original promise itself) tested Abraham's trust, and can describe the stars promise in their own words.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>CEV Bible (optional, for reading Genesis 15:4b–5 aloud)</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">1</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>A message you can't yet see</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Open with the sealed-message/time-capsule warm-up question to introduce the theme of trusting something you can't yet see come true.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Imagine you sealed up a message today that wouldn't be opened for years and years. Do you think it would be easy or hard to trust that it was still safely there, waiting, the whole time you couldn't see it?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Accept responses. This is the idea behind a time capsule — something sealed away and easy to forget about, even though it's still there. Use it to introduce this week's big theme: waiting for a promise you can't yet see come true.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"2\">2</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Recap and the problem</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2–3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Briefly recap last week's three promises, then explain that years passed with still no child for Abraham and Sarah.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">3</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Waiting shakes trust</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2–3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Ask why an ongoing wait can be harder to trust through than the original decision, letting students reflect honestly.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Abraham had trusted God enough to leave his whole home behind. But now, years later, with nothing to show for it yet, he was starting to doubt. Why do you think waiting can shake our trust more than the original hard decision did?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Guide toward: it's often easier to trust God for one big decision than to keep trusting him through a long, uncertain wait with no answer in sight — that ongoing trust is a different, harder kind of faith.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">4</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Count the stars</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3–4 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Read Genesis 15:4b–5 together and unpack why God used a promise too big to count.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Abraham couldn't count the stars — there were far too many. Why do you think God chose a promise like that, one that was literally impossible to count, instead of just giving Abraham a number?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Guide toward: it showed the promise was bigger than Abraham could ever measure or fully picture — trusting it meant trusting God's word, not just doing the maths.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"3\">5</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Memory verse</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">1–2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Close by explaining that Abraham's trust was credited to him as righteousness, then say this term's memory verse together.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Use the built-in verse block for Numbers 23:19, then the say-it-together activity block.</td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Day 2 shows that even Abraham and Sarah, while they waited, sometimes struggled to trust God and tried to take matters into their own hands.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Memory Verse</div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">“You will have a son of your own, and everything you have will be his.” Then the Lord took Abram outside and said, “Look at the sky and see if you can count the stars. That's how many descendants you will have.” — <em>Genesis 15:4b–5 (CEV)</em><br>“God is no mere human! He doesn't tell lies or change his mind. God always keeps his promises.” — <em>Numbers 23:19 (CEV)</em></blockquote></div>"},"Tuesday: Taking matters into their own hands":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">10–15 minutes</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Genesis 16</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Taking matters into their own hands</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">God kept every promise he made to Abraham and Sarah — but it took twenty-five long years. Waiting is hard, and Abraham and Sarah sometimes struggled to trust God's timing along the way. But God never forgot, and he never gave up. Christians believe God can always be trusted to keep his promises, even if he takes far longer than we expect.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning that Sarah and Abraham grew impatient waiting for God's promise and tried to solve it themselves, through Hagar and Ishmael.</li><li>We are learning that this shortcut caused jealousy and trouble in their family rather than solving anything.</li><li>I can explain that even faithful people can struggle to trust God's timing, and that God stayed faithful anyway.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Listen for whether students can retell what Sarah and Abraham did instead of waiting, and can describe the consequence without treating the story as simple failure — aim for empathy alongside honesty.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"2\">1</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Still waiting</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">1–2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Recap that despite the star promise, more time passed with still no child.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">2</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>The temptation to force it</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Ask the warm-up question so students can relate personally before hearing today's story.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Have you ever wanted something so badly, and waited so long for it, that you were tempted to just make it happen yourself instead of waiting any longer?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Accept honest responses — most people have felt this. Use it to set up today's story with empathy rather than judgement: waiting is genuinely hard, even for people the Bible holds up as examples of great faith.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"2\">3</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Sarah's plan</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Tell the story of Hagar and Ishmael and the trouble it caused, keeping the tone gentle rather than harshly critical.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">4</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Even faithful people struggle</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2–3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Ask what this reveals about how hard waiting on God can be, guiding students away from judging Abraham and Sarah too harshly.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Sarah and Abraham loved God and had already shown real faith in him — but they still tried to solve the waiting their own way, and it went badly. What does that tell us about how hard waiting on God can be, even for people who trust him?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Guide toward: even people the Bible admires for their faith still struggled with impatience — this isn't really a story about Abraham and Sarah failing so much as a story about how genuinely hard waiting is, and how easy it is to want to force our own solution instead of trusting God's timing.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"3\">5</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>God stayed faithful anyway</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">1–2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Emphasise that God didn't abandon Abraham and Sarah despite their shortcut, then say the memory verse together.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Use the built-in verse block for Numbers 23:19, then the say-it-together activity block.</td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Day 3 jumps forward to when Abraham is 99 years old, as God renews his promise and gives Abraham and Sarah brand new names.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Memory Verse</div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">“God is no mere human! He doesn't tell lies or change his mind. God always keeps his promises.” — <em>Numbers 23:19 (CEV)</em></blockquote></div>"},"Wednesday: Father of many nations":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">10–15 minutes</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Genesis 17:1–5, 15–17; 18:10a</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Father of many nations</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">God kept every promise he made to Abraham and Sarah — but it took twenty-five long years. Waiting is hard, and Abraham and Sarah sometimes struggled to trust God's timing along the way. But God never forgot, and he never gave up. Christians believe God can always be trusted to keep his promises, even if he takes far longer than we expect.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning that twenty-four years after the first promise, at age 99, Abraham still had no child of his own.</li><li>We are learning that God renewed his covenant with Abraham, changing his and Sarah's names to reflect the promise still to come.</li><li>I can explain that God gave Abraham and Sarah a specific one-year timeline for their son to be born.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Listen for whether students can state Abraham and Sarah's ages at this point in the story, and can explain why their names were changed before the promise came true.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>CEV Bible (optional, for reading Genesis 17:4–5, 15–16 and 18:10a aloud)</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">1</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Twenty-four birthdays later</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Emphasise just how long twenty-four years is, then ask the reflection question.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">If you'd been waiting twenty-four years for something you'd been promised, do you think you'd still be expecting it to happen — or would you have given up hope by then?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Accept honest responses. Use it to help students feel the weight of just how long this wait really was — this isn't a short story, it's a quarter of a century.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"2\">2</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>New names, renewed promise</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3–4 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Read Genesis 17:4–5, 15–16 together and explain the covenant renewal and name changes.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">3</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Why change the names now?</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2–3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Ask why God changed their names before the promise was fulfilled.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Abraham means 'father of many', and Sarah means 'mother of nations' — new names that only made sense if the promise came true. Why do you think God chose to change their names before Isaac had even been born?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Guide toward: it gave Abraham and Sarah a daily reminder of God's promise, every single time anyone said their names — a sign of God's certainty, even before the promise was fulfilled.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">4</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>One year from now</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2–3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Read Genesis 18:10a and ask how it would feel to finally have an exact timeline after so long.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">After twenty-four years of waiting with no timeline at all, God suddenly gave Abraham and Sarah an exact promise: one year. How do you think it would feel to finally have a date to look forward to, after waiting that long?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Accept responses — likely a mix of excitement, disbelief, and maybe still some doubt after so many years of nothing.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"3\">5</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Memory verse</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">1–2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Try the memory verse together, once without looking at the screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Use the built-in verse block for Numbers 23:19, then the say-it-together activity block.</td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Day 4 acts out that visit to Abraham's tent, hears Sarah's reaction, and finds out whether God really did keep his promise.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Memory Verse</div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">“I promise that you will be the father of many nations. That's why I now change your name from Abram to Abraham … Abraham, your wife's name will now be Sarah instead of Sarai. I will bless her, and you will have a son by her. She will become the mother of nations, and some of her descendants will even be kings.” — <em>Genesis 17:4–5, 15–16 (CEV)</em><br>One of the guests was the Lord, and he said, “I'll come back about this time next year, and when I do, Sarah will already have a son.” — <em>Genesis 18:10a (CEV)</em><br>“God is no mere human! He doesn't tell lies or change his mind. God always keeps his promises.” — <em>Numbers 23:19 (CEV)</em></blockquote></div>"},"Thursday: Is God a promise keeper?":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">10–15 minutes</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Genesis 18:1–15; 21:1–7</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Is God a promise keeper?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">God kept every promise he made to Abraham and Sarah — but it took twenty-five long years. Waiting is hard, and Abraham and Sarah sometimes struggled to trust God's timing along the way. But God never forgot, and he never gave up. Christians believe God can always be trusted to keep his promises, even if he takes far longer than we expect.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning what happened when the Lord visited Abraham's tent and told Sarah she would have a son within the year.</li><li>We are learning that despite Sarah's disbelief, God kept his promise exactly on time — Isaac was born when Abraham was 100 and Sarah was 90.</li><li>I can explain, in my own words, why the Bible calls God a promise keeper even when a promise takes a long time.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Listen for whether students can retell the visit to Abraham's tent and Sarah's reaction in their own words, and can explain why God is described as a promise keeper despite the twenty-five year wait.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Volunteers to read Abraham, the LORD, Sarah, and Narrator for the roleplay</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"3\">1</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Acting out the visit</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">4–5 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Choose four volunteers for Narrator, Abraham, the LORD, and Sarah, and run the built-in script as a fullscreen presenter for the whole class to watch.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Use the built-in roleplay block — tap 'Start the script on screen' to open the fullscreen presenter for your four volunteers.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">2</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Would you have believed it?</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Ask whether students think they'd have believed the promise in Sarah's position.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Sarah thought the idea of having a baby at 89 was so unlikely that she laughed. Do you think you would have believed the promise if you were her — after everything that had already happened?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Accept honest responses. Guide toward: it's completely understandable that Sarah struggled to believe it after so many years — the question isn't really whether her doubt makes sense, but whether God kept his word anyway.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"2\">3</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Isaac is born</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Tell how, exactly one year later, Isaac was born and explain the meaning of his name.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">4</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Promise keeper or promise breaker?</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2–3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Ask the central question of the week and guide students firmly toward the answer.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">So here's the big question this whole week has been building to: after twenty-five years of waiting, doubting, and even trying to take matters into their own hands — did God turn out to be a promise keeper, or a promise breaker?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Accept responses, but guide firmly toward: God is a promise keeper — he kept every single promise, exactly as he said he would, even though it took twenty-five years.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">5</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>What are you waiting for?</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2–3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Revisit Monday's sealed-message idea, then invite students to name something they're waiting on God for personally.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">God's timing wasn't the same as Abraham and Sarah's. Is there something in your own life that you're waiting for right now, where it's hard to trust God's timing?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Accept honest, personal responses — this can be kept private if a student prefers. Guide toward: waiting is genuinely hard, but this week's story is proof that God's promises are worth trusting even when the timing feels slow.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"3\">6</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Memory verse and closing prayer</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Finish the week's memory verse fully from memory, then close together with the prayer in the app.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Use the say-it-from-memory activity block, then the built-in prayer block to close.</td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 14 moves on to a drama based on the story of Isaac and Rebekah.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Memory Verse</div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">“God is no mere human! He doesn't tell lies or change his mind. God always keeps his promises.” — <em>Numbers 23:19 (CEV)</em></blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Roleplay: Lesson 13 script: Three visitors at Mamre</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-amber-soft\">Characters: Narrator, Abraham, The LORD, Sarah</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Narrator</th><td class=\"lt-td\">One hot afternoon, Abraham was resting at the entrance of his tent near the great trees of Mamre. He looked up and saw three visitors standing nearby.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>(stage direction)</em> (Abraham jumps up and hurries over, bowing low.)</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Abraham</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Please, don't go on without resting a while. Let me bring water to wash your feet, and some bread to give you strength before you continue your journey.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">The LORD</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Very well. Do as you have said.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>(stage direction)</em> (Abraham hurries to Sarah's tent.)</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Abraham</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Sarah — quickly! Take our best flour and bake some bread for our guests!</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>(stage direction)</em> (Abraham chooses a tender calf to be prepared, then serves the visitors as they eat under a tree.)</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">The LORD</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Where is your wife Sarah?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Abraham</th><td class=\"lt-td\">She is there, in the tent.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">The LORD</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I'll come back about this time next year, and when I do, Sarah will already have a son.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Narrator</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Sarah was listening at the entrance to the tent behind him. Abraham and Sarah were very old, and Sarah was well past the age for having children.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>(stage direction)</em> (Sarah laughs quietly to herself.)</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Sarah</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Now that I am worn out, and my husband is old, will I really know such happiness?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">The LORD</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Why did Sarah laugh and say, 'Can I really have a child now that I am so old?' Is anything too hard for the Lord? I'll come back about this time next year, and Sarah will have a son.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Sarah</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I didn't laugh.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">The LORD</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Yes, you did laugh.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Closing Prayer</div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Dear God, thank you for the promises you made to Abraham. Thank you that you kept your promise to give Sarah and Abraham a son. Thank you that the Bible shows us you are a promise keeper. Help us to enjoy learning about your promises this term. Amen.</blockquote></div>"}}},"Week 4: God's family grows":{"label":"Day","children":{"Week Overview":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Genesis 24</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Over sixty years after God first promised Abraham a family as countless as the stars, Isaac still had no wife — and without a wife, the promise couldn't grow any further. God used the careful obedience of an unnamed servant, and the willing trust of a young woman named Rebekah, to move his promise forward another step. Christians believe God can be trusted to work through ordinary people, and even the small details of an ordinary day, to keep his promises.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Source</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Connect B1 Upper Primary — Lesson 14</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Monday: A wife for Isaac":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">10–15 minutes</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Genesis 24:1–9</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">A wife for Isaac</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Over sixty years after God first promised Abraham a family as countless as the stars, Isaac still had no wife — and without a wife, the promise couldn't grow any further. God used the careful obedience of an unnamed servant, and the willing trust of a young woman named Rebekah, to move his promise forward another step. Christians believe God can be trusted to work through ordinary people, and even the small details of an ordinary day, to keep his promises.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning that over sixty years after God's first promise, Isaac was forty and still had no wife.</li><li>We are learning that Abraham sent his trusted servant to find a wife for Isaac from his own family and homeland, not from the local Canaanite people.</li><li>I can explain why Isaac having a wife mattered for God's promise to Abraham to keep moving forward.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Listen for whether students can explain, in their own words, why a wife for Isaac was necessary for God's promise of a great family to continue.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>CEV Bible (optional, for reading Genesis 24:1–9 aloud)</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">1</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Trusting someone else's choice</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Open with the warm-up question about trusting someone else with an important decision.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Has anyone ever made an important decision on your behalf — like a parent choosing your school, or a friend picking a gift for you? How did it feel to trust someone else with something that mattered?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Accept responses. Use this to set up today's story, where Abraham trusts his servant to make one of the most important decisions of Isaac's life for him.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"2\">2</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Sixty years on</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Recap the promise so far and introduce the problem: Isaac is forty and unmarried.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">3</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Why this moment mattered</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2–3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Ask why a wife for Isaac was so important for the promise, guiding toward the idea of the next generation.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Without a wife for Isaac, Abraham's family couldn't grow into the great nation God had promised. Why do you think this moment mattered so much for God's promise to keep moving forward?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Guide toward: the promise depended on Isaac having children of his own one day — no wife meant no next generation, and no next generation meant the promise couldn't grow any further.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">4</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Abraham sends his servant</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Tell how Abraham commissioned his servant, insisting Isaac's wife come from his own family and homeland rather than the local Canaanites.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">The servant wasn't so sure about this plan. What if the woman he found didn't want to leave her home and travel back with him? Should he just bring Isaac to meet her instead? What do you think Abraham said to that?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Accept guesses, then reveal: Abraham said no — Isaac must stay in the land God had promised him. If the woman wasn't willing to come, the servant was free to come back alone, but Isaac himself was not to leave.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"3\">5</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Memory verse</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">1–2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Explain the servant set off trusting God with the outcome, then say this week's memory verse together.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Use the built-in verse block for Numbers 23:19, then the say-it-together activity block.</td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Day 2 follows the servant to a well, where he prays for a very specific sign.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Memory Verse</div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">“God is no mere human! He doesn't tell lies or change his mind. God always keeps his promises.” — <em>Numbers 23:19 (CEV)</em></blockquote></div>"},"Tuesday: A prayer with a sign":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">10–15 minutes</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Genesis 24:10–21</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">A prayer with a sign</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Over sixty years after God first promised Abraham a family as countless as the stars, Isaac still had no wife — and without a wife, the promise couldn't grow any further. God used the careful obedience of an unnamed servant, and the willing trust of a young woman named Rebekah, to move his promise forward another step. Christians believe God can be trusted to work through ordinary people, and even the small details of an ordinary day, to keep his promises.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning that the servant prayed for a specific, testable sign at the well to know which young woman God had chosen for Isaac.</li><li>We are learning that Rebekah unknowingly matched the sign exactly, offering to water all ten camels without being asked.</li><li>I can explain what this story shows us about God working through ordinary moments and ordinary kindness.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Listen for whether students can describe the servant's specific test, and can explain why Rebekah's actions mattering so precisely shows God at work.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>CEV Bible (optional, for reading Genesis 24:12–14 aloud)</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"2\">1</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Arriving at the well</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">1–2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Set the scene: the servant arrives at the well in the evening, the place where the town's young women came to draw water.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">2</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>A risky kind of prayer</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Ask the warm-up question about how confident students would feel asking God for something that specific.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">If you had to pick out one specific person, out of everyone who might walk by, using only God's help — would you feel confident asking God for something that exact, or would it feel too risky?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Accept honest responses. Use this to set up how bold the servant's prayer is about to be — he's not just asking for guidance in general, but for one very specific, testable sign.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">3</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>The servant's test</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2–3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Explain the servant's specific prayer and sign — water for him and for all his camels.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Offering a stranger a drink would be normal politeness. But offering to water ten thirsty camels too — that's a lot of extra work no one would expect. Why do you think the servant chose a sign that was so specific and so costly to fulfil?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Guide toward: a small, ordinary kindness could happen by chance, but going out of her way to water ten camels as well would be a clear, unmistakable answer — proof that God himself was at work, not just good luck.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">4</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Rebekah arrives</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2–3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Tell how Rebekah appeared before the servant had even finished praying, and matched the sign exactly without knowing it.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Rebekah had no idea she was answering anyone's prayer, or being tested for anything at all — she was simply being kind to a tired traveller. What does it show us about God's promises that he can work through something as ordinary as one person's everyday kindness?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Guide toward: God doesn't need dramatic miracles to keep his promises — he can work through completely ordinary moments and ordinary kindness, even when the person involved has no idea they're part of God's plan.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"3\">5</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Memory verse</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">1–2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Say this week's memory verse together again.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Use the built-in verse block for Numbers 23:19, then the say-it-together activity block.</td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Day 3 follows the servant to Rebekah's family, and Rebekah's own choice about whether to go.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Memory Verse</div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">“God is no mere human! He doesn't tell lies or change his mind. God always keeps his promises.” — <em>Numbers 23:19 (CEV)</em></blockquote></div>"},"Wednesday: Rebekah says yes":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">10–15 minutes</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Genesis 24:22–58</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rebekah says yes</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Over sixty years after God first promised Abraham a family as countless as the stars, Isaac still had no wife — and without a wife, the promise couldn't grow any further. God used the careful obedience of an unnamed servant, and the willing trust of a young woman named Rebekah, to move his promise forward another step. Christians believe God can be trusted to work through ordinary people, and even the small details of an ordinary day, to keep his promises.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning that the servant explained his prayer and how God had answered it to Rebekah's father and brother, Laban.</li><li>We are learning that Rebekah's family chose to trust God, even though they didn't want her to leave home for a distant land.</li><li>I can explain why the Bible shows Rebekah being asked, and choosing for herself, to go and be Isaac's wife.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Listen for whether students can retell why Rebekah's family agreed to let her go, and can explain why Rebekah being asked personally matters.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"2\">1</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Meeting the family</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Tell how the servant gave gifts and explained his mission to Rebekah's father and Laban.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">2</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>A hard decision</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2–3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Ask what helped the family decide to trust God with such a big, sudden request.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">This was a huge, sudden request — a stranger asking a family to send their daughter far away to marry a man she'd never met. Rebekah's father and Laban didn't want her to leave home for a distant land. What do you think helped them decide what to do?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Accept responses. Reveal: they chose to trust that this was God's doing, because of how exactly the servant's prayer had been answered — so, despite not wanting Rebekah to leave, they agreed to let her choose for herself.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">3</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Rebekah's own choice</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2–3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Tell how Rebekah was asked directly, and said yes, then discuss why this detail matters.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">The Bible makes a point of showing that Rebekah's family asked HER, and that she made the choice herself, rather than her father or brother deciding for her. Why do you think that detail matters?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Guide toward: Rebekah's trust in God had to be her own, not just something decided for her — real faith means choosing to trust God yourself, even when it's a huge, costly decision, like leaving your whole family and home behind.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"2\">4</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Leaving home</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Tell how the family blessed Rebekah, and how she confirmed she was ready to leave straight away.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"3\">5</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Memory verse</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">1–2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Try saying this week's memory verse together once without looking.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Use the built-in verse block for Numbers 23:19, then the say-it-together activity block.</td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Day 4 acts out the whole story as a class script, then looks at how everyone in it trusted God.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Memory Verse</div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">“God is no mere human! He doesn't tell lies or change his mind. God always keeps his promises.” — <em>Numbers 23:19 (CEV)</em></blockquote></div>"},"Thursday: Acting it out: God's family grows":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">10–15 minutes</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Genesis 24 (recap)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Acting it out: God's family grows</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Over sixty years after God first promised Abraham a family as countless as the stars, Isaac still had no wife — and without a wife, the promise couldn't grow any further. God used the careful obedience of an unnamed servant, and the willing trust of a young woman named Rebekah, to move his promise forward another step. Christians believe God can be trusted to work through ordinary people, and even the small details of an ordinary day, to keep his promises.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are recapping the whole story of Genesis 24 by acting it out as a class.</li><li>We are learning to identify how each character in the story — Abraham, the servant, Rebekah, and her family — showed trust in God.</li><li>I can explain how this story shows God moving his promise to Abraham forward, one step at a time.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Listen for whether students can name how each character trusted God, and can explain that this story represents real (if partial) progress toward God's promise, not its full completion.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Four (or five) student volunteers to read the Abraham, Servant, Rebekah, and Laban parts on screen, with the teacher or another volunteer reading Narrator</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"2\">1</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Recap the week</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Briefly recap the week's story before acting it out together.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"3\">2</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Acting out Genesis 24</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">6–8 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Choose volunteers for Abraham, the Servant, Rebekah, and Laban, and run the built-in script as a fullscreen presenter for the whole class to watch and follow along.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Use the built-in roleplay block — tap 'Start the script on screen' to open the fullscreen presenter for your volunteers.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">3</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Who trusted God?</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2–3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Ask students to identify how each character in the story showed trust in God.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Think back over everyone in this story — Abraham, the servant, Rebekah, and even Rebekah's family. How did each of them show they trusted God, even when it was hard or uncertain?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Guide through each: Abraham trusted God enough to send his servant on the journey instead of going himself; the servant trusted God enough to pray for a specific sign and act on it; Rebekah trusted God enough to leave her whole family and home; her family trusted God enough to let her go, and to let her make that choice herself.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">4</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>God's promise, one step further</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Ask whether God was keeping his promise this week, and tie it back to the ongoing growth of Abraham's family.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Was God keeping his promise to grow Abraham's family into a great nation, through everything that happened this week?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Accept responses. Guide toward: yes — not the whole promise all at once, but another real step forward. Isaac and Rebekah's marriage was the next generation of the family God had promised, moving one step closer to becoming as many as the stars.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"3\">5</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Memory verse and closing prayer</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Finish the week's memory verse fully from memory, then close together with the prayer in the app.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Use the say-it-from-memory activity block, then the built-in prayer block to close.</td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 15 continues the story of Isaac and Rebekah's family.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Memory Verse</div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">“God is no mere human! He doesn't tell lies or change his mind. God always keeps his promises.” — <em>Numbers 23:19 (CEV)</em></blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Roleplay: Lesson 14 script: God's family grows</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-amber-soft\">Characters: Narrator, Abraham, Servant, Rebekah, Laban</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Narrator</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Isaac grew up and became a man. His mother Sarah had died and his father Abraham was very old. God had made Abraham a rich and successful man. Abraham called his servant to him one day for a very important chat.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Abraham</th><td class=\"lt-td\">You need to go and find a wife for my son Isaac. Go to the distant land I came from and find a wife for Isaac from there. Do not choose a wife from the land near here, where we live.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Narrator</th><td class=\"lt-td\">The servant wasn't too sure about this. He said:</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Servant</th><td class=\"lt-td\">What if the woman doesn't want to come with me and be Isaac's wife? Should I come back and get Isaac?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Abraham</th><td class=\"lt-td\">No, Isaac must stay here. If she isn't willing to come, you may come back alone.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Narrator</th><td class=\"lt-td\">So the servant loaded ten of Abraham's camels with valuable gifts for Isaac's new wife. He then set off for a city in northern Syria where Abraham's brother lived. When the servant arrived, he stopped at a well, and while the camels rested, he prayed.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Servant</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Dear God, please keep your promise to Abraham and let me find a wife for Isaac today. I will ask a lady for a drink of water for me and the camels. If she says yes and gets me one, I will know she is the one you have chosen to marry Isaac. Amen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Narrator</th><td class=\"lt-td\">While the servant was still praying, a beautiful young woman came up to get some water. She was the granddaughter of Abraham's brother — though the servant didn't know it.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Servant</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Excuse me. May I please have a drink of water?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rebekah</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I will be glad to give you one. And some for your camels too.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Narrator</th><td class=\"lt-td\">The servant was very excited at this. He gave the young woman some gold bracelets and a ring, and asked if he could meet her family right away, as he had something very important to tell them all. Rebekah and the servant ran straight home to her family.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Servant</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I need to tell you why I have come here. I am Abraham's servant. The Lord has been good to my master, Abraham, and made him very rich. He has many sheep, camels, goats, cattle and donkeys, gold, silver, and servants. But he has only one son, Isaac, and Isaac doesn't have a wife. I promised I would come and find a wife from Abraham's family for Isaac, so that Abraham's land will stay in his family. Today at the well I prayed that God would show me the young woman to be Isaac's wife. Rebekah came, and now I am here.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Narrator</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rebekah's dad and her brother, Laban, both wondered what she was going to say. This kind of thing didn't happen every day. They didn't want Rebekah to go away to a distant land, but they chose to trust God.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Laban</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Dad and I believe this is what God wants. Let's ask Rebekah what she wants to do.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Narrator</th><td class=\"lt-td\">They asked Rebekah if she would go with the servant. And she replied:</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rebekah</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Yes, I will be Isaac's wife.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Narrator</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rebekah's family gave her their blessing, and said they would pray that she would have many children and grandchildren. They were very sad to see her leave, but they knew that, somehow, this was all part of God's plan. The servant got ready to leave, but Laban, who loved his sister very much, tried to delay him.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Laban</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Please can you wait a week or ten days, so we can be with Rebekah longer?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Servant</th><td class=\"lt-td\">No, don't make me wait any longer. I must get back to my master Abraham.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Narrator</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Laban thought he'd try again.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Laban</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Let's ask Rebekah what she would like to do! Rebekah, are you happy to leave now to go and be Isaac's wife?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rebekah</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Yes. I am ready.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Narrator</th><td class=\"lt-td\">The servant and Rebekah loaded up the camels and then set off across the desert. The end.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Closing Prayer</div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Dear Lord, thank you that the servant and Rebekah trusted you and were obedient. Thank you for the way the Bible shows how you keep your promises, and that people today can trust you and your promises too. Amen.</blockquote></div>"}}},"Week 5: Chalk and cheese":{"label":"Day","children":{"Week Overview":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Genesis 25:19–34</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Twenty years after Isaac married Rebekah, God again answered prayer for a childless couple — but this time with unexpected twins who were fighting each other even before birth. God told Rebekah that the older twin would serve the younger, turning everyone's expectations upside down. As Jacob and Esau grew up to be complete opposites, favouritism split the family in two, and Esau carelessly traded away his birthright — and the promises attached to it — for a single bowl of stew. Christians believe God keeps his promises to his people, but not always through the people we'd expect, or in the way we'd expect.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Source</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Connect B1 Upper Primary — Lesson 15</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Monday: Fighting before they were even born":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">10–15 minutes</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Genesis 25:19–23</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Fighting before they were even born</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Twenty years after Isaac married Rebekah, God again answered prayer for a childless couple — but this time with unexpected twins who were fighting each other even before birth. God told Rebekah that the older twin would serve the younger, turning everyone's expectations upside down. As Jacob and Esau grew up to be complete opposites, favouritism split the family in two, and Esau carelessly traded away his birthright — and the promises attached to it — for a single bowl of stew. Christians believe God keeps his promises to his people, but not always through the people we'd expect, or in the way we'd expect.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning that Isaac and Rebekah, like Abraham and Sarah before them, were unable to have children for many years until God answered prayer.</li><li>We are learning that Rebekah's pregnancy was unusually difficult, and that God told her the older of her twin sons would serve the younger.</li><li>I can explain why God choosing the younger twin was the opposite of what people in that culture would have expected.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Listen for whether students can explain why the prophecy about the older serving the younger was so unexpected for its time.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>CEV Bible (optional, for reading Genesis 25:19–23 aloud)</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">1</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Rivals before they'd met</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Open with the warm-up question about two people becoming rivals before they've even met.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Do you think it's possible for two people to become rivals before they've even met properly, or said a single word to each other? What would that even look like?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Accept guesses. Use this to set up today's very unusual detail: two brothers who were already fighting each other before either of them was even born.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"2\">2</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Answered prayer, again</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Recap Isaac and Rebekah's marriage and their twenty years without children, until God answered prayer.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">3</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Why God keeps doing this</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2–3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Ask why God might have let another couple in this family struggle to have children before answering prayer.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">This is now the second time in this family's story that God's promise has depended on a couple who couldn't have children for years and years. Why do you think God kept letting this happen?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Guide toward: it makes it completely clear that any child born is a gift from God, answered through prayer — not something Isaac and Rebekah achieved by themselves. It shows the promise depends on God's power, not human effort.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"5\">4</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>A painful, unusual pregnancy</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2–3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Describe Rebekah's difficult pregnancy and show the image of her in distress, then ask the follow-up question.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Use the built-in image block showing Rebekah expecting the fighting twins.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">What do you think it might have felt like for Rebekah to feel her own unborn babies fighting inside her, even before she'd met them?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Accept honest responses — confusing, worrying, painful. This is what led her to go and ask God directly what was happening.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">5</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>The LORD's answer</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2–3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Reveal God's prophecy to Rebekah, then discuss how it reversed cultural expectations.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">In those days, everyone expected the oldest son to be the most important one, the one in charge. What does it tell us about God, that here he flips that expectation and chooses to bless the younger twin instead?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Guide toward: God doesn't work the way people expect him to. He isn't bound by human traditions about who deserves to be first — he's free to choose whoever he wants for his plans.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"3\">6</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Memory verse</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">1–2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Say this week's memory verse together.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Use the built-in verse block for Numbers 23:19, then the say-it-together activity block.</td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Day 2 follows the twins' births, and how completely different Jacob and Esau turned out to be.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Memory Verse</div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">“God is no mere human! He doesn't tell lies or change his mind. God always keeps his promises.” — <em>Numbers 23:19 (CEV)</em></blockquote></div>"},"Tuesday: Chalk and cheese":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">10–15 minutes</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Genesis 25:24–28</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Chalk and cheese</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Twenty years after Isaac married Rebekah, God again answered prayer for a childless couple — but this time with unexpected twins who were fighting each other even before birth. God told Rebekah that the older twin would serve the younger, turning everyone's expectations upside down. As Jacob and Esau grew up to be complete opposites, favouritism split the family in two, and Esau carelessly traded away his birthright — and the promises attached to it — for a single bowl of stew. Christians believe God keeps his promises to his people, but not always through the people we'd expect, or in the way we'd expect.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning how Esau and Jacob got their names, and how completely different they turned out to be as they grew up.</li><li>We are learning that Isaac favoured Esau while Rebekah favoured Jacob, splitting the family's loyalties.</li><li>I can explain what the saying 'chalk and cheese' means and how it applies to these two brothers.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Listen for whether students can describe at least two ways Jacob and Esau were different from each other, and explain how parental favouritism affected the family.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>CEV Bible (optional, for reading Genesis 25:24–28 aloud)</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"2\">1</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>The twins are born</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Tell how Esau and Jacob got their names at birth.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">2</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>A hint in Jacob's name</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Ask what Jacob grabbing his brother's heel might foreshadow about him.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Even the way Jacob was born — grabbing at his brother's heel — hints at something about him. What do you think that detail might suggest about how Jacob will act later in this story?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Accept guesses, then reveal: Jacob's name became linked to grabbing and getting ahead by any means — which foreshadows exactly how he behaves later, trying to grab hold of things that aren't his yet.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">3</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Chalk and cheese</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2–3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Describe how differently the boys grew up, then ask about the saying 'chalk and cheese.'</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Does anyone know what the saying 'chalk and cheese' means? Can you think of two people you know — maybe even in your own family — who are chalk and cheese?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">It means two things, or two people, that are completely different from each other, even though you might expect them to be similar.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">4</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>A divided family</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2–3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Explain how Isaac favoured Esau and Rebekah favoured Jacob, then discuss what that might have felt like.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">How do you think it might have felt to grow up in a family where each parent openly favoured a different child?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Accept responses — hurtful, confusing, likely to cause jealousy and rivalry between the brothers. This favouritism is part of what fuels the conflict still to come in this story.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"3\">5</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Memory verse</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">1–2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Say this week's memory verse together again.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Use the built-in verse block for Numbers 23:19, then the say-it-together activity block.</td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Day 3 sees the family's rivalry come to a head over a bowl of stew.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Memory Verse</div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">“God is no mere human! He doesn't tell lies or change his mind. God always keeps his promises.” — <em>Numbers 23:19 (CEV)</em></blockquote></div>"},"Wednesday: A bowl of stew":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">10–15 minutes</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Genesis 25:29–34</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">A bowl of stew</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Twenty years after Isaac married Rebekah, God again answered prayer for a childless couple — but this time with unexpected twins who were fighting each other even before birth. God told Rebekah that the older twin would serve the younger, turning everyone's expectations upside down. As Jacob and Esau grew up to be complete opposites, favouritism split the family in two, and Esau carelessly traded away his birthright — and the promises attached to it — for a single bowl of stew. Christians believe God keeps his promises to his people, but not always through the people we'd expect, or in the way we'd expect.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning what a birthright meant in this culture, and why it mattered especially for Isaac's family.</li><li>We are learning that Esau sold his birthright to Jacob for a single bowl of stew.</li><li>I can explain what this trade shows about how Esau and Jacob each valued God's promises.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Listen for whether students can explain why the birthright mattered so much for this family, and can describe what Esau's choice reveals about his priorities.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>CEV Bible (optional, for reading Genesis 25:29–34 aloud)</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">1</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>What a birthright meant</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2–3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Ask what the birthright represented for this particular family, beyond ordinary inheritance.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">As the eldest son, Esau's birthright entitled him to a double share of the family inheritance, and to become the family's leader when Isaac died. But for this particular family, it meant something even bigger. What do you think the birthright represented for Isaac's family, beyond just money or land?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Guide toward: for this family, the birthright carried God's promise to Abraham forward — the land, the great nation, the blessing for the whole world. It wasn't just an inheritance, it was part of God's plan.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"3\">2</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Esau comes home starving</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Tell how Esau came home exhausted and begged Jacob for stew.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Show the image of Jacob offering Esau the stew.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"2\">3</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>The trade</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Describe how Jacob demanded the birthright in exchange, and Esau agreed.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">4</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>What Esau's choice shows</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2–3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Ask what Esau's willingness to trade away his birthright reveals about his priorities.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Esau said, 'What good will those rights do me if I'm about to die?' What does this tell us about how much Esau actually valued the promises God had made to his family?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Guide toward: Esau cared more about the comfort of the moment than about something with lasting, far greater value — he despised his birthright rather than treasuring it.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">5</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>What Jacob's choice shows</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Ask what Jacob's willingness to exploit his brother reveals about him.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">And what does Jacob's willingness to take advantage of his own brother's hunger tell us about him?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Guide toward: Jacob was cunning and opportunistic, willing to exploit his brother's weak moment — even though, unlike Esau, he did seem to understand how valuable the birthright really was.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"3\">6</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Memory verse</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">1–2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Try saying this week's memory verse together once without looking.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Use the built-in verse block for Numbers 23:19, then the say-it-together activity block.</td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Day 4 reads back through the whole story together and reflects on what it shows us about God.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Memory Verse</div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">“God is no mere human! He doesn't tell lies or change his mind. God always keeps his promises.” — <em>Numbers 23:19 (CEV)</em></blockquote></div>"},"Thursday: Reading it together: Chalk and cheese":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">10–15 minutes</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Genesis 25:19–34 (recap)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Reading it together: Chalk and cheese</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Twenty years after Isaac married Rebekah, God again answered prayer for a childless couple — but this time with unexpected twins who were fighting each other even before birth. God told Rebekah that the older twin would serve the younger, turning everyone's expectations upside down. As Jacob and Esau grew up to be complete opposites, favouritism split the family in two, and Esau carelessly traded away his birthright — and the promises attached to it — for a single bowl of stew. Christians believe God keeps his promises to his people, but not always through the people we'd expect, or in the way we'd expect.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are recapping the whole story of Genesis 25:19–34 by reading it together as a class.</li><li>We are learning to identify how the story's ending was different from what people at the time would have expected.</li><li>I can explain that God's promise didn't depend on Jacob or Esau being especially good people, but on God's own power to keep his word.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Listen for whether students can retell how Jacob ended up with the birthright, and can explain why the outcome was unexpected.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A confident reader (or the teacher) to lead the class through the built-in dictation reading</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"2\">1</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Recap the week</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Briefly recap the week's story before reading it together.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"3\">2</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Reading Genesis 25:19–34 together</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">5–6 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Lead the class through the built-in dictation as a fullscreen read-together, or invite a confident reader to lead it.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Use the built-in dictation block — tap 'Start the script on screen' to open the fullscreen presenter for the whole class to read together.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">3</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Who won the promise?</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">1–2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Ask which brother ended up with the birthright, and how.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Out of the two brothers, who ended up walking away with the promises God had made to this family — even though he wasn't the one born first?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Jacob did — through Esau's own careless choice to sell his birthright away, not through anything unfair Jacob did to take it (yet — there's more to Jacob's story still to come).</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">4</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>An unexpected way of keeping a promise</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Ask whether God kept his promise the way people would have expected, tying back to the lesson's big idea.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Thinking back over this whole week — was God keeping his promise to Abraham and Isaac in the way we might have expected, or in a surprising way?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Guide toward: a surprising way. Nobody would have expected the younger twin to be chosen over the older, or for the promise to be won through a bowl of stew rather than a formal blessing. God keeps his promises but not always in the way we expect him to.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"3\">5</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Memory verse and closing prayer</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Finish the week's memory verse fully from memory, then close together with the prayer in the app.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Use the say-it-from-memory activity block, then the built-in prayer block to close.</td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 16 continues the story of Jacob and Esau.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Memory Verse</div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">“God is no mere human! He doesn't tell lies or change his mind. God always keeps his promises.” — <em>Numbers 23:19 (CEV)</em></blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Whole-Class Reading: Lesson 15 dictation: Genesis 25:19–34</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Isaac was the son of Abraham, and he was forty years old when he married Rebekah, the daughter of Bethuel. She was also the sister of Laban, the Aramean from northern Syria.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Almost twenty years later, Rebekah still had no children. So Isaac asked the LORD to let her have a child, and the LORD answered his prayer.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Before Rebekah gave birth, she knew she was going to have twins, because she could feel them inside her, fighting each other. She thought, 'Why is this happening to me?' Finally, she asked the LORD why her twins were fighting, and he told her:</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>'Your two sons will become two separate nations. The younger of the two will be stronger, and the older son will be his servant.'</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>When Rebekah gave birth, the first baby was covered with red hair, so he was named Esau. The second baby grabbed on to his brother's heel, so they named him Jacob. Isaac was sixty years old when they were born.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>As Jacob and Esau grew older, Esau liked the outdoors and became a good hunter, while Jacob settled down and became a shepherd. Esau would take the meat of wild animals to his father Isaac, and so Isaac loved him more, but Jacob was his mother's favourite son.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>One day, Jacob was cooking some stew, when Esau came home hungry and said, 'I'm starving to death! Give me some of that red stew right now!' That's how Esau got the name 'Edom.'</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Jacob replied, 'Sell me your rights as the firstborn son.' 'I'm about to die,' Esau answered. 'What good will those rights do me?'</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>But Jacob said, 'Promise me your birthright, here and now!' And that's what Esau did. Jacob then gave Esau some bread and some of the bean stew, and when Esau had finished eating and drinking, he just got up and left, showing how little he thought of his rights as the firstborn.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Closing Prayer</div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Dear God, thank you that you are in control. Thank you that you have always had a plan to save your people from sin through Jesus. Thank you that nothing can stand in the way of you keeping your promises. Thank you that you are a powerful promise keeper! Amen.</blockquote></div>"}}},"Week 6: Sibling rivalry":{"label":"Day","children":{"Week Overview":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Genesis 27:1–40</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">God had already chosen Jacob, before he was even born, to be the one through whom he would keep his promises. But everyone in Isaac's family went about it in the worst possible way. Isaac tried to hand Esau the blessing in secret, going around God's own words to Rebekah. Rebekah and Jacob answered secrecy with deceit, tricking a blind old man into giving away what he thought he was choosing freely. Nobody in this story played fair, and nobody comes out looking good. Yet the blessing still ended up exactly where God had already said it would. Christians believe this shows how powerful God is: he doesn't need anyone's good behaviour to keep his promises — not even the sinful scheming of the very family he's working through.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Source</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Connect B1 Upper Primary — Lesson 16</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Monday: Two brothers, still fighting":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">10–15 minutes</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Genesis 27:1–4 (context)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Isaac's secret plan</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Even though Isaac and Rebekah both already knew God had chosen Jacob to carry his promises, Isaac tried to give his final blessing to his favourite son Esau instead — and to do it secretly. This sets up a whole family who each try to handle God's promise their own way rather than trusting God with it.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are recapping Jacob and Esau's rivalry from last week.</li><li>We are learning that Isaac tried to give the blessing to Esau in secret, even though God had already chosen Jacob.</li><li>I can explain that no human secrecy or scheming can stop God from keeping his promises.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Listen for whether students can explain who Isaac tried to bless, and why that was surprising given what God had already said about Jacob.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Recap image: the Jacob-and-Esau stew scene from last week</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">1</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Way in: sibling rivalry</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Ask the warm-up question about animals or siblings fighting for dominance to set the scene for today's story.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Have you ever seen two animals — maybe dogs, or even kangaroos — squaring up and shoving each other, both trying to come out on top? What does that kind of fight usually look like?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Accept any answer describing pushing, posturing, or wrestling for dominance. Use this to introduce today's theme: Jacob and Esau are about to have their biggest fight yet — not with fists, but with tricks.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"3\">2</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Recap last week</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Recap that Esau sold his birthright, and that God had already chosen Jacob before he was born.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Display the recap image of the stew scene.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">3</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Isaac's secret plan</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">5–6 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Read through the story of Isaac's plan to secretly bless Esau, and Rebekah overhearing it.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">How do you feel, hearing that Isaac tried to do this important blessing quietly, behind his family's back — even though he already knew God had chosen Jacob?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Accept honest responses. Isaac's secrecy here is his own way of going around what he knew God had said — not so different from how Rebekah and Jacob are about to go around Isaac.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"3\">4</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Memory verse and closing prayer</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Read the memory verse together and close with the built-in prayer.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Use the verse block, then the built-in prayer block to close.</td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Tomorrow: Jacob disguises himself and steals the blessing meant for Esau.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Memory Verse</div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">“God is no mere human! He doesn't tell lies or change his mind. God always keeps his promises.” — <em>Numbers 23:19 (CEV)</em></blockquote></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Closing Prayer</div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Dear God, thank you that you are powerful enough to keep your promises no matter what people around us try to do. Help us to trust you instead of taking matters into our own hands. Amen.</blockquote></div>"},"Tuesday: Jacob steals the blessing":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">10–15 minutes</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Genesis 27:5–29</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Jacob steals the blessing</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rebekah and Jacob answered Isaac's secrecy with deceit of their own — disguising Jacob as Esau so thoroughly that Isaac was completely fooled, and gave Jacob the blessing that carried all of God's promises. The outcome matched what God had already planned, but the method was dishonest and wrong.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning how Rebekah and Jacob disguised Jacob to steal Esau's blessing.</li><li>We are learning to identify what was wrong with the way Jacob got the blessing.</li><li>I can explain that God's plan succeeding doesn't make Jacob and Rebekah's deception acceptable.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Listen for whether students can describe the disguise Jacob used and can identify that deceiving Isaac was wrong, even though the result matched God's plan.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Image: Isaac blessing the disguised Jacob</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">1</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Recap Rebekah's plan</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">1–2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Ask what students think Rebekah might have done after overhearing Isaac's plan, before revealing the actual story.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Yesterday we heard that Rebekah overheard Isaac's secret plan. If you were Rebekah, what might you have done next?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Accept any guesses. Rebekah's actual plan involved deception, not honesty — set up today's story with this question before revealing what she actually did.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"5\">2</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>The disguise</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">5–6 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Read through how Rebekah dressed Jacob in Esau's clothes and goat skin, and how Jacob deceived Isaac to receive the blessing.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Display the image of Isaac blessing the disguised Jacob.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">There's a problem with this plan straight away — something obvious that could give Jacob away the moment he walked in. What do you think it might be?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Guide toward: Esau was a hairy, outdoorsy hunter, while Jacob was smooth-skinned and stayed close to home. If Isaac touched Jacob, he might immediately know it wasn't Esau.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">3</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Was this okay?</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2–3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Discuss whether Jacob's method of getting the blessing was acceptable, even though the outcome matched God's plan.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Jacob now has the blessing God always intended him to have. But think carefully — was the way he got it a good thing to copy, even though the outcome matched what God had planned?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Guide toward: no. Getting the right result through lying and deceiving your own father is not something to imitate. God's plan succeeding doesn't make Jacob and Rebekah's methods okay — it shows God is powerful enough to work his plan out even through people behaving badly.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"3\">4</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Closing prayer</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">1–2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Close together with the built-in prayer.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Use the built-in prayer block to close.</td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Tomorrow: Esau returns and discovers what's happened.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Closing Prayer</div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Dear God, thank you that your plans don't depend on people doing the right thing. Help us to be honest with the people around us, even when it feels easier not to be. Amen.</blockquote></div>"},"Wednesday: Esau's fury":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">10–15 minutes</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Genesis 27:30–40</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Esau's fury</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">When Esau returned from hunting, he discovered that Jacob had already stolen his blessing by deception. Esau's fury reveals that not one person in this story — Isaac, Rebekah, Jacob, or Esau — acted well or deserved God's blessing on their own merit.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning what happened when Esau discovered Jacob had taken his blessing.</li><li>We are learning to identify that every character in this story acted selfishly or dishonestly.</li><li>I can explain that God's promises don't depend on any person deserving them.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Listen for whether students can explain why Esau was upset, and whether they can identify that no character in the story behaved well.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">1</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Way in: losing out</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Ask students to imagine working hard toward something only to find someone else already took it.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Imagine working hard on something — hunting, cooking, getting everything ready — only to find out someone else already took what you were working towards. How do you think that would feel?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Accept honest responses — frustrating, unfair, angry. This sets up how Esau is about to feel when he returns from the hunt.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">2</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Esau's discovery</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">5 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Read through Esau's return, Isaac's trembling, and Esau's bitter cry for a blessing.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">What was really wrong with how Jacob got this blessing? How did he actually get it?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Accept responses. Jacob got it by disguising himself and lying directly to his father's face — cheating and deceiving him, not by anything fair or honest.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">3</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Nobody's the good guy</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2–3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Discuss how every character in the story acted badly in some way.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Isaac tried to bless the wrong son in secret. Rebekah and Jacob lied and disguised themselves to steal the blessing. Esau cared more about winning than about God's promises. Was there anyone in this story who comes across as clearly the 'good guy'?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Guide toward: no — everyone in this family acted selfishly, secretly, or dishonestly in this story. Nobody here deserved God's blessing based on their own behaviour.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"3\">4</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Memory verse and closing prayer</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Read the memory verse together and close with the built-in prayer.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Use the verse block, then the built-in prayer block to close.</td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Tomorrow: we retell the whole story as a boxing match commentary, and talk about what it shows us about God.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Memory Verse</div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">“God is no mere human! He doesn't tell lies or change his mind. God always keeps his promises.” — <em>Numbers 23:19 (CEV)</em></blockquote></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Closing Prayer</div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Dear God, thank you that even when the people around us let us down or treat each other unfairly, your promises don't depend on any of us being good enough. Amen.</blockquote></div>"},"Thursday: Reading it as a boxing match":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">10–15 minutes</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Genesis 27:1–40 (recap)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Reading it as a boxing match: what this shows us about God</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Retelling the whole week's story as a boxing-match commentary helps highlight that nobody in this fight played fair — yet God's plan for Jacob to receive the blessing still went ahead exactly as promised. This shows how powerful and trustworthy God is: his promises don't depend on people's good behaviour.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are recapping the whole story of Genesis 27:1–40 through an acted-out boxing commentary.</li><li>We are learning to identify that everyone in the story played dishonestly, yet God's plan still succeeded.</li><li>I can explain that God is powerful enough to keep his promises even through sinful people.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Listen for whether students can explain what was wrong with how Jacob won the blessing, and what the story shows about God's power to keep his promises.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A few confident volunteers to read the Commentator, Team Esau, and Team Jacob parts, plus the rest of the class to join in cheering</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"3\">1</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Act it out: the boxing commentary</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">6–7 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Assign volunteers to the Commentator, Team Esau, and Team Jacob roles (or split the class into two cheering sides) and run the built-in roleplay script on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Use the built-in roleplay block — tap 'Start the script on screen' to open the fullscreen presenter.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">2</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>What was wrong with the win?</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Discuss what was actually wrong with how Jacob won the blessing.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Now that we've replayed the whole story as a boxing match — what was actually wrong with how Jacob won this 'fight'?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Guide toward: Jacob tricked and cheated his own father and brother to get the blessing, even though God had already promised it to him. Winning the right result the wrong way is still wrong.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">3</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>What this shows us about God</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2–3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Draw out the big idea: God is powerful enough to keep his promises even through a sinful, scheming family.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">It's hard to believe God would put up with a family behaving like this. So what does this story actually show us about God?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Guide toward: it shows how amazing and powerful God is — he planned to save people through this exact family, even though every single person in it acted selfishly, secretly, or dishonestly. Nothing they did could stop God's plan.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"3\">4</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Memory verse and closing prayer</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Finish the week's memory verse fully from memory, then close together with the prayer in the app.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Use the say-it-from-memory activity block, then the built-in prayer block to close.</td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 17 continues the story of Jacob.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Memory Verse</div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">“God is no mere human! He doesn't tell lies or change his mind. God always keeps his promises.” — <em>Numbers 23:19 (CEV)</em></blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Roleplay: Lesson 16 script: Jacob and Esau's title fight</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-amber-soft\">Characters: Commentator, Team Esau, Team Jacob</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>(stage direction)</em> <strong>Commentator:</strong> Welcome, everyone, to the biggest title fight this family has ever seen — two brothers who've been battling each other since before they were even born!</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Commentator</th><td class=\"lt-td\">In the red corner, weighing in as the hairy outdoorsman and champion hunter — it's Esau!</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Team Esau</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Esau! Esau! Esau!</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Commentator</th><td class=\"lt-td\">And in the blue corner, the smooth-skinned home-body — it's Jacob!</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Team Jacob</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Jacob! Jacob! Jacob!</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Commentator</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Isaac is old now, and he's lost his sight. Not sure how much time he has left, he calls in his favourite son, Esau, wanting to give him the big blessing before he dies.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Team Esau</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Yes! That's our man!</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Commentator</th><td class=\"lt-td\">But here's the catch — Isaac wants to do this quietly, without telling Rebekah or Jacob a thing.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Team Jacob</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Boo! That's not fair play!</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Commentator</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Esau heads out to hunt down some wild game for his father's favourite meal, just like Isaac asked.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Team Esau</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Esau! Esau!</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Commentator</th><td class=\"lt-td\">But Rebekah has been listening at the tent the whole time!</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>(stage direction)</em> <strong>Team Jacob:</strong> Boo!</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Commentator</th><td class=\"lt-td\">She calls in her own favourite — Jacob — and comes up with a sneaky plan of her own.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Team Jacob</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Jacob! Jacob!</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>(stage direction)</em> <strong>Team Esau:</strong> Boo! Boo!</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Commentator</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Jacob does exactly what his mother tells him. He fetches two young goats from the flock...</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Team Jacob</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Jacob! Jacob!</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Commentator</th><td class=\"lt-td\">...Rebekah cooks up the meal, dresses Jacob in his brother's clothes, and straps goat skin to his hands and neck so he'll feel just like hairy Esau.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Commentator</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Jacob carries the food in to his father, pretending the whole time to be his brother!</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Team Jacob</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Jacob! Jacob! Jacob!</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Commentator</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Isaac isn't totally sure at first — something about that voice — but he's fooled! He gives Jacob the blessing meant for Esau!</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Team Jacob</th><td class=\"lt-td\">JACOB! JACOB! JACOB!</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Commentator</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Esau comes back from the hunt with a feast fit for a king, ready to finally get his blessing...</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Team Esau</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Esau! Esau!</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Commentator</th><td class=\"lt-td\">...only to find out there's nothing left. Jacob has already taken it!</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Team Jacob</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Jacob wins!</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Commentator</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Ding, ding, ding! Esau is furious, and he's already plotting revenge on his brother.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Team Esau</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Boo! Get him back, Esau!</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>(stage direction)</em> <strong>Commentator:</strong> So — Jacob has snuck away with the win. But let's be honest, everyone in this fight has played dirty, right from Isaac's secret plan to Rebekah and Jacob's disguise.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>(stage direction)</em> <strong>Team Esau:</strong> Boo! Boo! Boo!</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>(stage direction)</em> <strong>Team Jacob:</strong> Boo! Boo! Boo!</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Closing Prayer</div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Dear God, thank you that you are powerful in keeping your promises. Thank you that nothing can stand in the way of you keeping your promises — not even people's sinfulness. Thank you that you kept your promises to Abraham, Isaac, and Jacob, even though they didn't deserve it. Amen.</blockquote></div>"}}},"Week 7: Growing into greatness":{"label":"Day","children":{"Week Overview":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Genesis 29:31–30:24; 37</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">God had promised Abraham a family as countless as the stars, and that promise kept growing — through Isaac, through Jacob, and now through Jacob's twelve sons, the beginning of the twelve tribes of Israel. But this family was far from perfect. Jacob loved Rachel's son Joseph best of all, and made that favouritism obvious with a special coat, which only deepened the hatred his brothers already felt. When Joseph made things worse by sharing dreams of his whole family bowing down to him, his brothers' hatred turned into a plan to get rid of him. They didn't kill him, but they did sell him into slavery in Egypt. It looked like the worst thing that could happen to this growing family — but Christians believe God was already at work, even through this terrible act, planning to use Joseph to rescue and preserve his people.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Source</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Connect B1 Upper Primary — Lesson 17</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Monday: Jacob's family keeps growing":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">10–15 minutes</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Genesis 29:31–30:24</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Jacob's family keeps growing</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Jacob, who once tricked his own father and brother, was tricked himself by his uncle Laban into marrying Leah before Rachel. Jacob ended up married to both sisters, and amid their rivalry, Jacob's family grew to twelve sons — God's promise to Abraham of a great family continuing to unfold.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning how Jacob was tricked by Laban into marrying Leah before Rachel.</li><li>We are learning that Jacob's family grew to twelve sons through Leah, Rachel, and their servants.</li><li>I can explain that this week's new memory verse teaches that God is always at work for good, even in a rivalry-filled family.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Listen for whether students can explain how Laban tricked Jacob, and whether they can recite or paraphrase this week's new memory verse.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">1</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Way in: does trickery catch up with you?</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Ask the warm-up question about whether tricking people usually catches up with someone eventually.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Last week, Jacob tricked his own brother to get what he wanted. Do you think someone who tricks people usually gets away with it forever, or does it usually catch up with them somehow?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Accept any answer. Today's story shows the trickster get tricked himself — Jacob is about to find out what it feels like to be on the receiving end.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">2</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Jacob is tricked by Laban</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">5 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Read through Jacob falling in love with Rachel, working seven years for her, and being tricked into marrying Leah first.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">How do you think it felt for Jacob to be deceived like this, after everything he'd done to deceive his own father and brother?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Accept honest responses. It's a fitting turn — the deceiver gets deceived. This doesn't erase what Jacob did to Esau, but it shows Jacob is now living with the same kind of hurt he caused.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"5\">3</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>New memory verse</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3–4 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Introduce this week's new memory verse and discuss what it might mean given Jacob's messy, rivalry-filled family.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Display the verse block.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">This week we've got a brand new memory verse. Even with all this family rivalry and trickery going on, what do you think this verse means for how God was working in Jacob's messy family?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Guide toward: God was still at work for good, even through a complicated, rivalry-filled family. This verse will keep coming back all week, including tomorrow when God speaks to Jacob directly, and later when we see it stretch all the way to Joseph.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"3\">4</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Closing prayer</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">1–2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Close together with the built-in prayer.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Use the built-in prayer block to close.</td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Tomorrow: God speaks to Jacob again and gives him a new name.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Memory Verse</div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">“We know that God is always at work for the good of everyone who loves him.” — <em>Romans 8:28a (CEV)</em></blockquote></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Closing Prayer</div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Dear God, thank you that you are always at work for good, even in families that don't always get along. Help us to trust that you're at work in our own families too. Amen.</blockquote></div>"},"Tuesday: A new name, an old promise":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">10–15 minutes</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Genesis 35:9–12; 49:28 (context)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">A new name, an old promise</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">God appeared to Jacob again, renamed him Israel, and renewed his promise of land and a great family. Jacob's twelve sons became the beginning of the twelve tribes of Israel, showing God's promise to Abraham continuing to unfold exactly as he'd said.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning that God renamed Jacob 'Israel' and renewed his promise to him.</li><li>We are learning the names of Jacob's twelve sons and that they became the twelve tribes of Israel.</li><li>I can explain that Jacob's growing family shows God keeping his promise to Abraham.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Listen for whether students can explain why God changed Jacob's name, and whether they can name several of Jacob's twelve sons.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>None — this is a text/discussion-based day (see the file's header notes for why this lesson ships without images)</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">1</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Way in: the meaning of a new name</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Ask the warm-up question about what a new name might be chosen to remind someone of.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">If someone gave you a brand new name to remind you of something important, what would you want that name to remind you of?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Accept any answer. Today we see God give Jacob a whole new name — one meant to remind him of God's promises for the rest of his life.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"5\">2</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>God renames Jacob 'Israel'</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">4–5 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Read God's words to Jacob from Genesis 35:9–12, renaming him Israel and renewing the promise of land and family.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Display the verse block with Genesis 35:9–12.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">God changed Jacob's name to Israel. Why do you think God wanted Jacob to have a new name, rather than just keep making the same promise using his old one?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Guide toward: the name itself became a permanent, everyday reminder of God's promise — every time someone said Jacob's new name, it pointed back to what God had promised him. It wasn't just words spoken once; it became part of who he was.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"5\">3</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Twelve sons, twelve tribes</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">4–5 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Introduce Jacob's twelve sons by name and try the ordering activity as a group.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Use the built-in activity block to try naming all twelve sons in birth order.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Near the end of his life, Jacob gathered all twelve of his sons to bless them. What do you think it shows us that the writer of Genesis records the names of every single one of Jacob's sons?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Guide toward: it shows that Jacob's family truly was becoming the great nation God had promised Abraham — each son mattered enough to be named, because each one would become the head of a whole tribe of Israel.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"3\">4</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Memory verse and closing prayer</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Read the memory verse together and close with the built-in prayer.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Use the verse block, then the built-in prayer block to close.</td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Tomorrow: we meet Jacob's favourite son, Joseph, and find out why his brothers grew to hate him.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Memory Verse</div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">God told Jacob: “I am God All-Powerful, and from now on your name will be Israel instead of Jacob. You will have many children, and your family will grow into whole nations. I will give you the same land I promised Abraham and Isaac, and it will belong to your family forever.” — <em>Genesis 35:9–12 (CEV, paraphrased)</em><br>“We know that God is always at work for the good of everyone who loves him.” — <em>Romans 8:28a (CEV)</em></blockquote></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Closing Prayer</div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Dear God, thank you that you kept your promise to Abraham by growing his family into the twelve tribes of Israel. Thank you that you're always at work for good, even when we can't see the whole plan yet. Amen.</blockquote></div>"},"Wednesday: Jacob's favourite son":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">10–15 minutes</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Genesis 37:2–11</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Jacob's favourite son</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Jacob's obvious favouritism toward Joseph — shown through the special coat — combined with Joseph's own bad reports and boastful dreams of ruling over his family, caused his ten older brothers' hatred to grow until they began plotting to get rid of him.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning why Joseph's brothers grew to hate him: favouritism, bad reports, and his dreams.</li><li>We are learning to identify why sharing the dreams the way Joseph did wasn't wise.</li><li>I can explain how favouritism and jealousy built up inside Jacob's family.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Listen for whether students can explain the three reasons Joseph's brothers hated him, and whether they can evaluate why sharing the dreams wasn't wise.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">1</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Way in: obvious favourites</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Ask the warm-up question about how it feels when someone has an obvious favourite.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Have you ever felt like someone had an obvious favourite — maybe a favourite student, a favourite sibling, a favourite friend? How did that make everyone else feel?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Accept honest responses — left out, jealous, unfair. This sets up how Joseph's brothers are about to feel toward him.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">2</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>The coat and the dreams</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">5–6 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Read through Jacob's favouritism toward Joseph, the special coat, Joseph's bad reports, and his two dreams.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Do you think it was wise for Joseph to share these dreams with his brothers, who already hated him? Why or why not?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Guide toward: no — even if the dreams were true and came from God, sharing them this way, to brothers who already resented him, only poured fuel on the fire. Joseph wasn't necessarily wrong to have the dreams, but boasting about them wasn't wise.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">3</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Building anticipation</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Use the memory verse to build anticipation for tomorrow's story without giving away the ending.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Even though Joseph's brothers were about to do something terrible, this week's memory verse says God is always at work for the good of everyone who loves him. What do you think that might mean for what happens next?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Accept guesses — this is meant to build anticipation, not be fully answered yet. Guide toward: it hints that even the very worst things Joseph's brothers plan to do won't be outside of God's control — but don't give away tomorrow's ending yet.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"3\">4</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Closing prayer</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">1–2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Close together with the built-in prayer.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Use the built-in prayer block to close.</td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Tomorrow: Joseph's brothers finally act on their hatred, and we find out what God was really doing all along.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Memory Verse</div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">“We know that God is always at work for the good of everyone who loves him.” — <em>Romans 8:28a (CEV)</em></blockquote></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Closing Prayer</div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Dear God, thank you that even when families struggle with jealousy and favouritism, you are still at work. Help us to treat the people in our own families fairly, and to trust you when things feel unfair. Amen.</blockquote></div>"},"Thursday: Sold, but not forgotten":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">10–15 minutes</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Genesis 37:12–36</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Sold, but not forgotten</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Joseph's brothers, driven by hatred, sold him into slavery in Egypt and deceived their father into believing he was dead. It looked like the end for Joseph and for God's growing promise — but this sets up God working through Joseph's suffering for the good of his whole family, exactly as this week's memory verse describes.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning what happened when Joseph's brothers sold him into slavery.</li><li>We are learning how the brothers deceived Jacob into believing Joseph was dead.</li><li>I can explain that God was at work for good even through this terrible act, in line with this week's memory verse.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Listen for whether students can retell how Joseph was sold, and whether they can explain what this week's memory verse means in light of it.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">1</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Way in: imagining the plan</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Ask students to imagine what plan the brothers might have come up with, before revealing the real story.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Yesterday we heard Joseph's brothers were plotting to get rid of him. If you were one of the brothers, what's the worst plan you can imagine them coming up with?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Accept any guesses. The real story is bad enough without needing to imagine worse — use this to set up today's account.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">2</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Joseph is sold into slavery</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">5–6 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Read through Joseph being thrown into the well, sold to the Midianite traders, and taken to Egypt, and the brothers' lie to Jacob.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">From where we're sitting, this looks like one of the worst things that could happen to this family God had been growing all week. So why do you think this story is still part of God keeping his promise to Abraham?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Guide toward: even though Joseph's brothers meant this for evil, God was already at work — planning to use Joseph's time in Egypt to eventually save his whole family from a coming famine. This is exactly what this week's memory verse has been pointing toward.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"3\">3</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Memory verse from memory, and closing prayer</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Finish the week's memory verse fully from memory, then close together with the prayer in the app.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Use the say-it-from-memory activity block, then the built-in prayer block to close.</td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 18 continues Joseph's story in Egypt.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Memory Verse</div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">“We know that God is always at work for the good of everyone who loves him.” — <em>Romans 8:28a (CEV)</em></blockquote></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Closing Prayer</div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Dear God, thank you that you are always at work for good, even when things look their very worst. Thank you that nothing Joseph's brothers did could stop your plan for Joseph, or your promises to Abraham's family. Help us trust you the same way when hard things happen to us. Amen.</blockquote></div>"}}},"Week 8: Working for good":{"label":"Day","children":{"Week Overview":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Genesis 39–41</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Joseph was sold into slavery in Egypt, but Christians believe God had not abandoned him. Whether Joseph was a slave in Potiphar's house, forgotten in prison, or finally standing in front of Pharaoh himself, the Bible shows God being with him and blessing everything he did. God even gave Joseph the ability to interpret dreams, which eventually put him in charge of all Egypt during a terrible famine — saving not just Egypt, but the whole world, and keeping God's promise that Abraham's family would be a blessing to everyone.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Source</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Connect B1 Upper Primary — Lesson 18</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Monday: God was with Joseph, even as a slave":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">10–15 minutes</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Genesis 39:1–20</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">God was with Joseph, even as a slave</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Even after being sold as a slave in Egypt, Joseph found that God was with him and blessed everything he did. When Joseph refused to sin against God and was punished unfairly for it, he still trusted that God was at work for good.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning that God was with Joseph even though he was a slave in Potiphar's house.</li><li>We are learning that Joseph chose to obey God even when it cost him something.</li><li>I can explain that trusting God doesn't always mean things turn out fair straight away.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Listen for whether students can explain how Joseph was blessed in Potiphar's house, and why he ended up in prison despite doing the right thing.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Craft-stick puppets for Potiphar, Joseph, and Potiphar's wife (optional, for acting out the script)</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">1</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Way in: would God still be with you?</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Ask the warm-up question about whether students would expect God to be with them if they were sold as a slave far from home.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Last week we heard Joseph's brothers sold him as a slave into Egypt. If you were sold as a slave in a country far from home, would you expect God to still be with you there?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Accept any answer. This week's story keeps coming back to that exact question — and the Bible's answer is a clear yes.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">2</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Joseph in Potiphar's house</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Read through Joseph being sold to Potiphar and put in charge of his household.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Joseph had every reason to feel like God had forgotten him. What do you think the Bible says happened to Joseph in Potiphar's house?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Guide toward: the Bible says the Lord was with Joseph, and he became successful. Potiphar noticed and put Joseph in charge of his entire household.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"5\">3</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Act out Scene 1</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">6–7 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Act out or read through the Scene 1 script together as a class, covering Potiphar's wife's false accusation and Joseph being thrown in prison.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Use the roleplay block to act out the script on screen. Assign readers for Narrator, Potiphar, Joseph, and Potiphar's wife if acting it out live.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Joseph did nothing wrong, and he still ended up in prison. Why do you think Joseph still trusted God even when doing the right thing led to something so unfair?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Guide toward: Joseph knew that obeying God mattered more than the outcome. He couldn't control what Potiphar's wife did or how Potiphar reacted, but he could control his own faithfulness — and he trusted God with everything else.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"3\">4</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Closing prayer</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">1–2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Close together with the built-in prayer.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Use the built-in prayer block to close.</td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Tomorrow: Joseph is faithful to God even in prison, and interprets two dreams.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Memory Verse</div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">“We know that God is always at work for the good of everyone who loves him.” — <em>Romans 8:28a (CEV)</em></blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Roleplay: Lesson 18 script: Joseph in Egypt — Scene 1</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-amber-soft\">Characters: Narrator, Potiphar, Joseph, Potiphar's wife</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Narrator</th><td class=\"lt-td\">(Enter Potiphar and Joseph.) Joseph worked hard in Potiphar's house and God made him successful. Potiphar was so pleased with Joseph that he put him in charge of his whole household.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Potiphar</th><td class=\"lt-td\">With Joseph in charge, I don't need to worry about anything except for the food I eat! Things could not be better. Joseph really is the best!</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>(stage direction)</em> <strong>Narrator:</strong> (Potiphar leaves.) Joseph was great. God was with him in everything he did. But Joseph was also attractive.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Narrator</th><td class=\"lt-td\">(Enter Potiphar's wife.) Potiphar's wife liked Joseph too and wanted him to be like a husband to her whenever Potiphar was away. But Joseph kept saying …</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Joseph</th><td class=\"lt-td\">No, I will not sin against my master and my God!</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>(stage direction)</em> <strong>Narrator:</strong> (Turn Joseph side to side as though he is shaking his head to say 'no'.) Potiphar's wife tried one last time, and when Joseph said 'no' she grabbed him by the coat. Joseph wriggled out of his coat and ran out of the house, leaving his coat behind.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Narrator</th><td class=\"lt-td\">(Enter Potiphar.) When Potiphar got home from work that day, his wife told him this story …</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Potiphar's wife</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Joseph tried to hurt me when no-one else was around, but I screamed so he ran out of the house, leaving his cloak behind.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Narrator</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Potiphar was so mad he threw Joseph into the prison where the king's prisoners were kept.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Closing Prayer</div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Dear God, thank you that you were with Joseph even when he was a slave and even when he was treated unfairly. Help us to trust that you are with us too, even in hard or unfair situations. Amen.</blockquote></div>"},"Tuesday: Faithful in prison":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">10–15 minutes</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Genesis 39:21–40:23</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Faithful in prison</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">God was with Joseph even in prison, giving him favour with the jailer and the ability to correctly interpret the dreams of the king's cupbearer and baker. Even though the cupbearer forgot about Joseph for two years, God had not forgotten him.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning that God was with Joseph even in prison.</li><li>We are learning that Joseph correctly interpreted the dreams of the cupbearer and the baker, and gave God the credit for it.</li><li>I can explain what it might mean to trust God while waiting for a hard situation to change.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Listen for whether students can retell what happened with the cupbearer and the baker, and whether they can explain why Joseph gave God the credit for interpreting the dreams.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Craft-stick puppets for Jailer, Joseph, Cupbearer, and Baker (optional, for acting out the script)</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">1</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Way in: forgotten but not forgotten</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Ask students how they might feel about God if they were thrown in prison unfairly, to set up today's story.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Yesterday Joseph was thrown in prison for something he didn't even do. If that happened to you, how do you think you'd feel about God at that point?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Accept honest responses. It would be completely understandable to feel forgotten or angry. Today's story shows that even in prison, Joseph wasn't actually forgotten by God.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">2</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Joseph faithful in prison</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Read through Joseph being put in charge of the prisoners, and the cupbearer and baker's troubling dreams.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Joseph told the cupbearer and the baker he could help them with their dreams. Where do you think Joseph believed the power to understand dreams actually came from?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Guide toward: Joseph made it clear that dream interpretation belongs to God, not to Joseph himself. Even using this special gift, Joseph gave credit to God rather than taking it for himself.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"5\">3</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Act out Scene 2</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">6–7 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Act out or read through the Scene 2 script together as a class, covering the cupbearer and baker's dreams and their fulfilment.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Use the roleplay block to act out the script on screen. Assign readers for Narrator, Jailer, Joseph, Cupbearer, and Baker if acting it out live.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Joseph asked the cupbearer to remember him and tell the king about him. But the manual tells us the cupbearer forgot Joseph — for two whole years! What do you think it might feel like to keep trusting God while you're still waiting for things to change?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Accept honest responses. Guide toward: waiting on God, even when it feels like he's forgotten you or when other people forget their promises, is genuinely hard — but Joseph's story isn't over yet.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"3\">4</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Closing prayer</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">1–2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Close together with the built-in prayer.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Use the built-in prayer block to close.</td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Tomorrow: two years later, the King of Egypt has a troubling dream of his own.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Memory Verse</div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">“We know that God is always at work for the good of everyone who loves him.” — <em>Romans 8:28a (CEV)</em></blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Roleplay: Lesson 18 script: Joseph in Egypt — Scene 2</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-amber-soft\">Characters: Narrator, Jailer, Joseph, Cupbearer, Baker</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Narrator</th><td class=\"lt-td\">(Enter Jailer and Joseph.) Joseph was faithful to God even in prison. The jailer was so pleased with Joseph that he put him in charge of all the prisoners.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Jailer</th><td class=\"lt-td\">With Joseph in charge I don't need to worry at all!</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Narrator</th><td class=\"lt-td\">(Jailer leaves.) One day, two of the king's servants were thrown into prison by the king. These were his baker and his cupbearer — the guy who tested the king's drinks for poison before the king himself drank from his cup.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>(stage direction)</em> <strong>Narrator:</strong> (Enter Cupbearer and Baker.) On the same night both these men had a dream.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>(stage direction)</em> <strong>Narrator:</strong> (Lay both the Cupbearer and Baker down to 'sleep'.) The next morning they were troubled because of their dreams. No-one could tell them what they meant. Joseph knew that God is the one who gives dreams and their meanings, so he asked the men to tell him their dreams.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Cupbearer</th><td class=\"lt-td\">In my dream there was a vine. And on the vine there were three branches. As soon as the grapes had grown on the branches, I squeezed them into Pharaoh's cup and gave it to him.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Joseph</th><td class=\"lt-td\">The three branches are three days. In three days the king of Egypt will give you your job back. But when he does, please don't forget me. Please ask the king to let me out of prison.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Baker</th><td class=\"lt-td\">In my dream I was carrying three baskets of bread on my head. In the baskets were all different kinds of bread and baked goods for the King of Egypt. But as I carried them, the birds swooped down and pecked at the bread until there was none left!</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Joseph</th><td class=\"lt-td\">The three baskets are three days. In three days you will be gone.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Narrator</th><td class=\"lt-td\">(Joseph leaves.) Well, three days later the King of Egypt threw himself a huge birthday party! And in front of all his guests, he gave the Cupbearer his job back, and the Baker was no more.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Closing Prayer</div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Dear God, thank you that you were with Joseph even in prison, and that you gave him the ability to help others. Help us to trust you while we wait for things to change in our own lives too. Amen.</blockquote></div>"},"Wednesday: God gives Pharaoh's dream its meaning":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">10–15 minutes</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Genesis 41:1–41</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">God gives Pharaoh's dream its meaning</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Two years after being forgotten in prison, Joseph was brought before Pharaoh to interpret his troubling dreams. Joseph gave God the credit for the interpretation, correctly predicting seven years of plenty followed by seven years of famine — and Pharaoh put Joseph in charge of all Egypt as a result.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning how Joseph interpreted Pharaoh's dreams of seven years of plenty and seven years of famine.</li><li>We are learning that Joseph gave God the credit for understanding the dreams, not himself.</li><li>I can explain how God was at work through even the difficult parts of Joseph's story.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Listen for whether students can explain what Pharaoh's dreams meant, and whether they can explain how God had been at work through Joseph's whole story so far.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Craft-stick puppets for the King of Egypt, Joseph, and Cupbearer (optional, for acting out the script)</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">1</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Way in: does Joseph stay stuck?</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Ask students to guess whether Joseph stays stuck in prison forever, to build anticipation for today's story.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Yesterday we heard the cupbearer forgot all about Joseph. Do you think Joseph is going to stay stuck in prison forever, or does something change?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Accept guesses. Today we find out — two years later, something happens that finally gets Joseph out of prison.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">2</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Pharaoh's troubling dreams</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Read through Pharaoh's two dreams and how none of his wise men could interpret them, until the cupbearer remembered Joseph.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Once again, Joseph is about to interpret someone's dream. Do you think he's changed his answer about where his ability to do this comes from?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Guide toward: no — Joseph tells the king exactly the same thing he told the cupbearer and the baker: he can't tell the king what his dreams mean, but God can. Joseph never made this about his own cleverness.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"5\">3</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Act out Scene 3</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">6–7 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Act out or read through the Scene 3 script together as a class, covering Pharaoh's dreams, Joseph's interpretation, and his promotion to be in charge of Egypt.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Use the roleplay block to act out the script on screen. Assign readers for Narrator, King, Joseph, and Cupbearer if acting it out live.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">After everything Joseph had been through — sold as a slave, falsely accused, forgotten in prison — he ended up in charge of the whole of Egypt. What does this tell you about how God had been at work in Joseph's story the whole time, even in the bad parts?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Guide toward: nothing that happened to Joseph — not the slavery, not the false accusation, not the two years in prison — was wasted. God was working through every part of it to eventually put Joseph exactly where he needed to be.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"3\">4</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Closing prayer</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">1–2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Close together with the built-in prayer.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Use the built-in prayer block to close.</td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Tomorrow: the famine hits, and Joseph's story becomes a blessing for the whole world.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Memory Verse</div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">“We know that God is always at work for the good of everyone who loves him.” — <em>Romans 8:28a (CEV)</em></blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Roleplay: Lesson 18 script: Joseph in Egypt — Scene 3</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-amber-soft\">Characters: Narrator, King, Joseph, Cupbearer</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>(stage direction)</em> <strong>Narrator:</strong> (Lie the King of Egypt down as though he's asleep.) In his dream, the King of Egypt was standing by the Nile River when seven fat, healthy cows came out and ate at the edge of the river. After them, seven skinny, ugly cows came out and ate up the healthy ones. The king woke up because he was worried about this dream!</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>(stage direction)</em> <strong>Narrator:</strong> (Sit the king up. Finally, the king fell asleep again. But he had another dream! Lie the king down again.) This time, the king saw seven good, healthy heads of corn growing on a single stalk. After them seven other ears of corn sprouted — thin and burnt by the wind. The thin, burnt corn swallowed up the good, healthy corn. The king woke up again!</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">King</th><td class=\"lt-td\">These dreams worry me. I wonder if there is anyone who can tell me what they mean.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Narrator</th><td class=\"lt-td\">None of the wise men and magicians in the land could tell the King of Egypt what his dreams meant. (Enter Cupbearer.) Finally, the cupbearer remembered Joseph.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Cupbearer</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Your highness. There is one man who can tell you what your dreams mean …</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">King</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Get me this man!</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Narrator</th><td class=\"lt-td\">(The Cupbearer leaves and comes back with Joseph.) So the king told Joseph his dreams. And Joseph said …</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Joseph</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I cannot tell you what your dreams mean but God can. Both of the dreams mean the same thing. The seven cows and the seven ears of corn are seven years. God has shown you that for seven years there will be lots and lots of food in Egypt — more food than we can eat. But after that, there will be seven years of famine — there will be no food anywhere in Egypt or in the countries around it. And when the famine comes, the good seven years will be forgotten. This will happen soon!</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Narrator</th><td class=\"lt-td\">So the King of Egypt put Joseph in charge of all Egypt to collect the food and store it up for the famine.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Closing Prayer</div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Dear God, thank you that you gave Joseph the ability to understand Pharaoh's dream, and that you put him in charge of Egypt at exactly the right time. Help us trust that you're working through every part of our lives too, even the hard parts. Amen.</blockquote></div>"},"Thursday: Blessed to be a blessing":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">10–15 minutes</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Genesis 41:46–57</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Blessed to be a blessing</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Joseph's careful storing of grain during the seven years of plenty meant that when famine struck the whole world, people from every nation came to Egypt to buy food from him — fulfilling God's promise to Abraham that his family would be a blessing to the whole world.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning how Joseph's storing of grain saved Egypt and the surrounding world from famine.</li><li>We are learning how this connects to God's promise that Abraham's family would bless the whole world.</li><li>I can explain what it might look like to trust God in my own difficult situations, the way Joseph did.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Listen for whether students can retell how Joseph saved the world from famine, and whether they can connect this to God's promise to Abraham.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">1</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Way in: recap of the week</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Ask students to recap what's stood out to them from the week's story so far.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">This week we've followed Joseph from slavery, to prison, to being second-in-command of all Egypt. Out of everything we've heard so far, what part of Joseph's story has stood out to you the most?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Accept any answer — this is a recap question to get students thinking back over the whole week's story before we finish it.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">2</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>The famine and God's promise to Abraham</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">4–5 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Read through the seven years of plenty and famine, and connect Joseph saving the world to God's promise to Abraham in Genesis 12.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Right back in Lesson 12, God promised Abraham that his family would be blessed, and that the whole world would be blessed through his family. How do you think Joseph selling grain to the whole world connects to that promise God made to Abraham, generations earlier?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Guide toward: this is exactly God keeping his promise. Through Joseph, one member of Abraham's family, people from every nation were saved from starving during the famine. God's promise to bless the whole world through Abraham's family was coming true in a way nobody could have predicted.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"5\">3</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Memory verse from memory and personal application</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3–4 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Say this week's memory verse fully from memory, then ask students to think personally about trusting God in their own hard situations.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Use the say-it-from-memory activity block.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Sometimes we can find ourselves in difficult situations and it can feel like God doesn't care. While you're sitting there, have a think about a difficult situation you've found yourself in — you may be in one right now. What might it look like to trust God the way Joseph did, even before you can see how things will turn out?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Accept responses. This is a personal application question — there's no need for students to share out loud if they don't want to. The point is to connect Joseph's trust in God to something happening in their own lives.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"3\">4</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Closing prayer</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">1–2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Close together with the built-in prayer.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Use the built-in prayer block to close.</td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 19 continues the story as Joseph's brothers come to Egypt during the famine, not knowing who he is.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Memory Verse</div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">“We know that God is always at work for the good of everyone who loves him.” — <em>Romans 8:28a (CEV)</em></blockquote></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Closing Prayer</div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Dear God, thank you that you were with Joseph. Thank you that you were working everything out for his good, and that you had a plan for his life. Thank you that you promise to work all things for the good of those who love you. Amen.</blockquote></div>"}}},"Week 9: Mighty to save":{"label":"Day","children":{"Week Overview":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Genesis 41–50</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">God revealed to Pharaoh that seven years of plenty would be followed by seven years of famine, and gave Joseph the wisdom to store up grain so that Egypt — and the whole world — could be saved. When Joseph's own brothers came to Egypt for food, not recognising the brother they had sold into slavery, God used the famine to bring Jacob's whole family back together in Egypt, keeping his promise that Abraham's family would become a great nation and would bless the whole world.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Source</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Connect B1 Upper Primary — Lesson 19</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Monday: Joseph saves the world from famine":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">10–15 minutes</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Genesis 41:46–42:28</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Joseph saves the world from famine</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">God gave Joseph wisdom to prepare Egypt for a terrible famine, saving people from every nation — including Joseph's own family, who came to Egypt for food without recognising him.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning how Joseph's storing of grain saved Egypt and the surrounding world from famine.</li><li>We are learning how this connects to God's promise that Abraham's family would bless the whole world.</li><li>We are learning that Joseph's brothers came to Egypt for food, not recognising the brother they had sold into slavery.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Listen for whether students can retell how Joseph saved the world from famine, and can explain why his brothers didn't recognise him.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Optional: a map showing Egypt and Canaan, to point out how far the brothers travelled</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">1</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Way in: recapping Joseph's story</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Ask students to recap what they remember about how God was with Joseph through slavery, prison, and being put in charge of Egypt.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Last week we followed Joseph from slavery, to prison, to being second-in-command of all Egypt. What do you remember about how God was with Joseph through all of that?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Accept responses. God always works for the good of those who love him — even when Joseph's circumstances looked terrible, God was preparing him for something bigger.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">2</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>The famine and God's promise to Abraham</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">4–5 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Read through the seven years of plenty and famine, and connect Joseph saving the world to God's promise to Abraham in Genesis 12.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Right back in Lesson 12, God promised Abraham that his family would be a blessing, and that the whole world would be blessed through his family. How do you think Joseph selling grain to the whole world connects to that promise God made to Abraham, generations earlier?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Guide toward: this is God keeping his promise. Through Joseph, one member of Abraham's family, people from every nation were saved from starving — exactly what God had promised long ago.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">3</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>The brothers come to Egypt</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">4–5 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Read through the brothers' first trip to Egypt, being accused of spying, and Simeon being kept behind.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Joseph recognised his brothers straight away, but they had no idea who he was. Why do you think Joseph's brothers might not have recognised him?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Accept responses. Joseph had grown up, he was dressed like an Egyptian official, and he spoke to them through an interpreter — Joseph was the last person the brothers expected to see sitting on Egypt's throne.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"3\">4</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Closing prayer</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">1–2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Close together with the built-in prayer.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Use the built-in prayer block to close.</td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Tomorrow: the brothers return to Egypt with Benjamin, and Joseph tests them one final time.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Memory Verse</div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">“We know that God is always at work for the good of everyone who loves him.” — <em>Romans 8:28a (CEV)</em></blockquote></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Closing Prayer</div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Dear God, thank you that you had a plan for Joseph all along. Thank you that you used him to save so many people from starving, and that you always keep your promises. Amen.</blockquote></div>"},"Tuesday: One final test":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">10–15 minutes</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Genesis 43:1–45:15</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">One final test</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Joseph tested his brothers one last time with his silver cup, and when Judah offered to take Benjamin's punishment himself, Joseph saw that his brothers had truly changed and revealed who he really was.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning how Joseph tested his brothers with his silver cup.</li><li>We are learning that Judah's offer to protect Benjamin showed the brothers had changed.</li><li>We are learning how Joseph revealed himself to his brothers and forgave them.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Listen for whether students can explain what changed Joseph's mind about revealing himself, and how he responded to his brothers.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">1</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Way in: sending Benjamin to Egypt</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Ask students to imagine how Jacob felt sending Benjamin to Egypt after already losing Joseph.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Yesterday Jacob didn't want to let Benjamin go to Egypt. Eventually the food ran out again and he had no choice. If you were Jacob, how do you think you'd feel sending your last remaining son from Rachel into a country ruled by a stranger?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Accept responses. Jacob had already lost Joseph and was terrified of losing Benjamin too — this shows how much courage it took for him to let Benjamin go.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">2</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>The silver cup test</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">4–5 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Read through the feast, the silver cup being planted in Benjamin's sack, and Judah's offer to take his place.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Judah begged Joseph to take him as a slave instead of Benjamin, saying his father would die of grief if Benjamin didn't come home. Why do you think this moment finally changed everything for Joseph?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Guide toward: Joseph could see that his brothers had truly changed. Years earlier they had shown no mercy selling their own brother into slavery — now Judah was offering his own freedom to protect the youngest brother.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">3</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Joseph reveals himself</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">4–5 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Read through Joseph revealing his identity and forgiving his brothers, sending them home to bring Jacob and the whole family to Egypt.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">How do you think Joseph's brothers might have felt in that moment, discovering that the powerful Governor of Egypt was actually the brother they had wronged?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Accept responses. They may have felt relieved and amazed, but also frightened and deeply guilty for what they had done to him all those years before.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"3\">4</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Closing prayer</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">1–2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Close together with the built-in prayer.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Use the built-in prayer block to close.</td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Tomorrow: Jacob's whole family moves to Egypt, and God repeats his promise to make them into a great nation.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Memory Verse</div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">“We know that God is always at work for the good of everyone who loves him.” — <em>Romans 8:28a (CEV)</em></blockquote></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Closing Prayer</div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Dear God, thank you that Joseph forgave his brothers instead of taking revenge. Help us to forgive people who have hurt us, the way Joseph did. Amen.</blockquote></div>"},"Wednesday: God's promise travels to Egypt":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">10–15 minutes</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Genesis 46:1–7; 46:28–47:12; 49:33</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">God's promise travels to Egypt</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">As Jacob's family moved to Egypt, God repeated his promise that they would become a great nation, showing that God had never broken any of his promises to Abraham, Isaac, and Jacob.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning that God repeated his nation promise to Jacob on the way to Egypt.</li><li>We are learning that God did not break any of his promises to Abraham, Isaac, or Jacob.</li><li>We are learning that Jacob's family settled in Egypt and grew, and that Jacob eventually died there.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Listen for whether students can state what God promised Jacob in Egypt, and whether they can say that God kept every one of his promises.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">1</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Way in: recapping the three promises</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Ask students to recall the three promises God made to Abraham (land, nation, blessing), and which one connects to today's story.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Can anyone remember the three promises God made to Abraham, right back near the start of this term? (Land, nation, and blessing.) Which of those promises do you think is about to come true in today's story?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Guide toward the NATION promise — God promised Abraham's family would become a great nation, and today we see God confirm that this will happen in Egypt.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">2</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>God speaks to Jacob on the way to Egypt</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">4–5 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Read through God's promise to Jacob in Genesis 46:3–4, and discuss whether God has broken any promises so far this term.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Did God break any of his promises to Abraham, Isaac, and Jacob along the way?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Guide toward: No he didn't! God is faithful and gracious — when he makes a promise, he keeps it, even when it takes a very long time or looks impossible.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"2\">3</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Settling in Egypt, and Jacob's death</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3–4 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Read through the family settling in Goshen and Jacob's eventual death, and the brothers' renewed fear.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"3\">4</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Closing prayer</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">1–2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Close together with the built-in prayer.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Use the built-in prayer block to close.</td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Tomorrow: Joseph reassures his frightened brothers one final time, and we look back at everything God has done this term.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Memory Verse</div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">God told Jacob not to be afraid to go to Egypt, because he would make Jacob's family into a great nation there. God promised to go with Jacob to Egypt, and one day to bring his family back out again. — <em>Genesis 46:3–4 (CEV, paraphrased)</em><br>“We know that God is always at work for the good of everyone who loves him.” — <em>Romans 8:28a (CEV)</em></blockquote></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Closing Prayer</div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Dear God, thank you that you kept your promise to make Abraham's family into a great nation. Thank you that you are always faithful, even when we have to wait a long time to see your promises come true. Amen.</blockquote></div>"},"Thursday: God had a plan all along":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">10–15 minutes</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Genesis 50:15–21</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">God had a plan all along</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">When Joseph's brothers feared revenge after Jacob's death, Joseph reassured them that God had planned everything that happened to him for good — to save many people and to keep God's promises to Abraham's family.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning that Joseph forgave his brothers completely, even after their father died.</li><li>We are learning that God had a plan for Joseph's whole life, even through the hardest parts.</li><li>I can explain how this term's story shows that God is a promise keeper.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Listen for whether students can explain why Joseph forgave his brothers, and whether they can describe God's plan for Joseph's life in their own words.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">1</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Way in: why were the brothers afraid?</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Ask students why the brothers might have been afraid of Joseph once their father had died.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Why might Joseph's brothers have been afraid of Joseph after their father Jacob died?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Accept responses. They thought he might finally take revenge on them for selling him into slavery, now that Jacob wasn't there to keep the peace.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">2</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Joseph's forgiveness and God's plan</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">4–5 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Read Genesis 50:19–21 together and discuss what God's plan for Joseph's life actually was.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">God had a plan for Joseph the whole time — through slavery, prison, and finally being in charge of Egypt. What do you think that plan was?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Guide toward: God chose to use Joseph to save his people from the famine and to keep his promise to Abraham, Isaac and Jacob that their family would become a great nation and a blessing to the whole world.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"5\">3</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Memory verse from memory and term reflection</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3–4 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Say this term's memory verse fully from memory, then ask students to reflect on what's stood out to them about how God keeps his promises.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Use the say-it-from-memory activity block.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">This term we've followed God's promises all the way from Abraham to Joseph's family settling in Egypt. What's one thing that's stood out to you about the way God keeps his promises?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Accept any answer. This is a personal reflection question to finish the term's story of Abraham's family — there's no single right answer.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"3\">4</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Closing prayer</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">1–2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Close together with the built-in prayer.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Use the built-in prayer block to close.</td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 20 moves into a contemporary lesson about Holly and Compassion Australia, a change of pace from the Genesis narrative.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Memory Verse</div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Joseph told his brothers, “Don't be afraid! I have no right to change what God has decided. You tried to harm me, but God made it turn out for the best, so that he could save all these people, as he is now doing. I will take care of you and your children.” — <em>Genesis 50:19–21 (CEV)</em><br>“We know that God is always at work for the good of everyone who loves him.” — <em>Romans 8:28a (CEV)</em></blockquote></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Closing Prayer</div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Dear God, thank you that you had a plan for Joseph. Thank you that you used him to save your people and to keep your promises to Abraham, Isaac and Jacob. Thank you that you are always at work for the good of everyone who loves you. Amen.</blockquote></div>"}}},"Week 10: Holly and Compassion Australia":{"label":"Day","children":{"Week Overview":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Various (Matthew 14:14; Mark 6:34; Colossians 3:12; James 1:27; Luke 12:13-21)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Holly, a Compassion Australia volunteer, saw first-hand how Compassion Australia shows the love and compassion of Jesus to children and families living in poverty. Just as Jesus had compassion on the crowds and healed and taught them, God wants his people to have compassion on the poor and needy, and to look for ways to help make a difference.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Source</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Connect B1 Upper Primary — Lesson 20</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Monday: Meet Holly and Compassion Australia":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">10–15 minutes</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Introduction to Holly and Compassion Australia</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Meet Holly and Compassion Australia</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Holly is a young Australian Christian who volunteers for Compassion Australia, an organisation committed to holistically caring for children living in poverty around the world.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning who Holly is and how she came to be involved with Compassion Australia.</li><li>We are learning that Compassion Australia is committed to holistic development for children in poverty.</li><li>We are learning that God calls his people to be fair to the poor and to defend the helpless.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Listen for whether students can explain who Holly is and what holistic development means for Compassion Australia.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Optional: access to compassion.com.au to show students Holly's profile and the Compassion Australia home page</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">1</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Way in: richest and poorest countries</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3–4 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Ask students to guess some of the richest and poorest countries in the world, then share the 2017 lists from the manual.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">If you had to guess, which countries do you think are some of the richest in the world? And which do you think are some of the poorest?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Accept any guesses. In 2017, some of the richest countries in the world (by average spending per person) were Qatar, Luxembourg and Singapore. Some of the poorest were the Central African Republic, the Democratic Republic of the Congo and Burundi — in fact, all 10 of the poorest countries that year were in Africa.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"2\">2</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Meet Holly</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3–4 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Introduce Holly using her profile from the manual — her age, school year, favourite foods and hobbies.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">3</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Why does Holly care so much?</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3–4 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Discuss why Holly might care so much about children in poverty, then explain how her Indonesia trip led her to become a Christian and Compassion Australia volunteer.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Holly also cares a lot about children in our world who live in poverty. What are some reasons you think someone like Holly might care so much about children she's never even met?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Accept responses. Guide toward: caring for the poor and needy is something God asks his people to do, and once you understand how much difference it makes, it's hard not to care.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">4</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Holistic development</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3–4 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Explain that Compassion Australia is committed to holistic development — caring for a child's spiritual, economical, social, physical and emotional needs — and discuss why this matters.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Compassion Australia describes itself as 'an international Christian child development and child advocacy ministry' that is committed to holistic development for children in poverty — caring about a child's spiritual, economical, social, physical and emotional needs, not just their physical wellbeing. Why do you think caring for all of these parts of a child's life matters, not just making sure they have enough food or money?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Guide toward: God cares about the whole person, not just their physical needs. A child needs to be loved, to be taught about Jesus, to be safe, and to be given hope for their future — not only to be fed.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"3\">5</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Closing prayer</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">1–2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Close together with the built-in verse and prayer.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Use the built-in verse and prayer blocks to close.</td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Tomorrow: we look at the four programs Compassion Australia runs, and how Jesus' own compassion for people is the model for this work.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Memory Verse</div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">“Be fair to the poor and to orphans. Defend the helpless and everyone in need.” — <em>Psalm 82:3 (CEV)</em></blockquote></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Closing Prayer</div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Dear God, thank you for the work of Compassion Australia. Thank you that through Holly's passion for the poor, she has come into a saving knowledge of Jesus. Please help us to be fair to the poor and to defend those in need, just like your word asks us to. Amen.</blockquote></div>"},"Tuesday: Compassion Australia's programs and Jesus' compassion":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">10–15 minutes</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Matthew 14:14; Mark 6:34</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Compassion Australia's programs and Jesus' compassion</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Compassion Australia runs four programs to holistically care for children from before birth through to university, reflecting the same compassion Jesus showed when he healed and taught the crowds who came to him.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning about Compassion Australia's four programs: Child Survival, Child Sponsorship, Leadership Development, and Complementary Interventions.</li><li>We are learning that Compassion Australia partners with local churches in developing countries.</li><li>We are learning that Jesus' own compassion for the crowds is the model for how Compassion Australia cares for children.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Listen for whether students can name at least two of Compassion Australia's programs and explain how Jesus' compassion connects to this work.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Optional: access to compassion.com.au to show students the 'What we do' page and the list of 14 partner countries</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">1</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Way in: recapping holistic development</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Ask students to recall the different kinds of needs Compassion Australia cares about from yesterday's lesson.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Yesterday we learned that Compassion Australia cares about the whole child, not just their physical needs. Can you remember any of the different kinds of needs they care about?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Accept responses. Spiritual, economical, social, physical and emotional needs — this is called holistic development.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"2\">2</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>The four programs</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">4–5 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Read through Compassion Australia's four programs: Child Survival, Child Sponsorship, Leadership Development, and Complementary Interventions.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">3</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Partnering with local churches</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3–4 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Explain that Compassion Australia partners with local churches in 25 developing countries, and discuss why this matters.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Compassion Australia partners with local churches in 25 developing countries across Africa, Asia, and Central and South America to run these programs. Why do you think partnering with local churches, rather than just sending help from Australia, might be so important?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Guide toward: local churches understand what their own community really needs, and they're already in a trusted position to care for people long after any outside visitors have gone home.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">4</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Jesus' compassion for the crowds</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3–4 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Read Matthew 14:14 and Mark 6:34 together, and connect Jesus' compassion for the crowds to the holistic way Compassion Australia cares for children.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">In these two verses, Jesus healed people who were sick and taught people who needed guidance. How is this similar to the way Compassion Australia cares for the whole child — not just one need, but many?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Guide toward: Jesus' compassion wasn't only for people who were sick — he cared for the whole person, physically and spiritually. Compassion Australia reflects this same kind of compassion in the way it cares for children.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"3\">5</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Closing prayer</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">1–2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Close together with the built-in verse and prayer.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Use the built-in verse and prayer blocks to close.</td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Tomorrow: Holly's own trip to Indonesia, and what the Bible says about loving God by loving others.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Memory Verse</div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">“When Jesus got out of the boat, he saw the large crowd. He felt sorry for them and healed everyone who was sick.” — <em>Matthew 14:14 (CEV)</em><br>“When Jesus got out of the boat, he saw the large crowd that was like sheep without a shepherd. He felt sorry for the people and started teaching them many things.” — <em>Mark 6:34 (CEV)</em><br>“Be fair to the poor and to orphans. Defend the helpless and everyone in need.” — <em>Psalm 82:3 (CEV)</em></blockquote></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Closing Prayer</div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Dear God, thank you for the work of Compassion Australia's programs. Thank you that Jesus' own compassion for the sick and the lost is the example for how your people care for others today. Amen.</blockquote></div>"},"Wednesday: Holly's trip to Indonesia":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">10–15 minutes</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Colossians 3:12; James 1:27</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Holly's trip to Indonesia</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">On a school trip to Wangurer village in Indonesia, Holly saw first-hand how Compassion Australia reflects the love and compassion of Jesus for people living in poverty, both physically and spiritually.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning that Holly travelled to Wangurer village in Indonesia and saw Compassion Australia's work first-hand.</li><li>We are learning that Compassion Australia shares the hope of Jesus alongside physical help.</li><li>We are learning that loving God means loving others, especially the poor and needy.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Listen for whether students can retell what Holly experienced on her trip, and explain what James 1:27 says about true religion.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Optional: access to compassion.com.au or a printed copy of the photo of Holly and her school mates with locals from Wangurer village</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">1</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Way in: how did Holly find Compassion Australia?</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Ask students to recall how Holly first came to know about Compassion Australia.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">We've learned a lot about Compassion Australia's programs this week. Do you remember how Holly first found out about the work of Compassion Australia?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Accept responses. Holly came to know about Compassion Australia through a trip to Indonesia organised by her school.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">2</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Wangurer village</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">4–5 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Read through Holly's trip to Wangurer village, meeting the local staff, children and mothers, and read Colossians 3:12 together.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">This verse says that because God has loved and chosen us, we should be gentle, kind, humble, meek and patient with others. How do you think Holly showed these qualities on her trip to Indonesia?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Accept responses. Guide toward: Holly showed kindness and gentleness by meeting with the local mothers and children, listening to their stories, and caring about their lives rather than just observing from a distance.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"2\">3</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>True religion — James 1:27</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3–4 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Read James 1:27 together and explain that loving God means loving others, especially the poor and needy.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">4</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Reflection</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Ask students to reflect on how hearing about Holly and Compassion Australia has made them feel.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">How has hearing about Holly's trip and the work of Compassion Australia made you feel?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Accept responses. There's no single right answer here — this is a personal reflection question.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"3\">5</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Closing prayer</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">1–2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Close together with the built-in prayer.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Use the built-in prayer block to close.</td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Tomorrow: how Holly supports Compassion Australia at her own school, and what the Bible says about generosity.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Memory Verse</div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">“God loves you and has chosen you as his own special people. So be gentle, kind, humble, meek, and patient.” — <em>Colossians 3:12 (CEV)</em><br>“Religion that pleases God the Father must be pure and spotless. You must help needy orphans and widows and not let this world make you evil.” — <em>James 1:27 (CEV)</em></blockquote></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Closing Prayer</div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Dear God, thank you for the opportunity Holly had to go to Indonesia and see the work of Compassion Australia first-hand. Please help us to love you by loving the poor and needy, just as your word asks us to. Amen.</blockquote></div>"},"Thursday: How can I help?":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">10–15 minutes</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Luke 12:13–21</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">How can I help?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Holly finds creative ways to support Compassion Australia at her own school, and Jesus' story of the rich farmer reminds us that God wants us to be generous with what he has given us, rather than storing it up only for ourselves.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning some of the ways Holly supports Compassion Australia at her own school.</li><li>We are learning what the rich farmer in Jesus' story did wrong.</li><li>We are learning that God wants us to be generous with what he has given us, especially toward the poor and needy.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Listen for whether students can describe at least one way Holly supports Compassion Australia, and explain what God wants us to do with what he's given us.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Blank paper or the student Activity book, for students to write down their own ideas for how their school could support Compassion Australia</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">1</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Way in: ways to support Compassion Australia</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Ask students to brainstorm ways someone could support an organisation like Compassion Australia.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">What are some ways that someone might be able to support an organisation like Compassion Australia?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Accept responses. This connects to what students are about to hear about how Holly herself supports Compassion Australia.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"2\">2</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>How Holly helps</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3–4 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Read through the ways Holly supports Compassion Australia as an events volunteer and at her own school (mufti days, cake stalls, speeches, videos).</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">3</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>The rich farmer — Luke 12:13–21</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">4–5 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Tell the story of the rich farmer from Luke 12:13–21 and discuss what he did wrong and what God wants us to do instead.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">What does God want us to do with the things he's generously given us, instead of just storing them up for ourselves?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Guide toward: God wants us to be generous with what we've been given — sharing it to help others, especially the poor and needy, rather than hoarding it only for ourselves.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"3\">4</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Personal generosity activity</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2–3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Give students a moment to talk with a partner about one small way they could be generous with what they have.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Use the built-in partner-discussion activity block.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"3\">5</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Closing prayer</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">1–2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Close together with the built-in verse and prayer.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Use the built-in verse and prayer blocks to close.</td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">This is the final lesson of Term 2. Term 3 moves to a different level of the curriculum, Connect B2 Upper Primary, starting back at Lesson 1.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Memory Verse</div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">“Be fair to the poor and to orphans. Defend the helpless and everyone in need.” — <em>Psalm 82:3 (CEV)</em></blockquote></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Closing Prayer</div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Dear God, thank you for the work that Holly has been doing in promoting and raising money for Compassion Australia. Thank you for the opportunity to go over to Indonesia to see these programs first-hand. Please help Compassion Australia and Holly to make an impact in our world for those who are in poverty. Please also help us to think of ways that we can help make a difference too. Amen.</blockquote></div>"}}}}},"Term 3":{"label":"Week","children":{"Week 1: Survivor: Egypt Begins":{"label":"Day","children":{"Week Overview":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Exodus 1:1—2:10</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">God keeps his promises, even when things look hopeless.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Source</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Connect B2 Upper Primary — Lesson 1</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Monday: A big family in Egypt":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">10–15 minutes</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Exodus 1:1–7</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">A big family in Egypt</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">God keeps his promises, even when things look hopeless.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning how Abraham's family came to live in Egypt.</li><li>We are learning that God's promise to Abraham — that his family would become a great nation — was coming true, generation by generation.</li><li>I can explain why Jacob's family moved to Egypt.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Check that students can retell, in their own words, how Jacob's family ended up in Egypt and can connect their growth in numbers to God's promise to Abraham.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Screen/device to display the week's content (already embedded in the app)</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"2\">1</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Introducing a new story</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2–3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">“We're starting a brand new Bible story today, from a book called Exodus, about a man named Moses. But first we need to see how his people ended up in Egypt.” Briefly remind students of God's promise to Abraham (a great nation, descendants like the stars) if they covered this in earlier terms — otherwise just introduce it fresh.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">2</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Jacob's family moves to Egypt</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3–4 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Tell the story of Jacob moving his family of seventy — eleven sons and their households — to Egypt to be near Joseph, who had become a powerful leader there. Mention that they settled in Goshen, good land for their flocks, and that the family was finally reunited after everything that had happened between Joseph and his brothers.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Why do you think Jacob's family needed to move all the way to Egypt?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Guide toward: there was a severe famine in their own land, and Joseph — through God's plan — was in a position in Egypt to save his family from starving.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">3</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>A family that keeps growing</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3–4 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">“Years passed. Jacob, Joseph, and all of that generation eventually died. But their families kept having children, and grandchildren, and great-grandchildren — until the whole region of Goshen was full of them.” Explain that Jacob's twelve sons each became the father of a whole tribe — that's where the twelve tribes of Israel come from.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Remember God's promise to Abraham, that his family would become a great nation? What do you notice happening to that family here, generations later, in Egypt?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Guide toward: God's promise was quietly coming true, even in a foreign country, far from home — the Israelites were multiplying exactly as God had said they would.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"3\">4</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Retelling relay</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2–3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Pair students up and have them retell today's story in three sentences each — God's promise to Abraham, Jacob's move to Egypt, and the growth into twelve tribes.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Use the built-in retelling relay activity block.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"3\">5</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Memory verse</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2–3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Introduce this unit's memory verse, Deuteronomy 4:39 (CEV). Read it together two or three times, then try it once without looking.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Use the built-in verse block, then the say-it-together activity that follows it in the app.</td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Day 2 introduces a new king of Egypt who turns on the Israelites and forces them into slavery — even though it doesn't stop their numbers from growing.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Memory Verse</div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">“Remember that the Lord is the only true God, whether in the sky above or on the earth below.” — <em>Deuteronomy 4:39 (CEV)</em></blockquote></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Closing Prayer</div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Dear God, thank you that you are always faithful to your promises, even when we can't see it happening. Thank you for growing Abraham's family, just like you said you would. Help us trust that you keep your word. Amen.</blockquote></div>"},"Tuesday: A king who forgot":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">10–15 minutes</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Exodus 1:8–14</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">A king who forgot</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">God keeps his promises, even when things look hopeless.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning that a new king in Egypt turned against the Israelites out of fear.</li><li>We are learning that the Israelites were forced into hard slave labour, but kept growing in number anyway.</li><li>I can explain why the growth of Abraham's family both showed God's promise and provoked the king's fear.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Listen for whether students can articulate why the king felt threatened, and whether they can connect the Israelites' continued growth under oppression back to God's faithfulness.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Screen/device to display the comic panel and week's content (already embedded in the app)</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">1</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Recap and a new king</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2–3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">“Last time, we saw Jacob's family grow into a huge nation while living in Egypt. But years later, everything changed.” Introduce the new king who never knew Joseph and feared the Israelites.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Why do you think this new king felt threatened by how big the Israelite family had grown?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Guide toward: he worried they were too powerful, that they might join Egypt's enemies in a war, or even take over. Notice the irony — the very growth that showed God's promise is what frightened the king.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"3\">2</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Forced into slavery</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3–4 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Show the comic panel and explain that the king made the Israelites into slaves, forcing them into hard labour building his cities, Pithom and Rameses, under harsh taskmasters.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Show comic panel wk21-01-slave-labour.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"3\">3</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Feel the labour</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">1–2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Have the whole class stand and mime stacking heavy bricks as fast as they can for five seconds, then sit. Point out that the Israelites did that all day, every day, for years.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Use the built-in brick-miming activity block.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">4</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Growing anyway</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3–4 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">“The harder the Egyptians worked the Israelites, the more the Israelite families kept growing! Nothing the king did could stop it.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">The Israelites were suffering as slaves, yet they kept growing in number. What does that tell us about God's promises, even during hard and unfair times?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Guide toward: God's promise didn't stop just because life got hard — he was still faithfully keeping his word to Abraham, even in the middle of real suffering.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">5</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>A king versus God</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2–3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Open the second question about why the king couldn't stop what God had promised.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">The harder the king tried to crush the Israelites, the more God's promise kept coming true anyway. Why do you think the king couldn't stop something God had promised?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Guide toward: no earthly king, no matter how powerful, can overpower God's plans. This sets up the whole rest of the Exodus story — a human king going head-to-head with God, and losing.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"3\">6</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Memory verse</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Repeat this unit's memory verse, Deuteronomy 4:39 (CEV), together as a class.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Use the built-in verse block.</td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Day 3 shows the king's cruelty getting worse still, and a mother's brave plan to save her baby son.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Memory Verse</div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">“Remember that the Lord is the only true God, whether in the sky above or on the earth below.” — <em>Deuteronomy 4:39 (CEV)</em></blockquote></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Closing Prayer</div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Dear God, thank you that you don't stop keeping your promises, even when life feels hard or unfair. Help us to trust you when things around us don't make sense. Amen.</blockquote></div>"},"Wednesday: A basket of hope":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">10–15 minutes</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Exodus 1:15–22; 2:1–4</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">A basket of hope</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">God keeps his promises, even when things look hopeless.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning about Pharaoh's order to kill Israelite baby boys.</li><li>We are learning how one mother bravely protected her son by placing him in a basket on the Nile.</li><li>I can describe the courage and trust this mother showed in a frightening situation.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Check that students can retell the basket scene accurately and can articulate what trust in God might have looked like for this mother.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Screen/device to display the comic panel and week's content (already embedded in the app)</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">1</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Shiphrah and Puah's courage</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3–4 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Introduce Pharaoh's first, sneakier plan: telling the Hebrew midwives Shiphrah and Puah to secretly kill baby boys at birth. They feared God more than Pharaoh, and refused.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Why do you think the midwives were willing to disobey Pharaoh's direct order, even though it put them at risk?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Guide toward: their respect and fear of God went deeper than their fear of Pharaoh — they trusted that obeying God, even at personal cost, mattered more than obeying an unjust human command.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">2</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>The king's cruel order</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2–3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">“So the king came up with an even crueller plan: he ordered that every baby boy born to the Israelites should be thrown into the Nile River.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">How do you think Israelite parents felt when they heard this terrible order?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Accept any answer that captures fear, grief, or desperation — it must have felt like their hope for the future was under attack.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"3\">3</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>A mother's brave plan</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3–4 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Tell the story of the Levite mother hiding her son for three months, then placing him in a tar-covered basket among the reeds of the Nile, with his sister watching nearby. Show the comic panel.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Show comic panel wk21-02-basket-in-reeds.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">4</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Where did her courage come from?</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2–3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Pause on the enormity of what this mother did before opening the question to the class.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">This mother trusted God with her baby's life in an incredibly scary situation. What do you think gave her the courage to do this?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Guide toward: faith that the same God who had kept his promise to Abraham could be trusted with her son's life too, even when she couldn't control what happened next.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"3\">5</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Naming the courage</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">1–2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Have partners name and compare the two acts of courage from today's story: the midwives and the mother.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Use the built-in partner-discussion activity block.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"3\">6</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Memory verse</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Repeat this unit's memory verse, Deuteronomy 4:39 (CEV), together as a class.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Use the built-in verse block.</td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Day 4 reveals who finds the basket — and how this baby's rescue connects all the way back to God's promise to Abraham.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Memory Verse</div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">“Remember that the Lord is the only true God, whether in the sky above or on the earth below.” — <em>Deuteronomy 4:39 (CEV)</em></blockquote></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Closing Prayer</div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Dear God, please give us courage to trust you in scary situations, like the midwives and this mother did. Help us remember that you are watching over us even when we feel afraid. Amen.</blockquote></div>"},"Thursday: Pulled out of the water":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">10–15 minutes</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Exodus 2:5–10</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Pulled out of the water</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">God keeps his promises, even when things look hopeless.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning how Pharaoh's daughter found and adopted baby Moses.</li><li>We are learning that God was working behind the scenes to protect Moses and provide for him through his own mother.</li><li>I can connect Moses's rescue back to God's promise to Abraham about rescuing his people.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Check that students can retell how Moses was found and named, and can explain in their own words how his rescue connects to God's bigger promise to Abraham.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Screen/device to display the week's content (already embedded in the app)</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"2\">1</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>The princess finds the basket</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3–4 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">“One of Pharaoh's own daughters came down to bathe in the river, noticed the basket, and found the crying baby inside. She felt sorry for him and knew he must be one of the Hebrew babies.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">2</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>A surprising turn</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3–4 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">“The baby's sister bravely asked the princess if she wanted a Hebrew woman to nurse the baby — and without realising it, brought back the baby's own mother!”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Out of everyone in Egypt, this baby's own mother ended up being the one who got to raise him. What do you think this shows us about God?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Guide toward: God was working behind the scenes the whole time, even in what looked like pure chance, to protect and provide for this baby.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"2\">3</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Moses is named</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2–3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">“When the boy was old enough, his mother brought him to Pharaoh's daughter, who adopted him as her own son and named him Moses, because she said, ‘I pulled him out of the water.’”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">4</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Connecting back to the promise</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3–4 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Remind students of God's promise to Abraham that his family would be rescued from slavery in a foreign land, then open the closing question.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">How does baby Moses's rescue connect to God's promise to Abraham?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Guide toward: this baby, saved in such an unlikely way and raised right inside Pharaoh's own palace, was being prepared by God to one day become exactly the person he would use to rescue his people and keep his promise to Abraham.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"3\">5</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Whole-week recap relay</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2–3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">In table groups, have students retell the whole week's story in order, one sentence each: the family growing in Egypt, the cruel king and slavery, the basket in the reeds, and Moses's rescue and naming.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Use the built-in whole-week recap relay activity block.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"3\">6</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Memory verse, from memory</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Close the week by inviting the class to say the memory verse together entirely from memory, without looking at the screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Use the built-in verse block, then the say-it-together activity that follows it in the app.</td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Week 2 (Lesson 2) picks up with Moses as a grown man, his flight from Egypt, and God calling him at the burning bush.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Memory Verse</div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">“Remember that the Lord is the only true God, whether in the sky above or on the earth below.” — <em>Deuteronomy 4:39 (CEV)</em></blockquote></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Closing Prayer</div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Dear God, thank you that you had a plan all along for Moses, even when things looked hopeless. Thank you that you have a plan for us too. Help us trust you, even when we can't see how things will work out. Amen.</blockquote></div>"}}},"Week 2: The Leader":{"label":"Day","children":{"Week Overview":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Exodus 3:1—4:17</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">God chooses ordinary, reluctant people and equips them for his extraordinary plans.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Source</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Connect B2 Upper Primary — Lesson 2</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Monday: A blazing bush":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">10–15 minutes</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Exodus 3:1–6</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">A blazing bush</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">God chooses ordinary, reluctant people and equips them for his extraordinary plans.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning how Moses ended up as a shepherd in Midian, far from Egypt.</li><li>We are learning how God called out to Moses from a bush that burned but was never consumed.</li><li>I can explain what God's instruction about holy ground tells us about who God is.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Check that students can retell how Moses came to be in Midian and can describe what happened at the burning bush, including why God told him to remove his sandals.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Screen/device to display the week's content (already embedded in the app)</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"2\">1</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>From the palace to the desert</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3–4 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">“Last time, baby Moses was rescued from the Nile and raised in Pharaoh's palace. Today we jump ahead many years.” Explain that Moses killed an Egyptian who was mistreating an Israelite, then fled to Midian out of fear, where he became a shepherd and started a family. Pause on the huge change from palace prince to ordinary shepherd.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">2</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>A strange sight</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2–3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">“One ordinary day, while Moses was out with the sheep near Mount Horeb, he noticed a bush blazing with fire — but it never burned up.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Why do you think Moses stopped to look closer at the bush, instead of just walking past it?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Accept any answer that notices Moses's curiosity at something so unusual. This small choice to pay attention is what led him to encounter God.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"2\">3</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Holy ground</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3–4 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">“As Moses got closer, God called out to him from the bush and told him to take off his sandals, because the ground he stood on was holy.” Mention that God identified himself as the God of Abraham, Isaac, and Jacob, and that Moses hid his face, afraid to look at God directly.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"3\">4</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Taking off our shoes</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">1–2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Have the class mime removing their shoes like Moses, then discuss with a partner why that gesture mattered.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Use the built-in shoes-off activity block.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">5</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>What holy ground tells us</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2–3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Open the question about what this moment tells us about God's character.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">What does God's instruction about holy ground tell us about who God is?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Guide toward: God is holy and set apart from everything else, worthy of reverence and respect. The ordinary ground became special simply because God was present there.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"3\">6</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Memory verse</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2–3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Introduce this unit's memory verse, Deuteronomy 4:39 (CEV) — the same verse used across this unit. Read it together two or three times, then try it once without looking.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Use the built-in verse block, then the say-it-together activity that follows it in the app.</td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Day 2 hears God's rescue plan for Israel and the mission he gives Moses.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Memory Verse</div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">“Remember that the Lord is the only true God, whether in the sky above or on the earth below.” — <em>Deuteronomy 4:39 (CEV)</em></blockquote></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Closing Prayer</div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Dear God, thank you that you are holy, and that you still choose to speak to ordinary people like Moses — and like us. Help us to notice when you are trying to get our attention. Amen.</blockquote></div>"},"Tuesday: God's rescue plan":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">10–15 minutes</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Exodus 3:7–15</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">God's rescue plan</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">God chooses ordinary, reluctant people and equips them for his extraordinary plans.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning that God saw and heard the suffering of the Israelites in Egypt.</li><li>We are learning that God sent Moses with a mission to bring his people out of Egypt.</li><li>I can explain what God's name ‘I AM' tells us about who he is.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Check that students can explain God's rescue plan and mission for Moses, and can say in their own words what the name ‘I AM' reveals about God's character.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Screen/device to display the week's content (already embedded in the app)</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"2\">1</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>God has seen and heard</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3–4 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">“God told Moses, ‘I have seen how my people are suffering as slaves in Egypt. I have heard their cries, and I have come down to rescue them.'”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">2</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>The mission</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2–3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">“God said, ‘So now, go. I am sending you to Pharaoh, to bring my people out of Egypt.'”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">How do you think Moses felt in that moment, when God told him rescuing his whole people was going to be his job?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Guide toward: Moses likely felt shocked, unqualified, and afraid — he was a shepherd hiding out in the desert, not a powerful leader, and Pharaoh was the most powerful man in Egypt.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"2\">3</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>‘Who am I?'</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2–3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">“Moses's first response was, ‘Who am I, that I should go to Pharaoh?' God's answer was simple: ‘I will be with you.' The mission never depended on Moses feeling qualified.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"3\">4</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Making it personal</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">1–2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Have partners share what they'd say back if God asked them to do something that felt too big.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Use the built-in partner-sharing activity block.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">5</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>I AM WHO I AM</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3–4 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">“Moses asked what he should say God's name was. God replied, ‘I AM WHO I AM — tell them I AM has sent me to you,' explaining he was the same God who had promised Abraham, Isaac, and Jacob.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">What do you think it means that God calls himself ‘I AM'?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Guide toward: God has always existed and never changes — he is the same faithful God who made promises long ago, still showing up for his people exactly when they need him.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"3\">6</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Memory verse</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Repeat this unit's memory verse, Deuteronomy 4:39 (CEV), together as a class.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Use the built-in verse block.</td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Day 3 shows Moses making excuses — and the signs God gives him to prove he really was sent by God.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Memory Verse</div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">“Remember that the Lord is the only true God, whether in the sky above or on the earth below.” — <em>Deuteronomy 4:39 (CEV)</em></blockquote></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Closing Prayer</div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Dear God, thank you that you see and hear when your people are suffering, and that you always keep your promises. Thank you that you are the same faithful ‘I AM' today, and that you are with us even when we feel unqualified. Amen.</blockquote></div>"},"Wednesday: Moses makes excuses":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">10–15 minutes</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Exodus 3:16—4:9</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Moses makes excuses</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">God chooses ordinary, reluctant people and equips them for his extraordinary plans.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning that Moses doubted whether the Israelites would believe or listen to him.</li><li>We are learning about the miraculous signs God gave Moses as proof of his calling.</li><li>I can connect Moses's self-doubt to times I've felt unsure about something I was asked to do.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Check that students can describe at least one of the signs God gave Moses and can explain why God provided this kind of proof.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Screen/device to display the week's content (already embedded in the app)</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"2\">1</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>The plan so far</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2–3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Recap that God told Moses to gather the elders of Israel, explain the plan, and then go together to ask Pharaoh's permission to worship God in the wilderness.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"2\">2</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Moses worries — and the first sign</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2–3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">“Moses worried, ‘What if they don't believe me?' So God gave him a sign: his staff turning into a snake and back again.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"3\">3</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Act it out</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">1–2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Have the class mime holding a staff that suddenly becomes a snake, acting out Moses's fear, then bravely picking it back up.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Use the built-in staff-to-snake activity block.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">4</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Two more signs</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2–3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">“God made Moses's hand become diseased, then healed it. And a third sign: water from the Nile poured on the ground would turn to blood.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Why do you think God gave Moses these miraculous signs, instead of just telling him to trust that people would believe him?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Guide toward: the signs gave Moses — and later the Israelites and Pharaoh — real evidence that God's power was truly behind him. Seeing helped people believe.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">5</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Making it personal</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3–4 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Invite students to reflect honestly on times they've felt unqualified or unready for something.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Have you ever felt like Moses — like you weren't good enough or ready enough for something you were asked to do?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Accept personal responses. Connect it back to the big idea: God equips the people he calls: he doesn't wait for us to feel confident before he can use us.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"3\">6</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Memory verse</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Repeat this unit's memory verse, Deuteronomy 4:39 (CEV), together as a class.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Use the built-in verse block.</td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Day 4 shows Moses's final excuse — and how God provides his brother Aaron to help him.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Memory Verse</div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">“Remember that the Lord is the only true God, whether in the sky above or on the earth below.” — <em>Deuteronomy 4:39 (CEV)</em></blockquote></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Closing Prayer</div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Dear God, thank you that you don't need us to feel brave or ready before you can use us. Help us to trust you, even when we feel unsure of ourselves. Amen.</blockquote></div>"},"Thursday: Moses and his helper":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">10–15 minutes</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Exodus 4:10–17</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Moses and his helper</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">God chooses ordinary, reluctant people and equips them for his extraordinary plans.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning about Moses's final excuse — that he wasn't a good speaker.</li><li>We are learning how God provided Aaron to help Moses speak to Pharaoh.</li><li>I can explain what God providing Aaron shows us about how God equips the people he calls.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Check that students can explain Moses's final objection and describe how God's provision of Aaron addressed it, and can summarise how Moses changed across the week.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Screen/device to display the week's content (already embedded in the app)</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"2\">1</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>One last excuse</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2–3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">“Moses said, ‘Pardon me, Lord, I have never been a good speaker. I am slow of speech and tongue.' He begged God to send someone else instead.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">2</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>God's anger — and God's provision</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2–3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">“God's anger burned against Moses for still trying to get out of the job — but even angry, God didn't give up on him.” Explain that God reminded Moses his brother Aaron was already on his way to meet him, and that Aaron could speak on Moses's behalf while Moses told him what to say.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">What does it show us about God that he had already arranged for Aaron to help Moses, even before Moses asked, and even while God was frustrated with him?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Guide toward: God knows our weaknesses ahead of time and provides exactly what we need to obey him. His patience and his provision go together — he doesn't demand that we do everything completely alone, even when he's disappointed in our reluctance.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"3\">3</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Whole-week recap relay</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2–3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">In table groups, have students retell the whole week's story in order: the burning bush and holy ground, God's rescue plan and the name ‘I AM', Moses's doubts and the three signs, and God providing Aaron.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Use the built-in whole-week recap relay activity block.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">4</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Looking back over the week</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2–3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Invite students to reflect on how Moses changed across the whole week's story, from hiding by the bush to finally being ready to go.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Moses started out full of excuses and doubts. By the end, was he ready to go? What changed?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Guide toward: Moses wasn't naturally brave or qualified, but God's patient reassurance, the signs, and Aaron's help gave him what he needed to obey — even while he was probably still nervous.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"3\">5</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Memory verse, from memory</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Close the week by inviting the class to say the memory verse together entirely from memory, without looking at the screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Use the built-in verse block, then the say-it-together activity that follows it in the app.</td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Week 3 (Lesson 3) picks up with Moses and Aaron confronting Pharaoh, and the start of the plagues on Egypt.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Memory Verse</div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">“Remember that the Lord is the only true God, whether in the sky above or on the earth below.” — <em>Deuteronomy 4:39 (CEV)</em></blockquote></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Closing Prayer</div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Dear God, thank you that you knew exactly what Moses needed, even before he asked, and that you didn't give up on him even when he kept making excuses. Thank you that you know what we need too. Help us to trust you and obey, even when we feel unsure. Amen.</blockquote></div>"}}},"Week 3: The Challenge":{"label":"Day","children":{"Week Overview":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Exodus 7:1—10:29</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">God is more powerful than every false god Egypt worshipped — and no amount of stubbornness could stop his plan.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Source</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Connect B2 Upper Primary — Lesson 3</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Monday: Let my people go!":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">10–15 minutes</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Exodus 7:1–8:15</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Let my people go!</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">God is more powerful than every false god Egypt worshipped — and no amount of stubbornness could stop his plan.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning how Moses and Aaron first confronted Pharaoh, and how his heart stayed hard even after Aaron's staff-snake swallowed the magicians' snakes.</li><li>We are learning about the first two plagues — blood and frogs — and how Pharaoh's promises during the frogs plague didn't last.</li><li>I can describe the pattern of Pharaoh refusing, God sending a plague, and Pharaoh's heart hardening again.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Check that students can describe the confrontation with the staff-snake and retell the first two plagues in order, and can explain in their own words why Pharaoh's change of heart during the frogs plague didn't last.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Screen/device to display the week's content (already embedded in the app)</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">1</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Face to face with Pharaoh</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2–3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Recap that Moses and Aaron are now ready to confront Pharaoh. Tell how Aaron's staff became a snake, how the magicians copied the trick, and how Aaron's snake swallowed all of theirs — yet Pharaoh's heart stayed hard.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Aaron's snake swallowed all of the magicians' snakes — why do you think Pharaoh still refused to listen, even after seeing that?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Guide toward: the sign should have convinced anyone watching honestly, but Pharaoh's pride and stubbornness weren't really about evidence — he simply didn't want to give up his slaves or admit another king had power over him.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">2</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Plague 1: Blood</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Tell how Moses and Aaron warned Pharaoh at the Nile, how Aaron struck the water and it turned to blood, killing the fish and making the water undrinkable — but the magicians copied it, so Pharaoh's heart stayed hard for seven days.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Imagine living in Egypt for seven days with no clean water to drink, while Pharaoh just walked back into his palace. What does that tell you about the cost of one man's stubborn pride?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Guide toward: ordinary Egyptian families suffered real hardship because of Pharaoh's refusal to listen — his stubbornness wasn't a private matter, it affected everyone around him.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"3\">3</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Plague 2: Frogs</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3–4 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Tell how frogs swarmed every corner of Egypt, how Pharaoh finally begged Moses and Aaron to pray for relief and promised to let the people go — but broke his promise the moment the frogs were gone.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Use the built-in freeze-frame frogs activity block.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"3\">4</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Memory verse</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2–3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Introduce (or continue) this week's memory verse and practise it together as a class.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Use the built-in verse block, then the say-it-together activity that follows it in the app.</td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Day 2 continues the plagues with gnats, flies, and diseased livestock, and shows God protecting his own people in Goshen.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Memory Verse</div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">“Remember that the Lord is the only true God, whether in the sky above or on the earth below.” — <em>Deuteronomy 4:39 (CEV)</em></blockquote></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Closing Prayer</div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Dear God, thank you that you are more powerful than every false god and every stubborn heart. Thank you that you kept your promise to rescue your people, even when Pharaoh refused again and again. Help us to listen to you the first time, instead of only turning to you when things get hard. Amen.</blockquote></div>"},"Tuesday: Bugs, flies, and sick animals":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">10–15 minutes</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Exodus 8:16–9:7</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Bugs, flies, and sick animals</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">God is more powerful than every false god Egypt worshipped — and no amount of stubbornness could stop his plan.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning about the plagues of gnats, flies, and diseased livestock, and how each one Pharaoh's own advisors or actions confirmed came from God.</li><li>We are learning how God specifically protected the Israelites in Goshen during these plagues.</li><li>I can explain why God made the difference between Goshen and the rest of Egypt so obvious.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Check that students can describe all three plagues in this section and explain what the protection of Goshen showed about God's power and control.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Screen/device to display the week's content (already embedded in the app)</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">1</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Plague 3: Gnats</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2–3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Tell how Aaron struck the dust and it became gnats across Egypt, and how this time the magicians couldn't copy it — they had to admit to Pharaoh that this had to be God's own doing.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Pharaoh's own magicians told him this plague was from God. Why do you think that mattered, and why do you think Pharaoh still refused to listen?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Guide toward: it removed any excuse Pharaoh might have had — his continued refusal became entirely his own stubborn choice, not a lack of evidence.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"2\">2</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Plague 4: Flies (and Goshen protected)</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Tell how God promised Goshen would have no flies, how Pharaoh bargained and offered partial deals, and how his heart hardened again the moment relief came.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">3</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Plague 5: Livestock</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Tell how every Egyptian animal died from disease, but not one Israelite animal was touched — Pharaoh even checked and confirmed it himself, yet still refused.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Three plagues in a row now, God has drawn a clear line between Goshen and the rest of Egypt. Why do you think he kept making that difference so obvious?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Guide toward: it proved these plagues weren't random bad luck, but entirely under God's control — leaving Pharaoh with no excuse to pretend it was just misfortune.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"3\">4</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Partner talk and memory verse</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3–4 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Run the partner discussion activity, then practise the memory verse together.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Use the built-in partner-talk activity block, then the built-in verse block and say-it-together activity.</td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Day 3 continues with boils, the worst hailstorm in Egypt's history, and a devastating swarm of locusts.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Memory Verse</div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">“Remember that the Lord is the only true God, whether in the sky above or on the earth below.” — <em>Deuteronomy 4:39 (CEV)</em></blockquote></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Closing Prayer</div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Dear God, thank you that nothing that happens is outside of your control — not even swarms of insects or sick animals. Thank you for how carefully you looked after your people in Goshen. Help us trust that you're looking after us too, even when things around us feel chaotic. Amen.</blockquote></div>"},"Wednesday: Boils, hail, and locusts":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">10–15 minutes</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Exodus 9:8–10:20</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Boils, hail, and locusts</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">God is more powerful than every false god Egypt worshipped — and no amount of stubbornness could stop his plan.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning about the plagues of boils, hail, and locusts, and how each one grew more serious than the last.</li><li>We are noticing the shift in the story from Pharaoh hardening his own heart to the Lord hardening Pharaoh's heart.</li><li>I can explain the difference between Pharaoh's fear-driven promises and genuine repentance.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Check that students can describe the three plagues in order and can explain, in their own words, why Pharaoh's repeated promises during the hail and locusts didn't reflect real change.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Screen/device to display the week's content (already embedded in the app)</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"2\">1</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Plague 6: Boils</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Tell how Moses threw ashes into the air and boils broke out on people and animals, even affecting the magicians themselves. Note the shift in the text — now it says the Lord hardened Pharaoh's heart.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">2</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Plague 7: Hail</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Tell how God warned of the worst hailstorm in Egypt's history, how some officials heeded the warning and some didn't, how Goshen was untouched, and how Pharaoh's promise collapsed the moment the storm stopped.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Pharaoh kept promising to let Israel go the moment a plague hit hard, then broke his promise the instant the trouble stopped. What does that tell us about the difference between real regret and only being sorry you got caught?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Guide toward: Pharaoh's ‘sorry' was about escaping pain, not truly caring for the Israelites or turning back to God — real repentance leads to lasting change, not relief-driven bargaining.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"3\">3</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Act it out: fear vs. real change</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Run the partner act-it-out activity dramatising Pharaoh's pattern of promising then reneging.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Use the built-in act-it-out activity block.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"2\">4</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Plague 8: Locusts</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Tell how Pharaoh's own officials begged him to relent, how his final offer excluded the flocks and herds, and how the locust swarm stripped Egypt of everything green before being swept into the Red Sea — yet Pharaoh's heart was hardened again.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"3\">5</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Memory verse</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Practise the memory verse together, encouraging students to try it from memory.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Use the built-in verse block and say-it-together activity.</td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Day 4 brings the ninth and final plague of this lesson — three days of total darkness — and looks back over the whole week's story.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Memory Verse</div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">“Remember that the Lord is the only true God, whether in the sky above or on the earth below.” — <em>Deuteronomy 4:39 (CEV)</em></blockquote></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Closing Prayer</div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Dear God, thank you that you are patient, even with people who keep breaking their promises to you. Help us to be honest with you when we're sorry — not just sorry the trouble is happening, but truly ready to change. Thank you that your plan to rescue your people never stopped moving forward, no matter how many times Pharaoh said no. Amen.</blockquote></div>"},"Thursday: Three days of darkness":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">10–15 minutes</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Exodus 10:21–29</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Three days of darkness</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">God is more powerful than every false god Egypt worshipped — and no amount of stubbornness could stop his plan.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning about the ninth plague — three days of darkness — and Pharaoh's final, angry refusal in this lesson's passage.</li><li>We are looking back over the whole week to retell all nine plagues in order.</li><li>I can explain what the nine plagues, taken together, show us about God's power over Egypt's false gods.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Check that students can describe the plague of darkness and Pharaoh's final offer and refusal, can retell the whole week's sequence of plagues in order, and can explain what the plagues showed about God's power over Egypt's false gods.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Screen/device to display the week's content (already embedded in the app)</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">1</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Plague 9: Darkness</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Tell how Moses stretched out his hand and three days of total darkness covered Egypt, while the Israelites had light. Describe Pharaoh's final offer — letting everyone go but not their animals — and Moses's refusal, ending in Pharaoh's furious threat.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">After nine plagues, Pharaoh still refused to fully let God's people go. What does his refusal, even now, tell us about how far pride and stubbornness can carry a person?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Guide toward: Pharaoh's heart had become so hardened that even overwhelming evidence didn't change him — a sober reminder of how serious it is to keep refusing God, and how powerless his stubbornness really was against God's plan.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"3\">2</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Whole-week recap relay</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3–4 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">In table groups, have students retell the whole week's story in order: the staff-turned-snake, then all nine plagues — blood, frogs, gnats, flies, sick animals, boils, hail, locusts, and darkness.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Use the built-in whole-week recap relay activity block.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">3</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>What was God showing Egypt?</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2–3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Invite students to reflect on the purpose behind all nine plagues together.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Looking back over all nine plagues this week, what do you think God was trying to show Pharaoh and the whole of Egypt through them?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Guide toward: many of these plagues struck directly at things the Egyptians treated as gods — the Nile, frogs, livestock, the sun. Through every plague, the Lord kept proving that he alone is the true and living God.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"3\">4</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Memory verse, from memory</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Close the week by inviting the class to say the memory verse together entirely from memory, without looking at the screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Use the built-in verse block, then the say-it-together activity that follows it in the app.</td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Week 4 (Lesson 4) picks up with the tenth and final plague, and the story of the first Passover.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Memory Verse</div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">“Remember that the Lord is the only true God, whether in the sky above or on the earth below.” — <em>Deuteronomy 4:39 (CEV)</em></blockquote></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Closing Prayer</div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Dear God, thank you that you are the only true God, in the sky above and on the earth below, more powerful than every false god Egypt trusted in. Thank you that your plan to rescue your people never failed, even after nine plagues and a king who refused you every time. Help us to trust you fully, the first time you ask, rather than only turning to you when things get hard. Amen.</blockquote></div>"}}},"Week 4: The Rescue":{"label":"Day","children":{"Week Overview":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Exodus 11–14</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">God is willing and able to rescue his people from what looks like an impossible situation — from the plagues in Egypt, through the Passover, and all the way through the Red Sea. This same rescuing God still saves people from slavery to sin today, through Jesus.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Source</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Connect B2 Upper Primary — Lesson 4</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Monday: The last plague":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">10–15 minutes</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Exodus 11:1–10; 12:1–28</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">The last plague</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">God is willing and able to rescue his people from what looks like an impossible situation — from the plagues in Egypt, through the Passover, and all the way through the Red Sea. This same rescuing God still saves people from slavery to sin today, through Jesus.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning that the tenth plague was God's final and most serious warning to Pharaoh.</li><li>We are learning that God protected his people through the blood of the Passover lamb, on the condition they trusted and obeyed him.</li><li>I can begin learning this term's memory verse, Deuteronomy 4:39.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Listen for whether students can explain why the blood on the doorframes mattered, and can connect obeying God's instructions with being kept safe.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>CEV Bible (optional, for reading the Passover instructions aloud)</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">1</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Nine warnings so far</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Recap that God has already sent nine plagues and Pharaoh still refuses, then ask the warm-up question.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">So far, God has sent nine plagues on Egypt, but Pharaoh still won't let God's people go. If you were God, and you'd already given someone nine warnings, what would you do next?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Accept responses. Use this to set up how serious the tenth and final plague was — this wasn't God acting rashly, it was the last of ten clear warnings.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"2\">2</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>The tenth plague announced</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Tell how God warned Pharaoh of the death of the firstborn as his final, most serious plague.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"2\">3</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Blood on the doorframes</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3–4 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Explain the Passover instructions, and how the blood on the doorframes protected Israelite households.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">4</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Why did God ask for obedience?</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Ask why God required an action from his people rather than protecting them automatically.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Why do you think God asked his people to do something — painting blood on their doorframes — rather than just automatically protecting them?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Accept responses. Guide toward: God still asked for trust and obedience even in the middle of rescuing them. Their protection came from trusting the Lord and doing what he said, not from anything special about themselves.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"3\">5</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Memory verse</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">1–2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Introduce this term's new memory verse and try saying it together a few times.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Use the built-in verse block for Deuteronomy 4:39, then the say-it-together activity block.</td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Day 2 picks up right after the Passover, as Pharaoh finally lets Israel go — and then changes his mind.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Memory Verse</div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">“So remember that the Lord is the only true God, whether in the sky above or on the earth below.” — <em>Deuteronomy 4:39 (CEV)</em></blockquote></div>"},"Tuesday: Pharaoh's army":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">10–15 minutes</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Exodus 12:31–36; 13:17–22; 14:1–14</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Pharaoh's army</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">God is willing and able to rescue his people from what looks like an impossible situation — from the plagues in Egypt, through the Passover, and all the way through the Red Sea. This same rescuing God still saves people from slavery to sin today, through Jesus.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning that Pharaoh let Israel go, and that God led them with a pillar of cloud and fire.</li><li>We are learning that God deliberately led Israel to a place that looked like a trap, so he could show his power when Pharaoh's army pursued them.</li><li>I can explain what Moses meant when he told the frightened Israelites, 'The Lord will fight for you.'</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Listen for whether students can explain why God led Israel into a seemingly trapped position on purpose, and can describe Moses' response to the people's fear.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"2\">1</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Leaving Egypt at last</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2–3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Tell how Pharaoh finally let Israel go, and how the Egyptians sent them off with gold, silver and clothing.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">2</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Led into a trap — on purpose</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Describe the pillar of cloud and fire, and God's deliberate choice to camp near the Red Sea, then ask why.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Why do you think God deliberately led his people into a spot that looked like a trap, instead of a safer route?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Guide toward Exodus 14:1–4: God wanted to use this moment to show his power over Pharaoh and the Egyptian army, so that everyone would know he is the Lord — not because he'd made a mistake.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">3</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Pharaoh's army arrives</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Tell how Pharaoh changed his mind and pursued Israel, and how the people responded in fear.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">The Israelites had just watched God defeat Egypt through ten plagues and the Passover — yet now they were terrified again. Why do you think it's so easy to become afraid again, even after we've seen God come through before?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Accept responses. Guide toward: fear can make us forget what we already know about God, which is exactly why Moses' next words were so important.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"3\">4</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>'The Lord will fight for you'</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2–3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Read Moses' reassurance to the people, then close with the memory verse.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Use the built-in verse block for Deuteronomy 4:39, then the say-it-together activity block.</td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Day 3 finds out exactly how God rescues Israel from the Egyptian army at the Red Sea.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Memory Verse</div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">“So remember that the Lord is the only true God, whether in the sky above or on the earth below.” — <em>Deuteronomy 4:39 (CEV)</em></blockquote></div>"},"Wednesday: A road through the water":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">10–15 minutes</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Exodus 14:15–31</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">A road through the water</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">God is willing and able to rescue his people from what looks like an impossible situation — from the plagues in Egypt, through the Passover, and all the way through the Red Sea. This same rescuing God still saves people from slavery to sin today, through Jesus.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning exactly how God parted, and then closed, the Red Sea to rescue Israel and defeat the Egyptian army.</li><li>We are learning that God's angel and the pillar of cloud protected Israel by holding the Egyptians back overnight.</li><li>I can retell, in my own words, how God saved the Israelites from the Egyptian army.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Listen for whether students can retell the sequence of the Red Sea crossing accurately (parting, crossing, closing) using their own words.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">1</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>An impossible situation</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">1–2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Recap yesterday's cliffhanger and ask the warm-up question before revealing what happens.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Yesterday we left the Israelites trapped between Pharaoh's army and the Red Sea. If you were one of the Israelites at that moment, what do you think would be going through your mind?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Accept responses. This sets up just how impossible the situation looked, right before God acts.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"2\">2</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>The sea parts</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Tell how Moses stretched out his staff and the sea split, and how the cloud/angel protected Israel overnight.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"2\">3</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Through on dry ground</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Describe the crossing and the Egyptian army's pursuit and panic.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">4</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>The sea closes</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Tell how the sea rushed back and swept the Egyptian army away, then ask students to retell the whole rescue.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">How did God save the Israelites from the Egyptian army?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Accept responses that mention the parting of the sea, the pillar of cloud/angel holding back the Egyptians overnight, and the sea closing over the army the next morning. All three parts worked together.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"3\">5</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Memory verse</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">1–2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Try the memory verse together without looking, if the class is ready.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Use the say-it-from-memory activity block.</td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Day 4 asks what this rescue still means for people today.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Memory Verse</div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">“So remember that the Lord is the only true God, whether in the sky above or on the earth below.” — <em>Deuteronomy 4:39 (CEV)</em></blockquote></div>"},"Thursday: Rescued like Israel":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">10–15 minutes</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">1 Timothy 2:5</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rescued like Israel</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">God is willing and able to rescue his people from what looks like an impossible situation — from the plagues in Egypt, through the Passover, and all the way through the Red Sea. This same rescuing God still saves people from slavery to sin today, through Jesus.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning that the Exodus rescue points forward to people's need for rescue from sin.</li><li>We are learning that Jesus is the one who rescues people and brings them to God, as 1 Timothy 2:5 says.</li><li>I can say this week's memory verse, Deuteronomy 4:39, fully from memory.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Listen for whether students can explain, in simple terms, how Jesus' rescue relates to the rescue God gave Israel at the Red Sea.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>CEV Bible (optional, for reading 1 Timothy 2:5 aloud)</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">1</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>One more rescue</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Recap this week's rescue, then ask students to think of other rescues in the Bible.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Who else can you think of, from anything you already know about the Bible, that God has rescued?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Accept responses, e.g. Noah, or Jesus rescuing people from sin. This connects the Exodus rescue pattern to the wider Bible story students may already know.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"2\">2</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Rescued from sin</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2–3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Explain that all people need rescuing from sin today, and that God sent Jesus to do this.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">3</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>How Jesus rescues us</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Read 1 Timothy 2:5, then ask how Jesus rescues his people today.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">How does Jesus rescue his people today?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Accept responses. Guide toward: Jesus died on the cross and rose again so that everyone who trusts him can be forgiven and brought back into a right relationship with God — his death and resurrection do for us today what nothing else could.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"3\">4</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Memory verse and closing prayer</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Finish the week's memory verse fully from memory, then close together with the prayer in the app.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Use the say-it-from-memory activity block, then the built-in prayer block to close.</td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 5 moves on to Mount Sinai, where God gives his rescued people his Law to show them how to live as his special people.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Memory Verse</div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">“There is only one God, and Christ Jesus is the only one who can bring us to God. Jesus was truly human, and he gave himself to rescue all of us.” — <em>1 Timothy 2:5 (CEV)</em><br>“So remember that the Lord is the only true God, whether in the sky above or on the earth below.” — <em>Deuteronomy 4:39 (CEV)</em></blockquote></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Closing Prayer</div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Dear God, thank you that you rescued your people from Egypt, and thank you that you sent Jesus to rescue us from our sin. Help us to remember and trust that you are the one true God, in heaven and on earth. Amen.</blockquote></div>"}}},"Week 5: The Law":{"label":"Day","children":{"Week Overview":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Exodus 19–20</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">God rescued Israel out of slavery in Egypt, and now, at Mount Sinai, he gives them his Law to show his people how to live as his special, treasured people — not to earn his rescue, but because he had already saved them.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Source</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Connect B2 Upper Primary — Lesson 5</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Monday: God's promise to his people":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">10–15 minutes</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Exodus 19:1–8</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">God's promise to his people</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">God rescued Israel out of slavery in Egypt, and now, at Mount Sinai, he gives them his Law to show his people how to live as his special, treasured people — not to earn his rescue, but because he had already saved them.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning that God led Israel to Mount Sinai and offered them a special covenant relationship.</li><li>We are learning that Israel agreed to obey everything the Lord had said.</li><li>I can explain what it means that Israel would be God's 'treasured possession'.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Listen for whether students can explain, in their own words, what God offered Israel at Sinai and how the people responded.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">1</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>What comes after a rescue?</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Recap last week's rescue at the Red Sea, then ask the warm-up question.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Last week we saw God rescue Israel out of Egypt and through the Red Sea. If God has just done something amazing to save you, what do you think he might want from you next?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Accept responses. Guide toward: not payment or earning the rescue back, but a relationship — as we'll see today, God's answer was to invite Israel into a special relationship with him.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"2\">2</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Arriving at Sinai</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2–3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Tell how Israel travelled to Mount Sinai and Moses went up to meet with God.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">3</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>God's treasured possession</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3–4 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Read God's covenant offer to the people, then ask what being God's 'treasured possession' would mean.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">What do you think it would mean to be God's 'treasured possession' out of every nation on earth?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Accept responses. Guide toward: being specially chosen, loved, and set apart by God — not because Israel had earned it, but because God graciously chose them.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"3\">4</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>'We will do everything the Lord has said'</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Tell how the people responded together, then introduce the memory verse (carried over from last week).</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Use the built-in verse block for Deuteronomy 4:39, then the say-it-together activity block.</td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Day 2 finds out exactly what God's Law said, starting with the Ten Commandments.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Memory Verse</div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">“So remember that the Lord is the only true God, whether in the sky above or on the earth below.” — <em>Deuteronomy 4:39 (CEV)</em></blockquote></div>"},"Tuesday: Loving God first":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">10–15 minutes</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Exodus 20:1–8</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Loving God first</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">God rescued Israel out of slavery in Egypt, and now, at Mount Sinai, he gives them his Law to show his people how to live as his special, treasured people — not to earn his rescue, but because he had already saved them.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning the first four of the Ten Commandments, which are about our relationship with God.</li><li>We are learning that these commandments teach Israel to worship only the Lord, and to keep the Sabbath as belonging to him.</li><li>I can explain why commandments about God come before commandments about people.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Listen for whether students can name at least one of the first four commandments and explain, broadly, what it teaches about our relationship with God.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">1</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>What comes first?</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Ask the warm-up question before revealing how the Ten Commandments actually begin.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">If you were making a list of rules for how to live well, what do you think should come first on the list?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Accept responses. Use this to set up how the Ten Commandments begin — not with rules about people, but about our relationship with God first.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"2\">2</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>The first four commandments</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">4 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Read through the first four commandments together, about worship, idols, God's name, and the Sabbath.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">3</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Why God first?</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Ask why commandments about God come before commandments about people.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Why do you think God put commandments about himself first, before commandments about how to treat other people?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Guide toward: our relationship with God comes first and shapes everything else — how we treat others flows out of how we treat God.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"3\">4</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Memory verse</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">1–2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Say the memory verse together again.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Use the say-it-together activity block.</td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Day 3 covers the rest of the Ten Commandments — how to treat other people.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Memory Verse</div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">“So remember that the Lord is the only true God, whether in the sky above or on the earth below.” — <em>Deuteronomy 4:39 (CEV)</em></blockquote></div>"},"Wednesday: Loving our neighbour":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">10–15 minutes</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Exodus 20:12–17; 19:3–8 (recap)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Loving our neighbour</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">God rescued Israel out of slavery in Egypt, and now, at Mount Sinai, he gives them his Law to show his people how to live as his special, treasured people — not to earn his rescue, but because he had already saved them.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning the last six of the Ten Commandments, which are about how to treat other people.</li><li>We are learning that Israel agreed to keep God's Law only after God had already rescued them.</li><li>I can explain why the rescue came before the Law, not after it.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Listen for whether students can explain that obedience was Israel's grateful response to being rescued, not a way of earning God's rescue.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"2\">1</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>The last six commandments</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Read through the last six commandments together, about how to treat other people.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">2</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>The hardest one</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Ask which commandment students think would be hardest to keep, and why.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Which of these six commandments do you think would have been the hardest for people to keep, and why?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Accept any reasonable answer with a real reason. There's no wrong answer here — the goal is for students to notice these commandments are still genuinely hard to keep today.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">3</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Rescue before Law</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3–4 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Recap that Israel agreed to the Law only after being rescued, then ask why the order matters.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Why do you think it mattered that God rescued Israel before he gave them the Law, rather than the other way around?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Guide toward: obedience wasn't how Israel earned their rescue — the rescue came first. Keeping the Law was meant to be a grateful response to what God had already done for them, not a way of earning it.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"3\">4</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Memory verse</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">1–2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Try the memory verse together without looking, if the class is ready.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Use the say-it-from-memory activity block.</td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Day 4 looks at how Jesus summed up the whole Law in just two commandments.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Memory Verse</div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">“So remember that the Lord is the only true God, whether in the sky above or on the earth below.” — <em>Deuteronomy 4:39 (CEV)</em></blockquote></div>"},"Thursday: The Law summed up":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">10–15 minutes</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Matthew 22:36–40</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">The Law summed up</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">God rescued Israel out of slavery in Egypt, and now, at Mount Sinai, he gives them his Law to show his people how to live as his special, treasured people — not to earn his rescue, but because he had already saved them.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning that Jesus summed up the whole Law in two commandments: love God, and love others.</li><li>We are learning how the Ten Commandments fit under these two headings.</li><li>I can say this week's memory verse, Deuteronomy 4:39, fully from memory.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Listen for whether students can sort the Ten Commandments correctly under 'loving God' and 'loving others', and explain Jesus' summary in their own words.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>CEV Bible (optional, for reading Matthew 22:36–40 aloud)</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"2\">1</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Jesus is asked the question</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2–3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Read Jesus' answer about the most important commandments.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">2</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Connecting it back</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3–4 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Ask how Jesus' two commandments connect to the Ten Commandments learned this week.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">How do Jesus' two commandments — love God, and love others — connect to the Ten Commandments we've learned about this week?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Guide toward: the first four commandments are all about loving God, and the last six are all about loving others — Jesus wasn't giving a new rule, he was summing up the whole Law we've already learned.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">3</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>How to live today</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2–3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Ask what God has shown us about how to live today.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">What has God shown us about how to live today?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Accept responses. Guide toward: the Bible — God's word — still shows people today how to live as his people, out of love and gratitude for what he's done, not to earn his love.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"3\">4</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Memory verse and closing prayer</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Finish this week's memory verse fully from memory, then close together with the prayer in the app.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Use the say-it-from-memory activity block, then the built-in prayer block to close.</td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 6 moves on to the tabernacle — the special tent where God chose to live among his people.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Memory Verse</div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">“So remember that the Lord is the only true God, whether in the sky above or on the earth below.” — <em>Deuteronomy 4:39 (CEV)</em></blockquote></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Closing Prayer</div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Dear God, thank you that you rescued us first, before ever asking anything of us. Thank you for your Law that shows us how to live — to love you and love others. Help us to obey you out of love and gratitude, not because we have to earn your rescue. Amen.</blockquote></div>"}}},"Week 6: The Tabernacle":{"label":"Day","children":{"Week Overview":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Exodus 25–40</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">God chose to live among his people in the tabernacle — but because God is holy and his people are sinful, he also gave detailed instructions for priests and sacrifices, a way to be made right with him. This whole system points forward to Jesus, whose death lets us come into God's presence forever.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Source</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Connect B2 Upper Primary — Lesson 6</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Monday: God wants to live with his people":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">10–15 minutes</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Exodus 25:1–9</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">God wants to live with his people</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">God chose to live among his people in the tabernacle — but because God is holy and his people are sinful, he also gave detailed instructions for priests and sacrifices, a way to be made right with him. This whole system points forward to Jesus, whose death lets us come into God's presence forever.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning that God wanted to live among his people, and asked them to build him a sacred tent.</li><li>We are learning that God gave Moses an exact pattern to follow for the tabernacle.</li><li>I can begin learning this week's new memory verse, Psalm 117:2.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Listen for whether students can explain why God wanted a tabernacle, and why building it exactly to the pattern mattered.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">1</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>A home for God?</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Ask the warm-up question before describing the tabernacle.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">If God wanted to actually live among his people, right in the middle of their camp, what do you think that tent or building would need to be like?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Accept responses. Use this to set up just how detailed and careful God's instructions for the tabernacle were.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"2\">2</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>'Make me a sacred tent'</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Tell how God asked for gifts to build the tabernacle, so he could live among his people.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">3</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>An exact pattern</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3–4 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Describe how detailed God's instructions were, then ask why the exact pattern mattered.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Why do you think it mattered so much that Moses build the tabernacle exactly to God's pattern, instead of using his own ideas?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Guide toward Hebrews 8:1–5: the tabernacle was a copy of something real in heaven, so getting the earthly copy exactly right mattered — it wasn't just decoration, it showed something true about God.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"3\">4</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>New memory verse</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">1–2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Introduce this week's new memory verse, Psalm 117:2, and try saying it together.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Use the built-in verse block for Psalm 117:2, then the say-it-together activity block.</td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Day 2 looks at why priests and sacrifices were needed before anyone could enter God's presence.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Memory Verse</div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">“His love for us is wonderful, his faithfulness never ends. Shout praises to the Lord!” — <em>Psalm 117:2 (CEV)</em></blockquote></div>"},"Tuesday: Priests and sacrifices":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">10–15 minutes</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Exodus 40 (overview)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Priests and sacrifices</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">God chose to live among his people in the tabernacle — but because God is holy and his people are sinful, he also gave detailed instructions for priests and sacrifices, a way to be made right with him. This whole system points forward to Jesus, whose death lets us come into God's presence forever.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning that the tabernacle had separate areas, with the most holy place reserved for God's presence.</li><li>We are learning that priests and sacrifices were needed because of the seriousness of sin.</li><li>I can explain why not just anyone could enter the most holy place.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Listen for whether students can explain, simply, why priests and sacrifices were necessary before anyone could approach God.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"2\">1</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Areas of the tabernacle</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2–3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Describe the courtyard, holy place, and most holy place, and who could go where.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">2</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Priests and sacrifices</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Explain the role of priests and daily sacrifices in dealing with the people's sin.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Why do you think God set up such careful rules about who could come near him, and how?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Guide toward: God's holiness and people's sinfulness don't mix — the sacrifices and priesthood were God's own gracious way of dealing with sin so his people could still be near him safely, not humans just making it hard for themselves.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">3</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>How seriously God takes sin</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Ask what the sacrifice system tells us about how seriously God takes sin.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">What does this tell us about how seriously God takes sin?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Accept responses. Guide toward: sin is a genuinely serious problem that needed real dealing with — through the blood of an animal sacrifice, standing in for the person's sin, every single day.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"3\">4</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Memory verse</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">1–2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Say this week's memory verse together again.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Use the say-it-together activity block.</td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Day 3 finds out how Jesus' death changed all of this, once and for all.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Memory Verse</div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">“His love for us is wonderful, his faithfulness never ends. Shout praises to the Lord!” — <em>Psalm 117:2 (CEV)</em></blockquote></div>"},"Wednesday: Jesus, our once-for-all sacrifice":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">10–15 minutes</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Mark 15:37–39; Hebrews 10:11–14</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Jesus, our once-for-all sacrifice</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">God chose to live among his people in the tabernacle — but because God is holy and his people are sinful, he also gave detailed instructions for priests and sacrifices, a way to be made right with him. This whole system points forward to Jesus, whose death lets us come into God's presence forever.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning that the temple curtain tore in two when Jesus died, from top to bottom.</li><li>We are learning that Jesus offered himself once for all as our great high priest, so the old sacrifice system was no longer needed.</li><li>I can explain how Jesus' death is different from the priests' sacrifices.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Listen for whether students can connect the torn curtain to what Jesus' death achieved, and explain why his sacrifice was 'once for all'.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">1</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>The curtain tears</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2–3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Tell how the temple curtain tore when Jesus died, then ask why the direction of the tear matters.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Why do you think it's significant that the curtain tore from top to bottom, rather than being ripped from the bottom by a person?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Guide toward: it showed this was God's own doing, not a human tearing it — signalling that the barrier between God and his people had been dealt with by God himself, through Jesus.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"2\">2</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Our great high priest</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Explain that Jesus offered himself once for all as the perfect sacrifice.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">3</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Once for all</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Ask how Jesus' sacrifice is different from what the priests did about sin.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">How is what Jesus did different from what the priests did about sin?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Guide toward: priests could never take away sin, only cover it temporarily — but Jesus' one sacrifice deals with sin completely and forever, so that people who trust him are set free from sin.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"3\">4</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Memory verse</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">1–2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Say this week's memory verse together once more.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Use the say-it-together activity block.</td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Day 4 looks forward to when God's people will finally live with him face to face, forever.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Memory Verse</div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">“His love for us is wonderful, his faithfulness never ends. Shout praises to the Lord!” — <em>Psalm 117:2 (CEV)</em></blockquote></div>"},"Thursday: No more tabernacle needed":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">10–15 minutes</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Revelation 21:1–4</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">No more tabernacle needed</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">God chose to live among his people in the tabernacle — but because God is holy and his people are sinful, he also gave detailed instructions for priests and sacrifices, a way to be made right with him. This whole system points forward to Jesus, whose death lets us come into God's presence forever.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning that Revelation promises a future where God will live with his people forever, face to face.</li><li>We are learning that this future is what the whole tabernacle story, and Jesus' sacrifice, were pointing toward all along.</li><li>I can say this week's memory verse, Psalm 117:2, fully from memory.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Listen for whether students can explain, in their own words, how the tabernacle story ends with the promise in Revelation 21.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>CEV Bible (optional, for reading Revelation 21:1–4 aloud)</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"2\">1</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>The whole week, and beyond</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Recap the week's journey from the tabernacle to Jesus.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">2</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>God's home with his people</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Read the promise from Revelation 21, then ask students to imagine what it will be like.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">What do you think it will be like to live with God face to face, with no more sin, sadness, or separation between us and him?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Accept responses — encourage genuine imagination here. Guide toward: this is what the whole tabernacle system was pointing forward to all along — perfect, permanent closeness with God, made possible through Jesus.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">3</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Real hope</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Ask how this future promise changes the way we think about following Jesus today.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">How does knowing this future change the way we think about following Jesus today?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Accept responses. Guide toward: it gives us real hope — everything broken about our relationship with God because of sin will one day be completely fixed.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"3\">4</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Memory verse and closing prayer</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Finish this week's memory verse fully from memory, then close together with the prayer in the app.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Use the say-it-from-memory activity block, then the built-in prayer block to close.</td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">This closes out the B2 Upper Primary Exodus material built so far (Lessons 4–6). Lessons 1–3 for this track, and any lessons beyond 6, are not yet built.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Memory Verse</div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">“His love for us is wonderful, his faithfulness never ends. Shout praises to the Lord!” — <em>Psalm 117:2 (CEV)</em></blockquote></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Closing Prayer</div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Dear God, thank you that you have always wanted to live among your people — through the tabernacle, and now through Jesus. Thank you that one day we will live with you forever, with nothing broken between us. Help us trust you and love you until that day comes. Amen.</blockquote></div>"}}}}}}},"writing":{"label":"Term","children":{"Term 1":{"label":"Week","children":{"Week 1":{"label":"Day","children":{"Monday: Tier 2 vocabulary: descriptive adjectives":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">NZ Curriculum Link</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Writing — Vocabulary (Knowledge): words and phrases are chosen deliberately for effect, including Tier 2 vocabulary that is precise and cross-curricular. Students select words that shape meaning and impact for the reader.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>I Do — Watch Me Choose Better Words</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>I'm going to show you how to swap a plain word for a much more interesting one. Watch how I upgrade the word &quot;big&quot; in this sentence.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Example: The big dog ran across the field.</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Instead of &quot;big&quot; I could say: enormous, muscular, towering, hulking. I pick &quot;muscular&quot; because it tells us something extra — not just size, but strength. Watch: &quot;The muscular dog ran across the field.&quot; Much more vivid!</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Tip: Ask yourself: Does this word paint a picture? If not, upgrade it.</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>We Do — Upgrade Together</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Let's upgrade the plain adjectives in these sentences together. Shout out your ideas!</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The nice sunset filled the sky with colour.</li><li>She wore a pretty dress to the concert.</li><li>It was a bad storm that hit the town.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Discussion prompt: What word could replace the underlined one? Why is your choice better?</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>You Do — Your Turn</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Rewrite each sentence by replacing the plain adjective with a stronger, more descriptive one. Try to use a different word each time!</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The old bridge creaked in the wind.</li><li>We climbed a high mountain on our trip.</li><li>The puppy had a soft coat.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write your own sentence using two vivid adjectives.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Objectives (AOs)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Writing — Vocabulary (Knowledge)</em> — Word choice for effect: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Vocabulary (Knowledge): &quot;The meaning of words can be extended or changed by their context… vocabulary is chosen deliberately for effect, so students consider the connotations and precision of words.&quot; Students at Years 5–6 are expected to select Tier 2 words (precise, cross-curricular vocabulary such as vivid adjectives) that sharpen meaning and create impact for the reader.</li><li><em>Writing — Vocabulary (Practice)</em> — Selecting precise words: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Vocabulary (Practice): Students are expected to &quot;select and use words and phrases, including subject-specific vocabulary, that are fit for purpose and audience.&quot; This lesson builds the habit of upgrading vague adjectives to precise, purposeful ones — a core Phase 2 composing practice.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Tuesday: Nouns & proper nouns – identification & use":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">NZ Curriculum Link</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Writing — Sentence Structure &amp; Punctuation (Knowledge): sentences are made up of words that belong to different classes; proper nouns name specific people, places, and things and always begin with a capital letter.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>I Do — Spot the Noun</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>A noun is a naming word — it names a person, place, thing, or idea. A proper noun is the specific name of something and always starts with a capital letter. Watch me find and sort them.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Example: The girl visited Wellington with her family.</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&quot;girl&quot; = common noun (a person). &quot;Wellington&quot; = proper noun (specific place — capital W). &quot;family&quot; = common noun (a group). Proper nouns need a capital because they are the actual name.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Tip: Ask: Is this the specific name of someone or somewhere? → proper noun → capital letter!</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>We Do — Sort &amp; Capitalise</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Let's sort these words into common nouns and proper nouns together, and fix any missing capitals.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>my friend aisha lives in christchurch near the avon river.</li><li>the teacher read a book called the lion, the witch and the wardrobe.</li><li>on tuesday we visited the auckland museum with mrs chen.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Discussion prompt: Which words need a capital letter? Which are just common nouns?</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>You Do — Your Turn</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Rewrite each sentence correctly, adding capital letters for all proper nouns.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>last summer, my cousin jake visited queenstown with uncle dan.</li><li>our school, parkside primary, is near the hutt river.</li><li>i am reading a book by roald dahl called matilda.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write two sentences — one with 2 common nouns, one with 2 proper nouns.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Objectives (AOs)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Writing — Sentence Structure (Knowledge)</em> — Word classes: nouns: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Sentence Structure (Knowledge): &quot;Words belong to different classes (e.g. nouns, verbs, adjectives, adverbs, pronouns, prepositions, conjunctions) and understanding word classes helps writers make deliberate choices.&quot; Students learn to identify common and proper nouns as a foundation for accurate and purposeful writing.</li><li><em>Writing — Punctuation (Knowledge)</em> — Capitalisation of proper nouns: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Punctuation (Knowledge): &quot;Punctuation signals meaning to readers, including… capital letters at the start of sentences and for proper nouns.&quot; Students at Years 5–6 are expected to apply capitalisation conventions accurately and consistently in their own writing.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Wednesday: Simple sentence structure: subject + verb + object":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">NZ Curriculum Link</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Writing — Sentence Structure (Knowledge &amp; Practice): a simple sentence contains a subject and a verb; students construct grammatically complete sentences and use a variety of sentence structures for effect.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>I Do — Build a Simple Sentence</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Every sentence needs at least a subject (who/what) and a verb (action/state). We can also add an object (what receives the action). Watch me build one piece at a time.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Example: The cat | chased | the mouse.</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Subject = &quot;The cat&quot; (who is doing it). Verb = &quot;chased&quot; (the action). Object = &quot;the mouse&quot; (what it acted on). Put them together: complete thought, complete sentence!</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Tip: Check: Who is doing it? What are they doing? What are they doing it to?</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>We Do — Label &amp; Build</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Let's label each part (S/V/O) in these sentences, then try building our own on the board.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The goalkeeper saved the penalty.</li><li>A huge wave swallowed the sandcastle.</li><li>My little brother ate the last biscuit.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Discussion prompt: Point to the subject, verb, and object. What would happen if we removed the verb?</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>You Do — Your Turn</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Label each sentence with S (subject), V (verb), O (object). Then write three simple sentences of your own.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The chef prepared a delicious meal.</li><li>The explorer discovered a hidden cave.</li><li>Lightning struck the tall tree.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write an S+V+O sentence about something that happened at school this week.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Objectives (AOs)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Writing — Sentence Structure (Knowledge)</em> — Simple sentences: S + V + O: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Sentence Structure (Knowledge): &quot;A simple sentence contains one main clause with a subject and a verb… writers use a variety of sentence structures, including simple, compound, and complex sentences, for effect and clarity.&quot; This lesson establishes the simple sentence as the core building block students then extend and combine across the year.</li><li><em>Writing — Sentence Structure (Practice)</em> — Constructing grammatically correct sentences: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Sentence Structure (Practice): Students are expected to &quot;write a variety of grammatically correct sentences&quot; and &quot;use knowledge of sentence structure to clarify meaning.&quot; Identifying and labelling subject, verb, and object builds the grammatical awareness needed to write with precision and variety.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Thursday: Proofreading: capitals & full stops":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">NZ Curriculum Link</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Writing — Punctuation &amp; Writing Process (Knowledge &amp; Practice): sentences begin with a capital letter and end with a full stop, exclamation mark, or question mark; students plan and revise writing so that it is accurate and clear.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>I Do — Be the Editor</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Every sentence starts with a capital letter and ends with a full stop (or ! or ?). Watch me read slowly and fix the mistakes in this passage.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Example: the sun rose slowly over the mountains. birds began to sing in the trees it was going to be a perfect day the children raced outside.</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>I read aloud and pause at every sentence end. &quot;the sun&quot; → capital T. After &quot;mountains.&quot; ✓ already has one. After &quot;trees&quot; — no full stop! I add one. &quot;it&quot; → capital I. And so on. I'll mark every fix in red.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Tip: Read out loud — you naturally pause at sentence ends. That pause = full stop.</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>We Do — Fix It Together</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Let's fix this passage together. One student comes up and marks each correction.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>maya loved visiting her grandma on fridays they would bake together grandma always made chocolate biscuits maya's favourite was the ones with icing on top they would eat them while watching the birds in the garden</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Discussion prompt: Where do sentences end? Which words need capitals? How many sentences are in this passage?</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>You Do — Your Turn</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Rewrite this passage correctly, adding all missing capitals and full stops.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>last weekend dad and i went fishing at the lake we caught three fish but we threw them back the water was cold and clear i could see stones on the bottom dad said it was the best fishing spot he knew</li><li>✦ Bonus: Count how many sentences you created. Write your own 3-sentence paragraph with perfect punctuation.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Objectives (AOs)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Writing — Punctuation (Knowledge)</em> — Capitals &amp; full stops: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Punctuation (Knowledge): &quot;Punctuation signals meaning to readers, including capital letters at the start of sentences and for proper nouns, and end-of-sentence punctuation (full stop, exclamation mark, question mark).&quot; These are the foundational conventions students must control accurately before building to more complex punctuation.</li><li><em>Writing — Writing Process (Practice)</em> — Revising for accuracy: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Writing Process (Practice): Students are expected to &quot;reread and revise their writing to check that it makes sense and is accurate, including checking for correct punctuation and capitalisation.&quot; This editing lesson directly builds that revision habit — reading aloud to catch missing punctuation and incorrect capitalisation.</li></ul></div></div>"}}},"Week 2":{"label":"Day","children":{"Monday: Commas in lists – rules & practice":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">NZ Curriculum Link</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Writing — Punctuation (Knowledge): commas are used to separate items in a list; students use a range of punctuation accurately and for effect.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>I Do — Commas Keep Lists Tidy</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>When we list three or more things in a sentence, we separate them with commas. The last item uses &quot;and&quot; (not a comma). Watch me build a list sentence step by step.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Example: In my bag I have a pencil, a ruler, a water bottle and my lunchbox.</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>I list the items: pencil → comma → ruler → comma → water bottle → &quot;and&quot; → lunchbox. No comma before &quot;and&quot; in NZ/UK style. The commas stop the sentence becoming a jumbled mess!</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Tip: Read your list aloud. Every natural pause (except before &quot;and&quot;) = a comma.</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>We Do — Add the Commas</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>These list sentences are missing commas. Let's fix them together.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The market sold fresh tomatoes plump strawberries creamy cheese and warm bread.</li><li>Liam packed his boots his shin pads his water bottle his jersey and his mouthguard.</li><li>The painting used shades of crimson burnt orange golden yellow and deep purple.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Discussion prompt: Where do the pauses fall? Does &quot;and&quot; need a comma before it?</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>You Do — Your Turn</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Add commas to the list sentences, then write two of your own.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>At the beach we collected shells pebbles driftwood and sea glass.</li><li>The recipe called for flour sugar butter eggs and a pinch of salt.</li><li>Her favourite animals were dolphins elephants red pandas and otters.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write a sentence listing 5 things you would bring on a survival adventure.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Objectives (AOs)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Writing — Punctuation (Knowledge)</em> — Commas in lists: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Punctuation (Knowledge): &quot;Commas are used to separate items in a list and to clarify meaning within sentences.&quot; Students at Years 5–6 are expected to know and apply comma conventions accurately, including the NZ/UK convention of no serial comma before &quot;and&quot; in a list.</li><li><em>Writing — Punctuation (Practice)</em> — Applying punctuation accurately: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Punctuation (Practice): Students are expected to &quot;use a range of punctuation accurately and for effect, including commas.&quot; This lesson builds the habit of reading aloud to identify list pauses — a practical revision strategy that reinforces accurate punctuation in students' own writing.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Tuesday: Verbs: action vs. state verbs":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">NZ Curriculum Link</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Writing — Sentence Structure (Knowledge): words belong to different classes; verbs express actions, events, or states and are a core building block of every sentence.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>I Do — Two Kinds of Verbs</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Action verbs show physical or mental movement (run, think, build). State verbs describe a condition or state of being (is, seems, belongs, loves). Watch how I spot the difference.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Example: The hawk soared above the valley. | The valley is very wide.</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&quot;soared&quot; — can I picture the hawk doing this? Yes! Action verb. &quot;is&quot; — is the valley doing anything? Not really — it describes its state. State verb. State verbs often can't be continuous: you wouldn't say &quot;the valley is being wide.&quot;</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Tip: Can you picture someone physically doing it? → Probably action. Does it describe a condition? → State.</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>We Do — Sort Them</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Let's sort these verbs and sentences into action or state, then discuss why.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The students sprinted to the finish line.</li><li>This soup smells incredible.</li><li>She believes in always trying her best.</li><li>The puppy chewed through the shoe.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Discussion prompt: Action or state? Could you say &quot;is ___-ing&quot;? Would that sound strange?</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>You Do — Your Turn</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Label each underlined verb as ACTION or STATE. Then write one sentence for each type.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The river rushes over the boulders in winter.</li><li>My grandmother owns a beautiful garden.</li><li>He remembers every goal he ever scored.</li><li>The children splashed through the puddles.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write a sentence that has BOTH an action verb and a state verb.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Objectives (AOs)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Writing — Sentence Structure (Knowledge)</em> — Word classes: verbs: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Sentence Structure (Knowledge): &quot;Words belong to different classes (e.g. nouns, verbs, adjectives, adverbs…) and understanding word classes helps writers make deliberate choices.&quot; Distinguishing action verbs from state verbs deepens students' understanding of how verbs function, enabling more precise and purposeful verb selection in their own writing.</li><li><em>Writing — Vocabulary (Practice)</em> — Selecting precise verbs: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Vocabulary (Practice): Students are expected to &quot;select and use words and phrases… that are fit for purpose and audience,&quot; including choosing verbs that convey exactly the right meaning. Knowing the difference between action and state verbs helps students avoid weak verb constructions and select words with greater precision.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Wednesday: Expanding simple sentences with adjectives":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">NZ Curriculum Link</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Writing — Sentence Structure (Knowledge &amp; Practice): adjectives describe or modify nouns; students expand simple sentences by adding detail and description to communicate more precisely.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>I Do — Dress Up Your Sentences</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>A simple sentence is fine, but adjectives add colour and detail. Watch how I take a bare sentence and expand it by adding adjectives before nouns.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Example: Before: A dog barked at the stranger. → After: A scruffy, brown dog barked at the hooded stranger.</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>I ask: What kind of dog? → scruffy, brown. What kind of stranger? → hooded. I add them before the nouns. Notice I used a comma between two adjectives describing the same noun (scruffy, brown). But &quot;hooded&quot; is alone so no comma needed.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Tip: Ask &quot;what kind?&quot; or &quot;which one?&quot; before each noun to find adjective spots.</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>We Do — Expand Together</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Let's expand these simple sentences by adding at least one adjective to each noun. Call out your ideas!</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The cat sat on the wall.</li><li>A boat moved through the water.</li><li>The girl opened the door.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Discussion prompt: What adjectives could we add? Where exactly in the sentence do they go?</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>You Do — Your Turn</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Expand each sentence by adding at least two adjectives. You can also add adjectives to the verb (adverbs) as a bonus challenge.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The horse galloped across the field.</li><li>A bird landed on the fence.</li><li>The student opened the book.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write your own expanded sentence about a place you know, using at least 3 adjectives.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Objectives (AOs)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Writing — Sentence Structure (Knowledge)</em> — Adjectives &amp; noun modification: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Sentence Structure (Knowledge): &quot;Adjectives describe or modify nouns and can be used before or after the noun to add detail and precision.&quot; Students also learn that a comma is needed between two or more adjectives describing the same noun, connecting adjective use to punctuation knowledge.</li><li><em>Writing — Sentence Structure (Practice)</em> — Expanding sentences for detail: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Sentence Structure (Practice): Students are expected to &quot;expand simple sentences by adding words, phrases, and clauses to add detail and description.&quot; This lesson builds that core skill of moving from a bare S+V+O sentence to a richer, more detailed construction — a key step in developing writing craft at Years 5–6.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Thursday: Editing: fixing run-on sentences":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">NZ Curriculum Link</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Writing — Writing Process (Practice): students reread and revise writing to ensure sentences are correctly demarcated and ideas are clearly separated.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>I Do — Taming the Run-On</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>A run-on sentence is when two or more complete sentences crash together without proper punctuation. Watch me find and fix one.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Example: The rain hammered the roof the windows rattled and the lights flickered on and off everyone ran inside.</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>I read slowly. &quot;The rain hammered the roof&quot; — full thought. Full stop! &quot;the windows rattled and the lights flickered on and off&quot; — I could keep this as one sentence since &quot;and&quot; joins related ideas. Full stop! &quot;everyone ran inside.&quot; — done. Result: Three clear sentences.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Tip: Say each sentence aloud. If you run out of breath before a pause, it's probably too long.</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>We Do — Fix the Run-Ons</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Let's work through these run-on sentences together. Decide where to split them or what joining word to use.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Sophia loved drawing she spent every afternoon filling sketchbooks with dragons and stars her mum often had to call her in for dinner.</li><li>The match was tied with five minutes left the striker broke free she shot the ball it curved into the top corner the crowd erupted.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Discussion prompt: Where do the natural pauses fall? Should we split with a full stop, or join with &quot;and/but/so&quot;?</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>You Do — Your Turn</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Rewrite each run-on as two or three clear sentences. Choose the best punctuation or joining word.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>My dog loves the park he chases every stick he also tries to swim in the fountain the ranger always laughs.</li><li>The volcano had been quiet for years then one morning smoke rose from the crater by afternoon it was glowing red scientists rushed to the site.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write a paragraph of 3 sentences on any topic — no run-ons allowed!</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Objectives (AOs)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Writing — Punctuation (Knowledge)</em> — Sentence demarcation: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Punctuation (Knowledge): &quot;Sentences are demarcated by capital letters and end punctuation; run-on sentences occur when two or more complete clauses are joined without correct punctuation or an appropriate conjunction.&quot; Understanding sentence boundaries is foundational to all writing accuracy at Phase 2.</li><li><em>Writing — Writing Process (Practice)</em> — Revising run-on sentences: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Writing Process (Practice): Students are expected to &quot;reread and revise their writing to check that it makes sense and is accurate, including checking for correct punctuation.&quot; This lesson trains students to slow down, read aloud, and fix the most common structural error in Years 5–6 writing — the run-on sentence.</li></ul></div></div>"}}},"Week 3":{"label":"Day","children":{"Monday: Synonyms: choosing stronger words":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">NZ Curriculum Link</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Writing — Vocabulary (Knowledge): the meaning of words can be extended or changed by context; students select synonyms deliberately to match the intensity and precision needed for their purpose and audience.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>I Do — Level Up Your Words</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Synonyms are words with similar meanings — but they're not identical. Choosing the right synonym can completely change the feel of your writing. Watch me use a synonym scale.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Example: cold → chilly → freezing → glacial → arctic</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>All mean &quot;cold&quot; — but they get more intense. &quot;The water was cold&quot; is fine. &quot;The water was glacial&quot; paints a sharper picture. I match the word to the intensity of what I'm describing. A slightly cool breeze is &quot;chilly.&quot; A swimming hole in winter is &quot;glacial.&quot;</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Tip: Think: Is this word strong enough for what I'm describing, or do I need to turn up the dial?</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>We Do — Synonym Scales</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Let's build synonym scales for these words together, then choose the best one for each sentence.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>HAPPY → (let's find 4 synonyms, from mild to intense)</li><li>MOVE → (find synonyms that show different kinds of movement)</li><li>&quot;She was happy when she won the prize.&quot; → Which synonym fits best?</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Discussion prompt: Why would you choose &quot;elated&quot; over &quot;pleased&quot; in some sentences but not others?</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>You Do — Your Turn</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Replace the plain word in each sentence with a stronger synonym. Then create your own synonym scale for the word &quot;said.&quot;</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The athlete was tired after the marathon.</li><li>It was a good book — I finished it in one day.</li><li>The crowd was loud when the team scored.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Build a synonym scale for &quot;said&quot; with at least 6 words. Which would you use in a mystery story? A speech? An argument?</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Objectives (AOs)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Writing — Vocabulary (Knowledge)</em> — Synonyms &amp; word intensity: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Vocabulary (Knowledge): &quot;The meaning of words can be extended or changed by their context… vocabulary is chosen deliberately for effect, so students consider the connotations and precision of words.&quot; Understanding synonym scales — that words with similar meanings carry different intensities and connotations — is a key component of building Tier 2 vocabulary at Years 5–6.</li><li><em>Writing — Vocabulary (Practice)</em> — Selecting words for effect: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Vocabulary (Practice): Students are expected to &quot;select and use words and phrases… that are fit for purpose and audience,&quot; and to &quot;consider the effect of word choice on the reader.&quot; This lesson directly builds that skill by teaching students to match word intensity to the meaning they want to convey, rather than defaulting to the first word they think of.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Tuesday: Subject-verb agreement (singular/plural)":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">NZ Curriculum Link</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Writing — Sentence Structure (Knowledge &amp; Practice): a verb must agree in number with the subject of the clause; students write grammatically correct sentences and identify and correct errors in their own writing.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>I Do — Subjects &amp; Verbs Must Match</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The subject and verb in a sentence must agree in number. Singular subject = singular verb. Plural subject = plural verb. Watch how I check the match.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Example: The dog barks. ✓ | The dogs bark. ✓ | The dogs barks. ✗</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>I find the subject first: &quot;the dogs&quot; — plural. Now the verb: &quot;barks&quot; — singular (the -s gives it away). They don't match! Fix: &quot;bark.&quot; Rule: when the subject has an -s (plural), the verb usually doesn't, and vice versa.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Tip: Cross out any words between subject and verb — they can trick you. &quot;The group of students [are/is]...&quot; → subject is &quot;group&quot; (singular) → &quot;is.&quot;</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>We Do — Match the Verb</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Choose the correct verb form for each sentence and explain why.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The bunch of grapes (was / were) left on the bench.</li><li>Her three brothers (play / plays) for the same rugby team.</li><li>Everyone in the class (know / knows) the answer.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Discussion prompt: What is the true subject? Is it singular or plural?</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>You Do — Your Turn</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Circle the correct verb form and rewrite the sentences.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The flock of birds (move / moves) south in winter.</li><li>My best friend and I (love / loves) going to the movies.</li><li>Each of the sandwiches (need / needs) a label.</li><li>The team (was / were) celebrating their win on the field.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write two tricky subject-verb sentences for a partner to solve.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Objectives (AOs)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Writing — Sentence Structure (Knowledge)</em> — Subject-verb agreement: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Sentence Structure (Knowledge): &quot;A verb must agree in number and person with the subject of the clause. Errors in subject-verb agreement disrupt the grammatical accuracy of a sentence.&quot; Students learn to identify the true subject (ignoring intervening phrases) and select the correct verb form — including tricky cases such as collective nouns and indefinite pronouns.</li><li><em>Writing — Writing Process (Practice)</em> — Identifying and correcting errors: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Writing Process (Practice): Students are expected to &quot;reread and revise their writing to check that it makes sense and is accurate.&quot; Recognising and fixing subject-verb agreement errors is a core component of that accuracy check, and a skill students are expected to apply independently in their own writing at Years 5–6.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Wednesday: Compound sentences using 'and', 'but', 'so'":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">NZ Curriculum Link</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Writing — Sentence Structure (Knowledge &amp; Practice): compound sentences join two main clauses using a coordinating conjunction; students use a variety of sentence structures, including compound sentences, for effect and clarity.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>I Do — Join Two Ideas Together</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>A compound sentence joins two simple sentences using a coordinating conjunction: &quot;and&quot; (adds), &quot;but&quot; (contrasts), &quot;so&quot; (shows result). Watch me join two short sentences into one smooth compound sentence.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Example: The bell rang. The students rushed outside. → The bell rang, and the students rushed outside.</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>I put a comma before the conjunction when joining two full sentences. Let me try &quot;but&quot;: &quot;I wanted to go swimming, but the pool was closed.&quot; And &quot;so&quot;: &quot;It was raining heavily, so we moved the game inside.&quot; Each conjunction gives a different meaning!</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Tip: Remember the comma before the conjunction when joining two complete sentences.</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>We Do — Join the Pairs</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Join these pairs of sentences using &quot;and,&quot; &quot;but,&quot; or &quot;so.&quot; Discuss which conjunction fits best and why.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>She studied all weekend. She still felt nervous about the test.</li><li>The campfire burned brightly. Everyone gathered around it.</li><li>Jake forgot his lunch. His friend shared her sandwich with him.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Discussion prompt: Why does &quot;but&quot; work better than &quot;and&quot; in the first one? Where does the comma go?</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>You Do — Your Turn</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Join each pair of sentences with the best conjunction (and/but/so). Remember your comma!</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The explorers were exhausted. They pushed on through the jungle.</li><li>The dog dug a hole under the fence. It escaped into the neighbour's garden.</li><li>She practised every day. She became the best swimmer in her school.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write three compound sentences about your last school holiday — use &quot;and&quot; once, &quot;but&quot; once, and &quot;so&quot; once.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Objectives (AOs)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Writing — Sentence Structure (Knowledge)</em> — Compound sentences &amp; conjunctions: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Sentence Structure (Knowledge): &quot;A compound sentence consists of two or more main clauses joined by a coordinating conjunction (and, but, or, so, yet). A comma is placed before the conjunction when joining two complete clauses.&quot; Students learn that the choice of conjunction shapes the relationship between ideas — addition, contrast, or result.</li><li><em>Writing — Sentence Structure (Practice)</em> — Using sentence variety for effect: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Sentence Structure (Practice): Students are expected to &quot;write a variety of sentence structures — simple, compound, and complex — for effect and clarity.&quot; This lesson introduces compound sentences as the first step beyond the simple sentence, giving students a tool for connecting related ideas smoothly and varying the rhythm of their writing.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Thursday: Editing: identifying vague words & upgrading them":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">NZ Curriculum Link</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Writing — Vocabulary (Practice) &amp; Writing Process (Practice): students select precise words for effect and reread their writing to identify and replace vague or overused language.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>I Do — Hunt the Vague Words</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Vague words are words so overused they've lost their meaning: nice, good, bad, big, small, went, said, things, got. Watch me find them in this passage and replace them with precise language.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Example: It was a nice day and we went to a nice beach. The water was nice and cold and we had a good time.</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>I underline every vague word: nice (×3), went, good. Now I replace: &quot;It was a golden, windless day and we drove to a sparkling beach. The water was refreshingly cold and we had a brilliant time.&quot; Far more vivid!</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Tip: After writing, circle every &quot;nice,&quot; &quot;good,&quot; &quot;went,&quot; &quot;said,&quot; &quot;got&quot; and challenge yourself to upgrade at least half of them.</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>We Do — Upgrade the Passage</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Let's find and replace all the vague words in this passage together.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The big dog got up and went to the door. It made a loud noise and looked at us. We thought it was a good dog but it was a bit scary. Then things got better when its owner came.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Discussion prompt: Which words can we upgrade? What more precise/vivid option works in each spot?</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>You Do — Your Turn</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Rewrite this passage by replacing every underlined vague word with a more precise one.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The nice teacher said that our project was good. We had worked on it for a long time and got everything ready the night before. The big display looked great and all our stuff was neatly arranged.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Pull out your last piece of writing and hunt for three vague words to upgrade.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Objectives (AOs)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Writing — Vocabulary (Practice)</em> — Replacing vague language: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Vocabulary (Practice): Students are expected to &quot;select and use words and phrases… that are fit for purpose and audience,&quot; and to &quot;consider the effect of word choice on the reader.&quot; This lesson targets the most common vocabulary weakness at Years 5–6 — over-reliance on vague, all-purpose words — and builds the habit of deliberate, precise word selection.</li><li><em>Writing — Writing Process (Practice)</em> — Revising for word precision: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Writing Process (Practice): Students are expected to &quot;reread and revise their writing… making changes to vocabulary, punctuation, and structure to improve clarity and impact.&quot; This editing lesson directly practises that revision process: identifying weak language and replacing it with words that create a clearer, more vivid picture for the reader.</li></ul></div></div>"}}},"Week 4":{"label":"Day","children":{"Monday: Apostrophes for possession":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">NZ Curriculum Link</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Writing — Punctuation (Knowledge): apostrophes are used to show possession; the placement of the apostrophe changes depending on whether the owner is singular or plural.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>I Do — Who Does It Belong To?</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>An apostrophe + s ('s) shows that something belongs to someone. If the owner is singular, add 's. If the owner is plural and already ends in s, just add the apostrophe after: s'.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Example: Singular: the cat's collar | Plural: the cats' collars</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>One cat owns the collar → cat's (apostrophe before s). Multiple cats own collars → cats' (apostrophe after s). The trick: find the owner first. 'The collar belonging to the cat' = cat → cat's. 'The collars belonging to the cats' = cats → cats'.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Tip: Find the owner. If singular → add 's. If plural ending in s → add just '.</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>We Do — Find the Owner</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Let's rewrite these 'belonging' phrases using apostrophes, and sort them into singular vs. plural owner.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The jersey belonging to the player → ___</li><li>The homework belonging to the students → ___</li><li>The beak belonging to the parrot → ___</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Discussion prompt: Who is the owner? How many owners are there? Where does the apostrophe go?</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>You Do — Your Turn</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Rewrite each phrase using an apostrophe to show possession.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The den belonging to the fox → ___</li><li>The shoes belonging to the dancers → ___</li><li>The medal belonging to the swimmer → ___</li><li>The nests belonging to the birds → ___</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write four sentences about things you can see in the classroom using possessive apostrophes.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Objectives (AOs)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Writing — Punctuation (Knowledge)</em> — Apostrophes for possession: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Punctuation (Knowledge): &quot;Apostrophes are used to show possession — singular owners take 's (e.g. the cat's collar) and plural owners already ending in s take an apostrophe after the s (e.g. the cats' collars).&quot; Students at Years 5–6 are expected to use possessive apostrophes correctly and consistently in their own writing.</li><li><em>Writing — Punctuation (Practice)</em> — Applying apostrophes accurately: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Punctuation (Practice): Students are expected to &quot;use a range of punctuation accurately and for effect,&quot; including apostrophes for possession. This lesson builds a reliable two-step strategy — identify the owner, then count them — that students can apply independently when editing their own writing.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Tuesday: Pronouns: personal & possessive":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">NZ Curriculum Link</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Writing — Sentence Structure (Knowledge): pronouns are a word class that replace nouns to avoid repetition; students use personal and possessive pronouns accurately to create cohesion and clarity.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>I Do — Standing In for Nouns</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Pronouns replace nouns so we don't repeat the same word over and over. Personal pronouns (I, you, he, she, it, we, they) and possessive pronouns (my, your, his, her, its, our, their) are the most common. Watch me substitute them in.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Example: Maya left Maya's bag on Maya's seat when Maya went outside.</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This sounds clunky! Replace: &quot;Maya left her bag on her seat when she went outside.&quot; The pronoun &quot;her&quot; replaces &quot;Maya's&quot; (possessive). &quot;she&quot; replaces &quot;Maya&quot; (personal). Much smoother!</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Tip: Make sure your pronoun clearly refers back to the right noun — if there are two possible people, the reader gets confused.</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>We Do — Swap the Repeated Nouns</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Replace the repeated nouns with the correct pronouns.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The team trained hard. The team wanted the team's season to be the team's best ever.</li><li>Tom and Sarah arrived early. Tom and Sarah set up Tom and Sarah's stall at the market.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Discussion prompt: What pronoun replaces &quot;the team&quot;? What about &quot;Tom and Sarah&quot;? Which is possessive?</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>You Do — Your Turn</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Rewrite each sentence replacing the repeated nouns with correct personal or possessive pronouns.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The explorer found the explorer's compass in the explorer's coat pocket.</li><li>The dolphins leaped out of the water. The dolphins seemed to enjoy the dolphins' freedom.</li><li>Lily and Ben packed Lily and Ben's bags. Lily and Ben left early for Lily and Ben's trip.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write a paragraph where you use at least 4 different pronouns correctly.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Objectives (AOs)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Writing — Sentence Structure (Knowledge)</em> — Word classes: pronouns: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Sentence Structure (Knowledge): &quot;Words belong to different classes… pronouns are words that replace nouns and must agree with the nouns they refer to in number and person.&quot; Students learn to distinguish personal pronouns (replacing subjects and objects) from possessive pronouns (showing ownership) — two distinct functions that are frequently confused at Years 5–6.</li><li><em>Writing — Text Structure (Practice)</em> — Cohesion through pronoun use: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Text Structure (Practice): Students are expected to &quot;use cohesive devices — including pronouns, synonyms, and connectives — to link ideas within and across sentences and paragraphs.&quot; Using pronouns correctly is a primary cohesion strategy: it removes repetition and helps the writing flow smoothly from one sentence to the next.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Wednesday: Writing topic sentences for paragraphs":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">NZ Curriculum Link</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Writing — Text Structure (Knowledge &amp; Practice): paragraphs are organised around a main idea expressed in a topic sentence; students structure their writing with clear paragraph organisation.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>I Do — The Anchor Sentence</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>A topic sentence is the first sentence of a paragraph. It tells the reader the main idea. All the other sentences in the paragraph support or explain it. Watch me write one.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Example: Topic: Why exercise is important → Topic sentence: &quot;Exercise is one of the most important habits a person can build for a healthy life.&quot;</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>My topic sentence: (1) introduces the topic, (2) gives the writer's angle on it (not just &quot;exercise is good&quot; — too vague). The sentences that follow will each give a reason or example to support &quot;one of the most important habits.&quot; I won't switch to a new topic mid-paragraph.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Tip: Test your topic sentence: could every other sentence in your paragraph be &quot;evidence&quot; for it? If yes — it works!</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>We Do — Match &amp; Write</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Match each topic sentence to the paragraph that fits it best. Then write a topic sentence for an unpaired paragraph.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Topic sentence A: &quot;Dogs make loyal and loving companions for all ages.&quot;</li><li>Topic sentence B: &quot;The rainforest is home to an astonishing variety of wildlife.&quot;</li><li>Paragraph without a topic sentence: &quot;...Thousands of species of insects live under its canopy. Brightly coloured frogs cling to leaves, and jaguars prowl silently below. Even the trees themselves host miniature ecosystems...&quot;</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Discussion prompt: Which topic sentence fits the mystery paragraph? What clues told you?</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>You Do — Your Turn</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write a strong topic sentence for each group of supporting sentences.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Supporting sentences: &quot;First, it keeps your heart strong. It also helps you sleep better and boosts your mood. Even a short walk each day makes a difference.&quot;</li><li>Supporting sentences: &quot;Rubbish can entangle sea turtles and birds. Plastic bags are mistaken for jellyfish by whales. Tiny particles enter the food chain through fish.&quot;</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write a full paragraph of 4 sentences: topic sentence + 3 supports, on a topic of your choice.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Objectives (AOs)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Writing — Text Structure (Knowledge)</em> — Paragraph organisation &amp; topic sentences: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Text Structure (Knowledge): &quot;Paragraphs group related ideas together, usually organised around a main idea introduced in a topic sentence. Supporting sentences develop, explain, or provide evidence for the main idea.&quot; Students at Years 5–6 are expected to understand that each paragraph has a clear focus and that the topic sentence signals this focus to the reader.</li><li><em>Writing — Text Structure (Practice)</em> — Structuring paragraphs effectively: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Text Structure (Practice): Students are expected to &quot;organise ideas into paragraphs with a clear main idea, supported by relevant detail.&quot; This lesson builds the habit of writing a controlling topic sentence before adding supporting detail — a key structural skill for all text types at Years 5–6, from narrative to argument.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Thursday: Editing: checking pronoun-antecedent agreement":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">NZ Curriculum Link</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Writing — Sentence Structure (Practice) &amp; Writing Process (Practice): pronouns must agree with their antecedents in number; students identify and correct pronoun errors when revising their own writing.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>I Do — Does the Pronoun Match?</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>A pronoun must agree with its antecedent (the noun it replaces) in number and gender. Singular noun → singular pronoun. Plural noun → plural pronoun. Watch me find and fix the mismatches.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Example: The team picked up their trophy. ✗ → The team picked up its trophy. ✓</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&quot;The team&quot; is singular (one team) so the pronoun should be &quot;its&quot; not &quot;their.&quot; Now: &quot;Each student brought their lunch.&quot; Here &quot;each student&quot; is singular, so technically &quot;his or her lunch&quot; — but in modern usage &quot;their&quot; is widely accepted for singular subjects. Context matters!</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Tip: Find the noun the pronoun replaces. Count it: one → singular pronoun, many → plural.</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>We Do — Spot &amp; Fix</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Find and fix the pronoun agreement errors in these sentences.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Every player gave their best, but the team lost their match in the final minute.</li><li>The flock of geese spread their wings and took off across the lake.</li><li>Anyone who finishes early can bring their book to reading corner.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Discussion prompt: What is the antecedent? Is it singular or plural? Does the pronoun match?</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>You Do — Your Turn</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Fix any pronoun agreement errors. Write &quot;correct&quot; if there is no error.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The committee made their decision before the deadline.</li><li>Each of the birds had lost their feathers in the storm.</li><li>Neither of the girls wanted to give up their spot in the team.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write a paragraph about a sports team. Use at least 3 different pronouns correctly — and then swap with a partner to check.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Objectives (AOs)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Writing — Sentence Structure (Knowledge)</em> — Pronoun-antecedent agreement: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Sentence Structure (Knowledge): &quot;A pronoun must agree with its antecedent — the noun it replaces — in number (singular or plural) and person. Errors in pronoun agreement can confuse the reader about who or what is being referred to.&quot; Students also encounter the modern acceptance of singular 'they' and learn to navigate these nuances in context.</li><li><em>Writing — Writing Process (Practice)</em> — Editing for pronoun accuracy: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Writing Process (Practice): Students are expected to &quot;reread and revise their writing to check that it makes sense and is accurate.&quot; This editing lesson trains students to check every pronoun against its antecedent — a targeted revision strategy that improves grammatical accuracy and reader clarity in their own writing.</li></ul></div></div>"}}},"Week 5":{"label":"Day","children":{"Monday: Exclamation marks & question marks – when & why":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">NZ Curriculum Link</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Writing — Punctuation (Knowledge): sentences end with a full stop, question mark, or exclamation mark; the choice of end punctuation signals the purpose and tone of the sentence to the reader.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>I Do — The Right Ending</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Every sentence ends with a full stop, question mark, or exclamation mark. The wrong choice changes the reader's experience completely. Watch me demonstrate why the ending matters.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Example: &quot;Come back.&quot; vs &quot;Come back!&quot; vs &quot;Will you come back?&quot;</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&quot;Come back.&quot; — a calm instruction. &quot;Come back!&quot; — urgent, emotional, even desperate. &quot;Will you come back?&quot; — a genuine question, uncertain. The same two words — completely different meanings from punctuation alone. Exclamation marks are powerful, so use them sparingly or they lose impact.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Tip: Ask yourself: Am I expressing strong emotion or urgency? → ! | Am I asking a genuine question? → ? | Everything else → .</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>We Do — Choose the Right Mark</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Add the correct end punctuation and explain your choice.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>How did the magician make the coin disappear</li><li>Watch out for the wave behind you</li><li>The library opens at nine o'clock every morning</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Discussion prompt: What emotion or purpose is in each sentence? Would a different mark change the meaning?</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>You Do — Your Turn</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Add the correct end punctuation to each sentence and write a short explanation of your choice.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Did you hear that noise in the middle of the night</li><li>Never open that door again</li><li>The museum was closed on the day we planned to visit</li><li>I can't believe we actually won</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write one sentence where changing the end mark completely changes the meaning. Share with a partner.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Objectives (AOs)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Writing — Punctuation (Knowledge)</em> — End punctuation: . ? !: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Punctuation (Knowledge): &quot;Sentences end with a full stop, question mark, or exclamation mark. The choice of end punctuation signals the sentence's purpose to the reader: statements use full stops, questions use question marks, and exclamations express strong emotion or urgency.&quot; Students learn that punctuation is a meaning-making tool, not just a convention to follow.</li><li><em>Writing — Punctuation (Practice)</em> — Punctuation for effect: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Punctuation (Practice): Students are expected to &quot;use a range of punctuation accurately and for effect.&quot; This includes using exclamation marks sparingly for genuine impact, and understanding that overuse weakens their effect. Deliberate punctuation choices contribute to the tone, pace, and mood of a piece of writing.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Tuesday: Adjectives: degrees of comparison (er/est/more/most)":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">NZ Curriculum Link</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Writing — Sentence Structure (Knowledge): adjectives can be modified to express degrees of comparison using comparative (-er/more) and superlative (-est/most) forms, including irregular forms.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>I Do — Comparing with Adjectives</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>We use comparative adjectives to compare two things, and superlatives to compare three or more. Short adjectives add -er/-est. Longer adjectives use more/most. Watch me form them.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Example: fast → faster (comparative) → fastest (superlative)</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>beautiful → more beautiful → most beautiful</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Rule: 1–2 syllable adjectives → -er/-est: tall/taller/tallest. 3+ syllable adjectives → more/most: exciting/more exciting/most exciting. Exceptions: good/better/best, bad/worse/worst, far/further/furthest.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Tip: Try adding -er: if it sounds natural, use it. If it sounds awkward (intelligenter?), use &quot;more.&quot;</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>We Do — Form the Comparatives</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Form the comparative and superlative of each adjective and use each in a sentence.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>cold → <em><strong> → </strong></em></li><li>dangerous → <em><strong> → </strong></em></li><li>good → <em><strong> → </strong></em></li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Discussion prompt: Which rule applies? Are there any exceptions here? Can you use each in an example sentence?</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>You Do — Your Turn</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Complete the comparison chart and then write sentences using the correct form.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>happy → <em><strong> → </strong></em>  |  Use &quot;happiest&quot; in a sentence.</li><li>mysterious → <em><strong> → </strong></em>  |  Use &quot;more mysterious&quot; in a sentence.</li><li>bad → <em><strong> → </strong></em>  |  Use &quot;worst&quot; in a sentence.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write a paragraph comparing two animals you know, using at least four comparative or superlative adjectives.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Objectives (AOs)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Writing — Sentence Structure (Knowledge)</em> — Comparative &amp; superlative adjectives: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Sentence Structure (Knowledge): &quot;Adjectives can be modified to express degrees of comparison. Comparative forms (using -er or 'more') compare two things; superlative forms (using -est or 'most') compare three or more. Some adjectives have irregular comparative and superlative forms (good/better/best).&quot; Students learn and apply the spelling and usage rules for both regular and irregular forms.</li><li><em>Writing — Vocabulary (Practice)</em> — Precise comparative language: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Vocabulary (Practice): Students are expected to &quot;select and use words and phrases… that are fit for purpose and audience.&quot; Using comparative and superlative adjectives precisely — rather than defaulting to vague modifiers like 'very good' — is a key marker of more sophisticated vocabulary use at Years 5–6.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Wednesday: Sentence variety: short vs. long sentences":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">NZ Curriculum Link</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Writing — Sentence Structure (Practice): students use a variety of sentence lengths deliberately for effect, understanding that mixing short and long sentences creates rhythm and controls the pace of writing.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>I Do — Playing with Rhythm</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Good writers vary their sentence length on purpose. Short sentences create tension and punch. Long sentences slow the pace and build atmosphere. Watch how I mix them for effect.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Example: Long only: &quot;As the fog rolled silently in off the sea and crept through the narrow streets, wrapping itself around the lampposts and filling every doorway with a pale grey shadow, she stood completely still and listened to the sound of the city slowly disappearing.&quot; → Too slow.</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Now watch: &quot;The fog rolled in. It crept through the streets, wrapping itself around lampposts, filling every doorway. She stood still. Listened. The city was disappearing.&quot; The short sentences (She stood still. Listened.) hit hard. The long one builds atmosphere. Mixing them creates rhythm.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Tip: Read your writing aloud. Does it have a beat — fast/slow/fast? If everything is the same length, vary it.</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>We Do — Mix It Up</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This passage has all long sentences. Let's break some up to create rhythm together.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The ancient lighthouse had stood on the cliff for over two hundred years and had guided thousands of ships safely past the rocks and through the treacherous harbour entrance in every kind of weather imaginable. It had survived storms and earthquakes and the battering of winter waves and still it stood, its light still turning, still warning, still saving lives that would otherwise have been lost to the sea.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Discussion prompt: Where could we cut for impact? Which short sentence would create the most drama?</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>You Do — Your Turn</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Rewrite this all-short paragraph by combining some sentences into longer ones, while keeping 1–2 short ones for effect.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The fire started. It spread fast. Smoke filled the hall. People ran. They called for help. The engines arrived. The firefighters moved quickly. They brought it under control.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write 6 sentences about a storm — 2 short, 2 medium, 2 long. Read them aloud to check the rhythm.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Objectives (AOs)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Writing — Sentence Structure (Practice)</em> — Sentence length for effect: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Sentence Structure (Practice): Students are expected to &quot;write a variety of sentence structures for effect and clarity,&quot; including deliberately mixing sentence lengths. Short sentences create urgency and emphasis; long sentences build atmosphere and detail. This lesson develops students' awareness that sentence length is a craft choice, not an accident.</li><li><em>Writing — Composing (Practice)</em> — Controlling pace &amp; rhythm: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Composing (Practice): Students are expected to &quot;use composing strategies to create texts that engage the reader,&quot; including controlling pace and rhythm. Varying sentence length is one of the most accessible ways to create a sense of drama, tension, or calm — and this lesson gives students a practical tool to apply immediately in their own writing.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Thursday: Editing: peer-checking capitals & punctuation":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">NZ Curriculum Link</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Writing — Writing Process (Practice): students use an editing checklist to review writing for punctuation accuracy; peer editing builds the habit of reading writing critically and giving specific feedback.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>I Do — Using an Editing Checklist</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Professional editors use checklists so they don't miss anything. Watch me use our class punctuation checklist on a short passage — I'll mark each issue as I find it.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Example: checklist: ☐ Capital at start of every sentence ☐ Full stop/!/? at end ☐ Commas in lists ☐ Proper nouns capitalised ☐ Apostrophes correct</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>I read the passage once just to understand it. Then I go through with the checklist — one check at a time. I mark issues with a light pencil. I explain why each fix is needed, not just what to change.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Tip: Never try to check everything at once — work through one rule at a time on separate read-throughs.</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>We Do — Peer Edit in Pairs</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>With your partner, use the checklist to edit this passage. One person checks capitals; the other checks full stops and commas.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>last thursday, mr. tane's class visited te papa museum in wellington. the students saw a giant squid a huge whale skeleton and many taonga from around new zealand. aisha's favourite exhibit was the earthquake house she said &quot;it felt so real.&quot; everyone agreed it was an amazing trip</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Discussion prompt: What did you each find? Did you agree on every correction? Compare notes.</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>You Do — Your Turn</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Edit this passage alone using the full checklist. Mark and rewrite all corrections.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>on saturday morning, emma and her brother luca decided to bake a chocolate cake. they found flour eggs butter sugar and cocoa in the cupboard. the recipe was easy but luca forgot to add the baking powder. the cake came out flat and dense but mum said it still tasted delicious. emma said &quot;next time i'm doing it on my own&quot;</li><li>✦ Bonus: Swap with a partner and compare corrections. Discuss any differences.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Objectives (AOs)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Writing — Writing Process (Practice)</em> — Editing with a checklist: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Writing Process (Practice): Students are expected to &quot;reread and revise their writing to check that it makes sense and is accurate, including checking for correct punctuation and capitalisation.&quot; This lesson builds the systematic editing habit — one pass per rule — that prevents students from trying to fix everything at once and missing errors as a result.</li><li><em>Writing — Writing Process (Practice)</em> — Peer editing &amp; feedback: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Writing Process (Practice): Students are expected to &quot;share writing with others and respond to feedback to improve their work.&quot; Peer editing is an explicit Phase 2 expectation: students practise giving specific, rule-based feedback (not just &quot;it's good&quot;) and comparing their corrections to develop shared understanding of conventions.</li></ul></div></div>"}}},"Week 6":{"label":"Day","children":{"Monday: Word families – roots, prefixes, suffixes":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">NZ Curriculum Link</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Writing — Vocabulary (Knowledge): many English words are built from roots, prefixes, and suffixes; understanding word parts unlocks the meaning of unfamiliar words and expands vocabulary.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>I Do — Words Are Built from Parts</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Most English words are built from parts: a root (the core meaning), a prefix (added to the front), and/or a suffix (added to the end). Knowing the parts unlocks the meaning of unknown words. Watch me break one apart.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Example: unhappiness → un- (not) + happy (root) + -ness (state of being) = &quot;the state of not being happy&quot;</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Let me try &quot;prehistoric&quot;: pre- (before) + historic (relating to history) = &quot;before recorded history.&quot; Now &quot;transportation&quot;: trans- (across) + port (carry) + -ation (process) = &quot;the process of carrying things across.&quot; Once you know common roots, you can work out hundreds of words!</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Tip: Common roots to remember: port (carry), rupt (break), vis (see), struct (build), dict (say), graph (write).</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>We Do — Break Them Down</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Let's pull apart these words using our knowledge of prefixes, roots, and suffixes. What does each part tell us?</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>impossible → <em><strong> + </strong></em> = meaning?</li><li>prediction → <em><strong> + </strong></em> + ___ = meaning?</li><li>exportable → <em><strong> + </strong></em> + ___ = meaning?</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Discussion prompt: How does knowing the prefix &quot;im-&quot; help you with impossible, impolite, impractical?</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>You Do — Your Turn</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Break apart each word into its prefix/root/suffix. Write the meaning of each part, then the whole word.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>invisible → </li><li>reconstruct → </li><li>disagreement → </li><li>photography → </li><li>✦ Bonus: Use your knowledge of &quot;port&quot; (carry) to work out: import, export, portable, transport, reporter.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Objectives (AOs)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Writing — Vocabulary (Knowledge)</em> — Word roots, prefixes &amp; suffixes: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Vocabulary (Knowledge): &quot;Many English words are built from Latin and Greek roots, prefixes, and suffixes. Understanding the meaning of word parts enables students to work out the meaning of unfamiliar words and to build their vocabulary systematically.&quot; This lesson develops morphological awareness — a key component of Tier 2 and Tier 3 vocabulary development at Years 5–6.</li><li><em>Writing — Vocabulary (Practice)</em> — Using word knowledge in writing: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Vocabulary (Practice): Students are expected to &quot;use knowledge of word origins, roots, and word families to extend vocabulary.&quot; Understanding that &quot;port&quot; means carry (import, export, transport) gives students a transferable tool for both reading unfamiliar words and selecting more precise vocabulary in their own writing.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Tuesday: Adverbs: how, when, where":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">NZ Curriculum Link</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Writing — Sentence Structure (Knowledge): adverbs modify verbs, adjectives, or other adverbs and express manner, time, and place; students use adverbs deliberately to add precision and detail.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>I Do — Adverbs Tell Us More</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Adverbs modify (add information to) verbs, adjectives, or other adverbs. They answer How? When? Where? and To what extent? Watch me add adverbs to upgrade a simple sentence.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Example: The explorer walked. → The explorer walked cautiously (how) forward (where) yesterday (when).</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>I add them one at a time and notice how each one changes the meaning. How? → cautiously (tells us the manner). Where? → forward (tells us direction). When? → yesterday (tells us time). I can layer adverbs, but I should be selective — too many and the sentence becomes clunky.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Tip: Adverbs of manner often end in -ly (quickly, silently) — but not always (fast, hard, late).</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>We Do — Add the Right Adverb</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Add an adverb (how/when/where) to each sentence and label what type it is.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The hawk circled ___.</li><li>___ the team gathered for their final practice.</li><li>She whispered ___ so no one would hear.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Discussion prompt: What question does your adverb answer? Could you add a second adverb of a different type?</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>You Do — Your Turn</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Add one adverb of each type (how/when/where) to the sentence. Then write your own sentence using all three types.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The scientist worked <em><strong> (how) </strong></em> (when) ___ (where) to find an answer.</li><li>Add how, when, where: &quot;The wolves hunted.&quot;</li><li>✦ Bonus: Find three adverbs in a book you're reading. Write which verb each modifies and what question it answers.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Objectives (AOs)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Writing — Sentence Structure (Knowledge)</em> — Word classes: adverbs: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Sentence Structure (Knowledge): &quot;Adverbs modify verbs, adjectives, or other adverbs. They can express manner (how), time (when), place (where), and degree (to what extent). Adverbs of manner often end in -ly but not always.&quot; Students learn to identify adverb types and understand their grammatical role as modifiers within the sentence.</li><li><em>Writing — Sentence Structure (Practice)</em> — Adding precision with adverbs: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Sentence Structure (Practice): Students are expected to &quot;expand sentences by adding adverbs and adverbial phrases to express manner, time, and place.&quot; This lesson builds the habit of asking 'how? when? where?' when editing bare sentences — adding only the adverbial detail that genuinely improves precision, not padding.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Wednesday: Adding detail with 'who', 'where', 'when' phrases":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">NZ Curriculum Link</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Writing — Sentence Structure (Practice): students expand sentences by adding noun phrases, prepositional phrases, and relative clauses that answer who, where, and when to create a fuller picture for the reader.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>I Do — Dress the Sentence Up</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>A bare sentence tells us the basics. Adding WHO/WHERE/WHEN phrases gives the reader a full picture. Watch me build one sentence three ways.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Example: Base: The girl read a book. → + WHERE: The girl read a book in the shade of an old oak tree. → + WHO: The girl, who had borrowed it from the library that morning, read a book in the shade. → + WHEN: Just as the sun peaked at noon, the girl, who had borrowed it that morning, read a book in the shade.</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Each phrase layers in more context. The reader can now picture the scene, the person, and the moment. I don't have to add all three — I choose the ones that matter most for the story.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Tip: Ask: does my reader know enough to picture this? If not, add one specific WHERE, WHO, or WHEN phrase.</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>We Do — Layer the Detail</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Let's expand these sentences together by adding a who, where, and when phrase.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The dog barked.</li><li>A boat drifted.</li><li>The children cheered.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Discussion prompt: Which detail matters most for each sentence? Does every detail need to be there, or can we cut one?</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>You Do — Your Turn</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Expand each sentence by adding at least two detail phrases (who/where/when).</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The door slammed.</li><li>A light flickered.</li><li>The crowd fell silent.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write an expanded sentence about a character arriving somewhere unexpected. Include who they are, where exactly they arrived, and when.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Objectives (AOs)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Writing — Sentence Structure (Practice)</em> — Expanding sentences with detail phrases: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Sentence Structure (Practice): Students are expected to &quot;expand simple sentences by adding words, phrases, and clauses to add detail and description.&quot; Adding who, where, and when phrases is a concrete, scaffolded way to develop this skill — students practise choosing which type of detail best serves each sentence rather than adding detail randomly.</li><li><em>Writing — Composing (Practice)</em> — Creating vivid, specific writing: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Composing (Practice): Students are expected to &quot;use composing strategies to create texts that engage the reader,&quot; including providing specific, concrete detail. This lesson directly addresses the vagueness of bare sentences by giving students a systematic question framework (who? where? when?) to identify and fill gaps in their descriptions.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Thursday: Editing: tightening wordy sentences":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">NZ Curriculum Link</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Writing — Writing Process (Practice) &amp; Vocabulary (Practice): students revise writing to remove redundant words and replace wordy constructions with precise, concise language.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>I Do — Cut the Fluff</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Good writing is precise. Wordy sentences say in ten words what could be said in five. Watch me find and cut redundant words without losing meaning.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Example: &quot;Due to the fact that it was raining, we decided to stay inside the house.&quot; → &quot;Because it was raining, we stayed inside.&quot;</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&quot;Due to the fact that&quot; = just &quot;because.&quot; &quot;decided to stay&quot; = &quot;stayed.&quot; &quot;inside the house&quot; = &quot;inside&quot; (where else would you stay?). I cut 13 words to 7 — same meaning, cleaner punch. Phrases to watch: &quot;in order to&quot; (→ &quot;to&quot;), &quot;at this point in time&quot; (→ &quot;now&quot;), &quot;on a daily basis&quot; (→ &quot;daily&quot;).</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Tip: After writing a sentence, ask: can I cut any word without losing meaning? If yes — cut it.</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>We Do — Tighten Together</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Rewrite these wordy sentences concisely. Count the words before and after.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The reason why the match was cancelled was due to the fact that the field was too wet.</li><li>At this present moment in time, the students are in the process of completing their assignment.</li><li>She made the decision to walk in the direction of the library in order to return the book.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Discussion prompt: Which words are doing real work? Which are filler? What is the minimum word count that keeps full meaning?</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>You Do — Your Turn</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Rewrite each sentence as concisely as possible. Record the word count before and after.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>In the event that the weather is bad, we will need to make the decision to move the event inside. (_<strong>→</strong>_words)</li><li>He was a person who always showed a great deal of enthusiasm for all the different things that he was interested in. (_<strong>→</strong>_words)</li><li>✦ Bonus: Take a paragraph you've written recently and cut it by 20% — same meaning, fewer words.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Objectives (AOs)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Writing — Writing Process (Practice)</em> — Revising for conciseness: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Writing Process (Practice): Students are expected to &quot;reread and revise their writing… making changes to vocabulary, punctuation, and structure to improve clarity and impact.&quot; This lesson targets a specific and common revision skill: identifying and cutting wordy constructions that obscure meaning, teaching students that fewer, well-chosen words are almost always stronger than more.</li><li><em>Writing — Vocabulary (Practice)</em> — Precise word choice over circumlocution: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Vocabulary (Practice): Students are expected to &quot;select and use words and phrases… that are fit for purpose and audience.&quot; Replacing wordy phrases (&quot;made the decision to&quot;) with single precise verbs (&quot;decided&quot;) is a key vocabulary skill at Years 5–6 — it shows students understand that one exact word is more powerful than a cluster of vague ones.</li></ul></div></div>"}}},"Week 7":{"label":"Day","children":{"Monday: Colons & semicolons – introduction":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">NZ Curriculum Link</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Writing — Punctuation (Knowledge): a colon introduces a list or explanation; a semicolon joins two closely related independent clauses; both are used deliberately for clarity and effect.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>I Do — Two Powerful Marks</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>A colon (:) introduces something — a list, an explanation, or a dramatic reveal. A semicolon (;) joins two closely related complete sentences without a conjunction. Watch me use both.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Example: Colon: &quot;She needed three things: courage, patience, and luck.&quot;</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Semicolon: &quot;The sun set behind the hills; the valley grew cold.&quot;</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Colon: I use it after a complete sentence when what follows explains or lists. &quot;She had one goal: to finish the race.&quot; Semicolon: Both sides must be complete sentences that are closely linked in meaning. &quot;I love summer; the long evenings are my favourite part.&quot;</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Tip: Test the colon: could you put &quot;namely&quot; or &quot;that is&quot; there? Test the semicolon: are both halves complete sentences that relate to each other?</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>We Do — Colon or Semicolon?</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Decide whether each gap needs a colon or semicolon and explain why.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The storm brought everything with it ___ rain, hail, thunder, and a biting wind.</li><li>She loved the city ___ she had lived there all her life.</li><li>He only had one request ___ please don't wake him before 8 o'clock.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Discussion prompt: Does what follows LIST or EXPLAIN? Or are you joining two complete ideas? That tells you which to use.</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>You Do — Your Turn</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Add a colon or semicolon to each sentence and write a brief reason for your choice.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The menu offered four choices pizza, pasta, salad, and soup.</li><li>The hike was long and exhausting the view from the summit was worth every step.</li><li>She packed everything she would need a torch, a map, a compass, and enough food for two days.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write two of your own sentences — one using a colon correctly, one using a semicolon correctly.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Objectives (AOs)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Writing — Punctuation (Knowledge)</em> — Colons &amp; semicolons: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Punctuation (Knowledge): &quot;A colon is used after a complete clause to introduce a list, explanation, or quotation. A semicolon joins two independent clauses that are closely related in meaning, without using a conjunction.&quot; Students at Years 5–6 are introduced to these higher-level punctuation marks as tools that add clarity and sophistication to their writing.</li><li><em>Writing — Punctuation (Practice)</em> — Using colons &amp; semicolons for effect: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Punctuation (Practice): Students are expected to &quot;use a range of punctuation accurately and for effect,&quot; progressing beyond basic sentence-end punctuation to more complex marks. This lesson teaches the decision-making process behind each mark: 'Am I introducing something, or joining two related ideas?' — building usage that is intentional rather than guessed.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Tuesday: Tense consistency: simple past & present":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">NZ Curriculum Link</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Writing — Sentence Structure (Knowledge) &amp; Writing Process (Practice): verbs must remain consistent in tense throughout a piece of writing; students identify and correct tense inconsistencies when revising.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>I Do — Pick a Tense and Stay There</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>When you write a story or recount, you choose a tense — usually past or present — and stay in it the whole way through. Switching tenses mid-passage confuses the reader. Watch me spot and fix a tense switch.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Example: Past: &quot;The knight rode into the village. He looks around nervously. The villagers gathered in the square.&quot;</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&quot;rode&quot; — past. &quot;looks&quot; — suddenly present! &quot;gathered&quot; — back to past. The switch makes the reader stumble. Fix: &quot;The knight rode into the village. He looked around nervously. The villagers gathered in the square.&quot; — all past. Or I could rewrite all in present: &quot;The knight rides... He looks... The villagers gather...&quot; — both are valid, just choose one.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Tip: Circle all your verbs. Are they all the same tense? If not, decide which tense your piece should be in and adjust.</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>We Do — Spot the Switches</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Find and fix all tense inconsistencies in this passage. Rewrite it in consistent simple past.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The young inventor enters her workshop at dawn. She had spent months designing the machine. She checks each part carefully and placed the final gear. The machine starts to hum. It worked.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Discussion prompt: Which verbs are in the wrong tense? Rewrite in consistent past tense.</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>You Do — Your Turn</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Rewrite the passage in consistent simple present tense. Then identify and fix the errors.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Rewrite in present tense: &quot;The geologist climbed the rocky slope. She examined the layers of sediment. Each layer told a story about the earth's past. She makes careful notes and photographs each formation.&quot;</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write an 8-sentence recount (any topic) in consistent simple past tense. Swap with a partner to check.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Objectives (AOs)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Writing — Sentence Structure (Knowledge)</em> — Tense consistency: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Sentence Structure (Knowledge): &quot;Verbs must remain consistent in tense throughout a piece of writing. Shifts between past and present tense disrupt the reader's experience and signal a lack of control over the writing. Students choose a tense appropriate to text type and purpose and maintain it throughout.&quot; Simple past is standard for recounts and narratives; simple present for procedures and some descriptions.</li><li><em>Writing — Writing Process (Practice)</em> — Auditing tense in revision: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Writing Process (Practice): Students are expected to &quot;reread and revise their writing to check that it makes sense and is accurate.&quot; Doing a tense audit — underlining every verb and labelling it P (past) or Pr (present) — is a targeted revision strategy that catches the most common grammar error in Year 5–6 narrative and recount writing.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Wednesday: Complex sentences: main + subordinate clause":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">NZ Curriculum Link</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Writing — Sentence Structure (Knowledge &amp; Practice): a complex sentence contains a main clause and a subordinate clause joined by a subordinating conjunction; students use complex sentences to add detail, show relationships, and vary sentence structure.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>I Do — The Main Idea + Extra Detail</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>A complex sentence has a main clause (complete thought) and a subordinate clause (depends on the main clause for meaning). They are joined by a subordinating conjunction: because, although, when, if, since, while, unless, after, before. Watch me build one.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Example: Main clause: &quot;The crowd cheered.&quot; + Subordinating conjunction: &quot;when&quot; + Subordinate clause: &quot;the final whistle blew.&quot; → &quot;The crowd cheered when the final whistle blew.&quot;</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>I can also flip it — subordinate clause first: &quot;When the final whistle blew, the crowd cheered.&quot; Notice: when the subordinate clause comes first, I add a comma after it. When it comes second, no comma needed.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Tip: If you start with the subordinate clause, use a comma after it. Starter at the end? No comma.</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>We Do — Build the Complex Sentence</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Join these pairs into complex sentences using an appropriate subordinating conjunction. Try putting the subordinate clause at the start in one version and at the end in another.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>She missed the bus. She left the house late.</li><li>The crops failed. The summer was unusually dry.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Discussion prompt: Which conjunction fits? Does the meaning change if we swap the clause order? Where does the comma go?</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>You Do — Your Turn</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write complex sentences using the given conjunction. Try one with the subordinate clause first, one with it second.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Use &quot;although&quot;: ___ (subordinate clause first, with comma)</li><li>Use &quot;because&quot;: ___ (subordinate clause second, no comma)</li><li>Use &quot;unless&quot;: ___ (your choice of order)</li><li>✦ Bonus: Find three complex sentences in a book you're reading. Identify the main clause and subordinate clause in each.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Objectives (AOs)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Writing — Sentence Structure (Knowledge)</em> — Complex sentences &amp; subordinate clauses: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Sentence Structure (Knowledge): &quot;A complex sentence contains a main clause and one or more subordinate clauses joined by a subordinating conjunction (e.g. because, although, when, if, since, while, unless). When the subordinate clause is fronted, a comma separates it from the main clause.&quot; Understanding the structure of complex sentences is a key Phase 2 grammar expectation.</li><li><em>Writing — Sentence Structure (Practice)</em> — Using complex sentences for effect: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Sentence Structure (Practice): Students are expected to &quot;write a variety of sentence structures — including complex sentences — for effect and clarity,&quot; using subordinating conjunctions to express relationships of cause, contrast, condition, and time. This lesson gives students explicit control over clause order and the punctuation that comes with it.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Thursday: Editing: checking tense consistency in a passage":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">NZ Curriculum Link</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Writing — Writing Process (Practice): students apply a systematic verb audit to identify and correct tense inconsistencies in their own and modelled writing.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>I Do — The Tense Audit</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>I'll show you a systematic way to check tense in a piece of writing. I underline every verb, then check that they're all in the same tense.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Example: Step 1: Underline all verbs. Step 2: Label each P (past) or Pr (present). Step 3: Decide which tense the piece should be. Step 4: Fix mismatches.</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&quot;The explorer sets out at dawn. She walked for three hours before she reaches the ridge.&quot; — sets (Pr), walked (P), reaches (Pr). Two present, one past. I decide: past tense works better for a recount. Fix: &quot;The explorer set out at dawn. She walked for three hours before she reached the ridge.&quot;</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Tip: Be especially careful with &quot;was/is&quot; and &quot;said/says&quot; — these are the most common accidental switches.</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>We Do — Tense Audit Together</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Let's audit this passage together. Underline every verb, label its tense, then rewrite it in consistent past tense.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The market is busy on Saturday mornings. Stalls lined both sides of the street. Vendors called out their prices. The smell of fresh bread drifted through the air. Families stopped to chat and children ran between the stalls. It felt like the whole town was there.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Discussion prompt: Which verbs switch tense? What is the most common tense here? Rewrite consistently.</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>You Do — Your Turn</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Underline all verbs in this passage, label each one P or Pr, then rewrite it in consistent past tense.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The diver slips below the surface. The water was cold and very clear. She searches for the wreck that locals told her about. After ten minutes, she spots a shadow on the seabed. She swam closer and felt her heart race.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Take a piece of your own writing and do a full tense audit on it. Note how many switches you find.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Objectives (AOs)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Writing — Writing Process (Practice)</em> — Systematic tense audit: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Writing Process (Practice): Students are expected to &quot;reread and revise their writing to check that it makes sense and is accurate.&quot; This lesson teaches the tense audit as a specific, repeatable revision strategy — underline every verb, label the tense, decide on consistency, fix mismatches. It is a direct application of the Phase 2 expectation that students revise systematically, not just casually re-read.</li><li><em>Writing — Sentence Structure (Knowledge)</em> — Identifying tense in context: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Sentence Structure (Knowledge): &quot;Verbs carry tense and must remain consistent throughout a piece of writing.&quot; This editing lesson deepens grammatical knowledge by requiring students to identify verb tense in context — distinguishing simple past, simple present, and past continuous forms — before applying that knowledge to make accurate corrections.</li></ul></div></div>"}}},"Week 8":{"label":"Day","children":{"Monday: Homophones & commonly confused words":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">NZ Curriculum Link</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Writing — Conventions (Knowledge): homophones are words that sound the same but have different spellings and meanings; students use meaning and grammatical context to select the correct form.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>I Do — Same Sound, Different Meaning</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Homophones sound the same but have different spellings and meanings. Spellcheck won't catch these — you have to know them! Watch me use context to choose the right one.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Example: there / their / they're | to / too / two | your / you're</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&quot;Put it over <em><strong>.&quot; — &quot;there&quot; (place). &quot;It was </strong></em> idea.&quot; — &quot;their&quot; (belonging to them). &quot;___ late.&quot; — &quot;they're&quot; (they are). I always check: Does a pronoun meaning &quot;they are&quot; fit? → they're. Does &quot;belonging to them&quot; fit? → their. A place or pointing? → there.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Tip: Expand contractions to check: &quot;they're&quot; = &quot;they are.&quot; &quot;you're&quot; = &quot;you are.&quot; If the expansion doesn't make sense — use the other spelling.</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>We Do — Choose Correctly</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Choose the correct homophone for each gap and explain the rule that helped you.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em><strong> going to need </strong></em> bring ___ own gear. (they're/their/there | to/too/two)</li><li>I don't know <em><strong> </strong></em> ready yet — ___ bags are still in the hall. (whether/weather | you're/your | they're/their/there)</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Discussion prompt: Can you expand any of them? What meaning clue helped you choose?</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>You Do — Your Turn</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Fill in the correct homophone. Write the rule you used in brackets.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We packed <em><strong> bags the night before. (their/there/they're) [</strong></em>]</li><li>The <em><strong> yesterday was cold and grey. (weather/whether) [</strong></em>]</li><li>I've read this book <em><strong> many times. (to/too/two) [</strong></em>]</li><li>I can't decide <em><strong> to go or stay. (weather/whether) [</strong></em>]</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write four sentences of your own, each using a different homophone pair correctly.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Objectives (AOs)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Writing — Conventions (Knowledge)</em> — Homophones &amp; spelling in context: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Conventions (Knowledge): &quot;Homophones are words that sound the same but have different spellings and meanings (e.g. there/their/they're, to/too/two, your/you're). Selecting the correct form requires understanding meaning and grammatical function, as spellcheck cannot distinguish between them.&quot; Students develop the habit of checking homophone choices against context and meaning.</li><li><em>Writing — Writing Process (Practice)</em> — Editing for homophone errors: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Writing Process (Practice): Students are expected to &quot;reread and revise their writing to check that it makes sense and is accurate, including checking spelling.&quot; Homophone errors are among the most common and persistent spelling issues at Years 5–6, and this lesson directly addresses them by giving students a meaning-checking strategy (expand the contraction; ask what the word means here) they can apply independently.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Tuesday: Prepositional phrases":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">NZ Curriculum Link</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Writing — Sentence Structure (Knowledge): prepositions show relationships of location, time, and direction; prepositional phrases add precision and detail to sentences.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>I Do — Prepositions Set the Scene</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>A preposition shows a relationship between a noun and another part of the sentence — often location (in, on, under, beside, behind) or time (before, after, during, since). A prepositional phrase is a preposition + its noun phrase. Watch how it adds precision.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Example: The cat slept. → The cat slept on the warm windowsill. → The cat slept on the warm windowsill throughout the afternoon.</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&quot;on the warm windowsill&quot; = preposition (on) + noun phrase (the warm windowsill). It tells us WHERE. &quot;throughout the afternoon&quot; = preposition (throughout) + noun phrase (the afternoon). Tells us WHEN. I can stack them for a rich, detailed sentence.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Tip: Prepositions are almost always followed by a noun phrase, never a verb.</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>We Do — Add the Phrase</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Add a prepositional phrase (location, time, or both) to each sentence.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The scientist worked. → ___</li><li>A small fire burned. → ___</li><li>The children played. → ___</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Discussion prompt: Where is the action happening? When? Can you add both a time and a place phrase?</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>You Do — Your Turn</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Expand each sentence by adding at least two prepositional phrases.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The old man sat.</li><li>A bird sang.</li><li>The storm raged.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write a paragraph of 4 sentences about a place you know well, using at least one prepositional phrase in every sentence.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Objectives (AOs)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Writing — Sentence Structure (Knowledge)</em> — Prepositional phrases: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Sentence Structure (Knowledge): &quot;A preposition shows a relationship between a noun or pronoun and another element of the sentence, typically expressing location (in, on, under, beside), time (before, after, during, since), or direction (towards, through, across). A prepositional phrase consists of a preposition and its noun phrase.&quot; Students learn to identify and use prepositional phrases as a key tool for adding specific detail.</li><li><em>Writing — Sentence Structure (Practice)</em> — Using prepositional phrases for precision: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Sentence Structure (Practice): Students are expected to &quot;expand sentences by adding adverbial and prepositional phrases to express location, time, and manner.&quot; This lesson teaches students to think of prepositional phrases as scene-setting tools — they answer the reader's question 'but where exactly? but when exactly?' — and to stack them purposefully without overloading the sentence.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Wednesday: Fronted adverbials to vary sentence starts":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">NZ Curriculum Link</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Writing — Sentence Structure (Knowledge &amp; Practice): a fronted adverbial is an adverbial phrase placed at the start of a sentence; it requires a comma after it and provides an effective way to vary sentence openings.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>I Do — Don't Always Start with the Subject</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Most sentences start with the subject (The dog / She / The team). But you can start with an adverbial phrase instead — this adds variety and can emphasise time, place, or manner. Watch me move the adverbial to the front.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Example: Normal: &quot;She packed her bag quickly before sunrise.&quot;</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Fronted: &quot;Before sunrise, she packed her bag quickly.&quot;</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&quot;Before sunrise&quot; is the fronted adverbial — I moved it to the front and added a comma after it. This is the rule: fronted adverbials need a comma after them. The time/place/manner detail now hits the reader first, setting the scene before the action.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Tip: After writing a sentence, ask: could I move the time/place/manner phrase to the front? Try it — see if it sounds stronger.</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>We Do — Move It to the Front</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Rewrite each sentence with a fronted adverbial. Remember the comma!</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The team celebrated their victory loudly after the final whistle.</li><li>A vast flock of starlings moved through the valley at dusk.</li><li>She slipped out of the house quietly at midnight.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Discussion prompt: What is the adverbial phrase? Where does the comma go? Does it change the emphasis?</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>You Do — Your Turn</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Rewrite each sentence with a fronted adverbial, then write two original sentences that begin with a fronted adverbial.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The sailors battled the storm through the night.</li><li>He suddenly stopped walking at the edge of the cliff.</li><li>They arrived in the village on a cold November morning.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write a paragraph of 5 sentences. Make at least 3 of them start with a fronted adverbial.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Objectives (AOs)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Writing — Sentence Structure (Knowledge)</em> — Fronted adverbials: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Sentence Structure (Knowledge): &quot;A fronted adverbial is an adverbial word, phrase, or clause placed before the subject at the start of a sentence. It is followed by a comma. Fronted adverbials can express time, place, manner, or condition and are an effective way to vary sentence openings and create emphasis.&quot; This is an explicitly named feature of Phase 2 sentence structure knowledge.</li><li><em>Writing — Sentence Structure (Practice)</em> — Varying sentence openers: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Sentence Structure (Practice): Students are expected to &quot;vary sentence openings to avoid repetition and create effect.&quot; Fronted adverbials are the most accessible and impactful tool for this — they immediately change the rhythm of a piece and signal a more controlled, deliberate writing style. The associated comma rule gives students a clear, reliable convention to apply.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Thursday: Editing: adding missing detail to bare sentences":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">NZ Curriculum Link</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Writing — Writing Process (Practice) &amp; Composing (Practice): students revise bare sentences by identifying what detail is missing and adding only what the reader needs to picture the scene clearly.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>I Do — Bare Bones vs. Fully Dressed</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>A bare sentence gives the fact. A dressed sentence gives the experience. When editing, I look for sentences that tell me very little and add just enough detail to make them come alive. Watch me.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Example: &quot;The man walked to the door.&quot; → &quot;The elderly man shuffled to the creaking front door, pausing with his hand on the handle.&quot;</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>I added: WHO more specifically (elderly man), HOW (shuffled), WHICH door (creaking front door), and a bonus action (pausing with his hand). I didn't overwrite — I added what matters. The reader can now see it.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Tip: Ask bare sentences: WHO exactly? WHAT kind? HOW exactly? WHICH one? Add only what the reader needs to picture it.</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>We Do — Dress the Sentence</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Together, let's add meaningful detail to these bare sentences. Ask WHO? WHAT kind? HOW?</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A bird flew away.</li><li>She opened the box.</li><li>The car stopped.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Discussion prompt: What detail would create the most vivid picture? What detail is unnecessary?</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>You Do — Your Turn</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Expand each bare sentence into a fully detailed one. Add detail that serves the story.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The dog barked.</li><li>Rain fell.</li><li>He laughed.</li><li>She read the letter.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write a scene in 4 bare sentences. Then swap with a partner to dress each other's sentences up.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Objectives (AOs)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Writing — Writing Process (Practice)</em> — Revising bare sentences: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Writing Process (Practice): Students are expected to &quot;reread and revise their writing… making changes to vocabulary, punctuation, and structure to improve clarity and impact.&quot; This lesson builds a specific revision instinct: recognising a bare sentence, asking the reader's questions (who exactly? what kind? how?), and adding only the detail that genuinely improves the picture — not padding.</li><li><em>Writing — Composing (Practice)</em> — Creating vivid, detailed writing: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Composing (Practice): Students are expected to &quot;use composing strategies to create texts that engage the reader,&quot; including providing specific, concrete detail that allows the reader to visualise the scene. This lesson directly addresses one of the most common weaknesses in Years 5–6 writing — the bare, unelaborated sentence — and gives students a question framework to fix it independently.</li></ul></div></div>"}}},"Week 9":{"label":"Day","children":{"Monday: Onomatopoeia & sound words in writing":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">NZ Curriculum Link</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Writing — Vocabulary (Knowledge) &amp; Composing (Practice): language devices such as onomatopoeia create specific effects; students select and use sound words deliberately to make writing vivid and engaging.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>I Do — Let the Words Make Sound</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Onomatopoeia is when a word sounds like the thing it describes: crash, sizzle, whisper, gurgle. Using sound words makes writing come alive — readers don't just see the scene, they hear it. Watch me layer them in.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Example: Without: &quot;The kitchen was busy.&quot; → With: &quot;Pots clattered, oil sizzled and somewhere above the noise a smoke alarm began to shriek.&quot;</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>I chose sounds that belong to the kitchen — clattered (pots), sizzled (oil), shriek (alarm). Each sound word tells us something is happening AND what it sounds like. I don't need to say &quot;there was a loud noise&quot; — the word IS the noise.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Tip: Think: what does this place/event actually sound like? Find the word that IS the sound.</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>We Do — Listen &amp; Write</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Let's brainstorm sound words for each setting, then build a sentence that uses at least two.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Setting: a beach at high tide</li><li>Setting: a forest just after rain</li><li>Setting: a crowded school cafeteria</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Discussion prompt: What sounds would you hear? Which onomatopoeia words capture them best?</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>You Do — Your Turn</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write 3 sentences, each using at least one onomatopoeia word. Set each in a different place.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Sentence 1 — setting: a thunderstorm.</li><li>Sentence 2 — setting: a building site.</li><li>Sentence 3 — setting: a night in the bush.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write a 4-sentence paragraph about any noisy event. Use at least 4 onomatopoeia words, but don't make them feel forced.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Objectives (AOs)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Writing — Vocabulary (Knowledge)</em> — Language devices: onomatopoeia: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Vocabulary (Knowledge): &quot;Language devices such as onomatopoeia, simile, metaphor, and personification can be used deliberately to create specific effects for the reader. Onomatopoeia uses words that imitate the sounds they describe, making writing more vivid and immediate.&quot; Students learn to recognise onomatopoeia as a deliberate vocabulary choice, not just a novelty.</li><li><em>Writing — Composing (Practice)</em> — Using language devices for effect: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Composing (Practice): Students are expected to &quot;use language devices, including onomatopoeia, to create specific effects and engage the reader.&quot; This lesson develops the skill of selecting sound words that are genuinely apt (not forced) — understanding that an effective onomatopoeia word removes the need to describe the sound separately.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Tuesday: Conjunctions: coordinating vs. subordinating":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">NZ Curriculum Link</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Writing — Sentence Structure (Knowledge): coordinating conjunctions join two independent clauses as equals; subordinating conjunctions create a dependent clause; understanding the difference helps students build compound and complex sentences with precision.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>I Do — Two Kinds of Joiners</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Coordinating conjunctions (FANBOYS: For, And, Nor, But, Or, Yet, So) join two equal, independent clauses. Subordinating conjunctions (because, although, when, if, since, while, unless…) create a dependent clause — one that can't stand alone. Watch the difference.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Example: Coordinating: &quot;She ran fast, but she missed the bus.&quot; | Subordinating: &quot;She missed the bus because she ran too late.&quot;</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>In the coordinating example, both halves are complete sentences joined as equals with a comma + &quot;but.&quot; In the subordinating example, &quot;because she ran too late&quot; can't stand alone — it depends on the main clause. The meaning shifts: one shows contrast, one shows cause.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Tip: FANBOYS = coordinating (join equals). Because/although/when/if = subordinating (create dependence).</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>We Do — Sort &amp; Identify</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Label each conjunction as CO (coordinating) or SUB (subordinating) and explain how it affects meaning.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>He trained every day, yet he failed to make the team.</li><li>The game was cancelled because the field was flooded.</li><li>Although the climb was steep, they reached the summit by noon.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Discussion prompt: Could the underlined part stand alone? Does it show a relationship (cause/contrast/condition)?</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>You Do — Your Turn</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Fill in the best conjunction and label it CO or SUB.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>She wanted to stay home, ___ the trip sounded too exciting to miss. (CO/SUB)</li><li>They could not leave ___ the storm passed. (CO/SUB)</li><li>The museum was old, ___ the exhibits were modern and interactive. (CO/SUB)</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write 4 sentences about a challenge you overcame — use two coordinating and two subordinating conjunctions.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Objectives (AOs)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Writing — Sentence Structure (Knowledge)</em> — Coordinating vs. subordinating conjunctions: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Sentence Structure (Knowledge): &quot;Coordinating conjunctions (for, and, nor, but, or, yet, so) join two independent clauses of equal grammatical weight. Subordinating conjunctions (because, although, when, if, since, while, unless) introduce a dependent clause that cannot stand alone. Understanding the distinction enables students to build both compound and complex sentences accurately.&quot;</li><li><em>Writing — Sentence Structure (Practice)</em> — Building compound &amp; complex sentences: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Sentence Structure (Practice): Students are expected to &quot;write a variety of sentence structures — simple, compound, and complex — for effect and clarity.&quot; This lesson consolidates the distinction between compound sentences (coordinating conjunctions, comma before) and complex sentences (subordinating conjunctions, comma only when fronted) — a key structural knowledge milestone at Phase 2.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Wednesday: Paragraph cohesion – linking sentences":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">NZ Curriculum Link</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Writing — Text Structure (Knowledge &amp; Practice): cohesive paragraphs connect sentences using pronouns, repeated key ideas, and connectives; students revise disjointed writing to improve flow and coherence.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>I Do — Making Paragraphs Flow</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>A cohesive paragraph doesn't just put sentences next to each other — it connects them. I'll show three linking techniques: (1) repeating a key word/idea, (2) using a pronoun to refer back, (3) using a connective (however, also, in addition, as a result).</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Example: Disconnected: &quot;Honey bees are important. They pollinate flowers. Crops need pollinating. Bees are threatened.&quot; → Cohesive: &quot;Honey bees are vital to our food system. They pollinate the flowers of fruit and vegetable crops, making growth possible. However, bee populations worldwide are in decline, threatening the very harvests they support.&quot;</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&quot;They&quot; links back to &quot;honey bees.&quot; &quot;However&quot; signals a contrast. Repeating &quot;pollinate/pollinating&quot; echoes the key idea. The paragraph now reads as connected thought, not a list of facts.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Tip: After writing a paragraph, draw an arrow from each sentence to the one before it. If you can't find a link, add one.</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>We Do — Connect the Paragraph</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Rewrite this paragraph so sentences flow into each other using pronouns, connectives, and repeated ideas.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Coral reefs are important ecosystems. Coral reefs have high biodiversity. Many species live in coral reefs. Coral reefs are threatened by warming oceans. Warming oceans cause coral bleaching. Coral bleaching is very damaging.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Discussion prompt: Which sentences can be linked with a pronoun? Where would &quot;however&quot; or &quot;as a result&quot; work?</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>You Do — Your Turn</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Rewrite this disjointed paragraph, using at least three different linking techniques.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The ancient library was discovered last year. The library was buried underground. Archaeologists excavated the library. The library contained thousands of scrolls. The scrolls were written in three languages. The scrolls are being studied by experts.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write a cohesive 5-sentence paragraph about a natural phenomenon, using at least one of each linking technique.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Objectives (AOs)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Writing — Text Structure (Knowledge)</em> — Cohesion devices within paragraphs: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Text Structure (Knowledge): &quot;Cohesion is created within and across paragraphs through the use of pronouns to refer back to nouns, repeated key words and ideas to maintain focus, and connectives and linking adverbs (however, therefore, as a result) to signal relationships between ideas.&quot; Students at Years 5–6 are expected to understand these devices and apply them deliberately.</li><li><em>Writing — Text Structure (Practice)</em> — Creating cohesive paragraphs: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Text Structure (Practice): Students are expected to &quot;use cohesive devices — including pronouns, synonyms, and connectives — to link ideas within and across sentences and paragraphs.&quot; This lesson builds the skill of reading a paragraph as a connected whole rather than a list of sentences — and teaches students to add the linking tissue that makes their writing flow.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Thursday: Editing: identifying and removing repetition":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">NZ Curriculum Link</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Writing — Writing Process (Practice) &amp; Vocabulary (Practice): students revise writing to eliminate unnecessary word repetition, using synonyms, pronouns, and sentence restructuring to create variety and impact.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>I Do — Same Word, Again and Again</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Repetition drains the energy from writing. When a writer repeats the same word in close proximity, it usually means there's a more precise or interesting word available. Watch me identify and fix it.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Example: &quot;The game was exciting. It was an exciting match with exciting moments right up to the exciting end.&quot;</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&quot;exciting&quot; appears four times in two sentences. I fix: &quot;The game was thrilling. It was an intense match with breathtaking moments right up to the dramatic finish.&quot; Four different words, all meaning roughly the same — but now each adds its own shade of meaning.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Tip: Use Ctrl+F (find) to search for your most-used words. If any appear more than twice in a paragraph — you have a repetition problem.</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>We Do — Spot &amp; Replace</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Find the repeated words in this passage and replace them with synonyms or restructure the sentence.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The team walked to the field. The team warmed up on the field. The team captain looked at the other team. The team captain knew the other team was strong but the team was ready.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Discussion prompt: How many times does &quot;team&quot; appear? What are the best replacements? Can you also vary the sentence structure?</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>You Do — Your Turn</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Rewrite this passage by eliminating unnecessary repetition. Use synonyms, pronouns, or restructuring.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The mountain was tall. The mountain was covered in snow. The snow on the mountain was deep. The mountain had a steep path. The path up the mountain was dangerous. Climbers who climbed the mountain had to be careful.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Pull out a paragraph from your writing. Highlight every word that appears more than twice. Revise.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Objectives (AOs)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Writing — Writing Process (Practice)</em> — Editing for repetition: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Writing Process (Practice): Students are expected to &quot;reread and revise their writing… making changes to vocabulary, punctuation, and structure to improve clarity and impact.&quot; Eliminating word repetition is a core revision skill that requires students to read their writing through the reader's eyes — noticing patterns they've become blind to as the writer.</li><li><em>Writing — Vocabulary (Practice)</em> — Synonyms &amp; pronoun substitution: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Vocabulary (Practice): Students are expected to &quot;select and use words and phrases… that are fit for purpose,&quot; and to &quot;use cohesive devices including pronouns and synonyms.&quot; This lesson directly applies those two strategies in the context of revision — replacing repeated nouns with pronouns and synonyms to create variety, precision, and flow in their writing.</li></ul></div></div>"}}},"Week 10":{"label":"Day","children":{"Monday: Review: punctuation marks covered in Term 1":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">NZ Curriculum Link</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Writing — Punctuation (Knowledge &amp; Practice): review and consolidate all punctuation covered in Term 1 — full stops, commas, apostrophes, question marks, exclamation marks, colons, and semicolons.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>I Do — The Term 1 Punctuation Toolkit</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Let's do a full review of every punctuation mark we've learned this term. I'll show each one in action and remind you of the key rule.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Example: Full stop . | Comma , | Apostrophe ' | Question mark ? | Exclamation mark ! | Colon : | Semicolon ;</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Each mark has a job: full stop ends sentences. Comma separates list items and follows fronted adverbials. Apostrophe shows possession or contraction. ? asks a question. ! shows strong emotion. Colon introduces. Semicolon joins two related sentences.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Tip: For each mark, ask: what job is this doing in this sentence? If it's not doing a job — remove it.</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>We Do — Punctuation Challenge</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Add the correct punctuation to each sentence and name the rule.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Before the game began the captain gathered the team for a final talk</li><li>She packed the following items a torch a sleeping bag warm clothes and extra food</li><li>My brothers bike was left in the rain it was completely rusted by morning</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Discussion prompt: Which marks are needed and why? Could any sentence use more than one type?</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>You Do — Your Turn</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Add all missing punctuation to this passage. Name every rule you applied.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>at the end of last term our class held a debate the topic was should schools have longer lunch breaks three teams argued for three argued against some students made excellent points however others needed more evidence after the debate mrs kahu said that everyone had improved</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write a paragraph of 6 sentences that uses every Term 1 punctuation mark at least once. Label each one.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Objectives (AOs)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Writing — Punctuation (Knowledge)</em> — Term 1 punctuation review: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Punctuation (Knowledge): Students consolidate their knowledge of all punctuation covered in Term 1: sentence-end punctuation (. ? !), commas in lists and after fronted adverbials, apostrophes for possession and contraction, and introductory colons and semicolons. Review ensures students can identify the function of each mark, not just recognise its shape.</li><li><em>Writing — Punctuation (Practice)</em> — Applying punctuation accurately: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Punctuation (Practice): Students are expected to &quot;use a range of punctuation accurately and for effect.&quot; This consolidation lesson asks students to apply all Term 1 punctuation conventions in context — naming the rule behind each choice — building the metacognitive awareness needed to make punctuation decisions independently in their own writing.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Tuesday: Review: grammar concepts from Term 1":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">NZ Curriculum Link</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Writing — Sentence Structure (Knowledge): review and consolidate all word classes and grammar concepts from Term 1 — nouns, verbs, adjectives, adverbs, pronouns, conjunctions, and subject-verb agreement.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>I Do — Grammar Gallery Tour</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Let's revisit every grammar concept from Term 1. I'll use a &quot;grammar gallery&quot; — one concept per station with a mini-example.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Example: Stations: Nouns &amp; proper nouns → Verbs (action/state) → Subject-verb agreement → Adjectives &amp; comparison → Adverbs (how/when/where) → Pronouns → Conjunctions (CO &amp; SUB)</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Station 1: Nouns — &quot;The explorer found a map.&quot; (nouns: explorer, map). Station 2: Proper noun — &quot;She found it near Lake Taupo.&quot; Station 3: SVA — &quot;Each explorer has their role.&quot; Station 4: Adjectives — &quot;a tattered, ancient map.&quot; ...and so on through each concept.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Tip: Grammar is about understanding why a sentence works, not just labelling. Always connect the rule to the effect on meaning.</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>We Do — Grammar Challenge Quiz</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>I'll call out a sentence. You identify the grammar feature I'm asking about. We'll go through all Term 1 concepts.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>&quot;The exhausted climbers finally reached Base Camp.&quot; → Find: noun, adjective, adverb.</li><li>&quot;Neither of the answers is correct.&quot; → Identify: subject, verb. Is SVA correct?</li><li>&quot;Because the trail was icy, the rescue team moved slowly.&quot; → Label: conjunction type, main clause, subordinate clause.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Discussion prompt: Hands up for each one — can anyone give a different example using the same grammar feature?</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>You Do — Your Turn</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Complete the grammar review tasks.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Write a sentence containing: a proper noun, an action verb, and a fronted adverbial.</li><li>Write a compound sentence using &quot;but&quot; and a complex sentence using &quot;although.&quot;</li><li>Write a sentence with a comparative and one with a superlative adjective.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write a paragraph of 6 sentences that demonstrates at least 5 different Term 1 grammar concepts. Label them in the margin.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Objectives (AOs)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Writing — Sentence Structure (Knowledge)</em> — Term 1 grammar review: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Sentence Structure (Knowledge): Students consolidate their understanding of all word classes taught in Term 1: nouns (common, proper, collective, abstract), verbs (action, state, tense), adjectives (comparative, superlative), adverbs (manner, time, place), pronouns (personal, possessive), and conjunctions (coordinating, subordinating). They also review subject-verb agreement across a range of sentence types.</li><li><em>Writing — Sentence Structure (Practice)</em> — Applying grammar knowledge in sentences: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Sentence Structure (Practice): Students are expected to &quot;write grammatically correct sentences using a range of word classes and structures.&quot; This review lesson asks students to demonstrate that knowledge by combining multiple features in single sentences — moving from isolated recognition to integrated, purposeful use.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Wednesday: Review: sentence types practised in Term 1":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">NZ Curriculum Link</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Writing — Sentence Structure (Practice): review and apply the full range of sentence structures from Term 1 — simple, expanded, compound, complex, and fronted adverbial sentences — for effect and variety.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>I Do — Our Sentence Toolkit</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This term we built quite a sentence toolkit. Let me show you every type we practised and how to combine them for maximum effect.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Example: Simple | Simple + adjectives | Compound (FANBOYS) | Complex (subordinate clause) | Fronted adverbial | Who/where/when expanded</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Simple: &quot;The storm broke.&quot; | + adjectives: &quot;The fierce, sudden storm broke.&quot; | Compound: &quot;The storm broke, and the river rose rapidly.&quot; | Complex: &quot;Although residents had been warned, few expected the storm to be this severe.&quot; | Fronted: &quot;Just before dawn, the storm broke over the city.&quot; | Expanded: &quot;The storm, which had been building for three days, broke over the city just before dawn.&quot;</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Tip: The best writing mixes ALL these sentence types. No single type should dominate.</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>We Do — Sentence Type Auction</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>I'll read out sentences. You identify the type and explain how you know. Then we'll vote on which version of the same idea works best.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Version A: &quot;She won.&quot; vs Version B: &quot;After months of training, she finally won the regional championship.&quot; vs Version C: &quot;She won, but the victory felt hollow.&quot;</li><li>&quot;Despite the rain, the festival continued.&quot; — what type? What's the effect?</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Discussion prompt: Why might a writer choose Version A over B? When is shorter more powerful?</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>You Do — Your Turn</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write one sentence of each type listed below, all on the same topic (the ocean).</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>1. Simple sentence.</li><li>2. Simple sentence with 2 adjectives.</li><li>3. Compound sentence (use &quot;yet&quot;).</li><li>4. Complex sentence (start with &quot;Although&quot;).</li><li>5. Fronted adverbial sentence.</li><li>6. Expanded sentence with who/where/when.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Arrange your 6 sentences into a mini-paragraph that flows naturally.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Objectives (AOs)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Writing — Sentence Structure (Practice)</em> — Sentence variety: full toolkit: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Sentence Structure (Practice): Students are expected to &quot;write a variety of sentence structures — simple, compound, and complex — for effect and clarity,&quot; and to &quot;vary sentence openings&quot; to avoid repetition. This consolidation lesson asks students to demonstrate control over the full range of sentence types covered in Term 1, and to make deliberate choices about which type serves each moment in their writing.</li><li><em>Writing — Composing (Practice)</em> — Combining sentence types for effect: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Composing (Practice): Students are expected to &quot;use composing strategies to create texts that engage the reader,&quot; including controlling rhythm, pace, and emphasis through sentence variety. This lesson reinforces that effective writing is not about using any one sentence type — it is about choosing the right structure for the right moment.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Thursday: Editing: full-passage edit using Term 1 checklist":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">NZ Curriculum Link</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Writing — Writing Process (Practice): students apply all Term 1 editing skills in a systematic multi-pass edit, using the full Term 1 checklist to revise a passage for accuracy, vocabulary, and structure.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>I Do — The Full Edit</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Today I'm going to do a complete multi-pass edit on this passage using our full Term 1 checklist. Watch how I tackle one aspect at a time rather than trying to fix everything at once.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Example: Pass 1: Capitals &amp; full stops | Pass 2: Comma usage | Pass 3: Apostrophes | Pass 4: Word choice (vague words) | Pass 5: Sentence variety | Pass 6: Repetition</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>I read the passage once to understand it. Then Pass 1: I check every sentence begins with a capital and ends correctly. Pass 2: I look for lists and compound sentences — are commas right? Pass 3: Any apostrophes? Are they possession or contraction? Pass 4: I highlight &quot;nice,&quot; &quot;good,&quot; &quot;went,&quot; &quot;got&quot; — can I upgrade any? Pass 5: Are all sentences the same length? Pass 6: Any repeated words?</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Tip: Multi-pass editing catches more than single-pass. Each pass has a specific focus — you're not trying to see everything at once.</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>We Do — Class Edit</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Working through each pass together, let's edit this full passage as a class.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>last week my class went on a trip to the botanical gardens in the park. it was a nice day and everyone was excited. we walked around and saw lots of nice plants and flowers. our teacher mrs patel said that the gardens had been there for over one hundred years and that they had lots of rare plants from all over the world. we saw carnivorous plants cacti roses sunflowers and many types of ferns. the carnivorous plants were good and everyone wanted to feed them. after the trip we all wrote about our favourite plants in our science books</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Discussion prompt: Pass by pass — what are we fixing? Count the improvements at the end.</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>You Do — Your Turn</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Edit this passage using all six passes of the Term 1 checklist. Rewrite the improved version.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>on friday our school had a sports day the weather was nice so everyone went outside there were lots of different events like running jumping throwing and relay races my friend noah ran really fast and he won his race we all cheered for him. i came second in the long jump which was good. at the end mrs kahu gave out certificates and everyone got a good certificate for trying hard we went home tired but happy</li><li>✦ Bonus: Apply the same 6-pass edit to a paragraph from your writing journal.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Objectives (AOs)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Writing — Writing Process (Practice)</em> — Multi-pass editing: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Writing Process (Practice): Students are expected to &quot;reread and revise their writing… making changes to vocabulary, punctuation, and structure to improve clarity and impact.&quot; This lesson makes the revision process systematic and visible — a structured, multi-pass approach that builds the habits of a careful, independent editor. Students apply every skill from Term 1 in a single, integrated editing task.</li><li><em>Writing — Writing Process (Practice)</em> — Term 1 skills: integrated application: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Writing Process (Practice): This consolidation lesson asks students to demonstrate mastery of all Term 1 writing skills in one editing task: punctuation conventions, vocabulary precision, sentence variety, and paragraph cohesion. It directly mirrors the Phase 2 expectation that students &quot;apply writing knowledge and skills across a range of text types and purposes&quot; with increasing independence.</li></ul></div></div>"}}}}},"Term 2":{"label":"Week","children":{"Week 1":{"label":"Day","children":{"Monday: Tier 2 vocabulary: words for movement & action":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">NZ Curriculum Link</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Writing — Vocabulary (Knowledge &amp; Practice): precise verbs eliminate vague movement words; students select verbs that convey manner and quality of movement, reducing reliance on weak constructions.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>I Do — Swap the Vague Movement Verb</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Watch me swap plain movement verbs for precise ones that carry more information.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Example: &quot;The man went down the street.&quot; → &quot;The man strode down the street.&quot;</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Went → strode, shuffled, sprinted, lurched. Each verb tells us HOW as well as that movement happened. &quot;Strode&quot; tells us purposeful, confident movement. &quot;Shuffled&quot; tells us tired or reluctant movement. One word replaces a verb + adverb.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Tip: Precise verbs eliminate the need for adverbs: &quot;ran quickly&quot; → &quot;sprinted.&quot;</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>We Do — Upgrade the Verb</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Upgrade the underlined verbs with more precise movement words.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The bird went through the air.</li><li>She moved across the dance floor.</li><li>He went into the room.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Discussion prompt: What does the precise verb tell us that &quot;went&quot; does not?</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>You Do — Your Turn</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Replace each plain verb with the most precise one you can think of.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The snake went through the grass.</li><li>The toddler moved towards the door.</li><li>She went across the rooftops.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write 4 sentences each using a different precise movement verb. No two the same.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Objectives (AOs)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Writing — Vocabulary (Knowledge)</em> — Precise verbs for movement: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Vocabulary (Knowledge): &quot;Vocabulary is chosen deliberately for effect, so students consider the precision and connotations of words.&quot; Selecting a precise movement verb (strode, shuffled, lurched) rather than a generic one (went, moved) demonstrates Tier 2 vocabulary development — a core expectation at Years 5–6. Each verb carries manner, intent, and character without requiring additional adverbs.</li><li><em>Writing — Vocabulary (Practice)</em> — Selecting verbs fit for purpose: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Vocabulary (Practice): Students are expected to &quot;select and use words and phrases… that are fit for purpose and audience.&quot; This lesson builds the habit of interrogating verb choices — asking not just 'what is happening?' but 'exactly how is it happening?' — and finding the single word that answers both questions simultaneously.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Tuesday: Nouns: collective & abstract nouns":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">NZ Curriculum Link</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Writing — Sentence Structure (Knowledge): nouns include common, proper, collective, and abstract types; understanding the full range of noun classes develops students' grammatical knowledge and expands their writing vocabulary.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>I Do — Two Special Noun Types</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Collective nouns name a group. Abstract nouns name ideas, feelings, or concepts you cannot touch.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Example: Collective: a flock of birds | Abstract: courage, freedom, justice</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Flock, swarm, pride, pod — these are collective. Justice, hope, anger — these are abstract. You can't hold justice in your hand, but it's a noun because it names a concept.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Tip: If you can't touch it or see it, but it's a thing — it's probably abstract.</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>We Do — Sort the Nouns</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Sort these nouns into collective, abstract, or concrete.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>a herd of cattle</li><li>curiosity</li><li>a bouquet of flowers</li><li>loyalty</li><li>a school of fish</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Discussion prompt: How do you know? Could you take a photo of it? Touch it?</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>You Do — Your Turn</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Label each noun: collective (C), abstract (A), or concrete (Co).</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>a murder of crows —</li><li>envy —</li><li>a packet of biscuits —</li><li>a pride of lions —</li><li>determination —</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write a sentence for each type using a noun from today's lesson.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Objectives (AOs)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Writing — Sentence Structure (Knowledge)</em> — Word classes: collective &amp; abstract nouns: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Sentence Structure (Knowledge): &quot;Words belong to different classes… nouns include common, proper, collective (naming a group), and abstract (naming a concept, idea, or emotion) types.&quot; Understanding the full range of noun classes deepens grammatical awareness and expands the vocabulary available to students when composing at Years 5–6.</li><li><em>Writing — Vocabulary (Practice)</em> — Using abstract nouns for depth: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Vocabulary (Practice): Students are expected to &quot;select and use words and phrases… that are fit for purpose and audience.&quot; Using abstract nouns deliberately — courage, justice, determination — is a hallmark of sophisticated writing at Phase 2, allowing students to write about ideas and values, not just concrete events.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Wednesday: Simple sentences: revisit with action-verb focus":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">NZ Curriculum Link</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Writing — Sentence Structure (Practice): students revisit simple sentence structure with a focus on replacing weak verb constructions (do/make/give + noun) with single powerful action verbs.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>I Do — One Strong Verb Beats Three Weak Words</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Revisiting simple sentences — but this time the focus is on choosing a powerful action verb over a weak &quot;do + noun&quot; construction.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Example: Weak: &quot;The volcano did an explosion.&quot; → Strong: &quot;The volcano erupted.&quot;</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&quot;Do + noun&quot; is often the weak version. Replace with a direct action verb: made a decision → decided. Gave a performance → performed. Did an investigation → investigated. Each replacement tightens the sentence and strengthens the verb.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Tip: &quot;Did&quot;, &quot;made&quot;, &quot;gave&quot;, &quot;had&quot; + noun = usually replaceable with one strong verb.</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>We Do — Find the Strong Verb</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Replace the weak &quot;do + noun&quot; construction with one strong verb.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>She made a complaint about the noise.</li><li>They did a celebration when they won.</li><li>He gave a performance that amazed the crowd.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Discussion prompt: What single verb can replace each phrase?</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>You Do — Your Turn</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Rewrite each sentence using one strong action verb.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>She made a decision to leave early.</li><li>The team gave a demonstration of the new skill.</li><li>They did an investigation into the disappearance.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write 5 simple sentences using 5 different powerful action verbs.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Objectives (AOs)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Writing — Sentence Structure (Practice)</em> — Strong action verbs in simple sentences: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Sentence Structure (Practice): Students are expected to &quot;write grammatically correct sentences&quot; and to select verbs that convey meaning precisely. Replacing weak &quot;do/make/give + noun&quot; constructions with single powerful action verbs is a key revision skill — it tightens sentence structure and removes redundant words simultaneously.</li><li><em>Writing — Vocabulary (Practice)</em> — Precise verb selection: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Vocabulary (Practice): Students are expected to &quot;select and use words and phrases… that are fit for purpose and audience.&quot; This lesson reinforces that one exact verb (erupted, decided, investigated) is more powerful and precise than a vague verb + noun combination — a foundational principle of effective word choice at Phase 2.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Thursday: Proofreading: capitals for proper nouns in context":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">NZ Curriculum Link</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Writing — Punctuation (Knowledge) &amp; Writing Process (Practice): students apply capitalisation rules for proper nouns in complex, multi-sentence text, developing accuracy and consistency in editing.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>I Do — Proper Nouns in Longer Text</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>In this term's editing focus, we look specifically at proper noun capitals in longer, more complex text.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Example: &quot;last tuesday, ms brown took class 5b to the otago museum in dunedin.&quot;</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Fixed: &quot;Last Tuesday, Ms Brown took Class 5B to the Otago Museum in Dunedin.&quot; Every specific name, title, day, place, and class identifier needs a capital.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Tip: Scan specifically for: days, months, place names, people's names/titles, specific event names.</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>We Do — Capital Letter Hunt</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Find and fix all the proper noun capitalisation errors.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>on anzac day, uncle mike and aunt sarah drove to hamilton for the dawn parade at memorial park.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Discussion prompt: List every word that needs a capital. How many did you find?</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>You Do — Your Turn</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Rewrite this passage with all proper noun capitals correctly placed.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>my cousin riya is visiting from tauranga next friday. she goes to otumoetai college and is in year 10. on saturday we're going to visit the national aquarium of new zealand in napier. riya wants to see the tuatara display and the kiwi house afterwards we might have lunch at the hawke's bay farmers market.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write a paragraph about a real or imaginary trip. Include at least 6 proper nouns — all correctly capitalised.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Objectives (AOs)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Writing — Punctuation (Knowledge)</em> — Capitalisation: proper nouns in context: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Punctuation (Knowledge): &quot;Capital letters are used at the start of sentences and for all proper nouns — names of specific people, places, days, months, titles, organisations, and events.&quot; At Phase 2, students are expected to apply this convention consistently across longer, more complex text where proper nouns appear frequently and in varied contexts.</li><li><em>Writing — Writing Process (Practice)</em> — Editing for capitalisation accuracy: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Writing Process (Practice): Students are expected to &quot;reread and revise their writing to check that it makes sense and is accurate, including checking for correct capitalisation.&quot; This lesson trains the targeted scanning strategy — reading specifically for proper nouns — that helps students catch capitalisation errors they overlook in a general read-through.</li></ul></div></div>"}}},"Week 2":{"label":"Day","children":{"Monday: Commas after fronted adverbials":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">NZ Curriculum Link</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Writing — Punctuation (Knowledge &amp; Practice): a fronted adverbial is followed by a comma; students apply this convention accurately and understand why the comma signals a pause before the main clause.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>I Do — The Opener Comma</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>When a sentence starts with an adverbial phrase or clause (a fronted adverbial), we put a comma after it before the main clause begins.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Example: &quot;After the long match, the players collapsed on the grass.&quot; | &quot;During the school holidays, we visited Rotorua.&quot;</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&quot;After the long match&quot; is the fronted adverbial — it sets the scene. The comma after &quot;match&quot; separates it from the main clause. Without the comma, the reader rushes past the opener without pausing.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Tip: Read aloud. If you naturally pause after the opening phrase before the subject arrives — that pause is your comma.</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>We Do — Add the Comma</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>These sentences have fronted adverbials but are missing the comma. Find the opener and add the comma.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Before the sun had risen the fishing boats left the harbour.</li><li>On the last day of term the students performed their assembly item.</li><li>Despite the heavy rain the match went ahead as planned.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Discussion prompt: Where does the fronted adverbial end and the main clause begin? That's where the comma goes.</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>You Do — Your Turn</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Add the missing comma to each sentence and write the type of fronted adverbial (time/place/contrast).</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Throughout the long journey south the waka stayed on course.</li><li>Although the trail was steep the tamariki kept climbing.</li><li>By the time the tide turned the divers were already back on shore.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write 5 sentences that each begin with a different type of fronted adverbial. Use at least one time, one place, and one contrast opener.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Objectives (AOs)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Writing — Punctuation (Knowledge)</em> — Comma after fronted adverbials: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Punctuation (Knowledge): &quot;When an adverbial word, phrase, or clause is placed before the subject at the start of a sentence (a fronted adverbial), a comma is used to separate it from the main clause.&quot; This is a specific and frequently tested punctuation convention at Years 5–6, appearing across narrative, recount, and persuasive writing.</li><li><em>Writing — Punctuation (Practice)</em> — Applying fronted adverbial comma: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Punctuation (Practice): Students are expected to &quot;use a range of punctuation accurately and for effect.&quot; The comma after a fronted adverbial is both a convention (accuracy) and a craft tool (effect) — it signals a deliberate pause that gives the opener its weight. This lesson builds the read-aloud checking strategy: the natural pause reveals where the comma belongs.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Tuesday: Future tense: will & going to":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">NZ Curriculum Link</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Writing — Sentence Structure (Knowledge): the future tense is expressed using &quot;will&quot; or &quot;going to&quot;; the choice between them carries different shades of meaning — spontaneous decisions vs planned intentions.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>I Do — Two Ways to Talk About the Future</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>English expresses future time in two main ways: &quot;will&quot; + verb, and &quot;going to&quot; + verb. They are not always interchangeable. Watch me use each one and explain the difference.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Example: &quot;I will help you with that.&quot; (spontaneous decision) | &quot;We are going to plant native trees next month.&quot; (planned intention)</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&quot;Will&quot; is used for spontaneous decisions, offers, predictions, and promises made at the moment of speaking. &quot;Going to&quot; is used for plans already decided and for predictions based on current evidence. &quot;Look at those clouds — it's going to rain.&quot; (evidence right there).</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Tip: Planned ahead → going to. Decided right now → will. Evidence in front of you → going to.</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>We Do — Will or Going To?</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Choose &quot;will&quot; or &quot;going to&quot; and explain why.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The marae committee ___ (will/is going to) hold a hui next Saturday — it's been in the calendar for months.</li><li>&quot;I can't carry that box.&quot; &quot;Don't worry, I ___ (will/am going to) help you.&quot;</li><li>Look at those storm clouds — the weather ___ (will/is going to) turn soon.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Discussion prompt: Is this a plan, a spontaneous offer, or a prediction based on evidence?</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>You Do — Your Turn</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Complete each sentence with the correct future form. Write a brief reason for your choice.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The school ___ (will/is going to) hold a matariki celebration — we've been practising for weeks.</li><li>&quot;The printer is broken.&quot; &quot;I ___ (will/am going to) fix it.&quot; (spontaneous offer)</li><li>That kūmara plant ___ (will/is going to) need water soon — the soil is bone dry.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write a paragraph of 5 sentences about a future event you're genuinely planning. Use &quot;going to&quot; for plans and &quot;will&quot; for predictions or promises within it.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Objectives (AOs)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Writing — Sentence Structure (Knowledge)</em> — Future tense: will &amp; going to: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Sentence Structure (Knowledge): &quot;The future tense in English is expressed using 'will' + verb (for spontaneous decisions, offers, and promises) or 'going to' + verb (for pre-planned intentions and predictions based on present evidence). Understanding the distinction allows writers to express future time with precision rather than using the two forms interchangeably.&quot;</li><li><em>Writing — Sentence Structure (Practice)</em> — Using future tense accurately: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Sentence Structure (Practice): Students are expected to &quot;write grammatically correct sentences&quot; using a range of tenses accurately. Choosing between &quot;will&quot; and &quot;going to&quot; is a nuanced grammar decision that requires students to consider the context and intended meaning — a marker of grammatical sophistication at Years 5–6.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Wednesday: Compound sentences: new contexts & conjunctions":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">NZ Curriculum Link</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Writing — Sentence Structure (Practice): students use FANBOYS conjunctions in new writing contexts — recount, report, and argument — understanding that the same conjunction carries different weight depending on purpose and audience.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>I Do — FANBOYS in Fresh Contexts</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>We know FANBOYS conjunctions (for, and, nor, but, or, yet, so). This week we use them in new writing contexts to see how the same conjunction does different jobs.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Example: Recount: &quot;We arrived at Tāmaki Makaurau, and the whole whānau was waiting.&quot; | Argument: &quot;The policy is popular, yet the evidence does not support it.&quot;</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&quot;And&quot; adds. &quot;But/yet&quot; contrasts. &quot;So&quot; shows result. &quot;Or&quot; gives alternatives. &quot;For&quot; gives reason (formal). &quot;Nor&quot; adds a negative. The conjunction you choose shapes the relationship between the two ideas.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Tip: Before picking a conjunction, decide: am I adding, contrasting, showing a result, or giving a reason? That decides which FANBOYS fits.</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>We Do — Choose the Right FANBOYS</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Join each pair of sentences with the best FANBOYS conjunction. Add a comma before the conjunction.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The hīkoi had been going for three days. The marchers showed no sign of stopping.</li><li>You can present your findings as a poster. You can create a short video.</li><li>The experiment failed the first time. The team learnt something valuable from it.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Discussion prompt: What is the relationship between the two ideas? That decides the conjunction.</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>You Do — Your Turn</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write a compound sentence for each context using the conjunction given.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Write about a kaitiakitanga project using &quot;so.&quot;</li><li>Write about a time something went wrong using &quot;yet.&quot;</li><li>Write a sentence giving someone two choices using &quot;or.&quot;</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write a paragraph about an environmental issue. Use at least three different FANBOYS conjunctions. Underline each one.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Objectives (AOs)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Writing — Sentence Structure (Knowledge)</em> — FANBOYS in varied contexts: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Sentence Structure (Knowledge): &quot;Coordinating conjunctions (for, and, nor, but, or, yet, so) join two independent clauses of equal grammatical weight. Each conjunction signals a different relationship: addition (and), contrast (but/yet), result (so), alternative (or), reason (for), negative addition (nor).&quot; Students learn that the same conjunction carries different register and weight depending on text type and context.</li><li><em>Writing — Sentence Structure (Practice)</em> — Compound sentences across text types: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Sentence Structure (Practice): Students are expected to &quot;write a variety of sentence structures for effect and clarity&quot; across different text types. This lesson extends compound sentence use beyond narrative into recount, report, and argument — building students' ability to select the right conjunction for the relationship and context, not just the first one that fits grammatically.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Thursday: Editing: fixing comma splices":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">NZ Curriculum Link</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Writing — Punctuation (Knowledge) &amp; Writing Process (Practice): a comma splice joins two independent clauses with only a comma; students identify comma splices and correct them using a full stop, semicolon, or conjunction.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>I Do — The Comma That Tries to Do Too Much</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>A comma splice is when two complete sentences are joined with just a comma. A comma is not strong enough to hold two independent clauses together on its own. Watch me find and fix one.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Example: ✗ &quot;The waka was ready, the crew climbed aboard.&quot; → ✓ &quot;The waka was ready. The crew climbed aboard.&quot; OR ✓ &quot;The waka was ready, and the crew climbed aboard.&quot; OR ✓ &quot;The waka was ready; the crew climbed aboard.&quot;</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Three fixes: (1) full stop — two separate sentences. (2) comma + conjunction — compound sentence. (3) semicolon — joins two closely related sentences. All three are correct. Choose based on the relationship between the ideas.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Tip: Test: read each side of the comma. If both could stand alone as sentences — the comma is a splice. Fix it.</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>We Do — Fix the Splice</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Find the comma splice in each sentence and fix it three ways.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The tide was coming in, we had to leave quickly.</li><li>She had been training for months, the race was finally here.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Discussion prompt: Which fix sounds best for each sentence? Does the relationship between the ideas affect your choice?</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>You Do — Your Turn</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Fix each comma splice using the method shown in brackets.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The kākāpō is critically endangered, conservation efforts have helped increase numbers. [semicolon]</li><li>The hail hammered the roof, everyone moved away from the windows. [conjunction]</li><li>She had practised the speech a hundred times, she still felt her hands shake. [full stop]</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write 3 pairs of sentences — then deliberately create a comma splice, and fix it in two different ways each time.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Objectives (AOs)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Writing — Punctuation (Knowledge)</em> — Comma splices: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Punctuation (Knowledge): &quot;A comma splice occurs when two independent clauses are joined with only a comma, which is insufficient punctuation. It can be corrected by: (1) replacing the comma with a full stop; (2) adding a coordinating conjunction after the comma; or (3) replacing the comma with a semicolon.&quot; Understanding comma splices deepens students' knowledge of sentence boundaries and punctuation function.</li><li><em>Writing — Writing Process (Practice)</em> — Identifying and correcting comma splices: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Writing Process (Practice): Students are expected to &quot;reread and revise their writing to check that it makes sense and is accurate.&quot; Recognising and fixing comma splices is a targeted editing skill that requires students to identify sentence boundaries and select the punctuation or conjunction that best expresses the relationship between the two clauses.</li></ul></div></div>"}}},"Week 3":{"label":"Day","children":{"Monday: Antonyms & shades of meaning":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">NZ Curriculum Link</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Writing — Vocabulary (Knowledge): antonyms are words with opposite meanings; understanding the spectrum between antonyms develops nuanced vocabulary and helps students select words with the right shade of meaning.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>I Do — The Meaning Spectrum</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Antonyms are opposites — but between most opposites there's a whole spectrum of words. Understanding that spectrum lets you choose exactly the right word for your meaning.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Example: hot ←——————————————→ cold</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>scalding / burning / warm / tepid / cool / chilly / freezing / arctic</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&quot;Hot&quot; and &quot;cold&quot; are antonyms, but the spectrum between them has eight gradations. &quot;The water was cold&quot; is vague. &quot;The water was arctic&quot; is precise. Choosing from the spectrum — rather than defaulting to the antonym — gives writing its precision and texture.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Tip: Before writing a describing word, ask: where on the spectrum between the two extremes does this fall? Then find the word that sits exactly there.</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>We Do — Build the Spectrum</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Build meaning spectrums for each pair of antonyms. Aim for at least five words between the two extremes.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>happy ←—————————→ sad</li><li>loud ←—————————→ quiet</li><li>fast ←—————————→ slow</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Discussion prompt: Which word on the spectrum best describes: a library, a sports crowd, a funeral? Why?</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>You Do — Your Turn</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Choose the word from the spectrum that best fits each context. Explain your choice.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The old man's voice was ___ (choose from: booming / loud / clear / soft / faint / inaudible) as he told the story.</li><li>After the marathon, the runner felt ___ (choose from: exhausted / tired / weary / drained / spent / destroyed).</li><li>The stream moved ___ (choose from: rushing / flowing / trickling / seeping / still) through the valley.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Choose one antonym pair. Build a spectrum of 8 words. Then use three of them in three separate sentences about the same setting.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Objectives (AOs)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Writing — Vocabulary (Knowledge)</em> — Antonyms &amp; meaning spectrums: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Vocabulary (Knowledge): &quot;The meaning of words can be extended or changed by context; vocabulary is chosen deliberately for effect, so students consider the connotations and precision of words.&quot; Understanding antonyms and the spectrum of meaning between them is a key component of Tier 2 vocabulary development — it moves students beyond binary word choice (hot/cold) to nuanced selection along a continuum.</li><li><em>Writing — Vocabulary (Practice)</em> — Selecting words for precision: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Vocabulary (Practice): Students are expected to &quot;select and use words and phrases, including subject-specific vocabulary, that are fit for purpose and audience&quot; and to &quot;consider the effect of word choice on the reader.&quot; This lesson develops the habit of asking 'exactly where on the spectrum does this fall?' before committing to a word — the key discipline of precise vocabulary selection.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Tuesday: Subject-verb agreement: tricky cases (everyone, each)":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">NZ Curriculum Link</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Writing — Sentence Structure (Knowledge): indefinite pronouns such as everyone, each, neither, and someone are grammatically singular and require singular verb forms, even when they refer to multiple people.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>I Do — The Tricky Singular Words</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Most subject-verb agreement is straightforward. But some words look plural and feel plural yet are grammatically singular.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Example: &quot;Everyone in the two classes was invited.&quot; (not &quot;were&quot;) | &quot;Each of the players has a number.&quot; (not &quot;have&quot;)</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&quot;Everyone,&quot; &quot;everybody,&quot; &quot;someone,&quot; &quot;nobody,&quot; &quot;each,&quot; &quot;either,&quot; &quot;neither&quot; — all singular, even when they refer to many people. The trick: mentally replace with &quot;every single person&quot; — then the singular verb feels natural.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Tip: Everyone/each/either/neither/nobody = singular. Replace with &quot;every single one&quot; in your head — the right verb follows naturally.</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>We Do — Choose the Right Verb</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Choose the correct verb form for each sentence.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Everyone in the kapa haka group ___ (know/knows) the words.</li><li>Each of the entries ___ (was/were) judged on three criteria.</li><li>Neither of the referees ___ (was/were) happy with the decision.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Discussion prompt: Replace the subject with &quot;every single one&quot; — which verb sounds right now?</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>You Do — Your Turn</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Correct the subject-verb agreement error in each sentence.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Everyone in the syndicate have submitted their work.</li><li>Each of the kūmara plants were carefully watered.</li><li>Neither of the answers are correct.</li><li>Somebody have left their bag on the mat.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write 5 sentences, each using one of these subjects: everyone, each, neither, somebody, anyone. Make sure agreement is correct in every one.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Objectives (AOs)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Writing — Sentence Structure (Knowledge)</em> — SVA: indefinite pronouns: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Sentence Structure (Knowledge): &quot;Indefinite pronouns (everyone, everybody, someone, nobody, each, either, neither) are grammatically singular and require singular verb forms, even when they refer to multiple people or things. Errors in agreement with these pronouns are among the most common grammar mistakes at Years 5–6.&quot; Students learn to override the &quot;feels plural&quot; instinct by testing with &quot;every single one.&quot;</li><li><em>Writing — Writing Process (Practice)</em> — Editing for tricky SVA: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Writing Process (Practice): Students are expected to &quot;reread and revise their writing to check that it makes sense and is accurate.&quot; Identifying and correcting tricky subject-verb agreement errors — particularly with indefinite pronouns — requires students to understand the grammatical subject of the clause, not just the nearest noun, and is a key editing skill at Phase 2.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Wednesday: Expanding sentences using \"when\" and \"because\"":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">NZ Curriculum Link</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Writing — Sentence Structure (Practice): students expand simple sentences by adding subordinate clauses using &quot;when&quot; and &quot;because&quot; to show time relationships and causation.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>I Do — Add the Why and the When</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Two of the most useful subordinating conjunctions for expanding sentences are &quot;when&quot; (time) and &quot;because&quot; (cause/reason). Watch me use them to transform bare sentences.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Example: Base: &quot;The crowd cheered.&quot; → + when: &quot;The crowd cheered when the final whistle blew.&quot; → + because: &quot;The crowd cheered because the home team had just equalised.&quot;</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&quot;When&quot; tells us the time — the moment the action happened. &quot;Because&quot; tells us the cause — why it happened. Each adds a whole new layer of meaning to the same simple sentence. I can also put the &quot;when/because&quot; clause first: &quot;When the final whistle blew, the crowd cheered.&quot;</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Tip: &quot;When&quot; answers: at what moment? &quot;Because&quot; answers: for what reason? Add the one your sentence is missing.</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>We Do — Expand Together</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Expand each sentence by adding a &quot;when&quot; clause AND a &quot;because&quot; clause. Try both.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The pohutukawa bloomed.</li><li>The students fell silent.</li><li>The explorer turned back.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Discussion prompt: Which expansion is more interesting for each sentence — the when or the because? Why?</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>You Do — Your Turn</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Expand each sentence with the conjunction shown. Try putting the clause at the start AND at the end.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The river flooded. [because]</li><li>The kiwi came out. [when]</li><li>She changed her mind. [because]</li><li>The lights went out. [when]</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write a paragraph of 5 sentences. Use &quot;when&quot; in at least two and &quot;because&quot; in at least two. Vary whether the clause comes first or last.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Objectives (AOs)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Writing — Sentence Structure (Practice)</em> — Expanding with &quot;when&quot; &amp; &quot;because&quot;: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Sentence Structure (Practice): Students are expected to &quot;expand simple sentences by adding words, phrases, and clauses to add detail and description,&quot; including using subordinating conjunctions to express time (when) and cause (because). This lesson revisits and deepens Term 1 complex sentence work in a fresh context, reinforcing that clause order is flexible and that the fronted version requires a comma.</li><li><em>Writing — Sentence Structure (Knowledge)</em> — Subordinating conjunctions: time &amp; cause: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Sentence Structure (Knowledge): &quot;'When' is a subordinating conjunction that introduces a clause expressing time; 'because' introduces a clause expressing cause or reason. Both create complex sentences where one clause depends on the other for full meaning.&quot; Understanding the semantic difference between these two conjunctions helps students choose the right one for the relationship they want to express.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Thursday: Editing: upgrading bland verbs in a passage":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">NZ Curriculum Link</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Writing — Vocabulary (Practice) &amp; Writing Process (Practice): students identify weak, overused verbs in a passage and replace them with precise verbs that carry meaning, character, and energy.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>I Do — Bland Verbs Drain Energy</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Some verbs are so vague they tell us almost nothing: went, got, said, did, made, was. Watch me identify them in a passage and replace them with verbs that do real work.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Example: &quot;The children went to the beach. They got into the water. Dad said they should come out.&quot;</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&quot;went&quot; → &quot;raced&quot; | &quot;got&quot; → &quot;plunged&quot; | &quot;said&quot; → &quot;bellowed&quot;. New version: &quot;The children raced to the beach. They plunged into the water. Dad bellowed at them to come out.&quot; The scene comes alive.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Tip: Highlight every &quot;went,&quot; &quot;got,&quot; &quot;said,&quot; &quot;did,&quot; &quot;made,&quot; &quot;was&quot; in a passage. Each one is an upgrade opportunity.</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>We Do — Upgrade the Passage</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Replace every underlined bland verb with a more precise one. The new verb must match the context.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The kererū went from branch to branch, getting the ripest berries. It made a loud noise with its wings as it went away.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Discussion prompt: What is actually happening physically? What does a precise verb tell the reader that &quot;went&quot; and &quot;got&quot; don't?</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>You Do — Your Turn</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Rewrite this passage, upgrading every bland verb you can find.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>On field trip day, the class got on the bus early. They went to the wetland reserve near Ōtaki. The guide said something about the different birds. Some students went into the hide and got photos of a bittern. Everyone said it was great.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Count how many bland verbs you replaced. Write the before and after side by side. Which passage is more engaging and why?</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Objectives (AOs)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Writing — Vocabulary (Practice)</em> — Upgrading bland verbs: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Vocabulary (Practice): Students are expected to &quot;select and use words and phrases… that are fit for purpose and audience&quot; and to &quot;consider the effect of word choice on the reader.&quot; This lesson targets the most common verb weakness in Years 5–6 writing — overuse of generic verbs (went, got, said) — and builds the habit of asking 'what is ACTUALLY happening?' before selecting a more precise alternative.</li><li><em>Writing — Writing Process (Practice)</em> — Verb audit in revision: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Writing Process (Practice): Students are expected to &quot;reread and revise their writing… making changes to vocabulary, punctuation, and structure to improve clarity and impact.&quot; The verb audit — highlighting every weak verb and asking if it can be upgraded — is a targeted revision strategy that directly improves the energy and precision of students' writing at Phase 2.</li></ul></div></div>"}}},"Week 4":{"label":"Day","children":{"Monday: Apostrophes for contraction":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">NZ Curriculum Link</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Writing — Punctuation (Knowledge): a contraction apostrophe replaces omitted letters when two words are joined; students apply contraction apostrophes accurately and distinguish them from possessive apostrophes.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>I Do — The Apostrophe That Replaces Letters</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>A contraction apostrophe shows where one or more letters have been removed when two words are joined together.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Example: &quot;do not&quot; → &quot;don't&quot; (the apostrophe replaces the 'o') | &quot;they are&quot; → &quot;they're&quot; | &quot;I would&quot; → &quot;I'd&quot;</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&quot;cannot&quot; → &quot;can't&quot; — the apostrophe replaces &quot;no&quot;. &quot;would not&quot; → &quot;wouldn't&quot; — replaces &quot;o&quot;. &quot;she is&quot; → &quot;she's&quot; — replaces &quot;i&quot;. The apostrophe sits exactly where the missing letters were.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Tip: The apostrophe goes WHERE THE LETTERS WERE REMOVED — not just anywhere between the words.</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>We Do — Contract and Expand</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write the contraction for each pair of words, and expand each contraction back to the full form.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>we will → <em><strong>  |  it's → </strong></em></li><li>have not → <em><strong>  |  they'd → </strong></em></li><li>I am → <em><strong>  |  couldn't → </strong></em></li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Discussion prompt: Exactly which letters did the apostrophe replace? Could you be confused between &quot;it's&quot; (it is) and &quot;its&quot; (belonging to it)?</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>You Do — Your Turn</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Fix the apostrophe errors in these sentences — some are missing, some are in the wrong place.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Sh'es not sure if theyre coming to the hangi tonight.</li><li>I wo'nt be ready until the bus ha's already left.</li><li>Weve been waiting here for ages — it isnt fair.</li><li>They're dog isnt allowed inside but its a cold night.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write a conversation (6 lines of dialogue) between two friends making plans. Use at least 6 different contractions correctly.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Objectives (AOs)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Writing — Punctuation (Knowledge)</em> — Contraction apostrophes: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Punctuation (Knowledge): &quot;A contraction apostrophe replaces the letter or letters omitted when two words are combined (e.g. do not → don't, they are → they're, I would → I'd). The apostrophe is placed exactly where the letters were removed.&quot; Students also practise distinguishing contraction apostrophes from possessive apostrophes — a critical and frequently confused distinction at Years 5–6.</li><li><em>Writing — Punctuation (Practice)</em> — Applying contraction apostrophes accurately: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Punctuation (Practice): Students are expected to &quot;use a range of punctuation accurately and for effect,&quot; including apostrophes for contraction. The key test — expanding the contraction to check whether the apostrophe placement makes sense — gives students a reliable self-checking strategy they can apply independently when editing their own writing.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Tuesday: Relative pronouns: who, which, that":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">NZ Curriculum Link</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Writing — Sentence Structure (Knowledge): relative pronouns (who, which, that) introduce relative clauses that give additional information about a noun; the choice of pronoun and use of commas depends on whether the clause defines or adds extra information.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>I Do — Clauses That Give Extra Information</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>A relative clause is a type of subordinate clause that gives more information about a noun. It's introduced by a relative pronoun: &quot;who&quot; (for people), &quot;which&quot; (for things), &quot;that&quot; (for people or things in defining clauses).</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Example: &quot;The woman who won the award is our neighbour.&quot; | &quot;The bridge, which was built in 1902, is being restored.&quot; | &quot;The book that she recommended was brilliant.&quot;</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&quot;who&quot; → refers back to a person. &quot;which&quot; → refers back to a thing, adds non-essential info (use commas): &quot;The river, which floods every winter, is rising again.&quot; &quot;that&quot; → defines which one (no commas): &quot;The path that leads to the pā is steep.&quot;</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Tip: &quot;Who&quot; = people. &quot;Which&quot; = things + commas (extra info). &quot;That&quot; = defines which one, no commas.</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>We Do — Choose the Right Relative Pronoun</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Complete each sentence with who, which, or that.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The rangatira ___ led the iwi was known for her wisdom.</li><li>The old pōhutukawa, ___ had stood for 300 years, was finally protected.</li><li>This is the exact track ___ leads to the summit.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Discussion prompt: Does the clause define which one, or add extra information? Does it refer to a person or a thing?</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>You Do — Your Turn</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Add a relative clause to each sentence using the pronoun given.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The scientist ___ (who) discovered the new species published her findings last month.</li><li>Rotorua, ___ (which) is famous for its geothermal activity, attracts tourists year-round.</li><li>She finally found the notebook ___ (that) she had been searching for all morning.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write a paragraph about a local place or person. Include at least three relative clauses — one with who, one with which (with commas), and one with that.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Objectives (AOs)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Writing — Sentence Structure (Knowledge)</em> — Relative pronouns &amp; relative clauses: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Sentence Structure (Knowledge): &quot;Relative pronouns (who, which, that) introduce relative clauses that modify a noun. 'Who' refers to people; 'which' refers to things and introduces non-defining (parenthetical) clauses set off with commas; 'that' introduces defining clauses with no commas.&quot; Mastering this distinction is a key grammar milestone at Phase 2, with direct implications for comma use.</li><li><em>Writing — Sentence Structure (Practice)</em> — Using relative clauses for detail: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Sentence Structure (Practice): Students are expected to &quot;expand sentences by adding clauses to add detail and description.&quot; Relative clauses are a sophisticated tool for embedding additional information about a noun within a sentence — avoiding the need for a separate sentence and creating more fluent, complex writing at Years 5–6.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Wednesday: Topic sentences for persuasive paragraphs":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">NZ Curriculum Link</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Writing — Text Structure (Knowledge &amp; Practice): persuasive topic sentences state a clear position or claim; students write topic sentences that introduce the paragraph argument and signal the writer's stance.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>I Do — Topic Sentences That Argue</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>In Term 1 we wrote topic sentences for informational paragraphs. This week, topic sentences carry an opinion or argument — they tell the reader not just what the paragraph is about, but what position you're taking.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Example: Informational: &quot;Recycling reduces waste sent to landfill.&quot; | Persuasive: &quot;Recycling is not enough — we must stop producing unnecessary plastic altogether.&quot;</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The persuasive topic sentence does three jobs: (1) introduces the paragraph's main idea, (2) makes the writer's position clear, (3) makes the reader want to know the evidence. &quot;Everyone deserves access to clean water&quot; is both a topic sentence AND an argument.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Tip: A strong persuasive topic sentence takes a clear position. If someone could disagree with it — it's persuasive. If it's just a fact — it's informational.</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>We Do — Upgrade to Persuasive</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Turn each informational topic sentence into a persuasive one by adding a position or claim.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Smartphones are used by many young people.</li><li>Schools have canteens that sell food to students.</li><li>Exercise has effects on health.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Discussion prompt: What position could a writer take on each topic? Add the opinion to make it genuinely persuasive.</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>You Do — Your Turn</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write a persuasive topic sentence for each argument prompt.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Topic: whether students should have homework.</li><li>Topic: whether animals should be kept in zoos.</li><li>Topic: whether Aotearoa should have a four-day school week.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Choose one topic and write the full paragraph — topic sentence + at least two pieces of evidence or reasoning.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Objectives (AOs)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Writing — Text Structure (Knowledge)</em> — Persuasive topic sentences: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Text Structure (Knowledge): &quot;In persuasive writing, each paragraph is organised around a claim or position introduced in the topic sentence. A persuasive topic sentence makes the writer's stance clear and invites the reader to consider the supporting evidence that follows.&quot; Students learn that a topic sentence in argument writing must be contestable — if no one could disagree with it, it is not yet persuasive.</li><li><em>Writing — Text Structure (Practice)</em> — Structuring persuasive paragraphs: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Text Structure (Practice): Students are expected to &quot;organise ideas into paragraphs with a clear main idea, supported by relevant detail&quot; across a range of text types including persuasion. This lesson builds the skill of leading with a clear position before providing evidence — a structural discipline that is foundational to effective persuasive writing at Phase 2.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Thursday: Editing: removing unnecessary apostrophes":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">NZ Curriculum Link</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Writing — Punctuation (Knowledge) &amp; Writing Process (Practice): apostrophes are not used to form plurals; students identify and remove apostrophes used incorrectly in plural nouns.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>I Do — The Apostrophe That Shouldn't Be There</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>One of the most common apostrophe errors is using one to make a word plural. Apostrophes are NEVER used for plurals — only for possession and contraction. Watch me find and fix the errors.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Example: ✗ &quot;We have three cat's and two dog's.&quot; → ✓ &quot;We have three cats and two dogs.&quot;</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&quot;Cat's&quot; — is the cat possessing something? No. Is it a contraction? No. So no apostrophe! &quot;Dog's&quot; — same test, same result. The simple rule: to make a word plural, just add -s or -es. Never add an apostrophe.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Tip: Before adding an apostrophe, ask: is this possession or contraction? If neither — no apostrophe.</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>We Do — Find and Remove</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Remove every incorrect apostrophe from this passage.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>At the market we bought apple's, pear's, and two kind's of cheese. The stall's were busy all morning and the seller's were friendly. By noon, all the basket's were empty.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Discussion prompt: Which apostrophes are wrong? How do you know? What test did you apply?</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>You Do — Your Turn</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Rewrite each sentence, correcting all apostrophe errors. Some sentences may have no error.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The team's jerseys were hanging on hook's in the changing room.</li><li>Both runner's crossed the line within second's of each other.</li><li>The children's laughter echoed across the playground.</li><li>Three dog's barked at the cat's on the fence.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write 5 sentences that correctly use apostrophes for possession, contraction, and plural — making sure plurals never have apostrophes.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Objectives (AOs)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Writing — Punctuation (Knowledge)</em> — Apostrophes: not for plurals: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Punctuation (Knowledge): &quot;Apostrophes are used to show possession and to mark omitted letters in contractions. They are never used to form plurals. The 'greengrocer's apostrophe' (apple's, pear's) is one of the most common punctuation errors in English.&quot; Students develop the two-question test: Is this possession? Is this a contraction? If neither — no apostrophe.</li><li><em>Writing — Writing Process (Practice)</em> — Editing for apostrophe errors: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Writing Process (Practice): Students are expected to &quot;reread and revise their writing to check that it makes sense and is accurate, including checking punctuation.&quot; This lesson builds a targeted apostrophe audit: applying the possession/contraction test to every apostrophe in a passage and removing those that fail both tests — a practical and reliable editing strategy.</li></ul></div></div>"}}},"Week 5":{"label":"Day","children":{"Monday: Brackets & dashes for parenthetical information":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">NZ Curriculum Link</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Writing — Punctuation (Knowledge): brackets and dashes are used to add parenthetical (non-essential) information within a sentence; students choose between them based on the degree of emphasis and formality required.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>I Do — The Aside in a Sentence</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Sometimes we want to add extra information inside a sentence without breaking its flow. Brackets and dashes both do this job — but with different emphasis. Watch me use both.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Example: Brackets: &quot;The tūī (a native honeyeater) feeds on kōwhai nectar.&quot; | Dashes: &quot;The tūī — our boldest garden visitor — feeds on kōwhai nectar.&quot;</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Brackets add a quiet aside — the reader can almost skip it. Dashes shout for attention — they make the parenthetical feel dramatic or important. Use brackets for factual clarifications. Use dashes when you want the reader to pause and notice.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Tip: Remove the parenthetical — does the sentence still make sense? Good — it's truly parenthetical. Now choose: quiet (brackets) or dramatic (dashes)?</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>We Do — Choose the Right Mark</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>For each sentence, decide whether brackets or dashes are more appropriate. Add them correctly.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The pōhutukawa also called the New Zealand Christmas tree blooms in December.</li><li>The final score 14 to 12 in our favour was the best result of the season.</li><li>Captain Cook who arrived in 1769 was the first European to chart New Zealand's coastline.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Discussion prompt: Is this extra information quiet and factual, or dramatic and emphatic? That decides the mark.</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>You Do — Your Turn</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Add brackets or dashes to each sentence. Write a reason for your choice.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The Whanganui River legally recognised as a person in 2017 is sacred to the Māori people.</li><li>My brother the one who never reads finished the whole novel in a weekend.</li><li>Mount Taranaki also known as Egmont is one of New Zealand's most symmetrical volcanoes.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write 4 sentences of your own — two using brackets and two using dashes. Make sure the parenthetical is genuinely non-essential in each.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Objectives (AOs)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Writing — Punctuation (Knowledge)</em> — Brackets &amp; dashes for parentheticals: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Punctuation (Knowledge): &quot;Brackets and dashes are used to add parenthetical (non-essential) information within a sentence. Brackets indicate quiet, factual asides; dashes create emphasis and draw attention to the additional information. Both marks come in pairs, and the sentence must make grammatical sense if the parenthetical is removed.&quot; This is an explicitly expected Phase 2 punctuation skill.</li><li><em>Writing — Punctuation (Practice)</em> — Using parenthetical punctuation for effect: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Punctuation (Practice): Students are expected to &quot;use a range of punctuation accurately and for effect.&quot; Choosing between brackets and dashes is a purposeful punctuation decision — not a random one. This lesson teaches students that punctuation shapes the reader's experience of additional information: brackets whisper, dashes shout. That awareness is the foundation of punctuation as a craft tool.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Tuesday: Comparative & superlative adjectives":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">NZ Curriculum Link</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Writing — Sentence Structure (Knowledge): comparative adjectives compare two things; superlative adjectives compare three or more; students apply the rules for regular and irregular forms accurately.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>I Do — More, Most, -er, -est</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Comparative adjectives compare two things. Superlative adjectives compare three or more. Short adjectives add -er/-est; longer adjectives use more/most; some are irregular.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Example: fast → faster → fastest | beautiful → more beautiful → most beautiful | good → better → best</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Rule: 1–2 syllable adjectives → -er/-est: tall/taller/tallest. 3+ syllable adjectives → more/most: exciting/more exciting/most exciting. Exceptions: good/better/best, bad/worse/worst, far/further/furthest. Test: if &quot;-er&quot; sounds natural — use it.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Tip: Try adding -er: if it sounds natural, use it. If it sounds awkward (&quot;intelligenter?&quot;), use &quot;more.&quot;</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>We Do — Form the Comparatives</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Form the comparative and superlative of each adjective and use each in a sentence.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>cold → <em><strong> → </strong></em></li><li>dangerous → <em><strong> → </strong></em></li><li>good → <em><strong> → </strong></em></li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Discussion prompt: Which rule applies? Are there any exceptions here? Can you use each in an example sentence?</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>You Do — Your Turn</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Complete the comparison chart and write sentences using the correct form.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>happy → <em><strong> → </strong></em>  |  Use &quot;happiest&quot; in a sentence.</li><li>mysterious → <em><strong> → </strong></em>  |  Use &quot;more mysterious&quot; in a sentence.</li><li>bad → <em><strong> → </strong></em>  |  Use &quot;worst&quot; in a sentence.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write a paragraph comparing two animals, using at least four comparative or superlative adjectives.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Objectives (AOs)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Writing — Sentence Structure (Knowledge)</em> — Comparative &amp; superlative adjectives: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Sentence Structure (Knowledge): &quot;Adjectives can be modified to express degrees of comparison. Comparative forms (using -er or 'more') compare two things; superlative forms (using -est or 'most') compare three or more. Some adjectives have irregular comparative and superlative forms (good/better/best, bad/worse/worst).&quot; Students learn and apply both the rules and the exceptions.</li><li><em>Writing — Vocabulary (Practice)</em> — Precise comparative language: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Vocabulary (Practice): Students are expected to &quot;select and use words and phrases… that are fit for purpose and audience.&quot; Using precise comparative and superlative forms — rather than vague modifiers (&quot;more good,&quot; &quot;more bad&quot;) — is a key marker of vocabulary accuracy at Years 5–6. This lesson also reinforces that form choice follows a rule, not guesswork.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Wednesday: Combining short sentences for variety":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">NZ Curriculum Link</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Writing — Sentence Structure (Practice): students combine multiple short sentences into smoother compound and complex sentences, eliminating choppiness and creating better flow.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>I Do — Stitch the Choppy Sentences</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Too many short sentences in a row creates choppy, monotonous writing. Watch me combine short sentences into longer, smoother ones using conjunctions, relative clauses, and participial phrases.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Example: Choppy: &quot;The kererū landed. It ate the berries. It flew away. It was very fast.&quot; → Smooth: &quot;The kererū landed, ate the berries, and flew away with surprising speed.&quot;</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>I combined four sentences into one by removing repeated subjects and joining the actions with commas and &quot;and.&quot; I also replaced &quot;very fast&quot; with &quot;surprising speed&quot; — more precise and vivid. The rhythm is now smooth rather than clipped.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Tip: If three or more sentences in a row are short and start with the same subject — they can probably be combined.</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>We Do — Combine Together</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Combine each group of short sentences into one or two smooth sentences.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The storm arrived quickly. The wind was strong. Trees bent. Branches snapped.</li><li>She practised every day. She was nervous. The performance was coming soon.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Discussion prompt: Which sentences share a subject and can be combined? Which conjunction or relative clause best links the ideas?</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>You Do — Your Turn</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Combine each group into one well-constructed sentence.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The kaiako walked in. She smiled. She placed a pile of books on the desk. She waited.</li><li>The volcano was silent. It had been silent for years. Scientists were watching it. They were worried.</li><li>The dog sat by the door. It was whining. It wanted to go outside. It had been waiting for an hour.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Find a paragraph in your own writing that has more than three consecutive short sentences. Combine at least two pairs.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Objectives (AOs)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Writing — Sentence Structure (Practice)</em> — Combining sentences for flow: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Sentence Structure (Practice): Students are expected to &quot;write a variety of sentence structures for effect and clarity,&quot; including combining short sentences to improve rhythm and flow. Eliminating choppy repetition by merging sentences with conjunctions, relative clauses, and shared verb phrases is a key revision skill at Years 5–6 — it shows control over sentence construction, not just sentence production.</li><li><em>Writing — Writing Process (Practice)</em> — Revising for sentence variety: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Writing Process (Practice): Students are expected to &quot;reread and revise their writing… making changes to vocabulary, punctuation, and structure to improve clarity and impact.&quot; This lesson practises a specific revision strategy: identifying runs of short sentences, diagnosing why they feel choppy, and selecting the best combining method — conjunction, relative clause, or shared verb chain.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Thursday: Peer editing: punctuation variety":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">NZ Curriculum Link</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Writing — Writing Process (Practice): students apply a punctuation variety checklist to their own and a partner's writing, giving and receiving specific feedback on comma, colon, semicolon, and dash use.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>I Do — Reading for Punctuation Variety</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Today I'm going to show you how to read a piece of writing specifically to assess its punctuation variety — not just accuracy.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Example: Checklist: ☐ At least one comma after a fronted adverbial ☐ At least one colon ☐ At least one semicolon ☐ At least one pair of brackets or dashes ☐ No comma splices</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>I read the passage once for meaning. Then I read again specifically for punctuation. I mark each type I find. I notice what's missing. Then I give specific feedback: &quot;You've used commas well in lists, but there are no semicolons — try joining two of these related sentences with one.&quot;</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Tip: Specific feedback is helpful. &quot;Your punctuation is good&quot; is not. &quot;You need a comma after your fronted adverbial on line 2&quot; is.</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>We Do — Punctuation Variety Audit</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Let's audit this passage together for punctuation variety.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The science fair was held on Thursday. Every class had an entry. Our class made a working volcano. It erupted three times. The judges were impressed. We won first place. It was a good day.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Discussion prompt: Where could a semicolon replace a full stop? Where could a dash add drama? Is there a list that needs a colon?</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>You Do — Your Turn</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Swap a piece of your writing with a partner. Edit each other's work for punctuation variety using the checklist.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>✦ Checklist: At least one semicolon used correctly.</li><li>✦ Checklist: At least one colon introducing a list or explanation.</li><li>✦ Checklist: At least one dash or pair of brackets for parenthetical information.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Rewrite your partner's weakest paragraph — the one with the least punctuation variety — as a model of what it could look like.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Objectives (AOs)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Writing — Writing Process (Practice)</em> — Peer editing for punctuation variety: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Writing Process (Practice): Students are expected to &quot;share writing with others and respond to feedback to improve their work.&quot; Peer editing for punctuation variety is a specific application of this expectation — students give targeted, rule-based feedback (not just &quot;it's good&quot;) and practise reading writing through an editor's eye rather than a writer's eye.</li><li><em>Writing — Punctuation (Practice)</em> — Applying a punctuation checklist: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Punctuation (Practice): Students are expected to &quot;use a range of punctuation accurately and for effect.&quot; This lesson consolidates all Term 2 punctuation learning — commas after fronted adverbials, colons, semicolons, brackets, and dashes — in a peer editing context where students must both recognise correct use and explain the rule behind each mark.</li></ul></div></div>"}}},"Week 6":{"label":"Day","children":{"Monday: Greek & Latin roots in everyday words":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">NZ Curriculum Link</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Writing — Vocabulary (Knowledge): many English words are built from Greek and Latin roots; understanding common roots enables students to decode unfamiliar words and expand their vocabulary systematically.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>I Do — Crack the Code</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Many English words are built from Greek and Latin roots. Once you know a root, you can work out the meaning of dozens of words you've never seen before.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Example: &quot;tele&quot; (far) → telescope, television, telepathy | &quot;aqua&quot; (water) → aquatic, aquarium, aqueduct | &quot;bio&quot; (life) → biology, biography, biodiversity</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>If you see &quot;submarine,&quot; you might not know it — but &quot;sub&quot; means under and &quot;marine&quot; means sea. So submarine = under the sea. Same with &quot;microphone&quot; — &quot;micro&quot; (small) + &quot;phone&quot; (sound) = small sound amplifier. Roots unlock words like a key.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Tip: When you hit an unknown word, look for a root you recognise. Even a partial match gives you a clue.</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>We Do — Decode Together</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Use the roots provided to work out what each word means. Then check by looking at the context sentence.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>&quot;geo&quot; (earth): geology, geography, geothermal — what does &quot;geothermal&quot; likely mean?</li><li>&quot;script/scribe&quot; (write): manuscript, inscription, transcribe — what does &quot;transcribe&quot; likely mean?</li><li>&quot;spec/spect&quot; (look/see): inspect, spectator, spectacular — what does &quot;spectator&quot; likely mean?</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Discussion prompt: Can you find other words that share these roots? How does knowing the root help you read unfamiliar texts?</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>You Do — Your Turn</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Use your knowledge of roots to answer each question.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The root &quot;port&quot; means carry. What do these words mean: export, import, transport, portfolio?</li><li>The root &quot;vis/vid&quot; means see. Use it to work out: visible, invisible, vision, evidence.</li><li>The root &quot;terra&quot; means land/earth. What do: terrain, territory, terrarium, extraterrestrial mean?</li><li>✦ Bonus: Find 5 words in a book, magazine, or website that share a Greek or Latin root. List the root, its meaning, and the words that use it.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Objectives (AOs)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Writing — Vocabulary (Knowledge)</em> — Greek &amp; Latin roots: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Vocabulary (Knowledge): &quot;Many English words are built from Latin and Greek roots, prefixes, and suffixes. Understanding the meaning of word parts enables students to work out the meaning of unfamiliar words and to build their vocabulary systematically.&quot; This lesson develops morphological awareness — knowing that &quot;geo&quot; means earth unlocks geology, geography, geothermal, and geocentric simultaneously.</li><li><em>Writing — Vocabulary (Practice)</em> — Using root knowledge in writing: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Vocabulary (Practice): Students are expected to &quot;use knowledge of word origins, roots, and word families to extend vocabulary.&quot; Understanding that &quot;port&quot; means carry (import, export, transport) gives students a transferable tool — they can both decode unfamiliar words when reading and select more precise vocabulary when writing.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Tuesday: Adverbial phrases for time & manner":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">NZ Curriculum Link</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Writing — Sentence Structure (Practice): adverbial phrases extend single adverbs by adding precision and texture; students replace weak single adverbs with richer adverbial phrases that tell the reader more.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>I Do — Beyond Single Adverbs</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>A single adverb tells us how or when. An adverbial PHRASE does the same job but with more precision and colour.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Example: &quot;She spoke quietly.&quot; → &quot;She spoke in a hushed, urgent whisper.&quot; | &quot;He left quickly.&quot; → &quot;He left before anyone could ask questions.&quot;</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&quot;Quietly&quot; tells us the manner. &quot;In a hushed, urgent whisper&quot; tells us manner AND gives texture — we can picture it. &quot;Before anyone could ask questions&quot; tells us time AND hints at motivation. Adverbial phrases carry more weight than single adverbs.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Tip: Spot a lone adverb. Ask: can I replace it with a phrase that tells the reader more? If yes — upgrade it.</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>We Do — Upgrade Together</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Replace each underlined adverb with an adverbial phrase that adds more information.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The tūī sang loudly in the pōhutukawa.</li><li>The tamariki waited nervously for the results.</li><li>The boat left early.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Discussion prompt: What kind of adverbial phrase fits — one of manner, time, or place? Can you add detail and stay true to the context?</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>You Do — Your Turn</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Rewrite each sentence by replacing the adverb in brackets with a precise adverbial phrase.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The kaitiaki worked [hard] to restore the wetland.</li><li>The team celebrated [happily] after the final whistle.</li><li>She finished her speech [suddenly].</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write a paragraph of 5 sentences about an event or journey. Use at least three different adverbial phrases — one of time, one of manner, one of place.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Objectives (AOs)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Writing — Sentence Structure (Practice)</em> — Adverbial phrases for precision: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Sentence Structure (Practice): Students are expected to &quot;expand sentences by adding adverbial and prepositional phrases to express location, time, and manner.&quot; This lesson advances from single adverbs to adverbial phrases — teaching students that a phrase (&quot;in a hushed, urgent whisper&quot;) carries more information and creates a richer picture than a single word (&quot;quietly&quot;) ever can.</li><li><em>Writing — Vocabulary (Practice)</em> — Replacing weak adverbs with phrases: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Vocabulary (Practice): Students are expected to &quot;select and use words and phrases… that are fit for purpose and audience.&quot; Replacing a generic adverb with a specific adverbial phrase is a key vocabulary refinement skill at Phase 2 — it demonstrates that the student is thinking about the reader's experience and choosing language that creates a precise and vivid picture.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Wednesday: Who/where/when phrases in persuasive writing":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">NZ Curriculum Link</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Writing — Composing (Practice): specific who, where, and when details make persuasive arguments more credible and harder to dismiss; students embed precise detail in persuasive sentences and paragraphs.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>I Do — Detail That Persuades</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>In Term 1 we used who/where/when phrases to add descriptive detail. This week we use the same technique in persuasive writing — where specific detail makes arguments more convincing.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Example: Vague: &quot;Schools should have gardens.&quot; → Specific: &quot;Schools across Aotearoa, from urban Auckland to rural Northland, should have gardens where students can grow and harvest their own kai.&quot;</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The added phrases — &quot;across Aotearoa, from urban Auckland to rural Northland&quot; (where) and &quot;where students can grow and harvest their own kai&quot; (who/what) — make the claim feel specific and grounded. Vague arguments are easy to dismiss. Specific ones are harder to ignore.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Tip: In persuasion, specific detail builds credibility. WHO would benefit? WHERE would this happen? WHEN should it change? Answer those questions inside your sentences.</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>We Do — Make It Specific</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Add who, where, or when detail to each persuasive sentence to make it more convincing.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Students should have access to nature.</li><li>Communities need to protect waterways.</li><li>Young people deserve to have their voices heard.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Discussion prompt: Which type of detail makes each argument stronger — who, where, or when? Can you add more than one?</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>You Do — Your Turn</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Expand each bare persuasive claim using who/where/when phrases.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Claim: &quot;Aotearoa needs more native tree planting.&quot; Add a where and a when phrase.</li><li>Claim: &quot;Students learn better outdoors.&quot; Add a who and a where phrase.</li><li>Claim: &quot;Schools should start later.&quot; Add a who and a when phrase.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write a persuasive paragraph (5–6 sentences) on a topic you care about. Every sentence must include at least one who, where, or when detail.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Objectives (AOs)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Writing — Composing (Practice)</em> — Specific detail in persuasion: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Composing (Practice): Students are expected to &quot;use composing strategies to create texts that engage the reader&quot; and to &quot;provide specific, concrete detail that supports the purpose.&quot; In persuasive writing, specificity is a core argument strategy — claims supported by precise who, where, and when details are more credible and more resistant to dismissal than vague generalisations.</li><li><em>Writing — Text Structure (Practice)</em> — Building convincing persuasive paragraphs: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Text Structure (Practice): Students are expected to &quot;organise ideas into paragraphs with a clear main idea, supported by relevant detail&quot; in persuasive writing. This lesson directly builds the ability to support a persuasive topic sentence with specific, embedded detail — the who, where, and when that transforms a claim into a convincing argument.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Thursday: Editing: replacing overused words ('nice', 'good')":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">NZ Curriculum Link</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Writing — Vocabulary (Practice) &amp; Writing Process (Practice): students identify and replace the most overused and vague words in their writing with precise, purposeful alternatives.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>I Do — The Vague Word Hit List</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Some words appear in almost every piece of student writing: nice, good, bad, big, little, went, got, said, things, stuff. They're not wrong — they're just empty. Watch me hunt them down and replace them.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Example: &quot;It was a nice day and we had a good time doing lots of fun things.&quot; → &quot;The sun blazed over the harbour and we spent the afternoon kayaking, laughing until our arms gave out.&quot;</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&quot;Nice day&quot; → specific weather detail. &quot;Good time&quot; → specific activity. &quot;Lots of fun things&quot; → actual examples. The second version creates a picture. The first creates nothing.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Tip: The vague word hit list: nice, good, bad, big, little, went, got, said, things, stuff, very, really. Highlight every one. Each is an upgrade opportunity.</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>We Do — Upgrade Together</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Find every vague word in this passage and replace it with something precise.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We went to the nice beach and it was really good. We did lots of fun things and got really tired. The food was nice and everyone said it was a good day.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Discussion prompt: How many vague words can you find? What specific, precise word or phrase could replace each one?</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>You Do — Your Turn</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Rewrite each sentence, replacing every vague word with a precise alternative.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The movie was really good and the things that happened were very exciting.</li><li>She is a nice person who always says good things to people.</li><li>It was a bad situation and lots of bad things went wrong.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Take a paragraph from your own writing. Highlight every word from the vague word hit list. Rewrite the paragraph with every vague word upgraded.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Objectives (AOs)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Writing — Vocabulary (Practice)</em> — Replacing vague words: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Vocabulary (Practice): Students are expected to &quot;select and use words and phrases… that are fit for purpose and audience&quot; and to &quot;consider the effect of word choice on the reader.&quot; This lesson targets the most pervasive vocabulary weakness at Years 5–6 — over-reliance on generic, high-frequency words — and builds the habit of interrogating each word choice against a concrete alternative.</li><li><em>Writing — Writing Process (Practice)</em> — Vocabulary audit in revision: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Writing Process (Practice): Students are expected to &quot;reread and revise their writing… making changes to vocabulary, punctuation, and structure to improve clarity and impact.&quot; The vague word audit — highlighting every word from the hit list and challenging each one — is a practical revision strategy that directly improves the precision and vividness of students' writing at Phase 2.</li></ul></div></div>"}}},"Week 7":{"label":"Day","children":{"Monday: Colons to introduce lists":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">NZ Curriculum Link</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Writing — Punctuation (Knowledge): a colon introduces a list only when a complete sentence precedes it; students apply this rule accurately and correct common misuses of the colon.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>I Do — The Colon That Sets Up a List</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>We met colons in Term 1. This week we focus specifically on using them to introduce lists — and on the rule about what must come BEFORE the colon.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Example: ✓ &quot;The kete contained three items: a tī kōuka leaf, a piece of bone, and a carved pounamu.&quot; | ✗ &quot;The kete contained: a tī kōuka leaf, a piece of bone, and a carved pounamu.&quot;</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The colon introduces the list — but only when a complete sentence comes before it. &quot;The kete contained three items&quot; is a complete sentence ✓. &quot;The kete contained&quot; is NOT a complete sentence ✗ — so no colon. The rule: a complete sentence must precede the colon.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Tip: Before the colon = a complete sentence. After the colon = the list. Test: can the part before the colon stand alone? If not — remove the colon.</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>We Do — Fix or Confirm</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Decide whether the colon is used correctly in each sentence. Fix those that aren't.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The scientist needed: a microscope, slides, and a sample jar.</li><li>The rescue team brought the following equipment: ropes, a first-aid kit, and torches.</li><li>To make rewena bread you will need: flour, potato water, sugar, and salt.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Discussion prompt: Is the part before the colon a complete sentence? If not, what needs to change?</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>You Do — Your Turn</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Add a colon in the correct place, or rewrite so the colon is used correctly.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The pā required three things to defend effectively high ground, a clear view, and strong palisades.</li><li>There are many native birds in the Waikato region including: tūī, kererū, and pīwakawaka.</li><li>She packed everything she would need for the tramp: food, a sleeping bag, a compass, and wet-weather gear.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write five sentences, each using a colon to introduce a list. Make sure every sentence before the colon is grammatically complete.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Objectives (AOs)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Writing — Punctuation (Knowledge)</em> — Colons before lists: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Punctuation (Knowledge): &quot;A colon is used after a complete clause to introduce a list, explanation, or quotation. A common error is placing a colon after an incomplete clause (e.g. 'The bag contained:') — the part before the colon must be a grammatically complete sentence.&quot; Students learn to test for completeness before adding a colon, developing both accuracy and understanding of clause structure.</li><li><em>Writing — Punctuation (Practice)</em> — Applying colons accurately: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Punctuation (Practice): Students are expected to &quot;use a range of punctuation accurately and for effect,&quot; including colons. This lesson develops the completeness test as a reliable self-checking strategy — students identify whether the part before the colon could stand alone as a sentence before applying the mark, preventing the most common colon error at Years 5–6.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Tuesday: Progressive tenses: past & present continuous":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">NZ Curriculum Link</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Writing — Sentence Structure (Knowledge): past and present progressive tenses express ongoing actions; students form and use these tenses accurately, understanding how past progressive sets a background scene interrupted by simple past events.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>I Do — Actions in Progress</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The progressive (or continuous) tense shows an action that was happening over a period of time — not just a single completed event.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Example: Present progressive: &quot;The kākāpō is climbing the tree right now.&quot; | Past progressive: &quot;The kākāpō was climbing when we first spotted it.&quot;</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Present progressive = is/are + verb-ing: &quot;She is writing.&quot; Past progressive = was/were + verb-ing: &quot;She was writing when the bell rang.&quot; Key use: past progressive sets a scene that another action interrupts. &quot;He was reading when the power cut out.&quot;</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Tip: Past progressive sets the background scene. Simple past is the event that interrupts it. Together: &quot;She was sleeping when the earthquake struck.&quot;</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>We Do — Form and Use</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Complete each sentence with the correct progressive form.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The students ___ (prepare) their speeches when the fire alarm went off.</li><li>Look out the window — the kererū ___ (eat) all the berries!</li><li>While the waka ___ (cross) the harbour, the wind suddenly changed direction.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Discussion prompt: Is this happening right now (present) or was it in progress at a past moment (past)? That decides was/were vs is/are.</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>You Do — Your Turn</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write sentences using the progressive tense as directed.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Write a sentence using past progressive to set a scene, then use simple past for an interrupting event.</li><li>Describe what three different people are doing right now using present progressive.</li><li>Rewrite in past progressive: &quot;The rangatira spoke to the crowd. The sun set behind the hills.&quot;</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write a short recount (5 sentences) of a real or imagined event. Use past progressive at least twice to set the scene before key moments.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Objectives (AOs)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Writing — Sentence Structure (Knowledge)</em> — Progressive tenses: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Sentence Structure (Knowledge): &quot;The present progressive tense (is/are + verb-ing) describes an action happening at the moment of speaking. The past progressive tense (was/were + verb-ing) describes an action that was in progress at a past moment, often interrupted by another event in simple past. Both forms require the auxiliary verb 'be' and the present participle (-ing form).&quot;</li><li><em>Writing — Sentence Structure (Practice)</em> — Using progressive tense for narrative effect: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Sentence Structure (Practice): Students are expected to &quot;write grammatically correct sentences using a range of tenses.&quot; In narrative writing, past progressive is particularly powerful for scene-setting — it creates a sustained background action (&quot;The village was sleeping&quot;) that simple past then disrupts (&quot;when the alarm sounded&quot;). This lesson develops students' control of tense as a narrative craft tool.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Wednesday: Complex sentences using 'although', 'unless', 'while'":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">NZ Curriculum Link</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Writing — Sentence Structure (Knowledge &amp; Practice): 'although', 'unless', and 'while' are subordinating conjunctions that express contrast, condition, and simultaneity; students use them accurately to create nuanced complex sentences.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>I Do — Conjunctions That Add Nuance</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&quot;Although,&quot; &quot;unless,&quot; and &quot;while&quot; are subordinating conjunctions that add nuance — they create contrast, condition, and simultaneous action.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Example: &quot;Although it was raining, the match continued.&quot; | &quot;The match will continue unless there is lightning.&quot; | &quot;While the rain fell, the players kept their positions.&quot;</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&quot;Although&quot; = contrast (it happened despite something). &quot;Unless&quot; = condition (this won't happen IF something else does). &quot;While&quot; = at the same time. Each creates a different relationship — choosing the right conjunction is part of a writer's craft.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Tip: &quot;Although&quot; = despite this. &quot;Unless&quot; = except if. &quot;While&quot; = at the same time as. Choose based on the relationship between your two ideas.</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>We Do — Choose the Right Conjunction</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Complete each sentence with although, unless, or while.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>___ the journey was exhausting, Mere never once complained.</li><li>The kaiako won't begin ___ everyone is seated and listening.</li><li>___ the kettle boiled, she set out the cups and the tea.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Discussion prompt: What relationship exists between the two clauses — contrast, condition, or simultaneity?</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>You Do — Your Turn</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write two sentences for each conjunction — one with the subordinate clause first, one with it second.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>although (contrast)</li><li>unless (condition)</li><li>while (simultaneous)</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write a paragraph of 6 sentences about a challenge or difficult situation. Use although, unless, and while at least once each.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Objectives (AOs)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Writing — Sentence Structure (Knowledge)</em> — Subordinating conjunctions: although, unless, while: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Sentence Structure (Knowledge): &quot;'Although' expresses contrast (despite the subordinate clause, the main clause is still true); 'unless' expresses condition (the main clause won't happen except under the stated condition); 'while' expresses simultaneity (both actions happen at the same time). Each conjunction creates a different logical relationship between the clauses — understanding these distinctions is a key Phase 2 grammar expectation.&quot;</li><li><em>Writing — Sentence Structure (Practice)</em> — Complex sentences for nuance: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Sentence Structure (Practice): Students are expected to &quot;write a variety of sentence structures — including complex sentences — for effect and clarity.&quot; This lesson extends complex sentence use to three conjunctions that require genuine understanding of logical relationships: contrast, condition, and simultaneity — moving students beyond the familiar &quot;because&quot; and &quot;when&quot; into more nuanced subordination.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Thursday: Editing: checking tense consistency":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">NZ Curriculum Link</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Writing — Writing Process (Practice) &amp; Sentence Structure (Knowledge): students apply a systematic verb audit to identify and correct tense inconsistencies in modelled and their own writing.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>I Do — The Verb Audit</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>A tense audit means underlining every verb in a passage, labelling it as past or present, and then correcting any that break the pattern.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Example: &quot;The explorer sets out at dawn. She walked for three hours before she reaches the ridge.&quot; — sets (Pr), walked (P), reaches (Pr).</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Two present, one past — inconsistent. I decide: past tense works better for a recount. Fix: &quot;The explorer set out at dawn. She walked for three hours before she reached the ridge.&quot; — all past, all consistent.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Tip: Be especially careful with &quot;was/is&quot; and &quot;said/says&quot; — these are the most common accidental tense switches.</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>We Do — Audit Together</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Underline every verb, label each P (past) or Pr (present), then rewrite in consistent past tense.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The market is busy on Saturday mornings. Stalls lined both sides of the street. Vendors called out their prices. The smell of fresh bread drifts through the air. Families stopped to chat and children run between the stalls.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Discussion prompt: Which verbs switch tense? What is the dominant tense here? Rewrite consistently.</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>You Do — Your Turn</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Underline all verbs, label each one P or Pr, then rewrite in consistent past tense.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The diver slips below the surface. The water was cold and very clear. She searches for the wreck that locals told her about. After ten minutes, she spots a shadow on the seabed. She swam closer and felt her heart race.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Take a piece of your own writing and do a full tense audit on it. Note how many switches you find.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Objectives (AOs)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Writing — Writing Process (Practice)</em> — Tense audit as revision strategy: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Writing Process (Practice): Students are expected to &quot;reread and revise their writing to check that it makes sense and is accurate.&quot; The tense audit — underline every verb, label the tense, decide on consistency, fix mismatches — is a specific, repeatable revision strategy that goes beyond casual re-reading. It is a direct application of the Phase 2 expectation that students revise systematically.</li><li><em>Writing — Sentence Structure (Knowledge)</em> — Identifying tense in context: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Sentence Structure (Knowledge): &quot;Verbs carry tense and must remain consistent throughout a piece of writing. Tense shifts disrupt the reader's experience and signal a lack of control over the writing.&quot; This editing lesson deepens grammatical knowledge by requiring students to identify and label verb tense in context before applying corrections — connecting knowledge to practice.</li></ul></div></div>"}}},"Week 8":{"label":"Day","children":{"Monday: Spelling: tricky high-frequency words":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">NZ Curriculum Link</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Writing — Conventions (Knowledge): certain high-frequency words have irregular or non-phonetic spellings that must be learned; students apply strategies to memorise and recall these words accurately.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>I Do — Words That Break the Rules</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Some high-frequency words don't follow phonetic patterns — they must be memorised. Watch me demonstrate three strategies for locking these tricky spellings in.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Example: receive, separate, necessary, occurrence, privilege, conscience, rhythm, liaison</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Strategy 1 — Look, Cover, Write, Check: study the word, cover it, write it from memory, check. Strategy 2 — Find the word within the word: &quot;sep-a-rate&quot; (not sep-er-ate) — there's &quot;a rat&quot; in separate! Strategy 3 — Mnemonic: &quot;Rhythm Helps Your Two Hips Move&quot; — first letters spell RHYTHM.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Tip: When you spell a word wrong twice, look for a mnemonic or pattern that makes the correct spelling stick.</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>We Do — Spelling Strategies</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Use at least one strategy to help you learn each of these tricky spellings.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>necessary (one c, two s) — find a mnemonic.</li><li>occasion (two c, one s) — find the pattern.</li><li>definitely (not &quot;definately&quot;) — break it into parts.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Discussion prompt: Which strategy worked best for each word? Can you teach your strategy to a partner?</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>You Do — Your Turn</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Use Look, Cover, Write, Check for each word. Then write a sentence using each one correctly.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>accommodate (two c, two m)</li><li>embarrass (two r, two s)</li><li>government (silent n before m)</li><li>privilege (not &quot;priviledge&quot;)</li><li>✦ Bonus: Create a personal spelling list of 5 words you consistently misspell. Apply one strategy to each and test yourself tomorrow.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Objectives (AOs)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Writing — Conventions (Knowledge)</em> — Tricky high-frequency spellings: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Conventions (Knowledge): &quot;Some high-frequency words have irregular or non-phonetic spellings that must be memorised through repeated practice and strategic learning. Students develop spelling knowledge through a range of strategies including visual memory (Look, Cover, Write, Check), morphological analysis (breaking into meaningful parts), and mnemonics.&quot; Accurate spelling of high-frequency words is a core expectation at Years 5–6.</li><li><em>Writing — Writing Process (Practice)</em> — Checking and correcting spelling: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Writing Process (Practice): Students are expected to &quot;reread and revise their writing to check that it makes sense and is accurate, including checking spelling.&quot; This lesson builds students' metacognitive awareness of their own spelling weaknesses and gives them a toolkit of strategies — rather than relying on spellcheck — for identifying and fixing errors in their writing.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Tuesday: Prepositions of direction & movement":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">NZ Curriculum Link</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Writing — Sentence Structure (Knowledge): directional prepositions express movement through, across, towards, and along specific paths; students use them precisely to create clear, vivid spatial descriptions.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>I Do — Which Way? Prepositions of Direction</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Some prepositions express static location (in, on, under). Others express direction and movement — they tell us where something is going. Watch me use directional prepositions to make movement vivid.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Example: &quot;The waka moved across the harbour, through the channel, and towards the shore.&quot;</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&quot;Across&quot; — movement over a surface from one side to another. &quot;Through&quot; — movement from one side of something to the other by passing inside it. &quot;Towards&quot; — movement in the direction of something. &quot;Along&quot; — movement following the length of something. Each is precise about the geometry of the movement.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Tip: Visualise the movement and ask: is it over a surface (across), inside something (through), in a direction (towards), or following a line (along)?</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>We Do — Choose the Right Preposition</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Choose the most accurate directional preposition for each gap.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The children ran ___ (across/through/along) the beach to the water's edge.</li><li>The tuna moved ___ (across/through/towards) the dark water of the river.</li><li>She walked ___ (along/towards/through) the ridge, keeping the sea in sight.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Discussion prompt: Picture the movement precisely. Which preposition describes the path most accurately?</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>You Do — Your Turn</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Add the correct directional preposition and then rewrite the sentence with more specific detail.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The kererū flew <em><strong> the canopy and </strong></em> the clearing.</li><li>The river flows <em><strong> the gorge and </strong></em> the sea.</li><li>She moved <em><strong> the crowd and </strong></em> the stage.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write a paragraph of 5 sentences describing a journey — by foot, waka, or bike. Use at least four different directional prepositions precisely.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Objectives (AOs)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Writing — Sentence Structure (Knowledge)</em> — Directional prepositions: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Sentence Structure (Knowledge): &quot;Prepositions of direction (across, through, towards, along, past, into, out of) express movement in relation to a surface, an interior, or a destination. Choosing the correct directional preposition requires visualising the geometry of the movement — is it over a surface, inside something, towards a target, or along a path?&quot; Students learn to distinguish between similar prepositions by thinking spatially.</li><li><em>Writing — Sentence Structure (Practice)</em> — Precise spatial writing: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Sentence Structure (Practice): Students are expected to &quot;expand sentences by adding prepositional phrases to express location&quot; and direction. Using directional prepositions precisely — rather than vague alternatives (&quot;went near,&quot; &quot;moved by&quot;) — is a key skill in descriptive and narrative writing at Phase 2, enabling students to place the reader inside the movement they are describing.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Wednesday: Making vague sentences specific":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">NZ Curriculum Link</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Writing — Composing (Practice) &amp; Vocabulary (Practice): students transform vague, general sentences into specific, detailed ones by adding precise nouns, verbs, and modifiers that answer the reader's implicit questions.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>I Do — Vague vs. Specific</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Vague sentences tell the reader almost nothing. Specific sentences give them something to picture. Watch me transform a vague sentence into a specific one by answering the reader's questions.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Example: Vague: &quot;A bird made a sound in the tree.&quot; → Specific: &quot;A tūī burst into a cascading trill from the topmost branch of the pōhutukawa.&quot;</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Reader's questions: WHAT bird? → tūī. WHAT sound? → cascading trill. WHERE in the tree? → topmost branch. WHAT tree? → pōhutukawa. Answering each question turns a generic sentence into one that only this moment could have produced.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Tip: For every noun, ask: what exactly? For every verb, ask: how exactly? For every place, ask: where exactly?</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>We Do — Make It Specific</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Rewrite each vague sentence by answering every implied reader question.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A person walked past.</li><li>The weather changed.</li><li>Something fell.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Discussion prompt: What are the reader's questions for each sentence? What specific detail answers each one?</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>You Do — Your Turn</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Transform each vague sentence into a specific one. Aim for a sentence only THIS moment could produce.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A vehicle went by.</li><li>The animal made a noise.</li><li>She did something with the object.</li><li>The food smelled good.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write 4 intentionally vague sentences, then rewrite each one with maximum specificity. Compare the two versions.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Objectives (AOs)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Writing — Composing (Practice)</em> — Creating specific, vivid writing: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Composing (Practice): Students are expected to &quot;use composing strategies to create texts that engage the reader,&quot; including providing specific, concrete detail that allows the reader to visualise the scene. This lesson teaches the &quot;reader's question&quot; technique — systematically asking 'what exactly? how exactly? where exactly?' to identify and fill gaps that leave writing vague and generic.</li><li><em>Writing — Vocabulary (Practice)</em> — Precise nouns and verbs: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Vocabulary (Practice): Students are expected to &quot;select and use words and phrases… that are fit for purpose and audience,&quot; choosing Tier 2 vocabulary that is precise and cross-curricular. Replacing generic nouns (&quot;bird&quot;) with specific ones (&quot;tūī&quot;) and generic verbs (&quot;made a sound&quot;) with precise ones (&quot;burst into a cascading trill&quot;) is the most direct application of this principle — specificity IS vocabulary.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Thursday: Editing: strengthening narrative openings":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">NZ Curriculum Link</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Writing — Writing Process (Practice) &amp; Composing (Practice): students evaluate narrative openings against criteria for engagement and revise weak openings to draw the reader immediately into the story.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>I Do — First Sentences That Work</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>A weak opening loses the reader before the story begins. Watch me evaluate three narrative openings against criteria, then revise the weakest one.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Example: Weak: &quot;My name is Jake and this is a story about something that happened to me last summer.&quot; | Strong: &quot;The day I found the box, I almost walked straight past it.&quot;</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The weak opening: tells rather than shows, introduces the narrator self-consciously, contains no tension or mystery. The strong opening: drops us into a specific moment, creates immediate curiosity (&quot;what was in the box?&quot;), and doesn't explain itself. Criteria for a strong opening: specific moment, no explaining, creates a question in the reader's mind.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Tip: Test: does the opening make the reader ask a question? If not — rewrite until it does.</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>We Do — Diagnose and Revise</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Evaluate each opening against our three criteria. Then revise the weakest one together.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>&quot;Once upon a time there was a girl who wanted to be an explorer.&quot;</li><li>&quot;The engine coughed twice, then fell silent. In the distance, the lights of the mainland blinked out.&quot;</li><li>&quot;I am going to write about the best day I ever had which was when we went to the beach.&quot;</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Discussion prompt: Which opening drops you into a specific moment? Which creates a question? Which explains too much?</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>You Do — Your Turn</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Rewrite each weak opening so it meets all three criteria.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>&quot;This is the story of how I found out my neighbour was not who she said she was.&quot;</li><li>&quot;One day something strange happened and I will tell you about it in this story.&quot;</li><li>&quot;It was a morning like any other until it wasn't.&quot;</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write three alternative openings for a story of your choice. Choose the best one and explain why.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Objectives (AOs)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Writing — Writing Process (Practice)</em> — Evaluating and revising narrative openings: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Writing Process (Practice): Students are expected to &quot;reread and revise their writing… making changes to vocabulary, punctuation, and structure to improve clarity and impact.&quot; This lesson applies that revision process to the highest-stakes part of any narrative — the opening sentence. Students evaluate against explicit criteria (specific moment, no explaining, reader question) and revise until the opening works.</li><li><em>Writing — Composing (Practice)</em> — Engaging the reader from the first sentence: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Composing (Practice): Students are expected to &quot;use composing strategies to create texts that engage the reader.&quot; Narrative opening strategy is a key composing skill at Phase 2 — students learn that the most engaging openings drop the reader directly into a specific moment, create immediate curiosity, and resist the urge to explain or introduce the narrator self-consciously.</li></ul></div></div>"}}},"Week 9":{"label":"Day","children":{"Monday: Alliteration & assonance":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">NZ Curriculum Link</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Writing — Vocabulary (Knowledge) &amp; Composing (Practice): alliteration (repeated consonant sounds) and assonance (repeated vowel sounds) are sound devices that create rhythm, mood, and texture in writing.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>I Do — Making Writing Sound as Good as It Reads</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Alliteration repeats the same consonant sound at the start of neighbouring words. Assonance repeats the same vowel sound in the middle of words. Both create rhythm and texture that the reader can hear.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Example: Alliteration: &quot;The silent snake slipped through the sedge.&quot; | Assonance: &quot;The rain-slain plain.&quot;</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Alliteration: the &quot;s&quot; sound creates a hissing, slithering effect — perfect for a snake. Assonance: the &quot;ai&quot; sound gives a slow, mournful quality — good for a grey, flat landscape. Both devices work best when they reinforce the meaning or mood, not when they're forced.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Tip: Sound devices should serve the meaning. If the reader notices the device more than the content — it's too obvious.</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>We Do — Hear the Sound</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Brainstorm sound words for each setting, then build a sentence using alliteration or assonance.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Setting: a beach at high tide</li><li>Setting: a forest just after rain</li><li>Setting: a crowded school cafeteria</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Discussion prompt: What sounds would you hear? What sounds could you create with alliteration or assonance?</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>You Do — Your Turn</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write sentences using the device specified.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Write an alliterative sentence about a NZ animal using the &quot;k&quot; sound (kiwi, kererū, kākāpō, kārearea).</li><li>Write a sentence about the sea using assonance with the &quot;ō&quot; or &quot;oo&quot; sound.</li><li>Write a description of a storm using both alliteration and assonance in the same sentence.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write a 4-line descriptive poem about a NZ landscape. Use alliteration in at least two lines and assonance in at least one.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Objectives (AOs)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Writing — Vocabulary (Knowledge)</em> — Sound devices: alliteration &amp; assonance: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Vocabulary (Knowledge): &quot;Language devices such as alliteration (repeated consonant sounds) and assonance (repeated vowel sounds) create specific effects for the reader — rhythm, mood, and texture. They are most effective when they reinforce the meaning of the writing rather than being applied for their own sake.&quot; Students learn to use sound devices deliberately and discriminatingly, not mechanically.</li><li><em>Writing — Composing (Practice)</em> — Using sound devices for effect: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Composing (Practice): Students are expected to &quot;use language devices to create specific effects and engage the reader.&quot; Alliteration and assonance are sound-based composing tools — they work on the reader's ear as well as their mind. This lesson develops the discipline of asking 'what mood am I creating?' before selecting which sound to repeat and how often.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Tuesday: Coordinating vs. subordinating conjunctions: review":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">NZ Curriculum Link</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Writing — Sentence Structure (Knowledge): students consolidate their understanding of coordinating (FANBOYS) and subordinating conjunctions, applying the distinction to build and identify compound and complex sentences accurately.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>I Do — Two Types, Two Jobs</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>We've studied both types of conjunction across Terms 1 and 2. Today we consolidate: coordinating conjunctions join equal clauses; subordinating conjunctions create a main clause and a dependent clause.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Example: Coordinating: &quot;The hīkoi was long, but the marchers kept going.&quot; | Subordinating: &quot;The marchers kept going although the hīkoi was exhausting.&quot;</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>FANBOYS (for, and, nor, but, or, yet, so) = coordinating — both sides could stand alone. AAAWWUBBIS (after, although, as, when, while, until, because, before, if, since) = subordinating — the dependent clause leans on the main clause.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Tip: Test: remove the conjunction and split the sentence in two. If both halves make sense alone — coordinating. If one half sounds incomplete — subordinating.</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>We Do — Classify and Use</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Identify whether the underlined conjunction is coordinating or subordinating, then explain how you know.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The kaiako waited until the class was completely silent.</li><li>The experiment failed, yet the students learnt something important.</li><li>Because the tide was out, we could walk across to the island.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Discussion prompt: Could both sides stand alone? What type of relationship does each conjunction create?</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>You Do — Your Turn</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write sentences using the conjunction type shown.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Write a compound sentence using a coordinating conjunction that shows contrast.</li><li>Write a complex sentence using a subordinating conjunction that shows cause.</li><li>Write a complex sentence where the subordinate clause comes FIRST (with a comma after it).</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write a paragraph of 5 sentences about a place you know well. Use at least two coordinating and two subordinating conjunctions. Label each one.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Objectives (AOs)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Writing — Sentence Structure (Knowledge)</em> — Coordinating vs. subordinating conjunctions: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Sentence Structure (Knowledge): &quot;Coordinating conjunctions (FANBOYS: for, and, nor, but, or, yet, so) join two independent clauses of equal grammatical weight — both sides can stand alone. Subordinating conjunctions (because, although, when, unless, while, until, since, as, before, after, if) introduce a dependent clause that cannot stand alone. Understanding this distinction is essential for building compound and complex sentences accurately.&quot;</li><li><em>Writing — Sentence Structure (Practice)</em> — Compound &amp; complex sentence consolidation: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Sentence Structure (Practice): Students are expected to &quot;write a variety of sentence structures — simple, compound, and complex — for effect and clarity.&quot; This review lesson consolidates the distinction between compound sentences (coordinating conjunctions, comma before) and complex sentences (subordinating conjunctions, comma only when fronted) — integrating Term 1 and Term 2 sentence structure learning at Phase 2.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Wednesday: Paragraph cohesion: pronouns as linking devices":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">NZ Curriculum Link</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Writing — Text Structure (Practice): students use personal pronouns, demonstrative pronouns (&quot;this,&quot; &quot;these&quot;), and pronoun reference to create cohesion within paragraphs, linking sentences into a connected whole.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>I Do — Pronouns That Hold a Paragraph Together</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>We used connective words in Term 1. This week, we focus specifically on pronouns as cohesive devices — how &quot;he,&quot; &quot;she,&quot; &quot;they,&quot; &quot;it,&quot; &quot;this,&quot; and &quot;these&quot; reach back to a previous sentence and pull ideas together.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Example: &quot;The tuatara is one of New Zealand's most ancient reptiles. It has barely changed in 200 million years. This makes it a living link to the age of the dinosaurs.&quot;</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&quot;It&quot; in sentence 2 refers back to &quot;tuatara&quot; — no need to repeat the noun. &quot;This&quot; in sentence 3 refers back to the whole idea of sentence 2. Each pronoun acts like a thread, stitching sentences into a paragraph rather than a list.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Tip: After writing a paragraph, draw an arrow from each pronoun to what it refers to. Every pronoun should have a clear, unambiguous referent.</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>We Do — Stitch It Together</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Replace the repeated nouns with appropriate pronouns to improve paragraph cohesion.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The pūkeko is a bold and territorial bird. The pūkeko will often chase away much larger animals. The pūkeko's bright red beak makes the pūkeko easy to spot in wetland areas. Despite the pūkeko's aggression, the pūkeko is a beloved part of the New Zealand landscape.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Discussion prompt: Which nouns can be replaced with pronouns? Are there any where replacing would cause confusion?</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>You Do — Your Turn</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Rewrite this paragraph so pronouns link the sentences without causing ambiguity.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The kākāpō is a large, flightless parrot. The kākāpō is critically endangered. Conservation teams monitor every single kākāpō. Each kākāpō has a name and a tracking pack. The tracking pack helps conservation teams follow each kākāpō's movements across the island.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write a paragraph of 5 sentences about a NZ animal or place. Use a different pronoun in at least three sentences to link back to the subject. Make sure every pronoun is unambiguous.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Objectives (AOs)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Writing — Text Structure (Practice)</em> — Pronoun cohesion within paragraphs: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Text Structure (Practice): Students are expected to &quot;use cohesive devices — including pronouns, synonyms, and connectives — to link ideas within and across sentences and paragraphs.&quot; This lesson focuses specifically on pronoun cohesion: using personal pronouns (it, they), possessive pronouns (its, their), and demonstrative pronouns (this, these) to refer back to nouns and ideas — creating flow rather than repetition.</li><li><em>Writing — Sentence Structure (Knowledge)</em> — Pronoun reference &amp; ambiguity: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Sentence Structure (Knowledge): &quot;Pronouns must refer unambiguously to their antecedent. When two or more nouns are present, pronouns can create confusion if the referent is unclear.&quot; This lesson develops the habit of checking pronoun reference — drawing arrows from each pronoun to its antecedent — and revising when the reference is ambiguous.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Thursday: Editing: identifying weak sentence openings":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">NZ Curriculum Link</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Writing — Writing Process (Practice) &amp; Sentence Structure (Practice): students diagnose monotonous sentence opening patterns and revise them using fronted adverbials, adjective openers, and rearranged clause order.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>I Do — The Weak Opening Pattern</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>When every sentence starts the same way — especially with &quot;The&quot; or &quot;I&quot; — writing becomes monotonous. Watch me diagnose a passage with weak openings and revise it.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Example: &quot;The sky was dark. The clouds gathered quickly. The wind picked up. The rain began to fall. The thunder rolled in from the south.&quot;</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Five sentences. Every one starts with &quot;The.&quot; Revision: &quot;Dark clouds gathered quickly. As the wind picked up, the first drops of rain began to fall. From the south, thunder rolled in — low and threatening.&quot; Varied openers: adjective, adverbial, fronted phrase. Immediately more dynamic.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Tip: List the first word of every sentence in your paragraph. If more than two start the same way — vary them.</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>We Do — Diagnose Together</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>List the first word of each sentence. Identify the pattern. Then rewrite three sentences with different openers.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I arrived at school early. I put my bag in my locker. I went to find my friends. I sat down and waited for the bell. I felt nervous about the day ahead.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Discussion prompt: What pattern do you see? What three different ways could you begin these sentences instead?</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>You Do — Your Turn</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Rewrite this paragraph, varying the sentence openings so no two consecutive sentences start the same way.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The match started well. The team scored early. The crowd cheered loudly. The other team fought back. The score was level at half time. The players looked tired. The coach spoke to them firmly. The second half began.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write a paragraph of 6 sentences about any topic. Make a rule: no two sentences may start with the same word. List the opening word of each sentence underneath.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Objectives (AOs)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Writing — Writing Process (Practice)</em> — Revising for sentence opener variety: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Writing Process (Practice): Students are expected to &quot;reread and revise their writing… making changes to vocabulary, punctuation, and structure to improve clarity and impact.&quot; This lesson teaches a specific diagnostic strategy — listing the first word of every sentence — that makes repetitive opener patterns immediately visible, enabling targeted revision.</li><li><em>Writing — Sentence Structure (Practice)</em> — Varying sentence openers: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Sentence Structure (Practice): Students are expected to &quot;vary sentence openings to avoid repetition and create effect,&quot; using fronted adverbials, adjective openers, and rearranged clause order. This lesson consolidates all sentence variety strategies from Terms 1 and 2, applying them in a revision context where students must diagnose the problem before selecting the solution.</li></ul></div></div>"}}},"Week 10":{"label":"Day","children":{"Monday: Review: Term 2 punctuation":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">NZ Curriculum Link</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Writing — Punctuation (Knowledge &amp; Practice): students consolidate all punctuation covered in Term 2 — commas after fronted adverbials, colons, semicolons, brackets, dashes, apostrophes for contraction, and comma splices.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>I Do — Term 2 Punctuation Gallery</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Let's revisit every punctuation mark from Term 2 and its rule.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Example: Comma after fronted adverbial | Colon before list | Semicolon joining related sentences | Brackets for quiet aside | Dashes for emphatic aside | Contraction apostrophe | No apostrophe for plurals | Comma splice fix</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&quot;Before the match began, the referee called the captains together. Each team needed the following things: a captain, a strategy, and confidence. The home team was ready; the visitors looked nervous. One player — the one who had trained hardest — stepped forward.&quot;</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Tip: For each mark, ask: what job is it doing here? If it's not doing a job, remove it.</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>We Do — Punctuation Challenge</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Add the correct punctuation to each sentence and name the rule.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Despite the strong wind the waka race continued as planned.</li><li>The pā required three things a strong location high walls and clear sightlines.</li><li>The tī kōuka also known as the cabbage tree is found throughout Aotearoa.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Discussion prompt: Which marks are needed and why? Could any sentence use more than one type?</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>You Do — Your Turn</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Add all missing punctuation to this passage. Name every rule you applied.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>although the weather was overcast the school cross-country went ahead as planned. the course covered three different terrains the playing field a bush track and the school road. hazel our fastest runner had trained every morning before school. she wasnt nervous she was ready. after two kilometres hazel was still in front the other runners couldnt keep up with her pace.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write a paragraph of 6 sentences that uses every Term 2 punctuation mark at least once. Label each one.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Objectives (AOs)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Writing — Punctuation (Knowledge)</em> — Term 2 punctuation review: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Punctuation (Knowledge): Students consolidate all punctuation covered in Term 2: commas after fronted adverbials, colons to introduce lists or explanations, semicolons to join related independent clauses, brackets and dashes for parenthetical information, apostrophes for contraction (not for plurals), and the identification and correction of comma splices. Review ensures students can name the function of each mark and apply the relevant rule.</li><li><em>Writing — Punctuation (Practice)</em> — Applying full range of punctuation: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Punctuation (Practice): Students are expected to &quot;use a range of punctuation accurately and for effect.&quot; This consolidation lesson asks students to apply all Term 2 punctuation conventions in an extended passage — naming the rule behind each choice — building the metacognitive awareness needed to make punctuation decisions independently across all writing contexts at Phase 2.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Tuesday: Review: Term 2 grammar":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">NZ Curriculum Link</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Writing — Sentence Structure (Knowledge): students consolidate all grammar covered in Term 2 — collective and abstract nouns, future tense, SVA tricky cases, relative pronouns, progressive tenses, and conjunction types.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>I Do — Term 2 Grammar Gallery</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Let's revisit every grammar concept from Term 2 — one mini-example each.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Example: Collective &amp; abstract nouns | Future tense: will/going to | SVA: everyone/each | Relative pronouns: who/which/that | Progressive tenses: was-ing/is-ing | Conjunction types: CO vs SUB</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&quot;A pride of lions (collective) shows remarkable loyalty (abstract). Each lion (SVA: singular) has its own role. The pride is going to move (future: planned) at dusk. The leader, who guides (relative: person) the hunt, was circling (past progressive) the herd when the weakest animal stumbled (simple past interrupts).&quot;</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Tip: Grammar is about understanding why a sentence works, not just labelling. Always connect the rule to the meaning it creates.</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>We Do — Grammar Challenge</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>For each sentence, identify the grammar feature underlined and explain what rule it follows.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A flock of starlings was swooping across the estuary.</li><li>Neither of the answers is correct.</li><li>The taonga, which had been in the whānau for generations, was carefully restored.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Discussion prompt: What type of noun/pronoun/verb is underlined? What rule governs it?</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>You Do — Your Turn</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Complete the grammar review tasks.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Write a sentence using a collective noun and an abstract noun.</li><li>Write two future tense sentences — one with &quot;will&quot; (spontaneous) and one with &quot;going to&quot; (planned).</li><li>Write a sentence using a relative clause with &quot;which&quot; (remember the commas).</li><li>Write a sentence using past progressive interrupted by simple past.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write a paragraph of 6 sentences demonstrating at least 5 different Term 2 grammar concepts. Label them in the margin.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Objectives (AOs)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Writing — Sentence Structure (Knowledge)</em> — Term 2 grammar review: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Sentence Structure (Knowledge): Students consolidate their understanding of all grammar covered in Term 2: collective and abstract nouns, future tense using will/going to, subject-verb agreement with indefinite pronouns (everyone, each, neither), relative pronouns (who, which, that), past and present progressive tenses, and the distinction between coordinating and subordinating conjunctions. Review connects knowledge to meaning and use.</li><li><em>Writing — Sentence Structure (Practice)</em> — Integrated grammar application: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Sentence Structure (Practice): Students are expected to &quot;write grammatically correct sentences using a range of word classes and structures.&quot; This review lesson asks students to demonstrate all Term 2 grammar knowledge in context — combining multiple features in single sentences — moving from isolated recognition to integrated, purposeful use at Phase 2.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Wednesday: Review: Term 2 sentence structures":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">NZ Curriculum Link</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Writing — Sentence Structure (Practice): students consolidate all sentence structures from Term 2 — compound sentences in new contexts, complex sentences using although/unless/while, sentence combining, and fronted adverbial sentences.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>I Do — The Term 2 Sentence Toolkit</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This term we added powerful new tools to our sentence toolkit. Let me show each one in action.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Example: FANBOYS in context | Complex: although/unless/while | Combined sentences | Fronted adverbials | Who/where/when expanded</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&quot;Although the hīkoi had been going for three days, the marchers showed no sign of stopping (although). They would not rest unless they reached the marae (unless). While the leaders held talks, the rest of the group set up camp (while). Before dawn the next morning, they set out again (fronted adverbial).&quot;</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Tip: Variety is the goal. Read your writing aloud — if it has a rhythm that changes and shifts, you're using your toolkit well.</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>We Do — Sentence Type Identification</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Identify the sentence type and explain the structural feature that defines it.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Despite the forecast, the outdoor event went ahead without a hitch.</li><li>She wanted to give up, yet something kept her moving forward.</li><li>The waka, which had been carved by hand over two years, was finally blessed.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Discussion prompt: What type is each sentence? What structural feature tells you?</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>You Do — Your Turn</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write one sentence of each type, all on the topic of the sea.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Compound sentence using &quot;yet.&quot;</li><li>Complex sentence starting with &quot;Although.&quot;</li><li>Complex sentence using &quot;unless&quot; — subordinate clause second.</li><li>Complex sentence using &quot;while.&quot;</li><li>Fronted adverbial sentence.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Combine all five sentences into a cohesive paragraph about the sea. Edit for flow and variety.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Objectives (AOs)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Writing — Sentence Structure (Practice)</em> — Term 2 sentence structure review: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Sentence Structure (Practice): Students consolidate all sentence structures from Term 2: FANBOYS compound sentences in varied contexts, complex sentences using although/unless/while, combining choppy sentences for flow, and fronted adverbial sentences with correct comma placement. Students demonstrate the ability to identify structural features by name and produce each type on demand.</li><li><em>Writing — Composing (Practice)</em> — Sentence variety across a paragraph: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Composing (Practice): Students are expected to &quot;use composing strategies to create texts that engage the reader,&quot; including controlling rhythm and variety through sentence structure. This consolidation task — writing five different sentence types on the same topic and combining them into a paragraph — directly tests whether students can select the right structure for each moment rather than defaulting to a favourite pattern.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Thursday: Full-passage edit: Term 2 checklist":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">NZ Curriculum Link</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Writing — Writing Process (Practice): students apply all Term 2 editing skills in a systematic multi-pass edit, using the full Term 2 checklist to revise a passage for punctuation, grammar, vocabulary, and structure.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>I Do — The Full Term 2 Edit</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Today I do a complete multi-pass edit using our full Term 2 checklist. One pass per focus — never try to fix everything at once.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Example: Pass 1: Comma after fronted adverbials | Pass 2: Colons, semicolons, brackets, dashes | Pass 3: Apostrophes (possession AND contraction — no plurals) | Pass 4: Comma splices | Pass 5: Vague verb/word upgrades | Pass 6: Sentence opener variety</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>I read the passage once for meaning. Then Pass 1: check every sentence that starts with an adverbial phrase — is the comma there? Pass 2: look for lists — do they use colons correctly? Pass 3: every apostrophe — possession, contraction, or error? And so on.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Tip: Multi-pass editing catches more than single-pass. Each pass has a specific focus — you're not trying to see everything at once.</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>We Do — Class Edit</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Work through each pass together on this passage.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>despite the rain our school fair went ahead last saturday. the organisers needed three things good volunteers lots of stalls and the right attitude. the fair included many activities a cake stall a bouncy castle a talent show and a silent auction. it wasnt perfect but everyones effort paid off. by the end of the afternoon the school had raised over two thousand dollars for the librarys new books.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Discussion prompt: Pass by pass — what are we fixing? Count the total improvements.</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>You Do — Your Turn</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Edit this passage using all six passes of the Term 2 checklist. Rewrite the improved version.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>on thursday afternoon the year 5 and 6 students went to the wetland reserve near school. the reserve contained many things native plants birds insects and a small stream. although the track was muddy the students werent put off. the guide who had worked there for twenty years showed them a bittern its one of new zealands rarest birds. everyone said it was the best trip theyd ever been on.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Apply the full 6-pass edit to a paragraph from your own writing journal.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Objectives (AOs)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Writing — Writing Process (Practice)</em> — Multi-pass editing: Term 2 checklist: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Writing Process (Practice): Students are expected to &quot;reread and revise their writing… making changes to vocabulary, punctuation, and structure to improve clarity and impact.&quot; This lesson makes the revision process systematic and visible — a structured, six-pass approach that integrates every editing skill from Term 2. Students apply punctuation, grammar, and vocabulary knowledge simultaneously in a single extended editing task.</li><li><em>Writing — Writing Process (Practice)</em> — Term 2 skills: integrated application: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Writing Process (Practice): This consolidation lesson asks students to demonstrate mastery of all Term 2 writing skills in one editing task: fronted adverbial commas, colons and semicolons, brackets and dashes, apostrophes, comma splice correction, vocabulary precision, and sentence opener variety. It directly mirrors the Phase 2 expectation that students &quot;apply writing knowledge and skills across a range of text types and purposes&quot; with increasing independence.</li></ul></div></div>"}}}}},"Term 3":{"label":"Week","children":{"Week 1":{"label":"Day","children":{"Monday: Similes for vivid comparison":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">NZ Curriculum Link</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Writing — Vocabulary (Knowledge): figurative language such as similes compares two unlike things using 'like' or 'as' to create a vivid image; students select figurative language deliberately to enhance meaning for the reader.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>I Do — Comparing Two Unlike Things</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>A simile compares one thing to something else using 'like' or 'as', to help the reader picture it more vividly. Watch me build one.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Example: The waves crashed. → The waves crashed like a thousand drums.</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>I ask: what does this remind me of? Crashing waves — loud, rhythmic, powerful. 'A thousand drums' captures the sound and force. A weak simile (waves like water) tells us nothing new — a strong one adds a fresh image.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Tip: Avoid overused similes (cold as ice, brave as a lion). Push for an original comparison instead.</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>We Do — Build the Comparison</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Let's build a simile for each sentence together. Reject the first cliché answer and push for something fresher.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The thunder was loud.</li><li>Her smile was bright.</li><li>The old house was quiet.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Discussion prompt: What does this remind you of? Is your first idea a cliché — can you find something more original?</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>You Do — Your Turn</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Add an original simile to each sentence using 'like' or 'as'.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The wind howled through the trees.</li><li>The river moved slowly.</li><li>His hands were rough.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write two sentences describing the school playground, each using a different, original simile.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Objectives (AOs)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Writing — Vocabulary (Knowledge)</em> — Figurative language: similes: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Vocabulary (Knowledge): &quot;Figurative language, including similes, compares two unlike things using 'like' or 'as' to create a vivid image for the reader. Students consider the connotations and freshness of a comparison rather than relying on clichés.&quot; This lesson establishes simile as a deliberate, controllable craft tool.</li><li><em>Writing — Vocabulary (Practice)</em> — Selecting original comparisons: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Vocabulary (Practice): Students are expected to &quot;select and use words and phrases, including figurative language, that are fit for purpose and audience.&quot; This lesson builds the habit of rejecting an overused first idea in favour of an original comparison.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Tuesday: Punctuating direct speech":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">NZ Curriculum Link</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Writing — Punctuation (Knowledge): direct speech is enclosed in speech marks, with a comma separating the reporting clause, end punctuation inside the speech marks, and a capital letter starting the spoken words; a new speaker begins a new line.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>I Do — The Rules of Speech Marks</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>When we write exactly what someone says, we use speech marks (&quot; &quot;). There are four rules: 1) speech marks go around the spoken words only, 2) a capital letter starts the spoken words, 3) end punctuation goes inside the speech marks, 4) a comma separates the speech from the reporting clause. Watch me apply all four.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Example: the boy said i am not going home yet</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Fix: &quot;I am not going home yet,&quot; the boy said. — Capital 'I' starts the speech. Comma after 'yet' (inside the marks) before closing them. Lower-case 'the boy said' since it continues the sentence. If the reporting clause comes first: The boy said, &quot;I am not going home yet.&quot;</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Tip: Read the spoken words only — would a capital letter naturally start them? Then check the comma and end mark sit inside the speech marks.</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>We Do — Punctuate the Dialogue</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Add speech marks and correct punctuation to these lines together.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>the teacher said sit down please</li><li>wait for me called Aroha</li><li>I dont understand he whispered</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Discussion prompt: Where do the speech marks go? Where does the comma sit — inside or outside?</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>You Do — Your Turn</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Rewrite each line with correct speech-mark punctuation.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>are we there yet asked the little girl</li><li>the captain shouted all hands on deck</li><li>I will never give up she said quietly</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write a 4-line dialogue between two characters, starting a new line for each new speaker.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Objectives (AOs)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Writing — Punctuation (Knowledge)</em> — Punctuating direct speech: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Punctuation (Knowledge): &quot;Direct speech is enclosed in speech marks. A capital letter begins the spoken words, end punctuation sits inside the speech marks, and a comma separates the speech from the reporting clause. A new line begins each time the speaker changes.&quot; This is an explicit Phase 2 punctuation expectation.</li><li><em>Writing — Punctuation (Practice)</em> — Applying dialogue conventions accurately: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Punctuation (Practice): Students are expected to &quot;use a range of punctuation accurately and for effect,&quot; including the conventions of direct speech, which are tested through both editing and their own narrative writing.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Wednesday: Sentence openers: -ing clauses":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">NZ Curriculum Link</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Writing — Sentence Structure (Knowledge &amp; Practice): a sentence can open with a present participle (-ing) clause that describes an action happening at the same time as the main clause, adding variety to sentence beginnings.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>I Do — Starting with an Action</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Instead of always starting with the subject, we can open a sentence with an -ing clause describing an action happening at the same time as the main action. Watch me build one.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Example: Normal: The dog burst through the door. It was barking loudly. → Combined: Barking loudly, the dog burst through the door.</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Both actions (barking, bursting through) happen at the same time, so I can combine them. The -ing clause goes first, followed by a comma, then the main clause. This creates a more dynamic opening than 'The dog burst through the door, barking loudly.'</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Tip: Check: are both actions truly happening at the same moment? If one happens before the other, this structure won't make sense.</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>We Do — Combine with an -ing Opener</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Combine each pair of sentences using an -ing clause opener.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The climber gripped the rock face. She was gasping for breath.</li><li>The crowd surged forward. They were cheering wildly.</li><li>The cat crept along the fence. It was watching the bird.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Discussion prompt: Are both actions happening at the same time? Where does the comma go?</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>You Do — Your Turn</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Combine each pair using an -ing opener, remembering the comma.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The waves pounded the shore. They were roaring furiously.</li><li>The toddler reached for the toy. She was giggling with excitement.</li><li>The soldiers marched into camp. They were singing a marching song.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write two original sentences that begin with an -ing clause.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Objectives (AOs)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Writing — Sentence Structure (Knowledge)</em> — -ing clause sentence openers: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Sentence Structure (Knowledge): &quot;A sentence can open with a present participle (-ing) clause describing an action simultaneous with the main clause, followed by a comma. This is one of several techniques for varying sentence openings beyond the subject.&quot; Students learn the simultaneity condition that makes this structure work.</li><li><em>Writing — Sentence Structure (Practice)</em> — Varying sentence openings for effect: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Sentence Structure (Practice): Students are expected to &quot;write a variety of sentence structures for effect and clarity,&quot; including varied sentence openers. This builds directly on Term 1's fronted adverbial work, adding a new opener type to the student's toolkit.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Thursday: Editing: show, don't tell":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">NZ Curriculum Link</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Writing — Composing (Practice) &amp; Writing Process (Practice): students revise writing by replacing 'telling' statements about emotion or quality with specific sensory and behavioural detail that allows the reader to infer it themselves.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>I Do — Let the Reader Work It Out</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>'Telling' states an emotion directly (she was sad). 'Showing' gives details that let the reader infer the emotion themselves. Watch me convert one to the other.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Example: Telling: She was nervous before the race.</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>What does nervousness look like physically? Showing: Her hands trembled as she rubbed them on her shorts, and she couldn't stop glancing at the starting line. I haven't used the word 'nervous' once — but the reader feels it.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Tip: Ask: what would a camera see? Convert the emotion word into an action, gesture, or physical sensation.</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>We Do — Convert Together</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Convert each 'telling' sentence into 'showing' detail.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>He was angry.</li><li>She was excited about the trip.</li><li>The dog was scared of the storm.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Discussion prompt: What physical signs would show this emotion? What would the character do or say?</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>You Do — Your Turn</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Rewrite each telling sentence using show-don't-tell.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The old man was tired.</li><li>The children were bored in class.</li><li>She was proud of her painting.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Take a sentence from your own writing that 'tells' an emotion, and rewrite it to 'show' instead.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Objectives (AOs)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Writing — Composing (Practice)</em> — Show, don't tell: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Composing (Practice): Students are expected to &quot;use composing strategies to create texts that engage the reader,&quot; including conveying emotion through specific, concrete, sensory detail rather than direct statement. This is a key narrative craft technique at Years 5–6.</li><li><em>Writing — Writing Process (Practice)</em> — Revising for engagement: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Writing Process (Practice): Students are expected to &quot;reread and revise their writing… making changes to vocabulary, punctuation, and structure to improve clarity and impact.&quot; This lesson gives students a specific, repeatable revision question — 'what would a camera see?' — to apply to their own narrative writing.</li></ul></div></div>"}}},"Week 2":{"label":"Day","children":{"Monday: Personification":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">NZ Curriculum Link</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Writing — Vocabulary (Knowledge): personification gives human qualities or actions to non-human things, creating atmosphere and making descriptive writing more vivid and engaging.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>I Do — Giving Objects Human Behaviour</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Personification gives a non-human thing a human action or feeling. Watch me transform a flat description using it.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Example: The wind blew through the trees. → The wind whispered secrets through the trees.</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Wind can't literally whisper — but giving it that human action creates atmosphere (something mysterious or gentle). I match the personification to the mood I want: a storm might 'scream' or 'tear', a calm wind might 'sigh' or 'tiptoe'.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Tip: Choose a human verb that matches the mood you're creating — gentle, threatening, playful.</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>We Do — Bring It to Life</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Add personification to each sentence to create the mood shown.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The fire (mood: angry/threatening) ___</li><li>The moon (mood: calm/watchful) ___</li><li>The old gate (mood: tired/creaky) ___</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Discussion prompt: What human action or feeling matches this mood? Does it fit the object?</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>You Do — Your Turn</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Add personification to each sentence.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The rain fell on the roof.</li><li>The shadows stretched across the lawn.</li><li>The kettle began to whistle.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write two sentences describing a storm, using personification in each to build a frightening mood.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Objectives (AOs)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Writing — Vocabulary (Knowledge)</em> — Figurative language: personification: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Vocabulary (Knowledge): &quot;Figurative language, including personification, gives human qualities to non-human things to create atmosphere and engage the reader. Word choice should be deliberate and matched to the intended mood.&quot; This extends Term 3 Week 1's simile work into a second figurative device.</li><li><em>Writing — Composing (Practice)</em> — Creating mood and atmosphere: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Composing (Practice): Students are expected to &quot;use composing strategies, including figurative language, to create texts that engage the reader.&quot; This lesson connects vocabulary choice directly to the deliberate creation of mood and atmosphere in descriptive and narrative writing.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Tuesday: Active and passive voice":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">NZ Curriculum Link</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Writing — Sentence Structure (Knowledge): in the active voice the subject performs the action; in the passive voice the subject receives the action. Students identify and form both, understanding when each is appropriate.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>I Do — Who Is Doing What?</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>In the active voice, the subject does the action (The dog chased the cat). In the passive voice, the subject receives the action (The cat was chased by the dog). Watch me convert one to the other.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Example: Active: The storm destroyed the bridge. → Passive: The bridge was destroyed by the storm.</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>I move the object ('the bridge') to the front as the new subject, use a form of 'to be' + past participle ('was destroyed'), and the original subject ('the storm') becomes optional, introduced by 'by'. Active voice is usually punchier; passive voice is useful when the action matters more than who did it.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Tip: Test for passive voice: can you add '...by zombies' after the verb and it still makes sense? (was destroyed by zombies ✓) — that's a sign of passive voice.</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>We Do — Convert Together</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Convert each active sentence to passive, and discuss why a writer might choose one over the other.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The council planted the trees.</li><li>A thief stole the painting.</li><li>The scientists discovered the fossil.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Discussion prompt: What is the new subject? What form of 'to be' do we need? Does this sentence sound better active or passive?</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>You Do — Your Turn</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Convert each sentence as instructed.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Convert to passive: 'The chef prepared the meal.'</li><li>Convert to active: 'The window was broken by the storm.'</li><li>Convert to passive: 'The librarian organised the books.'</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write one active and one passive sentence about the same event — which version do you prefer, and why?</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Objectives (AOs)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Writing — Sentence Structure (Knowledge)</em> — Active &amp; passive voice: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Sentence Structure (Knowledge): &quot;In the active voice, the subject performs the action of the verb; in the passive voice, the subject receives the action, formed using 'to be' plus the past participle. Writers choose between active and passive voice deliberately, depending on what they want to emphasise.&quot; This is a key Phase 2 grammar concept underpinning formal and explanation writing.</li><li><em>Writing — Sentence Structure (Practice)</em> — Choosing voice for purpose: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Sentence Structure (Practice): Students are expected to &quot;write a variety of sentence structures for effect and clarity&quot; and to make deliberate grammatical choices appropriate to text type — active voice typically suits narrative, while passive voice often suits formal explanation and report writing.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Wednesday: Joining ideas with semicolons":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">NZ Curriculum Link</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Writing — Sentence Structure (Practice): a semicolon joins two closely related independent clauses without a conjunction, creating a more sophisticated link between balanced ideas than a full stop or comma splice.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>I Do — A Sharper Link Than a Full Stop</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>We met the semicolon in Term 1. Today we focus on using it to join two closely related, balanced ideas for sophisticated effect.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Example: The crowd fell silent. The referee blew the whistle. → The crowd fell silent; the referee blew the whistle.</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Both sides are complete sentences, closely linked in time and meaning — a full stop works, but the semicolon shows the reader these two moments are connected. A comma alone here would be a comma splice (an error) — only a semicolon, full stop, or conjunction can join two complete sentences.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Tip: Test: could both halves stand alone as separate sentences? If yes, and they're closely related — a semicolon is a good choice.</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>We Do — Join with a Semicolon</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Join each pair using a semicolon. Discuss why these two ideas are closely related enough to deserve one.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The sky darkened. The first drops of rain began to fall.</li><li>She trained for months. Her hard work finally paid off.</li><li>The engine spluttered. Then it died completely.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Discussion prompt: Are both halves complete sentences? Are they closely related in meaning?</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>You Do — Your Turn</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Join each pair with a semicolon.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The team celebrated. They had won the championship.</li><li>The fire alarm rang. Everyone filed outside calmly.</li><li>He apologised sincerely. She decided to forgive him.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write two of your own semicolon sentences about a sporting event.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Objectives (AOs)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Writing — Sentence Structure (Practice)</em> — Semicolons for sophisticated links: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Sentence Structure (Practice): Students are expected to &quot;use a range of punctuation accurately and for effect,&quot; including semicolons to join closely related independent clauses. This Term 3 lesson revisits and deepens Term 1's introduction, focusing on the judgement of when two ideas are 'closely related enough' to deserve one.</li><li><em>Writing — Punctuation (Knowledge)</em> — Avoiding comma splices: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Punctuation (Knowledge): &quot;Two independent clauses cannot be joined by a comma alone (a comma splice); they must be joined by a full stop, a semicolon, or a coordinating conjunction.&quot; This lesson reinforces that boundary while extending students' control of the semicolon as a deliberate stylistic choice.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Thursday: Editing dialogue punctuation":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">NZ Curriculum Link</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Writing — Writing Process (Practice): students apply the rules of direct speech punctuation systematically when revising a passage of dialogue, checking speech marks, capitalisation, end punctuation, and new lines for new speakers.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>I Do — The Dialogue Edit</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>I'll show you how to check a whole passage of dialogue against our four rules: speech marks around spoken words only, capital letter starting speech, end punctuation inside the marks, comma before the reporting clause, and a new line for each new speaker.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Example: hello said Sam how are you today im fine thanks replied Mia</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>I read slowly and apply one rule at a time. Fix: &quot;Hello,&quot; said Sam. &quot;How are you today?&quot; — new line — &quot;I'm fine, thanks,&quot; replied Mia. Notice Sam's two sentences both need their own speech marks since 'said Sam' interrupts.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Tip: If the reporting clause interrupts mid-speech, close the speech marks before it and reopen them after, with their own punctuation each time.</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>We Do — Fix the Dialogue</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Fix the punctuation in this short dialogue together, one rule at a time.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>where are you going asked dad im going to the library said Leo can I come too he asked sure replied Leo</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Discussion prompt: Where does each speaker change? Where do the speech marks, capitals, and commas go?</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>You Do — Your Turn</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Rewrite this dialogue with full, correct punctuation and new lines for each speaker.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>did you finish your homework asked mum not yet I was about to start said Tom you'd better hurry she said the bus leaves soon</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write your own 4-line dialogue exchange between two characters arguing about something small, with perfect punctuation.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Objectives (AOs)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Writing — Writing Process (Practice)</em> — Systematic dialogue editing: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Writing Process (Practice): Students are expected to &quot;reread and revise their writing to check that it makes sense and is accurate, including checking for correct punctuation.&quot; This lesson applies that revision process specifically to direct speech — one of the most error-prone punctuation areas for Years 5–6 writers.</li><li><em>Writing — Punctuation (Practice)</em> — Applying speech punctuation accurately: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Punctuation (Practice): Students are expected to &quot;use a range of punctuation accurately and for effect,&quot; including the conventions of direct speech. Editing a multi-line dialogue passage consolidates the rule set introduced in Week 1 into an independent, transferable checking strategy.</li></ul></div></div>"}}},"Week 3":{"label":"Day","children":{"Monday: Metaphors":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">NZ Curriculum Link</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Writing — Vocabulary (Knowledge): a metaphor describes one thing directly as another (without 'like' or 'as') to create a powerful, often surprising image; students use metaphor deliberately and avoid mixing metaphors.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>I Do — Saying One Thing Is Another</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Unlike a simile, a metaphor says something IS something else — no 'like' or 'as'. It's bolder and can be more powerful. Watch me build one.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Example: Simile: Her eyes were like diamonds. → Metaphor: Her eyes were diamonds, glittering in the firelight.</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Removing 'like' makes the comparison feel more absolute and dramatic. I extend it slightly ('glittering in the firelight') to make the image fuller. Careful: I shouldn't then say her eyes 'sparkled like stars' in the same sentence — that mixes two different metaphors and confuses the reader.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Tip: Stick to ONE metaphor per image. Mixing metaphors (her eyes were diamonds, twinkling like a lighthouse) confuses rather than clarifies.</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>We Do — Build the Metaphor</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Turn each description into a metaphor.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The classroom (mood: chaotic) was ___</li><li>The mountain (mood: ancient, unmoving) was ___</li><li>Her voice (mood: soft, calming) was ___</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Discussion prompt: What single bold image captures this mood? Are you keeping it to one consistent image?</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>You Do — Your Turn</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write a metaphor for each prompt.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Describe a busy motorway using a metaphor.</li><li>Describe a quiet library using a metaphor.</li><li>Describe an angry sky before a storm using a metaphor.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write two sentences about the ocean — one using a simile, one using a metaphor. Which is more powerful, and why?</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Objectives (AOs)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Writing — Vocabulary (Knowledge)</em> — Figurative language: metaphor: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Vocabulary (Knowledge): &quot;A metaphor describes one thing directly as another, without using 'like' or 'as', to create a powerful and often surprising image. Effective metaphors are sustained and not mixed with unrelated comparisons.&quot; This builds on Week 1's simile work to complete the simile/metaphor distinction.</li><li><em>Writing — Vocabulary (Practice)</em> — Avoiding mixed metaphors: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Vocabulary (Practice): Students are expected to &quot;select and use words and phrases, including figurative language, that are fit for purpose and audience,&quot; and to consider the coherence of the images they create — recognising and avoiding mixed metaphors is part of controlling figurative language deliberately.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Tuesday: Modal verbs":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">NZ Curriculum Link</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Writing — Sentence Structure (Knowledge): modal verbs (can, could, may, might, must, should, will, would) express degrees of possibility, certainty, permission, or obligation; students select modal verbs deliberately to convey precise meaning.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>I Do — Shades of Possibility</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Modal verbs (can, could, may, might, must, should, will, would) change how certain or possible something sounds. Watch how the same sentence shifts meaning with different modals.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Example: It will rain tomorrow. (certain) | It might rain tomorrow. (possible) | It must have rained last night. (strong inference)</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>'Will' = confident prediction. 'Might'/'may' = uncertain possibility. 'Must' = strong logical conclusion based on evidence. 'Should' = expectation or recommendation. Choosing the right modal tells the reader exactly how sure you are.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Tip: Before choosing a modal, ask: how certain am I, really? Match the word to your actual confidence.</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>We Do — Choose the Right Modal</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Choose the modal verb that best fits the level of certainty described.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>(Strong recommendation) You ___ wear a helmet when cycling.</li><li>(Pure possibility, 50/50) The bus ___ be late today.</li><li>(Near-certain conclusion from evidence) She ___ have left already — her bag is gone.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Discussion prompt: How certain is the speaker? Which modal verb matches that level of certainty exactly?</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>You Do — Your Turn</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Complete each sentence with the most precise modal verb, and explain your choice.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Based on the dark clouds, a storm ___ be coming.</li><li>Students ___ complete their homework by Friday (obligation).</li><li>I ___ be free this weekend, but I'm not certain yet.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write three sentences about a mystery, using a different modal verb in each to show increasing certainty.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Objectives (AOs)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Writing — Sentence Structure (Knowledge)</em> — Modal verbs and certainty: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Sentence Structure (Knowledge): &quot;Modal verbs (can, could, may, might, must, should, will, would) express degrees of possibility, permission, obligation, or certainty. Precise modal choice allows a writer to convey exactly how confident or tentative a claim is.&quot; This is an important grammar tool for persuasive and explanatory writing at Phase 2.</li><li><em>Writing — Vocabulary (Practice)</em> — Precision in modal choice: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Vocabulary (Practice): Students are expected to &quot;select and use words and phrases… that are fit for purpose and audience.&quot; Choosing the correct modal verb is a precision skill directly relevant to persuasive argument and explanation writing, where overstating or understating certainty weakens credibility.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Wednesday: Sentence openers: -ed clauses":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">NZ Curriculum Link</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Writing — Sentence Structure (Knowledge &amp; Practice): a sentence can open with a past participle (-ed) clause describing a completed state or action affecting the subject of the main clause, adding further variety to sentence beginnings.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>I Do — Starting with a Completed State</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>An -ed clause opener describes a state or completed action affecting the subject, before the main action. Watch me build one.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Example: Normal: The hikers were exhausted. They collapsed at the summit. → Combined: Exhausted, the hikers collapsed at the summit.</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>'Exhausted' describes their state before/as they collapsed. I drop the subject and verb from the first sentence, keep just the -ed word (or phrase), add a comma, then the main clause. Try a longer one: 'Battered by the storm, the old fishing boat limped into harbour.'</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Tip: -ed openers often describe a state (tired, battered, convinced) rather than an ongoing action — that's the key difference from -ing openers.</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>We Do — Combine with an -ed Opener</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Combine each pair using an -ed clause opener.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The team was defeated. They trudged off the field in silence.</li><li>The cake was burnt. It was thrown straight in the bin.</li><li>The students were convinced by the argument. They voted unanimously in favour.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Discussion prompt: What -ed word or phrase captures the state? Where does the comma go?</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>You Do — Your Turn</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Combine each pair using an -ed opener.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The dog was soaked from the rain. It shook itself violently in the hallway.</li><li>The old bridge was weakened by years of neglect. It finally collapsed in the flood.</li><li>The crowd was thrilled by the performance. They leapt to their feet.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write two original sentences that begin with an -ed clause.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Objectives (AOs)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Writing — Sentence Structure (Knowledge)</em> — -ed clause sentence openers: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Sentence Structure (Knowledge): &quot;A sentence can open with a past participle (-ed) clause describing a completed state affecting the subject of the main clause, followed by a comma. This complements -ing clause openers as a further technique for varying sentence beginnings.&quot; Students learn to distinguish state (-ed) from ongoing action (-ing) openers.</li><li><em>Writing — Sentence Structure (Practice)</em> — Sentence opener variety: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Sentence Structure (Practice): Students are expected to &quot;write a variety of sentence structures for effect and clarity.&quot; This lesson completes a two-part sequence (Week 1: -ing, Week 3: -ed) giving students two further reliable, controllable sentence-opening techniques beyond fronted adverbials.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Thursday: Editing for active voice":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">NZ Curriculum Link</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Writing — Writing Process (Practice) &amp; Sentence Structure (Practice): students identify overused passive voice constructions in a passage and convert them to active voice where this strengthens clarity and energy.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>I Do — Hunting the Passive</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Passive voice has its uses, but overusing it makes writing feel vague and distant. Watch me find and convert unnecessary passive sentences.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Example: The ball was kicked by the striker. It was caught by the goalkeeper.</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Test: can I add '...by zombies' and it still works? Yes — passive. Who is doing the action? The striker, the goalkeeper. Convert: The striker kicked the ball. The goalkeeper caught it. Much more direct and energetic — better for action narrative.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Tip: Highlight every 'was/were + past participle' in a passage. Ask: does the reader need to know WHO did it? If yes, switch to active.</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>We Do — Convert Together</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Find and convert the unnecessary passive sentences in this passage.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The ball was thrown by Mia. It was caught by Sam. A point was scored by the team. The crowd was excited by the win.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Discussion prompt: Which sentences would be stronger as active voice? Who is doing each action?</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>You Do — Your Turn</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Rewrite this passage, converting passive sentences to active where it strengthens the writing.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The trophy was lifted by the captain. Photos were taken by the parents. Cheers were given by everyone. The team was congratulated by the coach.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Find a piece of your own writing with passive voice. Decide if it should stay passive (formal/unknown doer) or switch to active (energy, clarity).</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Objectives (AOs)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Writing — Writing Process (Practice)</em> — Editing overused passive voice: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Writing Process (Practice): Students are expected to &quot;reread and revise their writing… making changes to vocabulary, punctuation, and structure to improve clarity and impact.&quot; This lesson teaches the 'by zombies' test as a quick, memorable way to spot unnecessary passive constructions during self-editing.</li><li><em>Writing — Sentence Structure (Practice)</em> — Choosing voice deliberately: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Sentence Structure (Practice): Students are expected to make deliberate choices between active and passive voice depending on purpose. This lesson reinforces Week 2's grammar knowledge by applying it as a genuine editing decision rather than a grammar exercise alone.</li></ul></div></div>"}}},"Week 4":{"label":"Day","children":{"Monday: Connectives for persuasive writing":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">NZ Curriculum Link</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Writing — Vocabulary (Knowledge): cohesive connectives (firstly, furthermore, however, therefore, in addition, on the other hand) signal the relationship between ideas and structure an argument logically for the reader.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>I Do — Signposting Your Argument</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Persuasive connectives tell the reader how one idea relates to the next: adding (furthermore, in addition), contrasting (however, on the other hand), or concluding (therefore, as a result). Watch me use them to structure an argument.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Example: Schools should start later. Students get more sleep. Some people disagree. Later starts may clash with parents' work hours. We should still consider it.</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>With connectives: Schools should start later. Firstly, students would get more sleep. However, later starts may clash with parents' work hours. Nevertheless, the benefits to student wellbeing make this worth considering. The connectives turn a list of ideas into a structured argument.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Tip: Match the connective to the job: adding evidence? furthermore/moreover. Contrasting a counter-point? however/on the other hand. Drawing a conclusion? therefore/as a result.</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>We Do — Choose the Right Connective</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Choose the connective that best links each pair of ideas.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Recycling reduces landfill waste. ___, it conserves natural resources. (adding)</li><li>Exercise has many benefits. ___, not everyone has time for it. (contrasting)</li><li>Plastic pollutes the ocean. ___, we should reduce single-use plastic. (concluding)</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Discussion prompt: Is this adding, contrasting, or concluding? Which connective fits that job?</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>You Do — Your Turn</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Add an appropriate connective to link each pair of sentences.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Homework helps students practise skills. ___, too much homework can cause stress.</li><li>Cities need more cycle lanes. ___, this would reduce traffic congestion.</li><li>Some students prefer working alone. ___, group work builds important teamwork skills.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write a 4-sentence persuasive paragraph on a topic of your choice, using at least three different connectives.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Objectives (AOs)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Writing — Vocabulary (Knowledge)</em> — Persuasive connectives: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Vocabulary (Knowledge): &quot;Cohesive connectives signal the logical relationship between ideas — addition, contrast, cause, or conclusion — and are essential to structuring a persuasive argument clearly for the reader.&quot; Students learn to categorise connectives by function rather than memorising them as a random list.</li><li><em>Writing — Text Structure (Practice)</em> — Structuring an argument: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Text Structure (Practice): Students are expected to &quot;use cohesive devices to link ideas within and across sentences and paragraphs&quot; in persuasive writing. This lesson gives students a functional connective bank organised by the job each one does within an argument.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Tuesday: Coordinating vs subordinating conjunctions":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">NZ Curriculum Link</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Writing — Sentence Structure (Knowledge): coordinating conjunctions (and, but, or, so) join equal ideas to form compound sentences, while subordinating conjunctions (because, although, when, if) join a dependent clause to a main clause to form complex sentences.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>I Do — Equal Partners vs. Unequal Partners</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Coordinating conjunctions (and, but, or, so, yet) join two equal, independent ideas — a compound sentence. Subordinating conjunctions (because, although, when, if, since) join a dependent clause to a main clause — a complex sentence. Watch the difference.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Example: Compound: She trained hard, and she won the race. | Complex: She won the race because she trained hard.</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>In the compound sentence, both halves could stand alone as full sentences — they're equal partners joined by 'and'. In the complex sentence, 'because she trained hard' cannot stand alone — it depends on the main clause for its full meaning.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Tip: Test: read the clause after the conjunction alone. If it makes complete sense by itself — coordinating. If it sounds incomplete — subordinating.</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>We Do — Sort and Join</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Decide whether each conjunction is coordinating or subordinating, then use it to join the sentence pair correctly.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>because — The match was cancelled. The field was flooded.</li><li>but — She wanted to go outside. It was raining heavily.</li><li>although — He was tired. He kept practising.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Discussion prompt: Is this conjunction joining two equal ideas, or making one idea depend on the other?</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>You Do — Your Turn</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Join each pair using the conjunction given, and label the sentence as compound or complex.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Use 'so': The bus was late. We missed the start of the film.</li><li>Use 'when': The bell rang. The students rushed to lunch.</li><li>Use 'or': You can hand in your work today. You can hand it in tomorrow.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write one compound and one complex sentence about the same event — point out which conjunction made each one which type.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Objectives (AOs)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Writing — Sentence Structure (Knowledge)</em> — Coordinating vs subordinating conjunctions: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Sentence Structure (Knowledge): &quot;Coordinating conjunctions (and, but, or, so, yet) join two independent clauses of equal grammatical weight to form a compound sentence. Subordinating conjunctions (because, although, when, if, since) join a dependent clause to an independent main clause to form a complex sentence.&quot; This lesson consolidates Term 1–2 sentence work into one explicit comparison.</li><li><em>Writing — Sentence Structure (Practice)</em> — Choosing sentence type for purpose: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Sentence Structure (Practice): Students are expected to &quot;write a variety of sentence structures — simple, compound, and complex — for effect and clarity,&quot; and to understand the grammatical reasoning behind each type, not just produce them by imitation.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Wednesday: PEEL paragraphs for persuasive writing":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">NZ Curriculum Link</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Writing — Text Structure (Knowledge &amp; Practice): the PEEL structure (Point, Evidence, Explain, Link) organises a persuasive paragraph so that each claim is supported by evidence, explained for the reader, and linked back to the overall argument.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>I Do — Building an Argument Paragraph</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>PEEL stands for Point (the claim), Evidence (proof or example), Explain (why this evidence supports the point), and Link (connect back to the argument or next paragraph). Watch me build one.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Example: Point: Schools should have longer lunch breaks. Evidence: Studies show students concentrate better after proper rest. Explain: This means more break time would directly improve learning in afternoon classes. Link: For this reason, schools should reconsider their lunch schedules.</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Each sentence does one specific job. Point states the claim clearly. Evidence backs it with something concrete. Explain bridges the evidence to the claim — this is the step many writers skip! Link closes the loop back to the argument.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Tip: If your 'explain' sentence just repeats the evidence, it's not doing its job — it must say WHY the evidence proves the point.</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>We Do — Build a PEEL Paragraph</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Build a PEEL paragraph together for this point.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Point: Students should be allowed to choose their own reading books.</li><li>Evidence: ___ (what proof or example supports this?)</li><li>Explain: ___ (why does this evidence support the point?)</li><li>Link: ___ (connect back to the argument)</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Discussion prompt: What evidence is convincing? Does your 'explain' sentence actually explain, or just repeat?</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>You Do — Your Turn</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write a full PEEL paragraph for the given point, labelling each sentence P/E/E/L.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Point: Recycling should be compulsory in all schools.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write a second PEEL paragraph arguing the opposite side of any topic you choose — practise constructing a balanced argument.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Objectives (AOs)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Writing — Text Structure (Knowledge)</em> — PEEL paragraph structure: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Text Structure (Knowledge): &quot;Persuasive paragraphs are commonly structured using Point, Evidence, Explain, Link — each sentence performing a distinct function: stating a claim, providing supporting evidence, explaining the connection between evidence and claim, and linking back to the overall argument.&quot; This is the central organisational structure expected for persuasive writing at Phase 2.</li><li><em>Writing — Text Structure (Practice)</em> — Constructing reasoned argument: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Text Structure (Practice): Students are expected to &quot;organise ideas into paragraphs with a clear main idea, supported by relevant detail and reasoning.&quot; PEEL gives students an explicit, repeatable framework for building this structure independently in persuasive writing.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Thursday: Editing PEEL paragraphs":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">NZ Curriculum Link</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Writing — Writing Process (Practice): students evaluate a persuasive paragraph against the PEEL structure, identifying missing or weak elements (point, evidence, explain, link) and revising accordingly.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>I Do — Diagnosing a Weak Paragraph</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>I'll show you how to check a paragraph against the PEEL structure and find what's missing.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Example: Recycling is good for the environment. Lots of countries recycle a lot. It really helps.</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Point ✓ (recycling is good). Evidence — weak ('lots of countries recycle a lot' is vague, not real evidence). Explain — missing entirely! 'It really helps' doesn't say HOW or WHY. Link — missing. Fix: add specific evidence (e.g. a statistic or example), explain the connection, and link back to the argument.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Tip: Read your paragraph and label each sentence P, E, E, or L in the margin. Any letter missing or weak is your revision target.</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>We Do — Diagnose Together</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Label each sentence P/E/E/L in this paragraph. What's missing or weak?</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Students should have more PE time at school. Exercise is good for you. Schools should change their timetables.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Discussion prompt: Which part is missing? Is the evidence specific enough? Where's the explain sentence?</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>You Do — Your Turn</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This paragraph is missing its Explain and Link sentences. Add them.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Point: Libraries should stay open later. Evidence: Many students don't have a quiet place to study at home. [Add an Explain sentence] [Add a Link sentence]</li><li>✦ Bonus: Take a PEEL paragraph you wrote on Wednesday and check it against the four labels — strengthen any weak part.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Objectives (AOs)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Writing — Writing Process (Practice)</em> — Diagnosing paragraph structure: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Writing Process (Practice): Students are expected to &quot;reread and revise their writing… making changes to structure to improve clarity and impact.&quot; Labelling each sentence against the PEEL framework gives students a precise diagnostic tool for identifying structural weaknesses in persuasive paragraphs.</li><li><em>Writing — Text Structure (Practice)</em> — Strengthening argument paragraphs: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Text Structure (Practice): Students are expected to &quot;organise ideas into paragraphs with a clear main idea, supported by relevant detail and reasoning.&quot; This editing lesson consolidates Wednesday's PEEL construction work by practising the reverse skill: recognising and repairing a paragraph that doesn't yet meet the structure.</li></ul></div></div>"}}},"Week 5":{"label":"Day","children":{"Monday: Connotation: positive vs negative word choice":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">NZ Curriculum Link</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Writing — Vocabulary (Knowledge): words with similar denotations can carry different connotations (positive, negative, or neutral associations); writers select words deliberately to shape the reader's attitude towards a subject.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>I Do — The Same Fact, Different Feelings</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Connotation is the feeling or association a word carries, beyond its dictionary meaning. Two words can describe the same thing but make the reader feel differently about it. Watch me compare.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Example: He was stubborn. vs. He was determined. | She was nosy. vs. She was curious.</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>'Stubborn' and 'determined' both mean someone won't change their mind — but 'stubborn' sounds negative, 'determined' sounds admirable. Same fact, opposite feeling. Writers choose connotation deliberately depending on whether they want the reader to sympathise or disapprove.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Tip: Ask: do I want the reader to feel positively, negatively, or neutrally about this? Choose your word to match.</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>We Do — Choose the Connotation</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>For each neutral idea, choose a positive word AND a negative word with the same basic meaning.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A person who saves money carefully → positive: <em><strong> negative: </strong></em></li><li>A person who speaks their mind → positive: <em><strong> negative: </strong></em></li><li>A child who asks lots of questions → positive: <em><strong> negative: </strong></em></li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Discussion prompt: What word makes the reader admire this trait? What word makes them disapprove of it?</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>You Do — Your Turn</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Rewrite each neutral sentence twice — once with positive connotation, once with negative — describing the same event.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The politician changed her opinion on the policy. (positive / negative)</li><li>The team took a long time to finish the project. (positive / negative)</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write a short paragraph describing the same character twice — once so the reader likes them, once so the reader dislikes them — using only connotation, not different facts.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Objectives (AOs)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Writing — Vocabulary (Knowledge)</em> — Connotation and word choice: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Vocabulary (Knowledge): &quot;Words with similar meanings can carry different connotations — positive, negative, or neutral associations — and writers select vocabulary deliberately to shape the reader's attitude.&quot; This is an advanced application of the precision vocabulary work begun in Term 1 and Term 2.</li><li><em>Writing — Composing (Practice)</em> — Shaping reader attitude: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Composing (Practice): Students are expected to &quot;consider the effect of word choice on the reader.&quot; Understanding connotation is essential for persuasive and characterisation writing, where the writer deliberately steers how the reader judges a person, action, or idea.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Tuesday: Nominalisation":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">NZ Curriculum Link</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Writing — Sentence Structure (Knowledge): nominalisation turns a verb or adjective into a noun (decide → decision, careless → carelessness), producing the more formal, condensed sentence style typical of explanation and report writing.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>I Do — Turning Actions into Things</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Nominalisation changes a verb or adjective into a noun. It creates a more formal, compact sentence — common in reports, explanations, and academic writing.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Example: Informal/verb-based: The council decided to build a new park, which many people were happy about. → Nominalised: The council's decision to build a new park led to widespread happiness.</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>'Decided' (verb) → 'decision' (noun). 'Happy' (adjective) → 'happiness' (noun). The nominalised version packs the idea into a noun phrase, sounding more formal and report-like — useful for explanation writing, but can feel stiff if overused in narrative.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Tip: Common pattern: verb + -ion/-ment/-ation, adjective + -ness/-ity. Try it, then ask: does this sentence still sound natural?</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>We Do — Nominalise Together</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Rewrite each sentence using a nominalisation of the underlined word.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The scientists discovered a new species.</li><li>The students were curious about the experiment.</li><li>The government announced new road safety rules.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Discussion prompt: What noun form of this word exists? Does the new sentence still make sense?</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>You Do — Your Turn</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Rewrite each sentence using nominalisation to create a more formal tone.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The team improved their performance over the season.</li><li>Many people are concerned about climate change.</li><li>The company expanded into new markets.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write two versions of a sentence about a school event — one informal/verb-based, one nominalised and formal. Which suits a newsletter? Which suits a casual conversation?</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Objectives (AOs)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Writing — Sentence Structure (Knowledge)</em> — Nominalisation: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Sentence Structure (Knowledge): &quot;Nominalisation converts a verb or adjective into a noun (decide → decision, careless → carelessness), producing a more condensed, formal sentence style typical of explanation and report text types.&quot; This is an advanced Phase 2 grammar concept connecting word formation to register.</li><li><em>Writing — Vocabulary (Practice)</em> — Register-appropriate word forms: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Vocabulary (Practice): Students are expected to &quot;select and use words and phrases… that are fit for purpose and audience.&quot; Recognising when nominalisation suits formal writing (and when it makes narrative writing feel stiff) builds students' awareness of register as a deliberate choice.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Wednesday: Cause and effect sentences":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">NZ Curriculum Link</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Writing — Sentence Structure (Practice): cause-and-effect connectives (because, as a result, consequently, due to, this means that) construct sentences that clearly link an action or event to its outcome, essential for explanation writing.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>I Do — Linking Cause to Effect</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Explanation writing relies on clear cause-and-effect links. Watch me build sentences using a range of cause-and-effect connectives, not just 'because'.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Example: Cause: Deforestation removes trees that absorb carbon dioxide. Effect: Greenhouse gases increase. → Deforestation removes trees that absorb carbon dioxide; as a result, greenhouse gases increase.</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>I could also write: 'Because deforestation removes trees... greenhouse gases increase' or 'Deforestation removes trees... Consequently, greenhouse gases increase.' Varying the connective (because, as a result, consequently, due to, this means that) avoids repetition across a whole explanation text.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Tip: If you've used 'because' three times already in a paragraph, reach for 'as a result', 'consequently', or 'this means that' instead.</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>We Do — Link Cause and Effect</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Join each cause and effect using a different connective each time.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Cause: Ice caps are melting. Effect: Sea levels are rising.</li><li>Cause: Plastic takes hundreds of years to break down. Effect: It pollutes oceans for generations.</li><li>Cause: Bees pollinate crops. Effect: Many food sources depend on them.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Discussion prompt: Which connective haven't you used yet? Does the sentence still make logical sense?</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>You Do — Your Turn</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write a cause-and-effect sentence for each pair, using a different connective in each.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Cause: Native forests are cleared for farmland. Effect: habitats are lost.</li><li>Cause: Regular exercise strengthens the heart. Effect: overall health improves.</li><li>Cause: Overfishing reduces fish populations. Effect: fishing communities suffer economically.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write a 4-sentence explanation paragraph about why recycling matters, using at least three different cause-and-effect connectives.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Objectives (AOs)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Writing — Sentence Structure (Practice)</em> — Cause-and-effect connectives: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Sentence Structure (Practice): Students are expected to &quot;use a range of connectives to link cause and effect within explanation writing,&quot; varying beyond 'because' to include 'as a result', 'consequently', 'due to', and 'this means that'. This is a core grammatical requirement of explanation text type.</li><li><em>Writing — Text Structure (Knowledge)</em> — Explanation text structure: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Text Structure (Knowledge): &quot;Explanation texts are structured around clear cause-and-effect relationships, typically organised sequentially or logically.&quot; This lesson builds the sentence-level tool that underpins the larger explanation text-type structure students are expected to control by Years 5–6.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Thursday: Editing for formal register":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">NZ Curriculum Link</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Writing — Writing Process (Practice) &amp; Vocabulary (Practice): students identify informal language (contractions, slang, casual connectives) inappropriate for formal writing and revise it to match a formal register.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>I Do — Matching Language to Audience</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Formal writing (reports, persuasive essays, explanations) avoids contractions, slang, and casual phrasing. Watch me formalise an informal passage.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Example: Informal: Kids shouldn't have to do tons of homework, it's just not fair and it's really stressful.</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Fix: Students should not be required to complete excessive amounts of homework, as it places unnecessary stress upon them. 'Shouldn't' → 'should not'. 'Kids' → 'students' (more formal noun). 'tons of' → 'excessive amounts of'. 'it's just not fair' → cut, replaced with a more formal justification.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Tip: Hunt for contractions (don't, it's, can't), slang (kids, tons, stuff), and casual fillers (just, really, like) — these are the first things to formalise.</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>We Do — Formalise Together</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Rewrite this passage in a formal register, removing contractions and casual language.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I don't think we should have so much plastic packaging, it's bad for the environment and it's just wasteful.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Discussion prompt: Which words are too casual for formal writing? What more formal word or phrase could replace each one?</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>You Do — Your Turn</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Rewrite each sentence in a formal register.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Schools shouldn't ban phones, it's not really fair on kids who need them.</li><li>I reckon the council should fix the playground, it's been broken for ages.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Take an informal sentence you might say to a friend and rewrite it as if it were appearing in a formal letter to your school principal.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Objectives (AOs)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Writing — Vocabulary (Practice)</em> — Formal vs informal register: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Vocabulary (Practice): Students are expected to &quot;select and use words and phrases… that are fit for purpose and audience,&quot; including matching register (formal/informal) to text type and audience. This editing lesson targets the most common register slip in Years 5–6 persuasive and explanation writing — contractions and casual phrasing.</li><li><em>Writing — Writing Process (Practice)</em> — Editing for audience and purpose: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Writing Process (Practice): Students are expected to &quot;reread and revise their writing… making changes to vocabulary… to improve clarity and impact,&quot; considering audience and purpose. This lesson builds a specific register-checking strategy students can apply to any formal writing task.</li></ul></div></div>"}}},"Week 6":{"label":"Day","children":{"Monday: Formal vs informal register":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">NZ Curriculum Link</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Writing — Vocabulary (Knowledge): register refers to the level of formality in language, signalled through vocabulary, sentence length, and contractions; writers match register to audience and purpose.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>I Do — Two Ways to Say the Same Thing</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Register is how formal or informal language is. The same message can be said very differently depending on audience and purpose. Watch me compare.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Example: To a friend: Hey, can you grab me a drink? | To a stranger/formal: Excuse me, would you mind passing me a drink, please?</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Both ask the same thing! But word choice ('hey'/'excuse me'), contractions, and politeness markers ('would you mind', 'please') signal formality. I choose register based on WHO I'm writing to and WHY.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Tip: Ask: would I say this to my principal? If not, it's probably too informal for formal writing — and vice versa.</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>We Do — Identify the Register</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Decide if each sentence is formal or informal, and explain the clues.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Yo, that movie was awesome!</li><li>The committee respectfully requests your attendance at the meeting.</li><li>I reckon we should just go for it.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Discussion prompt: What words or phrases gave away the register? Could you rewrite it in the opposite register?</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>You Do — Your Turn</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Rewrite each informal sentence in formal register, and each formal sentence in informal register.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Informal → Formal: 'Can you tell the boss I'm gonna be late?'</li><li>Formal → Informal: 'We would be grateful if you could submit your assignment by Friday.'</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write the same short message (e.g. asking for help with a project) twice — once to a teacher (formal), once to a friend (informal).</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Objectives (AOs)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Writing — Vocabulary (Knowledge)</em> — Register: formal and informal language: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Vocabulary (Knowledge): &quot;Register refers to the level of formality in language, signalled through word choice, contractions, sentence length, and politeness markers. Writers match register deliberately to audience and purpose.&quot; This is a key Phase 2 expectation underpinning all text types.</li><li><em>Writing — Composing (Practice)</em> — Matching language to audience: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Composing (Practice): Students are expected to &quot;use composing strategies appropriate to audience and purpose.&quot; This lesson makes register an explicit, comparable choice rather than an instinctive one, preparing students for the formal-register editing work later in the term.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Tuesday: Reported (indirect) speech":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">NZ Curriculum Link</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Writing — Sentence Structure (Knowledge): reported speech conveys what someone said without quotation marks, requiring a backshift in tense and changes to pronouns and time/place words; students convert between direct and reported speech accurately.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>I Do — Speech Without the Speech Marks</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Reported speech tells us what someone said without quoting their exact words — no speech marks needed. But several things change: the verb tense usually shifts back, and pronouns/time words adjust. Watch me convert.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Example: Direct: Maya said, &quot;I am going to the shop now.&quot; → Reported: Maya said that she was going to the shop then.</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>'am going' (present) → 'was going' (past) — tense shifts back. 'I' → 'she' — pronoun changes to match the new speaker perspective. 'now' → 'then' — time word shifts since we're reporting it later. No speech marks needed at all.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Tip: Three changes to check: 1) does the tense shift back? 2) do pronouns need to change? 3) do time/place words need to change (now→then, here→there, today→that day)?</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>We Do — Convert to Reported Speech</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Convert each direct speech sentence to reported speech.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Tom said, &quot;I am tired.&quot;</li><li>She said, &quot;I will finish my homework tomorrow.&quot;</li><li>&quot;We are leaving now,&quot; they said.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Discussion prompt: Does the tense shift back? Do the pronouns change? Do any time words need to change?</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>You Do — Your Turn</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Convert each sentence as instructed.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Convert to reported speech: Aroha said, &quot;I love this book.&quot;</li><li>Convert to reported speech: &quot;I can't come today,&quot; he said.</li><li>Convert to direct speech: She said that she was going home then.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write a short conversation in direct speech, then rewrite the whole thing as a reported-speech summary.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Objectives (AOs)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Writing — Sentence Structure (Knowledge)</em> — Direct and reported (indirect) speech: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Sentence Structure (Knowledge): &quot;Reported speech conveys what someone said without quotation marks, typically requiring a backshift in verb tense and corresponding changes to pronouns and time or place adverbs.&quot; This consolidates Week 1's direct speech punctuation knowledge by introducing its grammatical counterpart.</li><li><em>Writing — Sentence Structure (Practice)</em> — Converting between direct and reported speech: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Sentence Structure (Practice): Students are expected to &quot;write grammatically correct sentences using a range of tenses&quot; and structures. Reported speech is a common feature of recount and news-style writing, requiring students to apply tense and pronoun agreement knowledge simultaneously.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Wednesday: Balancing sentence rhythm":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">NZ Curriculum Link</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Writing — Sentence Structure (Practice): writers deliberately balance the length and structure of sentences across a paragraph to control pace, building on short-vs-long sentence variety with attention to rhythm across a whole passage.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>I Do — Listening to a Paragraph's Rhythm</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>We've practised varying single sentences. Today, listen to the rhythm of a WHOLE paragraph and learn to balance it across multiple sentences.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Example: All medium-length paragraph: 'The waves crashed against the rocks. The wind howled through the cliffs. Seabirds circled overhead. The storm grew worse by the minute.' → all the same length — monotonous.</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Rebalanced: 'The waves crashed against the rocks, and the wind howled through the cliffs. Seabirds circled. The storm grew worse and worse, the sky darkening with every passing minute.' Now there's a long-short-long pattern — a rhythm, not a drumbeat.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Tip: Read your paragraph aloud. If every sentence takes the same breath to say, vary at least one to break the pattern.</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>We Do — Rebalance Together</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This paragraph has a flat, repetitive rhythm. Let's rebalance it by combining some sentences and keeping others short.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The fire spread quickly. The smoke filled the room. The alarm rang loudly. Everyone ran outside. The firefighters arrived fast.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Discussion prompt: Which sentences could combine? Which single short sentence should stay for impact?</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>You Do — Your Turn</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Rebalance this paragraph's rhythm by combining at least two sentences while keeping one short for impact.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The race began. The runners sprinted forward. The crowd cheered loudly. One runner pulled ahead. She crossed the line first.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write a 5-sentence paragraph about an exciting moment, deliberately mixing short and long sentences for a clear rhythm. Read it aloud to check.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Objectives (AOs)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Writing — Sentence Structure (Practice)</em> — Balancing sentence rhythm across a paragraph: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Sentence Structure (Practice): Students are expected to &quot;write a variety of sentence structures for effect and clarity,&quot; extended here to consider rhythm across a whole paragraph rather than a single sentence. This builds on Term 1's short-vs-long sentence work with a wider lens.</li><li><em>Writing — Composing (Practice)</em> — Controlling pace across a passage: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Composing (Practice): Students are expected to &quot;use composing strategies to create texts that engage the reader,&quot; including controlling pace and rhythm across an extended piece of writing, not just within a single sentence.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Thursday: Editing for cohesion":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">NZ Curriculum Link</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Writing — Text Structure (Practice) &amp; Writing Process (Practice): students revise a multi-paragraph passage to add cohesive devices (connectives, pronouns, repeated key words) that link ideas between paragraphs.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>I Do — Linking Paragraphs Together</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Cohesion means ideas flow smoothly between sentences AND paragraphs. Watch me fix a passage where paragraphs feel disconnected.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Example: Para 1 ends: ...recycling reduces landfill waste. Para 2 starts: Pollution is also a big problem.</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The jump from 'recycling' to 'pollution' feels abrupt — no link. Fix: 'In addition to reducing landfill waste, recycling also helps tackle a second major issue: pollution.' I added a connective ('in addition to') and referenced the previous idea before introducing the new one.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Tip: At the start of a new paragraph, briefly nod back to the previous idea before introducing the new one — this is what creates flow.</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>We Do — Add the Links</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>These two paragraph openings feel disconnected. Add a cohesive link between them.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Para 1 ends: ...native birds are losing their habitats. Para 2 starts: ___ Predators are another major threat to their survival.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Discussion prompt: How can you connect the idea of habitat loss to the new idea of predators?</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>You Do — Your Turn</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Add a cohesive link sentence or phrase between each pair of paragraph endings/openings.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Para 1 ends: ...exercise improves physical health. Para 2 starts: ___ Mental health benefits are just as important.</li><li>Para 1 ends: ...plastic pollution harms marine life. Para 2 starts: ___ Solutions are within our reach.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Take two paragraphs from a piece of your own writing and check whether they connect smoothly — add a link if they don't.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Objectives (AOs)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Writing — Text Structure (Practice)</em> — Cohesion across paragraphs: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Text Structure (Practice): Students are expected to &quot;use cohesive devices — including connectives, pronouns, and repeated key words — to link ideas within and across sentences and paragraphs.&quot; This lesson extends sentence-level cohesion (Term 1) to the more demanding skill of linking entire paragraphs.</li><li><em>Writing — Writing Process (Practice)</em> — Revising for flow: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Writing Process (Practice): Students are expected to &quot;reread and revise their writing… making changes to structure to improve clarity and impact.&quot; This lesson gives students a specific revision technique — briefly referencing the previous idea before introducing a new one — to apply when their writing feels disjointed between paragraphs.</li></ul></div></div>"}}},"Week 7":{"label":"Day","children":{"Monday: Onomatopoeia":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">NZ Curriculum Link</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Writing — Vocabulary (Knowledge): onomatopoeia uses words that imitate the sound they describe, adding sensory immediacy to descriptive and narrative writing.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>I Do — Words That Sound Like What They Mean</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Onomatopoeia words imitate sounds (crash, sizzle, hiss). They make writing more sensory and immediate. Watch me weave one into a sentence.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Example: The pan was hot. The bacon went in. → The bacon sizzled as it hit the scorching pan.</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>'Sizzled' lets the reader almost hear the sound, instead of just being told something happened. I choose the precise onomatopoeic word to match the exact sound — not just 'made a noise', but the specific noise (sizzle, not crackle, not hiss).</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Tip: Don't overuse onomatopoeia — one well-placed sound word is more powerful than five crammed into one sentence.</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>We Do — Add the Sound</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Add an onomatopoeic word to bring each sentence to life.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The branch ___ as it broke underfoot.</li><li>The balloon ___ when the pin touched it.</li><li>Rain ___ against the tin roof.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Discussion prompt: What exact sound would this make? Is there a more precise onomatopoeic word?</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>You Do — Your Turn</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Add an onomatopoeic word to each sentence.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The old door ___ open slowly.</li><li>Bees ___ around the flowers.</li><li>The fireworks ___ overhead.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write a 3-sentence description of a thunderstorm using at least three different onomatopoeic words.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Objectives (AOs)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Writing — Vocabulary (Knowledge)</em> — Onomatopoeia: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Vocabulary (Knowledge): &quot;Onomatopoeia uses words that imitate the sound they describe, adding sensory immediacy to writing. Like other figurative and sound devices, it is most effective when used sparingly and precisely.&quot; This continues the term's exploration of figurative and sensory language devices.</li><li><em>Writing — Composing (Practice)</em> — Sensory detail in description: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Composing (Practice): Students are expected to &quot;use composing strategies, including sensory and figurative language, to create texts that engage the reader.&quot; Onomatopoeia adds an auditory dimension to the sensory toolkit students are building across the term.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Tuesday: Expanded noun phrases":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">NZ Curriculum Link</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Writing — Sentence Structure (Knowledge): a noun phrase can be expanded with pre-modifiers (adjectives before the noun) and post-modifiers (prepositional or relative clauses after the noun) to add precise, layered detail.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>I Do — Building a Noun Phrase from Both Sides</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>A noun phrase can be expanded BEFORE the noun (pre-modifiers: adjectives) and AFTER the noun (post-modifiers: prepositional phrases or relative clauses). Watch me build one from a bare noun.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Example: Bare: the dog → Pre-modified: the scruffy, three-legged dog → Fully expanded: the scruffy, three-legged dog that lived under the bridge.</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>'Scruffy, three-legged' goes before the noun (pre-modification). 'That lived under the bridge' goes after (post-modification, a relative clause). Together they build a rich, specific noun phrase — much more detailed than 'the dog' alone.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Tip: Pre-modifiers answer 'what kind?'. Post-modifiers answer 'which one, more specifically?' — use both for maximum detail.</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>We Do — Expand the Noun Phrase</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Expand each bare noun phrase with both a pre-modifier and a post-modifier.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>the house</li><li>the teacher</li><li>the river</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Discussion prompt: What adjectives go before? What phrase or clause could go after to add more specific detail?</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>You Do — Your Turn</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Expand each noun phrase using both pre- and post-modification.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>the car</li><li>the mountain</li><li>the book</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write one sentence using a fully expanded noun phrase (pre- and post-modified) as the subject of the sentence.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Objectives (AOs)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Writing — Sentence Structure (Knowledge)</em> — Expanded noun phrases: pre- and post-modification: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Sentence Structure (Knowledge): &quot;A noun phrase can be expanded through pre-modification (adjectives placed before the noun) and post-modification (prepositional phrases or relative clauses placed after the noun), allowing writers to layer precise detail around a single noun.&quot; This is an advanced Phase 2 grammar skill combining several earlier concepts (adjectives, prepositional phrases, relative clauses).</li><li><em>Writing — Sentence Structure (Practice)</em> — Building detailed noun phrases: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Sentence Structure (Practice): Students are expected to &quot;expand sentences by adding words, phrases, and clauses to add detail and description.&quot; This lesson draws together adjective, prepositional phrase, and relative clause knowledge from across the year into a single, powerful descriptive tool.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Wednesday: Rhetorical questions":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">NZ Curriculum Link</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Writing — Sentence Structure (Practice): a rhetorical question is asked for effect rather than to elicit an answer, used in persuasive writing to provoke thought and draw the reader into agreeing with the writer's position.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>I Do — Asking Without Expecting an Answer</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>A rhetorical question isn't asked to get an answer — it's asked to make the reader think, and often the answer is obvious. Watch me use one to open a persuasive paragraph.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Example: Plain: Plastic pollution is a serious problem. → Rhetorical: Do we really want future generations to inherit oceans choked with plastic?</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The rhetorical question forces the reader to imagine the consequence themselves — more persuasive than simply stating a fact. The implied answer ('No, of course not') pulls the reader towards agreeing with the writer.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Tip: Use rhetorical questions sparingly — one per paragraph at most, usually at the start or end, or they lose their power.</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>We Do — Turn Statements into Rhetorical Questions</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Convert each plain statement into a rhetorical question that makes the same point more persuasively.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Animals should not be kept in tiny cages.</li><li>Wasting food is unacceptable when so many people go hungry.</li><li>Every student deserves access to a good education.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Discussion prompt: What question could you ask whose obvious answer makes your point for you?</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>You Do — Your Turn</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write a rhetorical question for each persuasive topic.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Topic: protecting native wildlife.</li><li>Topic: reducing screen time for children.</li><li>Topic: keeping local libraries open.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write a short persuasive paragraph that opens AND closes with a rhetorical question.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Objectives (AOs)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Writing — Sentence Structure (Practice)</em> — Rhetorical questions for persuasive effect: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Sentence Structure (Practice): Students are expected to &quot;use a variety of sentence structures and types — including questions — for effect,&quot; specifically rhetorical questions in persuasive writing, where they are used to provoke thought rather than seek information.</li><li><em>Writing — Composing (Practice)</em> — Engaging the reader in argument: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Composing (Practice): Students are expected to &quot;use composing strategies to create texts that engage the reader,&quot; particularly in persuasive writing where drawing the reader into actively considering the argument (rather than passively receiving it) strengthens persuasive impact.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Thursday: Editing repetitive sentence openers":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">NZ Curriculum Link</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Writing — Writing Process (Practice): students identify repeated sentence-opening patterns (especially repeated subjects) across a passage and revise using the variety of openers learned this term.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>I Do — Spotting the Repetition</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>If every sentence in a paragraph starts the same way, it feels monotonous. Watch me find and fix repeated openers using this term's toolkit (fronted adverbials, -ing/-ed clauses, varied subjects).</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Example: The dog ran across the field. The dog jumped over the fence. The dog barked at the cat.</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Every sentence starts with 'The dog' — repetitive. Fix using variety: 'Barking excitedly, the dog ran across the field and jumped over the fence to chase the cat.' I combined ideas AND used an -ing opener instead of repeating the subject three times.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Tip: Underline the first two or three words of every sentence in a paragraph. If you see the same pattern twice in a row — vary it.</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>We Do — Fix the Repetition</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This paragraph repeats its sentence openers. Let's vary them together using techniques from this term.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The rain started to fall. The rain grew heavier. The rain flooded the streets. The rain finally stopped at midnight.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Discussion prompt: Which sentences could combine? Which opener type (fronted adverbial, -ing, -ed) could replace a repeated subject?</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>You Do — Your Turn</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Rewrite this paragraph, fixing the repetitive sentence openers using at least two different techniques from this term.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The girl walked into the room. The girl looked around nervously. The girl saw her friends waiting. The girl smiled with relief.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Check a paragraph of your own writing for repeated sentence openers and fix at least two.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Objectives (AOs)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Writing — Writing Process (Practice)</em> — Editing for sentence opener variety: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Writing Process (Practice): Students are expected to &quot;reread and revise their writing… making changes to structure to improve clarity and impact.&quot; This lesson trains students to scan specifically for repeated sentence openings — a common Years 5–6 weakness — and apply the term's full toolkit (fronted adverbials, -ing/-ed clauses) to fix it.</li><li><em>Writing — Sentence Structure (Practice)</em> — Applying sentence opener variety: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Sentence Structure (Practice): Students are expected to &quot;write a variety of sentence structures for effect and clarity.&quot; This editing lesson consolidates every sentence-opener technique taught this term into one applied skill.</li></ul></div></div>"}}},"Week 8":{"label":"Day","children":{"Monday: Idioms and figurative expressions":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">NZ Curriculum Link</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Writing — Vocabulary (Knowledge): idioms are fixed figurative expressions whose meaning cannot be worked out literally from the individual words; students use idioms appropriately and understand their effect on tone.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>I Do — Expressions That Don't Mean What They Say</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>An idiom is a fixed expression whose meaning isn't literal — you can't guess it just from the individual words. Watch me use one correctly and explain its effect.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Example: 'It's raining cats and dogs' doesn't mean animals are falling from the sky — it means it's raining very heavily.</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Idioms add personality and a conversational tone, which suits informal narrative or dialogue — but they can sound out of place in formal writing. 'She was over the moon about her results' fits a casual recount, but not a formal report.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Tip: Idioms suit informal/narrative writing and dialogue. Avoid them in formal persuasive or explanation writing, where literal precision matters more.</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>We Do — Use Idioms Appropriately</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Decide whether each idiom fits the writing context given, and explain why or why not.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>'It cost an arm and a leg' — in a casual recount about a holiday.</li><li>'It cost an arm and a leg' — in a formal report on government spending.</li><li>'Break the ice' — in a story where two characters meet for the first time.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Discussion prompt: Does this idiom suit the tone and formality of the writing? What might be a more precise alternative for formal writing?</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>You Do — Your Turn</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Use each idiom correctly in a sentence, matching it to an appropriately informal context.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Use the idiom 'once in a blue moon' in a sentence.</li><li>Use the idiom 'hit the nail on the head' in a sentence.</li><li>Use the idiom 'under the weather' in a sentence.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Find an idiom you've heard before. Explain its literal words, its real (figurative) meaning, and write a sentence using it correctly.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Objectives (AOs)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Writing — Vocabulary (Knowledge)</em> — Idioms and figurative expressions: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Vocabulary (Knowledge): &quot;Idioms are fixed figurative expressions whose meaning cannot be derived literally from their individual words. Their use is generally suited to informal or conversational registers.&quot; This completes the term's run of figurative vocabulary devices (simile, personification, metaphor, onomatopoeia, idiom).</li><li><em>Writing — Vocabulary (Practice)</em> — Matching figurative language to register: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Vocabulary (Practice): Students are expected to &quot;select and use words and phrases… that are fit for purpose and audience.&quot; This lesson connects directly to the term's register work (Week 5–6), reinforcing that even figurative language must be matched to the formality of the text.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Tuesday: Parenthesis review: commas, brackets, dashes":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">NZ Curriculum Link</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Writing — Punctuation (Knowledge): commas, brackets, and dashes can all mark parenthetical information; students choose between the three based on the level of formality and emphasis required, consolidating a full term's worth of punctuation knowledge.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>I Do — Three Tools, One Job</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Commas, brackets, and dashes can all set off extra (parenthetical) information — but each creates a different feel. Watch me use all three for the same information.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Example: Commas (neutral): The kiwi, our national bird, is flightless. | Brackets (quiet aside): The kiwi (our national bird) is flightless. | Dashes (emphatic): The kiwi — our national bird — is flightless.</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Same information, three different effects. Commas are the most neutral and common choice. Brackets feel like a quiet, almost whispered aside. Dashes draw attention and create drama. The choice depends on how much emphasis the information deserves.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Tip: Default to commas unless you have a reason to choose otherwise: brackets for a quiet factual aside, dashes for emphasis or drama.</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>We Do — Choose the Right Mark</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Add parenthetical punctuation to each sentence, and justify whether commas, brackets, or dashes suit it best.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Mount Cook the highest peak in New Zealand attracts climbers worldwide.</li><li>My brother who is usually quite shy gave a brilliant speech.</li><li>The final score a stunning 3-0 surprised everyone.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Discussion prompt: Is this a neutral aside, a quiet fact, or something worth emphasising? Which punctuation matches that?</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>You Do — Your Turn</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Add the most appropriate parenthetical punctuation to each sentence and explain your choice.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The Treaty of Waitangi signed in 1840 is central to New Zealand's history.</li><li>My dog who never listens finally came when called.</li><li>The exam worth 50% of the final grade is next week.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write three sentences about the same fact, using commas in one, brackets in the second, and dashes in the third — explain how the feeling changes each time.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Objectives (AOs)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Writing — Punctuation (Knowledge)</em> — Parenthesis: commas, brackets, and dashes compared: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Punctuation (Knowledge): &quot;Commas, brackets, and dashes can all mark parenthetical (non-essential) information within a sentence. The choice between them signals different levels of formality and emphasis: commas are neutral, brackets are understated, and dashes are emphatic.&quot; This consolidates parenthesis punctuation taught separately across Term 1 and Term 2.</li><li><em>Writing — Punctuation (Practice)</em> — Choosing punctuation deliberately: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Punctuation (Practice): Students are expected to &quot;use a range of punctuation accurately and for effect.&quot; This review lesson requires students to make an explicit, justified choice between three previously-separate punctuation tools, deepening their control over punctuation as a craft decision.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Wednesday: Embedding clauses for sophistication":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">NZ Curriculum Link</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Writing — Sentence Structure (Practice): writers combine multiple clause types (relative, subordinate, and participial) within a single sentence to create sophisticated, information-dense sentences appropriate for Years 5–6 writing.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>I Do — Stacking Clauses Together</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>A sophisticated sentence can combine several clause types — relative, subordinate, and participial — within one sentence. Watch me build one, layer by layer.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Example: Base: The scientist made a discovery. → + relative clause: The scientist, who had studied the reef for ten years, made a discovery. → + subordinate clause: The scientist, who had studied the reef for ten years, made a discovery that would change marine biology forever, because the species had never been recorded before.</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>I started simple, then added a relative clause (who had studied...), then a subordinate clause (because the species...). Each addition adds genuine information, not padding. The sentence stays grammatically correct throughout — I check it still makes sense after each addition.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Tip: Add one clause at a time and re-read the whole sentence. If it becomes confusing or ungrammatical, simplify — don't just keep piling clauses on.</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>We Do — Build a Layered Sentence</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Build a sentence together, adding one clause type at a time.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Base: The bridge collapsed. (add a relative clause about its age, then a subordinate clause explaining why)</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Discussion prompt: Does each addition add real information? Is the sentence still grammatically correct and clear?</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>You Do — Your Turn</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Build a layered sentence for each base, adding at least two different clause types.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Base: The expedition succeeded.</li><li>Base: The painting sold for a record price.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write one fully layered sentence (at least three clauses) about a topic of your choice. Read it aloud to check it's still clear, not just complicated.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Objectives (AOs)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Writing — Sentence Structure (Practice)</em> — Embedding multiple clause types: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Sentence Structure (Practice): Students are expected to &quot;write a variety of sentence structures for effect and clarity,&quot; including sentences that combine relative, subordinate, and participial clauses. This is one of the most demanding Phase 2 sentence-construction skills, drawing together a full year of grammar learning.</li><li><em>Writing — Writing Process (Practice)</em> — Checking complexity against clarity: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Writing Process (Practice): Students are expected to &quot;reread and revise their writing to check that it makes sense.&quot; This lesson explicitly teaches that sentence sophistication must not come at the cost of clarity — students learn to test each addition against comprehensibility.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Thursday: Self-editing with a full checklist":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">NZ Curriculum Link</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Writing — Writing Process (Practice): students apply a comprehensive, multi-pass editing checklist (punctuation, grammar, vocabulary, sentence variety, paragraph cohesion) to independently revise a piece of writing.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>I Do — One Pass Per Rule</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Professional writers don't try to fix everything at once. I'll model a full self-edit using one pass per category: punctuation, grammar, vocabulary, sentence variety, cohesion.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Example: Checklist: ☐ Capitals &amp; end punctuation ☐ Subject-verb agreement ☐ No vague words (nice, good, went) ☐ Varied sentence openers ☐ Paragraphs linked with connectives</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Pass 1: I read only for punctuation, marking fixes. Pass 2: I read only for grammar errors. Pass 3: I hunt vague words and upgrade them. Pass 4: I check sentence openers for repetition. Pass 5: I check paragraph links. Five short, focused passes catch far more than one rushed read-through.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Tip: Trying to fix everything in one read is why errors get missed. Slow down — one category, one pass.</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>We Do — Edit Together, One Pass at a Time</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Let's edit this paragraph together, doing one checklist pass at a time as a class.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>the dog ran fast it was a nice dog it went to the park the park was nice too everyone said it was a good day</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Discussion prompt: What did punctuation pass find? What did the vague-word pass find? What about sentence variety?</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>You Do — Your Turn</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Edit this paragraph using the full five-pass checklist. Mark and rewrite all your corrections.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>my family went to the beach it was a good day we did lots of fun things the weather was nice we had a good time and went home tired</li><li>✦ Bonus: Apply the full five-pass checklist to a piece of your own writing from earlier this term.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Objectives (AOs)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Writing — Writing Process (Practice)</em> — Comprehensive self-editing checklist: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Writing Process (Practice): Students are expected to &quot;reread and revise their writing to check that it makes sense and is accurate, including checking punctuation, grammar, vocabulary, and structure.&quot; This lesson teaches the multi-pass strategy — one category per read-through — as the most reliable method for thorough self-editing.</li><li><em>Writing — Writing Process (Knowledge)</em> — Systematic revision strategies: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Writing Process (Knowledge): &quot;Effective revision involves applying a systematic checklist across multiple read-throughs, rather than attempting to address all aspects of writing quality simultaneously.&quot; This consolidates every editing strategy taught across the year into one transferable habit.</li></ul></div></div>"}}},"Week 9":{"label":"Day","children":{"Monday: Expanding noun phrases for detail":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">NZ Curriculum Link</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Writing — Sentence Structure (Practice): students apply pre- and post-modification of noun phrases within full sentences and paragraphs, choosing which nouns deserve expansion for maximum descriptive impact.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>I Do — Choosing Which Nouns Deserve Detail</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Not every noun needs expanding — that would be exhausting to read. Today we practise choosing the most important nouns in a passage to expand. Watch me decide.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Example: Plain: A man walked into a room and saw a painting. → Selectively expanded: A tall, weathered man, his coat dripping from the storm outside, walked into the dim room and saw a painting that seemed to watch him back.</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>I didn't expand every noun — 'the room' got a little detail (dim), but I focused most of my effort on 'the man' (important character) and 'the painting' (the focus of the scene). Expanding everything would slow the pace too much.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Tip: Ask: which 1–2 nouns in this sentence matter most to the reader? Save your detail for those.</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>We Do — Choose and Expand</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>In this sentence, decide which noun is most important and expand only that one fully.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A dog ran towards a boy near a tree.</li><li>A ship sailed into a harbour past a lighthouse.</li><li>A girl opened a door into a room.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Discussion prompt: Which noun is the focus of this sentence? How much detail does it deserve compared to the others?</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>You Do — Your Turn</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Rewrite each sentence, selectively expanding only the most important 1–2 nouns.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A bird landed on a branch above a river.</li><li>A car stopped outside a shop on a street.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write a 3-sentence description of an arrival scene (someone or something arriving somewhere), selectively expanding only the most important nouns in each sentence.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Objectives (AOs)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Writing — Sentence Structure (Practice)</em> — Selective noun phrase expansion: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Sentence Structure (Practice): Students are expected to &quot;expand sentences by adding words, phrases, and clauses to add detail and description,&quot; while also exercising judgement about which elements of a sentence most need expansion. This extends Week 7's expanded noun phrase work into a genuine compositional choice.</li><li><em>Writing — Composing (Practice)</em> — Controlling pace through selective detail: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Composing (Practice): Students are expected to &quot;use composing strategies to create texts that engage the reader.&quot; Over-expanding every noun slows pace and overwhelms the reader — this lesson teaches students to use detail strategically, a hallmark of more mature writing.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Tuesday: Passive voice in explanation writing":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">NZ Curriculum Link</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Writing — Sentence Structure (Practice): passive voice is the conventional choice in explanation and procedural writing when the process or outcome matters more than who performed the action.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>I Do — When Passive Voice Is the Right Choice</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>We learned passive voice can weaken narrative writing — but in explanation and procedural writing, it's often the BEST choice, because the process matters more than who did it. Watch me apply it correctly.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Example: Active (narrative feel): Workers mix the concrete and pour it into moulds. → Passive (explanation feel): The concrete is mixed and poured into moulds.</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>In an explanation of how concrete is made, we don't care WHO mixes it — we care about the process. Passive voice removes the unnecessary 'workers' and keeps focus on the steps. This is the opposite lesson from our earlier 'cut the passive voice' editing work — context decides which is correct.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Tip: Ask: does this text type focus on a process/outcome (passive often works) or on characters taking action (active usually works better)?</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>We Do — Choose the Right Voice for the Text Type</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Decide whether each sentence works better as active or passive, based on its text type, and convert if needed.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Explanation: A chef adds the yeast to the flour. (how bread is made)</li><li>Narrative: The flour was added by the chef as the storm raged outside.</li><li>Explanation: People recycle the bottles at the depot.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Discussion prompt: Is this explanation (focus on process) or narrative (focus on character)? Which voice suits it better?</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>You Do — Your Turn</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Rewrite each sentence in the voice that best suits its text type, and explain your choice.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Explanation: People filter the water before it reaches our taps.</li><li>Explanation: Engineers test the bridge for safety every year.</li><li>Narrative: The cake was baked by Grandma with so much love.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write a 3-sentence explanation of how something works (e.g. how rain forms), using passive voice deliberately and correctly throughout.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Objectives (AOs)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Writing — Sentence Structure (Practice)</em> — Passive voice in explanation text type: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Sentence Structure (Practice): Students are expected to make &quot;deliberate grammatical choices appropriate to text type,&quot; recognising that passive voice is the conventional and often preferred construction in explanation and procedural writing, where process matters more than agent.</li><li><em>Writing — Text Structure (Knowledge)</em> — Voice and text-type convention: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Text Structure (Knowledge): &quot;Different text types favour different grammatical conventions; explanation writing typically favours passive constructions that foreground process over agent.&quot; This lesson deliberately revisits and complicates Term 3 Week 2's active-voice editing lesson, teaching students that 'rules' depend on text-type purpose.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Wednesday: Sequencing sentences in recount and procedure":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">NZ Curriculum Link</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Writing — Text Structure (Knowledge &amp; Practice): time connectives (firstly, next, after that, meanwhile, finally) sequence events clearly in recount and procedural writing, signalling the order in which actions occur.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>I Do — Guiding the Reader Through Time</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Recount and procedural writing need clear time connectives to guide the reader through the sequence of events. Watch me sequence a set of jumbled steps.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Example: Jumbled: We arrived at the beach. We packed our togs. We went swimming. We had lunch.</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Sequenced: Firstly, we packed our togs. Then, we arrived at the beach. After that, we went swimming. Finally, we had lunch. The connectives make the order crystal clear and stop the reader from having to guess what happened when.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Tip: Vary your time connectives — don't just use 'then' for every step. Try firstly, next, after that, meanwhile, eventually, finally.</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>We Do — Sequence Together</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Put these jumbled events in a logical order, adding a time connective to each.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The seeds were watered every day. The seeds were planted in the soil. The seedlings were transplanted outside. The plant finally flowered.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Discussion prompt: What is the correct order? Which connective best introduces each step?</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>You Do — Your Turn</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write a sequenced procedure or recount using these jumbled events, with a different time connective for each step.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Jumbled: The bread rose. The dough was kneaded. The bread was baked. The ingredients were mixed.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write a 5-step recount of your morning routine, using a different time connective to introduce each step.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Objectives (AOs)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Writing — Text Structure (Knowledge)</em> — Time connectives in recount and procedure: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Text Structure (Knowledge): &quot;Recount and procedural texts are organised sequentially, signalled to the reader through a range of time connectives (firstly, next, after that, meanwhile, finally). Sequencing connectives should be varied rather than repetitive.&quot; This is a core text-type structural feature at Phase 2.</li><li><em>Writing — Text Structure (Practice)</em> — Sequencing events clearly: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Text Structure (Practice): Students are expected to &quot;organise ideas… using a range of connectives&quot; appropriate to text type. This lesson gives students a varied bank of time connectives to replace the over-reliance on 'then' common in Years 5–6 recount writing.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Thursday: Peer editing for text-type structure":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">NZ Curriculum Link</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Writing — Writing Process (Practice): students apply text-type-specific structural checklists when peer editing, checking that a partner's writing matches the expected structure for narrative, persuasive, or explanation text types.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>I Do — Editing for Structure, Not Just Sentences</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Today's edit isn't about commas or spelling — it's about whether the WHOLE piece follows its text type's expected structure. Watch me check a persuasive paragraph against a structural checklist.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Example: Checklist for persuasive paragraph: ☐ Clear position stated early ☐ PEEL structure followed ☐ At least one persuasive connective ☐ Formal register maintained</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>I read the whole paragraph once for structure only — ignoring small grammar issues for now. Does it open with a clear position? Yes. Does it follow Point-Evidence-Explain-Link? Partially — there's no clear Explain sentence. I note this as the priority fix, more important right now than a missing comma.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Tip: Structural problems matter more than small errors — fix the shape of the piece first, then polish sentence-level details.</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>We Do — Structural Check Together</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Check this persuasive paragraph against the structural checklist together. What's missing?</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Plastic bags should be banned. They take years to break down. Many shops have already stopped using them.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Discussion prompt: Is there a clear position? Does it follow PEEL? Is anything structurally missing?</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>You Do — Your Turn</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Swap a piece of persuasive or explanation writing with a partner. Check it against the relevant structural checklist and give one specific piece of feedback.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>✦ Checklist (persuasive): clear position, PEEL structure, persuasive connectives, formal register.</li><li>✦ Checklist (explanation): clear introduction, logical cause-and-effect sequence, time/cause connectives.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Identify the single most important structural fix your partner's piece needs, and explain why it matters more than smaller errors.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Objectives (AOs)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Writing — Writing Process (Practice)</em> — Peer editing for structure: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Writing Process (Practice): Students are expected to &quot;share writing with others and respond to feedback to improve their work,&quot; including feedback on text-type structure specifically, not only sentence-level accuracy. This lesson trains students to prioritise structural feedback over minor error-spotting.</li><li><em>Writing — Text Structure (Practice)</em> — Applying text-type checklists: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Text Structure (Practice): Students are expected to &quot;organise ideas into paragraphs with a clear main idea&quot; matching the conventions of the text type being written. This consolidates the term's persuasive (PEEL) and explanation (cause-effect) structural work into an applied peer-review skill.</li></ul></div></div>"}}},"Week 10":{"label":"Day","children":{"Monday: Term review: choosing the best figurative device":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">NZ Curriculum Link</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Writing — Vocabulary (Knowledge &amp; Practice): students review and select the most effective figurative device (simile, metaphor, personification, onomatopoeia, idiom) for a given descriptive purpose, consolidating the term's vocabulary work.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>I Do — One Sentence, Many Possible Devices</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This term we learned five figurative devices: simile, metaphor, personification, onomatopoeia, and idiom. Today, watch me choose the BEST one for a specific purpose, not just any one.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Example: Describing a fierce wind: Simile — the wind howled like a wolf. Metaphor — the wind was a wolf, snarling at the windows. Personification — the wind tore at the rooftops in fury.</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>All three work, but personification feels most powerful here because it makes the wind an active, threatening force throughout a whole passage — not just one comparison. I choose based on which device best sustains the mood across multiple sentences.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Tip: Don't just pick the first device that comes to mind — try two or three, then choose the one that creates the strongest, clearest image.</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>We Do — Try Multiple Devices</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>For each description, try at least two different figurative devices, then decide which works best and why.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A calm lake at sunrise</li><li>A crowded, chaotic market</li><li>An old, creaking ship</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Discussion prompt: Which device created the clearest, most original image? Why does it suit this scene better than the others?</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>You Do — Your Turn</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Choose the best figurative device for each scene and write one strong sentence using it.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A roaring waterfall.</li><li>A sleepy, quiet village at dusk.</li><li>A blazing, uncontrolled bushfire.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write a short paragraph (4–5 sentences) describing a thunderstorm, deliberately combining at least three different figurative devices from this term.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Objectives (AOs)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Writing — Vocabulary (Knowledge)</em> — Figurative language: term review: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Vocabulary (Knowledge): &quot;Figurative language devices — including simile, metaphor, personification, onomatopoeia, and idiom — each create different effects for the reader. Skilled writers select the device best suited to their purpose rather than defaulting to a single familiar technique.&quot; This consolidates the full range of figurative language taught across Term 3.</li><li><em>Writing — Vocabulary (Practice)</em> — Deliberate device selection: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Vocabulary (Practice): Students are expected to &quot;select and use words and phrases, including figurative language, that are fit for purpose and audience.&quot; This review lesson moves students from being able to use each device individually to making a genuine craft choice between them.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Tuesday: Term review: mixed grammar":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">NZ Curriculum Link</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Writing — Sentence Structure (Knowledge &amp; Practice): students apply and distinguish between this term's grammar concepts — active/passive voice, modal verbs, direct/reported speech, and conjunction types — in a mixed review.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>I Do — Spotting Which Rule Applies</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This term covered active/passive voice, modal verbs, direct/reported speech, and conjunction types. Today's challenge is recognising which rule applies to a given sentence. Watch me work through a mixed set.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Example: &quot;The trophy must have been won by the strongest team,&quot; she said.</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>I check each feature: 'must have been' = a modal verb (must) combined with passive voice (been won). 'she said' with speech marks = direct speech. This single sentence uses three of our term's grammar concepts at once — recognising them is the skill we're practising today.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Tip: Work through one grammar category at a time: first check voice, then modals, then speech type, then conjunctions — don't try to spot everything in one glance.</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>We Do — Identify the Features</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Identify which grammar features (voice, modal verbs, speech type, conjunction type) appear in each sentence.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>She said that she might be late.</li><li>The cake was baked by my grandmother, although she rarely cooks.</li><li>&quot;We could win this,&quot; the coach shouted, &quot;if we work together.&quot;</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Discussion prompt: Is this active or passive? Is there a modal verb? Direct or reported speech? Coordinating or subordinating conjunction?</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>You Do — Your Turn</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Identify the grammar features in each sentence, then write one original sentence combining at least two features from this term.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Identify the features: &quot;The match was cancelled because of the storm, and everyone went home disappointed.&quot;</li><li>Identify the features: &quot;You should always check your work,&quot; the teacher reminded them.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write one sentence that combines passive voice, a modal verb, AND reported speech.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Objectives (AOs)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Writing — Sentence Structure (Knowledge)</em> — Mixed grammar review: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Sentence Structure (Knowledge): consolidates Term 3's grammar strand — voice (active/passive), modal verbs, direct and reported speech, and conjunction types — requiring students to recognise multiple grammatical features operating within a single sentence, a key indicator of secure grammatical understanding at the end of Phase 2.</li><li><em>Writing — Sentence Structure (Practice)</em> — Applying grammar knowledge flexibly: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Sentence Structure (Practice): Students are expected to &quot;write grammatically correct sentences using a range of tenses and structures.&quot; This review lesson tests flexible application rather than isolated practice, mirroring how grammar features combine in real, independent writing.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Wednesday: Term review: constructing a flexible paragraph":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">NZ Curriculum Link</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Writing — Sentence Structure (Practice) &amp; Composing (Practice): students construct a single paragraph that deliberately applies a range of this term's sentence-building techniques — varied openers, embedded clauses, semicolons, and rhetorical questions.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>I Do — Bringing It All Together</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Today we build one paragraph using as many of this term's sentence techniques as possible, while keeping it clear and purposeful. Watch me plan and write one.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Example: Topic: arriving at a new school.</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>I plan first: opener type? (-ing clause). A semicolon link? A rhetorical question to close? Draft: &quot;Clutching her new backpack tightly, Mere stepped through the gates for the first time; everything felt unfamiliar, from the unfamiliar faces to the strange smell of fresh paint. Would anyone even notice her standing there?&quot; I checked off three techniques deliberately, not by accident.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Tip: Plan your techniques BEFORE drafting — decide which 2–3 you'll use, then write with that plan in mind, rather than hoping they appear naturally.</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>We Do — Plan and Build Together</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>As a class, plan which techniques to use, then build a paragraph together about this topic.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Topic: a sudden change in weather during a sports match. Plan: opener type? semicolon or colon? a figurative device? a rhetorical question?</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Discussion prompt: Which techniques work together naturally? Does the paragraph still read smoothly, or does it feel forced?</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>You Do — Your Turn</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Plan and write a paragraph (5–6 sentences) on the topic given, deliberately including at least four different techniques from this term (e.g. varied opener, semicolon, figurative device, rhetorical question, modal verb).</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Topic: discovering something unexpected.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Swap with a partner and identify which techniques they used — did you find all of them?</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Objectives (AOs)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Writing — Sentence Structure (Practice)</em> — Deliberate combination of sentence techniques: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Sentence Structure (Practice): Students are expected to &quot;write a variety of sentence structures for effect and clarity,&quot; demonstrated here through planned, deliberate combination of multiple techniques within a single coherent paragraph — the culmination of the term's sentence-building strand.</li><li><em>Writing — Composing (Practice)</em> — Planning before drafting: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Composing (Practice): Students are expected to &quot;use composing strategies to create texts that engage the reader,&quot; including planning specific techniques before drafting rather than relying on them to occur incidentally. This is a key independent-writing habit for Years 5–6.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Thursday: Whole-piece edit: applying the term's checklist":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">NZ Curriculum Link</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Writing — Writing Process (Practice): students apply the full Term 3 editing checklist (figurative language, voice, register, cohesion, sentence variety, punctuation) to a complete piece of their own writing as the term's culminating revision task.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>I Do — The Complete Edit</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Today is the term's biggest edit — applying everything we've learned to a whole piece of writing. Watch me model the full checklist on a sample paragraph.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Example: Full checklist: ☐ Figurative language used purposefully ☐ Voice (active/passive) suits text type ☐ Register matches audience ☐ Paragraphs cohesively linked ☐ Sentence openers varied ☐ Punctuation accurate (including speech, semicolons, parenthesis)</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>I work through each item on a sample paragraph, one at a time, making at least one specific improvement per category where needed — not just ticking boxes, but genuinely revising. This is exactly the process a published writer goes through before a piece is finished.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Tip: Don't rush the final categories because you're tired — punctuation and cohesion are just as important as the first checks.</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>We Do — Edit a Sample Together</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Apply the full checklist to this short paragraph as a class, fixing at least three issues.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The fire was started by accident. It was bad. Everyone got out ok. Firefighters came and put it out it was a relief for everyone.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Discussion prompt: What does each checklist category reveal? Which fix matters most?</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>You Do — Your Turn</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Apply the full Term 3 editing checklist to a piece of your own writing from this term (or the paragraph you wrote on Wednesday). Mark every change you make.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>✦ Work through each checklist category in turn: figurative language, voice, register, cohesion, sentence variety, punctuation.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write a short reflection — which checklist category improved your writing the most this term, and why?</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Objectives (AOs)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Writing — Writing Process (Practice)</em> — Whole-piece systematic editing: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Writing Process (Practice): Students are expected to &quot;reread and revise their writing to check that it makes sense and is accurate, including checking vocabulary, punctuation, and structure.&quot; This culminating lesson requires students to apply a comprehensive checklist independently to a complete piece, demonstrating the full range of Phase 2 writing process skills.</li><li><em>Writing — Writing Process (Knowledge)</em> — Reflecting on growth as a writer: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Writing Process (Knowledge): &quot;Students reflect on their development as writers, identifying which strategies have most improved their writing.&quot; This metacognitive reflection closes the term, connecting explicit skill instruction to students' growing self-awareness as writers.</li></ul></div></div>"}}}}},"Term 4":{"label":"Week","children":{"Week 1":{"label":"Day","children":{"Monday: Precise verb choice: replacing weak verbs":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">NZ Curriculum Link</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Writing — Vocabulary (Practice): precise, specific verbs carry more meaning than vague general ones; replacing weak verbs (went, said, did, got, moved) with precise alternatives makes writing more vivid and economical.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>I Do — One Strong Verb Does the Work of Three Weak Ones</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Weak verbs like 'went', 'said', 'did', 'moved', and 'got' are placeholder verbs — they tell us almost nothing about HOW something happened. Watch me replace them with precise alternatives that carry the scene.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Example: She went quickly across the room and said in a loud voice that she was angry. → She stormed across the room and snapped that she was furious.</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>'Went quickly' = two words doing a weak job. 'Stormed' = one word that gives direction, speed, AND mood. 'Said in a loud voice that she was angry' = seven words. 'Snapped' = one word that gives volume, emotion, AND manner. Precise verbs make writing leaner and more powerful simultaneously.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Tip: When you find a weak verb, ask: HOW did they do it? The answer is usually your precise verb (crept, bolted, muttered, slammed).</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>We Do — Upgrade the Verb</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Replace the weak verb in each sentence with a more precise one.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The cat moved along the fence in the dark.</li><li>He said something under his breath and went out.</li><li>She did the maths quickly and got the right answer.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Discussion prompt: What does HOW it happened tell us — is it fast, slow, fearful, angry? What single verb captures that?</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>You Do — Your Turn</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Replace every weak verb (went, said, got, did, moved, looked) in each sentence with a precise alternative.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The dog went to the door and looked at us.</li><li>She said she was going and got her bag.</li><li>He moved through the crowd and did his best to get past.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Take three consecutive sentences from a piece of your own writing. Find every weak verb and upgrade each one.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Objectives (AOs)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Writing — Vocabulary (Practice)</em> — Precise verb choice: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Vocabulary (Practice): Students are expected to select and use words and phrases that are fit for purpose and audience, specifically choosing precise, specific verbs over vague placeholders. Precise verb choice is one of the highest-leverage vocabulary skills for Years 5–6 writing quality.</li><li><em>Writing — Composing (Practice)</em> — Economy and precision in word choice: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Composing (Practice): Students are expected to use composing strategies to create texts that engage the reader, including selecting vocabulary that is economical — doing more work with fewer words. This lesson establishes precise verb choice as the cornerstone vocabulary skill of Term 4.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Tuesday: Colons — introducing lists and elaboration":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">NZ Curriculum Link</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Writing — Punctuation (Knowledge): a colon introduces what follows — a list, an explanation, or an elaboration of the idea in the main clause; the clause before the colon must be a complete sentence.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>I Do — The Colon Announces What's Coming</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>A colon says: here comes the detail, the list, or the explanation. The clause before the colon must be a complete sentence. Watch me use it two ways.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Example: List: She packed everything she needed: a torch, a sleeping bag, and three days' worth of food. | Elaboration: There was only one problem: nobody had thought to bring a map.</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Before each colon I have a complete sentence. After the colon comes either a list (items separated by commas) or an elaboration that explains or expands the main clause idea. I don't put a capital after a colon unless it's a proper noun.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Tip: Test: can the words before the colon stand alone as a complete sentence? If not, you need more before the colon — or replace it with a comma.</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>We Do — Place the Colon</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Add a colon to each sentence in the right place, then check the words before it form a complete sentence.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>There are three rules in this classroom respect others, work hard, and stay curious.</li><li>The forecast was grim heavy rain, strong winds, and possible flooding.</li><li>She had one ambition to represent New Zealand at the Olympics.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Discussion prompt: Where does the list or elaboration begin? Is everything before that point a complete sentence?</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>You Do — Your Turn</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Add a colon to each sentence and check it is correctly placed.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The museum contained many fascinating things ancient fossils, hand-painted maps, and a real Viking helmet.</li><li>His decision surprised everyone he chose to walk away from the prize.</li><li>The recipe requires four key ingredients flour, eggs, butter, and sugar.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write two original sentences using colons — one introducing a list, one introducing an elaboration.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Objectives (AOs)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Writing — Punctuation (Knowledge)</em> — Colons for lists and elaboration: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Punctuation (Knowledge): A colon introduces what follows — a list, an explanation, or an elaboration of the main clause idea. The clause preceding the colon must be grammatically complete. The colon is a key Phase 2 punctuation mark expected in more sophisticated explanation and persuasive writing.</li><li><em>Writing — Punctuation (Practice)</em> — Applying colons accurately: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Punctuation (Practice): Students are expected to use a range of punctuation accurately and for effect, including the colon for introducing lists and elaboration in explanation and persuasive writing.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Wednesday: Using a colon to introduce and expand an idea":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">NZ Curriculum Link</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Writing — Sentence Structure (Practice): students build sentences that use a colon to set up a main idea and then expand or clarify it in the second part, creating a sophisticated two-part sentence structure common in formal writing.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>I Do — Building the Two-Part Sentence</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The most powerful use of a colon is in a two-part sentence where part one makes a claim and part two proves, explains, or dramatically reveals it. Watch me build one.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Example: Claim then proof: The ancient kauri was extraordinary: it had stood for over 2,000 years, surviving storms, drought, and the arrival of humans. | Build-up then reveal: She opened the door and stopped: the room was completely empty.</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>In the first example, the colon promises an explanation of 'extraordinary' and delivers one. In the second, the colon creates a pause that makes the reveal more dramatic. Both structures set up reader expectation with part one, then satisfy it with part two.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Tip: Read part one aloud: does it feel like it's promising something? If yes, the colon is probably right. If part one already feels complete and closed, a full stop may suit better.</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>We Do — Complete the Two-Part Sentence</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Complete each sentence starter with a strong part-two after the colon.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The team had one weakness:</li><li>The explorer made a startling discovery:</li><li>There was only one thing left to do:</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Discussion prompt: What does part one promise? Does part two deliver a strong explanation, detail, or reveal?</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>You Do — Your Turn</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write the full two-part sentence for each prompt, using a colon to link them.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Start with: 'The storm had one final surprise'</li><li>Start with: 'Every great piece of writing shares one quality'</li><li>Start with: 'She had been preparing for this moment for ten years'</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write a paragraph opening (3–4 sentences) where the first sentence uses a colon for dramatic elaboration.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Objectives (AOs)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Writing — Sentence Structure (Practice)</em> — Two-part colon sentence structure: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Sentence Structure (Practice): Students are expected to write a variety of sentence structures for effect and clarity, including the two-part colon sentence where the first clause makes a claim or builds anticipation and the second clause provides elaboration or revelation.</li><li><em>Writing — Composing (Practice)</em> — Building anticipation and revelation: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Composing (Practice): Students are expected to use composing strategies to create texts that engage the reader, including structural choices that create anticipation. The two-part colon sentence is one of the most reliable tools for creating a moment of revelation or emphasis in both narrative and persuasive writing.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Thursday: Editing: upgrading weak verbs throughout a passage":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">NZ Curriculum Link</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Writing — Writing Process (Practice): students systematically identify and replace weak, vague verbs throughout a whole passage, applying the precise verb skill from Monday in a sustained editing context.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>I Do — The Weak Verb Hunt</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Today I'll show you how to edit a whole passage for weak verbs — methodically, not randomly. I highlight every weak verb first, then replace each one before moving on.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Example: Jake went to school. He looked at his friends and said hello. They all went into class and did their work quietly. The teacher looked up and said they could go.</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Highlight: went (x2), looked (x2), said (x2), did. Replace: Jake trudged / He glanced at his friends and nodded. They filed into class and worked quietly. The teacher glanced up and murmured they were free to go. Now every verb earns its place.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Tip: Highlight ALL weak verbs before changing any of them — this stops you from improving the first sentence and forgetting the rest.</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>We Do — Highlight Then Replace</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Highlight every weak verb in this passage first, then replace them one at a time.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The soldiers went across the field. They moved carefully because they didn't want to be seen. The leader said they should stop. Everyone stopped and looked around nervously.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Discussion prompt: How many weak verbs did we find? Which replacement verb best captures the mood — tense, cautious, military?</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>You Do — Your Turn</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Edit this passage by replacing every weak verb with a more precise one. Aim to cut word count at the same time.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The girl went into the kitchen and looked in the fridge. She got out some food and did some cooking. She said to herself that it smelled good. Her mum came in and said she was impressed.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Count the total words before and after your edit. How many words did you save while making the writing stronger?</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Objectives (AOs)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Writing — Writing Process (Practice)</em> — Systematic weak-verb editing: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Writing Process (Practice): Students are expected to reread and revise their writing, making changes to vocabulary to improve clarity and impact. This lesson teaches a systematic, two-step editing strategy — highlight all targets first, then replace — applied specifically to weak verbs across a whole passage.</li><li><em>Writing — Vocabulary (Practice)</em> — Verb precision across a sustained passage: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Vocabulary (Practice): Sustaining precise verb choice across an entire passage — not just a single sentence — is the more demanding and authentic application of this skill at Phase 2.</li></ul></div></div>"}}},"Week 2":{"label":"Day","children":{"Monday: Pathetic fallacy: weather and setting reflecting mood":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">NZ Curriculum Link</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Writing — Vocabulary (Knowledge) &amp; Composing (Practice): pathetic fallacy is a literary technique in which the natural environment or weather reflects or reinforces the emotional mood of a scene or character.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>I Do — When the World Matches the Feeling</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Pathetic fallacy is when the weather or setting mirrors the mood of the scene — the storm that breaks during a crisis, the sunshine that appears at a moment of hope. Watch me use it deliberately.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Example: Neutral: It was raining as she read the letter. → Pathetic fallacy: The rain hammered against the windows as she read the letter, each drop driving harder as if sharing her despair.</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>In the neutral version, rain is just a fact. In the pathetic fallacy version, the rain actively reflects and amplifies her emotional state. I chose 'hammered' and 'driving harder' to match 'despair' — the word choice in the weather description mirrors the emotional register of the scene.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Tip: The weather shouldn't just be present — it should seem to participate in the mood. Match your weather verbs and adjectives to the emotion of the scene.</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>We Do — Match Weather to Mood</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write a pathetic fallacy description of the weather that reinforces the mood given.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Mood: dread and foreboding (a character approaches an abandoned house)</li><li>Mood: joy and relief (a team wins an important match)</li><li>Mood: loneliness and isolation (someone waits at an empty bus stop)</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Discussion prompt: What weather suits this mood? What verbs and adjectives make the weather seem to share the feeling?</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>You Do — Your Turn</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write 1–2 sentences of pathetic fallacy for each scene.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A character receives unexpected good news on a grey morning.</li><li>A villain makes their entrance during a hot, still afternoon.</li><li>Two old friends meet again after many years apart.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write a short paragraph (4–5 sentences) opening a story, where pathetic fallacy in the first two sentences sets the emotional tone for everything that follows.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Objectives (AOs)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Writing — Vocabulary (Knowledge)</em> — Pathetic fallacy: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Vocabulary (Knowledge): Pathetic fallacy is a literary technique in which the natural environment or weather is described as reflecting or participating in the emotional atmosphere of a scene. Effective use requires matching weather vocabulary — verbs and adjectives — to the emotional register being conveyed. This is a key narrative craft technique at Year 5–6.</li><li><em>Writing — Composing (Practice)</em> — Creating mood through setting: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Composing (Practice): Students are expected to use composing strategies, including figurative language and setting description, to create texts that engage the reader. Pathetic fallacy is the deliberate integration of setting and mood — a hallmark of more accomplished narrative writing at Phase 2.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Tuesday: Verb tense consistency in narrative":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">NZ Curriculum Link</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Writing — Sentence Structure (Knowledge &amp; Practice): narrative writing must maintain a consistent past or present tense throughout; tense shifts within a passage are errors unless used deliberately for effect.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>I Do — Staying in One Tense</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Most narrative writing is written consistently in either the past tense or the present tense. Accidentally switching between them mid-passage is one of the most common errors at Years 5–6. Watch me find and fix tense shifts.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Example: She walked into the room and sees a shadow by the window. She stops and her heart races. She had looked around but nothing moves.</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>'Walked' = past. 'Sees', 'stops', 'races' = present — tense shift. 'Had looked' = past perfect (unnecessary here). Fix: She walked into the room and saw a shadow by the window. She stopped and her heart raced. She looked around but nothing moved. All consistent past tense now.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Tip: Before editing for tense, decide: is this piece past tense or present tense? Then read through looking ONLY for verbs, checking every one matches your decision.</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>We Do — Find and Fix the Tense Shifts</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Find every tense error in this passage and fix it to maintain consistent past tense.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The door creaks open and Hemi steps inside. He looks around the dark room. A noise comes from upstairs. He grabbed his torch and moves to the stairs.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Discussion prompt: Which verbs are in the wrong tense? What is the consistent tense this passage should use?</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>You Do — Your Turn</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Edit this passage to make it consistently past tense throughout.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The team arrives at the stadium early. They warm up on the field and the coach gives them a talk. Everyone had felt nervous, but when the whistle blew they play their best game of the season.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write a paragraph of your own in present tense (as if the reader is watching a scene unfold right now) and check it is fully consistent.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Objectives (AOs)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Writing — Sentence Structure (Knowledge)</em> — Verb tense consistency: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Sentence Structure (Knowledge): Narrative writing must maintain a consistent verb tense — past or present — throughout a passage. Unintentional tense shifts are grammatical errors that disrupt the reader's experience. Deliberate tense shifts such as past perfect for flashback must be clearly controlled and purposeful.</li><li><em>Writing — Writing Process (Practice)</em> — Editing for tense consistency: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Writing Process (Practice): Students are expected to reread and revise their writing to check that it makes sense and is accurate, including checking verb tense. This lesson makes tense consistency an explicit editing skill — deciding the tense first, then reading specifically for verb forms.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Wednesday: Past perfect for flashback":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">NZ Curriculum Link</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Writing — Sentence Structure (Knowledge &amp; Practice): the past perfect tense (had + past participle) signals that an action was completed before the main narrative past tense; it is the grammatical tool for writing flashback sequences.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>I Do — Going Further Back in Time</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>When we're writing in past tense and want to refer to something that happened even earlier — a flashback — we shift into the past perfect (had + past participle). Watch me build a flashback using it.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Example: Main narrative (past): She stared at the old photograph. Flashback (past perfect): She had found it tucked inside her grandmother's journal, on the morning everything had changed. Return to narrative: Now, ten years later, she finally understood what it meant.</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The flashback uses 'had found' and 'had changed' — past perfect — to signal these events happened BEFORE 'stared'. When returning to the main narrative, I switch back to simple past. The tense change IS the signal to the reader that we've moved in time.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Tip: You only need a few past perfect verbs to establish a flashback. Once the reader is clearly in the past, you can drop back to simple past for the body of the flashback.</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>We Do — Build the Flashback</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Use past perfect to add a short flashback to each sentence.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>She looked at the trophy on the shelf. (Add a flashback to the day she won it.)</li><li>He paused at the gate of the old house. (Add a flashback to his childhood there.)</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Discussion prompt: What past perfect verbs open the flashback? How do you signal the return to the main narrative?</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>You Do — Your Turn</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write a short paragraph (3–5 sentences) for each prompt, using past perfect to signal a flashback and simple past to return.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A character finds a faded letter and remembers the day it was written.</li><li>An athlete stands at the start line and recalls her first ever race.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Take a piece of narrative writing you've done this year and add a flashback sentence using past perfect to deepen the backstory.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Objectives (AOs)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Writing — Sentence Structure (Knowledge)</em> — Past perfect for flashback: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Sentence Structure (Knowledge): The past perfect tense (had + past participle) signals that an action was completed before the point established by the main narrative past tense. It is the grammatical mechanism for writing flashback sequences, allowing writers to move back in time within a past-tense narrative.</li><li><em>Writing — Sentence Structure (Practice)</em> — Controlling narrative time: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Sentence Structure (Practice): Students are expected to write grammatically correct sentences using a range of tenses and to use tense deliberately as a structural tool. The past perfect flashback is a significant expansion of students' narrative toolkit — allowing them to manage non-linear storytelling at the sentence-grammar level.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Thursday: Editing for tense consistency":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">NZ Curriculum Link</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Writing — Writing Process (Practice): students apply a tense-consistency editing pass to a multi-paragraph narrative, identifying unintentional tense shifts, distinguishing them from deliberate past perfect flashbacks, and correcting errors systematically.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>I Do — The Tense Edit Pass</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Today I'll model the tense editing pass on a full paragraph — circling every verb, labelling it (simple past / past perfect / present), deciding if shifts are errors or intentional, and fixing the errors.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Example: The explorers push through the undergrowth. It had been three days since they had lost the path. Now the forest grows darker around them. Last year, Mia had studied this very region — she was their only hope.</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Verbs: 'push' (present — error), 'had been' (past perfect — intentional), 'had lost' (past perfect — intentional), 'grows' (present — error), 'had studied' (past perfect — intentional). Fix: 'pushed', 'grew'. The past perfect verbs stay because they correctly reach back in time.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Tip: Circle verbs in pencil first, label each one, THEN erase and fix — never edit while circling or you'll lose track of the pattern.</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>We Do — Circle, Label, Fix</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Together, circle every verb in this passage, label it (simple past / past perfect / present), and fix any unintentional tense shifts.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Aroha runs toward the finish line. She had trained for six months for this moment. Her legs ache but she keeps going. Back in January, she had nearly quit. Now she crossed the line first.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Discussion prompt: Which verbs are errors? Which past perfect verbs are intentional? How do you tell the difference?</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>You Do — Your Turn</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Edit this passage for tense consistency. Fix unintentional shifts — keep intentional past perfect verbs.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The old lighthouse stood at the edge of the cliffs. For years, it had guided ships through the dangerous strait. Now, the light was broken and the keeper has long since gone. He had left one winter when a storm had damaged the lens beyond repair. Today the building sits empty, its windows dark.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Swap a piece of narrative writing with a partner. Each of you do a tense edit pass and report back what you found.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Objectives (AOs)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Writing — Writing Process (Practice)</em> — Tense consistency editing pass: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Writing Process (Practice): Students are expected to reread and revise their writing to check accuracy, including verb tense. This lesson makes tense editing a deliberate, methodical pass — circle all verbs, label, then fix — distinguishing between unintentional errors and purposeful past perfect flashbacks.</li><li><em>Writing — Sentence Structure (Practice)</em> — Distinguishing error from intention in tense: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Sentence Structure (Practice): Students are expected to use tense deliberately for effect, which requires the meta-skill of distinguishing an intentional past perfect construction from an accidental tense shift. This editing lesson develops exactly that judgement within the context of whole-passage revision.</li></ul></div></div>"}}},"Week 3":{"label":"Day","children":{"Monday: Emotive language in persuasive writing":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">NZ Curriculum Link</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Writing — Vocabulary (Knowledge &amp; Practice): emotive language deliberately targets the reader's feelings — using words with strong emotional connotations — to make a persuasive argument feel more urgent, personal, and compelling.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>I Do — Making the Reader Feel the Argument</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Emotive language doesn't just state facts — it makes the reader feel something about them. Watch me compare a neutral and an emotive version of the same argument.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Example: Neutral: Some animals are kept in small spaces in zoos. This is not always good for them. → Emotive: Magnificent wild creatures are condemned to pace the same few square metres for their entire lives — robbed of everything nature intended for them.</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>'Animals' becomes 'magnificent wild creatures'. 'Small spaces' becomes 'the same few square metres'. 'Not always good' becomes 'condemned', 'robbed' — strong verbs with very negative connotations. The facts are the same — the emotional charge is completely different.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Tip: Choose emotive words that match your audience's values — what do THEY care about? Tap into those feelings specifically.</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>We Do — Upgrade to Emotive</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Rewrite each neutral statement using emotive language that targets the reader's feelings.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Some children don't have enough food.</li><li>Old people sometimes feel alone.</li><li>The native forest is being cut down.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Discussion prompt: What emotions should the reader feel? Which words or phrases most powerfully trigger those feelings?</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>You Do — Your Turn</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Rewrite each statement using emotive language to make it more persuasive.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Plastic bags are found in the ocean.</li><li>Animals are tested on in laboratories.</li><li>Children spend a lot of time on screens.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write two versions of a persuasive opening sentence about a cause you care about — one neutral, one emotive. Explain what each version does differently to the reader.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Objectives (AOs)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Writing — Vocabulary (Knowledge)</em> — Emotive language in persuasion: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Vocabulary (Knowledge): Emotive language deliberately selects words with strong emotional connotations to make persuasive writing feel urgent and personal, targeting the reader's feelings rather than only their reason. Emotive vocabulary choices are matched to the values and concerns of the intended audience.</li><li><em>Writing — Vocabulary (Practice)</em> — Selecting words for emotional impact: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Vocabulary (Practice): Students are expected to select and use words and phrases that are fit for purpose and audience, specifically selecting vocabulary for deliberate emotional impact in persuasive writing. This extends Term 3's connotation work into the persuasive writing context.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Tuesday: Conditional sentences":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">NZ Curriculum Link</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Writing — Sentence Structure (Knowledge): conditional sentences express situations and their consequences using if/unless/provided that; students distinguish real (first) from unreal (second) conditionals and use them in persuasive and explanation writing.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>I Do — What Depends on What</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Conditional sentences link a condition to a consequence. First conditional (real/possible): If + present simple, will + infinitive. Second conditional (unreal/hypothetical): If + past simple, would + infinitive. Watch me use both.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Example: First (real): If we reduce plastic use, we will see cleaner oceans within a decade. | Second (unreal): If every child had access to books, the world would be a very different place.</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>First conditional = a realistic possibility and its likely result. Second conditional = a hypothetical scenario and its imagined result. In persuasive writing, first conditionals make arguments feel achievable; second conditionals paint a vivid picture of what could be.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Tip: Check the tense pair: If + present then will (first). If + past then would (second). Mixing them creates an error.</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>We Do — Build the Conditional</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Complete each conditional sentence with the correct form, then label it first or second.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>If schools banned single-use plastics, they ___ (reduce) their environmental footprint.</li><li>If every student reads for 20 minutes a day, their vocabulary ___ (improve).</li><li>If there were more cycle paths, more people ___ (choose) to cycle to work.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Discussion prompt: Is this a real/likely scenario (first) or a hypothetical one (second)? What verb form does each need?</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>You Do — Your Turn</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write a conditional sentence for each persuasive topic, choosing first or second conditional and explaining your choice.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Topic: reducing food waste.</li><li>Topic: planting more native trees.</li><li>Topic: giving students more choice in what they study.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write two persuasive sentences about the same topic — one using a first conditional and one using a second conditional. Explain how each creates a different effect on the reader.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Objectives (AOs)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Writing — Sentence Structure (Knowledge)</em> — Conditional sentences: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Sentence Structure (Knowledge): Conditional sentences link a condition to a consequence. First conditional (if + present simple, will + infinitive) expresses a real or likely possibility; second conditional (if + past simple, would + infinitive) expresses a hypothetical or unreal scenario. Both are important tools in persuasive writing at Phase 2.</li><li><em>Writing — Sentence Structure (Practice)</em> — Using conditionals in persuasive writing: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Sentence Structure (Practice): Students are expected to write grammatically correct sentences using a range of structures, including conditional sentences used deliberately for persuasive effect — first conditional for achievable arguments, second conditional for vivid hypothetical scenarios.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Wednesday: Counter-argument and rebuttal sentence structure":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">NZ Curriculum Link</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Writing — Text Structure (Practice): a sophisticated persuasive text acknowledges the opposing view (counter-argument) and then dismisses or qualifies it (rebuttal); students practise the structural pattern for doing this within a single paragraph.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>I Do — Showing Both Sides, Winning One</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>A strong persuasive writer doesn't ignore the other side — they raise it themselves and then knock it down. This shows intellectual honesty and makes the argument more credible. Watch me build a counter-argument and rebuttal.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Example: Counter: Some people argue that banning plastic bags is inconvenient and costly for businesses. Rebuttal: However, the long-term environmental cost of plastic pollution — estimated at billions of dollars in ocean clean-up — far outweighs any short-term inconvenience.</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>I raised the opposing view fairly. Then I used 'however' to pivot and answered it with evidence that outweighs the objection. The rebuttal must be STRONGER than the counter-argument — if it isn't, I haven't won the point.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Tip: Signal words for counter-argument: 'Some argue that', 'Critics claim'. Signal words for rebuttal: 'However', 'Nevertheless', 'Yet', 'Despite this'.</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>We Do — Build the Counter-Rebuttal Pair</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>For each position, build a counter-argument sentence and then a rebuttal sentence.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Position: School uniforms should be compulsory.</li><li>Position: Students should have phones allowed in class.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Discussion prompt: What is the strongest objection someone would make? Is the rebuttal stronger than the objection?</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>You Do — Your Turn</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write a full counter-argument and rebuttal paragraph (3–4 sentences) for the given position.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Position: All schools should have a vegetarian-only canteen.</li><li>Position: Homework should be banned for students under 12.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Take a PEEL paragraph you wrote earlier this term and add a counter-argument and rebuttal to it, making it into a fuller, more sophisticated persuasive paragraph.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Objectives (AOs)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Writing — Text Structure (Practice)</em> — Counter-argument and rebuttal: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Text Structure (Practice): Students are expected to organise ideas into paragraphs with a clear main idea, supported by relevant detail and reasoning, including in persuasive writing the acknowledgement and dismissal of opposing views. Counter-argument and rebuttal is an advanced structural expectation for Year 5–6 persuasive writing.</li><li><em>Writing — Composing (Practice)</em> — Building credibility through balance: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Composing (Practice): Students are expected to use composing strategies to create texts that engage the reader, including in persuasive writing the strategy of conceding then rebutting, which builds the writer's credibility and authority.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Thursday: Editing: strengthening a weak persuasive argument":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">NZ Curriculum Link</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Writing — Writing Process (Practice): students diagnose the specific weaknesses in a persuasive paragraph (vague evidence, missing rebuttal, informal register, weak connectives) and make targeted revisions to strengthen it.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>I Do — Diagnosing What's Weak</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Not all persuasive problems are the same. Watch me read a weak persuasive paragraph and name exactly what's wrong before fixing anything.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Example: I think everyone should recycle because it's really important. Lots of people already do it. Some people don't bother but they should. Recycling is just a good thing to do.</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Diagnosis: (1) Informal register — 'I think', 'really', 'just'. (2) No specific evidence — 'lots of people' is vague. (3) Counter-rebuttal is present but undeveloped. (4) No persuasive connectives. (5) No PEEL structure. Now I know exactly what to fix, in order of importance.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Tip: Always diagnose BEFORE revising — if you just start rewriting, you might fix one problem and miss four others.</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>We Do — Diagnose Together</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Read this paragraph and produce a diagnosis list before making any changes.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Kids should be able to choose what they eat at school. The food at school isn't very nice. Some people say healthy food is important but lots of kids don't like it. So schools should let kids choose.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Discussion prompt: How many problems can we name? Which is the most serious? What is the priority order for fixing them?</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>You Do — Your Turn</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Diagnose then rewrite this paragraph to fix all its weaknesses.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Zoos are bad for animals. Animals don't like being in cages. People argue that zoos help conservation but animals are still unhappy. We should close zoos.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Diagnose a piece of your own persuasive writing from earlier this term using the same checklist. Write a brief diagnosis, then make the most important fixes.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Objectives (AOs)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Writing — Writing Process (Practice)</em> — Diagnosing persuasive weaknesses: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Writing Process (Practice): Students are expected to reread and revise their writing, making changes to vocabulary, punctuation, and structure to improve clarity and impact. This lesson teaches diagnosis as a deliberate pre-revision step — naming specific weaknesses before beginning revision.</li><li><em>Writing — Text Structure (Practice)</em> — Targeted persuasive revision: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Text Structure (Practice): Students are expected to organise ideas into paragraphs with a clear main idea, supported by relevant detail and reasoning. This editing lesson consolidates all of Term 4's persuasive skills — register, PEEL, counter-rebuttal, connectives, and emotive language — into a single diagnosis-and-repair process.</li></ul></div></div>"}}},"Week 4":{"label":"Day","children":{"Monday: Technical vocabulary in explanation writing":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">NZ Curriculum Link</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Writing — Vocabulary (Knowledge &amp; Practice): explanation writing uses precise technical vocabulary specific to the topic being explained; students learn to introduce technical terms with definitions and use them consistently.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>I Do — Using the Right Words for the Job</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Explanation writing uses technical terms — the exact, agreed vocabulary of a subject. Watch me introduce a technical term correctly and then use it consistently.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Example: Weak: Water turns into gas when it gets hot enough. → With technical vocab: When water reaches 100 degrees Celsius, it undergoes evaporation — the process by which liquid water converts into water vapour. This vapour rises and forms part of the water cycle.</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>I introduced the technical terms 'evaporation' and 'water cycle' with built-in definitions, then used them consistently instead of replacing them with vaguer words. Technical writing is precise because its audience expects accuracy.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Tip: Introduce each technical term with a definition the first time, then use it consistently. Don't replace it with a vague synonym — that reduces precision.</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>We Do — Introduce and Use</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Upgrade each sentence by introducing a technical term with a definition, then use it in a follow-up sentence.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The moon's pull makes the ocean water move up and down. (technical term: tidal force / tides)</li><li>Plants use sunlight to make food. (technical terms: photosynthesis, chlorophyll)</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Discussion prompt: What is the correct technical term? How do we define it for the reader the first time?</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>You Do — Your Turn</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Rewrite each sentence by introducing the appropriate technical term and defining it, then write a second sentence using the term correctly.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Rocks are broken into smaller pieces by weather and water. (key term: erosion/weathering)</li><li>The heart pumps blood around the body. (key terms: circulation, artery/vein)</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write a 4-sentence explanation paragraph about a process you know well, using at least two technical terms correctly introduced with definitions.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Objectives (AOs)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Writing — Vocabulary (Knowledge)</em> — Technical vocabulary in explanation writing: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Vocabulary (Knowledge): Explanation writing uses precise technical vocabulary specific to its subject. Technical terms are introduced with clear definitions when first used and then applied consistently — not replaced with vague synonyms — because precision is central to the explanation text type at Phase 2.</li><li><em>Writing — Vocabulary (Practice)</em> — Consistent use of technical terms: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Vocabulary (Practice): Students are expected to select and use words and phrases that are fit for purpose and audience, including accurate technical vocabulary appropriate to explanation writing. This lesson makes technical term introduction and consistent usage an explicit writing strategy.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Tuesday: Present tense conventions in explanation writing":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">NZ Curriculum Link</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Writing — Sentence Structure (Knowledge): explanation writing typically uses the simple present tense because it describes how things generally work (timeless truth), not what happened on one specific occasion.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>I Do — Timeless Truth vs. One-Time Event</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Explanation writing describes how things GENERALLY work — so it uses the simple present tense (timeless truth), not the past tense (which suggests it only happened once). Watch me compare.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Example: Past tense (narrative): The volcano erupted and lava flowed down the hillside. → Present tense (explanation): When pressure builds beneath the earth's surface, magma forces its way through cracks and erupts as lava.</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The past tense version sounds like a specific historical event. The present tense version describes the general, repeatable process — which is what explanation writing does. 'Forces', 'erupts' = timeless present = this is how it always works.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Tip: If your explanation says what happened, switch it to say what happens. That one word change shifts a narrative sentence into an explanation sentence.</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>We Do — Switch to Explanation Tense</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Rewrite each past-tense narrative sentence as a present-tense explanation sentence.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The roots of the plant absorbed water from the soil and carried it to the leaves.</li><li>The caterpillar made a cocoon and changed into a butterfly inside.</li><li>The snow melted when the temperature rose above zero.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Discussion prompt: Does the rewritten sentence describe a general, repeatable process? Does it still make sense in the present tense?</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>You Do — Your Turn</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Rewrite each sentence in the timeless present tense appropriate for explanation writing.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The earthquake shook the buildings and caused cracks in the roads.</li><li>The bee flew from flower to flower and collected pollen on its legs.</li><li>The light passed through the prism and split into seven colours.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write a 3-sentence explanation of how something natural works, consistently in the timeless present tense.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Objectives (AOs)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Writing — Sentence Structure (Knowledge)</em> — Present tense in explanation writing: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Sentence Structure (Knowledge): Explanation writing typically uses the simple present tense to convey timeless truth — describing how processes generally work, not what happened on one specific occasion. This distinguishes explanation from narrative and recount text types, which use past tense for specific events.</li><li><em>Writing — Text Structure (Knowledge)</em> — Text type and tense convention: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Text Structure (Knowledge): Different text types use different tense conventions: narrative and recount use past tense for specific events; explanation uses simple present for general processes; procedure uses imperative or present tense for instructions. Understanding tense as a text-type signal is a key Phase 2 metalinguistic understanding.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Wednesday: Structuring a full explanation paragraph":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">NZ Curriculum Link</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Writing — Text Structure (Practice): students build a complete, well-structured explanation paragraph with a topic sentence, logically sequenced cause-and-effect sentences using technical vocabulary, and a concluding link sentence.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>I Do — The Explanation Paragraph Blueprint</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>A well-structured explanation paragraph follows a clear pattern: topic sentence (what you're explaining) → cause-and-effect body sentences (the how/why) → concluding link (the outcome or significance). Watch me build one.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Example: Topic: How does rain form? Topic sentence: Rain forms through a continuous cycle of evaporation, condensation, and precipitation. Body: When the sun heats surface water, evaporation occurs — liquid water converts to water vapour and rises into the atmosphere. As the vapour cools at higher altitudes, it condenses around tiny dust particles, forming clouds. When cloud droplets combine and grow heavy enough, they fall as precipitation. Link: This cycle repeats continuously, driven by solar energy, and is essential to all life on Earth.</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Every sentence does a specific job. Topic sentence names the process and its stages. Body sentences use cause-and-effect connectives and technical terms. Concluding link gives the broader significance.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Tip: Read your topic sentence and your concluding link together. They should form a logical bracket around the paragraph.</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>We Do — Build Together</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Together, build a complete explanation paragraph for the topic given.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Topic: How does a rainbow form? (Key terms: refraction, spectrum, wavelength)</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Discussion prompt: What goes in the topic sentence? What are the key cause-and-effect steps? What does the concluding link say?</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>You Do — Your Turn</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write a complete explanation paragraph for the topic given, using the full blueprint: topic sentence → cause-and-effect body → concluding link.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Topic: How do volcanoes form? (Key terms: magma, tectonic plates, eruption, lava)</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write an explanation paragraph on a topic from another subject you're studying at the moment, applying the same blueprint and using correct technical vocabulary.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Objectives (AOs)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Writing — Text Structure (Practice)</em> — Structuring an explanation paragraph: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Text Structure (Practice): Students are expected to organise ideas into paragraphs with a clear main idea, supported by relevant detail and reasoning specific to the explanation text type. This lesson gives students an explicit, repeatable blueprint — topic sentence, cause-and-effect body, concluding link — as a transferable cross-curricular writing skill.</li><li><em>Writing — Text Structure (Knowledge)</em> — Explanation paragraph structure: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Text Structure (Knowledge): A well-structured explanation paragraph opens with a topic sentence naming the process to be explained, develops through logically sequenced cause-and-effect sentences using appropriate technical vocabulary, and closes with a linking sentence that states the outcome or broader significance.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Thursday: Editing explanation writing for clarity and accuracy":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">NZ Curriculum Link</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Writing — Writing Process (Practice): students apply an explanation-specific editing checklist — checking tense, technical vocabulary, cause-and-effect connectives, paragraph structure, and factual accuracy — to revise a weak explanation paragraph.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>I Do — The Explanation Edit Checklist</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Explanation writing has its own specific editing checklist. Watch me apply it to a weak paragraph.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Example: The water cycle happens when water goes up into the sky. It then came back down as rain. The clouds made from water make rain fall. This is called the water cycle and it happened all the time.</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Checklist: Present tense? — 'came' = past tense error. Technical vocabulary? — 'goes up' should be 'evaporates'. Cause-and-effect connectives? — none used. Topic sentence? — present but vague. Concluding link? — 'happened' = tense error; 'all the time' = informal. Fix each in order.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Tip: Accuracy matters in explanation writing — if you're not sure a fact or term is correct, flag it for checking rather than leaving an error in.</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>We Do — Apply the Checklist Together</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Apply the explanation editing checklist to this paragraph together — check each item in order.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Photosynthesis is when plants made food. They use the sunlight which hits the leaves. The green stuff in leaves (chlorophyll) catches the sun. Carbon dioxide went in and oxygen comes out. Plants need this for living.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Discussion prompt: Which checklist item has the most errors? What is the priority order for fixing this paragraph?</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>You Do — Your Turn</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Edit this explanation paragraph using the full checklist — tense, technical vocabulary, connectives, structure, and accuracy.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Erosion happens when rocks and soil got moved by wind and water. Rivers carried rocks downstream and they break into smaller pieces over time. This is why valleys formed and the coastline changes. Erosion happened over millions of years.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Apply the explanation editing checklist to the paragraph you wrote on Wednesday. What did you find?</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Objectives (AOs)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Writing — Writing Process (Practice)</em> — Explanation-specific editing checklist: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Writing Process (Practice): Students are expected to reread and revise their writing, making changes to vocabulary, punctuation, and structure to improve clarity and impact, specifically applying text-type criteria to explanation writing. This lesson gives students a five-point explanation checklist as a transferable cross-curricular editing tool.</li><li><em>Writing — Text Structure (Practice)</em> — Revising explanation structure: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Text Structure (Practice): Students are expected to organise explanation writing with clear cause-and-effect structure and appropriate language features. The editing checklist consolidates all four days of this week's explanation writing work into one applied revision skill.</li></ul></div></div>"}}},"Week 5":{"label":"Day","children":{"Monday: Synonyms for said — precise dialogue tags":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">NZ Curriculum Link</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Writing — Vocabulary (Practice): 'said' is a neutral dialogue tag; replacing it with a precise verb (muttered, snapped, insisted, whispered) tells the reader HOW something was said and adds characterisation without additional description.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>I Do — Said Tells Us Nothing</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>'Said' is invisible — readers skip over it. A precise dialogue tag tells the reader the manner, tone, and emotion of speech in one word. Watch me upgrade dialogue by choosing precise tags.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Example: 'I don't know where it is,' she said. → 'I don't know where it is,' she muttered, not meeting his eyes.</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>'Muttered' immediately tells us she's evasive, uncertain, perhaps embarrassed. I also added a small beat ('not meeting his eyes') to show rather than tell the emotion further. The combination of precise tag + action beat is the most powerful dialogue technique at this level.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Tip: Match the tag to the emotion, not just the volume: 'shouted' only tells us it was loud. 'Snarled', 'barked', 'demanded' tell us it was loud AND angry/aggressive.</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>We Do — Choose the Precise Tag</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Replace 'said' with the most precise dialogue tag for the context described.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>'Leave me alone,' she said. (emotion: angry, defensive)</li><li>'I think... maybe... we could try it,' he said. (emotion: uncertain, nervous)</li><li>'I know exactly where it is,' she said. (emotion: confident, perhaps smug)</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Discussion prompt: What emotion AND manner is the character expressing? Which verb captures both?</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>You Do — Your Turn</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Replace 'said' with a precise dialogue tag and add a short action beat to show the emotion further.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>'That's not fair,' she said.</li><li>'Come on, we'll be fine,' he said.</li><li>'I didn't do it,' the boy said.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write a 4-line dialogue between two characters in conflict, using a different precise dialogue tag for each line — no 'said' allowed.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Objectives (AOs)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Writing — Vocabulary (Practice)</em> — Precise dialogue tags: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Vocabulary (Practice): Students are expected to select and use words and phrases that are fit for purpose and audience, including precise dialogue tags that convey manner and emotion rather than simply signalling speech. This is a specific application of the term's precise-verb-choice work to dialogue writing.</li><li><em>Writing — Composing (Practice)</em> — Characterisation through dialogue: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Composing (Practice): Students are expected to use composing strategies to create texts that engage the reader, including characterisation through dialogue. Precise dialogue tags and action beats together create character voice and emotional subtext without relying on explicit description.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Tuesday: Concessive clauses":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">NZ Curriculum Link</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Writing — Sentence Structure (Knowledge): a concessive clause (introduced by although, even though, despite, while, whereas) acknowledges one idea while asserting a contrasting or more important one, adding sophistication to both persuasive and analytical writing.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>I Do — Acknowledging Before Asserting</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>A concessive clause admits something is true, then asserts a more important contrasting truth. It's the grammar of nuance. Watch me build sentences using it.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Example: Plain: Some students find homework hard. Many still benefit from it. → Concessive: Although some students find homework challenging, the majority benefit significantly from the practice it provides.</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>'Although' introduces the concession (the admitted truth), then the main clause asserts the stronger claim. Other concessive words: 'even though', 'despite', 'while', 'whereas'. Notice that 'despite' takes a noun phrase, not a clause: 'Despite its challenges, homework...' — not 'Despite it is challenging'.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Tip: 'Despite' is followed by a noun or -ing form, not a full clause. 'Despite its being difficult' is correct. 'Despite it is difficult' is not.</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>We Do — Build the Concessive</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Combine each pair using a concessive clause opener.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>It was raining heavily. The match continued. (use: despite)</li><li>The new policy has its critics. It has already reduced pollution by 20%. (use: although)</li><li>The climb was gruelling. The view from the summit made it worthwhile. (use: even though)</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Discussion prompt: Which part is the concession and which is the main assertion? Does 'despite' need a noun or -ing form?</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>You Do — Your Turn</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write a concessive sentence for each pair using the conjunction given.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Use 'although': Electric cars are currently expensive / they produce far lower emissions over their lifetime.</li><li>Use 'despite': The project took three years / the results exceeded all expectations.</li><li>Use 'whereas': Some students prefer group work / others find individual tasks more productive.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write three sentences about the same controversial topic, each using a different concessive conjunction. Do they create subtly different effects?</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Objectives (AOs)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Writing — Sentence Structure (Knowledge)</em> — Concessive clauses: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Sentence Structure (Knowledge): A concessive clause, introduced by although, even though, despite, while, or whereas, acknowledges one idea while asserting a contrasting or more significant one. 'Despite' takes a noun phrase or gerund, not a full clause. Concessive clauses are a hallmark of sophisticated Phase 2 persuasive and analytical writing.</li><li><em>Writing — Sentence Structure (Practice)</em> — Nuanced sentence construction: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Sentence Structure (Practice): Students are expected to write a variety of sentence structures for effect and clarity, including concessive constructions that create nuance by balancing two contrasting ideas within a single sentence. This is closely related to the counter-argument work of Week 3.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Wednesday: Building tension with sentence length variation":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">NZ Curriculum Link</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Writing — Sentence Structure (Practice) &amp; Composing (Practice): writers deliberately manipulate sentence length to control pace and tension — short sentences slow the reader down and create impact, long sentences sustain flow or build complexity.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>I Do — Controlling the Reader's Heartbeat</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Short sentences make the reader stop, breathe, and feel the weight of a moment. Long sentences carry them forward in a rush of action or detail. The writer's job is to control this deliberately. Watch me use both.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Example: Long then short: The crowd surged forward, voices rising to a single deafening roar as the scoreboard flickered, counted down, and finally froze on zero. Then: silence.</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The long sentence mimics the building rush of noise and action. 'Then: silence.' — two words, the colon, the full stop — brings everything to a dead halt. The contrast makes both sentences more powerful than if the whole passage were the same length.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Tip: For a sudden shock or revelation, make it the shortest sentence in the paragraph. The contrast with the sentences around it does the work.</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>We Do — Design the Tension</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Rewrite this passage by varying the sentence lengths to build tension toward the last sentence.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>She walked to the door. She opened it. There was a figure standing outside. She screamed.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Discussion prompt: Which sentences should be long (building dread)? Which single sentence should be the shortest (impact point)?</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>You Do — Your Turn</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write a tense paragraph (5–7 sentences) for each scenario, deliberately varying sentence length to build toward a single short impact sentence at the end.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Scenario: a player takes the last penalty in a shootout.</li><li>Scenario: a character reaches the top of a mountain and sees what's on the other side.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Find a tense moment in a book you're reading. Count the sentence lengths. Does the author use this technique? Copy the pattern into your own writing about a different scenario.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Objectives (AOs)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Writing — Sentence Structure (Practice)</em> — Sentence length variation for tension: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Sentence Structure (Practice): Students are expected to write a variety of sentence structures for effect and clarity, specifically using deliberate sentence-length contrast to control pace and tension in narrative writing. This formalises the intuitive understanding of rhythm developed in Term 3 into a conscious narrative tool.</li><li><em>Writing — Composing (Practice)</em> — Controlling narrative pace and tension: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Composing (Practice): Students are expected to use composing strategies to create texts that engage the reader, including deliberate sentence-length manipulation for pacing and tension. This is one of the highest-level narrative craft skills at Phase 2.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Thursday: Editing narrative pacing and tension":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">NZ Curriculum Link</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Writing — Writing Process (Practice): students apply a pacing-and-tension editing lens to a narrative passage — identifying where pace is too flat, adding sentence-length variation, short impact sentences, and precise verbs to heighten tension.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>I Do — Reading for Pace</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>When editing for pacing, I read the passage aloud at its current rhythm and ask: where does it drag? Where does it rush past something important? Watch me diagnose and fix a flat passage.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Example: He walked to the edge. He looked down. It was very high. He felt scared. He stepped back.</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Every sentence is the same length — the rhythm is completely flat. Fix: He crept to the edge, his breath shallow and quick. He looked down. Far below, the river glittered, cold and indifferent. He was so high. His foot found the edge — and he stepped back. Now there's a build, a reveal, a moment of dread, an impact, and a release.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Tip: Read the passage aloud. If every sentence takes the same breath to say — vary at least one.</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>We Do — Diagnose and Fix</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Read this passage aloud. Identify where the pacing is flat, then rewrite it with deliberate sentence-length variation.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The storm was getting worse. The boat rocked from side to side. A wave hit the deck. Everyone held on. Another wave came. The boat tilted dangerously.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Discussion prompt: Where should pace slow down to build dread? Where should a single short sentence hit like a punch?</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>You Do — Your Turn</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Edit this passage for pacing and tension using sentence-length variation, precise verbs, and one short impact sentence.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The lion moved through the grass. It was looking at the zebra. The zebra didn't see it. The lion got closer. Then it ran at the zebra.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Take the most tense moment from a piece of your own narrative writing. Read it aloud, identify the flattest part, and revise specifically for sentence-length variation and precise verb choice.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Objectives (AOs)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Writing — Writing Process (Practice)</em> — Editing for pacing and tension: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Writing Process (Practice): Students are expected to reread and revise their writing, making changes to vocabulary and structure to improve clarity and impact. This lesson teaches reading aloud as a pacing diagnostic tool and then applies sentence-length variation and precise verbs as the specific revision tools.</li><li><em>Writing — Composing (Practice)</em> — Pacing as a craft decision: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Composing (Practice): Students are expected to use composing strategies to create texts that engage the reader, including managing narrative pacing as a deliberate craft choice. This editing lesson applies Wednesday's sentence-length technique to a real revision context.</li></ul></div></div>"}}},"Week 6":{"label":"Day","children":{"Monday: Cohesive substitution — avoiding repetition":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">NZ Curriculum Link</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Writing — Vocabulary (Practice): cohesive substitution replaces repeated nouns or noun phrases with synonyms, pronouns, or general-reference nouns to create flow without monotony, while maintaining clarity about who or what is being referred to.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>I Do — Saying the Same Thing a Different Way</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Repeating the same noun in every sentence is monotonous. But replacing it carelessly can confuse the reader. Watch me substitute carefully.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Example: Repetitive: The kiwi is a flightless bird. The kiwi is nocturnal. The kiwi is endangered. The kiwi can only be found in New Zealand. → Substituted: The kiwi is a flightless, nocturnal bird. This remarkable creature is endangered and can only be found in New Zealand — our unique national symbol.</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>'The kiwi' becomes 'this remarkable creature' (adds description) and then 'our unique national symbol' (adds perspective). Both substitutions keep it clear we're still talking about the kiwi, while adding new information.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Tip: Never substitute in a way that could confuse the reader about who or what you mean. If the substitution could refer to something else, keep the noun.</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>We Do — Substitute Without Confusing</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Rewrite this passage by substituting the repeated noun at least twice — without confusing the reader.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The kauri tree is one of New Zealand's most ancient trees. The kauri tree can live for thousands of years. The kauri tree was heavily logged in the 19th century. The kauri tree is now a protected species.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Discussion prompt: What synonyms or pronoun references could replace 'the kauri tree'? Does each substitution still clearly refer to the kauri?</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>You Do — Your Turn</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Rewrite each passage using cohesive substitution to reduce repetition without losing clarity.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Elephants are the largest land animals on Earth. Elephants live in family groups. Elephants have excellent memories. Elephants are threatened by habitat loss and poaching.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Take a paragraph of your own writing and highlight every repeated noun. Try at least two substitutions — then check that a reader could still follow clearly who or what you're talking about.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Objectives (AOs)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Writing — Vocabulary (Practice)</em> — Cohesive substitution: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Vocabulary (Practice): Students are expected to select and use words and phrases that are fit for purpose and audience, including using synonyms, pronouns, and reference nouns as cohesive substitution devices to reduce repetition while maintaining textual clarity. This is an advanced vocabulary and cohesion skill at Phase 2.</li><li><em>Writing — Text Structure (Practice)</em> — Cohesion through substitution: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Text Structure (Practice): Students are expected to use cohesive devices — including pronouns and reference words — to link ideas within and across sentences. This lesson extends Term 3's cohesion work beyond connectives into the more subtle substitution devices that distinguish mature Phase 2 writing.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Tuesday: Relative clauses for embedded definitions":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">NZ Curriculum Link</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Writing — Sentence Structure (Practice): a relative clause (introduced by which, who, where, whose) can embed a definition or explanation directly inside a sentence, avoiding a clunky separate definition sentence — a key technique in explanation and formal writing.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>I Do — Defining Without Interrupting</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Instead of writing a separate definition sentence, I can embed the definition inside the main sentence using a relative clause. Watch me do it.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Example: Separate: Photosynthesis is a process. It converts sunlight into energy. Plants use it. → Embedded: Photosynthesis, which converts sunlight into chemical energy, is the process that sustains almost all plant life on Earth.</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The relative clause 'which converts sunlight into chemical energy' is embedded between commas, defining the term right where it appears. The reader gets the definition without the flow being interrupted by a separate sentence.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Tip: If the relative clause is essential to identifying WHICH thing you mean, don't use commas. If it's adding extra information about something already identified, use commas around it.</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>We Do — Embed the Definition</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Combine each pair into one sentence using a relative clause to embed the definition.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Erosion is a process. It gradually wears away rock and soil.</li><li>Te Tiriti o Waitangi is a document. It was signed in 1840 between Maori chiefs and the Crown.</li><li>The ozone layer is a region of Earth's atmosphere. It absorbs most of the sun's ultraviolet radiation.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Discussion prompt: Does the relative clause add extra information (commas) or identify which one (no commas)?</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>You Do — Your Turn</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Rewrite each pair as a single sentence, embedding the definition with a relative clause.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Metamorphosis is a process. It transforms a caterpillar into a butterfly.</li><li>New Zealand is a country. It was the first in the world to grant women the right to vote.</li><li>A neuron is a cell. It transmits electrical signals through the nervous system.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Find a technical term in your reading or class notes. Write a sentence that introduces and defines it using an embedded relative clause.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Objectives (AOs)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Writing — Sentence Structure (Practice)</em> — Relative clauses for embedded definitions: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Sentence Structure (Practice): Students are expected to write a variety of sentence structures for effect and clarity, including using relative clauses to embed definitions or elaborations directly within a sentence — a key tool for explanation and formal writing. This consolidates relative clause knowledge from earlier terms into a specific, functional application.</li><li><em>Writing — Vocabulary (Practice)</em> — Technical vocabulary integration: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Vocabulary (Practice): Students are expected to select and use words and phrases that are fit for purpose and audience, including integrating technical terms with their definitions smoothly and economically within explanation sentences. The embedded relative clause definition is the most sophisticated tool for this at Phase 2.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Wednesday: Topic sentences and paragraph-closing sentences":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">NZ Curriculum Link</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Writing — Text Structure (Practice): a well-constructed paragraph opens with a topic sentence that states the main idea and closes with a sentence that clinches or links that idea; students distinguish strong from weak topic sentences and write effective closers.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>I Do — Opening and Closing a Paragraph Cleanly</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The topic sentence is a promise to the reader about what this paragraph will deliver. The closing sentence clinches that promise or links to the next paragraph. Watch me write both.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Example: Weak topic sentence: 'This paragraph is about recycling.' → Strong: 'Recycling reduces the pressure on landfill sites while conserving the natural resources needed to produce new materials.' Weak closer: 'So that is why recycling is good.' → Strong: 'For these reasons, recycling is not merely a personal choice — it is a shared environmental responsibility.'</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The strong topic sentence makes a specific claim that the paragraph can prove. The strong closer doesn't just repeat the topic — it elevates it, leaving the reader with a stronger impression.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Tip: Test your topic sentence: could the reader predict what your paragraph will contain? Test your closer: does it add something, or just repeat the topic sentence?</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>We Do — Improve the Topic and Closer</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Improve the weak topic sentence and closer for this paragraph.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Weak topic: 'New Zealand has a lot of native birds.' [body: kiwi, kakapo, takahe details] Weak closer: 'So New Zealand native birds are quite unique.'</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Discussion prompt: What specific claim could the topic sentence make? What does the closer need to add — elevation, significance, or a link?</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>You Do — Your Turn</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write a strong topic sentence and closing sentence for each paragraph body given.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Body: sentences about how exercise releases endorphins, reduces stress hormones, and improves sleep quality.</li><li>Body: sentences about how deforestation reduces biodiversity, increases carbon emissions, and leads to soil erosion.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Take any paragraph from a piece of your own writing. Rewrite its topic sentence to make a more specific claim, and rewrite its closing sentence to elevate or link rather than just repeat.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Objectives (AOs)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Writing — Text Structure (Practice)</em> — Topic and closing sentences: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Text Structure (Practice): Students are expected to organise ideas into paragraphs with a clear main idea established by a strong topic sentence, and to close paragraphs with a sentence that clinches or links — not merely repeats — the main idea. Strong topic and closing sentences are hallmarks of well-controlled Phase 2 writing.</li><li><em>Writing — Composing (Practice)</em> — Paragraph-level composition: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Composing (Practice): Students are expected to use composing strategies to create texts that engage the reader, including at the paragraph level — opening with a specific, testable claim and closing with an elevated or linking sentence.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Thursday: Adapting a piece for a different audience":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">NZ Curriculum Link</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Writing — Composing (Practice) &amp; Vocabulary (Practice): students revise a piece of writing for a different audience, changing vocabulary, register, examples, and level of detail to suit the new reader without changing the core information or argument.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>I Do — Same Message, Different Reader</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The same information can be written very differently depending on the audience. Watch me adapt a paragraph about recycling from a formal audience to a young audience.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Example: Formal: Recycling reduces landfill pressure and conserves finite natural resources, contributing to a circular economy. | Young audience: When we recycle, we help keep rubbish out of enormous rubbish dumps called landfills, and we save natural materials so they can be used again.</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Same facts, completely different vocabulary, sentence structure, and examples. Neither version is wrong — they're both right for their audience.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Tip: Ask three questions before adapting: (1) How much does this audience already know? (2) What vocabulary will they understand? (3) What examples will feel relevant to their lives?</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>We Do — Adapt Together</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Adapt this paragraph — written for adults — for an audience of Year 4 students.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>New Zealand's native biodiversity is under significant threat from introduced predators, particularly rats, stoats, and possums, which compete with or prey directly upon endemic species.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Discussion prompt: What vocabulary needs changing? What background knowledge can we assume? What everyday examples could replace the technical ones?</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>You Do — Your Turn</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Adapt each passage for the new audience given.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Original (for Year 6): 'Photosynthesis is the process by which plants convert solar energy into glucose using chlorophyll.' → Adapt for Year 2.</li><li>Original (for Year 2): 'Leaves are green because of a special ingredient called chlorophyll.' → Adapt for an adult science magazine.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Take a paragraph from your own persuasive or explanation writing and adapt it for two different audiences — your school principal, and a Year 3 student. What changes between the two versions?</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Objectives (AOs)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Writing — Composing (Practice)</em> — Audience adaptation: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Composing (Practice): Students are expected to use composing strategies appropriate to audience and purpose, including adapting existing writing for a new reader by changing vocabulary, register, sentence structure, and examples while preserving the core content. Audience adaptation is a key Phase 2 composing skill with direct cross-curricular application.</li><li><em>Writing — Vocabulary (Practice)</em> — Register and vocabulary for audience: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Vocabulary (Practice): Students are expected to select and use words and phrases that are fit for purpose and audience. This lesson applies the full year's vocabulary and register work to a genuine revision task — rewriting for a different audience — making register not just a label but an active writing decision.</li></ul></div></div>"}}},"Week 7":{"label":"Day","children":{"Monday: Hyperbole for effect":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">NZ Curriculum Link</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Writing — Vocabulary (Knowledge): hyperbole is deliberate and obvious exaggeration used for humorous, dramatic, or emphatic effect — not intended to be taken literally.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>I Do — The Art of Enormous Exaggeration</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Hyperbole is deliberate over-the-top exaggeration for effect. Unlike lying, the reader KNOWS it's exaggerated — that's the point. Watch me use it for comic and dramatic effect.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Example: Comic: I've told you a million times to clean your room. | Dramatic: The silence in the hall was so complete you could have heard a heartbeat three streets away.</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>'A million times' is obviously untrue — its comic effect comes from the scale of the exaggeration. 'Heard a heartbeat three streets away' is physically impossible — its dramatic effect comes from the image of extreme, unnatural stillness.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Tip: Hyperbole works best when it's CLEARLY impossible — the more obviously exaggerated, the better. Mild exaggeration just sounds like an inaccuracy.</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>We Do — Build the Hyperbole</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write a hyperbolic sentence for each situation, making the exaggeration as vivid and obviously impossible as possible.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The bag was very heavy.</li><li>The test was very long.</li><li>He was extremely hungry.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Discussion prompt: Is the exaggeration obvious enough to be clearly hyperbole? Does it create a vivid image?</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>You Do — Your Turn</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write a hyperbolic sentence for each prompt.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>She ran very fast.</li><li>The crowd was enormous.</li><li>It was a very boring speech.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write a short comic paragraph (3–4 sentences) about a small everyday annoyance using at least two hyperboles. Aim to make the reader laugh.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Objectives (AOs)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Writing — Vocabulary (Knowledge)</em> — Hyperbole: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Vocabulary (Knowledge): Hyperbole is deliberate and obvious exaggeration used for humorous, dramatic, or emphatic effect — not intended to be taken literally by the reader. Its effectiveness depends on the scale of the exaggeration being recognisably impossible. This completes the year's range of figurative language devices.</li><li><em>Writing — Composing (Practice)</em> — Comic and dramatic voice: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Composing (Practice): Students are expected to use composing strategies to create texts that engage the reader, including humour as a deliberate compositional choice. Hyperbole is one of the primary tools for creating comic voice in writing.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Tuesday: Sentence fragments for deliberate effect":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">NZ Curriculum Link</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Writing — Sentence Structure (Knowledge): a sentence fragment lacks a subject, verb, or complete thought; while fragments are errors in formal writing, skilled authors use them deliberately in narrative writing for pace, emphasis, and dramatic effect.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>I Do — Breaking the Rules on Purpose</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>A fragment is technically incomplete — but skilled writers use them deliberately in narrative for impact. The key is: it must be clearly intentional, not accidental. Watch me use a fragment deliberately.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Example: Complete sentences: He reached the top of the hill and looked out at the view. He had never seen anything like it. → With deliberate fragment: He reached the top of the hill and looked out. Nothing could have prepared him. Endless. Wild. Breathtaking.</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>'Endless. Wild. Breathtaking.' — three fragments in a row. They create a staccato, breathless effect, mimicking the gasp of someone overwhelmed by a view. The incompleteness IS the effect.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Tip: Only use deliberate fragments in creative/narrative writing — NEVER in formal, persuasive, or explanation writing. And always be able to explain why the fragment is there.</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>We Do — Add a Deliberate Fragment</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Add one or two deliberate fragments to heighten the impact of each passage.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>She opened the box. Inside was a letter she had been waiting for.</li><li>The final whistle blew. The scoreboard showed the result.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Discussion prompt: Where would a single-word or short fragment create the most impact? Is the fragment placement clearly intentional?</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>You Do — Your Turn</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Add deliberate fragments to each narrative moment to heighten the effect. Mark each fragment with [F] so it's clear it's intentional.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>He stepped off the plane into the cold air of a new country.</li><li>She looked at the test results for a long time.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write a paragraph describing an overwhelming moment that uses at least three deliberate fragments for effect.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Objectives (AOs)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Writing — Sentence Structure (Knowledge)</em> — Deliberate sentence fragments: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Sentence Structure (Knowledge): A sentence fragment is a grammatically incomplete sentence lacking a subject, verb, or complete thought. While fragments are errors in formal writing, skilled authors use them deliberately in narrative writing to create pace, emphasis, or a staccato dramatic effect. Deliberate fragments must be clearly intentional and contextually justified.</li><li><em>Writing — Composing (Practice)</em> — Style and craft in narrative voice: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Composing (Practice): Students are expected to use composing strategies to create texts that engage the reader. Understanding when and why to break a grammar rule — and being able to justify the choice — is a hallmark of the most sophisticated writing at Phase 2 and into Year 7.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Wednesday: Opening hooks for different text types":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">NZ Curriculum Link</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Writing — Composing (Practice): an effective opening hook immediately engages the reader; different text types use different hook strategies — narrative (in-scene, sensory), persuasive (bold claim, statistic, rhetorical question), explanation (surprising fact, question).</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>I Do — The First Sentence Does the Heavy Lifting</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The opening sentence determines whether the reader continues. Watch me write strong hooks for three different text types.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Example: Narrative hook (in-scene): The smell of smoke reached her before the alarm did. | Persuasive hook (bold claim): Every day, the equivalent of 2,000 trees' worth of paper is wasted in New Zealand's schools. | Explanation hook (surprising fact): Despite its name, a starfish is not actually a fish — and cannot survive outside saltwater for more than a few minutes.</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Each hook uses a different strategy: narrative puts the reader immediately into a sensory moment. Persuasive opens with a specific, credible statistic. Explanation opens with a counterintuitive fact that creates curiosity.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Tip: The hook's job is to make the reader ask a question they want answered. What question does each of your hooks make the reader want to answer?</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>We Do — Write the Hook</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write a strong opening hook for each text type and topic.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Narrative: a character who wakes up in an unfamiliar place.</li><li>Persuasive: why zoos should be redesigned as conservation sanctuaries.</li><li>Explanation: how the human eye works.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Discussion prompt: Which strategy suits each text type? Does your hook make the reader want to read the next sentence?</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>You Do — Your Turn</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write two different opening hooks for each topic — one using a narrative strategy, one using a persuasive or explanation strategy.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Topic: the ocean.</li><li>Topic: learning a new skill.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Go back to a piece of writing you completed earlier this term. Rewrite its opening sentence using a different hook strategy — does the new hook work better?</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Objectives (AOs)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Writing — Composing (Practice)</em> — Opening hooks by text type: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Composing (Practice): Students are expected to use composing strategies to create texts that engage the reader, including selecting an appropriate opening hook strategy matched to the text type and purpose. Narrative hooks use sensory/in-scene openings; persuasive hooks use bold claims, statistics, or rhetorical questions; explanation hooks use surprising or counterintuitive facts.</li><li><em>Writing — Text Structure (Practice)</em> — Text-type appropriate openings: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Text Structure (Practice): Students are expected to organise ideas with openings appropriate to the text type, including hooks that immediately signal purpose and engage the reader.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Thursday: Structural edit — whole-text level":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">NZ Curriculum Link</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Writing — Writing Process (Practice): a structural edit evaluates the organisation of a complete piece of writing — checking that the opening hooks, paragraphs are in the right order, transitions are clear, and the ending resolves what the beginning promised.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>I Do — Editing the Shape, Not the Sentences</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>A structural edit ignores spelling and grammar — it only asks: does the whole piece have the right shape? Watch me apply a structural checklist to a piece plan.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Example: Structural checklist: Does the opening hook immediately engage? Are paragraphs in the most logical/effective order? Is there a clear climax or argument progression? Do transitions between paragraphs feel inevitable? Does the ending resolve or reflect back on what the opening promised?</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>I apply this top-down, not bottom-up. A structural problem (paragraph 3 should be paragraph 1) is more important than a word choice issue — fix structure first, always.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Tip: Print or copy your writing and cut it into paragraphs. Can you rearrange them into a better order? If one paragraph could be removed without the reader noticing — it probably should be.</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>We Do — Structural Diagnosis</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Apply the structural checklist to this piece outline together. What needs moving or improving?</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>1. Conclusion: recycling is important. 2. Evidence: statistics about landfill. 3. Counter-argument. 4. Hook: what if every piece of rubbish you ever threw away was still here? 5. Main argument. 6. Rebuttal.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Discussion prompt: Is this in the right order? Where does the hook belong? What should the conclusion say rather than just 'recycling is important'?</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>You Do — Your Turn</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Apply the structural checklist to a full piece of your own writing. List at least two structural improvements, then make the most important one.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Does the opening hook immediately engage?</li><li>Are paragraphs in the most effective order?</li><li>Is there a clear progression/climax?</li><li>Do transitions feel inevitable?</li><li>Does the ending deliver on the opening's promise?</li><li>✦ Bonus: Swap with a partner and do a structural edit of their piece. Tell them: what the shape is, what is working, one specific structural change you'd recommend.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Objectives (AOs)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Writing — Writing Process (Practice)</em> — Structural editing: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Writing Process (Practice): Students are expected to reread and revise their writing, making changes to structure to improve clarity and impact. This lesson distinguishes structural editing (whole-text organisation) from sentence-level editing — establishing that structure is always revised first, before sentence-level polish.</li><li><em>Writing — Text Structure (Practice)</em> — Whole-text organisation: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Text Structure (Practice): Students are expected to organise ideas into paragraphs with clear whole-text shape — appropriate opening, logical progression, and satisfying ending. The structural edit is the culminating text-structure skill of the year, applied independently to a complete piece.</li></ul></div></div>"}}},"Week 8":{"label":"Day","children":{"Monday: Vocabulary of concession":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">NZ Curriculum Link</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Writing — Vocabulary (Knowledge &amp; Practice): concessive adverbs and adverbial phrases (admittedly, granted, nevertheless, even so, that said) allow writers to acknowledge a valid opposing point while maintaining their own position, adding nuance and credibility to argument.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>I Do — Conceding Without Surrendering</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Concessive vocabulary lets you admit something is true (concede a point) while keeping your main argument firmly in place. Watch me use several different concessive words to do this.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Example: Admittedly, electric cars are currently more expensive to purchase. Nevertheless, their lower running costs and environmental benefits make them the more sensible long-term investment.</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>'Admittedly' signals: I acknowledge this objection. 'Nevertheless' signals: but here's why my argument still holds. Other pairs: 'Granted... even so'; 'It is true that... yet'; 'That said...'. The concession makes the writer seem balanced and credible.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Tip: The concessive word and the rebuttal word are a pair — don't use one without the other, or your argument sounds unfinished.</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>We Do — Choose the Right Concessive</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Add an appropriate concessive phrase and a rebuttal to each situation.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Some people argue that homework causes too much stress. [Concede + rebut]</li><li>Visiting zoos may be educational for some children. [Concede + rebut]</li><li>Online learning is undeniably more flexible than traditional schooling. [Rebut this concession]</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Discussion prompt: Which concessive word fits best — admittedly, granted, that said, even so? Does the rebuttal actually outweigh the concession?</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>You Do — Your Turn</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write a concede-and-rebut sentence pair for each topic, using a different concessive word each time.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Topic: whether competitive sport is good for children.</li><li>Topic: whether social media should be available in schools.</li><li>Topic: whether animals should be kept as pets.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write a full paragraph on a topic of your choice that uses two different concessive phrases — one near the beginning and one near the end — to build a nuanced, credible argument.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Objectives (AOs)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Writing — Vocabulary (Knowledge)</em> — Vocabulary of concession: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Vocabulary (Knowledge): Concessive adverbs and adverbial phrases (admittedly, granted, nevertheless, even so, that said) allow writers to acknowledge valid opposing points while maintaining their own argument. They are always paired with a rebuttal signal word that reasserts the writer's position. This vocabulary extends Term 3's connective work and Term 4 Week 3's counter-argument structure into a more sophisticated one-sentence technique.</li><li><em>Writing — Vocabulary (Practice)</em> — Nuance in persuasive vocabulary: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Vocabulary (Practice): Students are expected to select and use words and phrases that are fit for purpose and audience, including the vocabulary of concession in persuasive writing — words that signal intellectual balance and credibility rather than one-sided assertion.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Tuesday: Review: subordination vs coordination across a paragraph":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">NZ Curriculum Link</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Writing — Sentence Structure (Knowledge &amp; Practice): students review and apply the distinction between coordinating and subordinating conjunctions across a full paragraph, ensuring controlled variety of simple, compound, and complex sentences.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>I Do — The Full Mix in One Paragraph</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Today we review the year's conjunction work and check we can mix simple, compound, and complex sentences fluently in one paragraph. Watch me read a paragraph and label each sentence type.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Example: The hawk circled silently. [simple] It rode the thermal higher and higher, and the valley fell away beneath it. [compound] Although the air was still warm, the bird sensed the approaching evening. [complex] It folded its wings and dropped. [compound-simple]</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Reading through: every sentence is grammatically correct. No two consecutive sentences are the same type. Short simple sentences contrast with longer compound and complex ones. The variety creates rhythm and sustains the reader's interest.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Tip: Write a paragraph, then label each sentence S / Co / Cx. If you have three in a row of the same type — vary the next one.</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>We Do — Label and Improve</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Label each sentence in this paragraph S / Co / Cx and identify where the variety breaks down.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The boat left the harbour. The sea was calm and the wind was light. They sailed north. The islands came into view. They anchored and they went ashore. They found the beach deserted.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Discussion prompt: Where is the variety lacking? Which sentences could be combined or restructured to create better rhythm?</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>You Do — Your Turn</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write a paragraph of 5–7 sentences on the topic given, then label each sentence S / Co / Cx. Ensure no three consecutive sentences share the same type.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Topic: arriving at a new place for the first time.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Take a paragraph from your own writing. Label every sentence S / Co / Cx. If any type is missing — add or convert a sentence to include it.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Objectives (AOs)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Writing — Sentence Structure (Knowledge)</em> — Sentence variety: S/Co/Cx review: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Sentence Structure (Knowledge): Consolidates the year's sentence-structure strand — simple, compound, and complex sentences created through coordinating and subordinating conjunctions — requiring students to apply all three types within a single paragraph with controlled variety. This is a comprehensive review of the year's grammar in context.</li><li><em>Writing — Sentence Structure (Practice)</em> — Controlled sentence variety in a paragraph: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Sentence Structure (Practice): Students are expected to write a variety of sentence structures for effect and clarity. This review lesson requires students to demonstrate that sentence variety is not accidental but planned — labelling sentence types and deliberately ensuring variety across a paragraph, as they will be expected to do independently in Year 7.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Wednesday: Combining techniques — planning before drafting":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">NZ Curriculum Link</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Writing — Composing (Practice) &amp; Writing Process (Knowledge): students plan a short piece of writing by deliberately selecting which techniques to deploy (vocabulary devices, sentence structures, punctuation) BEFORE drafting, then write to their plan.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>I Do — The Technique Plan</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The most controlled, effective writing comes from planning which techniques to use BEFORE you start, not hoping they appear. Watch me make a technique plan for a short piece.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Example: Topic: the moment before a performance. Plan: Hook — in-scene sensory opening. Vocabulary — precise verbs, pathetic fallacy. Sentences — -ing opener, long-then-short contrast for tension. Punctuation — colon for revelation, deliberate fragment for effect. Register — narrative, first person.</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Having this plan means every choice I make while drafting is purposeful. I'm not searching for a technique — I'm executing a plan. This is how published writers work.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Tip: Aim for 4–5 techniques in your plan. More than that and the piece feels forced — every sentence feels like it's showing off rather than telling a story.</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>We Do — Plan Together</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Together, build a technique plan for this topic before writing a single sentence.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Topic: the last five minutes of a championship match.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Discussion prompt: What hook strategy? Which vocabulary techniques? Sentence structures for tension? Punctuation for effect? What register and person?</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>You Do — Your Turn</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Build a technique plan, then write a paragraph (5–7 sentences) to your plan. Label each technique you used.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Topic: discovering something unexpected in an old building.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write the same topic twice — once with a plan, once without. Which version is more controlled and effective? What does the planning add?</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Objectives (AOs)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Writing — Composing (Practice)</em> — Planning technique use before drafting: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Composing (Practice): Students are expected to use composing strategies to create texts that engage the reader, including planning specific techniques before drafting — selecting vocabulary devices, sentence structures, and punctuation choices in advance rather than improvising. Planning-before-drafting is a key indicator of writing maturity at Phase 2.</li><li><em>Writing — Writing Process (Knowledge)</em> — Pre-drafting planning strategies: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Writing Process (Knowledge): Effective writers plan their use of techniques — vocabulary devices, sentence structures, punctuation choices, and register — before beginning to draft, rather than hoping these features will appear incidentally. This lesson formalises the planning stage of the writing process as an explicit, teachable skill.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Thursday: Peer editing for voice and style":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">NZ Curriculum Link</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Writing — Writing Process (Practice): students apply a voice-and-style checklist when peer editing, evaluating whether a partner's writing has a consistent, distinctive voice and whether stylistic choices are deliberate and effective.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>I Do — Reading for Voice</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Voice is the sense that a real person is behind the words — consistent in register, distinctive in word choice, deliberate in style. Watch me read two versions of the same passage and identify which has voice and why.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Example: Version A: The old man sat in the chair by the window. He looked at the street. It was busy. Version B: Every morning, he claimed his chair by the window like a king ascending a throne — watching the street below with the patient authority of someone who had earned the right to simply observe.</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Version A is accurate but voiceless. Version B has consistent register (slightly elevated, respectful), metaphor (king/throne), precise verb ('claimed'), and rhythm. The style feels like a choice, not a default.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Tip: Ask: if I removed the name from this piece, would I know who wrote it? If yes, there's voice. If it could have been written by anyone — it probably needs more.</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>We Do — Voice Checklist</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Apply the voice checklist to this passage together.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The forest was dark and quiet. We walked through it. There were lots of trees and bushes. We heard some birds. It was a nice experience.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Discussion prompt: What voice checklist items are missing? Register? Distinctive word choice? Figurative language? Sentence variety?</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>You Do — Your Turn</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Swap a piece of writing with a partner. Apply the voice-and-style checklist and write specific feedback — one strength and one specific suggestion.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Is register consistent throughout?</li><li>Are word choices distinctive — or could anyone have written this?</li><li>Is figurative language present and purposeful — or absent/cliched?</li><li>Is sentence variety clearly deliberate — or flat?</li><li>Does the piece sound like a real, specific writer?</li><li>✦ Bonus: Using your partner's feedback, revise the weakest section of your own piece for voice — aim to make it clearly, identifiably yours.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Objectives (AOs)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Writing — Writing Process (Practice)</em> — Peer editing for voice and style: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Writing Process (Practice): Students are expected to share writing with others and respond to feedback to improve their work, including receiving and giving feedback specifically on voice and style — the most subjective but also most important dimension of writing quality at Phase 2.</li><li><em>Writing — Composing (Practice)</em> — Developing a distinctive writing voice: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Composing (Practice): Students are expected to use composing strategies to create texts that engage the reader, including developing a consistent, distinctive writing voice through deliberate choices of register, vocabulary, figurative language, and sentence structure. This is the culminating compositional skill of the year.</li></ul></div></div>"}}},"Week 9":{"label":"Day","children":{"Monday: Year vocabulary review — choosing your best tool":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">NZ Curriculum Link</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Writing — Vocabulary (Knowledge &amp; Practice): students review the full year's vocabulary toolkit and practise selecting the best tool for a given writing purpose rather than applying devices mechanically.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>I Do — The Full Toolkit</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This year we built a vocabulary toolkit. Today, the challenge is not 'can you use this device' but 'which device should you use here, and why?' Watch me make that choice explicitly.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Example: Prompt: describe a wildfire approaching a town. Options: simile? metaphor? personification? precise verbs? pathetic fallacy?</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Personification — the fire devoured the hillside — works because fire as a predator creates dread. Pathetic fallacy — if the sky turns blood-red — sets mood before the fire arrives. Precise verbs — the fire crept, then surged, then consumed — gives pace and progression. Best combination: start with pathetic fallacy for mood, then precise verbs for pace, then one strong personification at the peak.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Tip: The question is never 'which device do I know?' It's always 'which device does this moment need?'</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>We Do — Toolkit Decision</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>For each writing prompt, decide which vocabulary technique(s) would be most effective and explain why — before writing any sentences.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Persuasive: argue for protecting New Zealand's native forests.</li><li>Explanation: describe how a glacier forms.</li><li>Narrative: the moment a character realises they are lost.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Discussion prompt: Which vocabulary device(s) suit each text type and purpose? Why are some devices better fits than others here?</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>You Do — Your Turn</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>For each prompt, choose your two best vocabulary tools, explain your choice, and write two sentences using them.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A character watches their home disappear as a ship leaves port.</li><li>An explanation of why coral reefs are important ecosystems.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write a paragraph for one of the prompts above, using at least four different vocabulary techniques deliberately. Label each one in the margin.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Objectives (AOs)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Writing — Vocabulary (Knowledge)</em> — Year vocabulary toolkit review: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Vocabulary (Knowledge): Consolidates the full year's vocabulary strand — precise verbs, figurative language (simile, metaphor, personification, pathetic fallacy, hyperbole, onomatopoeia, idiom), connotation, emotive language, technical vocabulary, and register — into a meta-level skill: choosing the best vocabulary tool for a specific writing purpose rather than applying devices mechanically.</li><li><em>Writing — Vocabulary (Practice)</em> — Strategic vocabulary selection: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Vocabulary (Practice): Students are expected to select and use words and phrases, including figurative language, that are fit for purpose and audience. This review lesson assesses whether students have moved beyond technique knowledge to genuine technique selection — the highest-level vocabulary skill at Phase 2.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Tuesday: Year grammar review — mixed features from all four terms":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">NZ Curriculum Link</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Writing — Sentence Structure (Knowledge &amp; Practice): students review the full year's grammar strand — sentence types, conjunctions, tenses, voice, modal verbs, reported speech, conditional sentences, and clauses — applying them in a mixed, integrated context.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>I Do — One Sentence, Many Grammar Features</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>By now, a sophisticated sentence can contain multiple grammar features at once. Watch me build one deliberately, naming each feature as I add it.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Example: Build-up: Base: The scientists made a discovery. Add passive: A discovery was made. Add modal: A significant discovery may have been made. Add relative clause: ... by the scientists, who had been working on the project for over a decade. Add concessive: Although the research had taken over a decade, a significant discovery may finally have been made — one that could change everything.</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Each step adds a grammatical feature: passive voice, modal verb, relative clause, concessive conjunction. The sentence remains grammatically correct throughout. This is the level of control expected heading into Year 7.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Tip: If you're unsure whether a multi-feature sentence is correct, simplify it. A clear, correct simpler sentence is better than a tangled complex one.</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>We Do — Identify All Features</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Identify every grammar feature from the year's learning in this sentence.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Despite the fact that the match had been postponed three times, the players, who had trained for months, insisted that they would be ready — if the ground was deemed safe.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Discussion prompt: Concessive clause? Passive voice? Past perfect? Relative clause? Conditional? Modal verb? How many features can we find?</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>You Do — Your Turn</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Identify the grammar features in each sentence, then write one original sentence that combines at least three features from the year.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>'I should have been told earlier,' she muttered, even though she suspected she wouldn't have listened.</li><li>The ancient bridge, which had stood for 400 years, was finally demolished — a decision that the council had debated for over a decade.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write three sentences — one from each main text type (narrative, persuasive, explanation) — each containing at least three grammar features from the year. Label each feature.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Objectives (AOs)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Writing — Sentence Structure (Knowledge)</em> — Year grammar toolkit review: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Sentence Structure (Knowledge): Consolidates the full year's grammar strand — sentence types (S/Co/Cx), conjunction types, tense system (simple past, present, past perfect), active/passive voice, modal verbs, direct and reported speech, conditional sentences, and clause types (relative, subordinate, concessive, participial) — in an integrated, multi-feature context.</li><li><em>Writing — Sentence Structure (Practice)</em> — Integrated grammar application: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Sentence Structure (Practice): Students are expected to write grammatically correct sentences using a range of tenses and structures. This review lesson assesses the highest-level Phase 2 grammatical skill: combining multiple grammatical features within a single, still-correct sentence — the standard expected at Year 7.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Wednesday: Planning and drafting a complete chosen-text-type piece":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">NZ Curriculum Link</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Writing — Composing (Practice) &amp; Writing Process (Knowledge): students independently plan and draft a complete piece in their chosen text type, applying the year's full toolkit — deliberately planned before drafting.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>I Do — The Full Pre-Draft Plan</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Today we write a complete piece. But first — the full plan. Watch me build one for a persuasive piece in 5 minutes, covering all the dimensions we've learned.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Example: Topic: why every school should have a dedicated nature area. Text type: persuasive. Hook: rhetorical question and statistic. Structure: intro, 3 PEEL paragraphs, counter and rebuttal, conclusion. Vocabulary: emotive language, concessive phrases, precise verbs. Grammar: conditional sentences, relative clauses for definitions. Sentence variety: -ing opener, colon for revelation, deliberate fragment for closing. Register: formal, third person.</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Everything is decided before I write word one. The drafting is now execution, not exploration. This means the draft will be more controlled and better structured from the start.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Tip: Your plan should be specific enough that someone else could write the piece from it. If it's vague, it won't help you when you're actually drafting.</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>We Do — Plan Together</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>As a class, build the full plan for a narrative piece about an unexpected discovery.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Topic: a character finds something buried in their garden that changes everything.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Discussion prompt: What goes in the plan? Is it specific enough that the draft will write itself from it?</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>You Do — Your Turn</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Choose your text type. Build your full plan. Then draft your complete piece (minimum 3–4 paragraphs). Label at least 5 techniques in the draft.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Choose one: Narrative (a moment of unexpected change) / Persuasive (a cause you believe in) / Explanation (a natural process you understand well).</li><li>✦ Bonus: After drafting, circle every deliberate technique you used. Were there any in your draft that weren't in your plan? Were there any in your plan that you forgot to include?</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Objectives (AOs)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Writing — Composing (Practice)</em> — Independent complete-piece composition: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Composing (Practice): Students are expected to use composing strategies to create texts that engage the reader, demonstrating the full integration of the year's vocabulary, grammar, sentence structure, and text organisation strategies in an independently composed, complete piece of writing.</li><li><em>Writing — Writing Process (Knowledge)</em> — Planning as part of the writing process: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Writing Process (Knowledge): Effective writers plan systematically before drafting, making deliberate decisions about text type, structure, vocabulary, grammar, and sentence variety that guide and control the drafting process. This lesson demonstrates that planning-before-drafting is the foundation of quality independent writing at Phase 2 and beyond.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Thursday: Full publication-ready edit of own writing":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">NZ Curriculum Link</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Writing — Writing Process (Practice): students apply a comprehensive, sequential editing process (structural, paragraph-level, sentence-level, word-level, final proofread) to the piece they drafted on Wednesday, producing a publication-ready version.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>I Do — The Five-Pass Edit</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Professional writers edit in passes — top down, not randomly. Watch me apply all five passes to a paragraph.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Example: Five passes: 1. STRUCTURAL — is the whole piece in the right shape? 2. PARAGRAPH — do topic sentences and closers work? 3. SENTENCE — variety, sentence types, clause work. 4. WORD — precise verbs, figurative language, connotation, register. 5. PROOFREAD — punctuation, spelling, grammar errors.</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Pass 1: structure check — nothing to fix. Pass 2: first topic sentence is weak — fix it. Pass 3: two consecutive simple sentences — combine one. Pass 4: 'went' appears twice — replace both. Pass 5: missing comma after fronted adverbial. Five focused passes catch what one frantic reading misses.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Tip: Do NOT proofread first. Structural problems waste your proofreading effort on sentences you might delete.</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>We Do — Pass by Pass</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Together, take a sample paragraph through all five passes in order, making one fix per pass.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The forest was big and old. Many animals lived there. People were cutting it down and this was bad for the animals. Something should be done about this. It needs to stop.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Discussion prompt: Pass 1: Does this have the right structure for its type? Pass 2: Topic sentence? Pass 3: Sentence variety? Pass 4: Precise verbs and word choice? Pass 5: Punctuation errors?</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>You Do — Your Turn</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Apply all five passes — in order — to the piece you drafted on Wednesday. Mark every change and note which pass caught it.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Pass 1: Structure check</li><li>Pass 2: Paragraph-level check</li><li>Pass 3: Sentence-level check</li><li>Pass 4: Word-level check</li><li>Pass 5: Final proofread</li><li>✦ Bonus: Count how many changes each pass found. Which pass was most productive for your piece? What does that tell you about where you most need to improve as a writer?</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Objectives (AOs)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Writing — Writing Process (Practice)</em> — Five-pass sequential editing: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Writing Process (Practice): Students are expected to reread and revise their writing to check that it makes sense and is accurate, including checking vocabulary, punctuation, and structure. The five-pass sequential edit — structural, paragraph, sentence, word, proofread — is the publication-standard editing process that consolidates every editing skill developed across the year.</li><li><em>Writing — Writing Process (Knowledge)</em> — Sequential editing as professional practice: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Writing Process (Knowledge): Effective editing is sequential — structural problems are addressed before paragraph-level issues, which are addressed before sentence-level issues, which are addressed before word choice, before proofreading. This is the capstone editing lesson of the year.</li></ul></div></div>"}}},"Week 10":{"label":"Day","children":{"Monday: Year celebration — vocabulary Hall of Fame":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">NZ Curriculum Link</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Writing — Vocabulary (Knowledge &amp; Practice): students reflect on and celebrate the most powerful vocabulary tools they have learned across the year, selecting their personal Hall of Fame and articulating why each device is effective.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>I Do — What Made the Biggest Difference?</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>We've built a huge vocabulary toolkit this year. Today, I'll share my Hall of Fame — the vocabulary techniques that make the biggest difference to writing quality — and explain exactly why.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Example: My Hall of Fame nominations: 1. Precise verbs — because one strong verb does what ten weak words can't. 2. Pathetic fallacy — because it makes the whole world part of the story. 3. Connotation — because the difference between 'determined' and 'stubborn' is the difference between a hero and a villain.</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>For each, I give a reason that goes beyond 'it sounds better'. The reflection IS the learning.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Tip: Your Hall of Fame should be personal — the techniques that changed YOUR writing, not just the ones that sound impressive to mention.</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>We Do — Class Hall of Fame</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>As a class, nominate vocabulary techniques for the class Hall of Fame and make the case for each one.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Nominations open: which vocabulary technique made the biggest difference to your writing this year? Make the case in one sentence.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Discussion prompt: Why THIS technique specifically? What does it let a writer do that they couldn't do without it?</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>You Do — Your Turn</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write your personal vocabulary Hall of Fame — three techniques, with one paragraph of justification for each.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Choice 1 and why it matters.</li><li>Choice 2 and why it matters.</li><li>Choice 3 and why it matters.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write a short paragraph that deliberately uses all three of your Hall of Fame techniques. Annotate it so a reader could see exactly where each one appears and what it does.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Objectives (AOs)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Writing — Vocabulary (Knowledge)</em> — Metacognitive vocabulary reflection: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Vocabulary (Knowledge): Synthesises the year's vocabulary strand through metacognitive reflection — students evaluate which vocabulary tools have been most effective for their own writing development and articulate the reason, demonstrating the highest-level understanding: not just knowing a technique, but understanding why it works.</li><li><em>Writing — Vocabulary (Practice)</em> — Personal vocabulary Hall of Fame: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Vocabulary (Practice): Students demonstrate ownership of vocabulary skills by selecting, justifying, and applying their most personally significant vocabulary techniques. This culminating activity confirms that vocabulary knowledge has become internalised and transferable — the standard expected at the transition to Year 7.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Tuesday: Year celebration — grammar mastery challenge":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">NZ Curriculum Link</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Writing — Sentence Structure (Knowledge &amp; Practice): students complete a mixed grammar mastery challenge drawing on the full year's grammar strand, demonstrating secure knowledge of all major Phase 2 sentence structure concepts.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>I Do — The Grammar Full Circuit</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Today we go back to the beginning and run the full grammar circuit — every major concept from all four terms. Watch me work through a mixed challenge, naming each feature as I go.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Example: Challenge: rewrite this passage to include a complex sentence, past perfect flashback, reported speech, passive voice, modal verb, and concessive clause.</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Original: The team won. The captain was happy. She said she was proud. → Rewrite: Although the season had been difficult — they had nearly given up after a losing streak early on — the team finally won the championship. The captain was reported to have said that she was overwhelmingly proud. It was a result that should have come sooner.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Tip: Work feature by feature through the challenge list, not all at once. Tick each one off before moving to the next.</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>We Do — Mixed Challenge</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Complete this grammar challenge together — one feature at a time.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Base sentence: The school held a special event. It was a surprise. Everyone enjoyed it. Challenge: add a relative clause, a past perfect, a modal verb, and a concessive clause.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Discussion prompt: Which feature do we add first? Does the sentence remain grammatically correct after each addition?</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>You Do — Your Turn</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Complete the grammar mastery challenge. Rewrite the passage using ALL the grammar features listed.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Base: Scientists found a new species. They had worked for years. The discovery was important. Required features: passive voice, past perfect, modal verb, relative clause, concessive clause, reported speech.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write a fully original paragraph — topic of your choice — that contains every grammar feature from the list above, correctly used. Annotate each one.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Objectives (AOs)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Writing — Sentence Structure (Knowledge)</em> — Year grammar mastery challenge: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Sentence Structure (Knowledge): The year's grammar mastery challenge synthesises all major Phase 2 sentence structure concepts — sentence types, conjunctions, tense system, voice, modal verbs, direct and reported speech, conditional sentences, concessive clauses, and clause types — in a single integrated task that confirms secure grammatical understanding heading into Year 7.</li><li><em>Writing — Sentence Structure (Practice)</em> — Grammar integration at Year 7 readiness level: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Sentence Structure (Practice): Students demonstrate that they can write grammatically correct sentences using the full range of Phase 2 structures simultaneously — the standard expected for Year 7 transition.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Wednesday: Year celebration — flexible writing showcase":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">NZ Curriculum Link</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Writing — Composing (Practice): students write a short, polished piece in their chosen text type on a topic of their choice, showcasing the full range of the year's techniques as a celebration of writing growth.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>I Do — Writing with Everything You Know</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Today is a free write — but 'free' doesn't mean unplanned. It means you choose the topic, the text type, and the techniques. You decide what this piece is and what it does. Watch me make those decisions confidently and fast.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Example: My choice: narrative. Topic: the last day of summer. Techniques: pathetic fallacy for the opening, precise verbs throughout, one deliberate fragment, and a colon for the emotional revelation in the final paragraph. Register: reflective, first person. Length: 4 paragraphs.</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>I commit to a plan in 2 minutes, then draft with confidence. The decisions are fast because I trust my toolkit. That's what a year of writing practice is supposed to produce.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Tip: Don't spend more than 3 minutes planning. Make confident choices and write. The editing pass comes afterwards.</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>We Do — Share Plans</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Each student shares their plan (text type, topic, 3 technique choices) with a partner before writing. Partners give a 30-second response: does the plan sound like it will produce a strong piece?</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Share: text type, topic, three technique choices, and your intended opening hook.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Discussion prompt: Does the plan hang together? Does the hook suit the text type and topic?</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>You Do — Write Your Showcase Piece</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Plan (3 minutes), draft (15 minutes), quick self-edit (5 minutes). Aim for 3–5 paragraphs.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Choose your text type and topic freely — any text type, any subject that genuinely interests you.</li><li>Aim to use at least 5 techniques from across the year's learning.</li><li>After drafting, do a quick 5-minute self-edit — one pass for the most important fix.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Annotate your showcase piece — circle and label every technique you used deliberately. This is your record of the year's learning in action.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Objectives (AOs)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Writing — Composing (Practice)</em> — Independent flexible writing showcase: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Composing (Practice): Students independently compose a complete, polished piece in their chosen text type, demonstrating the full range of Phase 2 composing strategies — including planning, technique selection, deliberate vocabulary and grammar choices, and self-editing — without teacher scaffolding. This is the summative composing task for the year.</li><li><em>Writing — Writing Process (Practice)</em> — Planning to publication independently: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Writing Process (Practice): Students independently manage the full writing process — planning, drafting, and self-editing — with confident, fast decision-making that reflects internalised Phase 2 writing knowledge. This independent control of the writing process is the central goal of the year's programme.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Thursday: Portfolio piece — the whole-year best edit":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">NZ Curriculum Link</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Writing — Writing Process (Practice): students select their best piece of writing from across the year, apply the five-pass edit to bring it to portfolio quality, and write a brief reflection on their growth as a writer.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>I Do — What Makes a Portfolio Piece?</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>A portfolio piece is the best version of your best work. Today I'll show you how to choose it and how to bring it to the highest standard it can reach.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Example: What to look for when choosing: the piece that has the most interesting ideas. The piece where you took the most risks. The piece that is closest to being great — not already perfect, but closest to having genuine power.</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The best portfolio piece is often not the longest or the most technically polished — it's the one with the strongest ideas and the most personal voice. Apply the five-pass edit to bring the execution up to the level of the ideas.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Tip: Edit the ideas first (structural pass), then everything else. A beautifully proofread piece with weak ideas is still a weak piece.</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>We Do — Select and Plan the Edit</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>As a class, share: which piece are you choosing for your portfolio and why? What does the structural pass show needs fixing first?</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Share: title of your chosen piece, reason for choosing it, and one structural improvement you know it needs.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Discussion prompt: Is the reason for choosing it about ideas and voice, not just length or neatness? What is the most important fix?</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>You Do — Final Five-Pass Edit and Reflection</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Apply the five-pass edit to your chosen portfolio piece. Then write a 100-150 word reflection.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Five-pass edit: structural, paragraph, sentence, word, proofread.</li><li>Reflection: What was your writing like at the beginning of the year? What can you do now that you couldn't do then? What technique made the biggest difference? What do you want to get better at in Year 7?</li><li>✦ This is your final writing task for the year. Make it the best version of your best work.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Objectives (AOs)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Writing — Writing Process (Practice)</em> — Portfolio-quality editing: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Writing Process (Practice): Students select, revise, and edit a piece to portfolio standard, applying the year's full editing sequence and reflecting metacognitively on their growth. This is the capstone task of the Year 5–6 writing programme.</li><li><em>Writing — Writing Process (Knowledge)</em> — Metacognitive reflection on writing growth: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Writing Process (Knowledge): Students reflect on their development as writers, identifying which strategies have most improved their writing and setting goals for future development. This final lesson closes the year's writing programme by consolidating not just skills but self-awareness — the foundation for continued growth as a writer into Year 7 and beyond.</li></ul></div></div>"}}}}}}},"spelling":{"label":"Term","children":{"Term 1":{"label":"Week","children":{"Week 1":{"label":"Day","children":{"Week Overview":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-green\"><th class=\"lt-th\">This week's word list (sample, 10 words)</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><code>accident</code>, <code>actually</code>, <code>adventure</code>, <code>afraid</code>, <code>against</code>, <code>August</code>, <code>beautiful</code>, <code>bicycle</code>, <code>bought</code>, <code>breath</code></td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Monday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning to recognise the correct letter sequence of our spelling words within a field of distractor letters.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can spot my whole spelling word, in order, hidden across, down, or diagonally.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 🔎 Word Search Hunt</strong> <em>(Individual · ~20 min)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Hunt for this week's spelling words hidden in a letter grid — words can run across, down, or diagonally.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>You'll need:</em> Printed or on-screen word search · Pencil or highlighter</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>2 min</strong> — Look at the word list and read each word aloud before you start hunting.</li><li><strong>15 min</strong> — Circle or highlight every word as you find it in the grid.</li><li><strong>3 min</strong> — Tell a partner which word was trickiest to spot and why.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Tuesday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning to reconstruct a spelling word when its letters are presented out of order.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can unscramble a mixed-up word back into its correct spelling, quickly and confidently.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 🌀 Speedy Scramble Swap</strong> <em>(Pairs · ~20 min)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Partners take turns unscrambling each other's mixed-up spelling words as fast as they can.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>You'll need:</em> Whiteboard or paper · Timer</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>3 min</strong> — Partner A reads out the scrambled letters for word 1 — Partner B unscrambles and spells it aloud.</li><li><strong>12 min</strong> — Take turns through the whole list, swapping roles each word.</li><li><strong>5 min</strong> — Whoever unscrambled the most words correctly picks the next warm-up game!</li></ul></div></div>"},"Wednesday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning to build muscle memory for tricky spelling patterns through repeated, multisensory writing.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can write each word correctly three times, saying each letter as I go.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 🌈 Rainbow Write</strong> <em>(Individual · ~20 min)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write each spelling word three times, using a different colour pencil each time, tracing over the same letters.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>You'll need:</em> 3+ coloured pencils or markers · Plain paper</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>2 min</strong> — Choose your 3 colours for today.</li><li><strong>15 min</strong> — Write each word once in each colour, saying each letter as you write it.</li><li><strong>3 min</strong> — Pick your favourite rainbow word and show a partner.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Thursday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning to deepen our memory of a spelling word by connecting its spelling, its meaning, and its use in a sentence.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can write my word correctly three times, draw a picture that shows its meaning, and write a sentence that matches my picture.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 🛑🔤👃 Stop, Spell, Smell</strong> <em>(Individual · ~20 min)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Choose 5 of this week's words. For each one: write it correctly 3 times, draw a picture showing what it means, then write a sentence using the word that matches the picture.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>You'll need:</em> Worksheet or notebook · Pencil · Colouring pencils/markers</p></div></div>"},"Friday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning to spell this week's words correctly and independently, without support.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can write each dictated word with the correct spelling, unaided.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 📝 Friday Spelling Test</strong> <em>(Individual · teacher-administered)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Teacher reads each word aloud (in a sentence if helpful), student writes it, then the teacher marks each word ✓ correct or ✗ incorrect. Any word marked incorrect automatically reappears on next week's list for review; mastered words are replaced with new words from the Year 5 bank.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&lt;a id=&quot;term-1-week-2&quot;&gt;&lt;/a&gt;</p></div></div>"}}},"Week 2":{"label":"Day","children":{"Week Overview":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-green\"><th class=\"lt-th\">This week's word list (sample, 10 words)</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><code>breathe</code>, <code>business</code>, <code>chocolate</code>, <code>colour</code>, <code>complete</code>, <code>consider</code>, <code>continue</code>, <code>country</code>, <code>cousin</code>, <code>describe</code></td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Monday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning to build up a word sound-by-sound and letter-by-letter, strengthening our sense of letter order.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can build a correct letter pyramid for every word on my list.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 🔺 Pyramid Words</strong> <em>(Individual · ~20 min)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Build each spelling word one letter at a time, like a growing staircase, until the whole word is spelled out.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>You'll need:</em> Paper · Pencil</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>2 min</strong> — Teacher shows an example pyramid on the board (e.g. b / be / bec / beca...).</li><li><strong>15 min</strong> — Build a pyramid for every word on your list.</li><li><strong>3 min</strong> — Trade books with a neighbour and check each other's pyramids.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Tuesday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning to spell words aloud fluently and automatically, one letter at a time, under light time pressure.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can say my share of the letters correctly without hesitating, and say the whole word at the end.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 🎾 Word Tennis</strong> <em>(Pairs · ~20 min)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Partners &quot;volley&quot; a word back and forth, each saying the next letter — miss a letter and you lose the point!</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>You'll need:</em> Just your voices!</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>2 min</strong> — Decide who serves first for word 1.</li><li><strong>15 min</strong> — Rally through the whole list — one letter each, back and forth, then say the whole word together at the end.</li><li><strong>3 min</strong> — Total up the points and celebrate the winner.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Wednesday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning to recall and say a spelling word correctly from memory, under a small amount of performance pressure.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can spell my word aloud, letter by letter, without looking at the list.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 🎤 Spelling Bee Countdown</strong> <em>(Team · ~20 min)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Teams take turns spelling words aloud — get it right and stay in, get it wrong and sit down. Last team standing wins!</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>You'll need:</em> Word list (teacher calls words) · Nothing else needed</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>3 min</strong> — Split into teams of 3–4 and line up.</li><li><strong>14 min</strong> — Teacher calls a word, first player in line spells it aloud. Correct = go to the back of the line. Wrong = sit down.</li><li><strong>3 min</strong> — Last team with players standing wins the round!</li></ul></div></div>"},"Thursday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning to deepen our memory of a spelling word by connecting its spelling, its meaning, and its use in a sentence.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can write my word correctly three times, draw a picture that shows its meaning, and write a sentence that matches my picture.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 🛑🔤👃 Stop, Spell, Smell</strong> <em>(Individual · ~20 min)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Choose 5 of this week's words. For each one: write it correctly 3 times, draw a picture showing what it means, then write a sentence using the word that matches the picture.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>You'll need:</em> Worksheet or notebook · Pencil · Colouring pencils/markers</p></div></div>"},"Friday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning to spell this week's words correctly and independently, without support.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can write each dictated word with the correct spelling, unaided.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 📝 Friday Spelling Test</strong> <em>(Individual · teacher-administered)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Teacher reads each word aloud (in a sentence if helpful), student writes it, then the teacher marks each word ✓ correct or ✗ incorrect. Any word marked incorrect automatically reappears on next week's list for review; mastered words are replaced with new words from the Year 5 bank.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&lt;a id=&quot;term-1-week-3&quot;&gt;&lt;/a&gt;</p></div></div>"}}},"Week 3":{"label":"Day","children":{"Week Overview":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-green\"><th class=\"lt-th\">This week's word list (sample, 10 words)</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><code>difficult</code>, <code>disappeared</code>, <code>divide</code>, <code>easy</code>, <code>equal</code>, <code>everybody</code>, <code>everywhere</code>, <code>excited</code>, <code>exercise</code>, <code>experience</code></td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Monday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning to write a spelling word correctly and legibly at speed.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can run up, write my word correctly first try, and tag my teammate.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 🌍 Around the World Relay</strong> <em>(Team · ~20 min)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>A running relay — race to the board, write the next word correctly, then tag your teammate.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>You'll need:</em> Whiteboard/chalkboard or paper on a wall · Markers</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>3 min</strong> — Split into even teams and line up single-file.</li><li><strong>14 min</strong> — On &quot;go&quot;, the first runner races up, writes word 1 correctly, runs back and tags the next player who writes word 2, and so on.</li><li><strong>3 min</strong> — Check every team's words together — first fully-correct list wins.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Tuesday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning to use knowledge of spelling patterns to predict letters that are missing from a word.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can fill every gap in a word with the correct missing letter(s).</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 🕳️ Mystery Missing Letters</strong> <em>(Individual · ~20 min)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Some letters have vanished from each spelling word — figure out which ones and fill in the gaps.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>You'll need:</em> Worksheet or notebook · Pencil</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>2 min</strong> — Look at the first mystery word and predict the missing letters.</li><li><strong>15 min</strong> — Fill in every missing letter for all the words on your list.</li><li><strong>3 min</strong> — Swap with a partner and check each other's answers.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Wednesday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning to spell a word correctly when we hear it inside a full sentence, rather than in isolation.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can listen for my word inside a sentence and write it with the correct spelling.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: ✍️ Partner Dictation Challenge</strong> <em>(Pairs · ~20 min)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>One partner reads a word inside a sentence, the other writes it down — then swap and check together.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>You'll need:</em> Paper · Pencil</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>2 min</strong> — Decide who dictates first.</li><li><strong>14 min</strong> — Dictator says a sentence containing the spelling word; writer writes just the word. Swap roles halfway through the list.</li><li><strong>4 min</strong> — Compare your writing to the real spelling and fix any mistakes together.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Thursday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning to deepen our memory of a spelling word by connecting its spelling, its meaning, and its use in a sentence.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can write my word correctly three times, draw a picture that shows its meaning, and write a sentence that matches my picture.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 🛑🔤👃 Stop, Spell, Smell</strong> <em>(Individual · ~20 min)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Choose 5 of this week's words. For each one: write it correctly 3 times, draw a picture showing what it means, then write a sentence using the word that matches the picture.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>You'll need:</em> Worksheet or notebook · Pencil · Colouring pencils/markers</p></div></div>"},"Friday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning to spell this week's words correctly and independently, without support.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can write each dictated word with the correct spelling, unaided.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 📝 Friday Spelling Test</strong> <em>(Individual · teacher-administered)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Teacher reads each word aloud (in a sentence if helpful), student writes it, then the teacher marks each word ✓ correct or ✗ incorrect. Any word marked incorrect automatically reappears on next week's list for review; mastered words are replaced with new words from the Year 5 bank.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&lt;a id=&quot;term-1-week-4&quot;&gt;&lt;/a&gt;</p></div></div>"}}},"Week 4":{"label":"Day","children":{"Week Overview":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-green\"><th class=\"lt-th\">This week's word list (sample, 10 words)</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><code>experiment</code>, <code>extreme</code>, <code>February</code>, <code>finally</code>, <code>follow</code>, <code>forty</code>, <code>frightened</code>, <code>guard</code>, <code>happened</code>, <code>height</code></td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Monday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning to break spelling words into syllables to help us spell longer or trickier words.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can clap and count the syllables in each word and agree on the total with my partner.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 👏 Syllable Clap Count</strong> <em>(Pairs · ~20 min)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Clap out the syllables in each spelling word, then work out how many &quot;chunks&quot; it breaks into.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>You'll need:</em> Just your hands!</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>3 min</strong> — Warm up by clapping your own name.</li><li><strong>14 min</strong> — Take turns saying and clapping each spelling word, then agree on the syllable count and write it down.</li><li><strong>3 min</strong> — Find the word on your list with the most syllables — can you beat it next week?</li></ul></div></div>"},"Tuesday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning to notice patterns across our spelling words (e.g. syllables, tricky letter strings, silent letters) and use them to group words.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can sort every word into a group using a spelling pattern I chose myself.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 🗂️ Word Sort Categories</strong> <em>(Pairs · ~20 min)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Sort this week's words into your own categories — by syllables, tricky letter patterns, or silent letters.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>You'll need:</em> Small cards or sticky notes with words written on them · Table or floor space</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>3 min</strong> — Write each word onto its own card.</li><li><strong>14 min</strong> — Choose a sorting rule together (e.g. number of syllables) and sort every word into groups.</li><li><strong>3 min</strong> — Explain your sorting rule to another pair.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Wednesday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning to reinforce the correct letter sequence of a word through a creative, visual activity.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can draw and colour each word so that every letter is in the correct order.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 🎨 Bubble Letter Word Art</strong> <em>(Individual · ~20 min)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Draw each spelling word in big bubble or graffiti letters and decorate it.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>You'll need:</em> Plain paper · Pencil, then colouring pencils/markers</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>2 min</strong> — Look at an example bubble-letter word on the board.</li><li><strong>15 min</strong> — Draw and colour bubble letters for as many words as you can.</li><li><strong>3 min</strong> — Show off your favourite word-art creation to the class.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Thursday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning to deepen our memory of a spelling word by connecting its spelling, its meaning, and its use in a sentence.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can write my word correctly three times, draw a picture that shows its meaning, and write a sentence that matches my picture.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 🛑🔤👃 Stop, Spell, Smell</strong> <em>(Individual · ~20 min)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Choose 5 of this week's words. For each one: write it correctly 3 times, draw a picture showing what it means, then write a sentence using the word that matches the picture.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>You'll need:</em> Worksheet or notebook · Pencil · Colouring pencils/markers</p></div></div>"},"Friday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning to spell this week's words correctly and independently, without support.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can write each dictated word with the correct spelling, unaided.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 📝 Friday Spelling Test</strong> <em>(Individual · teacher-administered)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Teacher reads each word aloud (in a sentence if helpful), student writes it, then the teacher marks each word ✓ correct or ✗ incorrect. Any word marked incorrect automatically reappears on next week's list for review; mastered words are replaced with new words from the Year 5 bank.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&lt;a id=&quot;term-1-week-5&quot;&gt;&lt;/a&gt;</p></div></div>"}}},"Week 5":{"label":"Day","children":{"Week Overview":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-green\"><th class=\"lt-th\">This week's word list (sample, 10 words)</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><code>history</code>, <code>holidays</code>, <code>hospital</code>, <code>imagine</code>, <code>increase</code>, <code>instead</code>, <code>interest</code>, <code>interesting</code>, <code>island</code>, <code>January</code></td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Monday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning to recall spelling words correctly on demand, in a low-pressure game format.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can spell the word correctly before I take my turn to move.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 🐍 Spelling Snakes &amp; Ladders</strong> <em>(Team · ~20 min)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Play snakes and ladders — but you can only move if you spell a word correctly first!</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>You'll need:</em> Snakes &amp; ladders board (drawn or printed) · Dice · Counters</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>2 min</strong> — Set up the board and counters in groups of 3–4.</li><li><strong>15 min</strong> — Roll the dice — before moving, spell the next word on your list aloud. Get it right to move, wrong to stay put.</li><li><strong>3 min</strong> — First to reach the end (or whoever's furthest when time is up) wins.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Tuesday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning to use our spelling words correctly and meaningfully within a sentence of our own.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can add a sentence to our class story that uses my spelling word correctly.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 📖 Silly Sentence Story Chain</strong> <em>(Team · ~20 min)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Build one giant silly class story together, where each sentence must use the next spelling word.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>You'll need:</em> Paper or shared doc to record the story</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>3 min</strong> — Sit in a circle with your team and decide on a story character.</li><li><strong>14 min</strong> — Take turns adding one sentence to the story, each one using a spelling word correctly.</li><li><strong>3 min</strong> — Read your finished silly story aloud to another team.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Wednesday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning to recall and say a spelling word correctly from memory in a fun, active game.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can spell my word aloud correctly when I catch the beanbag.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 🎯 Beanbag Toss Spelling</strong> <em>(Team · ~20 min)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Toss a beanbag or soft ball — whoever catches it has to spell the next word out loud.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>You'll need:</em> Beanbag or soft ball · Open space</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>2 min</strong> — Stand in a circle with your team.</li><li><strong>15 min</strong> — Toss the beanbag — catcher spells the next word aloud. Correct = toss again for the next word; incorrect = get a hint and try once more.</li><li><strong>3 min</strong> — Celebrate every word your team got through together.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Thursday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning to deepen our memory of a spelling word by connecting its spelling, its meaning, and its use in a sentence.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can write my word correctly three times, draw a picture that shows its meaning, and write a sentence that matches my picture.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 🛑🔤👃 Stop, Spell, Smell</strong> <em>(Individual · ~20 min)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Choose 5 of this week's words. For each one: write it correctly 3 times, draw a picture showing what it means, then write a sentence using the word that matches the picture.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>You'll need:</em> Worksheet or notebook · Pencil · Colouring pencils/markers</p></div></div>"},"Friday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning to spell this week's words correctly and independently, without support.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can write each dictated word with the correct spelling, unaided.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 📝 Friday Spelling Test</strong> <em>(Individual · teacher-administered)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Teacher reads each word aloud (in a sentence if helpful), student writes it, then the teacher marks each word ✓ correct or ✗ incorrect. Any word marked incorrect automatically reappears on next week's list for review; mastered words are replaced with new words from the Year 5 bank.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&lt;a id=&quot;term-1-week-6&quot;&gt;&lt;/a&gt;</p></div></div>"}}},"Week 6":{"label":"Day","children":{"Week Overview":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-green\"><th class=\"lt-th\">This week's word list (sample, 10 words)</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><code>knowledge</code>, <code>laugh</code>, <code>library</code>, <code>lightning</code>, <code>material</code>, <code>measure</code>, <code>medicine</code>, <code>mention</code>, <code>money</code>, <code>natural</code></td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Monday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning that understanding what a word means helps us remember how to spell it.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can guess a sensible definition for each word, then check it against the real meaning.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 🕵️ Definition Detective</strong> <em>(Individual · ~20 min)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Before checking a dictionary, guess what each spelling word means — then check how close you were.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>You'll need:</em> Notebook · Dictionary or teacher's help</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>2 min</strong> — Pick the first word and think about where you've seen or heard it before.</li><li><strong>15 min</strong> — Write your own guessed definition for each word, then check the real meaning.</li><li><strong>3 min</strong> — Share the definition that surprised you most.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Tuesday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning to match a spelling word to its meaning or use in context.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can identify the correct word on my card when a clue or definition is read aloud.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 🎱 Word Bingo Blast</strong> <em>(Team · ~20 min)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Fill a bingo card with this week's words — teacher reads a definition or sentence, you mark the matching word.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>You'll need:</em> Bingo card with words written in a grid · Counters or pencil to mark squares</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>3 min</strong> — Write your spelling words into a 3x3 or 4x4 grid in any order.</li><li><strong>14 min</strong> — Teacher reads a clue for a word — if it's on your card, mark it. First to a full line calls &quot;Bingo!&quot;</li><li><strong>3 min</strong> — Play again with a fresh grid if there's time.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Wednesday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning to reinforce spelling through large-scale kinesthetic movement rather than only pen and paper.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can air-write my word clearly enough for my partner to guess it correctly.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 💫 Air Writing Wiggle</strong> <em>(Pairs · ~20 min)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&quot;Write&quot; a spelling word in the air with your finger, elbow, or nose — your partner has to guess it!</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>You'll need:</em> Just your body — no equipment needed</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>2 min</strong> — Practise writing one letter big and slow in the air.</li><li><strong>15 min</strong> — Take turns air-writing each word while your partner guesses, then check the spelling together.</li><li><strong>3 min</strong> — Try writing your favourite word with your elbow for a giggle.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Thursday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning to deepen our memory of a spelling word by connecting its spelling, its meaning, and its use in a sentence.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can write my word correctly three times, draw a picture that shows its meaning, and write a sentence that matches my picture.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 🛑🔤👃 Stop, Spell, Smell</strong> <em>(Individual · ~20 min)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Choose 5 of this week's words. For each one: write it correctly 3 times, draw a picture showing what it means, then write a sentence using the word that matches the picture.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>You'll need:</em> Worksheet or notebook · Pencil · Colouring pencils/markers</p></div></div>"},"Friday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning to spell this week's words correctly and independently, without support.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can write each dictated word with the correct spelling, unaided.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 📝 Friday Spelling Test</strong> <em>(Individual · teacher-administered)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Teacher reads each word aloud (in a sentence if helpful), student writes it, then the teacher marks each word ✓ correct or ✗ incorrect. Any word marked incorrect automatically reappears on next week's list for review; mastered words are replaced with new words from the Year 5 bank.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&lt;a id=&quot;term-1-week-7&quot;&gt;&lt;/a&gt;</p></div></div>"}}},"Week 7":{"label":"Day","children":{"Week Overview":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-green\"><th class=\"lt-th\">This week's word list (sample, 10 words)</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><code>nearly</code>, <code>ninth</code>, <code>occasionally</code>, <code>opposite</code>, <code>ordinary</code>, <code>pattern</code>, <code>peculiar</code>, <code>popular</code>, <code>position</code>, <code>possible</code></td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Monday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning to write spelling words correctly and legibly while moving at pace.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can chalk my word correctly before tagging the next teammate.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 🖍️ Chalk &amp; Talk Relay</strong> <em>(Team · ~20 min)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>An outdoor (or hallway) relay where teams race to chalk out each spelling word correctly.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>You'll need:</em> Chalk (or paper strips indoors) · Open path or hallway</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>3 min</strong> — Split into teams and line up at the start line.</li><li><strong>14 min</strong> — One at a time, race up and chalk the next word correctly before running back to tag the next teammate.</li><li><strong>3 min</strong> — Walk along and check every team's words together.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Tuesday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning to reconstruct the correct letter order of a word as quickly as possible.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can flip and arrange my letter cards into the correct spelling before my partner does.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 🔄 Flip &amp; Spell Showdown</strong> <em>(Pairs · ~20 min)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Each player writes a spelling word's letters onto flip cards, shuffles them face-down, then races to flip them over in the correct order — whoever spells it correctly first wins the point!</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>You'll need:</em> Blank cards or small paper squares — one per letter · A pen each</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>3 min</strong> — Both players write the letters of word 1 onto their own set of cards, then shuffle and lay them face-down.</li><li><strong>14 min</strong> — On &quot;go&quot;, both players flip and rearrange their cards into the correct spelling order. First to spell it correctly wins the point — then reshuffle for the next word.</li><li><strong>3 min</strong> — Total up the points and celebrate the round's champion speller.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Wednesday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning that understanding the individual letters inside a word can help us see spelling patterns within other, smaller words.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can find smaller real words hidden inside a spelling word, using only its letters.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 💰 Letter Loot Challenge</strong> <em>(Team · ~20 min)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Race a 30-second clock to spell as many smaller words as you can using only the letters from one spelling word. Score points for every letter you use — and grab a +2 bonus if you use every single letter!</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>You'll need:</em> Paper &amp; pencil (or call out words aloud) · 30-second timer — built in below!</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>3 min</strong> — Explain the scoring: each word you find scores points equal to how many letters it uses. Use up every letter from the original word across your words to earn a +2 bonus.</li><li><strong>14 min</strong> — Run a 30-second round for each spelling word — hunt for smaller words hiding inside it, then use the score checker below to total your points.</li><li><strong>3 min</strong> — Add up every round's score to crown the class Letter Loot Champion.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Thursday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning to deepen our memory of a spelling word by connecting its spelling, its meaning, and its use in a sentence.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can write my word correctly three times, draw a picture that shows its meaning, and write a sentence that matches my picture.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 🛑🔤👃 Stop, Spell, Smell</strong> <em>(Individual · ~20 min)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Choose 5 of this week's words. For each one: write it correctly 3 times, draw a picture showing what it means, then write a sentence using the word that matches the picture.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>You'll need:</em> Worksheet or notebook · Pencil · Colouring pencils/markers</p></div></div>"},"Friday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning to spell this week's words correctly and independently, without support.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can write each dictated word with the correct spelling, unaided.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 📝 Friday Spelling Test</strong> <em>(Individual · teacher-administered)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Teacher reads each word aloud (in a sentence if helpful), student writes it, then the teacher marks each word ✓ correct or ✗ incorrect. Any word marked incorrect automatically reappears on next week's list for review; mastered words are replaced with new words from the Year 5 bank.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&lt;a id=&quot;term-1-week-8&quot;&gt;&lt;/a&gt;</p></div></div>"}}},"Week 8":{"label":"Day","children":{"Week Overview":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-green\"><th class=\"lt-th\">This week's word list (sample, 10 words)</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><code>pressure</code>, <code>probably</code>, <code>problem</code>, <code>programme</code>, <code>promise</code>, <code>purpose</code>, <code>quarter</code>, <code>received</code>, <code>regular</code>, <code>remember</code></td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Monday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning to recognise the correct letter sequence of our spelling words within a field of distractor letters.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can spot my whole spelling word, in order, hidden across, down, or diagonally.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 🔎 Word Search Hunt</strong> <em>(Individual · ~20 min)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Hunt for this week's spelling words hidden in a letter grid — words can run across, down, or diagonally.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>You'll need:</em> Printed or on-screen word search · Pencil or highlighter</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>2 min</strong> — Look at the word list and read each word aloud before you start hunting.</li><li><strong>15 min</strong> — Circle or highlight every word as you find it in the grid.</li><li><strong>3 min</strong> — Tell a partner which word was trickiest to spot and why.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Tuesday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning to reconstruct a spelling word when its letters are presented out of order.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can unscramble a mixed-up word back into its correct spelling, quickly and confidently.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 🌀 Speedy Scramble Swap</strong> <em>(Pairs · ~20 min)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Partners take turns unscrambling each other's mixed-up spelling words as fast as they can.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>You'll need:</em> Whiteboard or paper · Timer</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>3 min</strong> — Partner A reads out the scrambled letters for word 1 — Partner B unscrambles and spells it aloud.</li><li><strong>12 min</strong> — Take turns through the whole list, swapping roles each word.</li><li><strong>5 min</strong> — Whoever unscrambled the most words correctly picks the next warm-up game!</li></ul></div></div>"},"Wednesday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning to build muscle memory for tricky spelling patterns through repeated, multisensory writing.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can write each word correctly three times, saying each letter as I go.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 🌈 Rainbow Write</strong> <em>(Individual · ~20 min)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write each spelling word three times, using a different colour pencil each time, tracing over the same letters.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>You'll need:</em> 3+ coloured pencils or markers · Plain paper</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>2 min</strong> — Choose your 3 colours for today.</li><li><strong>15 min</strong> — Write each word once in each colour, saying each letter as you write it.</li><li><strong>3 min</strong> — Pick your favourite rainbow word and show a partner.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Thursday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning to deepen our memory of a spelling word by connecting its spelling, its meaning, and its use in a sentence.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can write my word correctly three times, draw a picture that shows its meaning, and write a sentence that matches my picture.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 🛑🔤👃 Stop, Spell, Smell</strong> <em>(Individual · ~20 min)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Choose 5 of this week's words. For each one: write it correctly 3 times, draw a picture showing what it means, then write a sentence using the word that matches the picture.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>You'll need:</em> Worksheet or notebook · Pencil · Colouring pencils/markers</p></div></div>"},"Friday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning to spell this week's words correctly and independently, without support.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can write each dictated word with the correct spelling, unaided.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 📝 Friday Spelling Test</strong> <em>(Individual · teacher-administered)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Teacher reads each word aloud (in a sentence if helpful), student writes it, then the teacher marks each word ✓ correct or ✗ incorrect. Any word marked incorrect automatically reappears on next week's list for review; mastered words are replaced with new words from the Year 5 bank.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&lt;a id=&quot;term-1-week-9&quot;&gt;&lt;/a&gt;</p></div></div>"}}},"Week 9":{"label":"Day","children":{"Week Overview":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-green\"><th class=\"lt-th\">This week's word list (sample, 10 words)</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><code>separate</code>, <code>since</code>, <code>special</code>, <code>strength</code>, <code>suppose</code>, <code>table</code>, <code>temperature</code>, <code>they're</code>, <code>though</code>, <code>tomorrow</code></td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Monday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning to build up a word sound-by-sound and letter-by-letter, strengthening our sense of letter order.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can build a correct letter pyramid for every word on my list.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 🔺 Pyramid Words</strong> <em>(Individual · ~20 min)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Build each spelling word one letter at a time, like a growing staircase, until the whole word is spelled out.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>You'll need:</em> Paper · Pencil</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>2 min</strong> — Teacher shows an example pyramid on the board (e.g. b / be / bec / beca...).</li><li><strong>15 min</strong> — Build a pyramid for every word on your list.</li><li><strong>3 min</strong> — Trade books with a neighbour and check each other's pyramids.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Tuesday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning to spell words aloud fluently and automatically, one letter at a time, under light time pressure.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can say my share of the letters correctly without hesitating, and say the whole word at the end.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 🎾 Word Tennis</strong> <em>(Pairs · ~20 min)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Partners &quot;volley&quot; a word back and forth, each saying the next letter — miss a letter and you lose the point!</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>You'll need:</em> Just your voices!</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>2 min</strong> — Decide who serves first for word 1.</li><li><strong>15 min</strong> — Rally through the whole list — one letter each, back and forth, then say the whole word together at the end.</li><li><strong>3 min</strong> — Total up the points and celebrate the winner.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Wednesday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning to recall and say a spelling word correctly from memory, under a small amount of performance pressure.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can spell my word aloud, letter by letter, without looking at the list.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 🎤 Spelling Bee Countdown</strong> <em>(Team · ~20 min)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Teams take turns spelling words aloud — get it right and stay in, get it wrong and sit down. Last team standing wins!</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>You'll need:</em> Word list (teacher calls words) · Nothing else needed</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>3 min</strong> — Split into teams of 3–4 and line up.</li><li><strong>14 min</strong> — Teacher calls a word, first player in line spells it aloud. Correct = go to the back of the line. Wrong = sit down.</li><li><strong>3 min</strong> — Last team with players standing wins the round!</li></ul></div></div>"},"Thursday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning to deepen our memory of a spelling word by connecting its spelling, its meaning, and its use in a sentence.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can write my word correctly three times, draw a picture that shows its meaning, and write a sentence that matches my picture.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 🛑🔤👃 Stop, Spell, Smell</strong> <em>(Individual · ~20 min)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Choose 5 of this week's words. For each one: write it correctly 3 times, draw a picture showing what it means, then write a sentence using the word that matches the picture.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>You'll need:</em> Worksheet or notebook · Pencil · Colouring pencils/markers</p></div></div>"},"Friday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning to spell this week's words correctly and independently, without support.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can write each dictated word with the correct spelling, unaided.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 📝 Friday Spelling Test</strong> <em>(Individual · teacher-administered)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Teacher reads each word aloud (in a sentence if helpful), student writes it, then the teacher marks each word ✓ correct or ✗ incorrect. Any word marked incorrect automatically reappears on next week's list for review; mastered words are replaced with new words from the Year 5 bank.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&lt;a id=&quot;term-1-week-10&quot;&gt;&lt;/a&gt;</p></div></div>"}}},"Week 10":{"label":"Day","children":{"Week Overview":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-green\"><th class=\"lt-th\">This week's word list (sample, 5 words)</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><code>twelfth</code>, <code>until</code>, <code>various</code>, <code>voice</code>, <code>weight</code></td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Monday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning to write a spelling word correctly and legibly at speed.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can run up, write my word correctly first try, and tag my teammate.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 🌍 Around the World Relay</strong> <em>(Team · ~20 min)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>A running relay — race to the board, write the next word correctly, then tag your teammate.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>You'll need:</em> Whiteboard/chalkboard or paper on a wall · Markers</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>3 min</strong> — Split into even teams and line up single-file.</li><li><strong>14 min</strong> — On &quot;go&quot;, the first runner races up, writes word 1 correctly, runs back and tags the next player who writes word 2, and so on.</li><li><strong>3 min</strong> — Check every team's words together — first fully-correct list wins.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Tuesday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning to use knowledge of spelling patterns to predict letters that are missing from a word.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can fill every gap in a word with the correct missing letter(s).</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 🕳️ Mystery Missing Letters</strong> <em>(Individual · ~20 min)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Some letters have vanished from each spelling word — figure out which ones and fill in the gaps.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>You'll need:</em> Worksheet or notebook · Pencil</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>2 min</strong> — Look at the first mystery word and predict the missing letters.</li><li><strong>15 min</strong> — Fill in every missing letter for all the words on your list.</li><li><strong>3 min</strong> — Swap with a partner and check each other's answers.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Wednesday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning to spell a word correctly when we hear it inside a full sentence, rather than in isolation.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can listen for my word inside a sentence and write it with the correct spelling.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: ✍️ Partner Dictation Challenge</strong> <em>(Pairs · ~20 min)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>One partner reads a word inside a sentence, the other writes it down — then swap and check together.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>You'll need:</em> Paper · Pencil</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>2 min</strong> — Decide who dictates first.</li><li><strong>14 min</strong> — Dictator says a sentence containing the spelling word; writer writes just the word. Swap roles halfway through the list.</li><li><strong>4 min</strong> — Compare your writing to the real spelling and fix any mistakes together.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Thursday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning to deepen our memory of a spelling word by connecting its spelling, its meaning, and its use in a sentence.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can write my word correctly three times, draw a picture that shows its meaning, and write a sentence that matches my picture.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 🛑🔤👃 Stop, Spell, Smell</strong> <em>(Individual · ~20 min)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Choose 5 of this week's words. For each one: write it correctly 3 times, draw a picture showing what it means, then write a sentence using the word that matches the picture.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>You'll need:</em> Worksheet or notebook · Pencil · Colouring pencils/markers</p></div></div>"},"Friday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning to spell this week's words correctly and independently, without support.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can write each dictated word with the correct spelling, unaided.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 📝 Friday Spelling Test</strong> <em>(Individual · teacher-administered)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Teacher reads each word aloud (in a sentence if helpful), student writes it, then the teacher marks each word ✓ correct or ✗ incorrect. Any word marked incorrect automatically reappears on next week's list for review; mastered words are replaced with new words from the Year 5 bank.</p></div></div>"}}}}},"Term 2":{"label":"Week","children":{"Week 1":{"label":"Day","children":{"Week Overview":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Sample student has already mastered the entire Year 5 word bank (completed Week 10, Term 1) — no new sample word list to show. The day structure below still applies for any student still progressing through their own list.</div></div>"},"Monday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning to break spelling words into syllables to help us spell longer or trickier words.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can clap and count the syllables in each word and agree on the total with my partner.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 👏 Syllable Clap Count</strong> <em>(Pairs · ~20 min)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Clap out the syllables in each spelling word, then work out how many &quot;chunks&quot; it breaks into.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>You'll need:</em> Just your hands!</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>3 min</strong> — Warm up by clapping your own name.</li><li><strong>14 min</strong> — Take turns saying and clapping each spelling word, then agree on the syllable count and write it down.</li><li><strong>3 min</strong> — Find the word on your list with the most syllables — can you beat it next week?</li></ul></div></div>"},"Tuesday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning to notice patterns across our spelling words (e.g. syllables, tricky letter strings, silent letters) and use them to group words.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can sort every word into a group using a spelling pattern I chose myself.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 🗂️ Word Sort Categories</strong> <em>(Pairs · ~20 min)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Sort this week's words into your own categories — by syllables, tricky letter patterns, or silent letters.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>You'll need:</em> Small cards or sticky notes with words written on them · Table or floor space</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>3 min</strong> — Write each word onto its own card.</li><li><strong>14 min</strong> — Choose a sorting rule together (e.g. number of syllables) and sort every word into groups.</li><li><strong>3 min</strong> — Explain your sorting rule to another pair.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Wednesday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning to reinforce the correct letter sequence of a word through a creative, visual activity.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can draw and colour each word so that every letter is in the correct order.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 🎨 Bubble Letter Word Art</strong> <em>(Individual · ~20 min)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Draw each spelling word in big bubble or graffiti letters and decorate it.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>You'll need:</em> Plain paper · Pencil, then colouring pencils/markers</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>2 min</strong> — Look at an example bubble-letter word on the board.</li><li><strong>15 min</strong> — Draw and colour bubble letters for as many words as you can.</li><li><strong>3 min</strong> — Show off your favourite word-art creation to the class.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Thursday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning to deepen our memory of a spelling word by connecting its spelling, its meaning, and its use in a sentence.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can write my word correctly three times, draw a picture that shows its meaning, and write a sentence that matches my picture.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 🛑🔤👃 Stop, Spell, Smell</strong> <em>(Individual · ~20 min)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Choose 5 of this week's words. For each one: write it correctly 3 times, draw a picture showing what it means, then write a sentence using the word that matches the picture.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>You'll need:</em> Worksheet or notebook · Pencil · Colouring pencils/markers</p></div></div>"},"Friday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning to spell this week's words correctly and independently, without support.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can write each dictated word with the correct spelling, unaided.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 📝 Friday Spelling Test</strong> <em>(Individual · teacher-administered)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Teacher reads each word aloud (in a sentence if helpful), student writes it, then the teacher marks each word ✓ correct or ✗ incorrect. Any word marked incorrect automatically reappears on next week's list for review; mastered words are replaced with new words from the Year 5 bank.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&lt;a id=&quot;term-2-week-2&quot;&gt;&lt;/a&gt;</p></div></div>"}}},"Week 2":{"label":"Day","children":{"Week Overview":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Sample student has already mastered the entire Year 5 word bank (completed Week 10, Term 1) — no new sample word list to show. The day structure below still applies for any student still progressing through their own list.</div></div>"},"Monday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning to recall spelling words correctly on demand, in a low-pressure game format.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can spell the word correctly before I take my turn to move.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 🐍 Spelling Snakes &amp; Ladders</strong> <em>(Team · ~20 min)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Play snakes and ladders — but you can only move if you spell a word correctly first!</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>You'll need:</em> Snakes &amp; ladders board (drawn or printed) · Dice · Counters</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>2 min</strong> — Set up the board and counters in groups of 3–4.</li><li><strong>15 min</strong> — Roll the dice — before moving, spell the next word on your list aloud. Get it right to move, wrong to stay put.</li><li><strong>3 min</strong> — First to reach the end (or whoever's furthest when time is up) wins.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Tuesday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning to use our spelling words correctly and meaningfully within a sentence of our own.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can add a sentence to our class story that uses my spelling word correctly.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 📖 Silly Sentence Story Chain</strong> <em>(Team · ~20 min)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Build one giant silly class story together, where each sentence must use the next spelling word.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>You'll need:</em> Paper or shared doc to record the story</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>3 min</strong> — Sit in a circle with your team and decide on a story character.</li><li><strong>14 min</strong> — Take turns adding one sentence to the story, each one using a spelling word correctly.</li><li><strong>3 min</strong> — Read your finished silly story aloud to another team.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Wednesday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning to recall and say a spelling word correctly from memory in a fun, active game.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can spell my word aloud correctly when I catch the beanbag.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 🎯 Beanbag Toss Spelling</strong> <em>(Team · ~20 min)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Toss a beanbag or soft ball — whoever catches it has to spell the next word out loud.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>You'll need:</em> Beanbag or soft ball · Open space</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>2 min</strong> — Stand in a circle with your team.</li><li><strong>15 min</strong> — Toss the beanbag — catcher spells the next word aloud. Correct = toss again for the next word; incorrect = get a hint and try once more.</li><li><strong>3 min</strong> — Celebrate every word your team got through together.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Thursday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning to deepen our memory of a spelling word by connecting its spelling, its meaning, and its use in a sentence.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can write my word correctly three times, draw a picture that shows its meaning, and write a sentence that matches my picture.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 🛑🔤👃 Stop, Spell, Smell</strong> <em>(Individual · ~20 min)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Choose 5 of this week's words. For each one: write it correctly 3 times, draw a picture showing what it means, then write a sentence using the word that matches the picture.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>You'll need:</em> Worksheet or notebook · Pencil · Colouring pencils/markers</p></div></div>"},"Friday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning to spell this week's words correctly and independently, without support.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can write each dictated word with the correct spelling, unaided.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 📝 Friday Spelling Test</strong> <em>(Individual · teacher-administered)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Teacher reads each word aloud (in a sentence if helpful), student writes it, then the teacher marks each word ✓ correct or ✗ incorrect. Any word marked incorrect automatically reappears on next week's list for review; mastered words are replaced with new words from the Year 5 bank.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&lt;a id=&quot;term-2-week-3&quot;&gt;&lt;/a&gt;</p></div></div>"}}},"Week 3":{"label":"Day","children":{"Week Overview":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Sample student has already mastered the entire Year 5 word bank (completed Week 10, Term 1) — no new sample word list to show. The day structure below still applies for any student still progressing through their own list.</div></div>"},"Monday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning that understanding what a word means helps us remember how to spell it.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can guess a sensible definition for each word, then check it against the real meaning.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 🕵️ Definition Detective</strong> <em>(Individual · ~20 min)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Before checking a dictionary, guess what each spelling word means — then check how close you were.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>You'll need:</em> Notebook · Dictionary or teacher's help</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>2 min</strong> — Pick the first word and think about where you've seen or heard it before.</li><li><strong>15 min</strong> — Write your own guessed definition for each word, then check the real meaning.</li><li><strong>3 min</strong> — Share the definition that surprised you most.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Tuesday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning to match a spelling word to its meaning or use in context.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can identify the correct word on my card when a clue or definition is read aloud.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 🎱 Word Bingo Blast</strong> <em>(Team · ~20 min)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Fill a bingo card with this week's words — teacher reads a definition or sentence, you mark the matching word.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>You'll need:</em> Bingo card with words written in a grid · Counters or pencil to mark squares</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>3 min</strong> — Write your spelling words into a 3x3 or 4x4 grid in any order.</li><li><strong>14 min</strong> — Teacher reads a clue for a word — if it's on your card, mark it. First to a full line calls &quot;Bingo!&quot;</li><li><strong>3 min</strong> — Play again with a fresh grid if there's time.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Wednesday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning to reinforce spelling through large-scale kinesthetic movement rather than only pen and paper.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can air-write my word clearly enough for my partner to guess it correctly.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 💫 Air Writing Wiggle</strong> <em>(Pairs · ~20 min)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&quot;Write&quot; a spelling word in the air with your finger, elbow, or nose — your partner has to guess it!</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>You'll need:</em> Just your body — no equipment needed</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>2 min</strong> — Practise writing one letter big and slow in the air.</li><li><strong>15 min</strong> — Take turns air-writing each word while your partner guesses, then check the spelling together.</li><li><strong>3 min</strong> — Try writing your favourite word with your elbow for a giggle.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Thursday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning to deepen our memory of a spelling word by connecting its spelling, its meaning, and its use in a sentence.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can write my word correctly three times, draw a picture that shows its meaning, and write a sentence that matches my picture.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 🛑🔤👃 Stop, Spell, Smell</strong> <em>(Individual · ~20 min)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Choose 5 of this week's words. For each one: write it correctly 3 times, draw a picture showing what it means, then write a sentence using the word that matches the picture.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>You'll need:</em> Worksheet or notebook · Pencil · Colouring pencils/markers</p></div></div>"},"Friday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning to spell this week's words correctly and independently, without support.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can write each dictated word with the correct spelling, unaided.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 📝 Friday Spelling Test</strong> <em>(Individual · teacher-administered)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Teacher reads each word aloud (in a sentence if helpful), student writes it, then the teacher marks each word ✓ correct or ✗ incorrect. Any word marked incorrect automatically reappears on next week's list for review; mastered words are replaced with new words from the Year 5 bank.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&lt;a id=&quot;term-2-week-4&quot;&gt;&lt;/a&gt;</p></div></div>"}}},"Week 4":{"label":"Day","children":{"Week Overview":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Sample student has already mastered the entire Year 5 word bank (completed Week 10, Term 1) — no new sample word list to show. The day structure below still applies for any student still progressing through their own list.</div></div>"},"Monday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning to write spelling words correctly and legibly while moving at pace.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can chalk my word correctly before tagging the next teammate.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 🖍️ Chalk &amp; Talk Relay</strong> <em>(Team · ~20 min)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>An outdoor (or hallway) relay where teams race to chalk out each spelling word correctly.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>You'll need:</em> Chalk (or paper strips indoors) · Open path or hallway</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>3 min</strong> — Split into teams and line up at the start line.</li><li><strong>14 min</strong> — One at a time, race up and chalk the next word correctly before running back to tag the next teammate.</li><li><strong>3 min</strong> — Walk along and check every team's words together.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Tuesday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning to reconstruct the correct letter order of a word as quickly as possible.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can flip and arrange my letter cards into the correct spelling before my partner does.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 🔄 Flip &amp; Spell Showdown</strong> <em>(Pairs · ~20 min)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Each player writes a spelling word's letters onto flip cards, shuffles them face-down, then races to flip them over in the correct order — whoever spells it correctly first wins the point!</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>You'll need:</em> Blank cards or small paper squares — one per letter · A pen each</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>3 min</strong> — Both players write the letters of word 1 onto their own set of cards, then shuffle and lay them face-down.</li><li><strong>14 min</strong> — On &quot;go&quot;, both players flip and rearrange their cards into the correct spelling order. First to spell it correctly wins the point — then reshuffle for the next word.</li><li><strong>3 min</strong> — Total up the points and celebrate the round's champion speller.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Wednesday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning that understanding the individual letters inside a word can help us see spelling patterns within other, smaller words.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can find smaller real words hidden inside a spelling word, using only its letters.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 💰 Letter Loot Challenge</strong> <em>(Team · ~20 min)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Race a 30-second clock to spell as many smaller words as you can using only the letters from one spelling word. Score points for every letter you use — and grab a +2 bonus if you use every single letter!</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>You'll need:</em> Paper &amp; pencil (or call out words aloud) · 30-second timer — built in below!</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>3 min</strong> — Explain the scoring: each word you find scores points equal to how many letters it uses. Use up every letter from the original word across your words to earn a +2 bonus.</li><li><strong>14 min</strong> — Run a 30-second round for each spelling word — hunt for smaller words hiding inside it, then use the score checker below to total your points.</li><li><strong>3 min</strong> — Add up every round's score to crown the class Letter Loot Champion.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Thursday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning to deepen our memory of a spelling word by connecting its spelling, its meaning, and its use in a sentence.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can write my word correctly three times, draw a picture that shows its meaning, and write a sentence that matches my picture.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 🛑🔤👃 Stop, Spell, Smell</strong> <em>(Individual · ~20 min)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Choose 5 of this week's words. For each one: write it correctly 3 times, draw a picture showing what it means, then write a sentence using the word that matches the picture.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>You'll need:</em> Worksheet or notebook · Pencil · Colouring pencils/markers</p></div></div>"},"Friday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning to spell this week's words correctly and independently, without support.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can write each dictated word with the correct spelling, unaided.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 📝 Friday Spelling Test</strong> <em>(Individual · teacher-administered)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Teacher reads each word aloud (in a sentence if helpful), student writes it, then the teacher marks each word ✓ correct or ✗ incorrect. Any word marked incorrect automatically reappears on next week's list for review; mastered words are replaced with new words from the Year 5 bank.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&lt;a id=&quot;term-2-week-5&quot;&gt;&lt;/a&gt;</p></div></div>"}}},"Week 5":{"label":"Day","children":{"Week Overview":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Sample student has already mastered the entire Year 5 word bank (completed Week 10, Term 1) — no new sample word list to show. The day structure below still applies for any student still progressing through their own list.</div></div>"},"Monday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning to recognise the correct letter sequence of our spelling words within a field of distractor letters.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can spot my whole spelling word, in order, hidden across, down, or diagonally.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 🔎 Word Search Hunt</strong> <em>(Individual · ~20 min)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Hunt for this week's spelling words hidden in a letter grid — words can run across, down, or diagonally.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>You'll need:</em> Printed or on-screen word search · Pencil or highlighter</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>2 min</strong> — Look at the word list and read each word aloud before you start hunting.</li><li><strong>15 min</strong> — Circle or highlight every word as you find it in the grid.</li><li><strong>3 min</strong> — Tell a partner which word was trickiest to spot and why.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Tuesday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning to reconstruct a spelling word when its letters are presented out of order.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can unscramble a mixed-up word back into its correct spelling, quickly and confidently.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 🌀 Speedy Scramble Swap</strong> <em>(Pairs · ~20 min)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Partners take turns unscrambling each other's mixed-up spelling words as fast as they can.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>You'll need:</em> Whiteboard or paper · Timer</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>3 min</strong> — Partner A reads out the scrambled letters for word 1 — Partner B unscrambles and spells it aloud.</li><li><strong>12 min</strong> — Take turns through the whole list, swapping roles each word.</li><li><strong>5 min</strong> — Whoever unscrambled the most words correctly picks the next warm-up game!</li></ul></div></div>"},"Wednesday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning to build muscle memory for tricky spelling patterns through repeated, multisensory writing.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can write each word correctly three times, saying each letter as I go.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 🌈 Rainbow Write</strong> <em>(Individual · ~20 min)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write each spelling word three times, using a different colour pencil each time, tracing over the same letters.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>You'll need:</em> 3+ coloured pencils or markers · Plain paper</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>2 min</strong> — Choose your 3 colours for today.</li><li><strong>15 min</strong> — Write each word once in each colour, saying each letter as you write it.</li><li><strong>3 min</strong> — Pick your favourite rainbow word and show a partner.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Thursday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning to deepen our memory of a spelling word by connecting its spelling, its meaning, and its use in a sentence.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can write my word correctly three times, draw a picture that shows its meaning, and write a sentence that matches my picture.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 🛑🔤👃 Stop, Spell, Smell</strong> <em>(Individual · ~20 min)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Choose 5 of this week's words. For each one: write it correctly 3 times, draw a picture showing what it means, then write a sentence using the word that matches the picture.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>You'll need:</em> Worksheet or notebook · Pencil · Colouring pencils/markers</p></div></div>"},"Friday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning to spell this week's words correctly and independently, without support.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can write each dictated word with the correct spelling, unaided.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 📝 Friday Spelling Test</strong> <em>(Individual · teacher-administered)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Teacher reads each word aloud (in a sentence if helpful), student writes it, then the teacher marks each word ✓ correct or ✗ incorrect. Any word marked incorrect automatically reappears on next week's list for review; mastered words are replaced with new words from the Year 5 bank.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&lt;a id=&quot;term-2-week-6&quot;&gt;&lt;/a&gt;</p></div></div>"}}},"Week 6":{"label":"Day","children":{"Week Overview":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Sample student has already mastered the entire Year 5 word bank (completed Week 10, Term 1) — no new sample word list to show. The day structure below still applies for any student still progressing through their own list.</div></div>"},"Monday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning to build up a word sound-by-sound and letter-by-letter, strengthening our sense of letter order.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can build a correct letter pyramid for every word on my list.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 🔺 Pyramid Words</strong> <em>(Individual · ~20 min)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Build each spelling word one letter at a time, like a growing staircase, until the whole word is spelled out.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>You'll need:</em> Paper · Pencil</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>2 min</strong> — Teacher shows an example pyramid on the board (e.g. b / be / bec / beca...).</li><li><strong>15 min</strong> — Build a pyramid for every word on your list.</li><li><strong>3 min</strong> — Trade books with a neighbour and check each other's pyramids.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Tuesday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning to spell words aloud fluently and automatically, one letter at a time, under light time pressure.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can say my share of the letters correctly without hesitating, and say the whole word at the end.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 🎾 Word Tennis</strong> <em>(Pairs · ~20 min)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Partners &quot;volley&quot; a word back and forth, each saying the next letter — miss a letter and you lose the point!</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>You'll need:</em> Just your voices!</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>2 min</strong> — Decide who serves first for word 1.</li><li><strong>15 min</strong> — Rally through the whole list — one letter each, back and forth, then say the whole word together at the end.</li><li><strong>3 min</strong> — Total up the points and celebrate the winner.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Wednesday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning to recall and say a spelling word correctly from memory, under a small amount of performance pressure.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can spell my word aloud, letter by letter, without looking at the list.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 🎤 Spelling Bee Countdown</strong> <em>(Team · ~20 min)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Teams take turns spelling words aloud — get it right and stay in, get it wrong and sit down. Last team standing wins!</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>You'll need:</em> Word list (teacher calls words) · Nothing else needed</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>3 min</strong> — Split into teams of 3–4 and line up.</li><li><strong>14 min</strong> — Teacher calls a word, first player in line spells it aloud. Correct = go to the back of the line. Wrong = sit down.</li><li><strong>3 min</strong> — Last team with players standing wins the round!</li></ul></div></div>"},"Thursday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning to deepen our memory of a spelling word by connecting its spelling, its meaning, and its use in a sentence.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can write my word correctly three times, draw a picture that shows its meaning, and write a sentence that matches my picture.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 🛑🔤👃 Stop, Spell, Smell</strong> <em>(Individual · ~20 min)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Choose 5 of this week's words. For each one: write it correctly 3 times, draw a picture showing what it means, then write a sentence using the word that matches the picture.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>You'll need:</em> Worksheet or notebook · Pencil · Colouring pencils/markers</p></div></div>"},"Friday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning to spell this week's words correctly and independently, without support.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can write each dictated word with the correct spelling, unaided.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 📝 Friday Spelling Test</strong> <em>(Individual · teacher-administered)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Teacher reads each word aloud (in a sentence if helpful), student writes it, then the teacher marks each word ✓ correct or ✗ incorrect. Any word marked incorrect automatically reappears on next week's list for review; mastered words are replaced with new words from the Year 5 bank.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&lt;a id=&quot;term-2-week-7&quot;&gt;&lt;/a&gt;</p></div></div>"}}},"Week 7":{"label":"Day","children":{"Week Overview":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Sample student has already mastered the entire Year 5 word bank (completed Week 10, Term 1) — no new sample word list to show. The day structure below still applies for any student still progressing through their own list.</div></div>"},"Monday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning to write a spelling word correctly and legibly at speed.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can run up, write my word correctly first try, and tag my teammate.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 🌍 Around the World Relay</strong> <em>(Team · ~20 min)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>A running relay — race to the board, write the next word correctly, then tag your teammate.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>You'll need:</em> Whiteboard/chalkboard or paper on a wall · Markers</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>3 min</strong> — Split into even teams and line up single-file.</li><li><strong>14 min</strong> — On &quot;go&quot;, the first runner races up, writes word 1 correctly, runs back and tags the next player who writes word 2, and so on.</li><li><strong>3 min</strong> — Check every team's words together — first fully-correct list wins.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Tuesday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning to use knowledge of spelling patterns to predict letters that are missing from a word.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can fill every gap in a word with the correct missing letter(s).</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 🕳️ Mystery Missing Letters</strong> <em>(Individual · ~20 min)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Some letters have vanished from each spelling word — figure out which ones and fill in the gaps.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>You'll need:</em> Worksheet or notebook · Pencil</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>2 min</strong> — Look at the first mystery word and predict the missing letters.</li><li><strong>15 min</strong> — Fill in every missing letter for all the words on your list.</li><li><strong>3 min</strong> — Swap with a partner and check each other's answers.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Wednesday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning to spell a word correctly when we hear it inside a full sentence, rather than in isolation.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can listen for my word inside a sentence and write it with the correct spelling.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: ✍️ Partner Dictation Challenge</strong> <em>(Pairs · ~20 min)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>One partner reads a word inside a sentence, the other writes it down — then swap and check together.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>You'll need:</em> Paper · Pencil</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>2 min</strong> — Decide who dictates first.</li><li><strong>14 min</strong> — Dictator says a sentence containing the spelling word; writer writes just the word. Swap roles halfway through the list.</li><li><strong>4 min</strong> — Compare your writing to the real spelling and fix any mistakes together.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Thursday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning to deepen our memory of a spelling word by connecting its spelling, its meaning, and its use in a sentence.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can write my word correctly three times, draw a picture that shows its meaning, and write a sentence that matches my picture.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 🛑🔤👃 Stop, Spell, Smell</strong> <em>(Individual · ~20 min)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Choose 5 of this week's words. For each one: write it correctly 3 times, draw a picture showing what it means, then write a sentence using the word that matches the picture.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>You'll need:</em> Worksheet or notebook · Pencil · Colouring pencils/markers</p></div></div>"},"Friday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning to spell this week's words correctly and independently, without support.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can write each dictated word with the correct spelling, unaided.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 📝 Friday Spelling Test</strong> <em>(Individual · teacher-administered)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Teacher reads each word aloud (in a sentence if helpful), student writes it, then the teacher marks each word ✓ correct or ✗ incorrect. Any word marked incorrect automatically reappears on next week's list for review; mastered words are replaced with new words from the Year 5 bank.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&lt;a id=&quot;term-2-week-8&quot;&gt;&lt;/a&gt;</p></div></div>"}}},"Week 8":{"label":"Day","children":{"Week Overview":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Sample student has already mastered the entire Year 5 word bank (completed Week 10, Term 1) — no new sample word list to show. The day structure below still applies for any student still progressing through their own list.</div></div>"},"Monday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning to break spelling words into syllables to help us spell longer or trickier words.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can clap and count the syllables in each word and agree on the total with my partner.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 👏 Syllable Clap Count</strong> <em>(Pairs · ~20 min)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Clap out the syllables in each spelling word, then work out how many &quot;chunks&quot; it breaks into.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>You'll need:</em> Just your hands!</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>3 min</strong> — Warm up by clapping your own name.</li><li><strong>14 min</strong> — Take turns saying and clapping each spelling word, then agree on the syllable count and write it down.</li><li><strong>3 min</strong> — Find the word on your list with the most syllables — can you beat it next week?</li></ul></div></div>"},"Tuesday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning to notice patterns across our spelling words (e.g. syllables, tricky letter strings, silent letters) and use them to group words.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can sort every word into a group using a spelling pattern I chose myself.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 🗂️ Word Sort Categories</strong> <em>(Pairs · ~20 min)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Sort this week's words into your own categories — by syllables, tricky letter patterns, or silent letters.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>You'll need:</em> Small cards or sticky notes with words written on them · Table or floor space</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>3 min</strong> — Write each word onto its own card.</li><li><strong>14 min</strong> — Choose a sorting rule together (e.g. number of syllables) and sort every word into groups.</li><li><strong>3 min</strong> — Explain your sorting rule to another pair.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Wednesday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning to reinforce the correct letter sequence of a word through a creative, visual activity.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can draw and colour each word so that every letter is in the correct order.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 🎨 Bubble Letter Word Art</strong> <em>(Individual · ~20 min)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Draw each spelling word in big bubble or graffiti letters and decorate it.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>You'll need:</em> Plain paper · Pencil, then colouring pencils/markers</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>2 min</strong> — Look at an example bubble-letter word on the board.</li><li><strong>15 min</strong> — Draw and colour bubble letters for as many words as you can.</li><li><strong>3 min</strong> — Show off your favourite word-art creation to the class.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Thursday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning to deepen our memory of a spelling word by connecting its spelling, its meaning, and its use in a sentence.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can write my word correctly three times, draw a picture that shows its meaning, and write a sentence that matches my picture.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 🛑🔤👃 Stop, Spell, Smell</strong> <em>(Individual · ~20 min)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Choose 5 of this week's words. For each one: write it correctly 3 times, draw a picture showing what it means, then write a sentence using the word that matches the picture.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>You'll need:</em> Worksheet or notebook · Pencil · Colouring pencils/markers</p></div></div>"},"Friday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning to spell this week's words correctly and independently, without support.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can write each dictated word with the correct spelling, unaided.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 📝 Friday Spelling Test</strong> <em>(Individual · teacher-administered)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Teacher reads each word aloud (in a sentence if helpful), student writes it, then the teacher marks each word ✓ correct or ✗ incorrect. Any word marked incorrect automatically reappears on next week's list for review; mastered words are replaced with new words from the Year 5 bank.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&lt;a id=&quot;term-2-week-9&quot;&gt;&lt;/a&gt;</p></div></div>"}}},"Week 9":{"label":"Day","children":{"Week Overview":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Sample student has already mastered the entire Year 5 word bank (completed Week 10, Term 1) — no new sample word list to show. The day structure below still applies for any student still progressing through their own list.</div></div>"},"Monday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning to recall spelling words correctly on demand, in a low-pressure game format.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can spell the word correctly before I take my turn to move.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 🐍 Spelling Snakes &amp; Ladders</strong> <em>(Team · ~20 min)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Play snakes and ladders — but you can only move if you spell a word correctly first!</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>You'll need:</em> Snakes &amp; ladders board (drawn or printed) · Dice · Counters</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>2 min</strong> — Set up the board and counters in groups of 3–4.</li><li><strong>15 min</strong> — Roll the dice — before moving, spell the next word on your list aloud. Get it right to move, wrong to stay put.</li><li><strong>3 min</strong> — First to reach the end (or whoever's furthest when time is up) wins.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Tuesday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning to use our spelling words correctly and meaningfully within a sentence of our own.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can add a sentence to our class story that uses my spelling word correctly.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 📖 Silly Sentence Story Chain</strong> <em>(Team · ~20 min)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Build one giant silly class story together, where each sentence must use the next spelling word.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>You'll need:</em> Paper or shared doc to record the story</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>3 min</strong> — Sit in a circle with your team and decide on a story character.</li><li><strong>14 min</strong> — Take turns adding one sentence to the story, each one using a spelling word correctly.</li><li><strong>3 min</strong> — Read your finished silly story aloud to another team.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Wednesday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning to recall and say a spelling word correctly from memory in a fun, active game.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can spell my word aloud correctly when I catch the beanbag.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 🎯 Beanbag Toss Spelling</strong> <em>(Team · ~20 min)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Toss a beanbag or soft ball — whoever catches it has to spell the next word out loud.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>You'll need:</em> Beanbag or soft ball · Open space</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>2 min</strong> — Stand in a circle with your team.</li><li><strong>15 min</strong> — Toss the beanbag — catcher spells the next word aloud. Correct = toss again for the next word; incorrect = get a hint and try once more.</li><li><strong>3 min</strong> — Celebrate every word your team got through together.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Thursday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning to deepen our memory of a spelling word by connecting its spelling, its meaning, and its use in a sentence.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can write my word correctly three times, draw a picture that shows its meaning, and write a sentence that matches my picture.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 🛑🔤👃 Stop, Spell, Smell</strong> <em>(Individual · ~20 min)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Choose 5 of this week's words. For each one: write it correctly 3 times, draw a picture showing what it means, then write a sentence using the word that matches the picture.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>You'll need:</em> Worksheet or notebook · Pencil · Colouring pencils/markers</p></div></div>"},"Friday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning to spell this week's words correctly and independently, without support.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can write each dictated word with the correct spelling, unaided.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 📝 Friday Spelling Test</strong> <em>(Individual · teacher-administered)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Teacher reads each word aloud (in a sentence if helpful), student writes it, then the teacher marks each word ✓ correct or ✗ incorrect. Any word marked incorrect automatically reappears on next week's list for review; mastered words are replaced with new words from the Year 5 bank.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&lt;a id=&quot;term-2-week-10&quot;&gt;&lt;/a&gt;</p></div></div>"}}},"Week 10":{"label":"Day","children":{"Week Overview":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Sample student has already mastered the entire Year 5 word bank (completed Week 10, Term 1) — no new sample word list to show. The day structure below still applies for any student still progressing through their own list.</div></div>"},"Monday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning that understanding what a word means helps us remember how to spell it.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can guess a sensible definition for each word, then check it against the real meaning.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 🕵️ Definition Detective</strong> <em>(Individual · ~20 min)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Before checking a dictionary, guess what each spelling word means — then check how close you were.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>You'll need:</em> Notebook · Dictionary or teacher's help</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>2 min</strong> — Pick the first word and think about where you've seen or heard it before.</li><li><strong>15 min</strong> — Write your own guessed definition for each word, then check the real meaning.</li><li><strong>3 min</strong> — Share the definition that surprised you most.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Tuesday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning to match a spelling word to its meaning or use in context.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can identify the correct word on my card when a clue or definition is read aloud.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 🎱 Word Bingo Blast</strong> <em>(Team · ~20 min)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Fill a bingo card with this week's words — teacher reads a definition or sentence, you mark the matching word.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>You'll need:</em> Bingo card with words written in a grid · Counters or pencil to mark squares</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>3 min</strong> — Write your spelling words into a 3x3 or 4x4 grid in any order.</li><li><strong>14 min</strong> — Teacher reads a clue for a word — if it's on your card, mark it. First to a full line calls &quot;Bingo!&quot;</li><li><strong>3 min</strong> — Play again with a fresh grid if there's time.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Wednesday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning to reinforce spelling through large-scale kinesthetic movement rather than only pen and paper.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can air-write my word clearly enough for my partner to guess it correctly.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 💫 Air Writing Wiggle</strong> <em>(Pairs · ~20 min)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&quot;Write&quot; a spelling word in the air with your finger, elbow, or nose — your partner has to guess it!</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>You'll need:</em> Just your body — no equipment needed</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>2 min</strong> — Practise writing one letter big and slow in the air.</li><li><strong>15 min</strong> — Take turns air-writing each word while your partner guesses, then check the spelling together.</li><li><strong>3 min</strong> — Try writing your favourite word with your elbow for a giggle.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Thursday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning to deepen our memory of a spelling word by connecting its spelling, its meaning, and its use in a sentence.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can write my word correctly three times, draw a picture that shows its meaning, and write a sentence that matches my picture.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 🛑🔤👃 Stop, Spell, Smell</strong> <em>(Individual · ~20 min)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Choose 5 of this week's words. For each one: write it correctly 3 times, draw a picture showing what it means, then write a sentence using the word that matches the picture.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>You'll need:</em> Worksheet or notebook · Pencil · Colouring pencils/markers</p></div></div>"},"Friday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning to spell this week's words correctly and independently, without support.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can write each dictated word with the correct spelling, unaided.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 📝 Friday Spelling Test</strong> <em>(Individual · teacher-administered)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Teacher reads each word aloud (in a sentence if helpful), student writes it, then the teacher marks each word ✓ correct or ✗ incorrect. Any word marked incorrect automatically reappears on next week's list for review; mastered words are replaced with new words from the Year 5 bank.</p></div></div>"}}}}},"Term 3":{"label":"Week","children":{"Week 1":{"label":"Day","children":{"Week Overview":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Sample student has already mastered the entire Year 5 word bank (completed Week 10, Term 1) — no new sample word list to show. The day structure below still applies for any student still progressing through their own list.</div></div>"},"Monday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning to write spelling words correctly and legibly while moving at pace.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can chalk my word correctly before tagging the next teammate.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 🖍️ Chalk &amp; Talk Relay</strong> <em>(Team · ~20 min)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>An outdoor (or hallway) relay where teams race to chalk out each spelling word correctly.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>You'll need:</em> Chalk (or paper strips indoors) · Open path or hallway</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>3 min</strong> — Split into teams and line up at the start line.</li><li><strong>14 min</strong> — One at a time, race up and chalk the next word correctly before running back to tag the next teammate.</li><li><strong>3 min</strong> — Walk along and check every team's words together.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Tuesday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning to reconstruct the correct letter order of a word as quickly as possible.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can flip and arrange my letter cards into the correct spelling before my partner does.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 🔄 Flip &amp; Spell Showdown</strong> <em>(Pairs · ~20 min)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Each player writes a spelling word's letters onto flip cards, shuffles them face-down, then races to flip them over in the correct order — whoever spells it correctly first wins the point!</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>You'll need:</em> Blank cards or small paper squares — one per letter · A pen each</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>3 min</strong> — Both players write the letters of word 1 onto their own set of cards, then shuffle and lay them face-down.</li><li><strong>14 min</strong> — On &quot;go&quot;, both players flip and rearrange their cards into the correct spelling order. First to spell it correctly wins the point — then reshuffle for the next word.</li><li><strong>3 min</strong> — Total up the points and celebrate the round's champion speller.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Wednesday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning that understanding the individual letters inside a word can help us see spelling patterns within other, smaller words.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can find smaller real words hidden inside a spelling word, using only its letters.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 💰 Letter Loot Challenge</strong> <em>(Team · ~20 min)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Race a 30-second clock to spell as many smaller words as you can using only the letters from one spelling word. Score points for every letter you use — and grab a +2 bonus if you use every single letter!</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>You'll need:</em> Paper &amp; pencil (or call out words aloud) · 30-second timer — built in below!</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>3 min</strong> — Explain the scoring: each word you find scores points equal to how many letters it uses. Use up every letter from the original word across your words to earn a +2 bonus.</li><li><strong>14 min</strong> — Run a 30-second round for each spelling word — hunt for smaller words hiding inside it, then use the score checker below to total your points.</li><li><strong>3 min</strong> — Add up every round's score to crown the class Letter Loot Champion.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Thursday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning to deepen our memory of a spelling word by connecting its spelling, its meaning, and its use in a sentence.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can write my word correctly three times, draw a picture that shows its meaning, and write a sentence that matches my picture.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 🛑🔤👃 Stop, Spell, Smell</strong> <em>(Individual · ~20 min)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Choose 5 of this week's words. For each one: write it correctly 3 times, draw a picture showing what it means, then write a sentence using the word that matches the picture.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>You'll need:</em> Worksheet or notebook · Pencil · Colouring pencils/markers</p></div></div>"},"Friday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning to spell this week's words correctly and independently, without support.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can write each dictated word with the correct spelling, unaided.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 📝 Friday Spelling Test</strong> <em>(Individual · teacher-administered)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Teacher reads each word aloud (in a sentence if helpful), student writes it, then the teacher marks each word ✓ correct or ✗ incorrect. Any word marked incorrect automatically reappears on next week's list for review; mastered words are replaced with new words from the Year 5 bank.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&lt;a id=&quot;term-3-week-2&quot;&gt;&lt;/a&gt;</p></div></div>"}}},"Week 2":{"label":"Day","children":{"Week Overview":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Sample student has already mastered the entire Year 5 word bank (completed Week 10, Term 1) — no new sample word list to show. The day structure below still applies for any student still progressing through their own list.</div></div>"},"Monday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning to recognise the correct letter sequence of our spelling words within a field of distractor letters.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can spot my whole spelling word, in order, hidden across, down, or diagonally.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 🔎 Word Search Hunt</strong> <em>(Individual · ~20 min)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Hunt for this week's spelling words hidden in a letter grid — words can run across, down, or diagonally.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>You'll need:</em> Printed or on-screen word search · Pencil or highlighter</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>2 min</strong> — Look at the word list and read each word aloud before you start hunting.</li><li><strong>15 min</strong> — Circle or highlight every word as you find it in the grid.</li><li><strong>3 min</strong> — Tell a partner which word was trickiest to spot and why.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Tuesday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning to reconstruct a spelling word when its letters are presented out of order.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can unscramble a mixed-up word back into its correct spelling, quickly and confidently.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 🌀 Speedy Scramble Swap</strong> <em>(Pairs · ~20 min)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Partners take turns unscrambling each other's mixed-up spelling words as fast as they can.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>You'll need:</em> Whiteboard or paper · Timer</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>3 min</strong> — Partner A reads out the scrambled letters for word 1 — Partner B unscrambles and spells it aloud.</li><li><strong>12 min</strong> — Take turns through the whole list, swapping roles each word.</li><li><strong>5 min</strong> — Whoever unscrambled the most words correctly picks the next warm-up game!</li></ul></div></div>"},"Wednesday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning to build muscle memory for tricky spelling patterns through repeated, multisensory writing.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can write each word correctly three times, saying each letter as I go.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 🌈 Rainbow Write</strong> <em>(Individual · ~20 min)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write each spelling word three times, using a different colour pencil each time, tracing over the same letters.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>You'll need:</em> 3+ coloured pencils or markers · Plain paper</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>2 min</strong> — Choose your 3 colours for today.</li><li><strong>15 min</strong> — Write each word once in each colour, saying each letter as you write it.</li><li><strong>3 min</strong> — Pick your favourite rainbow word and show a partner.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Thursday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning to deepen our memory of a spelling word by connecting its spelling, its meaning, and its use in a sentence.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can write my word correctly three times, draw a picture that shows its meaning, and write a sentence that matches my picture.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 🛑🔤👃 Stop, Spell, Smell</strong> <em>(Individual · ~20 min)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Choose 5 of this week's words. For each one: write it correctly 3 times, draw a picture showing what it means, then write a sentence using the word that matches the picture.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>You'll need:</em> Worksheet or notebook · Pencil · Colouring pencils/markers</p></div></div>"},"Friday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning to spell this week's words correctly and independently, without support.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can write each dictated word with the correct spelling, unaided.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 📝 Friday Spelling Test</strong> <em>(Individual · teacher-administered)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Teacher reads each word aloud (in a sentence if helpful), student writes it, then the teacher marks each word ✓ correct or ✗ incorrect. Any word marked incorrect automatically reappears on next week's list for review; mastered words are replaced with new words from the Year 5 bank.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&lt;a id=&quot;term-3-week-3&quot;&gt;&lt;/a&gt;</p></div></div>"}}},"Week 3":{"label":"Day","children":{"Week Overview":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Sample student has already mastered the entire Year 5 word bank (completed Week 10, Term 1) — no new sample word list to show. The day structure below still applies for any student still progressing through their own list.</div></div>"},"Monday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning to build up a word sound-by-sound and letter-by-letter, strengthening our sense of letter order.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can build a correct letter pyramid for every word on my list.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 🔺 Pyramid Words</strong> <em>(Individual · ~20 min)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Build each spelling word one letter at a time, like a growing staircase, until the whole word is spelled out.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>You'll need:</em> Paper · Pencil</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>2 min</strong> — Teacher shows an example pyramid on the board (e.g. b / be / bec / beca...).</li><li><strong>15 min</strong> — Build a pyramid for every word on your list.</li><li><strong>3 min</strong> — Trade books with a neighbour and check each other's pyramids.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Tuesday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning to spell words aloud fluently and automatically, one letter at a time, under light time pressure.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can say my share of the letters correctly without hesitating, and say the whole word at the end.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 🎾 Word Tennis</strong> <em>(Pairs · ~20 min)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Partners &quot;volley&quot; a word back and forth, each saying the next letter — miss a letter and you lose the point!</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>You'll need:</em> Just your voices!</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>2 min</strong> — Decide who serves first for word 1.</li><li><strong>15 min</strong> — Rally through the whole list — one letter each, back and forth, then say the whole word together at the end.</li><li><strong>3 min</strong> — Total up the points and celebrate the winner.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Wednesday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning to recall and say a spelling word correctly from memory, under a small amount of performance pressure.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can spell my word aloud, letter by letter, without looking at the list.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 🎤 Spelling Bee Countdown</strong> <em>(Team · ~20 min)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Teams take turns spelling words aloud — get it right and stay in, get it wrong and sit down. Last team standing wins!</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>You'll need:</em> Word list (teacher calls words) · Nothing else needed</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>3 min</strong> — Split into teams of 3–4 and line up.</li><li><strong>14 min</strong> — Teacher calls a word, first player in line spells it aloud. Correct = go to the back of the line. Wrong = sit down.</li><li><strong>3 min</strong> — Last team with players standing wins the round!</li></ul></div></div>"},"Thursday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning to deepen our memory of a spelling word by connecting its spelling, its meaning, and its use in a sentence.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can write my word correctly three times, draw a picture that shows its meaning, and write a sentence that matches my picture.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 🛑🔤👃 Stop, Spell, Smell</strong> <em>(Individual · ~20 min)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Choose 5 of this week's words. For each one: write it correctly 3 times, draw a picture showing what it means, then write a sentence using the word that matches the picture.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>You'll need:</em> Worksheet or notebook · Pencil · Colouring pencils/markers</p></div></div>"},"Friday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning to spell this week's words correctly and independently, without support.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can write each dictated word with the correct spelling, unaided.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 📝 Friday Spelling Test</strong> <em>(Individual · teacher-administered)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Teacher reads each word aloud (in a sentence if helpful), student writes it, then the teacher marks each word ✓ correct or ✗ incorrect. Any word marked incorrect automatically reappears on next week's list for review; mastered words are replaced with new words from the Year 5 bank.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&lt;a id=&quot;term-3-week-4&quot;&gt;&lt;/a&gt;</p></div></div>"}}},"Week 4":{"label":"Day","children":{"Week Overview":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Sample student has already mastered the entire Year 5 word bank (completed Week 10, Term 1) — no new sample word list to show. The day structure below still applies for any student still progressing through their own list.</div></div>"},"Monday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning to write a spelling word correctly and legibly at speed.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can run up, write my word correctly first try, and tag my teammate.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 🌍 Around the World Relay</strong> <em>(Team · ~20 min)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>A running relay — race to the board, write the next word correctly, then tag your teammate.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>You'll need:</em> Whiteboard/chalkboard or paper on a wall · Markers</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>3 min</strong> — Split into even teams and line up single-file.</li><li><strong>14 min</strong> — On &quot;go&quot;, the first runner races up, writes word 1 correctly, runs back and tags the next player who writes word 2, and so on.</li><li><strong>3 min</strong> — Check every team's words together — first fully-correct list wins.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Tuesday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning to use knowledge of spelling patterns to predict letters that are missing from a word.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can fill every gap in a word with the correct missing letter(s).</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 🕳️ Mystery Missing Letters</strong> <em>(Individual · ~20 min)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Some letters have vanished from each spelling word — figure out which ones and fill in the gaps.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>You'll need:</em> Worksheet or notebook · Pencil</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>2 min</strong> — Look at the first mystery word and predict the missing letters.</li><li><strong>15 min</strong> — Fill in every missing letter for all the words on your list.</li><li><strong>3 min</strong> — Swap with a partner and check each other's answers.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Wednesday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning to spell a word correctly when we hear it inside a full sentence, rather than in isolation.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can listen for my word inside a sentence and write it with the correct spelling.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: ✍️ Partner Dictation Challenge</strong> <em>(Pairs · ~20 min)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>One partner reads a word inside a sentence, the other writes it down — then swap and check together.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>You'll need:</em> Paper · Pencil</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>2 min</strong> — Decide who dictates first.</li><li><strong>14 min</strong> — Dictator says a sentence containing the spelling word; writer writes just the word. Swap roles halfway through the list.</li><li><strong>4 min</strong> — Compare your writing to the real spelling and fix any mistakes together.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Thursday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning to deepen our memory of a spelling word by connecting its spelling, its meaning, and its use in a sentence.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can write my word correctly three times, draw a picture that shows its meaning, and write a sentence that matches my picture.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 🛑🔤👃 Stop, Spell, Smell</strong> <em>(Individual · ~20 min)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Choose 5 of this week's words. For each one: write it correctly 3 times, draw a picture showing what it means, then write a sentence using the word that matches the picture.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>You'll need:</em> Worksheet or notebook · Pencil · Colouring pencils/markers</p></div></div>"},"Friday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning to spell this week's words correctly and independently, without support.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can write each dictated word with the correct spelling, unaided.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 📝 Friday Spelling Test</strong> <em>(Individual · teacher-administered)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Teacher reads each word aloud (in a sentence if helpful), student writes it, then the teacher marks each word ✓ correct or ✗ incorrect. Any word marked incorrect automatically reappears on next week's list for review; mastered words are replaced with new words from the Year 5 bank.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&lt;a id=&quot;term-3-week-5&quot;&gt;&lt;/a&gt;</p></div></div>"}}},"Week 5":{"label":"Day","children":{"Week Overview":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Sample student has already mastered the entire Year 5 word bank (completed Week 10, Term 1) — no new sample word list to show. The day structure below still applies for any student still progressing through their own list.</div></div>"},"Monday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning to break spelling words into syllables to help us spell longer or trickier words.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can clap and count the syllables in each word and agree on the total with my partner.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 👏 Syllable Clap Count</strong> <em>(Pairs · ~20 min)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Clap out the syllables in each spelling word, then work out how many &quot;chunks&quot; it breaks into.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>You'll need:</em> Just your hands!</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>3 min</strong> — Warm up by clapping your own name.</li><li><strong>14 min</strong> — Take turns saying and clapping each spelling word, then agree on the syllable count and write it down.</li><li><strong>3 min</strong> — Find the word on your list with the most syllables — can you beat it next week?</li></ul></div></div>"},"Tuesday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning to notice patterns across our spelling words (e.g. syllables, tricky letter strings, silent letters) and use them to group words.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can sort every word into a group using a spelling pattern I chose myself.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 🗂️ Word Sort Categories</strong> <em>(Pairs · ~20 min)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Sort this week's words into your own categories — by syllables, tricky letter patterns, or silent letters.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>You'll need:</em> Small cards or sticky notes with words written on them · Table or floor space</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>3 min</strong> — Write each word onto its own card.</li><li><strong>14 min</strong> — Choose a sorting rule together (e.g. number of syllables) and sort every word into groups.</li><li><strong>3 min</strong> — Explain your sorting rule to another pair.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Wednesday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning to reinforce the correct letter sequence of a word through a creative, visual activity.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can draw and colour each word so that every letter is in the correct order.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 🎨 Bubble Letter Word Art</strong> <em>(Individual · ~20 min)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Draw each spelling word in big bubble or graffiti letters and decorate it.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>You'll need:</em> Plain paper · Pencil, then colouring pencils/markers</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>2 min</strong> — Look at an example bubble-letter word on the board.</li><li><strong>15 min</strong> — Draw and colour bubble letters for as many words as you can.</li><li><strong>3 min</strong> — Show off your favourite word-art creation to the class.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Thursday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning to deepen our memory of a spelling word by connecting its spelling, its meaning, and its use in a sentence.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can write my word correctly three times, draw a picture that shows its meaning, and write a sentence that matches my picture.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 🛑🔤👃 Stop, Spell, Smell</strong> <em>(Individual · ~20 min)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Choose 5 of this week's words. For each one: write it correctly 3 times, draw a picture showing what it means, then write a sentence using the word that matches the picture.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>You'll need:</em> Worksheet or notebook · Pencil · Colouring pencils/markers</p></div></div>"},"Friday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning to spell this week's words correctly and independently, without support.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can write each dictated word with the correct spelling, unaided.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 📝 Friday Spelling Test</strong> <em>(Individual · teacher-administered)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Teacher reads each word aloud (in a sentence if helpful), student writes it, then the teacher marks each word ✓ correct or ✗ incorrect. Any word marked incorrect automatically reappears on next week's list for review; mastered words are replaced with new words from the Year 5 bank.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&lt;a id=&quot;term-3-week-6&quot;&gt;&lt;/a&gt;</p></div></div>"}}},"Week 6":{"label":"Day","children":{"Week Overview":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Sample student has already mastered the entire Year 5 word bank (completed Week 10, Term 1) — no new sample word list to show. The day structure below still applies for any student still progressing through their own list.</div></div>"},"Monday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning to recall spelling words correctly on demand, in a low-pressure game format.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can spell the word correctly before I take my turn to move.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 🐍 Spelling Snakes &amp; Ladders</strong> <em>(Team · ~20 min)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Play snakes and ladders — but you can only move if you spell a word correctly first!</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>You'll need:</em> Snakes &amp; ladders board (drawn or printed) · Dice · Counters</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>2 min</strong> — Set up the board and counters in groups of 3–4.</li><li><strong>15 min</strong> — Roll the dice — before moving, spell the next word on your list aloud. Get it right to move, wrong to stay put.</li><li><strong>3 min</strong> — First to reach the end (or whoever's furthest when time is up) wins.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Tuesday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning to use our spelling words correctly and meaningfully within a sentence of our own.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can add a sentence to our class story that uses my spelling word correctly.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 📖 Silly Sentence Story Chain</strong> <em>(Team · ~20 min)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Build one giant silly class story together, where each sentence must use the next spelling word.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>You'll need:</em> Paper or shared doc to record the story</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>3 min</strong> — Sit in a circle with your team and decide on a story character.</li><li><strong>14 min</strong> — Take turns adding one sentence to the story, each one using a spelling word correctly.</li><li><strong>3 min</strong> — Read your finished silly story aloud to another team.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Wednesday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning to recall and say a spelling word correctly from memory in a fun, active game.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can spell my word aloud correctly when I catch the beanbag.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 🎯 Beanbag Toss Spelling</strong> <em>(Team · ~20 min)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Toss a beanbag or soft ball — whoever catches it has to spell the next word out loud.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>You'll need:</em> Beanbag or soft ball · Open space</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>2 min</strong> — Stand in a circle with your team.</li><li><strong>15 min</strong> — Toss the beanbag — catcher spells the next word aloud. Correct = toss again for the next word; incorrect = get a hint and try once more.</li><li><strong>3 min</strong> — Celebrate every word your team got through together.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Thursday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning to deepen our memory of a spelling word by connecting its spelling, its meaning, and its use in a sentence.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can write my word correctly three times, draw a picture that shows its meaning, and write a sentence that matches my picture.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 🛑🔤👃 Stop, Spell, Smell</strong> <em>(Individual · ~20 min)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Choose 5 of this week's words. For each one: write it correctly 3 times, draw a picture showing what it means, then write a sentence using the word that matches the picture.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>You'll need:</em> Worksheet or notebook · Pencil · Colouring pencils/markers</p></div></div>"},"Friday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning to spell this week's words correctly and independently, without support.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can write each dictated word with the correct spelling, unaided.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 📝 Friday Spelling Test</strong> <em>(Individual · teacher-administered)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Teacher reads each word aloud (in a sentence if helpful), student writes it, then the teacher marks each word ✓ correct or ✗ incorrect. Any word marked incorrect automatically reappears on next week's list for review; mastered words are replaced with new words from the Year 5 bank.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&lt;a id=&quot;term-3-week-7&quot;&gt;&lt;/a&gt;</p></div></div>"}}},"Week 7":{"label":"Day","children":{"Week Overview":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Sample student has already mastered the entire Year 5 word bank (completed Week 10, Term 1) — no new sample word list to show. The day structure below still applies for any student still progressing through their own list.</div></div>"},"Monday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning that understanding what a word means helps us remember how to spell it.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can guess a sensible definition for each word, then check it against the real meaning.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 🕵️ Definition Detective</strong> <em>(Individual · ~20 min)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Before checking a dictionary, guess what each spelling word means — then check how close you were.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>You'll need:</em> Notebook · Dictionary or teacher's help</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>2 min</strong> — Pick the first word and think about where you've seen or heard it before.</li><li><strong>15 min</strong> — Write your own guessed definition for each word, then check the real meaning.</li><li><strong>3 min</strong> — Share the definition that surprised you most.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Tuesday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning to match a spelling word to its meaning or use in context.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can identify the correct word on my card when a clue or definition is read aloud.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 🎱 Word Bingo Blast</strong> <em>(Team · ~20 min)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Fill a bingo card with this week's words — teacher reads a definition or sentence, you mark the matching word.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>You'll need:</em> Bingo card with words written in a grid · Counters or pencil to mark squares</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>3 min</strong> — Write your spelling words into a 3x3 or 4x4 grid in any order.</li><li><strong>14 min</strong> — Teacher reads a clue for a word — if it's on your card, mark it. First to a full line calls &quot;Bingo!&quot;</li><li><strong>3 min</strong> — Play again with a fresh grid if there's time.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Wednesday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning to reinforce spelling through large-scale kinesthetic movement rather than only pen and paper.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can air-write my word clearly enough for my partner to guess it correctly.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 💫 Air Writing Wiggle</strong> <em>(Pairs · ~20 min)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&quot;Write&quot; a spelling word in the air with your finger, elbow, or nose — your partner has to guess it!</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>You'll need:</em> Just your body — no equipment needed</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>2 min</strong> — Practise writing one letter big and slow in the air.</li><li><strong>15 min</strong> — Take turns air-writing each word while your partner guesses, then check the spelling together.</li><li><strong>3 min</strong> — Try writing your favourite word with your elbow for a giggle.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Thursday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning to deepen our memory of a spelling word by connecting its spelling, its meaning, and its use in a sentence.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can write my word correctly three times, draw a picture that shows its meaning, and write a sentence that matches my picture.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 🛑🔤👃 Stop, Spell, Smell</strong> <em>(Individual · ~20 min)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Choose 5 of this week's words. For each one: write it correctly 3 times, draw a picture showing what it means, then write a sentence using the word that matches the picture.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>You'll need:</em> Worksheet or notebook · Pencil · Colouring pencils/markers</p></div></div>"},"Friday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning to spell this week's words correctly and independently, without support.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can write each dictated word with the correct spelling, unaided.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 📝 Friday Spelling Test</strong> <em>(Individual · teacher-administered)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Teacher reads each word aloud (in a sentence if helpful), student writes it, then the teacher marks each word ✓ correct or ✗ incorrect. Any word marked incorrect automatically reappears on next week's list for review; mastered words are replaced with new words from the Year 5 bank.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&lt;a id=&quot;term-3-week-8&quot;&gt;&lt;/a&gt;</p></div></div>"}}},"Week 8":{"label":"Day","children":{"Week Overview":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Sample student has already mastered the entire Year 5 word bank (completed Week 10, Term 1) — no new sample word list to show. The day structure below still applies for any student still progressing through their own list.</div></div>"},"Monday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning to write spelling words correctly and legibly while moving at pace.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can chalk my word correctly before tagging the next teammate.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 🖍️ Chalk &amp; Talk Relay</strong> <em>(Team · ~20 min)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>An outdoor (or hallway) relay where teams race to chalk out each spelling word correctly.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>You'll need:</em> Chalk (or paper strips indoors) · Open path or hallway</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>3 min</strong> — Split into teams and line up at the start line.</li><li><strong>14 min</strong> — One at a time, race up and chalk the next word correctly before running back to tag the next teammate.</li><li><strong>3 min</strong> — Walk along and check every team's words together.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Tuesday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning to reconstruct the correct letter order of a word as quickly as possible.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can flip and arrange my letter cards into the correct spelling before my partner does.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 🔄 Flip &amp; Spell Showdown</strong> <em>(Pairs · ~20 min)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Each player writes a spelling word's letters onto flip cards, shuffles them face-down, then races to flip them over in the correct order — whoever spells it correctly first wins the point!</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>You'll need:</em> Blank cards or small paper squares — one per letter · A pen each</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>3 min</strong> — Both players write the letters of word 1 onto their own set of cards, then shuffle and lay them face-down.</li><li><strong>14 min</strong> — On &quot;go&quot;, both players flip and rearrange their cards into the correct spelling order. First to spell it correctly wins the point — then reshuffle for the next word.</li><li><strong>3 min</strong> — Total up the points and celebrate the round's champion speller.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Wednesday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning that understanding the individual letters inside a word can help us see spelling patterns within other, smaller words.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can find smaller real words hidden inside a spelling word, using only its letters.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 💰 Letter Loot Challenge</strong> <em>(Team · ~20 min)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Race a 30-second clock to spell as many smaller words as you can using only the letters from one spelling word. Score points for every letter you use — and grab a +2 bonus if you use every single letter!</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>You'll need:</em> Paper &amp; pencil (or call out words aloud) · 30-second timer — built in below!</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>3 min</strong> — Explain the scoring: each word you find scores points equal to how many letters it uses. Use up every letter from the original word across your words to earn a +2 bonus.</li><li><strong>14 min</strong> — Run a 30-second round for each spelling word — hunt for smaller words hiding inside it, then use the score checker below to total your points.</li><li><strong>3 min</strong> — Add up every round's score to crown the class Letter Loot Champion.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Thursday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning to deepen our memory of a spelling word by connecting its spelling, its meaning, and its use in a sentence.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can write my word correctly three times, draw a picture that shows its meaning, and write a sentence that matches my picture.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 🛑🔤👃 Stop, Spell, Smell</strong> <em>(Individual · ~20 min)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Choose 5 of this week's words. For each one: write it correctly 3 times, draw a picture showing what it means, then write a sentence using the word that matches the picture.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>You'll need:</em> Worksheet or notebook · Pencil · Colouring pencils/markers</p></div></div>"},"Friday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning to spell this week's words correctly and independently, without support.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can write each dictated word with the correct spelling, unaided.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 📝 Friday Spelling Test</strong> <em>(Individual · teacher-administered)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Teacher reads each word aloud (in a sentence if helpful), student writes it, then the teacher marks each word ✓ correct or ✗ incorrect. Any word marked incorrect automatically reappears on next week's list for review; mastered words are replaced with new words from the Year 5 bank.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&lt;a id=&quot;term-3-week-9&quot;&gt;&lt;/a&gt;</p></div></div>"}}},"Week 9":{"label":"Day","children":{"Week Overview":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Sample student has already mastered the entire Year 5 word bank (completed Week 10, Term 1) — no new sample word list to show. The day structure below still applies for any student still progressing through their own list.</div></div>"},"Monday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning to recognise the correct letter sequence of our spelling words within a field of distractor letters.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can spot my whole spelling word, in order, hidden across, down, or diagonally.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 🔎 Word Search Hunt</strong> <em>(Individual · ~20 min)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Hunt for this week's spelling words hidden in a letter grid — words can run across, down, or diagonally.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>You'll need:</em> Printed or on-screen word search · Pencil or highlighter</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>2 min</strong> — Look at the word list and read each word aloud before you start hunting.</li><li><strong>15 min</strong> — Circle or highlight every word as you find it in the grid.</li><li><strong>3 min</strong> — Tell a partner which word was trickiest to spot and why.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Tuesday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning to reconstruct a spelling word when its letters are presented out of order.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can unscramble a mixed-up word back into its correct spelling, quickly and confidently.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 🌀 Speedy Scramble Swap</strong> <em>(Pairs · ~20 min)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Partners take turns unscrambling each other's mixed-up spelling words as fast as they can.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>You'll need:</em> Whiteboard or paper · Timer</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>3 min</strong> — Partner A reads out the scrambled letters for word 1 — Partner B unscrambles and spells it aloud.</li><li><strong>12 min</strong> — Take turns through the whole list, swapping roles each word.</li><li><strong>5 min</strong> — Whoever unscrambled the most words correctly picks the next warm-up game!</li></ul></div></div>"},"Wednesday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning to build muscle memory for tricky spelling patterns through repeated, multisensory writing.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can write each word correctly three times, saying each letter as I go.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 🌈 Rainbow Write</strong> <em>(Individual · ~20 min)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write each spelling word three times, using a different colour pencil each time, tracing over the same letters.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>You'll need:</em> 3+ coloured pencils or markers · Plain paper</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>2 min</strong> — Choose your 3 colours for today.</li><li><strong>15 min</strong> — Write each word once in each colour, saying each letter as you write it.</li><li><strong>3 min</strong> — Pick your favourite rainbow word and show a partner.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Thursday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning to deepen our memory of a spelling word by connecting its spelling, its meaning, and its use in a sentence.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can write my word correctly three times, draw a picture that shows its meaning, and write a sentence that matches my picture.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 🛑🔤👃 Stop, Spell, Smell</strong> <em>(Individual · ~20 min)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Choose 5 of this week's words. For each one: write it correctly 3 times, draw a picture showing what it means, then write a sentence using the word that matches the picture.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>You'll need:</em> Worksheet or notebook · Pencil · Colouring pencils/markers</p></div></div>"},"Friday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning to spell this week's words correctly and independently, without support.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can write each dictated word with the correct spelling, unaided.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 📝 Friday Spelling Test</strong> <em>(Individual · teacher-administered)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Teacher reads each word aloud (in a sentence if helpful), student writes it, then the teacher marks each word ✓ correct or ✗ incorrect. Any word marked incorrect automatically reappears on next week's list for review; mastered words are replaced with new words from the Year 5 bank.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&lt;a id=&quot;term-3-week-10&quot;&gt;&lt;/a&gt;</p></div></div>"}}},"Week 10":{"label":"Day","children":{"Week Overview":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Sample student has already mastered the entire Year 5 word bank (completed Week 10, Term 1) — no new sample word list to show. The day structure below still applies for any student still progressing through their own list.</div></div>"},"Monday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning to build up a word sound-by-sound and letter-by-letter, strengthening our sense of letter order.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can build a correct letter pyramid for every word on my list.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 🔺 Pyramid Words</strong> <em>(Individual · ~20 min)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Build each spelling word one letter at a time, like a growing staircase, until the whole word is spelled out.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>You'll need:</em> Paper · Pencil</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>2 min</strong> — Teacher shows an example pyramid on the board (e.g. b / be / bec / beca...).</li><li><strong>15 min</strong> — Build a pyramid for every word on your list.</li><li><strong>3 min</strong> — Trade books with a neighbour and check each other's pyramids.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Tuesday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning to spell words aloud fluently and automatically, one letter at a time, under light time pressure.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can say my share of the letters correctly without hesitating, and say the whole word at the end.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 🎾 Word Tennis</strong> <em>(Pairs · ~20 min)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Partners &quot;volley&quot; a word back and forth, each saying the next letter — miss a letter and you lose the point!</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>You'll need:</em> Just your voices!</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>2 min</strong> — Decide who serves first for word 1.</li><li><strong>15 min</strong> — Rally through the whole list — one letter each, back and forth, then say the whole word together at the end.</li><li><strong>3 min</strong> — Total up the points and celebrate the winner.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Wednesday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning to recall and say a spelling word correctly from memory, under a small amount of performance pressure.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can spell my word aloud, letter by letter, without looking at the list.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 🎤 Spelling Bee Countdown</strong> <em>(Team · ~20 min)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Teams take turns spelling words aloud — get it right and stay in, get it wrong and sit down. Last team standing wins!</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>You'll need:</em> Word list (teacher calls words) · Nothing else needed</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>3 min</strong> — Split into teams of 3–4 and line up.</li><li><strong>14 min</strong> — Teacher calls a word, first player in line spells it aloud. Correct = go to the back of the line. Wrong = sit down.</li><li><strong>3 min</strong> — Last team with players standing wins the round!</li></ul></div></div>"},"Thursday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning to deepen our memory of a spelling word by connecting its spelling, its meaning, and its use in a sentence.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can write my word correctly three times, draw a picture that shows its meaning, and write a sentence that matches my picture.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 🛑🔤👃 Stop, Spell, Smell</strong> <em>(Individual · ~20 min)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Choose 5 of this week's words. For each one: write it correctly 3 times, draw a picture showing what it means, then write a sentence using the word that matches the picture.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>You'll need:</em> Worksheet or notebook · Pencil · Colouring pencils/markers</p></div></div>"},"Friday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning to spell this week's words correctly and independently, without support.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can write each dictated word with the correct spelling, unaided.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 📝 Friday Spelling Test</strong> <em>(Individual · teacher-administered)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Teacher reads each word aloud (in a sentence if helpful), student writes it, then the teacher marks each word ✓ correct or ✗ incorrect. Any word marked incorrect automatically reappears on next week's list for review; mastered words are replaced with new words from the Year 5 bank.</p></div></div>"}}}}},"Term 4":{"label":"Week","children":{"Week 1":{"label":"Day","children":{"Week Overview":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Sample student has already mastered the entire Year 5 word bank (completed Week 10, Term 1) — no new sample word list to show. The day structure below still applies for any student still progressing through their own list.</div></div>"},"Monday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning to write a spelling word correctly and legibly at speed.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can run up, write my word correctly first try, and tag my teammate.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 🌍 Around the World Relay</strong> <em>(Team · ~20 min)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>A running relay — race to the board, write the next word correctly, then tag your teammate.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>You'll need:</em> Whiteboard/chalkboard or paper on a wall · Markers</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>3 min</strong> — Split into even teams and line up single-file.</li><li><strong>14 min</strong> — On &quot;go&quot;, the first runner races up, writes word 1 correctly, runs back and tags the next player who writes word 2, and so on.</li><li><strong>3 min</strong> — Check every team's words together — first fully-correct list wins.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Tuesday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning to use knowledge of spelling patterns to predict letters that are missing from a word.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can fill every gap in a word with the correct missing letter(s).</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 🕳️ Mystery Missing Letters</strong> <em>(Individual · ~20 min)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Some letters have vanished from each spelling word — figure out which ones and fill in the gaps.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>You'll need:</em> Worksheet or notebook · Pencil</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>2 min</strong> — Look at the first mystery word and predict the missing letters.</li><li><strong>15 min</strong> — Fill in every missing letter for all the words on your list.</li><li><strong>3 min</strong> — Swap with a partner and check each other's answers.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Wednesday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning to spell a word correctly when we hear it inside a full sentence, rather than in isolation.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can listen for my word inside a sentence and write it with the correct spelling.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: ✍️ Partner Dictation Challenge</strong> <em>(Pairs · ~20 min)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>One partner reads a word inside a sentence, the other writes it down — then swap and check together.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>You'll need:</em> Paper · Pencil</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>2 min</strong> — Decide who dictates first.</li><li><strong>14 min</strong> — Dictator says a sentence containing the spelling word; writer writes just the word. Swap roles halfway through the list.</li><li><strong>4 min</strong> — Compare your writing to the real spelling and fix any mistakes together.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Thursday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning to deepen our memory of a spelling word by connecting its spelling, its meaning, and its use in a sentence.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can write my word correctly three times, draw a picture that shows its meaning, and write a sentence that matches my picture.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 🛑🔤👃 Stop, Spell, Smell</strong> <em>(Individual · ~20 min)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Choose 5 of this week's words. For each one: write it correctly 3 times, draw a picture showing what it means, then write a sentence using the word that matches the picture.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>You'll need:</em> Worksheet or notebook · Pencil · Colouring pencils/markers</p></div></div>"},"Friday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning to spell this week's words correctly and independently, without support.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can write each dictated word with the correct spelling, unaided.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 📝 Friday Spelling Test</strong> <em>(Individual · teacher-administered)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Teacher reads each word aloud (in a sentence if helpful), student writes it, then the teacher marks each word ✓ correct or ✗ incorrect. Any word marked incorrect automatically reappears on next week's list for review; mastered words are replaced with new words from the Year 5 bank.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&lt;a id=&quot;term-4-week-2&quot;&gt;&lt;/a&gt;</p></div></div>"}}},"Week 2":{"label":"Day","children":{"Week Overview":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Sample student has already mastered the entire Year 5 word bank (completed Week 10, Term 1) — no new sample word list to show. The day structure below still applies for any student still progressing through their own list.</div></div>"},"Monday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning to break spelling words into syllables to help us spell longer or trickier words.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can clap and count the syllables in each word and agree on the total with my partner.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 👏 Syllable Clap Count</strong> <em>(Pairs · ~20 min)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Clap out the syllables in each spelling word, then work out how many &quot;chunks&quot; it breaks into.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>You'll need:</em> Just your hands!</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>3 min</strong> — Warm up by clapping your own name.</li><li><strong>14 min</strong> — Take turns saying and clapping each spelling word, then agree on the syllable count and write it down.</li><li><strong>3 min</strong> — Find the word on your list with the most syllables — can you beat it next week?</li></ul></div></div>"},"Tuesday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning to notice patterns across our spelling words (e.g. syllables, tricky letter strings, silent letters) and use them to group words.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can sort every word into a group using a spelling pattern I chose myself.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 🗂️ Word Sort Categories</strong> <em>(Pairs · ~20 min)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Sort this week's words into your own categories — by syllables, tricky letter patterns, or silent letters.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>You'll need:</em> Small cards or sticky notes with words written on them · Table or floor space</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>3 min</strong> — Write each word onto its own card.</li><li><strong>14 min</strong> — Choose a sorting rule together (e.g. number of syllables) and sort every word into groups.</li><li><strong>3 min</strong> — Explain your sorting rule to another pair.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Wednesday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning to reinforce the correct letter sequence of a word through a creative, visual activity.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can draw and colour each word so that every letter is in the correct order.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 🎨 Bubble Letter Word Art</strong> <em>(Individual · ~20 min)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Draw each spelling word in big bubble or graffiti letters and decorate it.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>You'll need:</em> Plain paper · Pencil, then colouring pencils/markers</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>2 min</strong> — Look at an example bubble-letter word on the board.</li><li><strong>15 min</strong> — Draw and colour bubble letters for as many words as you can.</li><li><strong>3 min</strong> — Show off your favourite word-art creation to the class.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Thursday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning to deepen our memory of a spelling word by connecting its spelling, its meaning, and its use in a sentence.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can write my word correctly three times, draw a picture that shows its meaning, and write a sentence that matches my picture.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 🛑🔤👃 Stop, Spell, Smell</strong> <em>(Individual · ~20 min)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Choose 5 of this week's words. For each one: write it correctly 3 times, draw a picture showing what it means, then write a sentence using the word that matches the picture.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>You'll need:</em> Worksheet or notebook · Pencil · Colouring pencils/markers</p></div></div>"},"Friday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning to spell this week's words correctly and independently, without support.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can write each dictated word with the correct spelling, unaided.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 📝 Friday Spelling Test</strong> <em>(Individual · teacher-administered)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Teacher reads each word aloud (in a sentence if helpful), student writes it, then the teacher marks each word ✓ correct or ✗ incorrect. Any word marked incorrect automatically reappears on next week's list for review; mastered words are replaced with new words from the Year 5 bank.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&lt;a id=&quot;term-4-week-3&quot;&gt;&lt;/a&gt;</p></div></div>"}}},"Week 3":{"label":"Day","children":{"Week Overview":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Sample student has already mastered the entire Year 5 word bank (completed Week 10, Term 1) — no new sample word list to show. The day structure below still applies for any student still progressing through their own list.</div></div>"},"Monday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning to recall spelling words correctly on demand, in a low-pressure game format.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can spell the word correctly before I take my turn to move.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 🐍 Spelling Snakes &amp; Ladders</strong> <em>(Team · ~20 min)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Play snakes and ladders — but you can only move if you spell a word correctly first!</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>You'll need:</em> Snakes &amp; ladders board (drawn or printed) · Dice · Counters</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>2 min</strong> — Set up the board and counters in groups of 3–4.</li><li><strong>15 min</strong> — Roll the dice — before moving, spell the next word on your list aloud. Get it right to move, wrong to stay put.</li><li><strong>3 min</strong> — First to reach the end (or whoever's furthest when time is up) wins.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Tuesday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning to use our spelling words correctly and meaningfully within a sentence of our own.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can add a sentence to our class story that uses my spelling word correctly.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 📖 Silly Sentence Story Chain</strong> <em>(Team · ~20 min)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Build one giant silly class story together, where each sentence must use the next spelling word.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>You'll need:</em> Paper or shared doc to record the story</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>3 min</strong> — Sit in a circle with your team and decide on a story character.</li><li><strong>14 min</strong> — Take turns adding one sentence to the story, each one using a spelling word correctly.</li><li><strong>3 min</strong> — Read your finished silly story aloud to another team.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Wednesday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning to recall and say a spelling word correctly from memory in a fun, active game.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can spell my word aloud correctly when I catch the beanbag.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 🎯 Beanbag Toss Spelling</strong> <em>(Team · ~20 min)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Toss a beanbag or soft ball — whoever catches it has to spell the next word out loud.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>You'll need:</em> Beanbag or soft ball · Open space</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>2 min</strong> — Stand in a circle with your team.</li><li><strong>15 min</strong> — Toss the beanbag — catcher spells the next word aloud. Correct = toss again for the next word; incorrect = get a hint and try once more.</li><li><strong>3 min</strong> — Celebrate every word your team got through together.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Thursday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning to deepen our memory of a spelling word by connecting its spelling, its meaning, and its use in a sentence.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can write my word correctly three times, draw a picture that shows its meaning, and write a sentence that matches my picture.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 🛑🔤👃 Stop, Spell, Smell</strong> <em>(Individual · ~20 min)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Choose 5 of this week's words. For each one: write it correctly 3 times, draw a picture showing what it means, then write a sentence using the word that matches the picture.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>You'll need:</em> Worksheet or notebook · Pencil · Colouring pencils/markers</p></div></div>"},"Friday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning to spell this week's words correctly and independently, without support.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can write each dictated word with the correct spelling, unaided.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 📝 Friday Spelling Test</strong> <em>(Individual · teacher-administered)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Teacher reads each word aloud (in a sentence if helpful), student writes it, then the teacher marks each word ✓ correct or ✗ incorrect. Any word marked incorrect automatically reappears on next week's list for review; mastered words are replaced with new words from the Year 5 bank.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&lt;a id=&quot;term-4-week-4&quot;&gt;&lt;/a&gt;</p></div></div>"}}},"Week 4":{"label":"Day","children":{"Week Overview":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Sample student has already mastered the entire Year 5 word bank (completed Week 10, Term 1) — no new sample word list to show. The day structure below still applies for any student still progressing through their own list.</div></div>"},"Monday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning that understanding what a word means helps us remember how to spell it.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can guess a sensible definition for each word, then check it against the real meaning.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 🕵️ Definition Detective</strong> <em>(Individual · ~20 min)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Before checking a dictionary, guess what each spelling word means — then check how close you were.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>You'll need:</em> Notebook · Dictionary or teacher's help</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>2 min</strong> — Pick the first word and think about where you've seen or heard it before.</li><li><strong>15 min</strong> — Write your own guessed definition for each word, then check the real meaning.</li><li><strong>3 min</strong> — Share the definition that surprised you most.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Tuesday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning to match a spelling word to its meaning or use in context.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can identify the correct word on my card when a clue or definition is read aloud.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 🎱 Word Bingo Blast</strong> <em>(Team · ~20 min)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Fill a bingo card with this week's words — teacher reads a definition or sentence, you mark the matching word.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>You'll need:</em> Bingo card with words written in a grid · Counters or pencil to mark squares</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>3 min</strong> — Write your spelling words into a 3x3 or 4x4 grid in any order.</li><li><strong>14 min</strong> — Teacher reads a clue for a word — if it's on your card, mark it. First to a full line calls &quot;Bingo!&quot;</li><li><strong>3 min</strong> — Play again with a fresh grid if there's time.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Wednesday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning to reinforce spelling through large-scale kinesthetic movement rather than only pen and paper.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can air-write my word clearly enough for my partner to guess it correctly.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 💫 Air Writing Wiggle</strong> <em>(Pairs · ~20 min)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&quot;Write&quot; a spelling word in the air with your finger, elbow, or nose — your partner has to guess it!</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>You'll need:</em> Just your body — no equipment needed</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>2 min</strong> — Practise writing one letter big and slow in the air.</li><li><strong>15 min</strong> — Take turns air-writing each word while your partner guesses, then check the spelling together.</li><li><strong>3 min</strong> — Try writing your favourite word with your elbow for a giggle.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Thursday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning to deepen our memory of a spelling word by connecting its spelling, its meaning, and its use in a sentence.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can write my word correctly three times, draw a picture that shows its meaning, and write a sentence that matches my picture.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 🛑🔤👃 Stop, Spell, Smell</strong> <em>(Individual · ~20 min)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Choose 5 of this week's words. For each one: write it correctly 3 times, draw a picture showing what it means, then write a sentence using the word that matches the picture.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>You'll need:</em> Worksheet or notebook · Pencil · Colouring pencils/markers</p></div></div>"},"Friday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning to spell this week's words correctly and independently, without support.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can write each dictated word with the correct spelling, unaided.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 📝 Friday Spelling Test</strong> <em>(Individual · teacher-administered)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Teacher reads each word aloud (in a sentence if helpful), student writes it, then the teacher marks each word ✓ correct or ✗ incorrect. Any word marked incorrect automatically reappears on next week's list for review; mastered words are replaced with new words from the Year 5 bank.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&lt;a id=&quot;term-4-week-5&quot;&gt;&lt;/a&gt;</p></div></div>"}}},"Week 5":{"label":"Day","children":{"Week Overview":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Sample student has already mastered the entire Year 5 word bank (completed Week 10, Term 1) — no new sample word list to show. The day structure below still applies for any student still progressing through their own list.</div></div>"},"Monday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning to write spelling words correctly and legibly while moving at pace.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can chalk my word correctly before tagging the next teammate.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 🖍️ Chalk &amp; Talk Relay</strong> <em>(Team · ~20 min)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>An outdoor (or hallway) relay where teams race to chalk out each spelling word correctly.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>You'll need:</em> Chalk (or paper strips indoors) · Open path or hallway</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>3 min</strong> — Split into teams and line up at the start line.</li><li><strong>14 min</strong> — One at a time, race up and chalk the next word correctly before running back to tag the next teammate.</li><li><strong>3 min</strong> — Walk along and check every team's words together.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Tuesday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning to reconstruct the correct letter order of a word as quickly as possible.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can flip and arrange my letter cards into the correct spelling before my partner does.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 🔄 Flip &amp; Spell Showdown</strong> <em>(Pairs · ~20 min)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Each player writes a spelling word's letters onto flip cards, shuffles them face-down, then races to flip them over in the correct order — whoever spells it correctly first wins the point!</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>You'll need:</em> Blank cards or small paper squares — one per letter · A pen each</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>3 min</strong> — Both players write the letters of word 1 onto their own set of cards, then shuffle and lay them face-down.</li><li><strong>14 min</strong> — On &quot;go&quot;, both players flip and rearrange their cards into the correct spelling order. First to spell it correctly wins the point — then reshuffle for the next word.</li><li><strong>3 min</strong> — Total up the points and celebrate the round's champion speller.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Wednesday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning that understanding the individual letters inside a word can help us see spelling patterns within other, smaller words.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can find smaller real words hidden inside a spelling word, using only its letters.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 💰 Letter Loot Challenge</strong> <em>(Team · ~20 min)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Race a 30-second clock to spell as many smaller words as you can using only the letters from one spelling word. Score points for every letter you use — and grab a +2 bonus if you use every single letter!</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>You'll need:</em> Paper &amp; pencil (or call out words aloud) · 30-second timer — built in below!</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>3 min</strong> — Explain the scoring: each word you find scores points equal to how many letters it uses. Use up every letter from the original word across your words to earn a +2 bonus.</li><li><strong>14 min</strong> — Run a 30-second round for each spelling word — hunt for smaller words hiding inside it, then use the score checker below to total your points.</li><li><strong>3 min</strong> — Add up every round's score to crown the class Letter Loot Champion.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Thursday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning to deepen our memory of a spelling word by connecting its spelling, its meaning, and its use in a sentence.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can write my word correctly three times, draw a picture that shows its meaning, and write a sentence that matches my picture.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 🛑🔤👃 Stop, Spell, Smell</strong> <em>(Individual · ~20 min)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Choose 5 of this week's words. For each one: write it correctly 3 times, draw a picture showing what it means, then write a sentence using the word that matches the picture.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>You'll need:</em> Worksheet or notebook · Pencil · Colouring pencils/markers</p></div></div>"},"Friday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning to spell this week's words correctly and independently, without support.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can write each dictated word with the correct spelling, unaided.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 📝 Friday Spelling Test</strong> <em>(Individual · teacher-administered)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Teacher reads each word aloud (in a sentence if helpful), student writes it, then the teacher marks each word ✓ correct or ✗ incorrect. Any word marked incorrect automatically reappears on next week's list for review; mastered words are replaced with new words from the Year 5 bank.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&lt;a id=&quot;term-4-week-6&quot;&gt;&lt;/a&gt;</p></div></div>"}}},"Week 6":{"label":"Day","children":{"Week Overview":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Sample student has already mastered the entire Year 5 word bank (completed Week 10, Term 1) — no new sample word list to show. The day structure below still applies for any student still progressing through their own list.</div></div>"},"Monday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning to recognise the correct letter sequence of our spelling words within a field of distractor letters.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can spot my whole spelling word, in order, hidden across, down, or diagonally.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 🔎 Word Search Hunt</strong> <em>(Individual · ~20 min)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Hunt for this week's spelling words hidden in a letter grid — words can run across, down, or diagonally.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>You'll need:</em> Printed or on-screen word search · Pencil or highlighter</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>2 min</strong> — Look at the word list and read each word aloud before you start hunting.</li><li><strong>15 min</strong> — Circle or highlight every word as you find it in the grid.</li><li><strong>3 min</strong> — Tell a partner which word was trickiest to spot and why.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Tuesday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning to reconstruct a spelling word when its letters are presented out of order.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can unscramble a mixed-up word back into its correct spelling, quickly and confidently.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 🌀 Speedy Scramble Swap</strong> <em>(Pairs · ~20 min)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Partners take turns unscrambling each other's mixed-up spelling words as fast as they can.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>You'll need:</em> Whiteboard or paper · Timer</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>3 min</strong> — Partner A reads out the scrambled letters for word 1 — Partner B unscrambles and spells it aloud.</li><li><strong>12 min</strong> — Take turns through the whole list, swapping roles each word.</li><li><strong>5 min</strong> — Whoever unscrambled the most words correctly picks the next warm-up game!</li></ul></div></div>"},"Wednesday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning to build muscle memory for tricky spelling patterns through repeated, multisensory writing.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can write each word correctly three times, saying each letter as I go.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 🌈 Rainbow Write</strong> <em>(Individual · ~20 min)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write each spelling word three times, using a different colour pencil each time, tracing over the same letters.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>You'll need:</em> 3+ coloured pencils or markers · Plain paper</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>2 min</strong> — Choose your 3 colours for today.</li><li><strong>15 min</strong> — Write each word once in each colour, saying each letter as you write it.</li><li><strong>3 min</strong> — Pick your favourite rainbow word and show a partner.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Thursday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning to deepen our memory of a spelling word by connecting its spelling, its meaning, and its use in a sentence.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can write my word correctly three times, draw a picture that shows its meaning, and write a sentence that matches my picture.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 🛑🔤👃 Stop, Spell, Smell</strong> <em>(Individual · ~20 min)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Choose 5 of this week's words. For each one: write it correctly 3 times, draw a picture showing what it means, then write a sentence using the word that matches the picture.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>You'll need:</em> Worksheet or notebook · Pencil · Colouring pencils/markers</p></div></div>"},"Friday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning to spell this week's words correctly and independently, without support.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can write each dictated word with the correct spelling, unaided.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 📝 Friday Spelling Test</strong> <em>(Individual · teacher-administered)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Teacher reads each word aloud (in a sentence if helpful), student writes it, then the teacher marks each word ✓ correct or ✗ incorrect. Any word marked incorrect automatically reappears on next week's list for review; mastered words are replaced with new words from the Year 5 bank.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&lt;a id=&quot;term-4-week-7&quot;&gt;&lt;/a&gt;</p></div></div>"}}},"Week 7":{"label":"Day","children":{"Week Overview":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Sample student has already mastered the entire Year 5 word bank (completed Week 10, Term 1) — no new sample word list to show. The day structure below still applies for any student still progressing through their own list.</div></div>"},"Monday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning to build up a word sound-by-sound and letter-by-letter, strengthening our sense of letter order.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can build a correct letter pyramid for every word on my list.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 🔺 Pyramid Words</strong> <em>(Individual · ~20 min)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Build each spelling word one letter at a time, like a growing staircase, until the whole word is spelled out.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>You'll need:</em> Paper · Pencil</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>2 min</strong> — Teacher shows an example pyramid on the board (e.g. b / be / bec / beca...).</li><li><strong>15 min</strong> — Build a pyramid for every word on your list.</li><li><strong>3 min</strong> — Trade books with a neighbour and check each other's pyramids.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Tuesday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning to spell words aloud fluently and automatically, one letter at a time, under light time pressure.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can say my share of the letters correctly without hesitating, and say the whole word at the end.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 🎾 Word Tennis</strong> <em>(Pairs · ~20 min)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Partners &quot;volley&quot; a word back and forth, each saying the next letter — miss a letter and you lose the point!</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>You'll need:</em> Just your voices!</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>2 min</strong> — Decide who serves first for word 1.</li><li><strong>15 min</strong> — Rally through the whole list — one letter each, back and forth, then say the whole word together at the end.</li><li><strong>3 min</strong> — Total up the points and celebrate the winner.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Wednesday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning to recall and say a spelling word correctly from memory, under a small amount of performance pressure.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can spell my word aloud, letter by letter, without looking at the list.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 🎤 Spelling Bee Countdown</strong> <em>(Team · ~20 min)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Teams take turns spelling words aloud — get it right and stay in, get it wrong and sit down. Last team standing wins!</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>You'll need:</em> Word list (teacher calls words) · Nothing else needed</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>3 min</strong> — Split into teams of 3–4 and line up.</li><li><strong>14 min</strong> — Teacher calls a word, first player in line spells it aloud. Correct = go to the back of the line. Wrong = sit down.</li><li><strong>3 min</strong> — Last team with players standing wins the round!</li></ul></div></div>"},"Thursday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning to deepen our memory of a spelling word by connecting its spelling, its meaning, and its use in a sentence.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can write my word correctly three times, draw a picture that shows its meaning, and write a sentence that matches my picture.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 🛑🔤👃 Stop, Spell, Smell</strong> <em>(Individual · ~20 min)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Choose 5 of this week's words. For each one: write it correctly 3 times, draw a picture showing what it means, then write a sentence using the word that matches the picture.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>You'll need:</em> Worksheet or notebook · Pencil · Colouring pencils/markers</p></div></div>"},"Friday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning to spell this week's words correctly and independently, without support.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can write each dictated word with the correct spelling, unaided.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 📝 Friday Spelling Test</strong> <em>(Individual · teacher-administered)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Teacher reads each word aloud (in a sentence if helpful), student writes it, then the teacher marks each word ✓ correct or ✗ incorrect. Any word marked incorrect automatically reappears on next week's list for review; mastered words are replaced with new words from the Year 5 bank.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&lt;a id=&quot;term-4-week-8&quot;&gt;&lt;/a&gt;</p></div></div>"}}},"Week 8":{"label":"Day","children":{"Week Overview":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Sample student has already mastered the entire Year 5 word bank (completed Week 10, Term 1) — no new sample word list to show. The day structure below still applies for any student still progressing through their own list.</div></div>"},"Monday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning to write a spelling word correctly and legibly at speed.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can run up, write my word correctly first try, and tag my teammate.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 🌍 Around the World Relay</strong> <em>(Team · ~20 min)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>A running relay — race to the board, write the next word correctly, then tag your teammate.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>You'll need:</em> Whiteboard/chalkboard or paper on a wall · Markers</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>3 min</strong> — Split into even teams and line up single-file.</li><li><strong>14 min</strong> — On &quot;go&quot;, the first runner races up, writes word 1 correctly, runs back and tags the next player who writes word 2, and so on.</li><li><strong>3 min</strong> — Check every team's words together — first fully-correct list wins.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Tuesday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning to use knowledge of spelling patterns to predict letters that are missing from a word.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can fill every gap in a word with the correct missing letter(s).</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 🕳️ Mystery Missing Letters</strong> <em>(Individual · ~20 min)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Some letters have vanished from each spelling word — figure out which ones and fill in the gaps.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>You'll need:</em> Worksheet or notebook · Pencil</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>2 min</strong> — Look at the first mystery word and predict the missing letters.</li><li><strong>15 min</strong> — Fill in every missing letter for all the words on your list.</li><li><strong>3 min</strong> — Swap with a partner and check each other's answers.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Wednesday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning to spell a word correctly when we hear it inside a full sentence, rather than in isolation.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can listen for my word inside a sentence and write it with the correct spelling.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: ✍️ Partner Dictation Challenge</strong> <em>(Pairs · ~20 min)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>One partner reads a word inside a sentence, the other writes it down — then swap and check together.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>You'll need:</em> Paper · Pencil</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>2 min</strong> — Decide who dictates first.</li><li><strong>14 min</strong> — Dictator says a sentence containing the spelling word; writer writes just the word. Swap roles halfway through the list.</li><li><strong>4 min</strong> — Compare your writing to the real spelling and fix any mistakes together.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Thursday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning to deepen our memory of a spelling word by connecting its spelling, its meaning, and its use in a sentence.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can write my word correctly three times, draw a picture that shows its meaning, and write a sentence that matches my picture.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 🛑🔤👃 Stop, Spell, Smell</strong> <em>(Individual · ~20 min)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Choose 5 of this week's words. For each one: write it correctly 3 times, draw a picture showing what it means, then write a sentence using the word that matches the picture.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>You'll need:</em> Worksheet or notebook · Pencil · Colouring pencils/markers</p></div></div>"},"Friday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning to spell this week's words correctly and independently, without support.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can write each dictated word with the correct spelling, unaided.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 📝 Friday Spelling Test</strong> <em>(Individual · teacher-administered)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Teacher reads each word aloud (in a sentence if helpful), student writes it, then the teacher marks each word ✓ correct or ✗ incorrect. Any word marked incorrect automatically reappears on next week's list for review; mastered words are replaced with new words from the Year 5 bank.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&lt;a id=&quot;term-4-week-9&quot;&gt;&lt;/a&gt;</p></div></div>"}}},"Week 9":{"label":"Day","children":{"Week Overview":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Sample student has already mastered the entire Year 5 word bank (completed Week 10, Term 1) — no new sample word list to show. The day structure below still applies for any student still progressing through their own list.</div></div>"},"Monday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning to break spelling words into syllables to help us spell longer or trickier words.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can clap and count the syllables in each word and agree on the total with my partner.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 👏 Syllable Clap Count</strong> <em>(Pairs · ~20 min)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Clap out the syllables in each spelling word, then work out how many &quot;chunks&quot; it breaks into.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>You'll need:</em> Just your hands!</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>3 min</strong> — Warm up by clapping your own name.</li><li><strong>14 min</strong> — Take turns saying and clapping each spelling word, then agree on the syllable count and write it down.</li><li><strong>3 min</strong> — Find the word on your list with the most syllables — can you beat it next week?</li></ul></div></div>"},"Tuesday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning to notice patterns across our spelling words (e.g. syllables, tricky letter strings, silent letters) and use them to group words.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can sort every word into a group using a spelling pattern I chose myself.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 🗂️ Word Sort Categories</strong> <em>(Pairs · ~20 min)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Sort this week's words into your own categories — by syllables, tricky letter patterns, or silent letters.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>You'll need:</em> Small cards or sticky notes with words written on them · Table or floor space</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>3 min</strong> — Write each word onto its own card.</li><li><strong>14 min</strong> — Choose a sorting rule together (e.g. number of syllables) and sort every word into groups.</li><li><strong>3 min</strong> — Explain your sorting rule to another pair.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Wednesday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning to reinforce the correct letter sequence of a word through a creative, visual activity.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can draw and colour each word so that every letter is in the correct order.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 🎨 Bubble Letter Word Art</strong> <em>(Individual · ~20 min)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Draw each spelling word in big bubble or graffiti letters and decorate it.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>You'll need:</em> Plain paper · Pencil, then colouring pencils/markers</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>2 min</strong> — Look at an example bubble-letter word on the board.</li><li><strong>15 min</strong> — Draw and colour bubble letters for as many words as you can.</li><li><strong>3 min</strong> — Show off your favourite word-art creation to the class.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Thursday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning to deepen our memory of a spelling word by connecting its spelling, its meaning, and its use in a sentence.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can write my word correctly three times, draw a picture that shows its meaning, and write a sentence that matches my picture.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 🛑🔤👃 Stop, Spell, Smell</strong> <em>(Individual · ~20 min)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Choose 5 of this week's words. For each one: write it correctly 3 times, draw a picture showing what it means, then write a sentence using the word that matches the picture.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>You'll need:</em> Worksheet or notebook · Pencil · Colouring pencils/markers</p></div></div>"},"Friday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning to spell this week's words correctly and independently, without support.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can write each dictated word with the correct spelling, unaided.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 📝 Friday Spelling Test</strong> <em>(Individual · teacher-administered)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Teacher reads each word aloud (in a sentence if helpful), student writes it, then the teacher marks each word ✓ correct or ✗ incorrect. Any word marked incorrect automatically reappears on next week's list for review; mastered words are replaced with new words from the Year 5 bank.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&lt;a id=&quot;term-4-week-10&quot;&gt;&lt;/a&gt;</p></div></div>"}}},"Week 10":{"label":"Day","children":{"Week Overview":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Sample student has already mastered the entire Year 5 word bank (completed Week 10, Term 1) — no new sample word list to show. The day structure below still applies for any student still progressing through their own list.</div></div>"},"Monday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning to recall spelling words correctly on demand, in a low-pressure game format.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can spell the word correctly before I take my turn to move.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 🐍 Spelling Snakes &amp; Ladders</strong> <em>(Team · ~20 min)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Play snakes and ladders — but you can only move if you spell a word correctly first!</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>You'll need:</em> Snakes &amp; ladders board (drawn or printed) · Dice · Counters</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>2 min</strong> — Set up the board and counters in groups of 3–4.</li><li><strong>15 min</strong> — Roll the dice — before moving, spell the next word on your list aloud. Get it right to move, wrong to stay put.</li><li><strong>3 min</strong> — First to reach the end (or whoever's furthest when time is up) wins.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Tuesday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning to use our spelling words correctly and meaningfully within a sentence of our own.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can add a sentence to our class story that uses my spelling word correctly.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 📖 Silly Sentence Story Chain</strong> <em>(Team · ~20 min)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Build one giant silly class story together, where each sentence must use the next spelling word.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>You'll need:</em> Paper or shared doc to record the story</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>3 min</strong> — Sit in a circle with your team and decide on a story character.</li><li><strong>14 min</strong> — Take turns adding one sentence to the story, each one using a spelling word correctly.</li><li><strong>3 min</strong> — Read your finished silly story aloud to another team.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Wednesday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning to recall and say a spelling word correctly from memory in a fun, active game.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can spell my word aloud correctly when I catch the beanbag.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 🎯 Beanbag Toss Spelling</strong> <em>(Team · ~20 min)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Toss a beanbag or soft ball — whoever catches it has to spell the next word out loud.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>You'll need:</em> Beanbag or soft ball · Open space</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>2 min</strong> — Stand in a circle with your team.</li><li><strong>15 min</strong> — Toss the beanbag — catcher spells the next word aloud. Correct = toss again for the next word; incorrect = get a hint and try once more.</li><li><strong>3 min</strong> — Celebrate every word your team got through together.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Thursday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning to deepen our memory of a spelling word by connecting its spelling, its meaning, and its use in a sentence.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can write my word correctly three times, draw a picture that shows its meaning, and write a sentence that matches my picture.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 🛑🔤👃 Stop, Spell, Smell</strong> <em>(Individual · ~20 min)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Choose 5 of this week's words. For each one: write it correctly 3 times, draw a picture showing what it means, then write a sentence using the word that matches the picture.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>You'll need:</em> Worksheet or notebook · Pencil · Colouring pencils/markers</p></div></div>"},"Friday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning to spell this week's words correctly and independently, without support.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can write each dictated word with the correct spelling, unaided.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 📝 Friday Spelling Test</strong> <em>(Individual · teacher-administered)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Teacher reads each word aloud (in a sentence if helpful), student writes it, then the teacher marks each word ✓ correct or ✗ incorrect. Any word marked incorrect automatically reappears on next week's list for review; mastered words are replaced with new words from the Year 5 bank.</p></div></div>"}}}}}}},"tereo":{"label":"Term","children":{"Term 1":{"label":"Week","children":{"Week 1":{"label":"Day","children":{"Monday: Ngā mihi me ngā kupu hou":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Te Aho Arataki Marau — AO 1.1: Greet, farewell and acknowledge people · AO 1.6: Politeness conventions</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Objectives</th><td class=\"lt-td\">AO 1.1 — Greetings &amp; farewell; AO 1.6 — Politeness conventions</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">I Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tāua Kōrero — Listen &amp; discover</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Today we are going to learn our very first words in te reo Māori. I am going to say some words and phrases, and I want you to just listen and notice the sounds. There is nothing to write down — just watch and listen.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\"><strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=kia%20ora\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Kia ora</a></strong> — Hello / Thank you<br><strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=t%C4%93n%C4%81%20koe\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Tēnā koe</a></strong> — Hello (formal, to one person)<br><strong>Kei te <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=p%C4%93hea\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">pēhea</a> koe?</strong> — How are you?<br><strong>Kei te <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=pai\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">pai</a> au</strong> — I am good<br><strong>E <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=noho\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">noho</a> rā</strong> — Goodbye (to someone staying)<br><strong>Hei <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=kon%C4%81\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">konā</a> rā</strong> — Goodbye (casual)</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Notice the vowels. In te reo Māori, every vowel makes only ONE sound — always:</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>A</strong> = &quot;ah&quot;  ·  <strong>E</strong> = &quot;eh&quot;  ·  <strong>I</strong> = &quot;ee&quot;  ·  <strong>O</strong> = &quot;oh&quot;  ·  <strong>U</strong> = &quot;oo&quot;</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Say &quot;Kia ora&quot; — KIA = &quot;kee-ah&quot;, ORA = &quot;oh-rah&quot;. Kia ora. Simple, consistent, beautiful.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Tip: The vowel sounds never change. Once you know them, you can pronounce any Māori word. That's a superpower.</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">We Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Kia Tūhono — Build together</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Let's practise these greetings together. I will say the Māori — you say it back. Then we will figure out when you would use each one.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>Kia ora</strong> — When would you say this? (casual hello or thank you)</li><li><strong>Tēnā koe</strong> — This is more formal. When might a student use it? (to a teacher, elder, or visitor)</li><li><strong>Kei te pēhea koe?</strong> — Break it apart: Kei te = right now, pēhea = how, koe = you. Put it together!</li><li><strong>Kei te pai au</strong> — kei te = right now, pai = good, au = I/me. Say it: &quot;Right now good I.&quot;</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Discussion prompt: Which greeting would you use for your best friend? Which for the school principal? Why does te reo have different words for this?</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">You Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Māu Anō — Your turn</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Practise saying these out loud — alone or with a partner. Then try the written challenges.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Say <strong>Kia ora</strong> to three different people in the room right now.</li><li>Your teacher walks in. Which greeting do you use? Say it aloud: <strong>Tēnā koe</strong>.</li><li>Someone asks <strong>Kei te pēhea koe?</strong> — how do you reply? Write and say your answer.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Teach the five vowel sounds to a partner. Can they say <strong>Tēnā koe</strong> perfectly?</li></ul></div></div>"},"Tuesday: Ko wai koe? Ko wai au.":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Te Aho Arataki Marau — AO 1.2: Introduce self and others · AO 1.4: Personal information (name)</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Objectives</th><td class=\"lt-td\">AO 1.2 — Introductions; AO 1.4 — Personal information</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">I Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tāua Kōrero — Watch &amp; discover</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Yesterday we learned how to greet someone. Today we learn how to tell people who we are. In te reo Māori, there is a special word we use when we identify or name something. Watch how it works.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\"><strong>Ko</strong> Aroha <strong>tōku <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=ingoa\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">ingoa</a></strong>.<br>= My name is Aroha.<br><br><strong>Ko</strong> <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=wai\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">wai</a> <strong>tōu ingoa?</strong><br>= What is your name?<br><br><strong>Ko</strong> Wiremu <strong>tōna ingoa</strong>.<br>= His/her name is Wiremu.</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The word <strong>Ko</strong> is used whenever we are saying what something <strong>IS</strong> — its identity or name.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The pattern is simple:<br><strong>Ko</strong> + [name] + [whose] + <strong>ingoa</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&quot;Ingoa&quot; means &quot;name&quot;. &quot;Tōku&quot; = my. &quot;Tōu&quot; = your. &quot;Tōna&quot; = his or her.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Notice: te reo Māori does NOT have separate words for &quot;he&quot; and &quot;she&quot;. Tōna covers both.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Tip: Ko is like a pointer — it points at what something IS. Ko = &quot;it is&quot; or &quot;my name is.&quot;</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">We Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Kia Tūhono — Build together</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Let's build Ko sentences together. I will give you the pieces — you put them in the right order.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>How would you say &quot;My name is [your name]&quot;? What are the pieces? Ko + [your name] + tōku + ingoa</li><li>How would you ask someone their name? Remember: &quot;wai&quot; means &quot;who/what&quot;. Ko wai tōu ingoa?</li><li>Your friend's name is Hana. How would you tell someone her name? Ko Hana tōna ingoa.</li><li>Now put it all together — greet someone AND introduce yourself: <strong>Kia ora. Ko [name] tōku ingoa.</strong></li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Discussion prompt: Why do you think te reo Māori uses Ko rather than just saying &quot;my name is&quot;? What does it feel like to say your name this way?</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">You Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Māu Anō — Your turn</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Use the Ko structure to introduce yourself — written or spoken.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Write: <strong>Ko [your name] tōku ingoa.</strong> Say it aloud.</li><li>Ask the person next to you their name using <strong>Ko wai tōu ingoa?</strong> Then introduce them to someone else.</li><li>Write three Ko sentences: your name, a friend's name, your teacher's name.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Add a greeting. Write a two-sentence introduction: hello + your name. Practise saying it smoothly.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Wednesday: Kei te haere au — I am going":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Te Aho Arataki Marau — AO 1.5: Communicate about location · AO 2.5: Physical characteristics and feelings</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Objectives</th><td class=\"lt-td\">AO 1.5 — Location &amp; movement; AO 2.5 — Feelings &amp; states</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">I Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tāua Kōrero — Watch &amp; discover</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>So far we have learned how to greet people and say our names. Now we are going to learn how to talk about what is happening RIGHT NOW. Watch how te reo Māori builds a present-tense sentence.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\"><strong>Kei te</strong> <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=haere\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">haere</a> <strong>au</strong>.<br>= I am going.<br><br><strong>Kei te</strong> <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=kai\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">kai</a> <strong>koe</strong>.<br>= You are eating.<br><br><strong>Kei te</strong> <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=moe\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">moe</a> <strong>ia</strong>.<br>= He/she is sleeping.</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The pattern for right-now actions is always:</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Kei te</strong> + [action word] + [person]</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&quot;Kei te&quot; is like &quot;-ing&quot; in English — it shows the action is happening NOW.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Action words: <strong>haere</strong> = go/going · <strong>kai</strong> = eat/eating · <strong>moe</strong> = sleep/sleeping</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>People: <strong>au</strong> = I/me · <strong>koe</strong> = you · <strong>ia</strong> = he/she</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Tip: In te reo Māori, the action comes FIRST, then the person. &quot;Is-going I&quot; not &quot;I am going.&quot; The structure is always Time/Action → Action word → Person.</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">We Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Kia Tūhono — Build together</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Use the Kei te structure to build these sentences together. I will give you the pieces — you put them in order.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>How do you say &quot;I am eating&quot;? Pieces: Kei te · kai · au → <strong>Kei te kai au.</strong></li><li>How do you say &quot;She is sleeping&quot;? Pieces: Kei te · moe · ia → <strong>Kei te moe ia.</strong></li><li>How do you say &quot;You are going&quot;? Pieces: Kei te · haere · koe → <strong>Kei te haere koe.</strong></li><li>Now say all three out loud one after another — notice how the structure never changes, only the action word and person swap.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Discussion prompt: The structure Kei te + action + person never changes. How is that different from English, where we change &quot;I go / she goes&quot;? Which feels easier?</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">You Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Māu Anō — Your turn</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Build your own Kei te sentences using the words you have learned.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Write and say: <strong>Kei te kai au.</strong> Now swap to &quot;you&quot; — Kei te kai koe.</li><li>Use <strong>haere</strong> (going) in three sentences — one for au, one for koe, one for ia.</li><li>Look around the room. What are people doing? Write one Kei te sentence about what a classmate is doing.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Can you invent a sentence using a new action? Try <strong>kōrero</strong> (speaking) or <strong>noho</strong> (sitting).</li></ul></div></div>"},"Thursday: He aha tēnā? Kōrero!":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Te Aho Arataki Marau — AO 1.1: Greetings · AO 1.2: Introductions · AO 1.6: Politeness conventions</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Objectives</th><td class=\"lt-td\">AO 1.1 — Greetings; AO 1.2 — Introductions; AO 1.6 — Politeness</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">I Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tāua Kōrero — Watch &amp; discover</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Today we put everything together into a real conversation. Watch me roleplay a full exchange — from greeting to introducing to asking what someone is doing.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\"><strong>A:</strong> Kia ora! Ko wai tōu ingoa?<br><strong>B:</strong> Ko Mere tōku ingoa. Ko wai tōu?<br><strong>A:</strong> Ko Tama tōku ingoa. Kei te pēhea koe?<br><strong>B:</strong> Kei te pai au, kia ora. Kei te <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=aha\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">aha</a> koe?<br><strong>A:</strong> Kei te haere au ki te <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=kura\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">kura</a>.<br><strong>B:</strong> <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=%C4%80e\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Āe</a>! Hei konā rā.<br><strong>A:</strong> Hei konā rā!</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>New phrase: <strong>Kei te aha koe?</strong> — What are you doing?<br><strong>Aha</strong> = what. So &quot;Kei te aha koe?&quot; literally means &quot;Right now what you?&quot; → &quot;What are you doing?&quot;</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>New phrase: <strong>ki te kura</strong> — to school (<strong>ki</strong> = to, <strong>kura</strong> = school)</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>New word: <strong>Āe</strong> — Yes!</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>You now have everything you need for a genuine short conversation in te reo Māori.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Tip: A real conversation has a rhythm: greet → introduce → ask → answer → farewell. Once you know the pattern, you just swap in the pieces.</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">We Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Kia Tūhono — Practise together</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Let's build the conversation step by step as a class. I will be Person A — you are Person B. We go slowly and swap roles.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I say: <strong>Kia ora!</strong> — You reply with a greeting and ask my name.</li><li>I tell you my name is Rangi. You introduce yourself. Then ask: <strong>Kei te pēhea koe?</strong></li><li>I say: <strong>Kei te pai au.</strong> Now ask me what I am doing: <strong>Kei te aha koe?</strong></li><li>I say I am going to school. How do you say goodbye? Use <strong>Hei konā rā</strong> or <strong>E noho rā</strong>.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Discussion prompt: Which part of the conversation felt most natural? Which part do you want more practice with? What other things might you want to say that we haven't learned yet?</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">You Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Māu Anō — Your turn</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>With a partner, have a full conversation using only te reo Māori. Use every structure from this week.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>With a partner: greet each other using <strong>Kia ora</strong> or <strong>Tēnā koe</strong>.</li><li>Introduce yourselves using <strong>Ko [name] tōku ingoa</strong>. Ask each other's names.</li><li>Ask <strong>Kei te pēhea koe?</strong> and answer <strong>Kei te pai au</strong>.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Add <strong>Kei te aha koe?</strong> and answer with a Kei te sentence. Try to have a 6-line conversation entirely in te reo!</li></ul></div></div>"},"Friday: Aromatawai — Quiz & Review":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Monday questions</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Kei te pēhea koe? — What does this mean?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>Where are you going? </li><li>How are you? ✅</li><li>What is your name? </li><li>Are you good? </li></ul></li><li>Which vowel rule is correct for te reo Māori?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>Vowels change depending on surrounding letters </li><li>Each vowel has only one fixed sound, always ✅</li><li>Vowels are usually silent at the end of a word </li><li>Only &quot;a&quot; and &quot;e&quot; have consistent sounds </li></ul></li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tuesday questions</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>&quot;Ko Hana tōna ingoa.&quot; What does this mean?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>Ko is her greeting word </li><li>Her name is Hana ✅</li><li>Hana's name is different </li><li>Hana says hello </li></ul></li><li>How do you say &quot;What is your name?&quot; in te reo Māori?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>Ko wai tōku ingoa? </li><li>Ko wai tōna ingoa? </li><li>Ko wai tōu ingoa? ✅</li><li>Ko tōu wai ingoa? </li></ul></li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Wednesday questions</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>&quot;Kei te moe ia.&quot; The word &quot;ia&quot; means:<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>I / me </li><li>you </li><li>he or she ✅</li><li>going </li></ul></li><li>What is the correct order for a present-tense sentence in te reo Māori?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>Person → Kei te → action </li><li>Action → Kei te → person </li><li>Kei te → person → action </li><li>Kei te → action → person ✅</li></ul></li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Thursday questions</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>&quot;Kei te aha koe?&quot; — What does this question ask?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>How are you? </li><li>Where are you going? </li><li>What are you doing? ✅</li><li>Who are you? </li></ul></li><li>Which phrase means &quot;Goodbye&quot; to someone who is leaving?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>Kia ora </li><li>E noho rā </li><li>Hei konā rā ✅</li><li>Tēnā koe </li></ul></li></ul></div></div>"}}},"Week 2":{"label":"Day","children":{"Monday: Ngā kupu hou — Kīnaki me ngā kai":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Te Aho Arataki Marau — AO 1.3: Communicate about number · AO 2.5: Physical characteristics and feelings (food/taste)</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Objectives</th><td class=\"lt-td\">AO 1.3 — Number &amp; quantity; AO 2.5 — Feelings &amp; taste</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">I Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tāua Kōrero — Commas in lists and new food vocabulary</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This week we are building our vocabulary for food and drink — kupu pai mō te kai me te inu. We are also going to learn how to say lists of things in te reo Māori. Listen carefully to how we join words together.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\"><strong>He <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=%C4%81poro\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">āporo</a></strong> — an apple<br><strong>He <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=panana\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">panana</a></strong> — a banana<br><strong>He <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=t%C5%8Dmate\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">tōmate</a></strong> — a tomato<br><strong>He <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=par%C4%81oa\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">parāoa</a></strong> — bread<br><strong>He <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=miraka\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">miraka</a></strong> — milk<br><strong>He <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=wai\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">wai</a></strong> — water<br><strong>He <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=t%C4%AB\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">tī</a></strong> — tea<br><strong>He inu <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=k%C5%8Dhua\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">kōhua</a></strong> — hot drink / coffee</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>In te reo Māori, when we list things, we simply say them one after another — no word for &quot;and&quot; needed in a basic list:</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>He āporo, he panana, he tōmate.</strong><br>An apple, a banana, a tomato.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>But when we want to say &quot;and also&quot; to add something extra, we use <strong>me</strong>:</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>He āporo me he panana.</strong><br>An apple and a banana.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>And to say &quot;I want&quot; something, we combine what you already know:<br><strong>Kei te hiahia au ki te kai i te āporo.</strong><br>I want to eat the apple.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Tip: Me = and (joining two things). In a list of three or more, you can just list them — no joining word needed until the last item.</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">We Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Kia Tūhono — Build food lists together</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Let's practise the new food words and build some lists. I will show you the items — you say them in te reo.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Look at these three things: an apple, bread, water. How do you say them as a list? <strong>He āporo, he parāoa, he wai.</strong></li><li>Now say: &quot;I want to drink the water.&quot; Use Kei te hiahia + ki te inu + i te wai → <strong>Kei te hiahia au ki te inu i te wai.</strong></li><li>Add me to join two things: &quot;milk and tea&quot; → <strong>He miraka me he tī.</strong></li><li>Now put it together: &quot;I want bread and milk.&quot; → <strong>Kei te hiahia au ki te kai i te parāoa me te miraka.</strong></li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Discussion prompt: Which of these food words sound similar to the English word? Why do you think that is? What does this tell us about how languages borrow from each other?</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">You Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Māu Anō — Your turn with food words</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Use the new vocabulary to build your own sentences about food and drink.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Write a list of three foods you like using <strong>He… me he… me he…</strong></li><li>Say what you want to eat right now: <strong>Kei te hiahia au ki te kai i te…</strong></li><li>Ask a partner what they want to drink: <strong>Kei te hiahia koe ki te inu i te aha?</strong></li><li>✦ Bonus: Can you say five food words from memory without looking? Write them with their English meanings.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Tuesday: He aha tēnei? He aha tērā? — This and that":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Te Aho Arataki Marau — AO 1.2: Introduce self and others · AO 2.5: Physical descriptions — He structure with demonstratives</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Objectives</th><td class=\"lt-td\">AO 1.2 — Introductions &amp; identity; AO 2.5 — Descriptions — He structure</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">I Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tāua Kōrero — Demonstratives: tēnei, tēnā, tērā</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>You already know He — &quot;it is a…&quot;. Today we add pointer words so we can say THIS thing or THAT thing. Watch how they work.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\"><strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=t%C4%93nei\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">tēnei</a></strong> — this (near me)<br><strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=t%C4%93n%C4%81\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">tēnā</a></strong> — that (near you)<br><strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=t%C4%93r%C4%81\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">tērā</a></strong> — that (over there, away from both of us)<br><br><strong>He aha tēnei?</strong> — What is this?<br><strong>He āporo tēnei.</strong> — This is an apple.<br><strong>He aha tēnā?</strong> — What is that (near you)?<br><strong>He parāoa tēnā.</strong> — That is bread.<br><strong>He aha tērā?</strong> — What is that over there?<br><strong>He kura tērā.</strong> — That is a school.</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The three pointer words work just like &quot;this&quot; and &quot;that&quot; in English — they tell us HOW FAR AWAY the thing is.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>tēnei</strong> — near ME (the speaker)<br><strong>tēnā</strong> — near YOU (the listener)<br><strong>tērā</strong> — away from BOTH of us</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>They slot into your He sentence at the end, after the noun:<br><strong>He [noun] tēnei/tēnā/tērā.</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>And the question is always: <strong>He aha</strong> + the pointer word.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Tip: Think of tēnei/tēnā/tērā as three distances: HERE · THERE · WAY OVER THERE. They work the same way every time.</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">We Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Kia Tūhono — Point and describe together</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>I will point at things in the room. You tell me what they are in te reo using tēnei, tēnā, or tērā.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I hold up a pencil near me. You ask: <strong>He aha tēnā?</strong> I answer: <strong>He pene tēnā.</strong> (pene = pencil)</li><li>Something is on the far wall. How do you ask? → <strong>He aha tērā?</strong> How might you answer if it is a window (matapihi)? → <strong>He matapihi tērā.</strong></li><li>You hold something. I ask <strong>He aha tēnā?</strong> You answer with a food word from Monday. Try it!</li><li>Now add an adjective: &quot;This is a big apple.&quot; → <strong>He āporo nui tēnei.</strong> Remember — adjective comes AFTER the noun.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Discussion prompt: What is the difference between tēnā and tērā? If you had to explain it to a younger student, how would you describe it?</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">You Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Māu Anō — Point and say</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Walk around the room (or look around) and describe what you see using tēnei, tēnā, tērā.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Write three sentences about things near you using <strong>He [noun] tēnei.</strong></li><li>Write two sentences about things near your partner using <strong>He [noun] tēnā.</strong></li><li>Ask a partner <strong>He aha tēnā?</strong> about something they are holding. They answer in te reo.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Add an adjective to each of your sentences. E.g. <strong>He pene hou tēnei.</strong> (This is a new pencil.)</li></ul></div></div>"},"Wednesday: Expanding with adjectives — He [noun] [adjective] tēnei":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Te Aho Arataki Marau — AO 2.5: Communicate about physical characteristics · Grammar Level 1–2: He + noun + adjective</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Objectives</th><td class=\"lt-td\">AO 2.5 — Physical descriptions; AO 1.6 — Politeness &amp; classroom language</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">I Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tāua Kōrero — Building richer descriptions</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>You now have He, demonstratives, and adjectives. Today we combine them all into full, rich descriptions. Watch how a simple sentence grows.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\"><strong>He āporo.</strong><br>An apple.<br><br><strong>He āporo tēnei.</strong><br>This is an apple.<br><br><strong>He āporo <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=whero\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">whero</a> tēnei.</strong><br>This is a red apple.<br><br><strong>He āporo whero <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=nui\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">nui</a> tēnei.</strong><br>This is a big red apple.<br><br><strong>Kei te hiahia au ki te kai i tēnei āporo whero nui.</strong><br>I want to eat this big red apple.</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Notice how we can stack adjectives after the noun:<br><strong>He āporo [whero] [nui] tēnei.</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Colour words you now know:<br><strong>whero</strong> = red · <strong>kōwhai</strong> = yellow · <strong>kākāriki</strong> = green<br><strong>kikorangi</strong> = blue · <strong>mā</strong> = white · <strong>mangu</strong> = black</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>And that final sentence shows something powerful — you can plug your whole He description into a Kei te hiahia sentence as the object. The building blocks connect.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Tip: Noun first, then adjectives — always. You can have two or three adjectives, just keep stacking them after the noun.</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">We Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Kia Tūhono — Grow the sentence together</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Start with a bare noun and keep adding — I will prompt you for each piece.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Start: <strong>He whare.</strong> Add &quot;big&quot;: <strong>He whare nui.</strong> Add &quot;that over there&quot;: <strong>He whare nui tērā.</strong></li><li>Start: <strong>He panana.</strong> Add &quot;yellow&quot;: <strong>He panana kōwhai.</strong> Add &quot;this&quot;: <strong>He panana kōwhai tēnei.</strong></li><li>Now use Kei te with your full description: &quot;I am eating this yellow banana.&quot; → <strong>Kei te kai au i tēnei panana kōwhai.</strong></li><li>Try a new combo: He + [noun from Monday] + [colour] + [size] + tēnā. Build it piece by piece.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Discussion prompt: You now have three layers: He + noun + adjectives + demonstrative. How is building a sentence like stacking building blocks? What stays fixed and what changes?</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">You Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Māu Anō — Stack the building blocks</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Build the most complete description sentences you can using everything you know.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Write a two-layer sentence: <strong>He [noun] [adjective] tēnei.</strong></li><li>Write a three-layer sentence: <strong>He [noun] [colour] [size] tēnā.</strong></li><li>Write a full Kei te sentence using your description as the object: <strong>Kei te kai/inu au i tēnei…</strong></li><li>✦ Bonus: Describe a classmate's belongings in three sentences using tēnā (things near them). Read them aloud.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Thursday: Kōrero — He aha ōu hiahia? What do you want?":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Te Aho Arataki Marau — AO 1.1: Greetings · AO 1.6: Politeness conventions · AO 2.3: Communicate about likes and dislikes</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Objectives</th><td class=\"lt-td\">AO 2.3 — Likes, dislikes &amp; wants; AO 1.6 — Politeness conventions</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">I Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tāua Kōrero — A café conversation</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Today we put all of this week's learning into a real conversation. Imagine you are in a café in Tāmaki Makaurau. Listen to how the conversation builds using everything from Monday to Wednesday.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\"><strong>Kaimahi:</strong> Tēnā koe! He aha ōu <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=hiahia\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">hiahia</a>?<br><strong>Manuhiri:</strong> Tēnā koe. Kei te hiahia au ki te inu i tētahi tī <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=m%C4%81hana\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">māhana</a>.<br><strong>Kaimahi:</strong> He aha tēnā? He tī māmā, he tī <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=kaha\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">kaha</a> <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=r%C4%81nei\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">rānei</a>?<br><strong>Manuhiri:</strong> He tī māmā, tēnā koe.<br><strong>Kaimahi:</strong> He āporo, he panana rānei hei kai māu?<br><strong>Manuhiri:</strong> He āporo tēnā? Āe, tēnā koa.<br><strong>Kaimahi:</strong> Ka pai! E hia te <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=utu\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">utu</a>? E rima <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=t%C4%81ra\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">tāra</a>.<br><strong>Manuhiri:</strong> Tēnā koe. Hei konā rā!</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>New words from this conversation:<br><strong>Kaimahi</strong> = worker / staff<br><strong>Manuhiri</strong> = visitor / customer<br><strong>ōu hiahia</strong> = your wants / what you would like<br><strong>tētahi</strong> = a (certain) one<br><strong>māhana</strong> = warm<br><strong>māmā</strong> = light / mild<br><strong>kaha</strong> = strong<br><strong>rānei</strong> = or<br><strong>hei kai māu</strong> = for you to eat<br><strong>tēnā koa</strong> = yes please (polite)</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Notice: <strong>rānei</strong> is used for &quot;or&quot; in questions — just like FANBOYS in English but for choices.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Tip: tēnā koa = yes please. This is the polite, warm way to accept something offered to you. Use it generously!</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">We Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Kia Tūhono — Build the café conversation</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Let's build the conversation together, one line at a time. I will be the kaimahi — you are the manuhiri.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I greet you: Tēnā koe! You greet me back. Then I ask: <strong>He aha ōu hiahia?</strong> What do you say you want to drink?</li><li>I offer you a choice: <strong>He miraka, he wai rānei?</strong> — milk or water? You answer using <strong>He… tēnā koa.</strong></li><li>I ask: <strong>He aha ōu hiahia hei kai?</strong> — What would you like to eat? You tell me using <strong>Kei te hiahia au ki te kai i…</strong></li><li>I tell you the price: <strong>E whā tāra.</strong> You say thank you and goodbye. Which goodbye word do YOU use — and which do I use? Why?</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Discussion prompt: Tēnā koa vs Āe — when might you use each one? What does the &quot;koa&quot; add? Think about how politeness works differently in different situations.</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">You Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Māu Anō — Your café roleplay</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>With a partner, perform a full café conversation using this week's vocabulary and structures. Swap roles.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Partner A is the kaimahi — greet, ask what they want, offer a choice using rānei.</li><li>Partner B is the manuhiri — respond politely, say what you want, use tēnā koa.</li><li>Include at least one He description with an adjective: <strong>He [food] [colour/size].</strong></li><li>✦ Bonus: Extend the conversation to 10 lines. The kaimahi can describe the food: <strong>He āporo whero nui tēnei — he pai!</strong></li></ul></div></div>"},"Friday: Aromatawai — Quiz & Review":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Monday questions</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>&quot;He āporo me he panana.&quot; What does &quot;me&quot; mean here?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>but </li><li>or </li><li>and ✅</li><li>with </li></ul></li><li>How do you say &quot;I want to drink the water&quot; in te reo Māori?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>Kei te kai au i te wai </li><li>Kei te hiahia au ki te inu i te wai ✅</li><li>Kei te inu au ki te wai </li><li>He wai hiahia au </li></ul></li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tuesday questions</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>&quot;He aha tērā?&quot; — Which object is being asked about?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>Something next to the speaker </li><li>Something next to the listener </li><li>Something far from both speaker and listener ✅</li><li>Something the speaker is holding </li></ul></li><li>How do you say &quot;This is a big house&quot; in te reo Māori?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>He nui whare tēnei </li><li>He whare tēnei nui </li><li>He whare nui tēnei ✅</li><li>Tēnei he whare nui </li></ul></li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Wednesday questions</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>&quot;He āporo whero nui tēnei.&quot; What is the correct order here?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>Demonstrative → adjectives → noun </li><li>Noun → demonstrative → adjectives </li><li>Noun → adjectives → demonstrative ✅</li><li>Adjectives → noun → demonstrative </li></ul></li><li>What is the te reo Māori word for &quot;yellow&quot;?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>whero </li><li>kikorangi </li><li>kōwhai ✅</li><li>kākāriki </li></ul></li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Thursday questions</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A customer wants to say &quot;yes please&quot; politely when offered something. What do they say?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>Āe </li><li>Tēnā koa ✅</li><li>Kia ora </li><li>Kāo </li></ul></li><li>&quot;He miraka, he wai rānei?&quot; — What does rānei mean in this sentence?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>and </li><li>but </li><li>or ✅</li><li>also </li></ul></li></ul></div></div>"}}},"Week 3":{"label":"Day","children":{"Monday: Ngā kupumahi hou — More action words":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Te Aho Arataki Marau — AO 1.5: Communicate about location and movement · AO 2.5: Physical characteristics and feelings — expanding verb vocabulary</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Objectives</th><td class=\"lt-td\">AO 1.5 — Location &amp; movement; AO 2.5 — Actions &amp; feelings</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">I Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tāua Kōrero — Expanding your verb toolkit</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>You already know haere (go), kai (eat), inu (drink), moe (sleep), kōrero (speak), and ako (learn). Today we are going to add a whole new set of action words — kupumahi — so you can describe much more of what happens around you. Listen carefully and notice the sounds.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\"><strong>haere</strong> — go / travel<br><strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=hoki\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">hoki</a></strong> — return / come back<br><strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=t%C5%AB\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">tū</a></strong> — stand<br><strong>noho</strong> — sit / stay / live<br><strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=t%C4%81karo\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">tākaro</a></strong> — play<br><strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=mahi\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">mahi</a></strong> — work / do<br><strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=p%C4%81nui\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">pānui</a></strong> — read<br><strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=tuhituhi\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">tuhituhi</a></strong> — write<br><strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=whakarongo\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">whakarongo</a></strong> — listen<br><strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=titiro\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">titiro</a></strong> — look / watch<br><strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=omaoma\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">omaoma</a></strong> — run<br><strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=kaukau\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">kaukau</a></strong> — swim</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>You now have enough verbs to describe almost everything that happens in a school day.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>All of these slot straight into your <strong>Kei te</strong> structure:<br><strong>Kei te</strong> + [verb] + [person]</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Kei te tākaro ngā tamariki.</strong><br>The children are playing.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Kei te pānui ia.</strong><br>She is reading.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Kei te mahi au.</strong><br>I am working.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>New word: <strong>ngā tamariki</strong> = the children (ngā = the, plural)</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Tip: Every new verb you learn multiplies your sentences. One new verb = dozens of new sentences using structures you already know.</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">We Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Kia Tūhono — Build action sentences</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>I will call out a verb and a person — you build the Kei te sentence. Fast and fluent.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>&quot;She is swimming&quot; → <strong>Kei te kaukau ia.</strong> Now swap to &quot;I am swimming&quot; → <strong>Kei te kaukau au.</strong></li><li>&quot;The children are playing&quot; — new word: ngā tamariki = the children → <strong>Kei te tākaro ngā tamariki.</strong></li><li>&quot;I am writing&quot; → <strong>Kei te tuhituhi au.</strong> &quot;You are listening&quot; → <strong>Kei te whakarongo koe.</strong></li><li>Now try two in a row: &quot;She is reading and I am writing.&quot; Say both sentences back to back — notice the structure never changes.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Discussion prompt: You have now learned 12 action words. Which three do you think you will use most often in te reo? Why? What actions are missing that you would want to be able to say?</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">You Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Māu Anō — Action sentences</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Use the new verbs to describe what is happening around you right now.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Write three Kei te sentences about what three different people in the room are doing right now.</li><li>Write a sentence using <strong>ngā tamariki</strong> (the children) as the person doing the action.</li><li>Say five new verbs from memory — can you do it without looking at the list?</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write a four-sentence sequence describing what YOU do each morning from waking up to arriving at school. Use Kei te for each.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Tuesday: Ināianei — Kei te haere ngā tamariki ki hea?":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Te Aho Arataki Marau — AO 1.5: Communicate about location · AO 1.3: Number and reference · Grammar Level 1–2: ki hea (where to), i hea (where from)</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Objectives</th><td class=\"lt-td\">AO 1.5 — Location — ki hea &amp; i hea; AO 1.3 — Reference &amp; direction</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">I Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tāua Kōrero — Asking and answering where</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>You know ki already — it means &quot;to&quot;. Today we build on that to ask and answer &quot;where?&quot; questions. Watch how two small words unlock a whole new layer of conversation.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\"><strong>Ki hea?</strong> — Where (to)?<br><strong>I hea?</strong> — Where (from)? / Where (was it)?<br><strong>Kei hea?</strong> — Where (is it right now)?<br><br><strong>Kei te haere koe ki hea?</strong><br>Where are you going?<br><br><strong>Kei te haere au ki te kura.</strong><br>I am going to school.<br><br><strong>Kei hea a Hana?</strong><br>Where is Hana?<br><br><strong>Kei te kura ia.</strong><br>She is at school.<br><br><strong>I hea koe inanahi?</strong><br>Where were you yesterday?</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Three location question words, three different times:</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Kei hea?</strong> — WHERE IS IT right now? (present location)<br><strong>Ki hea?</strong> — WHERE TO? (direction of movement)<br><strong>I hea?</strong> — WHERE WAS IT? (past location)</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Place words to use in answers:<br><strong>te kura</strong> = school · <strong>te <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=k%C4%81inga\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">kāinga</a></strong> = home<br><strong>te <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=toa\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">toa</a></strong> = shop · <strong>te <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=m%C4%81ra\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">māra</a></strong> = garden<br><strong>te <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=awa\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">awa</a></strong> = river · <strong>te <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=roto\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">roto</a></strong> = lake<br><strong>te <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=h%C5%8Dpua\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">hōpua</a></strong> = swimming pool</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>New word: <strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=inanahi\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">inanahi</a></strong> = yesterday</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Tip: Kei hea = where IS it (now). Ki hea = where is it GOING. I hea = where WAS it. The first word tells you the time.</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">We Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Kia Tūhono — Where questions and answers</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>I ask the question — you build the answer. Then we swap and you ask me.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I ask: <strong>Kei te haere koe ki hea?</strong> You answer that you are going home → <strong>Kei te haere au ki te kāinga.</strong></li><li>I ask: <strong>Kei hea a Tama?</strong> (Where is Tama?) You say he is at the shop → <strong>Kei te toa ia.</strong></li><li>Now you ask ME where I am going. Use <strong>Kei te haere koe ki hea?</strong> I answer with a place — you decide if it makes sense.</li><li>Past tense: <strong>I hea koe inanahi?</strong> — Where were you yesterday? Try to answer using a place word: <strong>I te kura au.</strong></li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Discussion prompt: What is the difference between Kei hea and Ki hea? Can you make up a rule that helps you remember which one to use and when?</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">You Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Māu Anō — Location challenge</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Use ki hea, kei hea, and i hea in your own sentences and questions.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Write and ask a partner: <strong>Kei te haere koe ki hea?</strong> They answer with a real or imagined destination.</li><li>Write two sentences about where two different people are right now: <strong>Kei [place] ia.</strong></li><li>Write one past-tense location sentence: <strong>I [place] au inanahi.</strong></li><li>✦ Bonus: Write a four-line dialogue — one person asks where the other is going, why, and when they will come back (hoki mai). Use <strong>Āpōpō</strong> (tomorrow) in the answer.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Wednesday: Compound sentences — joining ideas with ā, engari, nō reira":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Te Aho Arataki Marau — AO 2.3: Communicate about likes, dislikes and reasons · Grammar Level 1–2: Conjunctions and compound sentences</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Objectives</th><td class=\"lt-td\">AO 2.3 — Reasons &amp; conjunctions; AO 1.5 — Extended communication</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">I Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tāua Kōrero — Joining sentences together</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>So far every sentence has stood alone. Today we learn how to join two ideas together — making compound sentences just like you do in English with &quot;and&quot;, &quot;but&quot;, and &quot;so&quot;. Watch how these three words work in te reo Māori.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\"><strong>ā</strong> — and then / and (joining actions in sequence)<br><strong>engari</strong> — but / however<br><strong>nō reira</strong> — so / therefore<br><br><strong>Kei te haere au ki te toa ā Kei te hoko au i te āporo.</strong><br>I am going to the shop and (then) I am buying an apple.<br><br><strong>Kei te hiahia au ki te tākaro engari kei te mahi au.</strong><br>I want to play but I am working.<br><br><strong>Kei te hiakai au nō reira kei te haere au ki te toa.</strong><br>I am hungry so I am going to the shop.</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>In English you might say &quot;and&quot;, &quot;but&quot;, &quot;so&quot;. In te reo:</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>ā</strong> — use this to chain two actions that happen in sequence or together<br><strong>engari</strong> — use this for contrast — something unexpected follows<br><strong>nō reira</strong> — use this for result or reason — &quot;therefore / so&quot;</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>New verb: <strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=hoko\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">hoko</a></strong> = buy</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The trick: each joining word tells you the RELATIONSHIP between the two ideas.<br>Adding? → ā<br>Contrasting? → engari<br>Showing result? → nō reira</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Tip: Before you choose the joining word, ask yourself: am I ADDING, CONTRASTING, or showing a RESULT? That decides which word fits.</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">We Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Kia Tūhono — Join the sentences</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>I give you two separate sentences — you decide which joining word fits and combine them.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>&quot;I am hungry.&quot; + &quot;I am going to the shop.&quot; — What is the relationship? → <strong>Kei te hiakai au nō reira kei te haere au ki te toa.</strong></li><li>&quot;She wants to swim.&quot; + &quot;She is working.&quot; — Contrast! → <strong>Kei te hiahia ia ki te kaukau engari kei te mahi ia.</strong></li><li>&quot;I am going to school.&quot; + &quot;I am learning te reo.&quot; — Sequence → <strong>Kei te haere au ki te kura ā kei te ako au i te reo Māori.</strong></li><li>Now make your own: give me a sentence, I will give you a second idea, you join them with the right word.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Discussion prompt: Which of the three joining words — ā, engari, nō reira — is most like the English word &quot;but&quot;? Which is most like &quot;therefore&quot;? Can you think of a sentence where you could use any of the three but the meaning would change?</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">You Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Māu Anō — Join your ideas</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write compound sentences in te reo using ā, engari, and nō reira.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Write a sentence using <strong>ā</strong> — two actions in sequence.</li><li>Write a sentence using <strong>engari</strong> — something unexpected.</li><li>Write a sentence using <strong>nō reira</strong> — a cause and result.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write a four-sentence paragraph about your morning — use at least two joining words. Read it aloud to a partner.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Thursday: Kōrero — He kōrero mō te kura":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Te Aho Arataki Marau — AO 1.7: Simple classroom language · AO 2.3: Likes and dislikes with reasons · AO 1.5: Location</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Objectives</th><td class=\"lt-td\">AO 1.7 — Classroom language; AO 2.3 — Likes, dislikes &amp; reasons</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">I Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tāua Kōrero — Talking about school</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Today we bring together all three days of this week — new verbs, location words, and joining sentences — in a conversation about school life. This is a conversation two students might have on the way to class.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\"><strong>A:</strong> Tēnā koe! Kei te haere koe ki hea?<br><strong>B:</strong> Kei te haere au ki te kura. Kei te ako au i te pāngarau ināianei.<br><strong>A:</strong> He pai ki a koe te pāngarau?<br><strong>B:</strong> Āe, he pai ki a au te pāngarau engari he uaua.<br><strong>A:</strong> He aha tōu akomanga?<br><strong>B:</strong> Ko te akomanga toru tōku. Kei hea tōu akomanga?<br><strong>A:</strong> Ko te akomanga whā tōku. Kei te haere tāua ki reira ā ka tīmata te akoranga!<br><strong>B:</strong> Āe! Kia tere!</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>New words from this conversation:<br><strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=p%C4%81ngarau\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">pāngarau</a></strong> = maths<br><strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=i%CC%84n%C4%81ianei\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">ināianei</a></strong> = right now / at this moment<br><strong>He pai ki a koe?</strong> = Do you like it? (literally: Is it good to you?)<br><strong>he <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=uaua\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">uaua</a></strong> = it is difficult<br><strong>tōu <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=akomanga\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">akomanga</a></strong> = your classroom<br><strong>akomanga toru/whā</strong> = classroom 3/4 (number after noun)<br><strong>tāua</strong> = we two (including you) — from Week 2!<br><strong>ki reira</strong> = there / to there<br><strong>ka <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=t%C4%ABmata\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">tīmata</a></strong> = it begins / is starting / is starting<br><strong>Kia <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=tere\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">tere</a>!</strong> = Hurry up! / Be quick! / Be quick!</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Tip: He pai ki a koe? = Do you like it? He pai ki a au = I like it. This is one of the most useful patterns in te reo — learn it cold.</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">We Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Kia Tūhono — School conversation</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Build the school conversation step by step. I will start each exchange — you complete it.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I ask: <strong>Kei te haere koe ki hea?</strong> You say you are going to your classroom (use your actual room number if you know it in Māori).</li><li>I ask: <strong>He pai ki a koe te [subject]?</strong> Choose a school subject. You answer with <strong>Āe/Kāo, he pai/uaua ki a au.</strong></li><li>Add a joining word: &quot;I like reading but it is difficult.&quot; → <strong>He pai ki a au te pānui engari he uaua.</strong></li><li>Use tāua: &quot;Let's go to class together.&quot; → <strong>Kei te haere tāua ki te akomanga.</strong></li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Discussion prompt: He pai ki a au = I like it. How does this differ from how you say &quot;I like it&quot; in English? What does &quot;ki a au&quot; literally mean — and what does that tell you about how te reo Māori thinks about liking things?</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">You Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Māu Anō — Your school kōrero</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>With a partner, have a full conversation about your school day using everything from this week.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Ask and answer: <strong>Kei te haere koe ki hea?</strong> Use a real location from your school.</li><li>Ask and answer: <strong>He pai ki a koe te [subject]?</strong> Give a reason using <strong>nō reira</strong> or <strong>engari</strong>.</li><li>Use <strong>tāua</strong> to suggest doing something together — go to class, eat lunch, play outside.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write a 6-line dialogue between two students that uses: a location question, a likes/dislikes exchange with a reason, and at least one joining word.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Friday: Aromatawai — Quiz & Review":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Monday questions</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>What is the te reo Māori word for &quot;write&quot;?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>pānui </li><li>tuhituhi ✅</li><li>whakarongo </li><li>titiro </li></ul></li><li>&quot;Kei te tākaro ngā tamariki.&quot; What does &quot;ngā tamariki&quot; mean?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>the teacher </li><li>the children ✅</li><li>the school </li><li>the classroom </li></ul></li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tuesday questions</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>&quot;Kei te haere koe ki hea?&quot; — What is this question asking?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>Where are you right now? </li><li>Where were you yesterday? </li><li>Where are you going? ✅</li><li>Where is she? </li></ul></li><li>Which question word asks about where something IS right now (not where it is going)?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>Ki hea </li><li>I hea </li><li>Kei hea ✅</li><li>Nō hea </li></ul></li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Wednesday questions</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>You want to say &quot;I am hungry SO I am going to the shop.&quot; Which joining word do you use?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>ā </li><li>engari </li><li>nō reira ✅</li><li>me </li></ul></li><li>&quot;Kei te hiahia au ki te kaukau engari kei te mahi au.&quot; What does engari signal here?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>A result — swimming caused the work </li><li>A sequence — swimming then working </li><li>A contrast — wanting to swim but working instead ✅</li><li>An addition — swimming and working </li></ul></li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Thursday questions</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>&quot;He pai ki a koe te pāngarau?&quot; — What is pāngarau?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>reading </li><li>writing </li><li>maths ✅</li><li>science </li></ul></li><li>How do you say &quot;Let's go to class&quot; using the inclusive we-two pronoun?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>Kei te haere māua ki te akomanga </li><li>Kei te haere tāua ki te akomanga ✅</li><li>Kei te haere mātou ki te akomanga </li><li>Kei te haere kōrua ki te akomanga </li></ul></li></ul></div></div>"}}},"Week 4":{"label":"Day","children":{"Monday: Nōku, nāku — mine! Possessive pronouns":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Te Aho Arataki Marau — AO 2.2: Communicate about possessions · Grammar Level 1–2: Possessive pronouns, ō/ā categories introduced</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Objectives</th><td class=\"lt-td\">AO 2.2 — Possessions; AO 1.4 — Personal information</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">I Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tāua Kōrero — Whose is it? Nō wai? Nā wai?</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Today we learn how to say who things belong to. In te reo Māori there are two ways to say &quot;mine&quot; or &quot;yours&quot; — and which one you use depends on what KIND of thing you are talking about. Listen carefully and you will start to feel the difference.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\"><strong>Nō wai tēnā?</strong> — Whose is that? (things you don't control)<br><strong>Nōku tēnā.</strong> — That is mine.<br><strong>Nōu tēnā.</strong> — That is yours.<br><strong>Nōna tēnā.</strong> — That is his/hers.<br><br><strong>Nā wai tēnā?</strong> — Whose is that? (things you do control)<br><strong>Nāku tēnā.</strong> — That is mine.<br><strong>Nāu tēnā.</strong> — That is yours.<br><strong>Nāna tēnā.</strong> — That is his/hers.</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This is one of the most interesting things about te reo Māori — things you POSSESS are divided into two groups:</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Ō-category (nō-)</strong> — things you do NOT control or things that are senior to you:<br>your name, your parents, your feelings, your land, your canoe, your school</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Ā-category (nā-)</strong> — things you DO control or things junior/subordinate to you:<br>your food, your tools, your book, your pet, your children (in a nurturing sense)</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>A quick feel for it:<br><strong>Nōku tōku ingoa</strong> — My name belongs to me (ō — you didn't choose it)<br><strong>Nāku tāku pukapuka</strong> — My book is mine (ā — you picked it up)</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Don't stress about getting it perfect yet — just start to notice the pattern.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Tip: Ō = things that own YOU or came to you. Ā = things YOU own or control. Your name, land, canoe → ō. Your food, book, pen → ā.</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">We Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Kia Tūhono — Sort and say</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>I will name an item. You decide: ō-category or ā-category? Then say who it belongs to.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A pencil (pene) — do you control it? Yes → ā-category. &quot;It is mine&quot; → <strong>Nāku tēnā pene.</strong></li><li>Your school (kura) — is it senior to you? Yes → ō-category. &quot;This school is mine/ours&quot; → <strong>Nōku tēnei kura.</strong></li><li>Your lunch (tina) — do you control it? Yes → ā. Someone asks Nā wai tēnā? You answer: <strong>Nāku tēnā.</strong></li><li>Your feelings (kei te pai au) — can you control your feelings? Mostly not → ō. &quot;My feeling is good&quot; — we use tōku: <strong>He pai tōku ngākau.</strong></li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Discussion prompt: Why do you think te reo Māori makes this distinction? What does it tell us about how Māori culture thinks about ownership and relationship? Can you think of something that might be tricky to categorise?</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">You Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Māu Anō — Mine, yours, his/hers</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Practise using nōku/nāku and nōu/nāu in real situations.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Point to three things on your desk. For each one, say whether it is ō or ā category and say <strong>Nāku tēnei</strong> or <strong>Nōku tēnei.</strong></li><li>Someone picks up your book. What do you say? Write and say it: <strong>Nāku tēnā!</strong></li><li>Ask a partner whose pencil case is: <strong>Nā wai tēnā kete pene?</strong> They answer using <strong>Nāku/Nāu/Nāna.</strong></li><li>✦ Bonus: Write four sentences — two using nō- words and two using nā- words. Make sure the category fits each item.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Tuesday: Tōku, tāku — my (as part of a sentence)":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Te Aho Arataki Marau — AO 2.2: Communicate about possessions · AO 1.4: Personal information · Grammar Level 1–2: tōku/tāku, tōu/tāu, tōna/tāna</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Objectives</th><td class=\"lt-td\">AO 2.2 — Possessives in sentences; AO 1.4 — Personal information</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">I Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tāua Kōrero — Tōku vs tāku inside a sentence</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Yesterday we used nōku and nāku to say &quot;it is mine.&quot; Today we learn how to say &quot;my [thing]&quot; INSIDE a sentence — like &quot;my book&quot; or &quot;my name&quot;. These are the words tōku and tāku. Watch how they work.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\"><strong>tōku</strong> — my (ō-category)<br><strong>tāku</strong> — my (ā-category)<br><strong>tōu</strong> — your (ō-category)<br><strong>tāu</strong> — your (ā-category)<br><strong>tōna</strong> — his/her (ō-category)<br><strong>tāna</strong> — his/her (ā-category)<br><br><strong>Ko [name] tōku ingoa.</strong><br>My name is [name]. (ingoa = ō — you didn't choose your name)<br><br><strong>Kei te pānui au i tāku <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=pukapuka\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">pukapuka</a>.</strong><br>I am reading my book. (pukapuka = ā — you control it)<br><br><strong>He pai tōna <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=ng%C4%81kau\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">ngākau</a>.</strong><br>His/her heart/feeling is good.</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The pattern is the same as nō/nā — just a different form used INSIDE a sentence:</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>tō-</strong> for ō-category things: tōku (my), tōu (your), tōna (his/her)<br><strong>tā-</strong> for ā-category things: tāku (my), tāu (your), tāna (his/her)</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>You already used tōku in Week 1 without knowing it:<br><strong>Ko [name] tōku ingoa.</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Now you know WHY it is tōku — ingoa (name) is ō-category.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Useful pairs to learn cold:<br><strong>tōku <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=ingoa\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">ingoa</a></strong> — my name · <strong>tāku <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=pukapuka\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">pukapuka</a></strong> — my food · <strong>tāku kai</strong> — my food<br><strong>tōku kura</strong> — my school · <strong>tāku <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=pene\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">pene</a></strong> — my pen</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Tip: Tōku before ō-things (name, feelings, land). Tāku before ā-things (food, book, pen). If you already know nō/nā, just swap the n for t.</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">We Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Kia Tūhono — My this, your that</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Build sentences using tōku, tāku, tōu, tāu. I give you the noun — you choose the right possessive and build the sentence.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>&quot;My name is…&quot; — ingoa is ō → <strong>Ko [name] tōku ingoa.</strong> Everyone say their own name using this.</li><li>&quot;I am reading my book&quot; — pukapuka is ā → <strong>Kei te pānui au i tāku pukapuka.</strong></li><li>&quot;Your school is big&quot; — kura is ō → <strong>He nui tōu kura.</strong></li><li>&quot;She is eating her food&quot; — kai is ā → <strong>Kei te kai ia i tāna kai.</strong></li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Discussion prompt: You now know tōku, tāku, tōu, tāu, tōna, tāna — six possessive words. How do they compare to English? In English we only have &quot;my&quot;, &quot;your&quot;, &quot;his/her&quot; — three words. Why does te reo Māori need six? What extra information do they carry?</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">You Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Māu Anō — Possessives in action</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Use tōku, tāku, tōu, tāu, tōna, tāna in full sentences.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Introduce yourself fully: Ko [name] tōku ingoa. Nō [place] au. Then add one more sentence using tāku.</li><li>Write two sentences about a classmate using tōna/tāna — one ō and one ā item.</li><li>Complete these: <strong>Kei te kai ia i <em><strong> kai.</strong> / <strong>Ko </strong></em> tōna ingoa.</strong></li><li>✦ Bonus: Write a four-sentence paragraph introducing yourself AND a friend. Use tōku, tāku, tōna, and tāna at least once each.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Wednesday: Topic sentences — He kōrero matua":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Te Aho Arataki Marau — AO 2.3: Communicate about likes and dislikes · AO 1.7: Classroom language · Grammar Level 1–2: He and Ko for topic sentences</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Objectives</th><td class=\"lt-td\">AO 2.3 — Expressing opinions &amp; topics; AO 1.7 — Structured classroom language</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">I Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tāua Kōrero — Starting a paragraph in te reo</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>In English, every paragraph starts with a topic sentence that tells the reader what the paragraph is about. Te reo Māori writing does the same thing. Today we learn how to write a strong opening sentence — a kōrero matua — using the structures you already know.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">A strong topic sentence in te reo Māori often uses <strong>He</strong> or <strong>Ko</strong>:<br><br><strong>He nui ngā āhuatanga pai o te kura.</strong><br>There are many good things about school.<br><br><strong>Ko te kura tōku wāhi pai rawa atu.</strong><br>School is my absolute favourite place.<br><br><strong>He tino pai tāku kura nō te mea he nui ngā hoa ōku.</strong><br>My school is very good because I have many friends.<br><br><strong>Ko te pānui tāku mahi pai rawa atu.</strong><br>Reading is my absolute favourite activity.</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>A topic sentence in te reo Māori does three things:</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>States the TOPIC clearly (what the paragraph is about)</li><li>Makes a CLAIM about it (a point of view, not just a fact)</li><li>Is strong enough to stand alone</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>New words:<br><strong>ngā <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=%C4%81huatanga\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">āhuatanga</a></strong> = the things / aspects / aspects<br><strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=w%C4%81hi\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">wāhi</a></strong> = place<br><strong>pai rawa atu</strong> = the very best / absolute favourite / absolute favourite / absolute favourite<br><strong>ngā <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=hoa\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">hoa</a></strong> = friends<br><strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=n%C5%8D+te+mea\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">nō te mea</a></strong> = because (more formal than nā te mea) (more formal than nā te mea)</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Notice: <strong>nō te mea</strong> and <strong>nā te mea</strong> both mean &quot;because&quot; — nō te mea is slightly more formal and used in writing.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Tip: A great topic sentence = TOPIC + CLAIM + (optional reason). He or Ko at the start signals &quot;here is what this is about.&quot;</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">We Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Kia Tūhono — Build topic sentences together</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>I give you a topic — you build a topic sentence using He or Ko. Then we improve it together.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Topic: your favourite food. Start with He: <strong>He [food] tāku kai pai rawa atu.</strong> Add a reason with nō te mea.</li><li>Topic: your school. Start with Ko: <strong>Ko tōku kura…</strong> What claim can you make? He nui? He pai? He tino ātaahua?</li><li>Topic: playing. Start with He: <strong>He pai ki a au te tākaro nō te mea…</strong> You finish it with your own reason.</li><li>Now make it stronger — take your first attempt and add either an adjective, a reason, or a demonstrative to make it more specific.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Discussion prompt: What makes a topic sentence strong? Compare these two: &quot;Ko tōku kura he nui&quot; vs &quot;Ko tōku kura tōku wāhi pai rawa atu nō te mea he nui ōku hoa.&quot; Which is better and why?</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">You Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Māu Anō — Write your own topic sentences</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write topic sentences in te reo for each of these topics.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Write a He topic sentence about your favourite sport or activity.</li><li>Write a Ko topic sentence about your school.</li><li>Write a topic sentence with a reason using <strong>nō te mea</strong>.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Choose one of your topic sentences and write two more sentences to follow it — make a mini paragraph in te reo Māori.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Thursday: Kōrero — Whakaatu i a koe anō — Introducing yourself fully":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Te Aho Arataki Marau — AO 1.2: Introduce self and others · AO 1.4: Personal information · AO 2.2: Possessions · AO 1.6: Politeness conventions</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Objectives</th><td class=\"lt-td\">AO 1.2 — Introductions; AO 1.4 — Personal information; AO 2.2 — Possessions</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">I Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tāua Kōrero — A full self-introduction</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>We have now learned Ko, nō, possessives, descriptions, and location. Today we bring them all together into a complete mihi — a self-introduction. This is one of the most important things you can do in te reo Māori. Listen to this full mihi and notice how each piece connects.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\"><strong>Tēnā koutou katoa.</strong><br>Greetings to you all.<br><br><strong>Ko [mountain] tōku maunga.</strong><br>… is my mountain.<br><br><strong>Ko [river] tōku awa.</strong><br>… is my river.<br><br><strong>Ko [iwi] tōku iwi.</strong><br>… is my tribe.<br><br><strong>Ko [school] tōku kura.</strong><br>… is my school.<br><br><strong>Ko [name] tōku ingoa.</strong><br>My name is …<br><br><strong>Nō [place] ahau.</strong><br>I am from …<br><br><strong>Tēnā koutou, tēnā koutou, tēnā koutou katoa.</strong><br>Greetings, greetings, greetings to you all.</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This is the structure of a traditional pepeha — a way of introducing yourself by connecting to the land and people you belong to.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Key new words:<br><strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=maunga\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">maunga</a></strong> = mountain<br><strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=awa\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">awa</a></strong> = river<br><strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=iwi\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">iwi</a></strong> = tribe / people<br><strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=katoa\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">katoa</a></strong> = all / everyone<br><strong>Tēnā koutou katoa</strong> = greetings to you all (koutou = you all, 3+)<br><strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=ahau\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">ahau</a></strong> = I / me (more formal form of au) (more formal form of au)</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Every Ko sentence here uses tōku — maunga, awa, iwi, kura, ingoa are all ō-category. They are senior to us. They connect us to something bigger than ourselves.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>You don't need to know your iwi or maunga perfectly yet — you can use your town or a nearby landmark for now.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Tip: Tēnā koutou katoa — said three times at the end of a mihi. Three times is a tradition of completeness and respect in te ao Māori.</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">We Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Kia Tūhono — Build your mihi together</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>We will build a mihi line by line as a class. I will prompt each piece — you supply the content.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Start with the greeting — which one do we use for the whole class? <strong>Tēnā koutou katoa.</strong> Everyone say it together.</li><li>Now add your school: <strong>Ko ___ tōku kura.</strong> Why is kura ō-category? (It is senior to you — it was here before you.)</li><li>Add your name: <strong>Ko <em><strong> tōku ingoa.</strong> Add where you are from: <strong>Nō </strong></em> ahau.</strong></li><li>Close with the three-part farewell: <strong>Tēnā koutou, tēnā koutou, tēnā koutou katoa.</strong> Say it all the way through.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Discussion prompt: Why do you think Māori introductions start with the land — mountains and rivers — before the person's name? What does this tell us about the relationship between people and place in te ao Māori?</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">You Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Māu Anō — Your own mihi</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write and practise your own mihi using the full structure.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Write your mihi in full — all six Ko/Nō lines plus the opening and closing greetings.</li><li>Practise saying it aloud until it feels smooth. Aim to say it without looking at the page.</li><li>Say your mihi to a partner. They listen and then say theirs back to you.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Add two extra lines to your mihi — one about your favourite place (wāhi pai) and one about what you love doing (mahi pai). Use tōku/tāku correctly.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Friday: Aromatawai — Quiz & Review":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Monday questions</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>&quot;Nāku tēnā pene.&quot; What does this sentence tell us about the pencil?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>It belongs to someone else </li><li>It is yours (ā-category — you control it) ✅</li><li>It is yours (ō-category — it is senior to you) </li><li>It is lost </li></ul></li><li>Your school (kura) is ō-category. How do you say &quot;That school is mine&quot;?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>Nāku tērā kura </li><li>Nōku tērā kura ✅</li><li>Tāku tērā kura </li><li>Tōku tērā kura </li></ul></li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tuesday questions</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>&quot;Ko [name] tōku ingoa.&quot; Why is tōku used here instead of tāku?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>Because ingoa is an ā-category word — you control your name </li><li>Because ingoa is an ō-category word — your name is senior to you and you did not choose it ✅</li><li>Because tōku is always used with Ko sentences </li><li>Because tāku is only used with food </li></ul></li><li>How do you say &quot;She is eating her food&quot;?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>Kei te kai ia i tōna kai </li><li>Kei te kai ia i tāna kai ✅</li><li>Kei te kai ia i nāna kai </li><li>Kei te kai tāna ia </li></ul></li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Wednesday questions</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>What does &quot;pai rawa atu&quot; mean?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>quite good </li><li>not good </li><li>the very best / absolute favourite ✅</li><li>good enough </li></ul></li><li>Which sentence is the strongest topic sentence?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>He kura tōku kura </li><li>Ko tōku kura he pai </li><li>Ko tōku kura tōku wāhi pai rawa atu nō te mea he nui ōku hoa ✅</li><li>He nui tōku kura </li></ul></li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Thursday questions</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>In a pepeha, why are maunga and awa mentioned before the person's name?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>Because mountains and rivers are more important than people </li><li>Because Māori introductions follow alphabetical order </li><li>Because connecting to land shows who you are and where you belong — land is senior to people ✅</li><li>Because it is easier to say </li></ul></li><li>How do you say &quot;Greetings to you all&quot; to a group of more than two people?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>Tēnā kōrua </li><li>Tēnā koe </li><li>Tēnā koutou katoa ✅</li><li>Tēnā tātou </li></ul></li></ul></div></div>"}}},"Week 5":{"label":"Day","children":{"Monday: Ngā pātai katoa — The full set of question words":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Te Aho Arataki Marau — AO 1.3: Number · AO 1.4: Personal information · AO 1.5: Location · Grammar Level 1–2: He aha, Ko wai, Kei hea, E hia, Pēhea</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Objectives</th><td class=\"lt-td\">AO 1.5 — Location questions; AO 1.3 — Number &amp; quantity questions</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">I Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tāua Kōrero — Every question word you need</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>You have been picking up question words gradually — Ko wai, He aha, Ki hea. Today we gather every key question word into one place and make sure you can use them all fluently. This is a big unlock — once you know all the question words, you can ask about anything.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\"><strong>Ko wai?</strong> — Who?<br><strong>He aha?</strong> — What?<br><strong>Kei hea?</strong> — Where (is it now)?<br><strong>Ki hea?</strong> — Where (is it going)?<br><strong>Nō hea?</strong> — Where (is it from)?<br><strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=%C4%80hea\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Āhea</a></strong> — When (future)?<br><strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=N%C5%8Dn%C4%81hea\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Nōnāhea</a></strong> — When (past)?<br><strong>E hia?</strong> — How many (things)?<br><strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=Tokohia\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Tokohia</a></strong> — How many (people)?<br><strong>Pēhea?</strong> — How? / What is it like?<br><strong>He aha te take?</strong> — Why? (What is the reason?)</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Each question word slips into a familiar sentence frame:</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Ko wai tōu ingoa?</strong> — Who are you? (What is your name?)<br><strong>He aha tēnei?</strong> — What is this?<br><strong>Kei hea tō kura?</strong> — Where is your school?<br><strong>Āhea tāua haere ai?</strong> — When are we going?<br><strong>E hia ngā ākonga?</strong> — How many students are there?<br><strong>Tokohia ngā kaiako?</strong> — How many teachers?<br><strong>Kei te pēhea koe?</strong> — How are you?<br><strong>He aha te take i haere ai koe?</strong> — Why did you go?</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Key distinction: <strong>E hia</strong> = how many THINGS. <strong>Tokohia</strong> = how many PEOPLE. Always.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Tip: E hia for things, Tokohia for people — never mix them. And Pēhea always asks about quality or condition: &quot;How is it? What is it like?&quot;</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">We Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Kia Tūhono — Question word rapid fire</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>I give you an answer — you give me the right question. Then we flip it.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Answer: &quot;Ko Mere tōku ingoa.&quot; → What was the question? → <strong>Ko wai tōu ingoa?</strong></li><li>Answer: &quot;E rua ngā pukapuka.&quot; (There are two books.) → Question about how many things → <strong>E hia ngā pukapuka?</strong></li><li>Answer: &quot;Tokorima ngā ākonga.&quot; (There are five students.) → <strong>Tokohia ngā ākonga?</strong> — Why tokohia not e hia?</li><li>Answer: &quot;Kei te pai au.&quot; → What question prompted this? → <strong>Kei te pēhea koe?</strong></li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Discussion prompt: E hia vs Tokohia — this distinction treats people differently from things. What does this tell you about how te reo Māori values people? Can you think of other languages that make a similar distinction?</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">You Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Māu Anō — Questions only</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Your challenge today: only write questions. No statements allowed.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Write one question using each of: <strong>Ko wai, He aha, Kei hea, E hia, Tokohia.</strong></li><li>Write a question using <strong>Pēhea</strong> that you would genuinely want to ask someone.</li><li>Write a question using <strong>Āhea</strong> about something happening this week.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write a 6-question interview in te reo Māori that you could use to find out about a new student. Cover name, origin, likes, and plans.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Tuesday: Ngā āhua — Degrees of adjectives: nui, nui ake, nui rawa atu":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Te Aho Arataki Marau — AO 2.5: Physical characteristics · Grammar Level 1–2: Comparative and superlative adjectives — ake, rawa atu, tino</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Objectives</th><td class=\"lt-td\">AO 2.5 — Descriptions — degree adjectives; AO 2.3 — Preferences &amp; comparisons</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">I Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tāua Kōrero — Good, better, best in te reo</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>You know adjectives like nui, pai, iti, roa. Today we learn how to compare things — to say something is BIGGER, or the BIGGEST, or SO big. Te reo Māori has a clean, consistent system for this. Watch how three words do all the work.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\"><strong>nui</strong> — big<br><strong>nui ake</strong> — bigger (comparative — more than one other)<br><strong>nui rawa atu</strong> — biggest / too big (superlative — most of all)<br><strong>tino nui</strong> — very big (intensifier)<br><br><strong>He nui tēnei <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=whare\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">whare</a>.</strong> — This house is big.<br><strong>He nui ake tērā whare.</strong> — That house is bigger.<br><strong>He nui rawa atu tērā whare.</strong> — That house is the biggest / too big.<br><strong>He <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=tino\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">tino</a> nui tērā whare.</strong> — That house is very big.</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The system works the same way for every adjective:</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>[adjective] = base<br>[adjective] + <strong>ake</strong> = more / -er (comparative)<br>[adjective] + <strong>rawa atu</strong> = most / -est / too (superlative)<br><strong>tino</strong> + [adjective] = very (intensifier)</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Let's try with pai (good):<br><strong>He pai</strong> — good<br><strong>He pai ake</strong> — better<br><strong>He pai rawa atu</strong> — best / the very best<br><strong>He tino pai</strong> — very good</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>And with iti (small):<br><strong>He iti ake</strong> — smaller<br><strong>He iti rawa atu</strong> — smallest<br><strong>He tino iti</strong> — very small</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Tip: Ake = more. Rawa atu = most/too much. Tino = very. They work with ANY adjective — learn the system, not each word separately.</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">We Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Kia Tūhono — Compare and contrast</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>I will give you two things to compare. You build the comparative sentence.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>This apple is big. That apple is bigger. → <strong>He nui tēnei āporo. He nui ake tērā āporo.</strong></li><li>Te reo Māori is good. Maths is better (for me). → <strong>He pai te reo Māori. He pai ake te pāngarau ki a au.</strong></li><li>My bag is small. Your bag is very small. → <strong>He iti tāku kete. He tino iti tāu kete.</strong></li><li>Now use rawa atu: &quot;This is the best food I have ever eaten.&quot; → <strong>He kai pai rawa atu tēnei.</strong></li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Discussion prompt: In English we say &quot;good / better / best&quot; — three different words. Te reo Māori uses &quot;pai / pai ake / pai rawa atu&quot; — the same root every time. Which system do you think is more logical? Which is easier to learn?</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">You Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Māu Anō — Bigger, better, best</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Use the comparison system with adjectives you already know.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Write three sentences comparing two objects in the room using <strong>[adjective] / [adjective] ake.</strong></li><li>Write a superlative sentence about something you love: <strong>He [adjective] rawa atu tāku…</strong></li><li>Use tino with three different adjectives: <strong>He tino [adj]…</strong></li><li>✦ Bonus: Write a short &quot;top three&quot; list in te reo — e.g. your three favourite foods ranked from good to best, using pai, pai ake, pai rawa atu.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Wednesday: Sentence variety — poto me te roa":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Te Aho Arataki Marau — AO 2.3: Extended communication · AO 2.5: Descriptive language · Grammar Level 1–2: Varying sentence length for effect</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Objectives</th><td class=\"lt-td\">AO 2.3 — Extended communication; AO 2.5 — Descriptive variety</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">I Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tāua Kōrero — Short sentences punch. Long sentences build a picture.</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Good writers — in any language — vary the length of their sentences. A short sentence lands hard. A long sentence with detail and joining words draws the reader in. Today we practise doing both deliberately in te reo Māori.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">SHORT — punchy, clear, immediate:<br><strong>Kei te ua.</strong> It is raining.<br><strong>Kei te hiakai au.</strong> I am hungry.<br><strong>Ka tū ia.</strong> She stood.<br><br>LONG — descriptive, connected, flowing:<br><strong>Kei te ua tino nui ā kei te <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=makariri\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">makariri</a> hoki au nō reira kei te hoki au ki tōku kāinga.</strong><br>It is raining very hard and I am cold too so I am going home.<br><br>MIXED — the most effective:<br><strong>Kei te ua. He <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=makariri\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">makariri</a>, he <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=kino\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">kino</a> hoki. Nō reira kei te hoki au ki tōku kāinga ā ka kai au i tāku tina.</strong><br>It is raining. It is cold and unpleasant. So I am going home and I will eat my lunch.</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Short sentences work best for:<br>— Drama and surprise<br>— Starting a new idea<br>— Describing sudden action</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Long sentences work best for:<br>— Building a scene or explaining a reason<br>— Connecting several related ideas<br>— Showing cause and effect</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>You already have all the tools:<br>Short: <strong>Kei te</strong> + verb + person<br>Long: add <strong>ā / engari / nō reira</strong> to join<br>Longer still: add <strong>tino / ake / rawa atu</strong> for degree<br>Longest: combine everything with a description and a location</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>New word: <strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=hoki\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">hoki</a></strong> = also / too (placed after the word it modifies)</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Tip: Read your te reo aloud. If every sentence sounds the same length, mix it up — drop in a short one for impact, or join two ideas for flow.</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">We Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Kia Tūhono — Mix the lengths</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>I give you a long sentence — you shorten it. I give you a short one — you extend it.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Shorten this: &quot;Kei te haere au ki te toa ā kei te hoko au i tāku kai.&quot; → <strong>Kei te haere au ki te toa.</strong> When would each version be better?</li><li>Extend this: &quot;Kei te ua.&quot; Add a feeling, a reason, and a result. → <strong>Kei te ua tino nui. Kei te makariri au nō reira kei te noho au i roto.</strong></li><li>Write one short dramatic sentence followed by one long explanatory sentence about something happening right now in the classroom.</li><li>Read your two sentences aloud. Does the contrast work? Does the short one feel punchy and the long one feel full?</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Discussion prompt: Listen to these two versions read aloud: &quot;Kei te ua. Kei te makariri.&quot; vs &quot;Kei te ua tino nui ā kei te makariri hoki au.&quot; Which version would you use in a story? Which in a quick text message? Why?</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">You Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Māu Anō — Control your sentence length</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write deliberately — choose your lengths with purpose.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Write three short sentences (under 5 words each) about what is happening right now.</li><li>Now write one long sentence that combines all three ideas using ā and nō reira.</li><li>Write a &quot;mixed&quot; paragraph: short sentence, long sentence, short sentence. Read it aloud — notice the rhythm.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Describe your morning using exactly five sentences — make the first and last short and punchy, and the middle three progressively longer.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Thursday: Kōrero — Whakaaro ōrite, whakaaro rerekē — Agree and disagree":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Te Aho Arataki Marau — AO 2.3: Likes and dislikes with reasons · AO 1.6: Politeness conventions · AO 2.5: Expressing opinions</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Objectives</th><td class=\"lt-td\">AO 2.3 — Opinions &amp; reasons; AO 1.6 — Polite disagreement</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">I Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tāua Kōrero — Agreeing and disagreeing politely</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>You can now describe, compare, question, and explain. Today we learn one of the most important conversation skills — how to agree, disagree, and give your opinion politely. Listen to how this conversation flows between two students debating which subject is better.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\"><strong>A:</strong> He pai ake te pāngarau i te tuhituhi ki a au.<br><strong>B:</strong> Āe, he tika tāu — he pai te pāngarau engari he pai ake ki a au te tuhituhi.<br><strong>A:</strong> Nā te aha?<br><strong>B:</strong> Nō te mea ka taea e au te whakaaro nui ake i te wā e tuhituhi ana au.<br><strong>A:</strong> Ka pai tāu whakaaro. Engari ki a au, he uaua rawa atu te tuhituhi.<br><strong>B:</strong> Āe pea. He rerekē ā tātou whakaaro!<br><strong>A:</strong> He rerekē — ā he pai tērā!</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>New phrases for agreeing and disagreeing:</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>He <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=tika\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">tika</a> tāu.</strong> — You are right. / That is correct.<br><strong>Āe, engari…</strong> — Yes, but…<br><strong>Kāo, ki a au…</strong> — No, in my opinion…<br><strong>Ki a au nei…</strong> — In my opinion… / I think…<br><strong>Ka pai tāu <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=whakaaro\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">whakaaro</a>.</strong> — That is a good point.<br><strong>Nā te aha?</strong> — Why? / What for? (asking for a reason)<br><strong>Ka taea e au te…</strong> — I am able to… / I can…<br><strong>Āe <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=pea\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">pea</a>.</strong> — Maybe. / Perhaps.<br><strong>He <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=rerek%C4%93\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">rerekē</a> ā tātou <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=whakaaro\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">whakaaro</a>.</strong> — Our opinions are different.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>i te wā e… ana</strong> = when I am [doing something] — a new time clause</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Tip: He tika tāu = &quot;you are right&quot; — a gracious way to acknowledge someone before offering your own view. Use it even when you are about to disagree.</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">We Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Kia Tūhono — Agree, disagree, explain</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>I will make a claim. You agree or disagree, then give a reason. We keep the conversation going for at least four exchanges.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I say: <strong>He pai ake te tākaro i te ako.</strong> (Playing is better than learning.) Do you agree? Respond with Āe/Kāo + a reason.</li><li>You make a claim using <strong>He [adjective] ake te X i te Y ki a au.</strong> I respond with <strong>Ka pai tāu whakaaro, engari…</strong></li><li>Practice: <strong>Nā te aha?</strong> — ask me why I said what I said. I must give a reason using <strong>nō te mea.</strong></li><li>Close the exchange: one person says <strong>He rerekē ā tātou whakaaro</strong> — the other responds with <strong>Āe — ā he pai tērā!</strong></li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Discussion prompt: Why is &quot;He tika tāu, engari…&quot; a more powerful response than just &quot;Kāo&quot;? What does acknowledging the other person's point do for the conversation? Can you think of a time when this approach would be really useful?</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">You Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Māu Anō — Te taupatupatu — The debate</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>With a partner, hold a mini debate in te reo Māori. Use all the new phrases.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Choose a topic to debate — which is better: te kura (school) or te kāinga (home)? Sport or reading? Summer or winter?</li><li>Each person makes their opening claim using <strong>He pai ake te X i te Y ki a au nō te mea…</strong></li><li>Respond to your partner using <strong>He tika tāu, engari…</strong> or <strong>Āe pea, engari ki a au…</strong></li><li>✦ Bonus: Write up your debate as a short dialogue — at least 6 lines, using he tika tāu, nā te aha, nō te mea, and he rerekē ā tātou whakaaro.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Friday: Aromatawai — Quiz & Review":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Monday questions</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>&quot;Tokohia ngā kaiako?&quot; vs &quot;E hia ngā pukapuka?&quot; — Why are different question words used?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>Tokohia is formal, e hia is casual </li><li>Tokohia asks about people, e hia asks about things — te reo Māori distinguishes between them ✅</li><li>They mean the same thing and can be swapped freely </li><li>E hia is only used for numbers over ten </li></ul></li><li>How do you ask &quot;When are we going?&quot; (future) in te reo Māori?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>Nōnāhea tāua haere ai? </li><li>Āhea tāua haere ai? ✅</li><li>He aha te wā? </li><li>Ki hea tāua haere ai? </li></ul></li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tuesday questions</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>&quot;He pai rawa atu tēnei kai.&quot; What does rawa atu tell us?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>The food is quite good </li><li>The food is better than other food </li><li>The food is the best / extremely good ✅</li><li>The food is too good to eat </li></ul></li><li>How do you say &quot;That house is bigger&quot; using the comparative form?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>He nui tino tērā whare </li><li>He nui rawa atu tērā whare </li><li>He nui ake tērā whare ✅</li><li>He tino nui ake tērā whare </li></ul></li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Wednesday questions</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The word &quot;hoki&quot; in &quot;Kei te makariri hoki au&quot; means:<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>but </li><li>because </li><li>also / too ✅</li><li>however </li></ul></li><li>A writer uses one short sentence followed by a long one. What is the main effect?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>It fills more space on the page </li><li>The short sentence loses impact next to the long one </li><li>The short sentence creates impact and the long one adds explanation and flow ✅</li><li>Long sentences should always come first </li></ul></li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Thursday questions</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>&quot;He tika tāu, engari…&quot; — What is the speaker doing?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>Completely agreeing with the other person </li><li>Changing the subject </li><li>Acknowledging the other point before offering a different view ✅</li><li>Asking a question </li></ul></li><li>How do you say &quot;In my opinion, school is better than home&quot; in te reo Māori?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>Ki a au nei, he pai ake te kura i te kāinga ✅</li><li>He pai ake te kura i te kāinga ki a koe </li><li>Nō te mea he pai te kura </li><li>He tika tāu he pai te kura </li></ul></li></ul></div></div>"}}},"Week 6":{"label":"Day","children":{"Monday: Ngā tūāhua — Adverbs: how, when, where":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Te Aho Arataki Marau — AO 1.5: Location · AO 2.4: Time and weather · Grammar Level 1–2: Adverbs of manner, time, and place</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Objectives</th><td class=\"lt-td\">AO 2.4 — Time expressions; AO 1.5 — Location &amp; manner</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">I Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tāua Kōrero — Adding when, where, and how to your sentences</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>You can already build solid Kei te sentences. Today we learn how to add extra information — when something happens, where it happens, and how it happens. These are adverbs — and in te reo Māori they are beautifully flexible. Watch where they sit in a sentence.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\"><strong>HOW (manner):</strong><br><strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=%C4%81ta\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">āta</a></strong> — slowly / carefully / gently / gently<br><strong>tere</strong> — quickly / fast<br><strong>kaha</strong> — strongly / hard<br><strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=m%C4%81rie\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">mārie</a></strong> — calmly / peacefully / peacefully<br><br><strong>WHEN (time):</strong><br><strong>ināianei</strong> — right now<br><strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=%C4%81p%C5%8Dp%C5%8D\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">āpōpō</a></strong> — tomorrow<br><strong>inanahi</strong> — yesterday<br><strong>ā tōnā <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=w%C4%81\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">wā</a></strong> — soon / in due course / in due course<br><strong>i <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=muri\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">muri</a> mai</strong> — afterwards / later / later<br><br><strong>WHERE (place):</strong><br><strong>i konei</strong> — here<br><strong>i reira</strong> — there (at that place)<br><strong>i <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=runga\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">runga</a></strong> — above / on top / on top<br><strong>i <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=raro\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">raro</a></strong> — below / underneath / underneath<br><strong>i <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=waho\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">waho</a></strong> — outside<br><strong>i <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=roto\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">roto</a></strong> — inside</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>In te reo Māori, adverbs usually come RIGHT AFTER the verb they describe:</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Kei te haere tere ia.</strong><br>She is going quickly.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Kei te kōrero āta ia.</strong><br>He is speaking carefully/slowly.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Kei te mahi kaha ngā ākonga.</strong><br>The students are working hard.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Time words often come at the START of a sentence (like in English):</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Āpōpō ka haere au ki te toa.</strong><br>Tomorrow I will go to the shop.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Inanahi i haere ia ki te kura.</strong><br>Yesterday she went to school.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Place words usually come AFTER the verb phrase:<br><strong>Kei te noho ia i konei.</strong><br>She is sitting here.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Tip: Manner adverbs (how) go right after the verb. Time words can go at the start. Place words go at the end. When in doubt — say it aloud and feel what sounds right.</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">We Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Kia Tūhono — Add the adverb</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>I give you a bare sentence — you add the adverb I specify. Then we check where it landed.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Bare: &quot;Kei te haere ia.&quot; Add &quot;quickly&quot; → <strong>Kei te haere tere ia.</strong> Now add &quot;tomorrow&quot; at the start → <strong>Āpōpō ka haere tere ia.</strong></li><li>Bare: &quot;Kei te kōrero ngā tamariki.&quot; Add &quot;loudly/hard&quot; → <strong>Kei te kōrero kaha ngā tamariki.</strong></li><li>Bare: &quot;Kei te noho au.&quot; Add &quot;here&quot; → <strong>Kei te noho au i konei.</strong> Now add &quot;calmly&quot; → <strong>Kei te noho mārie au i konei.</strong></li><li>Build a full sentence with ALL THREE — time + verb + manner + place: &quot;Yesterday she worked hard outside.&quot; → <strong>Inanahi i mahi kaha ia i waho.</strong></li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Discussion prompt: In English, adverbs can move around a lot — &quot;She quickly ran&quot; or &quot;She ran quickly&quot; or &quot;Quickly, she ran.&quot; Does te reo Māori feel more fixed or more flexible? Try moving the adverb in one of your sentences and see if it still works.</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">You Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Māu Anō — Adverb challenge</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Add at least one adverb to every sentence you write today.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Write three sentences — one with a manner adverb, one with a time adverb, one with a place adverb.</li><li>Write one sentence using ALL THREE types: time + manner + place.</li><li>Take this bare sentence and add two adverbs: <strong>Kei te ako au.</strong> (I am learning.)</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write five sentences describing your ideal day — every sentence must include at least one adverb. Read them aloud and listen to how much richer they sound than bare sentences.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Tuesday: Ngā kupu pakiaka — Word families and word building":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Te Aho Arataki Marau — AO 1.7: Classroom language · AO 2.5: Vocabulary development · Grammar Level 1–2: kai-, whaka-, -nga, tāngata compound words</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Objectives</th><td class=\"lt-td\">AO 1.7 — Vocabulary &amp; word building; AO 2.5 — Extended vocabulary</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">I Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tāua Kōrero — How te reo Māori builds new words</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>One of the most powerful things about te reo Māori is that it builds new words from existing ones using prefixes and suffixes. Once you know a handful of roots, you can decode and create dozens of new words. Watch how this works.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\"><strong>kai-</strong> prefix = person who does something (agent noun):<br><strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=kaimahi\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">kaimahi</a></strong> — worker (mahi = work) (mahi = work)<br><strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=kaiako\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">kaiako</a></strong> — teacher (ako = learn/teach) (ako = learn/teach)<br><strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=kaip%C4%81nui\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">kaipānui</a></strong> — reader (pānui = read) (pānui = read)<br><strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=kaituhituhi\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">kaituhituhi</a></strong> — writer (tuhituhi = write) (tuhituhi = write)<br><strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=kaik%C5%8Drero\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">kaikōrero</a></strong> — speaker (kōrero = speak) (kōrero = speak)<br><strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=kait%C4%81karo\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">kaitākaro</a></strong> — player (tākaro = play) (tākaro = play)<br><br><strong>whaka-</strong> prefix = to cause or make something happen:<br><strong>whakarongo</strong> — to listen (cause yourself to hear)<br><strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=whakam%C4%81ori\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">whakamāori</a></strong> — to translate into Māori into Māori<br><strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=whakaaro\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">whakaaro</a></strong> — thought / to think<br><strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=whakapai\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">whakapai</a></strong> — to improve / make better / make better<br><br><strong>-nga</strong> suffix = the act of / the result of:<br><strong>kōrero</strong> → <strong>kōrerorero</strong>/<strong>kōrerotanga</strong> — conversation<br><strong>ako</strong> → <strong>akoranga</strong> — lesson / learning<br><strong>mahi</strong> → <strong>mahinga</strong> — the work / the doing</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This is called word building — pakiaka (root) + prefix/suffix = new word.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>You can BUILD words you have never seen before:<br><strong>kai-</strong> + <strong>omaoma</strong> (run) = <strong>kaiomaoma</strong> — a runner<br><strong>kai-</strong> + <strong>kaukau</strong> (swim) = <strong>kaikaukau</strong> — a swimmer<br><strong>whaka-</strong> + <strong>nui</strong> (big) = <strong>whakanui</strong> — to celebrate / make bigger<br><strong>whaka-</strong> + <strong>iti</strong> (small) = <strong>whakaiti</strong> — to be humble / make smaller</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>And you can DECODE words you have never learned:<br>If you see <strong>kaitiaki</strong> — you know kai- means &quot;person who does&quot; and tiaki means &quot;to guard/protect&quot; → kaitiaki = guardian/protector</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>You have been a kaitiaki of your own learning all term.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Tip: See kai- at the start of a word → it is a person who does that action. See whaka- → something is being caused or made to happen. These two prefixes unlock hundreds of words.</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">We Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Kia Tūhono — Build and decode</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>I give you a root word — you build a new word using kai- or whaka-. Then I give you an unknown word — you decode it.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Root: <strong>haere</strong> (go). Build: kai- + haere = ? <strong>Kaihaere</strong> could mean &quot;traveller / one who goes.&quot; Does that feel right?</li><li>Root: <strong>pai</strong> (good). Build: whaka- + pai = <strong>whakapai</strong> — to make better / improve. Use it: <strong>Kei te whakapai ia i tāna mahi.</strong></li><li>Decode: <strong>kaitiaki</strong> — kai- = person who does + tiaki = guard/protect. What does kaitiaki mean? Why is this an important word in Aotearoa?</li><li>Decode: <strong>whakaaro</strong> — whaka- = cause/make + aro = face toward / attend to. So whakaaro = thought (turning your mind toward something). Use it: <strong>He pai tāu whakaaro.</strong></li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Discussion prompt: English builds words too — &quot;teacher&quot; comes from &quot;teach&quot;, &quot;runner&quot; from &quot;run&quot;. But te reo Māori is much more systematic — kai- ALWAYS means person who does. Why might a systematic system be easier to learn than the English system of -er, -or, -ist, -ian?</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">You Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Māu Anō — Word builder</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Practise building and using new words from roots you already know.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Build three new kai- words from verbs you know. Write each with its meaning.</li><li>Build two whaka- words. Use each in a sentence.</li><li>Decode this word you have never seen: <strong>kaiwhakaako</strong>. What does it mean? (whaka- + ako = to cause learning, kai- = person who does it)</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write a paragraph about your class using at least four kai- words and one whaka- word. E.g. &quot;In our class there is a kaiako, many kaipānui, and some kaituhituhi...&quot;</li></ul></div></div>"},"Wednesday: Expanding sentences — who, where, when phrases":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Te Aho Arataki Marau — AO 1.5: Location · AO 2.4: Time · Grammar Level 1–2: Expanding noun phrases and adding circumstantial information</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Objectives</th><td class=\"lt-td\">AO 1.5 — Location &amp; circumstance; AO 2.4 — Time phrases in sentences</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">I Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tāua Kōrero — Packing more into every sentence</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This week you learned adverbs (how/when/where) and word building. Today we combine them — learning how to pack time, place, and participant information into a single rich sentence. Think of it like adding layers to a painting.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Start bare:<br><strong>Kei te ako ngā tamariki.</strong><br>The children are learning.<br><br>Add WHERE:<br><strong>Kei te ako ngā tamariki i te akomanga.</strong><br>The children are learning in the classroom.<br><br>Add WHEN:<br><strong>Ināianei kei te ako ngā tamariki i te akomanga.</strong><br>Right now the children are learning in the classroom.<br><br>Add HOW:<br><strong>Ināianei kei te ako kaha ngā tamariki i te akomanga.</strong><br>Right now the children are learning hard in the classroom.<br><br>Add WHO WITH (using me — with):<br><strong>Ināianei kei te ako kaha ngā tamariki me tō rātou kaiako i te akomanga.</strong><br>Right now the children are learning hard with their teacher in the classroom.</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The layering order in a te reo Māori sentence:</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>[TIME] + Kei te + [verb] + [manner] + [person/subject] + [with whom: me] + [where: i/ki]</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>New word: <strong>me</strong> (when meaning &quot;with&quot; — different from &quot;me&quot; meaning &quot;and&quot;!) <br>→ <strong>Kei te haere au me ōku hoa.</strong> I am going with my friends.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>New possessive: <strong>tō rātou</strong> = their (ō-category, three or more)<br><strong>tā rātou</strong> = their (ā-category, three or more)</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>You can always strip back to the bare sentence — the extra layers are optional. But adding even one layer makes your language significantly richer.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Tip: Build sentences in layers — start bare, then add one piece at a time. You can always stop when it feels complete. More layers = more precise, but even two layers is a great sentence.</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">We Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Kia Tūhono — Layer by layer</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>We will build one sentence together, adding a new layer with each step. Everyone contributes one layer.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Layer 1 — bare: <strong>Kei te tākaro ngā tamariki.</strong> The children are playing.</li><li>Layer 2 — add where: <strong>Kei te tākaro ngā tamariki i waho.</strong> ...outside.</li><li>Layer 3 — add how: <strong>Kei te tākaro kaha ngā tamariki i waho.</strong> ...hard outside.</li><li>Layer 4 — add time at the start: <strong>Ināianei kei te tākaro kaha ngā tamariki i waho.</strong> Right now...</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Discussion prompt: At which layer did the sentence start to feel really alive? Was there a point where adding more felt like too much? What is the &quot;just right&quot; amount of information for a te reo sentence?</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">You Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Māu Anō — The layering challenge</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Take a bare sentence and build it up — then strip it back to find the best version.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Start with <strong>Kei te kai ia.</strong> Add three layers one at a time — write all four versions.</li><li>Start with <strong>Kei te kōrero ngā ākonga.</strong> Add time, manner, and place.</li><li>Now take your fullest sentence and remove layers until you have the most PRECISE version — not longest, best.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Describe what is happening outside your window right now (real or imagined) in three sentences — bare, medium, and fully layered. Read all three aloud and decide which you prefer.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Thursday: Kōrero — He kōrero hou mō tēnei wiki — This week in conversation":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Te Aho Arataki Marau — AO 2.4: Time and events · AO 2.3: Extended communication with reasons · AO 1.5: Location</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Objectives</th><td class=\"lt-td\">AO 2.4 — Talking about events &amp; time; AO 2.3 — Extended reasons &amp; opinions</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">I Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tāua Kōrero — Talking about your week</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Today we use everything from this week — adverbs, word-building words, layered sentences — in a real conversation about what has happened and what is coming up. This is the kind of conversation you might have with a friend on a Friday afternoon.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\"><strong>A:</strong> Tēnā koe! He pēhea tōu wiki?<br><strong>B:</strong> He tino pai! I mahi kaha au i tēnei wiki. I ako au i ngā kupu hou e tino pai ana ki a au.<br><strong>A:</strong> He aha ngā mea hou i ako ai koe?<br><strong>B:</strong> I ako au i ngā tūāhua — pēnei i te &quot;tere&quot; me te &quot;āta&quot;. Ka taea e au te kōrero ināianei me ērā kupu.<br><strong>A:</strong> Ka rawe! Āpōpō he aha āu mahi?<br><strong>B:</strong> Āpōpō ka haere tere au ki te hākinakina me ōku hoa. Ka tākaro kaha mātou i waho.<br><strong>A:</strong> He pai ake te hākinakina i te noho ki te kāinga ki a au!<br><strong>B:</strong> Āe! He tika tāu — engari he pai hoki te okioki!</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>New words and phrases from this conversation:</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>He pēhea tōu wiki?</strong> — How was your week?<br><strong>e tino pai ana ki a au</strong> — that I really liked / that are very good for me<br><strong>ngā mea hou</strong> — the new things<br><strong>i ako ai koe</strong> — that you learned (relative clause — more on this in Term 2!)<br><strong>pēnei i te</strong> — like / such as<br><strong>Ka taea e au te kōrero</strong> — I am able to speak<br><strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=h%C4%81kinakina\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">hākinakina</a></strong> — sport / physical activity / physical activity<br><strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=okioki\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">okioki</a></strong> — rest / to rest / to rest<br><strong>ērā kupu</strong> — those words (ērā = those, plural of tērā)</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Notice how the conversation uses adverbs naturally — tere, kaha, tino pai, ināianei, āpōpō, i waho. This is everything from this week embedded in real speech.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Tip: He pēhea tōu wiki? — How was your week? Learn this cold. It is a conversation starter you will use every single Friday for the rest of the year.</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">We Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Kia Tūhono — Friday kōrero</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Build the &quot;how was your week&quot; conversation step by step. I start — you respond, then lead the next exchange.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I ask: <strong>He pēhea tōu wiki?</strong> You answer with at least two sentences — what you did and how (use an adverb).</li><li>I ask: <strong>He aha ngā mea hou i ako ai koe?</strong> — What new things did you learn? Name something from this week using a kai- word if you can.</li><li>I say something I liked: <strong>I pai ki a au te ako i ngā tūāhua.</strong> You agree or add your own view using <strong>He tika tāu / Āe, engari…</strong></li><li>Close: I ask <strong>Āpōpō he aha āu mahi?</strong> You tell me your plans — use āpōpō, a verb, and a place.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Discussion prompt: He pēhea tōu wiki? is a question you could ask and answer every Friday in te reo. If you did that for the whole year, what do you think would happen to your fluency? Why does regular, short conversation practice matter more than occasional long study sessions?</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">You Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Māu Anō — Your own Friday kōrero</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>With a partner, have a full &quot;end of week&quot; conversation using everything from Weeks 1–6.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Ask and answer: <strong>He pēhea tōu wiki?</strong> Both partners answer in at least three sentences.</li><li>Each person shares one new thing they learned using a kai- or whaka- word.</li><li>Make a plan for tomorrow using <strong>Āpōpō ka…</strong> — include a place and a manner adverb.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write up your full conversation as a dialogue — at least 8 lines. Use adverbs, word-building words, layered sentences, and the agree/disagree phrases from Week 5.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Friday: Aromatawai — Quiz & Review":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Monday questions</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>&quot;Kei te haere tere ia.&quot; Where does the adverb &quot;tere&quot; sit in this sentence?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>Before the verb </li><li>After the verb and before the subject ✅</li><li>At the start of the sentence </li><li>After the subject </li></ul></li><li>&quot;Inanahi i haere ia ki te kura.&quot; What type of adverb is &quot;inanahi&quot;?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>Manner — how she went </li><li>Place — where she went </li><li>Time — when she went ✅</li><li>Degree — how much she went </li></ul></li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tuesday questions</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The prefix &quot;kai-&quot; in te reo Māori makes a word that means:<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>to cause something to happen </li><li>a place where something happens </li><li>a person who does that action ✅</li><li>the result of an action </li></ul></li><li>What does &quot;whakapai&quot; most likely mean?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>a person who is good </li><li>to make something better / improve ✅</li><li>the goodness of something </li><li>a very good thing </li></ul></li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Wednesday questions</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>&quot;Kei te haere au me ōku hoa.&quot; What does &quot;me&quot; mean in this sentence?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>and (joining two sentences) </li><li>but </li><li>with ✅</li><li>also </li></ul></li><li>In a layered te reo sentence, where does a TIME word usually go?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>After the verb </li><li>After the subject </li><li>At the start of the sentence ✅</li><li>At the very end </li></ul></li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Thursday questions</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>How do you ask &quot;How was your week?&quot; in te reo Māori?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>He aha tōu wiki? </li><li>Kei te pēhea tōu wiki? </li><li>He pēhea tōu wiki? ✅</li><li>Ko wai tōu wiki? </li></ul></li><li>&quot;Ka taea e au te kōrero.&quot; What does this mean?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>I want to speak </li><li>I used to be able to speak </li><li>I am able to speak ✅</li><li>I am speaking quickly </li></ul></li></ul></div></div>"}}},"Week 7":{"label":"Day","children":{"Monday: Ngā rārangi — Lists, colons, and ko ēnei/ko ērā":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Te Aho Arataki Marau — AO 1.3: Number and reference · AO 2.5: Vocabulary and description · Grammar Level 1–2: Ko ēnei/ko ērā, listing structures</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Objectives</th><td class=\"lt-td\">AO 1.3 — Reference &amp; listing; AO 2.5 — Descriptive vocabulary</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">I Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tāua Kōrero — Making lists and introducing them</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>You have been building individual sentences well. Today we learn how to introduce a list of items — giving your listener a heads-up that several things are coming. In English you use a colon for this. Te reo Māori has its own elegant way of doing the same thing.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\"><strong>Ko ēnei</strong> — these are (introducing a list of things near you)<br><strong>Ko ērā</strong> — those are (introducing a list of things away from you)<br><strong>Ko ēnei ōku mea:</strong> — These are my things:<br><br><strong>Ko ēnei ōku mea: he pene, he pukapuka, he kete.</strong><br>These are my things: a pen, a book, a bag.<br><br><strong>Ko ērā ngā āhuatanga pai o tōku kura:</strong><br>Those are the good things about my school:<br><strong>he nui ngā hoa, he pai ngā kaiako, he ātaahua ngā papa tākaro.</strong><br>there are many friends, the teachers are good, the playgrounds are beautiful.</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>You already know tēnei/tēnā/tērā (this/that — singular).<br>Now meet their PLURAL cousins:</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=%C4%93nei\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">ēnei</a></strong> — these (plural of tēnei, near me) (plural of tēnei, near me)<br><strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=%C4%93n%C4%81\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">ēnā</a></strong> — those (plural of tēnā, near you) (plural of tēnā, near you)<br><strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=%C4%93r%C4%81\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">ērā</a></strong> — those (plural of tērā, away from both) (plural of tērā, away from both)</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>When used with Ko to introduce a list:<br><strong>Ko ēnei…</strong> = These are… (here is the list)</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>New descriptive words for the list:<br><strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=%C4%81taahua\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">ātaahua</a></strong> — beautiful / wonderful / wonderful<br><strong>papa tākaro</strong> — playground<br><strong>ngā kaiako</strong> — the teachers (plural — you built kaiako from kai- in Week 6!)</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The list items after the colon each start with <strong>he</strong> — just like individual He sentences, strung together with commas.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Tip: Ko ēnei/ko ērā = &quot;these are / those are&quot; — use them whenever you want to introduce a list. Think of them as te reo's colon.</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">We Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Kia Tūhono — Build lists together</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>I give you a topic — you introduce it with Ko ēnei or Ko ērā and then list three items.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Topic: things on your desk. → <strong>Ko ēnei ōku mea i runga i tōku tēpu: he pene, he pukapuka, he aho.</strong> (aho = ruler)</li><li>Topic: the good things about te reo Māori. → <strong>Ko ēnei ngā āhuatanga pai o te reo Māori: he ātaahua, he ngāwari, he tino pai hoki.</strong></li><li>Now try Ko ērā — things on the far side of the room: <strong>Ko ērā ngā mea i reira: he…, he…, he…</strong></li><li>Challenge: use Ko ēnei to introduce a list of three things you did yesterday — use the past tense I for each item in the list.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Discussion prompt: Ko ēnei works like a colon in English writing — it signals &quot;here comes the list&quot;. Can you think of other situations in daily life where introducing a list first would be useful? When would Ko ērā be better than Ko ēnei?</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">You Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Māu Anō — List maker</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Use Ko ēnei or Ko ērā to introduce lists in te reo.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Write a Ko ēnei sentence introducing three things you like: <strong>Ko ēnei ōku mea pai: he…, he…, he…</strong></li><li>Write a Ko ērā sentence about three things you can see across the room.</li><li>Write a list about your school using ātaahua, pai, and nui as describing words for each item.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write a &quot;Ko ēnei ngā āhuatanga pai o…&quot; paragraph about your town, your class, or your family. At least five items in your list.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Tuesday: Ngā wā — Mixing past and present tense fluently":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Te Aho Arataki Marau — AO 2.4: Time · AO 2.3: Extended communication · Grammar Level 1–2: I (past) and Kei te (present) used in the same passage</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Objectives</th><td class=\"lt-td\">AO 2.4 — Tense &amp; time; AO 2.3 — Extended communication</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">I Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tāua Kōrero — Switching between past and present</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>You know Kei te for now and I for the past. Real language constantly moves between tenses — you say what happened, then describe what is happening now, then say what you thought about it. Today we practise switching fluently without losing track of when things are happening.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Watch how tenses shift in this short account:<br><br><strong>Inanahi i haere au ki te hākinakina me ōku hoa.</strong><br>Yesterday I went to sport with my friends.<br><br><strong>I tākaro kaha mātou — he tino pai!</strong><br>We played hard — it was very good!<br><br><strong>Ināianei kei te ngenge au engari kei te hari hoki au.</strong><br>Right now I am tired but I am also happy.<br><br><strong>He pai ake te hākinakina i te noho ki te kāinga ki a au.</strong><br>Sport is better than staying home in my opinion.</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The time word at the START of a sentence tells your listener which tense is coming:</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Inanahi</strong> (yesterday) → use <strong>I</strong><br><strong>Ināianei</strong> (right now) → use <strong>Kei te</strong><br><strong>Āpōpō</strong> (tomorrow) → use <strong>Ka</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>New word: <strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=ngenge\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">ngenge</a></strong> — tired<br>New word: <strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=hari\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">hari</a></strong> — happy / joyful / joyful</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Think of time words as SIGNALS — they prime your listener for the tense before the verb even arrives. This is why putting time words at the START is so powerful in te reo Māori.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>When there is NO time word, the tense marker (Kei te / I / Ka) does all the work on its own.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Tip: Time word at the start = instant tense signal. Inanahi → I. Ināianei → Kei te. Āpōpō → Ka. Your listener knows what is coming before the verb.</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">We Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Kia Tūhono — Tense switching</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>I will give you a sequence of events across two time periods. You build each sentence with the right tense marker.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Yesterday: you went to the shop. Now: you are eating what you bought. → <strong>Inanahi i haere au ki te toa. Ināianei kei te kai au i tāku kai hou.</strong></li><li>Yesterday: she was tired. Now: she is happy. → <strong>Inanahi i ngenge ia. Ināianei kei te hari ia.</strong></li><li>Yesterday: the children played outside. Now: they are sitting inside. → <strong>Inanahi i tākaro ngā tamariki i waho. Ināianei kei te noho rātou i roto.</strong></li><li>Now try WITHOUT time words — just the tense markers. Tell me two things that happened yesterday and one thing happening right now.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Discussion prompt: In English we rely on verb endings to show tense — &quot;played&quot; vs &quot;play&quot;. In te reo Māori the verb never changes — only the tense marker changes. Which system do you think is easier for a learner? Why?</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">You Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Māu Anō — Past and present</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write a short account that moves between past and present — at least four sentences.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Write two past-tense sentences about something you did inanahi (yesterday).</li><li>Write two present-tense sentences about what you are doing ināianei (right now).</li><li>Combine them into a flowing four-sentence account — use time words to signal the switches.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write a six-sentence account of your day so far — start in the past (this morning), arrive in the present (right now), and end with a Ka sentence about what you will do next.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Wednesday: Complex sentences — i te wā, ahakoa, mehemea":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Te Aho Arataki Marau — AO 2.3: Extended communication with reasons · Grammar Level 1–2: Subordinate clauses — when, although, if</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Objectives</th><td class=\"lt-td\">AO 2.3 — Complex reasoning; AO 2.4 — Time clauses</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">I Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tāua Kōrero — When, although, if — subordinate clauses</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Last week you learned to join sentences with ā, engari, and nō reira. Today we go one step further — subordinate clauses. These are mini sentences that add a condition, a time, or a concession to your main sentence. Three small words unlock a huge range of complex ideas.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\"><strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=i+te+w%C4%81\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">i te wā</a></strong> — when (referring to a time in the past or ongoing) (referring to a time in the past or ongoing)<br><strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=ahakoa\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">ahakoa</a></strong> — although / even though / even though<br><strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=mehemea\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">mehemea</a></strong> — if / supposing that / supposing that<br><br><strong>I te wā i haere au ki te kura, i kite au i tōku hoa.</strong><br>When I went to school, I saw my friend.<br><br><strong>Ahakoa kei te ua, kei te tākaro ngā tamariki i waho.</strong><br>Although it is raining, the children are playing outside.<br><br><strong>Mehemea ka haere koe ki te toa, hoko mai he miraka māku.</strong><br>If you go to the shop, buy some milk for me.</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The pattern for each:</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>i te wā</strong> + [past clause] + , + [main clause]<br>→ When [this happened], [that happened].</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>ahakoa</strong> + [clause] + , + [main clause]<br>→ Although [this is true], [this still happens].</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>mehemea</strong> + [Ka clause] + , + [instruction or result]<br>→ If [this happens], [then do/say this].</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>New words:<br><strong>i <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=kite\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">kite</a></strong> — saw / noticed (past of kite = see/find) / noticed (past of kite = see/find)<br><strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=hoko\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">hoko</a> mai</strong> — buy and bring back (hoko = buy, mai = towards speaker) (hoko = buy, mai = towards speaker)<br><strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=m%C4%81ku\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">māku</a></strong> — for me (mā = for + ku = me) (mā = for + ku = me)</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>These three words turn simple sentences into sophisticated ones — and they all work the same way: subordinate clause first, comma, main clause.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Tip: Subordinate clause FIRST, then comma, then main clause. The smaller idea sets up the bigger one. Same rule for i te wā, ahakoa, and mehemea.</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">We Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Kia Tūhono — Build complex sentences</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>I give you two simple sentences. You join them with i te wā, ahakoa, or mehemea — whichever fits.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>&quot;She was tired.&quot; + &quot;She finished her work.&quot; — when? → <strong>I te wā i ngenge ia, i mutu tonu tāna mahi.</strong></li><li>&quot;It is cold.&quot; + &quot;We are swimming.&quot; — although? → <strong>Ahakoa he makariri, kei te kaukau mātou.</strong></li><li>&quot;You go to the shop.&quot; + &quot;Please buy bread.&quot; — if? → <strong>Mehemea ka haere koe ki te toa, hoko mai he parāoa māku.</strong></li><li>Now write your own: use ahakoa to say something surprising — something that happens DESPITE an obstacle.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Discussion prompt: Ahakoa is particularly powerful — it signals that something unexpected is happening despite an obstacle. Think of a situation in your life right now where ahakoa would be the perfect word. Can you say it in te reo?</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">You Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Māu Anō — Subordinate clause challenge</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write three complex sentences — one with each new subordinating word.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Write a sentence using <strong>i te wā</strong> about something that happened this week.</li><li>Write a sentence using <strong>ahakoa</strong> about something surprising or unexpected.</li><li>Write a sentence using <strong>mehemea</strong> — give someone a condition and an instruction.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write a four-sentence paragraph that uses all three: i te wā, ahakoa, and mehemea. Tell a mini-story that has a condition, a time clause, and a surprising twist.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Thursday: Kōrero — He kōrero mō tērā rā — Telling a story about the past":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Te Aho Arataki Marau — AO 2.3: Extended communication · AO 2.4: Time and sequencing · AO 1.5: Location</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Objectives</th><td class=\"lt-td\">AO 2.3 — Storytelling &amp; sequencing; AO 2.4 — Past events &amp; time</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">I Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tāua Kōrero — Telling what happened</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Today we put this whole week together — lists, tense switching, and complex clauses — into a story about something that happened. Storytelling is one of the most important things you can do in any language. Listen to how these sentences flow.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\"><strong>Inanahi he rā tino pai.</strong><br>Yesterday was a very good day.<br><br><strong>I tīmata i te ata — i omaoma au i waho me ōku hoa.</strong><br>It started in the morning — I ran outside with my friends.<br><br><strong>I te wā i ngenge mātou, i hoki mātou ki roto ā i kai mātou.</strong><br>When we were tired, we went back inside and ate.<br><br><strong>Ko ēnei ngā mea i kainga e mātou: he parāoa, he āporo, he miraka.</strong><br>These are the things we ate: bread, an apple, milk.<br><br><strong>Ahakoa i makariri ā i ua hoki, he tino pai tō mātou rā.</strong><br>Although it was cold and raining too, our day was very good.<br><br><strong>Mehemea ka pērā anō āpōpō, ka haere anō mātou!</strong><br>If it is the same again tomorrow, we will go again!</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Notice what this story does:</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Opens with a scene-setting sentence (He rā tino pai)</li><li>Uses I te wā for the turning point</li><li>Uses Ko ēnei to introduce a food list</li><li>Uses ahakoa for the surprising twist</li><li>Closes with mehemea — looking forward</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>New words:<br><strong>i te ata</strong> — in the morning<br><strong>i tīmata</strong> — it started / began<br><strong>i kainga</strong> — was eaten (passive — the eating happened TO the food)<br><strong>e mātou</strong> — by us (the agent after a passive verb)<br><strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=p%C4%93r%C4%81\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">pērā</a> anō</strong> — the same again / like that again<br><strong>ka haere anō</strong> — will go again</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Tip: Every good story has a beginning (scene-set), a middle (what happened, including a surprise or challenge), and an end (result or look forward). Use your subordinate clauses for the middle.</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">We Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Kia Tūhono — Build the story together</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>We will build a shared class story about an imaginary school trip. I provide the skeleton — you fill in the details.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Open with a scene-setter: <strong>Inanahi i haere mātou ki te…</strong> (somewhere exciting — you choose the place).</li><li>What happened first? Use I + verb + a time word: <strong>I te ata, i… mātou.</strong></li><li>Add a surprise using ahakoa: <strong>Ahakoa…, i… tonu mātou.</strong> Something went wrong but you carried on.</li><li>Close with a Ko ēnei list of the best things about the trip, then a mehemea sentence about doing it again.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Discussion prompt: Good storytellers control time — they slow down for the important parts and speed up through the routine ones. Which sentence in our story felt most important? How could you make it slower and more detailed using the tools you have?</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">You Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Māu Anō — Tāu kōrero — Your story</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Tell a story about something that actually happened to you recently — entirely in te reo Māori.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Write an opening scene-setter: one He or I sentence that sets the time and mood.</li><li>Write the middle: at least two sentences using I, plus one i te wā or ahakoa sentence.</li><li>Include a Ko ēnei or Ko ērā list somewhere in your story.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write your full story — at least six sentences. Use past tense throughout, include a complex clause, a list, and close with a mehemea or Ka sentence looking forward.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Friday: Aromatawai — Quiz & Review":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Monday questions</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>&quot;Ko ēnei ōku mea: he pene, he pukapuka, he kete.&quot; What is the role of &quot;Ko ēnei&quot; here?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>It means &quot;these things are bad&quot; </li><li>It introduces a list — like a colon in English ✅</li><li>It is a question asking what these things are </li><li>It means &quot;I have a pen, book and bag&quot; </li></ul></li><li>What is the plural of tēnei?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>tēnā </li><li>tērā </li><li>ēnei ✅</li><li>ēnā </li></ul></li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tuesday questions</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Which tense marker do you use after the time word &quot;Inanahi&quot;?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>Kei te </li><li>Ka </li><li>I ✅</li><li>E…ana </li></ul></li><li>&quot;Ināianei kei te hari ia.&quot; What does hari mean?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>tired </li><li>hungry </li><li>happy / joyful ✅</li><li>cold </li></ul></li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Wednesday questions</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>&quot;Ahakoa kei te ua, kei te tākaro ngā tamariki i waho.&quot; What does ahakoa signal?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>A time — when the rain started </li><li>A cause — the rain made them play </li><li>A contrast — they play DESPITE the rain ✅</li><li>A condition — if it rains they will play </li></ul></li><li>&quot;Mehemea ka haere koe ki te toa, hoko mai he miraka māku.&quot; What does māku mean?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>for you </li><li>for them </li><li>for me ✅</li><li>for us </li></ul></li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Thursday questions</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>&quot;I te wā i ngenge mātou, i hoki mātou ki roto.&quot; What is the function of &quot;i te wā&quot; here?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>It means although we were tired </li><li>It means if we are tired </li><li>It sets a time — WHEN we were tired, something happened ✅</li><li>It means we were very tired </li></ul></li><li>In a te reo story, which sentence type works best to open with a scene-setting mood?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>A mehemea (if) sentence </li><li>A He or I sentence that sets the time and feeling ✅</li><li>A Ko ēnei list </li><li>A question using Ko wai </li></ul></li></ul></div></div>"}}},"Week 8":{"label":"Day","children":{"Monday: Ngā kupu ōrite — Words that sound alike":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Te Aho Arataki Marau — AO 1.6: Politeness conventions · AO 2.5: Vocabulary development · Grammar Level 1–2: Distinguishing similar-sounding words, macrons</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Objectives</th><td class=\"lt-td\">AO 2.5 — Vocabulary precision; AO 1.6 — Clear communication</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">I Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tāua Kōrero — Words that trip people up</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>As your vocabulary grows, you will notice some words look or sound very similar but mean completely different things. Today we look at the most common confusable pairs — and the key things that make them different. Pay close attention to the macrons (the long vowel marks) — they change everything.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\"><strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=w%C4%81\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">wā</a></strong> (long ā) — time / period / space / period / space<br><strong>wa</strong> (short a) — not a standard word on its own — macron matters!<br><br><strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=k%C4%93mu\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">kēmu</a></strong> — game<br><strong>kemu</strong> — would not mean game without the macron<br><br><strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=m%C5%8D\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">mō</a></strong> — for / about / concerning / concerning<br><strong>mo</strong> — a type of lizard (unlikely but real!)<br><br><strong>nō</strong> — belonging to (ō-category possession)<br><strong>nō</strong> vs <strong>nā</strong> — ō vs ā category — these two trip everyone up<br><br><strong>ō</strong> — of (ō-category connector)<br><strong>ā</strong> — of (ā-category connector)<br><br><strong>tōku</strong> — my (ō-category)<br><strong>tāku</strong> — my (ā-category)</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The most important thing about macrons:</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>A macron (the line over a vowel: ā ē ī ō ū) makes that vowel LONG — held twice as long as a short vowel. It is not just a spelling rule — it changes the MEANING of the word completely.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Most confusable pairs in te reo come from:</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>Macron vs no macron</strong>: <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=keke\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">keke</a> (cake) vs <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=k%C4%93k%C4%93\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">kēkē</a> (armpit!)</li><li><strong>Ō vs ā category</strong>: tōku vs tāku, nō vs nā</li><li><strong>Similar-looking prepositions</strong>: ki (to), i (at/past), o (of-ō), a (of-ā)</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>A useful pair to know:<br><strong>He aha tō ingoa?</strong> — What is your name? (tō = your, short form of tōu)<br><strong>He aha tā mahi?</strong> — What is the work/task? (tā = the, ā-category)</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>New word: <strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=keke\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">keke</a></strong> — cake · <strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=k%C4%93k%C4%93\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">kēkē</a></strong> — armpit (classic example!)</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Tip: When in doubt about ō or ā — ask yourself: is this thing senior to me and connected to identity (ō), or is it something I control and do things with (ā)? That feeling will guide you right more often than not.</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">We Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Kia Tūhono — Spot the difference</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>I will give you two similar-looking words or phrases. You tell me the difference and use each correctly in a sentence.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>tōku</strong> vs <strong>tāku</strong> — which one for &quot;my book&quot;? Which for &quot;my name&quot;? → tāku pukapuka · tōku ingoa</li><li><strong>nō</strong> vs <strong>nā</strong> — &quot;Whose is this house?&quot; (house = senior, ō) → <strong>Nō wai tēnei whare?</strong> &quot;Whose is this food?&quot; (food = ā) → <strong>Nā wai tēnei kai?</strong></li><li><strong>ki</strong> vs <strong>i</strong> — &quot;I am going TO school&quot; vs &quot;I went FROM/AT school&quot; → <strong>Kei te haere au ki te kura</strong> · <strong>I te kura au inanahi</strong></li><li><strong>keke</strong> vs <strong>kēkē</strong> — &quot;I want to eat the cake&quot; → <strong>Kei te hiahia au ki te kai i te keke.</strong> (NOT kēkē — very different!)</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Discussion prompt: Why do you think macrons matter so much in te reo Māori? If you left the macron off &quot;nō&quot;, what confusion might happen? Can you think of English words where a single letter changes the meaning completely — similar to how macrons work?</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">You Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Māu Anō — Precision practice</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write sentences that deliberately use confusable words correctly.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Write two sentences — one using <strong>tōku</strong> correctly and one using <strong>tāku</strong> correctly.</li><li>Write a sentence using <strong>mō</strong> (for/about): <strong>He kōrero mō te kura tēnei.</strong> (This is a talk about school.)</li><li>Write a sentence using <strong>ki</strong> (to) and one using <strong>i</strong> (at/past location) — make them clearly different.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write a short paragraph (4 sentences) that includes tōku, tāku, nō, and mō — all used correctly. Underline each one.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Tuesday: Ngā kupu tūāhua — Prepositions: i, ki, o, a, nō, mō, hei":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Te Aho Arataki Marau — AO 1.5: Location · AO 2.2: Possessions · Grammar Level 1–2: Prepositions of location, direction, possession, purpose</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Objectives</th><td class=\"lt-td\">AO 1.5 — Prepositions of location &amp; direction; AO 2.2 — Prepositions of possession &amp; purpose</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">I Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tāua Kōrero — The small words that hold everything together</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Prepositions are the small words that show relationships between things — where something is, where it is going, who it belongs to, what it is for. In te reo Māori, a small set of prepositions does enormous work. Today we look at them all together so you can see the full picture.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\"><strong>i</strong> — at (location in past) / from / by<br><strong>ki</strong> — to / towards / into<br><strong>kei</strong> — at (location right now)<br><strong>o / ō</strong> — of (ō-category possession/connection)<br><strong>a / ā</strong> — of (ā-category possession/connection)<br><strong>nō</strong> — belonging to (ō-category) / from (origin)<br><strong>nā</strong> — belonging to (ā-category) / by/through (agent)<br><strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=m%C5%8D\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">mō</a></strong> — for / about / concerning<br><strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=m%C4%81\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">mā</a></strong> — for (ā-category recipient)<br><strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=hei\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">hei</a></strong> — at (future location) / as / for (purpose) / as / for (purpose)<br><strong>ko</strong> — identity marker (not a preposition but works like one)</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>See them in action:</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Kei te noho ia i Ōtautahi.</strong> — She lives in Christchurch. (i = at, general location)<br><strong>Kei te haere au ki Ōtautahi.</strong> — I am going to Christchurch. (ki = to)<br><strong>Kei Ōtautahi ia.</strong> — She is in Christchurch right now. (kei = at, present)<br><strong>He kōrero mō Aotearoa tēnei.</strong> — This is a talk about New Zealand. (mō = about)<br><strong>Hoko mai he miraka māku.</strong> — Buy some milk for me. (mā = for, ā-recipient)<br><strong>He taonga hei homai māu.</strong> — A gift to give to you. (hei = for the purpose of)</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>New place name: <strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=%C5%8Ctautahi\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Ōtautahi</a></strong> = Christchurch</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Tip: Ki = to (movement toward). Kei = at (present location, no movement). I = at (past) or from. Mō = about/for (ō things). Mā = for (ā recipient). These five do 90% of the work.</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">We Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Kia Tūhono — Fill the gap</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>I give you a sentence with a gap — you choose the right preposition.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>&quot;I am going ___ school.&quot; Moving toward → <strong>ki</strong> → <strong>Kei te haere au ki te kura.</strong></li><li>&quot;She is ___ the shop right now.&quot; Present location → <strong>kei</strong> → <strong>Kei te toa ia.</strong></li><li>&quot;This book is ___ you.&quot; For a recipient (ā) → <strong>māu</strong> → <strong>He pukapuka māu tēnei.</strong></li><li>&quot;He is speaking ___ te reo Māori.&quot; About/concerning → <strong>mō</strong> → <strong>Kei te kōrero ia mō te reo Māori.</strong></li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Discussion prompt: English has dozens of prepositions — at, in, on, to, for, about, by, with, from, through… Te reo Māori does the same work with about seven. How does a smaller set of prepositions make a language easier or harder to learn?</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">You Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Māu Anō — Preposition challenge</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Use six different prepositions from today in six different sentences.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Write sentences using <strong>ki</strong>, <strong>kei</strong>, and <strong>i</strong> — make the difference between them clear.</li><li>Write a sentence using <strong>mō</strong> about something you care about.</li><li>Write a sentence using <strong>māku</strong> or <strong>māu</strong> — something for you or me.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write a short paragraph (5 sentences) about your day that uses at least five different prepositions. Underline each preposition and name it.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Wednesday: Ngā kupu matua i mua — Fronted time and place phrases":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Te Aho Arataki Marau — AO 2.4: Time · AO 1.5: Location · Grammar Level 1–2: Fronted adverbials — time and place phrases at the start of sentences</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Objectives</th><td class=\"lt-td\">AO 2.4 — Fronted time phrases; AO 1.5 — Fronted place phrases</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">I Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tāua Kōrero — Put time and place first for impact</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>In Week 6 you learned that time words can go at the start of a sentence. Today we go deeper — putting whole TIME PHRASES and PLACE PHRASES at the front, not just single words. This is called fronting, and it is one of the most powerful tools for creating rhythm and emphasis in your te reo.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Single time word at front (Week 6):<br><strong>Āpōpō ka haere au ki te toa.</strong><br>Tomorrow I will go to the shop.<br><br>Full time PHRASE at front:<br><strong>I te ahiahi o tērā rā, i haere au ki te toa.</strong><br>In the afternoon of that day, I went to the shop.<br><br>Place PHRASE at front:<br><strong>I Ōtautahi, i noho mātou i tētahi whare nui.</strong><br>In Christchurch, we stayed in a big house.<br><br>Both together:<br><strong>I te ata o te Mane, i roto i te kura, i ako ngā tamariki i te reo Māori.</strong><br>On Monday morning, inside the school, the children learned te reo Māori.</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The pattern for a fronted time phrase:<br><strong>I te [time word/period] o [day/event],</strong> + [main sentence]</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Useful time phrase builders:<br><strong>i te ata</strong> — in the morning<br><strong>i te <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=ahiahi\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">ahiahi</a></strong> — in the afternoon/evening/evening<br><strong>i te pō</strong> — at night<br><strong>i te <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=t%C4%ABmatanga\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">tīmatanga</a></strong> — at the beginning<br><strong>i te <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=mutunga\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">mutunga</a></strong> — at the end<br><strong>i tērā wiki</strong> — last week<br><strong>i tēnei rā</strong> — today / on this day</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The pattern for a fronted place phrase:<br><strong>I [place],</strong> + [main sentence]</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The comma after the fronted phrase signals to the listener: &quot;scene set — now here comes the main action.&quot;</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Tip: Front-loading gives your listener the CONTEXT before the action. It is like setting the stage before the actors enter. Always add a comma after a fronted phrase.</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">We Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Kia Tūhono — Front it!</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>I give you a plain sentence. You move the time or place phrase to the front and add a comma.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Plain: &quot;I learned te reo in the morning.&quot; → Front the time: <strong>I te ata, i ako au i te reo Māori.</strong></li><li>Plain: &quot;The children played outside yesterday.&quot; → Front the time: <strong>Inanahi, i tākaro ngā tamariki i waho.</strong></li><li>Plain: &quot;We ate in the classroom at lunchtime.&quot; → Front the place: <strong>I roto i te akomanga, i kai mātou i te tina.</strong></li><li>Now try both — front the time AND place: &quot;At the end of school, in the hall, we sang a waiata.&quot; → <strong>I te mutunga o te kura, i roto i te whare hui, i waiata mātou.</strong></li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Discussion prompt: Read these two versions aloud: &quot;I went to school in the morning yesterday&quot; vs &quot;Inanahi, i te ata, i haere au ki te kura.&quot; Which feels more dramatic? Why does putting time and place first change the feel of a sentence?</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">You Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Māu Anō — Front-loaded sentences</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write sentences that open with a time or place phrase.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Write two sentences with fronted TIME phrases — use i te ata and i te ahiahi.</li><li>Write two sentences with fronted PLACE phrases — use i roto i te kura and i waho.</li><li>Write one sentence with BOTH a fronted time phrase AND a fronted place phrase.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Rewrite this bare sentence with as much fronted detail as you can: &quot;We played.&quot; Aim for a sentence that paints a complete picture of when, where, and how — all before the main verb.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Thursday: Kōrero — He kōrero mō tōku kāinga — Talking about home":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Te Aho Arataki Marau — AO 1.4: Personal information · AO 2.2: Possessions · AO 2.5: Descriptions · AO 1.5: Location</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Objectives</th><td class=\"lt-td\">AO 1.4 — Personal information &amp; home; AO 2.5 — Describing your home</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">I Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tāua Kōrero — Describing where you live</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This week you learned confusable words, prepositions, and fronted phrases. Today we use all three in a conversation about home — where you live, what it is like, and what happens there. This is deeply personal language — the kind that helps you really connect with someone.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\"><strong>A:</strong> Nō hea koe?<br><strong>B:</strong> Nō Ōtautahi ahau. He tāone nui, he ātaahua hoki.<br><strong>A:</strong> Kei hea tō kāinga i ināianei?<br><strong>B:</strong> Kei te taha o te awa tōku kāinga. He whare hou, he whare māmā hoki.<br><strong>A:</strong> He aha ngā mea pai o tō wāhi noho?<br><strong>B:</strong> Ko ēnei ngā mea pai: he nui ngā rākau, he mārie te noho, ā he tata ki te kura hoki.<br><strong>A:</strong> Ka pai! I te ahiahi, he aha āu mahi i tō kāinga?<br><strong>B:</strong> I te ahiahi, i te māra, ka mahi au. Ka tiaki hoki au i ngā kararehe o mātou.</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>New words from this conversation:<br><strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=t%C4%81one\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">tāone</a></strong> — town / city<br><strong>te taha o</strong> — the side of / beside<br><strong>awa</strong> — river<br><strong>māmā</strong> — light / easy (also: mild earlier) — context tells you which<br><strong>wāhi noho</strong> — place of living / neighbourhood<br><strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=r%C4%81kau\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">rākau</a></strong> — tree / trees<br><strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=tata\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">tata</a></strong> — close / nearby<br><strong>māra</strong> — garden<br><strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=tiaki\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">tiaki</a></strong> — to look after / guard / guard<br><strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=kararehe\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">kararehe</a></strong> — animal / pet</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Notice the fronted phrase at the end: <strong>I te ahiahi</strong> — in the afternoon. It sets the time before the action. And <strong>ko ēnei ngā mea pai</strong> from Week 7 — introducing a list of good things.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Tip: Nō hea koe? = Where are you from? Kei hea tō kāinga? = Where is your home right now? Two different questions — know which one you are answering.</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">We Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Kia Tūhono — Describe your home</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Build the home conversation piece by piece. I ask — you answer using this week's structures.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I ask: <strong>Nō hea koe?</strong> You answer with your town/city and one He description of it.</li><li>I ask: <strong>Kei hea tō kāinga?</strong> Use a preposition and a location: <strong>Kei te taha o te…</strong> or <strong>Kei roto i te…</strong></li><li>I ask: <strong>He aha ngā mea pai o tō wāhi noho?</strong> Use Ko ēnei to introduce a list of three good things.</li><li>I ask: <strong>I te ahiahi, he aha āu mahi?</strong> Answer with a fronted time phrase and two activities.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Discussion prompt: Your home and where you are from is deeply connected to your identity in te ao Māori. The pepeha from Week 4 connected you to mountains, rivers, and iwi. How does today's conversation connect to that same idea — that place and identity are inseparable?</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">You Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Māu Anō — He kōrero mō tōku kāinga</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write a full description of your home and neighbourhood in te reo Māori.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Write where you are from and one thing that makes your town special: <strong>Nō… ahau. He…</strong></li><li>Write where your home is using a preposition: <strong>Kei te taha o / Kei roto i / Kei tata ki…</strong></li><li>Use Ko ēnei to list three good things about your neighbourhood.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write a six-sentence paragraph about your home using: a fronted time phrase, a Ko ēnei list, at least three prepositions, and one confusable word pair used correctly (tōku/tāku or nō/nā).</li></ul></div></div>"},"Friday: Aromatawai — Quiz & Review":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Monday questions</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>What is the difference between &quot;keke&quot; and &quot;kēkē&quot; in te reo Māori?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>They are spelling variations of the same word </li><li>keke = cake, kēkē = armpit — the macron completely changes the meaning ✅</li><li>keke is formal, kēkē is informal </li><li>kēkē is the plural of keke </li></ul></li><li>Which possessive would you use for &quot;my school&quot;? (kura is ō-category)<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>tāku kura </li><li>nāku kura </li><li>tōku kura ✅</li><li>māku kura </li></ul></li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tuesday questions</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>&quot;Kei te haere au ki te kura.&quot; Which preposition shows movement TOWARD a place?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>kei </li><li>i </li><li>ki ✅</li><li>o </li></ul></li><li>&quot;He pukapuka māu tēnei.&quot; What does māu mean?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>for me </li><li>for him/her </li><li>for you ✅</li><li>for us </li></ul></li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Wednesday questions</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>&quot;I te ata, i ako au i te reo Māori.&quot; What is the purpose of putting &quot;i te ata&quot; at the front?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>It makes the sentence shorter </li><li>It sets the time context before the main action — fronting for impact ✅</li><li>It is a grammar rule that verbs must come last </li><li>It replaces the need for a tense marker </li></ul></li><li>What punctuation always follows a fronted time or place phrase in te reo Māori?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>A full stop </li><li>A semicolon </li><li>A comma ✅</li><li>A colon </li></ul></li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Thursday questions</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>&quot;Nō hea koe?&quot; vs &quot;Kei hea tō kāinga?&quot; — what is the difference?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>They mean exactly the same thing </li><li>Nō hea asks where you are from (origin), Kei hea asks where your home is right now (location) ✅</li><li>Nō hea is formal, Kei hea is casual </li><li>Nō hea asks about the future, Kei hea asks about the past </li></ul></li><li>What does &quot;kararehe&quot; mean?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>garden </li><li>tree </li><li>animal / pet ✅</li><li>neighbour </li></ul></li></ul></div></div>"}}},"Week 9":{"label":"Day","children":{"Monday: Ngā kupu oro — Sound words and vivid language":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Te Aho Arataki Marau — AO 2.5: Descriptive and figurative language · AO 2.3: Expressive communication · Grammar Level 1–2: Onomatopoeia, sound imagery, expressive vocabulary</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Objectives</th><td class=\"lt-td\">AO 2.5 — Vivid &amp; expressive vocabulary; AO 2.3 — Expressive communication</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">I Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tāua Kōrero — Words that sound like what they mean</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Some of the most powerful words in any language are ones that imitate sounds or carry a strong physical feeling. Te reo Māori is rich with these — words that bring a scene alive the moment you hear them. Today we collect the best ones and learn to use them deliberately.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\"><strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=ngunguru\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">ngunguru</a></strong> — to growl / rumble (deep sound)<br><strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=h%C5%ABh%C5%AB\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">hūhū</a></strong> — to hum / buzz (insect or engine)<br><strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=pak%C5%AB\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">pakū</a></strong> — to bang / explode / crack<br><strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=ng%C4%81oro\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">ngāoro</a></strong> — to roar / rushing sound of water or wind<br><strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=tangi\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">tangi</a></strong> — to cry / sound of weeping (also: funeral / to ring)<br><strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=k%C5%8D\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">kō</a></strong> — the sound of a bird call<br><strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=oro\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">oro</a></strong> — sound / noise in general<br><strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=pap%C4%81\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">papā</a></strong> — to slap / crack loudly<br><strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=h%C5%ABp%C4%93\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">hūpē</a></strong> — runny, dripping (wet sound)<br><strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=hau\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">hau</a></strong> — wind / breath / spirit</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>These words do double work — they describe AND create the sound at the same time.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>In a sentence:<br><strong>I ngunguru te whatitoka i te hau kino.</strong><br>The door growled/rumbled in the fierce wind.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Ka pakū te whatitoka i tōna tūkitanga.</strong><br>The door cracked/banged at its closing.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>I tangi ngā tamariki ā te pō.</strong><br>The children cried through the night.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>New words:<br><strong>whatitoka</strong> — door<br><strong>hau kino</strong> — fierce wind / bad weather<br><strong>tūkitanga</strong> — a striking / closing / impact</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>You can also pair these sound words with tino for intensity:<br><strong>He tino ngāoro te awa i te ua nui.</strong><br>The river roared greatly in the heavy rain.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Tip: Sound words wake a listener up. Drop one into a sentence and the whole thing comes alive. Use them sparingly — one per paragraph is enough.</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">We Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Kia Tūhono — Bring the scene alive</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>I describe a scene — you choose a sound word that fits and build a sentence around it.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Scene: a storm outside at night. Which sound word fits? → ngāoro, hau, pakū → <strong>I ngāoro te ua i te pō — he pakū hoki te uira.</strong></li><li>Scene: a dog in the yard early morning. → <strong>I ngunguru te kurī i te ata.</strong> (kurī = dog)</li><li>Scene: a child who has hurt themselves. → <strong>I tangi kaha te tamaiti — i rongo ia i te mamae.</strong> (rongo = feel, mamae = pain)</li><li>Now you make one: pick a sound word and describe something you heard today using it in a Kei te or I sentence.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Discussion prompt: English has onomatopoeia like &quot;buzz&quot;, &quot;crash&quot;, &quot;splash&quot;. Which of today's te reo sound words do you think sounds most like what it means? Why do you think languages around the world independently develop words that imitate sounds?</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">You Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Māu Anō — Sound sentences</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Use sound words deliberately to make your writing vivid.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Write three sentences — each one using a different sound word from today.</li><li>Take this bare sentence and add a sound word: &quot;The wind came.&quot; → Make it vivid.</li><li>Describe your school at lunchtime using at least two sound words.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write a six-sentence scene — morning at your home, or a storm, or a sports match — using at least three sound words. Read it aloud. Does it sound alive?</li></ul></div></div>"},"Tuesday: Ngā kupu hono katoa — All conjunctions: ā, engari, nō reira, ahakoa, mehemea, i te wā":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Te Aho Arataki Marau — AO 2.3: Extended communication · Grammar Level 1–2: Full range of coordinating and subordinating conjunctions</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Objectives</th><td class=\"lt-td\">AO 2.3 — Conjunctions &amp; complex sentences; AO 2.5 — Connecting ideas</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">I Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tāua Kōrero — Your full conjunction toolkit</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Across this term you have learned conjunctions one by one. Today we gather them all in one place, compare them side by side, and practise choosing the right one deliberately. By the end of this lesson you should be able to join any two ideas in te reo Māori.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\"><strong>Coordinating (joining equal ideas):</strong><br><strong>ā</strong> — and then / and (sequence or addition)<br><strong>engari</strong> — but (contrast)<br><strong>nō reira</strong> — so / therefore (result)<br><strong>me</strong> — and / with (linking nouns or people)<br><br><strong>Subordinating (one idea depends on the other):</strong><br><strong>nō te mea / nā te mea</strong> — because (reason)<br><strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=ahakoa\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">ahakoa</a></strong> — although / even though / even though (concession)<br><strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=mehemea\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">mehemea</a></strong> — if / supposing that / supposing (condition)<br><strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=i+te+w%C4%81\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">i te wā</a></strong> — when (referring to a time in the past or ongoing) (time clause, past)<br><strong>ā tōnā wā</strong> — when (future — when the time comes)</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>See them all in action in one connected passage:</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>I haere au ki te kura i te ata, ā i ako mātou i te reo Māori.</strong><br>I went to school in the morning, and then we learned te reo Māori.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>I te wā i mutu te akoranga, i tākaro mātou i waho.</strong><br>When the lesson finished, we played outside.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Ahakoa i ua, i tākaro tonu mātou.</strong><br>Although it rained, we kept playing.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>I ngenge au nō reira i hoki au ki te kāinga.</strong><br>I was tired so I went home.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Mehemea ka pērā anō āpōpō, ka haere anō au.</strong><br>If it is the same again tomorrow, I will go again.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Five conjunctions, one story. Every sentence connected.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Tip: Ask yourself before you write: am I ADDING (ā/me), CONTRASTING (engari/ahakoa), SHOWING A RESULT (nō reira), giving a REASON (nō te mea), a CONDITION (mehemea), or a TIME (i te wā)? The answer tells you which conjunction to reach for.</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">We Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Kia Tūhono — Choose the right conjunction</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>I give you two ideas. You choose the conjunction that shows the right relationship and join them.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>&quot;She was tired.&quot; + &quot;She finished the work.&quot; Time relationship → <strong>i te wā</strong> → <strong>I te wā i ngenge ia, i mutu tonu tāna mahi.</strong></li><li>&quot;I want to swim.&quot; + &quot;It is cold.&quot; Contrast → <strong>ahakoa</strong> → <strong>Ahakoa he makariri, kei te hiahia au ki te kaukau.</strong></li><li>&quot;He worked hard.&quot; + &quot;He won.&quot; Result → <strong>nō reira</strong> → <strong>I mahi kaha ia nō reira i toa ia.</strong> (toa = win)</li><li>Now your turn: give me two ideas. The class decides which conjunction fits — and WHY.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Discussion prompt: Can you use the wrong conjunction and still be understood? Yes — but it changes the meaning. &quot;I mahi kaha ia ā i toa ia&quot; (he worked hard AND won) vs &quot;i toa ia nō reira i mahi kaha ia&quot; (he won, therefore he worked hard) — very different stories. Which conjunction you choose SHAPES the meaning.</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">You Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Māu Anō — Conjunction challenge</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write one sentence using each conjunction. Make each one clearly different.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Write sentences using <strong>ā</strong>, <strong>engari</strong>, and <strong>nō reira</strong> — one each.</li><li>Write sentences using <strong>ahakoa</strong>, <strong>mehemea</strong>, and <strong>i te wā</strong> — one each.</li><li>Write a four-sentence mini-story that uses at least three different conjunctions naturally.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write a full paragraph (6–8 sentences) about something that happened this term. Use every conjunction from today at least once. Underline each one and label which type it is.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Wednesday: He kōrero tūhono — Paragraph cohesion in te reo":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Te Aho Arataki Marau — AO 2.3: Extended and structured communication · Grammar Level 1–2: Paragraph structure, topic sentences, pronoun links, connective flow</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Objectives</th><td class=\"lt-td\">AO 2.3 — Cohesion &amp; paragraph structure; AO 1.7 — Structured written language</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">I Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tāua Kōrero — Making paragraphs flow</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>You can write great individual sentences. Today we work on how sentences CONNECT — how a paragraph flows so that each sentence leads naturally into the next. In te reo Māori, there are three main tools for cohesion: pronoun links, connective words, and sentence echoing.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">WEAK — no flow:<br><strong>He pai tōku kura. He nui ngā ākonga. He kaiako pai tōku kaiako. I ako ngā ākonga i te reo Māori.</strong><br><br>STRONG — connected:<br><strong>He pai tōku kura nō te mea he nui ngā ākonga. Ko rātou ngā tamariki māhoe o tō mātou hapori. I ako rātou i te reo Māori i tēnei wiki, ā i tino pai tā rātou mahi. Nō reira, he kura tino motuhake tōku kura.</strong></blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Three tools for paragraph cohesion:</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>1. Pronoun links</strong> — replace a noun with a pronoun so you don't repeat the noun:<br>&quot;ngā ākonga… Ko rātou… I ako rātou…&quot;<br>(the students… They are… They learned…)</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>2. Connective words</strong> — signal the relationship between sentences:<br>nō reira (therefore), ā (and then), engari (but), nō te mea (because)</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>3. Sentence echoing</strong> — repeat a key word or idea to tie sentences together:<br>&quot;He pai tōku kura… he kura tino motuhake tōku kura.&quot;<br>(My school is good… my school is truly special.)</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>New words:<br><strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=m%C4%81hoe\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">māhoe</a></strong> — talented / bright<br><strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=hapori\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">hapori</a></strong> — community<br><strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=motuhake\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">motuhake</a></strong> — special / unique / independent / independent</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Tip: After writing each sentence, ask: does this connect to what came before? If you had to jump to a new idea, use a connective. If you mentioned a noun, replace it with a pronoun next time. If you want to land a final point, echo your opening.</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">We Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Kia Tūhono — Fix the paragraph</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>I give you a weak, disconnected paragraph. We improve it together — one sentence at a time.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Sentence 1 is fine: <strong>He pai tōku kura.</strong> Sentence 2 repeats the noun: &quot;He nui ngā ākonga o tōku kura.&quot; → Replace with a pronoun: <strong>He nui ō rātou kaha.</strong> (There is much ability among them.)</li><li>Sentence 3 has no connective: &quot;I ako ngā ākonga.&quot; → Add one: <strong>I tēnei wiki, i ako rātou i ngā kupu hou.</strong></li><li>Sentence 4 needs to land with an echo: &quot;He kura pai.&quot; → Echo the opening: <strong>Nō reira, he kura tino motuhake tōku kura.</strong></li><li>Now read the full improved paragraph aloud together. Can you feel the difference in how it flows?</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Discussion prompt: A paragraph should feel like a conversation where each sentence responds to the last. Which of the three cohesion tools — pronoun links, connectives, or echoing — do you think is the most powerful? Why?</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">You Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Māu Anō — Write a cohesive paragraph</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write a full paragraph in te reo Māori that flows from beginning to end.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Write a topic sentence using <strong>He</strong> or <strong>Ko</strong> about your class, school, or something you care about.</li><li>Write two or three supporting sentences — use pronoun links to avoid repeating nouns.</li><li>Add a connective (nō reira / ā / engari) before one of your sentences.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write a full five-sentence paragraph and deliberately use all three cohesion tools — pronoun link, connective, and sentence echo. Highlight each tool in a different colour.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Thursday: Kōrero — He arotake — Looking back, moving forward":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Te Aho Arataki Marau — AO 2.3: Extended communication · AO 1.1: Greetings · AO 2.5: Expressive vocabulary · AO 1.4: Personal information</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Objectives</th><td class=\"lt-td\">AO 2.3 — Reflection &amp; extended kōrero; AO 2.5 — Expressive vocabulary review</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">I Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tāua Kōrero — Reflecting on what you have learned</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This is the second-to-last week of Term 1. Today we use everything you have built — vivid words, conjunctions, cohesion — in a reflective conversation about your learning journey this term. This kind of reflective language is important in te ao Māori and in life.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\"><strong>A:</strong> He pēhea tōu whakaaro mō tēnei wiki?<br><strong>B:</strong> He tino pai ki a au tēnei wiki. I ako au i ngā kupu oro — pēnei i te &quot;ngunguru&quot; me te &quot;pakū&quot;. Ka taea e au te whakamahi i ērā kupu ināianei.<br><strong>A:</strong> He aha te mea i uaua ki a koe?<br><strong>B:</strong> I uaua ki a au ngā kupu tūhono — engari ināianei, ka mārama ana au ki a rātou.<br><strong>A:</strong> He aha tāu whainga mō te kāhui o tērā wiki?<br><strong>B:</strong> Ko tāku whainga: ka ako au i ngā kupu arataki katoa o Wīki 10 — ā ka kōrero māmā au i te reo Māori!<br><strong>A:</strong> Ka pai tāu whakaaro! He ātaahua tō ara ako.</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>New words and phrases for reflection:</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>whakaaro</strong> — thought / opinion / idea<br><strong>He aha tāu whakaaro mō…?</strong> — What do you think about…?<br><strong>Ka taea e au te…</strong> — I am able to… / I can now…<br><strong>Ka mārama ana au</strong> — I understand / I am understanding<br><strong>i uaua ki a au</strong> — it was difficult for me<br><strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=whainga\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">whainga</a></strong> — goal / aim / target / target<br><strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=arataki\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">arataki</a></strong> — to guide / lead<br><strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=ara+ako\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">ara ako</a></strong> — learning pathway / journey / journey<br><strong>He ātaahua tō ara ako.</strong> — Your learning journey is beautiful.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Notice: <strong>ka… ana</strong> is a continuous present form — &quot;I am (currently) understanding.&quot; It implies the understanding is ongoing and growing.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Tip: Ka taea e au te kōrero i te reo Māori — I am able to speak te reo Māori. Learn this sentence cold. Say it at the start of every te reo session for the rest of the year.</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">We Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Kia Tūhono — Reflect together</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>We have a structured reflection conversation. I ask — you answer honestly, then ask me back.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I ask: <strong>He aha te mea i pai rawa atu ki a koe i tēnei wāhanga?</strong> (What did you like most this term?) Answer with at least two sentences.</li><li>I ask: <strong>He aha te mea i uaua ki a koe?</strong> (What was difficult for you?) Use <strong>i uaua ki a au… engari…</strong> to show you overcame it.</li><li>I ask: <strong>Ka taea e koe te kōrero i te reo Māori ināianei?</strong> (Can you speak te reo Māori now?) Answer using <strong>Ka taea e au te…</strong> + three things you can do.</li><li>Close: state your goal for Week 10 — <strong>Ko tāku whainga mō tērā wiki:</strong> + your aim.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Discussion prompt: He ātaahua tō ara ako. What does it mean to say your learning journey is beautiful? In te ao Māori, learning is not just about knowledge — it connects you to people, to land, and to identity. How has learning te reo Māori this term connected you to something bigger than just words?</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">You Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Māu Anō — Tōu ara ako — Your learning journey</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write a reflective piece about your learning this term — entirely in te reo Māori.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Write two sentences about what you can do now that you couldn't do in Week 1. Use <strong>Ka taea e au te…</strong></li><li>Write one sentence about something that was difficult using <strong>i uaua ki a au… engari…</strong></li><li>Write your goal for Week 10 using <strong>Ko tāku whainga:</strong></li><li>✦ Bonus: Write a full reflective paragraph (6 sentences) about your Term 1 journey. Use a topic sentence, pronoun links, a conjunction, and close with an echo of your opening. Share it with a partner.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Friday: Aromatawai — Quiz & Review":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Monday questions</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Which te reo Māori word best describes a deep rumbling or growling sound?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>tangi </li><li>pakū </li><li>ngunguru ✅</li><li>hūhū </li></ul></li><li>&quot;I tangi ngā tamariki ā te pō.&quot; What does tangi mean in this context?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>the children laughed through the night </li><li>the children cried through the night ✅</li><li>the children played through the night </li><li>the children slept through the night </li></ul></li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tuesday questions</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Which conjunction would you use to show a RESULT or CONCLUSION?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>ā </li><li>ahakoa </li><li>nō reira ✅</li><li>i te wā </li></ul></li><li>&quot;Ahakoa i ua, i tākaro tonu mātou.&quot; What relationship does ahakoa show?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>a time — when it rained we played </li><li>a result — because it rained we played </li><li>a concession — DESPITE the rain, we still played ✅</li><li>a condition — if it rains we will play </li></ul></li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Wednesday questions</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>What is &quot;sentence echoing&quot; in a paragraph?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>Saying the same sentence twice in a row </li><li>Repeating a key word or idea at the end to connect back to the opening ✅</li><li>Using the same verb in every sentence </li><li>Writing sentences that are all the same length </li></ul></li><li>What does &quot;motuhake&quot; mean?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>difficult </li><li>tired </li><li>special / unique / independent ✅</li><li>beautiful </li></ul></li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Thursday questions</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>&quot;Ka taea e au te kōrero i te reo Māori.&quot; What does this sentence mean?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>I want to speak te reo Māori </li><li>I used to be able to speak te reo Māori </li><li>I am able to speak te reo Māori ✅</li><li>I am learning to speak te reo Māori </li></ul></li><li>What does &quot;whainga&quot; mean?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>thought / opinion </li><li>learning journey </li><li>goal / aim / target ✅</li><li>community </li></ul></li></ul></div></div>"}}},"Week 10":{"label":"Day","children":{"Monday: Arotake — Term 1 vocabulary and pronunciation review":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Te Aho Arataki Marau — AO 1.1: Greetings · AO 1.3: Numbers · AO 2.5: Full vocabulary review · All Level 1–2 AOs consolidated</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Objectives</th><td class=\"lt-td\">AO 1.1 — Greetings review; AO 2.5 — Vocabulary consolidation</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">I Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tāua Kōrero — Everything we know: vocabulary celebration</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This is the final week of Term 1. Today we celebrate how much vocabulary you have built — from simple greetings in Week 1 to vivid sound words and reflective language in Week 9. We are going to do a full vocabulary review, organised by theme, and find the gaps worth filling before the end of term.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\"><strong>GREETINGS &amp; FAREWELL</strong> (Week 1)<br>Kia ora · Tēnā koe · Tēnā koutou katoa · E noho rā · Hei konā rā<br><br><strong>PEOPLE &amp; IDENTITY</strong> (Weeks 1–2, 4)<br>au · koe · ia · tāua · mātou · tātou · Ko [name] tōku ingoa · Nō [place] ahau<br><br><strong>FOOD &amp; DRINK</strong> (Week 2)<br>āporo · panana · parāoa · miraka · wai · tī · keke<br><br><strong>ACTIONS</strong> (Weeks 1, 3, 6)<br>haere · kai · inu · ako · kōrero · tākaro · mahi · pānui · tuhituhi · omaoma · kaukau<br><br><strong>PLACES</strong> (Weeks 3–4, 8)<br>kura · kāinga · toa · awa · roto · māra · akomanga<br><br><strong>DESCRIPTIONS</strong> (Weeks 2, 5, 7)<br>nui · iti · roa · poto · pai · kino · ātaahua · tino · ake · rawa atu<br><br><strong>TIME</strong> (Weeks 6–8)<br>ināianei · āpōpō · inanahi · i te ata · i te ahiahi · i te pō</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Let's count what you know:</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>✓ Greetings: 5+ phrases<br>✓ Personal pronouns: 8 (au, koe, ia, māua, tāua, mātou, tātou, koutou…)<br>✓ Verbs/actions: 12+<br>✓ Nouns (places, food, objects): 20+<br>✓ Adjectives: 10+<br>✓ Numbers: 1–100<br>✓ Question words: 11<br>✓ Conjunctions: 7<br>✓ Prepositions: 8<br>✓ Time expressions: 8+<br>✓ Possessives: 6 (tōku, tāku, tōu, tāu, tōna, tāna)</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>That is well over 100 pieces of te reo Māori in 9 weeks.<br><strong>Ka rawe!</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Tip: The fastest way to review vocabulary is to use it — not read it. Close the list and try to say everything from memory. What comes easily? What do you need to look at again? That gap is your target.</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">We Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Kia Tūhono — Vocabulary blitz</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Speed round — I call a category, you give me as many words as you can. Class builds the list together on the board.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>ACTIONS: How many verbs can we list in 60 seconds? Aim for 10+. Teacher writes each one on the board.</li><li>DESCRIPTIONS: Call out every adjective you know — including the comparison forms (ake, rawa atu, tino).</li><li>QUESTION WORDS: Can someone recite all 11 question words from memory? Ko wai, He aha, Kei hea…</li><li>WILD CARD: Say a sentence using a word from EACH category — greetings, action, description, time, place — all in one sentence.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Discussion prompt: Look at everything on the board. Which category surprised you — you know more than you thought? Which category has the most gaps? Set yourself a personal target: I will learn </em><strong> more words from </strong><em> category before the end of this week.</em></p></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">You Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Māu Anō — Personal vocabulary audit</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Do a personal review of your own vocabulary knowledge.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Without looking at the list: write down every te reo Māori word you can remember in 3 minutes. Count them.</li><li>Go back to Week 1 in your mind — what are the five most important things you learned that week? Write them.</li><li>Write the one word or phrase from this term that you find most useful. Write three sentences using it.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Create your own personal &quot;Term 1 kupu list&quot; — organised by category, from memory. Then check it against the lesson summaries. What did you miss? What did you remember perfectly?</li></ul></div></div>"},"Tuesday: Arotake — Term 1 grammar review":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Te Aho Arataki Marau — All Grammar Level 1–2 structures consolidated: Kei te, I, Ka, Ko, He, Kāore, possessives, prepositions, conjunctions</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Objectives</th><td class=\"lt-td\">AO 2.3 — Grammar consolidation; AO 2.5 — Structure review</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">I Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tāua Kōrero — Every structure you know</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Today we put every grammar structure from this term on the table. You have learned more than you realise. Watch how all the pieces fit together — and how one structure leads naturally into the next.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\"><strong>IDENTITY: Ko</strong><br>Ko Aroha tōku ingoa. Ko tōku kura tōku wāhi pai.<br><br><strong>DESCRIPTION: He</strong><br>He ātaahua tōku kura. He nui ake tēnei whare i tērā.<br><br><strong>PRESENT: Kei te</strong><br>Kei te ako au i te reo Māori. Kei te pēhea koe?<br><br><strong>PAST: I</strong><br>I haere au ki te kura inanahi. I ngenge ia nō reira i hoki ia ki te kāinga.<br><br><strong>NARRATIVE: Ka</strong><br>Ka haere ia ki te toa. Ka hoko ia i te āporo.<br><br><strong>NEGATIVE: <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=k%C4%81ore\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Kāore</a></strong><br>Kāore au e kai ana. Kāore au i haere.<br><br><strong>POSSESSION: tōku/tāku</strong><br>Ko Mere tōku ingoa. Kei te pānui au i tāku pukapuka.<br><br><strong>COMPLEX: ahakoa / mehemea / i te wā</strong><br>Ahakoa i ua, i haere tonu mātou. Mehemea ka haere koe, kōrero mai.</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The full picture of what you can do:</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>→ Identify things and people (Ko)<br>→ Describe things (He + adjective)<br>→ Talk about now (Kei te)<br>→ Talk about the past (I)<br>→ Tell a story (Ka)<br>→ Say something is NOT happening (Kāore)<br>→ Show who things belong to (tōku/tāku and friends)<br>→ Ask any question (Ko wai / He aha / Kei hea / E hia / Tokohia / Pēhea…)<br>→ Join ideas (ā / engari / nō reira / ahakoa / mehemea / i te wā)<br>→ Describe in detail (adjectives, adverbs, fronted phrases)</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>That is a complete communication toolkit. You can express almost any idea with these structures.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Tip: You do not need to remember the grammar names — you need to feel when to use each structure. The best way to do that is to speak and write as much as possible. The names help you think about it; the practice makes it automatic.</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">We Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Kia Tūhono — Structure challenge</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>I give you a concept to express — you choose the right structure and build the sentence.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Express who you are: → Ko + name/identity. Express what something is like: → He + description.</li><li>Express something happening right now AND something that happened yesterday: → Kei te… / I…</li><li>Say something is NOT happening: → Kāore au e… ana. Say it did NOT happen: → Kāore au i…</li><li>Join these with the right conjunction: &quot;I was tired&quot; + &quot;I kept working&quot; → <strong>Ahakoa i ngenge au, i mahi tonu au.</strong></li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Discussion prompt: Which structure do you feel most confident with? Which one still feels uncertain? Be honest — the gaps you name now are the ones you can fix in Week 10 and Term 2.</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">You Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Māu Anō — Grammar self-assessment</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Test yourself on every structure from this term.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Write one sentence using each of: Ko, He, Kei te, I, Ka, Kāore. (Six sentences.)</li><li>Write one sentence using tōku and one using tāku — make sure the category is right.</li><li>Write a complex sentence using one of: ahakoa, mehemea, i te wā.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write a ten-sentence paragraph that uses EVERY structure from this term at least once. Tell a story about a day in your life — past, present, description, and all.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Wednesday: Arotake — Term 1 sentence types review":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Te Aho Arataki Marau — AO 2.3: Extended communication · AO 2.5: Descriptive language · All sentence types from Term 1 consolidated</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Objectives</th><td class=\"lt-td\">AO 2.3 — Sentence variety review; AO 2.5 — Rich description review</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">I Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tāua Kōrero — From bare to brilliant: the full sentence toolkit</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Today we review every sentence type you have built this term — simple, compound, complex, descriptive, fronted, layered — and practise moving between them fluently. A skilled speaker can shift from a short punchy sentence to a long flowing one in a heartbeat. Let's do that.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\"><strong>SIMPLE — bare and clear:</strong><br>Kei te ua. Ka tū ia. He pai tēnei.<br><br><strong>COMPOUND — joined:</strong><br>I haere au ki te toa ā i hoko au i te āporo.<br><br><strong>COMPLEX — subordinate clause:</strong><br>Ahakoa i ua, i haere tonu mātou ki te kura.<br><br><strong>DESCRIPTIVE — layered:</strong><br>He āporo whero nui tēnei — he tino pai hoki!<br><br><strong>FRONTED — time or place first:</strong><br>I te ata o Mane, i roto i te akomanga, i ako ngā tamariki.<br><br><strong>LISTED — Ko ēnei:</strong><br>Ko ēnei ngā mea i ako ai mātou: he kupu hou, he hanganga hou, he kōrero hou.<br><br><strong>COMPARATIVE:</strong><br>He pai ake te reo Māori i te pāngarau ki a au — he pai rawa atu!</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The progression from bare to brilliant:</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>BARE: <strong>Kei te ako ngā tamariki.</strong><br>+WHERE: <strong>Kei te ako ngā tamariki i te akomanga.</strong><br>+HOW: <strong>Kei te ako kaha ngā tamariki i te akomanga.</strong><br>+TIME: <strong>Ināianei, kei te ako kaha ngā tamariki i te akomanga.</strong><br>+WITH WHOM: <strong>Ināianei, kei te ako kaha ngā tamariki me tō rātou kaiako i te akomanga.</strong><br>+JOINED: <strong>Ināianei, kei te ako kaha ngā tamariki me tō rātou kaiako i te akomanga — ā he tino pai tā rātou mahi!</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Six layers. One idea. Completely alive.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Tip: You do not need to use every layer every time. The skill is knowing WHICH layers to add and WHEN. Short when you want impact. Long when you want richness. You now have full control.</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">We Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Kia Tūhono — Build up and strip back</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>We take one bare sentence and build it to maximum richness — then strip back to find the &quot;just right&quot; version.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Start: <strong>I tākaro ia.</strong> Add where, how, time, with whom — one layer at a time. Stop when it feels complete.</li><li>Start: <strong>He pai tōku kura.</strong> Add a reason, a comparison, and a Ko ēnei list. How long can it get?</li><li>Now strip back your longest sentence to its most precise version — not shortest, most precise.</li><li>Read both versions aloud — the fully layered one and the stripped-back one. Which works better? When would you use each?</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Discussion prompt: You have been building sentences for ten weeks. Look back at a sentence you wrote in Week 1. How would you write it differently now? What does that difference tell you about how much you have grown?</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">You Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Māu Anō — Showcase sentences</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write the best sentences you have ever written in te reo Māori.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Write your best SIMPLE sentence — punchy and precise.</li><li>Write your best COMPLEX sentence — with a subordinate clause.</li><li>Write your best LAYERED sentence — time, manner, place, with whom.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write a &quot;best of Term 1&quot; paragraph — eight sentences that show off everything you have learned. Use every sentence type at least once. This is your showcase. Make it sing.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Thursday: Kōrero — He whakamātautau — End-of-term spoken assessment":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Te Aho Arataki Marau — All AOs 1.1–1.7 and 2.1–2.5 assessed · All six language modes: Whakarongo, Kōrero, Pānui, Tuhituhi, Mātakitaki, Whakaatu</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Objectives</th><td class=\"lt-td\">AO 1.1 — Greetings; AO 1.2 — Introductions; AO 1.4 — Personal information; AO 2.3 — Extended communication</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">I Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tāua Kōrero — What a Term 1 conversation looks like</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Today is your end-of-term spoken assessment. Before we begin, let's look at what a full Term 1 conversation sounds like when everything comes together. This is the standard — and you are closer to it than you think.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\"><strong>A:</strong> Tēnā koe. Ko wai tōu ingoa?<br><strong>B:</strong> Tēnā koe. Ko Hēmi tōku ingoa. Nō Ōtautahi ahau. Ko wai koe?<br><strong>A:</strong> Ko Aroha tōku ingoa. Nō <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=T%C4%81maki+Makaurau\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Tāmaki Makaurau</a> ahau. Kei te pēhea koe i tēnei rā?<br><strong>B:</strong> Kei te pai au, kia ora. Ahakoa he makariri, kei te hari au — he wiki pai tēnei.<br><strong>A:</strong> He aha ngā mea hou i ako ai koe i tēnei wāhanga?<br><strong>B:</strong> I ako au i ngā hanganga katoa — Kei te, I, Ka, Ko, He, me ngā kupu hono. Ka taea e au te kōrero pai ake ināianei.<br><strong>A:</strong> Ka pai! He aha tāu whainga mō tērā wāhanga?<br><strong>B:</strong> Ko tāku whainga: ka ako au i ngā tūāhua o Wāhanga Rua — ā ka kōrero māmā ake au.<br><strong>A:</strong> Ka rawe! Tēnā koe, tēnā koe, tēnā koe.<br><strong>B:</strong> Tēnā koe, tēnā koe, tēnā koe.</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>What this conversation demonstrates:<br>✓ AO 1.1: Tēnā koe · greeting and farewell<br>✓ AO 1.2: Ko [name] tōku ingoa · introduction<br>✓ AO 1.4: Nō [place] ahau · personal information<br>✓ AO 1.6: Tēnā koe (×3) · politeness convention<br>✓ AO 2.3: Extended kōrero with reasons and reflection<br>✓ AO 2.5: Descriptive and expressive vocabulary<br>✓ Grammar: Kei te, I, Ka, Ko, He, ahakoa, Ka taea e au te</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>You have everything you need. Now go show it.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Tip: If you forget a word mid-conversation, do not stop — keep going. Say what you CAN say. A real conversation in te reo Māori, even with gaps, is far more impressive than silence.</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">We Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Kia Tūhono — Final practice round</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>One full practice conversation with a partner before the assessment. Use the full structure from the model above.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Greet and introduce: Tēnā koe · Ko [name] tōku ingoa · Nō [place] ahau.</li><li>Ask and answer: Kei te pēhea koe? Answer with a feeling AND a reason using ahakoa or nō te mea.</li><li>Ask and answer: He aha ngā mea hou i ako ai koe? Name three things using Ka taea e au te…</li><li>State your Term 2 goal and close with the three-part farewell: Tēnā koe, tēnā koe, tēnā koe.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Discussion prompt: You started this term not knowing a single word of te reo Māori. You are finishing it able to have a real conversation. What does that feel like? What would you tell your Week 1 self about what is coming?</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">You Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Māu Anō — He whakamātautau — Your assessment</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Perform your end-of-term conversation assessment with a partner.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>Task 1:</strong> Have a full conversation — at least 8 lines each. Cover greeting, introduction, how you are feeling, what you learned, and a goal.</li><li><strong>Task 2:</strong> Self-assess against the checklist below. Be honest.</li><li><strong>Task 3:</strong> Write three sentences in te reo about how you feel about your learning this term.</li><li>✦ Checklist: ☐ Greeted my partner correctly · ☐ Introduced myself with Ko and Nō · ☐ Used at least one tense (Kei te / I / Ka) · ☐ Used at least one conjunction · ☐ Said something descriptive · ☐ Closed with Tēnā koe (×3)</li></ul></div></div>"},"Friday: Aromatawai — Quiz & Review":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Monday questions</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Which of these is the correct plural demonstrative for &quot;those things over there&quot; (plural of tērā)?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>ēnei </li><li>ēnā </li><li>ērā ✅</li><li>tērā </li></ul></li><li>&quot;Ka taea e au te kōrero i te reo Māori&quot; contains which grammar structure?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>Kāore negation </li><li>Ka taea e… te — I am able to… ✅</li><li>Kei te present tense </li><li>Ko identity marker </li></ul></li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tuesday questions</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Which sentence correctly uses Kāore in the PAST tense negative?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>Kāore au e haere ana </li><li>Kāore au i haere ✅</li><li>Kāore haere au </li><li>Kāore au ki haere </li></ul></li><li>You want to say &quot;Although it was cold, we kept working.&quot; Which conjunction do you use?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>nō reira </li><li>ā </li><li>ahakoa ✅</li><li>i te wā </li></ul></li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Wednesday questions</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>What is the main purpose of &quot;sentence echoing&quot; in a paragraph?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>To fill space when you run out of ideas </li><li>To confuse the reader with repetition </li><li>To tie the paragraph together by returning to a key word or idea from the opening ✅</li><li>To make every sentence the same length </li></ul></li><li>&quot;I te ata o Māne, i roto i te akomanga, i ako ngā tamariki.&quot; What type of sentence is this?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>A simple sentence </li><li>A negative sentence </li><li>A fronted sentence with time and place phrases before the main clause ✅</li><li>A comparative sentence </li></ul></li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Thursday questions</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>In the end-of-term conversation, which greeting is used when addressing MORE than two people formally?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>Tēnā koe </li><li>Tēnā kōrua </li><li>Tēnā koutou katoa ✅</li><li>Kia ora </li></ul></li><li>He wāhanga tino pai tēnei. What does &quot;wāhanga&quot; mean?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>sentence </li><li>question </li><li>term / section / part ✅</li><li>week </li></ul></li></ul></div></div>"}}}}},"Term 2":{"label":"Week","children":{"Week 1":{"label":"Day","children":{"Monday: I haere au — The past tense unlocked":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Te Aho Arataki Marau — AO 2.4: Time · AO 2.3: Extended communication · Grammar Level 1–2: I (past tense marker), action verbs in past</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Objectives</th><td class=\"lt-td\">AO 2.4 — Past tense &amp; time; AO 2.3 — Retelling events</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">I Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tāua Kōrero — Welcome back: from now to then</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Nau mai, hoki mai — welcome back. Last term you mastered Kei te for right now. This term we begin by going back in time. You already met the past tense briefly in Term 1 — today we make it solid. Watch how simple the switch is.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\"><strong>Kei te</strong> haere au. — I am going. (RIGHT NOW)<br><strong>I</strong> haere au. — I went. (PAST)<br><br><strong>Kei te</strong> kai ia. — She is eating.<br><strong>I</strong> kai ia. — She ate.<br><br><strong>Kei te</strong> ako ngā tamariki. — The children are learning.<br><strong>I</strong> ako ngā tamariki. — The children learned.<br><br><strong>Kei te</strong> tākaro rātou i waho. — They are playing outside.<br><strong>I</strong> tākaro rātou i waho inanahi. — They played outside yesterday.</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The switch from present to past in te reo Māori is the simplest thing you will ever do:</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Replace <strong>Kei te</strong> with <strong>I</strong>.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>That is it. The verb does not change. The person does not change. The word order does not change. Only the tense marker changes.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Kei te</strong> → RIGHT NOW<br><strong>I</strong> → PAST (it happened, it is done)</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>In English, you must change the verb: &quot;go → went&quot;, &quot;eat → ate&quot;, &quot;run → ran&quot; — and you must memorise dozens of irregular forms. Te reo Māori never does this. The verb is ALWAYS the same. Only the marker changes.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Time words that go with I:<br><strong>inanahi</strong> — yesterday<br><strong>i tērā wiki</strong> — last week<br><strong>i tērā tau</strong> — last year<br><strong>i mua</strong> — before / previously<br><strong>i tēnei ata</strong> — this morning</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Tip: Kei te = now. I = then. Swap the marker, keep everything else exactly the same. That is the whole rule.</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">We Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Kia Tūhono — Switch the tense</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>I give you a present tense sentence — you switch it to past. Then we add a time word.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Present: <strong>Kei te haere au ki te kura.</strong> → Past: <strong>I haere au ki te kura inanahi.</strong></li><li>Present: <strong>Kei te kai rātou i te tina.</strong> → Past: <strong>I kai rātou i te tina i tērā wiki.</strong></li><li>Present: <strong>Kei te mahi kaha ngā ākonga.</strong> → Past: <strong>I mahi kaha ngā ākonga i tēnei ata.</strong></li><li>Now you give ME a present tense sentence — I will switch it to past. Then swap roles.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Discussion prompt: In English, switching to past tense means changing the verb — &quot;play → played&quot;, &quot;go → went&quot;, &quot;eat → ate&quot;. Te reo Māori never changes the verb. Only the marker changes. Which system feels more logical to you? Which is easier to learn?</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">You Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Māu Anō — Tell me what happened</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write past tense sentences about real things that happened.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Write three past tense sentences about what you did inanahi (yesterday).</li><li>Write two past tense sentences about what happened i tērā wiki (last week).</li><li>Take this sentence and make it past: <strong>Kei te omaoma kaha ia i waho.</strong></li><li>✦ Bonus: Write a six-sentence recount of something that happened in the holidays. Use I for every sentence, and include at least two time words.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Tuesday: Ngā kupumahi o mua — More past tense action verbs":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Te Aho Arataki Marau — AO 1.5: Location · AO 2.3: Retelling · Grammar Level 1–2: Past tense with action verbs, object marker i</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Objectives</th><td class=\"lt-td\">AO 2.3 — Retelling past events; AO 1.5 — Past location</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">I Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tāua Kōrero — Actions in the past with objects</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Yesterday we switched tense markers. Today we add objects — the THINGS that actions happen to. In te reo Māori, when a verb has a direct object, you mark it with the small word i (different from the past tense I — notice the capital). Watch how they work together.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\"><strong>I hoko au</strong> i te āporo.<br>I bought an apple. (hoko = buy, i te āporo = the apple)<br><br><strong>I pānui ia</strong> i tāna pukapuka.<br>She read her book.<br><br><strong>I tuhituhi ngā tamariki</strong> i ā rātou reta.<br>The children wrote their letters. (<a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=reta\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">reta</a> = letter)<br><br><strong>I kite au</strong> i tōku hoa.<br>I saw my friend. (kite = see/find)<br><br><strong>I <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=rongo\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">rongo</a> ia</strong> i te tangi o te manu.<br>She heard the cry of the bird. (<a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=rongo\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">rongo</a> = hear/feel, <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=tangi\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">tangi</a> = cry/sound, <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=manu\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">manu</a> = bird)</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The structure with an object:</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>I</strong> + [verb] + [person] + <strong>i</strong> + [object]</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The small <strong>i</strong> before the object is the OBJECT MARKER — it signals &quot;and here is the thing the action happened to.&quot;</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Do not confuse:<br>→ <strong>I</strong> (capital, start of sentence) = PAST tense marker<br>→ <strong>i</strong> (lowercase, before object) = OBJECT marker</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Both are in this sentence:<br><strong>I</strong> hoko au <strong>i</strong> te āporo.<br>PAST + buy + I + OBJECT + the apple.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>New verbs:<br><strong>kite</strong> — see / find / discover<br><strong>rongo</strong> — hear / feel / sense<br><strong>hoatu</strong> — give (away from speaker)<br><strong>homai</strong> — give (toward speaker)<br><strong>tango</strong> — take / pick up<br><strong>reta</strong> — letter</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Tip: Capital I at the start = past tense. Lowercase i before an object = object marker. Same sound, completely different job. Context and position always make it clear.</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">We Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Kia Tūhono — Past tense with objects</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Build past tense sentences that include an object. I give you the verb and object — you build the sentence.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Verb: kite (see), Object: tōku hoa (my friend) → <strong>I kite au i tōku hoa.</strong></li><li>Verb: hoko (buy), Object: he panana (a banana) → <strong>I hoko ia i he panana.</strong></li><li>Verb: homai (give to me), Object: tāna pukapuka (her book) → <strong>I homai ia i tāna pukapuka ki a au.</strong></li><li>Now make your own: something you saw, heard, or found yesterday. Use I + verb + au + i + object.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Discussion prompt: The object marker i does the same job as &quot;the&quot; in &quot;I bought THE apple&quot; — it points at a specific thing. Why do you think te reo Māori needs a separate marker for this? What would happen to the sentence without it?</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">You Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Māu Anō — What did you do?</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write past tense sentences that include direct objects.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Write three sentences using different verbs: kite, rongo, hoko. Include an object in each.</li><li>Write a sentence using homai: something someone gave you.</li><li>Write a sentence using tango: something you picked up or took.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write a paragraph (5 sentences) about a trip to a shop or market. Use I for every sentence. Include at least three different objects using the object marker i.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Wednesday: Hanga rerenga — Building past tense stories":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Te Aho Arataki Marau — AO 2.3: Extended communication · AO 2.4: Time · Grammar Level 1–2: Sequencing past events with time words and conjunctions</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Objectives</th><td class=\"lt-td\">AO 2.3 — Story sequencing; AO 2.4 — Time &amp; sequence</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">I Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tāua Kōrero — Chaining past events into a story</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>You can write individual past tense sentences. Today we chain them into a flowing story — connecting events in sequence using time words and conjunctions you already know. Watch how a story builds from simple parts.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\"><strong>I <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=ara\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">ara</a> au i te ata.</strong><br>I woke up in the morning.<br><br><strong>I kai au i tōku <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=parakuihi\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">parakuihi</a>, ā i inu au i tōku tī.</strong><br>I ate my breakfast and I drank my tea.<br><br><strong>I te wā i mutu tōku parakuihi, i <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=horoi\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">horoi</a> au i ōku <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=niho\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">niho</a>.</strong><br>When my breakfast was finished, I brushed my teeth.<br><br><strong>I haere au ki te kura — i tere haere au nō te mea i tata ahau ki te <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=t%C5%ABreiti\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">tūreiti</a>!</strong><br>I went to school — I went quickly because I was nearly late!<br><br><strong>Ahakoa i <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=oma\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">oma</a> au, i <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=tae\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">tae</a> tonu ahau i muri i te <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=pirangi\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">pirangi</a>.</strong><br>Although I ran, I still arrived after the bell.</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>New words in this story:<br><strong>ara</strong> — wake up / rise<br><strong>parakuihi</strong> — breakfast<br><strong>horoi niho</strong> — brush teeth (horoi = wash/clean, niho = teeth)<br><strong>tūreiti</strong> — late<br><strong>tae</strong> — arrive / reach<br><strong>pirangi</strong> — bell<br><strong>i muri i</strong> — after<br><strong>tata</strong> — nearly / close to</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Notice the conjunctions doing their jobs:<br>→ <strong>ā</strong> joining two actions in sequence<br>→ <strong>i te wā</strong> setting a time clause<br>→ <strong>nō te mea</strong> giving a reason<br>→ <strong>ahakoa</strong> showing a surprise</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>All from Term 1 — now used in a past tense story.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Tip: A good past tense story has a beginning (scene-set), a middle (sequence of events), and an end (result or reflection). Use your conjunctions to connect the parts — they are the glue.</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">We Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Kia Tūhono — Build the morning story</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>We build a class morning story together. I give the first sentence — you add the next one, and we keep going.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Opening: <strong>I ara au i te ata tino mutunga.</strong> (I woke up very late.) What happened next? Add a sentence with ā.</li><li>Add a when-clause: <strong>I te wā i…, i…</strong> — something that happened when something else did.</li><li>Add a reason using nō te mea — why did something happen the way it did?</li><li>Close with ahakoa — something surprising happened despite the situation.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Discussion prompt: Notice how the conjunctions create tension and surprise in the story. &quot;Ahakoa i oma au, i tae tonu ahau i muri i te pirangi&quot; — the ahakoa sets up an expectation, then the main clause breaks it. How does that feel different from just saying &quot;I ran. I was late.&quot;?</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">You Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Māu Anō — Tell your morning story</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write a past tense story about your morning today — from waking up to arriving at school.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Write four I sentences about the sequence of what you did this morning.</li><li>Add at least one i te wā clause and one nō te mea reason.</li><li>Add one ahakoa sentence — something surprising or unexpected.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write the full story in eight sentences. Include: a fronted time phrase, an object marker, a conjunction, and one vivid sound word from Term 1 Week 9.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Thursday: Kōrero — He kōrero mō ngā hararei — Talking about the holidays":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Te Aho Arataki Marau — AO 2.3: Extended communication · AO 2.4: Time · AO 2.5: Expressive vocabulary</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Objectives</th><td class=\"lt-td\">AO 2.3 — Holiday recount; AO 2.4 — Past time expressions</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">I Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tāua Kōrero — What did you do in the holidays?</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The most natural first conversation after a holiday is &quot;what did you do?&quot; Today we have that conversation entirely in te reo Māori. Listen to how the past tense flows naturally in real back-and-forth dialogue.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\"><strong>A:</strong> Tēnā koe! He pēhea ōu hararei?<br><strong>B:</strong> He tino pai! I haere au ki Ōtautahi me tōku whānau.<br><strong>A:</strong> Ka pai! He aha ngā mea i mahia e koutou?<br><strong>B:</strong> I haere mātou ki te <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=taiao\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">taiao</a>, ā i kite mātou i ngā <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=manu\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">manu</a> ātaahua o reira.<br><strong>A:</strong> I ngenge koutou?<br><strong>B:</strong> Āe, i ngenge mātou — engari i hari hoki mātou! Ko tērā tōku hararei pai rawa atu.<br><strong>A:</strong> He pēhea te āhua o te <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=rangi\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">rangi</a>?<br><strong>B:</strong> I <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=makariri\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">makariri</a> engari i ātaahua hoki. I ua i tētahi rā — ahakoa i tākaro tonu mātou i waho!</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>New words for holiday talk:<br><strong>hararei</strong> — holiday/holidays<br><strong>whānau</strong> — family<br><strong>taiao</strong> — nature / the natural world / outdoors<br><strong>manu</strong> — bird<br><strong>reira</strong> — there / that place (referring back to somewhere mentioned)<br><strong>He aha ngā mea i mahia e koutou?</strong> — What things did you (all) do?<br><strong>i mahia</strong> — was done (passive — the doing happened to the things)<br><strong>e koutou</strong> — by you all (agent marker)<br><strong>He pēhea te āhua o te rangi?</strong> — What was the weather like?<br><strong>āhua</strong> — appearance / nature / what something is like<br><strong>Ko tērā tōku hararei pai rawa atu.</strong> — That was my best holiday ever.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Tip: He pēhea ōu hararei? — How were your holidays? Learn this cold. It is the first question of every new term for the rest of your school life.</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">We Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Kia Tūhono — Holiday conversation</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Build the holiday conversation together. I ask — you answer. Then swap.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I ask: <strong>He pēhea ōu <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=hararei\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">hararei</a>?</strong> You answer with at least two sentences — where you went and one thing you did.</li><li>I ask: <strong>He aha ngā mea i mahia e koe?</strong> List three things using Ko ēnei or just sequencing with ā.</li><li>I ask: <strong>He pēhea te āhua o te rangi?</strong> Describe the weather using He + adjective.</li><li>I ask: <strong>Ko ēhea ngā mea pai rawa atu?</strong> (What were the best things?) Use Ko tērā or He pai rawa atu…</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Discussion prompt: Ko tērā tōku hararei pai rawa atu. — That was my best holiday ever. What makes this sentence work so well as a closing line? How does it use Ko, possession, and a superlative all in one? Could you write an equally strong closing line about something else?</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">You Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Māu Anō — Tōu kōrero hararei</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>With a partner, have a full holiday conversation in te reo Māori.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Ask and answer: <strong>He pēhea ōu hararei?</strong> Both partners respond in at least three sentences.</li><li>Each person shares the best thing about their holidays: <strong>Ko tērā tōku mea pai rawa atu…</strong></li><li>Describe the weather during your holiday using past tense: <strong>I… te rangi.</strong></li><li>✦ Bonus: Write up your holiday recount as a paragraph (7 sentences). Use: past tense throughout, at least two conjunctions, a Ko ēnei list, and one fronted time phrase.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Friday: Aromatawai — Quiz & Review":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Monday questions</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>How do you change &quot;Kei te haere au&quot; (I am going) to past tense?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>Change &quot;haere&quot; to &quot;haerea&quot; </li><li>Replace &quot;Kei te&quot; with &quot;I&quot; — I haere au ✅</li><li>Add &quot;inanahi&quot; to the end only </li><li>Change &quot;au&quot; to &quot;ahau&quot; </li></ul></li><li>Which time expression means &quot;last week&quot;?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>i tērā wiki ✅</li><li>i tērā tau </li><li>inanahi </li><li>i tēnei ata </li></ul></li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tuesday questions</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>&quot;I hoko au i te āporo.&quot; What is the role of the lowercase &quot;i&quot; before &quot;te āporo&quot;?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>It is the past tense marker </li><li>It marks the object — the thing the action happened to ✅</li><li>It means &quot;at&quot; </li><li>It is part of the verb </li></ul></li><li>What does &quot;kite&quot; mean?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>hear / feel </li><li>give (toward speaker) </li><li>see / find / discover ✅</li><li>take / pick up </li></ul></li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Wednesday questions</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>&quot;I ara au i te ata.&quot; What does &quot;ara&quot; mean?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>eat breakfast </li><li>brush teeth </li><li>wake up / rise ✅</li><li>arrive </li></ul></li><li>You want to say &quot;When breakfast was finished, I brushed my teeth.&quot; Which conjunction do you use?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>ahakoa </li><li>ā </li><li>i te wā ✅</li><li>nō reira </li></ul></li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Thursday questions</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>How do you ask &quot;How were your holidays?&quot; in te reo Māori?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>He aha ōu hararei? </li><li>He pēhea ōu hararei? ✅</li><li>Kei te pēhea ōu hararei? </li><li>Ko wai ōu hararei? </li></ul></li><li>What does &quot;whānau&quot; mean?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>holiday </li><li>nature / outdoors </li><li>bird </li><li>family ✅</li></ul></li></ul></div></div>"}}},"Week 2":{"label":"Day","children":{"Monday: Ka haere ia — The Ka narrative tense":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Te Aho Arataki Marau — AO 2.3: Extended communication · AO 2.4: Time · Grammar Level 1–2: Ka (sequential/narrative tense marker)</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Objectives</th><td class=\"lt-td\">AO 2.3 — Narrative &amp; storytelling; AO 2.4 — Sequential time</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">I Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tāua Kōrero — Ka: the storytelling tense</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>You know Kei te (now) and I (past). Today we meet the third tense marker — Ka. Ka is the storyteller's tense. It moves a story forward, event by event, like a chain. Listen to how it feels different from I.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Using <strong>I</strong> — reporting what happened:<br><strong>I haere ia ki te toa. I hoko ia i te āporo. I hoki ia ki te kāinga.</strong><br>She went to the shop. She bought an apple. She returned home.<br><br>Using <strong>Ka</strong> — telling it as a story:<br><strong>Ka haere ia ki te toa. Ka hoko ia i te āporo. Ka hoki ia ki te kāinga.</strong><br>She went to the shop. She bought an apple. She went back home.<br><br>Combined — I for context, Ka for story movement:<br><strong>I te ahiahi o inanahi, ka haere ia ki te toa. Ka hoko ia i te āporo whero nui. Ka hoki ia ki te kāinga, ā ka kai ia i tāna āporo i te pō.</strong></blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>When to use <strong>Ka</strong>:<br>→ When telling a story and moving it forward step by step<br>→ When describing what happened in sequence (like &quot;and then… and then…&quot;)<br>→ When the action follows on naturally from what came before<br>→ In instructions: Ka tū koe, ka titiro, ka whakarongo (You stand, you look, you listen)</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>When to use <strong>I</strong>:<br>→ When reporting a single completed past event<br>→ When giving a specific past time reference (I haere au inanahi)<br>→ When negating: Kāore au i haere</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The feel: <strong>I</strong> = &quot;it happened.&quot; <strong>Ka</strong> = &quot;and then this happened, and then this…&quot;</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>New word: <strong>i te pō</strong> — at night / in the evening</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Tip: Ka is the &quot;and then&quot; of te reo Māori. Once a story is underway, Ka keeps it moving. Think of it as a chain: Ka… Ka… Ka… each link connected to the last.</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">We Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Kia Tūhono — Tell it as a story</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>I give you a sequence of events — you tell them as a Ka story.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Events: wake up → eat breakfast → go to school → learn te reo → come home. Tell it: <strong>Ka ara ia. Ka kai ia i tōna parakuihi. Ka haere ia ki te kura…</strong></li><li>Now combine I and Ka: set the scene with I (past context), then use Ka for the story: <strong>I te Mane ka haere ia ki te toa. Ka hoko ia…</strong></li><li>Try instructions with Ka: tell a partner how to make a cup of tea step by step. <strong>Ka tango koe i te tī. Ka ringihia e koe te wai…</strong> (ringihia = pour)</li><li>Now swap I and Ka in one of your sentences — how does the meaning or feel change?</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Discussion prompt: Ka is used in traditional Māori storytelling — kōrero pūrākau — to move the narrative forward. Why do you think storytellers prefer Ka over I? What does Ka add to the feeling of a story that I does not?</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">You Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Māu Anō — Tell a Ka story</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Use Ka to tell a story with at least five steps.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Write a five-step Ka story about a character going on a journey. Each sentence starts with Ka.</li><li>Combine I and Ka: open with I te [time], then continue with Ka sentences.</li><li>Write a Ka instruction sequence: how to do something (make a snack, pack a bag, play a game).</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write a full eight-sentence story using I for the opening scene-set and Ka for the body. Include an object marker, a conjunction, and a fronted phrase.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Tuesday: Kāore — Negation in present and past":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Te Aho Arataki Marau — AO 2.3: Extended communication · Grammar Level 1–2: Kāore e…ana (present negative), Kāore i (past negative)</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Objectives</th><td class=\"lt-td\">AO 2.3 — Negation; AO 2.4 — Negative tense forms</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">I Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tāua Kōrero — Saying what is NOT happening</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>You can say what IS happening. Now we learn how to say what is NOT. Negation in te reo Māori is completely regular — one word, Kāore, does all the work. Watch how the structure changes between present and past.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\"><strong>PRESENT NEGATIVE — Kāore… e… ana:</strong><br><strong>Kāore</strong> au <strong>e</strong> haere <strong>ana</strong>. — I am not going.<br><strong>Kāore</strong> ia <strong>e</strong> kai <strong>ana</strong>. — She is not eating.<br><strong>Kāore</strong> ngā tamariki <strong>e</strong> tākaro <strong>ana</strong>. — The children are not playing.<br><br><strong>PAST NEGATIVE — Kāore… i:</strong><br><strong>Kāore</strong> au <strong>i</strong> haere. — I did not go.<br><strong>Kāore</strong> ia <strong>i</strong> kai. — She did not eat.<br><strong>Kāore</strong> ngā tamariki <strong>i</strong> tākaro. — The children did not play.</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The pattern:</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>PRESENT: <strong>Kāore</strong> + [person] + <strong>e</strong> + [verb] + <strong>ana</strong><br>PAST: <strong>Kāore</strong> + [person] + <strong>i</strong> + [verb]</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Key differences from the positive:<br>→ The person comes AFTER Kāore (not after the verb)<br>→ Present adds e…ana around the verb<br>→ Past uses i (same as positive past, but person comes first)</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Compare:<br>Positive present: Kei te haere <strong>au</strong> → person at end<br>Negative present: Kāore <strong>au</strong> e haere ana → person right after Kāore</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Positive past: I haere <strong>au</strong> → person at end<br>Negative past: Kāore <strong>au</strong> i haere → person right after Kāore</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The person always moves to right after Kāore in negatives.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Tip: Kāore pushes the person forward — right after itself. Present negative wraps the verb in e…ana. Past negative uses bare i + verb. Two patterns, one rule about person position.</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">We Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Kia Tūhono — Make it negative</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>I give you a positive sentence — you make it negative. Both present and past.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Positive present: <strong>Kei te haere au ki te kura.</strong> → Negative: <strong>Kāore au e haere ana ki te kura.</strong></li><li>Positive past: <strong>I kai ia i te tina.</strong> → Negative: <strong>Kāore ia i kai i te tina.</strong></li><li>Positive: <strong>Kei te mōhio ngā tamariki.</strong> (The children know.) → Negative: <strong>Kāore ngā tamariki e mōhio ana.</strong></li><li>Now make your own — say something true that you did NOT do yesterday using Kāore au i…</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Discussion prompt: Kāore is one word that handles all negation in te reo Māori. English uses &quot;not&quot;, &quot;didn't&quot;, &quot;isn't&quot;, &quot;wasn't&quot;, &quot;haven't&quot;, &quot;can't&quot; and more. How does having one negation word make te reo Māori simpler? Can you think of any disadvantages?</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">You Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Māu Anō — The honest negative</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Practise both forms of negation — present and past.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Write three present negative sentences using Kāore… e… ana.</li><li>Write three past negative sentences using Kāore… i…</li><li>Write a pair: one positive and one negative about the same event (e.g. I went / I did not go).</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write a paragraph (5 sentences) where someone explains what they did NOT do and why. Use Kāore for at least three sentences, and include a reason using nō te mea.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Wednesday: Hanga rerenga — I, Ka, Kāore in one passage":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Te Aho Arataki Marau — AO 2.3: Extended communication · Grammar Level 1–2: Fluent use of multiple tense markers in connected text</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Objectives</th><td class=\"lt-td\">AO 2.3 — Fluent extended writing; AO 2.4 — Tense control</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">I Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tāua Kōrero — Three markers, one story</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>You now have I, Ka, and Kāore. Today we use all three in a single connected passage — controlling tense and negation deliberately, the way a confident te reo speaker does. Watch how the three markers each do a different job.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\"><strong>I tēnei ata</strong>, ka ara au i te tino ata. Ka <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=horoi\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">horoi</a> au i ōku <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=kanohi\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">kanohi</a> ā ka kai au i tōku parakuihi.<br>&lt;i&gt;This morning, I got up very early. I washed my face and I ate my breakfast.&lt;/i&gt;<br><br>Ka haere au ki te kura — <strong>kāore</strong> au i tūreiti i tēnei rā!<br>&lt;i&gt;I went to school — I was not late today!&lt;/i&gt;<br><br><strong>I</strong> kite au i tōku hoa Mere i te ara. <strong>Kāore</strong> ia e haere ana ki te kura i tēnei rā nō te mea <strong>i</strong> māuiui ia.<br>&lt;i&gt;I saw my friend Mere on the path. She is not going to school today because she was sick.&lt;/i&gt;<br><br>Ka <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=p%C5%8Duri\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">pōuri</a> au mōna — he hoa pai rawa atu ia.<br>&lt;i&gt;I felt sad for her — she is the very best friend.&lt;/i&gt;</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>See how the three markers divide the work:</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Ka</strong> — tells the story, moves it forward step by step (ka ara, ka horoi, ka kai, ka haere, ka pōuri)</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>I</strong> — reports specific completed events (i kite au, i <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=m%C4%81uiui\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">māuiui</a> ia)</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Kāore i</strong> — says something did NOT happen in the past (kāore au i tūreiti)<br><strong>Kāore e…ana</strong> — says something is NOT happening now (kāore ia e haere ana)</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>New words:<br><strong>kanohi</strong> — face<br><strong>ara</strong> — path / road (also: wake up — same word, different context)<br><strong>māuiui</strong> — sick / unwell<br><strong>pōuri</strong> — sad / dark<br><strong>mōna</strong> — for her/him (mō = for/about, na = him/her)</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Tip: Let Ka carry the story forward. Use I to anchor specific facts. Use Kāore to correct, deny, or show what did NOT happen. These three together give you full control of any narrative.</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">We Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Kia Tūhono — Control the tense</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>I give you a story prompt — we build it together, choosing the right marker for each sentence.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Story: a student wakes up late, rushes to school, but still makes it on time. Which markers? Ka for the rush, Kāore i for &quot;did not miss the bell&quot;.</li><li>Story: you were going to visit a friend but they were sick. Use I for the plan, Kāore i for what didn't happen, Kāore e…ana for what is currently not happening.</li><li>Add a pōuri (sadness) sentence using Ka: <strong>Ka pōuri au nō te mea…</strong></li><li>End the story with a positive Ka sentence — something that happened instead.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Discussion prompt: Look at the model passage. Count how many times each marker appears: Ka, I, Kāore. Which one appears most? Why do you think Ka dominates in storytelling? What would the passage feel like if you replaced every Ka with I?</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">You Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Māu Anō — Three-marker story</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write a passage that uses all three markers deliberately.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Write a Ka chain of at least four events — a morning sequence.</li><li>Add two I sentences — specific facts or things you saw/heard.</li><li>Add one Kāore i sentence and one Kāore e…ana sentence.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write a complete eight-sentence story that uses Ka for movement, I for facts, and Kāore for at least two negatives. Tell the story of a day where something went differently than expected.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Thursday: Kōrero — He kōrero kōrero — A flowing conversation":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Te Aho Arataki Marau — AO 2.3: Extended communication · AO 1.1: Greetings · AO 2.4: Past and present</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Objectives</th><td class=\"lt-td\">AO 2.3 — Fluent conversation; AO 1.1 — Greetings &amp; social language</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">I Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tāua Kōrero — A real conversation using all three tenses</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Today we put Term 2 Week 2 together in conversation. A natural conversation moves between past and present — what happened, what is happening now, what you think. Watch how Ka, I, and Kāore all appear naturally.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\"><strong>A:</strong> Tēnā koe! I haere koe ki hea i tērā rā?<br><strong>B:</strong> I haere au ki te hākinakina. Ka tākaro mātou ā te ahiahi katoa!<br><strong>A:</strong> Ka pai! I toa koutou?<br><strong>B:</strong> Kāore mātou i toa — i <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=toa\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">toa</a> ērā atu. Engari i tino pai tā mātou tākaro.<br><strong>A:</strong> He pēhea koe ināianei?<br><strong>B:</strong> Kāore au e ngenge ana — kei te hari tonu au! Ka haere anō mātou ā tērā wiki.<br><strong>A:</strong> Mā wai koutou e <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=%C4%81rahi\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">ārahi</a>?<br><strong>B:</strong> Mā tō mātou kaiako e <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=%C4%81rahi\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">ārahi</a>. He pai rawa atu ia!</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>New phrases from this conversation:<br><strong>I toa koutou?</strong> — Did you (all) win?<br><strong>i toa</strong> — won (toa = win/victorious)<br><strong>ērā atu</strong> — those others / the others<br><strong>tā mātou tākaro</strong> — our (exclusive) playing / our game<br><strong>Ka haere anō mātou</strong> — We will go again<br><strong>Mā wai koutou e ārahi?</strong> — Who will lead/coach you?<br><strong>Mā… e ārahi</strong> — [Person] will lead (mā = by/through for future agent)<br><strong>ārahi</strong> — to lead / guide / coach</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Tip: Mā wai e…? = Who will do [something]? Mā [person] e… = [Person] will do it. This is how you talk about who does future actions — very useful for classroom and sports contexts.</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">We Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Kia Tūhono — Sports or activity conversation</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Build a conversation about a sport or activity you enjoy. Use all three tense markers.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Opening: greet and ask where they went: <strong>I haere koe ki hea i tērā rā?</strong> Answer with a past event.</li><li>Ask about the result: <strong>I toa koutou?</strong> Answer honestly — win OR use Kāore mātou i toa.</li><li>Ask about now: <strong>He pēhea koe ināianei?</strong> Answer using Kāore e…ana or Kei te.</li><li>Ask who leads/coaches using Mā wai e ārahi? Answer with Mā [name/role] e ārahi.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Discussion prompt: Kāore mātou i toa — we did not win. But then: i tino pai tā mātou tākaro — our game was very good. This is gracious losing in te reo Māori. How does te ao Māori think about winning and losing? What values does this kind of language reflect?</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">You Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Māu Anō — Your activity conversation</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Have a full conversation about a sport or activity with a partner.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Ask and answer about a past sports or activity event — use I and Ka.</li><li>Ask about the result — use Kāore i toa or i toa.</li><li>Ask how they feel now — use Kei te pēhea koe and Kāore e…ana or Kei te.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write up the full conversation (8 lines minimum). Use every tense marker at least once. Include Mā wai e ārahi and end with a Ka haere anō sentence.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Friday: Aromatawai — Quiz & Review":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Monday questions</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>&quot;Ka haere ia ki te toa. Ka hoko ia i te āporo.&quot; What does Ka signal here?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>These are future events that have not happened yet </li><li>Ka marks the past tense like I does </li><li>Ka moves the story forward — and then this happened, and then this ✅</li><li>Ka is used only for commands </li></ul></li><li>When would you use I rather than Ka for a past event?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>When the event is part of a flowing story </li><li>When reporting a specific completed event or giving a time reference ✅</li><li>Ka and I mean exactly the same thing </li><li>I is only used with time words like inanahi </li></ul></li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tuesday questions</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>&quot;Kāore au e haere ana.&quot; What is the structure of this present negative?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>Kāore + verb + person + ana </li><li>Kāore + person + e + verb + ana ✅</li><li>Kāore + e + person + verb + ana </li><li>Person + Kāore + verb + e + ana </li></ul></li><li>&quot;Kāore ia i kai i te tina.&quot; What does this sentence mean?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>She is not eating lunch right now </li><li>She did not eat lunch ✅</li><li>She will not eat lunch </li><li>She never eats lunch </li></ul></li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Wednesday questions</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>What does &quot;māuiui&quot; mean?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>sad </li><li>tired </li><li>sick / unwell ✅</li><li>angry </li></ul></li><li>&quot;Ka pōuri au mōna.&quot; What does mōna mean?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>for me </li><li>for you </li><li>for him/her ✅</li><li>for them </li></ul></li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Thursday questions</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>&quot;Mā wai koutou e ārahi?&quot; — What is this question asking?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>Where are you all going? </li><li>Who will lead/coach you all? ✅</li><li>What will you all do? </li><li>How will you all get there? </li></ul></li><li>What does &quot;toa&quot; mean in the context of a game or competition?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>team </li><li>player </li><li>win / victorious ✅</li><li>score </li></ul></li></ul></div></div>"}}},"Week 3":{"label":"Day","children":{"Monday: Ngā tae — Colours and detailed description":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Te Aho Arataki Marau — AO 2.5: Physical characteristics · Grammar Level 1–2: Colour words, He structure with multiple adjectives</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Objectives</th><td class=\"lt-td\">AO 2.5 — Colours &amp; descriptions; AO 1.6 — Descriptive communication</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">I Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tāua Kōrero — Painting pictures with colour</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>You already know a few colours. Today we learn the full colour palette in te reo Māori — and something fascinating about how Māori colour words often come from nature. Listen carefully to the connections.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\"><strong>whero</strong> — red (from the redness of the tōtara tree heartwood)<br><strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=k%C4%81k%C4%81riki\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">kākāriki</a></strong> — green (from the kākāriki parrot — bright green bird) (from the kākāriki parrot — bright green bird)<br><strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=k%C5%8Dwhai\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">kōwhai</a></strong> — yellow (from the kōwhai flower) (from the kōwhai flower)<br><strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=kikorangi\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">kikorangi</a></strong> — blue (kiko = flesh/colour + rangi = sky) (kiko = flesh/colour + rangi = sky)<br><strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=m%C4%81whero\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">māwhero</a></strong> — pink (mā = white + whero = red) (mā = white + whero = red)<br><strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=karaka\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">karaka</a></strong> — orange (from the karaka tree berry) (from the karaka tree berry)<br><strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=parauri\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">parauri</a></strong> — brown (para = residue/sediment + uri = dark) (para = residue/sediment + uri = dark)<br><strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=m%C4%81\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">mā</a></strong> — white<br><strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=mangu\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">mangu</a></strong> — black<br><strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=hina\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">hina</a></strong> — grey / silver<br><strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=k%C5%8Dura\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">kōura</a></strong> — gold / golden (from the kōura/crayfish shell) (from the kōura/crayfish shell)</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Notice the pattern — almost every colour word connects to something in the natural world:<br>→ kākāriki (green) = the parrot<br>→ kōwhai (yellow) = the flower<br>→ kikorangi (blue) = sky-flesh (sky colour)<br>→ karaka (orange) = the tree</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This is te ao Māori — the Māori world view sees language growing from the land and its creatures.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>In a sentence, colours follow the noun as always:<br><strong>He pōtae kākāriki tāku.</strong> — I have a green hat. (pōtae = hat)<br><strong>He pukapuka kikorangi tēnā.</strong> — That is a blue book.<br><strong>He kuri parauri tō rātou.</strong> — They have a brown dog. (kuri = dog)</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>And you can stack them:<br><strong>He pōtae kākāriki māmā tēnei.</strong> — This is a light green hat.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Tip: Colour names in te reo come from nature — a parrot, a flower, the sky. Knowing the source helps you remember the colour. kākāriki = the bright green parrot = bright green.</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">We Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Kia Tūhono — Colour the room</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Look around the room. I point to objects — you describe them using He + noun + colour.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Your pencil case: <strong>He kete pene [tae] tāku.</strong> What colour is it? Fill in the colour.</li><li>The door: <strong>He whatitoka [tae] tērā.</strong> What colour? Add a size adjective too.</li><li>Something outside: <strong>He [noun] [tae] [tae2] tērā.</strong> Can you stack two colour/description words?</li><li>Ask a partner: <strong>He aha te tae o tōu [object]?</strong> (What colour is your [object]?) They answer with He…</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Discussion prompt: Almost all te reo Māori colour words come from plants, birds, or natural phenomena. English colour words come from many different places — &quot;orange&quot; from the fruit, &quot;purple&quot; from a sea snail dye, &quot;blue&quot; from Old French. What does it tell us about a culture when its colour words come from nature?</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">You Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Māu Anō — Colour descriptions</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Use colour words to describe things in detail.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Write five He sentences describing objects around you — each with a different colour word.</li><li>Write a description of your school bag using at least three descriptive words including a colour.</li><li>Write a He sentence using māwhero (pink) and one using kōura (gold).</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write a description of your favourite place using colours from nature — trees, sky, water, birds. Use at least four colour words and make your description feel like a painting.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Tuesday: He pai ake, he pai rawa atu — Comparatives in new contexts":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Te Aho Arataki Marau — AO 2.5: Descriptions · AO 2.3: Opinions · Grammar Level 1–2: Comparative ake, superlative rawa atu in persuasion context</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Objectives</th><td class=\"lt-td\">AO 2.5 — Comparative descriptions; AO 2.3 — Opinions &amp; argument</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">I Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tāua Kōrero — Comparing things to argue a point</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Last term you learned ake (more) and rawa atu (most/best). Today we use those same tools in a new context — argument and persuasion. When you want to convince someone of something, you compare. Watch how comparison becomes opinion.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\"><strong>He pai ake te hākinakina i te karo i.</strong><br>Sport is better than avoiding it.<br><br><strong>He ātaahua ake ngā manu o Aotearoa i ngā manu o ērā atu <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=whenua\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">whenua</a>.</strong><br>The birds of New Zealand are more beautiful than those of other countries.<br><br><strong>He nui ake ngā <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=hua\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">hua</a> o te ako i te reo Māori i ngā <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=uauatanga\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">uauatanga</a>.</strong><br>The benefits of learning te reo Māori are greater than the difficulties.<br><br><strong>Ko te reo Māori te reo tino ātaahua rawa atu o te ao.</strong><br>Te reo Māori is the most beautiful language in the world.</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The comparison structure:<br><strong>He [adjective] ake te X i te Y.</strong><br>[X] is more [adjective] than [Y].</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The i before Y means &quot;than&quot; in this comparison context — a new use of i you haven't seen before!</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>New words:<br><strong>hākinakina</strong> — sport / physical activity<br><strong>whenua</strong> — land / country<br><strong>hua</strong> — fruit / benefit / result<br><strong>uauatanga</strong> — difficulty / the state of being hard<br><strong>te ao</strong> — the world / the realm (literally: the daylight world)<br><strong>ērā atu whenua</strong> — other countries (ērā atu = those others)</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>And for superlatives in comparison:<br><strong>Ko te X te [adjective] rawa atu o [group].</strong><br>X is the most [adjective] of [group].</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Tip: He [adj] ake te X i te Y = X is more [adj] than Y. The i here means &quot;than&quot; — a third job for little i (past tense, object marker, and now &quot;than&quot; in comparisons).</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">We Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Kia Tūhono — Make your argument</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>I give you two things to compare — you build a comparative sentence and defend your position.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Compare: te reo Māori (te reo Māori) vs pāngarau (maths) for fun. → <strong>He ngāwari ake te reo Māori i te pāngarau ki a au.</strong></li><li>Compare: ināianei vs inanahi for weather. → Build a He ātaahua ake / He makariri ake sentence.</li><li>Use a superlative: <strong>Ko [something] te [adj] rawa atu o…</strong> Make a bold claim about something you love.</li><li>Now defend your superlative — can you add nō te mea + a reason to justify it?</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Discussion prompt: Ko te reo Māori te reo tino ātaahua rawa atu o te ao. Do you agree? How would you argue against this? In what ways is comparison a form of argument? Can comparison ever be unfair?</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">You Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Māu Anō — Your comparisons</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Use ake and rawa atu to express and defend opinions.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Write three He [adj] ake te X i te Y sentences — compare things you genuinely have opinions about.</li><li>Write one Ko te X te [adj] rawa atu o… sentence — a bold superlative claim.</li><li>Add a nō te mea reason to your superlative sentence.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write a five-sentence &quot;argument&quot; paragraph in te reo — make a claim, support it with two comparatives, add a superlative, and close with nō reira. Persuade your reader.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Wednesday: Hanga rerenga — SVA and tricky agreement":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Te Aho Arataki Marau — AO 2.3: Accurate communication · Grammar Level 1–2: Subject-verb-object alignment, plural markers ngā and rātou/mātou/tātou</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Objectives</th><td class=\"lt-td\">AO 2.3 — Grammatical accuracy; AO 2.5 — Plural agreement</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">I Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tāua Kōrero — Making singular and plural agree</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>In te reo Māori, making sure your nouns and pronouns agree is important for clarity. The key words are the articles — te (the, singular) and ngā (the, plural) — and the pronouns — ia (he/she, one person) vs rātou (they, three or more). Today we make sure these always line up.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\"><strong>SINGULAR — te and ia:</strong><br><strong>Kei te haere te <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=tamaiti\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">tamaiti</a>.</strong> — The child is going.<br><strong>Kei te haere ia.</strong> — He/she is going.<br><br><strong>PLURAL — ngā and rātou:</strong><br><strong>Kei te haere ngā <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=tamariki\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">tamariki</a>.</strong> — The children are going.<br><strong>Kei te haere rātou.</strong> — They (3+) are going.<br><br><strong>WATCH:</strong><br><strong>I kai te kaiako i tāna kai.</strong> — The teacher ate his/her food.<br><strong>I kai ngā kaiako i ā rātou kai.</strong> — The teachers ate their food.<br><br>Notice: tāna (his/her, singular ā) → ā rātou (their, plural ā)</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The agreement chain:</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>SINGULAR noun → te → ia → tāna/tōna<br>PLURAL noun → ngā → rātou/mātou/tātou → ā rātou/ō rātou</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Possessives also go plural:<br><strong>tāna</strong> (his/her, ā) → <strong>ā rātou</strong> (their, ā) — the ā stays, rātou replaces na<br><strong>tōna</strong> (his/her, ō) → <strong>ō rātou</strong> (their, ō) — the ō stays, rātou replaces na</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Common mistakes to avoid:<br>✗ Kei te haere <strong>ngā tamariki</strong>. Kei te noho <strong>ia</strong>. (mixing plural noun with singular pronoun)<br>✓ Kei te haere <strong>ngā tamariki</strong>. Kei te noho <strong>rātou</strong>.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>New words:<br><strong>kaiako</strong> — teacher (you built this with kai- in Term 1 Week 6!)<br><strong>ā rātou</strong> — their (ā-category)<br><strong>ō rātou</strong> — their (ō-category)</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Tip: Ngā needs rātou. Te needs ia. Never mix them. Check every pronoun — does it match the noun it refers back to? Singular or plural?</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">We Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Kia Tūhono — Spot and fix</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>I give you sentences — some correct, some with agreement errors. You find the error and fix it.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>&quot;Kei te ako ngā ākonga. Kei te mōhio ia.&quot; — Error? → ia should be rātou: <strong>Kei te mōhio rātou.</strong></li><li>&quot;I kai te kaiako i ō rātou kai.&quot; — Error? → One teacher (te) should have tōna: <strong>I kai te kaiako i tōna kai.</strong></li><li>&quot;Kei te haere mātou. I hoko tāna kai.&quot; — Error? → mātou is plural, tāna is singular: <strong>I hoko ā mātou kai.</strong></li><li>Now write two correct pairs yourself — one singular, one plural — showing perfect agreement.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Discussion prompt: Why does agreement matter? If you say &quot;ngā tamariki… ia&quot; (the children… he/she), your listener gets confused — who are we talking about now? Agreement is the glue that keeps a listener following the right thread through your sentences.</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">You Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Māu Anō — Agreement practice</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write sentences that demonstrate correct singular and plural agreement.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Write three pairs: one sentence with te/ia, one with ngā/rātou — same verb, matching articles and pronouns.</li><li>Write a sentence about your teacher (singular) using tōna/tāna correctly.</li><li>Write a sentence about your classmates (plural, 3+) using ō rātou or ā rātou correctly.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write a paragraph (6 sentences) about your class — mix singular and plural freely, but make sure every pronoun matches its noun. Check each sentence before moving to the next.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Thursday: Kōrero — He taupatupatu — A class debate":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Te Aho Arataki Marau — AO 2.3: Extended communication · AO 2.5: Formal vocabulary · AO 1.6: Politeness in disagreement</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Objectives</th><td class=\"lt-td\">AO 2.3 — Debate &amp; persuasion; AO 1.6 — Polite disagreement</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">I Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tāua Kōrero — Debating in te reo Māori</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This week you have learned colours, comparatives, and agreement. Today we put them all to work in a debate. Using comparatives to argue, politeness phrases to disagree, and accurate grammar to be credible — this is real te reo in action.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\"><strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=Kaiwhakahaere\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Kaiwhakahaere</a>:</strong> Ko tā tātou <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=kaupeka\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">kaupeka</a>: He pai ake te hākinakina i te karo i.<br><strong>Taha 1:</strong> Āe, he tika tērā. He nui ake ngā hua o te hākinakina — he <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=hauora\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">hauora</a>, he kaha, he hoa hou hoki.<br><strong>Taha 2:</strong> Kāo, kāore au e whakaae ana. He pai ake te noho ki te kāinga me ōu hoa tata nō te mea he mārie ake tērā.<br><strong>Taha 1:</strong> Ka pai tāu whakaaro, engari ko ngā hua hauora o te hākinakina he nui ake i ngā hua o te noho.<br><strong>Taha 2:</strong> He rerekē ā tātou whakaaro — engari he pai tērā! Ko tēnei te akoranga o tēnei kōrero.</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>New debate vocabulary:<br><strong>Kaiwhakahaere</strong> — chairperson / facilitator (whaka + haere = one who makes things go)<br><strong>kaupeka</strong> — topic / branch / proposition<br><strong>Taha 1 / Taha 2</strong> — Side 1 / Side 2 (taha = side)<br><strong>whakaae</strong> — agree / consent<br><strong>Kāore au e whakaae ana.</strong> — I do not agree.<br><strong>he hauora</strong> — health / wellbeing<br><strong>he hoa hou</strong> — new friends<br><strong>ko ngā hua hauora</strong> — the health benefits<br><strong>Ko tēnei te akoranga o tēnei kōrero.</strong> — This is the lesson of this discussion.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Debate structure in te reo:</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>State position (Ko tā tātou kaupeka…)</li><li>Support with comparatives (He nui ake ngā hua…)</li><li>Respond politely (Ka pai tāu whakaaro, engari…)</li><li>Close graciously (He rerekē ā tātou whakaaro — he pai tērā!)</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Tip: He rerekē ā tātou whakaaro — our opinions are different. This is the gracious way to end a debate in te ao Māori. Disagreement is acknowledged — but the relationship is preserved.</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">We Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Kia Tūhono — Mini debate</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Class splits into two sides. Teacher (kaiwhakahaere) introduces the topic. Each side argues in te reo.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Kaupeka: <strong>He pai ake te reo Māori i te pāngarau.</strong> Side 1 agrees — argue why using He nui ake ngā hua…</li><li>Side 2 disagrees: <strong>Kāore mātou e whakaae ana. He pai ake te pāngarau nō te mea…</strong></li><li>Side 1 responds: <strong>Ka pai tā koutou whakaaro, engari…</strong> Add a comparative to counter.</li><li>Close: everyone together: <strong>He rerekē ā tātou whakaaro — he pai tērā!</strong></li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Discussion prompt: Ko tēnei te akoranga o tēnei kōrero — this is the lesson of this discussion. What IS the lesson? Not who won — but what did the discussion teach you? In te ao Māori, a debate is not about defeating the other side. What is it about?</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">You Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Māu Anō — Your debate argument</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write your side of a debate argument in te reo Māori.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Choose a side on: &quot;He pai ake te ako i te kāinga i te ako i te kura.&quot; Write your position statement.</li><li>Write two supporting comparatives using He [adj] ake…</li><li>Write a polite response to the other side: Ka pai tā koutou whakaaro, engari…</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write a full six-sentence debate contribution — position, two supports, a response to opposition, a superlative claim, and a gracious close with He rerekē ā tātou whakaaro.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Friday: Aromatawai — Quiz & Review":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Monday questions</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Which te reo Māori colour word comes from a New Zealand bird?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>kikorangi </li><li>kōwhai </li><li>kākāriki ✅</li><li>karaka </li></ul></li><li>&quot;He pōtae kikorangi tāku.&quot; What colour is the hat?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>green </li><li>yellow </li><li>blue ✅</li><li>red </li></ul></li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tuesday questions</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>&quot;He pai ake te reo Māori i te pāngarau.&quot; What does the &quot;i&quot; before &quot;te pāngarau&quot; mean here?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>at </li><li>than ✅</li><li>past tense marker </li><li>object marker </li></ul></li><li>How do you say &quot;Te reo Māori is the most beautiful language in the world&quot; using a superlative?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>He ātaahua rawa atu te reo Māori i ērā atu reo </li><li>Ko te reo Māori te reo tino ātaahua rawa atu o te ao ✅</li><li>Te reo Māori he ātaahua ake i ngā reo katoa </li><li>He reo ātaahua rawa atu te reo Māori </li></ul></li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Wednesday questions</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>&quot;Kei te ako ngā ākonga. Kei te mōhio ia.&quot; What is wrong with this passage?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>The verb is incorrect </li><li>ia (singular he/she) does not match ngā ākonga (plural) — should be rātou ✅</li><li>The tense marker is wrong </li><li>There is no object marker </li></ul></li><li>What is the plural ā-category possessive for &quot;their&quot; (three or more people)?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>tā rātou </li><li>ā rātou ✅</li><li>ō rātou </li><li>tō rātou </li></ul></li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Thursday questions</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>What does &quot;whakaae&quot; mean?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>disagree </li><li>argue </li><li>agree / consent ✅</li><li>debate </li></ul></li><li>&quot;He rerekē ā tātou whakaaro — he pai tērā!&quot; How is this phrase used in a debate?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>To declare a winner </li><li>To insult the other side </li><li>To graciously acknowledge different views and preserve the relationship ✅</li><li>To ask for more time </li></ul></li></ul></div></div>"}}},"Week 4":{"label":"Day","children":{"Monday: Māua, tāua — Dual pronouns: we two":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Te Aho Arataki Marau — AO 2.1: Relationships between people · Grammar Level 1–2: Dual pronouns māua/tāua, kōrua, rāua</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Objectives</th><td class=\"lt-td\">AO 2.1 — Relationships — dual pronouns; AO 1.6 — Inclusive &amp; exclusive we</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">I Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tāua Kōrero — We two (and why it matters which &quot;we&quot;)</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>English has one word for &quot;we&quot; — it covers two people, ten people, and everyone in between, and it doesn't tell you if the person you're talking to is included. Te reo Māori has a separate word for every combination. Today we learn the &quot;we two&quot; set — and discover something beautiful about how te reo Māori thinks about relationships.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\"><strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=m%C4%81ua\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">māua</a></strong> — we two (me and someone else — NOT including you) — NOT including you)<br><strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=t%C4%81ua\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">tāua</a></strong> — we two (me and you — INCLUDING you) — INCLUDING you)<br><strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=k%C5%8Drua\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">kōrua</a></strong> — you two<br><strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=r%C4%81ua\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">rāua</a></strong> — they two / those two / those two<br><br><strong>Ka haere māua ki te toa.</strong><br>We two (not you) are going to the shop.<br><br><strong>Ka haere tāua ki te toa.</strong><br>We two (you and I) are going to the shop.<br><br><strong>Kei te kōrero rāua.</strong><br>Those two are talking.<br><br><strong>He aha ā kōrua mahi?</strong><br>What are you two doing?</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The inclusive/exclusive distinction:</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>tāua</strong> = WE TWO INCLUDING YOU → &quot;come with me&quot;<br><strong>māua</strong> = WE TWO EXCLUDING YOU → &quot;just us, not you&quot;</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This distinction exists because in te ao Māori, being included or excluded from a group matters deeply — it affects relationships, responsibilities, and mana.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Think of it this way:<br>→ &quot;Ka haere tāua&quot; = an invitation (we're doing this TOGETHER)<br>→ &quot;Ka haere māua&quot; = an announcement (we're going — you're not part of this)</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The dual pronouns at a glance:<br><strong>tāua</strong> — we two (inclusive)<br><strong>māua</strong> — we two (exclusive)<br><strong>kōrua</strong> — you two<br><strong>rāua</strong> — they two</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Tip: Tāua includes the person you're talking to — it's an invitation. Māua excludes them — it's just you and a third person. When in doubt: would you invite them along? Tāua. Would you not? Māua.</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">We Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Kia Tūhono — Choose the right we</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>I describe a situation — you choose tāua or māua and explain why.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>You and your best friend are planning a trip. You're talking to your friend about it. → <strong>tāua</strong> — they ARE included: &quot;Ka haere tāua ki te moana.&quot;</li><li>You and your mum went to the shop. You're telling your friend about it now. → <strong>māua</strong> — friend was NOT there: &quot;I haere māua ko tōku māmā ki te toa.&quot;</li><li>You're looking at two students across the room: → <strong>rāua</strong>: &quot;Kei te kōrero rāua.&quot;</li><li>You're asking two students directly what they're doing: → <strong>kōrua</strong>: &quot;He aha ā kōrua mahi?&quot;</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Discussion prompt: English speakers often find the inclusive/exclusive distinction the hardest thing about te reo Māori pronouns — because English doesn't have it at all. But many languages do — Japanese, Fijian, and many others. What does having this distinction tell us about what te ao Māori values in human relationships?</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">You Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Māu Anō — We two in action</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Use dual pronouns in real situations.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Write two sentences using tāua — invite someone to do something with you.</li><li>Write two sentences using māua — report something you and a third person did (excluding the listener).</li><li>Write one sentence about two others using rāua, and one question to two people using kōrua.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write a short dialogue (6 lines) between two students planning something together. Use tāua at least three times. Make the &quot;inclusive we&quot; feel warm and inviting.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Tuesday: Mātou, tātou — Plural pronouns: three or more":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Te Aho Arataki Marau — AO 2.1: Relationships · Grammar Level 1–2: Plural pronouns mātou/tātou, koutou, rātou</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Objectives</th><td class=\"lt-td\">AO 2.1 — Group relationships; AO 1.6 — Inclusive &amp; exclusive plurality</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">I Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tāua Kōrero — We many (and the same choice)</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Yesterday you learned māua/tāua (we two). The same inclusive/exclusive choice applies when there are THREE OR MORE people. Meet the group pronouns.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\"><strong>mātou</strong> — we (3+ people, NOT including you)<br><strong>tātou</strong> — we (3+ people, INCLUDING you)<br><strong>koutou</strong> — you all (3+ people)<br><strong>rātou</strong> — they (3+ people)<br><br><strong>Ka haere mātou ki te kura.</strong><br>We (our group, not you) are going to school.<br><br><strong>Ka haere tātou ki te kura.</strong><br>We all (including you) are going to school.<br><br><strong>Kei te ako koutou i te reo Māori.</strong><br>You all are learning te reo Māori.<br><br><strong>I tākaro rātou i waho inanahi.</strong><br>They (all those people) played outside yesterday.</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The full pronoun set — now complete:</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>SINGULAR:<br>→ au/ahau (I) · koe (you) · ia (he/she)</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>DUAL:<br>→ tāua (we 2, incl.) · māua (we 2, excl.) · kōrua (you 2) · rāua (they 2)</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>PLURAL:<br>→ tātou (we 3+, incl.) · mātou (we 3+, excl.) · koutou (you 3+) · rātou (they 3+)</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The pattern:<br>→ tā- = inclusive we<br>→ mā- = exclusive we<br>→ kou-/kō- = you (plural)<br>→ rā- = they/those</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Every pronoun you will ever need — all sixteen.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Tip: tā- always means &quot;including you&quot; (tāua, tātou). mā- always means &quot;not including you&quot; (māua, mātou). The prefix tells you the relationship.</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">We Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Kia Tūhono — All the pronouns</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Rapid fire — I describe who is involved, you give the right pronoun.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The whole class, including the teacher, and you are all in the discussion. → <strong>tātou</strong></li><li>Your team of five played a game. You're telling someone who wasn't there. → <strong>mātou</strong></li><li>You're talking to the whole class (everyone there, 3+). → <strong>koutou</strong></li><li>Three students across the room are working together. → <strong>rātou</strong></li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Discussion prompt: Tātou is one of the most beautiful words in te reo Māori — it means &quot;all of us, including you.&quot; It is an act of inclusion. How might saying tātou change the feeling of a meeting, a classroom, or a family gathering? When a rangatira says &quot;tātou katoa&quot; (all of us together) — what is the effect?</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">You Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Māu Anō — All sixteen pronouns</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Use the full range of pronouns in your writing today.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Write a sentence using each of: mātou, tātou, koutou, rātou.</li><li>Write a pair: one with mātou (excluding listener) and one with tātou (including listener) about the same event.</li><li>Write a question to the whole class using koutou.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write a paragraph (6 sentences) about a class event or group activity. Use at least four different pronouns from the full set — singular, dual, and plural.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Wednesday: Hanga rerenga — Pronouns in complex sentences":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Te Aho Arataki Marau — AO 2.1: Relationships · AO 2.3: Extended communication · Grammar Level 1–2: Pronoun consistency and reference across sentences</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Objectives</th><td class=\"lt-td\">AO 2.1 — Pronoun reference; AO 2.3 — Cohesive pronoun use</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">I Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tāua Kōrero — Keeping track of who is who</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Now that you have all sixteen pronouns, the challenge is keeping track of them in longer passages. When you shift from talking about rāua (two people) to rātou (a group), or from tāua (we two) to tātou (all of us), the reader needs to follow. Today we practise pronoun clarity.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">UNCLEAR — who is &quot;rātou&quot; here?<br><strong>Ka haere māua ko Hana ki te kura. I kite māua i ō māua hoa. Ka tākaro rātou.</strong><br>We two (Hana and I) went to school. We saw our friends. They played.<br>(Who played? Just the friends? Or us too?)<br><br>CLEAR — pronoun signals the group precisely:<br><strong>Ka haere māua ko Hana ki te kura. I kite māua i ō māua hoa tokowhitu. Ka tākaro rātou katoa — ko māua ko rātou.</strong><br>Hana and I went to school. We saw our seven friends. They all played — us two and them.</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Tools for pronoun clarity:</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>State the group first</strong>, then use the pronoun:</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&quot;I kite māua i ō māua hoa. Ka tākaro <strong>rātou</strong>.&quot; — ✗ unclear<br>&quot;Ko māua ko rātou — ka tākaro <strong>tātou</strong> katoa.&quot; — ✓ clear (now = all of us)</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>Use katoa (all) to be explicit:</strong></li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Ka tākaro rātou katoa.</strong> — They all played.<br><strong>Ka tākaro tātou katoa.</strong> — We all played.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>Name the people when switching:</strong></li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&quot;Ko māua ko rātou&quot; = &quot;the two of us and them&quot;</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>New words:<br><strong>tokowhitu</strong> — seven (people) (toko- + whitu = counting people)<br><strong>katoa</strong> — all / everyone (you've seen this before in Tēnā koutou katoa)<br><strong>Ko māua ko rātou</strong> — us two and them</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Tip: When you change who you're talking about, signal it clearly — name the group, use katoa, or restate with Ko [person] ko [person]. Never leave your listener guessing who &quot;rātou&quot; is.</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">We Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Kia Tūhono — Clarify the pronouns</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>I give you an unclear passage — we improve the pronoun clarity together.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Unclear: &quot;Ka haere rātou. Ka noho ia.&quot; → Who is ia? → Make it clear: state the person or repeat the noun.</li><li>Unclear: &quot;I kite māua i rātou. Ka tākaro mātou.&quot; → Who is in mātou now? → Add &quot;Ko māua ko rātou&quot; to clarify.</li><li>Practice: write two sentences where you clearly shift from tāua (we two) to tātou (all of us).</li><li>Challenge: write a four-sentence story that uses four different pronouns — and make every one crystal clear.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Discussion prompt: Pronoun clarity is an act of respect for your listener. When you are vague about who you mean, your listener has to guess — and guessing takes energy and creates confusion. How does clear pronoun use connect to the value of manaaki (caring for others)?</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">You Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Māu Anō — Pronoun precision</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write passages with deliberate, clear pronoun use.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Write a four-sentence passage about a group event — use three different pronouns clearly.</li><li>Write a pair of sentences that shift from māua to tātou — make the shift clear.</li><li>Write a sentence using Ko māua ko rātou to clarify a group.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write a paragraph (6 sentences) about your class doing an activity together. Use singular, dual, and plural pronouns — make sure every pronoun is crystal clear. Read it aloud and ask a partner if they always knew who you meant.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Thursday: Kōrero — He hui ā-kāinga — A family gathering":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Te Aho Arataki Marau — AO 2.1: Relationships · AO 1.4: Personal information · AO 2.3: Extended communication</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Objectives</th><td class=\"lt-td\">AO 2.1 — Family &amp; relationships; AO 1.4 — Personal &amp; family information</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">I Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tāua Kōrero — Talking about family</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This week's pronouns are really about relationships — who is in your group and whether you are included. Today we use them to talk about the most important group of all — whānau. Listen to how the pronouns naturally reflect the warmth and structure of a family gathering.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\"><strong>A:</strong> He pēhea tō <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=wh%C4%81nau\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">whānau</a>?<br><strong>B:</strong> He pai rātou katoa — he nui hoki ō mātou whānau!<br><strong>A:</strong> Tokohia koutou i roto i tō whānau?<br><strong>B:</strong> Tokowhitu mātou — ko ōku <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=m%C4%81tua\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">mātua</a>, ko ōku <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=tu%C4%81kana\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">tuākana</a> tokorua, ko ōku <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=t%C4%93ina\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">tēina</a> tokotoru, ko au.<br><strong>A:</strong> Ka haere koutou ki hea i ngā hararei?<br><strong>B:</strong> Ka haere tātou katoa ki tō mātou <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=marae\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">marae</a> i ngā hararei. He tino pai — ka <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=hui\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">hui</a> mātou, ka kai tātou, ka <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=waiata\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">waiata</a> tātou!<br><strong>A:</strong> He ātaahua tērā! Ko wai te <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=kai%C4%81rahi\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">kaiārahi</a> o tō koutou whānau?<br><strong>B:</strong> Ko tōku kuia — ko ia te <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=pou\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">pou</a> o tō mātou whānau.</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>New family and relationship vocabulary:<br><strong>mātua</strong> — parents (plural of mātua/matua)<br><strong>tuākana</strong> — older sibling of the same gender<br><strong>tēina</strong> — younger sibling of the same gender<br><strong>tokorua / tokotoru / tokowha…</strong> — two / three / four (people) (toko- prefix for counting people)<br><strong>marae</strong> — marae (the communal gathering place)<br><strong>hui</strong> — gather / meeting<br><strong>waiata</strong> — sing / song<br><strong>kaiārahi</strong> — leader / guide (kai- + ārahi = person who leads)<br><strong>kuia</strong> — elder woman / grandmother<br><strong>pou</strong> — pillar / post / foundation<br><strong>Ko ia te pou o tō mātou whānau.</strong> — She is the pillar of our family.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Tip: Toko- is the prefix for counting PEOPLE: tokorua (2), tokotoru (3), tokowha (4), torima (5), tokoono (6), tokowhitu (7), tokowaru (8), tokoriwa (9), tekau (10). Use it whenever you count people specifically.</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">We Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Kia Tūhono — Describe your whānau</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Build a whānau conversation using this week's pronouns and new family vocabulary.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I ask: <strong>Tokohia koutou i roto i tō whānau?</strong> You answer using tokorua/tokotoru etc. + list who is in your family.</li><li>I ask: <strong>Ka haere koutou ki hea i ngā hararei?</strong> Answer using Ka haere tātou/mātou and a place.</li><li>I ask: <strong>Ko wai te pou o tō koutou whānau?</strong> Answer: Ko [name/role] — ia te pou o tō mātou whānau.</li><li>Ask ME a question about my family using Tokohia or He pēhea.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Discussion prompt: Ko ia te pou o tō mātou whānau — she is the pillar of our family. The word pou (pillar/post) comes from the central pillar of a wharenui (meeting house). What does calling someone the pou of the family say about their role? What makes someone a pou in your whānau?</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">You Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Māu Anō — Tōu whānau</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Describe your family using this week's pronouns and vocabulary.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Write who is in your family using Tokohia and Ko [name] tōku [relationship].</li><li>Write what your family does together: Ka [verb] tātou/mātou…</li><li>Write who the pou of your family is: Ko [name] te pou o tō mātou whānau nō te mea…</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write a six-sentence paragraph about your whānau. Use: tokohia, tātou/mātou (and explain the choice), a Ka sequence of what you do together, and close with a Ko sentence about who your family's pou is.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Friday: Aromatawai — Quiz & Review":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Monday questions</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>&quot;Ka haere tāua ki te moana.&quot; Who is going to the beach?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>The speaker and a third person (not the listener) </li><li>The speaker and the listener — both of them together ✅</li><li>The speaker alone </li><li>Three or more people </li></ul></li><li>You went to town with your mum. You're telling your friend (who wasn't there). Which pronoun?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>tāua </li><li>tātou </li><li>māua ✅</li><li>mātou </li></ul></li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tuesday questions</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>What does &quot;tātou&quot; mean?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>we (3+, not including you) </li><li>you all (3+) </li><li>we (3+, including you) ✅</li><li>they (3+) </li></ul></li><li>Which prefix is always associated with an inclusive &quot;we&quot;?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>mā- </li><li>kou- </li><li>rā- </li><li>tā- ✅</li></ul></li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Wednesday questions</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>&quot;I kite māua i ō māua hoa. Ka tākaro rātou.&quot; Why is this unclear?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>The tense is wrong </li><li>Rātou is unclear — it is not certain whether māua is included in rātou or not ✅</li><li>Māua is the wrong pronoun </li><li>There is no object marker </li></ul></li><li>What does &quot;katoa&quot; mean?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>some </li><li>none </li><li>all / everyone ✅</li><li>a few </li></ul></li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Thursday questions</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>What is the toko- prefix used for?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>Counting things </li><li>Counting places </li><li>Counting people ✅</li><li>Counting time </li></ul></li><li>&quot;Ko ia te pou o tō mātou whānau.&quot; What does pou mean here?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>the youngest member </li><li>the leader who travels most </li><li>the pillar / foundation of the family ✅</li><li>the cook </li></ul></li></ul></div></div>"}}},"Week 5":{"label":"Day","children":{"Monday: Tekau mā tahi — Numbers 11–20":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Te Aho Arataki Marau — AO 1.3: Number · Grammar Level 1–2: Numbers 11–20, tekau mā pattern</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Objectives</th><td class=\"lt-td\">AO 1.3 — Numbers 11–20</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">I Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tāua Kōrero — Ten and one more: the tekau mā pattern</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>In Term 1 you learned tahi through tekau (1–10). Today we discover how te reo Māori builds numbers above ten — and it is beautifully logical. Once you know the pattern, you never need to memorise another number combination. Watch.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\"><strong>tekau mā tahi</strong> — 11 (ten and one)<br><strong>tekau mā rua</strong> — 12 (ten and two)<br><strong>tekau mā toru</strong> — 13<br><strong>tekau mā whā</strong> — 14<br><strong>tekau mā rima</strong> — 15<br><strong>tekau mā ono</strong> — 16<br><strong>tekau mā whitu</strong> — 17<br><strong>tekau mā waru</strong> — 18<br><strong>tekau mā iwa</strong> — 19<br><strong>rua tekau</strong> — 20 (two tens)</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The pattern:<br><strong>tekau mā</strong> + [ones digit] = 11 through 19</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>tekau</strong> = 10<br><strong>mā</strong> = and (joining tens and ones)<br><strong>tahi</strong> = 1 → tekau mā tahi = 11<br><strong>rua</strong> = 2 → tekau mā rua = 12<br>…and so on up to 19.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>For twenty: <strong>rua tekau</strong> (two tens) — the tens digit comes FIRST.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Used in real contexts:<br><strong>E tekau mā rima ngā ākonga i tēnei akomanga.</strong><br>There are 15 students in this classroom.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tokorua tekau mā tahi ō mātou whānau.</strong><br>There are 21 people in our family.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Kei te hoko mātou i te miraka — e tekau mā rua tāra te utu.</strong><br>We are buying milk — it costs $12.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Tip: Tekau mā = &quot;ten and&quot;. Just add the ones digit after it. For twenty onward: [tens digit] + tekau. Rua tekau = 20. Toru tekau = 30. It could not be more logical.</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">We Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Kia Tūhono — Count together</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Call and response — I say a number in English, you say it in te reo. Then we use numbers in sentences.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Numbers 11–19: rapid fire. Teacher says &quot;thirteen&quot; — students say <strong>tekau mā toru.</strong> Keep going to 19.</li><li>In a sentence: &quot;There are 14 students going.&quot; → <strong>Tokorua tekau mā whā ngā ākonga e haere ana.</strong> Wait — how many? Count who is actually going.</li><li>Price: &quot;The book costs $16.&quot; → <strong>E tekau mā ono tāra te pukapuka.</strong></li><li>Age: &quot;I am 11 years old.&quot; → <strong>Tekau mā tahi ōku tau.</strong> (tau = years/age)</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Discussion prompt: The te reo Māori number system is completely regular — no &quot;eleven&quot; or &quot;twelve&quot; (irregular English words from old Germanic). Every number above ten follows the same pattern. How does a regular system help language learners? Are there any disadvantages to regular systems?</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">You Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Māu Anō — Numbers in context</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Use numbers 11–20 in real sentences.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Write your age in te reo using tekau mā [number] ōku tau.</li><li>Write how many students are in your class: E [number] ngā ākonga i tēnei akomanga.</li><li>Write three prices using E [number] tāra te [item].</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write five sentences using numbers 11–20 in different contexts — age, price, quantity, distance, time. Use E hia? to ask how many, and answer each one.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Tuesday: Rua tekau, toru tekau — Numbers 20–100":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Te Aho Arataki Marau — AO 1.3: Number · Grammar Level 1–2: Tens and compound numbers to 100</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Objectives</th><td class=\"lt-td\">AO 1.3 — Numbers 20–100</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">I Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tāua Kōrero — All the way to one hundred</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Yesterday you learned 11–20. The same logic takes you all the way to 100. Te reo Māori numbers are completely regular — once you know 1–10 and the pattern, you know every number.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\"><strong>rua tekau</strong> — 20<br><strong>rua tekau mā tahi</strong> — 21<br><strong>rua tekau mā rima</strong> — 25<br><strong>toru tekau</strong> — 30<br><strong>toru tekau mā whā</strong> — 34<br><strong>whā tekau</strong> — 40<br><strong>rima tekau</strong> — 50<br><strong>ono tekau</strong> — 60<br><strong>whitu tekau</strong> — 70<br><strong>waru tekau</strong> — 80<br><strong>iwa tekau</strong> — 90<br><strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=kotahi\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">kotahi</a> <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=rau\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">rau</a></strong> — 100 (one hundred) (one hundred)</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The full pattern:<br>[tens digit] + tekau = the tens number<br>[tens digit] + tekau + mā + [ones digit] = the in-between number</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Examples:<br><strong>whā tekau</strong> = 40<br><strong>whā tekau mā rua</strong> = 42<br><strong>iwa tekau mā iwa</strong> = 99<br><strong>kotahi rau</strong> = 100</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Used in context:<br><strong>E iwa tekau mā rua ngā tau o tōku tīpuna.</strong><br>My ancestor is 92 years old. (tīpuna = ancestor/grandparent)</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>E rima tekau tāra te utu o tērā kākahu.</strong><br>That piece of clothing costs $50. (kākahu = clothing)</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>E toru tekau mā whā ngā ākonga i tō mātou kura.</strong><br>There are 34 students in our school.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Tip: The pattern never breaks: [tens] tekau (mā [ones]). Memorise the tens (rua tekau, toru tekau… iwa tekau) and you can say any number to 99. Add kotahi rau for 100.</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">We Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Kia Tūhono — Numbers in real life</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Use numbers 20–100 in sentences about real things.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>How many days in a year? → <strong>E toru rau ono tekau mā rima ngā rā i tētahi tau.</strong> Wait — that's 365. Break it into hundreds: toru rau + ono tekau mā rima.</li><li>Your school: how many students? → <strong>E [number] ngā ākonga i tō mātou kura.</strong></li><li>A price: something that costs between $20 and $99. → <strong>E [number] tāra te [item].</strong></li><li>An age: how old is someone in your whānau? → <strong>E [number] ōna tau.</strong></li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Discussion prompt: Three hundred and sixty-five in te reo: toru rau ono tekau mā rima. Every single digit stated in order — no shortcuts. Compare to English: &quot;three hundred and sixty-five&quot; — same structure! What does this tell you about how number systems often work across languages?</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">You Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Māu Anō — Number sentences</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Use numbers 20–100 across a range of real contexts.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Write how old three people in your whānau are using E [number] ōna/ōna tau.</li><li>Write three prices for things you know using E [number] tāra te [item].</li><li>Write how many students are in your year group and school.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write a &quot;number story&quot; — seven sentences, each using a different number between 20 and 100 in a different context (age, price, distance, quantity, score, year, temperature).</li></ul></div></div>"},"Wednesday: Hanga rerenga — Numbers in extended sentences":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Te Aho Arataki Marau — AO 1.3: Number · AO 2.3: Extended communication · Grammar Level 1–2: Numbers in complex sentence contexts</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Objectives</th><td class=\"lt-td\">AO 1.3 — Numbers in context; AO 2.3 — Extended number sentences</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">I Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tāua Kōrero — Using numbers to tell stories</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Numbers become powerful when they appear inside rich sentences. Today we practise embedding numbers into the complex sentence structures you have built this term and last — past tense, comparatives, fronted phrases, and lists.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">With past tense:<br><strong>I haere mātou tokowhitu ki te hākinakina inanahi.</strong><br>Seven of us went to sport yesterday.<br><br>With comparison:<br><strong>He nui ake ngā ākonga i tēnei akomanga i tērā — e toru tekau mā rua ki e rua tekau mā whā.</strong><br>There are more students in this class than that one — 32 versus 24.<br><br>With Ko ēnei:<br><strong>Ko ēnei ngā utu: e whā tekau tāra mō te <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=p%C5%8Dtae\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">pōtae</a>, e rua tekau mā rima tāra mō ngā <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=t%C5%AB\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">tū</a>.</strong><br>These are the prices: $40 for the hat, $25 for the shoes. (tū = shoes/footwear)<br><br>With a fronted phrase:<br><strong>I te Hātarei o tērā wiki, e toru tekau mā rua ngā <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=t%C4%81ngata\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">tāngata</a> i haere mai ki tō mātou hui.</strong><br>Last Saturday, 32 people came to our meeting.</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Numbers in different positions:</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>As SUBJECT count: <strong>E [number] ngā [noun]</strong> — there are [number] [things]<br>As PEOPLE count: <strong>Toko[number] mātou</strong> — there are [number] of us<br>In COMPARISON: <strong>e [number] ki e [number]</strong> — [number] versus [number]<br>In a LIST: <strong>e [number] tāra mō te [item]</strong> — [number] dollars for the [item]</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>New words:<br><strong>Hātarei</strong> — Saturday<br><strong>hui</strong> — meeting / gathering<br><strong>tū</strong> — shoes / footwear<br><strong>haere mai</strong> — come (toward the speaker)</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Tip: Put the number as close to the noun it counts as possible. E rua tekau ngā ākonga — 20 students. The number and the noun stay together like a pair.</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">We Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Kia Tūhono — Numbers in context</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Build sentences that embed numbers naturally in complex structures.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Past + people count: &quot;14 of us played sport yesterday.&quot; → <strong>I tākaro mātou tokorua tekau mā whā i te hākinakina inanahi.</strong></li><li>Comparison with numbers: &quot;This class has more students than that one — 28 vs 21.&quot; → <strong>He nui ake ngā ākonga i tēnei akomanga — e rua tekau mā waru ki e rua tekau mā tahi.</strong></li><li>Fronted phrase + number: &quot;Last Friday, 45 people came to the school concert.&quot; → Build it step by step.</li><li>Ko ēnei list with prices: List three items and their prices using Ko ēnei ngā utu…</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Discussion prompt: Numbers make claims precise. &quot;Many people came&quot; is vague. &quot;E whā tekau mā rima ngā tāngata i haere mai&quot; is exact. When does precision matter? Can being too precise ever be inappropriate in te ao Māori contexts?</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">You Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Māu Anō — Rich number sentences</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write sentences that use numbers inside complex structures.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Write a past tense sentence with a people count using toko[number].</li><li>Write a comparison sentence using two numbers and &quot;ki&quot; (versus).</li><li>Write a Ko ēnei list with prices for three items.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write a paragraph (6 sentences) about an event — a sports day, a school fair, or a family gathering. Include at least four numbers in different positions: as a count, as a price, in a comparison, and in a fronted phrase.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Thursday: Kōrero — He toa hākinakina — Sports day":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Te Aho Arataki Marau — AO 1.3: Number · AO 2.3: Extended communication · AO 2.1: Relationships</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Objectives</th><td class=\"lt-td\">AO 1.3 — Numbers in sports context; AO 2.3 — Sports event conversation</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">I Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tāua Kōrero — Talking about sports day</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Today we use numbers, pronouns, tenses, and comparatives in a sports day conversation. Sports is one of the richest contexts for te reo — scores, people, comparisons, and feelings all come together.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\"><strong>A:</strong> He pēhea tā koutou toa hākinakina inanahi?<br><strong>B:</strong> He tino pai! I toa mātou — e whā tekau mā rima ki e rua tekau mā toru ngā ira!<br><strong>A:</strong> Tokohia koutou i tākaro?<br><strong>B:</strong> Tokorua tekau mā rua mātou. I mahi kaha tātou katoa!<br><strong>A:</strong> Ko wai te kaitākaro pai rawa atu?<br><strong>B:</strong> Ko tōku hoa ko Rangi — i eke ia i ngā <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=ira\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">ira</a> e tekau mā whā!<br><strong>A:</strong> Ka rawe! He pai ake tēnei hua i tērā tau?<br><strong>B:</strong> Āe — he nui ake ngā ira i tēnei tau! He tino <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=harikoa\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">harikoa</a> mātou katoa.</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>New sports and achievement vocabulary:<br><strong>toa hākinakina</strong> — sports competition / tournament (toa = victorious/champion)<br><strong>ira</strong> — points / score<br><strong>eke</strong> — to score / reach / mount<br><strong>kaitākaro</strong> — player (kai- + tākaro)<br><strong>harikoa</strong> — joyful / elated / very happy<br><strong>i <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=eke\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">eke</a> ia i ngā ira e tekau mā whā</strong> — she scored 14 points (eke = scored/reached)<br><strong>hua</strong> — result / outcome (also: benefit, from Term 2 Week 3)<br><strong>i tērā tau</strong> — last year</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Notice the score format:<br><strong>e [score1] ki e [score2] ngā ira</strong> — [score1] to [score2] points</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Tip: E [number] ki e [number] ngā ira = [number] to [number] the score. This is how you report any score in te reo Māori. Learn it cold — you will use it constantly in sports contexts.</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">We Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Kia Tūhono — Sports day conversation</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Build a sports day conversation using numbers, pronouns, and tenses.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I ask: <strong>He pēhea tā koutou toa hākinakina?</strong> Answer with a result — did you win? Use I toa or Kāore mātou i toa.</li><li>I ask: <strong>Tokohia koutou i tākaro?</strong> Answer using toko[number].</li><li>I ask: <strong>He aha te ira mutunga?</strong> (What was the final score?) Answer: E [number] ki e [number] ngā ira.</li><li>I ask: <strong>Ko wai te kaitākaro pai rawa atu?</strong> Name someone and say what they did: i eke ia i ngā ira e [number].</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Discussion prompt: I eke ia i ngā ira e tekau mā whā — she scored 14 points. The word &quot;eke&quot; also means &quot;to mount&quot; or &quot;to ride&quot; — as in riding a wave or climbing a hill. Why might te reo Māori use the same word for scoring points as for riding a wave? What does that connection suggest about achievement?</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">You Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Māu Anō — Your sports day report</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write a sports day report in te reo Māori.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Write the result: I toa/Kāore i toa + E [score] ki e [score] ngā ira.</li><li>Write how many players: Toko[number] mātou i tākaro.</li><li>Write who the best player was: Ko [name] te kaitākaro pai rawa atu — i eke ia i ngā ira e [number].</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write a full sports report (7 sentences) — result, score, player count, best player, atmosphere (harikoa/pōuri), and a comparison with last year. Use at least five different numbers.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Friday: Aromatawai — Quiz & Review":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Monday questions</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>How do you say &quot;17&quot; in te reo Māori?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>tekau mā ono </li><li>tekau mā whitu ✅</li><li>tekau whitu </li><li>whitu tekau </li></ul></li><li>&quot;Tekau mā rima ōku tau.&quot; What does this sentence tell us?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>There are 15 things </li><li>I am 15 years old ✅</li><li>The price is $15 </li><li>There are 15 people </li></ul></li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tuesday questions</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>How do you say &quot;43&quot; in te reo Māori?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>toru tekau mā whā </li><li>whā tekau mā toru ✅</li><li>toru whā tekau </li><li>whā tekau toru </li></ul></li><li>What is &quot;kotahi rau&quot; in English?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>ten </li><li>fifty </li><li>one hundred ✅</li><li>one thousand </li></ul></li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Wednesday questions</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>&quot;E rua tekau mā waru ki e rua tekau mā tahi ngā ira.&quot; What does this describe?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>A comparison of prices </li><li>A score of 28 to 21 ✅</li><li>The number of students in two classes </li><li>Ages of two people </li></ul></li><li>&quot;Tokorua tekau mā rua mātou i tākaro.&quot; What does toko- before a number indicate?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>The number refers to things </li><li>The number refers to people ✅</li><li>The number is approximate </li><li>The number is a price </li></ul></li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Thursday questions</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>&quot;I eke ia i ngā ira e tekau mā whā.&quot; What happened?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>She missed 14 points </li><li>She scored 14 points ✅</li><li>She played for 14 minutes </li><li>She won by 14 points </li></ul></li><li>What does &quot;harikoa&quot; mean?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>tired after sport </li><li>sad about losing </li><li>joyful / elated / very happy ✅</li><li>competitive </li></ul></li></ul></div></div>"}}},"Week 6":{"label":"Day","children":{"Monday: Wā, rā, marama, tau — Days, months, and years":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Te Aho Arataki Marau — AO 1.3: Days, months, dates · AO 2.4: Time · Grammar Level 1–2: Days of the week, months of the year, date expressions</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Objectives</th><td class=\"lt-td\">AO 1.3 — Days, months &amp; dates; AO 2.4 — Time expressions</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">I Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tāua Kōrero — When? The days, months, and years</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>AO 1.3 asks us to communicate about number, days, months, and dates. Today we complete that picture — you already have numbers to 100. Now we add the days of the week, months of the year, and how to say a date.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\"><strong>DAYS OF THE WEEK:</strong><br><strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=R%C4%81hina\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Rāhina</a></strong> — Monday<br><strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=R%C4%81t%C5%AB\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Rātū</a></strong> — Tuesday<br><strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=R%C4%81apa\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Rāapa</a></strong> — Wednesday<br><strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=R%C4%81pare\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Rāpare</a></strong> — Thursday<br><strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=R%C4%81mere\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Rāmere</a></strong> — Friday<br><strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=R%C4%81horoi\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Rāhoroi</a></strong> — Saturday<br><strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=R%C4%81tapu\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Rātapu</a></strong> — Sunday<br><br><strong>MONTHS:</strong><br><strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=Kohit%C4%81tea\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Kohitātea</a></strong> — January · <strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=Huitanguru\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Huitanguru</a></strong> — February · <strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=Pout%C5%ABterangi\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Poutūterangi</a></strong> — March<br><strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=Paengawh%C4%81wh%C4%81\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Paengawhāwhā</a></strong> — April · <strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=Haratua\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Haratua</a></strong> — May · <strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=Pipiri\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Pipiri</a></strong> — June<br><strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=H%C5%8Dngongoi\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Hōngongoi</a></strong> — July · <strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=Hereturik%C5%8Dk%C4%81\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Hereturikōkā</a></strong> — August · <strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=Mahuru\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Mahuru</a></strong> — September<br><strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=Whiringa-%C4%81-nuku\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Whiringa-ā-nuku</a></strong> — October · <strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=Whiringa-%C4%81-rangi\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Whiringa-ā-rangi</a></strong> — November · <strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=Hakihea\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Hakihea</a></strong> — December</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>How to say a date:<br><strong>I te [number] o [month], [year]</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>E.g. &quot;On the 14th of March, 2024&quot;:<br><strong>I te tekau mā whā o Poutūterangi, rua mano rua tekau mā whā.</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Simpler date questions:<br><strong>Ko tēhea rā tēnei?</strong> — What day is today?<br><strong>Ko Rāhina tēnei.</strong> — Today is Monday.<br><strong>Ko tēhea marama tēnei?</strong> — What month is this?<br><strong>Ko Pipiri tēnei.</strong> — This is June.<br><strong>Ko tēhea tau tēnei?</strong> — What year is this?</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Days of the week come from:<br>Rā- (sun/day) + the number of the day (Rāhina = Moon-day, Rātū = Tuesday…)</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Tip: Ko tēhea rā/marama/tau tēnei? = What day/month/year is this? Ko [answer] tēnei. = This is [answer]. These two sentences handle almost every date question you will ever need.</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">We Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Kia Tūhono — Days, months, dates</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Answer date questions and use them in sentences.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I ask: <strong>Ko tēhea rā tēnei?</strong> Answer with today's day.</li><li>I ask: <strong>Ko tēhea marama tēnei?</strong> Answer with this month.</li><li>Use a day in a sentence: &quot;Last Monday I went to…&quot; → <strong>I te Rāhina o tērā wiki, i haere au ki…</strong></li><li>Say today's full date: <strong>Ko te [number] o [month] tēnei rā.</strong></li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Discussion prompt: The Māori month names are not translations of English months — they come from the traditional Māori lunar calendar and describe natural events: Pipiri (June) = the fetal position of animals huddling for warmth; Mahuru (September) = the earth becomes warm. What does this tell you about how Māori understood time and nature?</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">You Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Māu Anō — Dates in context</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Use days, months, and dates in real sentences.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Write today's full date: Ko te [number] o [month], [year] tēnei rā.</li><li>Write what day your birthday falls on this year.</li><li>Write three sentences using different days of the week in past tense: I te Rāhina, i te Rātū…</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write a weekly diary — one sentence for each day of the school week (Rāhina to Rāmere) about what happened or what will happen. Use past tense for days gone, Ka for future days.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Tuesday: He aha te wā? — Telling the time":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Te Aho Arataki Marau — AO 1.3: Time · AO 2.4: Time expressions · Grammar Level 1–2: Clock time, He aha te wā?</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Objectives</th><td class=\"lt-td\">AO 1.3 — Clock time; AO 2.4 — Daily time expressions</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">I Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tāua Kōrero — What time is it?</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>You know how to say dates. Now let's tell the time. Te reo Māori time-telling uses a simple structure that relies on your numbers — another reason why knowing your numbers to 60 is so useful.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\"><strong>He aha te <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=w%C4%81\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">wā</a>?</strong> — What time is it?<br><br><strong>Ko te [hour] <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=karaka\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">karaka</a>.</strong> — It is [hour] o'clock.<br><strong>Ko te toru karaka.</strong> — It is 3 o'clock.<br><br><strong>Ko te whā karaka mā rua tekau.</strong> — It is 4:20.<br>(hour + karaka + mā + minutes)<br><br><strong>Ko te iwa karaka māwhā tekau mā rima.</strong> — It is 9:45.<br><br><strong>Ā te [hour] karaka.</strong> — At [hour] o'clock. (for scheduling)<br><strong>Ā te rima karaka ka tīmata te hākinakina.</strong><br>At 5 o'clock sport starts.</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The structure:<br><strong>Ko te [hour] karaka</strong> = It is [hour] o'clock<br><strong>Ko te [hour] karaka mā [minutes]</strong> = It is [hour]:[minutes]</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>karaka</strong> = clock (from English &quot;clock&quot; → karaka)<br><strong>mā</strong> = and (joining hours and minutes — same mā as in tekau mā tahi)</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Time of day expressions:<br><strong>i te ata</strong> — in the morning / AM<br><strong>i te ahiahi</strong> — in the afternoon / evening<br><strong>i te pō</strong> — at night</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Full time with AM/PM:<br><strong>Ko te tekau karaka i te ata.</strong> — It is 10 AM.<br><strong>Ko te rua karaka i te ahiahi.</strong> — It is 2 PM.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Tip: Ko te [hour] karaka mā [minutes]. Just put the hour, say karaka (clock), add mā (and), then the minutes. Exactly like tekau mā tahi — but for time.</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">We Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Kia Tūhono — What time is it?</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>I show you a time — you say it in te reo. Then use it in a sentence.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>3:00 PM → <strong>Ko te toru karaka i te ahiahi.</strong></li><li>8:30 AM → <strong>Ko te waru karaka mā toru tekau i te ata.</strong></li><li>Scheduling: &quot;School starts at 9 AM.&quot; → <strong>Ā te iwa karaka i te ata ka tīmata te kura.</strong></li><li>Ask and answer: <strong>He aha te wā?</strong> → <strong>Ko te [current time] karaka.</strong> What time is it right now?</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Discussion prompt: Te reo Māori uses &quot;karaka&quot; (from English &quot;clock&quot;) for time-telling. This is a loanword — a word borrowed from another language. Why do languages borrow words? Can you think of other loanwords in te reo Māori? In English?</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">You Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Māu Anō — Your daily timetable</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write your school day timetable in te reo Māori.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Write what time school starts and finishes: Ā te [time] karaka ka tīmata/mutu te kura.</li><li>Write three things you do at specific times today using Ā te [time] karaka ka…</li><li>Ask a partner: He aha te wā i tīmata ai tō kura? (What time did your school start?) They answer.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write your full school day as a timetable — every lesson with its start time, using Ā te [time] karaka ka [subject/activity]. At least six time entries.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Wednesday: Hanga rerenga — Dates, times, and events in full sentences":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Te Aho Arataki Marau — AO 1.3: Number/date/time · AO 2.4: Time · AO 2.3: Extended communication</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Objectives</th><td class=\"lt-td\">AO 1.3 — Dates &amp; times in context; AO 2.3 — Scheduling &amp; events</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">I Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tāua Kōrero — Invitations, events, and schedules</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Dates and times become truly useful when you can use them to make plans, describe events, and write schedules. Today we combine everything from this week — days, months, times, numbers — into the kinds of sentences you would use for a real school event.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\"><strong>He hui tēnei ā te Rāpare, ko te tekau mā rima o Hōngongoi, ā te whitu karaka i te ahiahi.</strong><br>There is a meeting this Thursday, the 15th of July, at 7 PM.<br><br><strong>Ka tīmata te hākinakina ā te Rāhoroi i te iwa karaka i te ata.</strong><br>Sport starts on Saturday at 9 AM.<br><br><strong>I haere mātou ki te hui ā te Rāhina. Tokowhitu mātou. Ka tīmata ā te rua tekau mā rua karaka, ā ka mutu ā te toru karaka i te ahiahi.</strong><br>We went to the meeting on Monday. There were seven of us. It started at 12:22 and finished at 3 PM.</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Building event sentences:</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>[WHAT] + [WHEN: day] + [WHEN: time] + [WHEN: date]</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Key connectors for scheduling:<br><strong>ā te [day]</strong> — on [day] (upcoming)<br><strong>i te [day]</strong> — on [day] (past)<br><strong>ā te [time] karaka</strong> — at [time] o'clock<br><strong>ka tīmata</strong> — starts / will start<br><strong>ka mutu</strong> — finishes / will end<br><strong>mō te [duration]</strong> — for [duration]</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>New words:<br><strong>hui</strong> — meeting / gathering (you've seen this)<br><strong>duration: mō ngā haora e [number]</strong> — for [number] hours (haora = hour, from English)</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Tip: Build event sentences in layers: what → what day → what time → how many people → what happened. Each layer adds precision. The listener always knows exactly what, when, and who.</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">We Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Kia Tūhono — Plan an event</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Together we plan and describe a school event in te reo Māori.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Name the event and day: <strong>He [event] ā te [day].</strong> What is happening this week at school?</li><li>Add the date and time: <strong>Ko te [number] o [month]. Ka tīmata ā te [time] karaka.</strong></li><li>Add how many people: <strong>Toko[number] mātou/tātou.</strong></li><li>Describe what will happen: Use Ka sentences to sequence the events of the evening.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Discussion prompt: I te iwa karaka i te ata ka tīmata te kura. Does your school really start at 9? What time does it start in te reo? The act of putting your real life into te reo Māori — your actual school time, your actual class size — makes the language genuinely yours. Why does that matter?</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">You Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Māu Anō — Design your event</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Plan and write about a real or imagined school event.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Write what the event is and what day it is: He [event] ā te [day], ko te [date] o [month].</li><li>Write when it starts and finishes: Ka tīmata ā te [time] karaka, ka mutu ā te [time] karaka.</li><li>Write how many people will be there and what will happen: Ka [verb] tātou…</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write a full event invitation (7 sentences) for a real school event — include the event name, day, full date, start and end time, how many people, what will happen, and who will lead (Mā wai e ārahi).</li></ul></div></div>"},"Thursday: Kōrero — He pānui — Reading and giving announcements":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Te Aho Arataki Marau — AO 1.3: Date/time · AO 1.7: Classroom language · AO 2.3: Extended communication</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Objectives</th><td class=\"lt-td\">AO 1.3 — Announcements with dates/times; AO 1.7 — Classroom &amp; school language</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">I Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tāua Kōrero — Making and understanding announcements</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Today we use dates, times, and events in the most practical way possible — making real school announcements. This is AO 1.7 (classroom language) meeting AO 1.3 (dates and numbers) in real action.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\"><strong>Tēnā koutou katoa.</strong><br>Greetings to you all.<br><br><strong>He <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=p%C4%81nui\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">pānui</a> āku mōu katoa.</strong><br>I have an announcement for you all. (pānui = announcement/notice/reading)<br><br><strong>Ā te Rāpare, ko te rua tekau o Pipiri, ka tū he <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=hui\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">hui</a> mō ngā <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=m%C4%81tua\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">mātua</a> i te kura.</strong><br>On Thursday the 20th of June, there will be a parent meeting at school.<br><br><strong>Ka tīmata ā te whitu karaka i te ahiahi, ka mutu ā te iwa karaka.</strong><br>It starts at 7 PM and finishes at 9 PM.<br><br><strong>E tū ana i te <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=whare\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">whare</a> hui. Haere mai ngā mātua katoa!</strong><br>It is being held in the meeting room. All parents come!<br><br><strong>Tēnā koutou, tēnā koutou, tēnā koutou katoa.</strong></blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Announcement vocabulary:<br><strong>pānui</strong> — announcement / notice (also: to read — same word!)<br><strong>He pānui āku mōu.</strong> — I have an announcement for you.<br><strong>ka tū</strong> — will be held / will stand (tū = stand)<br><strong>E tū ana</strong> — is being held (ongoing location)<br><strong>whare hui</strong> — meeting room / hall<br><strong>Haere mai!</strong> — Come! (invitation toward the speaker)</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The announcement structure:</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Greet: Tēnā koutou katoa</li><li>Signal: He pānui āku</li><li>Event + day + date</li><li>Time (start and end)</li><li>Location</li><li>Call to action: Haere mai!</li><li>Close: Tēnā koutou (×3)</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Tip: He pānui āku mōu katoa — I have an announcement for you all. This is your attention-getting phrase. Say it with confidence and the class will listen.</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">We Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Kia Tūhono — Make an announcement</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Together we write and perform a class announcement for a real upcoming school event.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Opening: Everyone together — <strong>Tēnā koutou katoa.</strong> Signal: <strong>He pānui āku.</strong></li><li>Name the event and when: <strong>Ā te [day], ko te [date] o [month], ka tū he [event].</strong></li><li>Time: <strong>Ka tīmata ā te [time] karaka, ka mutu ā te [time] karaka.</strong></li><li>Location + call: <strong>E tū ana i te [place]. Haere mai [who]!</strong> Then close with Tēnā koutou ×3.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Discussion prompt: Making an announcement in te reo Māori to your class — even a simple one — is a real act of language use. How does it feel different from just writing sentences in an exercise? What does it add to do something with language that has a real purpose and audience?</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">You Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Māu Anō — Your announcement</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write and perform your own school announcement in te reo Māori.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Choose a real upcoming school event (or invent one).</li><li>Write the full announcement following the seven-step structure.</li><li>Practise delivering it — aim to say it without looking at the page.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Deliver your announcement to the class. The class listens and, without looking at a text, writes down the key details: event, day, date, time, location. Check for accuracy.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Friday: Aromatawai — Quiz & Review":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Monday questions</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>What is Wednesday in te reo Māori?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>Rāhina </li><li>Rāapa ✅</li><li>Rātū </li><li>Rāmere </li></ul></li><li>Which month is &quot;Pipiri&quot;?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>March </li><li>April </li><li>June ✅</li><li>July </li></ul></li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tuesday questions</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>&quot;Ko te whā karaka mā rua tekau.&quot; What time is this?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>4:02 </li><li>4:12 </li><li>4:20 ✅</li><li>24:00 </li></ul></li><li>&quot;Ā te rima karaka ka tīmata te hākinakina.&quot; What does this sentence tell us?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>Sport finished at 5 o'clock </li><li>Sport starts at 5 o'clock ✅</li><li>There are 5 players </li><li>The game lasted 5 hours </li></ul></li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Wednesday questions</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>&quot;Ka tīmata ā te rua tekau mā rua karaka, ka mutu ā te toru karaka.&quot; When does the event end?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>At 2:22 </li><li>At 3:00 ✅</li><li>At 22:03 </li><li>At 12:22 </li></ul></li><li>What is &quot;haora&quot; a loanword for?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>minute </li><li>second </li><li>hour ✅</li><li>day </li></ul></li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Thursday questions</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>&quot;He pānui āku mōu katoa.&quot; What is the speaker doing?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>Asking a question </li><li>Saying goodbye </li><li>Giving an announcement ✅</li><li>Introducing themselves </li></ul></li><li>What does &quot;Haere mai!&quot; mean?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>Go away! </li><li>Come here! (invitation toward speaker) ✅</li><li>Be quiet! </li><li>Sit down! </li></ul></li></ul></div></div>"}}},"Week 7":{"label":"Day","children":{"Monday: He pēhea te āhua o te rangi? — Weather and seasons":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Te Aho Arataki Marau — AO 2.4: Time, weather and seasons · Grammar Level 1–2: Weather expressions, Ka for weather narrative</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Objectives</th><td class=\"lt-td\">AO 2.4 — Weather &amp; seasons; AO 1.5 — Environmental description</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">I Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tāua Kōrero — Talking about the weather</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Weather is one of the most universal conversation topics. In te reo Māori, weather expressions are beautifully descriptive and often poetic — reflecting the deep connection between Māori culture and the natural environment. Today we build a full weather vocabulary.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\"><strong>WEATHER CONDITIONS:</strong><br><strong>He <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=ua\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">ua</a>.</strong> — It is raining. (ua = rain)<br><strong>He hau.</strong> — It is windy. (hau = wind)<br><strong>He rā pai.</strong> — It is a fine day.<br><strong>He <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=kapua\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">kapua</a>.</strong> — It is cloudy. (kapua = cloud)<br><strong>He <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=hukarere\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">hukarere</a>.</strong> — It is snowing. (hukarere = snow)<br><strong>He <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=%C4%81niwaniwa\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">āniwaniwa</a>.</strong> — There is a rainbow. (āniwaniwa = rainbow)<br><strong>He <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=uira\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">uira</a>.</strong> — There is lightning.<br><strong>He <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=whatitiri\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">whatitiri</a>.</strong> — There is thunder.<br><strong>He <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=kohu\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">kohu</a>.</strong> — There is fog/mist.<br><br><strong>TEMPERATURE:</strong><br><strong>He makariri.</strong> — It is cold.<br><strong>He <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=mahana\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">mahana</a>.</strong> — It is warm.<br><strong>He <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=wera\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">wera</a>.</strong> — It is hot.</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Asking about weather:<br><strong>He pēhea te āhua o te rangi?</strong> — What is the weather like?<br><strong>He pēhea te rangi?</strong> — How is the weather? (shorter form)</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Weather in the past:<br><strong>I pēhea te āhua o te rangi inanahi?</strong><br>What was the weather like yesterday?</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Weather with Ka (forecasting or narrative):<br><strong>Ka ua āpōpō.</strong> — It will rain tomorrow.<br><strong>Ka mahana te rangi ā tērā wiki.</strong> — The weather will be warm next week.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Seasons:<br><strong>kōanga</strong> — spring<br><strong>raumati</strong> — summer<br><strong>hōtoke</strong> — winter<br><strong>kārearea</strong> — NOT a season — it is a falcon! The season is...<br><strong>kōanga, raumati, ngahuru, hōtoke</strong> — spring, summer, autumn, winter</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Tip: He [weather word] for right now. I [weather word] te rangi for past. Ka [weather word] te rangi for future. Same pattern as any other tense — just weather vocabulary.</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">We Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Kia Tūhono — Weather report</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Together we create a week-long weather report for an imaginary or real location.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Today's weather: <strong>He pēhea te āhua o te rangi ināianei?</strong> → <strong>He [weather] te rangi.</strong></li><li>Yesterday's weather: <strong>I pēhea te āhua o te rangi inanahi?</strong> → <strong>I [weather] te rangi.</strong></li><li>Tomorrow's forecast: <strong>Ka pēhea te āhua o te rangi āpōpō?</strong> → <strong>Ka [weather] te rangi āpōpō.</strong></li><li>Compare two days: <strong>He [adj] ake te rangi o tēnei rā i tērā rā.</strong></li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Discussion prompt: The word āniwaniwa (rainbow) contains the word rangi (sky). Hukarere (snow) contains huka (foam/froth) + rere (to flow/fall). Many Māori weather words describe what is happening visually and physically. How does this way of naming things differ from English weather words like &quot;fog&quot; or &quot;snow&quot;?</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">You Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Māu Anō — Weather sentences</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Use weather vocabulary across past, present, and future.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Write today's weather report: He pēhea te āhua o te rangi ināianei? Answer with three details.</li><li>Write yesterday's weather using past tense: I pēhea…? Use at least two weather words.</li><li>Write a three-day forecast using Ka: Ka [weather] te rangi ā te [day].</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write a weather comparison paragraph — compare this week's weather to last week's using He [adj] ake and fronted time phrases. Include at least five different weather words.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Tuesday: Ngā āhuatanga o te taiao — Seasons and the natural world":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Te Aho Arataki Marau — AO 2.4: Seasons · AO 2.5: Natural world vocabulary · AO 1.4: Connection to land</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Objectives</th><td class=\"lt-td\">AO 2.4 — Seasons &amp; nature; AO 2.5 — Natural world vocabulary</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">I Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tāua Kōrero — The seasons and what they bring</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>In te ao Māori, the seasons are not just weather patterns — they mark the rhythms of planting, harvest, fishing, and ceremony. Today we learn how to describe each season and what happens in it.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\"><strong>Ko <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=k%C5%8Danga\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">kōanga</a> te wā o te tipu.</strong><br>Spring is the time of growth. (<a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=tipu\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">tipu</a> = grow/growth)<br><br><strong>Ko <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=raumati\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">raumati</a> te wā o te wera me te tākaro.</strong><br>Summer is the time of heat and play.<br><br><strong>Ko <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=ngahuru\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">ngahuru</a> te wā o te hauhake.</strong><br>Autumn is the time of harvest. (<a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=hauhake\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">hauhake</a> = harvest)<br><br><strong>Ko <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=h%C5%8Dtoke\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">hōtoke</a> te wā o te makariri me te okioki.</strong><br>Winter is the time of cold and rest.</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Season vocabulary:<br><strong>kōanga</strong> — spring<br><strong>raumati</strong> — summer<br><strong>ngahuru</strong> — autumn<br><strong>hōtoke</strong> — winter</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Ko [season] te wā o te [noun/activity].</strong><br>[Season] is the time of [noun/activity].</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Nature vocabulary:<br><strong>tipu</strong> — grow / plant / growth<br><strong>hauhake</strong> — harvest<br><strong>okioki</strong> — rest<br><strong>hua rākau</strong> — fruit (hua = fruit/benefit, rākau = tree)<br><strong>puāwai</strong> — blossom / flower / bloom<br><strong>rau</strong> — leaf / leaves<br><strong>repo</strong> — mud / swamp<br><strong>huka</strong> — frost / foam<br><strong>ngahere</strong> — forest / bush<br><strong>moana</strong> — sea / ocean / large lake</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Tip: Ko [season] te wā o te [activity]. This sentence works for ANY season and ANY activity — learn the frame, swap in the vocabulary.</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">We Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Kia Tūhono — Seasons and activities</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>We build descriptions of each season and what people do in them.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Describe kōanga: <strong>Ko kōanga te wā o te tipu. Ka puāwai ngā gete, ka hāpai te ngahere i ōna rau.</strong></li><li>Describe raumati: <strong>Ko raumati te wā o te wera. Ka kaukau ngā tamariki i te moana.</strong></li><li>Describe hōtoke: <strong>Ko hōtoke te wā o te makariri. Ka huka te whenua i te ata.</strong></li><li>Which season is your favourite? <strong>Ko tōku wā pai rawa atu ko [season] nō te mea…</strong></li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Discussion prompt: Ko hōtoke te wā o te okioki — winter is the time of rest. In te ao Māori, rest and conservation are values, not just necessities. How does framing winter as &quot;the time of rest&quot; change how you think about that season? What value does it give to rest?</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">You Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Māu Anō — Season descriptions</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write about the seasons using Ko [season] te wā o te… and nature vocabulary.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Write a Ko [season] te wā o te… sentence for all four seasons.</li><li>Write what you do in each season: Ko raumati tōku wā pai rawa atu. Ka [activity] au.</li><li>Write a sentence about nature in two seasons using rau (leaves), puāwai (blossom), huka (frost).</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write a poem or descriptive paragraph about your favourite season. Use at least six nature words, Ko [season] te wā o te… as your opening line, and at least one comparative (He [adj] ake te [season] i…).</li></ul></div></div>"},"Wednesday: Hanga rerenga — Weather in stories and recounts":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Te Aho Arataki Marau — AO 2.4: Weather/seasons · AO 2.3: Extended communication · Grammar Level 1–2: Weather as narrative context-setter</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Objectives</th><td class=\"lt-td\">AO 2.4 — Weather as scene-setting; AO 2.3 — Weather in recounts</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">I Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tāua Kōrero — Weather as the opening of a story</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Great writers use weather to set the scene — the opening line of a story often describes the weather because it tells us the mood before anything happens. Today we learn how to use weather as a powerful scene-setter in te reo Māori.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">WEATHER OPENING — sets the mood immediately:<br><strong>He pō makariri, he pō <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=k%C5%8Dpeka\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">kōpeka</a>.</strong><br>It was a cold night, a dark night.<br><br><strong>I te ata o Rāhina, ka ua kaha — ka tangi hoki te hau i ngā <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=r%C4%81kau\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">rākau</a>.</strong><br>On Monday morning, it rained hard — and the wind cried through the trees.<br><br><strong>He rā pai, he rā ātaahua — ko te raumati tonu.</strong><br>It was a fine day, a beautiful day — it was truly summer.<br><br><strong>I <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=ngaro\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">ngaro</a> te <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=r%C4%81\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">rā</a> i roto i ngā kapua makariri.</strong><br>The sun disappeared into the cold clouds.</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Weather as scene-setting techniques:</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>Double description</strong> (two weather words for emphasis):</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&quot;He pō makariri, he pō kōpeka.&quot; (cold night, dark night)</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>Weather + effect on nature</strong>:</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&quot;Ka ua kaha — ka tangi hoki te hau i ngā rākau.&quot;<br>(It rained hard — and the wind cried through the trees)</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>Weather + contrast</strong>:</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&quot;Ahakoa he ua, he ātaahua tonu te rangi.&quot;<br>(Although it rained, the sky was still beautiful)</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>New words:<br><strong>kōpeka</strong> — dark / gloomy<br><strong>i ngaro</strong> — disappeared / was lost<br><strong>ka tangi te hau</strong> — the wind cried/wailed (personification!)<br><strong>ngahere</strong> — forest / bush</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Tip: Open your story with weather to set the mood before a single character appears. Warm weather = possibility. Cold dark weather = difficulty ahead. Your reader will feel it before they know it.</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">We Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Kia Tūhono — Weather openings</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>I give you a story mood — you write a weather opening that creates that mood.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Mood: excitement and adventure → <strong>He rā pai, he rā ātaahua — ko te raumati tonu. Ka whiti te rā i ngā maunga.</strong></li><li>Mood: sadness or difficulty → <strong>He pō makariri, he pō kōpeka. I ngaro te marama i roto i ngā kapua.</strong></li><li>Mood: warmth and safety → Use mahana, ātaahua, and whiti (shine) to create the feeling.</li><li>Now write your own weather opening for a story about something that happened to you.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Discussion prompt: Ka tangi te hau i ngā rākau — the wind cried in the trees. This is personification — giving the wind a human emotion. Te reo Māori naturally personifies nature. How does this connect to the Māori understanding of the natural world as a living, feeling entity rather than a backdrop?</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">You Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Māu Anō — Story openings</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write three different story openings — each one starting with weather.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Write a weather opening for a story about a storm using pakū, hau, ua, and kapua.</li><li>Write a weather opening for a summer adventure using mahana, whiti, ātaahua, and moana.</li><li>Write a weather opening for a sad or difficult story using makariri, kōpeka, and i ngaro.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Take your best weather opening and write the next two sentences — what happens after you set the scene? Include a character, a Ka action sentence, and at least one conjunction.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Thursday: Kōrero — He kōrero mō te taiao — Nature conversation":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Te Aho Arataki Marau — AO 2.4: Weather/seasons · AO 1.4: Connection to land · AO 2.5: Natural world vocabulary</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Objectives</th><td class=\"lt-td\">AO 2.4 — Nature &amp; seasons; AO 1.4 — Connection to land &amp; environment</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">I Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tāua Kōrero — Talking about the natural world</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This week has been about weather, seasons, and nature. Today we bring it all together in a conversation about the natural world around us — connecting to the land in the way te ao Māori teaches us to.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\"><strong>A:</strong> He pēhea te āhua o te rangi i tēnei wiki?<br><strong>B:</strong> He makariri, he ua hoki — he hōtoke tonu tēnei!<br><strong>A:</strong> He aha āu i pai ai mō tēnei wā o te tau?<br><strong>B:</strong> He pai ki a au te hōtoke nō te mea ka okioki au, ka noho au i roto me tōku whānau.<br><strong>A:</strong> Kei hea koe i noho ai i ngā hararei?<br><strong>B:</strong> I noho mātou i te taha o te <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=moana\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">moana</a> — i ātaahua te rangi, ahakoa i makariri.<br><strong>A:</strong> He ātaahua! He pēhea te āhua o te moana i ērā rā?<br><strong>B:</strong> I nui, i parauri, i <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=ng%C4%81oro\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">ngāoro</a> hoki — ka pōuri au i te hoki ki te kāinga!</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>New conversational phrases about nature:<br><strong>He aha āu i pai ai mō tēnei wā o te tau?</strong><br>What do you like about this time of year?<br><strong>i pai ai</strong> — that you like (pai + ai = relative clause &quot;that is good/liked&quot;)<br><strong>wā o te tau</strong> — time of year<br><strong>te taha o te moana</strong> — the side of the ocean / by the sea<br><strong>i ērā rā</strong> — in those days / during that time<br><strong>parauri</strong> — brown (you learned this in Week 3!) — used for ocean colour<br><strong>i ngāoro</strong> — roared (sound word from Term 1 Week 9!)<br><strong>Ka pōuri au i te hoki ki te kāinga.</strong><br>I felt sad about going home.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Tip: He aha āu i pai ai mō tēnei wā o te tau? — What do you like about this time of year? This question is a beautiful invitation to talk about nature and seasons. Learn it and ask it often.</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">We Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Kia Tūhono — Nature conversation</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Build a conversation about the natural world and seasons.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I ask: <strong>He pēhea te āhua o te rangi i tēnei wiki?</strong> Answer with this week's actual weather.</li><li>I ask: <strong>He aha āu i pai ai mō tēnei wā o te tau?</strong> Answer with your favourite season and why.</li><li>I ask: <strong>He aha te wāhi āhua rawa atu i Aotearoa ki a koe?</strong> (What is the most beautiful place in NZ for you?) Answer using Ko and description.</li><li>Close: <strong>He taonga te taiao o Aotearoa.</strong> (The natural environment of NZ is a treasure.) Do you agree? Why?</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Discussion prompt: He taonga te taiao o Aotearoa — the natural environment of New Zealand is a treasure. What responsibilities come with living in a taonga? In te ao Māori, kaitiakitanga (guardianship) means caring for the natural world as a duty. What does kaitiaki mean for you personally?</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">You Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Māu Anō — Tōu wāhi āhua</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write about your favourite place in nature.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Name your favourite natural place and say why: Ko tōku wāhi āhua rawa atu ko [place] nō te mea…</li><li>Describe the weather there using at least three weather words.</li><li>Write what you do there using Ka sentences.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write a full description of your favourite natural place (7 sentences) — name it, describe the weather and seasons there, what you do there, why you love it. Include a Ko ēnei list of its best features and close with He taonga tēnei nō te mea…</li></ul></div></div>"},"Friday: Aromatawai — Quiz & Review":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Monday questions</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>&quot;He pēhea te āhua o te rangi?&quot; — What is this question asking?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>What time is it? </li><li>What is the weather like? ✅</li><li>What season is it? </li><li>How cold is it? </li></ul></li><li>What does &quot;hukarere&quot; mean?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>fog / mist </li><li>lightning </li><li>snow ✅</li><li>cloud </li></ul></li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tuesday questions</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>&quot;Ko ngahuru te wā o te hauhake.&quot; What does hauhake mean?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>growth </li><li>rest </li><li>harvest ✅</li><li>rain </li></ul></li><li>What are the four seasons in te reo Māori in order from spring?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>hōtoke, kōanga, raumati, ngahuru </li><li>kōanga, raumati, ngahuru, hōtoke ✅</li><li>raumati, ngahuru, hōtoke, kōanga </li><li>ngahuru, hōtoke, kōanga, raumati </li></ul></li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Wednesday questions</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>&quot;Ka tangi te hau i ngā rākau.&quot; What literary device is being used?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>simile — comparing wind to something </li><li>metaphor — replacing one word with another </li><li>personification — giving the wind a human emotion ✅</li><li>alliteration — repeated sounds </li></ul></li><li>What does &quot;kōpeka&quot; mean?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>cloudy </li><li>windy </li><li>rainy </li><li>dark / gloomy ✅</li></ul></li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Thursday questions</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>&quot;He taonga te taiao o Aotearoa.&quot; What does taonga mean in this context?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>problem </li><li>weather </li><li>treasure / something precious ✅</li><li>danger </li></ul></li><li>&quot;Ka pōuri au i te hoki ki te kāinga.&quot; What does pōuri mean here?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>happy </li><li>excited </li><li>tired </li><li>sad / heavy-hearted ✅</li></ul></li></ul></div></div>"}}},"Week 8":{"label":"Day","children":{"Monday: Ngā tūāhua o te hinengaro — Feelings and emotions":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Te Aho Arataki Marau — AO 2.5: Physical characteristics, personality and feelings · Grammar Level 1–2: Kei te/He for emotions, feeling vocabulary</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Objectives</th><td class=\"lt-td\">AO 2.5 — Feelings &amp; emotions; AO 2.3 — Expressing inner states</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">I Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tāua Kōrero — Saying how you feel inside</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>You already know a few feeling words — pai, ngenge, hari, pōuri. Today we build a full emotional vocabulary. Being able to express how you feel in te reo Māori is one of the most intimate and important things you can do in a language.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\"><strong>Kei te hari au.</strong> — I am happy / joyful.<br><strong>Kei te <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=p%C5%8Duri\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">pōuri</a> au.</strong> — I am sad.<br><strong>Kei te <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=mataku\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">mataku</a> au.</strong> — I am scared / frightened.<br><strong>Kei te <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=m%C4%81ia\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">māia</a> au.</strong> — I am brave / bold.<br><strong>Kei te <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=whakam%C4%81\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">whakamā</a> au.</strong> — I am embarrassed / shy.<br><strong>Kei te <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=h%C4%ABh%C4%AB\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">hīhī</a> au.</strong> — I am excited / eager.<br><strong>Kei te <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=riri\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">riri</a> au.</strong> — I am angry.<br><strong>Kei te <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=m%C4%81harahara\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">māharahara</a> au.</strong> — I am worried / anxious.<br><strong>Kei te <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=ohorere\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">ohorere</a> au.</strong> — I am surprised / startled.<br><strong>Kei te <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=h%C5%8Dh%C4%81\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">hōhā</a> au.</strong> — I am bored / fed up.<br><strong>Kei te <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=aroha\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">aroha</a> au.</strong> — I am feeling love / compassion.</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Emotion structure:<br><strong>Kei te [emotion] au.</strong> — I am feeling [emotion].</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>With a reason:<br><strong>Kei te pōuri au nō te mea…</strong> — I am sad because…<br><strong>Kei te hari au nā te mea…</strong> — I am happy because…</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Asking about feelings:<br><strong>Kei te pēhea tōu ngākau?</strong> — How is your heart/feeling?<br><strong>He aha tōu rongo?</strong> — What do you feel? (rongo = feeling/sense)</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>New concept: <strong>ngākau</strong> = heart (as the seat of emotion, not the physical heart which is manawa)</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Deep emotion phrase:<br><strong>Ka aroha au ki a koe.</strong> — I feel love/compassion toward you.<br><strong>Aroha</strong> — love, compassion, empathy (one of the most important words in te reo Māori)</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Tip: Kei te [emotion] au = I am feeling [emotion]. Add nō te mea + reason to explain why. The emotion word goes right after Kei te — just like any other verb.</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">We Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Kia Tūhono — Express the feeling</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>I describe a situation — you say how you would feel using Kei te [emotion] au.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>You just found out you won a prize. → <strong>Kei te hari au! Kei te ohorere hoki au!</strong></li><li>You have a big test tomorrow. → <strong>Kei te māharahara au mō āpōpō.</strong></li><li>Someone said something unkind to you. → <strong>Kei te pōuri au. Kei te riri hoki au.</strong></li><li>You are speaking te reo Māori in front of the class for the first time. → How do you feel? Be honest — use two emotion words.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Discussion prompt: Kei te aroha au ki a koe — I feel love/compassion toward you. The word aroha appears everywhere in te ao Māori — in greetings, farewells, and daily life. How is aroha different from just &quot;love&quot; in English? What does it include that &quot;love&quot; sometimes doesn't?</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">You Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Māu Anō — Feelings</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Express real emotions in te reo Māori.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Write how you feel right now using Kei te [emotion] au and give a reason.</li><li>Write how you felt inanahi — use past tense: I [emotion] au nō te mea…</li><li>Write three different emotions you have felt this week using three different emotion words.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write a &quot;feelings journal entry&quot; for today (6 sentences). Describe two emotions you have felt, why, how they changed during the day, and how you feel now. Use Kei te, I, and nō te mea.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Tuesday: Ahua tangata — Personality and character":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Te Aho Arataki Marau — AO 2.5: Personality · Grammar Level 1–2: He for character description, ko ia te tangata…</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Objectives</th><td class=\"lt-td\">AO 2.5 — Character &amp; personality; AO 2.1 — Describing relationships</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">I Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tāua Kōrero — Describing who someone is</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Last term you learned He + noun + adjective for describing things. Today we use that same structure to describe people's character — who they are as people, not just what they look like.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\"><strong>He tangata āpōpō ia.</strong> — She is a generous person. (āpōpō here = generous? No...)<br><br>Let's build the vocabulary first:<br><strong>He tangata <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=atawhai\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">atawhai</a> ia.</strong> — He is a kind/caring person./caring person. (atawhai = kind)<br><strong>He tangata <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=m%C4%81ia\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">māia</a> ia.</strong> — She is a brave person.<br><strong>He tangata <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=m%C5%8Dhio\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">mōhio</a> ia.</strong> — He is a knowledgeable/clever person.<br><strong>He tangata <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=h%C5%ABm%C4%81rie\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">hūmārie</a> ia.</strong> — She is a gentle/humble person.<br><strong>He tangata <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=m%C4%81ngere\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">māngere</a> ia.</strong> — He is a lazy person.<br><strong>He tangata urupā ia.</strong> — She is a hardworking person. (urupā? No — that's a cemetery!)<br><br>Let me give you the right words:<br><strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=urup%C4%81\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">urupā</a></strong> = cemetery · <strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=ng%C4%81kaunui\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">ngākaunui</a></strong> = enthusiastic/passionate · <strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=pukumahi\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">pukumahi</a></strong> = hardworking</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Character vocabulary:<br><strong>atawhai</strong> — kind / caring / generous<br><strong>māia</strong> — brave / bold<br><strong>mōhio</strong> — knowledgeable / clever / aware<br><strong>hūmārie</strong> — gentle / humble / peaceful<br><strong>māngere</strong> — lazy<br><strong>pukumahi</strong> — hardworking<br><strong>ngākaunui</strong> — enthusiastic / passionate (ngākau = heart, nui = big)<br><strong>aroha nui</strong> — loving / full of aroha<br><strong>māia</strong> — brave<br><strong>tūmataiti</strong> — shy / reserved</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Used in a sentence:<br><strong>He tangata pukumahi, he tangata atawhai hoki ia.</strong><br>She is a hardworking and kind person.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Ko ia te tangata ngākaunui rawa atu o tō mātou akomanga.</strong><br>She is the most enthusiastic person in our classroom.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Tip: He tangata [character word] ia — He/She is a [character] person. Stack two character words with &quot;he tangata X, he tangata Y hoki ia&quot; — or use the superlative: Ko ia te tangata [X] rawa atu o…</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">We Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Kia Tūhono — Describe someone's character</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Think of someone you admire. We will describe their character together using this week's vocabulary.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Who is your person? State: <strong>Ko [name] tōku [relationship].</strong> Then: <strong>He tangata [character] ia.</strong></li><li>Add a second character trait: <strong>He tangata [X] hoki ia.</strong> Stack two traits.</li><li>Give evidence using nō te mea: <strong>He tangata atawhai ia nō te mea ka [action] ia i ngā wā katoa.</strong></li><li>Use a superlative: <strong>Ko ia te tangata [character] rawa atu o tōku ao.</strong> (ao = world/life)</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Discussion prompt: Ko ia te tangata ngākaunui rawa atu o tōku ao — she is the most enthusiastic person in my world. What makes this sentence beautiful? What does &quot;tōku ao&quot; (my world) add that &quot;tōku akomanga&quot; (my classroom) doesn't? When would you use each?</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">You Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Māu Anō — Character descriptions</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write character descriptions of real people in your life.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Write a He tangata [character] ia sentence about someone you admire.</li><li>Write two or three stacked character traits for that person.</li><li>Write a Ko ia te tangata [X] rawa atu o… superlative about them.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write a &quot;tribute paragraph&quot; (6 sentences) about someone important to you — Ko [name] tōku [relationship]. Describe their character (3 traits), give evidence for one trait, and close with a superlative.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Wednesday: Hanga rerenga — Feelings and character in stories":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Te Aho Arataki Marau — AO 2.5: Feelings/personality · AO 2.3: Extended communication · Grammar Level 1–2: Emotion and character in narrative context</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Objectives</th><td class=\"lt-td\">AO 2.5 — Emotion in narrative; AO 2.3 — Character in stories</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">I Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tāua Kōrero — Show, don't tell — emotions in stories</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>There is a saying in writing: &quot;show, don't tell.&quot; Instead of writing &quot;she was happy&quot;, you show the happiness — what she did, how she moved, what she said. Today we practise this in te reo Māori — describing feelings and character through action rather than just naming them.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">TELLING (weak):<br><strong>Kei te hari ia.</strong> She is happy.<br><br>SHOWING (strong):<br><strong>Ka omaoma ia ki te kāinga. Ka karanga ia i tōna māmā. Ka oma hoki tōna māmā ki a ia, ka <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=awhi\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">awhi</a> rāua.</strong><br>She ran home. She called out to her mum. Her mum ran to her too, and they hugged.<br><br>TELLING (weak):<br><strong>He tangata atawhai ia.</strong> He is a kind person.<br><br>SHOWING (strong):<br><strong>I kite ia i te kurī i <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=mauiui\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">mauiui</a> ana. Ka <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=tango\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">tango</a> ia i tōna <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=koti\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">koti</a> ā ka <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=hipoki\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">hipoki</a> ia i te kurī. Ka noho ia ki reira ā te <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=taenga\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">taenga</a> mai o tōna <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=rangatira\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">rangatira</a>.</strong><br>He saw the sick dog. He took off his coat and covered the dog. He stayed there until its owner arrived.</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Show-don't-tell techniques:</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>Replace the emotion word with an action</strong>:</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Telling: Kei te hari au.<br>Showing: Ka oma au, ka waiata au, ka kata au! (run, sing, laugh)</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>Replace the character word with evidence</strong>:</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Telling: He tangata atawhai ia.<br>Showing: Ka tūāhu ia ki te tangata māuiui. Ka homai ia i āna kai.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>New words:<br><strong>karanga</strong> — to call out / summon<br><strong>awhi</strong> — to embrace / hug<br><strong>hipoki</strong> — to cover<br><strong>taenga mai</strong> — the arrival (tae = arrive, -nga = nominaliser)<br><strong>rangatira</strong> — chief / leader / owner (of an animal in this context)<br><strong>kata</strong> — to laugh<br><strong>tūāhu</strong> — to tend to / look after (person in need)</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Tip: To show an emotion — ask yourself: what does someone DO when they feel this? Then write the action, not the feeling word. Ka <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=kata\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">kata</a>, ka waiata, ka omaoma — these show happiness better than &quot;Kei te hari&quot;.</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">We Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Kia Tūhono — Show, don't tell</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>I give you a telling sentence — you turn it into a showing sentence using actions.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Telling: <strong>I pōuri ia.</strong> → Showing: What did she DO when she was sad? <strong>Ka noho ia i tōna rūma. Kāore ia i kōrero. Ka tangi ia i te pō.</strong></li><li>Telling: <strong>He tangata māia ia.</strong> → Showing: What did he DO to show bravery? <strong>Ka tū ia i mua i te whakamā. Ka kōrero ia ahakoa i ohorere ia.</strong></li><li>Telling: <strong>I harikoa ngā tamariki.</strong> → Showing: Three actions that show their joy.</li><li>Now you write one: tell me an emotion — then show it through three actions in te reo.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Discussion prompt: Ka kata, ka waiata, ka omaoma — a laughing, singing, running person. You have not said &quot;hari&quot; (happy) once. But the reader FEELS it. Why is showing more powerful than telling? When might telling be the right choice?</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">You Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Māu Anō — Show it</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Rewrite telling sentences as showing sentences in te reo Māori.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Telling: &quot;Kei te mataku ia.&quot; — Show the fear through three actions.</li><li>Telling: &quot;He tangata pukumahi ia.&quot; — Show the hard work through evidence.</li><li>Telling: &quot;I hari mātou katoa.&quot; — Show the group's joy through action.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write a scene (8 sentences) where a character experiences a strong emotion — but NEVER NAME the emotion. Only use actions, dialogue, and description. Read it to a partner — can they identify the emotion?</li></ul></div></div>"},"Thursday: Kōrero — He kōrero mō ngā tāngata — Talking about people":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Te Aho Arataki Marau — AO 2.5: Character/feelings · AO 2.1: Relationships · AO 1.2: Introducing others</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Objectives</th><td class=\"lt-td\">AO 2.5 — Character &amp; feelings; AO 1.2 — Introducing others</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">I Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tāua Kōrero — Introducing and describing people</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This week you have learned feelings and character. Today we use them to introduce and describe real people — friends, whānau, teachers — in a natural, warm conversation.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\"><strong>A:</strong> Ko wai tōu hoa pai rawa atu?<br><strong>B:</strong> Ko Hēni tōku hoa pai rawa atu. He tangata atawhai, he tangata ngākaunui hoki ia.<br><strong>A:</strong> He pēhea tōna āhua?<br><strong>B:</strong> He tangata <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=m%C4%81horahora\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">māhorahora</a> ia — ka kata ia i ngā wā katoa. Ka ora tōku ngākau i tōna āhua!<br><strong>A:</strong> Ka aroha! He aha āu e pai ai mō tōna āhua?<br><strong>B:</strong> Ka pai ki a au tōna māia — ka tū tonu ia ahakoa he uaua te āhua o ngā mea. He <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=t%C5%AB%C4%81huatanga\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">tūāhuatanga</a> tino nui tērā.<br><strong>A:</strong> E ai ki tōu kōrero, ko ia te hoa tino pai rawa atu o tō tātou ao!<br><strong>B:</strong> Āe! Ko ia te pou o tōku ao.</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>New conversational phrases about people:<br><strong>He pēhea tōna āhua?</strong> — What is he/she like? (personality/appearance)<br><strong>He tangata māhorahora ia.</strong> — He/she is a joyful/light-hearted person.<br><strong>Ka ora tōku ngākau i tōna āhua.</strong> — My heart comes alive at her presence.<br><strong>He aha āu e pai ai mō tōna āhua?</strong> — What do you like about their personality?<br><strong>Ka tū tonu ia ahakoa…</strong> — He/she stands firm even though…<br><strong>tūāhuatanga</strong> — quality / characteristic (tūāhua + tanga)<br><strong>E ai ki tōu kōrero</strong> — According to what you say<br><strong>Ko ia te pou o tōku ao.</strong> — She is the pillar of my world.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Tip: He pēhea tōna āhua? = What is their personality/character like? This is different from He pēhea ia? (How is he/she?). Āhua focuses on their nature — who they are as a person.</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">We Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Kia Tūhono — Introduce your favourite person</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Build a conversation introducing someone you admire.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I ask: <strong>Ko wai tōu hoa pai rawa atu?</strong> Introduce them: Ko [name] tōku [relationship].</li><li>I ask: <strong>He pēhea tōna āhua?</strong> Give two character traits using He tangata [X] ia.</li><li>I ask: <strong>He aha āu e pai ai mō tōna āhua?</strong> Show one quality through action (show don't tell).</li><li>Close: <strong>Ko ia te pou o tōku ao.</strong> Why is she/he your pou? Add nō te mea.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Discussion prompt: Ko ia te pou o tōku ao — she is the pillar of my world. Last week you heard &quot;Ko ia te pou o tō mātou whānau.&quot; Now it's &quot;tōku ao&quot; (my world) rather than &quot;tō mātou whānau&quot; (our family). What is the difference in feeling between these two? When would you use each?</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">You Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Māu Anō — He mihi ki tōu hoa</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write a tribute to someone important in your life.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Introduce them: Ko [name] tōku [relationship]. He tangata [character] ia.</li><li>Show their best quality through an action (show don't tell — no emotion words).</li><li>Write what they mean to you: Ka ora tōku ngākau i tōna āhua nō te mea…</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write a full tribute (8 sentences) about your most important person. Introduce them, describe two character traits, show one quality through evidence, say what they mean to you, and close with Ko ia te pou o tōku ao.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Friday: Aromatawai — Quiz & Review":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Monday questions</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>&quot;Kei te māharahara au.&quot; How is this person feeling?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>excited and eager </li><li>angry </li><li>worried / anxious ✅</li><li>surprised </li></ul></li><li>&quot;Kei te pēhea tōu ngākau?&quot; What does ngākau mean in this context?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>your body </li><li>your stomach </li><li>your heart (as the seat of emotions) ✅</li><li>your head </li></ul></li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tuesday questions</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>What does &quot;pukumahi&quot; mean?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>lazy </li><li>shy </li><li>passionate </li><li>hardworking ✅</li></ul></li><li>&quot;Ko ia te tangata ngākaunui rawa atu o tō mātou akomanga.&quot; What does ngākaunui mean?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>the most intelligent </li><li>the most hard-working </li><li>the most enthusiastic / passionate (big-hearted) ✅</li><li>the most kind </li></ul></li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Wednesday questions</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Which of these is an example of &quot;showing&quot; rather than &quot;telling&quot; an emotion?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>Kei te hari ia. </li><li>I pōuri ia nō te mea he uaua tāna mahi. </li><li>Ka omaoma ia, ka kata ia, ka waiata ia! ✅</li><li>He tangata māia ia. </li></ul></li><li>What does &quot;awhi&quot; mean?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>to call out </li><li>to cover </li><li>to embrace / hug ✅</li><li>to laugh </li></ul></li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Thursday questions</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>&quot;Ka ora tōku ngākau i tōna āhua.&quot; What does this sentence express?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>Her face is beautiful </li><li>My heart comes alive at her presence / personality ✅</li><li>She is the most important person </li><li>I feel sad when I see her </li></ul></li><li>&quot;He pēhea tōna āhua?&quot; — What is this question asking about?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>Their physical height </li><li>Their age </li><li>Their health today </li><li>Their character / personality / nature ✅</li></ul></li></ul></div></div>"}}},"Week 9":{"label":"Day","children":{"Monday: He kōrero mō te aho — Linking ideas: cohesion review":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Te Aho Arataki Marau — AO 2.3: Extended communication · Grammar Level 1–2: Connective vocabulary, paragraph cohesion across all text types</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Objectives</th><td class=\"lt-td\">AO 2.3 — Cohesion &amp; linking; AO 2.5 — Connective vocabulary review</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">I Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tāua Kōrero — All the linking tools, together</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This term you have learned many ways to link ideas — conjunctions, pronouns, connective words. Today we gather all the linking tools in one place and practise choosing between them deliberately. A well-connected passage is what separates good te reo from great te reo.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\"><strong>COORDINATING CONJUNCTIONS (equal ideas):</strong><br>ā · engari · nō reira · me<br><br><strong>SUBORDINATING CONJUNCTIONS (one idea depends on the other):</strong><br>nō te mea · nā te mea · ahakoa · mehemea · i te wā<br><br><strong>CONNECTIVE ADVERBS (linking sentences):</strong><br><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=heoi+an%C5%8D\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">heoi anō</a> — however / but anyway<br><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=%C4%81+muri+ake+nei\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">ā muri ake nei</a> — furthermore / going forward<br>hei tā tēnei — according to this / as a result<br>nō reira — therefore (can work as both conjunction and connective)<br><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=waihoki\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">waihoki</a> — moreover / and also<br>engari rā — however (more formal than engari alone)<br><br><strong>TIME CONNECTIVES (for sequence):</strong><br><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=i+mua+ake\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">i mua ake</a> — beforehand / previously<br><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=%C4%81+muri+iho\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">ā muri iho</a> — afterwards<br>i te wā hōhonu — in the long run</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Using connective adverbs (sentence starters):</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>I mahi kaha ngā ākonga. Heoi anō, i ngenge rātou ā te mutunga o te rā.</strong><br>The students worked hard. However, they were tired by the end of the day.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Ko te reo Māori he reo rangatira. Waihoki, he reo o te ngahere, o te moana, o ngā maunga.</strong><br>Te reo Māori is a chiefly language. Moreover, it is a language of the forest, the sea, and the mountains.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>I mahi kaha mātou. Ā muri iho, ka ora ai tā mātou mahi.</strong><br>We worked hard. Afterwards, our work flourished.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>New words:<br><strong>rangatira</strong> — chiefly / of high status<br><strong>ora</strong> — alive / well / flourishing<br><strong>heoi anō</strong> — however / but anyway<br><strong>waihoki</strong> — moreover / and also</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Tip: Connective adverbs (heoi anō, waihoki, ā muri iho) go at the START of a sentence and signal the relationship to what came before. They are stronger than conjunctions because they create a full pause — a new sentence — before the connection.</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">We Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Kia Tūhono — Connect the ideas</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>I give you two sentences — you connect them using the most appropriate linking word from today's list.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>&quot;The team won.&quot; &quot;They were still humble.&quot; → The contrast needs: <strong>heoi anō</strong> → <strong>I toa rātou. Heoi anō, i hūmārie tonu rātou.</strong></li><li>&quot;She studied hard.&quot; &quot;She passed the test.&quot; → Result: <strong>nō reira / ā muri iho</strong> → <strong>I ako kaha ia. Nō reira, i paahi ia i te whakamātautau.</strong></li><li>&quot;Te reo Māori is beautiful.&quot; &quot;It connects us to our ancestors.&quot; → Adding more: <strong>waihoki</strong>.</li><li>Write your own: two sentences about your school, connected with heoi anō OR waihoki.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Discussion prompt: Heoi anō and engari both mean &quot;however/but&quot;. Heoi anō starts a NEW sentence. Engari stays INSIDE a sentence. When would you choose heoi anō over engari? What does starting a new sentence add to the contrast?</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">You Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Māu Anō — Connected writing</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write a passage where every sentence connects to the next.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Write four sentences — connect each to the next with a different linking word.</li><li>Use heoi anō at the start of one sentence to create a contrast.</li><li>Use waihoki to add more information to a point.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write a persuasive paragraph (7 sentences) about something you care about — your favourite sport, your school, te reo Māori. Use at least four different linking words from today's list. Underline each one.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Tuesday: Arotake — Term 2 vocabulary and structures":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Te Aho Arataki Marau — All AOs 1.1–2.5 · Full Term 2 review · Grammar Levels 1–2 all structures</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Objectives</th><td class=\"lt-td\">AO 2.3 — Term 2 review; AO 2.5 — Vocabulary consolidation</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">I Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tāua Kōrero — Everything Term 2 has added</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Term 1 gave you the foundations. Term 2 has been building on them. Today we celebrate everything new that you have added — and see how much bigger your te reo Māori world has become.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\"><strong>TENSES you now control:</strong><br>Kei te (now) · I (past event) · Ka (story/sequence) · Kāore e…ana (not now) · Kāore i (didn't happen)<br><br><strong>PRONOUNS you now have:</strong><br>All 16: au, koe, ia / tāua, māua, kōrua, rāua / tātou, mātou, koutou, rātou<br><br><strong>NUMBERS you now know:</strong><br>1–100, dates, times, people counts (toko-)<br><br><strong>NEW VOCABULARY DOMAINS:</strong><br>Colours (12+) · Feelings (12+) · Character (8+) · Weather (10+) · Seasons (4) · Days/months (19) · Family vocabulary · Sports · Announcements</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The growth from Term 1 to Term 2:</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Term 1: <strong>Kei te haere au ki te kura.</strong><br>Term 2: <strong>Ā te Rāhina, i te iwa karaka i te ata, ka haere mātou tokowhitu ki te kura — ahakoa he ua tino nui, i tino pai tā mātou rā.</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Same basic idea — going to school. But now:<br>→ day of week (Rāhina)<br>→ time (iwa karaka i te ata)<br>→ Ka narrative<br>→ people count (tokowhitu)<br>→ ahakoa (despite)<br>→ past tense (i tino pai)<br>→ possessive (tā mātou rā)</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>That is what a term of daily practice gives you.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Tip: Read that Term 2 sentence aloud. You could not have written it in Week 1 of this term. That is your progress. Say it again and own it.</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">We Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Kia Tūhono — Term 2 blitz</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Speed review — category by category. Teacher calls a category, students generate examples.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>FEELINGS: How many emotion words can we list? Go — hari, pōuri, mataku… aim for 10+.</li><li>PRONOUNS: Say all 16 — starting with singular, then dual, then plural. Together.</li><li>NUMBERS: Count from 11 to 20 together. Then say these: 34, 57, 82, 99.</li><li>WEATHER: Give a full three-day forecast in te reo — today, tomorrow, next week.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Discussion prompt: Look back at Week 1 of this term — the holiday conversation. Could you have had that conversation at the start of Term 1? No. Could you have it now? Easily. What does it feel like to look back and see how far you have come?</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">You Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Māu Anō — Term 2 self-audit</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Test your own Term 2 knowledge.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Write all 16 pronouns from memory — organised by singular, dual, plural.</li><li>Write one sentence using Ka, one using I, one using Kāore i, one using Kāore e…ana.</li><li>Write today's date and time fully in te reo Māori.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write a &quot;Term 2 showcase sentence&quot; — one sentence that uses as many Term 2 structures as possible: past tense, a time/date, a number, a colour, a feeling, and a conjunction. Make it a real sentence, not just a list.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Wednesday: Hanga rerenga — Term 2 sentence type showcase":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Te Aho Arataki Marau — AO 2.3: Extended communication · All sentence types from Terms 1 and 2</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Objectives</th><td class=\"lt-td\">AO 2.3 — Sentence type review; AO 2.5 — Rich vocabulary in sentences</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">I Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tāua Kōrero — The full sentence toolkit, Terms 1 and 2</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Across two terms you have built an enormous range of sentence types. Today we display them all — see the full range you now control, and choose which ones to use deliberately.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\"><strong>IDENTITY:</strong> Ko Tāmara tōku ingoa. Nō Tāmaki Makaurau ahau.<br><strong>DESCRIPTION:</strong> He tangata pukumahi, he tangata atawhai hoki ia.<br><strong>PRESENT:</strong> Kei te ako kaha rātou i te reo Māori i tēnei wā.<br><strong>PAST:</strong> I te Rāhina o tērā wiki, i haere mātou tokowhitu ki te hākinakina.<br><strong>NARRATIVE:</strong> Ka ara ia i te ata, ka kai ia i tōna parakuihi, ka haere ia ki te kura.<br><strong>NEGATIVE:</strong> Kāore ia i tae i tēnei rā nō te mea i māuiui ia.<br><strong>COMPLEX:</strong> Ahakoa i ngenge mātou, i mahi tonu mātou nō te mea he mahi nui tō mātou.<br><strong>COMPARATIVE:</strong> He nui ake ngā ākonga i tēnei akomanga i tērā — e rua tekau mā waru ki e rua tekau mā tahi.<br><strong>FRONTED:</strong> I te ahiahi o Rāpare, i roto i te whare hui, ka kōrero tō mātou kaiako mō ngā whainga o tērā tau.<br><strong>LISTED:</strong> Ko ēnei ngā mea hou i ako ai mātou i tēnei wāhanga: ngā tae, ngā tau, ngā tūāhua, me ngā kupu hono.</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Ten sentence types. Two terms. You can write all of them.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The key to fluency is not knowing all the types — it is choosing the RIGHT type for what you want to say:</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>→ Want to identify? Ko<br>→ Want to describe? He<br>→ Want to say now? Kei te<br>→ Want to tell a story? Ka<br>→ Want to report a fact? I<br>→ Want to deny? Kāore<br>→ Want to connect? Conjunction<br>→ Want to compare? He [adj] ake<br>→ Want to set the scene? Fronted phrase<br>→ Want to list? Ko ēnei</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>You now have all ten. Use them.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Tip: The best writers don't use all ten types in every paragraph — they choose deliberately. Read your writing aloud and ask: does every sentence sound different from the last? If not, vary the type.</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">We Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Kia Tūhono — One prompt, ten types</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>I give you one topic — together we write one sentence of EACH type about it.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Topic: your school. Write a Ko identity sentence and a He description sentence.</li><li>Write a Kei te sentence (now), an I sentence (past), and a Ka sentence (narrative).</li><li>Write a Kāore sentence and a complex ahakoa sentence.</li><li>Write a comparative, a fronted phrase, and a Ko ēnei list. Ten sentence types, one topic.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Discussion prompt: Look at the ten sentences about your school. Pick the three that feel most alive — most like real te reo, not just an exercise. Why do those three feel stronger? What do they have that the others don't?</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">You Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Māu Anō — Sentence type showcase</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Choose a topic you care about and write all ten sentence types.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Choose your topic: sport, whānau, nature, music, food, or another.</li><li>Write sentences in types 1–5: Ko, He, Kei te, I, Ka.</li><li>Write sentences in types 6–10: Kāore, ahakoa/complex, comparative, fronted, Ko ēnei.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Take your ten sentences and weave them into a genuine paragraph — reorder them, add connectives, cut anything that doesn't flow. Aim for a paragraph that reads like real te reo, not a list of exercise sentences.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Thursday: Kōrero — He kōrero hou — A spiralled conversation":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Te Aho Arataki Marau — All Term 1 and 2 AOs · Full conversation practice</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Objectives</th><td class=\"lt-td\">AO 1.1 — Greetings; AO 1.2 — Introductions; AO 2.3 — Full conversation; AO 2.5 — Expressive vocabulary</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">I Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tāua Kōrero — A full Term 2 conversation</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The best test of language learning is conversation. Today we have the most complex conversation of the year so far — drawing on everything from both terms. Listen to how naturally the structures flow when you stop thinking about them individually.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\"><strong>A:</strong> Tēnā koe! He pēhea ōu hararei?<br><strong>B:</strong> He tino pai! I haere mātou ki te <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=ngahere\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">ngahere</a> ko tōku whānau. Ko ēnei ngā mea tino pai: he ātaahua ngā rākau, i ngāoro te awa, ā i kaukau mātou tokowhitu i te roto māhana!<br><strong>A:</strong> Ka rawe! He pēhea te āhua o te rangi i ērā rā?<br><strong>B:</strong> I makariri i te ata engari ka mahana i te ahiahi. Ahakoa he ua i tētahi rā, i haere tonu mātou ki waho.<br><strong>A:</strong> He aha tō āhua i ērā rā?<br><strong>B:</strong> I hari tino nui au! Ka ora tōku ngākau i te āhua o te taiao. Ko tōku māmā te tangata i ārahi i a mātou — he tangata mōhio, he tangata atawhai hoki ia.<br><strong>A:</strong> He ātaahua tērā. He aha tāu whainga mō tēnei wāhanga?<br><strong>B:</strong> Ko tāku whainga: ka ako māmā ake au i te reo Māori — ka kōrero au me tōku whānau i te kāinga!</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This conversation uses:<br>✓ Past tense (I haere, i ngāoro, i makariri, i hari)<br>✓ Ka narrative (ka mahana, ka kaukau, ka ora)<br>✓ Plural pronouns (mātou, tokowhitu)<br>✓ Weather (makariri, mahana, ua)<br>✓ Feelings (i hari, Ka ora tōku ngākau)<br>✓ Character description (He tangata mōhio, he tangata atawhai)<br>✓ Ko ēnei list (Ko ēnei ngā mea tino pai…)<br>✓ Comparative (ako māmā ake)<br>✓ Complex (ahakoa he ua, i haere tonu mātou)<br>✓ Goal statement (Ko tāku whainga…)</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>That is the whole of Terms 1 and 2 in one natural conversation.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Tip: Ko tāku whainga: ka kōrero au me tōku whānau i te kāinga. This is the goal — not just to speak te reo in class, but to take it home. What would you need to practise to have this conversation with your own whānau?</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">We Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Kia Tūhono — Full conversation practice</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Pairs. Use the model conversation as a guide — but make it your own. Your holidays, your family, your feelings.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Open with greetings and ask about holidays: Tēnā koe! He pēhea ōu hararei?</li><li>Report what happened: past tense + people count + Ko ēnei list.</li><li>Describe the weather: past + weather + ahakoa + kept going anyway.</li><li>Share a feeling + describe a person + state your goal for this term.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Discussion prompt: Ko tāku whainga: ka kōrero au me tōku whānau i te kāinga. Is this a realistic goal for you? What would your first te reo conversation with your whānau look like? What three sentences would you teach them to make it possible?</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">You Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Māu Anō — Tōu kōrero hou</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Have your own full Term 2 conversation and write it up.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Have a full conversation with a partner — at least 10 lines each — about your holidays.</li><li>Use: past tense, Ka narrative, a plural pronoun, a weather sentence, a feeling, and a goal.</li><li>Write up the conversation as a dialogue.</li><li>✦ Bonus: After writing your dialogue, annotate it — label every structure you used. How many different structures can you find? Aim for at least eight.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Friday: Aromatawai — Quiz & Review":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Monday questions</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>&quot;I mahi kaha ngā ākonga. Heoi anō, i ngenge rātou.&quot; What does heoi anō signal?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>Therefore — they worked hard as a result of being tired </li><li>Moreover — they were also hard workers </li><li>However — despite working hard, they were tired ✅</li><li>Because — tiredness caused them to work hard </li></ul></li><li>What does &quot;waihoki&quot; mean?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>however / but </li><li>therefore </li><li>moreover / and also ✅</li><li>because </li></ul></li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tuesday questions</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Which of these correctly uses Ka for a narrative sequence?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>Ka ara ia. Ka kai ia. Ka haere ia ki te kura. ✅</li><li>I ara ia. Ka kai ia. Kei te haere ia ki te kura. </li><li>Ka ara ia. I kai ia inanahi. Ka haere ia. </li><li>Ka ara, kai, haere ia ki te kura. </li></ul></li><li>&quot;I toa rātou. Heoi anō, i hūmārie tonu rātou.&quot; What does hūmārie mean?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>exhausted </li><li>angry </li><li>humble / gentle / peaceful ✅</li><li>excited </li></ul></li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Wednesday questions</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Which sentence type uses &quot;Ko ēnei&quot; to introduce a list?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>Identity sentence </li><li>Narrative sentence </li><li>Listed sentence ✅</li><li>Comparative sentence </li></ul></li><li>&quot;He nui ake ngā ākonga i tēnei akomanga i tērā.&quot; What sentence type is this?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>Ko identity </li><li>Kāore negative </li><li>He comparative ✅</li><li>Ka narrative </li></ul></li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Thursday questions</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>&quot;Ko tāku whainga: ka kōrero au me tōku whānau i te kāinga.&quot; What does whainga mean?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>problem </li><li>memory </li><li>goal / aim ✅</li><li>holiday </li></ul></li><li>&quot;Ka ora tōku ngākau i te āhua o te taiao.&quot; What does this express?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>The weather made me tired </li><li>My heart comes alive in the presence of nature ✅</li><li>The natural world is dangerous </li><li>I am going outside to rest </li></ul></li></ul></div></div>"}}},"Week 10":{"label":"Day","children":{"Monday: Arotake — Term 2 full vocabulary and structure review":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Te Aho Arataki Marau — All AOs 1.1–2.5 consolidated · All grammar structures Terms 1 and 2</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Objectives</th><td class=\"lt-td\">AO 1.1 — Greetings review; AO 2.3 — All structures; AO 2.5 — Vocabulary mastery</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">I Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tāua Kōrero — The full two-term vocabulary celebration</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This is the last week of Term 2. Today we count everything you now know and celebrate it. By the end of today's lesson you will have a complete picture of your two-term te reo Māori journey.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Two terms of learning. Here is what you can now do:<br><br><strong>COMMUNICATE ABOUT:</strong><br>Yourself, your family, your school, your home, your feelings, your character, the weather, the seasons, numbers 1–100, dates, times, sporting events, holiday recounts, announcements<br><br><strong>STRUCTURES YOU CONTROL:</strong><br>Ko · He · Kei te · I · Ka · Kāore e…ana · Kāore i · E hia / Tokohia · tōku/tāku/tōu/tāu · All 16 pronouns · Ahakoa · Mehemea · I te wā · Comparatives · Fronted phrases · Ko ēnei lists · Conjunctions (7+) · Connective adverbs (heoi anō, waihoki)</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The Term 2 additions to your Term 1 base:</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">TERM 1 base sentence</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&quot;Kei te haere au ki te kura.&quot;</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">TERM 2 full sentence</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&quot;I te Rāpare, ko te tekau mā ono o Pipiri, i haere mātou tokotoru ki te kura i te iwa karaka i te ata — ahakoa i ua tino nui, i tino hari mātou nō te mea he kaupeka hākinakina ā te ahiahi!&quot;</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Translation: On Thursday the 16th of June, three of us went to school at 9 in the morning — although it rained heavily, we were very happy because there was a sports competition in the afternoon!</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Count the structures: date, day, people count, time, ahakoa, feeling, nō te mea, Ka… You can do all of this now.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Tip: Say that last sentence aloud. Slowly. &quot;I te Rāpare, ko te tekau mā ono o Pipiri, i haere mātou tokotoru ki te kura…&quot; You are speaking te reo Māori. Real, complex, beautiful te reo Māori.</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">We Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Kia Tūhono — Two-term vocabulary blitz</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Category blitz — fastest hands/voices win.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>ALL EMOTIONS: How many can we list in 60 seconds? hari, pōuri, mataku, māia… aim for 12.</li><li>ALL WEATHER: Every weather word. ua, hau, hukarere, āniwaniwa… aim for 10.</li><li>ALL 16 PRONOUNS: Together, in order — singular, dual, plural.</li><li>WILD CARD: Make one sentence that includes a colour, a feeling, a number, and a conjunction. Go.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Discussion prompt: You have now been learning te reo Māori for two terms — roughly 20 weeks, 400 minutes of class time. That is less than seven hours of instruction. In seven hours, you have built a communication toolkit of over 200 words and 15+ grammar structures. What does this tell you about what is possible with daily practice?</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">You Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Māu Anō — Two-term audit</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>A personal vocabulary and structure audit.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Write down every te reo Māori word you can remember in 5 minutes. Count them.</li><li>Write the six tense/negative structures: Kei te, I, Ka, Kāore e…ana, Kāore i, E…ana.</li><li>Write your favourite sentence from all of Term 2 — and say why it is your favourite.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write your &quot;two-term milestone&quot; — a paragraph describing what you can now do in te reo that you could not do 20 weeks ago. Use as many structures as possible. This is your record of growth.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Tuesday: Arotake — Grammar and structure deep review":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Te Aho Arataki Marau — All grammar structures Terms 1–2 consolidated and tested</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Objectives</th><td class=\"lt-td\">AO 2.3 — Grammar consolidation; AO 2.4 — Tense mastery</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">I Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tāua Kōrero — Grammar in the wild</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Today we review grammar not through rules but through real sentences — seeing structures at work in natural te reo. You will identify the structure in each sentence, and then build your own.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Identify the structure in each sentence:<br><strong>1. Ko Mere tōku tuakana.</strong> → ___<br><strong>2. Kei te mahi kaha rātou i waho i tēnei wā.</strong> → ___<br><strong>3. I haere māua ki te tāone i te Hātarei.</strong> → ___<br><strong>4. Ka ara ia, ka horoi ia i tōna kanohi, ka haere ia ki te kura.</strong> → ___<br><strong>5. Kāore mātou i toa i tērā kēmu — he kino tō mātou raru.</strong> → ___<br><strong>6. Ahakoa i ua kaha, i tākaro tonu mātou i te hākinakina.</strong> → ___<br><strong>7. He nui ake ngā ira o tō mātou kapa i ā rātou ira — e rua tekau mā whā ki e tekau mā toru.</strong> → ___<br><strong>8. I te ahiahi o Rāpare, i roto i te whare hui, ka kōrero tō mātou kaiako.</strong> → ___</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Answers:</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Ko → Identity</li><li>Kei te + plural pronoun + adverb → Present</li><li>I + dual pronoun + past location → Past with people</li><li>Ka… Ka… Ka → Narrative sequence</li><li>Kāore i → Past negative + He description</li><li>Ahakoa → Complex sentence (concession)</li><li>He [adj] ake + numbers → Comparative with score</li><li>Fronted time + fronted place + Ka narrative</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Every one of these you can write. Every one.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Tip: When you see an unfamiliar te reo sentence, break it into parts: What is the tense marker? Who is the person? What is the action? What else is there? This analysis skill is what makes you independent — you can work out new sentences for yourself.</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">We Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Kia Tūhono — Grammar challenge</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Build sentences matching the structure I specify.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Build a Ka narrative — five steps. Something that happened yesterday.</li><li>Build a Kāore i sentence AND a Kāore e…ana sentence — both about the same topic.</li><li>Build a He [adj] ake sentence with two numbers (a score or comparison).</li><li>Build a fronted time + fronted place + I sentence — three layers, one sentence.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Discussion prompt: Look at sentence 8 from the I Do: &quot;I te ahiahi o Rāpare, i roto i te whare hui, ka kōrero tō mātou kaiako.&quot; Three fronted phrases before the main action. In English this would be unusual. In te reo Māori it is natural and elegant. Why does front-loading information feel different in te reo than in English?</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">You Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Māu Anō — Grammar self-test</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Test yourself on every structure.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Write one sentence using each of the eight structures from I Do.</li><li>Write a passage (5 sentences) that uses all of: Ko, I, Ka, Kāore i, ahakoa.</li><li>Write the most complex sentence you can — use at least five different structures in one sentence.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Take a sentence you wrote in Week 1 of this term. Rewrite it now with everything you have learned. How different is it? What does the difference tell you?</li></ul></div></div>"},"Wednesday: Hanga rerenga — Showcase sentences: Terms 1 and 2":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Te Aho Arataki Marau — AO 2.3: Extended communication · All sentence types Terms 1 and 2</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Objectives</th><td class=\"lt-td\">AO 2.3 — Showcase &amp; celebration; AO 2.5 — Full vocabulary</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">I Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tāua Kōrero — Your best te reo sentences of the year</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This is a celebration. Today you write the best te reo sentences of your life so far — drawing on everything from two terms. There is no new content today. Just you, your full toolkit, and the task of producing something beautiful.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Here are examples of student showcase sentences:<br><br><strong>&quot;I te ahiahi o Rāhoroi, ko tōku whānau me ōku hoa pukumahi i haere mātou tokotoru tekau ki te tākaro hākinakina — i toa mātou e rua tekau mā ono ki e tekau mā whā!&quot;</strong><br>(On Saturday afternoon, my family and my hardworking friends, thirty of us went to play sport — we won 26 to 14!)<br><br><strong>&quot;Ko te taiao o Aotearoa tōku wāhi okioki — ā, ko ia hoki tōku ara ora.&quot;</strong><br>(The natural environment of New Zealand is my place of rest — and it is also my pathway to life.)<br><br><strong>&quot;Ka ora tōku ngākau i ngā kupu o tēnei reo, nō te mea ko te reo Māori te reo o ōku tīpuna — he taonga tuku iho tēnei.&quot;</strong><br>(My heart comes alive at the words of this language, because te reo Māori is the language of my ancestors — it is a treasure passed down.)</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>What makes these sentences great:</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>→ They use REAL information (not just exercises)<br>→ They COMBINE multiple structures naturally<br>→ They say something the writer MEANS<br>→ They have a voice — you can hear a person behind the words</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Notice:<br>&quot;ko ia hoki tōku ara ora&quot; — and it is also my pathway to life (ara ora — a beautiful new compound: ara + ora = path + life)<br>&quot;he taonga tuku iho&quot; — a treasure passed down (tuku iho = handed down through generations)</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>These are not just grammar exercises. They are statements about identity.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Tip: The best sentence you can write today is not the longest or the most complex — it is the most true. Say something you mean. In te reo Māori.</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">We Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Kia Tūhono — Write together</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Class writes one showcase sentence together on the board. Everyone contributes one element.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Start: who and when? Ko + person + time/date.</li><li>What happened? I or Ka + action + place.</li><li>Add detail: number + weather or feeling.</li><li>Add meaning: ahakoa or nō te mea + result or reflection.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Discussion prompt: He taonga tuku iho — a treasure passed down through generations. The phrase tuku iho means &quot;handed down&quot; — from ancestors to descendants. Te reo Māori is a taonga tuku iho. What does it mean to receive something that was passed down across generations? What responsibility does that bring?</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">You Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Māu Anō — Your showcase sentences</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write the best te reo Māori sentences you have ever written.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Write your best SIMPLE sentence — punchy and true.</li><li>Write your best COMPLEX sentence — with a subordinate clause and real meaning.</li><li>Write your best DESCRIPTIVE sentence — rich with vocabulary.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write your SHOWCASE SENTENCE — the single best te reo Māori sentence you can produce. Make it say something true about you, your world, or te reo Māori itself. Read it to the class.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Thursday: Kōrero — He whakamātautau — End of Term 2 assessment":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Te Aho Arataki Marau — All AOs 1.1–2.5 · All six language modes: Whakarongo, Kōrero, Pānui, Tuhituhi, Mātakitaki, Whakaatu</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Objectives</th><td class=\"lt-td\">AO 1.1 — Greetings; AO 1.2 — Introductions; AO 2.3 — Full conversation; AO 2.5 — All vocabulary</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">I Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tāua Kōrero — The Term 2 standard</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Today is your end-of-term assessment. Before we begin, let's see what a full Term 2 level conversation looks like. This is the standard — it uses structures from both terms in a natural, warm exchange.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\"><strong>A:</strong> Tēnā koe! Ko wai tōu ingoa? Nō hea koe?<br><strong>B:</strong> Ko Rangi tōku ingoa. Nō Ōtautahi ahau — he tāone ātaahua, he tāone māhana hoki i te raumati.<br><strong>A:</strong> He pēhea ōu hararei?<br><strong>B:</strong> He tino pai! I haere mātou tokowhā ki te ngahere. I ātaahua te rangi — ahakoa i <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=hau\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">hau</a> kaha i tētahi rā.<br><strong>A:</strong> He pēhea tōu āhua ināianei?<br><strong>B:</strong> Kei te hari au, kei te hīhī hoki — e pai ana ki a au te kura hou!<br><strong>A:</strong> He aha āu whainga mō tēnei wāhanga?<br><strong>B:</strong> Ko ōku whainga: ka ako ake au i ngā tae, ka mōhio ake au ki ngā tau, ā ka kōrero māmā ake au i te reo Māori.<br><strong>A:</strong> Ka rawe! Tēnā koe, tēnā koe, tēnā koe.<br><strong>B:</strong> Tēnā koe, tēnā koe, tēnā koe.</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Assessment criteria — what this conversation shows:<br>✓ AO 1.1: Tēnā koe · correct greeting and closing<br>✓ AO 1.2: Ko [name] tōku ingoa · Nō [place] ahau<br>✓ AO 1.4: Origin and personal information<br>✓ AO 2.3: Extended kōrero with feelings, reasons, goals<br>✓ AO 2.4: Past events · Weather · Seasonal reference<br>✓ AO 2.5: Adjectives · Emotions · Character · Numbers<br>✓ Grammar: I, Ka, Kei te, ahakoa, Ko ēnei (implied), comparative (ake)</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Tip: Kei te hari au, kei te hīhī hoki — I am happy, I am also excited. Two feelings in one sentence. That is confident te reo. When you feel something strongly — name it twice.</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">We Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Kia Tūhono — Final practice round</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>One full practice conversation with a partner before the assessment.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Greet, introduce, say where you're from with one description of your town.</li><li>Describe your holidays: past tense + people count + weather + ahakoa.</li><li>Say how you feel now: two emotions + a reason.</li><li>State three goals for Term 3 using Ko ōku whainga: ka… ka… ā ka…</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Discussion prompt: Ko ōku whainga — my goals. What are your real goals for Term 3? Not just te reo goals — learning goals, relationship goals, personal goals. Can you say any of them in te reo Māori right now? What one goal would you most like to be able to say in te reo by the end of Term 3?</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">You Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Māu Anō — He whakamātautau — End of Term 2</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Your end-of-term spoken assessment.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>Task 1:</strong> Full conversation — at least 10 lines each. Cover greeting, introduction, holidays, feelings, goals.</li><li><strong>Task 2:</strong> Self-assess honestly against the checklist.</li><li><strong>Task 3:</strong> Write your Term 3 goal in te reo: Ko tāku whainga mō Wāhanga Toru:</li><li>✦ Checklist: ☐ Greeted correctly (Tēnā koe) · ☐ Introduced self (Ko/Nō) · ☐ Used past tense (I) · ☐ Used Ka narrative · ☐ Expressed a feeling (Kei te [emotion]) · ☐ Used a number or date · ☐ Used a conjunction · ☐ Closed with Tēnā koe ×3</li></ul></div></div>"},"Friday: Aromatawai — Quiz & Review":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Monday questions</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Which sentence correctly uses Ka for a narrative sequence?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>Ka ara ia, ka kai, ka haere ia ki te kura. ✅</li><li>I ara ia, ka kai ia, kei te haere ia. </li><li>Ka ara ia inanahi, ka kai ia āpōpō. </li><li>Ka ara. Kai. Haere ki te kura. </li></ul></li><li>&quot;Ko te reo Māori he taonga tuku iho.&quot; What does tuku iho mean?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>passed down through generations ✅</li><li>difficult to learn </li><li>belonging to one person only </li><li>recently created </li></ul></li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tuesday questions</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>&quot;Ahakoa i ua kaha, i tākaro tonu mātou.&quot; What structure is this?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>Past negative </li><li>Ka narrative </li><li>Complex concession with ahakoa ✅</li><li>Ko identity sentence </li></ul></li><li>&quot;He nui ake ngā ira o tō mātou kapa i ā rātou ira.&quot; What does this compare?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>The size of two teams </li><li>The scores of two teams ✅</li><li>The number of players </li><li>The colours of two teams </li></ul></li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Wednesday questions</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>&quot;Ko ia hoki tōku ara ora.&quot; What does ara ora mean?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>learning journey </li><li>favourite song </li><li>pathway to life ✅</li><li>daily routine </li></ul></li><li>What makes a &quot;showcase sentence&quot; different from an exercise sentence?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>It is longer </li><li>It uses more vocabulary </li><li>It says something the writer truly means and has a real voice ✅</li><li>It always uses Ka </li></ul></li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Thursday questions</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>&quot;Kei te hari au, kei te hīhī hoki.&quot; What does hīhī mean here?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>tired </li><li>sad </li><li>excited / eager ✅</li><li>surprised </li></ul></li><li>&quot;Ko ōku whainga: ka ako ake au…&quot; — What does ako ake mean?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>to stop learning </li><li>to learn less </li><li>to learn more / further ✅</li><li>to learn backwards </li></ul></li></ul></div></div>"}}}}},"Term 3":{"label":"Week","children":{"Week 1":{"label":"Day","children":{"Monday: Ngā wāhanga o te tinana — Parts of the body":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Te Aho Arataki Marau — AO 2.5: Physical characteristics · Grammar Level 1–2: Body vocabulary, E…ana progressive aspect</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Objectives</th><td class=\"lt-td\">AO 2.5 — Body parts &amp; physical description; AO 1.5 — Location on body</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">I Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tāua Kōrero — The body in te reo Māori</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This term we go deeper — more complex sentence structures, richer vocabulary, and longer conversations. We begin with the body, because body vocabulary connects everything: feelings (your heart, your stomach), action (your hands, your feet), and description (your face, your eyes). Listen and connect each word to what you feel.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\"><strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=m%C4%81hunga\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">māhunga</a></strong> — head<br><strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=rae\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">rae</a></strong> — forehead<br><strong>kanohi</strong> — face<br><strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=whatu\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">whatu</a></strong> / <strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=karu\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">karu</a></strong> — eye(s)<br><strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=ihu\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">ihu</a></strong> — nose<br><strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=waha\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">waha</a></strong> — mouth<br><strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=taringa\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">taringa</a></strong> — ear<br><strong>niho</strong> — teeth<br><strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=kaki\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">kaki</a></strong> — neck / throat<br><strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=pokohiwi\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">pokohiwi</a></strong> — shoulder<br><strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=ringa\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">ringa</a></strong> / <strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=ringaringa\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">ringaringa</a></strong> — hand / arm<br><strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=ng%C5%ABk%C5%AB\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">ngūkū</a></strong> — elbow<br><strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=puku\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">puku</a></strong> — stomach / belly<br><strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=tuar%C4%81\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">tuarā</a></strong> — back<br><strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=waewae\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">waewae</a></strong> — leg / foot<br><strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=turi\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">turi</a></strong> — knee<br><strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=waewae\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">waewae</a> matua</strong> — right foot<br><strong>waewae <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=t%C5%8Dng%C4%81\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">tōngā</a></strong> — left foot</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Body parts in a sentence:</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Kei te <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=mamae\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">mamae</a> tōku māhunga.</strong><br>My head is sore. (<a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=mamae\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">mamae</a> = sore/pain)</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>He whero ōna karu.</strong><br>His/her eyes are red.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>I horoi ia i ōna niho.</strong><br>She brushed her teeth. (horoi = clean/wash)</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Kei te ngongo tōku kaki.</strong><br>My throat is sore/tight. (ngongo = tight/constricted)</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Possessives with body parts:<br>→ Body parts are ō-category (they are part of you, senior to your control)<br>→ Use tōku, tōu, tōna, ō mātou, ō rātou</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>tōku māhunga</strong> — my head<br><strong>tōna kanohi</strong> — his/her face<br><strong>ō mātou waewae</strong> — our legs</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Tip: Body parts are ō-category — they are part of you, not things you control. So always tōku (not tāku) for body parts. Tōku māhunga, tōku ringa, tōku waewae.</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">We Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Kia Tūhono — Name and describe the body</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Point and name — I point to a body part, you name it in te reo. Then use it in a sentence.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I point to your ear: <strong>taringa</strong>. Use it: <strong>Kei te aha tōu taringa?</strong> — What is wrong with your ear?</li><li>Describe using He: <strong>He nui ōna karu.</strong> She has big eyes. <strong>He iti tōna ihu.</strong> His nose is small.</li><li>Use past tense: <strong>I mamae tōku puku inanahi.</strong> My stomach was sore yesterday.</li><li>Connect body + feeling: <strong>Ka hōhipū tōku ngākau.</strong> My heart leaps/jumps. (hōhipū = leap/jump with excitement)</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Discussion prompt: In English we say &quot;I have a sore head&quot; — but in te reo: &quot;Kei te mamae tōku māhunga&quot; — literally &quot;right now sore my head.&quot; The pain is the subject, not you. What does this tell us about how te reo Māori thinks about physical experience?</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">You Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Māu Anō — Body vocabulary</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Use body vocabulary in real sentences.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Write six body part sentences — three with Kei te and three with He.</li><li>Write a past tense sentence about a time something hurt: I mamae tōku… nō te mea…</li><li>Write a body sentence that connects to an emotion: Ka [emotion] tōku ngākau / puku / kanohi.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write a seven-sentence &quot;body check&quot; — from head to toe, describe the state of each body part right now. Are you tired? Sore? Alert? Express it through the body.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Tuesday: E haere ana, e kai ana — The progressive aspect E…ana":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Te Aho Arataki Marau — AO 2.3: Extended communication · Grammar Level 1–2: E…ana (present progressive — ongoing action)</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Objectives</th><td class=\"lt-td\">AO 2.3 — Progressive aspect; AO 2.4 — Ongoing action in time</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">I Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tāua Kōrero — The action is still happening</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>You know Kei te for what is happening right now. Today we learn a related but different structure — E…ana. While Kei te states a present action, E…ana emphasises that the action is ONGOING — still in progress, continuing, not yet finished. It is a subtle but powerful distinction.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\"><strong>Kei te kai ia.</strong> — She is eating. (present state)<br><strong>E kai ana ia.</strong> — She is eating (and still going — the eating is ongoing)<br><br><strong>E mahi ana rātou i tēnei wā.</strong><br>They are working right now (the work is ongoing).<br><br><strong>E ako ana tātou i te reo Māori.</strong><br>We are learning te reo Māori (an ongoing process — it continues).<br><br><strong>I te wā e mahi ana ia, ka tae mai tōna hoa.</strong><br>While she was working (ongoing), her friend arrived.<br><br><strong>Kāore ia e haere ana ki te kura āpōpō.</strong><br>She is not going to school tomorrow (ongoing plan that is not happening).</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>When to use <strong>E…ana</strong> instead of <strong>Kei te</strong>:</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>→ When the action is clearly ONGOING and UNFINISHED:<br>&quot;E hanga ana rātou i te whare.&quot; — They are building the house. (still under construction)</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>→ In relative clauses — describing something while it is happening:<br>&quot;I te wā e ako ana mātou&quot; — While we were learning</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>→ In negative future:<br>&quot;Kāore ia e haere ana&quot; — She is not going (as an ongoing fact)</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>→ For slower, ongoing processes:<br>&quot;E tipu ana ngā rākau.&quot; — The trees are growing. (gradual ongoing process)</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Kei te tends to be more immediate: Kei te kai au RIGHT NOW.<br>E…ana tends to be more continuous: E tipu ana te whenua — the land is ever growing.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Tip: E…ana = still happening, ongoing. Think of it as the -ing that is still in progress. Kei te = happening now. E…ana = happening now AND continuing. For processes, use E…ana.</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">We Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Kia Tūhono — Still going</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>I give you a Kei te sentence — you decide if E…ana would work better and why.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>&quot;Kei te horoi ia i tōna kanohi.&quot; → Could this be E horoi ana ia? Yes — if the face-washing is ongoing, not just a moment.</li><li>&quot;E tipu ana te ngahere o Aotearoa.&quot; — The forests of NZ are growing. Why E…ana not Kei te? Because this is a long-term ongoing process.</li><li>&quot;I te wā e mahi ana ia, ka tangi tōna pēpi.&quot; — While she was working, her baby cried. Build a similar sentence with i te wā e…ana.</li><li>&quot;Kāore mātou e haere ana ki te marae āpōpō.&quot; — We are not going to the marae tomorrow. Why Kāore e…ana for a future negative plan?</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Discussion prompt: E ako ana tātou i te reo Māori — we are learning te reo Māori (and the learning is ongoing). This sentence describes what you are doing this whole year. How does adding the ongoing aspect change the feeling of the sentence compared to &quot;Kei te ako tātou&quot;?</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">You Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Māu Anō — The ongoing action</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write sentences using E…ana for ongoing processes.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Write three E…ana sentences about ongoing things happening in your world right now.</li><li>Write an i te wā e…ana clause: &quot;While [something was ongoing], [something happened].&quot;</li><li>Write a Kāore e…ana negative about something you are not going to do.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write a paragraph (6 sentences) using E…ana for ongoing background actions, Kei te for immediate actions, and Ka for events that punctuate the ongoing scene. E.g. describe a busy classroom.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Wednesday: Hanga rerenga — Body and progressive in stories":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Te Aho Arataki Marau — AO 2.5: Physical description · AO 2.3: Extended narrative · Grammar Level 1–2: Body + E…ana in descriptive narrative</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Objectives</th><td class=\"lt-td\">AO 2.5 — Physical narrative; AO 2.3 — Descriptive story writing</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">I Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tāua Kōrero — Describing action through the body</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Great storytelling uses the body — characters feel things in their stomach, see with wide eyes, and run with aching legs. Today we combine body vocabulary and E…ana to write vivid physical descriptions in te reo Māori.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\"><strong>E omaoma ana ia, e <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=mamae\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">mamae</a> ana ōna waewae, engari kāore ia e tū ana.</strong><br>She is running, her legs aching, but she is not stopping.<br><br><strong>Ka tū ia. E hūhū ana tōna <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=ng%C4%81kau\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">ngākau</a>. Ka titiro ia ki runga ki ngā maunga.</strong><br>She stood. Her heart was pounding. She looked up at the mountains.<br><br><strong>I te wā e haere ana mātou ki te ngahere, ka kite mātou i tētahi manu nui — e <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=rere\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">rere</a> ana ia i runga i ō mātou māhunga!</strong><br>While we were walking to the forest, we saw a huge bird — it was flying above our heads!</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Techniques for physical storytelling:</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>Body + E…ana for ongoing physical state</strong>:</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&quot;E mamae ana ōna waewae&quot; — her legs aching (still ongoing)<br>&quot;E hūhū ana tōna ngākau&quot; — her heart pounding</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>Short Ka sentence to break the ongoing action</strong>:</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&quot;Ka tū ia.&quot; — She stopped. (Ka = sudden completed action breaking ongoing scene)</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>i te wā e…ana + Ka for simultaneous action</strong>:</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&quot;I te wā e haere ana mātou… ka kite mātou…&quot; — While we were walking… we saw…</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>New words:<br><strong>hūhū</strong> — to buzz / pound / vibrate (sound word from Term 1!)<br><strong>rere</strong> — to fly / flow / move swiftly<br><strong>i runga i</strong> — above / over</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Tip: E…ana sets the ongoing background. Ka punches through it with a sudden event. &quot;E omaoma ana ia… Ka tū ia.&quot; She was running… She stopped. The contrast creates drama.</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">We Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Kia Tūhono — Physical storytelling</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Build a scene using body vocabulary and E…ana together.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Set the ongoing scene: <strong>E omaoma ana ngā tamariki i waho.</strong> Now add a body detail: E mamae ana ō rātou…</li><li>Break the scene with Ka: <strong>Ka tū rātou katoa.</strong> Why did they stop? Add a reason.</li><li>Use i te wā e…ana: &quot;While they were running, they saw…&quot; → <strong>I te wā e omaoma ana rātou, ka kite rātou i…</strong></li><li>Close with a body reaction: heart, eyes, or legs responding to what happened.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Discussion prompt: E hūhū ana tōna ngākau — her heart was pounding. The word hūhū is a sound word — it describes a buzzing, pulsing sound. Why might a language use a sound word to describe an emotional physical sensation like a pounding heart? What does it make you feel when you hear it?</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">You Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Māu Anō — Physical scene</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write a short physical scene using body vocabulary and E…ana.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Write a three-sentence ongoing scene using E…ana for two different body states.</li><li>Add a Ka sentence that breaks the scene suddenly.</li><li>Add an i te wā e…ana sentence — something seen or heard while the scene was ongoing.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write an eight-sentence physical scene — a sports moment, a scary experience, or an exciting discovery. Use at least four body part words, E…ana for ongoing states, Ka for breaking events, and at least one sound word.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Thursday: Kōrero — He kōrero mō te hauora — Health conversation":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Te Aho Arataki Marau — AO 2.5: Physical feelings · AO 1.6: Politeness · AO 2.3: Extended communication</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Objectives</th><td class=\"lt-td\">AO 2.5 — Health &amp; wellbeing; AO 1.6 — Politeness in health contexts</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">I Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tāua Kōrero — At the sick bay</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Today we use body vocabulary and feelings in a real scenario — visiting the school sick bay. This is practical, important language that combines body parts, E…ana, feelings, and politeness.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\"><strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=Kaimahi\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Kaimahi</a> <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=hauora\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">hauora</a>:</strong> Tēnā koe. He aha tāu raruraru?<br><strong>Ākonga:</strong> Kei te mamae tōku māhunga, kei te ngongo hoki tōku kaki.<br><strong>Kaimahi:</strong> Ā, he tino kino tērā. E <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=mamae\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">mamae</a> ana tōu māhunga i ēhea wā?<br><strong>Ākonga:</strong> I tīmata i tēnei ata. Ka <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=huri\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">huri</a> noa tōku ao i ētahi wā.<br><strong>Kaimahi:</strong> Ka <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=m%C4%81rama\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">mārama</a>. Kei te māuiui koe — me <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=okioki\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">okioki</a> koe. He aha āu i kai ai mō tō parakuihi?<br><strong>Ākonga:</strong> Kāore au i kai i tēnei ata — i ngenge noa au.<br><strong>Kaimahi:</strong> Āe, ko tērā pea te <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=take\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">take</a>. Me kai koe ināianei. Ka taea koe te hoki ki tōu <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=akomanga\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">akomanga</a> ā te rua karaka.</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Health vocabulary:<br><strong>kaimahi hauora</strong> — health worker / school nurse (kai- + hauora)<br><strong>raruraru</strong> — problem / trouble<br><strong>ngongo</strong> — tight / sore (throat)<br><strong>Ka huri noa tōku ao.</strong> — My world just spins. (dizzy)<br><strong>huri</strong> — to turn / spin<br><strong>ao</strong> — world / cloud (here: the world around me)<br><strong>i ēhea wā</strong> — at what times<br><strong>Ka mārama.</strong> — I understand / I see. (literally: it is clear)<br><strong>me okioki koe</strong> — you should rest (me = should/must — a gentle instruction)<br><strong>ko tērā pea te take</strong> — that is perhaps the reason<br><strong>Ka taea koe te hoki</strong> — You are able to go back</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Tip: Me + verb = should / must do something. Me okioki koe = you should rest. Me kai koe = you should eat. Me is a gentle way to give advice or instruction — softer than a command.</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">We Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Kia Tūhono — Sick bay roleplay</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Roleplay the sick bay scene. One person is the kaimahi hauora, one is the ākonga.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Kaimahi asks: <strong>He aha tāu raruraru?</strong> Ākonga answers with two body complaints using Kei te mamae…</li><li>Kaimahi asks: <strong>E mamae ana tōu [body part] i ēhea wā?</strong> Ākonga answers with a time: I tīmata i…</li><li>Kaimahi uses me: <strong>Me okioki koe / me kai koe / me inu wai koe.</strong> (you should drink water)</li><li>Kaimahi closes: <strong>Ka taea koe te hoki ki tōu akomanga ā te [time] karaka.</strong></li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Discussion prompt: Me okioki koe — you should rest. The word me here is much gentler than a command like &quot;Okioki!&quot; (Rest!). How does the choice of me versus a direct command change the relationship between the speaker and listener? When would you use me versus a direct instruction?</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">You Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Māu Anō — Sick bay dialogue</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write a full sick bay dialogue with a partner.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Write the ākonga's part: three complaint sentences using Kei te mamae, E…ana, and a time.</li><li>Write the kaimahi's part: two me sentences giving advice.</li><li>Include Ka huri noa tōku ao or another vivid body expression.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write the full dialogue (10 lines). Include: He aha tāu raruraru, body vocabulary, E…ana, a time expression, me sentences, Ka mārama, and Ka taea koe te hoki ā te [time].</li></ul></div></div>"},"Friday: Aromatawai — Quiz & Review":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Monday questions</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>What possessive category are body parts in te reo Māori?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>ā-category — you control them </li><li>ō-category — they are part of you and senior to your control ✅</li><li>Neither — they use a special category </li><li>It depends on the body part </li></ul></li><li>&quot;Kei te mamae tōku māhunga.&quot; What does mamae mean?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>tired </li><li>sore / in pain ✅</li><li>spinning </li><li>swollen </li></ul></li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tuesday questions</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>What is the main difference between &quot;Kei te kai ia&quot; and &quot;E kai ana ia&quot;?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>They mean exactly the same thing </li><li>E kai ana emphasises the eating is ongoing and continuing; Kei te is more immediate ✅</li><li>Kei te is past tense; E…ana is present </li><li>E…ana is only used for future plans </li></ul></li><li>&quot;I te wā e mahi ana ia, ka tae mai tōna hoa.&quot; What happened while she was working?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>She finished the work </li><li>Her friend arrived ✅</li><li>She fell asleep </li><li>She started eating </li></ul></li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Wednesday questions</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>&quot;E omaoma ana ia… Ka tū ia.&quot; What effect does the Ka sentence create?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>It shows the running is continuing </li><li>It creates drama by breaking the ongoing action with a sudden stop ✅</li><li>It shows the running happened in the past </li><li>It describes where she ran </li></ul></li><li>What does &quot;rere&quot; mean?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>to fall </li><li>to fly / flow / move swiftly ✅</li><li>to cry </li><li>to laugh </li></ul></li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Thursday questions</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>&quot;Me okioki koe.&quot; What does me indicate here?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>A question </li><li>A past event </li><li>A gentle instruction / you should do this ✅</li><li>A command as strong as E tū! </li></ul></li><li>What does &quot;Ka huri noa tōku ao&quot; describe?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>The world is changing </li><li>A spinning / dizzy feeling ✅</li><li>The clouds are moving </li><li>Time passing </li></ul></li></ul></div></div>"}}},"Week 2":{"label":"Day","children":{"Monday: Kua mutu — The perfect aspect: Kua":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Te Aho Arataki Marau — AO 2.4: Time · AO 2.3: Extended communication · Grammar Level 1–2: Kua (perfect aspect — action completed with present relevance)</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Objectives</th><td class=\"lt-td\">AO 2.4 — Perfect aspect — Kua; AO 2.3 — Completed action with relevance</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">I Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tāua Kōrero — It is done: Kua</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>You have I for past events, Ka for narrative, and E…ana for ongoing actions. Today we meet the final tense marker — Kua. Kua describes an action that has been completed and whose result is still relevant right now. It is the difference between &quot;I went&quot; and &quot;I have gone (and I am still gone).&quot;</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\"><strong>Kua <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=mutu\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">mutu</a> te <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=akoranga\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">akoranga</a>.</strong><br>The lesson has finished. (and we are now in the after-lesson state)<br><br><strong>Kua <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=tae\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">tae</a> mai ia.</strong><br>She has arrived. (she is now here as a result)<br><br><strong>Kua <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=huri\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">huri</a> te ao.</strong><br>The world has changed. (and remains changed)<br><br><strong>Kua <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=m%C4%81rama\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">mārama</a> au.</strong><br>I have understood / I understand now. (the understanding has arrived)<br><br><strong>Kua <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=oti\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">oti</a> i a au.</strong><br>I have finished it. (<a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=oti\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">oti</a> = be completed, i a au = by me)<br><br><strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=K%C4%81ore+an%C5%8D\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Kāore anō</a> kia mutu.</strong><br>It has not finished yet. (kāore anō = not yet, kia = subjunctive with anō)</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>When to use <strong>Kua</strong>:</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>→ Something has just happened and the result matters NOW:<br>&quot;Kua tae mai ia.&quot; — She has arrived. (so she is here now — that is why it matters)</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>→ A state that has come about:<br>&quot;Kua mārama au.&quot; — I now understand. (the understanding has arrived)</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>→ Official announcements of completion:<br>&quot;Kua mutu te hui.&quot; — The meeting has ended.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Compare:<br><strong>I mutu te akoranga.</strong> — The lesson ended. (past fact, no connection to now)<br><strong>Kua mutu te akoranga.</strong> — The lesson has ended. (so what comes next? — the result matters)</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Negative: <strong>Kāore anō kia mutu</strong> — not yet finished<br>Question: <strong>Kua mutu?</strong> — Is it done yet?</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Tip: Kua = &quot;has [done] and the result is relevant now.&quot; Think of it as an announcement — something has changed state, and that new state is what matters. Kua tae mai = has arrived (and is now present).</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">We Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Kia Tūhono — Completed with relevance</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>I describe a situation — you use Kua to express the completed state.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The class has just ended. → <strong>Kua mutu te akoranga.</strong> What happens next as a result?</li><li>You have just understood something difficult. → <strong>Kua mārama au!</strong> Use it with an exclamation.</li><li>The food has all been eaten. → <strong>Kua kainga te kai katoa.</strong> (kainga = been eaten — passive)</li><li>Ask if someone has finished: <strong>Kua oti i a koe?</strong> Answer yes: <strong>Āe, kua oti i a au.</strong></li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Discussion prompt: &quot;Kua huri te ao&quot; — the world has changed. This phrase appears often in te reo Māori writing and speech, especially when reflecting on change over time. Why is Kua the right marker here rather than I or Ka? What does the choice of Kua add to the meaning of &quot;the world has changed&quot;?</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">You Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Māu Anō — Kua sentences</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Use Kua for completed actions whose results matter now.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Write three Kua sentences about things that have just happened in your classroom or school.</li><li>Write a Kāore anō kia mutu sentence about something not finished yet.</li><li>Write a Kua oti i a au sentence about something you have completed.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write a paragraph (6 sentences) using Kua, I, Ka, and Kei te all in the same passage — a moment-by-moment account of arriving at school today. Use each marker at least once and make sure each one is used correctly.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Tuesday: He aha ōu hiahia? — Wants, needs, and future intention":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Te Aho Arataki Marau — AO 2.3: Extended communication · Grammar Level 1–2: hiahia ki te, māku e, ka taea e au te</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Objectives</th><td class=\"lt-td\">AO 2.3 — Expressing wants &amp; intentions; AO 2.4 — Future intention</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">I Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tāua Kōrero — What do you want? What will you do?</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>You can describe what is happening, what happened, and what has happened. Today we look forward — expressing what you want, what you intend to do, and what you are able to do. These are the structures of plans, goals, and dreams.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\"><strong>WANT TO:</strong><br><strong>Kei te hiahia au ki te ako i te reo Māori.</strong><br>I want to learn te reo Māori.<br><br><strong>WILL DO (first person):</strong><br><strong>Māku e <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=horoi\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">horoi</a> i ngā <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=rihi\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">rihi</a>.</strong><br>I will wash the dishes. (māku = for me to do, e + verb = the action)<br><br><strong>WILL DO (third person):</strong><br><strong>Māna e hoko i te <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=miraka\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">miraka</a>.</strong><br>She will buy the milk. (māna = for her to do)<br><br><strong>ABLE TO:</strong><br><strong>Ka taea e au te <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=k%C5%8Drero\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">kōrero</a> i te reo Māori.</strong><br>I am able to speak te reo Māori.<br><br><strong>CANNOT:</strong><br><strong>E kore e taea e au te haere āpōpō.</strong><br>I will not be able to go tomorrow.</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Three structures for future intention:</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>Kei te hiahia au ki te [verb]</strong> = I want to [verb]</li><li><strong>Māku / Māna / Mā rātou e [verb]</strong> = I/she/they will [verb]</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>(mā + person + e + verb = that person is the one who will do it)</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Mā- takes the person:<br><strong>māku</strong> = for me to do<br><strong>māu</strong> = for you to do<br><strong>māna</strong> = for him/her to do<br><strong>mā mātou</strong> = for us (excl.) to do</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>Ka taea e au te [verb]</strong> = I am able to [verb]</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>E kore e taea e au te [verb]</strong> = I am not able to [verb]</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>New word: <strong>rihi</strong> — dishes / plates</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Tip: Māku e [verb] = I will [verb] (I am the one who will do it). Māna e [verb] = she will [verb]. The mā- word tells you WHO is responsible for the action.</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">We Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Kia Tūhono — Plans and intentions</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Build sentences about what you want, will do, and can do.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>What do you want to do after school? <strong>Kei te hiahia au ki te [verb] i muri i te kura.</strong></li><li>Assign a task: <strong>Māu e horoi i ngā rihi.</strong> You will wash the dishes. <strong>Māku e tahu i te ahi.</strong> I will light the fire.</li><li>What can you do in te reo? <strong>Ka taea e au te kōrero / pānui / tuhituhi i te reo Māori.</strong></li><li>What can you NOT do yet? <strong>E kore e taea e au te [verb] ināianei — engari āpōpō…</strong></li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Discussion prompt: Māku e [verb] — I am the one who will do this. This structure makes the person responsible very clear. In te ao Māori, naming who will do something is an act of commitment. How does saying &quot;māku e&quot; feel different from just saying &quot;I will&quot;? What does making yourself the named agent add?</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">You Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Māu Anō — Wants, plans, abilities</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write sentences expressing what you want, plan, and can do.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Write three hiahia sentences about things you want to do or learn this term.</li><li>Write two māku e sentences — commit to two things you will do.</li><li>Write two Ka taea e au te sentences about things you can now do in te reo.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write a &quot;Term 3 goals&quot; paragraph (7 sentences) — what you want to learn, what you commit to doing, what you can already do, and what you could not do yet but will be able to by end of term. Use hiahia, māku e, Ka taea, and E kore e taea.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Wednesday: Hanga rerenga — All tense markers together":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Te Aho Arataki Marau — AO 2.4: Time mastery · AO 2.3: Extended communication · Grammar Level 1–2: Kei te, I, Ka, Kua, E…ana in one passage</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Objectives</th><td class=\"lt-td\">AO 2.4 — Full tense mastery; AO 2.3 — Extended tense-controlled writing</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">I Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tāua Kōrero — All six tense markers in one passage</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>You now have the full tense toolkit: Kei te, I, Ka, Kua, E…ana, and the negatives Kāore e…ana and Kāore i. Today we use all of them in a single passage — showing how a skilled te reo speaker moves through time fluidly.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\"><strong>Kua tae mai au ki te kura i tēnei ata.</strong> (Kua — arrival complete, I am now here)<br><strong>I haere au i te Rāhina</strong> — he roa tōku huarahi. (I — specific past fact)<br><strong>E haere ana tēnā kura ki te hākinakina ā tērā wiki</strong> — ka tū tō mātou ope hei whakamau. (E…ana — ongoing plan; Ka — narrative result)<br><strong>Kei te whakarite mātou mō tērā kaupeka ināianei.</strong> (Kei te — what is happening right now)<br><strong>Kāore anō kua oti ā mātou whakarite</strong> — engari ka oti āpōpō. (Kua negative — not yet; Ka — future narrative)</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Each marker's job in this passage:</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Kua</strong> — arrival complete (I am here NOW as a result)<br><strong>I</strong> — specific past fact with detail<br><strong>E…ana</strong> — ongoing future plan (still being planned)<br><strong>Ka</strong> — narrative move (what will happen as a result)<br><strong>Kei te</strong> — what is happening right now<br><strong>Kāore anō kua</strong> — not yet completed</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>New words:<br><strong>huarahi</strong> — journey / path / road<br><strong>ope</strong> — team / group / squad<br><strong>hei whakamau</strong> — to compete against / to challenge (hei = for the purpose of, whakamau = focus on/oppose)<br><strong>whakarite</strong> — to prepare / arrange</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Tip: Read any te reo passage and identify the tense marker of each sentence. Once you can identify them easily, you can use them accurately. The markers are the skeleton — everything else hangs on them.</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">We Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Kia Tūhono — All markers, one story</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Together we write a story that uses all six markers deliberately. I specify which marker each sentence needs.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Sentence 1 — Kua: Something has just been completed with present relevance.</li><li>Sentence 2 — I: A specific past event with a time word.</li><li>Sentence 3 — E…ana + Ka: An ongoing background action interrupted by something.</li><li>Sentence 4 — Kei te: What is happening right now as a result.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Discussion prompt: You have now learned six tense/aspect markers: Kei te, I, Ka, Kua, E…ana, and Kāore. English has twelve tenses. Te reo Māori has six markers that between them cover the same ground — but with a different underlying logic. What is the logic? What question does each marker answer?</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">You Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Māu Anō — All six markers</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write a passage using all six markers at least once each.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Write a Kua sentence and a Kei te sentence — make sure the difference is clear.</li><li>Write an E…ana sentence interrupted by a Ka sentence.</li><li>Write an I sentence (specific past) and a Kāore anō kua sentence (not yet complete).</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write a complete eight-sentence passage about a project or event at school — use every marker at least once. Label each sentence with the marker you used and why.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Thursday: Kōrero — He kōrero mō ngā whāinga — Goals and plans conversation":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Te Aho Arataki Marau — AO 2.3: Extended communication · AO 2.4: Future intention · AO 1.7: School language</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Objectives</th><td class=\"lt-td\">AO 2.3 — Goals &amp; planning conversation; AO 2.4 — Future intention</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">I Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tāua Kōrero — Planning and goal-setting</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Today we use Kua, hiahia, māku e, and Ka taea in a conversation about goals and plans — the kind of conversation you might have with a teacher, a coach, or a friend about where you are headed.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\"><strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=Kaiwhakaako\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Kaiwhakaako</a>:</strong> Tēnā koe. Kua mutu tō akoranga mō tēnei wāhanga — he pēhea tōu whakaaro?<br><strong>Ākonga:</strong> Kua mārama ake au ki ngā <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=hanganga\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">hanganga</a> katoa. Ka taea e au te tuhituhi i ngā <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=rerenga\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">rerenga</a> <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=pakiaka\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">pakiaka</a> ināianei.<br><strong>Kaiwhakaako:</strong> Ka pai! He aha āu whainga mō tērā wāhanga?<br><strong>Ākonga:</strong> Kei te hiahia au ki te ako i ngā kupu hou o te taiao. Māku hoki e tuhituhi i ōku whakaaro i ngā rā katoa.<br><strong>Kaiwhakaako:</strong> He whainga pai ērā. Ka taea e koe te kōrero māmā ake āpōpō?<br><strong>Ākonga:</strong> Āe — e kore e taea e au te kōrero māmā ake ināianei, engari ā tērā wāhanga, ka taea!<br><strong>Kaiwhakaako:</strong> Ko tāu <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=whakapono\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">whakapono</a> tōu ara. Haere tonu!</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>New phrases for goals and planning:<br><strong>Kua mārama ake au ki…</strong> — I now understand more about…<br><strong>rerenga pakiaka</strong> — simple sentences (rerenga = sentence, pakiaka = root/basic)<br><strong>He whainga pai ērā.</strong> — Those are good goals.<br><strong>Ka taea e koe te…</strong> — Are you able to… / You can…<br><strong>Ko tāu whakapono tōu ara.</strong> — Your belief is your path. (whakapono = belief/faith)<br><strong>Haere tonu!</strong> — Keep going! / Press on!<br><strong>māmā ake</strong> — more easily / more fluently (māmā = light/easy, ake = more)</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Tip: Haere tonu! — Keep going! Tonu means &quot;still&quot; or &quot;continuously&quot; — haere tonu = keep travelling. It is one of the most encouraging things you can say in te reo. Use it.</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">We Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Kia Tūhono — Goal-setting conversation</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Build a goals conversation with a partner — one plays kaiwhakaako, one plays ākonga.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Kaiwhakaako: <strong>He pēhea tōu whakaaro mō tōu ako i tēnei wāhanga?</strong> Ākonga answers with Kua mārama ake au ki…</li><li>Kaiwhakaako: <strong>He aha āu whainga mō tērā wāhanga?</strong> Ākonga answers with hiahia and māku e.</li><li>Kaiwhakaako: <strong>Ka taea e koe te [skill] ināianei?</strong> Ākonga answers honestly — Ka taea OR E kore e taea engari…</li><li>Close: Kaiwhakaako says Ko tāu whakapono tōu ara. Haere tonu! Ākonga responds.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Discussion prompt: Ko tāu whakapono tōu ara — your belief is your path. This connects to the Māori concept of whakapono (faith/belief) as something that shapes your direction. How does believing you can do something create the path toward doing it? Can you think of a time your belief opened a path for you?</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">You Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Māu Anō — He whāinga — Your goals</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write and share your Term 3 goals.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Write three hiahia sentences about things you want to be able to do by end of Term 3.</li><li>Write two māku e sentences — things you commit to doing regularly.</li><li>Write what you can already do: Ka taea e au te… (three abilities).</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write a full goals paragraph (8 sentences) — what you have achieved (Kua), what you can do (Ka taea), what you want (hiahia), what you commit to (māku e), what you cannot yet do (E kore e taea), and your path forward (Ko tāu whakapono tōu ara).</li></ul></div></div>"},"Friday: Aromatawai — Quiz & Review":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Monday questions</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>&quot;Kua tae mai ia.&quot; What does this sentence tell us about her arrival?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>She arrived a long time ago </li><li>She arrived and as a result she is now here ✅</li><li>She is going to arrive soon </li><li>She was arriving when something interrupted her </li></ul></li><li>&quot;Kāore anō kia mutu.&quot; What does this mean?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>It has just finished </li><li>It will never finish </li><li>It has not finished yet ✅</li><li>It finished a long time ago </li></ul></li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tuesday questions</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>&quot;Māna e hoko i te miraka.&quot; Who will buy the milk?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>I will </li><li>You will </li><li>He/she will ✅</li><li>They will </li></ul></li><li>&quot;E kore e taea e au te haere āpōpō.&quot; What does this mean?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>I do not want to go tomorrow </li><li>I went yesterday instead </li><li>I will not be able to go tomorrow ✅</li><li>I am going tomorrow reluctantly </li></ul></li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Wednesday questions</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Which marker signals a completed action whose result is relevant right now?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>Kei te </li><li>Ka </li><li>I </li><li>Kua ✅</li></ul></li><li>What does &quot;whakarite&quot; mean?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>to compete </li><li>to arrive </li><li>to prepare / arrange ✅</li><li>to finish </li></ul></li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Thursday questions</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>&quot;Ko tāu whakapono tōu ara.&quot; What does whakapono mean?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>hard work </li><li>belief / faith ✅</li><li>goal </li><li>journey </li></ul></li><li>&quot;Haere tonu!&quot; — What does this expression encourage?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>Go home! </li><li>Stop what you are doing! </li><li>Keep going! / Press on! ✅</li><li>Go slowly! </li></ul></li></ul></div></div>"}}},"Week 3":{"label":"Day","children":{"Monday: Nō hea koe? — Origin, iwi, hapū, and identity":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Te Aho Arataki Marau — AO 1.4: Personal information — iwi, hapū, maunga, awa · AO 2.1: Relationships</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Objectives</th><td class=\"lt-td\">AO 1.4 — Identity — iwi &amp; hapū; AO 2.1 — Community relationships</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">I Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tāua Kōrero — Deeper identity: where you truly come from</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>In Week 4 of Term 1 you learned the basic pepeha — Ko [mountain] tōku maunga, Ko [river] tōku awa, Ko [name] tōku ingoa. Today we go deeper. We explore what iwi and hapū really mean, why they matter, and how to talk about community identity in te reo Māori.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\"><strong>Ko Tūhoe tōku <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=iwi\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">iwi</a>.</strong><br>Tūhoe is my tribe/people.<br><br><strong>Ko Ngāti Awa tōku <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=hap%C5%AB\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">hapū</a>.</strong><br>Ngāti Awa is my sub-tribe.<br><br><strong>Ko te Kāhui Ariki tōku whānau pani.</strong><br>The royal family is my extended family of guardians.<br><br><strong>Nō reira ko ahau ko [name], nō [place], nō [iwi].</strong><br>Therefore I am [name], from [place], of [iwi].<br><br><strong>He <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=uri\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">uri</a> ahau nō [ancestor].</strong><br>I am a descendant of [ancestor].</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Identity vocabulary:<br><strong>iwi</strong> — tribe / people (literally: bones — the framework that holds a people together)<br><strong>hapū</strong> — sub-tribe / pregnant (the sub-group that gives birth to new community)<br><strong>uri</strong> — descendant / offspring<br><strong>tīpuna / tupuna</strong> — ancestor / grandparent<br><strong>whakapapa</strong> — genealogy / to layer (whaka = to make, papa = layer/foundation)<br><strong>tangata whenua</strong> — the people of the land (the indigenous people)<br><strong>manuhiri</strong> — visitor / guest<br><strong>Nō reira</strong> — therefore / and so (as a conclusion)</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The full pepeha structure:</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Ko [maunga] tōku maunga.</li><li>Ko [awa] tōku awa.</li><li>Ko [waka] tōku waka.</li><li>Ko [iwi] tōku iwi.</li><li>Ko [hapū] tōku hapū.</li><li>Ko [marae] tōku marae.</li><li>Ko [school] tōku kura.</li><li>Ko [name] tōku ingoa.</li><li>Nō [place] ahau.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Tip: Whakapapa literally means &quot;to layer things&quot; — it is the layering of generations, one on top of another, connecting you to your beginnings. When you say your whakapapa, you are saying where you come from — not just geographically, but in every sense.</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">We Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Kia Tūhono — The full pepeha</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Build your full pepeha together. Each student adds one line.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Start: Ko [your nearest mountain or landmark] tōku maunga. Ko [your nearest river] tōku awa.</li><li>Add iwi/hapū if known: Ko [iwi] tōku iwi. Ko [hapū] tōku hapū. (If not known: Ko [school region] tōku whenua.)</li><li>Add marae and school: Ko [marae] tōku marae. Ko [school name] tōku kura.</li><li>Close: Ko [your name] tōku ingoa. Nō [your town] ahau. Nō reira ko ahau.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Discussion prompt: Iwi means &quot;bones&quot; — the framework of a people. Hapū means &quot;pregnant&quot; — the sub-group that creates new life. Why do you think Māori used these body words for social structures? What does it tell us about how Māori thought about community?</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">You Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Māu Anō — Tōu pepeha</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write and practise your full pepeha.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Write all nine lines of your pepeha. Research any lines you do not know.</li><li>Practise saying it aloud until it flows without looking at the page.</li><li>Say your pepeha to a partner. They listen and then say theirs.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write a paragraph (6 sentences) explaining what each line of your pepeha means to you personally. Why does it matter that your maunga is named? What does connecting to land in an introduction say about Māori values?</li></ul></div></div>"},"Tuesday: Te marae — The marae and its protocol":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Te Aho Arataki Marau — AO 1.4: Community · AO 1.6: Politeness and protocol · AO 2.1: Relationships</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Objectives</th><td class=\"lt-td\">AO 1.4 — Community &amp; marae; AO 1.6 — Protocol &amp; tikanga</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">I Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tāua Kōrero — The marae: home of the people</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The marae is the heart of Māori community life. It is where people gather for pōhiri (formal welcomes), tangi (funerals), hui (meetings), and celebration. Today we learn the language of the marae — what the parts are called, what happens there, and how to talk about it respectfully.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\"><strong>Parts of the marae:</strong><br><strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=marae\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">marae</a> <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=%C4%81tea\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">ātea</a></strong> — the open space in front of the wharenui (sacred space for formal encounter)<br><strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=wharenui\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">wharenui</a></strong> — the meeting house (the large house where people sleep and gather)<br><strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=wharekai\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">wharekai</a></strong> — dining room / food house<br><strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=wharepaku\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">wharepaku</a></strong> — toilet facilities (paku = small, whare paku = small house)<br><strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=waharoa\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">waharoa</a></strong> — gateway / entrance<br><br><strong>What happens at the marae:</strong><br><strong>Ka haere mātou ki te marae.</strong> We are going to the marae.<br><strong>Ka <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=p%C5%8Dhiritia\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">pōhiritia</a> mātou e ngā <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=tangata+whenua\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">tangata whenua</a>.</strong> We were welcomed by the hosts.<br><strong>Ka <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=whaikōrero\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">whaikōrero</a> ngā <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=rangatira\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">rangatira</a>.</strong> The leaders gave speeches.<br><strong>Ka waiata tātou.</strong> We all sang.</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Marae protocol vocabulary:<br><strong>pōhiri</strong> — formal welcome ceremony<br><strong>karanga</strong> — the calling (women's ceremonial call of welcome)<br><strong>whaikōrero</strong> — formal speech (whai = to pursue, kōrero = speech)<br><strong>haka</strong> — dance / challenge<br><strong>hongi</strong> — pressing of noses (sharing the breath of life)<br><strong>tangata whenua</strong> — hosts (the people of the land)<br><strong>manuhiri</strong> — guests / visitors<br><strong>Ka pōhiritia mātou.</strong> — We were welcomed. (passive)<br><strong>Ka whaikōrero ngā rangatira.</strong> — The leaders gave speeches.<br><strong>me noho</strong> — we should sit / must sit (protocol instruction)</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Tip: On the marae, women lead through karanga (calling) and men lead through whaikōrero (speech). Both are essential. Neither is less important. The marae is a place of equal and complementary roles.</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">We Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Kia Tūhono — Marae language</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Practise marae vocabulary in sentences.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Parts of marae: Point and name — wharenui, marae ātea, wharekai, waharoa.</li><li>Describe an action: <strong>Ka karanga ngā wāhine.</strong> The women called. <strong>Ka whaikōrero ngā tāne.</strong> The men spoke.</li><li>Passive: <strong>Ka pōhiritia mātou e ngā tangata whenua.</strong> We were welcomed by the hosts.</li><li>Protocol instruction: <strong>Me noho tātou ināianei.</strong> We should all sit now.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Discussion prompt: Ka pōhiritia mātou — we were welcomed. The passive voice here makes the welcome something that HAPPENED TO US — we received it. In te ao Māori, welcoming is a gift given by hosts to guests. How does the passive voice capture that relationship better than &quot;the hosts welcomed us&quot;?</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">You Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Māu Anō — Marae recount</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write about a marae visit — real or imagined.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Write what happened using Ka sentences: Ka haere mātou… Ka pōhiritia mātou… Ka waiata tātou…</li><li>Name three parts of the marae using Ko ēnei: Ko ēnei ngā wāhanga o te marae: he wharenui, he wharekai, he marae ātea.</li><li>Write a me sentence about marae protocol: Me [action] tātou i te marae.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write a full marae visit recount (8 sentences) — where you went, how you were welcomed (passive), what happened (Ka sequence), what you saw (Ko ēnei list), and how you felt (emotion vocabulary).</li></ul></div></div>"},"Wednesday: Hanga rerenga — Community and identity in extended writing":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Te Aho Arataki Marau — AO 1.4: Personal information · AO 2.1: Community · AO 2.3: Extended writing</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Objectives</th><td class=\"lt-td\">AO 1.4 — Identity writing; AO 2.1 — Community in writing</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">I Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tāua Kōrero — Writing about who you are and where you belong</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Today we combine the pepeha, the marae, and community vocabulary into extended writing. This is the kind of te reo writing you might produce for a school presentation, a wall display, or an end-of-year portfolio.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\"><strong>Ko Aoraki tōku maunga, ko Waitaki tōku awa.</strong><br><strong>Ko Ngāi Tahu tōku iwi, ko Ngāti Māmoe tōku hapū.</strong><br><strong>Nō Ōtautahi ahau.</strong><br><strong>Ko au ko [name].</strong><br><br>I tōku ao, he nui ngā āhuatanga pai — ko tōku whānau, ko ōku hoa, ko tōku <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=kura\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">kura</a>.<br>Ko ēnei ngā mea e mau ana ki a au: he aroha o tōku whānau, he kaha o ōku hoa, he <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=m%C4%81ramatanga\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">māramatanga</a> o tōku kura.<br>He uri ahau nō ngā tīpuna nō reira he <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=kawenga\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">kawenga</a> tōku — me tiaki au i tēnei reo, i tēnei <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=tikanga\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">tikanga</a>.</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>New vocabulary for identity writing:<br><strong>e mau ana ki a au</strong> — that hold fast to me / that I hold dear<br><strong>māramatanga</strong> — understanding / enlightenment / clarity<br><strong>kawenga</strong> — responsibility / obligation / burden<br><strong>tikanga</strong> — correct practice / custom / culture<br><strong>He uri ahau nō ngā tīpuna.</strong> — I am a descendant of the ancestors.<br><strong>nō reira he kawenga tōku</strong> — therefore I have a responsibility<br><strong>me tiaki au i tēnei reo</strong> — I should/must care for this language</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Structure for identity writing:</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Pepeha (mountain, river, iwi, hapū, place, name)</li><li>What matters to you (Ko ēnei ngā mea e mau ana ki a au)</li><li>Your identity statement (He uri ahau nō…)</li><li>Your responsibility (Nō reira he kawenga tōku — me [action])</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Tip: Me tiaki au i tēnei reo — I must care for this language. This is not just a sentence — it is a commitment. When you write it in your own pepeha, you are making te reo Māori part of your identity.</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">We Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Kia Tūhono — Extended identity writing</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>We write an extended identity piece together, sentence by sentence.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Pepeha: Ko [maunga] tōku maunga. Ko [awa] tōku awa. Ko [name] tōku ingoa. Nō [place] ahau.</li><li>What matters: Ko ēnei ngā mea e mau ana ki a au: he [noun], he [noun], he [noun].</li><li>Identity statement: He uri ahau nō ōku tīpuna nō reira…</li><li>Responsibility close: Me tiaki au i tēnei reo, i tēnei tikanga, i tēnei ao.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Discussion prompt: Me tiaki au i tēnei reo, i tēnei tikanga, i tēnei ao — I must care for this language, this culture, this world. Three parallel i phrases. What is the effect of that repetition? Why does saying three things in parallel make a stronger statement than just saying one?</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">You Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Māu Anō — Identity writing</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write your own extended identity piece in te reo Māori.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Write your full pepeha (all nine lines).</li><li>Write Ko ēnei ngā mea e mau ana ki a au — three things you hold dear.</li><li>Write your identity statement: He uri ahau nō…</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write your full identity piece (10 sentences) — pepeha, what matters, identity statement, and responsibility. Use: Ko, He, Kua (what you have become), hiahia, and me tiaki.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Thursday: Kōrero — He pōhiri — A welcome ceremony":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Te Aho Arataki Marau — AO 1.1: Greetings · AO 1.4: Community · AO 1.6: Protocol · AO 2.1: Relationships</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Objectives</th><td class=\"lt-td\">AO 1.1 — Formal greetings &amp; welcome; AO 1.6 — Marae protocol</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">I Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tāua Kōrero — Experiencing a pōhiri</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Today we experience the language of a pōhiri — a formal welcome ceremony. You will learn the key phrases, the structure, and what each part means. This is among the most important cultural language in te ao Māori.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\"><strong>THE &lt;A CLASS=&quot;TE-AKA-LINK&quot; HREF=&quot;HTTPS://MAORIDICTIONARY.CO.NZ/SEARCH?KEYWORDS=KARANGA&quot; TARGET=&quot;_BLANK&quot; REL=&quot;NOOPENER&quot;&gt;KARANGA&lt;/A&gt; (call of welcome — women):</strong><br><strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=Haere+mai\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Haere mai</a>, haere mai, haere mai!</strong><br>Come, come, come! (Repeated three times — three is completion)<br><br><strong>THE &lt;B&gt;&lt;A CLASS=&quot;TE-AKA-LINK&quot; HREF=&quot;HTTPS://MAORIDICTIONARY.CO.NZ/SEARCH?KEYWORDS=WHAIKŌRERO&quot; TARGET=&quot;_BLANK&quot; REL=&quot;NOOPENER&quot;&gt;WHAIKŌRERO&lt;/A&gt;&lt;/B&gt; (formal speech — opens and closes with):</strong><br><strong>Tēnā koutou, tēnā koutou, tēnā koutou katoa.</strong><br><strong>Ko [mountain] tōku maunga…</strong> (pepeha lines)<br><strong>Nō reira, tēnā koutou, tēnā koutou, tēnā koutou katoa.</strong><br><br><strong>THE WAIATA (song following each speech):</strong><br>After each whaikōrero, a waiata is sung by that person's group.<br><br><strong>THE &lt;B&gt;&lt;A CLASS=&quot;TE-AKA-LINK&quot; HREF=&quot;HTTPS://MAORIDICTIONARY.CO.NZ/SEARCH?KEYWORDS=HONGI&quot; TARGET=&quot;_BLANK&quot; REL=&quot;NOOPENER&quot;&gt;HONGI&lt;/A&gt;&lt;/B&gt; (greeting):</strong><br><strong>Ka <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=hongi\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">hongi</a> tātou.</strong> — We will press noses.<br><strong>Ko te hongi he <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=tohu\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">tohu</a> o te <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=aroha\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">aroha</a>.</strong> — The hongi is a sign of love/compassion.</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Pōhiri structure:</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>Karanga</strong> — women call the guests onto the marae ātea</li><li><strong>Whaikōrero</strong> — formal speeches by hosts then guests (alternating)</li><li><strong>Waiata</strong> — song after each speech</li><li><strong>Hongi</strong> — pressing of noses to seal the welcome</li><li><strong>Kai</strong> — sharing of food (the division between sacred and everyday is lifted)</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>After the hongi, manuhiri (guests) become tangata whenua (people of the place) — the distinction ends. This is profound: welcoming someone makes them part of you.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Key phrase:<br><strong>Nau mai, haere mai, tū mai, noho mai.</strong><br>Come, come, stand here, sit here. (full welcome phrase)</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Tip: Haere mai × 3 — three repetitions. In te ao Māori, saying something three times is a sign of completion and sincerity. Tēnā koutou × 3 at the end of a speech. The hongi shared three times. Three is the number of fullness.</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">We Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Kia Tūhono — Pōhiri practice</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Class performs a mini pōhiri — one group as tangata whenua, one as manuhiri.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Tangata whenua: Karanga — Haere mai, haere mai, haere mai! (call three times)</li><li>Tangata whenua whaikōrero: Tēnā koutou katoa. Ko [student pepeha lines]. Nō reira, tēnā koutou ×3.</li><li>Manuhiri respond: Their whaikōrero — Ko [their pepeha]. Tēnā koutou ×3.</li><li>Close: Ka hongi tātou. Ko te hongi he tohu o te aroha. Students share the hongi.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Discussion prompt: After the hongi, manuhiri become tangata whenua — guests become people of the place. The distinction between &quot;us&quot; and &quot;them&quot; ends. What does this say about the Māori philosophy of welcome? How is this different from how welcome is understood in other contexts you know?</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">You Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Māu Anō — He whaikōrero</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write your own short whaikōrero to deliver in a pōhiri.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Write your opening: Tēnā koutou katoa.</li><li>Write your pepeha — all nine lines.</li><li>Write two sentences about why you are here and what you bring: I haere mai ahau ki [reason]. Ka kawe mai ahau i [something you bring].</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write and practise your full whaikōrero (10–12 sentences). Open with Tēnā koutou katoa, give your pepeha, say why you are here, what you bring, what you hope for, and close with Nō reira, tēnā koutou, tēnā koutou, tēnā koutou katoa.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Friday: Aromatawai — Quiz & Review":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Monday questions</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>What does &quot;uri&quot; mean?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>ancestor </li><li>descendant / offspring ✅</li><li>cousin </li><li>elder </li></ul></li><li>&quot;He uri ahau nō ngā tīpuna.&quot; What does tīpuna mean?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>children </li><li>cousins </li><li>ancestors / grandparents ✅</li><li>tribe </li></ul></li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tuesday questions</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>What is the marae ātea?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>The dining room </li><li>The gateway </li><li>The open space in front of the wharenui — sacred space for formal encounter ✅</li><li>The sleeping area in the meeting house </li></ul></li><li>&quot;Ka pōhiritia mātou e ngā tangata whenua.&quot; What grammatical feature makes this sentence notable?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>It uses Ka for future tense </li><li>It uses the passive — we received the welcome from the hosts ✅</li><li>It uses an object marker i </li><li>It uses Kua for completed action </li></ul></li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Wednesday questions</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>What does &quot;kawenga&quot; mean?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>ancestor </li><li>mountain </li><li>responsibility / obligation ✅</li><li>genealogy </li></ul></li><li>&quot;Me tiaki au i tēnei reo, i tēnei tikanga, i tēnei ao.&quot; What literary technique uses the three parallel i phrases?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>Alliteration </li><li>Simile </li><li>Parallel structure / anaphora for emphasis ✅</li><li>Personification </li></ul></li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Thursday questions</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>What happens after the hongi in a pōhiri?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>The guests leave the marae </li><li>The manuhiri become tangata whenua — the distinction between guests and hosts ends ✅</li><li>The hosts perform a haka </li><li>The speeches begin </li></ul></li><li>Why is something said or done three times in te ao Māori protocol?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>Because three people must always agree </li><li>Three repetitions signal completion and sincerity ✅</li><li>It is just a tradition with no meaning </li><li>To allow everyone to hear clearly </li></ul></li></ul></div></div>"}}},"Week 4":{"label":"Day","children":{"Monday: Ngā kupu hou o te kura — School vocabulary":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Te Aho Arataki Marau — AO 1.7: Classroom language · AO 2.3: Extended communication · Grammar Level 1–2: School context vocabulary</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Objectives</th><td class=\"lt-td\">AO 1.7 — School &amp; classroom language; AO 2.3 — School communication</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">I Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tāua Kōrero — School life in te reo Māori</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>AO 1.7 asks us to use and respond to classroom language. Today we build a rich school vocabulary so that more and more of your school day can happen in te reo Māori. The goal is for te reo to move from the classroom into the corridor, the playground, and the staffroom.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\"><strong>SCHOOL PLACES:</strong><br><strong>akomanga</strong> — classroom<br><strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=tari\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">tari</a></strong> — office<br><strong>whare <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=t%C4%81karo\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">tākaro</a></strong> — gymnasium / sports room<br><strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=papa\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">papa</a> tākaro</strong> — playground<br><strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=p%C4%81taka\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">pātaka</a></strong> — library / resource room (literally: storehouse)<br><strong>kaukau</strong> — swimming pool (also: to swim — same word)<br><br><strong>SCHOOL ACTIVITIES:</strong><br><strong>Kei te ako mātou.</strong> — We are learning.<br><strong>Kei te <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=waihanga\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">waihanga</a> mātou.</strong> — We are creating/building.<br><strong>Kei te <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=rangahau\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">rangahau</a> mātou.</strong> — We are researching.<br><strong>Kei te whakaaro mātou.</strong> — We are thinking.<br><strong>Kei te <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=taunakitia\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">taunakitia</a> mātou.</strong> — We are being assessed.</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>School subject names:<br><strong>pāngarau</strong> — maths<br><strong>pānui</strong> — reading<br><strong>tuhituhi</strong> — writing<br><strong>akoranga reo</strong> — language lesson<br><strong>akoranga pūtaiao</strong> — science (pūtaiao = science/technology)<br><strong>akoranga tikanga</strong> — social studies / cultural studies<br><strong>akoranga hākinakina</strong> — PE / sports lesson<br><strong>akoranga toi</strong> — art (toi = art/creativity)<br><strong>akoranga puoro</strong> — music (puoro = music)</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Classroom commands (AO 1.7):<br><strong>Tū mai!</strong> — Stand up!<br><strong>Noho mai!</strong> — Sit down!<br><strong>Titiro mai!</strong> — Look this way!<br><strong>Whakarongo mai!</strong> — Listen!<br><strong>Āta whakaaro!</strong> — Think carefully!<br><strong>Mahi tahi!</strong> — Work together!</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Tip: Akoranga = lesson. Put any subject after it: akoranga pāngarau (maths lesson), akoranga toi (art lesson). You can create any school subject name with this word.</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">We Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Kia Tūhono — School day in te reo</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Describe your school day in te reo Māori — places, activities, subjects.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Where are you? <strong>Kei te akomanga au.</strong> Kei te pātaka ngā ākonga. Kei te papa tākaro rātou.</li><li>What subject? <strong>Kei te ako mātou i te pāngarau.</strong> I te akoranga toi tātou.</li><li>Command and response: Teacher calls <strong>Whakarongo mai!</strong> — class responds by listening. Teacher says <strong>Mahi tahi!</strong> — class works in pairs.</li><li>Report: <strong>I te Rāapa, i ako mātou i te akoranga pūtaiao — i ātaahua ngā hua o ā mātou rangahau.</strong></li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Discussion prompt: If your school moved to using te reo Māori for all classroom commands — Tū mai, Noho mai, Whakarongo mai — how do you think that would change the feel of the classroom? What would students need to make that work? What would teachers need?</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">You Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Māu Anō — School day recount</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write your school day in te reo Māori.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Write your timetable for today — each subject using akoranga [subject].</li><li>Write where you are in each lesson: Kei te [place] mātou i te [subject].</li><li>Write three things you did using past tense: I te [time], i [activity] mātou i te [subject].</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write a full school day recount (8 sentences) — every lesson, where you were, what you did, and how you felt about each one. Use Kei te, I, Ka, and E…ana at least once each.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Tuesday: He akoranga pai — What makes a good lesson":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Te Aho Arataki Marau — AO 2.3: Extended opinions · AO 1.7: School language · AO 2.5: Evaluative language</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Objectives</th><td class=\"lt-td\">AO 2.3 — Opinion &amp; evaluation; AO 1.7 — School context</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">I Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tāua Kōrero — Evaluating and giving opinions about learning</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Today we use evaluative language to talk about what makes learning good — what works, what doesn't, and what you prefer. This connects AO 2.3 (extended opinions) with AO 1.7 (school language).</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\"><strong>He akoranga pai te akoranga Rāhina nō te mea…</strong><br>Monday's lesson was a good lesson because…<br><br><strong>He pai ake te <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=mahi+tahi\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">mahi tahi</a> i te mahi <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=kotahi\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">kotahi</a> ki a au.</strong><br>Working together is better than working alone in my opinion.<br><br><strong>Ko tāku whakaaro, he nui ake ngā hua o te <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=k%C5%8Drero\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">kōrero</a> i te <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=tuhi\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">tuhi</a>.</strong><br>My opinion is that speaking has more benefits than writing.<br><br><strong>I uaua ki a au te [subject] nō te mea…engari kua mārama ake au.</strong><br>I found [subject] difficult because… but I now understand better.</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Evaluative language in te reo:</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>He akoranga pai/kino tērā.</strong> — That was a good/bad lesson.<br><strong>Ko tāku whakaaro…</strong> — In my opinion / My thought is…<br><strong>I uaua ki a au…</strong> — I found it difficult… (personal evaluation)<br><strong>I ngāwari ki a au…</strong> — I found it easy… (ngāwari = easy)<br><strong>Ka taea e au te…</strong> — I am able to… (showing learning)<br><strong>He nui ake ngā hua o te X i te Y.</strong> — X has more benefits than Y.<br><strong>He pai ake te X i te Y ki a au.</strong> — X is better than Y for me.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>New words:<br><strong>ngāwari</strong> — easy / simple<br><strong>hua</strong> — benefit / result (you've seen this before)<br><strong>mahi tahi</strong> — working together (collaborative work)<br><strong>mahi kotahi</strong> — working alone (kotahi = one/alone)</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Tip: Ko tāku whakaaro = my thought/opinion. Use this whenever you want to signal that what follows is your personal view. It is the te reo equivalent of &quot;I think&quot; or &quot;in my opinion.&quot;</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">We Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Kia Tūhono — Evaluate your learning</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Use evaluative language to assess your own learning.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Pick a lesson from this week: <strong>He akoranga pai tērā nō te mea…</strong> Why was it good?</li><li>Compare two ways of learning: <strong>He pai ake te mahi tahi i te mahi kotahi ki a au nō te mea…</strong></li><li>Self-evaluate: <strong>I uaua ki a au te [skill/topic] engari kua mārama ake au nō te mea…</strong></li><li>State what you can do now: <strong>Ka taea e au te [skill] ināianei — kāore i taea e au i tērā wiki.</strong></li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Discussion prompt: Ko tāku whakaaro, he nui ake ngā hua o te kōrero i te tuhi. Do you agree? Is speaking more valuable than writing for language learning? Or is it the opposite? Defend your view in te reo using He pai ake… nō te mea…</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">You Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Māu Anō — Learning evaluation</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write an honest evaluation of your learning this term.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Write He akoranga pai/uaua tērā nō te mea about one lesson from this week.</li><li>Write He pai ake te X i te Y ki a au about two ways of learning.</li><li>Write I uaua ki a au te [topic] engari kua mārama ake au.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write a full learning evaluation paragraph (7 sentences) — what you have learned this term, what was hard, what was easy, what you prefer, what you can do now that you couldn't before, and what your next step is. Use evaluative language throughout.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Wednesday: Hanga rerenga — School, community, and identity together":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Te Aho Arataki Marau — AO 1.4: Identity · AO 1.7: School language · AO 2.1: Community · AO 2.3: Extended writing</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Objectives</th><td class=\"lt-td\">AO 1.4 — Identity in school context; AO 2.1 — School community</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">I Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tāua Kōrero — School as community: writing about your place</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Today we combine the school vocabulary, identity language, and community structures into extended writing about what your school means to you and your community. This is the kind of writing that goes on the wall, into a portfolio, or is shared with whānau.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\"><strong>Ko tōku kura ko [school name].</strong><br>My school is [name].<br><br><strong>He kura ātaahua tōku kura — kei reira ōku hoa, ōku kaiako, ōku mōhiotanga katoa.</strong><br>My school is a beautiful school — my friends, my teachers, my entire knowledge are there.<br><br><strong>I tōku kura, kei te ako mātou i ngā mea katoa — te reo Māori, te pāngarau, te toi, te hākinakina.</strong><br>At my school, we learn everything — te reo Māori, maths, art, sport.<br><br><strong>Ko tōku kura he wāhi <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=tiaki\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">tiaki</a> mōku — ka <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=tipu\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">tipu</a> ōku whakaaro, ka tipu hoki tōku ngākau.</strong><br>My school is a place of nurturing for me — my thoughts grow, my heart grows too.</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>New community and school vocabulary:<br><strong>mōhiotanga</strong> — knowledge / understanding (mōhio + tanga)<br><strong>wāhi tiaki</strong> — place of nurturing / safe place (wāhi = place, tiaki = guard/care)<br><strong>ka tipu</strong> — grows (Ka for narrative/ongoing)<br><strong>i tōku kura</strong> — at my school<br><strong>kei reira</strong> — there / there it is (referring back)<br><strong>ko tōku kura he…</strong> — my school is a… (He sentence about identity)</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Structure for extended school writing:</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Identity: Ko tōku kura ko [name].</li><li>Description: He [adjective] tōku kura nō te mea…</li><li>What happens there: I tōku kura, kei te ako mātou i…</li><li>What it means: Ko tōku kura he wāhi… mōku.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Tip: Ko tōku kura he wāhi tiaki mōku — my school is a place of nurturing for me. Wāhi tiaki is a beautiful phrase — a place that guards and cares. What words would you use to describe your school as a place?</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">We Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Kia Tūhono — School identity writing</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Build a school identity piece together.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Identity line: Ko tōku kura ko [school name]. Add a He description: He [adjective] tōku kura.</li><li>Community line: Ko ēnei ngā tāngata o tōku kura: he kaiako [X], he ākonga [X]…</li><li>What happens: I tōku kura kei te ako mātou i [list of subjects].</li><li>Meaning line: Ko tōku kura he wāhi [word] mōku nō te mea…</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Discussion prompt: Ka tipu ōku whakaaro, ka tipu hoki tōku ngākau — my thoughts grow, my heart grows too. What does it mean for a school to grow both whakaaro (thoughts/intellect) and ngākau (heart/emotion)? What makes a school do both well?</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">You Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Māu Anō — School identity piece</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write your own extended school identity piece.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Write your school identity opening: Ko tōku kura ko [name]. He [adjective] tōku kura.</li><li>Write what happens there: Ko ēnei ngā mea pai o tōku kura… (Ko ēnei list).</li><li>Write what your school means: Ko tōku kura he wāhi [word] mōku nō te mea…</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write your full school identity piece (10 sentences) — identity, description, community, what happens, what you learn, what it means to you, and close with Ka tipu ōku whakaaro, ka tipu hoki tōku ngākau i tōku kura.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Thursday: Kōrero — He taumahatanga — Challenges and resilience":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Te Aho Arataki Marau — AO 2.3: Extended communication · AO 2.5: Evaluative vocabulary · AO 1.6: Discussing challenges</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Objectives</th><td class=\"lt-td\">AO 2.3 — Resilience &amp; challenge conversation; AO 2.5 — Evaluative vocabulary</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">I Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tāua Kōrero — Talking about challenges and getting through them</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Every learner faces challenges. Today we learn how to talk about challenges, struggles, and resilience in te reo Māori — an important set of language skills for real conversation.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\"><strong>A:</strong> He pēhea tōu ako i tēnei wāhanga?<br><strong>B:</strong> I uaua ki a au i te tīmatanga — kāore au e mārama ana ki ngā hanganga katoa.<br><strong>A:</strong> He aha te mea i uaua rawa atu ki a koe?<br><strong>B:</strong> Ko te Kua — e kore au e mōhio ana ko ēhea wā me whakamahi ai i tēnā.<br><strong>A:</strong> Āe, he uaua tēnā. He aha āu i mahi ai hei āwhina i a koe?<br><strong>B:</strong> I pātai au ki tōku kaiako — i āwhina ia i a au. Kua mārama ake au ināianei.<br><strong>A:</strong> Ka pai tōu māia! Ko tērā te ara o te <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=%C4%81konga\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">ākonga</a> pai — me <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=p%C4%81tai\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">pātai</a>, me mahi, me āta whakaaro.<br><strong>B:</strong> Āe. Haere tonu!</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Resilience vocabulary:<br><strong>taumahatanga</strong> — challenge / burden (taumaha = heavy)<br><strong>māia</strong> — brave / bold / resilient<br><strong>āwhina</strong> — help / support<br><strong>pātai</strong> — to ask a question (also: question)<br><strong>He aha āu i mahi ai hei āwhina i a koe?</strong><br>What did you do to help yourself?<br><strong>hei āwhina i a koe</strong> — to help yourself (hei = for the purpose of)<br><strong>Ko tērā te ara o te ākonga pai.</strong><br>That is the path of a good student.<br><strong>me pātai, me mahi, me āta whakaaro</strong><br>must ask, must work, must think carefully<br><strong>e kore au e mōhio ana ko ēhea wā me whakamahi ai</strong><br>I don't know which times I should use it</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Tip: Me pātai, me mahi, me āta whakaaro — three me instructions for a good learner. Ask, work, think carefully. These three together describe almost everything it takes to learn well in any language.</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">We Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Kia Tūhono — Resilience conversation</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Build a conversation about a challenge you have faced and how you got through it.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I ask: <strong>He aha te mea i uaua rawa atu ki a koe i tēnei wāhanga?</strong> Answer honestly.</li><li>I ask: <strong>He aha āu i mahi ai hei āwhina i a koe?</strong> Answer with what you did: I pātai au ki… / I mahi au… / I āta whakaaro au.</li><li>I confirm your progress: <strong>Kua mārama ake koe ināianei.</strong> You agree or add more.</li><li>Close with the ākonga pai formula: <strong>Ko tērā te ara o te ākonga pai — me pātai, me mahi, me āta whakaaro.</strong></li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Discussion prompt: Ko tērā te ara o te ākonga pai — me pātai, me mahi, me āta whakaaro. Three things: ask, work, think carefully. Is there anything missing from this list? What would YOU add as a fourth quality of a good learner? Can you say it in te reo?</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">You Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Māu Anō — Tōu taumahatanga</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write about a challenge you faced and how you got through it.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Name the challenge: He [noun] tōku taumahatanga — I uaua ki a au te [topic/skill].</li><li>Describe what you did: I pātai au ki… / I mahi au… / I āta whakaaro au.</li><li>Show your progress: Kua mārama ake au ināianei nō te mea…</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write a full resilience account (8 sentences) — name the challenge, when it started, how it felt (emotion vocabulary), what you did about it (three me instructions), who helped, what changed, and how you feel now. Close with Haere tonu!</li></ul></div></div>"},"Friday: Aromatawai — Quiz & Review":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Monday questions</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>What does &quot;pātaka&quot; mean in a school context?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>gymnasium </li><li>playground </li><li>library / resource room (storehouse) ✅</li><li>office </li></ul></li><li>&quot;Kei te rangahau mātou.&quot; What activity are they doing?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>creating / building </li><li>thinking </li><li>researching ✅</li><li>being assessed </li></ul></li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tuesday questions</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>What does &quot;ngāwari&quot; mean?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>difficult </li><li>easy / simple ✅</li><li>interesting </li><li>boring </li></ul></li><li>&quot;He pai ake te mahi tahi i te mahi kotahi ki a au.&quot; What is being compared?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>Two different school subjects </li><li>Working together versus working alone ✅</li><li>Two different teachers </li><li>Morning lessons versus afternoon lessons </li></ul></li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Wednesday questions</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>What does &quot;wāhi tiaki&quot; mean?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>a place to play </li><li>a place of learning </li><li>a place of nurturing / safe place ✅</li><li>a place to eat </li></ul></li><li>&quot;Ka tipu ōku whakaaro, ka tipu hoki tōku ngākau.&quot; What two things are growing?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>The student's height and their strength </li><li>Knowledge/thoughts and heart/emotion ✅</li><li>The school and the community </li><li>Friends and teachers </li></ul></li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Thursday questions</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>What does &quot;āwhina&quot; mean?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>challenge </li><li>burden </li><li>ask a question </li><li>help / support ✅</li></ul></li><li>&quot;Me pātai, me mahi, me āta whakaaro.&quot; What are the three qualities of a good learner?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>Listen, write, remember </li><li>Ask, work, think carefully ✅</li><li>Read, speak, practise </li><li>Study, sleep, repeat </li></ul></li></ul></div></div>"}}},"Week 5":{"label":"Day","children":{"Monday: Ngā kupu āhua — Adverbs of degree and intensity":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Te Aho Arataki Marau — AO 2.5: Descriptive language · Grammar Level 1–2: Degree adverbs tino, āhua, noa, rawa atu, atu, mai</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Objectives</th><td class=\"lt-td\">AO 2.5 — Degree adverbs &amp; intensity; AO 2.3 — Expressive intensity</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">I Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tāua Kōrero — How much, how intense, how degree</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>You know tino (very) and ake/rawa atu (more/most). Today we look at the full range of degree and intensity words in te reo Māori — the words that control how strong a statement feels.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\"><strong>tino</strong> — very / truly<br><strong>He tino pai tēnei.</strong> — This is very good.<br><br><strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=%C4%81hua\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">āhua</a></strong> — somewhat / quite / rather / quite / rather<br><strong>He āhua uaua tēnei.</strong> — This is quite difficult.<br><br><strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=noa\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">noa</a></strong> — just / merely / freely (reduces intensity or shows unconstrained action) / freely (reduces intensity or shows unconstrained action)<br><strong>Ka haere noa ia.</strong> — She just went / she wandered freely.<br><br><strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=tonu\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">tonu</a></strong> — still / continuously / right (emphasises continuity) / right (emphasises continuity)<br><strong>Kei te mahi tonu ia.</strong> — She is still working.<br><br><strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=an%C5%8D\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">anō</a></strong> — again / still / also (adds repetition or equivalence) / also (adds repetition or equivalence)<br><strong>Ka haere anō mātou.</strong> — We will go again.<br><br><strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=k%C4%93\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">kē</a></strong> — indeed / actually / on the contrary (emphasises or corrects) / on the contrary (emphasises or corrects)<br><strong>He pai kē tēnei!</strong> — This is actually really good! / This is even better!</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The degree adverb toolkit:</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>tino</strong> [adjective/verb] = very [adj]<br><strong>āhua</strong> [adjective] = somewhat/quite [adj]<br><strong>[verb] noa</strong> = just [verb] / freely [verb]<br><strong>[verb] tonu</strong> = still [verb] / continuously [verb]<br><strong>[verb] anō</strong> = [verb] again<br><strong>[adjective/verb] kē</strong> = actually [adj] / on the contrary<br><strong>[verb] atu / mai</strong> = [verb] away from me / toward me</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Examples:<br><strong>Ka kai noa ia.</strong> — She just eats (freely, casually).<br><strong>He pai tonu tēnei.</strong> — This is still good / continuously good.<br><strong>Ka hoki anō ia.</strong> — She came back again.<br><strong>He pai kē atu tēnei i tērā!</strong> — This is even better than that!</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>New word:<br><strong>Ka <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=wani\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">wani</a> kē!</strong> — That is amazing! (wani = excellent, kē = indeed)</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Tip: Kē adds a &quot;contrary to expectations&quot; feel — &quot;He pai kē!&quot; means &quot;it is actually good!&quot; (you might not have expected it). Ka wani kē! = That is amazing! (even more than expected). Use kē when something surprises you positively.</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">We Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Kia Tūhono — Degree challenge</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>I give you a plain sentence — you add a degree adverb and say how the meaning changes.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>&quot;He pai tēnei.&quot; Add tino → <strong>He tino pai tēnei.</strong> Add āhua → <strong>He āhua pai tēnei.</strong> What changed?</li><li>&quot;Kei te mahi ia.&quot; Add tonu → <strong>Kei te mahi tonu ia.</strong> What does tonu add?</li><li>&quot;Ka haere ia.&quot; Add noa → <strong>Ka haere noa ia.</strong> Add anō → <strong>Ka haere anō ia.</strong> Different meanings?</li><li>Express surprise: use kē — &quot;This is actually really delicious!&quot; → <strong>He reka kē tēnei!</strong> (reka = delicious)</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Discussion prompt: Ka haere noa ia (she just wanders freely) vs Ka haere tonu ia (she keeps going continuously). Both are about ongoing movement but with completely different feelings. What is the emotional difference? In what situation would you use each?</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">You Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Māu Anō — Degree sentences</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Use degree adverbs to add intensity and nuance to your sentences.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Write three sentences using tino, āhua, and noa — one each.</li><li>Write two sentences using tonu and anō — show the difference.</li><li>Write a kē sentence that expresses pleasant surprise: <strong>He [adjective] kē tēnei!</strong></li><li>✦ Bonus: Rewrite five plain sentences from your previous work by adding degree adverbs. Read the original and the new version aloud — notice how much more expressive the degree version sounds.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Tuesday: He aha te tikanga? — Explaining meaning and custom":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Te Aho Arataki Marau — AO 2.3: Extended explanation · AO 1.4: Cultural connection · Grammar Level 1–2: Explaining meaning structures</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Objectives</th><td class=\"lt-td\">AO 2.3 — Explanation &amp; meaning; AO 1.4 — Cultural meaning</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">I Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tāua Kōrero — Explaining what things mean</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Being able to EXPLAIN things in te reo — what a word means, why a custom exists, what a phrase does — is a sign of true fluency. Today we learn the structures for giving explanations and definitions in te reo Māori.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\"><strong>He aha te <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=tikanga\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">tikanga</a> o tēnei kupu?</strong><br>What is the meaning of this word?<br><br><strong>Ko te tikanga o &quot;aroha&quot; ko te…</strong><br>The meaning of &quot;aroha&quot; is…<br><br><strong>Ko te tikanga o te hongi ko te tiaki i te <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=manawa\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">manawa</a> ora.</strong><br>The meaning of the hongi is the keeping of the breath of life.<br><br><strong>Nā reira, ka <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=k%C4%ABia\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">kīia</a> ko &quot;aroha&quot; tērā.</strong><br>Therefore, that is called &quot;aroha&quot;.<br><br><strong>He mea nui te [custom] nō te mea…</strong><br>The [custom] is an important thing because…</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Explanation structures:</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>Ko te tikanga o [word/thing] ko te [explanation].</strong></li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The meaning of [word] is [explanation].</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>He mea nui/pai/tapu [custom] nō te mea…</strong></li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>[Custom] is important/good/sacred because…</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>Nā reira, ka kīia ko [name] tērā.</strong></li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Therefore, that thing is called [name].</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>Ko tā [word] mahi ko te [function].</strong></li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The function of [word] is [function].</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>New words:<br><strong>tikanga</strong> — meaning / custom / correct practice<br><strong>manawa ora</strong> — breath of life (manawa = breath, ora = life/wellbeing)<br><strong>tapu</strong> — sacred / restricted / forbidden<br><strong>noa</strong> — free from tapu / ordinary / unrestricted (also: just/freely)<br><strong>ka kīia</strong> — is called / is named (passive)</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Tip: Ko te tikanga o [X] ko te [explanation]. This is the te reo pattern for defining something. Master this and you can explain any word or concept in te reo Māori.</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">We Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Kia Tūhono — Define and explain</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Use the explanation structures to define te reo words and cultural concepts.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Define a word: <strong>Ko te tikanga o &quot;whakapapa&quot; ko te raupapa o ngā tīpuna.</strong> (raupapa = sequence/ordering)</li><li>Explain a custom: <strong>He mea nui te karanga nō te mea ko ia te reo o ngā wāhine ki ngā manuhiri.</strong></li><li>Use the kīia structure: <strong>Ko te wāhi e noho ai ngā tāngata i ngā hui, ka kīia ko &quot;wharenui&quot; tērā.</strong></li><li>Define a term from this lesson: <strong>Ko te tikanga o &quot;tapu&quot; ko te mea kua whakanoa rānei — he mea whakamōhio e ai ki te tikanga Māori.</strong></li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Discussion prompt: Ko te tikanga o &quot;manawa ora&quot; ko te tiaki i te mauri o te tangata — the meaning of &quot;breath of life&quot; is the preservation of a person's life force. The hongi shares this breath. What does this tell us about how Māori understood human connection and greeting? Is there anything like this in other cultures you know?</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">You Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Māu Anō — Explain and define</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write explanations of te reo words and cultural practices.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Write Ko te tikanga o [word] ko te [explanation] for three te reo words you know.</li><li>Write He mea nui [custom] nō te mea for one cultural practice.</li><li>Write a ka kīia sentence: define something by what it is called.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write a &quot;te reo glossary entry&quot; for five terms from this term — each entry with Ko te tikanga o… ko te…, He mea nui/pai/tapu… nō te mea…, and one example sentence. This could go on a classroom wall.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Wednesday: Hanga rerenga — Formal and informal register":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Te Aho Arataki Marau — AO 1.6: Politeness conventions · AO 2.3: Register · Grammar Level 1–2: Formal vs informal language choices</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Objectives</th><td class=\"lt-td\">AO 1.6 — Formal &amp; informal register; AO 2.3 — Register-appropriate language</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">I Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tāua Kōrero — Knowing when to be formal and when to be casual</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Good language users know that how you speak changes depending on who you are with and where you are. The same idea can be said formally (at a pōhiri) or casually (with your friends). Today we practise both registers in te reo Māori.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\"><strong>CASUAL (with friends, in class):</strong><br><strong>Kia ora! He aha tōu ingoa?</strong> — Hey! What's your name?<br><strong>Ka pai tāu!</strong> — Nice one! / Good on you!<br><strong>E haere ana koe ki hea?</strong> — Where are you going?<br><strong>Āe noa <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=iho\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">iho</a>!</strong> — Sure, no worries!<br><br><strong>FORMAL (pōhiri, meeting, addressing elders):</strong><br><strong>Tēnā koe / Tēnā koutou katoa.</strong> — Greetings (formal).<br><strong>Ko [name] tōku ingoa. Nō [place] ahau.</strong> (full pepeha)<br><strong>Ka <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=rawe\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">rawe</a> tāu i mahi ai.</strong> — What you have done is excellent.<br><strong>He nui tōku <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=mihi\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">mihi</a> ki a koutou.</strong> — My gratitude to you all is great.</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Register markers in te reo Māori:</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>FORMAL signals:<br>→ Use full pepeha rather than just your name<br>→ Tēnā koe/kōrua/koutou rather than Kia ora<br>→ He nui tōku mihi ki a koe (formal gratitude)<br>→ Full sentence structures, no shortcuts<br>→ Ka rawe, Ka tino pai (formal praise)</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>CASUAL signals:<br>→ Kia ora (friendly, warm)<br>→ Ka pai! (casual praise)<br>→ Shorter sentences<br>→ Direct questions without elaborate framing<br>→ Āe noa iho (sure, no problem)</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>New formal phrases:<br><strong>He nui tōku mihi ki a koe.</strong> — My gratitude to you is great.<br><strong>Ka rawe tāu i mahi ai.</strong> — What you have done is excellent.<br><strong>Ka ārahina tātou e koe.</strong> — You have led us. (passive)</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Tip: Register is about relationship and context, not about one being &quot;better&quot; than the other. Casual with friends shows warmth. Formal with elders shows respect. Knowing which to use is wisdom.</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">We Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Kia Tūhono — Switch the register</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>I give you a casual sentence — you make it formal. Then I give you a formal one — you make it casual.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Casual: &quot;Kia ora! He aha tōu ingoa?&quot; → Formal: <strong>Tēnā koe. Ko wai tō ingoa?</strong></li><li>Formal: &quot;He nui tōku mihi ki a koutou.&quot; → Casual: <strong>Kia ora koutou — ka nui ōku whakaaro ki a koutou!</strong></li><li>Casual: &quot;Ka pai tāu!&quot; → Formal: <strong>Ka rawe tāu i mahi ai. He mea nui tērā.</strong></li><li>Now write your own pair — one casual, one formal — about the same situation.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Discussion prompt: Āe noa iho — sure, no worries. Noa here makes it casual and easy. He nui tōku mihi ki a koe — my gratitude is great. The formal version uses nui (big) to show depth. The same idea — I'm thankful — expressed at completely different levels. What would happen if you used the formal phrase with a close friend? The casual one with an elder?</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">You Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Māu Anō — Register practice</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write the same content in two different registers.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Write a casual greeting to a friend and a formal greeting to a visitor — different words, same warmth.</li><li>Write a casual &quot;well done&quot; and a formal &quot;well done&quot; — different register, same meaning.</li><li>Write one sentence from your life in casual te reo, then rewrite it formally.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write a short speech (6 sentences) as if you were welcoming a visitor to your school — formal register throughout. Then rewrite it as something you would say to a friend visiting — casual register. Read both aloud and notice how different they feel.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Thursday: Kōrero — He kōrero ā-reo — A language conversation":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Te Aho Arataki Marau — AO 2.3: Extended conversation · AO 1.4: Cultural connection · AO 2.5: Language vocabulary</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Objectives</th><td class=\"lt-td\">AO 2.3 — Language &amp; learning conversation; AO 1.4 — Cultural identity through language</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">I Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tāua Kōrero — Talking about language itself</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Today we have a meta-conversation — we use te reo Māori to talk about te reo Māori. This is one of the highest-level language activities: using a language to reflect on itself, its beauty, its importance, and what it means to learn it.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\"><strong>A:</strong> He aha tō whakaaro mō te ako i te reo Māori?<br><strong>B:</strong> Ko tāku whakaaro, he taonga nui rawa atu te reo Māori — he reo <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=rangatira\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">rangatira</a>, he reo ātaahua.<br><strong>A:</strong> He aha ngā uauatanga i pā mai ki a koe?<br><strong>B:</strong> I uaua ki a au ngā hanganga o te tense — Ko Kua, Ko Ka — engari kua mārama ake au.<br><strong>A:</strong> He aha te mea i āwhina i a koe?<br><strong>B:</strong> Ko tēnei akoranga — ko tā mātou mahi tahi i ōku <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=%C4%81whina\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">āwhina</a>. Ka taea e au te kōrero māmā ake ināianei.<br><strong>A:</strong> He aha tāu e hiahia ana ki te ako āpōpō?<br><strong>B:</strong> Kei te hiahia au ki te kōrero māmā ake me tōku whānau — ko tērā tōku moemoeā.</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>New vocabulary for language conversation:<br><strong>ngā uauatanga</strong> — the difficulties (plural of uauatanga)<br><strong>i pā mai ki a koe</strong> — that came to you / that you encountered (pā = touch/affect)<br><strong>tā mātou mahi tahi</strong> — our working together (tā mātou = our ā-category thing)<br><strong>māmā ake</strong> — more fluently / more easily<br><strong>moemoeā</strong> — dream / aspiration<br><strong>He reo rangatira.</strong> — It is a chiefly language.<br><strong>ko tērā tōku moemoeā</strong> — that is my dream/aspiration</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Asking about language learning:<br><strong>He aha ngā uauatanga i pā mai ki a koe?</strong><br>What difficulties did you encounter?<br><strong>He aha te mea i āwhina i a koe?</strong><br>What helped you?</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Tip: Ko tērā tōku moemoeā — that is my dream. Moemoeā means both &quot;dream&quot; (sleeping) and &quot;aspiration&quot; (waking dream). Using it for a learning goal connects your aspiration to something deep and important.</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">We Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Kia Tūhono — Language conversation</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Build a conversation about te reo Māori and what it means to learn it.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I ask: <strong>He aha tō whakaaro mō te ako i te reo Māori?</strong> Answer with Ko tāku whakaaro…</li><li>I ask: <strong>He aha ngā uauatanga i pā mai ki a koe?</strong> Answer honestly with I uaua ki a au…</li><li>I ask: <strong>He aha te mea i āwhina i a koe?</strong> Answer with what helped: Ko tēnei akoranga / ko tōku hoa / ko…</li><li>I ask: <strong>Ko ēhea āu moemoeā mō tō reo Māori?</strong> Answer with Kei te hiahia au ki te… Ko tērā tōku moemoeā.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Discussion prompt: Ko tōku moemoeā ko te kōrero māmā me tōku whānau — my dream is to speak fluently with my family. Why might speaking te reo with your family feel like the ultimate goal? What does it mean when a language lives at home, not just at school?</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">You Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Māu Anō — Tōu moemoeā</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write about your te reo Māori journey and dream.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Write Ko tāku whakaaro mō te reo Māori: (your opinion about te reo).</li><li>Write the difficulties you encountered: I uaua ki a au… engari…</li><li>Write what helped: Ko [person/thing] i āwhina i a au.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write your full language reflection (8 sentences) — your opinion of te reo Māori, what you found hard, what helped, what you can do now (Ka taea e au te…), and your dream for your te reo (Ko tērā tōku moemoeā). Read it to a partner.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Friday: Aromatawai — Quiz & Review":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Monday questions</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>&quot;Ka haere noa ia.&quot; What does &quot;noa&quot; add to this sentence?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>She is going urgently </li><li>She just goes / wanders freely, casually ✅</li><li>She is going again </li><li>She is still going </li></ul></li><li>&quot;He pai kē tēnei!&quot; What does &quot;kē&quot; signal?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>This is somewhat good </li><li>This is not good </li><li>This is actually really good / even better than expected ✅</li><li>This is the same as before </li></ul></li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tuesday questions</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>&quot;Ko te tikanga o te hongi ko te tiaki i te manawa ora.&quot; What does manawa ora mean?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>the meeting house </li><li>the breath of life ✅</li><li>the welcome ceremony </li><li>the gathering of people </li></ul></li><li>What does &quot;tapu&quot; mean?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>ordinary / unrestricted </li><li>wrong / incorrect </li><li>sacred / restricted / forbidden ✅</li><li>beautiful </li></ul></li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Wednesday questions</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Which phrase signals a FORMAL register for expressing gratitude?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>Ka pai! </li><li>Āe noa iho! </li><li>He nui tōku mihi ki a koe. ✅</li><li>Kia ora! </li></ul></li><li>What does &quot;Āe noa iho!&quot; express?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>I strongly agree </li><li>I disagree completely </li><li>Sure, no worries! (casual agreement) ✅</li><li>That is very difficult </li></ul></li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Thursday questions</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>What does &quot;moemoeā&quot; mean?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>challenge / difficulty </li><li>the past </li><li>dream / aspiration ✅</li><li>hard work </li></ul></li><li>&quot;He aha ngā uauatanga i pā mai ki a koe?&quot; What is this question asking about?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>Where you went </li><li>What you like most </li><li>What difficulties you encountered ✅</li><li>Who helped you </li></ul></li></ul></div></div>"}}},"Week 6":{"label":"Day","children":{"Monday: Ngā mahi toi — Art, music, and creativity":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Te Aho Arataki Marau — AO 2.5: Expressive vocabulary · AO 2.3: Creative expression · AO 1.4: Cultural arts</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Objectives</th><td class=\"lt-td\">AO 2.5 — Arts &amp; creative vocabulary; AO 1.4 — Cultural arts connection</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">I Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tāua Kōrero — The arts in te reo Māori</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Art, music, dance, and craft are central to te ao Māori — they are not extras but essentials. Today we build vocabulary for the arts and creative expression so you can talk about what you make, perform, and love in te reo Māori.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\"><strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=toi\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">toi</a></strong> — art / creativity / expertise<br><strong>toi <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=whakairo\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">whakairo</a></strong> — carving (whakairo = to carve / engrave)<br><strong>toi <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=raranga\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">raranga</a></strong> — weaving (raranga = to weave)<br><strong>toi <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=t%C4%81+moko\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">tā moko</a></strong> — tā moko / traditional tattoo<br><strong>toi <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=puoro\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">puoro</a></strong> — music (puoro = music / musical instrument)<br><strong>toi <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=kori\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">kori</a></strong> — dance / movement arts (kori = movement)<br><strong>toi <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=ataata\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">ataata</a></strong> — film / visual arts (ataata = shadow / film)<br><strong>haka</strong> — haka (posture dance / ceremonial challenge)<br><strong>waiata</strong> — song (also: to sing)<br><strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=poi\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">poi</a></strong> — poi (ball on string, used in performance)</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Arts vocabulary in sentences:</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Ka whakairo ia i ngā mahi toi.</strong><br>She carves artworks.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>He tohunga raranga tōna māmā.</strong><br>Her mother is an expert weaver. (tohunga = expert)</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Kei te ako au i te haka.</strong><br>I am learning haka.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Ko te waiata tōku mahi toi pai rawa atu.</strong><br>Singing is my absolute favourite art form.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Ka kaoa e ia ngā poi.</strong><br>She swings the poi. (kaoa = swing / wave)</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>New words:<br><strong>tohunga</strong> — expert / skilled person / specialist<br><strong>mahi toi</strong> — artwork / creative work<br><strong>ahua</strong> — image / appearance / likeness<br><strong>kaoa</strong> — swing / wave / rotate</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Tip: Toi means both &quot;art&quot; and &quot;expertise&quot; in te reo Māori. Tohunga (expert) comes from the same root. In te ao Māori, art and skill are the same thing — to make something beautiful is to demonstrate mastery.</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">We Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Kia Tūhono — Arts vocabulary</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Use arts vocabulary in sentences about real creative activities.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Name your favourite art form: Ko tōku mahi toi pai rawa atu ko [art form] nō te mea…</li><li>Describe a skill: He tohunga [art form] tōku [family member/friend].</li><li>Past tense: what did you make or perform? I [verb] au i [art form/object].</li><li>Future intention: Kei te hiahia au ki te ako i te [art form]. Māku e [action].</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Discussion prompt: Toi means both art AND expertise. In English, we separate these — &quot;art&quot; and &quot;skill&quot; feel like different things. In te ao Māori, they are the same word. What does this tell us about how Māori understood creative practice? Can you think of examples where art and skill really ARE inseparable?</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">You Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Māu Anō — Arts sentences</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write about your creative life using arts vocabulary.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Write three sentences about arts activities using three different toi words.</li><li>Write a He tohunga sentence about someone you know who is skilled in an art form.</li><li>Write a past tense sentence about something you made or performed.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write a paragraph (7 sentences) about a creative project you have done or would like to do. Include: the art form, who it involved (pronouns), what you did (Ka sequence), how it felt (emotion vocabulary), and what it meant (He mea nui/taonga tēnei nō te mea…).</li></ul></div></div>"},"Tuesday: He kōrero mō te haka — The language of haka":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Te Aho Arataki Marau — AO 1.4: Cultural identity · AO 2.5: Expressive vocabulary · AO 2.3: Extended cultural explanation</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Objectives</th><td class=\"lt-td\">AO 1.4 — Haka &amp; cultural identity; AO 2.5 — Haka vocabulary</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">I Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tāua Kōrero — Understanding and talking about haka</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Haka is one of the most recognised Māori art forms in the world — but it is often misunderstood. Today we learn how to TALK about haka in te reo Māori, what it means, and when it is used.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\"><strong>Ko te <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=haka\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">haka</a> he mahi toi nō te reo Māori.</strong><br>Haka is an art form of te reo Māori.<br><br><strong>He haka mō ngā wā katoa — mō te hari, mō te pōuri, mō te <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=whakataetae\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">whakataetae</a>.</strong><br>There is a haka for every occasion — for joy, for sadness, for competition.<br><br><strong>Ko &quot;Ka Mate&quot; tētahi haka rongonui o Aotearoa.</strong><br>&quot;Ka Mate&quot; is a famous haka of New Zealand.<br><br><strong>He <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=tohu\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">tohu</a> <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=aroha\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">aroha</a> te haka — he tohu <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=kaha\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">kaha</a> hoki.</strong><br>Haka is a sign of love — it is also a sign of strength.</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Haka vocabulary:<br><strong>rongonui</strong> — famous / well-known<br><strong>whakataetae</strong> — competition<br><strong>tohu</strong> — sign / symbol / marker<br><strong>kaha</strong> — strength / powerful (also: hard/strongly as adverb)<br><strong>ngā wā katoa</strong> — every occasion / all times<br><strong>He haka mō te tangi.</strong> — A haka for a funeral/mourning.<br><strong>He haka mō te pōhiri.</strong> — A haka for a welcome.<br><strong>He haka mō te whakataetae.</strong> — A haka for competition.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Parts of haka:<br><strong>kūaha</strong> — opening<br><strong>whakakapi</strong> — conclusion<br><strong>reo</strong> — voice / words<br><strong>ātea</strong> — space / arena<br><strong>ngākau</strong> — heart / spirit (must be in the performance)</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Tip: He tohu aroha te haka — haka is a sign of love. This surprises many people who see only the fierce exterior. But the fierce face and the deep love are not opposites in te ao Māori — they are two expressions of the same passion.</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">We Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Kia Tūhono — Haka conversation</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Discuss haka using today's vocabulary.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Define haka: Ko te tikanga o te haka ko te [explanation]. Use Ko te tikanga structure from last week.</li><li>Give occasions: He haka mō te [occasion] — list three occasions when haka is performed.</li><li>Express what haka means: He tohu [word] te haka. He tohu [word] hoki.</li><li>Your experience: Kei te hiahia au ki te ako i te haka / I ako au i te haka i [time].</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Discussion prompt: He tohu aroha te haka — haka is a sign of love — he tohu kaha hoki — and a sign of strength. How can love and strength be expressed in the same action? Can you think of other examples in life where love and strength are not opposites but one and the same?</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">You Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Māu Anō — Haka explanation</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write an explanation of haka in te reo Māori — for someone who does not know what it is.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Define haka: Ko te tikanga o te haka ko te…</li><li>Give three occasions: He haka mō te [X], he haka mō te [Y], he haka mō te [Z].</li><li>Explain the meaning: He tohu [noun] te haka nō te mea…</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write a full explanation of haka (8 sentences) that could go in a school brochure or wall display. Cover: what it is, when it is done, what it means, why it matters to Māori culture, and what it means to you personally.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Wednesday: Hanga rerenga — Creative and cultural writing":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Te Aho Arataki Marau — AO 2.5: Creative expression · AO 1.4: Cultural identity · AO 2.3: Extended creative writing</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Objectives</th><td class=\"lt-td\">AO 2.5 — Creative writing; AO 1.4 — Cultural expression</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">I Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tāua Kōrero — Writing that is alive with culture</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The most powerful te reo writing does two things at once — it says something in te reo AND it says something through te reo, using the language itself as a window into te ao Māori. Today we write with that double purpose.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">An example of culturally rich writing:<br><br><strong>Ko te haka ko tōku reo — he reo ō-roto, he reo ō-waho.</strong><br>Haka is my language — an inward language, an outward language.<br><br><strong>Ka ara ōku ringa ki runga — ko tērā he <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=mihi\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">mihi</a> ki ngā <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=t%C4%ABpuna\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">tīpuna</a>.</strong><br>My arms rise — that is a greeting to the ancestors.<br><br><strong>Ka kōrero ōku waewae — ka tangi ōku karu.</strong><br>My feet speak — my eyes cry.<br><br><strong>Ko te haka tōku ara ki a rātou — ki ngā tīpuna, ki te ao <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=m%C4%81rama\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">mārama</a>.</strong><br>Haka is my path to them — to the ancestors, to the world of light.</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Techniques for culturally rich writing:</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>Use body + action to carry cultural meaning</strong>:</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&quot;Ka ara ōku ringa ki runga&quot; — arms rising as a greeting</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>Personify natural/cultural elements</strong>:</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&quot;Ka kōrero ōku waewae&quot; — feet speak</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>Connect personal action to ancestral meaning</strong>:</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Ko tērā he mihi ki ngā tīpuna — that is a greeting to the ancestors</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>Use Ko to make identity statements about art</strong>:</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&quot;Ko te haka tōku ara ki a rātou&quot;</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>New phrases:<br><strong>he reo ō-roto</strong> — an inner language<br><strong>he reo ō-waho</strong> — an outer language<br><strong>ao mārama</strong> — world of light (the realm of the living or of enlightenment)</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Tip: Ko te haka tōku ara ki a rātou — ki ngā tīpuna. The art form is a pathway. What art form is YOUR pathway — to understanding, to connection, to something beyond words? Can you say that in te reo Māori?</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">We Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Kia Tūhono — Culturally rich writing</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write a passage that uses an art form as a window into identity.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Start with a Ko statement about the art: Ko te [art form] ko tōku [what it is to you].</li><li>Describe a physical action: Ka [body action] ōku [body part] — ko tērā he [meaning].</li><li>Connect to the ancestors or community: Ko tērā tōku ara ki [who/where].</li><li>Close with a He identity statement: He [quality] te [art form] — he [quality] hoki.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Discussion prompt: Ko te haka ko tōku reo — haka is my language. What does it mean to say an art form IS your language? What can haka say that words cannot? Is there an art form that speaks for you when words fail?</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">You Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Māu Anō — Creative cultural writing</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write a passage about an art form that matters to you.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Write a Ko identity statement: Ko te [art form] ko tōku [meaning to you].</li><li>Write two body-action sentences that carry cultural meaning.</li><li>Connect to something beyond yourself — ancestors, community, nature.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write a full piece (8 sentences) about an art form that matters to you. Use: Ko for identity, body vocabulary for action, emotional vocabulary, cultural connection, personification, and close with Ko tērā tōku ara ki…</li></ul></div></div>"},"Thursday: Kōrero — He kōrero mō ngā mahi toi — Arts conversation":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Te Aho Arataki Marau — AO 2.5: Arts vocabulary · AO 1.4: Cultural arts · AO 2.3: Extended conversation</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Objectives</th><td class=\"lt-td\">AO 2.5 — Arts conversation; AO 1.4 — Arts &amp; identity</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">I Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tāua Kōrero — A conversation about creative life</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Today we have a full conversation about art, music, and creativity — drawing on everything from this week and the whole term.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\"><strong>A:</strong> Ko ēhea ngā mahi toi e hiahia ana koe ki te ako?<br><strong>B:</strong> Kei te hiahia au ki te ako i te haka me te waiata — he mahi toi ātaahua ērā ki a au.<br><strong>A:</strong> He aha tōu mahi toi ināianei?<br><strong>B:</strong> Kei te ako au i te raranga. He mea uaua engari he tino ātaahua ngā <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=hua\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">hua</a>!<br><strong>A:</strong> He <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=tohunga\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">tohunga</a> koe?<br><strong>B:</strong> Kāore anō — he ākonga noa iho au ināianei! Engari āpōpō, ka taea e au te <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=raranga\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">raranga</a> i ngā āhua rerekē.<br><strong>A:</strong> Ka pai tāu māia! Ko ēhea ngā āhuatanga pai rawa atu mōu mō tēnei mahi toi?<br><strong>B:</strong> Ko ēnei: he mārie te noho me te raranga, ka mārama hoki tōku ngākau i ngā āhua ka <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=puta\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">puta</a> mai.</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>New conversation vocabulary:<br><strong>Ko ēhea ngā mahi toi…?</strong> — Which art forms…?<br><strong>he mahi toi ātaahua ērā ki a au</strong> — those are beautiful art forms to me<br><strong>ngā hua</strong> — the results / the outcomes<br><strong>he ākonga noa iho au</strong> — I am just a learner (noa = just/merely, iho = downward particle, emphasising humility)<br><strong>āhua rerekē</strong> — different forms / various designs<br><strong>āhuatanga pai rawa atu</strong> — absolute best aspects / features<br><strong>ka puta mai</strong> — that emerge / that come forth (puta = emerge)<br><strong>ka mārama tōku ngākau</strong> — my heart becomes clear/enlightened</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Tip: He ākonga noa iho au — I am just a learner. The noa iho shows humility — making yourself small in a gracious way. In te ao Māori, calling yourself just a learner is not weakness — it is the correct relationship to knowledge.</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">We Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Kia Tūhono — Arts conversation</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Have a full conversation about your creative life in te reo Māori.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I ask: Ko ēhea ngā mahi toi e hiahia ana koe ki te ako? Answer with your arts interests.</li><li>I ask: He aha tōu mahi toi ināianei? Answer with what you do now.</li><li>I ask: He tohunga koe? Answer humbly — He ākonga noa iho au / Kāore anō.</li><li>I ask: Ko ēhea ngā āhuatanga pai rawa atu mōu? Answer with Ko ēnei: a list of what you love about it.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Discussion prompt: He ākonga noa iho au — I am just a learner. And ka mārama tōku ngākau i ngā āhua ka puta mai — my heart becomes clear at the forms that emerge. What does it mean for your heart to become clear through making something? Have you ever felt that? What were you doing?</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">You Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Māu Anō — Arts identity</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write about your creative life and what it means to you.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Write which art forms you love: Ko ēnei ōku mahi toi: he [X], he [Y], he [Z].</li><li>Write your current skill level: He ākonga noa iho au / He āhua tohunga au i te [art form].</li><li>Write what you love about your favourite art form: Ko ēnei ngā āhuatanga pai rawa atu: he…</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write a full arts identity paragraph (8 sentences). Include: which arts you love, your current level, what you love about it, one Ko statement about what the art means to you, a Ka taea sentence about what you can do, and a moemoeā about where you want to go.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Friday: Aromatawai — Quiz & Review":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Monday questions</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>What does &quot;tohunga&quot; mean?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>student / learner </li><li>expert / skilled person ✅</li><li>teacher </li><li>artist </li></ul></li><li>&quot;Ko tōku mahi toi pai rawa atu ko te waiata.&quot; What does waiata mean?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>carving </li><li>weaving </li><li>song / singing ✅</li><li>poi </li></ul></li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tuesday questions</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>What does &quot;rongonui&quot; mean?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>sacred </li><li>famous / well-known ✅</li><li>powerful </li><li>ancient </li></ul></li><li>&quot;He tohu aroha te haka — he tohu kaha hoki.&quot; What two things is haka a sign of?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>anger and power </li><li>sadness and strength </li><li>love and strength ✅</li><li>tradition and sport </li></ul></li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Wednesday questions</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>&quot;Ko te haka ko tōku reo.&quot; What does this Ko statement express?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>Haka and language are different things </li><li>Haka is the speaker's language — their way of expressing themselves ✅</li><li>Te reo Māori is a form of haka </li><li>The speaker cannot speak te reo </li></ul></li><li>What is &quot;ao mārama&quot;?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>a dark night </li><li>the world of light / realm of enlightenment ✅</li><li>the ocean </li><li>a rainy day </li></ul></li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Thursday questions</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>&quot;He ākonga noa iho au.&quot; What does this expression convey?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>I am an expert </li><li>I am refusing to learn </li><li>I am just a learner — humble acknowledgement of learning status ✅</li><li>I do not like learning </li></ul></li><li>&quot;Ka mārama tōku ngākau i ngā āhua ka puta mai.&quot; What does &quot;ka puta mai&quot; mean?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>that disappear </li><li>that are ancient </li><li>that emerge / that come forth ✅</li><li>that are difficult </li></ul></li></ul></div></div>"}}},"Week 7":{"label":"Day","children":{"Monday: He kōrero mō te taiao — Environment and kaitiakitanga":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Te Aho Arataki Marau — AO 1.4: Connection to land · AO 2.4: Natural world · AO 2.3: Extended environmental language</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Objectives</th><td class=\"lt-td\">AO 1.4 — Kaitiakitanga &amp; environment; AO 2.4 — Environmental vocabulary</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">I Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tāua Kōrero — Guardianship of the natural world</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Kaitiakitanga — guardianship of the natural environment — is one of the most important concepts in te ao Māori and in modern New Zealand. Today we build the vocabulary to talk about the environment, what threatens it, and what guardianship means in practice.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\"><strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=kaitiakitanga\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">kaitiakitanga</a></strong> — environmental guardianship / stewardship / stewardship<br><strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=kaitiaki\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">kaitiaki</a></strong> — guardian / protector (kai- + tiaki) / protector (kai- + tiaki)<br><strong>taiao</strong> — natural world / environment<br><strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=papat%C5%AB%C4%81nuku\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">papatūānuku</a></strong> — the earth / ground (Papa = earth mother) / ground (Papa = earth mother)<br><strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=tangaroa\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">tangaroa</a></strong> — the sea / Tangaroa (atua of the sea) / Tangaroa (atua of the sea)<br><strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=t%C4%81ne\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">tāne</a></strong> — the forest / Tāne (atua of the forest and birds) / Tāne (atua of the forest and birds)<br><strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=toit%C5%AB\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">toitū</a></strong> — sustainable / enduring / intact / enduring / intact<br><strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=paru\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">paru</a></strong> — polluted / dirty / muddy / dirty / muddy<br><strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=mauri\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">mauri</a></strong> — life force / vitality / vitality<br><strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=ora\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">ora</a></strong> — well / alive / thriving / thriving</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Environmental language in sentences:</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Ko tātou ngā kaitiaki o tēnei taiao.</strong><br>We are the guardians of this natural world.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Kāore e toitū ana te ao ināianei — he paru ngā awa, he ngaro ngā manu.</strong><br>The world is not sustainable right now — the rivers are polluted, the birds are disappearing.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Me tiaki tātou i te mauri o ngā awa, o ngā ngahere, o ngā maunga.</strong><br>We must protect the life force of the rivers, forests, and mountains.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Ka ora te <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=taiao\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">taiao</a>, ka ora tātou.</strong><br>When the environment thrives, we thrive.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>New words:<br><strong>ngaro</strong> — lost / disappeared / extinct<br><strong>tiakina</strong> — be protected (passive of tiaki)<br><strong>toitū te taiao</strong> — a sustainable/enduring environment</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Tip: Ka ora te taiao, ka ora tātou — when the environment is well, we are well. This sentence sums up kaitiakitanga perfectly. The earth's wellbeing and ours are not separate. Learn this sentence cold.</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">We Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Kia Tūhono — Environmental language</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Use environmental vocabulary in sentences about the real world.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>State our responsibility: <strong>Ko tātou ngā kaitiaki o tēnei taiao.</strong> Add a reason: nō te mea…</li><li>Name a problem: <strong>Kāore e toitū ana te [environmental feature] ināianei nō te mea…</strong></li><li>Use me for responsibility: <strong>Me tiaki tātou i te mauri o [feature].</strong></li><li>Close with the core principle: <strong>Ka ora te taiao, ka ora tātou.</strong> Discuss what this means.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Discussion prompt: Ka ora te taiao, ka ora tātou — the wellbeing of the environment is the wellbeing of people. In English we often talk about &quot;protecting the environment&quot; as if it is separate from us. How does the te reo sentence change that framing? What does it say about the relationship between people and the natural world?</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">You Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Māu Anō — Kaitiakitanga writing</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write about environmental guardianship in te reo Māori.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Write your responsibility statement: Ko au tētahi kaitiaki o te taiao nō te mea…</li><li>Name one environmental issue using Kāore e toitū ana… nō te mea…</li><li>Write what you will do: Māku e [action] hei tiaki i [environmental feature].</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write a kaitiakitanga statement (8 sentences) — what kaitiakitanga means, what the problems are, what must be done (me sentences), what you personally commit to (māku e), and close with Ka ora te taiao, ka ora tātou.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Tuesday: Ngā kupu o te ture — Rights, rules, and responsibilities":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Te Aho Arataki Marau — AO 2.3: Extended communication · AO 1.6: Social conventions · Grammar Level 1–2: Me, kaua, tikanga in social contexts</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Objectives</th><td class=\"lt-td\">AO 2.3 — Rights &amp; responsibilities; AO 1.6 — Social conventions &amp; rules</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">I Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tāua Kōrero — Rights, rules, and what we must do</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Language for rights and responsibilities is important for full participation in society. Today we build the vocabulary for rules, what is allowed, what must be done, and what we have the right to.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\"><strong>he <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=tikanga\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">tikanga</a></strong> — a rule / a correct practice / a right<br><strong>He tikanga tōku ki te kōrero.</strong><br>It is my right to speak.<br><br><strong>Me mahi tātou i tēnei.</strong> — We must do this.<br><strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=Kaua\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Kaua</a> e mahi i tēnā.</strong> — Do not do that. (kaua = do not — negative command)<br><strong>E <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=%C4%81hei\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">āhei</a> ana koe ki te…</strong> — You are permitted/able to…<br><strong>Kāore e āhei ana koe ki te…</strong> — You are not permitted to…<br><br><strong>He aha ngā tikanga o tō mātou kura?</strong><br>What are the rules of our school?</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Rights and rules vocabulary:<br><strong>āhei</strong> — permitted / allowed / able to<br><strong>kaua e [verb]</strong> — do not [verb] (negative command)<br><strong>kaua e tū ake</strong> — do not stand up<br><strong>kaua e kōrero kino</strong> — do not speak badly<br><strong>he tikanga</strong> — a rule / custom / right<br><strong>He tikanga tōku ki te [verb].</strong> — It is my right to [verb].<br><strong>He kawenga ōku ki te [verb].</strong> — It is my responsibility to [verb].<br><strong>tōpū</strong> — group / together (tōpū mahi = working group)<br><strong>whakaaro nui</strong> — consideration / deep thought</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>For rules:<br><strong>Me [verb] tātou</strong> — We must [verb] (rule/obligation)<br><strong>Kaua e [verb]</strong> — Do not [verb] (prohibition)<br><strong>E āhei ana koe ki te [verb]</strong> — You are allowed to [verb]</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Tip: Kaua e [verb] = do not [verb]. This is the negative command form. More polite than a bare command but still clear. Kaua e waiho i tō para ki reira! — Don't leave your rubbish there!</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">We Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Kia Tūhono — School rules in te reo</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Express your school rules in te reo Māori using me, kaua, and āhei.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A school rule: <strong>Me whakarongo mai ngā ākonga ki tō rātou kaiako.</strong> (Students must listen to their teacher.)</li><li>A prohibition: <strong>Kaua e omaoma i roto i ngā whare katoa.</strong> (Do not run inside the buildings.)</li><li>A right: <strong>He tikanga ō ngā ākonga katoa ki te pātai.</strong> (All students have the right to ask questions.)</li><li>A responsibility: <strong>He kawenga ō ngā ākonga ki te tiaki i tō rātou kura.</strong></li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Discussion prompt: He tikanga tōku ki te kōrero — it is my right to speak. This sentence connects to Article 13 of the UN Convention on the Rights of the Child. Te reo Māori has the vocabulary to talk about rights and justice. Why does a language need this vocabulary? What happens when a language cannot talk about rights?</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">You Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Māu Anō — Rights and rules</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write your own school or community rules in te reo Māori.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Write three school rules using me [verb] structure.</li><li>Write two prohibitions using kaua e [verb].</li><li>Write one right and one responsibility using He tikanga/kawenga…</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write a &quot;Student Bill of Rights&quot; in te reo (8 sentences) — three rights (He tikanga ōku ki te…), three responsibilities (He kawenga ōku ki te…), and two prohibitions (Kaua e…). This could go on a classroom wall.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Wednesday: Hanga rerenga — Argument and persuasion extended":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Te Aho Arataki Marau — AO 2.3: Extended persuasive writing · Grammar Level 1–2: Full argument toolkit in te reo</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Objectives</th><td class=\"lt-td\">AO 2.3 — Persuasive writing; AO 2.5 — Formal argument vocabulary</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">I Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tāua Kōrero — Making a case in te reo Māori</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>You have learned to argue, compare, and use linking words. Today we assemble the full argument toolkit into a persuasive piece — the kind of writing that could appear in a school speech, a debate, or a submission.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\"><strong>Tōku <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=t%C5%AB%C4%81huatanga\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">tūāhuatanga</a>:</strong> He nui ake ngā hua o te kaitiakitanga i ngā uauatanga.<br>&lt;i&gt;My position: The benefits of environmental guardianship are greater than the difficulties.&lt;/i&gt;<br><br><strong>Tāku taunakitanga tuatahi:</strong> Ka ora te taiao, ka ora tātou — ko tēnā he tauira o te pono.<br>&lt;i&gt;My first evidence: When the environment thrives, we thrive — that is an example of truth.&lt;/i&gt;<br><br><strong>Waihoki:</strong> He tikanga ōu mō āpōpō ō ngā uri — me <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=tiaki\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">tiaki</a> tātou ināianei.<br>&lt;i&gt;Moreover: It is your descendants' right for tomorrow — we must protect now.&lt;/i&gt;<br><br><strong>Nō reira:</strong> me mahi tātou katoa hei <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=kaitiaki\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">kaitiaki</a> o tēnei taiao.<br>&lt;i&gt;Therefore: we must all work as guardians of this environment.&lt;/i&gt;</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Full argument structure:</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>Tōku tūāhuatanga:</strong> [position] — My position is…</li><li><strong>Tāku taunakitanga tuatahi / tuarua:</strong> [evidence] — My first/second evidence</li><li><strong>Waihoki:</strong> [additional point] — Moreover</li><li><strong>Heoi anō:</strong> [concession to other side]</li><li><strong>Nō reira:</strong> [conclusion] — Therefore</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>New words:<br><strong>tūāhuatanga</strong> — position / characteristic / quality<br><strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=taunakitanga\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">taunakitanga</a></strong> — evidence (from last week!) / support / argument<br><strong>tuatahi / tuarua / tuatoru</strong> — first / second / third<br><strong>pono</strong> — true / honest / truth<br><strong>uri</strong> — descendants (you know this word now!)</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Tip: Tāku taunakitanga tuatahi / tuarua — my first/second evidence. Taunakitanga is the key word for evidence or supporting argument. Learn it — it makes your argument sound like real persuasive writing.</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">We Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Kia Tūhono — Build the argument</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Together we build a persuasive argument about kaitiakitanga using the full structure.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>State position: <strong>Tōku tūāhuatanga: me tiaki tātou i te taiao o Aotearoa nō te mea…</strong></li><li>First evidence: <strong>Tāku taunakitanga tuatahi: Ka ora te taiao, ka ora tātou.</strong></li><li>Second evidence with waihoki: <strong>Waihoki, he tikanga ōu mō āpōpō ō ngā uri.</strong></li><li>Conclusion with nō reira: <strong>Nō reira, me mahi tātou katoa hei kaitiaki.</strong></li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Discussion prompt: Tāku taunakitanga — my evidence. Good arguments don't just state opinions — they provide evidence. In te ao Māori, evidence often comes from proverbs (whakataukī), from the natural world, and from the actions of ancestors. What makes evidence convincing in te ao Māori? Is it different from what convinces in a Western argument?</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">You Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Māu Anō — Your argument</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write a persuasive argument in te reo Māori about a topic you care about.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>State your position: Tōku tūāhuatanga: [position].</li><li>Give two pieces of evidence: Tāku taunakitanga tuatahi / tuarua.</li><li>Add waihoki (moreover) and make a third point.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write a full persuasive piece (8 sentences) — position, three evidence points (tuatahi/tuarua/tuatoru), a waihoki point, a concession with heoi anō, and a nō reira conclusion. Choose a topic you care about.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Thursday: Kōrero — He kōrero mō Aotearoa — Talking about New Zealand":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Te Aho Arataki Marau — AO 1.4: Connection to land · AO 2.4: Natural world · AO 2.3: Extended conversation</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Objectives</th><td class=\"lt-td\">AO 1.4 — Aotearoa identity; AO 2.4 — NZ environment conversation</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">I Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tāua Kōrero — What is Aotearoa?</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Today we have a conversation about Aotearoa — what it is, what it means, and what our relationship to it is. This is identity conversation at the national level — connecting language, land, and belonging.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\"><strong>A:</strong> He aha tō whakaaro mō Aotearoa?<br><strong>B:</strong> Ko Aotearoa tōku kāinga — ko ia tōku wāhi tupu, tōku wāhi tiaki.<br><strong>A:</strong> He aha ngā āhuatanga pai rawa atu o Aotearoa ki a koe?<br><strong>B:</strong> Ko ēnei: he ātaahua te taiao, he <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=m%C4%81ia\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">māia</a> ngā tāngata, he rangatira hoki te reo Māori o tēnei <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=whenua\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">whenua</a>.<br><strong>A:</strong> He aha ōu mōrearea mō Aotearoa?<br><strong>B:</strong> Ko tōku <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=m%C5%8Drearea\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">mōrearea</a>: ka <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=ngaro\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">ngaro</a> ngā manu o ēnei <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=motu\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">motu</a>, ka ngaro hoki te reo — me tiaki tātou i ērā taonga.<br><strong>A:</strong> He aha tō kawenga ki tēnei whenua?<br><strong>B:</strong> He <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=kawenga\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">kawenga</a> ōku ki te ako i te reo Māori, ki te tiaki i te taiao, ā ki te whakaaro nui mō ngā <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=uri\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">uri</a>.</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>New Aotearoa vocabulary:<br><strong>wāhi tupu</strong> — place of growth / where you grew up<br><strong>mōrearea</strong> — concern / worry / risk<br><strong>motu</strong> — island / islands (Aotearoa is a motu)<br><strong>taonga</strong> — treasure (you know this well now)<br><strong>He aha ōu mōrearea?</strong> — What are your concerns?<br><strong>he rangatira hoki te reo Māori</strong> — te reo Māori is also a chiefly language<br><strong>kawenga ki tēnei whenua</strong> — responsibility to this land<br><strong>uri</strong> — descendants (used here for future generations)</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Tip: Ko Aotearoa tōku kāinga — ko ia tōku wāhi tupu, tōku wāhi tiaki. Three Ko statements in a row, connected by commas. This creates a feeling of layering — adding depth to what home means. Use layered Ko statements when you want to say something has many dimensions.</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">We Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Kia Tūhono — Aotearoa conversation</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Build a conversation about Aotearoa, its beauty, and your responsibilities to it.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I ask: He aha tō whakaaro mō Aotearoa? Answer with Ko Aotearoa tōku kāinga — ko ia…</li><li>I ask: He aha ngā āhuatanga pai rawa atu o Aotearoa ki a koe? Answer with Ko ēnei list.</li><li>I ask: He aha ōu mōrearea mō Aotearoa? Answer with Ko tōku mōrearea: ka ngaro…</li><li>I ask: He aha tōu kawenga ki tēnei whenua? Answer with He kawenga ōku ki te…</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Discussion prompt: He kawenga ōku ki te ako i te reo Māori, ki te tiaki i te taiao, ā ki te whakaaro nui mō ngā uri. Three responsibilities in one sentence. Of these three — learning the language, protecting the environment, thinking deeply about future generations — which feels most urgent to you? Why?</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">You Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Māu Anō — Tōu whanaungatanga ki Aotearoa</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write about your relationship with Aotearoa.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Write three Ko statements about what Aotearoa means to you.</li><li>Write Ko ēnei list of the best things about Aotearoa: Ko ēnei ngā āhuatanga pai rawa atu.</li><li>Write your mōrearea: Ko tōku mōrearea mō Aotearoa ko…</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write a full statement about your relationship with Aotearoa (8 sentences) — what it is to you, its best features, your concerns, your responsibilities, and what you will do. Close with Ka ora te taiao, ka ora tātou.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Friday: Aromatawai — Quiz & Review":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Monday questions</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>&quot;Ka ora te taiao, ka ora tātou.&quot; What does this sentence mean?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>The environment belongs to all of us </li><li>We must protect the environment or we will suffer </li><li>When the environment thrives, we thrive — our wellbeing is linked ✅</li><li>The natural world is beautiful </li></ul></li><li>What does &quot;toitū&quot; mean in an environmental context?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>polluted / dirty </li><li>lost / extinct </li><li>sustainable / enduring / intact ✅</li><li>sacred </li></ul></li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tuesday questions</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>&quot;Kaua e omaoma i roto i ngā whare.&quot; What type of sentence is this?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>A right </li><li>A past tense sentence </li><li>A negative command — do not do this ✅</li><li>A question </li></ul></li><li>&quot;E āhei ana koe ki te pātai.&quot; What does this sentence tell you?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>You must ask a question </li><li>You are not allowed to ask </li><li>You are permitted / able to ask ✅</li><li>You asked a question </li></ul></li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Wednesday questions</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>What does &quot;taunakitanga&quot; mean?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>position / viewpoint </li><li>evidence / supporting argument ✅</li><li>conclusion </li><li>topic </li></ul></li><li>&quot;Tāku taunakitanga tuarua.&quot; What does tuarua indicate?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>first </li><li>third ✅</li><li>second </li><li>fourth </li></ul></li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Thursday questions</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>What does &quot;mōrearea&quot; mean?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>responsibility </li><li>treasure </li><li>concern / worry / risk ✅</li><li>beautiful feature </li></ul></li><li>&quot;Ko Aotearoa tōku kāinga — ko ia tōku wāhi tupu, tōku wāhi tiaki.&quot; What does &quot;wāhi tupu&quot; mean?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>place of rest </li><li>place of learning </li><li>place of growth / where you grew up ✅</li><li>place of danger </li></ul></li></ul></div></div>"}}},"Week 8":{"label":"Day","children":{"Monday: Whakataukī — Proverbs and their wisdom":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Te Aho Arataki Marau — AO 2.5: Figurative language · AO 1.4: Cultural wisdom · AO 2.3: Extended explanation</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Objectives</th><td class=\"lt-td\">AO 2.5 — Proverbs &amp; figurative language; AO 1.4 — Cultural wisdom</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">I Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tāua Kōrero — The wisdom of whakataukī</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Whakataukī — proverbs — are one of the richest forms of te reo Māori. They carry generations of wisdom in a few words. Today we explore the most important whakataukī for your learning journey, understand their meaning, and learn to use them.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\"><strong>He aha te mea nui o te ao?</strong><br><strong>He tangata, he tangata, he tangata.</strong><br>What is the most important thing in the world? It is people, it is people, it is people.<br><br><strong>Hutia te <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=rito\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">rito</a> o te <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=harakeke\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">harakeke</a>,</strong><br><strong>kei hea te <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=k%C5%8Dmako\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">kōmako</a> e kō?</strong><br>If you pull the heart of the flax bush, where will the bellbird sing?<br>(meaning: if you destroy the centre of the family/community, what happens to its members?)<br><br><strong>Ehara tāku toa i te toa <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=takitahi\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">takitahi</a>,</strong><br><strong>he toa <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=takitini\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">takitini</a>.</strong><br>My strength is not the strength of one, it is the strength of many.<br><br><strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=Wh%C4%81ia\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Whāia</a> te iti <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=kahurangi\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">kahurangi</a>;</strong><br><strong>ki te <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=t%C5%ABōhu\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">tūohu</a> koe, me he maunga <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=teitei\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">teitei</a>.</strong><br>Pursue excellence; if you must bow your head, let it be to a high mountain.</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Understanding whakataukī:</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Whakataukī work on two levels:</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The LITERAL meaning — what the words say</li><li>The DEEPER meaning — what they teach</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&quot;Hutia te rito o te harakeke&quot; — literally about a flax bush and a bellbird. Deeper: the flax bush is the family; the rito (heart shoot) is the child or the core; if you destroy the centre, the community falls apart.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&quot;Whāia te iti kahurangi&quot; — pursue the small precious thing (excellence). If you must bow, bow only to something truly great (a high mountain). Meaning: only humble yourself before things truly worthy of respect.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>New words:<br><strong>harakeke</strong> — flax plant (native NZ flax)<br><strong>rito</strong> — the heart shoot of the flax / the central child<br><strong>kōmako</strong> — bellbird<br><strong>toa takitahi</strong> — the strength of one alone<br><strong>toa takitini</strong> — the strength of many together</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Tip: To explain a whakataukī: first state the literal meaning, then say Ko tā tēnei whakataukī mōhio ko te [deeper meaning]. Always explain BOTH levels.</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">We Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Kia Tūhono — Whakataukī meanings</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>For each whakataukī, we unpack the literal meaning and then the deeper meaning together.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>&quot;He aha te mea nui o te ao? He tangata, he tangata, he tangata.&quot; → Literal: what is the most important thing? People, people, people. → Deeper: people are at the centre of everything.</li><li>&quot;Ehara tāku toa i te toa takitahi, he toa takitini.&quot; → Literal: my strength is not from one, it is from many. → Deeper: collective strength is greater than individual strength.</li><li>&quot;Whāia te iti kahurangi.&quot; → Pursue excellence. When would you use this whakataukī? Who would you say it to?</li><li>Choose one whakataukī and explain it: Ko tā tēnei whakataukī mōhio ko te…</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Discussion prompt: &quot;Hutia te rito o te harakeke, kei hea te kōmako e kō?&quot; The flax family: the outer leaves protect the inner leaves which protect the rito (heart shoot/child). The rito must be protected. This is the architecture of the family in te ao Māori. How does a plant become a model for how families should work?</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">You Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Māu Anō — Whakataukī</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Explore and explain whakataukī in te reo Māori.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Write the literal meaning of one whakataukī: Ko te tikanga ōpaki o tēnei whakataukī ko te…</li><li>Write the deeper meaning: Ko tā tēnei whakataukī mōhio ko te…</li><li>Write when you would use this whakataukī and who to.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Choose your favourite whakataukī from today. Write a full explanation (8 sentences) — the words, the literal meaning, the deeper meaning, the context for using it, and why it speaks to you personally. End with how this whakataukī connects to your own life.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Tuesday: Ngā kupu hou o te pūtaiao — Science and discovery vocabulary":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Te Aho Arataki Marau — AO 2.5: Vocabulary development · AO 2.3: Extended explanation · Grammar Level 1–2: Science and inquiry vocabulary</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Objectives</th><td class=\"lt-td\">AO 2.5 — Science vocabulary; AO 2.3 — Inquiry &amp; explanation</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">I Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tāua Kōrero — Exploring and explaining the world</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Te reo Māori has its own words for scientific inquiry — observation, hypothesis, experiment, and discovery. Today we learn this vocabulary so that science learning can happen in te reo as naturally as in English.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\"><strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=p%C5%ABtaiao\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">pūtaiao</a></strong> — science / nature study / nature study<br><strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=matai\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">matai</a></strong> — to observe / examine / study / examine / study<br><strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=whakam%C4%81tau\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">whakamātau</a></strong> — experiment / to test / to test<br><strong>rangahau</strong> — research / to research<br><strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=kitenga\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">kitenga</a></strong> — discovery / finding (kite + nga) / finding (kite + nga)<br><strong>pātai</strong> — question / to ask (also: hypothesis)<br><strong>whakaaro</strong> — thought / theory / idea<br><strong>taunakitanga</strong> — evidence (from last week!)<br><strong>hua</strong> — result / outcome<br><strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=h%C4%81ng%C5%AB\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">hāngū</a></strong> — accurate / precise</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Science process in te reo:</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>Ka pātai</strong> mātou i tētahi pātai. — We asked a question.</li><li><strong>Ka whakaaro</strong> mātou mō tā mātou whakamātau. — We thought about our experiment.</li><li><strong>Ka whakamātau</strong> mātou. — We experimented.</li><li><strong>Ka matai</strong> mātou i ngā hua. — We observed the results.</li><li><strong>Ka kitea</strong> e mātou tētahi mea hou. — We discovered something new. (kitea = passive of kite)</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Explaining findings:<br><strong>I kitea e mātou ko te [finding].</strong><br>We discovered that [finding].</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>He pono tō mātou whakaaro nō te mea…</strong><br>Our theory is correct because…</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>He rereke ngā hua i ō mātou tūmanako.</strong><br>The results were different from our expectations. (tūmanako = expectations/hopes)</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Tip: Ka kitea e mātou — we discovered. Kitea is the passive of kite (see/find). The passive is used here because the discovery &quot;came to us&quot; rather than us actively finding it. This reflects a Māori way of seeing knowledge — it reveals itself to those who are ready.</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">We Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Kia Tūhono — Science inquiry sequence</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Build a science inquiry description using the Te Reo Māori process vocabulary.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Start with a question: Ka pātai mātou: <strong>He aha te mea ka hanga ai te kapua?</strong> (What makes a cloud?)</li><li>State the experiment: Ka whakamātau mātou — i mahi mātou i tētahi whakamātau mō te wai.</li><li>State findings: I kitea e mātou ko te [finding]. He [adjective] ngā hua.</li><li>Evaluate: He pono/hē tō mātou whakaaro? (pono = true, hē = wrong/incorrect)</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Discussion prompt: Ka kitea e mātou — we discovered. The passive voice makes the discovery something that came to you, not something you grabbed. In te ao Māori, knowledge is often described as something that reveals itself to a prepared mind. How is this different from the Western idea of &quot;finding&quot; knowledge? Which feels more accurate to how discovery actually happens?</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">You Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Māu Anō — Science recount</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write a science inquiry recount in te reo Māori.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Write your question: Ka pātai mātou:…</li><li>Write your experiment: Ka whakamātau mātou — Ka matai mātou i…</li><li>Write your findings: I kitea e mātou ko te…</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write a full science inquiry recount (8 sentences) — the question, the experiment, the observation, the findings, whether your theory was correct, and what you will explore next. Use Ka for the sequence, I for specific facts, and Kua for completed states.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Wednesday: Hanga rerenga — Research and explanation writing":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Te Aho Arataki Marau — AO 2.3: Extended informational writing · AO 2.5: Technical vocabulary · Grammar Level 1–2: Explanation text structures</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Objectives</th><td class=\"lt-td\">AO 2.3 — Informational &amp; explanatory writing; AO 2.5 — Technical writing</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">I Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tāua Kōrero — Writing to explain and inform</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Today we write in the explanation/information genre — the kind of writing that explains how something works, why something happens, or what something means. This is a different genre from narrative or argument, and it has its own vocabulary and structure.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\"><strong>Topic: He aha te āniwaniwa?</strong><br><br><strong>Ko te āniwaniwa he tohu ātaahua o te āhua o te rangi.</strong><br>The rainbow is a beautiful sign of the weather.<br><br><strong>Ka <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=puta\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">puta</a> mai te āniwaniwa ina <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=tutaki\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">tutaki</a> ana te rā me te ua.</strong><br>The rainbow appears when the sun and rain meet.<br><br><strong>Ka <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=wehewehea\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">wehewehea</a> e te rā ngā tae o te ua — ka puta mai ngā tae katoa.</strong><br>The sun separates the colours of the rain — all the colours emerge.<br><br><strong>He tohu aroha tēnei i te tikanga Māori — he tohu nō ngā <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=atua\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">atua</a>.</strong><br>In Māori tradition, this is a sign of love — a sign from the gods.</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Explanation text structure:</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>Topic sentence</strong> — Ko te [X] he [definition/description].</li><li><strong>Process</strong> — Ka [verb] ana i [X], ka [result].</li><li><strong>Evidence/example</strong> — He tauira: [example].</li><li><strong>Cultural/additional meaning</strong> — I te tikanga Māori, ko te tikanga o tēnei ko…</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>New explanatory vocabulary:<br><strong>ina… ana</strong> — when / whenever (conditional time clause)<br><strong>tutaki</strong> — meet / encounter<br><strong>wehewehe</strong> — separate / divide<br><strong>ka puta mai</strong> — emerge / come forth<br><strong>he tohu nō ngā atua</strong> — a sign from the gods/spiritual realm<br><strong>atua</strong> — god / spiritual being / ancestor deity</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Tip: Ina… ana = whenever / when (that happens). This is your explanation connector — Ka X ina Y ana = X happens when Y happens. It is the te reo equivalent of &quot;when&quot; or &quot;whenever&quot; in an explanation.</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">We Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Kia Tūhono — Write an explanation</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Together we write an explanation of a natural phenomenon in te reo Māori.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Topic sentence: Ko te [phenomenon] he [definition]. What natural phenomenon are we explaining?</li><li>Process: Ka puta mai te [X] ina [Y] ana.</li><li>Evidence: He tauira: [specific example].</li><li>Cultural meaning: I te tikanga Māori, ko te tikanga o tēnei ko te…</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Discussion prompt: He tohu nō ngā atua — a sign from the gods. In te ao Māori, natural phenomena are not just physical events — they carry meaning. A rainbow is not just refracted light; it is a sign from the spiritual realm. How does science and spiritual meaning exist together in te ao Māori? Can both be true at the same time?</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">You Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Māu Anō — Explanation writing</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write an explanation of a natural phenomenon or concept in te reo Māori.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Write a topic sentence: Ko te [phenomenon] he [definition].</li><li>Write the process using ina… ana: Ka [result] ina [cause] ana.</li><li>Write a cultural meaning: I te tikanga Māori, ko te tikanga o tēnei ko te…</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write a full explanation (8 sentences) about a natural phenomenon, cultural practice, or concept from this term. Include: definition, process, example, cultural meaning, and your own reflection on what this means to you.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Thursday: Kōrero — He kōrero hūnuku — A deep conversation":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Te Aho Arataki Marau — All Term 3 AOs · AO 2.3: Extended sophisticated conversation</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Objectives</th><td class=\"lt-td\">AO 2.3 — Extended sophisticated conversation; AO 1.4 — Identity &amp; culture; AO 2.5 — Sophisticated vocabulary</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">I Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tāua Kōrero — A conversation about what matters</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This is the penultimate week of Term 3. Today we have the deepest conversation of the year — using whakataukī, environmental language, arts, identity, and sophisticated vocabulary to talk about what truly matters.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\"><strong>A:</strong> He aha te mea nui rawa atu ki a koe i tō ao?<br><strong>B:</strong> Ko tōku whānau, ko tōku reo, ko tōku taiao — ko ērā ngā <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=pou\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">pou</a> o tōku ora.<br><strong>A:</strong> He aha tō moemoeā mō Aotearoa?<br><strong>B:</strong> Ko tōku moemoeā: ka <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=toit%C5%AB\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">toitū</a> te reo Māori, ka toitū hoki te taiao — ka ora tātou katoa.<br><strong>A:</strong> He aha tō whakataukī pai rawa atu?<br><strong>B:</strong> &quot;Ehara tāku toa i te toa takitahi, he toa takitini.&quot; Ko tā tēnei mōhio ko te <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=mana\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">mana</a> o ngā tāngata katoa.<br><strong>A:</strong> Ka wani kē! He aha tāu e tūmanako ana mō ō uri?<br><strong>B:</strong> Kei te tūmanako au ka kōrero pai rātou i te reo Māori, ka <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=tiaki\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">tiaki</a> hoki rātou i tēnei whenua ātaahua.</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>New sophisticated vocabulary:<br><strong>ngā pou o tōku ora</strong> — the pillars of my life (pou = pillar)<br><strong>ka toitū</strong> — will endure / remain sustainable<br><strong>Ka toitū te reo, ka toitū te taiao.</strong> — When the language endures, the environment endures.<br><strong>mana o ngā tāngata katoa</strong> — the dignity/authority of all people<br><strong>tūmanako</strong> — hope / expectation<br><strong>He aha tāu e tūmanako ana mō ō uri?</strong><br>What do you hope for your descendants?<br><strong>Ka kōrero pai rātou</strong> — they will speak well<br><strong>tēnei whenua ātaahua</strong> — this beautiful land</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Tip: Ko ērā ngā pou o tōku ora — those are the pillars of my life. Pou appears again — the pillar that supports everything. Whānau, reo, taiao — three pillars. What are the three pillars of YOUR life? Can you say them in te reo?</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">We Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Kia Tūhono — Deep conversation</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Build the deepest te reo conversation of the year — about what truly matters.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I ask: He aha te mea nui rawa atu ki a koe i tō ao? Answer with Ko [X], ko [Y], ko [Z] — ngā pou o tōku ora.</li><li>I ask: He aha tō moemoeā mō Aotearoa? Answer with Ka toitū + what you hope will endure.</li><li>I ask: He aha tō whakataukī pai rawa atu? Say the whakataukī and explain its meaning.</li><li>I ask: He aha tāu e tūmanako ana mō ō uri? Answer with Kei te tūmanako au ka [hope].</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Discussion prompt: He aha tāu e tūmanako ana mō ō uri? What do you hope for your descendants? You are Year 5–6 students — your descendants are decades away. But you are also somebody's descendant right now. What do you think the people who came before you hoped for you? Have those hopes been realised?</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">You Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Māu Anō — Ngā pou o tōku ora</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write about the pillars of your life and your hopes.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Write Ko [X], ko [Y], ko [Z] — ngā pou o tōku ora.</li><li>Write your moemoeā for Aotearoa: Ko tōku moemoeā: ka toitū…</li><li>Write your hope for your uri: Kei te tūmanako au ka [hope] ō mātou uri.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write a full reflection (8 sentences) on what matters most — your pillars, your dreams, a whakataukī that guides you, your hope for the future. This is your Term 3 statement of identity. Make it true.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Friday: Aromatawai — Quiz & Review":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Monday questions</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>&quot;Ehara tāku toa i te toa takitahi, he toa takitini.&quot; What does this whakataukī mean?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>Strength comes from working alone </li><li>My strength is not from one — it is the strength of many together ✅</li><li>Competition makes us strong </li><li>The strongest person wins </li></ul></li><li>What is the &quot;rito&quot; in the harakeke whakataukī?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>The outer leaves of the flax </li><li>The ground where the flax grows </li><li>The heart shoot of the flax — representing the child/core of the family ✅</li><li>The bellbird </li></ul></li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tuesday questions</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>What does &quot;whakamātau&quot; mean?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>to observe </li><li>to research </li><li>to discover </li><li>to experiment / to test ✅</li></ul></li><li>&quot;Ka kitea e mātou ko te [finding].&quot; What grammatical feature is notable here?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>Ka for future tense </li><li>Passive voice — the discovery came to us ✅</li><li>E…ana for ongoing action </li><li>Ko for identity </li></ul></li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Wednesday questions</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>&quot;Ka puta mai te āniwaniwa ina tutaki ana te rā me te ua.&quot; What does ina…ana mean?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>because of </li><li>although </li><li>when / whenever (conditional) ✅</li><li>if </li></ul></li><li>What does &quot;wehewehe&quot; mean?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>to meet / encounter </li><li>to emerge </li><li>to separate / divide ✅</li><li>to observe </li></ul></li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Thursday questions</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>&quot;Ko ērā ngā pou o tōku ora.&quot; What does this sentence mean?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>Those are the problems in my life </li><li>Those are the pillars of my life ✅</li><li>Those are my children </li><li>Those are my dreams </li></ul></li><li>What does &quot;tūmanako&quot; mean?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>fear / worry </li><li>challenge </li><li>hope / expectation ✅</li><li>memory </li></ul></li></ul></div></div>"}}},"Week 9":{"label":"Day","children":{"Monday: Arotake — Term 3 vocabulary and structure review":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Te Aho Arataki Marau — All Term 3 AOs consolidated · Grammar Levels 1–2 complete</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Objectives</th><td class=\"lt-td\">AO 2.3 — Term 3 review; AO 2.5 — All vocabulary</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">I Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tāua Kōrero — Three terms of te reo Māori</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This is the second-to-last week of Term 3 and of your formal learning year. Today we celebrate everything Term 3 has added to your te reo Māori world — and look at how far three terms of daily practice has taken you.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Term 3 has added these domains to your toolkit:<br><br><strong>BODY AND HEALTH:</strong> māhunga, kanohi, ringa, waewae, mamae, kaimahi hauora, me okioki<br><strong>TENSE MASTERY:</strong> Kua (completed), E…ana (progressive), hiahia ki te, māku e, Ka taea e au te<br><strong>IDENTITY AND COMMUNITY:</strong> pepeha (full), marae, pōhiri, hongi, whaikōrero, uri, tīpuna<br><strong>SCHOOL AND LEARNING:</strong> akoranga subjects, evaluative language, taunakitanga<br><strong>ARTS AND CULTURE:</strong> toi whakairo, raranga, haka, waiata, tohunga<br><strong>ENVIRONMENT:</strong> kaitiakitanga, toitū, mauri, Ka ora te taiao ka ora tātou<br><strong>WISDOM:</strong> whakataukī, tikanga, tapu, noa, mana<br><strong>SCIENCE:</strong> whakamātau, rangahau, kitenga</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The growth across three terms:</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Term 1: &quot;Kei te haere au ki te kura.&quot;<br>Term 2: &quot;I te Rāhina, i haere mātou tokowhā ki te kura…&quot;<br>Term 3: &quot;Ko au tētahi kaitiaki o tēnei taiao — e ako ana au i te reo Māori hei tiaki i tōku ao, hei mōhio ki ngā mōrearea, hei tupu tōku ngākau i ngā whakataukī o ōku tīpuna.&quot;</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Translation: &quot;I am a guardian of this natural world — I am learning te reo Māori to protect my world, to understand the challenges, to grow my heart through the proverbs of my ancestors.&quot;</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>That sentence uses: Ko identity, E…ana progressive, hei (purpose), ngā tīpuna (ancestors), whakataukī, ngākau (heart), and multiple subordinate connections. You can write this. You are writing this.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Tip: Read that Term 3 sentence aloud. Slowly. Own every word of it. You earned it.</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">We Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Kia Tūhono — Term 3 blitz</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Category rapid fire — cover the whole term.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>BODY: From māhunga to waewae — name ten body parts in order, head to toe.</li><li>WHAKATAUKĪ: Who can say all four whakataukī from Week 8 from memory?</li><li>ARTS: Name all the toi forms — toi whakairo, toi raranga, toi puoro… aim for 8.</li><li>ENVIRONMENT: Ka ora te taiao, ka ora tātou — what does it mean and when would you use it?</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Discussion prompt: You have been learning te reo Māori for three terms — about 600 minutes of class time. In that time you have built a vocabulary of over 400 words and mastered 15+ grammar structures. What would you have thought at the start of Term 1 if someone told you this? What does it tell you about what daily practice can achieve?</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">You Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Māu Anō — Three-term audit</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>A personal vocabulary and structure audit across all three terms.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Write down every te reo word you can remember in 5 minutes — all three terms.</li><li>Write one sentence using each of the six tense markers: Kei te, I, Ka, Kua, E…ana, Kāore i.</li><li>Write your favourite whakataukī and explain what it means to you.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write the most complex te reo sentence you have ever written. Draw on everything from all three terms. Make it say something true about who you are and what you value.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Tuesday: Arotake — Grammar deep review":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Te Aho Arataki Marau — All grammar structures Terms 1–3 consolidated</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Objectives</th><td class=\"lt-td\">AO 2.4 — Tense mastery; AO 2.3 — Grammar consolidation</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">I Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tāua Kōrero — The complete grammar picture</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Today we put the entire three-term grammar picture on the table. This is the complete toolkit — every structure you have learned, organised clearly so you can see how they connect.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\"><strong>IDENTITY: Ko</strong> — Ko [name/thing] tōku [relationship].<br><strong>DESCRIPTION: He</strong> — He [adjective/noun] tēnei. He [adj] ake tērā.<br><strong>PRESENT: Kei te</strong> — Kei te [verb] [person] i [place].<br><strong>ONGOING: E…ana</strong> — E [verb] ana [person] i tēnei wā.<br><strong>PAST: I</strong> — I [verb] [person] i [time/place].<br><strong>NARRATIVE: Ka</strong> — Ka [verb] ia. Ka [verb] anō ia.<br><strong>PERFECT: Kua</strong> — Kua [verb] ia. Kua mutu.<br><strong>NEGATIVES: Kāore</strong> — Kāore [person] e [verb] ana. / Kāore [person] i [verb].<br><strong>WANT: Hiahia</strong> — Kei te hiahia [person] ki te [verb].<br><strong>WILL: Māku e</strong> — Māku/Māna e [verb].<br><strong>ABLE: Ka taea</strong> — Ka taea e [person] te [verb].<br><strong>RULES: Me/Kaua</strong> — Me [verb] tātou. Kaua e [verb].<br><strong>COMPLEX: Ahakoa/Mehemea/I te wā/Nō te mea</strong></blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The test of grammar mastery is not reciting rules — it is fluent use.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>A truly fluent sentence from a Term 3 student:</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&quot;Ahakoa i uaua ki a au i te tīmatanga, kua mārama ake au ināianei — ka taea e au te kōrero i te reo Māori, ka taea e au te tuhituhi hoki, ā ko tōku <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=moemoe%C4%81\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">moemoeā</a>: ka kōrero tonu au i ēnei kupu tapu mō ōku uri.&quot;</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Although it was difficult for me at the beginning, I now understand better — I am able to speak te reo Māori, I am able to write too, and my dream is: I will continue to speak these sacred words for my descendants.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Ahakoa (complex), Kua (perfect), Ka taea (ability), Ko + moemoeā (dream), Ka tonu (future ongoing). Every structure in one sentence.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Tip: Write the test sentence above in your own words. Change the details to make it true for YOU. That is grammar mastery — not knowing the names, but being able to SAY it.</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">We Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Kia Tūhono — Grammar challenge</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Build sentences matching each structure one by one.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Ko + He + Kei te in one passage — three sentences about yourself right now.</li><li>I + Ka + Kua in sequence — three moments from this morning.</li><li>Kāore e…ana + Kāore i + mehemea — three negative/conditional sentences.</li><li>Ahakoa + nō te mea + nō reira — one complex sentence with all three.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Discussion prompt: Look at that test sentence: ahakoa, kua, Ka taea, ko, moemoeā, ka tonu, uri. Seven different structures in one sentence. At the start of Term 1, how many of those words did you know? Now? What is the relationship between vocabulary and grammar — can you have one without the other?</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">You Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Māu Anō — Grammar self-test</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Test yourself on every major structure from three terms.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Write a sentence using each of: Ko, He, Kei te, E…ana, I, Ka, Kua.</li><li>Write a sentence using: Kāore e…ana, Kāore i, hiahia ki te, māku e, Ka taea.</li><li>Write a sentence using: ahakoa, nō te mea, mehemea, i te wā.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write a ten-sentence passage that uses at least fifteen different grammar structures correctly. This is your three-term grammar showcase.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Wednesday: Hanga rerenga — Showcase sentences: three terms":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Te Aho Arataki Marau — All AOs Terms 1–3 · Full sentence type showcase</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Objectives</th><td class=\"lt-td\">AO 2.3 — Showcase; AO 2.5 — Full vocabulary &amp; expression</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">I Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tāua Kōrero — Your best te reo ever</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Today you write the best sentences of your three-term te reo journey. There is no new content. Just you, your toolkit, and the task of producing your finest te reo Māori.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Student showcase examples from across the year:<br><br><strong>&quot;Ko te reo Māori ko tōku ara ki ngā tīpuna — he ara māmā, he ara aroha.&quot;</strong><br>(Te reo Māori is my path to the ancestors — a light path, a loving path.)<br><br><strong>&quot;I te wā e ako ana au i tēnei reo tapu, ka mārama aku karu ki tētahi ao hou — ko te ao Māori, ko te ao ātaahua.&quot;</strong><br>(While I was learning this sacred language, my eyes opened to a new world — the Māori world, the beautiful world.)<br><br><strong>&quot;Ka toitū te reo, ka toitū te tangata, ka toitū hoki te taiao — ko tērā tōku whakapono, ko tērā hoki tōku moemoeā.&quot;</strong><br>(When language endures, people endure, and the natural world endures — that is my belief, and that is also my dream.)</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>What makes these sentences exceptional:</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>→ They are PERSONAL — these could not have been written by anyone else<br>→ They use MULTIPLE STRUCTURES seamlessly<br>→ They say something the writer DEEPLY BELIEVES<br>→ They use FIGURATIVE LANGUAGE (ara = path, literal AND metaphorical)<br>→ They CONNECT three terms of learning (tīpuna, tapu, mauri, moemoeā, whakapono)</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Your showcase sentence should:<br>→ Use at least three different structures<br>→ Say something genuinely true about you<br>→ Include at least one word or phrase from each term<br>→ Feel like te reo Māori, not like an English sentence translated</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Tip: The best te reo sentence you can write today is the one that is most true. Not the most complex, not the longest — the most true. Write it in te reo Māori.</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">We Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Kia Tūhono — Best sentences together</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Class writes one final group showcase sentence — everyone contributes one element.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Opening: a Ko statement about what te reo Māori is to us.</li><li>Process: what happens when we learn it — E…ana, Ka, Kua.</li><li>Result: what it gives us — He [noun/quality] tēnei.</li><li>Closing: our hope for the future — Ko tērā tōku/ō mātou moemoeā.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Discussion prompt: Ka toitū te reo, ka toitū te tangata, ka toitū hoki te taiao. Three things endure together. If language endures, people endure. If people endure, the natural world is protected. Three things, one survival. Is this true? Is it possible for a language to disappear without its people also losing something essential?</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">You Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Māu Anō — Your showcase sentences</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write your three finest te reo Māori sentences of the year.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Write your best SIMPLE sentence — true and precise.</li><li>Write your best COMPLEX sentence — multiple structures, deep meaning.</li><li>Write your SHOWCASE SENTENCE — the single finest te reo Māori sentence you have ever produced.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write your &quot;three-term testament&quot; — three sentences, one from each term's perspective: who you were (Term 1), who you are becoming (Term 2), and who you intend to be (Term 3). Each sentence should use at least three structures from that term.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Thursday: Kōrero — Preparing for the Term 3 assessment":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Te Aho Arataki Marau — All AOs 1.1–2.5 · All six language modes</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Objectives</th><td class=\"lt-td\">AO 1.1 — Greetings; AO 1.2 — Introductions; AO 2.3 — Full conversation; AO 2.5 — Sophisticated vocabulary</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">I Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tāua Kōrero — The Term 3 standard</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Next week is your end-of-term assessment. Today we see what the Term 3 standard looks like — a conversation that draws on all three terms, uses sophisticated vocabulary and structures, and sounds like genuine te reo Māori.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\"><strong>A:</strong> Tēnā koe. Ko wai tōu ingoa? He aha tō pepeha?<br><strong>B:</strong> Ko [name] tōku ingoa. Ko [maunga] tōku maunga, ko [awa] tōku awa, nō [place] ahau.<br><strong>A:</strong> He aha ngā mea nui rawa atu ki a koe i tō ao?<br><strong>B:</strong> Ko ngā pou o tōku ora ko tōku whānau, ko tōku reo, ko tōku taiao. E ako ana au i te reo Māori hei tiaki i ēnei taonga.<br><strong>A:</strong> Kua tae ki a koe tēnei mōhiotanga?<br><strong>B:</strong> Āe, kua mārama ake au i ia wiki, i ia wiki. Ka taea e au te kōrero i ngā hanganga katoa ināianei.<br><strong>A:</strong> He aha tōu whakataukī e tūāhuatia ana i a koe?<br><strong>B:</strong> &quot;Ehara tāku toa i te toa takitahi, he toa takitini.&quot; Ko tā tēnei mōhio ko te mana o te mahi ngātahi.<br><strong>A:</strong> Ka wani kē! Tēnā koe, tēnā koe, tēnā koe.<br><strong>B:</strong> Tēnā koe, tēnā koe, tēnā koe.</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>What this conversation demonstrates:<br>✓ AO 1.1: Tēnā koe + three-part close<br>✓ AO 1.2: Full introduction with Ko + pepeha<br>✓ AO 1.4: Pepeha (maunga, awa, origin)<br>✓ AO 2.3: Extended sophisticated kōrero<br>✓ AO 2.5: Pou, taonga, mōhiotanga, whakataukī<br>✓ Grammar: Ko, E…ana, Kua, Ka taea, whakataukī explanation</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>New phrase:<br><strong>He aha tōu whakataukī e tūāhuatia ana i a koe?</strong><br>What proverb characterises / represents you?<br><strong>e tūāhuatia ana</strong> — that characterises / represents (passive E…ana)</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Tip: He aha tōu whakataukī e tūāhuatia ana i a koe? — What proverb represents you? This is a beautiful question. Before the assessment, choose YOUR whakataukī. The one that speaks to who you are. Be ready to say it and explain it.</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">We Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Kia Tūhono — Assessment preparation</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Full practice conversation — using the Term 3 standard as your guide.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Greet formally + full pepeha — all nine lines.</li><li>He aha ngā mea nui rawa atu ki a koe i tō ao? Answer with Ko ngā pou o tōku ora ko…</li><li>State your learning progress: Kua mārama ake au. Ka taea e au te…</li><li>Share your whakataukī + explanation + three-part close.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Discussion prompt: He aha tōu whakataukī e tūāhuatia ana i a koe? Which whakataukī represents you? &quot;He toa takitini&quot; (strength of many) — &quot;Whāia te iti kahurangi&quot; (pursue excellence) — &quot;He tangata, he tangata, he tangata&quot; (it is people). Before you answer, sit with it. The right whakataukī should feel like it has always been yours.</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">You Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Māu Anō — Assessment preparation</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Prepare for the Term 3 end-of-term assessment.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Write and memorise your full pepeha — all nine lines.</li><li>Write Ko ngā pou o tōku ora ko… (three pillars of your life).</li><li>Choose your whakataukī — write it and its explanation: Ko tā tēnei mōhio ko te…</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write the full conversation you plan to have in the assessment (12 lines minimum). Practise it until you can say it without looking. Then say it to a family member tonight.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Friday: Aromatawai — Quiz & Review":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Monday questions</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Which of these is a Term 3 structure that was NOT taught in Terms 1 or 2?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>Kei te </li><li>Ko </li><li>Kua (perfect aspect) ✅</li><li>Kāore </li></ul></li><li>&quot;Ka ora te taiao, ka ora tātou.&quot; In what context would you most naturally use this sentence?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>When describing the weather </li><li>When talking about kaitiakitanga and environmental responsibility ✅</li><li>When explaining why you are late </li><li>When introducing yourself </li></ul></li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tuesday questions</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>&quot;Ahakoa i uaua ki a au i te tīmatanga, kua mārama ake au ināianei.&quot; What two structures are combined here?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>Ka and Kei te </li><li>Ahakoa (complex) and Kua (perfect aspect) ✅</li><li>I and E…ana </li><li>Ko and He </li></ul></li><li>&quot;Ka taea e au te kōrero i te reo Māori.&quot; What does this sentence express?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>I want to speak te reo Māori </li><li>I used to be able to speak te reo Māori </li><li>I am able to speak te reo Māori ✅</li><li>I am learning to speak te reo Māori </li></ul></li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Wednesday questions</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>What makes a showcase sentence different from a grammar exercise sentence?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>It is always longer </li><li>It uses more vocabulary words </li><li>It says something the writer truly means and has a genuine voice ✅</li><li>It always uses Kua </li></ul></li><li>&quot;Ko te reo Māori ko tōku ara ki ngā tīpuna.&quot; What does ara mean here?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>language </li><li>path / way ✅</li><li>ancestor </li><li>light </li></ul></li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Thursday questions</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>&quot;He aha tōu whakataukī e tūāhuatia ana i a koe?&quot; What does tūāhuatia mean here?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>that you have memorised </li><li>that characterises / represents you (passive) ✅</li><li>that you wrote yourself </li><li>that is the most famous </li></ul></li><li>What are the &quot;ngā pou o tōku ora&quot;?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>the rules of my school </li><li>the challenges of my life </li><li>the pillars of my life ✅</li><li>the goals for next term </li></ul></li></ul></div></div>"}}},"Week 10":{"label":"Day","children":{"Monday: Arotake — End-of-term vocabulary celebration":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Te Aho Arataki Marau — All AOs Terms 1–3 · Complete vocabulary review</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Objectives</th><td class=\"lt-td\">AO 1.1 — Greetings; AO 2.5 — All vocabulary; AO 2.3 — All structures</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">I Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tāua Kōrero — Three terms: a vocabulary celebration</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Today we celebrate. In three terms of daily warm-up practice, you have built something remarkable. Let us count it, celebrate it, and prepare to show it.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Three terms of te reo Māori. Here is your vocabulary toolkit:<br><br>TERM 1: Greetings · Ko · He · Kei te · Numbers 1–10 · food · places · wants · feelings · adjectives · conjunctions · adverbs · prepositions<br><br>TERM 2: I (past) · Ka (narrative) · Kua (perfect) · Kāore (negative) · All 16 pronouns · Numbers 1–100 · Dates/times/months · Colours · Comparatives · Weather · Seasons · Emotions · Character · Arts<br><br>TERM 3: Body · E…ana · Kua · hiahia · māku e · Ka taea · Pepeha (full) · Marae · Haka · Whakataukī · Kaitiakitanga · Science vocabulary · Formal/informal register · Argument and persuasion</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>What you can now DO:</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>✓ Have a sophisticated 10-minute conversation entirely in te reo Māori<br>✓ Write a full pepeha and deliver it from memory<br>✓ Explain a whakataukī and its deeper meaning<br>✓ Write a persuasive argument using taunakitanga<br>✓ Describe past, present, and future events fluently<br>✓ Express emotions, opinions, goals, and dreams<br>✓ Talk about the environment, the arts, the body, and the community<br>✓ Switch between formal and casual registers<br>✓ Use all 16 pronouns correctly<br>✓ Count to 100, say the time, and give the date<br>✓ Understand and produce complex sentences with subordinate clauses</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This is real. You did this. Ka tino pai!</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Tip: Ka tino pai! — genuinely, truly excellent. Not just &quot;good&quot; — tino good. You have earned tino.</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">We Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Kia Tūhono — Three-term blitz</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The fastest, most comprehensive vocabulary review of the year.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>PEPEHA: One student delivers their full pepeha from memory. Class listens and checks.</li><li>WHAKATAUKĪ: Who can say all four from Term 3 Week 8? Class helps where needed.</li><li>NUMBERS: Count from 1 to 100 as a class in te reo — everyone joins in.</li><li>SHOWCASE: Each student says their best single te reo sentence of the year. No repeats.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Discussion prompt: Your best single te reo sentence of the year. Not the longest. Not the most complicated. The most TRUE. What do you want to say, in te reo Māori, about your year of learning? Say it. Own it.</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">You Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Māu Anō — Final vocabulary audit</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Your last vocabulary audit of the year.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Write every te reo word you know in 5 minutes. Count them.</li><li>Write what you know today that you did not know at the start of Term 1.</li><li>Write your three best te reo sentences of the year.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write a letter to your next-year self in te reo Māori — what you have learned, what you struggled with, what you are proud of, and what you hope they will continue. At least ten sentences. This letter is evidence of your year.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Tuesday: Arotake — Grammar final review":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Te Aho Arataki Marau — All grammar structures Terms 1–3</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Objectives</th><td class=\"lt-td\">AO 2.4 — Tense mastery; AO 2.3 — Grammar review</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">I Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tāua Kōrero — Every structure, one final time</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Today we review every grammar structure from three terms — one final time. This is not about memorising rules. It is about confirming what you already know.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Every structure in one passage:<br><br><strong>Ko ahau ko [name]. He ākonga au i tēnei kura.</strong> (Ko · He)<br><strong>Kei te ako tonu au i te reo Māori — e tipu ana tōku māramatanga i ia rā, i ia rā.</strong> (Kei te · E…ana)<br><strong>I tīmata au i tērā tau — kāore au i mōhio ki ētahi kupu.</strong> (I · Kāore i)<br><strong>Ka haere au ki te kura. Ka ako au. Ka hari tōku ngākau.</strong> (Ka sequence)<br><strong>Kua mārama ake au — kua tipu tōku reo.</strong> (Kua)<br><strong>Ka taea e au te kōrero i te reo Māori, ka taea e au te tuhituhi hoki.</strong> (Ka taea)<br><strong>Ahakoa i uaua, i mahi tonu au nō te mea ko tōku <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=moemoe%C4%81\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">moemoeā</a>: ka kōrero tōku whānau i te reo Māori.</strong> (Ahakoa · Nō te mea · Ko)</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Count the structures in that passage:</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Ko (identity)</li><li>He (description)</li><li>Kei te (present)</li><li>E…ana (progressive)</li><li>I (past)</li><li>Kāore i (past negative)</li><li>Ka (narrative × 3)</li><li>Kua (perfect × 2)</li><li>Ka taea (ability × 2)</li><li>Ahakoa (complex concession)</li><li>Nō te mea (reason)</li><li>Ko + moemoeā (dream statement)</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Twelve structures in seven sentences. You know all of them.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Tip: You know all of these. You have used them all. Today's review is not about learning — it is about confidence. Read the passage aloud. Feel how natural it sounds. That is YOUR voice in te reo Māori.</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">We Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Kia Tūhono — Structure identification</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Read the I Do passage aloud together. Identify every structure as a class.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Identify: Ko, He, Kei te, E…ana — point to each one as you read.</li><li>Identify: I, Kāore i, Ka (three times), Kua (twice).</li><li>Identify: Ka taea (twice), Ahakoa, Nō te mea, Ko + moemoeā.</li><li>Now write your own passage — same structures, your own words and story.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Discussion prompt: Read your own three-term passage aloud. This is the grammar of your story — your Ko, your He, your Kua, your Ka taea. Every structure you know is in service of what YOU want to say. Grammar is not the goal — it is the tool. What story do you want to tell with these tools?</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">You Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Māu Anō — Grammar showcase</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write a passage that uses every major structure from three terms.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Write three sentences using Ko, He, and Kei te.</li><li>Write three sentences using I, Ka, and Kua.</li><li>Write three sentences using Ka taea, ahakoa, and nō te mea.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Combine all nine sentences into one flowing passage. Add connective words to make it flow. This is your three-term grammar showcase.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Wednesday: Hanga rerenga — Three-term portfolio sentence":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Te Aho Arataki Marau — All AOs · All sentence types Terms 1–3</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Objectives</th><td class=\"lt-td\">AO 2.3 — Portfolio showcase; AO 2.5 — Full expression</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">I Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tāua Kōrero — Your portfolio sentence</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Today you write THE sentence — your single finest piece of te reo Māori from three terms of learning. This sentence will go in your portfolio, on the wall, and in your memory. It should say who you are, what matters to you, and what you carry into the future.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Portfolio sentences from imagined students across the year:<br><br><strong>&quot;Ka toitū te reo o ōku tīpuna i roto i āku kupu, i roto i āku mahi, i roto i āku moemoeā.&quot;</strong><br>(The language of my ancestors endures within my words, within my actions, within my dreams.)<br><br><strong>&quot;He ākonga noa iho ahau — engari, ko tōku ngākau e hiahia ana ki te tiaki i tēnei <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=taonga\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">taonga</a> ātaahua, ko te reo Māori.&quot;</strong><br>(I am just a learner — but my heart desires to protect this beautiful treasure, te reo Māori.)<br><br><strong>&quot;I tīmata au i tētahi kupu — ināianei, kua tipu ōku rerenga ki tētahi ao hou, ko te ao Māori.&quot;</strong><br>(I started with one word — now, my sentences have grown into a new world, the Māori world.)</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>What makes a portfolio sentence:</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>→ It is PERSONAL — only you could have written it<br>→ It is TRUE — you believe every word<br>→ It CONNECTS to something bigger than yourself (reo, taiao, tīpuna, uri)<br>→ It uses MULTIPLE STRUCTURES seamlessly<br>→ It SOUNDS BEAUTIFUL — read it aloud and feel it</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The three examples above each use:<br>&quot;Ka toitū&quot; — enduring / the perfect aspect<br>&quot;Ko tōku ngākau e hiahia ana&quot; — heart progressive want<br>&quot;Kua tipu&quot; — has grown (perfect) into a new world</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Your portfolio sentence is not a homework task. It is a declaration.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Tip: He ākonga noa iho ahau — I am just a learner. And yet — ko tōku ngākau e hiahia ana ki te tiaki i tēnei taonga. The heart wants to protect it. Humility + passion = the perfect learner of te reo Māori.</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">We Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Kia Tūhono — Draft together</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Class writes one group portfolio sentence — and each student writes their own.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Class sentence: Ko mātou ngā ākonga o tēnei akomanga — ka tipu tō mātou reo i ia rā, i ia rā.</li><li>Now each student writes their own first draft — alone, in silence, in te reo.</li><li>Share with a partner — read aloud, give feedback, improve.</li><li>Final version: write it clearly, read it to the class.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Discussion prompt: I tīmata au i tētahi kupu — I started with one word. What was your first word in te reo Māori? Kia ora? Āe? Your name in Ko? How many words do you know now? What does that journey — from one word to hundreds — feel like to look back on?</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">You Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Māu Anō — Portfolio sentence</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write your portfolio sentence.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Draft: write three possible portfolio sentences — different approaches, different feelings.</li><li>Choose: which one is most true? Which one sounds most like you in te reo?</li><li>Final: write your chosen sentence carefully. This is your keepsake.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write a portfolio paragraph (5 sentences) — your portfolio sentence as the opening, followed by four sentences that expand on it. What does this sentence say about who you are, what you have learned, and where you are going?</li></ul></div></div>"},"Thursday: Kōrero — He whakamātautau — End of Term 3 assessment":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Te Aho Arataki Marau — All AOs 1.1–2.5 · All six language modes assessed</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Objectives</th><td class=\"lt-td\">AO 1.1 — Greetings; AO 1.2 — Introductions; AO 1.4 — Personal information; AO 2.3 — Full sophisticated conversation; AO 2.5 — Sophisticated vocabulary</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">I Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tāua Kōrero — The Term 3 standard conversation</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This is your final spoken assessment for Term 3. It is also the most sophisticated conversation you will have had in te reo Māori. Here is what the full Term 3 standard looks like:</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\"><strong>A:</strong> Tēnā koe. Ko wai koe? He aha tō pepeha?<br><strong>B:</strong> [Full nine-line pepeha from memory]<br><strong>A:</strong> Ka pai tō pepeha! He aha ngā pou o tōu ora?<br><strong>B:</strong> Ko ngā pou o tōku ora ko tōku whānau, ko tōku reo, ko tōku taiao — ko ērā ngā mea e tiaki ana i a au.<br><strong>A:</strong> He aha tō whakataukī e tūāhuatia ana i a koe?<br><strong>B:</strong> &quot;Ehara tāku toa i te toa takitahi, he toa takitini.&quot; Ko tā tēnei mōhio ko te mana o te mahi ngātahi — ko tērā tōku whakapono.<br><strong>A:</strong> He aha tōu moemoeā mō tō reo Māori?<br><strong>B:</strong> Ko tōku moemoeā: ka kōrero tōku whānau i te reo Māori, ka toitū hoki tēnei reo <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=tapu\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">tapu</a> mō ōku uri.<br><strong>A:</strong> Ka wani kē tāu kōrero! Tēnā koe, tēnā koe, tēnā koe.<br><strong>B:</strong> Tēnā koe, tēnā koe, tēnā koe.</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Assessment checklist:<br>✓ AO 1.1: Tēnā koe + Tēnā koe ×3 close<br>✓ AO 1.2: Full pepeha with Ko sentences<br>✓ AO 1.4: Maunga, awa, iwi, hapū, marae, kura, ingoa, origin<br>✓ AO 2.3: Extended sophisticated conversation<br>✓ AO 2.5: Pou, moemoeā, whakataukī, whakapono, uri, toitū<br>✓ Grammar: Ko ×9 (pepeha), E…ana, Ka, Kua, ahakoa (embedded)</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Tip: Deliver your pepeha with pride. It carries your mountain, your river, your people, and your name. There is no more important thing you can say in te reo Māori.</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">We Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Kia Tūhono — Final practice</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>One complete practice run with a partner before the assessment.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Full pepeha — partner listens and checks all nine lines.</li><li>Ko ngā pou o tōu ora ko… — three pillars, stated with confidence.</li><li>Whakataukī + explanation — say it, explain both levels of meaning.</li><li>Moemoeā + three-part close — Tēnā koe, tēnā koe, tēnā koe.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Discussion prompt: Ko tōku moemoeā: ka kōrero tōku whānau i te reo Māori. Is this your dream too? Or is it something else? Before your assessment, sit quietly for one minute and answer: what do YOU hope for your te reo Māori — not for the assessment, not for the teacher, but for yourself, for your life?</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">You Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Māu Anō — He whakamātautau — Term 3 assessment</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Your end-of-term spoken assessment.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>Task 1:</strong> Full pepeha from memory — all nine lines.</li><li><strong>Task 2:</strong> Ko ngā pou o tōu ora + whakataukī + moemoeā — at least 6 sentences.</li><li><strong>Task 3:</strong> Full conversation with a partner — at least 12 lines each.</li><li>✦ Assessment checklist: ☐ Pepeha (9 lines) · ☐ Ko ngā pou o tōu ora · ☐ Whakataukī + explanation · ☐ Moemoeā statement · ☐ Ka taea sentence · ☐ Tēnā koe ×3 close</li></ul></div></div>"},"Friday: Aromatawai — Quiz & Review":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Monday questions</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Which Term 3 structure expresses a completed action whose result is relevant now?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>Kei te </li><li>Ka </li><li>I </li><li>Kua ✅</li></ul></li><li>&quot;Ko ngā pou o tōku ora ko tōku whānau, ko tōku reo, ko tōku taiao.&quot; What are the three pou?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>school, friends, sport </li><li>family, language, natural world ✅</li><li>food, shelter, water </li><li>work, rest, play </li></ul></li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tuesday questions</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Identify the structure: &quot;E tipu ana tōku māramatanga i ia rā, i ia rā.&quot;<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>Kei te present </li><li>Ka narrative </li><li>E…ana progressive — ongoing growth ✅</li><li>I past </li></ul></li><li>&quot;Ahakoa i uaua, i mahi tonu au.&quot; What does ahakoa signal?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>a result — because it was hard I worked </li><li>a time — when it was hard I worked </li><li>a concession — DESPITE it being hard, I kept working ✅</li><li>a condition — if it is hard I will work </li></ul></li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Wednesday questions</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>What is a &quot;portfolio sentence&quot;?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>The longest sentence you can write </li><li>A sentence for grammar practice only </li><li>Your finest, most personal, most true te reo sentence — a declaration of who you are ✅</li><li>A sentence that uses every vocabulary word </li></ul></li><li>&quot;I tīmata au i tētahi kupu — ināianei, kua tipu ōku rerenga ki tētahi ao hou.&quot; What does &quot;ao hou&quot; mean?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>a new day </li><li>a new world / new realm ✅</li><li>a new language </li><li>a new friend </li></ul></li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Thursday questions</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>What is the correct order for a full pepeha?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>Name → mountain → river → iwi → school → origin </li><li>Mountain → river → waka → iwi → hapū → marae → school → name → origin ✅</li><li>Origin → name → iwi → mountain → river </li><li>School → name → iwi → hapū → origin </li></ul></li><li>&quot;Ko tōku moemoeā: ka kōrero tōku whānau i te reo Māori.&quot; What does this dream express?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>That the speaker will learn English </li><li>That the speaker's family will speak te reo Māori ✅</li><li>That te reo Māori will be taught in all schools </li><li>That the speaker will win a competition </li></ul></li></ul></div></div>"}}}}},"Term 4":{"label":"Week","children":{"Week 1":{"label":"Day","children":{"Monday: Tēnā koutou katoa — Formal greetings and register mastery":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Te Aho Arataki Marau — AO 1.1: Greetings · AO 1.6: Politeness conventions · AO 1.2: Formal introductions</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Objectives</th><td class=\"lt-td\">AO 1.1 — Formal greetings; AO 1.6 — Register mastery</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">I Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tāua Kōrero — The full greeting system</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Welcome to Term 4 — Kia Mārama, the term of fluency. This term we bring everything together and reach for genuine spontaneous conversation. We begin where te reo Māori always begins — with greetings. But this term, we master the FULL system — every register, every occasion, every nuance.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\"><strong>CASUAL (hoa, tamariki):</strong><br><strong>Kia ora!</strong> — Hello / Thank you<br><strong>Kia ora kōrua/koutou!</strong> — Hello you two / Hello everyone<br><br><strong>FORMAL SINGULAR:</strong><br><strong>Tēnā koe.</strong> — Greetings to you (one person)<br><br><strong>FORMAL DUAL:</strong><br><strong>Tēnā kōrua.</strong> — Greetings to you two<br><br><strong>FORMAL PLURAL:</strong><br><strong>Tēnā koutou.</strong> — Greetings to you all (3+)<br><strong>Tēnā koutou katoa.</strong> — Greetings to each and every one of you<br><br><strong>FAREWELL SYSTEM:</strong><br><strong>Hei konā rā.</strong> — Goodbye (speaker is leaving)<br><strong>E noho rā.</strong> — Goodbye (listener is staying)<br><strong>Ā, ka kite anō.</strong> — Until we meet again<br><strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=M%C4%81+te+w%C4%81\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Mā te wā</a>.</strong> — Until another time / Farewell (for a long parting)</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The greeting system is built on the pronoun system you know:</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>koe (you, 1) → Tēnā koe<br>kōrua (you 2) → Tēnā kōrua<br>koutou (you 3+) → Tēnā koutou</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>For acknowledgement and gratitude:<br><strong>Tēnā koe mō tāu āwhina.</strong><br>Thank you for your help.<br>(Tēnā koe + mō + reason for thanks)</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tēnā koutou mō ā koutou mahi.</strong><br>Thank you all for your work.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>For welcoming:<br><strong>Nau mai, haere mai!</strong> — Welcome! Come in!<br><strong>Nau mai ki tō mātou kura.</strong><br>Welcome to our school.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>New word:<br><strong>mō</strong> — for / about / on account of (used for giving thanks)</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Tip: Tēnā koe mō [reason] = thank you for [reason]. This is the complete thank-you formula in formal te reo. Tēnā koe alone = greeting. Tēnā koe mō tāu mahi = thank you for your work.</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">We Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Kia Tūhono — Full greeting system practice</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Rapid fire — I describe the situation, you give the right greeting.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Greeting the whole class formally: <strong>Tēnā koutou katoa.</strong> Now greet two students: <strong>Tēnā kōrua.</strong></li><li>Thanking a teacher for help: <strong>Tēnā koe mō tāu āwhina.</strong> Thanking the whole class: <strong>Tēnā koutou mō ā koutou mahi.</strong></li><li>Saying goodbye as you leave: <strong>Hei konā rā.</strong> Staying while someone else leaves: <strong>E noho rā.</strong></li><li>Welcoming a visitor: <strong>Nau mai, haere mai! Nau mai ki tō mātou kura.</strong></li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Discussion prompt: E noho rā (you stay) vs Hei konā rā (farewell where I am). These two farewells tell you who is moving. Why does te reo Māori distinguish who is leaving and who is staying? What does this say about how Māori thinks about presence and movement?</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">You Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Māu Anō — Greeting mastery</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Use the full greeting system in real sentences.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Write the correct greeting for: your teacher, two classmates, the whole school assembly.</li><li>Write a formal thank-you: Tēnā koe/koutou mō… (three different reasons for thanks).</li><li>Write the correct farewell for: you are leaving, your friend is leaving, it is the end of term.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write a full formal welcome speech (8 sentences) for a visitor to your school — Nau mai, greeting by role, thank them for coming, introduce the school, close with Tēnā koutou ×3.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Tuesday: Ngā kupu hono hou — Advanced linking and cohesion":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Te Aho Arataki Marau — AO 2.3: Extended communication · Grammar Level 1–2: Advanced cohesion, discourse markers</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Objectives</th><td class=\"lt-td\">AO 2.3 — Advanced cohesion; AO 2.5 — Discourse markers</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">I Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tāua Kōrero — Linking ideas like a fluent speaker</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Fluent speakers do not just string sentences together — they signal the relationship between ideas with precision. Today we learn the advanced linking toolkit — the words and phrases that mark transitions, summaries, contrasts, and conclusions in extended te reo Māori.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\"><strong>To add / expand:</strong><br><strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=waihoki\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">waihoki</a></strong> — moreover / and also<br><strong>ā, ko tērā anō</strong> — and there is also this<br><strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=k%C4%81ore+noa+iho\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">kāore noa iho</a></strong> — not only that<br><br><strong>To contrast:</strong><br><strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=heoi+an%C5%8D\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">heoi anō</a></strong> — however<br><strong>engari rā</strong> — but actually / however (stronger) / however (stronger)<br><strong>ahakoa ōna āhuatanga</strong> — despite its qualities<br><br><strong>To conclude:</strong><br><strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=n%C5%8D+reira\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">nō reira</a></strong> — therefore / so<br><strong>ko te <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=mutunga\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">mutunga</a></strong> — in conclusion<br><strong>hei <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=whakakapi\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">whakakapi</a></strong> — to conclude / to summarise / to summarise<br><br><strong>To illustrate:</strong><br><strong>hei <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=tauira\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">tauira</a></strong> — for example<br><strong>pēnei i te</strong> — such as / like<br><strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=ar%C4%81\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">arā</a></strong> — that is / namely</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>In use:</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>He taonga te reo Māori. Kāore noa iho — he taonga ā-ahurea hoki.</strong><br>Te reo Māori is a treasure. Not only that — it is also a cultural treasure.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>He nui ngā hua o te ako i te reo Māori. Heoi anō, he uaua hoki.</strong><br>There are many benefits of learning te reo. However, it is also difficult.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Ko te mutunga, me tiaki tātou i tēnei reo mō āpōpō ō ngā uri.</strong><br>In conclusion, we must protect this language for tomorrow's descendants.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Hei tauira, ko te haka tētahi mahi toi rongonui — arā, he tohu aroha me he tohu kaha.</strong><br>For example, haka is a famous art form — namely, a sign of love and a sign of strength.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>New words:<br><strong>ā-ahurea</strong> — cultural (ā + ahurea = cultural/relating to culture)<br><strong>hei whakakapi</strong> — to summarise / in summary</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Tip: Hei tauira = for example. Arā = namely / that is. Use these when you want to illustrate a point with a specific case. They signal: &quot;I am about to give you evidence for what I just said.&quot;</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">We Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Kia Tūhono — Advanced linking</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>I give you two sentences — you choose the best advanced linking word and explain why.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>&quot;He pai te reo Māori.&quot; + &quot;He tino rangatira hoki.&quot; → Expanding: <strong>Kāore noa iho — he tino rangatira hoki te reo Māori.</strong></li><li>&quot;He nui ngā ākonga i tēnei kura.&quot; + &quot;Ko tētahi āhuatanga pai tērā.&quot; → Concluding: <strong>Ko te mutunga, he kura nui tēnei.</strong></li><li>&quot;I mahi kaha mātou.&quot; + &quot;I toa mātou.&quot; → Giving an example: <strong>Hei tauira — i toa mātou i te kaupeka hākinakina o tērā wiki.</strong></li><li>Write your own: an argument about kaitiakitanga using waihoki, heoi anō, and nō reira.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Discussion prompt: Ko te mutunga — in conclusion. Hei whakakapi — to summarise. These are the phrases that close an argument. In te ao Māori, how you close a kōrero is as important as how you open it. Why? What does a strong conclusion do that a weak one doesn't?</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">You Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Māu Anō — Advanced linking sentences</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Use advanced discourse markers in extended writing.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Write three sentences using waihoki, heoi anō, and hei tauira — one each.</li><li>Write a Ko te mutunga or hei whakakapi conclusion sentence about something you believe.</li><li>Use arā to clarify a point: state something general, then use arā to specify.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write an argument paragraph (8 sentences) about te reo Māori. Use at least five different linking markers: kāore noa iho, heoi anō, hei tauira, arā, and Ko te mutunga.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Wednesday: Hanga rerenga — All structures, full fluency":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Te Aho Arataki Marau — AO 2.3: Extended communication · All grammar structures Terms 1–4</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Objectives</th><td class=\"lt-td\">AO 2.3 — Full fluency writing; AO 2.5 — Complete vocabulary</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">I Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tāua Kōrero — Writing with everything you have</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This is Term 4, Week 1. You have three terms of te reo Māori behind you. Today we write without guardrails — using everything you know, in whatever order serves what you want to say. This is what fluency looks like.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\"><strong>A Term 4 student's passage:</strong><br><br>Ko au ko Hēmi. He ākonga au i tēnei kura, he kaitiaki hoki o tēnei taiao.<br>I tīmata au i tētahi kupu — &quot;Kia ora&quot; — ināianei, kua <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=tipu\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">tipu</a> tōku reo ki tētahi mea nui ake.<br>E ako tonu ana au — kāore anō kua oti — engari, ka taea e au te kōrero i ngā āhuatanga nui o tōku ao ināianei.<br>Hei tauira: ka taea e au te <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=mihi\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">mihi</a>, ka taea e au te tuhituhi, ka taea e au te whakaaro i te reo Māori.<br>Ko te mutunga, ko tēnei reo tōku ara ki ngā <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=t%C4%ABpuna\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">tīpuna</a>, ki ngā <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=uri\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">uri</a>, ā ki tōku ake ngākau.</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Notice every structure in that passage:</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Ko (×2 — identity)<br>He (×2 — description)<br>I tīmata au — past<br>Kua tipu — perfect aspect<br>E ako tonu ana — E…ana progressive<br>Kāore anō kua oti — Kua negative<br>Ka taea e au te — ability (×3)<br>Hei tauira — for example<br>Ko te mutunga — in conclusion<br>Ko + Ko — identity statement chain</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>New phrase:<br><strong>tōku ake ngākau</strong> — my very own heart (ake = own/particular when after a possessive)<br><strong>Tōku ake</strong> = my very own (more emphatic than tōku alone)</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Tip: Tōku ake = my very own. Tāu ake = your very own. Ake after a possessive adds the sense of &quot;own&quot; — particular to you, belonging to no one else. Use it when you want to say something is truly, personally yours.</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">We Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Kia Tūhono — Free writing with everything</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>I give you a topic — you write freely, using any structures that serve what you want to say.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Topic: what te reo Māori means to you. Write four sentences — no structure requirements, just truth.</li><li>Topic: your biggest achievement this year. Write four sentences — past, present, future all welcome.</li><li>Topic: someone who has influenced you. Write four sentences — any structures, show don't tell.</li><li>Read your sentences aloud. Which one sounds most like YOUR voice in te reo? Why?</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Discussion prompt: Ko tēnei reo tōku ara ki ngā tīpuna, ki ngā uri, ā ki tōku ake ngākau. The language is a path to the ancestors, to the descendants, and to my very own heart. Three destinations. Which of the three feels most real to you right now?</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">You Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Māu Anō — Free writing</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write freely in te reo Māori for ten minutes. No constraints. Just truth.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Write for 5 minutes without stopping — anything on your mind in te reo.</li><li>Read back what you wrote. Underline the structures you used without thinking.</li><li>Choose your best sentence from your free writing. Improve it once.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write a full free-writing piece (10 sentences) on any topic you choose. After writing, annotate every structure you used. Count them. This is evidence of your fluency.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Thursday: Kōrero — He mihi whakamutunga — End of year farewell language":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Te Aho Arataki Marau — AO 1.1: Greetings/farewells · AO 1.6: Politeness · AO 2.3: Extended formal communication</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Objectives</th><td class=\"lt-td\">AO 1.1 — Farewell language; AO 1.6 — Formal occasion language</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">I Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tāua Kōrero — Language for endings and new beginnings</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Term 4 is a time of endings and transitions — the end of the year, farewells to classmates, transitions to new classes. Today we learn the language of endings and new beginnings in te reo Māori.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\"><strong>Mā te wā.</strong> — Until another time / Farewell (for a long parting)<br><strong>Ā, ka kite anō.</strong> — Until we meet again<br><strong>Hei konā rā.</strong> — Farewell (I am leaving)<br><strong>E noho rā.</strong> — Farewell (you stay, I go)<br><br><strong>For a year's end:</strong><br><strong>I tēnei tau, kua tipu tātou katoa.</strong><br>This year, we have all grown.<br><br><strong>He tau tino pai tēnei mōku.</strong><br>This has been a very good year for me.<br><br><strong>Tēnā koutou katoa mō ā koutou mahi i tēnei tau.</strong><br>Thank you all for your work this year.<br><br><strong>Ka nui ōku <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=mihi\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">mihi</a> ki a koutou.</strong><br>My greetings/gratitude to you all is great.</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Transition language:<br><strong>Ā tērā tau</strong> — next year<br><strong>Ā tērā kura</strong> — at the next school<br><strong>Ka tipu tonu</strong> — will keep growing<br><strong>Ka huri ake</strong> — will turn / transition<br><strong>Me mōhio tātou</strong> — we should know / let us remember<br><strong>Ko tōku inoi</strong> — my wish / prayer<br><strong>Ka nui ōku mihi</strong> — my greetings/thanks are great</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Farewell speech structure:</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Tēnā koutou katoa.</li><li>I tēnei tau, kua…</li><li>He tau [adjective] tēnei nō te mea…</li><li>Tēnā koutou mō…</li><li>Ko tōku inoi mō koutou: ka…</li><li>Tēnā koutou, tēnā koutou, tēnā koutou katoa.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>New phrase:<br><strong>Ko tōku inoi mō koutou</strong> — my wish/prayer for you all</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Tip: Ka nui ōku mihi ki a koutou — my greetings and thanks to you all are great. This phrase does double work — it both greets and thanks. In te ao Māori, mihi (greeting/tribute) and gratitude are the same action.</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">We Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Kia Tūhono — Farewell conversation</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Build a year-end farewell exchange.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Opening: <strong>Tēnā koutou katoa. I tēnei tau, kua tipu tātou katoa.</strong> What has grown? Add specifics.</li><li>Thank: <strong>Tēnā koutou mō ā koutou mahi i tēnei tau.</strong> What specific things are you thanking them for?</li><li>Wish: <strong>Ko tōku inoi mō koutou: ka [hope for them next year].</strong></li><li>Close: <strong>Ka nui ōku mihi ki a koutou. Tēnā koutou ×3. Mā te wā.</strong></li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Discussion prompt: Ko tōku inoi mō koutou — my wish/prayer for you. What IS your wish for your classmates for next year? In te reo Māori? Try to say it. Ka [verb] koutou — may you all [verb]. This is one of the most generous things you can say to someone.</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">You Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Māu Anō — End of year farewell speech</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write a farewell speech for the end of the school year.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Open: Tēnā koutou katoa. I tēnei tau, kua… (what has happened/changed).</li><li>Thank: Tēnā koutou mō ā koutou mahi — be specific about three things.</li><li>Wish: Ko tōku inoi mō koutou: ka [three wishes].</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write your full farewell speech (10 sentences) following the six-step structure. Deliver it to the class — without reading if possible.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Friday: Aromatawai — Quiz & Review":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Monday questions</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>You are leaving a room and someone is staying. Which farewell do YOU say?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>E noho rā </li><li>Hei konā rā ✅</li><li>Mā te wā </li><li>Ā ka kite anō </li></ul></li><li>&quot;Tēnā koe mō tāu āwhina.&quot; What is the speaker doing?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>Greeting someone formally </li><li>Asking for help </li><li>Thanking someone for their help ✅</li><li>Saying goodbye </li></ul></li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tuesday questions</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Which linking word means &quot;not only that&quot; — used to expand on a point?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>heoi anō </li><li>nō reira </li><li>kāore noa iho ✅</li><li>hei tauira </li></ul></li><li>&quot;Hei tauira, ko te haka tētahi mahi toi rongonui.&quot; What is the function of hei tauira?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>To conclude an argument </li><li>To introduce an example / illustration ✅</li><li>To contrast two ideas </li><li>To ask a question </li></ul></li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Wednesday questions</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>&quot;Tōku ake ngākau.&quot; What does ake add after the possessive here?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>It changes the meaning to &quot;my friends' heart&quot; </li><li>It adds emphasis — my VERY OWN heart, particularly mine ✅</li><li>It makes it past tense </li><li>It is a filler word with no meaning </li></ul></li><li>Which statement best describes Term 4 fluency?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>Knowing all the grammar rules by name </li><li>Using structures automatically in service of what you want to say ✅</li><li>Writing the longest sentences possible </li><li>Translating directly from English </li></ul></li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Thursday questions</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>&quot;Ko tōku inoi mō koutou.&quot; What does inoi mean?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>challenge </li><li>farewell </li><li>wish / prayer ✅</li><li>work </li></ul></li><li>&quot;Ka nui ōku mihi ki a koutou.&quot; What does this sentence express?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>I have many problems with you all </li><li>My greetings and gratitude to you all are great ✅</li><li>We have much work to do together </li><li>I wish you would come back </li></ul></li></ul></div></div>"}}},"Week 2":{"label":"Day","children":{"Monday: He kōrero ā-waha — Oral storytelling techniques":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Te Aho Arataki Marau — AO 2.3: Extended oral communication · AO 2.5: Narrative vocabulary · Grammar Level 1–2: Oral storytelling structures</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Objectives</th><td class=\"lt-td\">AO 2.3 — Oral storytelling; AO 2.5 — Narrative vocabulary</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">I Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tāua Kōrero — Telling a story that grips the listener</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Oral storytelling — <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=k%C5%8Drero+p%C5%ABr%C4%81kau\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">kōrero pūrākau</a> — is one of the oldest and most important skills in te ao Māori. Knowledge was kept in memory and transmitted through story. Today we learn the techniques that make a story gripping when spoken aloud.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\"><strong>OPENING the story:</strong><br><strong>I tērā wā, i tērā wā…</strong> — In that time, in that time… (traditional opening), in that time… (traditional opening)<br><strong>Nō ngā <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=r%C4%81\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">rā</a> o mua…</strong> — In the days of old…<br><strong>He pō, he pō…</strong> — One night, one night… (repetition for effect)<br><br><strong>BUILDING tension:</strong><br><strong>Ā, ka huri noa te ao.</strong> — And the world just spun (something changed)<br><strong>Ka <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=ohorere\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">ohorere</a> rātou katoa.</strong> — They were all startled / taken by surprise<br><strong>Kāore he tangata i kite.</strong> — No one saw.<br><br><strong>CLOSING the story:</strong><br><strong>Nō reira, ka <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=mutu\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">mutu</a>.</strong> — And so it ended.<br><strong>Tērā ia te <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=k%C5%8Drero\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">kōrero</a>.</strong> — That is the story.<br><strong>Ka <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=huri\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">huri</a>.</strong> — The telling turns. (traditional close). (traditional close)</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Storytelling techniques:</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>Repetition for emphasis</strong>:</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&quot;I tērā wā, i tērā wā…&quot; — repeating sets a rhythm and signals story time<br>&quot;He pō, he pō…&quot; — doubling creates atmosphere</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>Short sentences for drama</strong>:</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&quot;Ka tū ia. Ka titiro. Kāore he mea.&quot; — She stood. She looked. Nothing.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>Personifying nature</strong>:</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&quot;Ka tangi te hau.&quot; — The wind cried. (from Term 1!)<br>&quot;Ka ngunguru ngā whatitiri.&quot; — The thunder growled.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>Direct speech</strong>:</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&quot;Ka mea ia: 'Āe, ka haere au.'&quot; — She said: &quot;Yes, I will go.&quot;</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>New words:<br><strong>pūrākau</strong> — traditional story / myth / legend<br><strong>huri noa</strong> — just spun/turned (unexpected change)<br><strong>Ka huri.</strong> — The telling turns. (traditional story ending)</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Tip: I tērā wā, i tērā wā… — say it twice. Repetition at the opening tells the listener: we are leaving ordinary time and entering story time. This is ancient technology.</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">We Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Kia Tūhono — Story techniques in action</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>We build a short story together using each technique.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Open: <strong>I tērā wā, i tērā wā, i noho tētahi kōtiro i te taha o te awa.</strong> Set the scene — who, where.</li><li>Build tension with short Ka sentences: <strong>Ka rongo ia i tētahi oro. Ka tū ia. Ka titiro ia.</strong></li><li>Use personification: <strong>Ka ngunguru te ngahere. Ka tangi te hau i ngā rākau.</strong></li><li>Close: <strong>Nō reira, ka hoki ia ki tōna kāinga. Ka huri.</strong></li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Discussion prompt: Ka huri — the telling turns. This traditional ending signals that the story is complete and the teller is returning control to the listeners. Why do oral cultures develop formal story openings and closings? What do they do for the listener that a written story opening and closing can't?</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">You Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Māu Anō — Tell your story</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write a short pūrākau using the oral storytelling techniques.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Open with I tērā wā, i tērā wā or Nō ngā rā o mua — establish time, place, character.</li><li>Build tension with three short Ka sentences — action, pause, action.</li><li>Use at least one personification of nature: Ka [sound word] te [natural element].</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write a full pūrākau (10 sentences) — traditional opening, scene-setting, rising action with tension, a moment of surprise (Ka ohorere…), resolution, and Ka huri close. Read it aloud to a partner in your best storytelling voice.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Tuesday: Ngā rerenga pakiaka — Complex subordinate sentences":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Te Aho Arataki Marau — AO 2.3: Extended communication · Grammar Level 1–2: Complex subordination, relative clauses, purpose clauses</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Objectives</th><td class=\"lt-td\">AO 2.3 — Complex sentence mastery; AO 2.5 — Advanced structures</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">I Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tāua Kōrero — The most complex sentence structures in te reo</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>You have ahakoa, mehemea, i te wā, nō te mea. Today we add the final complex structures — relative clauses (describing noun phrases) and purpose clauses (saying why you do something). These are the structures that make te reo truly sophisticated.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\"><strong>RELATIVE CLAUSE (ai):</strong><br><strong>Ko ia te tangata i <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=%C4%81whina\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">āwhina</a> ai i a mātou.</strong><br>She is the person who helped us. (ai marks the relative clause)<br><br><strong>Ko tēnei te pukapuka e ako ana ngā ākonga.</strong><br>This is the book the students are learning from.<br><br><strong>PURPOSE CLAUSE (hei… / mō te…):</strong><br><strong>I haere au ki te <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=kura\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">kura</a> hei ako i te reo Māori.</strong><br>I went to school in order to learn te reo Māori.<br><br><strong>I mahi kaha ia mō te toa i te <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=whakataetae\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">whakataetae</a>.</strong><br>She worked hard in order to win the competition.<br><br><strong>REASON + PURPOSE together:</strong><br><strong>I ako au nō te mea kei te hiahia au ki te mōhio — hei āwhina i ōku <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=uri\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">uri</a> āpōpō.</strong><br>I learned because I want to know — in order to help my descendants tomorrow.</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The relative clause marker <strong>ai</strong>:</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Ai appears at the END of a relative clause — it turns a sentence back to describe a noun:</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&quot;Ko ia te tangata…&quot; + &quot;i āwhina ia i a mātou&quot; → &quot;Ko ia te tangata i āwhina <strong>ai</strong> i a mātou.&quot;<br>She is the person who helped us.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&quot;Ko tēnei te wā…&quot; + &quot;ka kōrero tātou i tēnei wā&quot; → &quot;Ko tēnei te wā ka kōrero <strong>ai</strong> tātou.&quot;<br>This is the time we speak.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Purpose clause: <strong>hei [verb]</strong> = in order to [verb]<br>&quot;I haere au ki te kura <strong>hei</strong> ako i te reo Māori.&quot;</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>New phrase:<br><strong>Ko tēnei te wā ka kōrero ai tātou.</strong><br>This is the time for us to speak.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Tip: Ai at the end of a clause says: &quot;look back — this clause is describing the noun I mentioned earlier.&quot; Think of it as an arrow pointing backward to the noun.</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">We Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Kia Tūhono — Complex structures</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Build relative clauses and purpose clauses.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Relative clause: &quot;She is the person who taught us.&quot; → <strong>Ko ia te tangata i ako ai i a mātou.</strong></li><li>Purpose clause: &quot;I am learning te reo in order to speak with my whānau.&quot; → <strong>Kei te ako au i te reo Māori hei kōrero me tōku whānau.</strong></li><li>Combine: &quot;I worked hard because I want to succeed — in order to help my community.&quot; → <strong>I mahi kaha au nō te mea kei te hiahia au ki te angitu — hei āwhina i tōku hapori.</strong></li><li>Write your own: a relative clause about your teacher, and a purpose clause about why you are here.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Discussion prompt: &quot;Hei āwhina i ōku uri āpōpō&quot; — in order to help my descendants tomorrow. You are a Year 5-6 student. Your descendants are decades away. But you are already thinking about them through this language. What does it mean to learn something NOW for people who do not yet exist?</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">You Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Māu Anō — Complex sentences</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write relative clauses and purpose clauses.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Write two relative clauses using ai: Ko ia te tangata/wāhi/wā… [ai clause].</li><li>Write two purpose clauses using hei: I/Kei te [action] au hei [purpose].</li><li>Combine reason and purpose: I [action] nō te mea… hei…</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write a paragraph (8 sentences) about your te reo learning journey using: a relative clause (ai), a purpose clause (hei), ahakoa, nō te mea, Kua, and Ko te mutunga. Make every structure serve something true.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Wednesday: Hanga rerenga — Extended narrative writing":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Te Aho Arataki Marau — AO 2.3: Extended narrative · AO 2.5: Full vocabulary · Grammar Level 1–2: All narrative structures</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Objectives</th><td class=\"lt-td\">AO 2.3 — Extended narrative; AO 2.5 — Narrative vocabulary</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">I Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tāua Kōrero — A full narrative in te reo Māori</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Today we write a complete narrative — beginning, middle, and end — using everything from four terms. This is the summit of te reo Māori writing for Year 5-6.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\"><strong>BEGINNING — set the scene:</strong><br>I tērā wā, i tērā wā, i noho tētahi kōtiro, ko Hine, i roto i te ngahere nui o Aotearoa. He ngahere ātaahua, engari he ngahere mōhio hoki — i rongo ia i ngā mea katoa.<br><br><strong>MIDDLE — build, tension, crisis:</strong><br>I tētahi rā, ka <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=rongo\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">rongo</a> a Hine i tētahi <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=tangi\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">tangi</a> kino i roto i ngā rākau. Ka <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=ohorere\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">ohorere</a> ia. Ka haere ia ki reira — ka kite ia i tētahi <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=manu\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">manu</a> tino iti e mamae ana i tōna <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=parirau\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">parirau</a>.<br><br><strong>END — resolution, reflection:</strong><br>Ka tango a Hine i te manu. Ka <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=tiaki\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">tiaki</a> ia i a ia ā te wā i ora ai. I te wā i rere anō te manu, ka tangi a Hine — ehara i te pōuri, he <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=hari\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">hari</a> kē. Ka huri.</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Narrative structure in te reo:</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>BEGINNING: I tērā wā + scene + character introduction<br>MIDDLE: Ka sequence + ohorere moment + crisis<br>END: Resolution + reflection + Ka huri</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>New words in this narrative:<br><strong>parirau</strong> — wing<br><strong>i ora ai</strong> — until it recovered (ora = well, ai = relative marker)<br><strong>ehara i te pōuri</strong> — it was not sadness<br><strong>he hari kē</strong> — it was actually joy (kē = on the contrary)</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Notice the final line:<br>&quot;Ehara i te pōuri, he hari kē.&quot;<br>Not sadness — actually joy. Kē creates the surprise twist. The tears at the end are tears of happiness, not grief.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Tip: Ehara i te [expected thing] — he [actual thing] kē. This is the te reo pattern for a plot twist or surprise revelation. &quot;It was not what you expected — it was actually this.&quot; Master this and your stories will always have a satisfying ending.</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">We Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Kia Tūhono — Build a narrative</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Together we build a full narrative using the three-part structure.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>BEGINNING: I tērā wā… + character + setting. Who? Where? What is the feeling?</li><li>MIDDLE TENSION: Ka rongo ia i… + Ka ohorere ia. + Ka haere ia ki reira.</li><li>CRISIS: Ka kite ia i… — describe what they found. Use body vocabulary and E…ana.</li><li>END: Ka [resolution]. Ka [reflection]. Ehara i te [expected] — he [actual] kē. Ka huri.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Discussion prompt: Ka tangi a Hine — ehara i te pōuri, he hari kē. The same action (crying) can mean opposite things. The kē creates the twist that reveals the true meaning. What does this teach us about the relationship between language and emotion? Can one word change everything?</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">You Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Māu Anō — Your pūrākau</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write a complete original narrative in te reo Māori.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Write your BEGINNING (3 sentences): I tērā wā + character + setting.</li><li>Write your MIDDLE (4 sentences): rising action + Ka ohorere moment + crisis.</li><li>Write your END (3 sentences): resolution + reflection + Ka huri.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write your full pūrākau (12 sentences minimum). Use: oral storytelling techniques (repetition, short Ka sentences, personification), all tense markers appropriately, body vocabulary, at least one relative clause (ai), and a kē twist ending.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Thursday: Kōrero — He kōrero pūrākau — Storytelling conversation":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Te Aho Arataki Marau — AO 2.3: Oral narrative · AO 2.5: Storytelling vocabulary · AO 1.1: Audience and formal register</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Objectives</th><td class=\"lt-td\">AO 2.3 — Oral narrative; AO 1.1 — Formal audience language</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">I Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tāua Kōrero — Stories as conversation</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>In te ao Māori, stories are not just for entertainment — they teach, they connect, they heal. Today we tell stories to each other and talk ABOUT stories — discussing what they mean, what they teach, and what they connect us to.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\"><strong>A:</strong> He aha tō kōrero pūrākau pai rawa atu?<br><strong>B:</strong> Ko tōku pūrākau pai rawa atu ko te kōrero mō Māui — ko ia tētahi rangatira o ngā pūrākau Māori.<br><strong>A:</strong> He aha tā tērā pūrākau e ako ana i a koe?<br><strong>B:</strong> Ko tā tērā pūrākau mōhio ko te māia o te tangata — ahakoa he pakiaka noa ia, i toa ia i ngā mea tino nui.<br><strong>A:</strong> Ka pai tāu whakaaro. He aha tō pūrākau ake?<br><strong>B:</strong> I ngā rā o mua, ko tōku <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=kuia\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">kuia</a> te <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=kaip%C5%ABr%C4%81kau\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">kaipūrākau</a> o tō mātou whānau. Ka noho mātou ā te pō, ka kōrero ia i ngā kōrero o ō mātou <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=t%C4%ABpuna\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">tīpuna</a>.<br><strong>A:</strong> He taonga tino nui tērā. Tēnā koe mō tāu kōrero.</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>New storytelling conversation vocabulary:<br><strong>kaipūrākau</strong> — storyteller (kai- + pūrākau)<br><strong>rangatira o ngā pūrākau</strong> — a chief of stories / a hero of legend<br><strong>pakiaka noa</strong> — just an ordinary person / of humble origin<br><strong>tā tērā pūrākau mōhio ko te…</strong> — what that story teaches is…<br><strong>ko tāu kōrero</strong> — your words / your telling<br><strong>He aha tā tērā pūrākau e ako ana i a koe?</strong><br>What does that story teach you?<br><strong>Ko tā tērā pūrākau mōhio ko te [lesson].</strong><br>What that story teaches is [lesson].</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Tip: Ko tā tērā pūrākau mōhio ko te [lesson] — what that story teaches is [lesson]. This is the te reo pattern for discussing the moral or meaning of a story. Learn it and you can discuss any pūrākau.</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">We Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Kia Tūhono — Storytelling conversation</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Build a conversation about a story you know.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I ask: He aha tō kōrero pūrākau pai rawa atu? Name a story and say why: Ko tōku pūrākau pai rawa atu ko… nō te mea…</li><li>I ask: He aha tā tērā pūrākau e ako ana i a koe? Answer: Ko tā tērā pūrākau mōhio ko te…</li><li>I ask: He aha tō pūrākau ake? Do you have your own family story? Share a detail.</li><li>I say: Tēnā koe mō tāu kōrero. You respond with Tēnā koe hoki.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Discussion prompt: Ko tā tērā pūrākau mōhio ko te māia o te tangata — what that story teaches is the bravery of a person. Every great story teaches something through action, not through statement. What story from your own life — something that actually happened to you — would make a good pūrākau? What would it teach?</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">You Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Māu Anō — He kōrero pūrākau</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Tell and discuss stories in te reo Māori.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Name your favourite story (from any culture) and say why: Ko tōku pūrākau pai rawa atu ko… nō te mea…</li><li>Write what it teaches: Ko tā tērā pūrākau mōhio ko te…</li><li>Describe a kaipūrākau from your own life — someone who told you stories.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write a storytelling conversation (10 lines) with a partner — introduce a story, discuss what it teaches, share a memory of being told a story, and close with Tēnā koe mō tāu kōrero.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Friday: Aromatawai — Quiz & Review":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Monday questions</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>&quot;I tērā wā, i tērā wā…&quot; What is the purpose of this opening phrase?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>It gives the exact date of the story </li><li>Repetition signals entry into story time — leaving ordinary time ✅</li><li>It means &quot;once upon a time&quot; and nothing more </li><li>It is a formal greeting before a story </li></ul></li><li>What is the traditional te reo Māori story ending?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>Nō reira ka mutu. </li><li>Tērā ia te kōrero. </li><li>Ka huri. ✅</li><li>Ko te mutunga. </li></ul></li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tuesday questions</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>&quot;Ko ia te tangata i āwhina ai i a mātou.&quot; What does ai indicate?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>Past tense </li><li>The end of a relative clause — pointing back to describe &quot;te tangata&quot; ✅</li><li>Future intention </li><li>A question marker </li></ul></li><li>&quot;I haere au ki te kura hei ako i te reo Māori.&quot; What does hei indicate here?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>Past tense of going </li><li>A purpose clause — in order to learn ✅</li><li>A negative </li><li>A question </li></ul></li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Wednesday questions</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>&quot;Ehara i te pōuri, he hari kē.&quot; What does kē signal in this sentence?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>The feeling is past </li><li>The feeling is very strong </li><li>Contrary to expectation — it was actually joy, not sadness ✅</li><li>The speaker is confused </li></ul></li><li>What are the three parts of a narrative structure in te reo?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>Introduction, body, conclusion </li><li>Beginning (scene), middle (tension/crisis), end (resolution/reflection) ✅</li><li>Problem, solution, evaluation </li><li>Past, present, future </li></ul></li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Thursday questions</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>What is a &quot;kaipūrākau&quot;?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>a type of story </li><li>a storyteller (kai- + pūrākau) ✅</li><li>a traditional opening phrase </li><li>a formal greeting </li></ul></li><li>&quot;Ko tā tērā pūrākau mōhio ko te māia o te tangata.&quot; What does this sentence discuss?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>The title of the story </li><li>The author of the story </li><li>What the story teaches — the bravery of a person ✅</li><li>How long the story is </li></ul></li></ul></div></div>"}}},"Week 3":{"label":"Day","children":{"Monday: He kōrero māpuna — Figurative language and imagery":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Te Aho Arataki Marau — AO 2.5: Figurative language · AO 2.3: Extended expressive communication</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Objectives</th><td class=\"lt-td\">AO 2.5 — Figurative language; AO 2.3 — Imagery &amp; metaphor</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">I Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tāua Kōrero — Language that paints pictures</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Te reo Māori is extraordinarily rich in figurative language — metaphor, simile, and imagery are woven into everyday speech. Today we explore the most beautiful and most useful figures of speech in te reo Māori.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\"><strong>SIMILE (āhua… ana):</strong><br><strong>E oma ana ia āhua te <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=whatitiri\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">whatitiri</a>.</strong><br>She runs like thunder.<br><br><strong>METAPHOR (Ko [X] ko [Y]):</strong><br><strong>Ko te reo he <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=aho\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">aho</a> e <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=whiri\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">whiri</a> ana i ngā tāngata.</strong><br>Language is a cord that weaves people together.<br><br><strong>PERSONIFICATION:</strong><br><strong>Ka tangi ngā <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=maunga\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">maunga</a> i ngā ua.</strong><br>The mountains weep in the rain.<br><br><strong>IMAGERY:</strong><br><strong>He rau <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=k%C5%8Dpura\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">kōpura</a> tōna kanohi i tōna kata.</strong><br>Her face was a golden leaf when she laughed. (kōpura = gold/copper)</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Te reo Māori figures of speech:</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Simile</strong> uses āhua (like/as) or me he (like a):<br>&quot;E oma ana ia āhua te whatitiri.&quot; — runs like thunder<br>&quot;Ka māia ia me he maunga.&quot; — brave like a mountain</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Metaphor</strong> uses Ko:<br>&quot;Ko te reo he aho e whiri ana i ngā tāngata.&quot;<br>Language is a cord that weaves people together.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Personification</strong> — giving nature human actions:<br>&quot;Ka tangi ngā maunga.&quot; — The mountains weep.<br>&quot;Ka karanga te moana.&quot; — The sea calls.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>New vocabulary:<br><strong>aho</strong> — cord / string / thread<br><strong>whiri</strong> — to weave / plait / braid<br><strong>kōpura</strong> — gold / copper (colour)<br><strong>māia</strong> — brave (you know this)<br><strong>rau kōpura</strong> — golden leaf</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Tip: Ko te reo he aho e whiri ana i ngā tāngata — language is a cord that weaves people together. This is one of the most beautiful metaphors in te reo Māori. Learn it cold. Use it when you want to explain why language matters.</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">We Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Kia Tūhono — Figurative sentences</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Build similes, metaphors, and personification in te reo Māori.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Simile: &quot;She works like the wind.&quot; → <strong>E mahi ana ia āhua te hau.</strong> Build your own simile about a classmate.</li><li>Metaphor: &quot;He is a pillar of the community.&quot; → <strong>Ko ia he pou o te hapori.</strong> Build a Ko metaphor.</li><li>Personification: give a natural element a human action. <strong>Ka waiata te awa i ngā kōrero o tōna ara.</strong></li><li>Write a simile + a metaphor about te reo Māori itself.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Discussion prompt: Ko te reo he aho e whiri ana i ngā tāngata — language is a cord that weaves people together. What does it mean for language to WEAVE people? What happens to a community when the cord is cut? When has language created a connection for you?</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">You Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Māu Anō — Figurative language</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Use similes, metaphors, and personification in your writing.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Write two similes using āhua or me he.</li><li>Write two Ko metaphors about people, places, or language.</li><li>Write two personification sentences using natural elements.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write a paragraph (8 sentences) about your year of te reo Māori learning — using at least one simile, one metaphor, and one personification. Make it beautiful.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Tuesday: He kōrero whakaaro — Expressing opinions with sophistication":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Te Aho Arataki Marau — AO 2.3: Extended opinions · Grammar Level 1–2: Sophisticated opinion structures</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Objectives</th><td class=\"lt-td\">AO 2.3 — Sophisticated opinions; AO 2.5 — Evaluative language</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">I Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tāua Kōrero — Saying what you think, elegantly</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>You have Ko tāku whakaaro (my thought), He pai ake (better), and debate language. Today we add the final layer of opinion expression — how to say what you think with nuance, qualification, and elegance.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\"><strong>STATING a strong opinion:</strong><br><strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=E+ai+ki\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">E ai ki</a> āku whakaaro…</strong> — According to my thinking…<br><strong>Ko tōku <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=t%C5%AB%C4%81huatanga\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">tūāhuatanga</a> ko te…</strong> — My position is that…<br><br><strong>QUALIFYING an opinion:</strong><br><strong>I ētahi wā…</strong> — Sometimes…<br><strong>Ka mutu, ko tōku whakaaro…</strong> — All things considered, my view is…<br><strong>Ahakoa ōna āhuatanga…</strong> — Despite its qualities…<br><br><strong>CONCEDING a point:</strong><br><strong>He <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=tika\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">tika</a> tāu ki tērā āhuatanga.</strong> — You are right about that point.<br><strong>Āe, he <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=pono\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">pono</a> tērā — engari rā…</strong> — Yes, that is true — however…<br><br><strong>CLOSING a view:</strong><br><strong>Nō reira, ko tōku whakaaro <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=m%C5%8Dtika\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">mōtika</a> ko te…</strong><br>Therefore, my considered view is…</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Sophisticated opinion structures:</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>E ai ki āku whakaaro</strong> — more formal than Ko tāku whakaaro<br><strong>Ka mutu</strong> — all things considered / when it is done<br><strong>mōtika</strong> — correct / right / genuine<br><strong>Ko tōku whakaaro mōtika</strong> — my considered/genuine view<br><strong>He pono tērā</strong> — that is true<br><strong>Āe, he pono tērā — engari rā…</strong><br>Yes, that is true — however…</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>New words:<br><strong>mōtika</strong> — correct / genuine / right<br><strong>Ka mutu</strong> — all things considered / when all is said<br><strong>E ai ki</strong> — according to<br><strong>tūāhuatanga</strong> — position / characteristic</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Tip: Āe, he pono tērā — engari rā. &quot;Yes, that is true — however.&quot; This is the most graceful way to concede a point before making your counter-argument. It shows you have listened. In te ao Māori, showing you have heard someone before you respond is a mark of respect.</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">We Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Kia Tūhono — Sophisticated opinions</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Express opinions with nuance and elegance.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Strong position: E ai ki āku whakaaro, ko te reo Māori te taonga nui rawa atu o Aotearoa.</li><li>Qualify: Ka mutu, ko tōku whakaaro… + your opinion on a topic with some nuance.</li><li>Concede + counter: Āe, he pono tērā… engari rā… + your counter-argument.</li><li>Close: Nō reira, ko tōku whakaaro mōtika ko te… + your final position.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Discussion prompt: Āe, he pono tērā — engari rā. When you say &quot;yes, that is true&quot; before arguing your point, you show you have genuinely listened. This is the opposite of winning an argument — it is trying to reach understanding. What would happen if all debates worked this way?</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">You Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Māu Anō — Sophisticated opinion writing</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write a sophisticated opinion piece in te reo Māori.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>State position: E ai ki āku whakaaro, ko [topic] te [opinion].</li><li>Qualify: Ka mutu, i ētahi wā… (acknowledge complexity).</li><li>Concede and counter: Āe, he pono tērā — engari rā…</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write a full opinion piece (8 sentences) about te reo Māori, kaitiakitanga, or education. Use: E ai ki āku whakaaro, Ka mutu, Āe he pono tērā engari rā, Ko tōku whakaaro mōtika, and Ko te mutunga.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Wednesday: Hanga rerenga — Year-long structure review":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Te Aho Arataki Marau — All AOs Terms 1–4 · All grammar structures consolidated</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Objectives</th><td class=\"lt-td\">AO 2.3 — Full year review; AO 2.5 — Complete vocabulary</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">I Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tāua Kōrero — The complete four-term picture</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This is Term 4 Week 3. Today we put the entire year on the table — every structure, every domain, every AO — and see the full picture of what you have built.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\"><strong>EVERYTHING you can now do:</strong><br><br>GREET &amp; FAREWELL at every level of formality<br>INTRODUCE yourself with a full pepeha<br>DESCRIBE people, places, feelings, weather, nature, arts<br>RECOUNT past events with I and Ka<br>NARRATE stories with Ka and oral techniques<br>EXPRESS ongoing actions with E…ana and Kua<br>COMMIT to future actions with māku e and Ka taea<br>NEGATE in present and past with Kāore<br>ARGUE and persuade with taunakitanga and discourse markers<br>EXPLAIN meanings with Ko te tikanga o…<br>EVALUATE learning with ngāwari/uaua and Ka taea<br>DISCUSS culture, art, environment, science, rights<br>PERFORM a pepeha, a pōhiri, a whaikōrero</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The year in one sentence:</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>&quot;Ko ahau ko [name] — he ākonga māia, he kaitiaki o tēnei reo rangatira; e ako tonu ana au, kua tipu tōku reo, ā ka kōrero māmā ake au i ia rā, i ia rā — nō reira, ko tōku moemoeā: ka toitū tēnei reo tapu mō ōku uri ā āpōpō.&quot;</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>→ Ko identity<br>→ He description ×2<br>→ E…ana progressive<br>→ Kua perfect<br>→ Ka narrative<br>→ Nō reira conclusion<br>→ Ko moemoeā dream<br>→ Ka toitū endurance<br>→ Relative: mō ōku uri ā āpōpō (for my future descendants)</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>That is one year of daily practice in one sentence.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Tip: Read that sentence aloud. Slowly. Every word of it is yours. You built this. Ka tino pai tāu mahi.</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">We Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Kia Tūhono — Year-long blitz</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The fastest comprehensive review of the year.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>PEPEHA: One student — full nine lines from memory. Class supports.</li><li>STRUCTURES: Call out a structure — class gives an example sentence. Ko, He, Kei te, I, Ka, Kua, E…ana, Kāore, ahakoa, ai, hei…</li><li>WHAKATAUKĪ: All four from Term 3 Week 8 — who can say them all?</li><li>SHOWCASE: Each student says the single best te reo sentence they have ever produced.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Discussion prompt: Ka tino pai tāu mahi — your work is genuinely, truly excellent. Not just good — tino good. You have earned this. What would your Week 1 Term 1 self think if they heard the sentence you just said?</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">You Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Māu Anō — Four-term audit</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Your final comprehensive vocabulary and structure audit.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Write every te reo word you know in 5 minutes. Count them. Compare to Week 1.</li><li>Write one sentence using each of the 14 structures from the I Do.</li><li>Write your best sentence of the year — and why it is your best.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write the year-summary sentence above in your own words — make every part true for you. Then write a second version that is even better.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Thursday: Kōrero — He kōrero māmā — Spontaneous conversation":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Te Aho Arataki Marau — All AOs · AO 2.3: Spontaneous extended conversation</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Objectives</th><td class=\"lt-td\">AO 2.3 — Spontaneous conversation; AO 2.5 — Full vocabulary in conversation</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">I Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tāua Kōrero — The spontaneous conversation</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The highest goal of language learning is spontaneous conversation — talking freely, without a script, responding to what the other person actually says. Today we practise this. No models, no sentence frames — just conversation.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\"><strong>CONVERSATION STARTERS you know:</strong><br>He pēhea tōu wiki? · He aha ngā mea hou? · Ko ēhea āu hiahia?<br>He aha tō whakaaro mō…? · He aha tāu e tūmanako ana?<br>Ko wai tōu hoa pai rawa atu? · He aha tā tērā pūrākau e ako ana?<br><br><strong>CONVERSATION EXTENDERS:</strong><br><strong>Ā nā?</strong> — And then? / What happened next?<br><strong>Pērā?</strong> — Like that? / Really?<br><strong>He aha tāu e kī ana?</strong> — What do you mean?<br><strong>Ka pai tāu kōrero — heoi anō…</strong> — Good point — however…<br><strong>Nā wai rā…</strong> — Eventually… / As time went on…<br><strong>Ā koia!</strong> — Exactly! / That's it!</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Tools for keeping a conversation going:</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Ā nā?</strong> — the simplest conversation extender. &quot;And then?&quot; invites the other person to continue.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Pērā?</strong> — &quot;Like that?&quot; shows you are surprised and engaged.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Ā koia!</strong> — &quot;Exactly!&quot; shows enthusiastic agreement. Use it to confirm and encourage.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Nā wai rā…</strong> — &quot;Eventually / as time went on…&quot; moves a story forward in time.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>He aha tāu e kī ana?</strong> — &quot;What do you mean?&quot; — asking for clarification. This is one of the most important phrases in any language.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>New word:<br><strong>Ā koia!</strong> — Exactly! That's it! (an emphatic agreement)<br><strong>Nā wai rā</strong> — Eventually / Over time / As things progressed</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Tip: Ā nā? is the single most useful conversation extender in any language. &quot;And then?&quot; makes the other person feel listened to and invites them to continue. Use it constantly.</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">We Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Kia Tūhono — Spontaneous conversation practice</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Pairs. Teacher gives a starting topic — students talk freely. No scripts.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Topic 1: He pēhea tōu wiki? Start — extend with Ā nā? — agree with Ā koia! — disagree with Āe, he pono tērā engari rā…</li><li>Topic 2: He aha tōu moemoeā mō te reo Māori? Start — ask He aha tāu e kī ana? for clarification.</li><li>Topic 3: He aha te pūrākau pai rawa atu ki a koe? Start — extend with Nā wai rā…</li><li>Topic 4: He aha tō whakaaro mō tō kura? Start — use Pērā? for surprise.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Discussion prompt: He aha tāu e kī ana? — What do you mean? This question shows that you are genuinely trying to understand, not just waiting for your turn to speak. In te ao Māori, listening and understanding before responding is a value. How would conversations change if everyone used this question more?</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">You Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Māu Anō — Free conversation</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Have a fully spontaneous conversation with a partner in te reo Māori — 5 minutes, no script, any topic.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Choose your topic together — something you both genuinely care about.</li><li>Use at least three conversation extenders: Ā nā, Pērā, Ā koia, He aha tāu e kī ana.</li><li>Try to include at least one whakataukī or figurative expression naturally.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Record your conversation (written transcript or voice note). After, count how many different structures and vocabulary domains you used naturally. This is evidence of your fluency.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Friday: Aromatawai — Quiz & Review":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Monday questions</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>&quot;E oma ana ia āhua te whatitiri.&quot; What figure of speech is this?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>metaphor </li><li>personification </li><li>simile ✅</li><li>alliteration </li></ul></li><li>&quot;Ko te reo he aho e whiri ana i ngā tāngata.&quot; What does aho mean?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>river </li><li>cord / string / thread ✅</li><li>mountain </li><li>ancestor </li></ul></li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tuesday questions</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>&quot;E ai ki āku whakaaro…&quot; What does this phrase signal?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>A question is coming </li><li>A formal greeting </li><li>The speaker is about to state their opinion ✅</li><li>A past event </li></ul></li><li>&quot;Āe, he pono tērā — engari rā…&quot; What is the speaker doing?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>Agreeing completely </li><li>Conceding a point graciously before making a counter-argument ✅</li><li>Ending the discussion </li><li>Asking for clarification </li></ul></li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Wednesday questions</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Which sentence correctly uses the relative clause marker ai?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>Ko ia te tangata ai āwhina i a mātou </li><li>Ko ia te tangata i āwhina ai i a mātou ✅</li><li>Ko ia te tangata āwhina ai </li><li>I āwhina ai ia ko ia te tangata </li></ul></li><li>&quot;Ka tino pai tāu mahi.&quot; What does tino add to this sentence?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>It makes the sentence past tense </li><li>It makes the praise genuinely / truly excellent — more emphatic ✅</li><li>It makes it a question </li><li>It negates the sentence </li></ul></li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Thursday questions</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>&quot;Ā koia!&quot; What does this expression mean?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>And then? </li><li>What do you mean? </li><li>Exactly! / That's it! ✅</li><li>Eventually </li></ul></li><li>&quot;He aha tāu e kī ana?&quot; — What is the speaker doing?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>Agreeing </li><li>Asking for clarification — what do you mean? ✅</li><li>Ending the conversation </li><li>Giving an opinion </li></ul></li></ul></div></div>"}}},"Week 4":{"label":"Day","children":{"Monday: He kōrero mō āpōpō — Future, plans, and aspirations":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Te Aho Arataki Marau — AO 2.3: Extended communication · AO 2.4: Future time · Grammar Level 1–2: Future tense mastery</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Objectives</th><td class=\"lt-td\">AO 2.4 — Future &amp; aspirations; AO 2.3 — Extended planning language</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">I Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tāua Kōrero — Talking about the future with confidence</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>You have hiahia, māku e, Ka taea, and Ka for future narrative. Today we consolidate the full future toolkit and add the most aspirational language — talking about what you hope for, dream of, and intend to make happen.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\"><strong>IMMEDIATE FUTURE (Ka):</strong><br><strong>Ka haere au āpōpō.</strong> — I will go tomorrow.<br><br><strong>INTENTION (māku/māna e):</strong><br><strong>Māku e tiaki i tēnei reo.</strong> — I will protect this language.<br><br><strong>HOPE (tūmanako):</strong><br><strong>Kei te <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=t%C5%ABmanako\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">tūmanako</a> au ka [future hope].</strong><br>I hope that [future hope] will happen.<br><br><strong>DREAM (moemoeā):</strong><br><strong>Ko tōku <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=moemoe%C4%81\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">moemoeā</a>: ka [dream].</strong><br>My dream is: that [dream] will happen.<br><br><strong>ASPIRATION (wawata):</strong><br><strong>Ko tōku <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=wawata\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">wawata</a>: ka [aspiration].</strong><br>My aspiration is: [aspiration].</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The future language spectrum:</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>IMMEDIATE → Ka [verb] āpōpō (happening soon)<br>INTENTION → Māku e [verb] (I commit to this)<br>HOPE → Kei te tūmanako au ka… (I hope this happens)<br>DREAM → Ko tōku moemoeā: ka… (deep personal dream)<br>ASPIRATION → Ko tōku wawata: ka… (highest aspiration)</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>wawata</strong> — aspiration / deepest longing / what you reach for<br><strong>wawata</strong> is stronger than moemoeā — it is not just a dream but a deep reaching-toward</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>New word:<br><strong>wawata</strong> — deep aspiration / yearning<br><strong>angitu</strong> — success / achievement<br><strong>Ka angitu koe.</strong> — You will succeed.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Tip: Moemoeā = dream (what you hope for in sleep and waking). Wawata = what you reach toward with your whole being — the deepest aspiration. Use moemoeā for personal dreams, wawata for the things that define your life direction.</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">We Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Kia Tūhono — Future language spectrum</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Express the same idea at different levels of future commitment.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Immediate Ka: <strong>Ka ako au i te reo Māori āpōpō.</strong> Now make it an intention: <strong>Māku e ako i te reo Māori.</strong></li><li>Hope: <strong>Kei te tūmanako au ka [something you hope will happen to your class next year].</strong></li><li>Dream: <strong>Ko tōku moemoeā: ka kōrero tōku whānau katoa i te reo Māori.</strong></li><li>Aspiration: <strong>Ko tōku <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=wawata\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">wawata</a>: ka toitū tēnei reo tapu mō ōku uri ā āpōpō.</strong></li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Discussion prompt: Ko tōku wawata — my deepest aspiration. The word wawata contains the idea of reaching outward and upward. In te ao Māori, a wawata is not just a personal desire — it often connects to the wellbeing of others. What is YOUR wawata? Does it only affect you — or does it reach further?</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">You Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Māu Anō — Future and aspiration language</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write about your future using all five levels of the future language spectrum.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Write a Ka sentence about something happening soon.</li><li>Write a māku e commitment about something you will do.</li><li>Write a tūmanako hope about something you hope will happen.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write a full future statement (8 sentences) — immediate plans, intentions, hopes, a moemoeā, and a wawata. Make the wawata the most ambitious and true thing you say. Close with Ka angitu koe — You will succeed.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Tuesday: Ngā kupu hou whakamutunga — Final new vocabulary for the year":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Te Aho Arataki Marau — AO 2.5: Advanced vocabulary · AO 2.3: Extended communication · All AOs</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Objectives</th><td class=\"lt-td\">AO 2.5 — Advanced vocabulary; AO 2.3 — Full communication</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">I Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tāua Kōrero — The final vocabulary additions</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>These are the last new vocabulary words of the year — carefully chosen to round out your te reo Māori and give you a few more powerful tools for the end-of-year assessment and beyond.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\"><strong>For describing growth and change:</strong><br><strong>tipu</strong> — grow (you know this) · <strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=huri\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">huri</a></strong> — change / turn · <strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=hou\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">hou</a></strong> — new<br><strong>Ka hou tōku whakaaro.</strong> — My thinking has become new / I think differently.<br><br><strong>For describing connection:</strong><br><strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=honohono\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">honohono</a></strong> — to connect / link<br><strong>Ka honohono tēnei reo i ngā tāngata.</strong><br>This language connects people.<br><br><strong>For describing achievement:</strong><br><strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=angitu\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">angitu</a></strong> — success · <strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=toa\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">toa</a></strong> — win/champion · <strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=eke\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">eke</a></strong> — rise/climb/score<br><strong>Ka <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=angitu\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">angitu</a> koe i āu mahi.</strong> — You will succeed in your work.<br><br><strong>For describing legacy:</strong><br><strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=tuku+iho\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">tuku iho</a></strong> — passed down / handed down (generations) / handed down (generations)<br><strong>taonga <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=tuku+iho\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">tuku iho</a></strong> — a treasure handed down<br><strong>He taonga tuku iho tēnei reo.</strong> — This language is a treasure handed down.</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Final vocabulary — how each word connects to the year:</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>honohono</strong> — connects back to aho (cord) and whiri (weave). Language connects.<br><strong>angitu</strong> — connects back to Ka taea (able) and toa (champion). You have succeeded.<br><strong>tuku iho</strong> — connects back to uri (descendants) and tīpuna (ancestors). The chain of generations.<br><strong>Ka hou tōku whakaaro</strong> — connects back to whakaaro (thought) and tipu (grow). Your thinking has grown new.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>These are not random new words — each one closes a circle from earlier in the year.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>New compound:<br><strong>honohono</strong> = hono (join) + hono (repeated for intensity) = to deeply connect</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Tip: He taonga tuku iho tēnei reo — this language is a treasure passed down. You have heard taonga throughout the year. You have heard tuku iho in Term 3. Now they come together in your final week. The phrase was always waiting for you at the end of the year.</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">We Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Kia Tūhono — Final vocabulary in action</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Use each new word in a sentence that connects it back to the year.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Honohono: <strong>Ka honohono tēnei reo i ōku hoa, i ōku tīpuna, i ōku uri katoa.</strong> Who does it connect?</li><li>Angitu: <strong>Ka angitu koe — kua tino pai tāu mahi i tēnei tau.</strong> What has been your angitu?</li><li>Tuku iho: <strong>He taonga tuku iho tēnei reo — nā ōku tīpuna ki a au, ā māku ki ōku uri.</strong></li><li>Hou: <strong>Ka hou tōku whakaaro mō te reo Māori i tēnei tau.</strong> How has your thinking changed?</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Discussion prompt: Nā ōku tīpuna ki a au, ā māku ki ōku uri — from my ancestors to me, and from me to my descendants. You are in the middle of this chain. What did your ancestors give you through language? What will you give your descendants?</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">You Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Māu Anō — Final vocabulary sentences</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Use the final year vocabulary in sentences that are true and meaningful.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Write a honohono sentence about what te reo Māori connects you to.</li><li>Write an angitu sentence about something you have achieved this year.</li><li>Write a taonga tuku iho sentence about the language.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write a four-sentence statement about your year of te reo Māori — using honohono, angitu, tuku iho, and Ka hou tōku whakaaro. Make every word count.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Wednesday: Hanga rerenga — The year in sentences":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Te Aho Arataki Marau — All AOs · All sentence types · Full year synthesis</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Objectives</th><td class=\"lt-td\">AO 2.3 — Year synthesis; AO 2.5 — Full expression</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">I Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tāua Kōrero — Writing the year</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Today we do something special — we write about the whole year. Not as a summary, but as a piece of genuine te reo expression that captures what this year of language learning has been and meant.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\"><strong>A year in nine sentences — one structure per term/topic:</strong><br><br>Ko ahau ko [name] — he ākonga, he kaitiaki, he tangata o tēnei ao. (Ko · He)<br>I <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=t%C4%ABmata\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">tīmata</a> au i tētahi kupu inanahi — ināianei, kua tipu tōku reo ki tētahi mea hou. (I · Kua)<br>E ako tonu ana au — e huri ana tōku whakaaro i ia rā, i ia rā. (E…ana ×2)<br>Ka taea e au te mihi, ka taea e au te tuhituhi, ka taea e au te whakaaro i te reo Māori. (Ka taea ×3)<br>Ko te reo he aho e whiri ana i a au ki ngā tīpuna, ki ngā uri, ki ngā hoa o tōku ao. (Ko metaphor · ai)<br>Ahakoa he uauatanga, i mahi tonu au — nō te mea ko tōku moemoeā tōku ara. (Ahakoa · Nō te mea · Ko)<br>He taonga <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=tuku+iho\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">tuku iho</a> tēnei reo — māku e tiaki, māku e <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=whakaahua\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">whakaahua</a> ake. (He · māku e ×2)<br>Nō reira, ko tōku wawata: ka toitū tēnei reo rangatira mō āpōpō ō ngā uri. (Nō reira · Ko wawata · Ka)<br>Ka angitu koe. Ka angitu mātou katoa. Ka huri. (Ka ×3 · Ka huri)</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Count the structures in those nine sentences:<br>Ko (×5), He (×4), I (past), Kua (perfect), E…ana (×2), Ka taea (×3), ai (relative), Ahakoa, Nō te mea, māku e (×2), Ko wawata, Ka (×4), Ka huri</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>That is the whole year in nine sentences. You can write every one of them.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Notice the final line:<br>&quot;Ka angitu koe. Ka angitu mātou katoa. Ka huri.&quot;<br>You will succeed. We will all succeed. The telling turns.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Even the ending is a pūrākau technique — Ka huri closes the year the way it closes a story.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Tip: Ka huri — the telling turns. This traditional story ending can also close a year. The year was a story. It had a beginning, a middle, and an end. Ka huri.</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">We Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Kia Tūhono — Write the year together</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Class writes one shared &quot;year in sentences&quot; — each student contributes one sentence.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Student 1: A Ko identity sentence about who we are as a class.</li><li>Student 2: A Kua sentence about what has been completed this year.</li><li>Student 3: An E…ana sentence about what is still ongoing in our learning.</li><li>Continue until every student has contributed. Close together: Ka angitu tātou katoa. Ka huri.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Discussion prompt: Ka angitu koe. Ka angitu mātou katoa. Ka huri. Success is named for you individually, then for all of us together, then the year turns. Why does the order matter — you first, then all of us? What does it say about how achievement in te ao Māori is understood?</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">You Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Māu Anō — The year in your sentences</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write your own &quot;year in sentences&quot; — nine sentences, one per major structure.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Write sentences 1–3: Ko identity, Kua perfect, E…ana progressive.</li><li>Write sentences 4–6: Ka taea ability, Ko metaphor with ai, Ahakoa + nō te mea.</li><li>Write sentences 7–9: māku e commitment, Ko wawata, Ka huri.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Refine all nine until each one is truly beautiful and true. Read the whole passage aloud. This is your year. Own it.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Thursday: Kōrero — He kōrero whakaaro — A philosophical conversation":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Te Aho Arataki Marau — AO 2.3: Extended sophisticated conversation · All AOs</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Objectives</th><td class=\"lt-td\">AO 2.3 — Philosophical conversation; AO 2.5 — Sophisticated vocabulary</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">I Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tāua Kōrero — Talking about big ideas</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The most advanced thing you can do in a language is discuss abstract ideas — concepts like time, identity, belonging, language, and legacy. Today we have that conversation in te reo Māori.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\"><strong>A:</strong> He aha te tikanga o te reo ki a koe ināianei, i tērā tau ake?<br><strong>B:</strong> I tērā tau, he mea ngaro noa iho te reo ki a au. Ināianei, ko ia tōku ara, tōku <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=hono\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">hono</a> ki ngā tīpuna.<br><strong>A:</strong> Ka pai tērā. He aha tā tēnei reo i homai ai ki a koe?<br><strong>B:</strong> I homai mai e ia ki a au he ara ki te ao hou — arā, ki ngā kupu o ōku tīpuna, ki ngā <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=m%C5%8Dhiotanga\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">mōhiotanga</a> hou, ki ōku hoa hou.<br><strong>A:</strong> E ai ki āku whakaaro, ko te reo he aho e whiri ana i ngā tāngata katoa. He tika tāu whakaaro?<br><strong>B:</strong> Āe, he tika — waihoki, ko te reo he <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=m%C4%81ramatanga\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">māramatanga</a> ki te ao mārama. Ka <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=honohono\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">honohono</a> ia i te ao o nāianei ki te ao o ōku tīpuna.<br><strong>A:</strong> Ā koia! Tēnā koe mō tāu kōrero tino <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=h%C5%8Dhonu\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">hōhonu</a>.<br><strong>B:</strong> Tēnā koe hoki.</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>New philosophical vocabulary:<br><strong>he mea ngaro noa iho</strong> — just an invisible/lost thing (ngaro = invisible/lost, noa iho = just/merely)<br><strong>ara ki te ao hou</strong> — a path to the new world<br><strong>māramatanga ki te ao mārama</strong> — enlightenment to the world of light<br><strong>ao o nāianei</strong> — the world of today (nāianei = nowadays)<br><strong>tino hōhonu</strong> — very deep / profound (hōhonu = deep)<br><strong>Tēnā koe mō tāu kōrero tino hōhonu.</strong><br>Thank you for your very profound words.<br><strong>he aho e whiri ana</strong> — a cord that weaves (from Week 3!)</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Tip: Ko te reo he māramatanga ki te ao mārama — the language is an enlightenment to the world of light. This brings together māramatanga (understanding/enlightenment) and ao mārama (world of light — both the realm of the living and the realm of clarity). Language as light.</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">We Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Kia Tūhono — Philosophical conversation</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Have a conversation about language, identity, and meaning.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I ask: He aha te tikanga o te reo ki a koe ināianei? Answer with a metaphor or deep statement.</li><li>I ask: He aha tā tēnei reo i homai ai ki a koe? Answer: I homai mai e ia ki a au he…</li><li>I make a claim: Ko te reo he aho e whiri ana i ngā tāngata. You agree or add: Āe, waihoki…</li><li>I close: Tēnā koe mō tāu kōrero tino hōhonu. You respond: Tēnā koe hoki.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Discussion prompt: Ko te reo he māramatanga ki te ao mārama. Language as light, as enlightenment, as access to a brighter world. What does language actually give you access to that you would not have without it? Think specifically — what can you understand, feel, or know in te reo Māori that you cannot in English?</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">You Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Māu Anō — Philosophical writing</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write your own philosophical reflection on language and identity.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Answer: He aha te tikanga o te reo ki a koe? (Not &quot;te reo Māori is important&quot; — something deeper.)</li><li>Write a metaphor: Ko te reo he [metaphor] — with an e…ana relative clause.</li><li>Write what language has given you: I homai mai e ia ki a au he…</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write a full philosophical reflection (8 sentences) — what language means to you, what it has given you, a whakataukī, a metaphor, a wawata, and close with Ko te reo he māramatanga ki te ao mārama — ko tērā tōku whakapono.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Friday: Aromatawai — Quiz & Review":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Monday questions</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>What is the difference between moemoeā and wawata?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>They mean exactly the same thing </li><li>Moemoeā is a dream; wawata is a deeper aspiration/yearning — what you reach toward with your whole being ✅</li><li>Moemoeā is formal; wawata is casual </li><li>Wawata is about the past; moemoeā is about the future </li></ul></li><li>&quot;Ka angitu koe.&quot; What does angitu mean?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>you will leave </li><li>you will succeed / achieve ✅</li><li>you will speak </li><li>you will learn </li></ul></li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tuesday questions</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>&quot;He taonga tuku iho tēnei reo.&quot; What does tuku iho mean?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>recently created </li><li>belonging to one person </li><li>passed down through generations ✅</li><li>difficult to learn </li></ul></li><li>What does &quot;honohono&quot; mean?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>to weave clothing </li><li>to separate / divide </li><li>to deeply connect / link ✅</li><li>to speak formally </li></ul></li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Wednesday questions</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The passage ends with &quot;Ka huri.&quot; In what other context is this phrase used?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>As a formal greeting </li><li>As a weather expression </li><li>As the traditional close of an oral story (pūrākau) ✅</li><li>As a way to say goodbye </li></ul></li><li>&quot;Ko ia tōku ara, tōku hono ki ngā tīpuna.&quot; What two things is the language being called?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>a path and a cord of connection ✅</li><li>a treasure and a dream </li><li>a challenge and a reward </li><li>a light and a gift </li></ul></li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Thursday questions</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>&quot;Ko te reo he māramatanga ki te ao mārama.&quot; What does māramatanga mean?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>darkness / confusion </li><li>difficulty / challenge </li><li>enlightenment / understanding / clarity ✅</li><li>silence </li></ul></li><li>&quot;Tēnā koe mō tāu kōrero tino hōhonu.&quot; What does hōhonu mean?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>short </li><li>confusing </li><li>loud </li><li>deep / profound ✅</li></ul></li></ul></div></div>"}}},"Week 5":{"label":"Day","children":{"Monday: He kōrero mō ōku ara ako — My learning pathways":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Te Aho Arataki Marau — AO 2.3: Extended reflection · All AOs consolidated · Whakaaro ako (metacognition)</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Objectives</th><td class=\"lt-td\">AO 2.3 — Learning reflection; AO 2.5 — Metacognitive vocabulary</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">I Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tāua Kōrero — Thinking about your thinking</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>In this second-to-last week, we turn inward — thinking about HOW we have learned, what strategies worked, and what kind of learner we have become. This is metacognition — thinking about thinking — expressed in te reo Māori.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\"><strong>He aha tāu <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=ara+ako\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">ara ako</a>?</strong> — What is your learning strategy/pathway?<br><br><strong>Ko tōku ara ako ko te [strategy].</strong><br>My learning strategy is [strategy].<br><br><strong>Ko ngā ara ako pai ki a au ko ēnei:</strong><br>he kōrero, he <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=whakarongo\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">whakarongo</a>, he tuhituhi, he mahi tahi.<br>The learning strategies that work for me are: speaking, listening, writing, working together.<br><br><strong>I tipu ōku ara ako i tēnei tau.</strong><br>My learning strategies grew this year.<br><br><strong>Ka pai ake tōku <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=ako\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">ako</a> ina [condition].</strong><br>I learn better when [condition].</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Metacognitive vocabulary:<br><strong>ara ako</strong> — learning pathway / learning strategy<br><strong>whakaaro hōhonu</strong> — deep thinking / reflection<br><strong>Ka pai ake tōku ako ina kōrero ana au.</strong><br>I learn better when I speak.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>He mōhiotanga ōku e pā ana ki te reo Māori.</strong><br>I have knowledge relating to te reo Māori.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Kua tino pai ake tōku mōhio i…</strong><br>My understanding of [X] has become much better.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>I tipu ōku ara ako i tēnei tau nō te mea…</strong><br>My learning strategies grew this year because…</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>New words:<br><strong>metacognition (whakaaro mō te whakaaro)</strong><br><strong>mōhiotanga</strong> — knowledge / understanding<br><strong>e pā ana ki</strong> — relating to / concerning</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Tip: Ka pai ake tōku ako ina [condition] ana au. &quot;I learn better when I [do this].&quot; This is one of the most useful self-knowledge sentences in any language. Knowing HOW you learn best is as important as what you learn.</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">We Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Kia Tūhono — Learning reflection</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Build sentences about how you learn best.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I ask: He aha tāu ara ako pai rawa atu? Answer: Ko tōku ara ako pai rawa atu ko te [strategy].</li><li>I ask: Ka pai ake tōu ako ina aha? Answer: Ka pai ake tōku ako ina [condition].</li><li>State growth: <strong>Kua tino pai ake tōku mōhio i ngā hanganga o te reo Māori i tēnei tau.</strong></li><li>State future strategy: <strong>Māku e [strategy] i muri ake nei hei tiaki i tōku reo.</strong></li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Discussion prompt: Ka pai ake tōku ako ina kōrero ana au — I learn better when I speak. Does this describe you? What IS your best learning strategy? If you could tell next year's te reo students one thing about how to learn it, what would you tell them?</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">You Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Māu Anō — Learning reflection writing</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write a reflection on your learning strategies and growth.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Write your best learning strategy: Ko tōku ara ako pai rawa atu ko te…</li><li>Write when you learn best: Ka pai ake tōku ako ina…</li><li>Write your growth: Kua tino pai ake tōku mōhio i… i tēnei tau.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write a full learning reflection (8 sentences) — your strategies, your growth, what was hard, what worked, what you wish you had done differently, and your advice to a future learner.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Tuesday: He hononga ki te ao — Connections to the world":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Te Aho Arataki Marau — AO 1.4: Connection to land and people · AO 2.1: Relationships · AO 2.3: Extended reflection</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Objectives</th><td class=\"lt-td\">AO 1.4 — Connections to world; AO 2.1 — Relationships &amp; community</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">I Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tāua Kōrero — What te reo Māori has connected you to</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Te reo Māori is not just a communication tool — it is a connector. Today we reflect on everything this language has connected you to across the year: people, places, ancestors, community, nature, and the future.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\"><strong>Ko ērā ngā hononga i hanga ai e tēnei reo:</strong><br>These are the connections this language has made:<br><br><strong>Ko ōku <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=hoa\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">hoa</a> hou</strong> — my new friends<br><strong>Ko ōku kaiako</strong> — my teachers<br><strong>Ko ngā <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=t%C4%ABpuna\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">tīpuna</a> o Aotearoa</strong> — the ancestors of New Zealand<br><strong>Ko ngā <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=uri\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">uri</a> ō āpōpō</strong> — the descendants of tomorrow<br><strong>Ko te taiao o Aotearoa</strong> — the natural world of New Zealand<br><strong>Ko ngā <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=tikanga\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">tikanga</a> o tōku ao</strong> — the customs of my world<br><br><strong>Ka <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=honohono\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">honohono</a> tēnei reo i ōku whakaaro, i ōku ngākau, i ōku ara katoa.</strong><br>This language connects my thoughts, my heart, and all my pathways.</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Reflecting on connections:</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Ko ērā ngā hononga i hanga ai e tēnei reo</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>These are the connections built by this language:<br>(hanga = build/make, ai = relative marker, e = by — passive)</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>I honohono tēnei reo i a au ki…</strong><br>This language connected me to…</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Ka honohono tonu tēnei reo i a au ki ngā tāngata o tēnei ao.</strong><br>This language will continue to connect me to the people of this world.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>New phrase:<br><strong>i hanga ai</strong> — that was/has been built (ai = relative past)<br><strong>Ko ērā ngā [things] i hanga ai e tēnei reo.</strong><br>Those are the [things] built by this language.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Tip: Ko ērā ngā hononga i hanga ai e tēnei reo — those are the connections built by this language. The passive (i hanga ai e) puts the language as the agent — it is the one that builds the connections. Language does something to you; you do not just use it.</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">We Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Kia Tūhono — Connection reflection</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Reflect on what te reo Māori has connected you to.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Ko ērā ngā hononga i hanga ai e tēnei reo: + Ko ēnei list of three connections.</li><li>I honohono tēnei reo i a au ki [specific connection] nō te mea…</li><li>Ka honohono tonu tēnei reo i a au ki [who/what] ā tōna wā.</li><li>Close: Ko tōku wawata: ka honohono tēnei reo i ngā tāngata katoa o Aotearoa.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Discussion prompt: Ka honohono tēnei reo i ōku whakaaro, i ōku ngākau, i ōku ara katoa — this language connects my thoughts, my heart, and all my pathways. Thoughts, heart, and pathways — three dimensions of a person. What would it mean for a language to connect all three? Has te reo Māori done that for you?</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">You Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Māu Anō — Connection writing</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write about the connections te reo Māori has made for you.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Write Ko ērā ngā hononga i hanga ai e tēnei reo + three specific connections.</li><li>Write I honohono tēnei reo i a au ki [specific] nō te mea…</li><li>Write what you hope it will connect you to in the future.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write a full connection reflection (8 sentences) — what te reo has connected you to (people, ancestors, nature, culture), how, why it matters, and your hope for those connections to grow.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Wednesday: Hanga rerenga — The end-of-year showcase":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Te Aho Arataki Marau — All AOs · All sentence types · Full year showcase</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Objectives</th><td class=\"lt-td\">AO 2.3 — Year showcase; AO 2.5 — Full expression</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">I Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tāua Kōrero — Your finest te reo Māori of the year</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This is the penultimate Wednesday lesson of the year. Today is the showcase — not a review, not an audit. A celebration. You write your finest te reo Māori. Not the most complex, not the longest — the most true, the most beautiful, the most you.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">The finest te reo sentences of the year — from any of you:<br><br><strong>&quot;Ko tōku reo he ara māmā ki ngā tīpuna — ka huri noa tōku ao i ngā kupu o ōku mōrearea, ā ka mārama ake i ngā kupu o tōku reo.&quot;</strong><br>(My language is a light path to the ancestors — my world just shifts in the words of my worries, and grows clearer in the words of my language.)<br><br><strong>&quot;He ākonga noa iho ahau — engari ko tōku ngākau e tipu ana i ōku kupu i ia rā, i ia rā.&quot;</strong><br>(I am just a learner — but my heart grows in my words day by day.)<br><br><strong>&quot;Ka toitū te reo, ka toitū tōku whakapono, ka toitū hoki ahau.&quot;</strong><br>(When the language endures, my belief endures, and I endure too.)</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>What makes these sentences the finest:</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>→ They use simple words in unexpected combinations<br>→ They reveal something true about the person<br>→ They use structures naturally, not as demonstrations<br>→ They would lose something if translated to English</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&quot;Ka toitū te reo, ka toitū tōku whakapono, ka toitū hoki ahau.&quot;</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Three Ka toitū. The language endures. My belief endures. And then — ka toitū hoki AHAU. I endure too. The speaker is not just an observer of endurance — they are part of it. The final line makes the abstract personal.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This is what fluency looks like.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Tip: The finest sentence you can write is not the one that uses the most structures — it is the one that uses exactly the right words to say exactly what you mean. Less is often more. Ka toitū ahau. That is enough.</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">We Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Kia Tūhono — Showcase sentences</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write and share your finest sentences. Class listens — no feedback, just appreciation.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Write in silence for 5 minutes — your finest sentence. No structure requirements.</li><li>Write a second sentence that continues the first.</li><li>Read both aloud to a partner. They listen. No comments — just presence.</li><li>Class share: each person reads their best sentence. Everyone listens.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Discussion prompt: Ka toitū te reo, ka toitū tōku whakapono, ka toitū hoki ahau. When the language endures, I endure. There is something radical about this claim — that YOUR survival is connected to the survival of te reo Māori. Do you believe it? In what sense might it be true?</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">You Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Māu Anō — The year showcase</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write your finest te reo Māori of the year.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Write your finest SENTENCE — not the most complex, the most true.</li><li>Write your finest PARAGRAPH — three to five sentences, beautifully connected.</li><li>Write your YEAR CLOSER — one sentence that closes your year of te reo Māori.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write your complete &quot;end of year te reo portfolio piece&quot; — ten sentences that capture who you were, what you learned, what connected you, what you believe, and where you are going. Read it aloud to someone you trust.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Thursday: Kōrero — He kōrero whakamutunga — The final conversation":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Te Aho Arataki Marau — All AOs 1.1–2.5 · All six language modes · Full year assessed</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Objectives</th><td class=\"lt-td\">AO 1.1 — Greetings; AO 1.2 — Introductions; AO 2.3 — Full sophisticated conversation; AO 2.5 — Full vocabulary</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">I Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tāua Kōrero — The full-year conversation</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This is the second-to-last lesson of the year. Today we see what a fully fluent Year 5-6 student can do in te reo Māori — the result of four terms of daily practice.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\"><strong>A:</strong> Tēnā koe. Ko wai koe? He aha tō pepeha?<br><strong>B:</strong> [Full pepeha — all nine lines]<br><strong>A:</strong> Ka pai! He aha ngā pou o tōu ora?<br><strong>B:</strong> Ko ngā pou o tōku ora ko tōku whānau, ko tōku reo, ko tōku taiao — ko ērā ngā mea e mau ana i a au.<br><strong>A:</strong> He aha tā tēnei reo i homai ai ki a koe i tēnei tau?<br><strong>B:</strong> I homai mai e ia ki a au he ara ki ngā tīpuna, he hononga ki ngā uri, ā he māramatanga ki tōku ake ao.<br><strong>A:</strong> He aha tō wawata mō tō reo Māori?<br><strong>B:</strong> Ko tōku wawata: ka toitū tēnei reo tapu mō ōku uri — ā, ka honohono ia i ngā tāngata katoa o tēnei ao ātaahua.<br><strong>A:</strong> Ā koia! Tēnā koe mō tāu kōrero tino hōhonu.<br><strong>B:</strong> Tēnā koe hoki. Ka nui ōku mihi ki a koe.</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This conversation demonstrates every AO:<br>✓ AO 1.1: Tēnā koe + Ka nui ōku mihi<br>✓ AO 1.2: Full pepeha<br>✓ AO 1.4: Identity through maunga, awa, iwi, kura<br>✓ AO 2.1: Relationships (whānau, taiao)<br>✓ AO 2.2: Connection to land<br>✓ AO 2.3: Sophisticated extended kōrero<br>✓ AO 2.5: Pou, hononga, māramatanga, wawata, uri<br>✓ Grammar: Ko, He, E mau ana, I…ai (relative), māramatanga, Ka toitū, Ā koia</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This is the standard. And you can do it.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Tip: Ka nui ōku mihi ki a koe — my greetings and thanks to you are great. This closes the conversation the way it began — with acknowledgement of the other person. In te ao Māori, beginning and ending with the other person is the mark of true manaaki.</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">We Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Kia Tūhono — Final full practice</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Pairs — the complete full-year conversation. No script after the first line.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Open formally + full pepeha from memory.</li><li>Ko ngā pou o tōu ora ko… + He aha tā tēnei reo i homai ai ki a koe?</li><li>He aha tō wawata mō tō reo Māori? + state your wawata.</li><li>Close: Tēnā koe mō tāu kōrero tino hōhonu. Ka nui ōku mihi ki a koe.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Discussion prompt: Ka nui ōku mihi ki a koe — my greetings and thanks to you are great. You are saying this to your conversation partner after they have spoken. But this year, who else do your mihi go to? Who has been part of your te reo journey? How would you say your mihi to them?</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">You Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Māu Anō — The full-year conversation</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Have the full-year conversation with a partner — your finest te reo Māori.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Full pepeha — all nine lines from memory.</li><li>Ko ngā pou o tōu ora + He aha tā tēnei reo i homai ai ki a koe.</li><li>He aha tō wawata + close with Ka nui ōku mihi.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write the full conversation (14 lines minimum). Then annotate every structure and vocabulary domain you used. How many AOs can you find? How many grammar structures? This is your four-term record.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Friday: Aromatawai — Quiz & Review":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Monday questions</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>&quot;Ka pai ake tōku ako ina kōrero ana au.&quot; What does this sentence express?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>I speak better than I learn </li><li>I learn better when I speak ✅</li><li>Speaking is the only way to learn </li><li>I prefer writing to speaking </li></ul></li><li>What does &quot;ara ako&quot; mean?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>learning result </li><li>learning pathway / strategy ✅</li><li>learning language </li><li>learning achievement </li></ul></li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tuesday questions</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>&quot;Ko ērā ngā hononga i hanga ai e tēnei reo.&quot; What does &quot;i hanga ai e tēnei reo&quot; mean?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>that I built through hard work </li><li>that this language built / made (passive) ✅</li><li>that were destroyed by time </li><li>that are yet to be made </li></ul></li><li>What does &quot;honohono&quot; express in the context of language?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>to separate people by language </li><li>to deeply connect people through language ✅</li><li>to translate between languages </li><li>to forget a language </li></ul></li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Wednesday questions</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>&quot;Ka toitū te reo, ka toitū tōku whakapono, ka toitū hoki ahau.&quot; What makes the final clause powerful?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>It uses the most complex grammar </li><li>It makes the abstract personal — the speaker themselves endures ✅</li><li>It is the longest clause </li><li>It uses a whakataukī </li></ul></li><li>What is the key difference between the finest sentence and the most complex sentence?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>The finest sentence is always the longest </li><li>The finest sentence uses the most vocabulary </li><li>The finest sentence says exactly what you mean — truth over complexity ✅</li><li>The finest sentence always uses Kua </li></ul></li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Thursday questions</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>&quot;Ka nui ōku mihi ki a koe.&quot; When is this phrase used?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>As an opening greeting only </li><li>To express great gratitude and acknowledgement — often at the close of a conversation ✅</li><li>As a farewell when you are angry </li><li>Only in formal whaikōrero </li></ul></li><li>A full-year te reo conversation at Term 4 Week 5 level should include which of these?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>Only greetings and farewells </li><li>Only past tense sentences </li><li>Full pepeha, sophisticated vocabulary, wawata, and at least six different grammar structures ✅</li><li>Only simple present tense sentences </li></ul></li></ul></div></div>"}}},"Week 6":{"label":"Day","children":{"Monday: Arotake — Full year vocabulary review and celebration":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Te Aho Arataki Marau — All AOs 1.1–2.5 · Complete year review</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Objectives</th><td class=\"lt-td\">AO 1.1 — Full year greetings; AO 2.5 — Full vocabulary</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">I Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tāua Kōrero — Everything you know: the full year celebration</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This is the final teaching week. Today we celebrate the full year — everything you have learned, everything you can do, and everything you carry forward. This is not a review drill. It is a celebration of mastery.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Four terms. Here is the full picture:<br><br><strong>TERM 1 (Tūāhuatanga):</strong> Ko · He · Kei te · Numbers 1–10 · greetings · food · places · wants · basic conversation<br><br><strong>TERM 2 (Kia Tipu):</strong> I · Ka · Kāore · All 16 pronouns · Numbers 1–100 · dates/times · colours · comparatives · weather · seasons · emotions · character<br><br><strong>TERM 3 (Kia Rea):</strong> Body · E…ana · Kua · māku e · Ka taea · pepeha · marae · haka · whakataukī · kaitiakitanga · science · formal/informal · argument<br><br><strong>TERM 4 (Kia Mārama):</strong> Full greeting system · advanced cohesion · oral storytelling · relative clauses (ai) · purpose clauses (hei) · narrative structure · figurative language · philosophical conversation · wawata · Ka huri</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>What you can do that most adults in New Zealand cannot:</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>→ Deliver a full pepeha from memory<br>→ Explain a whakataukī and its deeper meaning<br>→ Have a philosophical conversation about language and identity in te reo<br>→ Write a narrative with traditional storytelling techniques<br>→ Argue a position with taunakitanga and discourse markers<br>→ Move fluently between six tense markers<br>→ Use all 16 personal pronouns correctly<br>→ Discuss kaitiakitanga, arts, community, and the natural world</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>You did this in four terms of daily 20-minute warm-ups. That is approximately 800 minutes — just over 13 hours of instruction.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Ka tino pai tāu mahi.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Tip: Ka tino pai tāu mahi — your work is genuinely, truly excellent. This is the right closing phrase for the year. Not just &quot;ka pai&quot; — tino pai. You have earned every word of it.</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">We Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Kia Tūhono — Full year celebration blitz</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>One final blitz — the most joyful, celebratory version of everything you know.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>PEPEHA challenge: Three students deliver their full pepeha from memory. Class gives Tēnā koe after each.</li><li>WHAKATAUKĪ round: Class says all four whakataukī together. Then explain Ko tā tērā pūrākau mōhio ko te…</li><li>FAVOURITE SENTENCE: Every student says their single favourite te reo sentence of the year. No repeats.</li><li>CLOSE together: Ka toitū tēnei reo i a tātou katoa. Ka toitū tātou i tēnei reo. Ka huri.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Discussion prompt: Ka toitū tēnei reo i a tātou katoa — this language endures through all of us. And: Ka toitū tātou i tēnei reo — we endure through this language. The relationship goes both ways. The language needs you as much as you need it. Does that feel true? What does it mean to say you are part of the survival of a language?</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">You Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Māu Anō — Final year audit</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Your last vocabulary and reflection exercise of the year.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Write every te reo word you know in 5 minutes. Count them.</li><li>Write your five favourite sentences from the whole year.</li><li>Write: Ko tāku koha ki tēnei reo: [what you give to the language — your commitment going forward].</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write a &quot;letter to the language&quot; in te reo Māori — address te reo itself. Tell it what it has meant to you, what you will do to care for it, and what you hope for it. This is your final gift to your year.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Tuesday: He whakaaro whakamutunga — Final reflections":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Te Aho Arataki Marau — All AOs · Metacognitive reflection · Full year</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Objectives</th><td class=\"lt-td\">AO 2.3 — Final reflection; AO 2.5 — Full expression</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">I Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tāua Kōrero — Looking back, standing forward</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The last Tuesday of the year. Today we reflect on the journey — not to summarise, but to stand in this moment and feel the weight of what we have built. Then we turn to face forward.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\"><strong>Looking back:</strong><br><strong>I tēnei tau, i <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=tipu\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">tipu</a> tōku reo, i tipu hoki tōku ngākau.</strong><br>This year, my language grew, and my heart grew too.<br><br><strong>Ko ēnei ngā mea hou i ako ai au i tēnei tau:</strong><br>he reo, he tikanga, he ao hou — ko tērā tōku taonga.<br>These are the new things I learned this year: a language, a culture, a new world — that is my treasure.<br><br><strong>Standing forward:</strong><br><strong>Ka haere au ki tērā tau me tēnei <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=taonga\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">taonga</a> i ōku <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=ringaringa\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">ringaringa</a>.</strong><br>I go to next year with this treasure in my hands.<br><br><strong>Māku e <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=tiaki\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">tiaki</a>, māku e <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=whakaora\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">whakaora</a>, māku e whakaatu i tēnei reo <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=tapu\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">tapu</a>.</strong><br>I will protect it, I will nurture it, I will demonstrate this sacred language.</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Final reflection vocabulary:<br><strong>i ōku ringaringa</strong> — in my hands (ringaringa = hands)<br><strong>whakaora</strong> — to heal / revitalise / give life to<br><strong>whakaatu</strong> — to demonstrate / show / present<br><strong>Ko tērā tōku taonga.</strong> — That is my treasure.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Ko ēnei ngā mea hou i ako ai au</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>These are the new things I learned: (ai = relative clause marker)</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Three commitments:<br><strong>Māku e tiaki</strong> — I will protect it<br><strong>Māku e whakaora</strong> — I will nurture/revitalise it<br><strong>Māku e whakaatu</strong> — I will demonstrate it</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Three māku e sentences in a row — a commitment in three parts.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Tip: Māku e tiaki, māku e whakaora, māku e whakaatu — three commitments. In te ao Māori, a commitment said three times is a commitment made truly. Saying māku e three times signals that you are serious.</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">We Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Kia Tūhono — Three-part commitment</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Build your three-part commitment to te reo Māori.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Look back: <strong>I tēnei tau, i tipu tōku reo, i tipu hoki tōku [heart/mind/something specific].</strong></li><li>Name what you carry: <strong>Ka haere au ki tērā tau me [what you are taking with you].</strong></li><li>Three commitments: <strong>Māku e tiaki… Māku e whakaora… Māku e whakaatu…</strong></li><li>Close together: <strong>Ka nui ōku mihi ki a koutou katoa. Tēnā koutou, tēnā koutou, tēnā koutou katoa.</strong></li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Discussion prompt: Māku e tiaki, māku e whakaora, māku e whakaatu. Three commitments to the language. Which of these three feels most natural for you? Which feels most challenging? And which — if you only kept one — would matter most?</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">You Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Māu Anō — Final reflection</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write your final reflection on the year in te reo Māori.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I tēnei tau, i tipu tōku reo… describe three things that grew.</li><li>Ka haere au ki tērā tau me [three things you are taking forward].</li><li>Write your three māku e commitments.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write your final full reflection (10 sentences) — the year in three sentences, what you carry, your three commitments, a whakataukī that guides you, and close with Ka nui ōku mihi ki a koutou katoa.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Wednesday: Hanga rerenga — The year-end showcase and certificate":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Te Aho Arataki Marau — All AOs · Whakaatu (presenting) · Year celebration</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Objectives</th><td class=\"lt-td\">AO 2.3 — Year-end showcase; AO 1.1 — Formal presentation</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">I Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tāua Kōrero — He whakaaturanga — Presenting your year</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The final Wednesday lesson. Today each student prepares a short whakaaturanga — a presentation — of their year in te reo Māori. This is the Whakaatu (presenting) language mode in full action.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\"><strong>Structure for a whakaaturanga:</strong><br><br><strong>1. Open:</strong> Tēnā koutou katoa.<br><strong>2. Identity:</strong> Ko [name] tōku ingoa. [Two pepeha lines].<br><strong>3. Journey:</strong> I tīmata au i Wāhanga Tahi me tētahi kupu — &quot;Kia ora.&quot; Ināianei, kua tipu tōku reo ki tētahi mea nui ake.<br><strong>4. Best sentence:</strong> Ko tōku rerenga pai rawa atu i tēnei tau ko tēnei: [your best sentence].<br><strong>5. <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=Whakataukī\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Whakataukī</a>:</strong> Ko tōku whakataukī e tūāhuatia ana i a au ko tēnei: [whakataukī + explanation].<br><strong>6. <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=Wawata\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Wawata</a>:</strong> Ko tōku wawata mō tōku reo: [your aspiration].<br><strong>7. Close:</strong> Tēnā koutou, tēnā koutou, tēnā koutou katoa.</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The seven elements of the whakaaturanga:</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>Formal greeting</strong> — Tēnā koutou katoa</li><li><strong>Identity</strong> — Ko sentences, pepeha (abbreviated)</li><li><strong>Journey</strong> — where you started, where you are now (I, Kua)</li><li><strong>Best sentence</strong> — your finest te reo of the year</li><li><strong>Whakataukī</strong> — the proverb that represents you + Ko tā tērā mōhio ko te…</li><li><strong>Wawata</strong> — your deepest aspiration for your reo</li><li><strong>Formal close</strong> — Tēnā koutou ×3</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Duration: approximately 60–90 seconds per student. That is enough for a full life in te reo Māori.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Tip: Ko tōku rerenga pai rawa atu — my best sentence. Own it. Stand behind it. You chose it because it is true. When you say your best sentence in front of the class, say it like you mean it — because you do.</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">We Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Kia Tūhono — Whakaaturanga preparation</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students prepare their whakaaturanga — practise with a partner first.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Write the seven elements of your whakaaturanga — notes only, not a script.</li><li>Practise with a partner: deliver it fully, they listen without interrupting.</li><li>Partner gives one piece of feedback: &quot;He pai tō [element]. Me [one suggestion].&quot;</li><li>Second run: incorporate the feedback. Aim for 60–90 seconds, from the heart.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Discussion prompt: Ko tōku rerenga pai rawa atu i tēnei tau ko tēnei. Your best sentence of the year. What IS it? If you had to choose one sentence that captures something true about you in te reo Māori — what would it be? Choose it now. Write it down. It is yours.</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">You Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Māu Anō — Whakaaturanga</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Deliver your whakaaturanga to the class.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Deliver your full whakaaturanga — all seven elements — to the class.</li><li>Listen to your classmates' whakaaturanga — give a Tēnā koe after each one.</li><li>Write your whakaaturanga as a final written record.</li><li>✦ Bonus: After the class whakaaturanga, write a response: &quot;Ko ngā rerenga i pā mai ki a au ko ēnei…&quot; (The sentences that moved me were these…) — choose three sentences from classmates and write why each one moved you.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Thursday: Kōrero — He whakamātautau — Final end-of-year assessment":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Te Aho Arataki Marau — All AOs 1.1–2.5 · All six language modes · Full year assessed</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Objectives</th><td class=\"lt-td\">AO 1.1 — Greetings; AO 1.2 — Introductions; AO 1.4 — Identity; AO 2.3 — Full sophisticated conversation; AO 2.5 — Full vocabulary; AO 1.6 — Politeness</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">I Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tāua Kōrero — The final assessment</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This is the last lesson of the year. The end-of-year assessment is a full spontaneous conversation — no script, no model to follow. Just you and your reo. This is what you have been building toward since Week 1 of Term 1.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">What a full-year assessment conversation might sound like:<br><br><strong>A:</strong> Tēnā koe. Ko wai koe?<br><strong>B:</strong> [Full pepeha from memory]<br><strong>A:</strong> Ka pai! He aha ngā pou o tōu ora?<br><strong>B:</strong> Ko ngā pou o tōku ora ko tōku whānau, ko tōku reo, ko tōku taiao.<br><strong>A:</strong> He aha tā tēnei reo i homai ai ki a koe i tēnei tau?<br><strong>B:</strong> I homai mai e ia ki a au he ara ki ngā tīpuna, he hononga ki ōku hoa, ā he māramatanga ki tōku ake ao.<br><strong>A:</strong> He aha tō wawata?<br><strong>B:</strong> Ko tōku wawata: ka toitū tēnei reo tapu — ka <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=honohono\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">honohono</a> ia i ngā tāngata katoa o Aotearoa.<br><strong>A:</strong> Ā koia! Tēnā koe mō tāu kōrero tino hōhonu.<br><strong>B:</strong> Ka nui ōku mihi ki a koe. Mā te wā.<br><strong>A:</strong> Mā te wā.</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Final assessment criteria — what you are showing:</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>✓ Full pepeha (AO 1.2, 1.4)<br>✓ Sophisticated vocabulary: pou, hononga, māramatanga, wawata (AO 2.5)<br>✓ Complex structures: Ko ngā pou…, He aha…ai…, Ka toitū, Ka honohono (AO 2.3)<br>✓ Correct greeting and farewell system (AO 1.1, 1.6)<br>✓ Spontaneous extension beyond the question (AO 2.3)<br>✓ Natural closing: Ka nui ōku mihi. Mā te wā. (AO 1.1)</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>You do not need to be perfect. You need to be present, genuine, and in te reo Māori.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Ka angitu koe. You will succeed.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Tip: Mā te wā — until another time. This is the farewell for a long parting. At the end of the school year, Mā te wā is right. You are not saying goodbye forever — you are saying: I will carry this language with me, and we will meet again in it.</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">We Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Kia Tūhono — Final preparation</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>One last practice — quietly, with a partner, in your own words.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Sit with your partner. Deliver your full assessment — no notes.</li><li>They listen fully. Then they deliver theirs.</li><li>After: Ko tāu rerenga pai rawa atu ko tēhea? Tell your partner their finest moment.</li><li>Class together, final time: Ka toitū tēnei reo i a tātou. Ka huri.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Discussion prompt: Mā te wā. Until another time. But in te reo, every time there is an &quot;until another time&quot; rather than a goodbye. The language is waiting for you. What do you say to it before you go?</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">You Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Māu Anō — He whakamātautau — End of year</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Your final end-of-year assessment.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>Task 1:</strong> Full spontaneous conversation — at least 14 lines each. No script.</li><li><strong>Task 2:</strong> Self-assess against the criteria above. Be generous and honest.</li><li><strong>Task 3:</strong> Write your final sentence of the year: Ko tōku rerenga whakamutunga o tēnei tau ko tēnei: [sentence]. This is your seal on the year.</li><li>✦ Final checklist: ☐ Pepeha (9 lines) · ☐ Ko ngā pou o tōu ora · ☐ He aha tā tēnei reo i homai ai ki a koe · ☐ Wawata statement · ☐ Ka nui ōku mihi · ☐ Mā te wā</li></ul></div></div>"},"Friday: Aromatawai — Quiz & Review":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Monday questions</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>&quot;Ka tino pai tāu mahi.&quot; What makes this different from &quot;Ka pai tāu mahi&quot;?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>They mean exactly the same thing </li><li>Tino adds genuine emphasis — truly/genuinely excellent, not just good ✅</li><li>Ka tino is a past tense marker </li><li>Tino makes it a question </li></ul></li><li>&quot;Ko tāku koha ki tēnei reo.&quot; What does koha mean?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>challenge </li><li>question </li><li>gift / contribution / offering ✅</li><li>complaint </li></ul></li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tuesday questions</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>&quot;Māku e tiaki, māku e whakaora, māku e whakaatu.&quot; What is the significance of three māku e sentences?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>It is a grammar requirement for lists </li><li>Three statements signal a deep, complete commitment in te ao Māori ✅</li><li>It makes the sentence easier to remember </li><li>The three verbs all start with wha- </li></ul></li><li>What does &quot;whakaora&quot; mean?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>to demonstrate </li><li>to protect </li><li>to heal / revitalise / give life to ✅</li><li>to forget </li></ul></li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Wednesday questions</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>What are the seven elements of a whakaaturanga?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>greeting, weather, topic, questions, answers, debate, farewell </li><li>greeting, identity, journey, best sentence, whakataukī, wawata, close ✅</li><li>pepeha, whakataukī, debate, story, song, dance, farewell </li><li>greeting, questions, answers, conclusions, thanks </li></ul></li><li>&quot;Ko ngā rerenga i pā mai ki a au ko ēnei.&quot; What does &quot;i pā mai ki a au&quot; mean?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>that I wrote myself </li><li>that were too difficult for me </li><li>that moved/touched me ✅</li><li>that I memorised </li></ul></li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Thursday questions</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>&quot;Mā te wā.&quot; When is this farewell used rather than Hei konā rā?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>For a short parting like the end of a lesson </li><li>For a long parting — until another time (like the end of the year) ✅</li><li>Only at funerals </li><li>Only between very formal people </li></ul></li><li>What is the final criterion for the end-of-year assessment?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>Perfect grammar with no errors </li><li>The longest possible sentences </li><li>Being present, genuine, and in te reo Māori — with spontaneous extension ✅</li><li>Reading from a prepared script </li></ul></li></ul></div></div>"}}},"Week 7":{"label":"Day","children":{"Monday: Arotake ā-reo — Spiral: Greetings, pepeha, and identity (Terms 1–4)":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Te Aho Arataki Marau — AO 1.1: Greetings · AO 1.2: Introductions · AO 1.4: Personal information · AO 1.6: Politeness — full spiral review</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Objectives</th><td class=\"lt-td\">AO 1.1 — Greetings — full spiral; AO 1.2 — Introductions — full spiral; AO 1.4 — Identity — full spiral</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">I Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tāua Kōrero — From Kia ora to Ko tōku wawata: the identity spiral</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Welcome to Week 7. These final four weeks are your spiral review — we revisit every major domain from the year, going deeper each time. Today we spiral back to where we began: greetings, introductions, and identity. But look how far these have grown since Term 1 Week 1.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\"><strong>TERM 1 WEEK 1 level:</strong><br>Kia ora. Ko [name] tōku ingoa.<br><br><strong>TERM 4 WEEK 7 level:</strong><br>Tēnā koutou katoa.<br>Ko [maunga] tōku maunga, ko [awa] tōku awa.<br>Ko [waka] tōku waka, ko [iwi] tōku iwi.<br>Ko [hapū] tōku hapū, ko [marae] tōku marae.<br>Ko [kura] tōku kura, ko [name] tōku ingoa.<br>Nō [place] ahau. He uri ahau nō ōku tīpuna.<br>Ko ngā pou o tōku ora ko tōku whānau, ko tōku reo, ko tōku taiao.<br>Ko tōku wawata: ka toitū tēnei reo tapu mō ōku uri ā āpōpō.<br>Nō reira, tēnā koutou, tēnā koutou, tēnā koutou katoa.</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>What has grown from Term 1 to now:</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>TERM 1: Kia ora · Ko [name] tōku ingoa · Nō [place] ahau<br>TERM 2: Tēnā koe/kōrua/koutou · Thank-you formulas<br>TERM 3: Full 9-line pepeha · He uri ahau nō ōku tīpuna · Whaikōrero structure<br>TERM 4: Ko ngā pou o tōku ora · Ko tōku wawata · Ka nui ōku mihi</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The identity statement has grown from one line to a full whakaaturanga.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>New spiral phrase:<br><strong>Nō reira, ko ahau ko [name].</strong><br>Therefore, I am [name]. (Nō reira closes the pepeha and plants the speaker firmly in their identity)</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Tip: Nō reira, ko ahau ko [name] — therefore, I am [name]. This closing line of a pepeha is the most powerful. After all the mountain, river, iwi, and hapū — you arrive at yourself. The land and people make the person.</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">We Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Kia Tūhono — Identity spiral drill</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Speed round through the full identity progression — each level building on the last.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Level 1 (T1): Kia ora. Ko [name] tōku ingoa. Nō [place] ahau. Say it fast and smooth.</li><li>Level 2 (T2): Tēnā koe. Ko [name] tōku ingoa. Tēnā koe mō tāu āwhina. Add a thank-you.</li><li>Level 3 (T3): Full pepeha — all 9 lines from memory. Ko [maunga]… through Nō reira, ko ahau ko [name].</li><li>Level 4 (T4): Add Ko ngā pou o tōku ora + Ko tōku wawata. The complete identity statement.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Discussion prompt: Nō reira, ko ahau ko [name]. After the mountain, the river, the iwi — you arrive at yourself. The land and the people define you before you define yourself. In your culture or family, how is identity shaped by place and people? Is there a Western equivalent of the pepeha?</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">You Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Māu Anō — Full identity spiral</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write and say your complete identity statement — all four levels combined.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Write your full pepeha — all 9 lines — from memory. No looking.</li><li>Add Ko ngā pou o tōku ora ko… (three pillars).</li><li>Add Ko tōku wawata: ka… (your deepest aspiration).</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write a full whakaaturanga incorporating all four levels — pepeha, pillars, wawata, best sentence of the year, whakataukī, and close with Ka nui ōku mihi. Practise delivering it in under 90 seconds.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Tuesday: Arotake ā-reo — Spiral: Tense mastery (Terms 1–4)":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Te Aho Arataki Marau — AO 2.4: Time · AO 2.3: Extended communication · Grammar Level 1–2: All tense markers consolidated</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Objectives</th><td class=\"lt-td\">AO 2.4 — Tense mastery — full spiral; AO 2.3 — Extended tense-controlled writing</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">I Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tāua Kōrero — All six tense markers: the complete picture</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>You have learned six tense and aspect markers across four terms. Today we spiral back through all of them — not as a list to memorise, but as a living system to feel and use. Each marker answers a different question about time.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\"><strong>Kei te</strong> haere ia. — She is going. (RIGHT NOW — present state)<br><strong>E haere ana</strong> ia. — She is going (and still going — ONGOING)<br><strong>I</strong> haere ia inanahi. — She went yesterday. (COMPLETED PAST — specific event)<br><strong>Ka</strong> haere ia. — She goes / went / and then she went. (NARRATIVE — story movement)<br><strong>Kua</strong> haere ia. — She has gone. (PERFECT — completed, result matters now)<br><strong>Ka haere</strong> ia āpōpō. — She will go tomorrow. (FUTURE — Ka also signals future events)<br><br><strong>NEGATIVES:</strong><br><strong>Kāore</strong> ia <strong>e haere ana.</strong> — She is not going. (present negative)<br><strong>Kāore</strong> ia <strong>i haere.</strong> — She did not go. (past negative)<br><strong>Kāore anō kua</strong> haere ia. — She has not yet gone. (perfect negative)</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The question each marker answers:</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Kei te</strong> — What is happening RIGHT NOW?<br><strong>E…ana</strong> — What is STILL HAPPENING, ongoing?<br><strong>I</strong> — What HAPPENED at a specific time in the past?<br><strong>Ka</strong> — What happened NEXT in the story / what WILL happen next?<br><strong>Kua</strong> — What has BEEN COMPLETED whose result matters NOW?<br><strong>Ka [verb] āpōpō</strong> — What WILL happen?</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Remember: in te reo Māori, the VERB never changes. Only the MARKER changes. This is the fundamental beauty of the language — one verb, six markers, six relationships to time.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Tip: One verb, six markers. Haere is always haere — going. What changes is your relationship to that going: is it now, ongoing, past, narrative, perfect, or future? The marker answers that question.</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">We Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Kia Tūhono — Tense spiral drill</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>I give you one verb — you use it with all six markers in sequence.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Verb: kai (eat). Six sentences: Kei te kai ia · E kai ana ia · I kai ia · Ka kai ia · Kua kai ia · Ka kai ia āpōpō.</li><li>Verb: ako (learn). Your turn — six sentences with all six markers. Which feels most natural?</li><li>Now add negatives: Kāore ia e kai ana · Kāore ia i kai · Kāore anō kua kai ia.</li><li>Challenge: write one sentence that uses THREE different markers — different clauses, same paragraph.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Discussion prompt: The verb never changes in te reo Māori — only the marker does. English changes the verb: &quot;eat / ate / eaten / eating / will eat&quot;. If you were teaching a new learner of te reo Māori, how would you explain why the te reo system is actually EASIER than English? What is the hardest part to explain?</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">You Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Māu Anō — Tense spiral writing</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Use all six markers in a connected passage about your year.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Write Kei te and E…ana sentences about right now in class.</li><li>Write I and Ka sentences about what you did this term.</li><li>Write Kua and Ka [future] sentences about what has been achieved and what comes next.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write a &quot;year in tenses&quot; passage (10 sentences) — each sentence uses a different marker, tells a different moment of your year, and they connect to form one flowing account. Label each marker.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Wednesday: Hanga rerenga — Spiral: Complex sentences from all terms":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Te Aho Arataki Marau — AO 2.3: Extended communication · Grammar Level 1–2: All complex structures consolidated</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Objectives</th><td class=\"lt-td\">AO 2.3 — Complex sentence spiral; AO 2.5 — Full vocabulary in complex sentences</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">I Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tāua Kōrero — Every complex structure you know</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>You have learned a full toolkit of complex sentence structures. Today we spiral back through all of them — the joining words, the subordinate clauses, the discourse markers — and practise moving between them fluently.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\"><strong>COORDINATING:</strong> ā · engari · nō reira · me<br><strong>SUBORDINATING:</strong> nō te mea · ahakoa · mehemea · i te wā · ina…ana<br><strong>RELATIVE CLAUSE:</strong> [noun] + [clause] + ai<br><strong>PURPOSE:</strong> hei [verb]<br><strong>DISCOURSE MARKERS:</strong> waihoki · heoi anō · hei tauira · arā · Ko te mutunga · kāore noa iho<br><strong>DEGREE:</strong> tino · āhua · noa · tonu · anō · kē<br><br><strong>ALL IN ONE PASSAGE:</strong><br>I ako au i te reo Māori i tēnei tau — he mea uaua, <strong>engari</strong> he mea pai <strong>hoki</strong>. <strong>Ahakoa</strong> he uauatanga, i mahi tonu au <strong>nō te mea</strong> ko tōku moemoeā tōku ara. <strong>Kāore noa iho</strong> — ko tēnei reo tōku hononga ki ngā tīpuna, <strong>arā</strong>, ki ngā uri hoki. <strong>Ko te mutunga</strong>, ko tōku wawata: ka toitū tēnei reo <strong>hei</strong> oranga mō āpōpō ō ngā uri.</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Count the complex structures in that passage:<br>engari (contrast)<br>hoki (also)<br>ahakoa (concession)<br>nō te mea (reason)<br>kāore noa iho (not only that)<br>arā (namely)<br>Ko te mutunga (in conclusion)<br>hei (purpose clause)</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Eight complex structures in five sentences. Every one of them natural, every one of them in service of something true.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>New spiral word:<br><strong>oranga</strong> — wellbeing / health / vitality<br><strong>hei oranga</strong> — for the wellbeing of<br><strong>Ka toitū tēnei reo hei oranga mō ngā uri.</strong><br>This language will endure for the wellbeing of descendants.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Tip: Every complex structure you know is a tool for precision. Without them, you can say what happened. With them, you can say why, when, although, because, in order to, and despite. They are the difference between reporting and understanding.</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">We Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Kia Tūhono — Complex sentence spiral</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>I give you a plain sentence — you expand it using three different complex structures.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Plain: &quot;I ako au i te reo Māori.&quot; → Add ahakoa, nō te mea, and hei. Three expansions.</li><li>Plain: &quot;He pai tēnei reo.&quot; → Add kāore noa iho, arā, and Ko te mutunga.</li><li>Plain: &quot;Ka haere mātou ki te kura.&quot; → Add i te wā, engari, and ā.</li><li>Now write your own: one plain sentence about te reo Māori — expand it with five complex structures.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Discussion prompt: Ko te mutunga, ko tōku wawata: ka toitū tēnei reo hei oranga mō āpōpō ō ngā uri. Count the structures: Ko te mutunga (discourse marker), Ko (identity), Ka toitū (future), hei oranga (purpose), mō āpōpō ō ngā uri (time + possession). Five structures in one sentence. And yet it sounds natural. Why?</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">You Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Māu Anō — Complex sentence showcase</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write a paragraph using every complex structure type at least once.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Write a sentence with ahakoa + nō te mea in the same clause.</li><li>Write a sentence with a relative clause (ai) + purpose clause (hei).</li><li>Write a sentence using waihoki + arā + Ko te mutunga across three sentences.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write your finest complex te reo paragraph (8 sentences) — use all five categories: coordinating, subordinating, relative, purpose, and discourse markers. Make it a genuine statement about your year.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Thursday: Kōrero — Spiral: The full-year conversation challenge":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Te Aho Arataki Marau — All AOs 1.1–2.5 · Full spontaneous conversation</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Objectives</th><td class=\"lt-td\">AO 2.3 — Full conversation spiral; AO 1.1 — Greetings spiral; AO 2.5 — Full vocabulary spiral</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">I Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tāua Kōrero — The full-year conversation: every domain</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Today we have the most comprehensive conversation of the year — touching every major domain from Terms 1–4. This is the spiral in conversation form: identity, past events, feelings, weather, community, arts, environment, whakataukī, and wawata — all in one exchange.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\"><strong>A:</strong> Tēnā koe. Ko wai koe? [Full pepeha from B]<br><strong>B:</strong> [9-line pepeha] Nō reira, tēnā koe.<br><strong>A:</strong> He pēhea ōu hararei? He aha ngā mea hou?<br><strong>B:</strong> I haere māua ko tōku māmā ki te ngahere. I ātaahua te rangi — ahakoa i ua i tētahi rā, i tākaro tonu mātou i waho. Ka ora tōku ngākau i te āhua o te taiao.<br><strong>A:</strong> He aha tō whakataukī e tūāhuatia ana i a koe i tēnei wā?<br><strong>B:</strong> &quot;Ka toitū te reo, ka toitū te tangata, ka toitū hoki te taiao.&quot; Ko tā tērā mōhio ko te hononga o ngā mea katoa.<br><strong>A:</strong> He aha tō wawata mō āpōpō?<br><strong>B:</strong> Ko tōku wawata: ka honohono tēnei reo i ngā tāngata katoa o Aotearoa — ka toitū hoki ia hei oranga mō ōku uri.<br><strong>A:</strong> <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=%C4%80+koia\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Ā koia</a>! Ka nui ōku <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=mihi\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">mihi</a> ki a koe. Mā te wā.<br><strong>B:</strong> Mā te wā.</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Every domain in that conversation:</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Identity (T1/T3): full pepeha<br>Past events (T2): I haere māua · I ua i tētahi rā<br>Feelings (T2): Ka ora tōku ngākau<br>Weather (T2): i ātaahua te rangi · i ua<br>Nature (T2/T3): i te āhua o te taiao · i te ngahere<br>Concession (T1): ahakoa i ua, i tākaro tonu<br>Whakataukī (T3): stated and explained<br>Wawata (T4): Ko tōku wawata: ka honohono…<br>Purpose (T4): hei oranga mō ōku uri<br>Farewell (T4): Mā te wā</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Tip: Ka toitū te reo, ka toitū te tangata, ka toitū hoki te taiao. This whakataukī connects language, people, and the natural world. It is the perfect whakataukī for a conversation that has touched all three. Have it ready — it fits almost any deep te reo conversation.</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">We Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Kia Tūhono — Full-year conversation spiral</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Pairs. This conversation must touch every domain from the year. No scripts.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Open: formal greeting + full pepeha. (T1/T3 identity)</li><li>Exchange: holidays/past events + weather + feeling. (T2 domains)</li><li>Deepen: whakataukī + explanation + wawata. (T3/T4 domains)</li><li>Close: Ka nui ōku mihi + Mā te wā. (T4 farewell)</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Discussion prompt: Ka toitū te reo, ka toitū te tangata, ka toitū hoki te taiao. Language, people, natural world — all endure together. This year you have learned a language. In doing so, what else have you learned? What does learning te reo teach you about Aotearoa, about people, about yourself?</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">You Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Māu Anō — Full-year spiral conversation</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Have the most complete te reo conversation of your life — every domain, fully spontaneous.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Full pepeha from memory — no hesitation.</li><li>Past event + weather + feeling — at least four sentences.</li><li>Whakataukī + explanation + wawata — your genuine answers.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write up the full conversation (16+ lines). Annotate every domain and structure you used. How many of the 12 AOs appear in your conversation? Aim for all of them.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Friday: Aromatawai — Quiz & Review":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Monday questions</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>&quot;Nō reira, ko ahau ko [name].&quot; Where does this line appear in a pepeha and what does it do?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>It opens the pepeha as a greeting </li><li>It closes the pepeha — planting the speaker firmly in their identity after naming land and people ✅</li><li>It is a farewell at the end of a speech </li><li>It introduces the mountain and river </li></ul></li><li>What is the correct order of elements in a full pepeha?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>Name → mountain → river → iwi → school → origin </li><li>Mountain → river → waka → iwi → hapū → marae → school → name → origin (Nō reira ko ahau) ✅</li><li>Origin → name → iwi → mountain → river </li><li>School → name → iwi → hapū → origin </li></ul></li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tuesday questions</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>&quot;Kua haere ia.&quot; What does Kua signal about this action?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>She is going right now </li><li>She was going in the past at a specific time </li><li>She has gone and the result is relevant now ✅</li><li>She will go tomorrow </li></ul></li><li>Which tense marker signals an ONGOING action that is still continuing?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>Kei te </li><li>I </li><li>Ka </li><li>E…ana ✅</li></ul></li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Wednesday questions</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>&quot;Ka toitū tēnei reo hei oranga mō āpōpō ō ngā uri.&quot; What does &quot;hei oranga&quot; express?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>a past event </li><li>a purpose clause — for the wellbeing of ✅</li><li>a negative </li><li>a comparison </li></ul></li><li>&quot;Kāore noa iho — ko tēnei reo tōku hononga ki ngā tīpuna.&quot; What does kāore noa iho signal?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>It is not at all </li><li>Not only that — there is more ✅</li><li>It is just nothing </li><li>It is almost nothing </li></ul></li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Thursday questions</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>&quot;Ka toitū te reo, ka toitū te tangata, ka toitū hoki te taiao.&quot; What three things endure together?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>language, school, community </li><li>language, people, and the natural world ✅</li><li>language, ancestors, descendants </li><li>language, culture, money </li></ul></li><li>In the full-year conversation, which farewell is used for a long parting like the end of term?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>Hei konā rā </li><li>E noho rā </li><li>Ā ka kite anō </li><li>Mā te wā ✅</li></ul></li></ul></div></div>"}}},"Week 8":{"label":"Day","children":{"Monday: Arotake ā-reo — Spiral: Descriptions, comparisons, and figurative language":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Te Aho Arataki Marau — AO 2.5: Descriptions · AO 2.3: Opinions · Grammar Level 1–2: He, comparatives, simile, metaphor — full spiral</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Objectives</th><td class=\"lt-td\">AO 2.5 — Description spiral; AO 2.3 — Comparison &amp; figurative language</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">I Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tāua Kōrero — From He nui to Ko te reo he aho: the description spiral</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>In Term 1 you learned He nui (it is big). By Term 4 you are writing Ko te reo he aho e whiri ana i ngā tāngata (language is a cord that weaves people). Today we spiral through every layer of description — adjectives, comparatives, superlatives, degree words, simile, and metaphor.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\"><strong>TERM 1 level:</strong><br>He nui tēnei whare. He pai tōku kura.<br><br><strong>TERM 2 level:</strong><br>He nui ake tērā whare i tēnei. He tino pai tōku kura — he pai rawa atu i ērā atu kura.<br><br><strong>TERM 3 level:</strong><br>He tino ātaahua tōku kura, he wāhi tiaki mōku — ka tipu ōku whakaaro i konei.<br><br><strong>TERM 4 level (simile):</strong><br>E tipu ana tōku ngākau āhua te <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=r%C4%81kau\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">rākau</a> i te tōanga o te <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=k%C5%8Danga\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">koanga</a>.<br>My heart grows like a tree in the opening of spring.<br><br><strong>TERM 4 level (metaphor):</strong><br>Ko tōku kura he māramatanga ki te ao mārama — ko ia tōku <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=aho\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">aho</a> ki ngā tīpuna.</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The description spiral:</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>He [adjective] → basic description<br>He [adj] ake → comparative<br>He [adj] rawa atu → superlative<br>He tino [adj] → intensified<br>[verb] āhua/me he [comparison] → simile<br>Ko [X] he [metaphor] → metaphor</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>New spiral phrase:<br><strong>i te tōanga o te kōanga</strong> — in the opening of spring<br><strong>tō</strong> — the opening / unfolding (of something)<br><strong>E tipu ana tōku ngākau āhua te rākau i te tōanga o te kōanga.</strong><br>My heart grows like a tree in the opening of spring.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Tip: From He nui to Ko te reo he aho e whiri ana i ngā tāngata — that journey took you one year. The first sentence describes a size. The second describes a relationship between people and language. That is what learning a language does to your thinking.</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">We Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Kia Tūhono — Description spiral drill</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Take one noun through every level of description.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Noun: te reo Māori. Level 1: He [adjective] te reo Māori.</li><li>Level 2: He [adj] ake te reo Māori i [comparison].</li><li>Level 3: He tino [adj] te reo Māori — he [adj] rawa atu.</li><li>Level 4: Ko te reo Māori he [metaphor] — E [verb] ana ia āhua te [simile].</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Discussion prompt: E tipu ana tōku ngākau āhua te rākau i te tōanga o te kōanga. Heart. Tree. Spring. Three things connected through the word āhua (like). What is it about a tree opening in spring that captures how a heart grows? Why is this a better description than &quot;my heart is growing&quot;?</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">You Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Māu Anō — Full description spiral</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write descriptions of your year at every level from basic to figurative.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Write He [adj] and He [adj] ake sentences about te reo Māori.</li><li>Write a tino/rawa atu superlative about your best learning moment.</li><li>Write a simile (āhua/me he) about something that grew in you this year.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write a Ko [X] he [metaphor] sentence about te reo Māori — something as beautiful as Ko te reo he aho e whiri ana i ngā tāngata. Then write a short paragraph continuing the metaphor.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Tuesday: Arotake ā-reo — Spiral: Community, environment, and kaitiakitanga":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Te Aho Arataki Marau — AO 1.4: Connection to land · AO 2.1: Relationships · AO 2.4: Natural world — full spiral</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Objectives</th><td class=\"lt-td\">AO 1.4 — Land &amp; community spiral; AO 2.4 — Environment &amp; kaitiakitanga spiral</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">I Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tāua Kōrero — From Nō hea koe to Ka ora te taiao ka ora tātou: the community spiral</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>From Term 1 Week 4 (places and ki) to Term 3 Week 7 (kaitiakitanga and toitū) — the community and environment strand runs through the whole year. Today we spiral it all together: where you come from, who your community is, and your responsibility to the natural world.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\"><strong>TERM 1 level:</strong><br>Nō Ōtautahi ahau. Kei te haere au ki te toa.<br><br><strong>TERM 2 level:</strong><br>Nō Aotearoa ahau — he whenua ātaahua tēnei. Ko ēnei ngā āhuatanga pai o tōku tāone.<br><br><strong>TERM 3 level:</strong><br>Ko tātou ngā kaitiaki o tēnei taiao. Me tiaki tātou i te mauri o ngā awa, o ngā ngahere, o ngā maunga.<br><br><strong>TERM 4 level:</strong><br>Ka ora te taiao, ka ora tātou — he pono tēnei. Ko tōku wawata: ka toitū te taiao o Aotearoa hei oranga mō ōku uri. E ai ki āku whakaaro, he kawenga ōu mō tēnei whenua ātaahua.</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The community/environment spiral:</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>T1: Location (nō hea, ki hea, kei hea)<br>T2: Description of place (He ātaahua, Ko ēnei ngā āhuatanga pai)<br>T3: Kaitiakitanga (me tiaki, mauri, toitū)<br>T4: Philosophical connection (Ka ora te taiao, wawata, kawenga)</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Full statement:<br><strong>Ko au he uri o tēnei whenua — he kawenga ōku ki te tiaki i tōna oranga mō āpōpō ō ngā uri.</strong><br>I am a descendant of this land — I have a responsibility to protect its wellbeing for tomorrow's descendants.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>New spiral phrase:<br><strong>Ko au he uri o tēnei whenua.</strong><br>I am a descendant of this land.<br><strong>tōna oranga</strong> — its wellbeing / its vitality</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Tip: Ko au he uri o tēnei whenua — I am a descendant of this land. This sentence connects pepeha (belonging to land) with kaitiakitanga (responsibility to land). You are not separate from the land — you come FROM it, and you are responsible TO it.</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">We Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Kia Tūhono — Community and environment spiral</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Spiral through every level of community and environment language.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>T1/T2: Nō [place] ahau. He [adjective] tōku whenua/tāone. Ko ēnei ngā āhuatanga pai…</li><li>T3: Ko tātou ngā kaitiaki o tēnei taiao. Me tiaki tātou i te mauri o… nō te mea…</li><li>T4: Ka ora te taiao, ka ora tātou. Ko tōku wawata: ka toitū…</li><li>Full: Ko au he uri o tēnei whenua — he kawenga ōku ki te tiaki i tōna oranga mō ōku uri.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Discussion prompt: Ko au he uri o tēnei whenua. You are a descendant of the land. Not just of people — of the land itself. What would it mean to genuinely believe this? How would it change the way you live, eat, travel, and consume if you truly felt you were descended from the land and responsible to it?</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">You Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Māu Anō — Community and environment spiral</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write your full community and environment statement — all four levels.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Write Nō [place] ahau + He [adj] tōku whenua + Ko ēnei ngā āhuatanga pai.</li><li>Write Ko tātou ngā kaitiaki o tēnei taiao + Me tiaki tātou i… + reason.</li><li>Write Ka ora te taiao, ka ora tātou + Ko tōku wawata mō te taiao.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write a full kaitiakitanga statement (10 sentences) — your origin, your community, what you love about your place, what threatens it, your responsibility, what you commit to, and your wawata for the natural world of Aotearoa.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Wednesday: Hanga rerenga — Spiral: Argument, opinion, and persuasion":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Te Aho Arataki Marau — AO 2.3: Extended persuasive communication · Grammar Level 1–2: Full argument toolkit consolidated</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Objectives</th><td class=\"lt-td\">AO 2.3 — Argument &amp; persuasion spiral; AO 2.5 — Formal argument vocabulary</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">I Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tāua Kōrero — From He pai ake to Tōku tūāhuatanga: the argument spiral</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>You began arguing in Term 1 with He pai ake (better than). By Term 4 you are writing full structured arguments with taunakitanga, waihoki, and Ko te mutunga. Today we spiral the full argument toolkit — from simple comparison to philosophical persuasion.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\"><strong>TERM 1 level:</strong><br>He pai ake te hākinakina i te noho ki te kāinga ki a au nō te mea he hauora ake.<br><br><strong>TERM 2 level:</strong><br>He pai ake te mahi tahi i te mahi kotahi — he nui ake ngā hua. Ka pai tāu whakaaro, engari rā…<br><br><strong>TERM 3 level:</strong><br>Tōku tūāhuatanga: he nui ake ngā hua o te kaitiakitanga i ngā uauatanga. Tāku taunakitanga tuatahi: Ka ora te taiao, ka ora tātou.<br><br><strong>TERM 4 level:</strong><br>E ai ki āku whakaaro, ko te reo Māori te taonga rangatira rawa atu o Aotearoa. Kāore noa iho — ko ia hoki te ara ki te ao o ōku tīpuna. Waihoki, ka honohono ia i ngā tāngata katoa. Ko te mutunga, me tiaki tātou i tēnei taonga mō āpōpō ō ngā uri.</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The argument spiral:</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>T1: He pai ake + nō te mea (simple comparison + reason)<br>T2: Whakaae/whakaaro exchanges, He tika tāu engari rā<br>T3: Tūāhuatanga + taunakitanga tuatahi/tuarua<br>T4: E ai ki āku whakaaro + kāore noa iho + waihoki + Ko te mutunga</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The four-term argument:<br>Simple → Dialogic → Structured → Philosophical</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>New spiral phrase:<br><strong>taonga rangatira rawa atu</strong> — the most precious / chiefly treasure<br><strong>ko ia hoki te ara</strong> — it is also the path</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Tip: Ko te mutunga — in conclusion. This is your power phrase. After all the evidence, all the concessions, all the comparisons — Ko te mutunga lands your final point with authority. Use it when you mean it.</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">We Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Kia Tūhono — Argument spiral drill</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Build the same argument at four different levels of sophistication.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>T1 level: He pai ake te [X] i te [Y] ki a au nō te mea…</li><li>T2 level: He tika tāu whakaaro — engari rā, ko tāku whakaaro he [counter-view].</li><li>T3 level: Tōku tūāhuatanga: [position]. Tāku taunakitanga tuatahi: [evidence].</li><li>T4 level: E ai ki āku whakaaro… Kāore noa iho… Waihoki… Ko te mutunga…</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Discussion prompt: Ko te mutunga, me tiaki tātou i tēnei taonga mō āpōpō ō ngā uri. A conclusion that points to future generations. The best arguments in te ao Māori often end by considering uri (descendants). Why? What does including future generations in an argument do to its moral weight?</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">You Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Māu Anō — Full argument spiral</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write a four-level argument about te reo Māori — from simple to philosophical.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>T1 level: He pai ake te ako i te reo Māori i [alternative] nō te mea…</li><li>T3 level: Tōku tūāhuatanga + two taunakitanga (tuatahi/tuarua).</li><li>T4 level: E ai ki āku whakaaro + kāore noa iho + waihoki + Ko te mutunga.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write your finest persuasive piece (10 sentences) about why te reo Māori matters — using the full argument toolkit from all four terms. This is your year-end statement of belief.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Thursday: Kōrero — Spiral: He kōrero ā-iwi — A community conversation":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Te Aho Arataki Marau — AO 1.4: Community · AO 2.1: Relationships · AO 2.3: Extended conversation — all community domains</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Objectives</th><td class=\"lt-td\">AO 2.1 — Community relationships spiral; AO 1.4 — Identity &amp; community spiral</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">I Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tāua Kōrero — Everything about community in one conversation</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Today we spiral all the community language from the year — whānau, hapū, iwi, marae, school, environment, kaitiakitanga, relationships — into one rich conversation.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\"><strong>A:</strong> Tēnā koe. Nō hea koe ā ko wai ōu iwi?<br><strong>B:</strong> Nō Ōtautahi ahau. Ko Ngāi Tahu tōku iwi, ko Ngāti Māmoe tōku hapū.<br><strong>A:</strong> He aha ngā āhuatanga pai rawa atu o tō rohe ki a koe?<br><strong>B:</strong> Ko ēnei: he ātaahua te taiao, he māia ngā tāngata, ā he rangatira hoki te reo o tēnei whenua.<br><strong>A:</strong> He aha tō kawenga ki tōu hapori?<br><strong>B:</strong> He kawenga ōku ki te tiaki i te taiao, ki te kōrero i te reo Māori, ā ki te whakaaro nui mō ngā uri — nā ōku tīpuna ki a au, ā māku ki ōku uri.<br><strong>A:</strong> Ka wani kē! Ko tāu kōrero he māramatanga ki a au.<br><strong>B:</strong> Tēnā koe. Ka nui ōku mihi ki a koe mō tāu aro mai ki ōku kōrero.</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>New spiral phrases:<br><strong>tō rohe</strong> — your region / area / territory<br><strong>Ko ēnei: [list]</strong> — These are: (using Ko ēnei for listing community assets)<br><strong>nā ōku tīpuna ki a au</strong> — from my ancestors to me<br><strong>ā māku ki ōku uri</strong> — and from me to my descendants<br><strong>Ka wani kē!</strong> — That is amazing! (from Term 3 Week 5!)<br><strong>Ko tāu kōrero he māramatanga ki a au.</strong><br>Your words are an enlightenment to me.<br><strong>mō tāu aro mai ki ōku kōrero</strong> — for turning toward my words / for listening to me<br><strong>aro mai</strong> — turn toward / face toward / attend to</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Tip: Nā ōku tīpuna ki a au, ā māku ki ōku uri — from my ancestors to me, and from me to my descendants. This sentence places you in the middle of a chain that stretches from the past to the future. You are not the beginning or the end — you are the bridge.</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">We Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Kia Tūhono — Community spiral conversation</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Build a community conversation touching every domain from the year.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Open with formal greeting + Nō hea koe + ko wai ōu iwi/hapū.</li><li>Share Ko ēnei ngā āhuatanga pai rawa atu o tōku rohe: a Ko ēnei list.</li><li>State your kawenga: He kawenga ōku ki te [three responsibilities].</li><li>Close with nā ōku tīpuna ki a au, ā māku ki ōku uri + Ka nui ōku mihi.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Discussion prompt: Ko tāu kōrero he māramatanga ki a au — your words are an enlightenment to me. This is the highest compliment in a te reo conversation — you have not just been understood, you have brought light. When has someone's words brought light to YOU this year? In te reo or in any language?</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">You Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Māu Anō — Community spiral conversation</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Have a full community conversation — every domain from Terms 1–4.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Exchange origins, iwi/hapū, and region descriptions.</li><li>Share Ko ēnei ngā āhuatanga pai lists about your communities.</li><li>State your kawenga and the nā ōku tīpuna ki a au chain.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write the full conversation (14+ lines). Annotate which term each piece of language comes from — T1, T2, T3, or T4. How evenly distributed is it? Which term contributed the most to this conversation?</li></ul></div></div>"},"Friday: Aromatawai — Quiz & Review":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Monday questions</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>&quot;E tipu ana tōku ngākau āhua te rākau i te tōanga o te kōanga.&quot; What figure of speech is this?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>metaphor </li><li>personification </li><li>simile — the heart grows LIKE a tree in spring ✅</li><li>alliteration </li></ul></li><li>What is the difference between He nui (T1) and Ko te reo he aho e whiri ana i ngā tāngata (T4)?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>One is a description of size; the other is a metaphor about language connecting people ✅</li><li>They mean the same thing </li><li>One is formal, one is casual </li><li>One uses past tense, one uses present </li></ul></li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tuesday questions</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>&quot;Ko au he uri o tēnei whenua.&quot; What does this sentence claim about the speaker's relationship to the land?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>The speaker owns the land </li><li>The speaker is a guardian employed to protect the land </li><li>The speaker is a descendant of the land — belonging to it ✅</li><li>The speaker is a visitor from another land </li></ul></li><li>&quot;Ka ora te taiao, ka ora tātou.&quot; What principle does this whakataukī-style sentence express?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>The environment is more important than people </li><li>Our wellbeing and the environment's wellbeing are inseparable ✅</li><li>We must protect the environment because we are told to </li><li>The environment survives without people </li></ul></li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Wednesday questions</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>What is &quot;tūāhuatanga&quot; in an argument context?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>the conclusion </li><li>the evidence </li><li>your position / stance ✅</li><li>the introduction </li></ul></li><li>&quot;Ko te mutunga, me tiaki tātou i tēnei taonga mō āpōpō ō ngā uri.&quot; What does including &quot;ngā uri&quot; in a conclusion do?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>It weakens the argument by looking too far ahead </li><li>It adds moral weight by considering future generations ✅</li><li>It is required by te reo grammar </li><li>It replaces the need for evidence </li></ul></li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Thursday questions</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>&quot;Nā ōku tīpuna ki a au, ā māku ki ōku uri.&quot; What chain does this sentence describe?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>The chain of school learning </li><li>The chain of money and resources </li><li>The chain of responsibility from ancestors through the speaker to descendants ✅</li><li>The chain of greetings in a pōhiri </li></ul></li><li>&quot;Ko tāu kōrero he māramatanga ki a au.&quot; What is the speaker expressing?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>The other person is talking too much </li><li>Your words have brought enlightenment / light to me ✅</li><li>I do not understand what you said </li><li>Please speak more clearly </li></ul></li></ul></div></div>"}}},"Week 9":{"label":"Day","children":{"Monday: Arotake ā-reo — Spiral: Arts, culture, and whakataukī":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Te Aho Arataki Marau — AO 1.4: Cultural identity · AO 2.5: Arts and figurative language · AO 2.3: Extended cultural communication</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Objectives</th><td class=\"lt-td\">AO 1.4 — Arts &amp; culture spiral; AO 2.5 — Whakataukī &amp; figurative spiral</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">I Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tāua Kōrero — From toi whakairo to Ko te reo he aho: the culture spiral</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Today we spiral the arts, culture, and wisdom strand — haka, waiata, whakairo, raranga, whakataukī, tikanga. These are the domains that connect language to living culture. By Term 4, you can not just name them — you can explain, analyse, and connect them to identity.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\"><strong>TERM 3 level (naming):</strong><br>Ko te haka tētahi mahi toi rongonui o Aotearoa.<br>He tohu aroha te haka — he tohu kaha hoki.<br><br><strong>TERM 4 level (explaining):</strong><br>Ko te tikanga o te haka ko te whakamana i te tangata me tōna hapori.<br>Ko tā tēnei mahi toi mōhio ko te mana o te kaha me te aroha — ko ērā ngā mea e kore e wehewehea.<br><br><strong>WHAKATAUKĪ spiral:</strong><br>&quot;He aha te mea nui o te ao? He tangata, he tangata, he tangata.&quot;<br>Ko tā tēnei whakataukī mōhio ko te pūtake o ngā mea katoa — ko ngā tāngata.</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The culture spiral levels:</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Naming: Ko te haka tētahi mahi toi.<br>Describing: He tohu aroha, he tohu kaha.<br>Explaining: Ko te tikanga o te haka ko te…<br>Connecting to identity: Ko tā tēnei mōhio ko te mana o te tangata.<br>Using as evidence: Hei tauira, ko te haka — arā, ko ia he tohu o ngā mea e kore e wehewehea.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>New spiral phrase:<br><strong>whakamana</strong> — to empower / to honour / to give authority to<br><strong>Ko te tikanga o te haka ko te whakamana i te tangata.</strong><br>The meaning of haka is to honour/empower the person.<br><strong>e kore e wehewehea</strong> — that cannot be separated</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Tip: E kore e wehewehea — cannot be separated. Love and strength in haka. Language and identity in te reo. Land and people in kaitiakitanga. The most important things in te ao Māori e kore e wehewehea — they cannot be separated from each other.</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">We Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Kia Tūhono — Arts and culture spiral</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Spiral through every level of arts and culture language.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Name three mahi toi: Ko ēnei ngā mahi toi ōku: he [X], he [Y], he [Z].</li><li>Explain one: Ko te tikanga o te [art form] ko te [explanation].</li><li>Give deeper meaning: Ko tā tēnei mahi toi mōhio ko te [deeper lesson].</li><li>Use a whakataukī as evidence: Hei tauira, [whakataukī] — arā, [explanation].</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Discussion prompt: E kore e wehewehea — cannot be separated. What two things in YOUR life cannot be separated? Not just love and strength — something specific to you. And can you say it in te reo Māori?</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">You Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Māu Anō — Arts and culture spiral writing</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write about art, culture, and wisdom at Term 4 level.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Name your favourite art form and explain its tikanga: Ko te tikanga o te [art] ko te…</li><li>Give its deeper meaning: Ko tā tēnei mōhio ko te…</li><li>Use a whakataukī as evidence with hei tauira and arā.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write a full cultural explanation (8 sentences) of one art form or cultural practice — naming, describing, explaining tikanga, deeper meaning, whakataukī evidence, connection to identity, and a personal wawata about that art form.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Tuesday: Arotake ā-reo — Spiral: Full grammar — every structure in conversation":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Te Aho Arataki Marau — All Grammar Level 1–2 · All AOs — final spiral</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Objectives</th><td class=\"lt-td\">AO 2.3 — Full grammar spiral; AO 2.4 — Tense &amp; structure mastery</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">I Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tāua Kōrero — Every structure, natural and fluent</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This is the penultimate grammar lesson of the year. Today we use every major structure from the full year in a single natural passage — not to show off, but to feel how effortlessly they can work together when you are not thinking about them individually.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\"><strong>A four-term grammar showcase passage:</strong><br><br>Ko ahau ko [name] — he ākonga i tīmatahia noatia e tētahi kupu inanahi, ināianei, kua tipu ōku kupu ki tētahi ao hou.<br><br>E ako tonu ana au, kāore anō kua oti — engari, ahakoa he uauatanga i ōku ara, i mahi tonu au nō te mea ko tōku moemoeā tōku māramatanga.<br><br>Ka taea e au te mihi, ka taea e au te tuhituhi, ka taea e au te whakaaro i tēnei reo rangatira — ko ia te <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=aho\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">aho</a> e <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=whiri\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">whiri</a> ana i a au ki ngā tīpuna.<br><br>Māku e tiaki. Māku e whakaora. Māku e whakaatu.<br><br>Ko te mutunga, ko tōku wawata: ka toitū tēnei reo tapu hei oranga mō ōku uri ā āpōpō. Ka huri.</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Every structure in that passage:<br>Ko (identity ×2)<br>He (description)<br>I (past: i tīmatahia)<br>Kua (perfect: kua tipu, kua oti)<br>E…ana (progressive: E ako tonu ana)<br>Kāore anō kua (perfect negative)<br>Ahakoa (concession)<br>Nō te mea (reason)<br>Ko + moemoeā (dream)<br>Ka taea (ability ×3)<br>Ko [metaphor] (Ko ia te aho…)<br>ai (relative: e whiri ana i a au)<br>Māku e (commitment ×3)<br>Ko te mutunga (conclusion)<br>Ko + wawata (aspiration)<br>Hei oranga (purpose)<br>Ka huri (story close)</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>17 different structures. One passage. And it sounds like real te reo.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Tip: When you stop thinking about structures and start thinking about what you want to say, the structures take care of themselves. That is fluency. That is what this year has been building toward.</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">We Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Kia Tūhono — Final grammar spiral</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Read the showcase passage together aloud — then each student writes their own version.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Open: Ko ahau ko [name] — he ākonga i tīmatahia noatia e tētahi kupu inanahi, ināianei…</li><li>Middle: E ako tonu ana au, kāore anō kua oti — ahakoa… nō te mea…</li><li>Achievement: Ka taea e au te… ka taea e au te… ka taea e au te…</li><li>Commitment + close: Māku e tiaki. Māku e whakaora. Ko tōku wawata: ka… Ka huri.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Discussion prompt: Ko tōku moemoeā tōku māramatanga — my dream is my understanding/clarity. Not wealth, not fame, not even success — māramatanga. Clarity of thought and understanding. Is this your dream too? What does māramatanga mean to you — in te reo and in life?</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">You Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Māu Anō — Final grammar showcase</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write YOUR version of the four-term grammar showcase passage.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Write Ko + He sentences about your identity and your character.</li><li>Write I + Kua + E…ana about your learning journey.</li><li>Write Ka taea sentences about what you can do + Ko metaphor.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write your full four-term grammar showcase (12 sentences) — every structure from the year, in service of something true about you and your te reo journey. End with Ka huri.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Wednesday: Hanga rerenga — Spiral: He mihi ki a tātou — A tribute to each other":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Te Aho Arataki Marau — AO 2.3: Extended communication · AO 2.5: Full vocabulary · AO 1.1: Formal acknowledgement</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Objectives</th><td class=\"lt-td\">AO 2.3 — Tribute writing; AO 1.1 — Formal acknowledgement</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">I Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tāua Kōrero — Writing tributes to people who matter</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>In Term 2 Week 8 you wrote tributes to people you admire using He tangata atawhai, He tangata pukumahi, and Ko ia te pou o tōku ao. Today we spiral back to that — but at Term 4 level, writing tributes that use the full year's vocabulary and express deep appreciation.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\"><strong>TERM 2 level:</strong><br>Ko Hēmi tōku hoa pai rawa atu. He tangata atawhai, he tangata ngākaunui hoki ia.<br>Ka ora tōku ngākau i tōna āhua.<br><br><strong>TERM 4 level:</strong><br>Ko [name] tētahi o ngā tāngata nui rawa atu i tōku ao — ko ia he pou o tōku oranga.<br>I āwhina ia i a au i ngā wā katoa e uaua ana ōku ara — e kore e <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=wareware\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">wareware</a> ana i a au tōna <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=atawhai\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">atawhai</a>.<br>Ko tā tōna āhua mōhio ki a au ko te tikanga o te <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=toa+takitini\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">toa takitini</a> — ka toa tātou i a ia.<br>Nō reira, tēnā koe [name] mō tāu āwhina, mō tāu aroha, mō tāu oranga ki a au.</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The tribute spiral:</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>T2 level: He tangata [character] · Ko ia te pou · Ka ora tōku ngākau<br>T4 level: Ko ia he pou o tōku oranga · e kore e wareware ana · ko tā tōna āhua mōhio · tēnā koe mō tāu [X, Y, Z]</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>New tribute vocabulary:<br><strong>e kore e wareware ana i a au</strong> — I will never forget (wareware = forget)<br><strong>ko tā tōna āhua mōhio ki a au</strong> — what their character teaches me<br><strong>ka toa tātou i a ia</strong> — we are victorious/strong through them<br><strong>mō tāu aroha</strong> — for your love/compassion<br><strong>mō tāu oranga ki a au</strong> — for your life force toward me</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Tip: Tēnā koe mō tāu āwhina, mō tāu aroha, mō tāu oranga ki a au. Three mō phrases — three things you are grateful for. When you say someone's contributions in three, you are honouring them in the way te ao Māori honours everything important — three times, three ways.</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">We Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Kia Tūhono — Tribute writing</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write a tribute to someone in this class — your teacher, a classmate, or someone who helped you learn te reo this year.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Identity + character: Ko [name] tētahi o ngā tāngata nui rawa atu i tōku ao. He tangata [X], he tangata [Y] hoki ia.</li><li>What they did: I āwhina ia i a au i [specific moment/way] — e kore e wareware ana i a au.</li><li>What their character teaches: Ko tā tōna āhua mōhio ki a au ko te [lesson].</li><li>Close: Nō reira, tēnā koe [name] mō tāu [X], mō tāu [Y], mō tāu [Z].</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Discussion prompt: Mō tāu oranga ki a au — for your life force toward me. Oranga (wellbeing/vitality) is something you can give to another person — through your presence, your manaakitanga, your care. Who has given their oranga to you this year in this classroom? How would you say it in te reo Māori?</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">You Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Māu Anō — He mihi ki tētahi</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write a full tribute in te reo Māori to someone important to your learning journey this year.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Identity + Ko ia he pou o tōku [oranga/ako/ngākau].</li><li>What they did: I āwhina ia i a au i [specific moment] + e kore e wareware.</li><li>What they taught you: Ko tā tōna āhua mōhio ki a au ko te [lesson].</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write a full tribute (8 sentences) — identity, character (2 traits shown not told), what they did, what they taught you, how they have changed you (Kua hou tōku whakaaro nō tōna āwhina), and close with Tēnā koe mō tāu [X, Y, Z]. Read it aloud to them if you can.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Thursday: Kōrero — Spiral: He kōrero ā-tau — A year in conversation":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Te Aho Arataki Marau — All AOs 1.1–2.5 · Final comprehensive spiral conversation</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Objectives</th><td class=\"lt-td\">AO 2.3 — Year spiral conversation; AO 2.5 — Full year vocabulary; AO 1.1 — Formal greetings</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">I Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tāua Kōrero — The year in one conversation</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This is the penultimate Thursday lesson. Today we have a conversation that spirals through every term — a conversation that could only happen at the end of Year 5-6 te reo Māori, because it requires everything you have learned.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\"><strong>A:</strong> Tēnā koe. Ko wai koe, ā he aha tō pepeha?<br><strong>B:</strong> [Full 9-line pepeha + Nō reira ko ahau ko B]<br><strong>A:</strong> Ka pai! I tīmata koe i hea i tō ako i te reo Māori?<br><strong>B:</strong> I tīmata au i Wāhanga Tahi me tētahi kupu — &quot;Kia ora.&quot; Ināianei, e ako tonu ana au, kua tipu tōku reo ki tētahi mea nui ake.<br><strong>A:</strong> He aha ngā mea i uaua rawa atu ki a koe?<br><strong>B:</strong> I uaua ki a au ngā hanganga o te tense — Ko Kua, Ko Ka. Engari, ahakoa i uaua, i mahi tonu au nō te mea ko tōku moemoeā tōku māramatanga.<br><strong>A:</strong> He aha tāu whakataukī e tūāhuatia ana i a koe?<br><strong>B:</strong> &quot;Ehara tāku toa i te toa takitahi, he toa takitini.&quot; Ko tā tēnā mōhio ko te mana o tēnei akomanga — ko tā tātou mahi tahi i āwhina i a mātou katoa.<br><strong>A:</strong> Ā koia! Ko tāu kōrero he māramatanga ki a au. Mā te wā.<br><strong>B:</strong> Ka nui ōku mihi ki a koe. Mā te wā.</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This conversation touches every term:<br>T1: &quot;Kia ora&quot; as the starting word<br>T2: I tīmata au (past) · kua tipu (perfect) · e ako tonu ana (E…ana)<br>T3: Whakataukī + ko tā tēnā mōhio · moemoeā<br>T4: ko tā tātou mahi tahi (possession) · he māramatanga · Mā te wā</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>New phrase:<br><strong>ko tā tātou mahi tahi</strong> — our working together (ā-category possession of the group)<br><strong>i āwhina i a mātou katoa</strong> — helped all of us</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Tip: &quot;Kia ora&quot; was your first word. Mā te wā is how you close the year. Between those two phrases is everything. That is your year in te reo Māori.</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">We Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Kia Tūhono — Year spiral conversation</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The final group conversation practice — every term represented, no scripts.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>T1: Tēnā koe + full pepeha from memory.</li><li>T2: I tīmata au i… ināianei kua tipu tōku reo ki…</li><li>T3: Whakataukī + Ko tā tēnā mōhio ko te…</li><li>T4: Ko tāu kōrero he māramatanga ki a au + Mā te wā.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Discussion prompt: &quot;Kia ora&quot; was your first word. What will your LAST word in te reo Māori be — not at the end of today, but someday, in your life? What do you hope to be saying in te reo Māori in five years? In twenty?</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">You Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Māu Anō — Year spiral conversation</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Have the full year spiral conversation with a partner — touch every term.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Full pepeha + Nō reira ko ahau ko [name].</li><li>Learning journey: I tīmata + Kua tipu + E ako tonu ana.</li><li>Whakataukī + explanation + moemoeā.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write the full conversation (16+ lines). After, write a reflection: He aha te mea i mihi ana koe ki a koe ake? (What did you appreciate about yourself?) Ka angitu koe.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Friday: Aromatawai — Quiz & Review":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Monday questions</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>&quot;Ko te tikanga o te haka ko te whakamana i te tangata.&quot; What does whakamana mean?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>to punish </li><li>to empower / honour / give authority to ✅</li><li>to teach </li><li>to separate </li></ul></li><li>&quot;E kore e wehewehea&quot; — in the context of haka and aroha/kaha, what does this phrase mean?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>it should be separated into parts </li><li>it cannot be separated — the two things are inseparable ✅</li><li>it will never be performed again </li><li>it does not exist </li></ul></li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tuesday questions</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>How many different grammar structures appear in the four-term showcase passage?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>5 </li><li>9 </li><li>17 ✅</li><li>23 </li></ul></li><li>&quot;Ko tōku moemoeā tōku māramatanga.&quot; What aspiration does this express?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>My dream is wealth and success </li><li>My dream is my understanding/clarity ✅</li><li>My dream is to become a teacher </li><li>My dream is to travel </li></ul></li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Wednesday questions</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>&quot;E kore e wareware ana i a au tōna atawhai.&quot; What does wareware mean?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>to forgive </li><li>to remember </li><li>to forget ✅</li><li>to thank </li></ul></li><li>&quot;Tēnā koe mō tāu āwhina, mō tāu aroha, mō tāu oranga ki a au.&quot; What is the significance of three mō phrases?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>It is required by grammar </li><li>Three phrases honour the person in the way te ao Māori honours important things — three times, three ways ✅</li><li>It makes the sentence longer </li><li>Three is the number of people being thanked </li></ul></li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Thursday questions</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>&quot;I tīmata au i Wāhanga Tahi me tētahi kupu — Kia ora.&quot; What does this sentence reflect on?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>The difficulty of te reo Māori </li><li>The start of the learning journey — from a single word ✅</li><li>A complaint about Term 1 </li><li>The importance of greeting properly </li></ul></li><li>In the year spiral conversation, what two phrases mark the beginning and end of the whole year?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>&quot;Tēnā koe&quot; and &quot;E noho rā&quot; </li><li>&quot;Kia ora&quot; as the first word and &quot;Mā te wā&quot; as the year-end farewell ✅</li><li>&quot;Ko wai koe&quot; and &quot;Ka huri&quot; </li><li>&quot;He aha&quot; and &quot;Ka mutu&quot; </li></ul></li></ul></div></div>"}}},"Week 10":{"label":"Day","children":{"Monday: He rā whakanui — Celebration and culmination day":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Te Aho Arataki Marau — All AOs 1.1–2.5 · Whakaatu (presenting) · Full year celebrated</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Objectives</th><td class=\"lt-td\">AO 1.1 — Formal celebration; AO 2.3 — Full year expression; AO 2.5 — Complete vocabulary</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">I Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tāua Kōrero — He rā whakanui: a day of celebration</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Welcome to the final week. This week is a celebration of what you have built. Today is whakanui — honouring and making great. We do not review or test. We celebrate, perform, and share.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\"><strong>The whakanui structure for today:</strong><br><br><strong>1. Mihi whakatūwhera:</strong> Teacher opens with Tēnā koutou katoa + acknowledgement of the year.<br><strong>2. Pepeha pōhiri:</strong> Each student delivers their pepeha — class responds with Tēnā koe after each.<br><strong>3. Waiata tahi:</strong> Class sings a waiata together — Ka mate, or another known waiata.<br><strong>4. Kōrero pūrākau:</strong> Two or three students share their pūrākau from Term 4 Week 2.<br><strong>5. He whakaaro:</strong> Each student shares their favourite sentence of the year.<br><strong>6. Mihi whakamutunga:</strong> Teacher closes with Ko tā tātou mahi tahi i hanga ai tēnei — Ka nui ōku mihi ki a koutou katoa.</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The language of celebration:</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>He rā whakanui tēnei.</strong> — This is a day of celebration.<br><strong>Ka whakanuitia ā tātou mahi.</strong> — Our work is being honoured/celebrated.<br><strong>Ko tā tātou mahi tahi i hanga ai tēnei.</strong><br>This is what our working together has built.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Mihi whakatūwhera</th><td class=\"lt-td\">opening tribute</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Mihi whakamutunga</th><td class=\"lt-td\">closing tribute</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>whakanui</strong> — to celebrate / to make great / to honour<br><strong>Ka whakanuitia</strong> — is being celebrated/honoured (passive)<br><strong>He tino nui ōku mihi ki a koutou katoa.</strong><br>My tributes/thanks to you all are very great.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Tip: Whakanui — to make great, to celebrate, to honour. When you celebrate someone's achievement, you are not just praising them — you are making them great in the eyes of everyone present. That is what whakanui does.</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">We Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Kia Tūhono — Whakanui preparation</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Prepare for the full class whakanui celebration.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Pepeha: Every student practises their full pepeha one final time. No looking — from the heart.</li><li>Favourite sentence: Write your favourite te reo sentence of the year. Say it to yourself. Own it.</li><li>Pūrākau: Two volunteers prepare to share their pūrākau from Term 4 Week 2.</li><li>Waiata: Class practices the opening of Ka Mate or another class waiata together.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Discussion prompt: Ko tā tātou mahi tahi i hanga ai tēnei — this is what our working together has built. What has the class built together? Not just language skills — but what kind of learning community? What has this class been to you?</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">You Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Māu Anō — Whakanui contribution</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Prepare your contribution to the class whakanui celebration.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Your pepeha: write it from memory, check it, practise until it is completely yours.</li><li>Your favourite sentence: write it, say why it is your favourite.</li><li>A whakaaro for the class: Ko tā tō tātou akomanga hanga ai ko… (What your class has built is…)</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write a short mihi to the class (5 sentences) — acknowledge everyone, name three things the class has done together, and close with He tino nui ōku mihi ki a koutou katoa.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Tuesday: He whakaaturanga — The end-of-year performance":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Te Aho Arataki Marau — All AOs · Whakaatu (presenting) — final performance</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Objectives</th><td class=\"lt-td\">AO 2.3 — Final performance; AO 1.1 — Formal presentation; AO 2.5 — Full vocabulary performance</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">I Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tāua Kōrero — Your whakaaturanga: the full performance</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Today is the end-of-year whakaaturanga — each student performs their full presentation to the class. This is the Whakaatu (presenting) language mode in its fullest expression. You have been preparing for this all year.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\"><strong>The complete whakaaturanga structure:</strong><br><br><strong>1.</strong> Tēnā koutou katoa.<br><strong>2.</strong> Ko [maunga] tōku maunga… [full pepeha — all 9 lines]<br><strong>3.</strong> Nō reira ko ahau ko [name].<br><strong>4.</strong> I tīmata au i Wāhanga Tahi me tētahi kupu — &quot;Kia ora.&quot; Ināianei, kua tipu tōku reo ki tētahi mea nui ake.<br><strong>5.</strong> Ko ngā pou o tōku ora ko tōku whānau, ko tōku reo, ko tōku taiao.<br><strong>6.</strong> Ko tōku rerenga pai rawa atu i tēnei tau ko tēnei: [your best sentence].<br><strong>7.</strong> Ko tōku whakataukī e tūāhuatia ana i a au ko tēnei: [whakataukī]. Ko tā tērā mōhio ko te [meaning].<br><strong>8.</strong> Ko tōku wawata: [your deepest aspiration for your reo].<br><strong>9.</strong> Tēnā koutou, tēnā koutou, tēnā koutou katoa.</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Nine elements, approximately 90 seconds:</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Formal opening (Tēnā koutou katoa)</li><li>Pepeha (all 9 lines)</li><li>Nō reira ko ahau (arrival at yourself)</li><li>Learning journey (I tīmata + Kua tipu)</li><li>Pillars of life (Ko ngā pou o tōku ora)</li><li>Best sentence (Ko tōku rerenga pai rawa atu)</li><li>Whakataukī + meaning (Ko tā tērā mōhio)</li><li>Wawata (deepest aspiration)</li><li>Formal close (Tēnā koutou ×3)</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This is not a test. This is a gift — to yourself, to your class, and to te reo Māori.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Tip: Ko tōku rerenga pai rawa atu i tēnei tau ko tēnei — my best sentence of this year is this. When you say it, say it slowly. Let everyone hear it. It is yours. You made it. And it will exist in this classroom forever.</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">We Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Kia Tūhono — Final preparation</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Last preparation round — then the performances begin.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Silent practice: 3 minutes. Eyes closed. Pepeha from memory, then the rest.</li><li>Partner run-through: full whakaaturanga to your partner. They give one word of feedback.</li><li>Class: who goes first? Volunteers? Or a karakia to open?</li><li>Perform: each student delivers their whakaaturanga. Class responds with Tēnā koe after each.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Discussion prompt: Ko tōku rerenga pai rawa atu i tēnei tau ko tēnei. Before you perform — what IS your best sentence? Not the most impressive one. The most true. Say it quietly to yourself right now. Feel it. Now you are ready.</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">You Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Māu Anō — He whakaaturanga</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Deliver your whakaaturanga to the class.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Deliver all nine elements — from Tēnā koutou katoa to Tēnā koutou ×3.</li><li>After everyone has performed, write a mihi: Ko ngā rerenga i pā mai ki a au ko ēnei — [three classmates' sentences that moved you].</li><li>Write your own sentence about the class: Ko tā tō tātou akomanga hanga ai ko…</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write a full reflection on delivering your whakaaturanga (6 sentences) — how it felt to say your pepeha in front of everyone, which part felt most true, what you want to remember about this moment.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Wednesday: He koha ki te reo — A gift to the language":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Te Aho Arataki Marau — All AOs · Creative expression · Final creative writing</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Objectives</th><td class=\"lt-td\">AO 2.3 — Creative gift to the language; AO 2.5 — Full year vocabulary</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">I Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tāua Kōrero — What will you give back?</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>You have received so much from te reo Māori this year. Today we think about what we give BACK — a koha (gift/contribution) to the language itself. This might be a poem, a statement, a whakaaro, a pūrākau, or a promise. Whatever form it takes, it is yours and it is real.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\"><strong>Examples of koha ki te reo:</strong><br><br><strong>A promise:</strong><br>Ko tāku koha ki tēnei reo: māku e kōrero, māku e tiaki, māku e homai ki ōku tamariki āpōpō.<br>(My gift to this language: I will speak it, protect it, and give it to my children tomorrow.)<br><br><strong>A poem:</strong><br>He <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=reo\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">reo</a> koe<br>He ara koe<br>He <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=aho\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">aho</a> koe e <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=whiri\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">whiri</a> ana i ngā ngākau<br>He <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=m%C4%81ramatanga\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">māramatanga</a> koe ki te ao <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=pouri\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">pouri</a><br>Ko koe ko ahau.<br>(You are a language / You are a path / You are a cord that weaves hearts / You are light in the dark world / You are me.)<br><br><strong>A whakaaro:</strong><br>Ka toitū koe i ōku kupu. Ka toitū au i āu kupu. Ko tāua ko tāua — e kore e wehewehea.</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Koha forms:</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>PROMISE: Māku e [three commitments]. Ko tērā tāku koha ki a koe.<br>POEM: Use Ko/He/Ka sentences as lines. Let sound and image lead.<br>WHAKAARO: A single profound statement — Ko te reo [metaphor].<br>MIHI: Tēnā koe e te reo — ko tāku mihi ki a koe ko tēnei…</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>New creative phrase:<br><strong>ao pouri</strong> — dark world / world of difficulty<br><strong>Ko koe ko ahau.</strong> — You are me. (profound identification)<br><strong>e kore e wehewehea</strong> — cannot be separated (from Week 8!)<br><strong>Ka toitū koe i ōku kupu.</strong><br>You endure through my words.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Tip: Ko koe ko ahau — you are me. This is the most intimate thing you can say to a language. The language and the speaker have become one. Can you say this honestly about te reo Māori at the end of this year?</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">We Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Kia Tūhono — Create your koha</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write your koha to the language — in whatever form feels right.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Choose your form: promise, poem, whakaaro, or mihi to the language.</li><li>Draft: write freely for 5 minutes — no thinking about structures, just what you want to say.</li><li>Refine: read it aloud. Change anything that doesn't sound true. Keep everything that does.</li><li>Share: read your koha aloud to the class. They listen in silence.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Discussion prompt: Ka toitū koe i ōku kupu. Ka toitū au i āu kupu. The language endures through your words. You endure through the language's words. A reciprocal endurance. What does it feel like to think that your words — in te reo Māori — will endure? That you will leave something in the language?</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">You Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Māu Anō — He koha ki te reo</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write your final creative gift to te reo Māori.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Choose your form and write your koha — at least 5 sentences or lines.</li><li>Write in te reo Māori — let the language carry your meaning.</li><li>Read it aloud once more before you share. Make it yours completely.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write a bilingual version — your koha in te reo Māori alongside an English version. Then ask: which version feels more true? Which says it better? What does the language you chose reveal about what the words mean?</li></ul></div></div>"},"Thursday: He whakamātautau whakamutunga — The final assessment of the year":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Te Aho Arataki Marau — All AOs 1.1–2.5 · All six language modes · Final year assessment</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Objectives</th><td class=\"lt-td\">AO 1.1 — Greetings; AO 1.2 — Introductions; AO 1.4 — Identity; AO 2.3 — Full spontaneous conversation; AO 2.5 — Full vocabulary; AO 1.6 — Politeness</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">I Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tāua Kōrero — The end</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This is the last lesson of the year. There is no model conversation today. You know what to do. You have been preparing for this since Week 1 of Term 1. Go.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">What the final assessment asks of you:<br><br><strong>Open formally.</strong> Tēnā koe.<br><strong>Give your full <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=pepeha\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">pepeha</a>.</strong> All 9 lines. From your heart, not your memory.<br><strong>Talk about your year.</strong> What happened. How you felt. What grew.<br><strong>Share a <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=Whakataukī\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">whakataukī</a>.</strong> The one that represents you. Explain its meaning.<br><strong>State your <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=wawata\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">wawata</a>.</strong> Your deepest aspiration for your reo and your life.<br><strong>Close.</strong> Ka nui ōku mihi. Mā te wā.<br><br>That is the assessment. Not what structures you use — but whether you mean what you say.</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>What the assessor is listening for:</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>✓ Does this person MEAN what they are saying?<br>✓ Is the language NATURAL — not performed but inhabited?<br>✓ Do the structures SERVE the meaning, rather than the meaning serving the structures?<br>✓ Is the pepeha delivered with confidence and pride?<br>✓ Is the whakataukī CHOSEN, not recited?<br>✓ Is the wawata GENUINE — something the speaker actually aspires to?<br>✓ Is the farewell warm and complete?</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>You do not need to be perfect. You need to be present.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Ka angitu koe.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Tip: Ka angitu koe. You will succeed. Not because you are ready — but because you have been ready since before you knew it. The year built you for this moment. Trust it.</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">We Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Kia Tūhono — He karakia whakamutunga</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Before the assessments begin, the class says a karakia together — an acknowledgement and closing of the year.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Together: <strong>Unuhia, unuhia, unuhia ki uta!</strong> (Lift it, lift it, lift it to the shore!)</li><li>Together: <strong>Kia wātea, kia māmā.</strong> (Let it be free, let it be light.)</li><li>Together: <strong>Āe rā.</strong> (Indeed.)</li><li>Then: assessments begin. Each student takes their turn.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Discussion prompt: Kia wātea, kia māmā — let it be free, let it be light. The karakia releases the class from the constraints of ordinary learning into the freedom of genuine expression. What does it mean to be &quot;free and light&quot; in a language? Have you felt that this year, even once?</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">You Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Māu Anō — He whakamātautau whakamutunga</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Your final assessment. Your last act in te reo Māori this year.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>Full spontaneous conversation</strong> — no script. At least 16 lines. Every domain.</li><li><strong>Self-assessment:</strong> He aha tōku pai? He aha tōku uauatanga? He aha tāku ka ako tonu ai?</li><li><strong>Final written record:</strong> Ko tōku rerenga whakamutunga o tēnei tau ko tēnei: [your final sentence].</li><li>✦ Final checklist: ☐ Pepeha (9 lines, from the heart) · ☐ Learning journey (I/Kua/E…ana) · ☐ Whakataukī + meaning · ☐ Wawata (genuine) · ☐ Ka nui ōku mihi · ☐ Mā te wā · ☐ The whole thing meant.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Friday: Aromatawai — Quiz & Review":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Monday questions</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>&quot;Ka whakanuitia ā tātou mahi.&quot; What does whakanui mean?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>to test and grade </li><li>to review and correct </li><li>to celebrate / honour / make great ✅</li><li>to repeat and practise </li></ul></li><li>&quot;Ko tā tātou mahi tahi i hanga ai tēnei.&quot; What does this sentence reflect on?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>The teacher's individual work </li><li>What their working together has built as a class ✅</li><li>A past event from Term 1 </li><li>A future plan for next year </li></ul></li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tuesday questions</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>How many elements does the final whakaaturanga have?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>5 </li><li>7 </li><li>9 ✅</li><li>12 </li></ul></li><li>&quot;Ko tōku rerenga pai rawa atu i tēnei tau ko tēnei.&quot; What is the speaker doing?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>Asking for the best sentence </li><li>Sharing their finest sentence of the year ✅</li><li>Explaining a whakataukī </li><li>Describing their school </li></ul></li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Wednesday questions</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>&quot;Ko koe ko ahau.&quot; In the context of language, what does this mean?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>You and I are different </li><li>You are my teacher </li><li>The language and the speaker have become one — a profound identification ✅</li><li>I do not know you </li></ul></li><li>&quot;Ka toitū koe i ōku kupu. Ka toitū au i āu kupu.&quot; What relationship does this describe?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>A competitive relationship between speaker and language </li><li>A reciprocal endurance — the language endures through your words, you endure through the language's words ✅</li><li>A one-way gift from language to speaker </li><li>A past memory </li></ul></li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Thursday questions</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The final assessment asks whether you MEAN what you say. What does this reveal about the purpose of language learning?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>Grammar accuracy is the only goal </li><li>Language learning is about performing for assessment </li><li>Genuine expression — inhabiting the language rather than just performing it — is the highest achievement ✅</li><li>Vocabulary size determines success </li></ul></li><li>&quot;Kia wātea, kia māmā.&quot; What does this karakia invoke?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>Difficulty and challenge </li><li>Speed and accuracy </li><li>Freedom and lightness — releasing from constraint into genuine expression ✅</li><li>Silence and stillness </li></ul></li></ul></div></div>"}}}}}}},"reading":{"label":"Topic","children":{"Te Haerenga — Term Reading Voyage":{"label":"Week","children":{"Programme Overview":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Years 5–6 (NZC English, Phase 2) | 1 story per week | 4 lessons/week @ ~50 mins | 10-week term</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This is the planning skeleton we'll use to build the app. It defines the weekly rhythm, the day-by-day lesson shape, how each day ties to the NZ Curriculum, and the dropdown structure the app needs. Once you're happy with this, we build the real thing (story-by-story content + the app itself).</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">1. Story Bank (from your uploaded archive)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>11 texts found — mostly Connected/School Journal texts with a strong <strong>voyaging, migration &amp; discovery</strong> thread (waka, Moriori settlement of Rēkohu, Lapita migration, kūaka/godwit migration). Great built-in theme for the term if you want one, or they can stand alone.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-data\"><thead><tr><th class=\"lt-th-col\">#</th><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Title</th><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Type</th><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Rough level</th><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Theme</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">1</td><td class=\"lt-td\">The Journey (Robin Nathan)</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Fiction</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Yr 5/6</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Bird migration, courage, growing up</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">2</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Journey of a Waka</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Non-fiction (School Journal)</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Yr 5/6</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Voyaging, navigation, wayfinding</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">3</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Beyond the Reef</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Non-fiction (Moriori oral history)</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Yr 5/6</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Migration to Rēkohu</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">4</td><td class=\"lt-td\">A Short History of Rēkohu</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Non-fiction</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Yr 5/6</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Moriori history, place</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">5</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Where No Boat Could Live</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Non-fiction</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Yr 5/6</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Chatham Islands, weather/seafaring</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">6</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Te Pokohiwi-o-Kupe: Aotearoa's First Settlement</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Non-fiction (interview)</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Yr 6</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Wairau Bar archaeology</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">7</td><td class=\"lt-td\">The Long Pause</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Non-fiction</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Yr 6</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Lapita migration into the Pacific</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">8</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Kūaka: The Marathon Bird</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Non-fiction</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Yr 5/6</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Godwit migration</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">9</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Te Rā: The Last of Its Kind</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Non-fiction (Connected)</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Yr 5/6</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Taonga, weaving/sail</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">10</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Tahu and the Dancing Feathers</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Fiction (Connected)</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Yr 5/6</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Identity, taonga</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">11</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Woven Taonga / Over One, Under One</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Non-fiction (Connected)</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Yr 5/6</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Weaving, craft, culture</td></tr></tbody></table><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\"><strong>Final term running order:</strong> 10 stories, one per week — <em>Taki Tahi | Over One, Under One</em> was considered for Week 11 but was ultimately dropped, making this a clean 10-week term. The app assigns any story to any week freely, so the order can still be changed.</blockquote></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">2. The Weekly Rhythm (Mon–Fri, same shape every week)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>One story = one week. Same 4-day skeleton every time so it becomes routine for students, freeing up their energy for the actual reading rather than &quot;what do we do today.&quot;</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-data\"><thead><tr><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Day</th><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Focus</th><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Grouping</th><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Curriculum emphasis</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"lt-td\"><strong>Monday</strong></td><td class=\"lt-td\">Lead-in + whole-class vocab &amp; comprehension</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Whole class</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Oral language, vocabulary knowledge, activating prior knowledge</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\"><strong>Tuesday</strong></td><td class=\"lt-td\">Guided reading</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Level groups</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Reading progression — decoding, fluency, comprehension at instructional level</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\"><strong>Wednesday</strong></td><td class=\"lt-td\">Reading activities</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Pairs</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Comprehension strategies, collaborative talk, practice &amp; knowledge</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\"><strong>Thursday</strong></td><td class=\"lt-td\">Understanding project (part 1)</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Pairs/small groups</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Deeper comprehension, synthesis, creating a text/response</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\"><strong>Friday</strong></td><td class=\"lt-td\">Understanding project (part 2) + Presentations</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Pairs/small groups → whole class</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Oral language, presenting ideas, key competencies (relating to others, participating &amp; contributing)</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Every lesson ≈ 50 minutes.</strong> Suggested internal timing (adjustable per lesson type in the app):</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>5 min: settle + recap/hook</li><li>35–40 min: main activity</li><li>5–10 min: share-back / exit check</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">🟦 Monday — Whole Class: Lead-in, Vocabulary &amp; Comprehension</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Purpose</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Build the shared foundation everyone needs before independent/group work starts.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>Lead-in question(s)</strong> — 2–3 hook questions per story (predicting from title/cover image, connecting to students' own experience, activating background knowledge — e.g. &quot;Have you ever moved somewhere new? What would you take?&quot;)</li><li><strong>Vocabulary breakdown</strong> — pull 6–10 key/tricky words from the story; explicit teaching (meaning, syllables, example sentence, connect to kupu Māori where relevant)</li><li><strong>First read / read-aloud</strong> — teacher reads or plays audio of the whole text; whole-class basic comprehension check (literal questions — who/what/where/when)</li><li><strong>Curriculum link:</strong> oral language component of structured literacy; vocabulary knowledge; activating prior knowledge as a comprehension strategy (NZC English Phase 2, Years 4–6)</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">🟩 Tuesday — Level Groups: Guided Reading</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Purpose</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Reading practice matched to instructional level, teacher rotates or runs one group at a time.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Groups re-read the text (or a levelled excerpt) at their own pace/level</li><li>Teacher-led group gets explicit decoding/fluency/comprehension instruction</li><li>Other groups: independent re-read + simple task (e.g. sequence the events, find evidence for an answer)</li><li><strong>Curriculum link:</strong> differentiated instruction matched to reading progression; fluency and comprehension strategies at instructional level</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">🟨 Wednesday — Pairs: Reading Activities</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Purpose</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Active, hands-on comprehension work — this is where &quot;fun&quot; lives.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Paired/hands-on activities such as: sequencing cards, vocabulary matching games, &quot;hot seat&quot; character/interviewee questioning, comprehension scavenger hunt, draw-and-label diagrams, true/false detective (find evidence in text), question each other using question cubes/dice</li><li><strong>Curriculum link:</strong> collaborative talk, comprehension strategies (inference, evidence-finding), practice consolidation</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">🟧 Thursday — Understanding Project (Part 1)</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Purpose</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Students synthesise their understanding into something they create — start the project.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Pairs/small groups choose or are assigned a project format (rotate weekly for variety — see menu below)</li><li>Planning + drafting/building time</li><li>Teacher checks in with each group</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">🟥 Friday — Understanding Project (Part 2) + Presentations</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Purpose</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Finish, polish, and share.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>First ~20–25 min: finish project</li><li>Remaining time: presentations to class (2–3 min per group) + peer feedback (e.g. 2 stars and a wish, thumbs poll)</li><li><strong>Curriculum link:</strong> oral language — presenting ideas clearly; key competencies — participating &amp; contributing, relating to others</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Understanding Project menu (rotate through the term so it stays fresh)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Comic strip / storyboard retelling</li><li>Diorama or model of a key scene/setting</li><li>Podcast-style recorded interview (in role)</li><li>Poster/infographic (non-fiction texts — facts, timeline, diagram)</li><li>Short dramatic re-enactment / freeze-frame scenes</li><li>&quot;Fact file&quot; or travel brochure (great fit for the voyaging/migration texts)</li><li>Book trailer / pitch to sell the story to another class</li><li>Debate or &quot;would you have gone?&quot; persuasive mini-speech</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">4. Curriculum Alignment (NZC English, Phase 2 — Years 4–6, effective from 2025 refresh)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The app should tag each lesson against these Phase 2 strands so you can show/print curriculum coverage per week:</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>Reading progressions</strong> — decoding, vocabulary, fluency, comprehension (literal, inferential, evaluative)</li><li><strong>Oral language</strong> — spoken language development, structured talk, presenting</li><li><strong>Structured literacy components</strong> — explicit, systematic, cumulative teaching of oral language + reading + writing</li><li><strong>Texts reflecting Aotearoa's bicultural heritage</strong> — kupu Māori, Māori and Moriori narratives/perspectives (a strong fit given your story bank)</li><li><strong>Key competencies</strong> — thinking; using language, symbols &amp; text; managing self; relating to others; participating &amp; contributing</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Each week's lesson set (Mon–Fri) should show a short tick-list of which of these 5 areas it covers — this becomes your at-a-glance curriculum-coverage report for the term.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Christian character reflection</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Alongside the NZC strands, every week's full lesson plan includes <strong>one reflection question per school value</strong>, tied to that week's story:</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>Aroha</strong> (Love: God, Others, Self, World)</li><li><strong>Mōhio</strong> (Know: God, His story and our place in it)</li><li><strong>Manaaki</strong> (Serve: faithfully with our gifts and talents)</li><li><strong>Pānga</strong> (Impact: bringing God's Kingdom to earth)</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Soli Deo Gloria — To God Alone the Glory</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Core screens</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>Term Overview</strong> — grid of Week 1–11, each cell shows story title + status (planned/in progress/done), with an easy way to drag/reassign a story out to &quot;next term&quot; if the term runs shorter</li><li><strong>Week Planner</strong> — the main workhorse screen</li><li><strong>Story Library</strong> — list of the 11 stories with metadata (title, type, level, theme, uploaded file)</li><li><strong>Curriculum Coverage view</strong> — auto-generated report of which Phase 2 strands have been covered, week by week</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Dropdowns (as requested)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>On the <strong>Week Planner</strong> screen:</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>Dropdown 1: Week</strong> → Week 1, Week 2 … Week 11</li><li><strong>Dropdown 2: Day</strong> → Monday / Tuesday / Wednesday / Thursday / Friday</li><li>Selecting Week + Day loads that day's lesson: story reference, lead-in questions, vocab list, activity instructions, timing breakdown, curriculum tags — all editable/printable</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Additional dropdown/selector ideas worth building in:</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>Story assignment</strong> dropdown per week (pick which of the 11 stories runs that week — fully flexible, not locked to the table order)</li><li><strong>Level group</strong> selector (Tuesday) — set your own group names/levels once, reuse every week</li><li><strong>Understanding project type</strong> dropdown (Thu/Fri) — pick from the project menu above, or mark &quot;custom&quot;</li><li><strong>Print/export</strong> button — generate a one-page lesson summary for the day (handy to have on the whiteboard or printed for relievers)</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Data per story (what we'll build out next, story by story)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Title, file link, type (fiction/non-fiction), reading level</li><li>2–3 lead-in questions</li><li>Key vocabulary list (word + kid-friendly definition)</li><li>Basic comprehension questions (literal, for Monday)</li><li>Level-group discussion/task prompts (Tuesday)</li><li>Paired activity instructions (Wednesday)</li><li>Suggested understanding-project options for that specific story (Thu/Fri)</li><li>Curriculum tags (which Phase 2 strands this story/week covers)</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">6. Next Steps</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>✅ Confirm this backbone (rhythm, day purposes, curriculum tags, dropdown structure)</li><li>✅ Running as an <strong>11-week term</strong> — all 11 stories get a week; you decide later if one needs to drop to next term</li><li>✅ <strong>Proof-of-concept content pack built</strong> for Week 1 — <em>The Journey</em> (see <code>story-01-the-journey-content-pack.md</code>) — this is the template every other story will follow</li><li>Decide the <strong>running order</strong> for weeks 2–11 (or let me suggest one — e.g. building from lighter fiction into the denser non-fiction texts like Te Pokohiwi-o-Kupe and The Long Pause later in the term)</li><li>Once you're happy with the Week 1 template, we build out the same content pack for the remaining 10 stories</li><li>Build the actual app (dropdowns + content display) wired up to this content</li></ul></div></div>"},"Week 1 Content Pack — \"The Journey\" (Robin Nathan)":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Type</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Fiction | <strong>Reading level:</strong> Year 5/6</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Theme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Bird migration, courage, growing up</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum</th><td class=\"lt-td\">NZC English, Phase 2 (Years 4–6)</td></tr></tbody></table><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\"><strong>Note:</strong> this was the proof-of-concept template every later week's content pack followed.</blockquote></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Story Snapshot (for you)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Wilfred is a young godwit who doesn't want to join his flock's huge migration from Alaska to New Zealand, led by the respected Captain Thunder. Along the way the flock survives a storm, and Wilfred grows from reluctant and scared into brave, gaining a new understanding of duty and courage. It's a gentle, character-driven opener for the term — light on bicultural content by design, since it's a fictional lead-in to the real, non-fiction kūaka migration story that appears later (Week 8).</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Lead-in questions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Have you ever had to do something you didn't want to, even though everyone told you it was important? How did you feel?</li><li>Look at the title and first page. Who do you think &quot;Wilfred&quot; is? What might &quot;the Journey&quot; be?</li><li>Do you know any animals that travel huge distances every year? What do you already know about migration?</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Key vocabulary</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-data\"><thead><tr><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Word</th><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Kid-friendly meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">godwit</td><td class=\"lt-td\">a long-beaked shorebird that flies between Alaska and NZ every year</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">flock</td><td class=\"lt-td\">a large group of birds travelling or living together</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">solemnly</td><td class=\"lt-td\">in a serious, formal way</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">elder</td><td class=\"lt-td\">an older, respected member of a group</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">captain</td><td class=\"lt-td\">the leader in charge of a journey or crew</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">duty</td><td class=\"lt-td\">something you must do because it is your responsibility</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">exhausted</td><td class=\"lt-td\">extremely tired, with no energy left</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">amazement</td><td class=\"lt-td\">a feeling of great surprise</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">&quot;too chicken&quot;</td><td class=\"lt-td\">idiom — too scared to do something</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">&quot;birdbrain&quot;</td><td class=\"lt-td\">joking insult meaning silly (funny here — they ARE birds!)</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Basic comprehension questions (literal — whole class)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Where do the godwits live in summer, and where are they flying to?</li><li>About how far is the journey, in kilometres?</li><li>Why doesn't Wilfred want to go at first?</li><li>Who is Captain Thunder, and why does everyone respect him?</li><li>What happened to Flight Officer Wingspan during the storm?</li><li>How did Captain Thunder and the flock survive the storm?</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum tags</th><td class=\"lt-td\">oral language • vocabulary knowledge • activating prior knowledge • literal comprehension</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">🟩 TUESDAY — Level Groups: Guided Reading</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Group A — needs more support</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Re-read pages 1–3 together with teacher.</li><li>Task: sequence 4 picture/sentence cards in the order events happened.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Group B — at level (teacher-led guided group)</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Focus: how dialogue shows character.</li><li>Task: find 2 pieces of evidence Wilfred is nervous, and 2 that show he's brave by the end.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Group C — extending</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Focus: inference &amp; humour — why is calling a bird &quot;chicken&quot; or &quot;birdbrain&quot; funny?</li><li>Task: write one more line of dialogue Wilfred might say once they land in NZ, staying true to character.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum tags</th><td class=\"lt-td\">reading progression • fluency &amp; comprehension at instructional level • differentiated instruction</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">🟨 WEDNESDAY — Pairs: Reading Activities</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>Hot Seat: Interview Captain Thunder</strong> — One partner is Captain Thunder, the other a young godwit reporter. Ask about the storm, Wingspan, and why the Journey matters — then swap.</li><li><strong>Storm Sequencing</strong> — Cut up 6 sentences describing the storm scene. Pairs order them and explain how they knew.</li><li><strong>Vocabulary Match-Up</strong> — Card game matching each vocab word to its kid-friendly meaning — fastest correct pair wins a point.</li><li><strong>True or False Detective</strong> — Given 8 statements about the story, pairs find evidence in the text to prove or disprove each one.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum tags</th><td class=\"lt-td\">comprehension strategies (inference, evidence-finding) • collaborative talk</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">This week's project options (pairs choose one)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>Comic strip</strong> — retell the storm scene from Captain Thunder's memory, in 6 panels.</li><li><strong>&quot;Would you go?&quot; persuasive mini-speech</strong> — argue whether you'd join the Journey if you were Wilfred.</li><li><strong>Fact file / travel brochure</strong> — &quot;Visit New Zealand — Godwit Summer Home!&quot;</li><li><strong>Podcast-style recording</strong> — in role as Wilfred, record a &quot;voice diary&quot; the night before leaving Alaska.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Thursday (build/create time)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>~5 min: recap the story + choose project format</li><li>~35 min: plan and build/draft</li><li>~5–10 min: teacher check-in per pair</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Friday (finish + present)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>~20–25 min: finish and polish</li><li>~20–25 min: presentations (2–3 min per pair) + peer feedback (&quot;2 stars and a wish&quot;)</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum tags</th><td class=\"lt-td\">oral language — presenting ideas clearly • key competencies — participating &amp; contributing, relating to others • synthesis — creating a text in response to reading</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Christian Reflection — School Values</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Soli Deo Gloria — To God Alone the Glory</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-data\"><thead><tr><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Value</th><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Reflection question</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"lt-td\"><strong>Aroha</strong> (Love)</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Wilfred's family is patient and encouraging even when he grumbles and doesn't want to go. How can we show aroha to someone in our own family or class who is scared or reluctant, the way Wilfred was?</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\"><strong>Mōhio</strong> (Know)</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Godwits know exactly when and where to go because that's how God designed them. What does this remind us about trusting God's plan for our lives, even when we can't see the whole journey ahead?</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\"><strong>Manaaki</strong> (Serve)</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Captain Thunder used his courage and experience to protect and guide the whole flock. What gift or talent has God given you that you could use to serve others this week, even when it feels hard?</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\"><strong>Pānga</strong> (Impact)</td><td class=\"lt-td\">The godwits' journey brings new life to two different lands, every single year. How can facing a challenge we don't want to face still end up bringing good to others around us?</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Weekly Curriculum Coverage Tick-List</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>✅ Reading progressions</li><li>✅ Oral language</li><li>✅ Structured literacy components</li><li>✅ Key competencies</li><li>🌱 Bicultural/Aotearoa perspectives — light this week (fictional godwit family); flag the real kūaka migration and its significance to Māori as a bridge to the non-fiction companion text later in the term (Week 8)</li></ul></div></div>"},"Week 2 Content Pack — \"Journey of a Waka\" (School Journal)":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Type</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Non-fiction (School Journal) | <strong>Reading level:</strong> Year 5/6</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Theme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Voyaging, navigation, wayfinding</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum</th><td class=\"lt-td\">NZC English, Phase 2 (Years 4–6) — with strong bicultural links</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Story Snapshot (for you)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>A multi-section, non-fiction explainer on how the ancestors of Māori sailed to Aotearoa aboard waka hourua (double-hulled voyaging canoes) — covering the signs that told them it was time to leave, the essential items they carried, the four key crew roles (rangatira, tohunga, kaihautū, kaiurungi), and the signs that told them land was near. Strong bicultural content — real oral histories and voyaging knowledge, treated with genuine respect throughout.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Lead-in questions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>If you were about to sail across the world's biggest ocean with no map and no GPS, what would you pack? What would you need to know?</li><li>What clues in nature might tell you a storm is coming, or that land is near?</li><li>Have you heard of waka hourua, or Polynesian wayfinding/navigation? What do you already know?</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Key vocabulary</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-data\"><thead><tr><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Word</th><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Kid-friendly meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">waka hourua</td><td class=\"lt-td\">a large double-hulled voyaging canoe used for long ocean journeys</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">rangatira</td><td class=\"lt-td\">the captain/leader of the waka</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">tohunga</td><td class=\"lt-td\">the priest and navigator — reads the sky, stars and sea</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">kaihautū</td><td class=\"lt-td\">the person who directs the paddlers</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">kaiurungi</td><td class=\"lt-td\">the steerer(s) of the waka</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">adze</td><td class=\"lt-td\">a tool for shaping wood, like a curved axe</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">mana</td><td class=\"lt-td\">prestige, authority, and respect</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">oral histories</td><td class=\"lt-td\">stories and knowledge passed down by speaking, generation to generation</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">archaeology</td><td class=\"lt-td\">the study of the past through objects and sites people left behind</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">migrating</td><td class=\"lt-td\">travelling regularly between two places, often with the seasons</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Basic comprehension questions (literal — whole class)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Where did the ancestors of Māori sail from, and roughly how long ago did they begin arriving in Aotearoa?</li><li>Name three signs that told the ancestors it was the right time to leave.</li><li>Name three essential items carried on the waka.</li><li>What animals may have helped guide the waka on their journeys?</li><li>Name three signs that told the ancestors land was near.</li><li>What were the four main roles on a waka hourua, and what did each person do?</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum tags</th><td class=\"lt-td\">oral language • vocabulary knowledge (incl. kupu Māori) • non-fiction text features • literal comprehension • bicultural perspectives</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">🟩 TUESDAY — Level Groups: Guided Reading</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Group A — needs more support</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Re-read one section at a time together (e.g. &quot;Navigation,&quot; then &quot;Essential items&quot;).</li><li>Task: highlight or underline one key fact from each section read.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Group B — at level (teacher-led guided group)</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Focus: locating information across a non-linear, multi-section text.</li><li>Task: answer 3–4 questions requiring info from different sections; match each role name to its job.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Group C — extending</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Focus: comparing traditional and modern navigation.</li><li>Task: compare wayfinding (stars, currents, wind, birds) with modern GPS/compass. Which would you trust more, and why?</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum tags</th><td class=\"lt-td\">reading progression — using text features to locate information • differentiated instruction • bicultural perspectives</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">🟨 WEDNESDAY — Pairs: Reading Activities</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>Text Feature Scavenger Hunt</strong> — Pairs find and list every heading/section title, and explain what each section is about in their own words.</li><li><strong>Role Card Match</strong> — Match cards with role titles (rangatira, tohunga, kaihautū, kaiurungi) to a card describing that job — race another pair.</li><li><strong>Packing List Debate</strong> — Given the &quot;essential items&quot; list, pairs rank the top 5 most important for survival and justify their order.</li><li><strong>Nature-Clue Detectives</strong> — Sort mixed clue cards into: Signs it's time to leave / Signs land is near / Navigation method.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum tags</th><td class=\"lt-td\">non-fiction text features • comprehension strategies (locating &amp; categorising information) • collaborative talk • bicultural perspectives</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">This week's project options (pairs choose one)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>Design your own infographic</strong> — &quot;How to Survive a Waka Hourua Journey&quot; — in the same labelled-section style as the original.</li><li><strong>Role-play news report</strong> — interview a tohunga about how they read the stars, currents and wildlife to find their way.</li><li><strong>Labelled diagram/model</strong> — a waka hourua showing where key supplies, food, water and people would be positioned.</li><li><strong>Persuasive pitch</strong> — &quot;Why our waka should carry [an animal] on board&quot; using evidence about kurī or kiore.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Thursday (build/create time)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>~5 min: recap the text + choose project format</li><li>~35 min: plan and build/draft</li><li>~5–10 min: teacher check-in per pair</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Friday (finish + present)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>~20–25 min: finish and polish</li><li>~20–25 min: presentations (2–3 min per pair) + peer feedback (&quot;2 stars and a wish&quot;)</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum tags</th><td class=\"lt-td\">oral language — presenting ideas clearly • key competencies — participating &amp; contributing, relating to others • synthesis — creating a text in response to reading • bicultural perspectives</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Christian Reflection — School Values</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Soli Deo Gloria — To God Alone the Glory</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-data\"><thead><tr><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Value</th><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Reflection question</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"lt-td\"><strong>Aroha</strong> (Love)</td><td class=\"lt-td\">The waka carried whole families and communities together, all depending on each other to survive the journey. What does this show us about what it means to love and care for others as part of a team?</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\"><strong>Mōhio</strong> (Know)</td><td class=\"lt-td\">The ancestors used the stars, currents and signs in creation to find their way across the ocean. How is this similar to how we can look to God's creation and His Word to help guide our own lives?</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\"><strong>Manaaki</strong> (Serve)</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Every person on the waka had a role — navigator, steerer, paddler — and used their own skill to serve the whole crew. What's one gift God has given you that you could use to serve your class or family this week?</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\"><strong>Pānga</strong> (Impact)</td><td class=\"lt-td\">The voyage of the ancestors led to the beginning of a whole new nation. How can the choices we make now create a positive impact that reaches further than just ourselves?</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Weekly Curriculum Coverage Tick-List</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>✅ Reading progressions</li><li>✅ Oral language</li><li>✅ Structured literacy components</li><li>✅ Bicultural/Aotearoa perspectives</li><li>✅ Key competencies</li></ul></div></div>"},"Week 3 Content Pack — \"Beyond the Reef\" (Moriori oral history)":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Type</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Non-fiction (Moriori oral history) | <strong>Reading level:</strong> Year 5/6</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Theme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Migration to Rēkohu</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum</th><td class=\"lt-td\">NZC English, Phase 2 (Years 4–6) — with strong bicultural links</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Story Snapshot (for you)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Ri'i's village is preparing to secretly leave their home at night, after fighting has broken out and Ri'i's grandfather Horopapa — grieving the loss of his daughter — dreams of a peaceful new island. Ri'i is entrusted with a secret job to help prepare for departure, and has to keep it hidden even from his close friend Moe. The story follows the emotional lead-up to departure, the farewell, and the moment the canoes finally cross beyond the reef. This is a genuine Moriori oral history handed down for generations — treat its grief and hope with the same respect given to any real historical account.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Lead-in questions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Have you ever had to keep a big secret from a close friend — one you knew might hurt them? How did it feel?</li><li>Imagine leaving your home forever, at night, not knowing if you'd ever return. What would be running through your mind?</li><li>What do you already know about the Moriori people and Rēkohu (the Chatham Islands)?</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Key vocabulary</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-data\"><thead><tr><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Word</th><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Kid-friendly meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">paihihi</td><td class=\"lt-td\">canoes (as glossed in the text)</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">karakii</td><td class=\"lt-td\">a chant/incantation, said for strength or a safe journey</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">umere</td><td class=\"lt-td\">chants, part of the departure ritual</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">hunau</td><td class=\"lt-td\">family</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">etchu</td><td class=\"lt-td\">spiritual beings</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">ominous</td><td class=\"lt-td\">making you feel something bad is about to happen</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">ritual</td><td class=\"lt-td\">a set of actions done the same way, often for a special or spiritual reason</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">secrecy</td><td class=\"lt-td\">the state of keeping something hidden</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">navigator</td><td class=\"lt-td\">the person who works out the direction and course of a journey</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">hull</td><td class=\"lt-td\">the main body of a boat or canoe</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Basic comprehension questions (literal — whole class)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Who is Ri'i, and why is his village leaving Rēkohu?</li><li>What does Horopapa dream about, and why does it matter to him?</li><li>What was Ri'i's secret job in the weeks before departure?</li><li>Why couldn't Moe's family know about the departure plan?</li><li>What does Horopapa give Ri'i just before the canoes leave (the karakii)?</li><li>What happens as the canoes finally cross beyond the reef?</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum tags</th><td class=\"lt-td\">oral language • vocabulary knowledge (incl. Moriori/te reo terms) • literal comprehension • bicultural perspectives (handled sensitively)</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">🟩 TUESDAY — Level Groups: Guided Reading</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Group A — needs more support</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Re-read the three &quot;•••&quot; sections together with the teacher.</li><li>Task: sequence 6 event cards in order, from the fighting breaking out to the canoes crossing the reef.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Group B — at level (teacher-led guided group)</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Focus: understanding mixed emotions.</li><li>Task: find evidence Ri'i feels both fear and hope; discuss why the departure needed to stay secret.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Group C — extending</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Focus: inference &amp; cross-text connection.</li><li>Task: what might Horopapa's dream represent? Compare the roles/preparations here with <em>Journey of a Waka</em> (Week 2) — what's the same, what's different?</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum tags</th><td class=\"lt-td\">reading progression — inference, understanding time-shifts • differentiated instruction • bicultural perspectives</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">🟨 WEDNESDAY — Pairs: Reading Activities</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>Farewell Hot Seat</strong> — One partner is Ri'i, the other Moe, at their last meeting by the reef. Re-enact or extend their conversation, staying true to how each character feels.</li><li><strong>Event Sequencing</strong> — Pairs order 6–8 cut-up sentences describing key moments and justify using text evidence.</li><li><strong>Vocabulary Match-Up</strong> — Match Moriori/te reo terms (paihihi, karakii, umere, hunau, etchu) and other vocab to their meanings.</li><li><strong>Secret-Keeper Dilemma</strong> — Pairs discuss: was it right to keep the plan secret from Moe? Each argues one side, then write a joint conclusion.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum tags</th><td class=\"lt-td\">comprehension strategies (inference, evidence-finding) • collaborative talk • bicultural perspectives</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">This week's project options (pairs choose one)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>Comic strip</strong> — retell the farewell scene between Ri'i and Moe, in 6 panels.</li><li><strong>Diary entry</strong> — write as Ri'i, the night before leaving, describing his fears and hopes.</li><li><strong>Persuasive mini-speech</strong> — should the village council have listened to &quot;the way of no more fighting&quot; sooner?</li><li><strong>Labelled voyage map</strong> — draw and label the four canoes and their crew roles, linking back to <em>Journey of a Waka</em>.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Thursday (build/create time)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>~5 min: recap the story + choose project format</li><li>~35 min: plan and build/draft</li><li>~5–10 min: teacher check-in per pair</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Friday (finish + present)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>~20–25 min: finish and polish</li><li>~20–25 min: presentations (2–3 min per pair) + peer feedback (&quot;2 stars and a wish&quot;)</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum tags</th><td class=\"lt-td\">oral language — presenting ideas clearly • key competencies — participating &amp; contributing, relating to others • synthesis — creating a text in response to reading • bicultural perspectives</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Christian Reflection — School Values</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Soli Deo Gloria — To God Alone the Glory</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-data\"><thead><tr><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Value</th><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Reflection question</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"lt-td\"><strong>Aroha</strong> (Love)</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Even while hurting after his daughter's death, Horopapa chooses hope and peace instead of holding onto anger. How can we choose love over anger when we've been badly hurt by someone?</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\"><strong>Mōhio</strong> (Know)</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Horopapa holds onto the memory of a peaceful time and dreams of a better future for his people. How does knowing God's story help us hold onto hope even when things around us feel hard or uncertain?</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\"><strong>Manaaki</strong> (Serve)</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Ri'i is given an important job to help his people, even though he is still young. How can we serve faithfully with what we have, even when we feel too young, small, or unready?</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\"><strong>Pānga</strong> (Impact)</td><td class=\"lt-td\">The families' journey is meant to bring a new, peaceful way of life to a new land. What's one way you could help bring more peace into our own school or home this week?</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Weekly Curriculum Coverage Tick-List</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>✅ Reading progressions</li><li>✅ Oral language</li><li>✅ Structured literacy components</li><li>✅ Bicultural/Aotearoa perspectives</li><li>✅ Key competencies</li></ul></div></div>"},"Week 4 Content Pack — \"A Short History of Rēkohu\"":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Type</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Non-fiction | <strong>Reading level:</strong> Year 5/6</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Theme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Moriori history, place</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum</th><td class=\"lt-td\">NZC English, Phase 2 (Years 4–6) — Aotearoa histories</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Story Snapshot (for you)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>A timeline-structured non-fiction text tracing Moriori history from the Law of Nunuku (an agreement that ended warfare between Moriori tribes) through the 1835 invasion by Ngāti Mutunga and Ngāti Tama, the false story published about Moriori in a 1916 School Journal, the significance of Tame Horomona Rehe (Tommy Solomon), and up to today's efforts to recognise and support Moriori people and culture. This week is a serious, direct link to Aotearoa's histories — real injustice and real resilience — and should be taught with the same care given to any true account of harm done to real people.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Lead-in questions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Is it easy or hard to choose peace, even when you're being treated unfairly? Why?</li><li>Have you heard of the Moriori people or the Chatham Islands before today?</li><li>Why do you think it matters to learn the true history of a place and its people?</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Key vocabulary</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-data\"><thead><tr><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Word</th><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Kid-friendly meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">tchakat henu</td><td class=\"lt-td\">&quot;people of the land&quot; — how Moriori describe themselves</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">Law of Nunuku</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Nunuku-whenua's agreement that ended warfare between Moriori tribes forever</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">invasion</td><td class=\"lt-td\">when a group of people forcefully takes over land that belongs to others</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">slavery</td><td class=\"lt-td\">forcing people to work for others against their will, with no freedom</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">dendroglyphs</td><td class=\"lt-td\">carvings made into living trees — a form of Moriori art and record</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">marae</td><td class=\"lt-td\">a communal meeting ground, central to cultural life</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">Treaty settlement</td><td class=\"lt-td\">an agreement to address past injustice between government and iwi/group</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">resilience</td><td class=\"lt-td\">the ability to keep going and recover, even after very hard times</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">census</td><td class=\"lt-td\">an official count of a country's population</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">tribunal</td><td class=\"lt-td\">a type of court that hears and rules on claims</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Basic comprehension questions (literal — whole class)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>What does &quot;Rēkohu&quot; mean, and where is it located?</li><li>What is the Law of Nunuku, and who created it?</li><li>What happened when Ngāti Mutunga and Ngāti Tama arrived on Rēkohu in 1835?</li><li>Why was the story published in the 1916 School Journal untrue, and why did it matter?</li><li>Who was Tame Horomona Rehe (Tommy Solomon), and why is he significant?</li><li>Name two things being done today to recognise and support Moriori people and culture.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum tags</th><td class=\"lt-td\">oral language • vocabulary knowledge • non-fiction timeline text features • literal comprehension • bicultural/Aotearoa histories perspectives (handled with care)</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">🟩 TUESDAY — Level Groups: Guided Reading</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Group A — needs more support</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Re-read the timeline in date order with the teacher, from the Law of Nunuku through to 1900.</li><li>Task: with support, sequence 5–6 major event cards in order using the dates given.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Group B — at level (teacher-led guided group)</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Focus: explaining significance in own words.</li><li>Task: explain what the Law of Nunuku says and why it mattered; find evidence of what happened in 1835 and how Moriori responded.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Group C — extending</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Focus: critical thinking about historical narratives.</li><li>Task: why does the false 1916 story &quot;have a lasting impact&quot;? Compare that to today's efforts to reclaim the true history.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum tags</th><td class=\"lt-td\">reading progression — chronological/timeline features, cause and effect • differentiated instruction • bicultural/Aotearoa histories perspectives</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">🟨 WEDNESDAY — Pairs: Reading Activities</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>Timeline Sequencing</strong> — Pairs match event cards to their correct dates/order.</li><li><strong>Vocabulary Match-Up</strong> — Match key terms (tchakat henu, Law of Nunuku, dendroglyphs, marae, resilience) to their meanings.</li><li><strong>Law of Nunuku Discussion</strong> — Discuss respectfully: what does it mean to choose peace even when treated unfairly? Write a joint response.</li><li><strong>Fact-Check Detectives</strong> — Compare the false 1916 School Journal excerpt with the real history told elsewhere in the text.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum tags</th><td class=\"lt-td\">non-fiction text features • comprehension strategies (evidence-finding, comparing sources) • collaborative talk • bicultural/Aotearoa histories perspectives</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">This week's project options (pairs choose one)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>Annotated timeline poster</strong> — choose 8–10 key events from Rēkohu's history and explain each briefly.</li><li><strong>Persuasive speech</strong> — &quot;Why is it important to learn true history, even when it's hard?&quot;</li><li><strong>Fact file</strong> — research Kopinga Marae or the Chatham Island black robin conservation story.</li><li><strong>Respectful role-play interview</strong> — in role as a Moriori elder today, discuss the meaning of the Law of Nunuku.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Thursday (build/create time)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>~5 min: recap the timeline + choose project format</li><li>~35 min: plan and build/draft</li><li>~5–10 min: teacher check-in per pair</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Friday (finish + present)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>~20–25 min: finish and polish</li><li>~20–25 min: presentations (2–3 min per pair) + peer feedback (&quot;2 stars and a wish&quot;)</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum tags</th><td class=\"lt-td\">oral language — presenting ideas clearly • key competencies — participating &amp; contributing, relating to others, thinking critically • bicultural/Aotearoa histories perspectives</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Christian Reflection — School Values</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Soli Deo Gloria — To God Alone the Glory</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-data\"><thead><tr><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Value</th><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Reflection question</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"lt-td\"><strong>Aroha</strong> (Love)</td><td class=\"lt-td\">The Moriori chose not to fight back because of Nunuku's Law of peace, even when treated with great cruelty. What does this teach us about loving even those who hurt us, the way Jesus calls us to love our enemies?</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\"><strong>Mōhio</strong> (Know)</td><td class=\"lt-td\">For many years, an untrue story was taught about the Moriori people, and it caused harm for generations. Why does it matter that we know the truth — about God's story, and about the true stories of others — rather than believing false ones?</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\"><strong>Manaaki</strong> (Serve)</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Today, Moriori people are working hard to restore and share their true history and culture. How can we serve others by helping to tell true stories and treating people's histories with honour?</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\"><strong>Pānga</strong> (Impact)</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Places like Kopinga Marae show how a community can rebuild and bring good things back after great loss. How can we be part of bringing healing and restoration — a piece of God's Kingdom — into situations of hurt around us?</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Weekly Curriculum Coverage Tick-List</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>✅ Reading progressions</li><li>✅ Oral language</li><li>✅ Structured literacy components</li><li>✅ Bicultural/Aotearoa histories perspectives</li><li>✅ Key competencies</li></ul></div></div>"},"Week 5 Content Pack — \"Where No Boat Could Live\"":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Type</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Non-fiction | <strong>Reading level:</strong> Year 5/6</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Theme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Chatham Islands, weather/seafaring</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum</th><td class=\"lt-td\">NZC English, Phase 2 (Years 4–6) — with strong Science/Technology link</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Story Snapshot (for you)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>An explanation text on how Moriori engineered the wash-through waka — a design adapted for the &quot;roaring forties,&quot; the fierce seas around the Chatham Islands — after their original dugout canoes proved unsuited to the conditions. The text includes a 1791 quote from a European visitor, James Johnstone, who dismissively compared the waka to &quot;wheelbarrows,&quot; and then explains the real engineering genius behind the design. Continues the term's throughline of correcting outsiders' biased first impressions of Moriori ingenuity.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Lead-in questions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>If you had to build a boat to survive huge, stormy seas, what would you want it to do — float no matter what? Never sink? Carry a lot?</li><li>Have you ever made something clever out of natural materials (sticks, reeds, plants)? What did you make, and how did you solve a problem with it?</li><li>What do you know about the Chatham Islands and their weather?</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Key vocabulary</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-data\"><thead><tr><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Word</th><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Kid-friendly meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">roaring forties</td><td class=\"lt-td\">a stretch of ocean known for very strong winds and storms</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">dugout canoe</td><td class=\"lt-td\">a canoe carved from a single hollowed-out log</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">wash-through waka</td><td class=\"lt-td\">a boat built to let water flow through it, rather than keep it out</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">ballast</td><td class=\"lt-td\">something heavy that makes a boat stable</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">buoyant</td><td class=\"lt-td\">able to float</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">capsize</td><td class=\"lt-td\">to overturn in the water</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">bow</td><td class=\"lt-td\">the front of a boat</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">stern</td><td class=\"lt-td\">the back of a boat</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">hull</td><td class=\"lt-td\">the bottom/body of a boat</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">innovators</td><td class=\"lt-td\">people who invent new and clever solutions to problems</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Basic comprehension questions (literal — whole class)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Why are the waters around the Chatham Islands nicknamed &quot;the roaring forties&quot;?</li><li>Why didn't the Moriori's original dugout canoes work well in Rēkohu's seas?</li><li>What were the two types of wash-through waka, and how were they different in size?</li><li>What materials were used to build a wash-through waka?</li><li>Why didn't the wash-through waka need to be waterproof?</li><li>What did European visitors think of the waka at first, and why were they wrong?</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum tags</th><td class=\"lt-td\">oral language • vocabulary knowledge (nautical/technical terms) • literal comprehension • bicultural perspectives • cross-curricular Science/Technology link</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">🟩 TUESDAY — Level Groups: Guided Reading</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Group A — needs more support</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Re-read the sections on waka kōrari and waka pahī with the teacher.</li><li>Task: label a diagram of a wash-through waka with its key parts (bow, stern, hull, runners).</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Group B — at level (teacher-led guided group)</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Focus: explaining how and why the design worked.</li><li>Task: explain why letting water &quot;wash through&quot; made the waka safer; find evidence of how Europeans misunderstood the design.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Group C — extending</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Focus: evaluating bias in a primary source.</li><li>Task: compare Johnstone's 1791 &quot;wheelbarrows&quot; quote with what we now know. Why might a visitor misjudge something they don't understand? Connect to Week 4's false 1916 story.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum tags</th><td class=\"lt-td\">reading progression — technical vocabulary, evaluating primary sources • differentiated instruction • bicultural perspectives</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">🟨 WEDNESDAY — Pairs: Reading Activities</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>Label the Waka</strong> — Pairs label a diagram of a wash-through waka with its parts and materials (bow, stern, hull, runners, kelp, reeds).</li><li><strong>Vocabulary Match-Up</strong> — Match nautical/technical terms (ballast, buoyant, capsize, bow, stern, hull) to their meanings.</li><li><strong>First Impressions vs Facts</strong> — Compare Johnstone's dismissive 1791 quote with the true engineering facts. What did he get wrong, and why?</li><li><strong>Design Challenge Discussion</strong> — Pairs discuss: if you had to build a boat from natural materials for a stormy sea, what would you use, and why?</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum tags</th><td class=\"lt-td\">non-fiction text features (diagrams, primary sources) • comprehension strategies (comparing sources, evaluating bias) • collaborative talk • bicultural perspectives</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">This week's project options (pairs choose one)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>Labelled diagram or model</strong> of a wash-through waka, showing and naming all key parts and materials.</li><li><strong>Persuasive speech</strong> — &quot;Why the Moriori's boat design was actually genius, not primitive.&quot;</li><li><strong>News report/documentary style</strong> comparing the old dugout canoe with the new wash-through waka.</li><li><strong>Design challenge (STEM)</strong> — sketch your own natural-material boat for stormy seas, labelling why each part would work.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Thursday (build/create time)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>~5 min: recap the text + choose project format</li><li>~35 min: plan and build/draft</li><li>~5–10 min: teacher check-in per pair</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Friday (finish + present)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>~20–25 min: finish and polish</li><li>~20–25 min: presentations (2–3 min per pair) + peer feedback (&quot;2 stars and a wish&quot;)</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum tags</th><td class=\"lt-td\">oral language — presenting ideas clearly • key competencies — participating &amp; contributing, relating to others, thinking critically • bicultural perspectives</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Christian Reflection — School Values</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Soli Deo Gloria — To God Alone the Glory</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-data\"><thead><tr><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Value</th><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Reflection question</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"lt-td\"><strong>Aroha</strong> (Love)</td><td class=\"lt-td\">European visitors judged the Moriori's waka as &quot;clumsy and primitive&quot; without taking time to understand them. How does this remind us to love others by trying to understand them first, rather than judging by first impressions?</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\"><strong>Mōhio</strong> (Know)</td><td class=\"lt-td\">The Moriori used careful observation of the natural world — kelp, reeds, wind and sea — to build something that truly worked. How is learning to notice and understand God's creation part of knowing His story and our place in it?</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\"><strong>Manaaki</strong> (Serve)</td><td class=\"lt-td\">The Moriori used their skills and knowledge to serve their whole community's need for food and safety, inventing a completely new kind of boat. What skill or gift could you develop to serve others facing a real problem?</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\"><strong>Pānga</strong> (Impact)</td><td class=\"lt-td\">For years, outsiders misunderstood and dismissed Moriori ingenuity — but the truth about their skill is now being recognised. How can we help make sure good work and truth are recognised, even when others don't see it straight away?</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Weekly Curriculum Coverage Tick-List</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>✅ Reading progressions</li><li>✅ Oral language</li><li>✅ Structured literacy components</li><li>✅ Bicultural perspectives</li><li>✅ Key competencies</li></ul></div></div>"},"Week 6 Content Pack — \"Te Pokohiwi-o-Kupe: Aotearoa's First Settlement\" (interview)":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Type</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Non-fiction (interview) | <strong>Reading level:</strong> Year 6</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Theme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Wairau Bar archaeology</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum</th><td class=\"lt-td\">NZC English, Phase 2 (Years 4–6) — with strong Science and Aotearoa histories links</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Story Snapshot (for you)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>An interview text exploring the archaeological discoveries at Te Pokohiwi-o-Kupe (Wairau Bar), Aotearoa's earliest known settlement site — covering how scientists used DNA, radiocarbon dating, and evidence like a tropical shell chisel to piece together where the first settlers came from and how they lived. The text also covers the disrespectful removal of Rangitāne ancestral remains (kōiwi tangata) in the 1940s–50s, and the years-long fight by kaumātua Hohua Peter MacDonald and others for their return (repatriation). A serious, real account of harm and its resolution — teach with real weight given to both the injustice and the hopeful outcome.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Lead-in questions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>If archaeologists dug up your backyard in 700 years, what might they learn about you just from what you'd thrown away?</li><li>How do you think scientists can figure out how old something is, or even where a person was born, just from bones and teeth?</li><li>Why do you think it matters how we treat ancestral remains and burial sites?</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Key vocabulary</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-data\"><thead><tr><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Word</th><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Kid-friendly meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">tūpuna</td><td class=\"lt-td\">ancestors</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">kōiwi tangata</td><td class=\"lt-td\">ancestral (human) remains</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">wāhi tapu</td><td class=\"lt-td\">a sacred place</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">midden</td><td class=\"lt-td\">an ancient rubbish dump full of discarded bones, shells and tools</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">radiocarbon dating</td><td class=\"lt-td\">a scientific method used to estimate the age of things like bone or shell</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">DNA</td><td class=\"lt-td\">the genetic information in a cell that tells us about ancestry</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">argillite (pakohe)</td><td class=\"lt-td\">a hard stone, found only at the top of the South Island, used to make tools</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">taonga</td><td class=\"lt-td\">treasured cultural objects</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">repatriation</td><td class=\"lt-td\">returning ancestral remains or cultural objects to their community</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">hāngī pits</td><td class=\"lt-td\">pits used for traditional cooking, often for large feasts</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Basic comprehension questions (literal — whole class)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>What is Te Pokohiwi-o-Kupe also known as, and where is it located?</li><li>About how long ago was Te Pokohiwi first settled, and how do scientists know this?</li><li>What evidence suggests some of the people buried there were born outside Aotearoa?</li><li>Why was Te Pokohiwi a good place to settle?</li><li>What happened to the burial sites and taonga at Te Pokohiwi in the 1940s–50s?</li><li>What is being done today to address this, and who is leading it?</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum tags</th><td class=\"lt-td\">oral language • vocabulary knowledge • non-fiction interview text features • literal comprehension • bicultural/Aotearoa histories perspectives • cross-curricular Science link</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">🟩 TUESDAY — Level Groups: Guided Reading</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Group A — needs more support</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Re-read the interview Q&amp;A with the teacher, identifying who asks and who answers.</li><li>Task: sequence 6 key events in Te Pokohiwi's history in order.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Group B — at level (teacher-led guided group)</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Focus: understanding scientific evidence.</li><li>Task: explain what DNA, radiocarbon dating, and the tropical shell chisel each reveal; find evidence of the site's spiritual significance.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Group C — extending</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Focus: critical and ethical thinking.</li><li>Task: discuss why removing kōiwi tangata without full community agreement was wrong, and what repatriation means for healing today. Connect to Week 4.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum tags</th><td class=\"lt-td\">reading progression — interview text structure, evaluating evidence • differentiated instruction • bicultural/Aotearoa histories perspectives</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">🟨 WEDNESDAY — Pairs: Reading Activities</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>Interview Feature Hunt</strong> — Notice the Q&amp;A structure. Pairs write one new question they'd ask Dr Meihana, and a likely evidence-based answer.</li><li><strong>Vocabulary Match-Up</strong> — Match key terms (tūpuna, kōiwi tangata, wāhi tapu, midden, argillite, taonga, repatriation) to their meanings.</li><li><strong>Evidence Detective</strong> — Match each archaeological clue to what it tells scientists about the first settlers.</li><li><strong>Timeline Sequencing</strong> — Order 6–8 events in Te Pokohiwi's history, from first settlement to the return of kōiwi tangata.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum tags</th><td class=\"lt-td\">non-fiction text features (interview structure) • comprehension strategies (evidence-matching, sequencing) • collaborative talk • bicultural/Aotearoa histories perspectives</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">This week's project options (pairs choose one)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>Your own interview script</strong> — in role as an archaeologist, write a Q&amp;A about what was discovered at Te Pokohiwi.</li><li><strong>Persuasive speech</strong> — &quot;Why ancestral remains and taonga should be returned to their home communities.&quot;</li><li><strong>Fact file/poster</strong> — &quot;What Te Pokohiwi Teaches Us About Aotearoa's First Settlers,&quot; organised by evidence type.</li><li><strong>Timeline poster</strong> of Te Pokohiwi's history, from 1280 AD to today.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Thursday (build/create time)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>~5 min: recap the text + choose project format</li><li>~35 min: plan and build/draft</li><li>~5–10 min: teacher check-in per pair</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Friday (finish + present)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>~20–25 min: finish and polish</li><li>~20–25 min: presentations (2–3 min per pair) + peer feedback (&quot;2 stars and a wish&quot;)</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum tags</th><td class=\"lt-td\">oral language — presenting ideas clearly • key competencies — participating &amp; contributing, relating to others, thinking critically and ethically • bicultural/Aotearoa histories perspectives</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Christian Reflection — School Values</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Soli Deo Gloria — To God Alone the Glory</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-data\"><thead><tr><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Value</th><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Reflection question</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"lt-td\"><strong>Aroha</strong> (Love)</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Rangitāne's tūpuna were dug up and displayed without proper respect — even in a fish and chip shop. How does this help us understand why it's so important to treat every person, living or after they've died, with dignity and love, the way God calls us to?</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\"><strong>Mōhio</strong> (Know)</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Scientists used bones, teeth, DNA and even a small shell chisel to piece together the true story of where people came from. How is this similar to how we can carefully search for truth about God's story and our own history, rather than guessing or ignoring evidence?</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\"><strong>Manaaki</strong> (Serve)</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Kaumātua Hohua Peter MacDonald spent years serving his people by fighting for the return of their ancestors, even though he wasn't successful straight away. How can we serve others faithfully, even when results take a long time or don't come easily?</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\"><strong>Pānga</strong> (Impact)</td><td class=\"lt-td\">After many years, Rangitāne successfully negotiated the return of their tūpuna's remains. How can our own actions today help bring healing and put right something that was wronged in the past?</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Weekly Curriculum Coverage Tick-List</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>✅ Reading progressions</li><li>✅ Oral language</li><li>✅ Structured literacy components</li><li>✅ Bicultural/Aotearoa histories perspectives</li><li>✅ Key competencies</li></ul></div></div>"},"Week 7 Content Pack — \"The Long Pause\" (Connected, Level 3, by Dr Amber Aranui)":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Type</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Non-fiction (explanation text, multiple competing scientific theories) | <strong>Reading level:</strong> Year 6 (challenge text)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Theme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">The 1,000-year gap between the Lapita migration and the settlement of East Polynesia/Aotearoa</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum</th><td class=\"lt-td\">NZC English, Phase 2 (Years 4–6) — with strong Science (archaeology, hypothesis-testing) links</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Story Snapshot (for you)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>About 3,500 years ago, the Lapita people rapidly explored and settled West Polynesia (Fiji, Sāmoa, Tonga) in just 500 years. Then — mysteriously — exploration stopped for at least another 1,000 years, before people finally voyaged on to Hawai'i, Rapa Nui, and Aotearoa. This text presents <strong>three competing scientific theories</strong> for why: (1) canoe technology wasn't yet good enough for the longer, rougher East Polynesian crossings, (2) resource competition and possible droughts in West Polynesia may have pushed people to search for new land, and (3) computer models suggest wind patterns changed at just the right times to make eastward sailing possible. This is the most complex text of the term — a great one for teaching how scientists evaluate multiple, competing explanations rather than settling for a single simple answer.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Nice link back to earlier weeks</th><td class=\"lt-td\">radiocarbon dating (Week 6), and waka technology/the waka hourua (Weeks 2, 3 and 5) all reappear here — worth pointing out explicitly.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Lead-in questions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Imagine sailing into the ocean with no idea what's on the other side, or if there's anything there at all. What would make you brave enough to go?</li><li>Why do you think people might stop exploring somewhere for a very long time, then suddenly start again?</li><li>What do you already know about how the Pacific Islands, including Aotearoa, were first settled?</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Key vocabulary</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-data\"><thead><tr><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Word</th><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Kid-friendly meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">Lapita</td><td class=\"lt-td\">the early Pacific people who first migrated from the Bismarck Islands out into the Pacific</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">migration</td><td class=\"lt-td\">the movement of people from one place to settle in another</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">archaeologist</td><td class=\"lt-td\">a scientist who studies the past through objects and remains people left behind</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">hypothesis</td><td class=\"lt-td\">a possible explanation that still needs more evidence to prove it</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">outrigger canoe</td><td class=\"lt-td\">a canoe with a float attached to one side for balance, but limited in size and distance</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">waka hourua</td><td class=\"lt-td\">a double-hulled canoe that could travel further and carry more</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">drought</td><td class=\"lt-td\">a long period of very dry weather</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">computer models</td><td class=\"lt-td\">computer programs that use maths and data to predict what might have happened</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">hospitable</td><td class=\"lt-td\">pleasant and favourable for living in</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">radiocarbon dating</td><td class=\"lt-td\">measuring radioactive carbon in remains to estimate how long ago something lived</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Whole-class read</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>This is a denser text with multiple theories — read section by section, pausing after each theory to summarise it in one sentence as a class.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Basic comprehension questions (literal — whole class)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Who were the Lapita, and where did they first settle?</li><li>About how long was &quot;the long pause&quot; between the two migrations?</li><li>Name two possible reasons scientists suggest for the long pause.</li><li>What is the difference between an outrigger canoe and a waka hourua?</li><li>How do computer models help researchers understand wind patterns from the past?</li><li>Which was the last major area of the Pacific to be settled?</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum tags</th><td class=\"lt-td\">oral language • vocabulary knowledge • literal comprehension • bicultural/Aotearoa perspectives • cross-curricular Science link (hypothesis-testing, radiocarbon dating)</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">🟩 TUESDAY — Level Groups: Guided Reading</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Group A — needs more support</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Re-read the timeline section with the teacher.</li><li>Task: label a simple timeline with the key migration dates (Lapita arrive in Bismarck Islands → West Polynesia settled → the long pause → East Polynesia/Aotearoa settled).</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Group B — at level (teacher-led guided group)</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Focus: explaining one theory clearly.</li><li>Task: choose one of the three theories (technology, resources/drought, or wind patterns) and explain it in your own words, using evidence from the text.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Group C — extending</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Focus: evaluating multiple competing explanations.</li><li>Task: compare all three theories. Which do you find most convincing, and why? Discuss why scientists test several hypotheses rather than settling for one simple answer.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum tags</th><td class=\"lt-td\">reading progression — evaluating multiple explanations, technical vocabulary • differentiated instruction • bicultural perspectives</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">🟨 WEDNESDAY — Pairs: Reading Activities</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>Theory Sorting</strong> — Pairs sort evidence statements under the correct theory heading: Technology / Resources &amp; Drought / Wind Patterns.</li><li><strong>Vocabulary Match-Up</strong> — Match key terms (Lapita, hypothesis, outrigger canoe, waka hourua, drought, hospitable) to their meanings.</li><li><strong>Canoe Comparison</strong> — Compare the outrigger canoe and the waka hourua using a table or Venn diagram — what could each one do?</li><li><strong>Which Theory Convinces You?</strong> — Pairs discuss and write a short joint justification for which theory they find most convincing, using evidence from the text.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum tags</th><td class=\"lt-td\">non-fiction text features (diagrams, timelines) • comprehension strategies (comparing and evaluating explanations) • collaborative talk • bicultural perspectives</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">This week's project options (pairs choose one)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>Labelled timeline poster</strong> showing the Lapita migration → the long pause → the eventual settlement of Aotearoa.</li><li><strong>Persuasive speech</strong> — &quot;Which theory best explains the long pause?&quot;</li><li><strong>News report/documentary style</strong> comparing outrigger canoe and waka hourua technology.</li><li><strong>Detective case file</strong> — present the three theories as clues in a mystery investigation into &quot;the case of the long pause.&quot;</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Thursday (build/create time)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>~5 min: recap the text + choose project format</li><li>~35 min: plan and build/draft</li><li>~5–10 min: teacher check-in per pair</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Friday (finish + present)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>~20–25 min: finish and polish</li><li>~20–25 min: presentations (2–3 min per pair) + peer feedback (&quot;2 stars and a wish&quot;)</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum tags</th><td class=\"lt-td\">oral language — presenting ideas clearly • key competencies — participating &amp; contributing, relating to others, thinking (evaluating evidence) • synthesis — creating a text in response to reading • bicultural perspectives</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Christian Reflection — School Values</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Soli Deo Gloria — To God Alone the Glory</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-data\"><thead><tr><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Value</th><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Reflection question</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"lt-td\"><strong>Aroha</strong> (Love)</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Over generations, people kept improving their boats and knowledge so that one day, exploration could continue — even though they might never see the results themselves. How can loving others sometimes mean patiently working on something now, even if we don't see the outcome ourselves?</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\"><strong>Mōhio</strong> (Know)</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Scientists don't just guess about the past — they carefully gather evidence and test different explanations before deciding what's most likely true. How is this similar to how we should search carefully for truth about God and His story, rather than jumping to conclusions?</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\"><strong>Manaaki</strong> (Serve)</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Over generations, people worked together — developing new tools, boats and knowledge — to serve those who would come after them on this incredible journey. How can we use our own gifts to serve and prepare the way for people who come after us?</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\"><strong>Pānga</strong> (Impact)</td><td class=\"lt-td\">The eventual voyages to Aotearoa changed the future of an entire country forever. How can small, patient steps we take now end up having a huge impact later, even if we can't see it yet?</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Weekly Curriculum Coverage Tick-List</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>✅ Reading progressions (evaluating multiple explanations, technical vocabulary, comprehension)</li><li>✅ Oral language</li><li>✅ Structured literacy components (explicit vocabulary incl. science/archaeology terms)</li><li>✅ Bicultural/Aotearoa perspectives (Polynesian voyaging heritage)</li><li>✅ Key competencies (thinking — evaluating competing hypotheses; language, symbols &amp; text; relating to others; participating &amp; contributing)</li><li>🌟 Bonus: strong cross-curricular Science link (hypothesis-testing, radiocarbon dating, computer modelling)</li></ul></div></div>"},"Week 8 Content Pack — \"Kūaka: The Marathon Bird\" (School Journal)":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Type</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Non-fiction (illustrated magazine-style article) | <strong>Reading level:</strong> Year 5/6</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Theme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">The bar-tailed godwit's incredible non-stop migration, and both scientific and traditional Māori ways of understanding it</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum</th><td class=\"lt-td\">NZC English, Phase 2 (Years 4–6) — with strong Science and bicultural links</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Story Snapshot (for you)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Kūaka (bar-tailed godwits) make one of the most extraordinary journeys in the animal kingdom — flying non-stop between Alaska and New Zealand, a distance of around 11,000 km, without landing to rest, eat, or drink. Before departure their bodies transform: they fatten up dramatically and some of their internal organs shrink to save weight, then regrow once they arrive. Modern satellite tracking has revealed the details of this journey scientifically, while Māori traditional knowledge (maramataka, the lunar calendar, and whakataukī, traditional proverbs) has understood and marked the kūaka's arrival and departure as a seasonal signal for many generations. This is a great, uplifting text after some heavier weeks — genuine wonder at what creation can do, alongside real science.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Nice link back to earlier weeks</th><td class=\"lt-td\">this connects naturally to Week 1 (the fictional godwit story, <em>The Journey</em>) and continues the term's Science/bicultural threads from Weeks 6 and 7.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Lead-in questions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Could you run for 8–9 days without stopping to eat, drink, or rest? How do you think a bird could fly that long, non-stop?</li><li>What signs in nature (birds arriving, plants flowering) tell people that a new season is coming?</li><li>What do you already know about the kūaka/godwit and its incredible journey? (A nice link back to Week 1's story, <em>The Journey</em>.)</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Key vocabulary</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-data\"><thead><tr><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Word</th><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Kid-friendly meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">kūaka</td><td class=\"lt-td\">the Māori name for the bar-tailed godwit, a large migratory shorebird</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">migratory</td><td class=\"lt-td\">travelling regularly between two places, usually with the seasons</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">non-stop</td><td class=\"lt-td\">without any pause or rest along the way</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">satellite tracking</td><td class=\"lt-td\">using satellites to follow and record an animal's exact movements</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">estuary</td><td class=\"lt-td\">where a river meets the sea, often rich in food for wading birds</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">maramataka</td><td class=\"lt-td\">the traditional Māori lunar calendar</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">whakataukī</td><td class=\"lt-td\">a Māori proverb or saying that carries traditional wisdom</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">V formation</td><td class=\"lt-td\">a flying pattern where birds take turns leading to save energy</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">fuel up / fatten up</td><td class=\"lt-td\">eating heavily to store enough energy for a long journey</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">organs</td><td class=\"lt-td\">internal body parts (like the stomach or liver) that carry out life functions</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Whole-class read</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Read the article together, pausing at photos/diagrams of the migration route and body changes.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Basic comprehension questions (literal — whole class)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>What is a kūaka, and where does it migrate between?</li><li>About how far do kūaka fly, and how long does the journey take?</li><li>What happens to a kūaka's body before it begins its migration?</li><li>How do scientists track where kūaka go?</li><li>What role does the kūaka play in traditional Māori knowledge of the seasons?</li><li>Why do kūaka fly in a V formation?</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum tags</th><td class=\"lt-td\">oral language • vocabulary knowledge • literal comprehension • bicultural perspectives (maramataka, whakataukī) • cross-curricular Science link (adaptation, migration, tracking technology)</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">🟩 TUESDAY — Level Groups: Guided Reading</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Group A — needs more support</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Re-read the &quot;getting ready to fly&quot; section with the teacher.</li><li>Task: sequence the stages of a kūaka's migration preparation (fatten up → organs shrink → depart → fly non-stop → arrive and refuel → organs regrow).</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Group B — at level (teacher-led guided group)</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Focus: understanding scientific tracking.</li><li>Task: explain in your own words how satellite tracking works and what scientists have learned from it; find evidence of the physical toll the journey takes on the bird's body.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Group C — extending</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Focus: valuing two different ways of knowing.</li><li>Task: discuss why both Māori traditional knowledge (whakataukī, maramataka) and modern satellite science matter for understanding kūaka — two different, valuable ways of knowing the same bird. Connect this to Weeks 6 and 7's science themes.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum tags</th><td class=\"lt-td\">reading progression — sequencing, understanding scientific processes • differentiated instruction • bicultural perspectives</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">🟨 WEDNESDAY — Pairs: Reading Activities</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>Body Changes Sequencing</strong> — Pairs sequence cards showing the stages of a kūaka's body before, during, and after migration.</li><li><strong>Vocabulary Match-Up</strong> — Match key terms (kūaka, migratory, satellite tracking, maramataka, whakataukī, V formation) to their meanings.</li><li><strong>V-Formation Discussion</strong> — Pairs discuss and sketch why flying in a V formation might save energy for a flock of birds.</li><li><strong>Two Ways of Knowing</strong> — Pairs match pieces of evidence to whether they come from traditional Māori knowledge or modern scientific tracking — and discuss why both matter.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum tags</th><td class=\"lt-td\">non-fiction text features (diagrams, photos) • comprehension strategies (sequencing, comparing sources of knowledge) • collaborative talk • bicultural perspectives</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">This week's project options (pairs choose one)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>Labelled migration map/diagram</strong> showing the kūaka's route, with body changes noted at each stage.</li><li><strong>Persuasive speech</strong> — &quot;Why the kūaka's migration is one of the most incredible feats in the animal kingdom.&quot;</li><li><strong>News report/documentary style</strong> comparing traditional Māori knowledge and modern satellite tracking of kūaka.</li><li><strong>Fact file</strong> — &quot;Kūaka: The Marathon Bird&quot; — organised by distance, body changes, tracking science, and cultural significance.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Thursday (build/create time)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>~5 min: recap the text + choose project format</li><li>~35 min: plan and build/draft</li><li>~5–10 min: teacher check-in per pair</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Friday (finish + present)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>~20–25 min: finish and polish</li><li>~20–25 min: presentations (2–3 min per pair) + peer feedback (&quot;2 stars and a wish&quot;)</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum tags</th><td class=\"lt-td\">oral language — presenting ideas clearly • key competencies — participating &amp; contributing, relating to others, thinking • synthesis — creating a text in response to reading • bicultural perspectives</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Christian Reflection — School Values</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Soli Deo Gloria — To God Alone the Glory</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-data\"><thead><tr><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Value</th><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Reflection question</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"lt-td\"><strong>Aroha</strong> (Love)</td><td class=\"lt-td\">The kūaka take turns leading the flock in a V formation, sharing the hardest work so every bird can complete the journey together. How does this remind us of God's call to love others by sharing burdens and looking out for one another?</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\"><strong>Mōhio</strong> (Know)</td><td class=\"lt-td\">The kūaka's body knows exactly how to prepare for this incredible journey, and Māori knew for generations how to read the seasons by its arrival. How does this show us that both creation and traditional wisdom can help us know God's world more fully?</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\"><strong>Manaaki</strong> (Serve)</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Scientists have spent years carefully tracking and studying kūaka to help protect their journey and habitats for future generations. How can we serve and care for God's creation, even in small ways, in our own lives?</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\"><strong>Pānga</strong> (Impact)</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Every kūaka's journey — though it's just one small bird — has an impact on ecosystems and cultures across half the world. How can something that seems small in our own lives still have a big impact on the world around us?</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Weekly Curriculum Coverage Tick-List</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>✅ Reading progressions (sequencing, understanding scientific processes, comprehension)</li><li>✅ Oral language</li><li>✅ Structured literacy components (explicit vocabulary incl. science and te reo terms)</li><li>✅ Bicultural/Aotearoa perspectives (maramataka, whakataukī, traditional ecological knowledge)</li><li>✅ Key competencies (thinking — valuing multiple ways of knowing; language, symbols &amp; text; relating to others; participating &amp; contributing)</li><li>🌟 Bonus: strong cross-curricular Science link (adaptation, migration, satellite tracking technology)</li></ul></div></div>"},"Week 9 Content Pack — \"Te Rā: The Last of Its Kind\" (Connected 2024, an interview by Hamish Ansley)":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Type</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Non-fiction (interview/Q&amp;A format) | <strong>Reading level:</strong> Year 5/6</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Theme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Te Rā, an extraordinary 200+ year old Māori woven sail — mātauranga Māori, science, and coming home</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum</th><td class=\"lt-td\">NZC English, Phase 2 (Years 4–6) — with strong Science and bicultural links</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Story Snapshot (for you)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Te Rā is probably the only Māori woven sail of its kind left in the world — made from harakeke (flax) in Aotearoa in the 1700s, and kept for most of the last 200 years in the British Museum in London, without anyone quite knowing how it got there. In 2019, three researchers — a weaver/scholar, an expert weaver, and a materials scientist — travelled to London to study Te Rā, combining traditional Māori weaving knowledge (mātauranga) with modern science (like polarised light microscopy) to understand how it was made and what it reveals about Māori voyaging technology. The interview also covers the emotional experience of studying and briefly bringing home a taonga that had been away from its people for so long.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Nice link back to earlier weeks</th><td class=\"lt-td\">this continues the waka/sail technology thread from Weeks 2, 3, 5 and 7, and echoes Week 6's story of research, evidence, and the significance of bringing a taonga home.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Lead-in questions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>If your family owned something really old and important, and it ended up far away in another country's museum for 200 years, how would you feel about it coming home?</li><li>What is something in your own family or culture that is treasured and passed down? Why does it matter?</li><li>Have you ever used both traditional knowledge (like a family recipe or skill) and modern science or tools together to understand or check something?</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Key vocabulary</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-data\"><thead><tr><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Word</th><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Kid-friendly meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">taonga</td><td class=\"lt-td\">a treasured object of cultural, spiritual, or historical significance</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">mātauranga</td><td class=\"lt-td\">traditional Māori knowledge</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">whakapapa</td><td class=\"lt-td\">genealogy — the history and lineage of a person, family, or object</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">harakeke</td><td class=\"lt-td\">the Māori word for flax, a plant used for weaving</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">raranga</td><td class=\"lt-td\">the art of weaving</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">muka</td><td class=\"lt-td\">fibre prepared from flax, used for cord or binding</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">wānanga</td><td class=\"lt-td\">a gathering for learning and discussion</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">mauri</td><td class=\"lt-td\">the spirit or life-force of a person, place, or object</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">polarised light microscope</td><td class=\"lt-td\">a scientific tool used to examine fine details of materials like fibres</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">tūpuna</td><td class=\"lt-td\">ancestors</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Whole-class read</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Read as an interview, with different readers taking the parts of Donna, Rānui, and Catherine.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Basic comprehension questions (literal — whole class)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>What is Te Rā, and how old is it?</li><li>Where was Te Rā kept for most of the last 200 years?</li><li>What did the three researchers want to find out about Te Rā?</li><li>What scientific tool did they use, and what did it prove?</li><li>What might the kererū and kāhu feathers on the sail have been used for?</li><li>How did people feel when Te Rā was brought back to Aotearoa?</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum tags</th><td class=\"lt-td\">oral language • vocabulary knowledge • non-fiction interview text features • literal comprehension • bicultural perspectives (mātauranga Māori) • cross-curricular Science link (materials science, microscopy)</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">🟩 TUESDAY — Level Groups: Guided Reading</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Group A — needs more support</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Re-read the interview Q&amp;A with the teacher.</li><li>Task: identify the three researchers and their roles (weaver/artist/scholar, expert weaver, scientist).</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Group B — at level (teacher-led guided group)</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Focus: understanding how science and traditional knowledge worked together.</li><li>Task: explain in your own words what the polarised light microscope revealed, and why proving the harakeke scientifically mattered alongside traditional weaving knowledge.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Group C — extending</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Focus: emotional and cultural significance.</li><li>Task: discuss why not knowing Te Rā's full whakapapa (how it got to the British Museum) matters, and why bringing it home — even temporarily — was so emotionally significant. Compare this to Week 6's story about Te Pokohiwi.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum tags</th><td class=\"lt-td\">reading progression — interview text structure, understanding scientific evidence • differentiated instruction • bicultural perspectives</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">🟨 WEDNESDAY — Pairs: Reading Activities</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>Interview Feature Hunt</strong> — Pairs write one new question they'd ask one of the three researchers, and a likely evidence-based answer.</li><li><strong>Vocabulary Match-Up</strong> — Match key terms (taonga, mātauranga, harakeke, raranga, muka, wānanga, mauri) to their meanings.</li><li><strong>Evidence Detective</strong> — Match each scientific/weaving clue (the microscope images, the zigzag weave pattern, the split feathers) to what it reveals about Te Rā.</li><li><strong>How Would It Feel?</strong> — Pairs discuss and write about how it might feel to see or touch a 200-year-old taonga from your own culture for the first time.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum tags</th><td class=\"lt-td\">non-fiction text features (interview structure) • comprehension strategies (evidence-matching) • collaborative talk • bicultural perspectives</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">This week's project options (pairs choose one)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>Interview script</strong> — in role as one of the three researchers, write and perform a Q&amp;A about Te Rā.</li><li><strong>Persuasive speech</strong> — &quot;Why it's important for taonga like Te Rā to be studied and shared with their home communities.&quot;</li><li><strong>Fact file/poster</strong> on Te Rā — organised by materials, science, history, and cultural significance.</li><li><strong>Labelled diagram</strong> of Te Rā's sail parts (matairangi, huru manu, eyelets, zigzag weave), with captions.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Thursday (build/create time)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>~5 min: recap the text + choose project format</li><li>~35 min: plan and build/draft</li><li>~5–10 min: teacher check-in per pair</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Friday (finish + present)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>~20–25 min: finish and polish</li><li>~20–25 min: presentations (2–3 min per pair) + peer feedback (&quot;2 stars and a wish&quot;)</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum tags</th><td class=\"lt-td\">oral language — presenting ideas clearly • key competencies — participating &amp; contributing, relating to others, thinking • synthesis — creating a text in response to reading • bicultural perspectives</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Christian Reflection — School Values</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Soli Deo Gloria — To God Alone the Glory</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-data\"><thead><tr><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Value</th><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Reflection question</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"lt-td\"><strong>Aroha</strong> (Love)</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Donna described crying because she felt sorry for Te Rā being alone, away from its people for so long. How does this remind us that people — and even meaningful things — are made to belong and be loved within community, not to be alone?</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\"><strong>Mōhio</strong> (Know)</td><td class=\"lt-td\">The researchers combined careful science (the microscope) with traditional weaving knowledge to understand Te Rā fully. How is this like using both careful observation of creation and God's Word together to grow in knowing Him and His world?</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\"><strong>Manaaki</strong> (Serve)</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Donna, Rānui, and Catherine spent years using their skills and knowledge to serve their community by bringing understanding of Te Rā back home. How can we use our own skills to serve and preserve something important for others?</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\"><strong>Pānga</strong> (Impact)</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Even though Te Rā could only come home for a short time, its return had a huge impact — inspiring a child who saw it to want to make his own sail. How can something we do that seems small or temporary still leave a big, lasting impact on someone else?</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Weekly Curriculum Coverage Tick-List</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>✅ Reading progressions (interview/Q&amp;A text features, evidence-based comprehension)</li><li>✅ Oral language</li><li>✅ Structured literacy components (explicit vocabulary incl. te reo and science terms)</li><li>✅ Bicultural/Aotearoa perspectives (mātauranga Māori, taonga, weaving knowledge)</li><li>✅ Key competencies (thinking; language, symbols &amp; text; relating to others; participating &amp; contributing)</li><li>🌟 Bonus: strong cross-curricular Science link (materials science, microscopy)</li></ul></div></div>"},"Week 10 Content Pack — \"Tahu and the Dancing Feathers\" (Connected 2024, by André Ngāpō)":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Type</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Fiction (narrative) | <strong>Reading level:</strong> Year 5/6</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Theme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Patience, the value of small contributions, and understanding a sail's feathers</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum</th><td class=\"lt-td\">NZC English, Phase 2 (Years 4–6)</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Story Snapshot (for you)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Tahu desperately wants to help his sister Huia and the other weavers make a new sail, Hine-Rangimārie, to replace the old, worn-out Hinetakitaki. Instead, Whaea Pere gives him the job of splitting kererū feathers with a mussel shell — a job Tahu finds boring and unimportant compared to weaving. When he's told he's still too young to weave, he storms off, calling the feathers &quot;useless.&quot; But when the new sail is finally raised and Tahu watches the feathers tremble and dance in the wind, he suddenly understands: the feathers show which way the wind blows, and his &quot;small&quot; job was essential all along.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Nice link back to Week 9</th><td class=\"lt-td\">this fictional story explains the exact real detail from <em>Te Rā</em> — the kererū and kāhu feathers along a sail's edge showing wind direction. Pairing the two texts (fiction and real interview) is a great comprehension/synthesis opportunity.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Lead-in questions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Have you ever really wanted to help with something, but been told you were too young or not ready yet? How did that feel?</li><li>Do you think even small, boring-seeming jobs can be important? Can you think of an example?</li><li>What do you already know about how sails were made from harakeke (flax)? (Remember Te Rā from Week 9!)</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Key vocabulary</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-data\"><thead><tr><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Word</th><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Kid-friendly meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">raranga</td><td class=\"lt-td\">weaving</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">karakia</td><td class=\"lt-td\">a prayer or ritual chant</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">atua</td><td class=\"lt-td\">a god or deity — e.g. Tāwhirimātea, atua of the wind</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">e moko</td><td class=\"lt-td\">an affectionate term for a grandchild</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">koro</td><td class=\"lt-td\">grandfather, or a respected male elder</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">whaea</td><td class=\"lt-td\">aunty, or a respected female elder/teacher</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">ātaahua</td><td class=\"lt-td\">beautiful</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">kūtai</td><td class=\"lt-td\">mussel (used here as a cutting tool)</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">whanaunga</td><td class=\"lt-td\">relatives</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">huru manu</td><td class=\"lt-td\">feathers along the edge of a sail (recap from Week 9's Te Rā!)</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Whole-class read</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Read the story together, noticing how Tahu's feelings shift from frustration to pride and understanding.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Basic comprehension questions (literal — whole class)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>What job does Whaea Pere give Tahu while the others weave the new sail?</li><li>Why does Tahu want to help weave the sail instead?</li><li>What is the name of the old sail, and what is happening to it?</li><li>What is the new sail called, and what does Koro Hotu say it will do?</li><li>Why does Tahu get upset and knock over the kete of feathers?</li><li>What does Tahu finally realise about the feathers at the end of the story?</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum tags</th><td class=\"lt-td\">oral language • vocabulary knowledge (te reo Māori) • literal comprehension • bicultural perspectives</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">🟩 TUESDAY — Level Groups: Guided Reading</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Group A — needs more support</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Re-read the story with the teacher.</li><li>Task: sequence 6 key events in order (the waka returns → Tahu watches the weavers → Tahu splits feathers → Tahu asks to help weave → the new sail is raised → Tahu understands the feathers' purpose).</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Group B — at level (teacher-led guided group)</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Focus: tracking a character's changing feelings.</li><li>Task: find evidence of Tahu's feelings changing from frustration to pride and understanding; explain what Whaea Pere means by &quot;each job, big and small, helped make the sail.&quot;</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Group C — extending</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Focus: connecting texts.</li><li>Task: discuss the theme of patience and valuing different contributions. Connect this story's feathers to Te Rā's huru manu from Week 9 — this story explains <em>why</em> those feathers matter.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum tags</th><td class=\"lt-td\">reading progression — tracking character development, inference • differentiated instruction • bicultural perspectives</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">🟨 WEDNESDAY — Pairs: Reading Activities</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>Feather-Splitting Hot Seat</strong> — One partner is Tahu, the other is Whaea Pere; re-enact the conversation about wanting to help weave.</li><li><strong>Vocabulary Match-Up</strong> — Match key terms (raranga, karakia, atua, koro, whaea, kūtai, whanaunga) to their meanings.</li><li><strong>&quot;Every Job Matters&quot; Discussion</strong> — Pairs discuss and write about a time a &quot;small&quot; job turned out to be more important than it seemed.</li><li><strong>Comparing Texts</strong> — Pairs compare <em>Tahu and the Dancing Feathers</em> (fiction) with <em>Te Rā</em> (non-fiction interview) — what's similar, what's different, about how they present sails and feathers?</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum tags</th><td class=\"lt-td\">comprehension strategies (inference, comparing texts) • collaborative talk • bicultural perspectives</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">This week's project options (pairs choose one)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>Comic strip</strong> — retell Tahu's feather-splitting and his realisation moment, in 6 panels.</li><li><strong>Persuasive mini-speech</strong> — &quot;Why every job, big or small, matters&quot; — using evidence from the story.</li><li><strong>Diary entry</strong> — write as Tahu, the night after the new sail is raised.</li><li><strong>Fact-meets-fiction poster</strong> — comparing what <em>Te Rā</em> (real, Week 9) and <em>Hine-Rangimārie</em> (fictional, this week) both tell us about a sail's feathers.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Thursday (build/create time)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>~5 min: recap the story + choose project format</li><li>~35 min: plan and build/draft</li><li>~5–10 min: teacher check-in per pair</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Friday (finish + present)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>~20–25 min: finish and polish</li><li>~20–25 min: presentations (2–3 min per pair) + peer feedback (&quot;2 stars and a wish&quot;)</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum tags</th><td class=\"lt-td\">oral language — presenting ideas clearly • key competencies — participating &amp; contributing, relating to others • synthesis — creating a text in response to reading, comparing texts • bicultural perspectives</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Christian Reflection — School Values</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Soli Deo Gloria — To God Alone the Glory</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-data\"><thead><tr><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Value</th><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Reflection question</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"lt-td\"><strong>Aroha</strong> (Love)</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Whaea Pere and Koro treat Tahu with patience and kindness even when he's frustrated and knocks over the kete. How can we show love and patience to someone who is struggling with disappointment or impatience, like Tahu?</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\"><strong>Mōhio</strong> (Know)</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Tahu didn't understand the purpose of his job until he saw the finished sail in action. How is this like trusting that God has a good purpose for the small or hard tasks He gives us, even when we can't see the full picture yet?</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\"><strong>Manaaki</strong> (Serve)</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Even though splitting feathers felt small and unimportant to Tahu, it was a real act of service that helped the whole sail succeed. How can we serve faithfully in small tasks, trusting that they matter, even when we don't feel important?</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\"><strong>Pānga</strong> (Impact)</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Tahu's carefully split feathers ended up being an essential part of guiding the waka safely across the sea. How can small, faithful actions we do now end up making a real difference later, even if we don't realise it at the time?</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Weekly Curriculum Coverage Tick-List</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>✅ Reading progressions (tracking character development, inference, comprehension)</li><li>✅ Oral language</li><li>✅ Structured literacy components (explicit vocabulary incl. te reo Māori terms)</li><li>✅ Bicultural/Aotearoa perspectives (whānau roles, patience, te reo Māori)</li><li>✅ Key competencies (thinking; language, symbols &amp; text; relating to others; participating &amp; contributing)</li><li>🌟 Bonus: strong cross-text synthesis link with Week 9's Te Rā</li></ul></div></div>"},"Week 11 Content Pack — \"Taki Tahi | Over One, Under One\" (Connected 2024, a poem by Briar Grace-Smith)":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Type</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Poetry | <strong>Reading level:</strong> Year 5/6</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Theme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Weaving a waka's sail together — the rhythm of shared, skilled work, and the sail's first test at sea</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum</th><td class=\"lt-td\">NZC English, Phase 2 (Years 4–6)</td></tr></tbody></table><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\"><strong>A note on this text:</strong> this is a poem, so in line with copyright practice, this pack describes its content, structure and devices rather than quoting lines directly. Students will read the actual poem from their own copy of the text — the activities below ask them to find and discuss specific lines themselves.</blockquote></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Story Snapshot (for you)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This poem describes a group of weavers making a waka's sail from harakeke, working through the &quot;taki tahi&quot; pattern — over one, under one — a phrase that repeats through the poem like the rhythm of the weaving itself. While their hands work, the weavers chat about everyday things (sport, television) right alongside traditional stories and atua (gods) — showing that this skilled, sacred work is also warm and ordinary, done side by side. The poem follows the sail from its finishing, through a quiet moment of pride, to the moment it's finally raised and tested against the wind — and passes the test, powering the waka out across the sea.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Term capstone</th><td class=\"lt-td\">this is the third and final text from the same journal collection as Weeks 9 and 10 — <em>Te Rā</em> (a real interview about an ancient sail) and <em>Tahu and the Dancing Feathers</em> (a fictional story about a sail being made). All three texts explore weaving and waka sails through a different genre: non-fiction, fiction, and poetry. This is a great week to draw the whole collection — and arguably the whole term's voyaging theme — together.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Lead-in questions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Have you ever done a repetitive job (like kneading dough, or a chore) while chatting with friends or family about everyday things? What did you talk about?</li><li>Poems often repeat a phrase or line again and again. Why might a poet choose to do that?</li><li>What do you already know about weaving a waka's sail? (Remember Te Rā and Tahu and the Dancing Feathers!)</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Key vocabulary</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-data\"><thead><tr><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Word</th><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Kid-friendly meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">taki tahi</td><td class=\"lt-td\">the &quot;over one, under one&quot; weaving pattern used in raranga</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">refrain</td><td class=\"lt-td\">a repeated line or phrase in a poem, often for rhythm or emphasis</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">simile</td><td class=\"lt-td\">a comparison using &quot;like&quot; or &quot;as&quot;</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">metaphor</td><td class=\"lt-td\">a comparison that says one thing IS another, without using &quot;like&quot; or &quot;as&quot;</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">imagery</td><td class=\"lt-td\">descriptive language that helps the reader picture, hear, or feel something</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">ara</td><td class=\"lt-td\">a path, way, or pattern</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">Tāwhirimātea</td><td class=\"lt-td\">the atua (god) of wind and storms</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">karakia</td><td class=\"lt-td\">a prayer or ritual chant</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">&quot;Me inoi tātou&quot;</td><td class=\"lt-td\">a phrase meaning &quot;let us pray&quot; — an invitation to karakia</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">kaiwhatu / kairaranga</td><td class=\"lt-td\">a weaver — a person who weaves</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Whole-class read</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Read the poem aloud slowly, letting the repeated refrain build a rhythm — perhaps read it once through for the story, then again noticing the repetition.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Basic comprehension questions (literal — whole class)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>What are the weavers making in this poem?</li><li>What everyday things do the weavers talk about while they work?</li><li>What traditional stories or figures do the weavers also talk about?</li><li>What repeated phrase runs through the poem, describing the weaving pattern?</li><li>What happens to the sail at the end of the poem?</li><li>Which atua (god) is mentioned in connection with the wind testing the sail?</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum tags</th><td class=\"lt-td\">oral language • vocabulary knowledge (te reo Māori and poetic terms) • literal comprehension • bicultural perspectives • poetic form and devices</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">🟩 TUESDAY — Level Groups: Guided Reading</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Group A — needs more support</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Re-read the poem with the teacher, section by section.</li><li>Task: identify the repeated refrain in your own copy of the poem, and count how many times it appears. Discuss what's happening at each stage (starting to weave → chatting → finishing → raising the sail → sailing).</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Group B — at level (teacher-led guided group)</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Focus: identifying poetic devices.</li><li>Task: find one simile and one metaphor in your own copy of the poem. Copy each one down, then explain in your own words what is being compared.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Group C — extending</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Focus: interpreting meaning and connecting texts.</li><li>Task: discuss why the poet includes everyday chatter (sport, television) alongside sacred, traditional stories (Māui, gods). What does that tell us about weaving as both ordinary and special? Connect this poem's message to <em>Te Rā</em> and <em>Tahu and the Dancing Feathers</em>.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum tags</th><td class=\"lt-td\">reading progression — identifying poetic devices, interpreting meaning • differentiated instruction • bicultural perspectives</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">🟨 WEDNESDAY — Pairs: Reading Activities</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>Refrain Hunt</strong> — Pairs find and count the repeated refrain in the poem and discuss the effect it creates (rhythm, a sense of the steady weaving pattern).</li><li><strong>Vocabulary Match-Up</strong> — Match key terms (taki tahi, refrain, simile, metaphor, ara, Tāwhirimātea, karakia) to their meanings.</li><li><strong>Simile &amp; Metaphor Detective</strong> — Pairs find and copy down one simile and one metaphor from the poem, then explain what's being compared and why it's effective.</li><li><strong>Three Texts, One Story</strong> — Pairs compare all three texts in the collection (<em>Te Rā</em>, <em>Tahu and the Dancing Feathers</em>, and this poem) — what does each one contribute to understanding waka sails and weaving?</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum tags</th><td class=\"lt-td\">poetic devices (simile, metaphor, refrain) • comprehension strategies (comparing texts) • collaborative talk • bicultural perspectives</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">This week's project options (pairs choose one)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>Write your own &quot;over one, under one&quot; style poem</strong> about a repetitive activity you do, using your own refrain.</li><li><strong>Illustrated poster</strong> capturing the poem's journey from weaving to sailing.</li><li><strong>Term reflection speech</strong> — &quot;What have I learned about waka, weaving, and Aotearoa's voyaging heritage this term?&quot;</li><li><strong>&quot;Three Texts, One Journey&quot; fact file</strong> comparing <em>Te Rā</em>, <em>Tahu and the Dancing Feathers</em>, and this poem.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Thursday (build/create time)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>~5 min: recap the poem + choose project format</li><li>~35 min: plan and build/draft</li><li>~5–10 min: teacher check-in per pair</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Friday (finish + present)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>~20–25 min: finish and polish</li><li>~20–25 min: presentations (2–3 min per pair) + peer feedback (&quot;2 stars and a wish&quot;)</li><li>Consider making this final Friday a small celebration of the whole term's voyage!</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum tags</th><td class=\"lt-td\">oral language — presenting ideas clearly • key competencies — participating &amp; contributing, relating to others • synthesis — creating a text in response to reading, comparing texts across a term • bicultural perspectives</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Christian Reflection — School Values</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Soli Deo Gloria — To God Alone the Glory</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-data\"><thead><tr><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Value</th><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Reflection question</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"lt-td\"><strong>Aroha</strong> (Love)</td><td class=\"lt-td\">The weavers work side by side, sharing both everyday chatter and treasured stories while making something beautiful together. How does working alongside others in love and friendship, like the weavers do, reflect how God wants us to live in community?</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\"><strong>Mōhio</strong> (Know)</td><td class=\"lt-td\">The refrain &quot;over one, under one&quot; repeats throughout the poem, like a steady rhythm the weavers can trust. How is returning again and again to God's Word and story like this steady, trustworthy rhythm in our own lives?</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\"><strong>Manaaki</strong> (Serve)</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Each weaver serves the group by focusing on their part of the pattern, patiently and carefully, so the whole sail comes together. How can we serve our own family, class, or team with the same patience and care?</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\"><strong>Pānga</strong> (Impact)</td><td class=\"lt-td\">The finished sail carries the waka safely across the wild sea, its purpose fulfilled. As we finish this term's reading journey together, what impact do you hope the things you've learned will have on your own life going forward?</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Weekly Curriculum Coverage Tick-List</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>✅ Reading progressions (identifying poetic devices, interpreting meaning, comprehension)</li><li>✅ Oral language</li><li>✅ Structured literacy components (explicit vocabulary incl. te reo Māori and poetic terms)</li><li>✅ Bicultural/Aotearoa perspectives (traditional weaving, te reo Māori, atua)</li><li>✅ Key competencies (thinking — interpreting meaning; language, symbols &amp; text; relating to others; participating &amp; contributing)</li><li>🌟 Bonus: term capstone — connects across all three texts in this journal collection (Weeks 9–11), and offers a chance to reflect on the whole term's voyaging theme</li></ul></div></div>"}}}}},"topic":{"label":"Category","children":{"All":{"label":"Topic","children":{"Polynesian Migration (Wayfinder)":{"label":"Week","children":{"Week 1":{"label":"Day","children":{"Monday — History: Trial 1: The Shattered Shard":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">WHERE WE BEGAN — The Lapita People and the First Great Voyages</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Social Sciences</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Achievement Objectives</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Understanding the Past: investigate how the perspectives of people in the past differ, and how this is influenced by the limited and varied evidence available.</li><li>Understanding Place and Environment: explore how exploration and discovery have consequences for people and places.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Key Competencies</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Thinking; Language, Symbols &amp; Text; Relating to Others</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning to understand who the Lapita people were and where the first great Pacific voyages began.</li><li>We are learning how archaeologists use physical evidence — like pottery — to understand history that was never written down.</li><li>I can explain why some history has to be pieced together like a puzzle, rather than read in a book.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Teacher observation of student participation in the evidence jigsaw and class discussion. Review of student exit tickets and quest-log entries for understanding of how unwritten history is recovered.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Introduction</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Teacher holds up a torn, fragmented picture — pieces of a &quot;found journal&quot; prop, or simply a jigsaw envelope. &quot;Good morning, everyone. Today we begin a brand-new voyage through history — one that starts not with a date in a textbook, but with a single broken piece of pottery. Before anyone wrote a single word about it, real people set out across the largest ocean on Earth and settled it, island by island. We're going to meet them through the clues they left behind.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Let's start with a wonder question: if a people never wrote anything down, how could we possibly know anything true about their lives thousands of years later?</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Genesis reminds us that from the very beginning, God placed people across the earth with purpose. Acts 17:26 tells us God 'marked out their appointed times in history and the boundaries of their lands.' Today we're going to explore one of the greatest, and least written-about, journeys in human history — and see if we can find the truth in the clues it left behind.&quot;</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Main Content</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">What Was the Lapita Culture? (10 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Teacher Input: Around 3,000 years ago, archaeologists began finding a very particular kind of pottery scattered across islands of the western Pacific — decorated with fine, comb-like dots pressed into the clay before it was fired, called dentate-stamped pottery.</li><li>This pottery was first properly studied at a site called Lapita in New Caledonia in the 1950s–60s — which is where the name 'Lapita culture' comes from.</li><li>Show a world/Pacific map. Explain: archaeologists believe these people first appeared near the Bismarck Archipelago, close to Papua New Guinea, then spread an extraordinary distance — all the way to Fiji, Tonga and Samoa — in only a few hundred years. For people without metal tools, compasses, or maps, this was one of the fastest and farthest human migrations in the ancient world.</li><li>Key Question: &quot;If they didn't write anything down, how do we know any of this actually happened?&quot;</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Piecing Together the Evidence (15 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Teacher Input: explain that historians and archaeologists piece together unwritten history using several kinds of evidence: archaeology (broken pottery, old rubbish heaps called middens, tools), linguistics (related words for the same things — like 'canoe' or 'house' — showing up in many different Pacific languages, because they share a common ancestor language), and oral tradition (stories, chants and whakapapa passed down by families for generations).</li><li>Activity: In small groups, students piece together a jigsaw image of a Lapita-style pottery shard laid over a faint Pacific map. As the picture comes together, it reveals the region archaeologists believe is the likely homeland.</li><li>Discuss as each group finishes: what does this physical evidence tell us, and what doesn't it tell us? (E.g. pottery shows us where people lived and roughly when, but not their names, feelings, or exact reasons for moving.)</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Why the Timeline Matters (10 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Teacher Input: Important distinction for students — the Lapita spread (around 3,000 years ago) happened across the western Pacific (Papua New Guinea to Samoa/Tonga). The much later voyages into the 'Polynesian Triangle' — to Hawaii, Rapa Nui, and eventually Aotearoa — happened roughly 1,000–1,300 years after that, around 700–800 years ago. They are two different chapters of the same long story, not the same event.</li><li>Key Question: &quot;Why might it matter to get the order of events right, instead of treating it as one single voyage?&quot;</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Biblical Connection — Every Nation, Known to God (5 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>&quot;Acts 17:26–27 tells us God 'marked out their appointed times in history and the boundaries of their lands ... so that they would seek him.' Even though we only have fragments of evidence for the Lapita story, none of these real people and their journeys were lost to God, even when they were lost to written history.&quot;</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Exit Ticket</th><td class=\"lt-td\">On a small piece of paper, write down ONE type of evidence historians use to learn about history that was never written down, and explain why it's useful. Be ready to share your answer.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Faith Connection</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Ponder: God placed in us a longing to know where we come from. When we uncover ancient pottery shards, we glimpse the creativity of people made in His image — adventurers, makers, and storytellers, just like us.</li><li>Verse: &quot;From one man he made all the nations, that they should inhabit the whole earth; and he marked out their appointed times in history and the boundaries of their lands.&quot; — <em>Acts 17:26 (NIV)</em></li><li>Value: Mōhio  Know — Learning where we come from helps us know God's story and our place in it.</li><li>Discussion questions:<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>If God marked out appointed times and places for every nation, what does that tell us about the Lapita people — and about where YOU were born?</li><li>The voyagers left their mark in pottery. What marks are you making that might tell your story to someone a thousand years from now?</li><li>Tama is searching for where he comes from. How does knowing God as our Creator change the way we understand where we come from?</li></ul></li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Large Pacific Ocean map</li><li>Jigsaw sets: Lapita pottery image over a faint Pacific map (one per group)</li><li>Evidence cards (archaeology / linguistics / oral tradition)</li><li>Tama's Journal prop (or a sealed box/journal for the term-long story)</li><li>Small pieces of paper for exit tickets</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Suggested Videos</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Lapita pottery and the Pacific migration</em> — search: &quot;Lapita pottery Pacific migration explained&quot;. Gives a visual, age-appropriate overview of the real archaeological evidence behind this lesson.</li><li><em>Animated map of Pacific migration routes</em> — search: &quot;Pacific migration map animation&quot;. Helps students visualise the scale and direction of the spread discussed in class.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Student Agency Choices</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Art: Design Your Own Lapita-Style Pottery Pattern</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Real Lapita pottery was decorated using a technique called dentate-stamping — pressing a small comb-like tool into wet clay to leave rows of tiny dots and lines.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Instructions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>On paper (or air-dry clay/playdough if available), design your own repeating pattern using only dots and lines, the way a Lapita potter would have.</li><li>Use a real comb, the edge of a fork, or a rolled-up piece of card to press your pattern in, if working with clay/dough — or draw it carefully if working on paper.</li><li>Add a short caption explaining what makes this pottery style historically important.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">What does a pattern like this tell future archaeologists about the people who made it — and what doesn't it tell them?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Read/Write: An Archaeologist's Field Diary</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Imagine you are an archaeologist who has just uncovered a piece of genuine Lapita pottery on a dig.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Instructions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Write a field diary entry describing the moment you found it.</li><li>Describe what the pottery looks like, where you found it, and what you think it might tell us.</li><li>Include at least one sentence about why this discovery matters for understanding unwritten history.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Word Count Guide</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Aim for approximately 150–200 words.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">How does writing as an archaeologist help you understand how real historical knowledge is built from physical evidence?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Technology: Build a Digital Migration Timeline</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>You are going to create a simple digital timeline (using Canva, Google Slides, or a similar tool) showing the spread of the Lapita culture across the Pacific.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Instructions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Create at least 4 points on your timeline: the likely starting region (near Papua New Guinea), and three island groups it spread to (e.g. Fiji, Tonga, Samoa).</li><li>Add an approximate date or &quot;years ago&quot; label to each point.</li><li>Add one image or icon per point.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">What surprised you about how quickly this migration happened?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Watch &amp; Review: Watch and Note: Evidence of the Lapita Voyagers</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Watch a short video about the Lapita culture or early Pacific migration.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Search term</th><td class=\"lt-td\">&quot;Lapita pottery Pacific migration explained&quot;</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">What to Watch For</th><td class=\"lt-td\">the type of evidence used, the region the people came from, and roughly how long ago this happened.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Your Review Task</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Complete a simple table: Evidence Type | What It Shows | Why It Matters.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">What was the most convincing piece of evidence in the video, and why?</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Tuesday — Geography: Trial 2: How Far Is Far?":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">THE SCALE OF THE PACIFIC — Understanding the Polynesian Triangle</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Social Sciences (Geography)</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Achievement Objectives</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Understanding Place and Environment: explore how places influence people and people influence places.</li><li>Understanding Place and Environment: understand how people view and use places differently.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Key Competencies</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Thinking; Using Language, Symbols &amp; Text</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning to understand the true scale of the Pacific Ocean.</li><li>We are learning what the 'Polynesian Triangle' is and why it represents one of the greatest feats of human exploration.</li><li>I can compare real distances between Pacific island groups using a map and scale.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Teacher observation during the floor-map measuring activity. Review of star compass design and written distance comparisons.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Introduction</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&quot;Yesterday we found our first clue about where the Pacific voyagers began. Today, we need to understand exactly how big the challenge ahead of them really was.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Quick question before we start: how big do you think the Pacific Ocean actually is? Bigger than New Zealand? Bigger than Australia? Let's find out.&quot;</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Show a true-scale world map (not the commonly distorted Mercator-projection versions) to reveal the Pacific's real size relative to landmasses.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Main Content</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Just How Big Is the Pacific? (10 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Teacher Input: The Pacific Ocean covers about 165 million square kilometres — roughly one-third of the entire surface of the Earth, and bigger than all the continents on Earth combined.</li><li>Introduce the Polynesian Triangle: a region of the Pacific with three corner points — Hawaii in the north, Aotearoa New Zealand in the southwest, and Rapa Nui (Easter Island) in the southeast. Inside this triangle are over 1,000 islands, nearly all of them settled by voyaging Polynesians over several centuries.</li><li>Key Question: &quot;If you placed this triangle over a map of Europe, what do you think would happen?&quot; (It would swallow the entire continent several times over.)</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Measuring the Triangle (15 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Activity: On a large floor map or outdoor chalk map of the Pacific, use string/wool cut to a measured scale to physically lay out the distances between Hawaii, Aotearoa, and Rapa Nui, forming a giant triangle the class can stand inside or around.</li><li>Real distances for reference: Tahiti to Hawaii is roughly 4,400 km. Tahiti to Aotearoa is roughly 4,300 km. Tahiti to Rapa Nui is roughly 4,000 km. Hawaii to Rapa Nui — almost corner to corner of the whole triangle — is roughly 7,000 km.</li><li>Ask students to guess how many classrooms or school halls would fit end-to-end along just ONE side of the triangle, before revealing the real number.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Building a Tool for the Voyage (10 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Activity: In groups, design a simple paper star compass wheel — a circle divided into key directions, decorated and labelled (e.g. sunrise point, sunset point, key star positions). This becomes a tool used again later in the term.</li><li>Key Question: &quot;Why might a voyager need a tool like this, instead of just guessing direction by feel?&quot;</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Exit Ticket</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Write one sentence describing the real scale of the Pacific Ocean in your own words, using at least one number from today's lesson.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Faith Connection</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Ponder: The Polynesian Triangle covers a third of the Earth's surface — and people crossed it in wooden canoes guided by stars alone. God made us with an extraordinary capacity to explore and inhabit the world He created for us.</li><li>Verse: &quot;God blessed them and said to them, &quot;Be fruitful and increase in number; fill the earth and subdue it.&quot;&quot; — <em>Genesis 1:28 (NIV)</em></li><li>Value: Mōhio  Know — Understanding the full scale of Polynesian migration helps us grasp the wonder of how God spread His people across the earth.</li><li>Discussion questions:<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>The Pacific covers a third of the earth — what does the fact that people crossed it in wooden canoes tell you about how God designed humans?</li><li>If God called people to 'fill the earth,' do you think the Polynesian voyagers were part of that calling without knowing it?</li><li>What places in the world do you think God is calling people to reach in your generation?</li></ul></li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Large floor/wall map of the Pacific</li><li>A true-scale (not Mercator) world map for comparison</li><li>Wool/string cut to a measured scale</li><li>Paper, compasses (drawing), pencils for star compass craft</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Suggested Videos</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>How big is the Pacific Ocean, really?</em> — search: &quot;true size of the Pacific Ocean map comparison&quot;. Helps correct the common misconception (from standard maps) of how small the Pacific looks compared to its real size.</li><li><em>The Polynesian Triangle explained</em> — search: &quot;Polynesian Triangle map explained&quot;. Gives a clear visual explanation of the three-point region central to this lesson.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Student Agency Choices</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Art: Illustrate the Polynesian Triangle</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Draw your own decorated map of the Polynesian Triangle, marking Hawaii, Aotearoa, and Rapa Nui, plus at least 3 other island groups inside it.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Instructions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Sketch the rough outline of the Pacific.</li><li>Mark and label the three triangle points plus extra islands.</li><li>Add a decorative compass rose in one corner.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">What was the hardest part of getting the scale right?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Read/Write: A Letter Describing the Ocean Ahead</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write a short letter, in role as a young voyager, to a family member staying behind, describing how big and frightening the ocean ahead seems.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Instructions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Mention at least one real distance fact from today's lesson.</li><li>Describe how it feels to face that much open ocean.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Word Count Guide</th><td class=\"lt-td\">120–180 words.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">How did using a real number change the way you wrote about the ocean's size?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Technology: Build a Digital Distance Chart</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Use a spreadsheet or simple table tool (Google Sheets, Canva chart) to chart the four distances mentioned today (Tahiti–Hawaii, Tahiti–Aotearoa, Tahiti–Rapa Nui, Hawaii–Rapa Nui).</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Instructions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Enter each distance.</li><li>Create a simple bar chart comparing them.</li><li>Add one sentence noting which is the longest.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Did the chart change how you picture these distances compared to just hearing the numbers?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Watch &amp; Review: Watch and Note: Mapping the Pacific</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Watch a short video on the size of the Pacific Ocean or the Polynesian Triangle.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Search term</th><td class=\"lt-td\">&quot;Polynesian Triangle map explained&quot;</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">What to Watch For</th><td class=\"lt-td\">the three corner points, how many islands are inside, and any real distance figures mentioned.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">What was the most surprising fact you noted?</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Wednesday — Science: Trial 3: Reading the Sky":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">WEATHER vs. CLIMATE — What Every Voyager Needed to Know</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Science (Planet Earth and Beyond)</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Achievement Objectives</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Investigate weather phenomena and how they affect people and the environment.</li><li>Appreciate that science is a way of explaining the world, and that science knowledge changes over time.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Key Competencies</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Thinking; Participating &amp; Contributing</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning the difference between weather and climate.</li><li>We are learning how observing wind and sky helped real voyagers make safe decisions.</li><li>I can use real, observed conditions to make and justify a simple prediction.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Teacher observation of the card-sorting activity and outdoor wind observation. Review of students' written voyage forecasts.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Introduction</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&quot;Yesterday, we measured how big the ocean ahead really is. Today we need to understand something just as important: how do you know it's actually safe to go?</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Wonder question: what's the difference between knowing today's weather, and knowing what a whole season is usually like?</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Real voyagers couldn't check a weather app. They relied entirely on careful, repeated observation of the sky, wind, and sea — skills built up over a lifetime, and passed down through generations.&quot;</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Main Content</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Weather vs. Climate (10 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Teacher Input: Weather is what's happening in the sky right now — today's wind, rain, temperature, cloud cover. Climate is the long-term pattern of weather in a place, built up over many years (e.g. &quot;the Pacific has a wet season and a dry season&quot;).</li><li>Real example: in much of the South Pacific, cyclone season runs roughly from November to April. A voyager who understood climate would avoid setting out during this period, even on a calm day — because they understood the bigger seasonal pattern, not just that day's conditions.</li><li>Key Question: &quot;Why would a voyager need to know both the weather AND the climate before setting out on a long voyage?&quot;</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Sorting Weather from Climate (15 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Activity: In teams, race to physically sort scattered statement/picture cards into two zones — Weather (right now) and Climate (the long-term pattern). Example cards: &quot;It rained today&quot; (weather), &quot;December to March is generally hot and wet in the tropical Pacific&quot; (climate), &quot;There are strong winds this afternoon&quot; (weather).</li><li>Discuss as a class once sorted: what real decisions would each type of information help a voyager make?</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Reading the Real Sky (10 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Activity: Take the class outside (or to a window) for a genuine sky and wind observation using a simple pinwheel or paper streamer. Record wind direction, cloud type, and general conditions.</li><li>Teacher Input (brief, simplified): trade winds in the tropical Pacific blow fairly steadily from the east, because warm air rising near the equator and sinking further north and south creates a repeating pattern of wind. Voyagers used this knowledge to plan routes that worked with the wind, not against it.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Exit Ticket</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Based on today's real observation, write one sentence: would a voyager be safe to set sail today? Justify your answer using something you actually observed.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Faith Connection</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Ponder: The voyagers read the sky like a letter written by God. They had no instruments — only attention, and the sky was enough. The same heavens that guided their canoe have always been declaring God's glory.</li><li>Verse: &quot;The heavens declare the glory of God; the skies proclaim the work of his hands.&quot; — <em>Psalm 19:1 (NIV)</em></li><li>Value: Mōhio  Know — Learning to read God's creation helps us know the Creator who designed and sustains it all.</li><li>Discussion questions:<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>Psalm 19 says the sky 'proclaims' God's work without any words. What do you think the sky is saying?</li><li>The voyagers trusted the sky with their lives. What does it take to trust something that completely?</li><li>What part of creation speaks most powerfully to you about God's creativity?</li></ul></li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Weather/climate sorting cards</li><li>Pinwheels or paper streamers for outdoor wind test</li><li>Simple cloud-type reference chart</li><li>Paper for voyage forecast logs</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Suggested Videos</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Weather vs climate explained for kids</em> — search: &quot;weather vs climate explained for kids&quot;. A clear, age-appropriate explanation of the core science concept for this lesson.</li><li><em>What are trade winds?</em> — search: &quot;trade winds explained simple&quot;. Gives students a simplified but accurate picture of the real wind pattern referenced in class.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Student Agency Choices</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Art: Illustrate a Voyager Reading the Sky</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Draw a scene of a voyager studying the sky and sea before deciding whether to set sail.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Instructions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Include at least one weather clue (clouds, wind, waves) in your drawing.</li><li>Add a thought bubble showing what the voyager is deciding.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">What clue in your drawing would matter most, and why?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Read/Write: A Voyager's Forecast Log</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write a short &quot;ship's log&quot; entry in role as a voyager studying today's real sky and wind.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Instructions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Describe what you actually observed outside today.</li><li>State your decision: safe to sail, or not — and why.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Word Count Guide</th><td class=\"lt-td\">100–150 words.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">How confident would you really be making this decision with no weather app?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Technology: Build a Simple Weather-Tracking Sheet</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Create a simple digital or paper tracking sheet that records the weather for 5 days (could continue beyond today's lesson).</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Instructions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Record wind, cloud, and temperature each day.</li><li>After 5 days, write one sentence: is this weather, or is it starting to show a climate pattern?</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">How many days of data do you think you'd need before you could call something a &quot;pattern&quot; rather than just &quot;today's weather&quot;?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Watch &amp; Review: Watch and Note: Reading Wind and Sky</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Watch a short video about trade winds or weather basics.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Search term</th><td class=\"lt-td\">&quot;trade winds explained simple&quot;</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">What to Watch For</th><td class=\"lt-td\">why winds blow in consistent patterns, and how that might help (or hinder) a sea voyage.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">What was one fact that would have genuinely helped a real voyager?</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Thursday — Technology: Trial 4: The Floating Trial":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">WHY THINGS FLOAT — Buoyancy and the First Voyaging Vessels</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Technology</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Achievement Objectives</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Technological Knowledge: understand how materials are selected for use based on their physical and functional properties.</li><li>Nature of Technology: understand that technological outcomes are products or systems developed to meet a need.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Key Competencies</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Thinking; Managing Self</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning why some objects float and others sink.</li><li>We are learning how shape and material affect a vessel's ability to carry weight safely.</li><li>I can design, test, and improve a simple floating vessel using real engineering reasoning.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Teacher observation of the build-and-test challenge, including the redesign stage. Review of written design explanations.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Introduction</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&quot;We now know how big the ocean is, and how to read the sky. But none of it matters if the vessel itself doesn't survive the water. Today, we become engineers.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Wonder question: why does a heavy ship made of solid metal float, while a small stone sinks straight to the bottom?&quot;</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Main Content</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">The Real Science of Floating (10 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Teacher Input: An object floats when it pushes aside (displaces) enough water to support its own weight. This is sometimes called Archimedes' Principle, named after the ancient Greek scientist who first explained it. Shape matters hugely: a solid lump of clay sinks, but the same amount of clay shaped into a wide, hollow bowl floats — because the bowl shape displaces far more water relative to its weight.</li><li>Introduce density simply: density compares how much something weighs for its size. Denser materials need a shape that displaces more water in order to float.</li><li>Demonstrate: drop a solid ball of clay/foil into water (sinks), then reshape the same material into a wide, shallow bowl shape and try again (floats).</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Build and Test (15 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Activity: In teams, using a limited material kit (foil, a small amount of clay, straws, tape), build a small vessel that must float AND carry a 'cargo' (a marble or small weight) across a tub of water without sinking.</li><li>Test each design live in front of the class.</li><li>Key Question during testing: &quot;What made some designs more stable than others?&quot;</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Redesign and Improve (10 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Activity: After the first test, each team gets one chance to redesign and improve their vessel based on what failed the first time — this mirrors real engineering, where the first attempt is rarely the final design.</li><li>Discuss: what specific change did you make, and why did you expect it to help?</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Exit Ticket</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Write one sentence explaining, in your own words, why shape affects whether something floats.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Faith Connection</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Ponder: The waka was one of the most sophisticated vessels ever built by human hands — designed using nothing but observation, experience, and inherited wisdom. God gives wisdom to those who seek it, and the waka builders are evidence of that extraordinary gift.</li><li>Verse: &quot;By wisdom a house is built, and through understanding it is established; through knowledge its rooms are filled with rare and beautiful treasures.&quot; — <em>Proverbs 24:3-4 (NIV)</em></li><li>Value: Manaaki  Serve — Using our God-given skills to build something that serves and carries others is at the heart of Manaaki — faithful service with our gifts.</li><li>Discussion questions:<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>The waka builders had no computers or modern tools — how do you think God gave them the wisdom to build something so advanced?</li><li>If 'by wisdom a house is built,' what kind of wisdom do you think you're building right now in school that will serve others later?</li><li>How might God want you to use your particular skills to serve your community?</li></ul></li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Foil, clay/playdough, straws, tape (per team)</li><li>Tubs or basins of water</li><li>Marbles or small weights for &quot;cargo&quot;</li><li>Paper for design sketches/explanations</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Suggested Videos</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Archimedes' Principle for kids</em> — search: &quot;Archimedes principle buoyancy explained for kids&quot;. Explains the real scientific principle behind today's lesson in an age-appropriate way.</li><li><em>Why do boats float? Density explained</em> — search: &quot;why do boats float density explained kids&quot;. Reinforces the link between density, shape, and floating ahead of the hands-on build.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Student Agency Choices</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Art: Design a Vessel Blueprint</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Create a labelled &quot;blueprint&quot; style drawing of your ideal voyaging vessel.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Instructions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Draw your vessel from the side and from above.</li><li>Label at least 3 design features and explain what each one does.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Which feature do you think matters most for surviving open ocean, and why?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Read/Write: An Engineer's Test Report</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write a short report, in role as the engineer who built today's vessel, describing the test, what failed, and what you changed.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Instructions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Describe the first design and what happened when tested.</li><li>Describe your redesign and why you made that choice.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Word Count Guide</th><td class=\"lt-td\">120–180 words.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">How is this similar to how real engineers work?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Technology: Digital Design Poster</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Use Canva or a similar tool to create a digital poster of your vessel design, labelling key features.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Instructions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Add an image or drawing of your vessel.</li><li>Label at least 3 features with short explanations.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">What would you change if you had to build it twice as big?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Watch &amp; Review: Watch and Note: Why Boats Float</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Watch a short video explaining buoyancy or boat design.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Search term</th><td class=\"lt-td\">&quot;why do boats float density explained kids&quot;</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">What to Watch For</th><td class=\"lt-td\">the real scientific reason objects float, and how shape changes this.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Did the video change how you'd design your next vessel? How?</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Friday — Art: Trial 5: The First Quilt Square":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">THE FOLD-AND-CUT TECHNIQUE — Beginning a Pacific-Inspired Quilt</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">The Arts (Visual Art)</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Achievement Objectives</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Developing Practical Knowledge: explore a variety of materials and tools, and discover elements and techniques.</li><li>Understanding the Arts in Context: investigate the purpose of objects and images from the past and recognise that this reflects the technology and aesthetics of cultures and times.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Key Competencies</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Thinking; Using Language, Symbols &amp; Text</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning a fold-and-cut technique that produces symmetric, radiating patterns similar to traditional Pacific design.</li><li>We are learning that Pacific art often carries real cultural meaning, not just decoration.</li><li>I can create one considered, technique-based square as part of a small-group collaborative quilt.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Teacher observation of technique use, care taken with the fold-and-cut process, and group collaboration while assembling the quilt.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Introduction</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&quot;Every great journey deserves to be remembered — not just in words, but in something we can see. This term, instead of one giant class artwork, each of you will create your own small square every Friday, inspired by a real Pacific design tradition. Square by square, your small group's squares will be glued together into your own quilt, growing week by week.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Before we begin, it's worth knowing: across the real Pacific, art was never just decoration. Patterns and motifs often carried real meaning — telling stories of family, place, and belief. Today's square is inspired by the very first clue we found this term: Lapita pottery.&quot;</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Main Content</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Real Pacific Art Traditions (8 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Teacher Input: Introduce tapa cloth (also called ngatu in Tonga, siapo in Samoa) — a real, traditional Pacific art form made by beating the inner bark of the paper mulberry tree into a flat cloth, then decorating it with natural dyes using stencils and repeating patterns.</li><li>Explain that these patterns were not random — they often represented genealogy, status, or stories specific to a family or village, similar in purpose to the carved patterns in whakairo (Māori carving) or ta moko (Māori tattoo design).</li><li>Show 2–3 real examples of tapa/ngatu/siapo patterns, and the dentate-stamped (dotted/lined) Lapita pottery style referenced in Monday's trial, if available.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Teaching the Fold-and-Cut Technique (10 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Teacher demonstrates the technique step by step: 1) Trim an A4 sheet into a square. 2) Fold the square in half diagonally to form a triangle. 3) Fold that triangle in half again to form a smaller triangle. 4) Cut small notches, triangles, or dots into the folded edges — NOT all the way across, leaving the centre fold intact. 5) Carefully unfold to reveal a symmetric, radiating pattern.</li><li>This week's pattern inspiration: dots and short lines, echoing the dentate-stamped Lapita pottery pattern from Monday's trial.</li><li>Safety note: model safe scissor use on folded paper (multiple layers can be thicker to cut) before students begin independently.</li><li>Practise the fold and one or two test cuts together on scrap paper first, so students see the unfolded result before committing to their real square.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Making and Assembling the Quilt Squares (12 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Activity: split the class into small groups (4–5 students each). Each student creates their own square using today's technique and pattern inspiration.</li><li>Each group glues their finished squares onto a shared backing sheet (poster paper or card), beginning that group's own quilt — kept small and manageable rather than one large class piece.</li><li>Encourage genuine collaboration within each group — comparing techniques, helping each other line up cuts, discussing what to do with hidden secret cuts (see Quest below).</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Exit Ticket</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Write one sentence: what is one real purpose Pacific art traditions like tapa cloth served, beyond just looking decorative?</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Faith Connection</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Ponder: The Lapita pottery makers were artists — every dot and line a deliberate choice. When we create art, we are doing something deeply human: making things that reflect the imagination of the God who made us in His image.</li><li>Verse: &quot;For we are God's handiwork, created in Christ Jesus to do good works, which God prepared in advance for us to do.&quot; — <em>Ephesians 2:10 (NIV)</em></li><li>Value: Pānga  Impact — Every creative act done with care and intention brings a little of God's Kingdom creativity into the world.</li><li>Discussion questions:<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>If we are 'God's handiwork,' what does that tell us about how God feels about the things we make?</li><li>The Lapita people put their patterns on pottery — you put yours on paper. Why does it matter HOW we do our creative work?</li><li>How can making art be a way of bringing glory to God?</li></ul></li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A4 paper, one square per student (plus spares for mistakes)</li><li>Safety scissors</li><li>Glue and a backing sheet (poster paper or card) per small group</li><li>Reference images of tapa/ngatu/siapo cloth and Lapita pottery patterns</li><li>Scrap paper for technique practice</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Suggested Videos</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>How tapa cloth is made</em> — search: &quot;tapa cloth ngatu making process&quot;. Shows the real traditional technique referenced in this lesson, grounding the art activity in genuine cultural practice.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Student Agency Choices</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Art: Create Your First Quilt Square</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This week's main activity IS the art focus — create your fold-and-cut square and add it to your group's quilt.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">What does your square's pattern represent, and what did you hide as your &quot;secret&quot; cut?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Read/Write: Describe a Pacific Pattern</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Research (or use today's examples) one real tapa/ngatu/siapo pattern and write a short description of what it might represent.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Word Count Guide</th><td class=\"lt-td\">100–150 words.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Why might it matter that a pattern has real meaning, rather than being purely decorative?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Technology: Digital Pattern Design</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Use a simple drawing tool (Canva, Google Drawings) to design your own repeating pattern inspired by tapa cloth or Lapita pottery geometry.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">What part of real tapa cloth design did you borrow, and what part did you make your own?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Watch &amp; Review: Watch and Note: Making Tapa Cloth</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Watch a short video on how tapa cloth is traditionally made.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Search term</th><td class=\"lt-td\">&quot;tapa cloth ngatu making process&quot;</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">What part of the process surprised you most?</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"}}},"Week 2":{"label":"Day","children":{"Monday — History: Trial 6: Why Leave at All?":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">PUSH AND PULL — Why Real Voyagers Risked Everything to Leave</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Social Sciences</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Achievement Objectives</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Understanding the Past: investigate how people's lives have been shaped by significant events, and how they responded to these events.</li><li>Understanding Place and Environment: explore how people make decisions about resources and the consequences of these decisions.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Key Competencies</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Thinking; Relating to Others; Participating &amp; Contributing</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning the real reasons historians believe ancient Pacific peoples chose to set out on dangerous ocean voyages.</li><li>We are learning to tell the difference between a &quot;push&quot; factor and a &quot;pull&quot; factor.</li><li>I can weigh up evidence and form my own reasoned opinion about a historical decision.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Teacher observation of the village council debate and class vote. Review of student tokens and justification for what they would have carried.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Introduction</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&quot;Last week, we discovered who the first Pacific voyagers were and how big the ocean ahead of them truly was. But here's the real question historians still debate: why would anyone choose to leave everything they knew, to cross an ocean that had already swallowed others who tried?</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Wonder question: if your home was safe, would you ever choose to leave it for somewhere you'd never seen?</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This is not just an old question — it's one people still face today, all over the world. Understanding it helps us understand both the past and the present.&quot;</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Main Content</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Real Push Factors (10 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Teacher Input: Historians and anthropologists believe several real pressures pushed people to leave Pacific islands over time. Islands have limited land and limited fresh water — as populations grew over generations, food and resources could become stretched thin.</li><li>Pacific islands were also vulnerable to natural pressures like drought cycles and crop failures, which could make staying far riskier than it first seems.</li><li>Social pressures mattered too: historians believe disputes over land, status, or leadership sometimes led groups to leave rather than stay and fight.</li><li>Key Question: &quot;Why might 'staying' sometimes have been the more dangerous choice, even though leaving meant crossing open ocean?&quot;</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Real Pull Factors (10 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Teacher Input: It wasn't only danger driving people away — there were also real reasons to be drawn toward the unknown. Voyagers gained enormous prestige (sometimes called mana) from successfully finding and settling new land — discovery itself was a great achievement, not just a desperate escape.</li><li>A genuinely interesting real theory: some researchers believe voyagers may have used the seasonal migration of certain birds — like the long-tailed cuckoo, which migrates between the Pacific Islands and areas far to the south — as a clue that more land existed beyond the horizon.</li><li>Key Question: &quot;Why might curiosity and the chance for prestige have been just as powerful a reason to leave as fear or hardship?&quot;</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">The Village Council Debate (15 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Activity: Split the class into two camps — a 'Stay or Go' village council, in role as elders. Deal each camp evidence cards covering real push factors (overcrowding, drought, conflict) and real pull factors (prestige, curiosity, bird migration clues, rumours of other islands).</li><li>Each camp builds its case using only its dealt cards, then holds a genuine stand-up debate in role.</li><li>After the debate, the whole class votes in role as the village: Go, or Stay? Discuss as a class: was this an easy decision, or a genuinely difficult one?</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Exit Ticket</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Write one sentence identifying ONE real push factor and ONE real pull factor discussed today.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Faith Connection</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Ponder: Tama's community made one of the hardest decisions a human can make: to leave everything familiar for somewhere unknown. Abraham made the same choice — and both stories show us that God sometimes calls us to trust before we can see.</li><li>Verse: &quot;By faith Abraham, when called to go to a place he would later receive as his inheritance, obeyed and went, even though he did not know where he was going.&quot; — <em>Hebrews 11:8 (NIV)</em></li><li>Value: Aroha  Love — Leaving what is familiar for the sake of those you love — your children's future, your family's flourishing — is one of the deepest expressions of love.</li><li>Discussion questions:<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>Abraham left without knowing where he was going. Have you ever had to trust someone completely without being able to see the outcome?</li><li>The voyagers left because they loved their families enough to give them a better future. What do you love enough to make a hard sacrifice for?</li><li>What does it feel like to know that God sometimes calls people to go without knowing the destination?</li></ul></li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Evidence cards: push factors (overcrowding, drought, conflict) and pull factors (prestige, curiosity, bird migration, rumour of land)</li><li>Two clearly marked debate &quot;camps&quot; / spaces in the room</li><li>Small paper/token-making materials</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Suggested Videos</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Why did Polynesians migrate?</em> — search: &quot;why did Polynesians migrate across the Pacific&quot;. Provides a real, age-appropriate overview of the genuine historical theories behind Pacific migration.</li><li><em>How birds may have guided Pacific voyagers</em> — search: &quot;birds guiding Polynesian navigation migration clues&quot;. Introduces the real and genuinely interesting theory about bird migration as a navigation clue.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Student Agency Choices</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Art: Design a Token of What Mattered Most</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Create a small drawn or crafted token representing the one thing a real voyager might have chosen to bring with them, leaving everything else behind.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Instructions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Decide what your token represents (a tool, a memory, a family object).</li><li>Create it on paper or with simple materials.</li><li>Write a one-sentence caption explaining why it mattered enough to bring.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">What does your choice say about what you value most?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Read/Write: The Elder's Decision</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write a short speech, in role as a village elder, either arguing to stay or to go, using at least one real push or pull factor from today's lesson.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Instructions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Choose your position: Stay, or Go.</li><li>Use at least one real factor from class to support your argument.</li><li>End with a call to action for your &quot;village.&quot;</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Word Count Guide</th><td class=\"lt-td\">120–180 words.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Was it harder to argue for staying or for going? Why?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Technology: Build a Push/Pull Factor Chart</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Use a simple digital table or mind-map tool (Google Slides, Canva) to sort today's push and pull factors into two clear categories.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Instructions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>List at least 3 push factors and 3 pull factors.</li><li>Add a short icon or image for each.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Which single factor do you think mattered most, and why?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Watch &amp; Review: Watch and Note: Reasons for Migration</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Watch a short video on why Pacific peoples migrated.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Search term</th><td class=\"lt-td\">&quot;why did Polynesians migrate across the Pacific&quot;</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">What to Watch For</th><td class=\"lt-td\">any push factors and pull factors mentioned.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Did the video mention any factor we didn't discuss in class?</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Tuesday — Geography: Trial 7: The Ocean's Roads":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">OCEAN CURRENTS — The Pacific's Hidden Highways</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Social Sciences (Geography)</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Achievement Objectives</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Understanding Place and Environment: explore how natural features influence the ways people interact with an environment.</li><li>Understanding Place and Environment: understand how places are connected to other places through movement of people, goods, and information.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Key Competencies</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Thinking; Using Language, Symbols &amp; Text</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning what ocean currents are and how they move across the Pacific.</li><li>We are learning how real voyagers used currents to travel further with less effort.</li><li>I can explain why understanding a current is different from understanding the wind.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Teacher observation of the floor-map current activity and the class discussion on current vs. wind.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Introduction</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&quot;We know voyagers had to cross enormous distances. But here's something many people don't realise: the ocean itself has moving rivers within it, called currents — and a clever voyager could use them like a highway, travelling faster with far less effort.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Wonder question: if you dropped a bottle in the ocean today, do you think it would stay in one place, or travel somewhere completely different?&quot;</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Main Content</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">What Are Ocean Currents? (10 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Teacher Input: Ocean currents are large, fairly consistent streams of moving water within the ocean, caused mainly by wind patterns, the rotation of the Earth, and differences in water temperature and saltiness (density).</li><li>In the Pacific, several major currents move in fairly predictable patterns — including the South Equatorial Current and North Equatorial Current near the equator, which generally flow from east to west, and the Equatorial Countercurrent running between them, flowing the opposite way.</li><li>Key Question: &quot;If a current generally flows one direction, how might that help a voyager — and how might it also be a problem on the way home?&quot;</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Currents vs. Wind (10 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Teacher Input: It's an easy mix-up — wind moves the air above the water, while currents move the water itself. A skilled voyager needed to understand both separately, because a current could carry a vessel one direction even while the wind pushed the sail another.</li><li>Key Question: &quot;Why would a voyager need to plan around BOTH the current and the wind, not just one or the other?&quot;</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Mapping the Ocean's Roads (15 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Activity: On the large floor/wall Pacific map, lay down ribbon or string to represent major current 'roads.' In teams, move a waka token from a starting island to a destination, where landing on a current allows extra movement (simple board-game style mechanic).</li><li>Discuss after playing: which team reached their destination fastest, and why?</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Exit Ticket</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Write one sentence explaining the difference between an ocean current and the wind.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Faith Connection</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Ponder: The ocean currents are invisible roads that have been there since creation — God's hidden architecture written into the sea. The Psalmist says sailors who go out on the deep see God's wonderful deeds. The voyagers saw them every day.</li><li>Verse: &quot;Some went out on the sea in ships, trading on the mighty waters. They saw the works of the LORD, his wonderful deeds in the deep.&quot; — <em>Psalm 107:23-24 (NIV)</em></li><li>Value: Mōhio  Know — Knowing that God designed the currents and created the sea helps us understand His creation with wonder and gratitude.</li><li>Discussion questions:<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>The Psalm says sailors 'saw the works of the LORD' on the ocean. What do you think they saw?</li><li>The ocean currents are invisible but were always there — what other things has God made that are invisible but completely real?</li><li>How might looking at the natural world more closely change the way you pray or think about God?</li></ul></li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Large Pacific floor/wall map</li><li>Ribbon or string to mark current &quot;roads&quot;</li><li>Waka tokens for the movement game</li><li>Dice or a simple spinner for movement (optional)</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Suggested Videos</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>How ocean currents work</em> — search: &quot;how ocean currents work explained for kids&quot;. Gives a clear, age-appropriate explanation of the real science behind ocean currents.</li><li><em>Pacific Ocean currents map</em> — search: &quot;Pacific Ocean currents map explained&quot;. Shows the real major current systems referenced in this lesson.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Student Agency Choices</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Art: Illustrate the Ocean's Roads</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Draw the Pacific Ocean with its major currents shown as flowing, decorated &quot;roads&quot; of water.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Instructions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Sketch a simple Pacific outline.</li><li>Add at least 2 current &quot;roads&quot; with arrows showing direction.</li><li>Decorate each current with a unique colour or pattern.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Why did you choose the colours/patterns you used for each current?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Read/Write: Message in a Bottle</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write a short story or diary entry, in role as a message in a bottle, describing the journey you might take if dropped into a real Pacific current.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Word Count Guide</th><td class=\"lt-td\">120–180 words.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">How did thinking about a current's direction shape your story?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Technology: Animate a Current Route</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Use a simple slideshow tool (Google Slides, Canva) to create a 3–4 slide &quot;animation&quot; showing a waka travelling along a current from one island to another.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">What did you have to think about to make the journey look realistic?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Watch &amp; Review: Watch and Note: Ocean Currents</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Watch a short video explaining ocean currents.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Search term</th><td class=\"lt-td\">&quot;how ocean currents work explained for kids&quot;</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">What real-world example (e.g. floating debris, temperature) did the video use to show currents are real?</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Wednesday — Science: Trial 8: Where Wind Comes From":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">TRADE WINDS — Why the Sky Pushes the Same Way, Again and Again</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Science (Planet Earth and Beyond)</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Achievement Objectives</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Investigate weather phenomena and how they affect people and the environment.</li><li>Build science knowledge by exploring observations and ideas, and comparing these with current scientific understanding.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Key Competencies</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Thinking; Participating &amp; Contributing</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning why wind forms in the first place.</li><li>We are learning what trade winds are and why they blow in consistent patterns in the tropical Pacific.</li><li>I can use a simple model to explain a real weather pattern.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Teacher observation of the warm/cold air demonstration and outdoor wind test. Review of students' Wind Wager predictions once checked.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Introduction</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&quot;Yesterday we learned how ocean currents move like rivers within the sea. Today, we look up — because the sky has its own version of the same thing: winds that blow in steady, predictable patterns, called trade winds.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Wonder question: why do you think wind happens at all? What's actually moving, and why?&quot;</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Main Content</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Why Wind Happens (10 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Teacher Input: Wind is simply air moving from an area of higher pressure to an area of lower pressure. Near the equator, the sun heats the air strongly, causing it to rise. As this warm air rises, cooler air rushes in from further north and south to take its place — and that moving air is what we feel as wind.</li><li>Demonstration: use warm and cold coloured water in a clear container (warm rises, cold sinks) to visually model the same principle that drives this air movement, or have students physically act out 'warm air rising' and 'cool air rushing in.'</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">What Are Trade Winds? (10 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Teacher Input: Because the Earth is constantly spinning, this rising-and-sinking air pattern doesn't move in a straight line — it curves. This bending effect (sometimes called the Coriolis effect) is part of why trade winds blow fairly consistently from the east in much of the tropical Pacific.</li><li>These trade winds were hugely important to real voyagers: understanding which way they reliably blew helped voyagers plan routes that worked with the wind rather than constantly fighting against it.</li><li>Key Question: &quot;If trade winds usually blow one direction, what would a voyager need to think about for the journey home?&quot;</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Reading the Real Wind (10 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Activity: Take the class outside for a genuine wind observation using a pinwheel or paper streamer. Record the wind's direction and strength.</li><li>Activity: In groups, predict tomorrow's wind direction based on today's pattern, and log it visibly on a 'Wind Wager' board to check later.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Exit Ticket</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Write one sentence explaining, in your own words, why wind happens.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Faith Connection</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Ponder: Jesus used the wind to describe the Holy Spirit — mysterious, powerful, impossible to fully control or predict, yet clearly real and present. The voyagers knew this feeling every day: the wind was their engine, their partner, and sometimes their greatest danger.</li><li>Verse: &quot;The wind blows wherever it pleases. You hear its sound, but you cannot tell where it comes from or where it is going. So it is with everyone born of the Spirit.&quot; — <em>John 3:8 (NIV)</em></li><li>Value: Mōhio  Know — Understanding the science of wind deepens our knowledge of the creation — and connects us to the Creator whose Spirit moves like the wind.</li><li>Discussion questions:<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>Jesus compared the Holy Spirit to the wind. What are some things they have in common?</li><li>The voyagers depended on the wind for their survival. What does it mean to depend on something you can't see or fully control?</li><li>How do you recognise the Holy Spirit working in your life?</li></ul></li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Clear container, warm and cold coloured water</li><li>Pinwheels or paper streamers</li><li>Wind Wager prediction board</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Suggested Videos</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>What causes wind?</em> — search: &quot;what causes wind explained for kids&quot;. Gives a clear, age-appropriate explanation of the basic science behind wind formation.</li><li><em>What are trade winds?</em> — search: &quot;trade winds explained simple&quot;. Reinforces the specific real-world wind pattern central to this lesson.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Student Agency Choices</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Art: Illustrate the Wind Cycle</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Draw a labelled diagram showing warm air rising near the equator and cooler air rushing in to replace it.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Instructions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Draw the equator and the rising warm air.</li><li>Draw cooler air moving in from above and below.</li><li>Label each part simply.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">What was the trickiest part of the cycle to draw clearly?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Read/Write: A Voyager's Wind Diary</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write a short diary entry, in role as a voyager, describing how today's real wind would affect your plans to sail.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Word Count Guide</th><td class=\"lt-td\">100–150 words.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Would you have set sail today, based on the real wind you observed?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Technology: Build a Wind Direction Tracker</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Create a simple digital or paper tracker recording wind direction over several days (can continue beyond today).</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">After a few days, does the wind seem random, or is there a pattern forming?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Watch &amp; Review: Watch and Note: Trade Winds</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Watch a short video about trade winds or what causes wind.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Search term</th><td class=\"lt-td\">&quot;trade winds explained simple&quot;</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">What fact would have mattered most to a real voyager planning a long trip?</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Thursday — Technology: Trial 9: The Double-Hulled Secret":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">THE WAKA HOURUA — Real Engineering Behind the Voyaging Canoe</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Technology</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Achievement Objectives</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Technological Knowledge: understand how the properties of materials and structures affect the performance of a product.</li><li>Nature of Technology: understand that technological outcomes are evaluated for fitness for purpose.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Key Competencies</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Thinking; Managing Self</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning what a waka hourua (double-hulled canoe) is and why its design was so effective.</li><li>We are learning how stability and balance relate to vessel design.</li><li>I can test and explain a real engineering advantage using evidence from my own model.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Teacher observation of the comparative float test between single- and double-hulled designs. Review of written engineering explanations.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Introduction</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&quot;Last week we built a vessel that could float and carry cargo. But real Pacific voyagers needed something far more capable for an ocean crossing — a vessel that could stay upright in rough seas for weeks at a time. Today we explore one of the most important real inventions of Pacific voyaging: the waka hourua, the double-hulled canoe.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Wonder question: why might TWO connected hulls be far more stable than just one?&quot;</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Main Content</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">What Is a Waka Hourua? (10 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Teacher Input: A waka hourua is a voyaging canoe made of two hulls, lashed together with crossbeams (sometimes called kiato), with a deck built across the top. This design gave voyagers a much wider, more stable base than a single-hulled canoe, while still being light enough to paddle and sail efficiently.</li><li>Real-world example: in 1976, a reconstructed double-hulled voyaging canoe named Hōkūleʻa sailed from Hawaii to Tahiti using only traditional, instrument-free wayfinding — successfully proving that this ancient design and navigation method genuinely worked across a real, modern ocean crossing.</li><li>Key Question: &quot;Why do you think building TWO smaller hulls, instead of one giant hull, was a smarter engineering choice?&quot;</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">The Science of Stability (10 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Teacher Input: A wider base resists tipping more than a narrow one, because the vessel's weight is spread across a larger area, making it much harder for a wave to roll it over. This is the same basic principle that makes a wide-based table sturdier than a narrow one.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Build and Compare (15 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Activity: In teams, build a single-hull vessel and a double-hull vessel (two connected hulls) using the same limited materials (straws, foil, card). Test both against a simulated 'wave' (gently rocking the tub, or pouring water past it) to compare stability.</li><li>Discuss: using your own test results, explain in your own words why the double-hulled design resisted tipping better.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Exit Ticket</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Write one sentence explaining, using your own test results, why a double-hulled design is more stable than a single-hulled one.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Faith Connection</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Ponder: Every lashing on a waka, every curve of the hull, required accumulated wisdom passed down through generations. This is the kind of wisdom Proverbs describes — practical, careful, grounded in experience. God values and gives this kind of knowledge.</li><li>Verse: &quot;Blessed are those who find wisdom, those who gain understanding, for she is more profitable than silver and yields better returns than gold.&quot; — <em>Proverbs 3:13-14 (NIV)</em></li><li>Value: Manaaki  Serve — Developing skill and applying it carefully to build something that serves others faithfully is at the heart of Manaaki.</li><li>Discussion questions:<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>Proverbs says wisdom is worth more than silver or gold — do you believe that? Why or why not?</li><li>How is the wisdom to build something physical similar to the wisdom needed to make good decisions in life?</li><li>What skill are you developing right now that you might one day use to serve others?</li></ul></li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Straws, foil, card, tape (per team)</li><li>Tubs or basins of water</li><li>Paper for written explanations</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Suggested Videos</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>What is a waka hourua?</em> — search: &quot;waka hourua double-hulled voyaging canoe explained&quot;. Provides real visual and factual context for the vessel design studied in this lesson.</li><li><em>Hōkūleʻa voyaging canoe story</em> — search: &quot;Hokulea voyaging canoe Hawaii Tahiti traditional navigation&quot;. Connects the lesson to a genuine, well-documented modern voyage that proved traditional design and navigation worked.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Student Agency Choices</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Art: Design a Labelled Waka Hourua</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Create a detailed, labelled drawing of a waka hourua, showing both hulls, the connecting crossbeams, and the deck.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Instructions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Draw the vessel from above and from the side.</li><li>Label at least 4 parts.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Which labelled part do you think mattered most for surviving a long voyage?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Read/Write: The Hōkūleʻa Story</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Research (or use today's video) the real story of the Hōkūleʻa voyage and write a short recount of what happened and why it mattered.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Word Count Guide</th><td class=\"lt-td\">150–200 words.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Why do you think this voyage was considered such a big deal when it happened?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Technology: Digital Stability Comparison</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Create a simple digital chart or table comparing your single-hull and double-hull test results (e.g. how many &quot;waves&quot; each survived before tipping).</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Did your results match what you expected before testing?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Watch &amp; Review: Watch and Note: The Waka Hourua</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Watch a short video about waka hourua or the Hōkūleʻa voyage.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Search term</th><td class=\"lt-td\">&quot;waka hourua double-hulled voyaging canoe explained&quot;</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">What real detail from the video surprised you most?</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Friday — Art: Trial 10: Weaving the Sail":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">PAPER STRIP WEAVING — Pandanus Sail Patterns in a Quilt Square</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">The Arts (Visual Art)</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Achievement Objectives</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Developing Practical Knowledge: explore a variety of materials and processes, discovering how they can be used to make art works.</li><li>Communicating and Interpreting: share the ideas, feelings, and stories communicated by their own and others' art works.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Key Competencies</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Thinking; Managing Self; Relating to Others</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning that Pacific sail-weaving uses the same over-under rhythm as all weaving traditions worldwide.</li><li>We are practising the over-under weaving technique to create a geometric woven grid on a square.</li><li>I can explain how my woven square connects to the voyage story.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Completed woven square and written reflection connecting weaving to the voyage.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Introduction</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Show a photo of a real pandanus woven sail panel. Ask: &quot;How was this made — not sewn, not glued. What do you notice?&quot; After responses, introduce the over-under weaving technique. Roa, Tama's mother, is a weaver — today students work as she does.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Main Content</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Preparing the Materials (5 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Each student takes one A4 square base sheet and a second sheet to cut into strips approx 1–1.5cm wide.</li><li>Teacher demonstrates: make evenly-spaced slits across the base sheet stopping 2cm from each edge to create the warp.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Weaving the Square (15 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Students weave their strips over-and-under through the slits in alternating rows.</li><li>Teacher prompt: &quot;Keep your strips tight and straight — a sail that sags won't catch the wind.&quot;</li><li>When complete, tuck or tape the ends on the back. The result is a tight grid of horizontal and vertical strips.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Reflection and Quilt Assembly (10 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Write 2–3 sentences: what does the over-under rhythm of weaving remind you of from this week's voyage? How is patience in weaving similar to patience at sea?</li><li>Glue the woven square to the group's quilt backing, next to Week 1's square.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Exit Ticket</th><td class=\"lt-td\">One sentence: what would break down first on a real woven sail — and what would that mean for the voyage?</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Faith Connection</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Ponder: Every strand in a woven sail depends on every other strand — pull one out and the whole thing weakens. A sail is a picture of community: each person essential, each contribution holding the rest together. God designed us to work this way.</li><li>Verse: &quot;Two are better than one, because they have a good return for their labour: if either of them falls down, one can help the other up.&quot; — <em>Ecclesiastes 4:9-10 (NIV)</em></li><li>Value: Manaaki  Serve — Faithfully contributing your part to a shared work — like a strand in a woven sail — is how we serve together in God's way.</li><li>Discussion questions:<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>When you wove your paper strips, what happened if one strand went wrong? How is that like a community or team?</li><li>'Two are better than one' — what's something you've achieved with others that you could never have done alone?</li><li>How does working faithfully on something small — like a single woven strip — contribute to something much bigger?</li></ul></li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A4 paper squares (2 per student)</li><li>Rulers for cutting straight strips</li><li>Tape or glue for securing ends</li><li>Group quilt backing sheets</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Suggested Videos</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Traditional Pacific pandanus weaving</em> — search: &quot;Pacific pandanus weaving traditional technique mat sail&quot;. Shows the real material and technique students are referencing.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Student Agency Choices</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Art: Weave Your Pandanus Sail Square</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This week's main activity IS the art focus — weave your paper strip square and add it to your group's quilt.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">What surprised you about how the weaving held itself together?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Read/Write: Roa's Hands</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write a short scene (80–120 words) from Roa's perspective as she weaves a sail panel the night before departure.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">What details about the weaving process made the scene feel real?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Technology: Digital Weave Pattern</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Use a grid-based drawing tool to design a woven pattern digitally, alternating two colours in the over-under sequence.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">What was different about planning a weave on screen versus by hand?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Watch &amp; Review: Watch: Pacific Weaving Traditions</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Watch a short video on Pacific weaving traditions.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Search term</th><td class=\"lt-td\">&quot;Pacific pandanus weaving traditional technique mat sail&quot;</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">What did the weaver's hands do that surprised you?</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"}}},"Week 3":{"label":"Day","children":{"Monday — History: Trial 11: Leaving Sight of Land":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">COURAGE AND PROVISIONS — What It Really Took to Cross the Ocean</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Social Sciences</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Achievement Objectives</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Understanding the Past: investigate the relationship between people and significant historical events, and how people responded.</li><li>Understanding Place and Environment: explore how people's wellbeing is connected to their access to resources.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Key Competencies</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Thinking; Relating to Others; Managing Self</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning what real Pacific voyaging canoes carried, and why those choices mattered for survival.</li><li>We are learning to understand the real emotional and physical challenge of losing sight of land.</li><li>I can connect a historical moment to a real human experience of courage.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Teacher observation during the 'last sight of land' reflection and the trust walk. Review of students' captain's orders.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Introduction</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&quot;For two weeks we've prepared — we know the scale of the ocean, the currents, the winds, and the double-hulled vessel strong enough to survive it. Today we face the moment historians believe was often the hardest of the entire voyage: the last sight of land disappearing behind you.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Wonder question: what do you think would be harder — the physical danger of the ocean, or the feeling of having no land in sight for weeks at a time?&quot;</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Main Content</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">What a Real Voyaging Canoe Carried (10 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Teacher Input: Voyaging canoes weren't just carrying people — historians and archaeologists believe they carried entire futures. Real voyaging canoes are believed to have carried food crops like kūmara (sweet potato) and other plants, intended to be planted at any new land found, alongside dried food and water stored in gourds or coconut shells for the journey itself.</li><li>Some voyages are believed to have also carried animals such as dogs, pigs, or chickens — meaning a successful voyage wasn't just about survival at sea, but about being able to start an entirely new life upon arrival.</li><li>Key Question: &quot;If you could only bring a limited number of things to start a brand-new life somewhere unknown, what would actually be worth the space on board?&quot;</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">The Real Challenge of Losing Sight of Land (10 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Teacher Input: Historians and psychologists studying voyaging cultures note that losing sight of land was both a practical and an emotional turning point — practically, because all navigation now depended entirely on sky, sea and memory; emotionally, because there was no longer any visible link back to everything familiar.</li><li>Key Question: &quot;Why might a voyage's danger have been as much about the mind as it was about the body?&quot;</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Stepping Into the Moment (15 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Activity: Quietly narrate the last sight of land disappearing behind the voyage. In pairs, students share (low-stakes) a time they had to be brave doing something unfamiliar.</li><li>Activity: Run a blindfolded 'trust walk' in pairs across a short, safe course, mirroring the trust a voyager had to place in their crew and their preparation once land was gone.</li><li>Activity: In small groups, write a short 'captain's order' — a sentence a voyage leader might say to keep the crew's courage up at this exact moment.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Exit Ticket</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Write one sentence: what is ONE item historians believe real voyaging canoes carried, and why would it have mattered?</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Faith Connection</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Ponder: The moment land disappears behind you is one of the most frightening — and most freeing — moments of any voyage. Tama's hand is shaking as he writes. But God's promise in Jeremiah was written for exactly this moment: a future we can't see yet, held by a God who can.</li><li>Verse: &quot;&quot;For I know the plans I have for you,&quot; declares the LORD, &quot;plans to prosper you and not to harm you, plans to give you hope and a future.&quot;&quot; — <em>Jeremiah 29:11 (NIV)</em></li><li>Value: Aroha  Love — God's love for us is shown in the plans He holds for our future — even when we cannot see the destination from where we stand.</li><li>Discussion questions:<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>Tama can't see land behind him or land ahead. Have you ever been in a situation where you couldn't see the way forward? What helped?</li><li>God says His plans are 'to give you hope and a future.' How does it change things to know that Someone knows what's ahead, even when you don't?</li><li>How do you hold on to hope when you're in the 'middle of the ocean' — far from what was familiar and not yet at what's next?</li></ul></li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Blindfolds for the trust walk</li><li>A short, clear, safe course for the activity</li><li>Paper for captain's orders</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Suggested Videos</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>What did voyaging canoes carry?</em> — search: &quot;what did Polynesian voyaging canoes carry provisions&quot;. Provides real, age-appropriate detail on what voyaging canoes are believed to have carried.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Student Agency Choices</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Art: Illustrate the Loaded Canoe</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Draw a cross-section or &quot;cutaway&quot; view of a voyaging canoe, showing what real historians believe it would have carried.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Instructions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Include people, food crops, water storage, and one animal.</li><li>Label each item.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">What surprised you about how much had to be planned for, beyond just the people?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Read/Write: The Last Sight of Land</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write a short personal reflection, in role as a voyager, describing the exact moment land disappears from view.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Word Count Guide</th><td class=\"lt-td\">120–180 words.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">What emotion did you choose to focus on, and why?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Technology: Build a Provisions Packing List</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Create a simple digital checklist or table of everything a real voyaging canoe is believed to have carried, with a one-line reason for each item.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">If you had to remove one item to save space, which would you cut, and why?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Watch &amp; Review: Watch and Note: Voyaging Provisions</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Watch a short video on what voyaging canoes carried.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Search term</th><td class=\"lt-td\">&quot;what did Polynesian voyaging canoes carry provisions&quot;</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">What item did the video mention that you hadn't thought of?</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Tuesday — Geography: Trial 12: What the Ocean's Surface Tells You":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">WAVE PILOTING — Reading the Real Signs of Land</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Social Sciences (Geography)</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Achievement Objectives</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Understanding Place and Environment: explore how people use their understanding of natural features and patterns to make decisions.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Key Competencies</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Thinking; Using Language, Symbols &amp; Text</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning real techniques voyagers used to detect land long before it was visible.</li><li>We are learning how swells, birds, and floating debris each provided different real clues.</li><li>I can match a type of evidence to the conclusion it supports.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Teacher observation of the detective-style clue-matching activity and the &quot;report to the captain&quot; presentations.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Introduction</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&quot;Yesterday we faced the moment land disappeared. Today, we learn one of the most genuinely impressive real skills in human history: how voyagers could sense land was near long before they could see it, just by reading the ocean's surface.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Wonder question: how do you think someone could possibly know land was nearby, without seeing it?&quot;</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Main Content</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Wave Piloting — A Real, Documented Skill (12 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Teacher Input: In real Pacific navigation traditions — including ones still taught today in places like the Marshall Islands — skilled navigators learned to feel and read subtle changes in ocean swells. When swells hit an island, they bend and reflect off it in patterns that can be felt by an experienced navigator lying in the hull of a canoe, sometimes long before the island is visible.</li><li>Key Question: &quot;Why might FEELING a wave pattern be a skill that takes years to learn, compared to simply looking for land?&quot;</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Birds and Floating Clues (10 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Teacher Input: Certain real seabirds, like frigatebirds, generally don't land on the ocean's surface — so spotting one far out at sea is a genuine clue that land must be within flying range. Many seabirds also reliably return to land at dusk, meaning their direction of flight at the end of the day could point a voyager toward an island.</li><li>Floating plant matter, particular leaves, or changes in water colour near reefs were also real, used clues that land was close.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Detective Challenge: Match the Clue (15 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Activity: Set up a detective-style station challenge with scattered clue cards showing real wayfinding signs (a bird far from land, drifting plant matter, a swell pattern bending around an unseen island). In teams, match each clue to what it would tell a real voyager.</li><li>Activity: Teams present their best clue-match as a short 'report to the captain' — what does the evidence suggest right now?</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Exit Ticket</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Write one sentence naming ONE real clue voyagers used to detect land before seeing it.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Faith Connection</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Ponder: Jesus challenged the Pharisees for being able to read the weather but unable to read what God was doing right in front of them. The voyagers were extraordinary readers of the ocean's signs. God invites us to be readers of His signs too — attentive, curious, and awake.</li><li>Verse: &quot;You know how to interpret the appearance of the sky, but you cannot interpret the signs of the times.&quot; — <em>Matthew 16:3 (NIV)</em></li><li>Value: Mōhio  Know — Knowing God means learning to read what He is doing in the world — in nature, in history, and in our own lives.</li><li>Discussion questions:<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>The voyagers read the ocean's surface for signs of land. What 'signs' in your daily life might God be using to say something to you?</li><li>Jesus said the Pharisees could read the sky but missed what God was doing. What might you be missing that God is doing right now?</li><li>How do you practise being more attentive to what God might be saying?</li></ul></li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Clue cards: bird behaviour, floating debris, swell patterns</li><li>Simple Pacific map for context</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Suggested Videos</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Wave piloting — reading ocean swells</em> — search: &quot;wave piloting Marshall Islands navigation swells&quot;. Documents the real, still-taught navigation technique referenced in this lesson.</li><li><em>How birds helped Pacific navigators find land</em> — search: &quot;birds signs of land Pacific navigation&quot;. Reinforces the real bird-behaviour clues discussed in class.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Student Agency Choices</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Art: Illustrate a Wave Pattern Bending Around an Island</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Draw a simple diagram showing how ocean swells bend and reflect when they hit an island.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Instructions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Draw an island and incoming straight swell lines.</li><li>Show how the lines bend around the island.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Why would this pattern be detectable even before the island is visible?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Read/Write: A Navigator's Report</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write a short &quot;report to the captain&quot; in role as a navigator who has just noticed a real clue (bird, debris, or swell pattern) suggesting land is near.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Word Count Guide</th><td class=\"lt-td\">100–150 words.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">How confident would you really feel acting on just one clue?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Technology: Build a Clue-Matching Digital Quiz</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Create a simple digital matching activity (Google Slides, Canva) pairing each real clue (bird, debris, swell) with what it tells a voyager.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Which clue do you think would be hardest to learn to read well?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Watch &amp; Review: Watch and Note: Reading the Ocean</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Watch a short video on wave piloting or bird-based navigation.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Search term</th><td class=\"lt-td\">&quot;wave piloting Marshall Islands navigation swells&quot;</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">What part of this skill most impressed you?</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Wednesday — Science: Trial 13: Cloud Code":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">READING CLOUDS — A Real Trick for Finding Islands Before You See Them</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Science (Planet Earth and Beyond)</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Achievement Objectives</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Investigate weather phenomena and how they affect people and the environment.</li><li>Build science knowledge by exploring observations and comparing these with current scientific understanding.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Key Competencies</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Thinking; Participating &amp; Contributing</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning to identify basic cloud types and what each can indicate.</li><li>We are learning a real technique voyagers used: spotting a stationary cloud cap to find an island before seeing land.</li><li>I can make and justify a simple weather prediction using real observation.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Teacher observation of outdoor cloud observation and the class's 'voyage forecast' predictions.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Introduction</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&quot;Yesterday we learned how to read the sea's surface. Today we look up again — because clouds hold a genuinely clever real secret that helped voyagers find islands long before they could see them.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Wonder question: have you ever noticed a cloud that seems to just sit in one place, while others move past it?&quot;</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Main Content</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Basic Cloud Types (10 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Teacher Input: Introduce three simple cloud types — cumulus (puffy, often fair weather), cumulonimbus (tall, dark, often storm-bearing), and cirrus (thin, wispy, high up, often signalling a change in weather coming).</li><li>Show real images of each type for students to identify.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">The Real Island Cloud Trick (12 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Teacher Input: Here's a genuinely real and clever navigation trick — islands, especially ones with mountains, often cause a single cloud to form and sit almost stationary directly above them, because rising warm air off the land cools and condenses at a consistent point. A trained voyager could sometimes spot this 'island cloud' on the horizon well before the island itself came into view.</li><li>Key Question: &quot;Why would a cloud that doesn't move, while others around it drift past, be a genuinely useful clue?&quot;</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Real Sky Observation (8 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Activity: Take the class outside to observe real clouds, identifying any of today's three types and noting whether any appear to be staying in one place rather than moving.</li><li>Activity: In groups, use today's observation to make and justify a real 'voyage forecast' prediction.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Exit Ticket</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Write one sentence explaining the real &quot;island cloud&quot; trick discussed today.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Faith Connection</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Ponder: The cloud code the voyagers read was God's weather system — a complex, interconnected design that brings rain to mountains, signals danger to sailors, and tells stories to those who know how to look. Every cloud type is part of a system God designed and still maintains.</li><li>Verse: &quot;He covers the sky with clouds; he supplies the earth with rain and makes grass grow on the hills.&quot; — <em>Psalm 147:8 (NIV)</em></li><li>Value: Mōhio  Know — Understanding how God's creation works — from weather patterns to cloud types — deepens our knowledge of the wise Creator behind it all.</li><li>Discussion questions:<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>Psalm 147 says God 'covers the sky with clouds' — does knowing God made the weather system change how you look at clouds? How?</li><li>Hina taught Tama to read clouds like a letter. What 'language' do you think God uses most often to communicate with you?</li><li>Why do you think God designed a world that rewards careful observation and patient learning?</li></ul></li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Reference images of cumulus, cumulonimbus, and cirrus clouds</li><li>Paper for voyage forecast logs</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Suggested Videos</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Cloud types explained for kids</em> — search: &quot;cloud types explained for kids cumulus cirrus cumulonimbus&quot;. Gives a clear, age-appropriate overview of the basic cloud types covered in this lesson.</li><li><em>How clouds reveal hidden islands</em> — search: &quot;how clouds form over islands navigation clue&quot;. Explains the real, documented phenomenon central to this lesson's key navigation trick.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Student Agency Choices</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Art: Illustrate an Island Cloud</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Draw an island with its real &quot;island cloud&quot; forming above it, while other clouds drift past in the background.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Instructions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Draw the island and its stationary cloud.</li><li>Draw 2–3 other clouds clearly moving in a different direction.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">What did you do in your drawing to show one cloud is &quot;stuck&quot; while others move?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Read/Write: A Voyager Spots the Sign</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write a short story moment, in role as a voyager, describing the instant you notice a stationary cloud and realise it might mean land.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Word Count Guide</th><td class=\"lt-td\">100–150 words.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">How would you feel in that exact moment, after weeks without land?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Technology: Build a Cloud Identification Guide</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Create a simple digital reference guide (Canva, Google Slides) with an image and short description of each cloud type from today's lesson.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Which cloud type do you think would be easiest to misidentify, and why?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Watch &amp; Review: Watch and Note: Clouds and Islands</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Watch a short video on cloud types or the island-cloud phenomenon.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Search term</th><td class=\"lt-td\">&quot;how clouds form over islands navigation clue&quot;</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">What detail from the video would you tell a friend who didn't believe this trick was real?</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Thursday — Technology: Trial 14: Catch the Wind":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">THE CRAB CLAW SAIL — A Real, Remarkably Efficient Design</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Technology</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Achievement Objectives</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Technological Knowledge: understand how the shape and properties of materials affect the performance of a product.</li><li>Nature of Technology: understand that technological outcomes are evaluated and improved through testing.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Key Competencies</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Thinking; Managing Self</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning how sail shape and angle affect how efficiently a vessel catches wind.</li><li>We are learning about the real 'crab claw' sail design used across the Pacific.</li><li>I can test and improve a sail design using real engineering reasoning.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Teacher observation of the sail-testing and redesign process. Review of written explanations connecting shape to performance.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Introduction</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&quot;We've built a stable double-hulled vessel and learned to read sea, sky, and clouds for clues. Today we focus on the part that actually moves the whole vessel forward: the sail. And it turns out, Pacific sailors developed one of the most efficient sail shapes in the whole of human sailing history.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Wonder question: do you think a sail's shape actually matters, or is any large piece of material just as good at catching wind?&quot;</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Main Content</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">The Real Crab Claw Sail (10 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Teacher Input: Many traditional Pacific sailing vessels used a distinctive triangular sail shape, sometimes called a 'crab claw' sail because of its curved, claw-like silhouette. Modern sailing scientists studying traditional designs have found this shape to be genuinely, remarkably efficient at generating forward motion from the wind — among the most aerodynamically effective traditional sail shapes ever studied.</li><li>Key Question: &quot;Why might a society without modern science have still arrived at such an efficient design, just through generations of trial and observation?&quot;</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Shape, Angle, and Wind (10 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Teacher Input: A sail works by being angled so wind flowing across its surface creates forward force, similar to how an aeroplane wing generates lift. The exact angle and curve of a sail changes how efficiently it captures that force.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Test, Redesign, Improve (15 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Activity: In teams, design and test a simple sail (paper or fabric on a small mast) attached to a model vessel, testing in front of a fan or moving air to find the most effective shape and angle.</li><li>Activity: After the first test, redesign and re-test at least once, just as real sail-makers improved their designs over generations.</li><li>Key Question during testing: &quot;What specifically changed between your first and second design, and why did you expect it to help?&quot;</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Exit Ticket</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Write one sentence explaining why sail shape and angle affect how well a sail catches wind.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Faith Connection</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Ponder: A sail doesn't fight the wind — it trusts it. A sail trimmed correctly uses the wind's power to go places no amount of paddling could reach. Isaiah's image of soaring on wings is about the same kind of trust: not straining against the circumstances, but finding the right angle to let God's strength carry you.</li><li>Verse: &quot;Those who hope in the LORD will renew their strength. They will soar on wings like eagles; they will run and not grow weary, they will walk and not be faint.&quot; — <em>Isaiah 40:31 (NIV)</em></li><li>Value: Pānga  Impact — When we align our gifts with God's purposes — like a sail catching the wind — we have far greater impact than we could ever achieve alone.</li><li>Discussion questions:<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>A sail doesn't generate power — it harnesses what's already there. How might that be a picture of how God wants us to work?</li><li>'Soar on wings like eagles' — what does it feel like when everything is working the way it should, and you're moving with God's strength rather than just your own?</li><li>How do you 'trim your sail' — position yourself to catch God's strength rather than relying only on your own effort?</li></ul></li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Paper or light fabric, small masts/sticks, tape</li><li>A fan or open space with moving air for testing</li><li>Model vessels from Week 1/2 (optional, can reuse)</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Suggested Videos</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>The crab claw sail explained</em> — search: &quot;crab claw sail Pacific Polynesian efficient design&quot;. Documents the real, scientifically-studied sail design referenced in this lesson.</li><li><em>How sails work</em> — search: &quot;how sails work wind science explained&quot;. Gives a clear, age-appropriate explanation of the basic physics behind sailing.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Student Agency Choices</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Art: Design a Decorated Crab Claw Sail</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Draw a labelled crab claw sail design, decorated with a pattern that represents your &quot;voyage&quot; so far.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Instructions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Draw the distinctive curved triangular shape.</li><li>Add a decorative pattern.</li><li>Label the parts that catch the wind.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Why do you think this curved shape might work better than a flat rectangle?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Read/Write: A Sail-Maker's Notes</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write a short set of &quot;notes,&quot; in role as a real sail-maker, describing what you changed between your first and second sail design and why.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Word Count Guide</th><td class=\"lt-td\">120–180 words.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">How is this similar to how real inventors improve their designs over time?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Technology: Digital Sail Design Comparison</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Create a simple digital chart comparing your two sail test results (e.g. speed, stability, how well it caught the wind).</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Did your redesign perform the way you expected?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Watch &amp; Review: Watch and Note: The Crab Claw Sail</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Watch a short video about the crab claw sail or how sails work.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Search term</th><td class=\"lt-td\">&quot;crab claw sail Pacific Polynesian efficient design&quot;</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">What surprised you about how effective this traditional design really is?</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Friday — Art: Trial 15: Reading the Patterns":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">KŌWHAIWHAI DESIGN STUDY — Wave Scrolls, Colour &amp; Meaning</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">The Arts (Visual Art)</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Achievement Objectives</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Developing Practical Knowledge: explore the visual qualities of art works from a range of cultures and periods.</li><li>Communicating and Interpreting: identify and describe ways in which meanings are communicated through art works.</li><li>Understanding the Arts in Context: investigate the purpose of objects and images from past and present cultures.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Key Competencies</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Thinking; Relating to Others; Using Language, Symbols &amp; Text</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning to read and interpret kōwhaiwhai patterns as a visual language used by Māori artists.</li><li>We are understanding how colour (black, red/kōkōwhai, white) and repeating motifs create rhythm and meaning.</li><li>I can design my own wave-pattern panel inspired by kōwhaiwhai, and explain the choices I made.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Teacher observation of pattern study and student written reflection explaining motif choices and personal connection to the patterns.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Introduction</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Display the archival kōwhaiwhai reference image (Tikitiki Church, Arawa Rotorua, and Tikitiki panels). Give students 2 minutes to simply look — no talking — before asking: &quot;What do you notice about the colours? The shapes? Do you see any shapes repeating?&quot; Take 3–4 responses, then explain: &quot;These patterns are a language. Every scroll, every chevron tells a story about the ocean, about movement, about who these people were. Today we're going to learn to read that language — and then speak a little of it ourselves.&quot;</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Main Content</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Reading the Patterns Together (10 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Teacher Input: introduce the three key kōwhaiwhai patterns visible in the archival image — (1) the koru/scroll (a curling fern frond representing new life, growth, and the ocean returning on itself); (2) the chevron/arrow pointing forward (direction, courage, the voyage ahead); (3) the repeating band structure (the rhythm of the sea, one wave always following another).</li><li>Discuss: Māori artists traditionally used only three colours — black (charcoal), red/kōkōwhai (red ochre pigment), and white (pipe clay). Why might these specific colours have been chosen? What do they suggest? (Black: night sky, the deep ocean; Red: blood, life, fire, courage; White: the stars, seafoam, light.)</li><li>Point out: in the Arawa Rotorua panel (middle band), the scroll reverses direction as it repeats — ask students: what might that reversal represent? (The wave going out and coming back. The voyage leaving home and returning.)</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Guided Design Sketch (8 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Activity: give each student a landscape strip of paper (roughly A5 size, or half an A4). Students choose ONE motif from the reference image to practise — either the scroll/koru or the chevron — and repeat it across the strip as a border pattern, using pencil first.</li><li>Teacher circulates: the key question to prompt is 'What does your pattern look like it's doing?' — is it moving, breathing, surging? Help students feel the rhythm rather than just copy mechanically.</li><li>Encourage students to vary the size of repeats slightly — Māori artists were not rigid; the pattern breathes.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Adding Colour and Reflection (12 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Activity: students colour their pattern using only the three traditional colours — black, red, and white — using felt tips, dye, or paint as available. Remind them: the background matters as much as the motif.</li><li>Activity: in their journal or on the back of the strip, students write 2–3 sentences: what pattern they chose, what it reminds them of from this week's voyage, and what they think the original artist might have felt making it.</li><li>Optional extension: students may add a personal touch — a shape or line that is theirs — while still honouring the pattern's structure.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Exit Ticket</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Share one sentence with the class: &quot;My pattern is saying ____<strong> because </strong>____.&quot;</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Faith Connection</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Ponder: The kōwhaiwhai scroll is a pattern that keeps returning — the wave curls back on itself, the spiral never truly ends. Paul's words about Christ echo this rhythm: all things hold together in Him. Even when the wave breaks, even when the scroll reaches its edge, it begins again.</li><li>Verse: &quot;He is before all things, and in him all things hold together.&quot; — <em>Colossians 1:17 (NIV)</em></li><li>Value: Pānga  Impact — Art that carries meaning — like the kōwhaiwhai scroll — brings God's Kingdom creativity into the world and points to the One who holds all things together.</li><li>Discussion questions:<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>The kōwhaiwhai wave reverses direction and returns — 'going out and coming back.' How might that be a picture of something in your own faith journey?</li><li>If 'in him all things hold together,' what does that mean for the parts of your life that feel like they might be falling apart?</li><li>How can the art you make today be an act of worship?</li></ul></li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Printed or projected archival kōwhaiwhai reference image (Tikitiki Church / Arawa Rotorua panels)</li><li>A5 strips of white cartridge paper (or half A4), one per student</li><li>Pencils for sketching</li><li>Black, red, and white felt tips, paint, or dye</li><li>Student journals for written reflection</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Suggested Videos</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Introduction to Kōwhaiwhai Patterns</em> — search: &quot;kowhaiwhai patterns Maori art meaning for kids&quot;. Provides accessible context for students on the meaning and tradition behind kōwhaiwhai, supporting the pattern-reading discussion at the start of the lesson.</li><li><em>Māori Rafter Painting Technique</em> — search: &quot;Maori rafter painting kowhaiwhai technique traditional&quot;. Shows how kōwhaiwhai patterns are applied in real wharenui contexts, helping students understand the patterns as living tradition rather than historical artefact.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Student Agency Choices</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Art: Design Your Kōwhaiwhai Panel</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This week's main activity IS the art focus — study the archival reference, choose your motif, and design your own kōwhaiwhai wave-pattern strip using the three traditional colours.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">What does your pattern look like it's doing — moving, surging, breathing? How did you make that happen?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Read/Write: The Language of Patterns</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write a short piece (100–150 words) imagining you are the Māori artist who created the Arawa Rotorua panel. Why did you choose the scroll pattern? What were you trying to say about the ocean or the voyage?</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">How is designing a pattern similar to writing a sentence?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Technology: Digital Kōwhaiwhai</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Use a simple drawing tool to recreate one kōwhaiwhai motif digitally, then repeat it to form a border pattern. Use only black, red, and white.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">What was harder to control digitally — the shape, the spacing, or the colour? Why?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Watch &amp; Review: Watch and Note: Kōwhaiwhai Meaning</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Watch a short video on kōwhaiwhai patterns and their meaning in Māori art.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Search term</th><td class=\"lt-td\">&quot;kowhaiwhai patterns Maori art meaning for kids&quot;</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">What surprised you most about what these patterns represent?</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"}}},"Week 4":{"label":"Day","children":{"Monday — History: Trial 16: Lost Without Land":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">WAYFINDING — A Real Skill Trained Over a Lifetime</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Social Sciences</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Achievement Objectives</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Understanding the Past: investigate how a significant person or group has influenced the course of events.</li><li>Understanding the Past: understand how the perspectives of people in the past were shaped by the time and place in which they lived.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Key Competencies</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Thinking; Language, Symbols &amp; Text</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning what wayfinding really is, and why it took years to master.</li><li>We are learning about a real master navigator who helped revive this skill in modern times.</li><li>We are learning a genuinely real navigational idea called 'etak.'</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Teacher observation of the memory-sequence game and class discussion. Review of students' written reflections.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Introduction</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&quot;Over the last three weeks, we've learned to read currents, wind, swells, birds, and clouds. Today we bring it all together under one real word: wayfinding. This wasn't a single trick — it was an entire skill, trained over years, sometimes decades, and the knowledge was almost lost in the 20th century before being deliberately revived.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Wonder question: do you think you could memorise enough information to navigate across an entire ocean, with no instruments at all?&quot;</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Main Content</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">A Real Master Navigator (12 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Teacher Input: A real and important figure in this story is Mau Piailug, a master navigator from the Micronesian island of Satawal, born in 1932. He had learned traditional wayfinding from his elders using methods passed down for generations — at a time when this knowledge was disappearing across much of the Pacific.</li><li>In 1976, Mau Piailug navigated the double-hulled canoe Hōkūleʻa from Hawaii to Tahiti using only traditional wayfinding — no instruments at all — successfully proving the ancient method still worked, and helping spark a real revival of Pacific navigation traditions that continues today.</li><li>Key Question: &quot;Why do you think it mattered so much to prove this method still worked, rather than just writing down what was remembered about it?&quot;</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">A Real Navigational Idea: Etak (10 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Teacher Input: introduce a genuinely fascinating real concept used by traditional Micronesian/Carolinian navigators called etak. Rather than thinking of the canoe as moving toward a fixed island, navigators using etak imagined the canoe as staying still, while islands 'moved' past it in relation to reference stars. This mental framework helped navigators track progress without any instruments.</li><li>Key Question: &quot;Why might imagining yourself as 'staying still' actually make it easier to track a long journey, rather than imagining yourself constantly moving?&quot;</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Training the Memory (10 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Activity: Run a memory-sequence game — show a sequence of symbols (representing a 'star path') briefly, then hide it. Teams must recall and recreate the sequence accurately, just as real wayfinders trained their memory over years through repetition.</li><li>Discuss: why might memorising something be safer at sea than relying on a written chart that could be lost or ruined by water?</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Exit Ticket</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Write one sentence: who was Mau Piailug, and why does his 1976 voyage matter to this story?</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Faith Connection</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Ponder: After the storm, Tama doesn't know where he is. The stars are hidden. The ocean gives no clues. This is the feeling Psalm 119 was written for — the moment when you can't see the way forward and you need something beyond yourself to light the path. God's word is that lamp.</li><li>Verse: &quot;Your word is a lamp to my feet and a light to my path.&quot; — <em>Psalm 119:105 (NIV)</em></li><li>Value: Mōhio  Know — Knowing God and His word gives us a light to navigate by, even when everything around us is dark and disorienting.</li><li>Discussion questions:<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>Have you ever been in a situation where you genuinely didn't know what to do next? What helped you find direction?</li><li>A lamp only lights 'your feet' and 'your path' — not the whole landscape. What does it mean to trust God one step at a time?</li><li>How do you practically use God's word as a 'lamp' in your daily life?</li></ul></li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Symbol/sequence cards for the memory game</li><li>A simple timer for the memory challenge</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Suggested Videos</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Mau Piailug and the Hōkūleʻa voyage</em> — search: &quot;Mau Piailug master navigator Hokulea voyage&quot;. Documents the real historical figure and voyage central to this lesson.</li><li><em>What is etak navigation?</em> — search: &quot;etak navigation concept explained Micronesia&quot;. Explains the real, genuinely clever navigational concept introduced in this lesson.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Student Agency Choices</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Art: Illustrate Mau Piailug's Voyage</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Draw a scene depicting the 1976 Hōkūleʻa voyage, showing the double-hulled canoe and the open ocean it crossed.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Instructions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Include the canoe and a simple route from Hawaii to Tahiti.</li><li>Add a small label noting the real navigator's name.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Why do you think this moment in history is worth illustrating and remembering?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Read/Write: A Tribute to a Master Navigator</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write a short tribute or biography paragraph about Mau Piailug and why his knowledge mattered.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Word Count Guide</th><td class=\"lt-td\">120–180 words.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">What real skill of his impressed you most?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Technology: Build an Etak Diagram</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Use a simple drawing/slides tool to create a diagram explaining the etak concept — the canoe staying &quot;still&quot; while islands move past it.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">How did making the diagram change your understanding of the idea?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Watch &amp; Review: Watch and Note: A Real Master Navigator</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Watch a short video about Mau Piailug or the Hōkūleʻa voyage.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Search term</th><td class=\"lt-td\">&quot;Mau Piailug master navigator Hokulea voyage&quot;</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">What fact from the video would you want to share with someone who'd never heard this story?</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Tuesday — Geography: Trial 17: The Star Compass":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">NAVIGATING BY STARS — Real Names, Real Sky Positions</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Social Sciences (Geography)</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Achievement Objectives</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Understanding Place and Environment: explore how people view and use the natural environment differently, including for navigation and orientation.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Key Competencies</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Thinking; Using Language, Symbols &amp; Text</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning what a star compass is and how it works.</li><li>We are learning real Pacific names and meanings for key stars and constellations.</li><li>I can use a star compass to determine direction on a map.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Teacher observation of the star compass navigation activity on the floor map.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Introduction</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&quot;Yesterday we met a real navigator who used the stars to cross an entire ocean. Today, we build the actual tool in his mind: the star compass — and we learn some of the real star names that mattered to Pacific voyagers.&quot;</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Main Content</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">What Is a Star Compass? (10 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Teacher Input: A star compass is a mental (or sometimes physical) wheel of direction points, each linked to where a particular star or constellation reliably rises or sets on the horizon. Because stars rise and set in almost the same spot every night, a trained navigator could use them like a giant, glowing set of compass points.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Real Stars, Real Names (12 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Teacher Input: introduce a few real, genuinely used star reference points from Pacific navigation traditions. Matariki (the Pleiades star cluster) is well known across the Pacific and in Māori tradition, often associated with the new year and remembrance. Te Matau a Māui (Maui's fishhook, part of the constellation Scorpius) is a star pattern named in Māori tradition after the legendary fishhook of Māui. The Southern Cross, known in Māori as Te Punga (the anchor), is another key reference point still used for finding south in the southern hemisphere sky today.</li><li>Key Question: &quot;Why might giving stars meaningful names — instead of just numbers or letters — have helped voyagers remember them more easily across generations?&quot;</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Building and Using a Star Compass (13 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Activity: Build a more detailed star compass wheel, marking key real stars/constellations (Matariki, Te Matau a Māui, Te Punga/Southern Cross) at points around it.</li><li>Activity: Use the star compass to 'navigate' a paper waka token across a star-marked floor map between islands, choosing direction based on real star positions rather than guessing.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Exit Ticket</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Write one sentence naming ONE real star or constellation used in Pacific navigation, and what it's called.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Faith Connection</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Ponder: The voyagers memorised hundreds of stars to navigate thousands of kilometres of open ocean. The same stars that Hina taught Tama to read are the 'work of God's fingers' — set in place deliberately, as a gift to navigators and a declaration of His greatness.</li><li>Verse: &quot;When I consider your heavens, the work of your fingers, the moon and the stars, which you have set in place, what is mankind that you are mindful of them, human beings that you care for them?&quot; — <em>Psalm 8:3-4 (NIV)</em></li><li>Value: Mōhio  Know — Looking at the stars and learning their names is an act of knowing God — who named every star and knows us each by name too.</li><li>Discussion questions:<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>The Psalmist looks at the stars and asks 'who am I that God cares about me?' — do you ever feel that way? What do you do with that feeling?</li><li>God named every star — Matariki, Te Punga, the Southern Cross — and God knows your name. What does that tell you about how God sees you?</li><li>How does learning about the vastness of creation make you feel about God — and about yourself?</li></ul></li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Paper, compasses (drawing), pencils for star compass craft</li><li>A star-marked floor/wall map for the navigation activity</li><li>Reference images of Matariki, Te Matau a Māui, and the Southern Cross if available</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Suggested Videos</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>What is a star compass?</em> — search: &quot;Polynesian star compass navigation explained&quot;. Gives a clear, age-appropriate explanation of the real tool central to this lesson.</li><li><em>Matariki and Māori star navigation</em> — search: &quot;Matariki Te Matau a Maui star navigation Maori&quot;. Introduces real, culturally significant star names relevant to this lesson.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Student Agency Choices</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Art: Illustrate the Real Star Names</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Draw a night sky scene featuring Matariki, Te Matau a Māui, and Te Punga (the Southern Cross), labelled with their real names and meanings.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Which of these star names did you find most interesting, and why?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Read/Write: Why Names Matter</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write a short paragraph explaining why giving stars meaningful names (rather than just numbers) might have helped voyagers remember and use them.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Word Count Guide</th><td class=\"lt-td\">100–150 words.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Can you think of a modern example where a meaningful name helps you remember something better than a number would?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Technology: Digital Star Compass</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Use a simple drawing tool to build a digital star compass wheel, labelling at least 3 real star reference points.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">What was the trickiest part of placing the points accurately?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Watch &amp; Review: Watch and Note: Star Navigation</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Watch a short video about the Polynesian star compass or Matariki.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Search term</th><td class=\"lt-td\">&quot;Polynesian star compass navigation explained&quot;</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">What real star name or fact from the video stuck with you most?</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Wednesday — Science: Trial 18: Why the Sky Changes":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">THE REAL REASON FOR SEASONS — It's Not About Distance from the Sun</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Science (Planet Earth and Beyond)</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Achievement Objectives</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Investigate the components of the solar system, including the Earth's place within it.</li><li>Build science knowledge by exploring and comparing observations with current scientific understanding.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Key Competencies</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Thinking; Participating &amp; Contributing</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning the real scientific reason seasons happen — and addressing a common misconception.</li><li>We are learning why the night sky changes with the seasons.</li><li>I can use a simple model to explain a real astronomical phenomenon.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Teacher observation of the seasons demonstration and group discussion. Review of seasonal sky wheels.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Introduction</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&quot;This week we've learned real star names voyagers relied on. But here's an important real science question: why do different stars appear at different times of the year at all? And here's a common misconception worth tackling directly: many people think seasons happen because Earth gets closer to or further from the Sun. That's actually not true — let's find out the real reason.&quot;</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Main Content</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Addressing the Misconception (8 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Teacher Input: Ask the class directly: &quot;Why do you think we have seasons?&quot; Many will likely suggest Earth moves closer to or further from the Sun. Explain clearly: this is a common misconception — Earth's distance from the Sun barely changes across the year, and it isn't the real cause of seasons.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">The Real Cause: Earth's Tilt (12 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Teacher Input: Earth is tilted on its axis by about 23.5 degrees, and this tilt stays pointed in roughly the same direction as Earth orbits the Sun across the year. This means different parts of Earth receive more or less direct sunlight at different times of year, depending on whether they're tilted toward or away from the Sun — and that is the real cause of seasons.</li><li>Demonstration: use a torch as the 'sun' and have a student slowly orbit it holding a tilted globe (or ball with a marked tilt), observing how light hits different latitudes more directly at different points in the orbit.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Why the Night Sky Changes Too (10 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Teacher Input: As Earth orbits the Sun across the year, the part of space we face at night gradually changes too — which is why different stars and constellations (like Matariki) become visible at different times of year.</li><li>Key Question: &quot;Why would a voyager need to know not just the season, but how the season changes which stars they could rely on at night?&quot;</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Exit Ticket</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Write one sentence correcting the common misconception about why seasons happen.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Faith Connection</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Ponder: The seasons change because God built them into the fabric of creation — the tilt of the earth, the orbit around the sun, the rhythms of warm and cold. God designed a world that rewards the patient learner who pays attention to His patterns.</li><li>Verse: &quot;It was you who set all the boundaries of the earth; you made both summer and winter.&quot; — <em>Psalm 74:17 (NIV)</em></li><li>Value: Mōhio  Know — Learning why the sky changes — and that God set those rhythms in place — deepens our knowledge of the Creator who built order into all things.</li><li>Discussion questions:<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>God 'made both summer and winter.' How do you experience God differently in different seasons — both in nature and in your own life?</li><li>The changing sky confused navigators until they understood God's pattern behind it. What confusing things in your life might make more sense once you understand God's pattern?</li><li>What does it say about God that He built reliable, repeating rhythms into creation?</li></ul></li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A torch or lamp (&quot;the sun&quot;)</li><li>A globe or ball marked with a visible tilt</li><li>Paper for seasonal sky wheels</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Suggested Videos</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Why do we have seasons? (the real reason)</em> — search: &quot;why do we have seasons axial tilt explained for kids&quot;. Directly addresses and corrects the common 'distance from the sun' misconception with the real scientific explanation.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Student Agency Choices</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Art: Illustrate Earth's Tilt Through the Year</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Draw Earth at four points in its orbit around the Sun, clearly showing the consistent tilt at each point.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Instructions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Draw the Sun in the centre.</li><li>Draw Earth at four positions around it, tilted the same direction each time.</li><li>Label which hemisphere is tilted toward the Sun at each point.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">What was the hardest part of showing the tilt staying consistent?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Read/Write: Correcting the Myth</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write a short, clear explanation (as if to a younger student) correcting the &quot;distance from the sun&quot; myth and explaining the real cause of seasons.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Word Count Guide</th><td class=\"lt-td\">100–150 words.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Why do you think this myth is so common, even though it isn't correct?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Technology: Build a Seasonal Star Wheel</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Create a simple digital or paper wheel showing which real stars (e.g. Matariki) are visible in each season.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">How does this wheel connect back to what voyagers needed to know?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Watch &amp; Review: Watch and Note: The Real Reason for Seasons</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Watch a short video explaining the real scientific cause of seasons.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Search term</th><td class=\"lt-td\">&quot;why do we have seasons axial tilt explained for kids&quot;</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Did the video change or confirm what you already believed about seasons?</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Thursday — Technology: Trial 19: Old Ways, New Tools":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">THE MAGNETIC COMPASS — A Real Invention, Compared to Real Wayfinding</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Technology</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Achievement Objectives</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Nature of Technology: understand that technological outcomes are products or systems developed to meet a need, and can be evaluated for fitness for purpose.</li><li>Technological Knowledge: understand how a technological device makes use of a natural phenomenon to function.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Key Competencies</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Thinking; Participating &amp; Contributing</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning what a magnetic compass is, how it works, and its real history.</li><li>We are learning to compare traditional wayfinding with magnetic compass navigation.</li><li>I can form and justify an opinion using evidence from both navigation methods.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Teacher observation of the compass-vs-wayfinding comparison challenge and class debate.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Introduction</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&quot;We've spent four weeks learning real wayfinding skills passed down across generations. Today we meet a different real invention entirely: the magnetic compass — and ask an honest question: does having an instrument make a method 'better'?&quot;</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Main Content</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">A Real Invention with a Long History (10 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Teacher Input: The magnetic compass is believed to have first been developed in ancient China, with early versions used for navigation by around the 11th century. It works because Earth itself behaves like a giant magnet, and a free-spinning magnetised needle naturally aligns itself with Earth's magnetic field, consistently pointing toward magnetic north.</li><li>Key Question: &quot;Why might knowing the compass's real history help us appreciate it as just one more clever human invention, rather than something that makes older methods 'wrong'?&quot;</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Comparing the Two Methods (10 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Teacher Input: A magnetic compass is reliable and doesn't require years of training, but it can be affected by magnetic interference and tells you direction only — not your real position. Traditional wayfinding takes years to master, but combines direction, position, and environmental awareness (wind, swells, stars, birds, clouds) into one constantly-updating mental picture.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Test and Debate (15 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Activity: Give groups a real compass to experiment with alongside their star compass wheel from Tuesday. Navigate the same simple course twice — once using only the star compass/memory method, once using the real compass — and compare speed, accuracy, and how each felt to use.</li><li>Activity: Hold a short class debate: which method would you trust more on a real voyage, and why? Is there value in both?</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Exit Ticket</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Write one sentence: where and roughly when was the magnetic compass first developed?</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Faith Connection</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Ponder: GPS is extraordinary — but it needs a satellite signal, a battery, and a working device. The stars require nothing except eyes that have been taught to read them. Hina's wisdom was about knowing something so deeply that no technology can replace it. This is the trust in God that Proverbs describes: not leaning on tools alone, but on deep, abiding relationship.</li><li>Verse: &quot;Trust in the LORD with all your heart and lean not on your own understanding; in all your ways submit to him, and he will make your paths straight.&quot; — <em>Proverbs 3:5-6 (NIV)</em></li><li>Value: Manaaki  Serve — Developing deep wisdom that serves others — not just relying on shortcuts and tools — is at the heart of faithful service.</li><li>Discussion questions:<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>GPS tells you where to go without needing to understand why. How might that be different from the deep knowledge Hina passed to Tama?</li><li>'Lean not on your own understanding' — does this mean we shouldn't use our minds? Or does it mean something else?</li><li>Is there an area of your life where you've been relying too much on external shortcuts rather than developing real wisdom?</li></ul></li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Real compasses (one or more per group)</li><li>Star compass wheels from Tuesday</li><li>A simple course for the navigation comparison</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Suggested Videos</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>History of the magnetic compass</em> — search: &quot;history of the magnetic compass invention China&quot;. Provides real historical context for the invention discussed in this lesson.</li><li><em>How does a compass work?</em> — search: &quot;how does a compass work Earth magnetic field explained&quot;. Explains the real science behind the compass's function.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Student Agency Choices</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Art: Design a &quot;Hybrid&quot; Navigation Tool</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Design a tool on paper that combines the best of both traditional wayfinding and modern compass technology.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Instructions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Sketch your hybrid tool.</li><li>Label one traditional feature and one modern feature it combines.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Why did you choose to combine those two specific features?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Read/Write: The Compass's Real History</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write a short factual recount of the magnetic compass's real history and how it works.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Word Count Guide</th><td class=\"lt-td\">120–180 words.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">What part of its history surprised you most?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Technology: Digital Comparison Chart</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Create a simple digital table comparing traditional wayfinding and the magnetic compass across categories like: training time, reliability, and information provided.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Which method &quot;wins&quot; more categories — and does that settle the debate?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Watch &amp; Review: Watch and Note: The Compass</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Watch a short video on the magnetic compass's history or science.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Search term</th><td class=\"lt-td\">&quot;history of the magnetic compass invention China&quot;</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">What fact from the video would change someone's mind in today's debate?</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Friday — Art: Trial 20: Painting the Stars":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">MATARIKI STAR PAINTING — Star Map Quilt Square</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">The Arts (Visual Art)</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Achievement Objectives</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Developing Practical Knowledge: explore colour, line, and pattern to express ideas.</li><li>Communicating and Interpreting: identify how artists have used the environment as inspiration.</li><li>Understanding the Arts in Context: investigate the purpose of objects and images from past and present cultures.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Key Competencies</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Thinking; Using Language, Symbols &amp; Text</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning to use the night sky as an artistic reference, placing stars intentionally.</li><li>We are understanding how Matariki and Te Punga held meaning for Māori and Pacific navigators.</li><li>I can create a personal star map square showing at least two named star formations.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Completed star map square with at least two identifiable star formations and a written sentence.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Introduction</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Dim the lights if possible. Display the Milky Way image from Monday's trial. Ask: &quot;If this was your only road map, which dots would you memorise first?&quot; Introduce Matariki (nine stars of the Pleiades) and Te Punga (Southern Cross) — the two most important clusters for Pacific navigation.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Main Content</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Sketching the Star Positions (8 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Use white chalk or light pencil to mark star positions on dark paper — start with Matariki's tight cluster of nine, then Te Punga's four stars of the Southern Cross.</li><li>Encourage students to use actual relative positions from the star compass, not random placement.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Painting the Stars (12 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Using white and gold paint or pastel, paint each star — varying dot size to show brightness.</li><li>Optional: add a faint Milky Way streak using a dry brush or smudged white pastel.</li><li>Teacher prompt: &quot;A star that navigators trusted had to be remembered clearly. Make your important stars unmistakable.&quot;</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Labelling and Assembly (10 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Lightly label key star clusters in pencil: Matariki, Te Punga, any others known.</li><li>Write one sentence on the back: which star would YOU trust most on a dark night, and why?</li><li>Add the square to the group quilt and step back — compare with Weeks 1–3's squares.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Exit Ticket</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Point to one star on your square and say its name and what it tells a navigator.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Faith Connection</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Ponder: Matariki. Te Punga. Māui's fishhook. The voyagers gave stars names because names matter — names make things real, knowable, and part of your story. God does the same with every star in the universe. And He does it with us too: He knows your name.</li><li>Verse: &quot;He determines the number of the stars and calls them each by name.&quot; — <em>Psalm 147:4 (NIV)</em></li><li>Value: Pānga  Impact — Painting the stars and naming them reflects back the glory of the God who named them all — bringing wonder and worship into the world through our art.</li><li>Discussion questions:<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>God calls every star by name — and there are more stars than grains of sand on every beach on earth. What does that tell you about God's capacity to know and love?</li><li>If God names the stars, and names YOU, what does that mean for how God sees you on your hardest days?</li><li>As you painted your star map, how might you make that kind of work an act of worship rather than just an art activity?</li></ul></li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Dark blue or black paper squares (A4 trimmed to square)</li><li>White and gold paint, pastels, or chalk</li><li>Fine brushes or cotton buds for dot stars</li><li>Star compass reference image from Tuesday</li><li>Group quilt backing sheets</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Suggested Videos</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Matariki — the nine stars explained</em> — search: &quot;Matariki nine stars Maori New Year meaning kids&quot;. Provides context for each of the nine Matariki stars and their meanings.</li><li><em>Polynesian star navigation</em> — search: &quot;Polynesian star navigation Pacific wayfinding stars&quot;. Shows how specific star clusters were used in practice.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Student Agency Choices</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Art: Paint Your Star Map Square</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This week's main activity IS the art focus — paint your Matariki star map on dark paper and add it to the group quilt.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">What did you discover about placing stars intentionally versus randomly?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Read/Write: The Night Watch</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write a short journal entry (80–120 words) as Tama on night watch, identifying stars one by one.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Which star felt most like a friend by the end of your watch?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Technology: Digital Star Map</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Use a drawing tool to create a digital star map showing Matariki, Te Punga, and at least two other named stars with labels.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">What was the hardest thing about making stars look like stars on screen?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Watch &amp; Review: Watch: Matariki — the Stars Explained</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Watch a short video on Matariki and its nine stars.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Search term</th><td class=\"lt-td\">&quot;Matariki nine stars Maori New Year meaning kids&quot;</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Which of the nine stars' meanings surprised you most?</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"}}},"Week 5":{"label":"Day","children":{"Monday — History: Sea Trial — Station 1: True Voyager or Myth?":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">SEA TRIAL STATION 1 — Testing Real History Knowledge from Weeks 1–4</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Social Sciences</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Achievement Objectives</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Understanding the Past: demonstrate understanding of historical events, evidence, and the people involved, drawn from prior learning.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Key Competencies</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Thinking; Relating to Others</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are demonstrating what we have learned about real Pacific migration history from Weeks 1–4.</li><li>We are working as a team under time pressure to recall accurate historical facts.</li><li>I can tell the difference between a true historical fact and a common misconception.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">This IS the assessment for Weeks 1–4 History content. Score team performance on the quick-fire round and use results to identify any concepts needing revisiting before Week 6.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Introduction</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&quot;The Council of Wayfinders has summoned you. Before this voyage can continue, you must prove what you have truly learned across four weeks of trials. Today's first station: history itself. Quick wits and accurate memory will serve you far better than guessing.&quot;</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Main Content</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Station Setup (5 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Explain the format: this is a rapid-fire True/False round. Read each statement aloud; teams confer briefly (10–15 seconds) then signal their answer (show a card, raise a hand, or use a simple buzzer system).</li><li>Award one point per correct answer. Consider a bonus point for a team that can correctly explain WHY a false statement is false.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Quick-Fire Question Bank (20 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>1. The Lapita culture is named after a site in New Caledonia. (TRUE)</li><li>2. The Lapita people are believed to have first appeared near Papua New Guinea. (TRUE)</li><li>3. Voyagers reached Hawaii, Rapa Nui, and Aotearoa at the same time as the early Lapita spread. (FALSE — this happened roughly 1,000+ years later)</li><li>4. Voyaging canoes are believed to have carried food crops like kūmara, intended for planting at new lands. (TRUE)</li><li>5. Mau Piailug navigated the Hōkūleʻa using modern GPS technology. (FALSE — he used only traditional, instrument-free wayfinding)</li><li>6. The Hōkūleʻa voyage sailed from Hawaii to Tahiti in 1976. (TRUE)</li><li>7. Overcrowding and drought are real examples of 'pull' factors that drew people toward a new land. (FALSE — these are push factors that drove people away from a place)</li><li>8. Bird migration patterns may have been a real clue voyagers used to suspect that land existed somewhere unseen. (TRUE)</li><li>9. The magnetic compass was first invented in ancient Greece. (FALSE — it is believed to have first been developed in ancient China)</li><li>10. Etak is a real navigational idea where the canoe is imagined to stay still while islands move past it. (TRUE)</li><li>Optional extension: invite teams to write one additional True/False statement of their own from Weeks 1–4 content, to challenge another team.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Debrief (5 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Reveal team scores. Briefly revisit any question more than one team got wrong, with a short explanation, before moving to tomorrow's station.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Exit Ticket</th><td class=\"lt-td\">As a team, write down the ONE question from today you're most confident you'll remember correctly in a year's time — and why it stuck.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Quick-fire question bank (above) printed or read aloud</li><li>A simple way for teams to signal answers (cards, hands, buzzers)</li><li>A visible scoreboard</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Student Agency Choices</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Art: Design a Sea Trial Medal</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Design a medal or badge that could be awarded to a team that performs well in today's History Station.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Instructions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Sketch the medal design.</li><li>Include one symbol from this term's story (pottery shard, waka, compass, star).</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Why did you choose that particular symbol to represent History knowledge?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Read/Write: My History MVP Moment</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write a short reflection on the ONE piece of history content from Weeks 1–4 that you understood best, and why.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Word Count Guide</th><td class=\"lt-td\">80–120 words.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">How did you come to understand it so well — what helped it stick?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Technology: Build a Digital Scoreboard</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Create a simple digital scoreboard (spreadsheet or slide) to track team scores across all five Sea Trial stations this week.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">What information does a good scoreboard need to show clearly?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Watch &amp; Review: Review Your Own Quest Log</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Look back through your own Quest Log entries from Weeks 1–4 and note one thing you wrote that you'd improve on now, with what you know today.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">What does this tell you about how much you've learned this term?</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Tuesday — Geography: Sea Trial — Station 2: Place the Islands":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">SEA TRIAL STATION 2 — Testing Real Geography Knowledge from Weeks 1–4</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Social Sciences (Geography)</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Achievement Objectives</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Understanding Place and Environment: demonstrate understanding of geographic features, scale, and patterns drawn from prior learning.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Key Competencies</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Thinking; Using Language, Symbols &amp; Text</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are demonstrating our memory of real Pacific geography from Weeks 1–4.</li><li>We are working under pressure, using memory rather than guesswork.</li><li>I can recall and place real geographic features correctly without looking.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">This IS the assessment for Weeks 1–4 Geography content. Use results to identify which geographic concepts (scale, currents, navigation signs) may need revisiting.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Introduction</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&quot;Yesterday, your knowledge of history was tested. Today, the Council tests your knowledge of place itself. A real voyager could not always see — sometimes they had to know, from memory alone, where things truly were.&quot;</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Main Content</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Station Setup (5 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Lay out the large Pacific floor/wall map used throughout the term, but cover or remove the usual labels.</li><li>Explain the challenge: one team member is blindfolded (or simply closes their eyes), and must correctly place a token at a named location based purely on memory, guided only by verbal directions (not physical guidance) from their team.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Challenge Round (20 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Call out locations one at a time for each team to place: Hawaii, Aotearoa, Rapa Nui (the three points of the Polynesian Triangle), Tahiti, and one island group from Week 1's distance work.</li><li>Award points for correct or close placement. Follow with a short verbal recap round: ask teams to recall one real fact about ocean currents (South Equatorial Current, North Equatorial Current, Equatorial Countercurrent) and one real wave-piloting or bird-based navigation clue from Week 3.</li><li>Key Question for discussion: &quot;Which was harder — remembering WHERE something was, or remembering WHAT a clue meant?&quot;</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Debrief (5 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Reveal scores. Briefly re-show correct locations on the map for anything placed incorrectly.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Exit Ticket</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Write one sentence: which real Pacific location or geographic fact from this term do you feel most confident about, and why?</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Large Pacific floor/wall map (labels removed or covered)</li><li>Blindfolds (optional) and placement tokens</li><li>A visible scoreboard</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Student Agency Choices</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Art: Draw the Triangle from Memory</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Without looking at any reference, draw your best memory of the Polynesian Triangle and its three points.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Compare your drawing to a real map afterward — what did you get right, and what surprised you?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Read/Write: My Geography MVP Moment</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write a short reflection on the ONE piece of geography content from Weeks 1–4 you understood best.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Word Count Guide</th><td class=\"lt-td\">80–120 words.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">How did you remember it so clearly?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Technology: Digital Map Labelling Challenge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Use a simple digital map tool or slide to label as many real locations and currents from this term as you can recall, unaided.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">How many could you label without checking back?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Watch &amp; Review: Review the Term's Map Work</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Look back at your own star compass and distance work from Weeks 1 and 4. What's one detail you'd add now that you wouldn't have known back then?</p></div></div>"},"Wednesday — Science: Sea Trial — Station 3: Read the Conditions":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">SEA TRIAL STATION 3 — Testing Real Science Knowledge from Weeks 1–4</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Science</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Achievement Objectives</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Demonstrate understanding of weather, climate, and astronomical phenomena drawn from prior learning.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Key Competencies</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Thinking; Participating &amp; Contributing</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are demonstrating our understanding of real weather, wind, and seasonal science from Weeks 1–4.</li><li>We are using real observed or given conditions to make a justified decision.</li><li>I can apply scientific reasoning under realistic pressure, not just recall facts.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">This IS the assessment for Weeks 1–4 Science content. Use results to identify which scientific concepts (weather vs. climate, wind formation, cloud signs, seasons) may need revisiting.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Introduction</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&quot;A real voyager often had only moments to decide: is it safe to sail today, or not? Today, the Council gives you a real or simulated set of conditions. Your knowledge of sky and sea will be tested for real.&quot;</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Main Content</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Station Setup (5 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>If possible, take the class outside for genuine conditions; if not, prepare 3–4 written/illustrated weather scenario cards (e.g. &quot;calm seas, light cumulus clouds, steady wind from the east&quot; or &quot;darkening cumulonimbus clouds building rapidly, wind shifting&quot;).</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Challenge Round (20 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Present each scenario to teams in turn. Teams must decide: safe to sail, or not — and justify their answer using real content from this term (weather vs. climate, cloud types, trade wind patterns).</li><li>Include at least one question testing the real cause of seasons (Earth's axial tilt) and one testing the real 'island cloud' phenomenon from Week 3.</li><li>Award points for correct decisions AND for scientifically sound justifications, even if the final decision is debatable.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Debrief (5 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Reveal scores. Discuss any scenario where teams disagreed — was there a genuinely defensible case for both decisions?</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Exit Ticket</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Write one sentence: what real science concept from this term would you most want a real voyager to understand before setting sail?</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Weather scenario cards (or real outdoor conditions)</li><li>A visible scoreboard</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Student Agency Choices</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Art: Illustrate a &quot;Safe to Sail&quot; Scene</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Draw a sky/sea scene showing conditions a real voyager would consider genuinely safe to set sail in, labelling the clues that make it safe.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">What's the one clue you'd never ignore, even if everything else looked fine?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Read/Write: My Science MVP Moment</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write a short reflection on the ONE science concept from Weeks 1–4 you understood best.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Word Count Guide</th><td class=\"lt-td\">80–120 words.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">How would you explain it to someone who missed this term?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Technology: Build a Decision Flowchart</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Create a simple digital flowchart: &quot;Is it safe to sail?&quot; — branching through weather, cloud, and wind checks based on this term's content.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">What question did you put first in your flowchart, and why?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Watch &amp; Review: Review Your Voyage Forecasts</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Look back at your own voyage forecast entries from Weeks 1–3. Were your past decisions justified by real evidence, or guesswork? Reflect honestly.</p></div></div>"},"Thursday — Technology: Sea Trial — Station 4: The Final Float Test":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">SEA TRIAL STATION 4 — Testing Real Technology Knowledge and the Class Vessels</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Technology</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Achievement Objectives</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Demonstrate understanding of design, materials, and structures, drawn from prior learning and testing.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Key Competencies</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Thinking; Managing Self</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are demonstrating our real engineering knowledge from Weeks 1–4.</li><li>We are testing our own vessel designs under genuine, timed conditions.</li><li>I can explain why a real design choice worked, using evidence.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">This IS the assessment for Weeks 1–4 Technology content. Use the float-and-sail test and verbal explanations as evidence of understanding buoyancy, stability, and sail design.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Introduction</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&quot;Every voyager's fate rests on their vessel. Today, the Council asks for proof: bring forward your waka hourua and sail, built and improved across these four weeks, and show that it can truly survive the open sea.&quot;</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Main Content</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Station Setup (5 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Gather the class's waka hourua models (Week 2) and sail designs (Week 3). If models were not kept, allow a short rebuild using familiar materials.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">The Final Float-and-Sail Test (20 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Each team tests their vessel in front of the class: float test with cargo (Week 1–2 buoyancy knowledge), stability test against a simulated wave (Week 2 double-hull knowledge), and a sail test in front of a fan or moving air (Week 3 crab claw sail knowledge).</li><li>After each test, the team must verbally explain ONE real engineering reason their design succeeded or failed, using correct vocabulary from this term (displacement, density, stability, sail angle).</li><li>Award points for both performance AND for the quality/accuracy of the verbal explanation.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Debrief (5 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Reveal scores. Celebrate the strongest design feature seen across all teams, regardless of final score.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Exit Ticket</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Write one sentence: what is the single most important design feature your vessel needed to survive this final test?</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Class waka hourua and sail models from Weeks 1–3 (or rebuild materials)</li><li>Tubs of water, a fan or moving air source</li><li>A visible scoreboard</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Student Agency Choices</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Art: Design a &quot;Sea-Worthy&quot; Certificate</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Design a certificate that could be awarded to a vessel that passes today's final test, listing the real engineering features it demonstrated.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">What would a certificate need to show to prove a vessel is genuinely sea-worthy?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Read/Write: My Technology MVP Moment</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write a short reflection on the ONE piece of technology content from Weeks 1–4 you understood best.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Word Count Guide</th><td class=\"lt-td\">80–120 words.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">How did testing your own design help you understand it better than just reading about it?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Technology: Digital Design Evolution Poster</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Create a simple before/after digital poster showing your vessel's design at Week 1 versus its final, improved version today.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">What's the biggest improvement you made, and what taught you to make it?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Watch &amp; Review: Review Your Build Journey</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Look back at your design notes and sketches from Weeks 1–3. What's one change you made that you're proudest of?</p></div></div>"},"Friday — Bonus Station: Sea Trial — Bonus Station: Star Navigation & The Council's Verdict":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">SEA TRIAL FINALE — Star Navigation Test and the Council Ceremony</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Social Sciences / The Arts</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Achievement Objectives</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Demonstrate understanding of navigation knowledge and contribute meaningfully to a shared class outcome, drawn from a full term of prior learning.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Key Competencies</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Thinking; Participating &amp; Contributing; Relating to Others</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are demonstrating our ability to use a star compass for real navigation decisions.</li><li>We are reflecting honestly on a full term of learning and contribution.</li><li>I can recognise growth in my own understanding across an extended unit of work.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Teacher observation of the star navigation challenge. Use the Council Ceremony as a celebratory, low-pressure close to Weeks 1–4's assessment, not as a new graded task.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Introduction</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&quot;You have faced History, Geography, Science, and Technology. One final test remains, and then the Council of Wayfinders will render its verdict on this voyage so far.&quot;</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Main Content</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Bonus Station: Star Navigation (10 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Using the star compass wheels built in Week 4, teams must correctly 'steer' a token from one named island to another on the floor map, using real star reference points (Matariki, Te Matau a Māui, Te Punga/the Southern Cross) rather than guessing direction.</li><li>Award bonus points for correct use of the real star names alongside correct direction.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Tallying the Sea Trial (10 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Add up all points from Monday through Friday's bonus station. Announce results — frame this as a genuine achievement regardless of which team scores highest, since every team has demonstrated four weeks of real learning.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">The Council Ceremony (10 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>If the class has &quot;passed&quot; (a generous bar — this is about participation and growth, not a cutoff score), narrate the compass prop visibly coming back to life: spin it once, then let it settle.</li><li>Read a closing line in Tama's voice: &quot;You have proven yourselves true Wayfinders-in-training. The compass settles, ready for what comes next — for soon, after so long at sea, we will see land.&quot;</li><li>This sets up Week 6's arrival in Aotearoa.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Exit Ticket</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Write one sentence: looking back over Weeks 1–4, what is the one thing you understand now that you didn't at the start of the term?</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Star compass wheels from Week 4</li><li>The floor/wall Pacific map</li><li>The class's compass/journal prop for the ceremony</li><li>A running scoreboard from the week</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Student Agency Choices</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Art: Design a &quot;Wayfinder-in-Training&quot; Badge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Design a small badge or certificate every student could receive for completing the Sea Trial.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">What symbol best represents what you've learned this term so far?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Read/Write: A Letter to Myself at the Start of Term</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write a short letter to yourself from Week 1, telling your past self what you now understand about this voyage.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Word Count Guide</th><td class=\"lt-td\">120–180 words.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">What would surprise your Week 1 self the most?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Technology: Build a Term Highlights Slide</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Create one digital slide summarising your proudest moment or piece of learning from Weeks 1–4.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Why did you choose that particular highlight?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Watch &amp; Review: Review the Whole Voyage Quilt</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Look back over your group's quilt squares built across Weeks 1–4 and choose the one square you think tells the story best.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">What makes that square so effective at telling the story?</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"}}},"Week 6":{"label":"Day","children":{"Monday — History: Trial 21: Land!":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">ARRIVAL IN AOTEAROA — One of the Last Great Landmasses Settled by Humans</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Social Sciences</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Achievement Objectives</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Understanding the Past: investigate how the perspectives of people in the past differ, based on evidence available about their lives and choices.</li><li>Understanding Place and Environment: explore how the arrival of people in a new place changes both the people and the place.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Key Competencies</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Thinking; Relating to Others</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning roughly when and how Aotearoa was first settled, based on real evidence.</li><li>We are learning that Aotearoa was one of the last major landmasses on Earth to be reached by people.</li><li>I can use real evidence to support a claim about a historical arrival.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Teacher observation of the &quot;first 24 hours&quot; priority-ranking discussion. Review of student decisions and justifications.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Introduction</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&quot;After five weeks of preparing, sailing, and surviving, the voyage finally reaches land. But here's a fact that often surprises people: historians and archaeologists believe Aotearoa was one of the very last major landmasses on Earth to be settled by humans — likely only around 700 years ago, sometime around 1300 CE.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Wonder question: if humans had already been living on most other continents for tens of thousands of years, why might Aotearoa have been one of the last places reached?&quot;</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Main Content</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Real Evidence of Early Arrival (12 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Teacher Input: one of the most important real archaeological sites in this story is Wairau Bar, in Marlborough at the top of the South Island. Excavations there uncovered evidence of one of New Zealand's earliest known settlements, including tools, ornaments, and the bones of moa — a now-extinct giant flightless bird hunted by early arrivals — helping archaeologists estimate settlement dates of roughly 1280–1300 CE.</li><li>Key Question: &quot;Why might bones and tools left behind tell us more reliable information than a guess based on stories alone — and why might we still need both kinds of evidence together?&quot;</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Why So Late? (8 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Teacher Input: Aotearoa's extreme distance from other landmasses, combined with the genuinely difficult ocean crossing required to reach it, are real reasons historians believe it remained unsettled for so long compared to most of the rest of the world.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">The First 24 Hours (15 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Activity: close eyes and narrate the moment of stepping onto an unknown shore — the sounds, smells, the ground feeling strange after weeks at sea — then open eyes to a real image of Aotearoa's coastline.</li><li>Activity: in groups, rank the top 3 decisions a small group of new arrivals would genuinely need to make in their first 24 hours (shelter, fresh water, food, safety), and defend the chosen order.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Exit Ticket</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Write one sentence: roughly when do historians believe Aotearoa was first settled, and what is one piece of real evidence that supports this?</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Faith Connection</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Ponder: After weeks at sea, land appears — green mountains, birds, the smell of earth. This is the moment the whole voyage was for. The Bible is full of moments like this: God bringing His people to a new place after all the fear and waiting, and saying — here. This is it.</li><li>Verse: &quot;He brought us to this place and gave us this land, a land flowing with milk and honey.&quot; — <em>Deuteronomy 26:9 (NIV)</em></li><li>Value: Aroha  Love — God's love is shown in the places He brings us to — after the storms, after the long waiting, after the courage it cost.</li><li>Discussion questions:<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>Deuteronomy describes God 'bringing' His people to a new place. In what ways has God brought YOU somewhere new — a new school, a new friendship, a new understanding?</li><li>Tama's family carried plants and seeds to grow in the new land. What things do you carry with you that are important to who you are?</li><li>What would it mean for you to treat your home — your town, your school — as a gift from God worth caring for?</li></ul></li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Image of Aotearoa's coastline/landscape</li><li>Information or images relating to Wairau Bar (if available)</li><li>Paper for the priority-ranking activity</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Suggested Videos</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>When was Aotearoa first settled?</em> — search: &quot;when was New Zealand first settled by Polynesians Wairau Bar&quot;. Provides real, age-appropriate detail on the archaeological evidence behind this lesson's key dates.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Student Agency Choices</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Art: Illustrate the Moment of Arrival</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Draw the moment of first stepping onto Aotearoa's coastline, based on a real description of the landscape.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Instructions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Include the coastline, the waka, and the first arrivals.</li><li>Add one detail showing how different this land looks from a Pacific island.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">What did you choose to focus on to show this was a genuinely new and different place?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Read/Write: Wairau Bar: A Discovery Report</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write a short report, in role as an archaeologist, describing what was found at Wairau Bar and why it matters for understanding Aotearoa's early history.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Word Count Guide</th><td class=\"lt-td\">120–180 words.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Why does physical evidence like this matter so much when no written records exist?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Technology: Build a &quot;First 24 Hours&quot; Decision Tree</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Create a simple digital flowchart or decision tree showing the order of priorities a new arrival would face in their first 24 hours.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">What did you put first, and why?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Watch &amp; Review: Watch and Note: The First Arrivals</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Watch a short video on the early settlement of Aotearoa.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Search term</th><td class=\"lt-td\">&quot;when was New Zealand first settled by Polynesians Wairau Bar&quot;</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">What real evidence mentioned in the video surprised you most?</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Tuesday — Geography: Trial 22: A Different Land":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">A LAND UNLIKE ANY OTHER — Aotearoa's Real Ecological Differences</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Social Sciences (Geography)</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Achievement Objectives</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Understanding Place and Environment: explore how the natural features of a place affect the people who live there.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Key Competencies</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Thinking; Using Language, Symbols &amp; Text</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning real, significant differences between tropical Pacific islands and Aotearoa.</li><li>We are learning that Aotearoa had no native land mammals before humans arrived — a genuinely unusual fact.</li><li>I can compare two environments using real evidence and explain the impact of the differences.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Teacher observation during the comparison-chart activity and class discussion.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Introduction</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&quot;Yesterday, we arrived. Today, we need to understand just how different this new land really was. Wonder question: what do you think it would be like landing somewhere with no familiar animals at all — not even ones you might expect, like deer, foxes, or wild dogs?&quot;</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Main Content</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Scale and Climate (10 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Teacher Input: Aotearoa is far larger than most Pacific islands, with genuine mountain ranges — including the Southern Alps — and a temperate climate with four distinct seasons, very different from the tropical wet/dry seasonal pattern of most Pacific islands.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">A Genuinely Unusual Real Fact (10 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Teacher Input: Aotearoa is believed to have had no native land mammals at all before human arrival — no deer, no foxes, not even mice or rats, aside from bats. Instead, it had extraordinary, unique birdlife that had evolved without natural land predators, including the moa, a giant flightless bird that grew up to several metres tall.</li><li>Key Question: &quot;Why might birds evolve so differently on a land with no land predators, compared to most other places on Earth?&quot;</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Comparing the Two Environments (10 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Activity: sort image/fact cards comparing a typical Pacific island (small, warm, coral, limited land, familiar tropical animals) versus Aotearoa (large landmass, mountains, varied climate, no native land mammals, unique birdlife) into a physical comparison chart.</li><li>Activity: decide and justify which single difference would have been hardest for new arrivals to adjust to.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Exit Ticket</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Write one sentence: what is one genuinely unusual real fact about Aotearoa's wildlife before humans arrived?</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Faith Connection</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Ponder: Tama stands on a hill looking at mountains that stretch further than he has ever seen land go. This is the feeling of 'new' — overwhelming, wonderful, and a little frightening. Isaiah's God is the God of new things: not nostalgia, not just the past, but a living Creator still at work.</li><li>Verse: &quot;&quot;See, I am doing a new thing! Now it springs up; do you not perceive it? I am making a way in the wilderness and streams in the wasteland.&quot;&quot; — <em>Isaiah 43:19 (NIV)</em></li><li>Value: Pānga  Impact — God is always doing new things and calls us to be part of bringing His Kingdom into new places and new situations.</li><li>Discussion questions:<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>God asks: 'Do you not perceive it?' — what new thing might God be doing in your life right now that you might be missing?</li><li>Tama's first reaction to Aotearoa is awe — this land is enormous and strange. When have you experienced that kind of awe? What did it make you think about God?</li><li>How might you be someone who helps bring God's 'new things' into your school or community?</li></ul></li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Comparison cards: Pacific island vs. Aotearoa features</li><li>Images of moa and other unique native birds if available</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Suggested Videos</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>New Zealand before humans arrived</em> — search: &quot;New Zealand wildlife before human arrival moa no land mammals&quot;. Explains the real, unusual ecological history central to this lesson.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Student Agency Choices</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Art: Illustrate Aotearoa's Unique Birdlife</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Draw a moa or another unique native bird of Aotearoa, labelling at least one real fact about it.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">What about this bird makes it different from animals new arrivals would have known from the Pacific islands?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Read/Write: &quot;Welcome Guide&quot; for New Arrivals</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write a short &quot;welcome guide&quot; (in role) listing 3 real things to expect that are different about this new land.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Word Count Guide</th><td class=\"lt-td\">100–150 words.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Which difference do you think would need explaining most urgently?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Technology: Digital Comparison Chart</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Build a simple digital table comparing a typical Pacific island and Aotearoa across climate, size, and wildlife.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Which category shows the biggest difference?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Watch &amp; Review: Watch and Note: A Land Without Land Mammals</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Watch a short video about Aotearoa's wildlife before human arrival.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Search term</th><td class=\"lt-td\">&quot;New Zealand wildlife before human arrival moa no land mammals&quot;</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">What fact from the video would most surprise someone who'd never heard it?</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Wednesday — Science: Trial 23: New Seasons, New Rules":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">SURVIVING A COLDER YEAR — Real Adaptations for a New Climate</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Science (Planet Earth and Beyond)</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Achievement Objectives</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Investigate how living things, including humans, are suited to their particular way of life.</li><li>Build science knowledge connected to real survival and food-growing decisions.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Key Competencies</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Thinking; Managing Self</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning how a colder, four-season climate genuinely changed how new arrivals had to live.</li><li>We are learning a real food-storage technique developed to survive Aotearoa's winters.</li><li>I can plan a realistic seasonal survival strategy using real evidence.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Teacher observation of the survival-calendar planning activity and team presentations.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Introduction</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&quot;Yesterday we learned how different this land truly was. Today, we focus on a genuinely serious real challenge: Aotearoa's winters are far colder than anything most Pacific voyagers had ever experienced — and food crops they relied on didn't grow the same way here.&quot;</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Main Content</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">A Genuinely Harder Climate for Growing Food (10 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Teacher Input: kūmara (sweet potato), a crop voyagers are believed to have carried with them, struggled to grow in much of Aotearoa's cooler climate compared to the tropical Pacific. Early growers had to find sheltered, sun-facing slopes and develop new techniques to give it any real chance of growing well.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">A Real, Clever Solution: Storage Pits (10 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Teacher Input: a genuinely real and clever solution that developed was the rua kūmara — purpose-built storage pits dug into the ground, used to keep harvested kūmara protected from frost and cold through the winter months, ready for the following season.</li><li>Key Question: &quot;Why might keeping food safe through an entire cold season have been just as important as growing it in the first place?&quot;</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Planning a Survival Calendar (12 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Activity: in groups, plot a basic 'first year survival calendar,' deciding what should happen in each season (planting, storing, hunting, gathering) to survive a full year in this new climate.</li><li>Activity: present your team's survival calendar and defend one key decision in it.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Exit Ticket</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Write one sentence explaining what a rua kūmara was and why it mattered.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Faith Connection</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Ponder: Roa is already worried about keeping Pipi warm through the coming cold. The seasons of Aotearoa are completely different from what they knew. But God built seasons into creation on purpose — as rhythm, as rest, as reminder that time moves forward and He holds it all.</li><li>Verse: &quot;There is a time for everything, and a season for every activity under the heavens.&quot; — <em>Ecclesiastes 3:1 (NIV)</em></li><li>Value: Manaaki  Serve — Faithfully preparing for each season — whether literal or in life — and caring for those around us through change is at the heart of Manaaki.</li><li>Discussion questions:<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>Ecclesiastes says there is 'a season for every activity.' What season do you think you're in right now — in your life, your faith, your learning?</li><li>Roa prepares for winter by planning ahead for her family. What does faithful preparation for the people around you look like in your own life?</li><li>How does knowing that God holds all the seasons — including the difficult ones — change the way you face change?</li></ul></li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Simple seasonal calendar templates</li><li>Images of kūmara and storage pit reconstructions if available</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Suggested Videos</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>How early Māori grew and stored kūmara</em> — search: &quot;rua kumara storage pits early Maori farming&quot;. Documents the real, clever food-storage solution central to this lesson.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Student Agency Choices</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Art: Illustrate a Rua Kūmara</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Draw a labelled cross-section of a rua kūmara (storage pit), showing how it protected food through winter.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">What design feature do you think mattered most for keeping food safe?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Read/Write: A Grower's Notes</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write a short set of notes, in role as an early grower, explaining the challenge of growing kūmara in this new climate and the solution you found.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Word Count Guide</th><td class=\"lt-td\">120–180 words.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">What real-world problem-solving does this remind you of today?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Technology: Build a Digital Survival Calendar</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Create a simple digital seasonal calendar showing planting, storing, and gathering tasks across a full year in Aotearoa.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Which season looks the most demanding on your calendar?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Watch &amp; Review: Watch and Note: Storing Food for Winter</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Watch a short video about rua kūmara or early Māori food storage.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Search term</th><td class=\"lt-td\">&quot;rua kumara storage pits early Maori farming&quot;</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">What detail from the video impressed you most about this solution?</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Thursday — Technology: Trial 24: New Land, New Tools":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">ADZE AND POUNAMU — Real Tool Adaptation in a New Land</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Technology</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Achievement Objectives</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Technological Knowledge: understand how materials available in an environment shape the tools and technologies a society develops.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Key Competencies</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Thinking; Managing Self</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning how real tools and materials changed once people settled in Aotearoa.</li><li>We are learning about pounamu (greenstone), a genuinely significant material found here.</li><li>I can explain why available materials shape the technology a society develops.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Teacher observation of the tool redesign activity and written explanations.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Introduction</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&quot;New land. New challenges. Yesterday we focused on food. Today, we focus on tools — because the materials available in Aotearoa were genuinely different from what voyagers had used across the tropical Pacific, and that changed what tools they could make.&quot;</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Main Content</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">A Genuinely Significant New Material: Pounamu (10 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Teacher Input: Aotearoa's South Island contains pounamu (greenstone/NZ jade), a hard, beautiful stone that became hugely significant for both practical tools (like adzes, a type of cutting/shaping tool) and for ornaments carrying real cultural and spiritual value, still highly treasured today.</li><li>Key Question: &quot;Why might a material that is both useful AND beautiful become especially treasured by a society?&quot;</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Adapting Old Tools for New Tasks (10 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Teacher Input: voyaging-era tools designed for fishing and boat-building had to be adapted for entirely new tasks in Aotearoa — including hunting moa, working unfamiliar timbers, and building shelters suited to a much colder climate than most voyagers had known.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Redesign Challenge (10 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Activity: in groups, redesign a simple tool (fishing, building, or carrying) using 'new land' materials and constraints set by the teacher (e.g. a fishing tool needs to work in a river instead of open ocean).</li><li>Activity: explain what you changed about the original design and why the new land required it.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Exit Ticket</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Write one sentence: what is pounamu, and why did it become so significant in Aotearoa?</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Faith Connection</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Ponder: Tama's ocean tools don't work in the river. Everything he knew has to be adapted. James tells us that when we lack wisdom for exactly these moments — when the old ways don't fit the new place — we can ask God, who gives generously. The hīnaki was the answer to that prayer.</li><li>Verse: &quot;If any of you lacks wisdom, you should ask God, who gives generously to all without finding fault, and it will be given to you.&quot; — <em>James 1:5 (NIV)</em></li><li>Value: Manaaki  Serve — Seeking God's wisdom to serve our community well — even when we have to learn completely new ways — is at the heart of faithful service.</li><li>Discussion questions:<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>Have you ever been in a situation where what you knew before wasn't enough for where you were? What did you do?</li><li>The hīnaki was created by adapting Pacific weaving knowledge to a new challenge. How might God want you to adapt your gifts for a new situation?</li><li>James says to ask God for wisdom 'without doubting.' What would it look like to ask God for wisdom about a real challenge you're facing right now?</li></ul></li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Images of pounamu tools/ornaments if available</li><li>Materials for the tool redesign challenge (card, string, sticks)</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Suggested Videos</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>What is pounamu (greenstone)?</em> — search: &quot;pounamu greenstone New Zealand significance explained&quot;. Introduces the real, culturally significant material central to this lesson.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Student Agency Choices</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Art: Design a Pounamu-Inspired Tool or Ornament</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Draw a tool or ornament inspired by pounamu, labelling why both its usefulness and beauty might matter.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Why did you choose to make your design useful, decorative, or both?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Read/Write: Why Materials Matter</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write a short explanation of why the materials available in a place shape the tools a society can develop, using pounamu as your example.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Word Count Guide</th><td class=\"lt-td\">100–150 words.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Can you think of a modern example of a material shaping what we're able to build?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Technology: Digital Tool Redesign Poster</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Create a simple digital &quot;before and after&quot; poster showing your tool's original tropical design and its redesigned Aotearoa version.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">What was the most important change you made?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Watch &amp; Review: Watch and Note: Pounamu</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Watch a short video about pounamu and its significance.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Search term</th><td class=\"lt-td\">&quot;pounamu greenstone New Zealand significance explained&quot;</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">What fact from the video would you want to share with someone unfamiliar with pounamu?</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Friday — Art: Trial 30: Rock Art — Marking the Land":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">PACIFIC PETROGLYPH SKETCHING — Arrival Marks in a Quilt Square</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">The Arts (Visual Art)</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Achievement Objectives</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Developing Practical Knowledge: explore line, shape, and texture to make art works.</li><li>Understanding the Arts in Context: investigate the purpose of objects and images from past cultures.</li><li>Communicating and Interpreting: identify and describe ways in which meanings are communicated through art works.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Key Competencies</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Thinking; Using Language, Symbols &amp; Text</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning that Pacific petroglyphs used bold simple outlines to mark arrival and connection to place.</li><li>We are practising drawing with confident unshaded lines to create a carving-like effect.</li><li>I can sketch at least one petroglyph motif that connects to this week's landfall story.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Completed petroglyph square with at least one clear motif and a written sentence explaining its choice.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Introduction</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Show images of real Pacific petroglyphs — Hawaiian lava field carvings, NZ stone carvings. Ask: &quot;What do you notice about how these are drawn? No shading. No colour. Just lines.&quot; Explain: petroglyph artists worked with stone on stone — every mark was deliberate. Today students work the same way.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Main Content</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Choosing and Practising a Motif (8 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Display reference sheet of Pacific petroglyph motifs: sea turtle, fish, human figure, canoe/waka, bird, spiral.</li><li>Students choose 1–2 motifs connecting to Tama's landfall story. Sketch rough draft on scrap paper first.</li><li>Teacher prompt: &quot;These marks were made by people who had just arrived somewhere new. What would YOU carve to say: I was here?&quot;</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Drawing the Petroglyph Square (15 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Draw chosen motif(s) in pencil — bold simple outlines, no interior shading. Scale to fill most of the square.</li><li>Go over pencil lines with black ink pen or dark crayon, pressing firmly for strong confident marks.</li><li>Add a simple repeated border pattern around the edge (dots, dashes, or small triangles).</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Reflection and Assembly (7 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Write one sentence on back: which motif they chose and what it means for Tama's arrival.</li><li>Add square to group quilt. Compare: how does petroglyph square look different from woven, kōwhaiwhai, and star squares?</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Exit Ticket</th><td class=\"lt-td\">If you could carve ONE thing into the rocks at your landing place, what would it be and why?</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Faith Connection</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Ponder: The Pacific peoples carved their arrival into rock — turtles, canoes, spirals — not because they had to, but because they needed to say: I was here, this is who I am, this matters. God invites us to leave marks of His Kingdom in the world. What will yours say?</li><li>Verse: &quot;Let this be written for a future generation, that a people not yet created may praise the LORD.&quot; — <em>Psalm 102:18 (NIV)</em></li><li>Value: Pānga  Impact — The marks we leave — in art, in kindness, in truth-telling — can point future generations toward God, long after we are gone.</li><li>Discussion questions:<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>The Psalm says to write things 'for a future generation.' What are you doing now that might matter to someone who hasn't been born yet?</li><li>Pacific petroglyph artists carved their marks without knowing who would see them centuries later. How does that change the way you think about doing your work faithfully today?</li><li>If you could carve ONE thing into a rock that would be read in 1000 years, what would it say?</li></ul></li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A4 paper, one square per student (continuing the group quilt from previous weeks)</li><li>Black ink pens or dark wax crayons for confident outlines</li><li>Pencils and erasers for rough sketching</li><li>Reference sheet of Pacific petroglyph motifs (turtle, fish, human figure, canoe/waka, bird, spiral)</li><li>Scrap paper for practising the motif before committing to the final square</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Suggested Videos</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Pacific petroglyphs and rock art</em> — search: &quot;Pacific Hawaiian petroglyphs rock art&quot;. Shows real examples of Pacific petroglyphs so students can see the bold, simple carving style before sketching their own.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Student Agency Choices</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Art: Sketch Your Petroglyph Square</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This week's main activity IS the art focus — sketch and ink your petroglyph motif and add it to your group's quilt.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">What motif did you choose, and what does it say about arriving somewhere new?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Read/Write: Explain Your Motif</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write a short explanation of the petroglyph motif you chose and why it connects to Tama's landfall story.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Word Count Guide</th><td class=\"lt-td\">80–120 words.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Why might carving something in stone make a message last longer than writing it on paper?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Technology: Digital Petroglyph Design</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Use a simple drawing tool (Canva, Google Drawings) to design a bold, simple-line petroglyph-style motif of your own.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">What did keeping the design bold and simple, without shading, change about how you drew it?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Watch &amp; Review: Watch and Note: Rock Art</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Watch a short video on real Pacific petroglyphs.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Search term</th><td class=\"lt-td\">&quot;Pacific Hawaiian petroglyphs rock art&quot;</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">What surprised you about where or how these carvings were made?</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"}}},"Week 7":{"label":"Day","children":{"Monday — History: Trial 26: Not Just One Voyage":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">THE LONG PAUSE — A Real Mystery in the Pacific Settlement Story</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Social Sciences</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Achievement Objectives</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Understanding the Past: investigate how a historical event can be understood differently depending on the evidence available, and recognise genuine ongoing debate among historians.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Key Competencies</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Thinking; Language, Symbols &amp; Text</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning the real, two-stage timeline of Pacific settlement.</li><li>We are learning about a genuine, still-debated mystery in Pacific history: the &quot;long pause.&quot;</li><li>I can engage thoughtfully with a question historians themselves have not fully answered.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Teacher observation of the match-the-voyage activity and the class discussion on the &quot;long pause&quot; mystery.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Introduction</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&quot;We've arrived in Aotearoa — but this was not one single voyage. Today we discover that the full settlement of the Pacific happened in two real, distinct stages, separated by a genuine mystery historians still debate today.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Wonder question: if a journey can be made successfully once, why might people then wait over a thousand years before continuing it further?&quot;</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Main Content</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Two Real Stages of Settlement (10 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Teacher Input: recall from Week 1 that the Lapita spread reached Tonga and Samoa roughly 2,800–2,900 years ago. Here is the genuinely surprising part: archaeological evidence suggests voyagers then did NOT continue further east into the rest of the Pacific for roughly 1,000 to 1,500 years.</li><li>Only around 1,000–1,200 CE did voyaging resume and spread rapidly into what's called Eastern Polynesia — reaching Tahiti and the Society Islands, then Hawaii and Rapa Nui (Easter Island) around 1200 CE, and finally Aotearoa by roughly 1300 CE.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">A Genuine, Real Historical Mystery (10 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Teacher Input: historians and archaeologists genuinely do not fully agree on why this 'long pause' happened. Some suggest changes in voyaging technology or sailing conditions made the next stage possible only later; others wonder if it relates to population growth, climate shifts, or simply hasn't yet been fully explained by current evidence.</li><li>Key Question: &quot;Why is it actually more honest for historians to say 'we don't fully know' rather than guessing confidently? What does this teach us about how real history works?&quot;</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Match the Voyage (15 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Activity: a 'match the voyage' card game — place/fact cards (Tonga, Samoa, Hawaii, Rapa Nui, Aotearoa) matched and placed correctly onto the big Pacific floor map, then trace voyage routes between island groups with string, visually building the full spread of settlement across both real stages.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Exit Ticket</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Write one sentence: what is the &quot;long pause,&quot; and why is it still considered a genuine historical mystery?</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Faith Connection</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Ponder: Tama discovers that people like him reached Hawaii, Tonga, Samoa, Rapa Nui — the farthest corners of the Pacific. John's vision in Revelation pictures the same beautiful truth: at the end of all things, people from every nation, tribe, and language stand together before God. The voyagers were part of that story.</li><li>Verse: &quot;After this I looked, and there before me was a great multitude that no one could count, from every nation, tribe, people and language, standing before the throne.&quot; — <em>Revelation 7:9 (NIV)</em></li><li>Value: Mōhio  Know — Knowing that God's story includes every nation and every people helps us understand our place in His much larger plan.</li><li>Discussion questions:<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>Revelation pictures people from every nation and language together. What does that tell us about what God values?</li><li>Tama didn't know about the other voyages that spread his people across the Pacific. What parts of God's bigger story might you not know about yet?</li><li>How does knowing that people from every culture are part of God's plan change how you think about people who are different from you?</li></ul></li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Match-the-voyage cards (Tonga, Samoa, Hawaii, Rapa Nui, Aotearoa)</li><li>Large Pacific floor/wall map</li><li>String for tracing routes</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Suggested Videos</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>The &quot;long pause&quot; in Pacific settlement</em> — search: &quot;long pause Polynesian settlement history mystery&quot;. Explores the real, still-debated historical question central to this lesson.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Student Agency Choices</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Art: Illustrate the Two-Stage Timeline</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Create a visual timeline showing the Lapita spread (West Polynesia) and the much later Eastern Polynesian expansion, with the &quot;long pause&quot; clearly marked between them.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">How did you choose to visually represent a gap that historians still don't fully understand?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Read/Write: A Historian's Honest Answer</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write a short paragraph, in role as a historian, honestly explaining what we know and don't know about the &quot;long pause.&quot;</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Word Count Guide</th><td class=\"lt-td\">120–180 words.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Why might admitting uncertainty actually make a historian more trustworthy, not less?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Technology: Build a Two-Stage Digital Timeline</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Create a simple digital timeline showing both real stages of Pacific settlement, with approximate dates for each.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">What surprised you most about how long the pause really was?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Watch &amp; Review: Watch and Note: The Long Pause</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Watch a short video about the Pacific settlement timeline.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Search term</th><td class=\"lt-td\">&quot;long pause Polynesian settlement history mystery&quot;</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">What theory mentioned in the video did you find most convincing, and why?</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Tuesday — Geography: Trial 27: How Far, Really?":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">THE GATEWAY DISTANCE — Real Numbers Behind the Long Pause</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Social Sciences (Geography)</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Achievement Objectives</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Understanding Place and Environment: use scale and distance to interpret and compare real geographic challenges.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Key Competencies</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Thinking; Using Language, Symbols &amp; Text</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning the real distance between West and East Polynesia — the &quot;gateway&quot; voyage of the long pause.</li><li>We are learning to compare multiple real voyage distances to understand relative difficulty.</li><li>I can use real measurement to support reasoning about a historical decision.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Teacher observation of the measuring activity and the team rankings/justifications.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Introduction</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&quot;Yesterday we discovered the long pause. Today, we measure the real distance voyagers eventually had to cross to end it — and compare it honestly against other voyages we've already studied this term.&quot;</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Main Content</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">The Real Gateway Distance (10 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Teacher Input: the voyage from the Samoa/Tonga region (West Polynesia) to Tahiti and the Society Islands (the gateway into East Polynesia) covers roughly 2,500 kilometres of open ocean — a genuinely serious crossing in its own right, before any further voyages to Hawaii, Rapa Nui, or Aotearoa could even begin.</li><li>Key Question: &quot;Why might crossing this 'gateway' distance first, successfully, have mattered so much for everything that came after it?&quot;</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Measuring and Comparing (15 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Activity: using the measuring-string technique from Week 1, measure and compare the Samoa/Tonga-to-Tahiti distance against the previously studied Tahiti–Hawaii (~4,400 km), Tahiti–Aotearoa (~4,300 km), and Tahiti–Rapa Nui (~4,000 km) distances.</li><li>Activity: rank all four voyages from shortest to longest, and discuss which required the most courage — and why distance isn't the only thing that makes a voyage hard.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Discussion (5 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Key Question: &quot;Now that you know the real numbers, does the 'long pause' make more or less sense to you? Why?&quot;</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Exit Ticket</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Write one sentence: roughly how far is the real &quot;gateway&quot; voyage from Samoa/Tonga to Tahiti?</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Faith Connection</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Ponder: Tahiti to Rapa Nui: 3,700 kilometres of open ocean — the longest migration voyage in human history. And yet Paul writes that nothing in all creation, no height or depth, can separate us from the love of God. No distance is too great for that.</li><li>Verse: &quot;Neither height nor depth, nor anything else in all creation, will be able to separate us from the love of God that is in Christ Jesus our Lord.&quot; — <em>Romans 8:39 (NIV)</em></li><li>Value: Aroha  Love — The love of God stretches further than any ocean voyage — and reaches every person on every island, no matter how remote.</li><li>Discussion questions:<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>Paul says nothing can separate us from God's love — not even 3,700 kilometres of open ocean. Does that feel real to you? What would change if it did?</li><li>The people who settled Rapa Nui were the most isolated humans on earth — and yet God's love still reached them. What does that tell you about God?</li><li>Is there a part of your life or your story where you've felt 'too far out' — separated from God or from others? What does Paul's promise mean for that?</li></ul></li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Large Pacific floor/wall map</li><li>Measuring string from Week 1</li><li>A simple ranking chart</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Suggested Videos</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Mapping the Polynesian voyaging routes</em> — search: &quot;Polynesian voyaging routes map distances explained&quot;. Reinforces the real distances and routes discussed in this lesson.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Student Agency Choices</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Art: Illustrate the Gateway Voyage</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Draw the route from Samoa/Tonga to Tahiti on a simple map, labelling the real distance.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">How does drawing the actual distance change how you picture this voyage?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Read/Write: A Voyager's Calculation</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write a short diary entry, in role as a voyager planning the gateway crossing, weighing up the real distance and the risk involved.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Word Count Guide</th><td class=\"lt-td\">100–150 words.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">What real number from today would have weighed most heavily on your decision?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Technology: Digital Distance Ranking Chart</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Build a simple digital bar chart ranking all four voyage distances studied today and this term.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Which voyage surprised you most once placed on the same chart?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Watch &amp; Review: Watch and Note: Voyaging Distances</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Watch a short video mapping Polynesian voyaging routes.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Search term</th><td class=\"lt-td\">&quot;Polynesian voyaging routes map distances explained&quot;</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">What route shown in the video did you not know about before?</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Wednesday — Science: Trial 28: Different Skies, Different Seas":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">LATITUDE AND CLIMATE — Real Numbers Behind Each New Home</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Science (Planet Earth and Beyond)</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Achievement Objectives</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Investigate how latitude affects climate and conditions experienced in different places.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Key Competencies</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Thinking; Participating &amp; Contributing</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning the real latitude of each major island group settled this term.</li><li>We are learning how latitude genuinely changes climate, star visibility, and seasons.</li><li>I can use real latitude figures to predict a location's likely climate.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Teacher observation of the latitude-line activity and climate-matching task.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Introduction</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&quot;Each island group settled this term sits at a genuinely different latitude — and that changes almost everything about daily life there. Wonder question: do you think Tonga and Aotearoa would feel like the same kind of place to live, or completely different?&quot;</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Main Content</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Real Latitude Figures (10 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Teacher Input: Tonga and Samoa sit at roughly 13–21° south of the equator (tropical), Tahiti at around 17° south (tropical), Hawaii at around 20° north (subtropical), Rapa Nui at around 27° south (subtropical), and Aotearoa stretches from roughly 35° to 47° south (temperate) — a genuinely huge real range across the voyaging story.</li><li>Key Question: &quot;Based on these real numbers, which island group do you think experienced the biggest climate shock moving south?&quot;</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Why Latitude Matters (10 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Teacher Input: recall from Week 4 that Earth's tilt causes seasons — latitude determines how strongly that tilt affects a place. Locations closer to the equator experience milder, more consistent year-round warmth, while locations further from it (like Aotearoa) experience much greater seasonal swings between summer and winter.</li><li>Latitude also changes which stars are visible at night — voyagers travelling to a very different latitude, like Aotearoa, would have noticed real, genuine differences in their night sky compared to their tropical homeland.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Latitude Line Activity (10 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Activity: stretch a 'latitude line' across the floor/room, with students standing at different points representing each island group's real latitude, predicting (then checking) typical weather/temperature for each point.</li><li>Activity: match each island group to a climate description (tropical, subtropical, temperate) and justify using the real latitude figures from today.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Exit Ticket</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Write one sentence: which island group studied this term sits at the most extreme latitude, and what climate does that latitude produce?</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Faith Connection</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Ponder: Hawaii is tropical; Aotearoa is temperate. The same ocean, completely different weather — because of where each island sits in relation to the sun. God built latitude into creation. He built order and variety into the same world, so that every place and every people would be different.</li><li>Verse: &quot;The heavens declare the glory of God; the skies proclaim the work of his hands. Day after day they pour forth speech; night after night they reveal knowledge.&quot; — <em>Psalm 19:1-2 (NIV)</em></li><li>Value: Mōhio  Know — Learning how God designed climate and latitude deepens our wonder at a Creator who made variety and order work together across the whole earth.</li><li>Discussion questions:<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>The sky 'pours forth speech' day after day. What do you think the sky in your part of the world is saying about God?</li><li>God designed climate zones so that every region of the earth is different. What does that variety tell you about God's creativity?</li><li>How might understanding the science of why our weather is the way it is change the way you feel about the place where you live?</li></ul></li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A long rope or marked line for the latitude activity</li><li>Latitude reference cards for each island group</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Suggested Videos</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>How latitude affects climate</em> — search: &quot;how latitude affects climate explained for kids&quot;. Gives a clear, age-appropriate explanation of the real science behind this lesson.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Student Agency Choices</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Art: Illustrate the Latitude Range</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Draw a simple diagram showing each island group placed at its real latitude, from Hawaii in the north to Aotearoa in the south.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">What does the spread of latitudes tell you about how varied this voyaging story really was?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Read/Write: A Voyager Notices the Sky Change</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write a short reflection, in role as a voyager travelling from Tonga to Aotearoa, noticing the real changes in climate and night sky along the way.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Word Count Guide</th><td class=\"lt-td\">100–150 words.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">What change do you think would have been most noticeable first?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Technology: Digital Latitude-Climate Chart</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Build a simple digital chart matching each island group's real latitude to its climate type.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Did any result surprise you, based on what you expected?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Watch &amp; Review: Watch and Note: Latitude and Climate</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Watch a short video about how latitude affects climate.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Search term</th><td class=\"lt-td\">&quot;how latitude affects climate explained for kids&quot;</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">What real example from the video helped the idea make the most sense?</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Thursday — Technology: Trial 29: Then and Now: Sailing Technology":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">FROM WAKA HOURUA TO MODERN CATAMARANS — A Real Engineering Legacy</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Technology</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Achievement Objectives</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Nature of Technology: understand how the same underlying engineering principle can appear in both historical and modern technologies.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Key Competencies</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Thinking; Participating &amp; Contributing</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning that the double-hull principle behind the waka hourua is still used in modern sailing technology today.</li><li>We are learning to fairly compare traditional and modern vessel technology.</li><li>I can identify a genuine engineering connection between historical and modern design.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Teacher observation of the feature-sorting activity and class discussion.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Introduction</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&quot;We met the waka hourua back in Week 2 — a double-hulled design built for stability. Here's a genuinely interesting real fact: that same basic principle is still used in some of the fastest sailing vessels in the world today.&quot;</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Main Content</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">A Real Modern Connection (10 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Teacher Input: modern competitive sailing, including vessels used in events like the America's Cup, often uses multi-hulled designs (catamarans) for exactly the same fundamental reason the waka hourua worked centuries ago — a wider base spread across two hulls resists tipping and allows for greater speed and stability than a single hull alone.</li><li>Key Question: &quot;Why might a genuinely good engineering idea show up again and again across completely different times and cultures?&quot;</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">What Has Actually Changed (10 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Teacher Input: while the core stability principle has carried through, materials, navigation tools, and speed have changed dramatically — modern vessels use lightweight modern materials and GPS navigation instead of woven sails and star compasses.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Feature Sort and Discuss (15 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Activity: sort feature cards (sail material, navigation method, speed, materials, crew size, hull design) into 'Then,' 'Now,' or 'Both,' comparing a traditional waka hourua to a modern sailing vessel.</li><li>Activity: pick one technology that's changed the most and explain why that change mattered; pick one that's stayed essentially the same and explain why it never needed to change.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Exit Ticket</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Write one sentence: what real engineering principle connects the waka hourua to some modern sailing vessels today?</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Faith Connection</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Ponder: The waka hourua with kōwhaiwhai sails and solar panels isn't a contradiction — it's a sign. Traditional wisdom and modern technology can serve the same mission. God's Kingdom works the same way: ancient truth, lived out in a new generation.</li><li>Verse: &quot;Jesus said to them, &quot;Therefore every teacher of the law who has become a disciple in the kingdom of heaven is like the owner of a house who brings out of his storeroom new treasures as well as old.&quot;&quot; — <em>Matthew 13:52 (NIV)</em></li><li>Value: Mōhio  Know — Knowing both what is ancient and true AND what is new and useful helps us serve God's Kingdom wisely in the world we actually live in.</li><li>Discussion questions:<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>Jesus says a wise disciple brings out 'new treasures as well as old.' What 'old treasures' of your faith do you most want to carry into the future?</li><li>The modern waka uses solar panels alongside traditional carvings. What new tools might God want you to use to carry ancient truths into your generation?</li><li>What traditions or wisdom from your family, church, or culture do you most want to preserve — and how?</li></ul></li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Feature sort cards: Then / Now / Both</li><li>Images of modern sailing catamarans if available</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Suggested Videos</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Modern catamarans and ancient double-hull design</em> — search: &quot;catamaran double hull design history Polynesian influence&quot;. Documents the real engineering connection between traditional and modern vessel design central to this lesson.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Student Agency Choices</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Art: Design a &quot;Bridge&quot; Vessel</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Design a vessel that visually bridges the waka hourua and a modern catamaran, labelling one feature kept from each era.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Why did you choose to keep those specific features?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Read/Write: A Letter Between Eras</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write a short imagined letter from a waka hourua builder to a modern catamaran engineer, comparing their designs.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Word Count Guide</th><td class=\"lt-td\">120–180 words.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">What do you think each builder would be most impressed by in the other's work?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Technology: Digital Then/Now/Both Chart</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Build a simple digital three-column chart sorting vessel features into Then, Now, or Both.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Which column ended up with the most features, and why might that be?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Watch &amp; Review: Watch and Note: The Double-Hull Legacy</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Watch a short video about modern catamarans and their historical design roots.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Search term</th><td class=\"lt-td\">&quot;catamaran double hull design history Polynesian influence&quot;</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">What part of the video most clearly showed the real connection between old and new?</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Friday — Art: Trial 35: Many Hands, One Ocean":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">REGIONAL PACIFIC PATTERN PAINTING — One Tradition per Square</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">The Arts (Visual Art)</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Achievement Objectives</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Developing Practical Knowledge: explore and use art-making conventions from different cultural traditions.</li><li>Understanding the Arts in Context: investigate how cultural identity is expressed through visual art.</li><li>Communicating and Interpreting: describe how their own art work communicates cultural connection.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Key Competencies</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Thinking; Relating to Others; Using Language, Symbols &amp; Text</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning that different Pacific cultures developed distinct visual traditions from a shared ancestor.</li><li>We are practising painting in the conventions of ONE chosen Pacific regional style.</li><li>I can explain the artistic choices I made and how they connect to the region I chose.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Completed regional pattern square with written identification of region, colour choices, and motif.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Introduction</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Display three examples side by side: Hawaiian kapa geometric bands, Tongan ngatu tapa diamonds, Māori kōwhaiwhai scrolls. Ask: &quot;These all came from the same ancestral Pacific culture — how did they become so different?&quot; Today each student chooses ONE and works entirely within its conventions.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Main Content</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Choosing a Regional Style and Planning (8 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Choose one: Hawaiian (bold horizontal bands in earthy red/black/brown), Tongan (repeating diamonds and crosses in dark brown/black on white), or Māori (kōwhaiwhai koru scrolls in black/red/white).</li><li>Sketch a rough plan in pencil — what pattern fills the square? What colour palette? How does it repeat?</li><li>Teacher prompt: &quot;Is this pattern speaking in one voice, or mixing styles? Keep it clean and single-region.&quot;</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Painting the Regional Pattern (15 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Paint chosen pattern using only 2–3 colours from that region.</li><li>Focus on repetition and rhythm. The pattern should feel like it could extend beyond the edges.</li><li>Teacher prompt: &quot;Pacific artists worked within rules. The rules are what made the pattern recognisable as theirs.&quot;</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Assembly and Comparison (7 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Write one sentence identifying the chosen region and why they chose it.</li><li>Groups assemble this week's squares — the quilt shows three different Pacific traditions side by side.</li><li>Ask groups: &quot;Which square looks most different from the others? Why might that region have developed so differently?&quot;</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Exit Ticket</th><td class=\"lt-td\">One sentence: what did working within strict colour and pattern rules feel like?</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Faith Connection</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Ponder: Hawaiian kapa, Tongan ngatu, Māori kōwhaiwhai — three different traditions from one shared ancestor. Every Pacific tradition is a different expression of the same human impulse: to make beauty, and to say something true through it. God is glorified in all of it.</li><li>Verse: &quot;There is neither Jew nor Gentile, neither slave nor free, nor is there male and female, for you are all one in Christ Jesus.&quot; — <em>Galatians 3:28 (NIV)</em></li><li>Value: Aroha  Love — Every person and culture is equally loved by God — and our different ways of creating beauty reflect the richness of His image across humanity.</li><li>Discussion questions:<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>Paul says we are 'all one in Christ' while remaining different people from different places. What does unity look like that doesn't erase difference?</li><li>Your regional pattern today is unique to one tradition. What do you think would be lost if all Pacific cultures had looked the same?</li><li>How might celebrating the art traditions of other cultures be an act of love?</li></ul></li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Reference images of Hawaiian kapa, Tongan ngatu, and Māori kōwhaiwhai</li><li>A4 paper squares</li><li>Paint, dye, or felt tip in regional colour palettes</li><li>Pencils for planning sketches</li><li>Group quilt backing sheets</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Suggested Videos</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Pacific tapa cloth traditions</em> — search: &quot;Pacific tapa cloth ngatu kapa bark cloth tradition&quot;. Shows the range of tapa/kapa traditions and how they are made.</li><li><em>Kōwhaiwhai patterns in context</em> — search: &quot;kowhaiwhai patterns wharenui Maori art rafter&quot;. Shows kōwhaiwhai patterns in real wharenui, reinforcing the convention-based nature of the art form.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Student Agency Choices</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Art: Paint Your Regional Pattern Square</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This week's main activity IS the art focus — paint your chosen regional pattern using only that region's colours and motifs.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">What rule did you find hardest to stick to?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Read/Write: The Same Ancestors</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write 2–3 sentences: if Hawaiian, Tongan, and Māori art all came from the same original Pacific culture, what does that tell us about how cultures change over time?</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">What does &quot;the same but different&quot; mean to you?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Technology: Digital Regional Pattern</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Use a drawing tool to create a digital version of your chosen regional pattern using only that region's colours.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Did working digitally make it easier or harder to stick to the regional conventions?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Watch &amp; Review: Watch: Pacific Tapa Cloth Traditions</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Watch a short video on Pacific tapa cloth traditions.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Search term</th><td class=\"lt-td\">&quot;Pacific tapa cloth ngatu kapa bark cloth tradition&quot;</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">What connected the different regional tapa traditions even though they look different?</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"}}},"Week 8":{"label":"Day","children":{"Monday — History: Trial 31: Passed Down, Not Written Down":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">WHAKAPAPA AND ORAL TRADITION — A Real, Sophisticated System of Memory</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Social Sciences</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Achievement Objectives</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Understanding the Past: investigate how stories, traditions, and customs are passed down across generations, and recognise this as a valid form of historical record.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Key Competencies</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Thinking; Language, Symbols &amp; Text; Relating to Others</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning what whakapapa is and how it functions as a genuine system of recorded history.</li><li>We are learning real techniques (rhythm, repetition, pattern) that help oral traditions survive accurately across generations.</li><li>I can explain why oral tradition is a legitimate historical record, not just &quot;stories.&quot;</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Teacher observation of the memory-chain activity and class discussion. Review of student-created chants for understanding of rhythm/repetition techniques.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Introduction</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&quot;For seven weeks we've followed Tama's voyage using a journal that magically records itself. But real history mostly wasn't recorded that way at all. Today we explore something genuinely impressive: how an entire history can be carried accurately across hundreds of years, without a single page of paper.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Wonder question: if you had to memorise something important enough that it had to be passed down perfectly for 20 generations, how would you even begin?&quot;</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Main Content</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">What Is Whakapapa? (10 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Teacher Input: whakapapa is a genealogical framework — a way of tracing lines of descent connecting people back through generations, often all the way to significant ancestors, and even to atua (deities) in traditional belief. It is far more than a simple list of names; it functions as a structured, memorised historical record, carrying real information about identity, land connections, and significant events alongside the genealogy itself.</li><li>Key Question: &quot;Why might knowing exactly who you're descended from also mean knowing a great deal of real history at the same time?&quot;</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Real Techniques for Accurate Memory (12 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Teacher Input: oral traditions across many cultures, including in the Pacific, rely on real, deliberate techniques to help information survive accurately across generations — rhythm and repetition (making information easier to recall correctly, the same reason songs are easier to remember than plain speech), waiata (song) and karakia (chant) as structured formats for preserving important information, and the practice of regular, deliberate retelling, often in formal or ceremonial settings, which helped check and correct the story over time.</li><li>Key Question: &quot;Why might a story told the SAME way, in a formal setting, every single time, be less likely to change accidentally than a casual story told differently each time?&quot;</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Memory Chain Activity (10 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Activity: teach a short, simple chant or rhythmic phrase (summarising part of this term's voyage story) and pass it person-to-person around the room purely by listening and repeating, then compare the start and end versions.</li><li>Discuss: what changed (if anything), and what made the parts that survived intact easier to remember?</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Exit Ticket</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Write one sentence: what is whakapapa, and why is it more than just a list of names?</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Faith Connection</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Ponder: Hina taught Tama the chants, generation after generation, no paper required. Moses commanded Israel to teach God's words to their children constantly — in the morning, at bedtime, walking the road. Oral tradition was how God's story survived for centuries before a single word was written down.</li><li>Verse: &quot;Impress these commandments on your children. Talk about them when you sit at home and when you walk along the road, when you lie down and when you get up.&quot; — <em>Deuteronomy 6:7 (NIV)</em></li><li>Value: Manaaki  Serve — Faithfully passing on what we've received — stories, wisdom, faith — is one of the most important ways we serve the generations who come after us.</li><li>Discussion questions:<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>God commanded parents to talk about His words 'when you walk along the road.' Who in your life has passed something important to you just through ordinary conversation?</li><li>Oral tradition kept the Pacific peoples' history alive for centuries. What story from your family or faith community do YOU need to be a keeper of?</li><li>What would you most want to pass on to the generation after yours — and how would you do it without any technology?</li></ul></li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A simple chant/phrase prepared in advance for the memory chain activity</li><li>Paper for student-created chants</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Suggested Videos</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>What is whakapapa?</em> — search: &quot;what is whakapapa explained Maori genealogy&quot;. Provides a real, respectful, age-appropriate explanation of whakapapa as a genuine historical record system.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Student Agency Choices</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Art: Illustrate a Whakapapa-Style Chart</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Create a simple, respectful illustrated diagram showing how a genealogical chart connects generations, using a fictional family line as your example (not a real whakapapa, out of respect).</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">What surprised you about how much information a chart like this can hold?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Read/Write: A Story Worth Remembering</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write a short chant or rhyme (in your own words) summarising one key moment from this term's voyage, using rhythm and repetition to make it memorable.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Word Count Guide</th><td class=\"lt-td\">60–100 words.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">What choices did you make to help your chant be easy to remember accurately?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Technology: Record a Spoken Chant</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>If equipment allows, record yourself or your group performing a chant created in class, and consider how recording changes (or doesn't change) the oral tradition.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Does recording a chant change what makes oral tradition special?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Watch &amp; Review: Watch and Note: Whakapapa</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Watch a short video explaining whakapapa.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Search term</th><td class=\"lt-td\">&quot;what is whakapapa explained Maori genealogy&quot;</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">What detail from the video most changed how you think about &quot;history&quot; as a concept?</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Tuesday — Geography: Trial 32: The Ocean Today":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">THE GREAT PACIFIC GARBAGE PATCH — A Real, Current Challenge</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Social Sciences (Geography)</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Achievement Objectives</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Understanding Place and Environment: explore how people's actions affect the sustainability of natural environments, both locally and globally.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Key Competencies</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Thinking; Participating &amp; Contributing</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning what is genuinely happening in the Pacific Ocean today, connecting back to currents studied in Week 2.</li><li>We are learning that the same forces that helped real voyagers can also carry real problems.</li><li>I can identify a realistic, age-appropriate action connected to a real environmental issue.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Teacher observation of the sorting activity and the realistic action-pledge discussion.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Introduction</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&quot;In Week 2, we learned how ocean currents acted like roads, carrying real voyagers across the Pacific. Today we look honestly at something happening in those same waters right now — gently, but truthfully.&quot;</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Main Content</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">A Real, Current Issue (10 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Teacher Input: the same ocean currents studied in Week 2 are real, and they are known to gather floating plastic debris into large, diffuse collection zones — the best known being the Great Pacific Garbage Patch, located between Hawaii and California. It is important to clarify accurately: it isn't a solid 'island' of rubbish, but a vast area where plastic debris, much of it broken into small fragments, becomes more concentrated than surrounding ocean water.</li><li>Key Question: &quot;Why might it matter to describe this accurately, rather than imagining something more dramatic (like a solid floating island) than what's really there?&quot;</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Connecting It Back (8 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Teacher Input: revisit Week 2's current map — the very same real current systems that carried voyaging canoes can also carry plastic debris released into the ocean today, gathering in similar predictable patterns.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">A Realistic Response (12 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Activity: sort picture cards of ocean debris versus natural materials into two groups.</li><li>Activity: in groups, brainstorm one realistic action your class/school could genuinely take connected to ocean care (e.g. reducing single-use plastic, a litter-free lunch initiative, writing to a local council) — keep it achievable, not symbolic.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Exit Ticket</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Write one sentence accurately describing what the Great Pacific Garbage Patch actually is (and isn't).</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Faith Connection</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Ponder: The same currents that carried the voyagers now carry plastic. Genesis 2 says God put humans in the garden to 'tend and keep' it — to work it and care for it. That responsibility didn't end in Eden. It travels with us to every new shore.</li><li>Verse: &quot;The LORD God took the man and put him in the Garden of Eden to work it and take care of it.&quot; — <em>Genesis 2:15 (NIV)</em></li><li>Value: Pānga  Impact — Caring for the ocean — and all of creation — is part of bringing God's Kingdom to earth: tending and keeping what He made and called 'very good.'</li><li>Discussion questions:<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>God put humans in the garden to 'work it AND take care of it.' What do you think the difference is between those two things?</li><li>Tama asked you to 'show him there is still hope' for the ocean. What hope can you genuinely offer?</li><li>What is one concrete thing you could do this week to be a better 'keeper' of the part of creation you live in?</li></ul></li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Picture cards: ocean debris vs. natural materials</li><li>The Week 2 ocean current map for reference</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Suggested Videos</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>What is the Great Pacific Garbage Patch?</em> — search: &quot;Great Pacific Garbage Patch explained for kids&quot;. Provides an accurate, age-appropriate explanation, avoiding the common &quot;floating island&quot; misconception.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Student Agency Choices</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Art: Illustrate the Real Scale</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Draw a diagram accurately showing the Great Pacific Garbage Patch as a diffuse area of scattered debris (not a solid island), labelled with its real location between Hawaii and California.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Why does an accurate drawing matter more than a dramatic one here?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Read/Write: A Realistic Pledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write a short, realistic pledge for your class or school connected to ocean care, explaining why you chose that specific action.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Word Count Guide</th><td class=\"lt-td\">80–120 words.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">What makes your pledge realistic rather than just a nice idea?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Technology: Digital Awareness Poster</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Create a simple, accurate digital poster about the Great Pacific Garbage Patch, correcting one common misconception.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">What misconception did you choose to correct, and why?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Watch &amp; Review: Watch and Note: Ocean Plastic</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Watch a short video on the Great Pacific Garbage Patch.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Search term</th><td class=\"lt-td\">&quot;Great Pacific Garbage Patch explained for kids&quot;</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">What fact from the video surprised you most?</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Wednesday — Science: Trial 33: When the Sky Turns Violent":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">PACIFIC CYCLONES — Real Science, Real Preparedness</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Science (Planet Earth and Beyond)</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Achievement Objectives</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Investigate extreme weather phenomena and how communities prepare for and respond to them.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Key Competencies</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Thinking; Participating &amp; Contributing</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning the real science of how tropical cyclones form.</li><li>We are learning genuine, real ways Pacific communities prepare for extreme weather today.</li><li>I can combine traditional and modern knowledge into a realistic safety plan.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Teacher observation of the checklist-building activity. Review of student understanding connecting cyclone formation to earlier weather/climate content (Week 1).</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Introduction</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&quot;Back in Week 1, we learned the real difference between weather and climate. Today we look at one of the most powerful real weather events in the Pacific: tropical cyclones — and how real communities prepare for them, both today and in the past.&quot;</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Main Content</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">How Cyclones Really Form (12 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Teacher Input: tropical cyclones form over warm ocean water (generally above 26.5°C), where warm, moist air rises rapidly, creating an area of low pressure. As more warm, moist air rushes in to replace it, the system begins to rotate (influenced by the Earth's rotation, similar to the trade wind effect studied in Week 2), eventually forming the organised, swirling storm system recognisable as a cyclone.</li><li>Key Question: &quot;Why does it make sense that the warm tropical Pacific — the very same region voyagers like Tama sailed through — is also a real region where cyclones commonly form?&quot;</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Real Modern Preparedness (10 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Teacher Input: Pacific nations today use real, modern early-warning systems — satellite tracking, weather forecasting services, and official cyclone warning categories — to give communities real advance notice, often days ahead, to prepare or evacuate if necessary.</li><li>Key Question: &quot;What real advantage does days of advance warning give a community, compared to a voyager reading the sky just hours before a storm hit?&quot;</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Building a Combined Checklist (8 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Activity: in groups, create a 'storm preparedness checklist' combining one traditional sky/sea-reading sign (from earlier weeks) with one modern safety practice (early warning systems, secured shelter, emergency supplies).</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Exit Ticket</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Write one sentence explaining, in your own words, how a tropical cyclone actually forms.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Faith Connection</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Ponder: Tama's storm in Week 3 was terrifying. The cyclones in your time are stronger. But Mark 4 records a moment when Jesus stood in a boat being swamped by waves — and spoke three words that stopped the storm. He is Lord over the very things we fear most.</li><li>Verse: &quot;He got up, rebuked the wind and said to the waves, &quot;Quiet! Be still!&quot; Then the wind died down and it was completely calm.&quot; — <em>Mark 4:39 (NIV)</em></li><li>Value: Aroha  Love — The God who calms the storm loves us enough to be with us in the storm — and has power over every force we face.</li><li>Discussion questions:<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>The disciples were terrified even though Jesus was IN the boat. Have you ever been scared of something even while knowing God was with you? What happened?</li><li>Jesus didn't stop the storm before it happened — He was in it with them first. What does that tell you about how God works with us in hard times?</li><li>What is the storm in your life right now that you most need to hear 'Peace! Be still!' spoken into?</li></ul></li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Simple diagram of cyclone formation if available</li><li>Checklist templates for the combined activity</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Suggested Videos</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>How do cyclones form?</em> — search: &quot;how do tropical cyclones form explained for kids&quot;. Gives a clear, accurate, age-appropriate explanation of the real science behind this lesson.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Student Agency Choices</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Art: Illustrate Cyclone Formation</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Draw a labelled diagram showing warm ocean water, rising air, and the rotating storm system forming above it.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">What part of the process was trickiest to show clearly in a drawing?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Read/Write: A Community Preparedness Notice</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write a short, clear preparedness notice (as if for a real community) combining a traditional sign and a modern safety step.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Word Count Guide</th><td class=\"lt-td\">100–150 words.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Why might combining both kinds of knowledge make a community safer than relying on just one?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Technology: Digital Cyclone Tracker Mockup</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Create a simple digital mockup of a cyclone tracking map, showing a storm's path and a warning zone.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">What information would be most important to show clearly on a real tracker?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Watch &amp; Review: Watch and Note: Cyclone Science</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Watch a short video about how cyclones form.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Search term</th><td class=\"lt-td\">&quot;how do tropical cyclones form explained for kids&quot;</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">What real fact from the video would you want a younger student to know?</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Thursday — Technology: Trial 34: A Voice from the Sky":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">GPS — Real Technology, Honestly Compared to Real Wayfinding</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Technology</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Achievement Objectives</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Nature of Technology: understand that technologies have a real impact on society, and that no technology is purely &quot;better&quot; without considering trade-offs.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Key Competencies</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Thinking; Participating &amp; Contributing</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning what GPS actually is and how it genuinely works.</li><li>We are learning to fairly compare GPS with traditional wayfinding, without assuming newer is automatically better.</li><li>I can evaluate two technologies in different realistic scenarios rather than declaring one universally superior.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Teacher observation of the scenario-decision activity and class discussion on trade-offs.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Introduction</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&quot;We've spent this whole term learning real, sophisticated traditional wayfinding. Today we meet GPS honestly — not as something that makes the old ways obsolete, but as one more real tool with its own real strengths and real limitations.&quot;</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Main Content</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">How GPS Actually Works (10 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Teacher Input: GPS (Global Positioning System) relies on a network of satellites orbiting Earth, each constantly broadcasting a precise time signal. A GPS receiver (like one in a phone or ship) calculates its exact position by measuring the tiny time differences between signals received from several satellites at once — a genuinely clever piece of real engineering.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Real Strengths and Real Limitations (10 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Teacher Input: GPS is extremely accurate and doesn't require years of training to use — but it depends entirely on functioning satellites, a working receiver, and battery power, and it provides only position and direction, not the rich environmental awareness (wind, swells, wildlife, sky signs) a trained wayfinder builds over years.</li><li>Key Question: &quot;In what realistic situation might GPS actually fail you, when traditional wayfinding skills wouldn't?&quot;</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Scenario Decisions (10 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Activity: present realistic scenarios (clear night with good stars vs. GPS device with a dead battery; cloudy night with no stars but working GPS; a sudden equipment failure mid-voyage) and have teams decide which method they'd trust in each case, with reasoning.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Exit Ticket</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Write one sentence: name one real strength and one real limitation of GPS navigation.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Faith Connection</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Ponder: GPS tells you exactly where you are and exactly where to go. But it requires a signal, a device, and a battery. Walking with God is more like the star compass — slower to learn, but internalized, always available, and pointing truly.</li><li>Verse: &quot;For we live by faith, not by sight.&quot; — <em>2 Corinthians 5:7 (NIV)</em></li><li>Value: Mōhio  Know — Knowing God deeply — not just using spiritual 'tools' — means developing a relationship with Him that guides us from the inside, not just the outside.</li><li>Discussion questions:<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>'By faith, not by sight' — what is the difference between trusting something you can see and trusting something you can't? Which is harder?</li><li>Hina's star knowledge was memorised, internalised, always available. What spiritual truths do you have so deeply in you that they guide you automatically?</li><li>In what area of your life right now are you being asked to walk by faith rather than by what you can see?</li></ul></li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Scenario cards for the decision activity</li><li>A real GPS device or phone map app for demonstration if available</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Suggested Videos</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>How does GPS work?</em> — search: &quot;how does GPS work satellites explained for kids&quot;. Gives a clear, accurate, age-appropriate explanation of the real technology discussed in this lesson.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Student Agency Choices</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Art: Illustrate GPS Satellites at Work</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Draw a diagram showing satellites in orbit sending signals down to a receiver, labelling the basic process.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">What part of how GPS works surprised you most?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Read/Write: A Fair Comparison</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write a short, balanced comparison of GPS and traditional wayfinding, including a genuine strength and limitation of each.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Word Count Guide</th><td class=\"lt-td\">120–180 words.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Why is it more honest to compare fairly than to declare one method simply &quot;better&quot;?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Technology: Digital Scenario Decision Chart</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Build a simple digital chart listing today's scenarios and which navigation method you'd choose for each, with a one-line reason.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Did any scenario genuinely surprise you in your own decision?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Watch &amp; Review: Watch and Note: How GPS Works</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Watch a short video explaining GPS technology.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Search term</th><td class=\"lt-td\">&quot;how does GPS work satellites explained for kids&quot;</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">What real-world example from the video helped GPS make sense to you?</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Friday — Art: Trial 40: Old Motifs, New Square":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">BLENDED DESIGN — Drawing and Painting Two Traditions Together</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">The Arts (Visual Art)</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Achievement Objectives</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Developing Practical Knowledge: use drawing and painting to combine and transform visual ideas.</li><li>Communicating and Interpreting: explain how their art work expresses a personal response to cultural traditions.</li><li>Understanding the Arts in Context: explore how contemporary Pacific artists blend traditional and modern influences.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Key Competencies</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Thinking; Managing Self; Using Language, Symbols &amp; Text</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning how contemporary Pacific artists combine traditional motifs to create something new.</li><li>We are practising intentional design — making choices about why two motifs belong together.</li><li>I can create a blended design square and explain the relationship between the two motifs I chose.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Completed blended square and written explanation of the two motifs chosen and how they connect.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Introduction</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Share an example of contemporary Pacific art blending traditions. Ask: &quot;How do you know this is both traditional AND modern? What rules did the artist keep? What did they change?&quot; Today's square is a dialogue between two earlier traditions the student has already practised.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Main Content</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Choosing and Planning the Combination (8 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Look back at the group's quilt squares from Weeks 1–7. Choose TWO motifs to bring together: e.g. kōwhaiwhai scroll with petroglyph turtle, woven grid background with star cluster dots, regional tapa pattern with kōwhaiwhai border.</li><li>Sketch a rough plan: which motif is the dominant layer? Which is the accent? How will they sit together without one overwhelming the other?</li><li>Teacher prompt: &quot;This is not a random combination. Why do THESE two belong together on YOUR square?&quot;</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Drawing and Painting the Blended Square (15 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Draw the background motif in pencil first — lightly, as a foundation layer. Paint in its traditional colours.</li><li>Add the second motif on top or interwoven, using its own colours. Allow overlap where it feels intentional.</li><li>Teacher prompt: &quot;Does this feel like a conversation between two ideas, or just two things on one page?&quot;</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Reflection and Assembly (7 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Write 2 sentences on the back: which two motifs combined and what the combination is trying to say.</li><li>Add square to group quilt. Groups step back and look at the full sequence of 8 squares.</li><li>Ask: &quot;What story does your group's quilt tell from Week 1 to Week 8?&quot;</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Exit Ticket</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Describe your blended square in one sentence, starting with: &quot;This square shows...&quot;</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Faith Connection</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Ponder: Today's square blends two earlier motifs into something new. This is exactly what Paul describes: we are God's masterpiece, His workmanship, crafted with care and purpose. And masterpieces take time, layering, and intention.</li><li>Verse: &quot;For we are God's handiwork, created in Christ Jesus to do good works, which God prepared in advance for us to do.&quot; — <em>Ephesians 2:10 (NIV)</em></li><li>Value: Pānga  Impact — Every act of creation done with intention and care reflects the God who made us — and brings His Kingdom creativity into the world.</li><li>Discussion questions:<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>Paul calls us God's 'handiwork' — literally His masterpiece. Does that feel true for you? What makes it hard to believe sometimes?</li><li>Your blended square combines two traditions into something new. How might God be combining different parts of YOUR life and experience into something that couldn't exist any other way?</li><li>God 'prepared in advance' the good works He has for you. What does it feel like to know your life's purpose was thought about before you were born?</li></ul></li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A4 paper, one square per student (the final square for the group quilt)</li><li>Pencils, erasers, and paint or coloured pencils matching earlier weeks' palettes</li><li>Students' completed quilt squares from Weeks 1–7 for reference</li><li>Reference images of contemporary Pacific artists blending traditional and modern styles</li><li>Scrap paper for planning the motif combination before painting</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Suggested Videos</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Contemporary Pacific art blending traditions</em> — search: &quot;contemporary Pacific Island artists traditional motifs&quot;. Shows real contemporary Pacific artists combining traditional motifs in new ways, modelling the blending task students are asked to do.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Student Agency Choices</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Art: Create Your Blended Quilt Square</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This week's main activity IS the art focus — combine two earlier motifs into one blended square and add it as the final piece in your group's quilt.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Why do your two chosen motifs belong together on this square?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Read/Write: Explain Your Blend</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write a short explanation of the two motifs you combined and what their combination says about the whole voyage.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Word Count Guide</th><td class=\"lt-td\">100–150 words.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">How is combining two traditions different from just copying one?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Technology: Digital Blended Design</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Use a simple drawing tool (Canva, Google Drawings) to layer two motifs from earlier weeks into one digital design.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">What was harder about combining two ideas than using just one?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Watch &amp; Review: Watch and Note: Blending Traditions</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Watch a short video on contemporary Pacific artists blending traditional and modern styles.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Search term</th><td class=\"lt-td\">&quot;contemporary Pacific Island artists traditional motifs&quot;</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">What did the artist keep from tradition, and what did they change?</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"}}},"Week 9":{"label":"Day","children":{"Monday — History: Trial 36: Tama's Descendants":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">PACIFIC PEOPLES IN AOTEAROA TODAY — A Living, Continuing Story</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Social Sciences</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Achievement Objectives</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Understanding the Past: understand how the past is connected to the present, and that historical events have living, ongoing consequences.</li><li>Understanding Diversity: understand how cultural identity is shaped by both heritage and present-day community.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Key Competencies</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Thinking; Relating to Others; Participating &amp; Contributing</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning that the Pacific migration story studied this term is not only history — it has living, present-day continuation.</li><li>We are learning that Aotearoa today is home to large, real, and diverse Pacific communities.</li><li>I can recognise and respectfully discuss the connection between a historical event and present-day people and communities.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Teacher observation of the class discussion and the acknowledgement statement created together. This is a sensitive, relational lesson — assess respectful engagement and thoughtful reflection over factual recall alone.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Introduction</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&quot;For eight weeks, we've followed a single voyage from thousands of years ago. Today, we close the gap between then and now: the real descendants of voyages like Tama's are alive today, all around us, including right here in Aotearoa.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Wonder question: have you ever thought about how a story from thousands of years ago could still be genuinely connected to people living in your own town today?&quot;</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Main Content</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">A Living, Continuing Story (10 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Teacher Input: the Pacific migration story studied this term did not end in the past — Aotearoa today is home to large, vibrant Pacific communities, including people of Samoan, Tongan, Cook Islands, Niuean, Fijian, and many other Pacific heritages, alongside tangata whenua Māori whose own history traces directly back to the voyaging story explored this term.</li><li>Key Question: &quot;Why might it matter, after a whole term studying this as 'history,' to also recognise it as a living, present-day reality for real people today?&quot;</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Handling This Respectfully (8 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Teacher Input: this is a moment for genuine respect and care. If any students in the class have personal or family connections to this story, this is an opportunity for pride and voice, never an obligation or a spotlight. Make clear that sharing is always entirely optional, and that everyone's connection to today's lesson, personal or purely academic, is equally valid.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">A Class Acknowledgement (12 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Activity: as a respectful class discussion/circle, talk about Pacific communities living in Aotearoa today — their cultures, languages, and continuing connection to the voyaging story learned this term.</li><li>Activity: as a class, create a simple, sincere acknowledgement statement recognising the achievement of the original voyagers, to read aloud together as a meaningful close to the discussion.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Exit Ticket</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Write one sentence: why does it matter that the story we've studied this term is not only history, but also a living, present-day reality?</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Faith Connection</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Ponder: Tama says his descendants might be in this very room. Kapa haka is the living proof — the chant survived, the culture survived, the story survived. Psalm 145 describes exactly this: one generation commending God's works to the next, the chain unbroken across centuries.</li><li>Verse: &quot;One generation commends your works to another; they tell of your mighty acts.&quot; — <em>Psalm 145:4 (NIV)</em></li><li>Value: Manaaki  Serve — Faithfully passing on what we have received — stories, culture, faith — is how we serve the generations who come after us.</li><li>Discussion questions:<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>Psalm 145 says each generation commends God's works to the next. What has been commended to you — by a parent, a grandparent, a teacher — that you don't want to lose?</li><li>Kapa haka kept the culture alive across centuries with no written records. What is something in YOUR culture or faith that must be passed on person to person, not just written down?</li><li>Who are you already 'commending God's works' to — even if you don't think of it that way?</li></ul></li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>No specific physical resources required — this lesson centres on respectful class discussion</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Suggested Videos</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Pacific communities in Aotearoa today</em> — search: &quot;Pacific peoples in New Zealand today communities culture&quot;. Provides real, respectful, contemporary context connecting this term's history to present-day communities.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Student Agency Choices</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Art: Illustrate &quot;Then and Now&quot; Side by Side</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Create a simple illustration showing a voyager from this term's story on one side, and a contemporary scene of Pacific community life today on the other, connected by a single thread or line.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">What did you choose to connect the two halves of your illustration, and why?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Read/Write: A Letter Across Time</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write a short, respectful letter from a present-day perspective to a voyager like Tama, telling them what their courage helped make possible today.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Word Count Guide</th><td class=\"lt-td\">120–180 words.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">What did you most want a voyager from the past to know about today?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Technology: Digital Acknowledgement Display</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Create a simple digital slide or poster of the class's acknowledgement statement, suitable for sharing or displaying.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">What made your class's wording feel genuine and sincere, rather than generic?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Watch &amp; Review: Watch and Note: Living Connections</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Watch a short video about Pacific communities in Aotearoa today.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Search term</th><td class=\"lt-td\">&quot;Pacific peoples in New Zealand today communities culture&quot;</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">What connection between past and present stood out to you most?</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Tuesday — Geography: Trial 37: Caring for the Ocean":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">REAL PACIFIC OCEAN CONSERVATION — Genuine Hope in Action</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Social Sciences (Geography)</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Achievement Objectives</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Understanding Place and Environment: explore how individuals and communities take real, active responsibility for environmental sustainability.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Key Competencies</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Thinking; Participating &amp; Contributing</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning about real, genuine ocean conservation efforts happening in the Pacific today.</li><li>We are learning to design a realistic, achievable action connected to ocean care.</li><li>I can identify a real example of positive environmental action and explain why it matters.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Teacher observation of the research/discussion activity and the realistic pledge created.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Introduction</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&quot;Last week, we faced an honest, difficult truth about plastic in the Pacific. Today, we look for real, genuine hope — because real people and real communities across the Pacific are actively working to protect the ocean right now.&quot;</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Main Content</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Real Conservation in Action (12 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Teacher Input: present 1–2 real, simplified examples of Pacific ocean conservation. One genuine example: large-scale Marine Protected Areas have been established by several Pacific nations (such as the Cook Islands' Marae Moana, one of the world's largest protected ocean areas) to limit harmful fishing practices and protect marine ecosystems. Community-led beach and reef clean-up initiatives are also real, ongoing efforts across many Pacific communities, including in Aotearoa.</li><li>Key Question: &quot;Why might protecting a very large area of ocean, rather than just a small part, make a genuinely bigger real difference?&quot;</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Researching and Sharing (10 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Activity: in small groups, research or discuss (using teacher-provided simplified material) one real example of ocean conservation, then present it back to the class as &quot;good news for Tama.&quot;</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">A Realistic Pledge (8 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Activity: design a realistic, genuinely doable conservation pledge for your own class or school to commit to — emphasise achievable action over symbolic gestures.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Exit Ticket</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Write one sentence naming ONE real, genuine example of Pacific ocean conservation discussed today.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Faith Connection</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Ponder: Tama asks you to show him there is still hope for the ocean. The answer — healthy fish, marine reserves, communities taking care of what they received — is the answer Genesis always pointed to: we were made to tend and keep, and when we do, life returns.</li><li>Verse: &quot;God saw all that he had made, and it was very good.&quot; — <em>Genesis 1:31 (NIV)</em></li><li>Value: Pānga  Impact — Caring for creation — keeping it 'very good' — is one of the most direct ways we bring God's Kingdom purposes to earth.</li><li>Discussion questions:<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>God called creation 'very good.' What does it mean for us to be guardians of something God Himself declared good?</li><li>The healthy fish in the marine reserve show what creation looks like when humans protect rather than exploit it. What does that give you hope about?</li><li>What one specific action could you commit to this term that helps 'keep' part of God's good creation?</li></ul></li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Simplified research material on real conservation examples (teacher-prepared)</li><li>Paper for pledge-writing</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Suggested Videos</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Pacific Ocean conservation success stories</em> — search: &quot;Pacific Ocean conservation marine protected area success&quot;. Provides real, genuine examples of positive environmental action in the Pacific, central to this lesson's hopeful framing.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Student Agency Choices</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Art: Illustrate a Protected Ocean Area</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Draw a scene showing a thriving, protected area of ocean, labelling at least one reason it's protected.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">What did you choose to show thriving in your protected ocean scene?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Read/Write: Good News for Tama</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write a short, hopeful letter to Tama sharing the real conservation example you researched today.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Word Count Guide</th><td class=\"lt-td\">100–150 words.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Why did you choose to share this particular piece of good news with him?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Technology: Digital Conservation Fact File</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Create a simple digital fact file about one real Pacific conservation initiative, including what it protects and why it matters.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">What real number or fact from your research surprised you most?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Watch &amp; Review: Watch and Note: Real Ocean Hope</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Watch a short video about a real Pacific Ocean conservation success.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Search term</th><td class=\"lt-td\">&quot;Pacific Ocean conservation marine protected area success&quot;</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">What part of the video felt most genuinely hopeful to you?</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Wednesday — Science: Trial 38: A Rising Sea":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">SEA LEVEL RISE — Real Science, Gently and Honestly Explained</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Science (Planet Earth and Beyond)</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Achievement Objectives</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Investigate how environmental change affects places and communities, using real scientific evidence appropriate to the age group.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Key Competencies</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Thinking; Participating &amp; Contributing</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning the real, basic science behind sea level rise.</li><li>We are learning why this issue genuinely matters more for some Pacific communities than others.</li><li>I can explain a real environmental process using a simple, accurate model.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Teacher observation of the demonstration and group discussion. This is a sensitive topic — assess understanding and thoughtful engagement, framed with genuine care rather than alarm.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Introduction</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&quot;Today's topic deserves real gentleness and real honesty, both at once. We're going to understand, simply and accurately, why sea levels are rising — and why this matters so much to some of the very places connected to our voyage this term.&quot;</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Main Content</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">The Real, Basic Science (10 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Teacher Input: sea levels are rising for two main real reasons: thermal expansion (warmer ocean water genuinely takes up slightly more space than cooler water) and melting land ice (glaciers and ice sheets on land, when they melt, add genuinely new water into the ocean that wasn't there before).</li><li>Demonstration: use a clear container with ice and water to gently demonstrate (note: ice already floating in water melting does NOT raise the water level much, since it already displaces its own weight — but ice melting that was sitting on LAND and running into the ocean genuinely does add new water. This is an important, accurate distinction worth making clearly to avoid a common misconception.)</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Why It Matters More for Some Places (10 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Teacher Input: low-lying Pacific island nations (some with average heights only a few metres above sea level) face genuinely real, serious challenges from even relatively small rises in sea level, in ways that don't affect higher, larger landmasses like Aotearoa in the same way.</li><li>Key Question: &quot;Why might the very same amount of sea level rise affect two different real places so differently?&quot;</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Holding Hope and Honesty Together (10 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Discuss as a class: why might it matter to understand a real challenge clearly AND hold onto real hope at the same time, rather than only focusing on one or the other?</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Exit Ticket</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Write one sentence explaining, accurately, ONE real reason sea levels are rising.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Faith Connection</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Ponder: Tuvalu is disappearing into the ocean. Paul writes that creation 'groans' — and that it waits for God's people to respond. Romans 8 frames this not as hopeless but as a groan of expectation: things are not as they should be, and God's people are called to act.</li><li>Verse: &quot;We know that the whole creation has been groaning as in the pains of childbirth right up to the present time.&quot; — <em>Romans 8:22 (NIV)</em></li><li>Value: Pānga  Impact — Responding to creation's 'groan' by working for justice and care is one of the clearest ways we can bring God's Kingdom to earth.</li><li>Discussion questions:<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>Paul says creation 'groans.' When you look at the Tuvalu image, what does that groan sound like to you?</li><li>Romans 8 says this groaning is like 'the pains of childbirth' — painful, but leading somewhere. What might God be bringing forth through this crisis, if His people respond faithfully?</li><li>What would it look like for YOUR school or community to respond to creation's groan in a way that brings God's Kingdom closer?</li></ul></li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A clear container, ice, and water for the demonstration</li><li>A simple world map showing low-lying Pacific nations if available</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Suggested Videos</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Why are sea levels rising?</em> — search: &quot;why are sea levels rising explained for kids gently&quot;. Gives an accurate, gentle, age-appropriate explanation of the real science behind this sensitive topic.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Student Agency Choices</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Art: Illustrate the Two Real Causes</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Draw two simple labelled diagrams: one showing thermal expansion (warmer water taking up more space) and one showing melting land ice adding new water to the ocean.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Which of the two causes was trickier to show clearly, and why?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Read/Write: A Message of Hope and Honesty</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write a short, gentle message that holds both the real challenge and real hope together, as if writing to someone living in a low-lying Pacific community.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Word Count Guide</th><td class=\"lt-td\">100–150 words.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">How did you balance honesty and hope in your message?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Technology: Digital Before/After Diagram</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Create a simple digital diagram showing a low-lying coastline before and after a small rise in sea level.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">What did the comparison help you understand that words alone might not?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Watch &amp; Review: Watch and Note: Rising Seas</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Watch a short, gentle video explaining sea level rise.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Search term</th><td class=\"lt-td\">&quot;why are sea levels rising explained for kids gently&quot;</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">What part of the explanation made the most sense to you?</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Thursday — Technology: Trial 39: The Waka of the Future":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">SUSTAINABLE OCEAN TECHNOLOGY — Real Innovation Inspired by Tradition</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Technology</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Achievement Objectives</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Nature of Technology: understand how technological development can draw on both historical knowledge and new innovation to address real present-day needs.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Key Competencies</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Thinking; Participating &amp; Contributing</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning about real, genuine examples of sustainable ocean technology being developed today.</li><li>We are learning to design a vessel that combines traditional wisdom, modern technology, and sustainability.</li><li>I can synthesise a full term's learning into one original, considered design.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Teacher observation of the design process and final presentations. This is a genuine culminating task — assess synthesis of prior learning across the term, not just creativity alone.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Introduction</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&quot;This is genuinely the last new content of the term before our final celebration. Today, we look at real sustainable ocean technology being developed right now — and then design something of our own, combining everything we've learned this entire term.&quot;</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Main Content</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Real Sustainable Innovation Today (10 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Teacher Input: real modern sailing and ocean technology increasingly draws on sustainable design principles — including solar-powered vessels, sails made from recyclable or more sustainable materials, and a genuine renewed interest in wind-powered cargo shipping (sometimes called 'sail cargo') as a lower-emission alternative to fully fuel-powered shipping.</li><li>Key Question: &quot;Why might modern engineers be looking back toward wind power, something voyagers like Tama relied on completely, as part of a more sustainable future?&quot;</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Designing the Waka of the Future (20 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Activity: in teams, design (sketch or build a simple model) a 'future waka' combining traditional wayfinding wisdom (from this term's content), modern technology, and sustainable/eco-friendly materials or features.</li><li>Activity: present your design, explaining one traditional feature kept, one modern feature added, and one sustainable choice made — drawing explicitly on real content from across the whole term.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Exit Ticket</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Write one sentence: what ONE traditional feature from this term would you never leave out of a &quot;waka of the future,&quot; and why?</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Faith Connection</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Ponder: Tama asks: if you were to build the next great voyaging vessel, what would it look like — knowing the old wisdom, the new tools, AND everything the ocean still needs from us? That question is exactly the one God asks His people in every generation: what are you building, and who are you building it for?</li><li>Verse: &quot;&quot;For I know the plans I have for you,&quot; declares the LORD, &quot;plans to prosper you and not to harm you, plans to give you hope and a future.&quot;&quot; — <em>Jeremiah 29:11 (NIV)</em></li><li>Value: Pānga  Impact — Building toward God's Kingdom — with the gifts, wisdom, and creativity He's given us — is what it means to bring His future into the present.</li><li>Discussion questions:<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>God says He has plans for you — 'plans to give you hope and a future.' How does that change what you think you're capable of designing and building?</li><li>Your 'future waka' must carry the old wisdom AND the new tools. What old wisdom from this term do you most want to carry into your future?</li><li>If you were building something for God's Kingdom — not a waka but something in your actual life — what would it be, and who would it serve?</li></ul></li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Materials for building simple vessel models (card, straws, foil)</li><li>Paper for design sketches</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Suggested Videos</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Sustainable sailing technology today</em> — search: &quot;sustainable sail cargo ships wind power today&quot;. Introduces real, genuine examples of sustainable ocean technology inspired by traditional sail power.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Student Agency Choices</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Art: Design and Illustrate Your Future Waka</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Create a detailed, labelled illustration of your future waka design, clearly showing the traditional, modern, and sustainable features you chose.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Which single feature are you proudest of including, and why?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Read/Write: Pitching the Future Waka</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write a short &quot;pitch&quot; explaining your future waka design and why each feature was chosen, as if presenting it to the Council of Wayfinders.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Word Count Guide</th><td class=\"lt-td\">120–180 words.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">What part of your pitch do you think would convince the Council most?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Technology: Digital Design Poster</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Create a simple digital poster of your future waka design, labelling its traditional, modern, and sustainable features.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">How did combining all three types of feature change your design process?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Watch &amp; Review: Watch and Note: Sustainable Sailing</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Watch a short video about sustainable sail-powered shipping today.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Search term</th><td class=\"lt-td\">&quot;sustainable sail cargo ships wind power today&quot;</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">What real example from the video most inspired your own design?</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Friday — Art: Trial 40: The Map Is Complete":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">COMPLETING THE VOYAGE QUILT — A Term-Long Artwork, Finished</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">The Arts (Visual Art)</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Achievement Objectives</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Communicating and Interpreting: share the ideas in a completed body of work, and reflect on the development of that work over time.</li><li>Developing Ideas: develop and refine ideas using a range of materials and techniques across an extended project.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Key Competencies</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Thinking; Using Language, Symbols &amp; Text; Relating to Others</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are completing a genuine, term-long collaborative artwork.</li><li>We are reflecting on how a shared artwork develops and changes over an extended period.</li><li>I can narrate and take pride in a finished, collaborative piece of work.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Teacher observation of the final quilt square contribution and each group's 'walk the quilt' narration. This is the culminating, celebratory assessment of the whole term's Art strand.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Introduction</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&quot;Eight weeks ago, your first quilt square was a simple folded, cut piece of paper. Today, each group finishes its quilt and looks back at the whole journey it tells, square by square.&quot;</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Main Content</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Final Quilt Square (15 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Activity: each student creates their final quilt square — inspired by Thursday's 'waka of the future' designs. This is an open, free-choice square: use any technique from across the term (dots, woven lines, wave burst, star burst, silhouette, regional style) or combine elements. The focus is on making this final square feel like a considered ending to the set.</li><li>Glue all final squares onto each group's backing sheet, completing the quilt at eight or nine squares per group.</li><li>Once glued, lay every group's finished quilt out side by side so the whole class can see the full range at once.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Walking the Quilt (15 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Activity: in small groups, do a final 'walk the quilt' moment — narrate your own group's quilt from its very first square (Week 1's Lapita pottery pattern) to its last (today's future waka square), in order, as if telling the full story to someone seeing it for the very first time.</li><li>Optional: if time allows, swap and narrate another group's quilt too — noticing how the same journey looks different through another group's choices.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Exit Ticket</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Write one sentence: looking at your group's finished quilt, what part are you proudest to have contributed, and why?</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Faith Connection</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Ponder: Tama looks at the quilts and says: look how far you've come. This is the last square — your square, your voice, your part of the story. Revelation 21:5 describes God saying the same thing at the end of all things: 'I am making everything new.' Your square is a small echo of that promise.</li><li>Verse: &quot;He who was seated on the throne said, &quot;I am making everything new!&quot;&quot; — <em>Revelation 21:5 (NIV)</em></li><li>Value: Pānga  Impact — Every act of faithful creativity — done to God's glory — is a small participation in the new creation God is bringing about.</li><li>Discussion questions:<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>God says 'I am making everything new.' What would you most like to see made new — in your life, your community, your world?</li><li>This is your final square — free choice, your voice. What did you choose to put in it, and why?</li><li>The whole quilt is made of different squares, different voices, different parts of the story. How is that like the church — the body of Christ?</li></ul></li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A4 paper squares for the final square</li><li>Safety scissors</li><li>Each group's quilt backing sheet</li><li>Space to lay all quilts out side by side</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Student Agency Choices</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Art: Create and Complete Your Final Quilt Square</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This week's main activity IS the art focus — create your final quilt square using any technique from this term, and complete your group's quilt.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Looking at all your squares from Week 1 to now, what changed the most about how you use the technique?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Read/Write: Narrate the Complete Journey</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write a short narration, as if guiding a visitor along your group's finished quilt from start to finish, describing the whole voyage in your own words.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Word Count Guide</th><td class=\"lt-td\">150–200 words.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">What moment in the whole nine-week journey did you most enjoy retelling?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Technology: Digital Quilt Documentary</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Compile photos of your group's quilt squares across the term (if available) into a simple digital slideshow telling the story from start to finish.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">What changed the most between your Week 1 square and your final one?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Watch &amp; Review: Reflect on the Term's Art Journey</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Look back through your own quest log entries from across the whole term and choose the one quilt square you're proudest of.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">What does that square show about how your skills or understanding grew this term?</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"}}},"Week 10":{"label":"Day","children":{"Monday — History: Wayfinder's Examination — Station 1: Arrival and Legacy":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">FINAL EXAMINATION STATION 1 — Testing Real History Knowledge from Weeks 6–9</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Social Sciences</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Achievement Objectives</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Understanding the Past: demonstrate understanding of historical events, evidence, and the people involved, drawn from a full term of prior learning.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Key Competencies</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Thinking; Relating to Others</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are demonstrating what we have learned about arrival, settlement, and legacy from Weeks 6–9.</li><li>We are working as a team under time pressure to recall accurate historical facts.</li><li>I can connect real historical evidence to real present-day significance.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">This IS the assessment for Weeks 6–9 History content. Score team performance on the quick-fire round and use results to identify any concepts needing revisiting before the end of term.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Introduction</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&quot;The Council has called us back one final time. Today's station tests everything we've learned since arriving in Aotearoa — the landing itself, the long pause, the scattering across the Pacific, and the living legacy that continues into today.&quot;</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Main Content</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Station Setup (5 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Explain the format: this is a rapid-fire True/False round, same as Week 5. Read each statement aloud; teams confer briefly (10–15 seconds) then signal their answer.</li><li>Award one point per correct answer, with a bonus point for a team that can correctly explain WHY a false statement is false.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Quick-Fire Question Bank (20 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>1. Aotearoa was one of the very first landmasses on Earth settled by humans. (FALSE — it was one of the LAST major landmasses settled, around 1300 CE)</li><li>2. Wairau Bar is a real archaeological site providing evidence of early settlement in Aotearoa. (TRUE)</li><li>3. Aotearoa had many native land mammals before humans arrived, similar to most other landmasses. (FALSE — it had no native land mammals aside from bats)</li><li>4. The rua kūmara was a real storage pit used to protect kūmara through Aotearoa's colder winters. (TRUE)</li><li>5. Pounamu (greenstone) became significant in Aotearoa for both practical tools and valued ornaments. (TRUE)</li><li>6. Pacific settlement happened all at once, in a single continuous wave of voyaging. (FALSE — there was a real, still-debated 'long pause' of roughly 1,000+ years between the Lapita spread and the later voyages to Hawaii, Rapa Nui, and Aotearoa)</li><li>7. Whakapapa is simply a list of names with no other real information attached. (FALSE — it is a structured historical record carrying real information about identity, land, and events)</li><li>8. The same double-hull principle behind the waka hourua is still used in some modern sailing vessels today. (TRUE)</li><li>9. Aotearoa today has no real, living connection to the Pacific migration story studied this term. (FALSE — Aotearoa is home to large, living Pacific communities with direct, real connection to this story)</li><li>10. Real, contemporary Pacific and Māori artists still actively practice traditions like weaving and carving today. (TRUE)</li><li>Optional extension: invite teams to write one additional True/False statement of their own from Weeks 6–9 content, to challenge another team.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Debrief (5 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Reveal team scores. Briefly revisit any question more than one team got wrong, with a short explanation, before moving to tomorrow's station.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Exit Ticket</th><td class=\"lt-td\">As a team, write down the ONE fact from Weeks 6–9 you're most confident you'll remember in a year's time — and why it stuck.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Quick-fire question bank (above) printed or read aloud</li><li>A simple way for teams to signal answers (cards, hands, buzzers)</li><li>A visible scoreboard</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Student Agency Choices</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Art: Design a Final Wayfinder Medal</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Design a medal or badge that could be awarded to a team that performs well across the whole Wayfinder's Examination.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Instructions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Sketch the medal design.</li><li>Include a symbol representing the whole term's journey, not just one week.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Why did you choose that particular symbol to represent the entire term?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Read/Write: My Term History MVP Moment</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write a short reflection on the ONE piece of history content from across the WHOLE term you understood best, and why.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Word Count Guide</th><td class=\"lt-td\">80–120 words.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Looking back over ten weeks, how did your understanding change from Week 1 to now?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Technology: Build a Final Digital Scoreboard</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Create a simple digital scoreboard to track team scores across all of this week's stations, building on Week 5's format.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">What would make a scoreboard feel like a fitting close to a whole term's effort?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Watch &amp; Review: Review Your Whole Term's Quest Log</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Look back through your own Quest Log entries from across the entire term and choose the one you're proudest of.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">What does that entry show about how much you've grown this term?</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Tuesday — Geography: Wayfinder's Examination — Station 2: Distance and Climate":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">FINAL EXAMINATION STATION 2 — Testing Real Geography Knowledge from Weeks 6–9</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Social Sciences (Geography)</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Achievement Objectives</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Understanding Place and Environment: demonstrate understanding of geographic features, scale, climate, and environmental change drawn from a full term of prior learning.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Key Competencies</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Thinking; Using Language, Symbols &amp; Text</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are demonstrating our memory of real Pacific geography from Weeks 6–9.</li><li>We are working under pressure, using memory and reasoning rather than guesswork.</li><li>I can connect real geographic concepts (latitude, distance, ocean health) into one coherent understanding.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">This IS the assessment for Weeks 6–9 Geography content. Use results to identify which geographic concepts (latitude, distance, ocean conservation) may need revisiting.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Introduction</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&quot;Yesterday tested your memory of history. Today, the Council tests your understanding of place itself — distance, climate, and the real state of the ocean that carried this whole story.&quot;</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Main Content</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Station Setup (5 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Lay out the large Pacific floor/wall map used throughout the term. Prepare a set of challenge cards covering: real distances (gateway voyage, Tahiti–Hawaii, Tahiti–Rapa Nui, Tahiti–Aotearoa), real latitude figures for each island group, and real ocean conservation facts from Week 9.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Challenge Round (20 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Round 1: call out two locations at a time (e.g. &quot;Samoa/Tonga to Tahiti&quot; or &quot;Tahiti to Rapa Nui&quot;) and have teams estimate and rank the real distance compared to the others, without looking at notes.</li><li>Round 2: call out an island group and have teams correctly identify its real climate type (tropical, subtropical, temperate) based on latitude.</li><li>Round 3: ask teams to recall one real example of genuine Pacific ocean conservation from Week 9, and explain why it matters.</li><li>Key Question for discussion: &quot;Which was harder to remember accurately — a number, like distance or latitude, or a real-world example, like a conservation project? Why?&quot;</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Debrief (5 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Reveal scores. Briefly re-show correct answers on the map for anything answered incorrectly.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Exit Ticket</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Write one sentence: which real Pacific geography fact from this term do you feel most confident about, and why?</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Large Pacific floor/wall map</li><li>Challenge cards: distances, latitude, conservation facts</li><li>A visible scoreboard</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Student Agency Choices</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Art: Draw the Whole Voyage Route from Memory</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Without looking at any reference, draw your best memory of the full voyage route studied this term, from the Lapita homeland to Aotearoa and beyond.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Compare your drawing to a real map afterward — what did you remember well, and what surprised you?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Read/Write: My Term Geography MVP Moment</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write a short reflection on the ONE piece of geography content from across the whole term you understood best.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Word Count Guide</th><td class=\"lt-td\">80–120 words.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">How did you come to understand it so clearly?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Technology: Digital Term Map Challenge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Use a simple digital map tool to label as many real locations, distances, and climate zones from across the term as you can recall, unaided.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">How many could you label correctly without checking back?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Watch &amp; Review: Review Your Term's Map Work</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Look back over your star compass, distance, and latitude work from across the whole term. What's the one geography skill you feel most confident in now?</p></div></div>"},"Wednesday — Science: Wayfinder's Examination — Station 3: The Real World, Honestly":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">FINAL EXAMINATION STATION 3 — Testing Real Science Knowledge from Weeks 6–9</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Science</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Achievement Objectives</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Demonstrate understanding of seasonal change, extreme weather, and environmental science drawn from a full term of prior learning.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Key Competencies</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Thinking; Participating &amp; Contributing</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are demonstrating our understanding of real seasonal, weather, and environmental science from Weeks 6–9.</li><li>We are using real content to reason through honest, sometimes difficult scenarios.</li><li>I can apply scientific understanding to real present-day issues with care and accuracy.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">This IS the assessment for Weeks 6–9 Science content. Use results to identify which scientific concepts (seasonal survival, cyclones, sea level rise) may need revisiting.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Introduction</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&quot;Today's station asks you to bring together everything we've learned about real science this term — from surviving Aotearoa's first winter, to understanding the real forces shaping the ocean's future.&quot;</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Main Content</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Station Setup (5 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Prepare 4–5 scenario cards covering: surviving Aotearoa's seasons (Week 6), how cyclones form (Week 8), and the real, gentle science of sea level rise (Week 9).</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Challenge Round (20 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Present each scenario to teams in turn. Teams must respond using real content from this term — for example: &quot;Explain, using real science, why early Aotearoa settlers needed the rua kūmara,&quot; or &quot;Explain, using real science, why sea levels are rising.&quot;</li><li>Award points for both accuracy AND for genuinely thoughtful, well-reasoned answers, especially on the more sensitive topics (sea level rise, cyclones).</li><li>Key Question for discussion: &quot;Which real science concept from this term do you think matters most for understanding the world you actually live in today?&quot;</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Debrief (5 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Reveal scores. Use this moment to reinforce that understanding difficult real-world science, calmly and accurately, is itself a genuine achievement.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Exit Ticket</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Write one sentence: what real science concept from this term do you think will stay with you the longest, and why?</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Scenario cards covering seasons, cyclones, and sea level rise</li><li>A visible scoreboard</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Student Agency Choices</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Art: Illustrate &quot;Understanding, Not Fear&quot;</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Draw an image representing how real scientific understanding (rather than fear) helps people face a real environmental challenge.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">What did you choose to show as the source of hope in your illustration?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Read/Write: My Term Science MVP Moment</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write a short reflection on the ONE science concept from across the whole term you understood best.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Word Count Guide</th><td class=\"lt-td\">80–120 words.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">How would you explain it clearly to someone who missed this whole term?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Technology: Build a Term Science Concept Map</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Create a simple digital concept map connecting the science topics studied this term (weather, seasons, cyclones, sea level rise) and how they relate to each other.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">What connection between topics surprised you most?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Watch &amp; Review: Review Your Voyage Forecasts and Science Notes</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Look back at your science notes and forecasts from across the term. Has your confidence in explaining real weather and environmental science grown? How can you tell?</p></div></div>"},"Thursday — Technology: Wayfinder's Examination — Station 4: Then, Now, and What's Next":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">FINAL EXAMINATION STATION 4 — Testing Real Technology Knowledge and the Future Waka Pitch</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Technology</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Achievement Objectives</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Demonstrate understanding of design, materials, and technological development drawn from a full term of prior learning and a culminating design task.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Key Competencies</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Thinking; Managing Self; Participating &amp; Contributing</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are demonstrating our real engineering and technology knowledge from across the whole term.</li><li>We are presenting our &quot;waka of the future&quot; designs as the culminating task of the term.</li><li>I can explain why a design choice draws meaningfully on both tradition and innovation.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">This IS the assessment for Weeks 6–9 Technology content, alongside the term's culminating design task from Week 9. Use the pitch presentations as evidence of synthesis across the whole term's Technology learning.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Introduction</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&quot;This is the final technology test, and it brings together the whole term: from the waka hourua, to GPS, to the 'waka of the future' you designed last week. Today, you pitch it properly to the Council.&quot;</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Main Content</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Station Setup (5 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Gather the 'waka of the future' designs created in Week 9. If not already presented in full, allow a few minutes for teams to finalise their pitch.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Then, Now, and What's Next Quick Round (10 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Quick-fire questions connecting Technology content across the whole term: &quot;What real engineering principle connects the waka hourua to modern catamarans?&quot; &quot;What is one real strength and one real limitation of GPS?&quot; &quot;What is one real example of sustainable ocean technology today?&quot;</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Future Waka Pitch (10 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Activity: each team formally pitches their 'waka of the future' design to the Council (the class/teacher), explaining the traditional feature kept, the modern feature added, and the sustainable choice made.</li><li>Award points for both technical accuracy and the quality of the pitch's reasoning.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Exit Ticket</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Write one sentence: what is the single most important lesson about technology and design you're taking away from this whole term?</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Teams' 'waka of the future' designs from Week 9</li><li>A visible scoreboard</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Student Agency Choices</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Art: Design a Final Pitch Poster</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Create a polished, presentation-ready poster of your &quot;waka of the future&quot; design for today's pitch.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">What did you refine or improve since first designing it last week?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Read/Write: My Term Technology MVP Moment</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write a short reflection on the ONE piece of technology content from across the whole term you understood best.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Word Count Guide</th><td class=\"lt-td\">80–120 words.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">How did building and testing real models help you understand this better than just reading about it?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Technology: Digital Term Technology Timeline</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Build a simple digital timeline showing the technology studied across the whole term, from the waka hourua to the waka of the future.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">What does this timeline show about how technology builds on what came before it?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Watch &amp; Review: Review Your Whole Build Journey</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Look back at every design, sketch, and model you've created this term. What's the single biggest improvement you can see in your own thinking?</p></div></div>"},"Friday — Bonus Station: Wayfinder's Examination — Finale: The Wayfinder Ceremony":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">TERM FINALE — Final Star Navigation Challenge and the Wayfinder Ceremony</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Social Sciences / The Arts</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Achievement Objectives</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Demonstrate understanding and contribute meaningfully to a shared class outcome, drawn from a full term of prior learning, and reflect on growth across an extended unit of work.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Key Competencies</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Thinking; Participating &amp; Contributing; Relating to Others</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are demonstrating our ability to use real navigation knowledge built across the whole term.</li><li>We are formally reflecting on a full term of learning, growth, and contribution.</li><li>I can recognise and celebrate genuine growth in my own understanding across an extended unit of work.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Teacher observation of the final star navigation challenge. Use the Wayfinder Ceremony as a genuinely celebratory close to the whole term — not a new graded task, but a meaningful culmination of ten weeks of learning.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Introduction</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&quot;This is it, crew. The final challenge, and then — if the Council agrees — a celebration ten weeks in the making.&quot;</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Main Content</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Final Star Navigation Challenge (10 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Using everything learned across the term (star compass from Week 4, latitude from Week 7), teams must correctly 'steer' a token across the floor map from the voyage's starting point all the way to Aotearoa, using real star reference points and reasoning rather than guessing.</li><li>Award bonus points for teams who can also correctly explain WHY their chosen route makes sense, using real content from across the term.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Tallying the Final Examination (10 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Add up all points from Monday through Friday's final challenge. Announce results, framing this as a genuine, shared achievement — every team has demonstrated a full term's worth of real learning, regardless of final score.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">The Wayfinder Ceremony (15 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Narrate the compass prop coming fully, calmly to life one final time — steady now, not restless.</li><li>Read Tama's closing words aloud to the class (from the Boss Battle page): his thanks, and the moment he formally calls the class &quot;Wayfinders&quot; for the first time.</li><li>Present each student or team with a simple certificate, badge, or token marking their completion of the term, in role as the Council's formal recognition.</li><li>Lay out every group's completed quilt side by side one final time as a class, looking back over the whole ten-week journey together.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Exit Ticket</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Write one sentence: looking back over the WHOLE term, what is the one thing you're proudest of having learned or done?</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Star compass wheels and the floor/wall Pacific map</li><li>The class's compass/journal prop for the ceremony</li><li>Simple certificates or badges for each student/team</li><li>Each group's completed voyage quilt</li><li>A running scoreboard from the week</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Student Agency Choices</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Art: Design Your Own Wayfinder Certificate</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Design a personal certificate marking your completion of &quot;The Wayfinder's Journal,&quot; including one symbol representing your favourite part of the term.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">What symbol did you choose, and why does it represent your term best?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Read/Write: A Letter to Next Year's Class</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write a short letter to the students who will do this voyage next year, telling them what to expect and what mattered most to you.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Word Count Guide</th><td class=\"lt-td\">120–180 words.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">What's the one piece of advice you'd most want them to have?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Technology: Build a Term Highlights Reel</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Create one final digital slide or page summarising your proudest moment, design, or piece of learning from the entire term.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Why did you choose that particular highlight out of everything from this term?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Watch &amp; Review: Review the Whole Voyage, Start to Finish</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Look back over your group's entire completed quilt and your full Quest Log from Week 1 to now.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Looking at where you started in Week 1 and where you are now, what's the biggest change you can see in yourself?</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"}}}}},"Zimbabwe and South Africa":{"label":"Week","children":{}}}}}},"homework":{"label":"Category","children":{"Weekly Wonder":{"label":"Week","children":{"Week 1":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Question</th><td class=\"lt-td\">This remains the same size throughout our lifetime.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Answer</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Your Eyes</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Facts</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The human eye blinks an average of 4,200,000 times a year. This means if you were given 10 cents for every time you blinked you would make $420,000 annually.</li><li>Your eye is the fastest muscle in your body. Hence, the phrase: “In the blink of an eye.”</li><li>Your iris has 256 unique characteristics, while your fingerprint only has 40.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Week 2":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Question</th><td class=\"lt-td\">This is the first food to be eaten in space.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Answer</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Applesauce</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Facts</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Since it is high in pectin, apple sauce is a homemade remedy for children with diarrhea or stomach problems.</li><li>Why do apples float? It’s because they’re 25 percent air, giving us the ability to bob for apples in a barrel of water.</li><li>The largest apple peel was created by Kathy Wafler from New York in 1976. The prize-winning peel was 52.5m long.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Week 3":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Question</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Albert Einstein said this was the smartest person who ever lived.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Answer</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Isaac Newton</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Facts</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>During the Great Plague, Isaac Newton spent two years in study and isolation at his home developing his theories on calculus, gravity, and the laws of motion.</li><li>His mother wanted him to be a farmer so took him out of school. He didn’t enjoy it so was soon back at school again.</li><li>In Isaac’s time the calendar was different, and even though by today’s calendar he was born in January, by that time’s calendar he was considered to be born on Christmas Day.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Week 4":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Question</th><td class=\"lt-td\">This is one of the best pastimes for people who want to reduce the risk of having heart disease or a stroke.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Answer</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Cycling</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Facts</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The same energy that is expended for walking can be used when cycling to go three times faster.</li><li>Bicycles save over 900 million litres of fuel every year.</li><li>The fastest measured speed of riding a bicycle on a flat surface is 133.75 km/h.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Week 5":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Question</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Over the centuries and around the world, lizard soup, blood-letting, leeches, turnips, plum pickle and the familiar chicken soup have all been used to try to heal this.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Answer</th><td class=\"lt-td\">The Common Cold</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Facts</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>There are plenty of good reasons to eat vitamin C packed foods like oranges and broccoli. However, there is currently little evidence to suggest that taking this vitamin reduces your chances of getting a cold.</li><li>A single sneeze can spray 100,000 germs into the air, which is why you should keep 2 meters distance from a sneezing sick person.</li><li>Being cold or wet doesn’t cause colds or make you more likely to get sick.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Week 6":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Question</th><td class=\"lt-td\">You are more likely to see this in the Summer than the Winter.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Answer</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rainbows</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Facts</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>You are less likely to see a rainbow in the winter – but why? Because of snow! A rainbow is the result of light passing through a spectrum – in most cases, a collection of raindrops.</li><li>The average rainbow is observable for less than an hour. But in 2017 in Taiwan, people observed a rainbow for 8 hours and 58 minutes.</li><li>A double rainbow appears when light is reflected twice in a raindrop. The secondary rainbow – which will be a little bit higher and fainter in color – will actually have its colors reversed.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Week 7":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Question</th><td class=\"lt-td\">This invertebrate was once displayed in a museum thought to be dead until it started moving several years later.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Answer</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Snails</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Facts</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Certain land snails can sleep for up to three years in hibernation, but generally, snails will sleep on and off in between periods of 13 to 15 hours. Afterwards, they experience a sudden jolt of energy for the next 30 hours, where they get all their snail chores done!</li><li>The speed of snails is around 12-20 mm per second. If they moved without stopping, it would take more than a week to complete 1 kilometer.</li><li>The mucus of the garden snail is used to treat wrinkles, spots, and scars on the skin.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Week 8":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Question</th><td class=\"lt-td\">This snack was invented because chocolate melted too fast in the sun.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Answer</th><td class=\"lt-td\">M&amp;Ms</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Facts</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>M&amp;Ms were made for soldiers in WWII so chocolate wouldn’t melt in their hands.</li><li>The “M” stands for the names Mars and Murrie.</li><li>They were originally only made in the color brown.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Week 9":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Question</th><td class=\"lt-td\">This creature has blue blood!</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Answer</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Horseshoe Crabs</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Facts</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Horseshoe crabs have blue blood because it contains copper instead of iron.</li><li>Their blood is used to test medicine safety — it's super sensitive to bacteria.</li><li>They have strong shells and live in muddy ocean shores.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Week 10":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Question</th><td class=\"lt-td\">The world’s first hydroelectric power plant was created here.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Answer</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Niagara Falls</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Facts</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>More than 8 million visitors explore Niagara Falls State Park annually, coming from all over the world.</li><li>The first person to go over Niagara Falls in a barrel was 63-year-old schoolteacher Annie Edson Taylor — she suffered only a concussion and a minor cut on her head.</li><li>Fish do plunge over the Falls, and it’s estimated that almost all of them survive, thanks to their ability to flow with the water. Sometimes, however, they are a bit “stunned” after making the journey.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Week 11":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Question</th><td class=\"lt-td\">This wonder can be seen from space (with binoculars) if the conditions are right.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Answer</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Great Wall of China</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Facts</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Many workers lost their lives during the construction of the wall.</li><li>It stretches across Northern China and covers over 21,000 kms.</li><li>Construction started over 2000 years ago in the 7th century BC.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Week 12":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Question</th><td class=\"lt-td\">This travels at about 300,000 km’s per second.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Answer</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Light</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Facts</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The color of an object that we see is actually the color of the light that it reflects — e.g. a red apple appears red because it reflects red light and absorbs all other colors.</li><li>The Northern Lights, or Aurora Borealis, are caused by charged particles from the sun colliding with particles in the Earth's atmosphere, creating a beautiful display of light in the sky.</li><li>Different animals can see different ranges of light. For example, some birds can see ultraviolet light, which humans can't see.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Week 13":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Question</th><td class=\"lt-td\">This famous rugby player was knighted for their services to the Pacific community and youth.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Answer</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Sir Michael Jones</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Facts</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>He was known for his Christian beliefs, which meant he chose not to play on Sundays.</li><li>He played once for Western Samoa and 55 times for New Zealand.</li><li>He was part of the All Black team that won the very first Rugby World Cup in 1987.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Week 14":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Question</th><td class=\"lt-td\">There are approximately 20,000 known species of these worldwide.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Answer</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Butterflies</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Facts</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Some butterflies have a wingspan of 1.3 cms whilst another, named ‘Queen Alexandra’s’, has a wingspan of 30cm.</li><li>Monarch butterfly migrations can cover up to 4,800 kms (more than twice the length of NZ).</li><li>Fastest butterflies: Convolvulus hawk-moth = 100 km/hr, Skipper = 60 km/hr, Hawk Moth (general) = 50 km/hr.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Week 15":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Question</th><td class=\"lt-td\">You need to climb 251 steps to get to the top.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Answer</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Leaning Tower of Pisa</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Facts</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>It was not originally leaning when it was built.</li><li>Pisa survived 4 earthquakes.</li><li>It took almost 2 centuries to build because of wars and battles that interrupted its construction.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Week 16":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Question</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Americans eat 350 pieces of this every second.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Answer</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Pizza</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Facts</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Luis XIII in Salerno, Italy has the world’s most expensive pizza at $12,000! It includes three types of caviar, lobster, bufala mozzarella cheese, and grains of pink Australian sea salt.</li><li>At the World Pizza Championships, dough-spinning has its own professional-level sporting event, with five competitive events: Freestyle Acrobatic Dough Tossing, Fastest Dough, Largest Dough Stretch, Fastest Pizza Box Folding, and the Pizza Triathlon.</li><li>The Hawaiian pizza was invented in Canada. With only three toppings available — mushroom, bacon, or pepperoni — Sam Panopoulos decided to throw a can of pineapple on his pizza.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Week 17":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Question</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Extracts from these have been used to treat Alzheimer's disease.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Answer</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Daffodils</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Facts</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The bulbs can be poisonous to animals. Squirrels won't eat daffodils because of this, and you should make sure you plant daffodil bulbs where cats and dogs can’t get them.</li><li>Don't mix them with other flowers in a vase — daffodils release latex from their stems once cut, which can shorten the life of other flowers.</li><li>Daffodils are one of the first signs of Spring and are also strongly associated with Easter because of this.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Week 18":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Question</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Known for their incredible jumping ability, water conservation skills, and nocturnal habits.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Answer</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Kangaroo Rat</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Facts</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The kangaroo rat gets all its water from seeds, and never needs to drink!</li><li>Their powerful hind legs allow them to leap up to 2.7 meters in one bound, helping them escape predators.</li><li>They have fur-lined food storage pouches in their cheeks to collect and transport seeds and other food items.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Week 19":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Question</th><td class=\"lt-td\">This animal has cube-shaped poop!</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Answer</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Wombats</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Facts</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Wombats use cube-shaped poop to mark territory — it doesn’t roll away!</li><li>Their butt is their main form of defence — they can crush a predator’s skull with their behind.</li><li>They have strong back ends to block tunnels from predators.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Week 20":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Question</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Movies such as Mission Impossible Fallout, Lord of the Rings, The Lion Witch and the Wardrobe, Prince Caspian, and Wolverine were filmed here.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Answer</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Queenstown</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Facts</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Lonely Planet made some statistics saying Lake Wakatipu has some of the cleanest water in the world.</li><li>There are still opportunities to make money gold prospecting, as gold is still found on the Shotover River.</li><li>Queenstown has the 4th busiest airport in New Zealand, which is impressive considering it only has the 27th largest urban population.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Week 21":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Question</th><td class=\"lt-td\">This country has a waterfall that is 3 times the height of Niagara Falls.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Answer</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Scotland</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Facts</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The highest proportion of redheads in the world are in Scotland.</li><li>The official animal of Scotland is the unicorn, and according to legend it is considered one of the only animals powerful enough to defeat formidable opponents, such as elephants, in battle.</li><li>The raincoat was invented in Scotland by a man named Charles Macintosh, who was born in Glasgow.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Week 22":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Question</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Some cultures (most notably Japan) use the fiber from this food plant to make fabric and sometimes even paper.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Answer</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Bananas</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Facts</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Bananas have around 30,000 genes — that’s more than humans!</li><li>Bananas are naturally slightly radioactive (thanks to their potassium content) — but the level of radiation is not high enough to cause harm.</li><li>If you rub the inside of a banana peel on a scrape or burn, it will help the pain go away, keep the swelling down, and keep the wound from getting infected.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Week 23":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Question</th><td class=\"lt-td\">This can be used underwater, in space, and upside down.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Answer</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Pencil</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Facts</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>On average a pencil can be sharpened 17 times, draw a line 56 kms long, and can write approximately 45,000 words.</li><li>The most expensive pencil in the world is the Graf von Faber-Castell Perfect Pencil. It’s crafted from 240-year-old olive wood and 18-karat white gold and costs $12,800.</li><li>An average size tree will make approximately 300,000 pencils.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Week 24":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Question</th><td class=\"lt-td\">This toy was inspired by a walking factory spring.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Answer</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Slinky</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Facts</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The Slinky was invented when a spring fell off a desk and “walked.” It was originally being created as a part of a ship.</li><li>During the Vietnam War, soldiers used Slinkys as mobile antennas.</li><li>The first Slinky demonstration at Gimbel’s Department Store in Philadelphia in 1945 sold all 400 units in 90 minutes.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Week 25":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Question</th><td class=\"lt-td\">This tiny animal can survive freezing, heat, and even space!</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Answer</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Tardigrades (Water Bears)</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Facts</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Tardigrades (water bears) curl up into a dry ball called a 'tun.'</li><li>They’ve survived in space and came back alive!</li><li>They're so small you need a microscope to see them.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Week 26":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Question</th><td class=\"lt-td\">This animal can regrow part of its brain!</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Answer</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Axolotls</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Facts</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Axolotls can regrow body parts like tails and limbs.</li><li>They breathe through gills and lungs!</li><li>They're known as the 'smiling salamander.'</li></ul></div></div>"},"Week 27":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Question</th><td class=\"lt-td\">This bird can sleep while flying!</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Answer</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Frigatebird</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Facts</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Frigatebirds nap by shutting down half their brain at a time.</li><li>They can fly for weeks without stopping.</li><li>Magnificent frigatebirds practice kleptoparasitism — stealing food (fish and squid) from other seabirds, like blue-footed boobies.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Week 28":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Question</th><td class=\"lt-td\">This was inspired by an adopted black and white dog called Spike.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Answer</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Snoopy (Peanuts)</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Facts</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Though we never saw the inside of Snoopy’s doghouse in the comic strip, it was revealed over the years that it held a lot of personal possessions, including records, books, an original Vincent van Gogh painting, and a pool table.</li><li>In 2015, Snoopy received a coveted star on the Walk of Fame. Appropriately, it’s located right next to Charles Schulz’s.</li><li>Snoopy has an active imagination. Sometimes he pretends to be college student Joe Cool, other times he thinks he’s Flying Ace, a World War I pilot.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Week 29":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Question</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Too many of these can make your skin turn yellow. The condition is also named after these.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Answer</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Carrots</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Facts</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The most common carrots are orange, but they also come in white, yellow, and purple colors.</li><li>Baby carrots are just regular carrots that have been pre-shaped, cut, and peeled to look appealing.</li><li>Many say that raw vegetables are better for you than cooked ones, but the opposite is true for carrots.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Week 30":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Question</th><td class=\"lt-td\">In India, illegal mining of this item is the country's largest organized criminal activity. The black market for it is estimated to be worth 200-300 billion dollars per year.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Answer</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Sand</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Facts</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>If you put grains under a microscope you will see different shapes, colors, and even tiny fossils.</li><li>In only a few sand dunes around the world there are singing sand dunes. They make weird, loud humming noises when the sand moves.</li><li>Not all sand is yellow or white! It can be pink (Bahamas — from crushed shells), green (Hawai‘i — from olivine crystals), black (Iceland — from volcanic rock), or red (Australia — from iron-rich sandstone).</li></ul></div></div>"},"Week 31":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Question</th><td class=\"lt-td\">He was the first billionaire athlete.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Answer</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Michael Jordan</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Facts</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Jordan tried to get into the varsity basketball team. At the time he was 180.3 cm tall and quite skinny, so he wasn't allowed on the team.</li><li>Michael Jordan retired in 1993 from the Chicago Bulls after the death of his father, who was very close to him.</li><li>Jordan has a fear of water from a tragic incident during his childhood — he saw a good friend drown, and shortly after, he almost drowned himself while at baseball camp.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Week 32":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Question</th><td class=\"lt-td\">A dessert was named in honor of this famous dancer.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Answer</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Anna Pavlova</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Facts</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Anna gave over four thousand ballet performances during the years between 1913 and 1930.</li><li>She had no children of her own, but following the devastation of World War I, she established a home for Russian orphans.</li><li>Worn out by touring and the stress of performing, she died of pneumonia in 1931 at the age of 51.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Week 33":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Question</th><td class=\"lt-td\">This bird got its common name from the loud territorial sound that it makes. The calls are often mistaken for many different animals, such as donkeys or monkeys.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Answer</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Kookaburra</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Facts</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>As kookaburras can be heard very early in the morning they are called the ‘bushman’s clock’.</li><li>The kookaburra uses its brown and white feathers to blend into its environment, making it harder for both prey and predators to spot.</li><li>Baby kookaburras are born naked and blind, but they have a very sharp hook at the end of their beak. The first chick that hatches may turn on their younger siblings, bite them, or even kill them if there isn’t enough food.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Week 34":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Question</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Climbing of this rock is prohibited, but Prince Charles and Princess Diana still climbed it.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Answer</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Ayers Rock (Uluru)</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Facts</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>It varies from year to year, but Uluru receives around 300 millimetres of rain on average each year. The rain creates waterfalls and brings new life to the rock and surrounding desert.</li><li>Towering 348 metres above the surrounding plain, Uluru is taller than the Eiffel Tower in Paris, the Chrysler Building in New York, and the Great Pyramid of Giza.</li><li>Uluru is 3.6 km long and 1.9 km wide, with a total circumference of 9.4 km. It takes about 3½ hours to walk around the base.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Week 35":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Question</th><td class=\"lt-td\">This was banned 3 times in Scotland because it interfered with military training.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Answer</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Golf</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Facts</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Tiger Woods took an interest in golf at only six months old, and his father started teaching him at two years old. He got his first hole in one at 8 years old.</li><li>Early golf balls were made of leather and feathers. Manufacturers would wet the feathers to make them stick, then wrap them around the leather.</li><li>The golf tee wasn’t created until the 1920s. Before that, golfers would use mounds of sand to elevate their golf balls for the opening shot.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Week 36":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Question</th><td class=\"lt-td\">People lose on average about 50-150 of these a day. In general, females lose more than males.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Answer</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Hair</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Facts</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A single strand of hair can support up to 3 kgs of weight. That means a whole head of hair can support up to 2000 kgs!</li><li>About 95% of the total skin area is covered in hair. Who knew you were so, well, hairy.</li><li>Someone purchased a lock of Elvis’ hair at an auction for $15,000.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Week 37":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Question</th><td class=\"lt-td\">This city has more fountains than any other city on the planet.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Answer</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rome</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Facts</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Harming cats is punishable by Roman law. They can stay where they want and you cannot move them.</li><li>A special light show happens at the Pantheon every year to celebrate Rome’s birthday.</li><li>Coins thrown into Trevi fountain add up to over 1 million euros a year.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Week 38":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Question</th><td class=\"lt-td\">This thing has its own national day on September 30th. National — day.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Answer</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Gum</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Facts</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The record for the most gum chewed at a time is 250 pieces.</li><li>Regular chewing gum is made of plastic — ‘polyethylene’, which is also used to make tires and glue.</li><li>Iran chews the most gum in the world. Shop keepers will give gum instead of change if they don’t have enough.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Week 39":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Question</th><td class=\"lt-td\">New Zealand eats the most of this per capita (person) in the world.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Answer</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Ice Cream</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Facts</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Many people think this was invented in Italy because of Gelato, but it was actually invented in China.</li><li>Japan has some of the most interesting flavors of ice cream, such as basil, eggplant, oyster, eel, beef tongue, coal, and Indian curry.</li><li>The average number of licks to finish a scoop of ice cream is 50.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Week 40":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Question</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Despite being one of the wealthiest artists of his time, he lived like a hermit and rarely met people.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Answer</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Michelangelo</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Facts</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>His famous statue ‘David’ was made from a discarded block of marble.</li><li>When he was young, his nose was broken by a jealous rival — he was punched so hard he had the damage for the rest of his life.</li><li>Many of his works were created using his own likeness.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Week 41":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Question</th><td class=\"lt-td\">This animal can reach speeds of up to 193 kms per hour.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Answer</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Golden Eagle</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Facts</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The Golden Eagle can swim if it is totally necessary.</li><li>In winter and early spring they defend their territories with a ‘sky dance’.</li><li>Parents use their bodies to insulate chicks in extreme temperatures.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Week 42":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Question</th><td class=\"lt-td\">This is one of the oldest sports in the world, and the first known usage of it was in 2340 BC by the Babylonians.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Answer</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Archery</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Facts</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Olympic archers used to shoot at pigeons.</li><li>Archery has been said to be 3x safer than golf, with only 1 injury per 2000 participants. It is also safer than bowling.</li><li>Archery was the first sport to allow women to compete in the Olympic Games.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Week 43":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Question</th><td class=\"lt-td\">The standard measurement used to measure the height of this animal is ‘hands’.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Answer</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Horse</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Facts</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Because of the one-way mode of their digestive tract, horses cannot burp.</li><li>You can estimate a horse’s age by its teeth, although if it’s old and its teeth are bad it will need dental work done.</li><li>Most Arabic breeds of horses have fewer bones than other breeds — in their tail, vertebrae, and ribs.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Week 44":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Question</th><td class=\"lt-td\">In America, these get lost, sat on, or broken every 14 minutes. That means over 37,500 every year.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Answer</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Sunglasses</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Facts</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Only 25% of sunglasses actually protect your eyes from the sun’s harmful rays. The other 75% are only useful as a fashion accessory.</li><li>Different color lenses were developed by James Ayscough, in the hope of resolving color blindness.</li><li>They were invented 2000 years ago by the Inuit. They were made from animal bone, leather, and wood, with small slits cut so only a tiny amount of light could get through.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Week 45":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Question</th><td class=\"lt-td\">This is the most stolen book in the world. It's regularly taken from hotels, hospitals, and book stores.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Answer</th><td class=\"lt-td\">The Bible</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Facts</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>It is also the most-read book in the world, with over 100 million copies sold or donated every year.</li><li>The Bible has about 40 different authors and was originally written in 3 different languages: Hebrew, Aramaic, and Greek.</li><li>It is over 600,000 words long and has about 1189 chapters — 929 in the Old Testament and 260 in the New Testament.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Week 46":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Question</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Ancient Egyptians and Mesopotamians were known to do this to their food to preserve it through long journeys and harsh seasons.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Answer</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Pickling (Pickles)</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Facts</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Pickles are actually cucumbers that have been brined in a solution of vinegar, water, and spices.</li><li>Cleopatra supposedly credited her good looks to her love of pickles, and Julius Caesar was said to have fed pickles to his troops in the belief it would make them stronger.</li><li>In Connecticut, legend has it that state law requires that a pickle must bounce in order to legally be called a pickle.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Week 47":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Question</th><td class=\"lt-td\">This person is sometimes nicknamed ‘La Pulga’ (‘The Flea’) because of their quick and amazing movements.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Answer</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lionel Messi</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Facts</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>He had a growth deficiency growing up, but when he moved to Barcelona at 13 years old, the club paid for his growth hormone treatment.</li><li>Messi made his debut for Barcelona’s first team in 2004, when he was only 17 years old.</li><li>He has scored 665 goals with his left foot, 100 with his right, and 26 with his head.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Week 48":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Question</th><td class=\"lt-td\">In ancient times, soldiers in this country sometimes wore armour made from paper.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Answer</th><td class=\"lt-td\">China</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Facts</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Put together, all of China’s railway lines could loop around the world twice.</li><li>Parts of China can range from 49 degrees Celsius in summer to -27 degrees in winter.</li><li>In 2010, a 2,400-year-old pot of soup was unearthed in Xi’an, China.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Week 49":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Question</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Over 75 different pieces of wood are used to build a modern version of one of these.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Answer</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Violin</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Facts</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Playing the violin burns about 170 calories per hour — an hour of walking burns between 200-300 calories.</li><li>Violin strings were originally made of sheep gut (commonly known as catgut), which was stretched, dried, and twisted.</li><li>The world record for cycling backwards while playing a violin is 60.45 kms, set in 5 hours and 8 seconds.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Week 50":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Question</th><td class=\"lt-td\">These can reach a temperature of almost 30,000 degrees Celsius — 5 times hotter than the surface of the sun.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Answer</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lightning</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Facts</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The flash we see travels at the speed of light, about 900,000,000 kph. The lightning itself only travels around 400,000 kph — at that speed it would take 55 minutes to reach the moon.</li><li>The actual width of a lightning bolt is only 2-3 cms, whereas its length averages 3-5 kms.</li><li>When lightning strikes sand or sandy soil, it fuses the grains together to create a small glass-like tube known as fulgurite.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Week 51":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Question</th><td class=\"lt-td\">The first of these were sticks used by ancient Egyptians, Greeks, Romans, and Indians — frayed on one end to make them work more effectively.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Answer</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Toothbrush</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Facts</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Until the early 1900s, toothbrush bristles were made from Siberian hog hair, until the Miracle Tuft soft-bristled toothbrush was invented.</li><li>One in ten people admit that they regularly forget to brush their teeth.</li><li>Dentists recommend keeping your toothbrush at least 2 meters away from the toilet, to prevent the spread of airborne bacteria when flushing.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Week 52":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Question</th><td class=\"lt-td\">A person with good eyesight can probably see between 2000-2500 of these at a time.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Answer</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Stars</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Facts</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Usually red means hot and blue means cold, but stars are the opposite — red stars are cooler and blue stars are hotter.</li><li>On a good night you can see 17,000,000,000,000,000 miles — that’s 17 quadrillion miles to the bright star Deneb.</li><li>Every star you can see in our night sky is bigger than our sun. Only a few faint stars are about the same size as the sun.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Week 53":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Question</th><td class=\"lt-td\">His boat ‘The Endeavour’ almost sank when he hit sharp coral in the Great Barrier Reef, Australia.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Answer</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Captain Cook</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Facts</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Cook was famous for his mapmaking, and was the first European to define the outline of New Zealand.</li><li>NASA named two of their space shuttles after Cook’s ships, HMS Enterprise and Endeavour. Astronauts also wore special medals to honor Cook.</li><li>He was killed in Hawaii by local natives — beaten with clubs, and then stabbed in the back. He was later given a king’s burial.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Week 54":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Question</th><td class=\"lt-td\">This person refused to participate in his main event at the Olympics because the qualifying round was on a Sunday and he was a devout Christian.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Answer</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Eric Liddell</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Facts</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>He was born in China to Scottish parents who were missionaries there.</li><li>In 1943, during WW2, he was imprisoned in Weihsien internment camp, where he organised games for children, who called him ‘Uncle Eric’.</li><li>He was a gifted athlete, excelling at both running and rugby. He won an Olympic gold medal in the 400m — which wasn’t even his strongest event.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Week 55":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Question</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Because this country is the closest to the west side of the International Date Line, it is the first to see the new day — and therefore the first to celebrate the new year.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Answer</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Samoa</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Facts</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Samoa was known as Western Samoa up until 1997 — that’s why the currency ‘Samoan Tala’ is sometimes referred to as ‘WST’.</li><li>Samoans often sit on a mat on the floor with their legs crossed beneath them, with the lavalava covering their toes — it’s considered rude to point your toes at someone.</li><li>There is a special day in October called ‘White Sunday’. Children are celebrated on this day, made their favorite foods, and given special gifts.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Week 56":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Question</th><td class=\"lt-td\">This place is geographically unique because it is one of only 8 cities that have a near-exact antipodal city.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Answer</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Christchurch</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Facts</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>It's believed the Christchurch area was first settled in 1250 by tribes hunting the now-extinct moa.</li><li>Canterbury Pilgrims had goals of building a new city around a cathedral and college, like Christ Church, Oxford in England — hence the name Christchurch.</li><li>On July 31, 1856, Christchurch became the first official city in New Zealand, and it quickly grew as more European settlers arrived.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Week 57":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Question</th><td class=\"lt-td\">In 2013, Aaron ‘Wheelz’ Fotheringham became the first person to successfully complete a backflip using one of these.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Answer</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Wheelchair</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Facts</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Wheelchair basketball was invented by American World War II veterans with spinal cord injuries. The first official game was played in 1946.</li><li>In some countries, laws require public buildings to be wheelchair accessible — these laws have helped make public spaces more inclusive for people with disabilities.</li><li>The Guinness World Record for the fastest wheelchair speed is held by Heinz Frei from Switzerland, who reached 40.6 kms per hour in 2011.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Week 58":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Question</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Vincent Van Gogh painted four still-life paintings featuring these — one was called ‘The (this) Eaters’.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Answer</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Potatoes</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Facts</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Marie Antoinette liked potatoes so much that she wore their blossoms in her hair.</li><li>The Incas of Peru were the first to cultivate potatoes, around 8000 BC to 5000 BC.</li><li>Early growers discovered that just one acre of potatoes could feed ten people — much better than wheat or oats.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Week 59":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Question</th><td class=\"lt-td\">The South American one of these is so big that if it were a country, it would be the ninth biggest in the world.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Answer</th><td class=\"lt-td\">The Amazon Rainforest</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Facts</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>It can take ten minutes for a falling raindrop to travel from the rainforest’s thick canopy down to the floor.</li><li>A quarter of the ingredients in modern medicines come from rainforest plants.</li><li>A tree known as the idiot fruit grows in Australia’s Daintree rainforest.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Week 60":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Question</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Laid end to end, the number of these sold in a year would reach more than five times around the world.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Answer</th><td class=\"lt-td\">LEGO</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Facts</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The world’s tallest LEGO tower is 28.7m high, made from 465,000 bricks!</li><li>During the Christmas season, almost 28 LEGO sets are sold every second!</li><li>LEGO Minifigures are the world’s largest population, with over 4 billion of them around the world!</li></ul></div></div>"},"Week 61":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Question</th><td class=\"lt-td\">These don’t have bones — their skeletons are made of cartilage, the same soft, flexible stuff your ears and the tip of your nose are made of.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Answer</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Sharks</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Facts</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Most sharks can see well in dimly lit areas, have fantastic night vision, and can see colors.</li><li>Shark skin feels exactly like sandpaper because it’s made up of tiny teeth-like structures called placoid scales, also known as dermal denticles.</li><li>When you flip a shark upside down, it goes into a trance-like state called tonic immobility.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Week 62":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Question</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Between 1784 and 1811, these were taxed in the UK. If you sold them you needed a special licence, and buyers had to pay a tax too — a way for the government to get money from the rich.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Answer</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Hats</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Facts</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>In Fargo, North Dakota, USA, it’s illegal to wear a hat while dancing — it’s also illegal to wear a hat in a place where dancing might take place.</li><li>There are over 100 types of hats, including bucket hats, beanies, and baseball caps — plus berets, fezzes, sombreros, cowboy hats, trilbies, and top hats!</li><li>The world’s tallest hat is 4.8 metres tall. It was made by Odilon Ozare, who had to wear it and walk over 10 metres to get the world record!</li></ul></div></div>"},"Week 63":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Question</th><td class=\"lt-td\">This sport is so popular that it’s considered the second most-loved sport in the world, enjoyed by more than 2.5 billion fans in 180 countries.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Answer</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Cricket</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Facts</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Cricket actually began in 16th century England, when children used to play it on the streets.</li><li>Originally, cricket wickets had two stumps and only one bail — the third stump wasn’t introduced until 1775.</li><li>The first cricket World Cup was played by women, not men, two years before the men’s tournament arrived. The inaugural Women’s World Cup was held in 1973, hosted by England.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Week 64":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Question</th><td class=\"lt-td\">The world record for the longest of these goes to Kevin ‘Catfish’ McCarthy, who spent 340 hours and 40 minutes in one.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Answer</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Showers</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Facts</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Research has shown that 42% of people pee in the shower. Another study found that 7% of people openly admit to not bathing at all — be that a shower or a bath!</li><li>Showers are a great money saver compared to baths, which use around 170 litres of water — a 5-minute shower uses just 70–115 litres.</li><li>The world’s most expensive shower costs $160,400 NZD, from a company called Sliver Tag. With an array of super geeky gadgets, the only thing this shower doesn’t do is dry you afterwards!</li></ul></div></div>"},"Week 65":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Question</th><td class=\"lt-td\">In the right environment, these are known to live 100 years on average and can grow up to 46 metres high.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Answer</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Pine Trees (Christmas Trees)</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Facts</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>They can survive at elevations up to 13,000 metres, and are found in many mountain ranges and peaks.</li><li>Pines never shed their leaves — their leaves stay green all year round, and most pines have thick, scaly bark that bleeds resin.</li><li>Christmas trees are really pine trees — because they stay green year-round and are very hardy, they can handle all manner of decorations.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Week 66":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Question</th><td class=\"lt-td\">This doesn’t have an expiry date, but it will change over time — which is why you should use it within the first three years.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Answer</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Perfume</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Facts</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>You shouldn’t rub your wrists together after putting on perfume — the friction creates a reaction between the fragrance and your skin’s oils, which can change the scent.</li><li>The same scent can smell different on two different people, because perfume mixes with your skin’s own chemistry.</li><li>Research shows perfume can alter your mood depending on its ingredients — lavender helps you relax, while a citrus-infused scent awakens your senses.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Week 67":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Question</th><td class=\"lt-td\">During World War II, a type of this plant fiber was used to stuff life jackets — about six times more buoyant than cork.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Answer</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Weeds</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Facts</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Mustard weeds are edible — there are countless varieties of mustard, and while some are more palatable than others, many can be used in salads or for making mustard.</li><li>In 1941, a Swiss hiker came home from a walk in the Alps with burrs all over his clothes and his dog. Looking at the burrs under a microscope, he saw they were covered with millions of tiny hooks — and went on to invent Velcro.</li><li>Pigweed grows 1–3 inches a day, drawing huge amounts of water and nutrients. It can reach the size of a small tree, produces up to a million seeds per plant, and has become resistant to Roundup and several other herbicides.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Week 68":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Question</th><td class=\"lt-td\">They have a highly specialized cardiovascular system to manage blood flow to their brains. Their hearts are incredibly strong and large — about 0.6 metres long and weighing around 11 kgs.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Answer</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Giraffes</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Facts</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Giraffes primarily feed on the leaves, fruits, and flowers of woody plants, with acacia species being a favorite. Their tongues can be up to 45 cms long.</li><li>Giraffes usually sleep standing up, which helps them escape predators quickly if needed. They typically sleep for just a few minutes at a time, totalling about 30 minutes to 2 hours in a 24-hour period — one of the shortest sleep requirements in the animal kingdom.</li><li>Giraffe pregnancies last about 15 months. The female gives birth standing up, so the newborn’s first experience outside the womb is almost a 2-metre drop to the ground.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Week 69":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Question</th><td class=\"lt-td\">A study by the University of Pennsylvania found that neighborhoods with more of these had lower crime rates.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Answer</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Christmas Lights</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Facts</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>In 1882, Edison’s colleague Edward H. Johnson strung 80 colored electric lights around a Christmas tree in Edison’s New York City laboratory. They were a huge success and quickly became a popular way to decorate for the holiday season.</li><li>Early electric Christmas lights were hand-wired with delicate glass bulbs, making them very expensive. It wasn’t until the early 1900s that they became affordable for the average family.</li><li>In 2014, a Christmas tree in Norway was decorated with 500,000 lights. The tree was over 200 feet tall and could be seen for miles.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Week 70":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Question</th><td class=\"lt-td\">This sea creature has three hearts and blue blood.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Answer</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Octopuses</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Facts</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Octopus blood is blue because it's copper-based instead of iron-based like ours.</li><li>They have no bones, so they can squeeze through any gap bigger than their beak, the only hard part of their body.</li><li>Octopuses can taste things just by touching them with their arms and suckers.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Week 71":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Question</th><td class=\"lt-td\">This snack can pop up to 3 feet into the air.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Answer</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Popcorn</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Facts</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Each kernel contains a tiny bit of water, which turns to steam and builds pressure until the kernel explodes.</li><li>Native Americans were popping corn thousands of years before Europeans ever arrived.</li><li>The world's largest popcorn ball on record weighed over 9,000 kgs — heavier than an elephant.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Week 72":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Question</th><td class=\"lt-td\">You actually can't fully sink in this, no matter what the movies show.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Answer</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Quicksand</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Facts</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Quicksand is just ordinary sand that's been saturated with water, which makes it behave like a liquid until you move.</li><li>Since your body is less dense than quicksand, you naturally float instead of sinking all the way — panicking and thrashing is what actually pulls people under.</li><li>The safest way out is to move slowly, lean back to spread your weight, and paddle toward firmer ground.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Week 73":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Question</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Some of these shoot out strands of glass instead of lava.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Answer</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Volcanoes</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Facts</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Certain eruptions produce thin strands of volcanic glass nicknamed ‘Pele’s hair’, after the Hawaiian goddess of volcanoes.</li><li>Volcanoes exist on other planets too — Mars is home to Olympus Mons, the largest volcano in the entire solar system.</li><li>There are roughly 1,500 potentially active volcanoes around the world, not even counting the ones hidden underwater.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Week 74":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Question</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Time moves slower the closer you get to one of these.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Answer</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Black Holes</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Facts</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Black holes form when massive stars collapse in on themselves at the end of their life.</li><li>Nothing can escape a black hole once it crosses the ‘event horizon’ — not even light.</li><li>There's a supermassive black hole sitting at the very center of our own galaxy, the Milky Way.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Week 75":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Question</th><td class=\"lt-td\">The very first versions of this ride were made of ice.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Answer</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Roller Coasters</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Facts</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>In 17th century Russia, people built giant ice slides and rode down them on sleds — an early ancestor of the roller coaster.</li><li>The first wheeled roller coaster was built in France after warmer weather made the ice slides impossible to keep running year-round.</li><li>The tallest roller coaster in the world today stands taller than a 45-story building.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Week 76":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Question</th><td class=\"lt-td\">This toy was once used as a hunting weapon.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Answer</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Yo-Yos</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Facts</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Ancient Filipino hunters are believed to have used large yo-yo-like weapons on ropes that could be thrown and pulled back.</li><li>The yo-yo is one of the oldest toys in the world, with versions dating back over 2,500 years to ancient Greece.</li><li>The world's largest working yo-yo weighs almost 2 tonnes.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Week 77":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Question</th><td class=\"lt-td\">This piece of equipment was studied to help train pilots and astronauts.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Answer</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Trampolines</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Facts</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Early trampolines were inspired by circus performers who used safety nets to bounce after trapeze acts.</li><li>NASA studied trampoline-style bouncing because it builds coordination in ways similar to moving in zero gravity.</li><li>Trampolining became an official Olympic sport in the year 2000.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Week 78":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Question</th><td class=\"lt-td\">This sport started out being called ‘sidewalk surfing’.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Answer</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Skateboarding</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Facts</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Skateboarding began in California in the 1950s, when surfers wanted something to ride when the waves were flat.</li><li>Early skateboards were often homemade, using wooden boards with roller skate wheels bolted on.</li><li>Skateboarding became an official Olympic sport in 2020.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Week 79":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Question</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Despite the name, this creature isn't actually a worm.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Answer</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Glow Worms</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Facts</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Glow worms are really the larvae of certain beetles and flies, not true worms at all.</li><li>Their glow comes from a chemical reaction inside their bodies called bioluminescence.</li><li>They use their glow to attract prey — insects fly toward the light and get caught in sticky threads.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Week 80":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Question</th><td class=\"lt-td\">This plant can count to five.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Answer</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Venus Flytraps</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Facts</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A Venus flytrap's trap only snaps shut after something touches its trigger hairs twice within about 20 seconds — a built-in counter that stops it wasting energy on false alarms.</li><li>It takes several more touches after that before the trap actually starts digesting its prey.</li><li>Each individual trap can only close and reopen a handful of times before it stops working and falls off.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Week 81":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Question</th><td class=\"lt-td\">This creature isn't actually an eel at all.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Answer</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Electric Eels</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Facts</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Electric eels are more closely related to catfish and carp than to true eels.</li><li>They can produce a shock of up to 600 volts — enough to stun a horse.</li><li>They need to surface for air every so often, since they breathe partly through their mouths.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Week 82":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Question</th><td class=\"lt-td\">This mountain grows a few millimeters taller every single year.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Answer</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Mount Everest</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Facts</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Everest is still growing because of the ongoing collision between the Indian and Eurasian tectonic plates.</li><li>Its summit is so high that some planes fly below it.</li><li>Hundreds of climbers have died attempting to reach the top, and many remain on the mountain because it's too dangerous to bring them down.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Week 83":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Question</th><td class=\"lt-td\">This animal can hold its breath longer than a dolphin.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Answer</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Sloths</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Facts</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Sloths can slow their heart rate right down and hold their breath for up to 40 minutes underwater.</li><li>They only come down from the trees about once a week, usually just to go to the bathroom.</li><li>Algae often grows in a sloth's fur, giving it a greenish tint that helps camouflage it in the trees.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Week 84":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Question</th><td class=\"lt-td\">This animal has fingerprints that are almost identical to a human's.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Answer</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Koalas</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Facts</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Koala fingerprints are so similar to human ones that they've occasionally confused crime scene investigators.</li><li>Koalas sleep between 18 and 22 hours a day.</li><li>Their diet is almost entirely eucalyptus leaves, which are toxic to most other animals.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Week 85":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Question</th><td class=\"lt-td\">This animal glows under UV light.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Answer</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Platypuses</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Facts</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Platypus fur glows a blue-green color under ultraviolet light, though scientists aren't fully sure why.</li><li>Male platypuses have venomous spurs on their hind legs.</li><li>They're one of the only mammals in the world that lay eggs instead of giving birth to live young.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Week 86":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Question</th><td class=\"lt-td\">This animal's famous ‘horn’ is actually a giant tooth.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Answer</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Narwhals</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Facts</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A narwhal's tusk is really an overgrown canine tooth that can grow up to 3 metres long.</li><li>Usually only males grow the tusk, though some females can too.</li><li>The tusk is packed with nerve endings and is thought to help narwhals sense changes in their environment.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Week 87":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Question</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Males of this bird lose their spectacular tail feathers every single year.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Answer</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Peacocks</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Facts</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Peacocks molt their long tail feathers after breeding season and grow a brand new set before the next one.</li><li>Only males have the famous colorful tail — females, called peahens, are much more plain.</li><li>A peacock's tail feathers can make up over 60% of its total body length.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Week 88":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Question</th><td class=\"lt-td\">This animal doesn't mainly change color to hide from predators.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Answer</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Chameleons</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Facts</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Chameleons mostly change color to communicate mood or to help regulate their body temperature, not primarily for camouflage.</li><li>Their eyes can move independently, letting them look in two different directions at the same time.</li><li>A chameleon's tongue can shoot out faster than a Formula 1 car accelerates.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Week 89":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Question</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Some versions of this toy are designed to never come back.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Answer</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Boomerangs</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Facts</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Returning boomerangs are actually the exception — most boomerangs, historically used for hunting, were designed to fly straight and not return.</li><li>The curved shape of a returning boomerang works a bit like an airplane wing, creating lift as it spins through the air.</li><li>The oldest known boomerang is over 20,000 years old, found in a cave in Poland.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Week 90":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Question</th><td class=\"lt-td\">The colors in this display come from burning different metals.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Answer</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Fireworks</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Facts</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Different metal compounds create different colors — strontium makes red, copper makes blue, and barium makes green.</li><li>Fireworks were invented in China over 1,000 years ago, originally as a way to scare off evil spirits.</li><li>Blue is the hardest firework color to create, since the copper compounds involved are especially unstable at high heat.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Week 91":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Question</th><td class=\"lt-td\">This dinosaur had a bite force strong enough to crush a car.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Answer</th><td class=\"lt-td\">T-Rex</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Facts</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A T-Rex's bite force is estimated at up to 12,800 kgs — among the strongest of any land animal ever.</li><li>Despite its huge size, a T-Rex's arms were surprisingly short — only about the length of an adult human's arm.</li><li>Scientists believe young T-Rexes may have had feathers.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Week 92":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Question</th><td class=\"lt-td\">This bird proposes to its mate with a pebble.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Answer</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Penguins</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Facts</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Some penguin species, like Adelie penguins, offer a smooth pebble to a potential mate as a kind of proposal.</li><li>Penguins can't fly, but they ‘fly’ through water instead, reaching speeds of up to 35 km/h.</li><li>Emperor penguin dads keep their egg warm on their feet for around two months without eating, while the mothers hunt at sea.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Week 93":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Question</th><td class=\"lt-td\">This insect never sleeps.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Answer</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Honey Bees</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Facts</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Honey bees don't sleep the way humans do, but they do have rest periods where their antennae droop and they go still.</li><li>A single bee produces only about one twelfth of a teaspoon of honey in its entire lifetime.</li><li>Bees tell each other where to find food using a special ‘waggle dance’.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Week 94":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Question</th><td class=\"lt-td\">This sweet treat was originally made from a plant root.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Answer</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Marshmallows</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Facts</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Marshmallows get their name from the marsh mallow plant, whose root sap was originally used to make the treat.</li><li>Ancient Egyptians were among the first to combine marsh mallow sap with honey to create an early version of the candy.</li><li>Modern marshmallows don't use the plant at all anymore — they're made mainly from sugar, corn syrup, and gelatin.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Week 95":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Question</th><td class=\"lt-td\">This packing material was originally invented as wallpaper.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Answer</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Bubble Wrap</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Facts</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Bubble wrap was invented in 1957 when two engineers tried to make textured wallpaper by sealing two shower curtains together.</li><li>After the wallpaper idea failed, they tried marketing it as greenhouse insulation before it finally caught on as packaging material.</li><li>There's an unofficial holiday called ‘Bubble Wrap Appreciation Day’, celebrated on the last Monday of January.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Week 96":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Question</th><td class=\"lt-td\">This puzzle has over 43 quintillion possible combinations.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Answer</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rubik's Cube</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Facts</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A Rubik's Cube has roughly 43 quintillion possible arrangements — that's a 43 followed by 18 zeros.</li><li>It was invented in 1974 by a Hungarian professor, originally as a teaching tool to help explain 3D geometry.</li><li>The current world record for solving a Rubik's Cube is under 4 seconds.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Week 97":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Question</th><td class=\"lt-td\">This invisible force can make your hair stand straight up.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Answer</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Static Electricity</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Facts</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Static electricity happens when tiny charged particles called electrons build up on a surface and have nowhere to go.</li><li>Lightning is really just a giant, supercharged version of the same static shock you get from touching a doorknob.</li><li>Dry winter air makes static shocks more common, since moisture in the air normally helps the charge drain away harmlessly.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Week 98":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Question</th><td class=\"lt-td\">This animal is often called the world's happiest animal because of its permanent smile.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Answer</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Quokkas</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Facts</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Quokkas are small marsupials native to Australia, and the shape of their face just naturally looks like a big smile.</li><li>They're famously unafraid of humans, which has made ‘quokka selfies’ a popular trend.</li><li>Quokkas are nocturnal and spend most of the day resting in shady spots.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Week 99":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Question</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Babies do this while they're still inside the womb.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Answer</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Hiccups</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Facts</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Unborn babies can hiccup for minutes at a time, and scientists think it might help develop their breathing muscles.</li><li>The longest recorded case of hiccups in a person lasted 68 years.</li><li>Hiccups happen when your diaphragm suddenly contracts, and the ‘hic’ sound is your vocal cords snapping shut right after.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Week 100":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Question</th><td class=\"lt-td\">This insect's light is nearly 100% efficient, wasting almost no energy as heat.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Answer</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Fireflies</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Facts</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A regular light bulb wastes most of its energy as heat, but a firefly's glow is almost entirely light — one of the most efficient light sources known.</li><li>Firefly light comes from a chemical reaction involving a compound called luciferin.</li><li>Each species of firefly has its own unique flashing pattern, which helps them recognize potential mates in the dark.</li></ul></div></div>"}}},"Memory Verse":{"label":"Theme","children":{"Aroha · Love":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Memory Verses</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-data lt-tbl-striped\"><thead><tr><th class=\"lt-th-col\" style=\"width:44px;\">#</th><th class=\"lt-th-col\" style=\"width:190px;\">Reference</th><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Verse</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">1</td><td class=\"lt-td\"><strong>John 3:16</strong></td><td class=\"lt-td\">For God so loved the world, that he gave his only born Son, that whoever believes in him should not perish, but have eternal life.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">2</td><td class=\"lt-td\"><strong>1 John 4:19</strong></td><td class=\"lt-td\">We love him, because he first loved us.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">3</td><td class=\"lt-td\"><strong>Mark 12:30–31</strong></td><td class=\"lt-td\">You shall love the Lord your God with all your heart, with all your soul, with all your mind, and with all your strength. This is the first commandment. The second is like this: You shall love your neighbour as yourself. There is no other commandment greater than these.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">4</td><td class=\"lt-td\"><strong>1 Corinthians 13:4</strong></td><td class=\"lt-td\">Love is patient and is kind. Love doesn’t envy. Love doesn’t brag, is not proud.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">5</td><td class=\"lt-td\"><strong>1 Corinthians 13:13</strong></td><td class=\"lt-td\">But now faith, hope, and love remain — these three. The greatest of these is love.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">6</td><td class=\"lt-td\"><strong>John 13:34</strong></td><td class=\"lt-td\">A new commandment I give to you, that you love one another. Just as I have loved you, you also love one another.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">7</td><td class=\"lt-td\"><strong>Romans 5:8</strong></td><td class=\"lt-td\">But God shows his own love toward us, in that while we were still sinners, Christ died for us.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">8</td><td class=\"lt-td\"><strong>1 John 4:7</strong></td><td class=\"lt-td\">Beloved, let us love one another, for love is of God, and everyone who loves is born of God and knows God.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">9</td><td class=\"lt-td\"><strong>Ephesians 4:32</strong></td><td class=\"lt-td\">Be kind to one another, tender-hearted, forgiving each other, just as God also in Christ forgave you.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">10</td><td class=\"lt-td\"><strong>Colossians 3:14</strong></td><td class=\"lt-td\">Above all these things, put on love, which is the bond of perfection.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">11</td><td class=\"lt-td\"><strong>1 Peter 4:8</strong></td><td class=\"lt-td\">Above all things be earnest in your love for one another, for love covers a multitude of sins.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">12</td><td class=\"lt-td\"><strong>John 15:13</strong></td><td class=\"lt-td\">Greater love has no one than this, that someone lay down his life for his friends.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">13</td><td class=\"lt-td\"><strong>1 John 4:11</strong></td><td class=\"lt-td\">Beloved, if God loved us this way, we also ought to love one another.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">14</td><td class=\"lt-td\"><strong>Proverbs 17:17</strong></td><td class=\"lt-td\">A friend loves at all times, and a brother is born for adversity.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">15</td><td class=\"lt-td\"><strong>Galatians 5:22–23</strong></td><td class=\"lt-td\">But the fruit of the Spirit is love, joy, peace, patience, kindness, goodness, faith, gentleness, and self-control. Against such things there is no law.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">16</td><td class=\"lt-td\"><strong>Psalm 136:1</strong></td><td class=\"lt-td\">Give thanks to the LORD, for he is good, for his loving kindness endures forever.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">17</td><td class=\"lt-td\"><strong>Romans 12:10</strong></td><td class=\"lt-td\">In love of the family, be tenderly affectioned one to another. In honour, prefer one another.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">18</td><td class=\"lt-td\"><strong>1 John 3:18</strong></td><td class=\"lt-td\">Little children, let us not love in word only, or with the tongue only, but in deed and truth.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">19</td><td class=\"lt-td\"><strong>Zephaniah 3:17</strong></td><td class=\"lt-td\">The LORD your God is among you, a mighty one who will save. He will rejoice over you with joy. He will calm you with his love. He will rejoice over you with singing.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">20</td><td class=\"lt-td\"><strong>Jeremiah 31:3</strong></td><td class=\"lt-td\">The LORD appeared long ago to me, saying, “I have loved you with an everlasting love. Therefore I have continued to be loving and kind to you.”</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">21</td><td class=\"lt-td\"><strong>Deuteronomy 6:5</strong></td><td class=\"lt-td\">You shall love the LORD your God with all your heart, with all your soul, and with all your strength.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">22</td><td class=\"lt-td\"><strong>John 14:15</strong></td><td class=\"lt-td\">If you love me, keep my commandments.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">23</td><td class=\"lt-td\"><strong>1 Corinthians 16:14</strong></td><td class=\"lt-td\">Let all that you do be done in love.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">24</td><td class=\"lt-td\"><strong>Matthew 22:37–39</strong></td><td class=\"lt-td\">You shall love the Lord your God with all your heart, with all your soul, and with all your mind. This is the first and great commandment. And a second is like it: You shall love your neighbour as yourself.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">25</td><td class=\"lt-td\"><strong>Romans 13:8</strong></td><td class=\"lt-td\">Owe no one anything, except to love one another, for the one who loves his neighbour has fulfilled the law.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Mōhio · Know":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Memory Verses</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-data lt-tbl-striped\"><thead><tr><th class=\"lt-th-col\" style=\"width:44px;\">#</th><th class=\"lt-th-col\" style=\"width:190px;\">Reference</th><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Verse</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">26</td><td class=\"lt-td\"><strong>Jeremiah 29:11</strong></td><td class=\"lt-td\">For I know the plans I have for you, says the LORD: plans for peace, and not for disaster, to give you a future and a hope.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">27</td><td class=\"lt-td\"><strong>Proverbs 3:5–6</strong></td><td class=\"lt-td\">Trust in the LORD with all your heart, and don’t lean on your own understanding. In all your ways acknowledge him, and he will make your paths straight.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">28</td><td class=\"lt-td\"><strong>Psalm 139:14</strong></td><td class=\"lt-td\">I will give thanks to you, for I am fearfully and wonderfully made. Your works are wonderful. My soul knows that very well.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">29</td><td class=\"lt-td\"><strong>Genesis 1:1</strong></td><td class=\"lt-td\">In the beginning, God created the heavens and the earth.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">30</td><td class=\"lt-td\"><strong>John 1:1</strong></td><td class=\"lt-td\">In the beginning was the Word, and the Word was with God, and the Word was God.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">31</td><td class=\"lt-td\"><strong>Isaiah 40:31</strong></td><td class=\"lt-td\">Those who wait for the LORD will renew their strength. They will mount up with wings like eagles. They will run and not be weary. They will walk and not faint.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">32</td><td class=\"lt-td\"><strong>Psalm 119:105</strong></td><td class=\"lt-td\">Your word is a lamp to my feet, and a light for my path.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">33</td><td class=\"lt-td\"><strong>2 Timothy 3:16</strong></td><td class=\"lt-td\">Every Scripture is God-breathed and profitable for teaching, for reproof, for correction, and for training in righteousness.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">34</td><td class=\"lt-td\"><strong>Psalm 46:10</strong></td><td class=\"lt-td\">Be still, and know that I am God. I will be exalted among the nations. I will be exalted in the earth.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">35</td><td class=\"lt-td\"><strong>Isaiah 55:8–9</strong></td><td class=\"lt-td\">“For my thoughts are not your thoughts, and your ways are not my ways,” says the LORD. “For as the heavens are higher than the earth, so are my ways higher than your ways, and my thoughts than your thoughts.”</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">36</td><td class=\"lt-td\"><strong>Psalm 100:3</strong></td><td class=\"lt-td\">Know that the LORD, he is God. It is he who made us, and we are his. We are his people, and the sheep of his pasture.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">37</td><td class=\"lt-td\"><strong>Exodus 20:3</strong></td><td class=\"lt-td\">You shall have no other gods before me.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">38</td><td class=\"lt-td\"><strong>Deuteronomy 6:4</strong></td><td class=\"lt-td\">Hear, O Israel: the LORD is our God. The LORD is one.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">39</td><td class=\"lt-td\"><strong>Psalm 90:2</strong></td><td class=\"lt-td\">Before the mountains were born, before you had formed the earth and the world, from everlasting to everlasting, you are God.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">40</td><td class=\"lt-td\"><strong>Colossians 1:16</strong></td><td class=\"lt-td\">By him all things were created, in the heavens and on the earth, visible and invisible. All things have been created through him and for him.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">41</td><td class=\"lt-td\"><strong>Hebrews 11:1</strong></td><td class=\"lt-td\">Now faith is being sure of what we hope for, and certain of what we do not see.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">42</td><td class=\"lt-td\"><strong>Romans 8:28</strong></td><td class=\"lt-td\">We know that all things work together for good for those who love God, for those who are called according to his purpose.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">43</td><td class=\"lt-td\"><strong>Psalm 139:13</strong></td><td class=\"lt-td\">You formed my inmost being. You knit me together in my mother’s womb.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">44</td><td class=\"lt-td\"><strong>John 14:6</strong></td><td class=\"lt-td\">Jesus said to him, “I am the way, the truth, and the life. No one comes to the Father except through me.”</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">45</td><td class=\"lt-td\"><strong>Isaiah 41:10</strong></td><td class=\"lt-td\">Don’t be afraid, for I am with you. Don’t be dismayed, for I am your God. I will strengthen you. I will help you. I will uphold you with my righteous right hand.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">46</td><td class=\"lt-td\"><strong>Psalm 23:1</strong></td><td class=\"lt-td\">The LORD is my shepherd; I shall lack nothing.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">47</td><td class=\"lt-td\"><strong>Matthew 6:33</strong></td><td class=\"lt-td\">But seek first God’s Kingdom and his righteousness, and all these things will be given to you as well.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">48</td><td class=\"lt-td\"><strong>Proverbs 1:7</strong></td><td class=\"lt-td\">The fear of the LORD is the beginning of knowledge, but fools despise wisdom and instruction.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">49</td><td class=\"lt-td\"><strong>John 8:32</strong></td><td class=\"lt-td\">You will know the truth, and the truth will set you free.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">50</td><td class=\"lt-td\"><strong>2 Corinthians 5:17</strong></td><td class=\"lt-td\">Therefore if anyone is in Christ, he is a new creation. The old things have passed away. See, all things have become new.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Manaaki · Serve":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Memory Verses</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-data lt-tbl-striped\"><thead><tr><th class=\"lt-th-col\" style=\"width:44px;\">#</th><th class=\"lt-th-col\" style=\"width:190px;\">Reference</th><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Verse</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">51</td><td class=\"lt-td\"><strong>1 Peter 4:10</strong></td><td class=\"lt-td\">As each has received a gift, use it to serve one another, as good managers of God’s grace in its many forms.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">52</td><td class=\"lt-td\"><strong>Galatians 5:13</strong></td><td class=\"lt-td\">For you were called to freedom. Only don’t use your freedom as an opportunity for the flesh, but through love serve one another.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">53</td><td class=\"lt-td\"><strong>Colossians 3:23</strong></td><td class=\"lt-td\">Whatever you do, work at it heartily, as for the Lord and not for men.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">54</td><td class=\"lt-td\"><strong>Mark 10:45</strong></td><td class=\"lt-td\">For even the Son of Man came not to be served, but to serve, and to give his life as a ransom for many.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">55</td><td class=\"lt-td\"><strong>Joshua 24:15</strong></td><td class=\"lt-td\">Choose for yourselves today whom you will serve...but as for me and my house, we will serve the LORD.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">56</td><td class=\"lt-td\"><strong>Matthew 20:26–28</strong></td><td class=\"lt-td\">Whoever wants to become great among you shall be your servant. Whoever wants to be first among you shall be your slave.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">57</td><td class=\"lt-td\"><strong>Romans 12:6</strong></td><td class=\"lt-td\">Having gifts that differ according to the grace given to us, let us use them well.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">58</td><td class=\"lt-td\"><strong>Ephesians 2:10</strong></td><td class=\"lt-td\">For we are God’s workmanship, created in Christ Jesus for good works, which God prepared beforehand, that we should walk in them.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">59</td><td class=\"lt-td\"><strong>1 Corinthians 12:27</strong></td><td class=\"lt-td\">Now you are the body of Christ, and individually members of it.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">60</td><td class=\"lt-td\"><strong>Philippians 2:3–4</strong></td><td class=\"lt-td\">Do nothing through selfish ambition or vanity, but in humility count others better than yourselves, each looking not to his own interests, but to the interests of others.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">61</td><td class=\"lt-td\"><strong>Galatians 6:9</strong></td><td class=\"lt-td\">Let us not grow weary in doing good, for we will reap a harvest in due season, if we don’t give up.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">62</td><td class=\"lt-td\"><strong>1 Timothy 4:12</strong></td><td class=\"lt-td\">Let no one look down on your youth, but be an example to the believers in word, in conduct, in love, in spirit, in faith, and in purity.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">63</td><td class=\"lt-td\"><strong>Hebrews 6:10</strong></td><td class=\"lt-td\">For God is not unjust, so as to forget your work and the love you showed for his name, in that you served the saints, and still do serve them.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">64</td><td class=\"lt-td\"><strong>1 Peter 4:11</strong></td><td class=\"lt-td\">...so that in everything God may be glorified through Jesus Christ, to whom belong the glory and the power forever and ever. Amen.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">65</td><td class=\"lt-td\"><strong>James 1:22</strong></td><td class=\"lt-td\">But be doers of the word, and not hearers only, deceiving yourselves.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">66</td><td class=\"lt-td\"><strong>Matthew 25:40</strong></td><td class=\"lt-td\">...Truly I tell you, whatever you did for one of the least of these brothers and sisters of mine, you did for me.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">67</td><td class=\"lt-td\"><strong>Proverbs 3:27</strong></td><td class=\"lt-td\">Don’t withhold good from those to whom it is due, when it is in your power to do it.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">68</td><td class=\"lt-td\"><strong>Luke 6:38</strong></td><td class=\"lt-td\">Give, and it will be given to you: good measure, pressed down, shaken together, and running over, will be given to you.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">69</td><td class=\"lt-td\"><strong>2 Corinthians 9:7</strong></td><td class=\"lt-td\">Let each one give as he has decided in his heart, not reluctantly or under compulsion, for God loves a cheerful giver.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">70</td><td class=\"lt-td\"><strong>Romans 12:1</strong></td><td class=\"lt-td\">I urge you, brothers and sisters, by the mercies of God, to offer your bodies as a living sacrifice, holy and pleasing to God — this is your true and proper worship.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">71</td><td class=\"lt-td\"><strong>1 Corinthians 10:31</strong></td><td class=\"lt-td\">So whether you eat or drink, or whatever you do, do it all for the glory of God.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">72</td><td class=\"lt-td\"><strong>Matthew 5:16</strong></td><td class=\"lt-td\">Let your light shine before others, that they may see your good works and give glory to your Father who is in heaven.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">73</td><td class=\"lt-td\"><strong>Acts 20:35</strong></td><td class=\"lt-td\">...It is more blessed to give than to receive.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">74</td><td class=\"lt-td\"><strong>Ephesians 6:7</strong></td><td class=\"lt-td\">With good will doing service, as to the Lord, and not to men.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">75</td><td class=\"lt-td\"><strong>Colossians 3:17</strong></td><td class=\"lt-td\">Whatever you do, in word or in deed, do it all in the name of the Lord Jesus, giving thanks to God the Father through him.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Pānga · Impact":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Memory Verses</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-data lt-tbl-striped\"><thead><tr><th class=\"lt-th-col\" style=\"width:44px;\">#</th><th class=\"lt-th-col\" style=\"width:190px;\">Reference</th><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Verse</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">76</td><td class=\"lt-td\"><strong>Matthew 5:14</strong></td><td class=\"lt-td\">You are the light of the world. A city set on a hill cannot be hidden.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">77</td><td class=\"lt-td\"><strong>Matthew 28:19–20</strong></td><td class=\"lt-td\">Go and make disciples of all nations, baptising them in the name of the Father and of the Son and of the Holy Spirit, teaching them to obey everything I have commanded you. And remember, I am with you always, to the very end of the age.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">78</td><td class=\"lt-td\"><strong>Matthew 6:10</strong></td><td class=\"lt-td\">Let your Kingdom come. Let your will be done on earth, as it is in heaven.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">79</td><td class=\"lt-td\"><strong>Philippians 4:13</strong></td><td class=\"lt-td\">I can do all things through Christ, who strengthens me.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">80</td><td class=\"lt-td\"><strong>2 Corinthians 5:20</strong></td><td class=\"lt-td\">We are therefore ambassadors for Christ, as though God were making his appeal through us. We beg you on Christ’s behalf: be reconciled to God.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">81</td><td class=\"lt-td\"><strong>Isaiah 6:8</strong></td><td class=\"lt-td\">...Here I am. Send me!</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">82</td><td class=\"lt-td\"><strong>Matthew 5:13</strong></td><td class=\"lt-td\">You are the salt of the earth. But if the salt loses its saltiness, how can it be made salty again?</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">83</td><td class=\"lt-td\"><strong>Acts 1:8</strong></td><td class=\"lt-td\">You will receive power when the Holy Spirit comes on you, and you will be my witnesses in Jerusalem, and in all Judea and Samaria, and to the ends of the earth.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">84</td><td class=\"lt-td\"><strong>Romans 12:2</strong></td><td class=\"lt-td\">Don’t be conformed to this world, but be transformed by the renewing of your mind, so that you may know what is the good, pleasing, and perfect will of God.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">85</td><td class=\"lt-td\"><strong>Ephesians 2:8–9</strong></td><td class=\"lt-td\">For it is by grace you have been saved, through faith — and this is not from yourselves, it is the gift of God — not by works, so that no one can boast.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">86</td><td class=\"lt-td\"><strong>James 2:17</strong></td><td class=\"lt-td\">So faith, if it has no works, is dead, being alone.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">87</td><td class=\"lt-td\"><strong>1 Peter 2:9</strong></td><td class=\"lt-td\">But you are a chosen people, a royal priesthood, a holy nation, God’s special possession, that you may proclaim the excellencies of him who called you out of darkness into his marvellous light.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">88</td><td class=\"lt-td\"><strong>Matthew 5:9</strong></td><td class=\"lt-td\">Blessed are the peacemakers, for they shall be called children of God.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">89</td><td class=\"lt-td\"><strong>Galatians 6:2</strong></td><td class=\"lt-td\">Carry each other’s burdens, and so fulfil the law of Christ.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">90</td><td class=\"lt-td\"><strong>2 Timothy 1:7</strong></td><td class=\"lt-td\">For God gave us a spirit not of fear, but of power, love, and self-control.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">91</td><td class=\"lt-td\"><strong>Joshua 1:9</strong></td><td class=\"lt-td\">Haven’t I commanded you? Be strong and courageous. Don’t be afraid. Don’t be discouraged, for the LORD your God is with you wherever you go.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">92</td><td class=\"lt-td\"><strong>Philippians 1:6</strong></td><td class=\"lt-td\">Being confident of this very thing, that he who began a good work in you will carry it on to completion until the day of Christ Jesus.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">93</td><td class=\"lt-td\"><strong>Esther 4:14</strong></td><td class=\"lt-td\">...Who knows whether you have come to the kingdom for such a time as this?</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">94</td><td class=\"lt-td\"><strong>Matthew 5:6</strong></td><td class=\"lt-td\">Blessed are those who hunger and thirst for righteousness, for they shall be filled.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">95</td><td class=\"lt-td\"><strong>Romans 10:14</strong></td><td class=\"lt-td\">How then can they call on the one they have not believed in? And how can they believe in the one of whom they have not heard? And how can they hear without someone preaching to them?</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">96</td><td class=\"lt-td\"><strong>1 Corinthians 15:58</strong></td><td class=\"lt-td\">Therefore, my beloved brothers and sisters, be steadfast, immovable, always excelling in the Lord’s work, because you know that your labour is not in vain in the Lord.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">97</td><td class=\"lt-td\"><strong>Ephesians 3:20</strong></td><td class=\"lt-td\">Now to him who is able to do far more abundantly than all we ask or think, according to the power that works within us.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">98</td><td class=\"lt-td\"><strong>Micah 6:8</strong></td><td class=\"lt-td\">...what does the LORD require of you, but to act justly, to love mercy, and to walk humbly with your God?</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">99</td><td class=\"lt-td\"><strong>Colossians 4:6</strong></td><td class=\"lt-td\">Let your speech always be gracious, seasoned with salt, so that you may know how to answer everyone.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">100</td><td class=\"lt-td\"><strong>Revelation 21:5</strong></td><td class=\"lt-td\">...Behold, I am making all things new...</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"}}}}},"maths":{"label":"Chapter","children":{"Chapter 1: Numbers to 1 000 000 — Place Value":{"label":"Lesson","children":{"Chapter Overview":{"label":"Chapter Overview","content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Chapter Overview</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>In this chapter, students will be looking at numbers and their place value to 1 000 000. The chapter begins reviewing how to read and write numbers to 100 000, quickly moving onto numbers to 1 000 000. Time is spent using concrete materials to represent numbers to 1 000 000, including place-value counters and place-value charts. Students then compare numbers to 1 000 000 using their knowledge of place value in addition to bar model supports to assist them. Students complete the unit by making number patterns and rounding numbers to the nearest 100, 1000, 10 000 and 100 000.</p></div></div>"},"Lesson 1: Reading and Writing Numbers to 100 000":{"label":"Lesson 1: Reading and Writing Numbers to 100 000","content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson Type</th><td class=\"lt-td\">COMBINED</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Textbook Page(s)</th><td class=\"lt-td\">2 - 5</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson Objective</th><td class=\"lt-td\">To be able to read and represent numbers to 100 000.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Lesson Approach</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>To begin this lesson, provide students with place-value counters and show them the Explore task. Ask them if Ruby will need more counters, or other types of counters. How many more? Allow students time to work on this and show how they would create the given number. Ask them if it is true that because the ten-thousands column holds the highest value, it will always require the most number of counters. How do we know? Tell them to come up with scenarios that disprove this statement. Ask students if they are able to tell you the exact number of counters needed to represent a number without counting the counters. How would they represent a number above 9999 without place-value counters for 10 000? Is it possible? Work through the Master section to recap how the given number can be represented using place-value counters and place-value cards. Explore the value of each digit in a number. Ask students how we would represent zero as a placeholder using a variety of methods: with counters, in words, in a place-value chart and in the\nexpanded form. During Guided Practice, students are showing numbers using place-value\ncounters and place-value charts, writing numbers in numerals and in\nwords, and determining the value of a digit in a number.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-green\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Place-value cards (one set between two)</li><li>Place-value counters (one set between two)</li><li>Place-value chart (one between two)</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-gold\">Maths Language &amp; Variation</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Maths Language</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>It is important students are able to understand and use the following mathematical terms in this lesson:</li><li>ones</li><li>tens</li><li>hundreds</li><li>thousands</li><li>ten thousands</li><li>place value</li></ul></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Variation</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Example 1: Showing 5-digit numbers using place-value charts, and representing the numbers in expanded form; two of the numbers have zeros as placeholders.</li><li>Example 2: Writing 5-digit numbers in expanded form and in words.</li><li>Example 3: Identifying 5-digit numbers shown with place-value counters and writing the numbers in numerals and in words.</li><li>Example 4: Identifying the value of the digit 8 in 5-digit numbers.</li><li>Example 5: Writing numbers shown as words using numerals.</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assess Learning</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-data\"><thead><tr><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Misconceptions</th><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Non-Negotiable</th><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Formative Assessment</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">Students do not know what to do with zeros in relationship to place value.</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can represent numbers to 100 000 using place-value counters and place-value cards.</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can write numbers to 100 000. Students can represent numbers less than 100 000 using place-value counters.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">Students have difficulty saying numbers above 10 000.</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can represent numbers to 100 000 using a place-value chart.</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can write numbers less than 100 000 using words and numerals.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">Students show 56 thousand using 56 thousands rather than 5 ten thousands and 6 thousands.</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can identify the value of a digit in numbers to 100 000. Students can write numbers to 100 000 using words and numerals.</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can represent numbers to 100 000 on a place-value chart. Students can recognise the values of digits in a place-value chart.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can add the values of each place-value column together to make a number.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Check the Curriculum</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">National Curriculum</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Identify, read, write, compare, and order whole numbers up to 100,000, and represent them using base 10 structure.</li></ul></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Content Priority</th><td class=\"lt-td\">This lesson can be combined with Lessons 2 and 3.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Outcomes</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Students learn that the value of a digit within a number is determined by the place it holds. They learn that 10 units in each place can be renamed to make a greater value unit and that 1 unit in each place can be renamed to 10 units of a lesser value. So, 10 tens becomes 1 hundred and 1 ten becomes 10 ones.</li><li>This provides the foundation for understanding decimal fractions.</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Lesson 2: Reading and Writing Numbers to 1 000 000":{"label":"Lesson 2: Reading and Writing Numbers to 1 000 000","content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson Type</th><td class=\"lt-td\">COMBINED</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Textbook Page(s)</th><td class=\"lt-td\">6 - 9</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson Objective</th><td class=\"lt-td\">To be able to read and represent numbers to 1 000 000.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Lesson Approach</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>To begin this lesson, provide students with place-value counters and place-value charts. Show them the Explore task and allow them time to work on it. After a few minutes, ask students if they know a simpler way of showing the number, using only tens and ones. Ask them if this is possible and if it is easier. What might some of the challenges be in representing a large number using only tens and ones? Are you able to show the number using any other materials? Work through Master with the class showing the number using place-value counters, a place-value chart and the expanded form using addition. Allow them time to do this with another 6-digit number and discuss how they would do it. Recap how to represent zero as a placeholder using a variety of methods. During Guided Practice, students are writing and representing numbers in a variety of ways.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-green\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Place-value counters (one set between two)</li><li>Place-value chart (one between two)</li><li>Place-value cards (one set between two)</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-gold\">Maths Language &amp; Variation</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Maths Language</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>It is important students are able to understand and use the</li><li>following mathematical terms in this lesson:</li><li>ones</li><li>tens</li><li>hundreds</li><li>thousands</li><li>ten thousands</li><li>hundred thousands</li><li>place value</li></ul></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Variation</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Example 1: Writing 6-digit numbers in words and deciding on a</li><li>second way to show the numbers.</li><li>Example 2: Writing 6-digit numbers shown in words using expanded form.</li><li>Example 3: Identifying the value of the digit 9 in 6-digit numbers.</li><li>Example 4: Identifying 6-digit numbers shown as place-value counters and writing the numbers in numerals and in words.</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Check the Curriculum</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">National Curriculum</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Identify, read, write, compare, and order whole numbers up to 100,000, and represent them using base 10 structure.</li></ul></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Content Priority</th><td class=\"lt-td\">This lesson can be combined with Lessons 2 and 3.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Outcomes</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Students learn that the value of a digit within a number is determined by the place it holds. They learn that 10 units in each place can be renamed to make a greater value unit and that 1 unit in each place can be renamed to 10 units of a lesser value. So, 10 tens becomes 1 hundred and 1 ten becomes 10 ones.</li><li>This provides the foundation for understanding decimal fractions.</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Lesson 3: Reading and Writing Numbers to 1 000 000":{"label":"Lesson 3: Reading and Writing Numbers to 1 000 000","content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson Type</th><td class=\"lt-td\">COMBINED</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Textbook Page(s)</th><td class=\"lt-td\">10 - 13</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson Objective</th><td class=\"lt-td\">To be able to read and represent numbers to 1 000 000 using place-value counters.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Lesson Approach</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>To begin this lesson, show students the Explore task. Allow them time to complete the main task creating the greatest and smallest numbers using six digit cards. After some time, introduce misconceptions. For example, state that they can make the greatest number by placing the 7 in the hundred-thousands column, and allow them time to discuss this. As students find the greatest and smallest numbers they can make, label the numbers with their names, for example, Lulu's group's greatest number, Charles's group's smallest number and so on. Ask them to explain why they believe their numbers are the greatest or smallest possible. Once they have found the greatest and smallest numbers, let them discuss and discover a generalisation (a 'rule') they can use to determine the next smallest and greatest numbers. Ask them to use this rule to determine the greatest and smallest numbers using the digit cards in Master 2. During Guided Practice, students are writing numbers in numerals and in words.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-green\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Place-value counters (one set between two)</li><li>Place-value chart (one between two)</li><li>0–9 digit cards (one set between two)</li><li>Counters (one set between two)</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-gold\">Maths Language &amp; Variation</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Maths Language</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Students use 0 as a digit in the hundred thousands column when trying to find the smallest 6-digit number.</li></ul></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Variation</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Students can represent numbers to 1 000 000 using a place-value chart. Students can identify the value of a digit in numbers to 1 000 000.</li><li>Students can write numbers to 1 000 000 using words and numerals.</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assess Learning</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-data\"><thead><tr><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Misconceptions</th><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Non-Negotiable</th><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Formative Assessment</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">Students see the counters as representative of a place-value column.</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can represent numbers to 1 000 000 using counters.</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can use place-value counters to represent place value.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">Students use 0 as a digit in the hundred thousands column when trying to find the smallest 6-digit number.</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can represent numbers to 1 000 000 using a place-value chart. Students can identify the value of a digit in numbers to 1 000 000.</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can represent numbers in a place-value chart. Students can use and apply the terms 'greatest' and 'smallest'.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can write numbers to 1 000 000 using words and numerals.</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can write numbers represented by place-value counters in words and numerals.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Check the Curriculum</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">National Curriculum</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Identify, read, write, compare, and order whole numbers up to 1,000,000, and represent them using base 10 structure.</li></ul></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Content Priority</th><td class=\"lt-td\">This lesson can be combined with Lessons 1 and 2.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Outcomes</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Students learn that the value of a digit within a number is determined by the place it holds. They learn that 10 units in each place can be renamed to make a greater value unit and that 1 unit in each place can be renamed to 10 units of a lesser value. So, 10 tens becomes 1 hundred and 1 ten becomes 10 ones.</li><li>This provides the foundation for understanding decimal fractions.</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Lesson 4: Comparing Numbers to 1 000 000":{"label":"Lesson 4: Comparing Numbers to 1 000 000","content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson Type</th><td class=\"lt-td\">KEY LESSON</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Textbook Page(s)</th><td class=\"lt-td\">10 - 13</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson Objective</th><td class=\"lt-td\">To be able to compare numbers to 1 000 000 using place value.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Lesson Approach</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>To begin this lesson, give each student a 9-sided dice and ask them to work in pairs. Show them the Explore task and ask them to play the game against each other. Ask them to find the easiest way to compare numbers. After some time, introduce misconceptions. Ask for two numbers the students have made and write these on the board. Tell them your friend says the easiest way to compare numbers is by looking at the ones; the number with the greater amount of ones is the greater number. When they disagree ask them to explain why this is not correct. How does the position of a digit in a number affect its value? Does the greatest digit have the greatest value? Is this ever the case? Then ask them to work in pairs to come up with a rule for comparing numbers. Provide students with numbers that have the same digits in different place-value columns. Allow them time to work out what to do when this is the case and then let them share their methods with the rest of the class. During Guided Practice, students are comparing numbers using the terms 'greater than' and 'less than', arranging numbers in order and deciding whether it is possible to compare numbers with missing digits. smallest and greatest numbers. Ask them to use this rule to determine the greatest and smallest numbers using the digit cards in Master 2. During Guided Practice, students are writing numbers in numerals and in words.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-green\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Place-value chart (one each)</li><li>9-sided dice (one each)</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-gold\">Maths Language &amp; Variation</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Maths Language</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>hundred thousands</li><li>place value</li><li>greater, greatest</li><li>smaller, smallest</li><li>greater than</li><li>less than</li></ul></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Variation</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>same.</li><li>Example 3: Arranging four 6-digit numbers in order from greatest to smallest.</li><li>Example 4: Comparing two 6-digit numbers and deciding which is the greater/smaller number. (a) The first digit of each number is the same. (b) The first two digits of each number are the same.</li><li>Example 5: Comparing two 6-digit numbers and deciding whether it is possible to identify the greater number. (a) One number has two digits missing and the other number has one digit missing. (b)–(c) Both numbers have one digit missing.</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assess Learning</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-data\"><thead><tr><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Misconceptions</th><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Non-Negotiable</th><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Formative Assessment</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">Students place the higher-value digits in</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can compare numbers using a</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can compare numbers to 1 000</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">the place-value columns with the least</td><td class=\"lt-td\">place-value chart or digit cards.</td><td class=\"lt-td\">000.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">value.</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can identify 'greater than' and</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can read numbers to 1 000 000.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">Students look at the wrong position to</td><td class=\"lt-td\">'less than' when comparing two numbers</td><td class=\"lt-td\"></td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">determine the value of a number.</td><td class=\"lt-td\">that are partially formed.</td><td class=\"lt-td\"></td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Check the Curriculum</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">National Curriculum</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Identify, read, write, compare, and order whole numbers up to 1,000,000, and represent them using base 10 structure.</li></ul></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Content Priority</th><td class=\"lt-td\">This is a key lesson.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Outcomes</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Students learn that the value of a digit within a number is determined by the place it holds. They learn that 10 units in each place can be renamed to make a greater value unit and that 1 unit in each place can be renamed to 10 units of a lesser value. So, 10 tens becomes 1 hundred and 1 ten becomes 10 ones.</li><li>This provides the foundation for understanding decimal fractions.</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Lesson 5: Comparing Numbers to 1 000 000":{"label":"Lesson 5: Comparing Numbers to 1 000 000","content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson Type</th><td class=\"lt-td\">IF TIME ALLOWS</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Textbook Page(s)</th><td class=\"lt-td\">19 - 22</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson Objective</th><td class=\"lt-td\">To be able to compare numbers to 1 000 000 using place value.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Lesson Approach</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>To begin this lesson, show students the Explore task and give them time to work in pairs. Ask them what is different about the three numbers. Tell them your friend said they knew which one was the greatest number straight away. How did they know this? How can we describe these numbers? Students should notice that there are two 5-digit numbers and one 6-digit number. Do they know that a 6-digit number is always greater than a 5-digit number? Ask them how they can compare numbers if they have the same number of digits. Work through the Master section to reinforce students' understanding of\n5-digit and 6-digit numbers and the value of each digit within those\nnumbers. During Guided Practice, students are arranging numbers in order from\nsmallest to greatest and vice versa. The terms 'ascending' and\n'descending' are introduced. Students are also comparing 5-digit and\n6-digit numbers with at least one missing digit in each number.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-green\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>0–9 digit cards (one set each)</li><li>9-sided dice (one each)</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assess Learning</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-data\"><thead><tr><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Misconceptions</th><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Non-Negotiable</th><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Formative Assessment</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">Students use the left-most digits to compare numbers when one number has 6 digits and the other only has 5 digits.</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can compare numbers using a place-value chart. Students can correctly use the &gt; and &lt;</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can compare numbers to 1 000 000. Students can read numbers to 1 000 000.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">Students look at the wrong position to determine the value of a number. Students incorrectly use the &gt; and &lt; signs to mean their opposites.</td><td class=\"lt-td\">signs to compare numbers. Students can identify 'greater than' and 'less than' when numbers are partially formed. Students can read numbers to 1 000 000. Students can construct 6-digit numbers and read them</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can determine the values of digits in a 6-digit number. Students can use the terms ‘greater than’ and ‘less than’ to compare numbers. Students can use the symbols &gt; and &lt; to compare numbers.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Check the Curriculum</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">National Curriculum</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Identify, read, write, compare, and order whole numbers up to 1,000,000, and represent them using base 10 structure.</li></ul></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Content Priority</th><td class=\"lt-td\">This lesson can be completed if time allows.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Outcomes</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Students learn that the value of a digit within a number is determined by the place it holds. They learn that 10 units in each place can be renamed to make a greater value unit and that 1 unit in each place can be renamed to 10 units of a lesser value. So, 10 tens becomes 1 hundred and 1 ten becomes 10 ones.</li><li>This provides the foundation for understanding decimal fractions.</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Lesson 6: Comparing Numbers to 1 000 000":{"label":"Lesson 6: Comparing Numbers to 1 000 000","content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson Type</th><td class=\"lt-td\">KEY LESSON</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Textbook Page(s)</th><td class=\"lt-td\">23 - 26</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson Objective</th><td class=\"lt-td\">To be able to compare numbers to 1 000 000 using pictorial representations and proportionality.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Lesson Approach</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>To begin this lesson, show students the Explore task and allow them time to compare the numbers using addition and subtraction with their partner. Tell them they can represent the problems using a bar model. Is this possible with such large numbers? Give the class some time to draw comparison models to represent the numbers and the difference between the number of visitors as you work through Master 1 and 2 with them. Ask students to represent 215 000 using a bar, then ask them how they can represent 100 000. If necessary, prompt them to recognise that 100 000 is just under half of the bar. Allow students time to work out how to show the proportions using fractions and breaking the bars into parts. Then ask them to compare the number of visitors the wildlife park and the water park had by drawing another bar model. Ask students if it is possible to represent large numbers on a number line. Ask them to show you what that might look like by using the numbers from Master 1 and 2, then work through Master 3 and 4 together. Students often find number lines confusing as they don't know what scale to use, what the end points are, where to place the marks and what the value is of the space in between the marks. It is worth spending time making sure this is fully understood as number lines can be an incredibly powerful tool once mastered. During Guided Practice, students are comparing two numbers and working out how much more or less one number is compared to the other. Students are also using &gt; and &lt; to compare two numbers.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-green\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Place-value counters (one set between two)</li><li>Blank number lines (increments marked)</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-gold\">Maths Language &amp; Variation</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Maths Language</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>It is important students are able to understand and use the</li><li>following mathematical terms and phrases in this lesson:</li><li>ten thousands</li><li>hundred thousands</li><li>place value</li></ul></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Variation</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Examples 1–2: Comparing two 6-digit numbers; determining</li><li>how many ten thousands less one number is than the other. Number line provided with increments.</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assess Learning</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-data\"><thead><tr><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Misconceptions</th><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Non-Negotiable</th><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Formative Assessment</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">Students believe they have to look at the entire number when its value increases or decreases by a multiple of 10 000 or 100 000.</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can identify which digits will be affected by a change of multiples of 10 000 and 100 000. Students can use place-value counters to change numbers by multiples of 10 000 and 100 000.</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can compare numbers to 1 000 000. Students can read numbers to 1 000 000. Students can determine the values of digits in a 6-digit number.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can use number lines to change numbers by multiples of 10 000 and 100 000. Students can correctly identify whether a number will be 'greater than' or 'less than' another number.</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can use the terms ‘greater than’ and ‘less than’ to compare numbers. Students can use the symbols &gt; and &lt; to compare numbers. Students can order numbers in ascending and descending order. Students can recognise which part of a number will change if it is increased or</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Check the Curriculum</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">National Curriculum</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Identify, read, write, compare, and order whole numbers up to 1,000,000, and represent them using base 10 structure.</li></ul></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Content Priority</th><td class=\"lt-td\">This is a key lesson.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Outcomes</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Students learn that the value of a digit within a number is determined by the place it holds. They learn that 10 units in each place can be renamed to make a greater value unit and that 1 unit in each place can be renamed to 10 units of a lesser value. So, 10 tens becomes 1 hundred and 1 ten becomes 10 ones.</li><li>This provides the foundation for understanding decimal fractions.</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Lesson 7: Comparing Numbers to 1 000 000":{"label":"Lesson 7: Comparing Numbers to 1 000 000","content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson Type</th><td class=\"lt-td\">KEY LESSON</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Textbook Page(s)</th><td class=\"lt-td\">27 - 30</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson Objective</th><td class=\"lt-td\">To be able to compare numbers to 1 000 000 using number bonds and number lines.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Lesson Approach</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>To begin this lesson, show students the Explore task and ask them how the numbers can be compared. Is it easier to identify the smallest than the greatest number? Why? Where should we look to determine the greatest number? Allow students time to discuss the number of digits in each number and how this helps us to compare them. They should also discuss how they can use the place value of each digit to work systematically when comparing numbers. Allow students some time to come up with their own methods and then work through Master to demonstrate the methods used in the examples. What is the relationship between addition and subtraction when the terms 'more than' and 'less than' are used? Tell them your friend says that when they see 'more than' they always add and when they see 'less than' they always subtract. Does this work? Why or why not? (see wording in Guided Practice) During Guided Practice, students are comparing numbers in multiples of 10 000 and 100 000. They are also increasing or decreasing given numbers by multiples of 10 000 and 100 000.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-green\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>0–9 digit cards (one set between two)</li><li>Place-value counters (one set between two)</li><li>Blank number lines (increments marked)</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-gold\">Maths Language &amp; Variation</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Maths Language</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Students believe they have to look at the entire number when its value increases or decreases by a multiple of 10 000 or 100 000.</li></ul></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Variation</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Students can identify which digits will be affected by a change of multiples of 10 000 and 100 000. Students can use place-value counters to change numbers by multiples of 10 000 and 100 000.</li><li>Students can use number lines to change numbers by multiples of 10 000 and 100 000. Students can correctly identify whether a number will be 'greater than' or 'less than' another number.</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assess Learning</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-data\"><thead><tr><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Misconceptions</th><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Non-Negotiable</th><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Formative Assessment</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">Students believe they have to look at the entire number when its value increases or decreases by a multiple of 10 000 or 100 000.</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can identify which digits will be affected by a change of multiples of 10 000 and 100 000. Students can use place-value counters to change numbers by multiples of 10 000 and 100 000.</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can compare numbers to 1 000 000. Students can read numbers to 1 000 000. Students can determine the values of digits in a 6-digit number.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can use number lines to change numbers by multiples of 10 000 and 100 000. Students can correctly identify whether a number will be 'greater than' or 'less than' another number.</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can use the terms ‘greater than’ and ‘less than’ to compare numbers. Students can use the symbols &gt; and &lt; to compare numbers. Students can recognise which part of a number will change if it is increased or decreased by a multiple of 10 000 or 100 000.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Check the Curriculum</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">National Curriculum</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Identify, read, write, compare, and order whole numbers up to 1,000,000, and represent them using base 10 structure.</li></ul></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Content Priority</th><td class=\"lt-td\">This is a key lesson.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Outcomes</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Students learn that the value of a digit within a number is determined by the place it holds. They learn that 10 units in each place can be renamed to make a greater value unit and that 1 unit in each place can be renamed to 10 units of a lesser value. So, 10 tens becomes 1 hundred and 1 ten becomes 10 ones.</li><li>This provides the foundation for understanding decimal fractions.</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Lesson 8: Making Number Patterns":{"label":"Lesson 8: Making Number Patterns","content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson Type</th><td class=\"lt-td\">KEY LESSON</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Textbook Page(s)</th><td class=\"lt-td\">31 - 33</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson Objective</th><td class=\"lt-td\">To be able to make and identify increasing number patterns using knowledge of place value.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Lesson Approach</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>To begin this lesson, provide students with digit cards and ask them to make the first number in the pattern with their partner. Ask them what they will have to change to make the next number and then the number after that. Is the change the same? How can the change be described? Explain to the students that we call this change 'the rule'. Determine the rule of the number pattern with the students. Show students another number pattern where the digit in the thousands, ten thousands, or hundred thousands column changes. Have them come up with rules for the pattern which are easy to understand as shown in the Master section. Ask them if they are able to create patterns if the digit in the ones column changes. Would these numbers still work? How else\nwould they need to change? Ask them if they can create their own number\npattern using 5-digit or 6-digit numbers. If there is a pattern that is unique\nto the class, tell them you will name the pattern after that student and keep\nit posted in the classroom. During Guided Practice, students are completing increasing number\npatterns.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-green\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>0–9 digit cards (one set between two)</li><li>Place-value cards (one set between two)</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-gold\">Maths Language &amp; Variation</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Maths Language</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>number patterns</li><li>increases</li></ul></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Variation</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Example 2: Finding the rule and the missing numbers in each number pattern. (a) The numbers are increasing by 100 000 each time. (b) The numbers are increasing by 4000 each time. (c) The numbers are increasing by 70 000 each time.</li><li>Example 3: Finding the rule and the 8th number in each number pattern. (a) The numbers are increasing by 5000 each time. (b) The numbers are increasing by 100 000 each time. (c) The numbers are increasing by 60 000 each time.</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assess Learning</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-data\"><thead><tr><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Misconceptions</th><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Non-Negotiable</th><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Formative Assessment</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">Students only see ‘635’ (the last three digits of the number, which in this case, stay constant) as the number pattern and cannot detect the change in the other numbers.</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can recognise and complete number patterns by identifying what is changing, and what is not, in a sequence.</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can compare numbers to 1 000 000. Students can read numbers to 1 000 000. Students can determine the values of digits in a 6-digit number. Students can find patterns in numbers greater than 10 000. Students can arrange numbers from smallest to greatest and vice versa. Students can complete number patterns.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Check the Curriculum</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">National Curriculum</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Recognise and describe the rule for a growing pattern using words, tables, and diagrams, and make conjectures about further elements in the pattern.</li></ul></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Content Priority</th><td class=\"lt-td\">This is a key lesson.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Outcomes</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Students should notice a change in a particular place, reinforcing the base 10 nature of the number system. The pattern of change remains consistent in whole numbers and decimal numbers.</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Lesson 9: Making Number Patterns":{"label":"Lesson 9: Making Number Patterns","content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson Type</th><td class=\"lt-td\">KEY LESSON</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Textbook Page(s)</th><td class=\"lt-td\">34 - 37</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson Objective</th><td class=\"lt-td\">To be able to make number patterns that decrease in multiples of 10 000 or 100 000.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Lesson Approach</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>To begin this lesson, show students the Explore task. Give them some time to find the 'rule' and then ask them to explain how they know it is the 'rule'. Ask them if it is possible for one of the numbers in the pattern to be 784 312. Why or why not? Allow them time to prove that this idea is correct or incorrect with support. Once they realise it is not possible, ask them if it is true that the pattern's smallest possible number is 24 312. How can we know this without counting backwards all the way? Ask the students what the starting number would need to be to have a\nsmaller smallest possible number. What would the starting number need to\nbe to have a larger smallest possible number? Allow students time to\nexplore these concepts and then work through Master with the class to\nreinforce learning. During Guided Practice, students are completing decreasing number\npatterns.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-green\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>0–9 digit cards (four sets between two)</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-gold\">Maths Language &amp; Variation</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Maths Language</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>It is important students are able to understand and use the</li><li>following mathematical terms in this lesson:</li><li>number pattern</li><li>decreases</li></ul></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Variation</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>number patterns with 6-digit numbers. Each pair will need four</li><li>sets of 0–9 digit cards for this activity.</li><li>Variation Example 1: Finding the rule and the missing numbers in each number pattern. (a) The numbers are decreasing by 1000 each time. (b) The numbers are decreasing by 40 000 each time.</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assess Learning</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-data\"><thead><tr><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Misconceptions</th><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Non-Negotiable</th><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Formative Assessment</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">Misconceptions Students get stuck when crossing the boundaries forwards and backwards.</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Non-Negotiable Students can recognise and complete number patterns by identifying what is changing, and what is not, in a sequence.</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Formative Assessment Students can count backwards by 1, 10, 100, 1000 and 10 000. Students can recognise number patterns that increase and decrease by 1, 10, 100, 1000 and 10 000. Students can recognise which numbers in a pattern are changing. Pupils can read numbers to 1 000 000. Students can determine the values of digits in a 6-digit number.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Check the Curriculum</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">National Curriculum</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Recognise and describe the rule for a growing pattern using words, tables, and diagrams, and make conjectures about further elements in the pattern.</li></ul></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Content Priority</th><td class=\"lt-td\">This is a key lesson.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Outcomes</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Students should notice a change in a particular place, reinforcing the base 10 nature of the number system. The pattern of change remains consistent in whole numbers and decimal numbers.</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Lesson 10: Rounding Numbers to the Nearest 10 000":{"label":"Lesson 10: Rounding Numbers to the Nearest 10 000","content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson Type</th><td class=\"lt-td\">IF TIME ALLOWS</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Textbook Page(s)</th><td class=\"lt-td\">38 - 40</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson Objective</th><td class=\"lt-td\">To be able to round numbers to the nearest 10 think Holly worked out that there were 10 000 more marathon runners in 000 using number lines. New York than in Boston.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Lesson Approach</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>To begin this lesson, show students the Explore task. Ask them how they Give them time to work on the problem with their partners. Ask students if they know of any special ways to round numbers. Suggest\nusing a number line. Is this possible? How would a number line need to be\nconstructed if we were rounding numbers to the nearest 10 000? What\nabout if we were rounding to the nearest 1000? Would the number line be\ndifferent? If so, why? Work through the Master section to show the class how number lines can\nbe used to round numbers.\nDuring Guided Practice, students are using number lines to compare\nnumbers and round them to the nearest 10 000.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-green\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Blank number lines (increments marked)</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-gold\">Maths Language &amp; Variation</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Variation</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Examples 1–3: Rounding numbers to the nearest 10 000.</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assess Learning</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-data\"><thead><tr><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Misconceptions</th><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Non-Negotiable</th><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Formative Assessment</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">Students only change the rounded digit and not all of the subsequent numbers. Students are unsure of which digit to consider for rounding to 10, 100, 1000 and 10 000.</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can use a number line to round numbers by looking at which number is closest.</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can round numbers to the nearest 10, 100, 1000 and 10 000. Students can determine when rounding to the nearest 10, 100, 1000 or 10 000, which would be most appropriate. Students can use a number line to round numbers.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Check the Curriculum</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">National Curriculum</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Round whole numbers to the nearest ten thousand, thousand, hundred, or ten, and round tenths to the nearest whole number.</li></ul></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Content Priority</th><td class=\"lt-td\">This lesson can be completed if time allows.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Outcomes</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Students need to understand what place value a number is being rounded to. Once they know this, they learn to look at the next smallest place value to determine whether the number rounds up, rounds down or stays the same.</li><li>This rule applies to both whole numbers in this chapter and decimal numbers later on.</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Lesson 11: Rounding Numbers to the Nearest 100 000":{"label":"Lesson 11: Rounding Numbers to the Nearest 100 000","content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson Type</th><td class=\"lt-td\">KEY LESSON</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Textbook Page(s)</th><td class=\"lt-td\">41 - 44</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson Objective</th><td class=\"lt-td\">To be able to round numbers to the nearest 100 000 using number lines.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Lesson Approach</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>To begin this lesson, show students the table from the Explore task. Give them some time to work on and prove whether they agree with Sam's statement. Can they think of a scenario in which this statement would no longer be true? What if we were rounding to the nearest 1000? Ask them if they are able to prove their thinking and answers by using a number line. What might that look like? Work through the Master section with the class. During Guided Practice, students are rounding numbers to the nearest 100 000. In the last question, students need to determine the smallest and greatest possible numbers that could be rounded to 200 000 when rounded to the nearest 100 000.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-green\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Blank number lines (increments marked)</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-gold\">Maths Language &amp; Variation</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Maths Language</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>It is important students are able to understand and use the</li><li>following mathematical terms and phrases in this lesson:</li><li>rounded to the nearest 100 000</li><li>approximate number</li><li>approximately equal to</li><li>smallest/greatest possible number</li></ul></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Variation</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Example 1: Rounding numbers to the nearest 100 000; number lines provided as support.</li><li>Example 2a: Rounding numbers to the nearest 100 000.</li><li>Example 2b: Determining the smallest and greatest possible numbers that could be rounded to 200 000 when rounded to the nearest 100 000.</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assess Learning</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-data\"><thead><tr><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Misconceptions</th><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Non-Negotiable</th><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Formative Assessment</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">Students try to round to the nearest 1000 or 10 000.</td><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\"></td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Check the Curriculum</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">National Curriculum</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Round whole numbers to a specified power of 10, and round tenths and hundredths to the nearest whole number or one decimal place.</li></ul></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Content Priority</th><td class=\"lt-td\">This is a key lesson.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Outcomes</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Students need to understand what place value a number is being rounded to. Once they know this, they learn to look at the next smallest place value to determine whether the number rounds up, rounds down or stays the same.</li><li>This rule applies to both whole numbers in this chapter and decimal numbers later on.</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Lesson 12: Rounding Numbers":{"label":"Lesson 12: Rounding Numbers","content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson Type</th><td class=\"lt-td\">IF TIME ALLOWS</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Textbook Page(s)</th><td class=\"lt-td\">45 - 49</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson Objective</th><td class=\"lt-td\">To be able to round numbers to the nearest 100, 1000, 10 000 and 100 000 using number lines.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Lesson Approach</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>To begin this lesson, show students the Explore task and ask how we can get approximate amounts. In this situation would it be best to round to the nearest 100 000, 10 000, 1000, 100 or 10? How should we decide what we should round to? Discuss a situation in which rounding to these numbers would be reasonable. During Guided Practice, students are rounding numbers to the nearest 100, 1000, 10 000 and 100 000. The Guided Practice could be extended into investigating under what conditions the rounded values are the same irrespective of the precision of the rounding.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-green\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Blank number lines (increments marked)</li><li>Place-value counters (one set between two)</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-gold\">Maths Language &amp; Variation</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Maths Language</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>It is important students are able to understand and use the following mathematical terms and phrases in this lesson:</li><li>rounded to the nearest 100, 1000, 10 000, and 100 000</li><li>approximate</li><li>approximately equal to</li></ul></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Variation</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Example 1: Rounding to the nearest 100, 1000 and 10 000. Examples 2–3: Rounding to the nearest 1000, 10 000 and 100 000.</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assess Learning</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-data\"><thead><tr><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Misconceptions</th><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Non-Negotiable</th><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Formative Assessment</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">Students try to round to the wrong place.</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can use a number line to round numbers by looking at which number is closest.</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can round numbers to the nearest 10, 100, 1000, 10 000 and 100 000.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can determine, when rounding to the nearest 10, 100, 1000, 10 000 or</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Check the Curriculum</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">National Curriculum</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Round whole numbers to a specified power of 10, and round tenths and hundredths to the nearest whole number or one decimal place.</li><li>Use rounding and estimation to predict results and to check the reasonableness of calculations.</li><li>Use rounding, estimation, and benchmarks to predict results and to check the reasonableness of calculations.</li></ul></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Content Priority</th><td class=\"lt-td\">This lesson can be completed if time allows.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Outcomes</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Students need to understand what place value a number is being rounded to. Once they know this, they learn to look at the next smallest place value to determine whether the number rounds up, rounds down or stays the same.</li><li>This rule applies to both whole numbers in this chapter and decimal numbers later on.</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Lesson 13: Chapter Consolidation":{"label":"Lesson 13: Chapter Consolidation","content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Textbook Page(s)</th><td class=\"lt-td\">50</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson Objective</th><td class=\"lt-td\">To be able to apply knowledge of numbers to 1 000 000 to solve problems.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Lesson Approach</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students need to work out what two numbers Jacob could be thinking of, based on the information given, and then what numbers Jacob could pick to give the greatest difference and the smallest difference between the two numbers.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension &amp; Reflection</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Mind Challenge</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Students need to work out what two numbers Jacob could be thinking of, based on the information given, and then what numbers Jacob could pick to give the greatest difference and the smallest difference between the two numbers.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-amber\"><th class=\"lt-th\">My Maths Journal</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Students consider a statement about rounding numbers, decide whether or not it is correct and explain their answer.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Review</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Complete this with pupils as a chapter summary and determine if any of them need extra support.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"}}},"Chapter 2: Addition & Subtraction":{"label":"Lesson","children":{"Chapter Overview":{"label":"Chapter Overview","content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Chapter Overview</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This chapter covers addition and subtraction. The chapter starts off with simple addition before moving on to addition where renaming is required. Subtraction is also covered in a similar way where simple subtraction is mastered before moving to subtraction where renaming is required. Once students master addition and subtraction, they start to look at problem-solving questions and practice using bar models. \n\nThis chapter uses three different ways to show addition and subtraction visually. This will help students develop flexibility, however, some students may need additional support and time in order to be able to use all of the methods fluently.</p></div></div>"},"Lesson 1: Counting On to Add":{"label":"Lesson 1: Counting On to Add","content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson Type</th><td class=\"lt-td\">KEY LESSON</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Textbook Page(s)</th><td class=\"lt-td\">52 - 55</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson Objective</th><td class=\"lt-td\">To be able to add using the 'counting on' strategy to solve the problem with their partners. Tell them they can count on in with concrete materials and number lines.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Lesson Approach</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>To begin this lesson, show students the Explore task and allow them time thousands to get to the final answer. Is this correct? Is this all we need to do? Ask students if there is a faster way of finding the answer by partitioning 24 000. How would they partition the number to make it easier to count on? Provide students with a number line and place-value counters. Ask them to make 32 541 with the place-value counters. How many thousands do we need to count on? How many ten thousands do we need? Work through Master to demonstrate how the place-value counters and number lines can be used to solve this problem. During Guided Practice, students are counting on in tens, hundreds, thousands, ten thousands and hundred thousands and adding mentally.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-green\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Place-value counters (one set between two)</li><li>Blank number lines (increments marked)</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-gold\">Maths Language &amp; Variation</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Maths Language</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>It is important students are able to understand and use the following mathematical terms in this lesson:</li><li>##### Variation</li><li>Example 1: Counting on from the same number to get 6 more numbers in multiples of 10, 100, 1000, 10 000 and 100 000.</li><li>- ones</li><li>- tens</li><li>- hundreds</li><li>- thousands</li><li>- ten thousands</li><li>- hundred thousands</li><li>Example 2a–b: Adding mentally from the same number in multiples of 100 and 1000.</li><li>Example 2c–d: Adding mentally from the same number in multiples of 10 000 and 100 000.</li><li>- place value</li><li>- counting on</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Check the Curriculum</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">National Curriculum</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Add and subtract any whole numbers.</li></ul></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Content Priority</th><td class=\"lt-td\">This is a key lesson.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Outcomes</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>This is a key lesson which supports a number of learning outcomes, including:</li><li>Students understand that the value of a digit within a number is determined by the place it holds.</li><li>Students add whole numbers up to 1 000 000 by counting on in thousands and ten thousands.</li><li>Students can count on in multiples of 100, 1000, 10 000 and 100 000 depending on what's appropriate.</li><li>##### ● Students can add whole numbers with more than 4 digits mentally.</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Lesson 2: Adding within 1 000 000":{"label":"Lesson 2: Adding within 1 000 000","content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson Type</th><td class=\"lt-td\">KEY LESSON</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Textbook Page(s)</th><td class=\"lt-td\">56 - 58</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson Objective</th><td class=\"lt-td\">To be able to add numbers within 1 000 000 using approximate number of programmes required by rounding the numbers in rounding and concrete materials. the table.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Lesson Approach</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>To begin this lesson, show students the Explore task and allow them some time to work on the solution. Ask students if they could find the How would they round the numbers in the table to find an approximate total? As there are no ones, tens or hundreds in the numbers would it be best to round to the nearest ten thousand or hundred\nthousand? Is it possible to round to both? Discuss why it would not be a\ngood idea to round to the nearest hundred thousand. Would it make sense\nto round the number of programmes for sailing, track cycling and table\ntennis to zero? Allow students to use place-value counters to help them\nfind the answer. Tell the class they could use a bar graph to help them round the numbers.\nWhat would that look like? Could we find the answer roughly if we did that?\nAsk them if it is possible to find the answer if we create all the numbers\nusing place-value counters and then just add them all together. Would that\nbe by adding the numbers roughly, or by adding exactly? Work through\nMaster to discuss the examples.\nDuring Guided Practice, students are adding numbers within 1 000 000.\nAsk students if it is possible to use a number line or place-value counters\nto add numbers of this size and if it is possible to add them mentally.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-green\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Place-value counters (one set between two)</li><li>Blank number lines (increments marked)</li><li>Maths Language Variation It is important students are able to understand and use the Example 1: Adding using the column method. following mathematical terms in this lesson:</li><li>ones ● tens Example 2: Adding by adding the thousands mentally. ● hundreds ● thousands ● ten thousands</li><li>hundred thousands</li><li>place value</li><li>approximate</li><li>rounding to the nearest 10 000</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assess Learning</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-data\"><thead><tr><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Misconceptions</th><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Non-Negotiable</th><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Formative Assessment</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">Students place digits in the wrong place-value columns when adding.</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can add numbers greater than 10 000 accurately using place-value counters.</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can use place-value counters to add numbers. Students can round numbers to the nearest 1000 and 10 000. Students can add using the column method.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can add rounded numbers mentally.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Check the Curriculum</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">National Curriculum</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Add and subtract any whole numbers.</li></ul></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Content Priority</th><td class=\"lt-td\">This is a key lesson.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Outcomes</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>This is a key lesson which supports a number of learning outcomes, including:</li><li>Students understand that the value of a digit within a number is determined by the place it holds.</li><li>Students add whole numbers with more than 4 digits mentally.</li><li>Students add whole numbers up to 1 000 000, including using column addition.</li><li>Students use rounding to check answers to addition and to determine levels of accuracy in the context of a problem.</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Lesson 3: Lesson 3":{"label":"Lesson 3: Lesson 3","content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson Type</th><td class=\"lt-td\">KEY LESSON</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Textbook Page(s)</th><td class=\"lt-td\">59 - 62</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson Objective</th><td class=\"lt-td\">To be able to add numbers within 1 000 000 using the column method.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Lesson Approach</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>To begin this lesson, show students the Explore task. Before they begin solving the required task, ask them why the question uses the term 'approximately'. Tell them it is used because it means 'half'. Is this true? Does this make sense? Allow them time to discuss. Recap on what the term 'approximately' means and ask students if they can determine what range the numbers could be when rounded to 15 000 and 17 000. Give them some time to work this out and then provide them with place-value counters and ask them if they are able to solve the Explore task. Tell them they could add 20 000 and 20 000, as they want to get an approximate total. Is this a good idea? Why might this be a bad idea? Allow them some time to work out the approximate total. Next, provide students with the actual amounts from Master 2. Do they fall within the range of numbers we initially thought of? Ask them to add those numbers together to find the actual total. Tell them your friend said that to add numbers using the column method, we need to add one column at a time. Is this true? Does it matter where we start? Is there more than one way to show the totals? Work through Master to recap on the method used. During Guided Practice, students are adding with renaming.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-green\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Place-value counters (one set between two)</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-gold\">Maths Language &amp; Variation</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Maths Language</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>It is important students are able to understand and use the following mathematical terms in this lesson:</li><li>ones</li><li>tens</li></ul></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Variation</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Adding with renaming. Examples 1–2: Column method.</li><li>Example 3: No method suggested.</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assess Learning</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-data\"><thead><tr><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Misconceptions</th><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Non-Negotiable</th><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Formative Assessment</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">Students place digits in the wrong place-value columns when adding. Students are uncertain of how to cross boundaries when adding.</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can show how to rename when adding using place-value counters and the column method.</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can use place-value counters to add numbers. Students can estimate and round numbers to the nearest 1000 and 10 000. Students can add using the column method.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can add rounded numbers mentally.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can rename numbers when adding using place-value counters and the column method.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Check the Curriculum</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">National Curriculum</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Add and subtract any whole numbers.</li></ul></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Content Priority</th><td class=\"lt-td\">This is a key lesson.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Outcomes</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>This is a key lesson which supports a number of learning outcomes, including:</li><li>Students understand that the value of a digit within a number is determined by the place it holds.</li><li>Students add whole numbers with more than 4 digits mentally (up to 1 000 000) and by using formal written methods.</li><li>Students use rounding to estimate and check answers to addition and to determine levels of accuracy in the context of a problem.</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Lesson 4: Adding within 1 000 000":{"label":"Lesson 4: Adding within 1 000 000","content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson Type</th><td class=\"lt-td\">IF TIME ALLOWS</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Textbook Page(s)</th><td class=\"lt-td\">63 - 66</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson Objective</th><td class=\"lt-td\">To be able to consolidate and refine addition skills and place-value knowledge to solve addition problems.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Lesson Approach</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>To begin this lesson, provide students with digit cards 0–9. Show them the Explore task and allow them to work in pairs or small groups. What 5-digit numbers could Tama have made? Give students time to play with the position of the numbers. Tell the students that your friend said that they knew that if the first digits of the two numbers were 3 and 4, then the second digits could only be certain numbers. They also said that if the first digits of the two numbers were 2 and 3 then the second digits could only be certain numbers. What did your friend mean by this? Ask students a variety of questions as they use the cards to find various solutions. Tasks can include: finding the greatest sum that Tama could have made; finding the smallest sum that Tama could have made; finding the greatest and smallest numbers Tama could have made. During Guided Practice, students are estimating the total of two 5-digit numbers, then using the column method to find the sum.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-green\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Place-value counters (one set between two)</li><li>0–9 digit cards</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-gold\">Maths Language &amp; Variation</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Maths Language</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>place value</li><li>rename/renaming</li><li>estimation/estimate</li><li>round to the nearest 10 000</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assess Learning</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-data\"><thead><tr><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Misconceptions</th><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Non-Negotiable</th><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Formative Assessment</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">Students place digits in the wrong place-value columns when adding. Students are uncertain of how to cross boundaries when adding.</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can rename numbers when adding using place-value counters and the column method.</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can use place-value counters to add numbers. Students can estimate and round numbers to the nearest 1000 and 10 000. Students can add using the column method. Students can add rounded numbers mentally.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can rename numbers when adding using place-value counters and the column method. Students can generate questions for addition.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Check the Curriculum</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">National Curriculum</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Add and subtract any whole numbers.</li></ul></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Content Priority</th><td class=\"lt-td\">This lesson can be completed if time allows.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Outcomes</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>This lesson supports a number of learning outcomes, including:</li><li>Students understand that the value of a digit within a number is determined by the place it holds.</li><li>Students add and subtract whole numbers up to 1 000 000, including using columnar addition.</li><li>Students use rounding to check answers to calculations and to determine levels of accuracy in the context of a problem.</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Lesson 5: Counting Backwards to Subtract":{"label":"Lesson 5: Counting Backwards to Subtract","content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson Type</th><td class=\"lt-td\">KEY LESSON</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Textbook Page(s)</th><td class=\"lt-td\">67 - 70</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson Objective</th><td class=\"lt-td\">To be able to subtract using the 'counting number line. Are we going to count backwards or forwards to find the backwards' strategy with concrete materials.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Lesson Approach</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>To begin this lesson, show students the Explore task and allow them time to work on this. Ask the students how we could show this problem using a amount Matua Henare has in his account now? Once the initial amount is\nshown on the number line and it has been established that we will be\ncounting backwards, ask what we should count back in. Would it make\nsense to count back in ones? Is it possible to use multiple number lines\ncounting back in different amounts? Why do they think Charles has\npartitioned 730 000 in the way that he has? How could that help us to\ncount backwards? Ask the children to discuss how counting backwards in\nhundred thousands, ten thousands and thousands can be shown on a\nnumber line.\nOnce students understand how to represent the problem using a number\nline, give them place-value counters and ask them to represent the\nproblem using the counters. Ask them to count back as they remove the\ncounters. Students should be encouraged to see that they can use\nplace-value to subtract by counting backwards in hundred thousands, ten\nthousands and thousands as they have done when using the number\nlines.\nIn Master 3 students are shown how they can exchange 10 000 for ten\n1000s to count back using place-value counters.\nDuring Guided Practice, students are counting backwards in tens,\nhundreds, thousands, ten thousands and hundred thousands.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-green\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Place-value counters (one set between two)</li><li>Blank number lines (increments marked)</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-gold\">Maths Language &amp; Variation</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Maths Language</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>It is important students are able to understand and use the following mathematical terms in this lesson:</li><li>- ones</li><li>##### Variation</li><li>Example 1: Counting back from the same number to get 5 more numbers in multiples of 10, 100, 1000, 10 000 and 100 000.</li><li>- tens</li><li>- hundreds</li><li>- thousands</li><li>Example 2: Subtracting mentally from the same number in multiples of 100, 1000, 10 000 and 100 000.</li><li>- ten thousands</li><li>- hundred thousands</li><li>- place value</li><li>- count back</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Check the Curriculum</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">National Curriculum</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Add and subtract any whole numbers.</li></ul></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Content Priority</th><td class=\"lt-td\">This is a key lesson.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Outcomes</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>This is a key lesson which supports a number of learning outcomes, including:</li><li>Students understand that the value of a digit within a number is determined by the place it holds.</li><li>Students subtract numbers mentally with increasingly large numbers.</li><li>Students subtract whole numbers up to 1 000 000 by counting backwards in thousands and ten thousands.</li><li>Students use rounding to check answers to calculations.</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Lesson 6: Subtracting within 1 000 000":{"label":"Lesson 6: Subtracting within 1 000 000","content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson Type</th><td class=\"lt-td\">KEY LESSON</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Textbook Page(s)</th><td class=\"lt-td\">71 - 74</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson Objective</th><td class=\"lt-td\">To be able to subtract using the column method we compare numbers? Are they attached or is one above the other? and place-value counters using numbers to 1 000 000. Allow students time to compare the number of runners with their partners.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Lesson Approach</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>To begin this lesson, show students the Explore task. Tell them that when you are asked to compare numbers, you should add them together. Is this true? How can we know? What might a bar model diagram look like when Which ones have the greatest difference and by how much approximately? By how much exactly? Which numbers have the smallest difference? What are the approximate number of runners in each city? Are they the same? Should we round to the nearest 100, 1000, 10 000 or 100 000 when comparing these numbers? Work through Master to recap on the methods used. During Guided Practice, students are subtracting numbers requiring renaming.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-green\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Place-value counters (one set between two)</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-gold\">Maths Language &amp; Variation</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Maths Language</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Students place digits in the wrong place-value columns when subtracting. Students are uncertain of how to cross boundaries when subtracting.</li></ul></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Variation</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Students can show how to rename numbers when subtracting using place-value counters and the column method.</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assess Learning</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-data\"><thead><tr><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Misconceptions</th><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Non-Negotiable</th><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Formative Assessment</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">Students place digits in the wrong place-value columns when subtracting. Students are uncertain of how to cross boundaries when subtracting.</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can show how to rename numbers when subtracting using place-value counters and the column method.</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can use place-value counters to subtract numbers. Students can estimate and round numbers to the nearest 1000, 10 000 and 100 000.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can subtract using the column method.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can subtract rounded numbers mentally. Students can rename numbers using place-value counters and the column method.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Check the Curriculum</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">National Curriculum</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Add and subtract any whole numbers.</li></ul></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Content Priority</th><td class=\"lt-td\">This is a key lesson.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Outcomes</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>This is a key lesson which supports a number of learning outcomes, including:</li><li>Students understand that the value of a digit within a number is determined by the place it holds.</li><li>Students subtract whole numbers with more than 4 digits, including using formal written methods (columnar subtraction).</li><li>Students use rounding to check answers to subtraction and to determine levels of accuracy in the context of a problem.</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Lesson 7":{"label":"Lesson 7","content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson Type</th><td class=\"lt-td\">IF TIME ALLOWS</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Textbook Page(s)</th><td class=\"lt-td\">75 - 79</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson Objective</th><td class=\"lt-td\">To be able to subtract using the column method&lt;br&gt;and place-value counters using numbers to 1&lt;br&gt;000 000.|hundreds is straightforward, but it becomes more difficult when we try to&lt;br&gt;subtract the thousands.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Lesson Approach</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>To begin this lesson, show students the Explore task and ask them: 'How might we find the answer?' Allow them time to work on this with their partners. Suggest to the students that subtracting the ones, tens and To be able to subtract using the column method\nand place-value counters using numbers to 1\n000 000. hundreds is straightforward, but it becomes more difficult when we try to\nsubtract the thousands. What is it about subtracting the thousands that\nmakes it more difficult? Once it has been established that there are not\nenough thousands to carry out the written calculation ask the students\nwhat they have done in previous years in similar situations. How do they\nknow when they need to rename?\nAsk students to discuss the renaming process they have used in previous\nyears and how that can be applied here.\nDuring Guided Practice, students are subtracting with renaming.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-green\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Place-value counters (one set between two)</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-gold\">Maths Language &amp; Variation</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Maths Language</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Example 5: A word problem involving subtracting a 5-digit number from a 6-digit number.</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assess Learning</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-data\"><thead><tr><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Misconceptions</th><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Non-Negotiable</th><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Formative Assessment</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">Students place digits in the wrong place-value columns when subtracting. Students are uncertain of how to cross boundaries when subtracting.</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can rename numbers using place-value counters and the column method to subtract.</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can use place-value counters to subtract numbers. Students can estimate and round numbers to the nearest 1000, 10 000 and 100 000. Students can subtract using the column method.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can subtract rounded numbers mentally.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can rename numbers using place-value counters and the column method. Students can generate questions for subtraction.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Check the Curriculum</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">National Curriculum</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Add and subtract any whole numbers.</li></ul></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Content Priority</th><td class=\"lt-td\">This lesson can be completed if time allows.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Outcomes</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>This lesson supports a number of learning outcomes, including:</li><li>Students understand that the value of a digit within a number is determined by the place it holds.</li><li>Students subtract whole numbers with more than 4 digits, including using formal written methods (columnar subtraction).</li><li>Students use addition to check answers to subtraction.</li><li>Students use rounding to check answers to subtraction and to determine levels of accuracy in the context of a problem.</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Lesson 8":{"label":"Lesson 8","content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson Type</th><td class=\"lt-td\">IF TIME ALLOWS</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Textbook Page(s)</th><td class=\"lt-td\">80 - 84</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson Objective</th><td class=\"lt-td\">To be able to consolidate and refine subtraction by parcels returned and how can this be found? Students should see that skills and place-value knowledge to solve they are looking for the difference between two amounts. Ask the students subtraction problems.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Lesson Approach</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>To begin this lesson, show students the Explore task. Ask the students what they are trying to find in the final column of the table. What is meant how they can find the difference between two numbers. Once it has been established that subtraction can be used, ask the students if they can see that there are going to be some calculations that need renaming and some that don't. How do they know this by looking at the numbers without starting the calculations? Allow them time to work on this with their partners. During the Master section subtraction with and without renaming is reinforced. During Guided Practice, students are subtracting with renaming.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-green\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Place-value counters (one set between two)</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assess Learning</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-data\"><thead><tr><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Misconceptions</th><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Non-Negotiable</th><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Formative Assessment</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">Students place digits in the wrong place-value columns when subtracting. Students are uncertain of how to cross boundaries when subtracting.</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can rename numbers using place-value counters and the column method to subtract.</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can use place-value counters to subtract numbers. Students can estimate and round numbers to the nearest 1000, 10 000 and 100 000.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can subtract using the column method. Students can subtract rounded numbers mentally.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can rename numbers using place-value counters and the column method.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can generate questions for subtraction.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Check the Curriculum</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">National Curriculum</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Add and subtract any whole numbers.</li></ul></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Content Priority</th><td class=\"lt-td\">This lesson can be completed if time allows.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Outcomes</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>This lesson supports a number of learning outcomes, including:</li><li>Students understand that the value of a digit within a number is determined by the place it holds.</li><li>Students subtract whole numbers with more than 4 digits, including using formal written methods (columnar subtraction).</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Lesson 9":{"label":"Lesson 9","content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson Type</th><td class=\"lt-td\">KEY LESSON</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Textbook Page(s)</th><td class=\"lt-td\">85 - 88</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson Objective</th><td class=\"lt-td\">To be able to use addition and subtraction to solve comparison problems with numbers to 1 000 000.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Lesson Approach</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>To begin this lesson, show students the Explore task. Tell them that to compare the number of spectators, they would need to use addition and subtraction. What does that mean? Why would they need to use more than one operation to solve the problem? Once students understand that they need to add together the number of spectators on each day before subtracting from another day, allow them time to complete the operations. Provide them with place-value counters to represent the number of spectators to help them with addition and subtraction. Ask students which day had the greatest number of spectators? Which day had the smallest number of spectators? What is the difference between the day with the greatest and the smallest number of spectators? Use Master to consolidate learning. During Guided Practice, students are adding and subtracting numbers to 1 000 000. Encourage them to try multiple methods, including mental methods, written methods and by using place-value counters.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-green\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Place-value counters (one set between two)</li><li>Maths Language Variation It is important students are able to understand and use the Example 1: Finding the difference using the column method. following mathematical terms in this lesson:</li><li>thousands</li><li>difference Example 2: Adding or subtracting using any method(s).</li><li>Example 3: A word problem involving addition and subtraction.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assess Learning</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-data\"><thead><tr><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Misconceptions</th><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Non-Negotiable</th><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Formative Assessment</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">Students place digits in the wrong place-value columns when adding and/or subtracting.</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can add and subtract numbers accurately using place-value counters.</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can use place-value counters to add and subtract numbers. Students can estimate and round numbers to the nearest 1000, 10 000 and 100 000.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can add and subtract using the column method.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can add and subtract rounded numbers mentally.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Check the Curriculum</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">National Curriculum</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Add and subtract any whole numbers.</li></ul></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Content Priority</th><td class=\"lt-td\">##### This is a key lesson.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Outcomes</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>This is a key lesson which supports a number of learning outcomes, including:</li><li>Students understand that the value of a digit within a number is determined by the place it holds.</li><li>Students add and subtract whole numbers with more than 4 digits, including using formal written methods (columnar addition and subtraction).</li><li>Students solve addition and subtraction multi-step problems in contexts, deciding which operations and methods to use and why.</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Lesson 10":{"label":"Lesson 10","content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson Type</th><td class=\"lt-td\">IF TIME ALLOWS</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Textbook Page(s)</th><td class=\"lt-td\">89 - 93</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson Objective</th><td class=\"lt-td\">To be able to add and subtract using the column method and number bonds with numbers to 1 000 000.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Lesson Approach</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>### To begin this lesson, provide students with the Explore task. Ask the students what type of question is being asked. Will there be any renaming in any of the columns? Ask students to come up with questions based on the information in the Explore section. Tasks could include: addition questions with renaming; subtraction questions without renaming; subtraction with renaming; which two cities' populations would add to give the largest total, which two cities' populations would add to give the smallest total; which two cities' populations have the greatest difference; which two cities' populations have the smallest difference. Ask students to extend the questions beyond two cities and try to create an exhaustive list of possible questions that can be asked. During the Master section, examples of questions that could be asked are worked through. During Guided Practice, students are adding and subtracting numbers to 1 000 000.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-green\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Place-value counters (one set between two)</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Check the Curriculum</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">National Curriculum</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Add and subtract any whole numbers.</li></ul></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Content Priority</th><td class=\"lt-td\">This lesson can be completed if time allows.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Outcomes</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>This lesson supports a number of learning outcomes, including:</li><li>Students understand that the value of a digit within a number is determined by the place it holds.</li><li>Students add and subtract whole numbers with more than 4 digits, including using formal written methods (columnar addition and subtraction).</li><li>Students solve addition and subtraction multi-step problems in contexts, deciding which operations and methods to use and why.</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Lesson 11: Chapter Consolidation":{"label":"Lesson 11: Chapter Consolidation","content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Textbook Page(s)</th><td class=\"lt-td\">93 - 94</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson Objective</th><td class=\"lt-td\">To be able to apply knowledge of addition and subtraction of whole numbers to solve problems.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Lesson Approach</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students work out the missing numbers in a subtraction calculation using the column method.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension &amp; Reflection</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Mind Challenge</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Students work out the missing numbers in a subtraction calculation using the column method.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-amber\"><th class=\"lt-th\">My Maths Journal</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Students find some real life examples of where addition or subtraction of large numbers is needed.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Review</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Complete this with students as a chapter summary and determine if any of them need extra support.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"}}},"Chapter 3: Multiplication & Division":{"label":"Lesson","children":{"Chapter Overview":{"label":"Chapter Overview","content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Chapter Overview</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>In this unit, students are multiplying and dividing 3- and 4-digit numbers by single- and double-digit numbers. The unit begins by finding and defining multiples, factors and common factors. Students begin to work with prime numbers and determine what makes a number prime or composite. \n\nAfter this, they work with square and cube numbers before moving on to multiplying by 10, 100 and 1000. When multiplying, students are encouraged to use a variety of methods, including: number bonds, column methods and the grid method. \n\nNumber bonds are used to represent multiplicative word problems. Students then move on to multiply by 2-digit numbers before beginning to divide by 10, 100 and 1000. The unit ends as students learn to divide, giving rise to remainders using multiple methods, including number bonds and long and short division.</p></div></div>"},"Lesson 1: Finding Multiples":{"label":"Lesson 1: Finding Multiples","content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson Type</th><td class=\"lt-td\">KEY LESSON</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Textbook Page(s)</th><td class=\"lt-td\">96 - 100</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson Objective</th><td class=\"lt-td\">To be able to consolidate and review multiplication; to be able to find the result of multiplying by a number.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Lesson Approach</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>To begin this lesson, show students the Explore task and ask them how many possible answers they can come up with. Tell them there are only 12 multiples of 8 because there are only 12 numbers in the times table. Is this true? How can we check? Let students discuss this idea and work on the task with their partner. Then tell them that the tenth multiple of 8 is 72. Is this true? How can we find out? What do we need to do? Give students time to determine what the tenth multiple of 8 is and then ask them if they know what the tenth number is, then what is the twentieth number and the thirtieth? Can they come up with a rule for multiples? What about the fifth multiple? Ask students if this works for every number and then allow them time to discuss this idea. During Guided Practice, students are finding multiples of other numbers to reinforce the concept.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-green\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Counters/cubes (one set between two)</li><li>Number square or multiplication square</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-gold\">Maths Language &amp; Variation</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Maths Language</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>It is important students are able to understand and use the following mathematical terms in this lesson: ● multiple</li><li>##### Variation</li><li>Example 1: Finding multiples of 7 in a number chart. Example 2: Finding specific multiples of a number.</li><li>Example 3: Deciding whether statements about multiples of specific numbers are true or false.</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Check the Curriculum</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">National Curriculum</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Recall multiplication facts to at least 10 × 10 and corresponding division facts.</li></ul></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Content Priority</th><td class=\"lt-td\">This is a key lesson.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Outcomes</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>This is a key lesson which supports a number of learning outcomes, including:</li><li>Students explain how to identify a multiple of a number.</li><li>Students use knowledge of properties of number to solve problems in a range of contexts.</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Lesson 2: Finding Factors":{"label":"Lesson 2: Finding Factors","content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson Type</th><td class=\"lt-td\">KEY LESSON</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Textbook Page(s)</th><td class=\"lt-td\">101 - 103</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Lesson Approach</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>To be able to consolidate and review multiplication; to be able to find the factors of a To begin this lesson, provide students with 24 counters or objects. Show them the Explore task and ask how many ways they can arrange the counters into equal groups. Allow students time to work on this and come up with a few answers. Then ask them to explore which statement is given number. correct: 'A factor is the number of counters in each group.' 'A factor is the\nnumber of groups.' Can both statements be correct? Give students time to\ndiscuss this. Ask them whether 24 is a factor of 24. How can we check?\nAsk students if they are able to find the factors of a greater number, such\nas 48. Do the number of factors always increase as the number increases?\nWhat about 31; would it have more or fewer factors than 28? Why? How\ncan we be sure? Can we represent that by using the counters? During Guided Practice, students are finding factors of different numbers.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-green\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Counters (24 each)</li><li>Dot cards (one set between two)</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-gold\">Maths Language &amp; Variation</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Variation</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Example 2a: Finding all the factors of 12.</li><li>Example 2b: Finding all the factors of 16.</li><li>Example 3: Finding the number that has 2, 4 and 5 as factors and is less than 25.</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Check the Curriculum</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">National Curriculum</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Identify factors of numbers up to 100.</li><li>Identify square numbers and factors of numbers up to 125.</li><li>Recall multiplication facts to at least 10 × 10 and identify and describe the divisibility rules for 2, 3, 5, 9, and 10.</li><li>Identify and describe the divisibility rules for 2–11.</li></ul></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Content Priority</th><td class=\"lt-td\">This is a key lesson.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Outcomes</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>This is a key lesson which supports a number of learning outcomes, including:</li><li>Students explain what a factor is and how to use arrays and multiplication/division facts to find them.</li><li>Students explain how to systematically find all factors of a number and how they know when they have found them all.</li><li>Students use knowledge of properties of number to solve problems in a range of contexts.</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Lesson 3: Finding Common Factors":{"label":"Lesson 3: Finding Common Factors","content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson Type</th><td class=\"lt-td\">KEY LESSON</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Textbook Page(s)</th><td class=\"lt-td\">104 - 107</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson Objective</th><td class=\"lt-td\">To be able to define and find common factors of numbers to 100.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Lesson Approach</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>To begin this lesson, show students the Explore task and give them time to think about the answer. Ask them if they can find the common factors of 10 and 15 and allow them time to work it out. Tell students that 10 and 15 are both in the 5 times table, so the common factor is 5. Is this true? How can we find out? Ask them to see if it is always the case that numbers that are in a common times table have a common factor by checking if this rule works with 12 and 30. After students have had some time to find the answer, tell them that if 3 is a common factor for both numbers, then 6 must always be a factor. Can we prove that? When might it not be true? Ask them if they spot any patterns in the common factors of 12 and 30. Ask them to circle all of the common factors. Use Master to reinforce learning. Review Master 3 with students and ask them to describe the pattern that they spot. During Guided Practice, students are finding the common factors of a variety of numbers.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-green\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Counters (50 between two)</li><li>Number square or multiplication square (one between two)</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-gold\">Maths Language &amp; Variation</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Maths Language</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>It is important students are able to understand and use the</li><li>following mathematical terms in this lesson:</li><li>factor</li><li>common factor</li></ul></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Variation</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>40.</li><li>Example 2: Finding the factors of 18 and whether it has any common factors with 36.</li><li>Example 3: Finding two numbers that have 1, 2 and 5 as common factors.</li><li>Example 4: Finding a common factor of three numbers – 15, 12 and 9.</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assess Learning</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-data\"><thead><tr><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Misconceptions</th><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Non-Negotiable</th><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Formative Assessment</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">Students confuse the terms 'multiple' and 'factor'. Students find common multiples instead of common factors.</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can use a number square and knowledge of their times tables to find common factors. Students can record their thinking in an organised way.</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can use their knowledge of times tables to support finding common factors. Students can use a number square to help them identify common factors.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can find the common factors of numbers to 20.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can find the common factors of numbers to 40.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Check the Curriculum</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">National Curriculum</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Identify factors of numbers up to 100.</li><li>Identify square numbers and factors of numbers up to 125.</li></ul></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Content Priority</th><td class=\"lt-td\">This is a key lesson.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Outcomes</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>This is a key lesson which supports a number of learning outcomes, including:</li><li>Students explain how to identify a common factor.</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Lesson 4: Multiplying by 10, 100 and 1000":{"label":"Lesson 4: Multiplying by 10, 100 and 1000","content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson Type</th><td class=\"lt-td\">KEY LESSON</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Textbook Page(s)</th><td class=\"lt-td\">104 - 107</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson Objective</th><td class=\"lt-td\">To be able to define and find common factors of numbers to 100.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Lesson Approach</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>### To begin this lesson, show students the Explore task. Provide them with place-value counters for 10, 100 and 1000 and allow them time to represent the problem, discuss what they need to find out and solve it. What counters will you use to represent the packets? What about for each bag or each box? Tell students that your friend says that 100 is 10 times greater than 10. Is that true? How can you prove it? Work through Master 1–3 with the class, so students can check their answers. There are 5000 beads in 5 boxes. Then ask: 'What would happen if we added on more so that there were 11 packets in each bag and 11 bags in each box? Show me with the place-value counters.' During Guided Practice, students are multiplying single digits by 10, 100 and 1000 and multiples of 10, 100 and 1000. They are also completing equations by finding the missing multiplicand or multiplier and multiplying 2-digit numbers by 100.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-green\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Place-value counters (one set between two)</li><li>Place-value chart (one between two)</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-gold\">Maths Language &amp; Variation</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Maths Language</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Example 4a: Multiplying a single-digit number by 100.</li><li>Example 4b: Multiplying a 2-digit number by 100.</li><li>Example 4c: Multiplying a single-digit number by 1000.</li><li>Example 4d: Multiplying a 2-digit number by 100.</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Check the Curriculum</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">National Curriculum</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Multiply multi-digit whole numbers (e.g., 54 × 112).</li><li>Multiply and divide numbers by 10, 100, and 1,000.</li></ul></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Content Priority</th><td class=\"lt-td\">This is a key lesson.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Outcomes</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>This is a key lesson which supports a number of learning outcomes, including:</li><li>● Students explain how to multiply, and the effect of multiplying, a number by 10, 100 and 1000.</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Lesson 5":{"label":"Lesson 5","content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson Type</th><td class=\"lt-td\">KEY LESSON</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Textbook Page(s)</th><td class=\"lt-td\">111 - 114</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Lesson Approach</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>To be able to multiply 2-digit and 3-digit To begin this lesson, provide students with place-value counters for 1, 10 and 100. It is important in this lesson that they see each value multiplied to numbers by a single-digit number using\nmultiple strategies. understand how and why the column method works in addition to other\nmethods.\nShow students the Explore task and ask them to determine which is the\nbest deal and why. Ask them what $14 would look like using place-value\ncounters and if they can show the total of $14 × 5. Ask students how many\nways they can represent this (using place-value counters, number bonds\nand the column method). Tell them you think that one way to find out the\nbest deal would be to multiply $14 by 5 and then see if that total is more\nthan the 'special deal' of buying 5 buckets of golf balls at the same time.\nWould it be better to buy 3 individual buckets at $14 each or buy 3 at the\nsame time? Can you think of another way to buy 5 buckets of golf balls?\nWork through Master so that students can check their answers.\nDuring Guided Practice, students are practising multiplying 2-digit and\n3-digit numbers by a single-digit number. Guided Practice 1 is structured to\nshow how breaking apart numbers is helpful when multiplying. Guide\nstudents to see this if they are unable to see it for themselves.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-green\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Place-value counters (one set between two)</li><li>Place-value chart (one between two)</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Check the Curriculum</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">National Curriculum</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Multiply multi-digit whole numbers (e.g., 54 × 112).</li></ul></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Content Priority</th><td class=\"lt-td\">This is a key lesson.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Outcomes</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>This is a key lesson which supports a number of learning outcomes, including:</li><li>Students multiply a 2-digit or a 3-digit number by a single-digit number using partitioning and representations (no regrouping).</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Lesson 6: Multiplying 4-Digit Numbers":{"label":"Lesson 6: Multiplying 4-Digit Numbers","content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson Type</th><td class=\"lt-td\">KEY LESSON</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Textbook Page(s)</th><td class=\"lt-td\">115 - 117</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson Objective</th><td class=\"lt-td\">To be able to multiply 4-digit numbers by a single-digit number.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Lesson Approach</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>To begin this lesson, show students the Explore task. Ask them how we could represent the number of loaves using a bar model. Is it possible to do this? Allow them time to work on this. Once they have constructed the\nappropriate bar model diagram, ask them what is missing. What\ninformation do we need? Prompt them to see that we either need the value\nof one unit to find the number of loaves the baker made in December, or\nwe need the total number of loaves made in December to find the value of\none unit. We can label the bar representing September, as the number of\nloaves made in September is given.\nAsk the class how we can use this information to find the total number of\nloaves made in December. Tell them you know an easier way to multiply a\nlarge number than using the column method. Is this true? Is there an\neasier way? Ask students to find different ways.\nPrompt students to see how 1232 can be split in different ways to multiply\nmore quickly; by 1000, 200, 30 and 2, or 1000, 100, 100, 30 and 2? Do\nthese work? What might make this an easier way to multiply? Can we\nmultiply numbers mentally using this method? Ask students to work out the\nnumber of loaves made in December, then use Master to demonstrate the\ncalculation and check students' understanding.\nDuring Guided Practice, students are multiplying 4-digit numbers by a\nsingle-digit number in different ways. Guided Practice 2 is structured to show how to break apart larger numbers using our knowledge of 1, 10, 100 and 1000. Guided Practice 3 encourages the use of the column method.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-green\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Place-value counters (one set between two)</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-gold\">Maths Language &amp; Variation</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Maths Language</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>It is important students are able to understand and use the following mathematical terms in this lesson:</li><li>ones</li><li>tens</li><li>hundreds</li><li>thousands</li><li>product</li></ul></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Variation</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Example 1: Finding the product of a multiple of 1000 and a single-digit number.</li><li>Example 2: Using the same multiplier to multiply single-digit,</li><li>2-digit, 3-digit and 4-digit numbers; the final answer can be</li><li>obtained by adding the first four products.</li><li>Example 3: Multiplying a 4-digit number by a single-digit number; column method suggested.</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Check the Curriculum</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">National Curriculum</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Multiply multi-digit whole numbers (e.g., 54 × 112).</li></ul></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Content Priority</th><td class=\"lt-td\">● This is a key lesson. \n\n● This lesson is useful because it includes the partitioning method.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Lesson 7: Multiplying 4-Digit Numbers":{"label":"Lesson 7: Multiplying 4-Digit Numbers","content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson Type</th><td class=\"lt-td\">KEY LESSON</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Textbook Page(s)</th><td class=\"lt-td\">118 - 121</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson Objective</th><td class=\"lt-td\">To be able to multiply 4-digit numbers by a single-digit number with renaming, using a variety of strategies.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Lesson Approach</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>To begin this lesson, show students the Explore task and ask them how we could estimate the number of roof tiles needed for 3 new homes. Allow them to work on this with their partner. Ask the class to work out exactly how many roof tiles Amira's dad needs to order for 3 new homes. How many ways can we represent this? Can we use place-value counters, bar models, or the column method? Tell them that you think this problem is tricky because when you multiply the tens, you get 12. Where does the 10 go? Can we have 12 tens? How might we represent that? What might it look like using different methods? Does this happen again in this question? Will we have to rename? Highlight the different methods, showing the class examples of more than one way to do the column method in addition to place-value counters. Review Master 3 which shows a repeated addition method. Which method do you prefer? Which is most efficient? During Guided Practice, students are multiplying 4-digit numbers by single-digit numbers using the expanded form and the column method.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-green\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Place-value counters (one set between two)</li><li>0–9 digit cards (one set each)</li><li>Place-value chart (one between two)</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assess Learning</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-data\"><thead><tr><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Misconceptions</th><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Non-Negotiable</th><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Formative Assessment</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">multiply the digits instead of their values.</td><td class=\"lt-td\">multiplication using pictures or concrete materials and by breaking down a number into ones, tens, hundreds and thousands.</td><td class=\"lt-td\">number by a single-digit number. Students can use the column method as a strategy.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can partition numbers to multiply.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can use place-value counters to multiply.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Check the Curriculum</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">National Curriculum</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Multiply multi-digit whole numbers (e.g., 54 × 112).</li></ul></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Content Priority</th><td class=\"lt-td\">- This is a key lesson. \n\n- Connections between different methods are shown in this lesson.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Lesson 8: Multiplying 4-Digit Numbers":{"label":"Lesson 8: Multiplying 4-Digit Numbers","content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson Type</th><td class=\"lt-td\">IF TIME ALLOWS</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Textbook Page(s)</th><td class=\"lt-td\">122 - 124</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Lesson Approach</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>To begin this lesson, show students the Explore task and as a class identify the key information in the problem. Can we represent this problem using a bar model? What might that look like? Draw some bar models but label them incorrectly. Label the 9 bars with 1218 kg and the single unit tens and hundreds, using multiple methods. with a question mark. Proceed as if nothing is wrong and as you try to solve the problem using this diagram think out loud for the students to hear you correct yourself. It is important for them to hear what making a mistake and correcting it while solving a problem sounds like. Once you have re-labelled the bars correctly, allow students time to solve the problem with their partners and draw and label the bars correctly. Discuss the students' ideas as a class. Tell them you think it is easier to find the total mass of 10 cars and then subtract 1218 kg. Would this work? How can we be sure? What should we do when there are more than 9 ones, tens, hundreds, and so on, after we multiply? Can you show me what to do using place-value counters? Can you draw place-value counters to show how to rename? Does this work? Can we check? Work through Master to take the class through the process of multiplying and renaming. During Guided Practice, students are multiplying 4-digit numbers by a single-digit number using the column method and then have the choice of which method to use.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-green\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Place-value counters (one set between two)</li><li>Place-value chart (one between two)</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assess Learning</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-data\"><thead><tr><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Misconceptions</th><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Non-Negotiable</th><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Formative Assessment</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">Students disregard place value and multiply the digits instead of their values.</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can represent and solve multiplication using pictures or concrete materials and by breaking down a number into ones, tens, hundreds and thousands.</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can multiply a single-digit number by a single-digit number. Students can use the column method as a strategy. Students can partition numbers to multiply. Students can use place-value counters to multiply. Students can use bar models to represent multiplication.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Check the Curriculum</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">National Curriculum</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Multiply multi-digit whole numbers (e.g., 54 × 112).</li></ul></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Content Priority</th><td class=\"lt-td\">- This lesson can be completed if time allows. \n\n- No new concepts are introduced in this lesson, therefore it can be omitted if time is an issue.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Lesson 9: Multiplying a 2-Digit Number by a 2-Digit Number":{"label":"Lesson 9: Multiplying a 2-Digit Number by a 2-Digit Number","content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson Type</th><td class=\"lt-td\">KEY LESSON</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Textbook Page(s)</th><td class=\"lt-td\">125 - 127</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson Objective</th><td class=\"lt-td\">To be able to multiply 2-digit numbers by 2-digit numbers using multiple methods.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Lesson Approach</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>To begin this lesson, show students the Explore task. Tell them your friend says you can multiply 16 by 10 and then by 2 to find the answer. Is this true? The question suggests multiplying by 12, not 10 and 2. What does the friend mean? Can we prove this? Allow students time to work on this with their partners. Tell them you think that it can also work if you break 16 into 10 and 6, and then multiply by 12. Is this possible? Will that lead us to the same result? Which is the better number to partition and why? Give them some time to discuss this and then explain their thinking. Then discuss which method of partitioning would be better in Master 2. Ask students to compare the estimations in Master 3. Which estimate is closest? Which would you choose? Encourage students to explain why one estimate may be closer than another. During Guided Practice, students are using multiple methods and estimation to multiply.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-green\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Place-value counters (one set between two) ● Place-value chart (one between two)</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-gold\">Maths Language &amp; Variation</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Maths Language</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>tens</li><li>hundreds</li><li>estimate/estimation</li></ul></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Variation</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Example 2: Using estimation to approximate the products; calculating the actual products using chosen method.</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Check the Curriculum</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">National Curriculum</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Multiply a three-digit by one-digit number and two two-digit whole numbers (e.g., 245 × 6, 34 × 83).</li></ul></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Content Priority</th><td class=\"lt-td\">This is a key lesson.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Outcomes</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>This is a key lesson which supports a number of learning outcomes, including: ● Pupils use estimation to support accurate calculation.</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Lesson 10: Multiplying a 2-Digit Number by a 2-Digit Number":{"label":"Lesson 10: Multiplying a 2-Digit Number by a 2-Digit Number","content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson Type</th><td class=\"lt-td\">KEY LESSON</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Textbook Page(s)</th><td class=\"lt-td\">128 - 132</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Lesson Approach</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>### To be able to multiply a 2-digit number by a 2-digit number using multiple methods, including the grid method, partitioning numbers To begin this lesson, show students the Explore task and tell them they can use a grid to solve this problem. What does this mean? Is it possible to break apart 35 and 28 so those numbers are easier to multiply? What might that look like? How can we organise our thinking to solve the and column method, with renaming. problem?\nAllow students time to design a grid that might be useful with their\npartners. If they have never seen this method before, then model its design\nand provide support by completing some of the grid for them. Allow\nstudents to continue to complete the grid and determine how to use those\nnumbers to solve the problem. Once they have understood this method,\nask if they know of any other ways to solve the problem and allow them\ntime to try those other methods.\nTell the class you are more comfortable with using number bonds. Is that\npossible when both numbers are 2 digits? What might that look like? Most\nstudents will break 35 into 30 and 5, and 28 into 20 and 8. Anticipate this\nand then follow up with a question about whether or not we can make 30\neasier to use (10, 10 and 10). Prompt students to use the column method\nto show their thinking. Ask them if it is possible to multiply by 30 and then\nsubtract to get the answer. How can we show that? Use the Master section\nto work through the different methods.\nDuring Guided Practice, students are using their knowledge of multiplying\nby tens to support multiplying by numbers that are greater than 10 but less\nthan 100.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-gold\">Maths Language &amp; Variation</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Maths Language</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>It is important students are able to understand and use the Example 1a–c: Multiplying a multiple of 10 and a 2-digit</li><li>following mathematical terms in this lesson: number with support.</li><li>grid/grid method</li><li>ones</li><li>tens Example 1d–e: Multiplying a 2-digit number by a 2-digit number</li><li>with support.</li><li>hundreds</li><li>multiple</li><li>double Example 2: Finding the product of two 2-digit numbers; no</li><li>suggested method.</li><li>halve</li><li>product</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assess Learning</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-data\"><thead><tr><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Misconceptions</th><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Non-Negotiable</th><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Formative Assessment</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">Students disregard place value and multiply the digits instead of their values. Students have difficulty in choosing the number to break down when multiplying.</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can represent and solve multiplication using pictures or concrete materials and by breaking down a number into ones, tens, hundreds and thousands.</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can multiply a single-digit number by a single-digit number. Students can use the column method as a strategy.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can use a secondary method, such as partitioning numbers or the grid method, to solve multiplication problems in a more formal way.</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can partition numbers to multiply. Students can use place-value counters to multiply.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can multiply 2-digit numbers by single-digit numbers.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Check the Curriculum</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">National Curriculum</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Multiply a three-digit by one-digit number and two two-digit whole numbers (e.g., 245 × 6, 34 × 83).</li></ul></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Content Priority</th><td class=\"lt-td\">This is a key lesson.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Lesson 11: Multiplying a 3-Digit Number by a 2-Digit Number":{"label":"Lesson 11: Multiplying a 3-Digit Number by a 2-Digit Number","content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson Type</th><td class=\"lt-td\">COMBINED</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Textbook Page(s)</th><td class=\"lt-td\">133 - 135</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson Objective</th><td class=\"lt-td\">To be able to multiply a 3-digit number by a 2-digit number, using the column method as a key strategy.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Lesson Approach</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>To begin this lesson, show students the Explore task and ask them how we can find the number of taonga in the collection. Is it possible to represent this problem using place-value counters or a bar model? Allow students time to discuss and work on different methods they could use to solve the problem. Tell them that a bar model might work for this problem, but you find using the written column method easier. Is this true? Is it easier to use? What would it look like? How can we find out if the column method is easier to use? Ask them if they know any other methods. Allow students time to discuss the problem and the three methods shown in Master 1–3 to determine which is best for them. Ask them to talk through each method in turn and make links between them. During Guided Practice, students are using the column method as the key method for multiplying.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-green\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Place-value counters (one set between two)</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-gold\">Maths Language &amp; Variation</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Maths Language</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Students disregard place value and multiply the digits instead of their values. Students round off 3-digit numbers to the nearest hundred instead of ten when estimating.</li></ul></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Variation</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Students can represent and solve multiplication using pictures or concrete materials and by breaking down a number into ones, tens, hundreds and thousands. Students can use a secondary method, such as partitioning numbers, to solve multiplication in a more formal way.</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assess Learning</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-data\"><thead><tr><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Misconceptions</th><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Non-Negotiable</th><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Formative Assessment</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">Students disregard place value and multiply the digits instead of their values. Students round off 3-digit numbers to the nearest hundred instead of ten when estimating.</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can represent and solve multiplication using pictures or concrete materials and by breaking down a number into ones, tens, hundreds and thousands. Students can use a secondary method, such as partitioning numbers, to solve multiplication in a more formal way.</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can multiply a single-digit number by a single-digit number. Students can use the column method as a strategy. Students can partition numbers to multiply. Students can use place-value counters to multiply. Students can multiply multiple-digit numbers by single-digit numbers. Students can use the grid method of multiplication.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Check the Curriculum</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">National Curriculum</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Multiply a three-digit by one-digit number and two two-digit whole numbers (e.g., 245 × 6, 34 × 83).</li></ul></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Content Priority</th><td class=\"lt-td\">This lesson can be combined with Lesson 12. \n\nLessons 11 and 12 build upon previous lessons and do not introduce any new concepts, therefore these lessons can be combined or omitted if time is an issue.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Lesson 12: Lesson 12":{"label":"Lesson 12: Lesson 12","content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson Type</th><td class=\"lt-td\">COMBINED</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Textbook Page(s)</th><td class=\"lt-td\">136 - 138</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson Objective</th><td class=\"lt-td\">To be able to multiply a 3-digit number by a 2-digit number with renaming, using the column method as the key strategy.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Lesson Approach</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>To begin this lesson, show students the Explore task and ask them to discuss the question. Then, take their thoughts as a class. What information do they have? What do they need to find out? Allow them some time to work on the problem with their partners to find out how many pencils are in the boxes altogether. Work through Master 1 highlighting the column method and the links to the place-value counters shown. The focus of this lesson is to use the column method as the key strategy for multiplication. Ask students to do the multiplications shown in Master 2 and 3 using the column method. During Guided Practice, students are solving a word problem involving multiplication linked to the Explore task. Students can choose the method that best suits them.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-green\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Place-value counters (one set between two)</li><li>Place-value chart (one between two)</li><li>1–5 digit cards (one set between two)</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-gold\">Maths Language &amp; Variation</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Maths Language</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>It is important students are able to understand and use the</li><li>following mathematical terms in this lesson:</li></ul></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Variation</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>After the Master section, ask students to work in pairs using</li><li>digit cards 1–5 to make one 3-digit and one 2-digit number.</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Check the Curriculum</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">National Curriculum</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Multiply a three-digit by one-digit number and two two-digit whole numbers (e.g., 245 × 6, 34 × 83).</li></ul></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Content Priority</th><td class=\"lt-td\">This lesson can be combined with Lesson 11. \n\nLessons 11 and 12 build upon previous lessons and do not introduce any new concepts, therefore these lessons can be combined or omitted if time is an issue.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Lesson 13: Dividing by 10, 100 and 1000":{"label":"Lesson 13: Dividing by 10, 100 and 1000","content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson Type</th><td class=\"lt-td\">KEY LESSON</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Textbook Page(s)</th><td class=\"lt-td\">139 - 141</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson Objective</th><td class=\"lt-td\">To be able to find thousands, hundreds and tens in a 4-digit number using concrete materials.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Lesson Approach</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>To begin this lesson, show students the Explore task. Tell them this question is suggesting that we break 3564 into thousands, hundreds, tens and ones. Is this possible? Can we use a number bond diagram to do this? How many thousands are there in 3564? Allow them time to work on the ##### problem with their partners. Continue along this line of questioning and work through the Master. Tell students that if we divide 3564 by 1, we would get 3564, so if we divide 3564 by 10, it must be 356. Is this true? Have I made a mistake? Can you show this using place-value counters? Can you draw this to show where the mistake is? What is the remainder? What should we do with the remainder? During Guided Practice, students are dividing multiples of 1000 by 1000, 100 and 10 and other multiples of 1000, 100 and 10. Encourage them to look for connections between the equations, For example, in Guided Practice 2, can they make a connection between 4000 divided by 2000 and 4000 divided by 20? Similarly in Guided Practice 3, can they use the answer to 5000 divided by 500 to help them with 5000 divided by 50?</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-green\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Place-value counters (one set between two)</li><li>Place-value chart (one between two)</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-gold\">Maths Language &amp; Variation</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Maths Language</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>It is important students are able to understand and use the</li><li>following mathematical terms in this lesson:</li><li>ones</li><li>tens</li><li>hundreds</li></ul></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Variation</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Example 1: Dividing a multiple of 1000 by 1000, 100 and 10.</li><li>Example 2: Dividing a multiple of 1000 by 1000, 2000, 100, 200, 10 and 20.</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assess Learning</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-data\"><thead><tr><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Misconceptions</th><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Non-Negotiable</th><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Formative Assessment</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">Students disregard place value and divide the digits instead of their values.</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can partition numbers and divide by 10, 100 and 1000 using the number bond strategy.</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can divide concrete materials into equal groups. Students can divide numbers to 100.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can use their knowledge of times tables to support division.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can partition numbers and/or use number bonds to support division.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can divide by 10.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can divide by 100.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can divide by 1000.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Check the Curriculum</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">National Curriculum</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Divide up to a four-digit whole number by a one-digit divisor, with a remainder (e.g., 198 ÷ 7, 4154 ÷ 8).</li></ul></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Content Priority</th><td class=\"lt-td\">This is a key lesson.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Outcomes</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>This is a key lesson which supports a number of learning outcomes, including: ● Pupils explain how to divide, and the effect of dividing, a number by 10, 100 and 1000.</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Lesson 14: Dividing without Remainder":{"label":"Lesson 14: Dividing without Remainder","content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson Type</th><td class=\"lt-td\">KEY LESSON</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Textbook Page(s)</th><td class=\"lt-td\">142 - 144</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson Objective</th><td class=\"lt-td\">To be able to divide 3-digit and 4-digit numbers by a single-digit number, using number bonds and long division as key methods.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Lesson Approach</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>To begin this lesson, show students the Explore task and allow them time to solve the problem. While they work suggest to them they can use number bonds to break apart the number, which will make dividing easier. How can we break it apart so that it is easy to divide? Allow students to discuss this and prompt them by looking at the divisor. Tell them that because 2 is the divisor, 640 can easily be broken into 600 and 40 because both of those numbers can be divided by 2 easily. Is there another way that we can break apart the number? Allow them to use place-value counters to represent the number and divide it into equal groups. Once students are comfortable with number bonds, show them the symbol for long division and place the numbers of the equation into the method. Guide them through this step by step, for example: 'First, we need to look at the hundreds column,' and so on. Ask them how many twos there are in 600. This may require some modelling if none of the children have used this method before. Use Master to show the class how the number bond method and long division are asking the same questions. During Guided Practice, students are using both methods to solve division problems.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-green\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Place-value counters (one set between two)</li><li>Place-value chart (one between two)</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Check the Curriculum</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">National Curriculum</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Divide up to a three-digit whole number by a one-digit divisor, with no remainder (e.g., 65 ÷ 5).</li><li>Divide up to a four-digit whole number by a one-digit divisor, with a remainder (e.g., 198 ÷ 7, 4154 ÷ 8).</li></ul></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Content Priority</th><td class=\"lt-td\">This is a key lesson.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Outcomes</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>This is a key lesson which supports a number of learning outcomes, including:</li><li>Pupils divide a three-digit number by a single-digit number using partitioning and representations (no exchanging, no remainders).</li><li>Pupils will use efficient strategies of division to solve problems.</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Lesson 15: Dividing without Remainder":{"label":"Lesson 15: Dividing without Remainder","content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson Type</th><td class=\"lt-td\">KEY LESSON</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Textbook Page(s)</th><td class=\"lt-td\">145 - 148</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Lesson Approach</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>To begin this lesson, show students the Explore task and ask them how we could work out how much each of the classes received. Allow them time to work on this with their partners. Work through Master 1 with them. Lulu's long division as the key methods. school raised $1968 and shared the money equally between 6 classes. Is\nthis possible? How do we know? Can we show this by using concrete\nmaterials or drawings? Can we think of other questions? Allow them time\nto come up with other questions.\nTell them that the easiest way to solve this question is by using number\nbonds. How would this look? Tell them it is quite simple — you just partition\nthe number into 1000, 900, 60 and 8. Will this work? How can we check?\nGuide students to recognise that all these numbers cannot be divided\nexactly by the divisor, which is 6, so we need to try again. Try different\nvariations with the students. Do 1100, 1200 or 1300 divide by 6 exactly?\nWhat about 1800? What would be left over? Can 168 be divided by 6\nexactly? What about 120? What would be left over, would it be 48? Can 48\nbe divided by 6 exactly?\nUsing number bonds as a strategy is pivotal in further mathematics. This is\na lesson where it is important for students to embed this convention\ndeeply. Ask students if it is possible to use long division to solve this\nproblem. How are the two strategies similar? How are they different? Use\nMaster to show students how the two methods are recorded.\nDuring Guided Practice, students are using both methods to solve division\nproblems.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-gold\">Maths Language &amp; Variation</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Maths Language</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>It is important students are able to understand and use the following mathematical terms in this lesson:</li><li>ones</li><li>tens</li><li>hundreds</li><li>thousands</li><li>partition</li></ul></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Variation</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Dividing a 4-digit number by single-digit number. Examples 1–2: Using both the number bond strategy and long division.</li><li>Example 3: No method suggested.</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Check the Curriculum</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">National Curriculum</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Divide up to a three-digit whole number by a one-digit divisor, with no remainder (e.g., 65 ÷ 5).</li><li>Divide up to a four-digit whole number by a one-digit divisor, with a remainder (e.g., 198 ÷ 7, 4154 ÷ 8).</li></ul></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Content Priority</th><td class=\"lt-td\">This is a key lesson.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Outcomes</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>This is a key lesson which supports a number of learning outcomes, including:</li><li>Students divide a two-digit number by a single-digit number using partitioning and representations (no remainders).</li><li>Students divide a three-digit number by a single-digit number using partitioning and representations (no remainders). ● Students will use efficient strategies of division to solve problems.</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Lesson 16: Dividing with Remainder":{"label":"Lesson 16: Dividing with Remainder","content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson Type</th><td class=\"lt-td\">KEY LESSON</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Textbook Page(s)</th><td class=\"lt-td\">149 - 152</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Lesson Approach</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>To begin this lesson, show students the Explore task and ask them to review the problem and discuss what they are being asked. What information do they need? Once they have ascertained that the problem will require division, ask them to talk about their preferred method of remainders. calculation. Can you partition 469 into chunks that are easily divisible by 6\nor use a short division method? Allow them time to work on the problem\nwith their partners and encourage them to use both methods to find the\nanswer.\nOnce they are able to work out both methods, remind them of the initial\nquestion about dividing the kūmara into boxes of 6. Is this possible? Can\nwe divide 469 kūmara into boxes of 6? Why, or why not? Can the 1\nremaining kūmara be divided between the boxes equally? What if it was\nnot kūmara? Draw their attention to Master 2. Can we divide 1 gram of\nsugar flowers between 6 cakes? What might that look like using a bar\nmodel; would that help us work this out?\nDuring Guided Practice, students are using short division and number\nbonds to solve division problems. In Guided Practice 4, using a bar model\nis expected.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-gold\">Maths Language &amp; Variation</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Maths Language</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>It is important students are able to understand and use the following mathematical terms in this lesson:</li><li>ones</li></ul></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Variation</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Example 1: Dividing a 3-digit number by a single-digit number with a remainder. Number bond and short division answers are given as support with only the remainder to find.</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assess Learning</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-data\"><thead><tr><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Misconceptions</th><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Non-Negotiable</th><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Formative Assessment</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">Students disregard place value and divide the digits instead of their values.</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can partition numbers and divide by 10, 100 and 1000 using the number bond strategy. Students can divide using the long division strategy.</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can divide concrete materials into equal groups. Students can divide numbers to 100. Students can use their knowledge of times tables to support division.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can partition numbers and/or use number bonds to support division.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can divide by 10.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can divide by 100.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Check the Curriculum</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">National Curriculum</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Divide up to a four-digit whole number by a one-digit divisor, with a remainder (e.g., 198 ÷ 7, 4154 ÷ 8).</li></ul></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Content Priority</th><td class=\"lt-td\">This is a key lesson.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Outcomes</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>This is a key lesson which supports a number of learning outcomes, including:</li><li>Students divide a 3-digit number by a single-digit number using partitioning and short division (with exchanging and remainders).</li><li>Students solve short division problems accurately when the hundreds digit is smaller than the divisor.</li><li>Students will use efficient strategies of division to solve problems.</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Lesson 17: Chapter Consolidation":{"label":"Lesson 17: Chapter Consolidation","content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Textbook Page(s)</th><td class=\"lt-td\">153 - 154</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson Objective</th><td class=\"lt-td\">To be able to apply knowledge of multiplication and division of whole numbers to solve problems.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Lesson Approach</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students work out the solution to a word problem involving multiplying a 2-digit number by a 2-digit number, dividing a 4-digit number by a 2-digit number, and then multiplying a 3-digit number by a single-digit number.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension &amp; Reflection</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Mind Challenge</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Students work out the solution to a word problem involving multiplying a 2-digit number by a 2-digit number, dividing a 4-digit number by a 2-digit number, and then multiplying a 3-digit number by a single-digit number.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-amber\"><th class=\"lt-th\">My Maths Journal</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Students decide how to partition a 3-digit number if they were dividing the number by 3, 4, 5, 6, 7 and 8 and explain their decisions. They then write a division story for one of the division calculations.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Review</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Complete this with students as a chapter summary and determine if any of them need extra support.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"}}},"Chapter 4: Multi-Step Word Problems":{"label":"Lesson","children":{"Chapter Overview":{"label":"Chapter Overview","content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Chapter Overview</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>In this chapter, students are solving word problems that involve multiple steps and a variety of operations. Students begin the unit by simply choosing the correct operation before moving onto representing the key information using bar models. \n\nApplying the strategies learned in previous units is key in solving the challenges. The chapter ends with complex representations of numbers and change using advanced bar models.</p></div></div>"},"Lesson 1: Solving Word Problems Using Multiplication and Division":{"label":"Lesson 1: Solving Word Problems Using Multiplication and Division","content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson Type</th><td class=\"lt-td\">IF TIME ALLOWS</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Textbook Page(s)</th><td class=\"lt-td\">156 - 159</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson Objective</th><td class=\"lt-td\">To be able to solve word problems involving multiple operations; to be able to identify the operation required to carry out the plan.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Lesson Approach</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>To begin this lesson, show students an enlarged version of the Explore task. If that is not possible, ask them what information they will need to be able to solve the problem. Do we have enough information to solve it? Tell them that identifying the key information in a word problem is important. What does this mean? Allow students to explore the key information from the problem with their partner. How many boxes? How many bags? How many bags in each box? What do all of these questions lead us to do: add, subtract, multiply or divide? We also need to know how many lollies were packed into each bag. Do we need to multiply again to find the number of packets required? Will this tell us how many packets we need? Let students work out that this method is incorrect and identify that we need to divide. Allow them to\nemploy a range of strategies to multiply and divide, and then ask students\nif they can show you multiple ways to get to the answer, using as many\nstrategies as possible. Allow them time to work on the tasks with their\npartner. Work through Master to demonstrate the steps involved in problem solving. This lesson introduces the steps to problem solving set out by Polya in the 1950s. Emphasise to the children the four steps so that they have this in mind when they are problem solving. 1) Understand the problem. 2) Form a plan 3) Action the plan 4) Check your answer. During Guided Practice, students are exploring dividing a total number into different amounts.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-green\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Place-value counters (one between two)</li><li>Place-value chart (one between two)</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-gold\">Maths Language &amp; Variation</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Maths Language</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>It is important students are able to understand and use the</li><li>following mathematical terms in this lesson:</li><li>Multiply</li><li>Divide</li></ul></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Variation</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Example 1: Using the information provided about the number of</li><li>lollies in a packet to find the total number of lollies in a box of 12 packets; using this information to find out how many lollies in 8 boxes.</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assess Learning</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-data\"><thead><tr><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Misconceptions</th><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Non-Negotiable</th><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Formative Assessment</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">Students believe they need to multiply or divide, not both. Students multiply when they should divide, and vice versa.</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can identify key information from the problem, and describe the steps they must apply to solve the problem.</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can break problems down into their key parts. Students can identify the key information in a word problem.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can determine where they need to begin solving a problem.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can use a variety of multiplication and division strategies to solve problems.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can use bar models to represent multiplication and division.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Check the Curriculum</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">National Curriculum</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Multiply a three-digit by one-digit number and two two-digit whole numbers (e.g., 245 × 6, 34 × 83).</li><li>Divide up to a three-digit whole number by a one-digit divisor, with a remainder (e.g., 83 ÷ 5 = 16, remainder 3). &lt;u&gt;Ready to Progress&lt;/u&gt;</li><li>This lesson can be completed if time allows.</li></ul></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Content Priority</th><td class=\"lt-td\">##### Learning Outcomes \n\n● This lesson supports a number of learning outcomes, including: students identify key information in word problems</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Outcomes</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>● This lesson supports a number of learning outcomes, including: students identify key information in word problems</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Lesson 2: Solving Word Problems Using Bar Models":{"label":"Lesson 2: Solving Word Problems Using Bar Models","content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson Type</th><td class=\"lt-td\">IF TIME ALLOWS</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Textbook Page(s)</th><td class=\"lt-td\">160 - 162</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson Objective</th><td class=\"lt-td\">To be able to solve word problems involving multiplication and division, using bar models as the main heuristic.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Lesson Approach</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>To begin this lesson, show students the Explore task and ask them what the question is asking and how to identify the key information that will help them solve the problem. Who has more money, Charles or Sam? How do we know? What do we need to find out to work out how much money Charles has saved? Once students have determined that first they need to use the information about Sam's spending to work out his savings, allow time for them to discuss what operation they need to use. Is it possible to represent this problem using a bar model? Allow students time to solve the task using a bar model heuristic. Work through Master to show children that they can show 4 quarters with a\nbar model to find Sam's total savings. Then move on to the next step. It says that Charles has 6 times as much\nsaved as Sam. Agree that a bar model can also be used for the second\npart of the problem. What would the bar model look like to work out how\nmuch money Charles has saved? If a student draws the model correctly,\nthen show it to the class for approval. Alternatively ask students to talk\nthrough the bar model in Master.\nHow can we check if we found the correct amount; should we multiply or\ndivide? Agree that as we used multiplication to work out the answer, we\nshould use division to check.\nDuring Guided Practice, students are given some other word problems.\nEncourage them to use the four-step process introduced in Lesson 1:\nUnderstand, Form a plan, Action the plan, Check the answer.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-green\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Place-value counters</li><li>Place-value chart (one between two)</li><li>Counters</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-gold\">Maths Language &amp; Variation</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Maths Language</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>It is important students are able to understand and use the following mathematical terms in this lesson:</li><li>Multiply</li></ul></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Variation</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Example 1: Using multiplication and division to solve a problem Example 2: Using division and subtraction to solve a problem.</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assess Learning</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-data\"><thead><tr><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Misconceptions</th><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Non-Negotiable</th><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Formative Assessment</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">Students believe they need to multiply or divide, not both. Students multiply when they should divide, and vice versa. Students incorrectly draw and/or label bar models.</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can identify key information from the problem, and describe the steps they must apply to solve the problem. Students can represent numbers in the problem with bar models.</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can break problems down into their key parts. Students can identify the key information in a word problem. Students can determine where they need to begin solving a problem.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can use a variety of multiplication and division strategies to solve problems.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can use bar models to represent multiplication and division.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Check the Curriculum</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">National Curriculum</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Use known multiplication facts to scale a quantity.</li></ul></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Content Priority</th><td class=\"lt-td\">This lesson can be completed if time allows.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Outcomes</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>This lesson supports a number of learning outcomes, including:</li><li>Students identify key information in word problems.</li><li>Students multiply and divide numbers mentally drawing upon known facts.</li><li>Students use efficient strategies of division to solve problems.</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Lesson 3: Lesson 3":{"label":"Lesson 3: Lesson 3","content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson Type</th><td class=\"lt-td\">IF TIME ALLOWS</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Textbook Page(s)</th><td class=\"lt-td\">163 - 165</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson Objective</th><td class=\"lt-td\">To be able to solve word problems involving multiple operations, identifying key information and representing information using bar model diagrams.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Lesson Approach</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>To begin this lesson, show students the Explore task. Ask them to work in pairs to point out the key information in the problem, and then discuss it with their partners. How many steps are needed? Which operations will you use? How can we tell? Allow students time to discuss their proposed plans. Share as a class, did you form similar plans? Give students time to solve the problem with their partner. Let students work through Master 1 to solve the problem. What have they discovered? Did they work it out the same way? Is there a better method? Did you use multiplication or addition to find the mass of the two cubes? Could number bonds help us? Work through Master 2 to 4 with students. For each word problem, encourage students to discuss each one first to make sure they understand the problem and have a plan. Can you use information from a previous problem to help? If not, can you use both? Can you use a bar model for the questions? During Guided Practice, students are applying their knowledge of problem solving to solve multi-step word problems.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-green\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Place-value counters</li><li>Place-value chart (one between two)</li><li>Counters</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-gold\">Maths Language &amp; Variation</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Maths Language</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Students believe they need to multiply or divide, not both. Students multiply when they should divide, and vice versa.</li><li>Students incorrectly draw and/or label bar models.</li><li>Check the curriculum</li><li>National Curriculum</li><li>Recall multiplication and corresponding di</li></ul></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Variation</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Students can represent numbers in a problem using bar models, to solve the problem.</li><li>vision facts for 4s and 6s.</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assess Learning</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-data\"><thead><tr><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Misconceptions</th><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Non-Negotiable</th><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Formative Assessment</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">Students believe they need to multiply or divide, not both. Students multiply when they should divide, and vice versa.</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can represent numbers in a problem using bar models, to solve the problem.</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can break problems down into their key parts. Students can identify the key information in a word problem.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">Students incorrectly draw and/or label bar models.</td><td class=\"lt-td\">vision facts for 4s and 6s.</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can determine where they need to begin solving a problem. Students can use a variety of multiplication and division strategies to solve problems. Students can use bar models to represent multiplication and division. Students can use place-value counters, or other concrete materials, to show division and multiplication.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">Check the curriculum</td><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\"></td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">National Curriculum</td><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\"></td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">Recall multiplication and corresponding di</td><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\"></td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Check the Curriculum</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">National Curriculum</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Recall multiplication and corresponding di vision facts for 4s and 6s.</li><li>Multiply a two-digit by one-digit number and two one-digit whole numbers (e.g., 23 × 5, 7 × 8).</li><li>Use doubling or halving to scale a quantity (e.g., to double or halve a recipe).</li></ul></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Content Priority</th><td class=\"lt-td\">This lesson can be completed if time allows.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Outcomes</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>This lesson supports a number of learning outcomes, including:</li><li>● Students multiply, divide, add and subtract numbers mentally drawing upon known facts.</li><li>● Students identify key information in multi-step word problems.</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Lesson 4: Lesson 4":{"label":"Lesson 4: Lesson 4","content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson Type</th><td class=\"lt-td\">IF TIME ALLOWS</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Textbook Page(s)</th><td class=\"lt-td\">166 - 168</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson Objective</th><td class=\"lt-td\">To be able to solve word problems involving multiple operations, using bar models as the main heuristic to represent key information.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Lesson Approach</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>To begin this lesson, show students the Explore task and ask them how we could work out the greatest number of bags Charles could fill. Allow them time to work on the problem with their partner, encouraging them to talk to each other about how to find the key information needed to answer the problem. Agree that first of all they will need to find out how many bags of 8 marbles can be made from 570 marbles. What operation will you use for this step? Once they know the greatest number of bags Charles can fill, ask them to discuss how they will find out the number of blue marbles he will need. Once the plan is formed, ask students to discuss how they are going to action the plan. What method will you use for the division? What about the multiplication? Work through Master 1 with them comparing their thinking with their friends in the book. Discuss using estimation to check the answer. Is your answer reasonable? Work through Master 2 in a similar fashion. During Guided Practice, students are applying their knowledge of problem solving to solve multi-step word problems.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-green\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Place-value counters</li><li>Place-value chart (one between two)</li><li>Counters</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-gold\">Maths Language &amp; Variation</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Maths Language</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Students believe they need to multiply or divide, not both.</li><li>Students multiply when they should divide, and vice versa.</li><li>Students incorrectly draw and/or label bar models.</li></ul></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Variation</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Students can represent numbers in the problem with pictures and solve.</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assess Learning</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-data\"><thead><tr><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Misconceptions</th><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Non-Negotiable</th><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Formative Assessment</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">Students believe they need to multiply or divide, not both.</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can represent numbers in the problem with pictures and solve.</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can break problems down into their key parts.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">Students multiply when they should divide, and vice versa.</td><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can identify the key information in a word problem.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">Students incorrectly draw and/or label bar models.</td><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can determine where they need to begin solving a problem. Students can use a variety of multiplication and division strategies to solve problems. Students can use bar models to represent multiplication and division. Students can use place-value counters, or other concrete materials, to show division and multiplication.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Check the Curriculum</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">National Curriculum</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Divide up to a three-digit whole number by a one-digit divisor, with no remainder (e.g., 65 ÷ 5).</li><li>Use known multiplication facts to scale a quantity.</li></ul></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Content Priority</th><td class=\"lt-td\">This lesson can be completed if time allows.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Outcomes</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>This lesson supports a number of learning outcomes, including:</li><li>Students multiply, divide, add and subtract numbers mentally drawing upon known facts.</li><li>Students identify key information in multi-step word problems.</li><li>Students use estimation to support accurate calculation.</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Lesson 5: Chapter Consolidation":{"label":"Lesson 5: Chapter Consolidation","content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Textbook Page(s)</th><td class=\"lt-td\">169 - 170</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson Objective</th><td class=\"lt-td\">To be able to apply knowledge of whole numbers to solve problems.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Lesson Approach</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students work out their solution to the problem.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension &amp; Reflection</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Mind Challenge</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Students work out their solution to the problem.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-amber\"><th class=\"lt-th\">My Maths Journal</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Students write a word problem based on a given bar model.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Review</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Complete this with students as a chapter summary and determine if any of them need extra support.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"}}},"Chapter 5: Tables & Line Graphs":{"label":"Lesson","children":{"Chapter Overview":{"label":"Chapter Overview","content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Chapter Overview</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>In this chapter, students read and interpret information in tables and line graphs. The first lesson requires students to read and interpret information presented in a table showing flights between Singapore and London. In the next lesson, they are required to use the data to answer questions, however the data has restrictions and must be sorted. \n\nThe final lesson on tables leaves out key information, such as omitting a train time to indicate the train does not stop at a specific station. Then there are four lessons on line graphs, beginning with a single line to represent a given set of data, followed by constructing line graphs that have more than one data set to represent.</p></div></div>"},"Lesson 1: Reading Tables":{"label":"Lesson 1: Reading Tables","content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson Type</th><td class=\"lt-td\">KEY LESSON</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Textbook Page(s)</th><td class=\"lt-td\">172 - 175</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson Objective</th><td class=\"lt-td\">To be able to read the information presented in a table (including timetables) and interpret its meaning.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Lesson Approach</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>To begin this lesson, show students the Explore task and ask them to work in pairs to discuss the various items of information presented in the timetable. What can they see? What does it mean? Give them time to discuss and interpret what the different times mean. Tell them that the journey from Swanson to Britomart always stops at Newmarket. Is this true? How can we check? Where should we look for that information? Ask students to look at the 06:55 journey. If two friends both took the same train, but then one got off the train at Newmarket and the other got off at Britomart, how long was each of them on the train? Can we write these times in minutes, and in hours and minutes? What is the difference in journey times? Give students time to work out the answers. Allow students time to review the information presented in the train timetable, Then ask them to work in pairs work out the answers to the Explore questions. Encourage students to support one another in interpreting the information from the timetable to find which train each friend should take. Work through Master 1–4 to show students how to work out the best journey options for the characters who are travelling. During Guided Practice, students are using timelines and information from the table to answer the questions.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-green\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Copy of actual train timetables (optional for advanced learners)</li><li>Enlarged printed copy of the Explore timetable (between 2 or 1 each)</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-gold\">Maths Language &amp; Variation</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Maths Language</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>It is important pupils are able to understand and use the following mathematical terms in this lesson:</li><li>duration</li><li>hour</li><li>minute</li><li>a.m.</li><li>p.m.</li></ul></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Variation</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Example 1: Navigating a variety of information to determine the best car given the specific criteria.</li><li>Example 2: Calculating journey length when reading a timetable.</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assess Learning</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-data\"><thead><tr><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Misconceptions</th><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Non-Negotiable</th><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Formative Assessment</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">Students have trouble reading 24-hour time. Students are unsure of how to manage the absence of information in a table. Students use addition or subtraction to find time instead of using a timeline i.e. they use base 10 rather than calculate in minutes and hours.</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can locate and read information listed in a table when they are asked a specific question.</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can read simple tables. Students can read the columns and rows in a table. Students can interpret the absence of information in a table. Students can interpret information from a table.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Check the Curriculum</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">National Curriculum</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Create and describe data visualisations to make meaning from the data, with statements including the names of the variable and group of interest.</li><li>Create and describe data visualisations to make meaning from the data, with statements including the names of the variable and group of interest.</li><li>Create and describe a variety of data visualisations to make meaning from the data, identifying features, patterns, and trends in context, and including the variable and group of interest.</li><li>Read, interpret, and use timetables and charts that present information about duration.</li><li>Read, interpret, and use timetables, charts, and results that present information about duration.</li></ul></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Content Priority</th><td class=\"lt-td\">This is a key lesson.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Outcomes</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>This is a key lesson which supports this learning outcome:</li><li>● Students solve problems using information from tables.</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Lesson 2: Reading Tables":{"label":"Lesson 2: Reading Tables","content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson Type</th><td class=\"lt-td\">KEY LESSON</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Textbook Page(s)</th><td class=\"lt-td\">176 - 179</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson Objective</th><td class=\"lt-td\">To be able to read and answer questions based on information presented in a table including finding start time, end time and duration.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Lesson Approach</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>To begin this lesson, show students the Explore task which shows the start times of various classes at a dance studio, together with how long each class is. Allow them time to review the information presented in the studio timetable including noticing that not all the classes are in the morning. Then ask them to work in pairs to answer the Explore question. Encourage students to support one another in interpreting the information from the timetable, including the use of the 24-hour clock. Work through Master 1 together first to guide students in using a timeline to solve the problem and then go through Master 2–4 after students have attempted the questions so that they may check their answers. During Guided Practice, students are using the information in the timetable to answer the problems.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-green\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Enlarged printed copy of the Explore timetable (between 2 or 1 each)</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-gold\">Maths Language &amp; Variation</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Maths Language</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>It is important students are able to understand and use the following mathematical terms in this lesson:</li><li>duration</li><li>24-hour time</li><li>hour</li><li>minute</li><li>a.m.</li><li>p.m.</li><li>start time</li><li>end time</li><li>midday</li></ul></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Variation</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Example 1: Interpreting information from a table; using information given to answer questions relating to time and duration.</li><li>Example 2: Interpreting information from a table; looking at information given to choose a departure time.</li><li>Example 3: Interpreting information from a table to answer a question when given set criteria.</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assess Learning</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-data\"><thead><tr><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Misconceptions</th><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Non-Negotiable</th><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Formative Assessment</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">Students have trouble reading 24-hour time.</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can locate and read information in a table when they are asked a specific question.</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can read simple tables. Students can read the columns and rows</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">Students use addition or subtraction to</td><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\">in a table.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">find time instead of using a timeline i.e. they use base 10 rather than calculate in minutes and hours.</td><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can interpret information from a table.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Check the Curriculum</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">National Curriculum</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Create and describe data visualisations to make meaning from the data, with statements including the names of the variable and group of interest.</li><li>Create and describe a variety of data visualisations to make meaning from the data, identifying features, patterns, and trends in context, and including the variable and group of interest.</li></ul></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Content Priority</th><td class=\"lt-td\">This is a key lesson.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Outcomes</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>This is a key lesson which supports this learning outcome:</li><li>● Students solve problems using information from tables.</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Lesson 3: Reading Tables":{"label":"Lesson 3: Reading Tables","content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson Type</th><td class=\"lt-td\">IF TIME ALLOWS</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Textbook Page(s)</th><td class=\"lt-td\">180 - 183</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson Objective</th><td class=\"lt-td\">To be able to read and answer questions based on information presented in a table including finding start time, end time and duration.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Lesson Approach</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>To begin this lesson, show students the Explore task which shows the start times of various classes at a dance studio, together with the duration of each class. Allow them time to review the information presented in the studio timetable. Allow students time to work out the answer. Work through Master 1 together to find out how long Lulu's mum will be at the dance studio. Talk about the two different methods shown. Which do you prefer, using the timeline or adding the 3 durations? Allow students time to use the two methods to answer Master 2. Ask students if they think it is a challenging question. What is difficult about the question? What makes it harder to solve than the first question? Ask them to explain their strategies for calculating the passage of time. Can they use minutes? Can they use hours and minutes? During Guided Practice, students are solving problems based on the information that is presented about Tama and the dance studio timetable.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-green\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Enlarged printed copy of the Explore timetable (between 2 or 1 each)</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-gold\">Maths Language &amp; Variation</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Maths Language</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>It is important students are able to understand and use the following mathematical terms in this lesson:</li><li>duration</li><li>24-hour time</li><li>hour</li><li>minute</li><li>a.m.</li><li>p.m.</li></ul></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Variation</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Example 1: Calculating time of departure using given criteria and information in a timetable.</li><li>Example 2: Calculating time of arrival by adding durations of time from a starting time.</li><li>Example 3: Calculating duration based on the answers to examples 1 and 2.</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assess Learning</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-data\"><thead><tr><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Misconceptions</th><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Non-Negotiable</th><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Formative Assessment</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">Students have trouble reading 24-hour time.</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can locate and read information in a table when they are asked a specific question.</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can read simple tables. Students can read columns and rows in a</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">Students use addition or subtraction to</td><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\">table.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">find time instead of using a timeline or number bonds i.e. they use base 10 rather than calculate in minutes and</td><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can interpret the absence of information in a table.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">hours.</td><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can interpret the information from a table.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Check the Curriculum</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">National Curriculum</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Use multivariate data to investigate summary, comparison, and time-series situations, by:</li><li>posing an investigative question that can be answered with data</li><li>making conjectures or assertions about expected findings&quot;.</li><li>Identify, explain, check, and, if necessary, improve features in others' data investigations (e.g., biased survey questions, misleading information or statements).</li></ul></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Content Priority</th><td class=\"lt-td\">This lesson can be completed if time allows.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Outcomes</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>This lesson supports this learning outcome:</li><li>● Students solve problems using information from tables.</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Lesson 4: Reading Line Graphs":{"label":"Lesson 4: Reading Line Graphs","content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson Type</th><td class=\"lt-td\">KEY LESSON</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Textbook Page(s)</th><td class=\"lt-td\">184 - 187</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson Objective</th><td class=\"lt-td\">To be able to read and interpret information provided in different types of graphs such as a strip graph, a pie chart and a line graph.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Lesson Approach</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>To begin this lesson, give students some time to study the different data presentations in the Explore task. Ask if they are all presenting the same data and have students give examples to confirm this. Discuss with students some similarities and advantages of the different types of presentations. For example, highlight that a strip graph is similar to a pie chart because like pie charts, a strip graph is broken into sections which make up a whole. Discuss also why someone might choose to use a line graph instead of a bar chart. How might a line graph be more helpful? Next, get students to look at the question shown on the page, and have them share their thoughts. Then, go through Master 1 to discuss the answer and consolidate their understanding. Ask students how many other questions they can develop based on the information provided. Allow students time to develop their own questions. Use Master 2 to explore how the greatest and least number of passengers can be determined by looking at a pie chart. Ask students if it is easier to gather this information from a pie chart or a line graph. Use Master 3 to explore with students what the slope of the line graph tells us. Does the number of passengers go up when the line goes up between 11:00 and 12:00? What happens to the number of passengers between 12:00 and 13:00? Continue this discussion by exploring what a straight horizontal line means on a line graph. During Guided Practice, students are looking at data from a strip graph and placing that information into a table. They then answer different questions about the same data using different types of graphs.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-green\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Blank table to complete</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-gold\">Maths Language &amp; Variation</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Maths Language</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>It is important students are able to understand and use the following mathematical terms in this lesson:</li><li>strip graph</li><li>pie chart</li></ul></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Variation</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Example 1a): Reading and interpreting data from a strip graph and entering it into a table.</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Check the Curriculum</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">National Curriculum</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Answer the investigative question, comparing findings with initial conjectures or assertions and their existing knowledge of the world.</li></ul></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Content Priority</th><td class=\"lt-td\">This is a key lesson.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Outcomes</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>This is a key lesson which supports a number of learning outcomes, including: ● Students read, interpret and complete information in a table. ● Students read and interpret a graph. ● Students solve problems using information from tables and graphs.</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Lesson 5: Reading Line Graphs":{"label":"Lesson 5: Reading Line Graphs","content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson Type</th><td class=\"lt-td\">KEY LESSON</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Textbook Page(s)</th><td class=\"lt-td\">188 - 192</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson Objective</th><td class=\"lt-td\">To be able to read and interpret information presented on a line graph, where the data is represented by more than one line.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Lesson Approach</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>To begin this lesson, show students the Explore task and give them some time to make sense of the information that is being presented. Ask students what they are able to tell from the line graph. What can they say about the sales of ice creams and candy floss? Is it easy to compare the two? Draw their attention to the y-axis. What do they notice? Confirm that the number of sales have been rounded to the nearest 100. Discuss the statements in Master 1. Are they correct? How can you check? Then work through Master 2, supporting students as they use the\ninformation in the line graph to answer the questions. Allow them to\nanswer the questions in Masters 3 and 4 before reviewing the questions\nwith them so they may check their answers. During Guided Practice, students are reading information from a line\ngraph, and then placing the information onto a table. They answer\nquestions based on the information and decide which questions are easier\nto answer using the table and which using the line graph.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-green\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Rulers (two each)</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-gold\">Maths Language &amp; Variation</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Maths Language</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>It is important students are able to understand and use the following mathematical terms in this lesson:</li><li>line graph</li><li>slope</li><li>increased</li><li>decreased</li><li>axis</li><li>horizontal</li></ul></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Variation</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Example 1: Taking information from the line graph and placing it into a table.</li><li>Examples 2–6: Answering questions relating to the information in the graph and table.</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Check the Curriculum</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">National Curriculum</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Plan how to collect primary data or how to use provided data, including identifying the variables of interest and, for provided data:</li><li>identifying who the data was collected from</li><li>identifying the original investigator’s purpose for collecting the data</li><li>deciding if the source is reliable (e.g., by checking if survey questions appear to be biased towards a particular point of view)&quot;.</li></ul></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Content Priority</th><td class=\"lt-td\">This is a key lesson.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Outcomes</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>This is a key lesson which supports a number of learning outcomes, including:</li><li>● Students read, interpret and complete information in a table.</li><li>● Students read and interpret a line graph.</li><li>● Students solve problems using information from tables and line graphs.</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Lesson 6: Reading Line Graphs":{"label":"Lesson 6: Reading Line Graphs","content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson Type</th><td class=\"lt-td\">COMBINED</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Textbook Page(s)</th><td class=\"lt-td\">193 - 199</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson Objective</th><td class=\"lt-td\">To be able to read and interpret information presented on a line graph, where the data is represented by more than one line.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Lesson Approach</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>To begin this lesson, show students the Explore task and allow them time to talk about what the table tells them. Ask them what we can say about the monthly sales of each of the different comics. Allow them time to study the information and ask them if they would be able to construct a line graph that represents all the data. Why might a line graph be useful here? How many lines would they need? What would each axis stand for? Tell students that the axes would be the months of the year and the number of comic sales. Is this correct? Would this be helpful? How would we represent each comic and the number of sales? Allow students to discuss this. Then display Master 1 to show what the line graph should look like. Work through the rest of Master 1 and then Masters 2–4 with the class. What does it mean when a line is horizontal on the graph? Is this information easier to see on the line graph or in the table? During Guided Practice, students are reading and interpreting the information presented in the line graph.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-green\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Blank graph with pre-constructed x and y axis, labelled with the correct information from Explore</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-gold\">Maths Language &amp; Variation</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Maths Language</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Students confuse one line for the other when there is more than one set of data.</li></ul></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Variation</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Students can turn a table of information into a line graph with more than one data set (more than one line).</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assess Learning</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-data\"><thead><tr><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Misconceptions</th><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Non-Negotiable</th><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Formative Assessment</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">Students confuse one line for the other when there is more than one set of data.</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can turn a table of information into a line graph with more than one data set (more than one line).</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can read basic information from a line graph. Students can put the information from a line graph into a table.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can make inferences from the information shown on a line graph.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can see the data as cumulative and independent.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can interpret the relationship between two data sets.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Check the Curriculum</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">National Curriculum</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Answer the investigative question, comparing findings with initial conjectures or assertions and their existing knowledge of the world.</li></ul></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Content Priority</th><td class=\"lt-td\">This lesson can be combined with Lesson 7. \n\nLessons 6 and 7 are consolidation lessons and therefore can be combined or omitted if time is an issue.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Outcomes</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>This lesson supports a number of learning outcomes, including:</li><li>● Students read and interpret a line graph.</li><li>● Students solve problems using information from tables and line graphs.</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Lesson 7: Reading Line Graphs":{"label":"Lesson 7: Reading Line Graphs","content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson Type</th><td class=\"lt-td\">COMBINED</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Textbook Page(s)</th><td class=\"lt-td\">200 - 202</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson Objective</th><td class=\"lt-td\">To be able to read and interpret information presented in a table and turn it into a line graph. To be able to determine the relationships between data sets.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Lesson Approach</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>To begin this lesson, show students the Explore task and ask them to discuss what is being shown in the table. Ask students if they are able to create a line graph to represent the information shown. Give them time to discuss and then construct the graph. Then display the graph in Master 1 to show them what it should look like. Work through Masters 2 and 3 for students to answer the questions to analyse the data shown in the line graph. During Guided Practice, students are analysing the weekly growth of a plant from a line graph. They are also required to read specific information from the graph to answer the questions.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-green\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Blank graph with pre-constructed x and y axis, labelled with the correct information from Explore</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assess Learning</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-data\"><thead><tr><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Misconceptions</th><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Non-Negotiable</th><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Formative Assessment</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">relevant data on.</td><td class=\"lt-td\">into a line graph.</td><td class=\"lt-td\">a line graph. Students can put the information from a line graph into a table. Students can make inferences from the information shown on a line graph. Students can see the data as cumulative and independent. Students can interpret the relationship between two data sets. Students can create line graphs from a set of data.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Check the Curriculum</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">National Curriculum</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Plan how to collect primary data or how to use provided data, including identifying the variables of interest and, for provided data: ● identifying who the data was collected from</li><li>- identifying the original investigator’s purpose for collecting the data</li><li>- deciding if the source is reliable (e.g., by checking if survey questions appear to be biased towards a particular point of view).</li><li>Collect primary data and check for errors, and provide information about variables in secondary data (e.g., how data was collected for them and possible outcomes for them).</li></ul></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Content Priority</th><td class=\"lt-td\">This lesson can be combined with Lesson 6. \n\nLessons 6 and 7 are consolidation lessons and therefore can be combined or omitted if time is an issue.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Outcomes</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>This lesson supports a number of learning outcomes, including:</li><li>Students read and interpret a line graph.</li><li>Students solve problems using information from tables and line graphs.</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Lesson 8: Chapter Consolidation":{"label":"Lesson 8: Chapter Consolidation","content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Textbook Page(s)</th><td class=\"lt-td\">203 - 204</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson Objective</th><td class=\"lt-td\">To be able to apply knowledge of line graphs to solve problems.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Lesson Approach</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students use the given information to draw a line graph.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension &amp; Reflection</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Mind Challenge</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Students use the given information to draw a line graph.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-amber\"><th class=\"lt-th\">My Maths Journal</th><td class=\"lt-td\">This journal task may take more than one lesson and could be completed in pairs or groups. Explain to students that they will be carrying out their own statistical investigation, requiring them to collect, record, and present the findings of their chosen investigative question. Discuss the importance of choosing a topic and creating an investigative question that can be answered with data, and how data-collection or survey questions need to be consistent. It may help to show students models of tables, surveys, and questionnaires. Once students have gathered their data, they decide the most appropriate way to display it (drawn by hand or created online) to help answer their investigative question, then discuss describing and analysing their data.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"}}},"Chapter 6: Fractions":{"label":"Lesson","children":{"Chapter Overview":{"label":"Chapter Overview","content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Chapter Overview</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This chapter develops students' ability to handle more diverse problems involving fractions, including dividing and multiplying fractions by whole numbers. To begin the chapter, students divide whole numbers by whole numbers, giving rise to fractions. Students then show improper fractions and mixed numbers using pictures. \n\nAs they progress through the unit, they find equivalent fractions, compare and order fractions and utilise the number bond strategy, known as number pairs, in their work with fractions. Next, students review adding fractions, with a focus on fractions with different denominators and fractions that create improper fractions and mixed numbers. \n\nThen they subtract fractions that are different, finding common denominators and subtracting mixed numbers and improper fractions. At the end of the chapter, students begin to multiply fractions by whole numbers and multiply mixed numbers by whole numbers. The final lesson involves solving word problems that require multiple steps and bar model representations.</p></div></div>"},"Lesson 1: Dividing to Make Fractions":{"label":"Lesson 1: Dividing to Make Fractions","content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson Type</th><td class=\"lt-td\">KEY LESSON</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Textbook Page(s)</th><td class=\"lt-td\">206 - 208</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson Objective</th><td class=\"lt-td\">To be able to divide whole numbers to create fractions; to be able to create mixed numbers and improper fractions when dividing whole numbers.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Lesson Approach</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>To begin this lesson, show students the Explore task and ask them to describe how the pani popo have been arranged into the 4 boxes. How many pani popo are there? (8) How many boxes? (4) How many pani popo are in each box? (2) How can we show this as a fraction? What information must be included? Is the number of boxes here important? What about the number of pani popo? Can we show this by using a picture? What about using numbers? Allow students time to solve this problem using concrete materials, and then work through Master 1 and 2 together. Allow students time to discuss the problem in Master 3 and then ask them if they think it is possible share 7 pani popo equally between 4 plates. How can we be sure? What would that look like using pictures and numbers? Ask students what types of fractions they can create when dividing 7 pani popo among 4 plates. Tell them your friend says she can create improper fractions. What does that mean? What is improper about them? What is it if it is not an improper fraction? Work through Master 3 and 4 and discuss the different methods. During Guided Practice, students are creating improper fractions and mixed numbers by dividing.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-green\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Coloured paper strips</li><li>Paper circles</li><li>Fraction wall</li><li>Cubes/counters</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-gold\">Maths Language &amp; Variation</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Maths Language</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>quarters</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assess Learning</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-data\"><thead><tr><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Misconceptions</th><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Non-Negotiable</th><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Formative Assessment</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">Students do not share items equally. Students divide items equally until there is a remainder and this is left out.</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can divide each whole item by the number of people/plates it is being shared with to create a fraction.</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can divide an even number of objects equally between 2. Students can divide an even number of objects equally among 10.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can divide an even number of objects equally among 4. Students can divide and share concrete materials. Students can represent fractions as a bar model.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can represent fractions in pictorial form.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can use the terms 'numerator' and 'denominator'. Students can divide one object equally among more than one person.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Check the Curriculum</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">National Curriculum</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Convert between mixed numbers and improper fractions with denominators of up to 10.</li><li>Find a fraction of a whole number, using multiplication and division facts and where the answer is a whole number (e.g., 2/3 of 24).</li></ul></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Content Priority</th><td class=\"lt-td\">This is a key lesson.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Outcomes</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>This is a key lesson which supports a number of learning outcomes, including:</li><li>● Students explain and represent how to divide 1 into different amounts of equal parts.</li><li>Students recognise mixed numbers and improper fractions.</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Lesson 2: Writing Improper Fractions and Mixed Numbers":{"label":"Lesson 2: Writing Improper Fractions and Mixed Numbers","content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson Type</th><td class=\"lt-td\">KEY LESSON</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Textbook Page(s)</th><td class=\"lt-td\">209 - 212</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson Objective</th><td class=\"lt-td\">To be able to write improper fractions and mixed numbers using a number line and pictorial methods.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Lesson Approach</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>To begin this lesson, show students the Explore task and ask them to work with a partner to label the number line. Give them time to explore together. Ask them if they thought the numbers were already in order. If not, why not? Tell them your friend says that there is only one number larger than 1 as there is only one number with a 1 before the fraction. Is that true? Refer to Master 1 and check students' understanding of proper fractions. Refer to Master 2 and check students' understanding of 5/5 = 1 and 10/5 = 2 Refer to Master 3: Tell the students that you think it is not possible to turn 1 2/5 into fifths only. Is this true? Is it possible to turn a mixed number into an improper fraction? What do we need to do? Allow students to discuss this. Can we draw a picture of 1 2/5? Would that be helpful in turning it into an improper fraction? Refer to Master 4: Relate the numbers to the bar models/fraction walls and to the number line. Refer to Master 5: Can students notice the multiples of 5 in the numerators when converting whole numbers into improper fractions? During Guided Practice, students are turning mixed numbers and whole numbers into improper fractions and vice versa, using the method they are most comfortable with.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-green\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Coloured paper strips</li><li>Fraction wall</li><li>Blank paper number lines</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-gold\">Maths Language &amp; Variation</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Variation</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Example 1: Placing fractions on a number line.</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assess Learning</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-data\"><thead><tr><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Misconceptions</th><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Non-Negotiable</th><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Formative Assessment</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">Students confuse the terms 'denominator', 'numerator', 'mixed numbers' and 'improper fractions'.</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can convert mixed numbers into improper fractions, and vice versa, using multiplication and division.</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can represent mixed numbers and improper fractions using bar models or other pictures.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">Students add the whole number to the denominator and numerator to get the improper fraction, and vice versa.</td><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can place fractions and mixed numbers on a number line.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">Students believe that fifths are bigger than quarters becaue 5 is bigger than 4.</td><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\"></td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Check the Curriculum</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">National Curriculum</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Convert between mixed numbers and improper fractions with denominators of up to 10.</li></ul></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Content Priority</th><td class=\"lt-td\">This is a key lesson.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Outcomes</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>This is a key lesson which supports a number of learning outcomes, including:</li><li>Students explain and represent how to divide 1 into different amounts of equal parts.</li><li>Students recognise mixed numbers and improper fractions.</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Lesson 3: Finding Equivalent Fractions":{"label":"Lesson 3: Finding Equivalent Fractions","content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson Type</th><td class=\"lt-td\">KEY LESSON</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Textbook Page(s)</th><td class=\"lt-td\">213 - 216</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson Objective</th><td class=\"lt-td\">To be able to find equivalent fractions using pictorial methods.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Lesson Approach</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>To begin this lesson, show students the Explore task. Ask students to think about the different ways 1/2 of the plot could be divided into smaller plots. Allow them to use paper to fold or draw on to recreate the vegetable plot in the picture, and give them time to make their own fractions. Refer to Master 1. Ask the students if the representation looks like theirs.\nWhy/Why not? Is it possible to cut half of the plot into smaller pieces, but\nnot the other half? Allow students to discuss what needs to happen. Agree\nthat when 1/2 is divided into 2 equal parts, 1/2 becomes 2/4. 1/2 and 2/4\nare equivalent fractions. Move to Master 2. What if we divide half of the plot into 3 equal pieces?\nWhat would we have? Why is this the case?\nMonitor as students work on Master 3 where they are asked to divide the\nwhole plot to make hundredths. How many hundredths would be in half of\nthe plot? How can we check?\nIf the size of the plot does not change, then what can we say about 1/2\nand 4/8? What about 1/2 and 5/10? Are there any other things we can say\nabout these fractions? Can we say that 4/8 and 50/100 are equivalent? Is\nthis possible? How can we check?\nUse Master 4 to further clarify and explain how to find equivalent fractions.\nRefer to Master 5 to show the equivalence on the number line. Students\nshould be encouraged to make the link between the equivalent fractions,\nnoticing that the numerator and denominator have the same proportional\nrelationship.\nDuring Guided Practice, students are finding equivalent fractions, where\nthe new denominator and numerator are multiples of the original.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-green\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Coloured paper strips</li><li>Paper squares</li><li>Fraction wall</li><li>Blank paper number lines</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-gold\">Maths Language &amp; Variation</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Maths Language</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Students divide part of the pictorial representation, but don't divide ALL parts.</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assess Learning</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-data\"><thead><tr><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Misconceptions</th><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Non-Negotiable</th><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Formative Assessment</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">plot into more pieces', and then use division to attempt this.</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can simplify fractions using division.</td><td class=\"lt-td\">2-digit numbers by a single digit. Students can divide single-digit and</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">Students divide part of the pictorial representation, but don't divide ALL parts.</td><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\">2-digit numbers by a single digit. Students can represent mixed numbers and improper fractions using bar models or other pictures.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can place fractions and mixed numbers on a number line.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can read a fraction written in words and numbers.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can recognise a fraction represented as a picture.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can find equivalent fractions.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Check the Curriculum</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">National Curriculum</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>For fractions with denominators of 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 8, 10, 12, or 100:</li><li>● compare and order the fractions ● identify when two fractions are equivalent.</li></ul></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Content Priority</th><td class=\"lt-td\">This is a key lesson.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Outcomes</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>This is a key lesson which supports a number of learning outcomes, including:</li><li>Students correctly use the language of equivalent fractions.</li><li>Students explain the vertical relationship between numerators and denominators within equivalent fractions (1/5, 1/3 and equivalent).</li><li>Students use their knowledge of equivalent fractions to solve problems.</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Lesson 4: Comparing and Ordering Fractions":{"label":"Lesson 4: Comparing and Ordering Fractions","content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson Type</th><td class=\"lt-td\">KEY LESSON</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Textbook Page(s)</th><td class=\"lt-td\">217 - 219</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson Objective</th><td class=\"lt-td\">To be able to compare and order fractions using the pictorial method.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Lesson Approach</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>To begin this lesson, show students the Explore task and ask them if they are able to draw or fold paper to show the amount of paper Charles and Tama each took. Give them some time to determine how this should look. Tell students that your friend thinks Tama uses 4/6 of the original paper. Is this true? How can we check? Review together the fraction strips in Master 1 and 2 to confirm that in order to compare the two amounts of paper used, the whole needs to be divided into 9 and that Tama uses 4/9 of the original paper. Ask students to compare 1/3 and 4/9. Which is greater? How can we tell? Ask students if they know how to draw 1/2, 1/3 and 5/12 to show which is the biggest. What do we need to ensure we do if we are going to compare fractions? Does the size of the whole matter? What if we use a big piece of paper to show 1/2, but a small piece of paper to show 3/4? Would that make a difference? How can we check? Which is the correct method? Use the images and methods from Master 3 to compare the fractions. Emphasise the importance of using the same size for the whole when comparing fractions. During Guided Practice, students are comparing and ordering fractions using the pictorial method.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-green\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Coloured paper strips</li><li>Paper circles</li><li>Fraction wall</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-gold\">Maths Language &amp; Variation</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Maths Language</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>It is important students are able to understand and use the</li><li>following mathematical terms and phrases in this lesson:</li></ul></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Variation</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Example 1: Using &gt;, &lt; or = to compare fractions using</li><li>pre-designed pictorial support.</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assess Learning</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-data\"><thead><tr><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Misconceptions</th><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Non-Negotiable</th><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Formative Assessment</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">Students rename the remaining pieces of the fraction and forget about the original number of pieces (for example, dividing 3 pieces into 6 pieces may make some students think the new fraction is sixths).</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can compare fractions in pictorial format from pre-designed pictures and their own creations.</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can represent fractions as a picture. Students can represent fractions as a bar model.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">Students divide part of the pictorial representation, but don't divide ALL parts.</td><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can divide a fraction into its equivalence. Students can compare fractions using equivalent fractions.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can arrange fractions from smallest to greatest and vice versa.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Check the Curriculum</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">National Curriculum</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>For fractions with denominators of 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 8, 10, 12, or 100:</li><li>compare and order the fractions</li><li>identify when two fractions are equivalent.</li></ul></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Content Priority</th><td class=\"lt-td\">This is a key lesson.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Outcomes</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>This is a key lesson which supports a number of learning outcomes, including:</li><li>Students use representations to describe and compare two fractions.</li><li>Students correctly use the language of equivalent fractions.</li><li>Students use their knowledge of equivalent fractions to solve problems.</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Lesson 5: Comparing and Ordering Improper Fractions":{"label":"Lesson 5: Comparing and Ordering Improper Fractions","content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson Type</th><td class=\"lt-td\">KEY LESSON</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Textbook Page(s)</th><td class=\"lt-td\">220 - 222</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson Objective</th><td class=\"lt-td\">To be able to compare and order improper fractions using the pictorial method.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Lesson Approach</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>To begin this lesson, show students the Explore task. Ask them if they are able to recreate the task by folding paper or using pictures. Allow students time to discuss their ideas and begin folding or drawing. Refer to Master 1: Tell them that Tama took 12 pieces, so he took more chocolate. Is this correct? Allow students to prove that this is incorrect. How much did Tama actually take? Ask them to compare the two amounts to see which is the largest. Tell students that because 1/5 is bigger than 1/10, 8/5 must be the largest. Is this true? How can we be sure? If this is true, what about 8/5 and 12/10? Is it true that because the numerator and the denominator are smaller in 8/5, then it must be the smaller fraction? How can we prove that this is incorrect? What is the relationship between the digit in the denominator's place and the size of the pieces? If students struggle with this, tell them that as the denominator gets smaller, the pieces get bigger. Is this true? Is it always true? How can we be sure? Work through Master 2 and 3 using the images to illustrate how to compare and order improper fractions. During Guided Practice, students are comparing fractions using a method they are comfortable with. They are also arranging fractions from smallest to greatest.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-green\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Coloured paper strips</li><li>Fraction wall</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-gold\">Maths Language &amp; Variation</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Maths Language</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>It is important students are able to understand and use the</li><li>following mathematical terms and phrases in this lesson:</li><li>common denominator</li></ul></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Variation</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Example 1: Comparing fractions using &gt; or &lt; with pictorial</li><li>support.</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Check the Curriculum</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">National Curriculum</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>For fractions with denominators of 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 8, 10, 12, or 100:</li><li>compare and order the fractions</li><li>identify when two fractions are equivalent.</li></ul></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Content Priority</th><td class=\"lt-td\">This is a key lesson.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Outcomes</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>This is a key lesson which supports a number of learning outcomes, including:</li><li>Students use representations to describe and compare two fractions.</li><li>Students correctly use the language of equivalent fractions.</li><li>Students use their knowledge of equivalent fractions to solve problems.</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Lesson 6: Comparing and Ordering Mixed Numbers":{"label":"Lesson 6: Comparing and Ordering Mixed Numbers","content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson Type</th><td class=\"lt-td\">KEY LESSON</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Textbook Page(s)</th><td class=\"lt-td\">223 - 224</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Lesson Approach</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>To begin this lesson, show students the Explore task. Allow them time to discuss the different amounts and how they can represent these amounts in different ways. Tell them that they can use bar models to show the common denominators, where one fraction is\nalready the common denominator for all\nfractions in the question. fractions and find out which is the smallest amount. What does that mean?\nHow can mixed numbers be represented in bar models? Can this be\ndone? What has to be the same about the models? What will be different?\nGive them time to work on this with their partner. Once students have determined how to compare the different amounts,\nallow them time to work out which recipe contains the least amount of flour.\nWork through Master 1 to check their answers and then Master 2 to find\nwhich is more — 2 3/4 or 2 5/8.\nNow ask students to look at Master 3. What can we do to make it easier to\ncompare these amounts? Allow students to discuss and solve the problem.\nAsk them if they are able to arrange the fractions using the symbols &gt; and\n&lt;. Is this possible? Allow some time for students to order the fractions and\nthen discuss as a class to check students' solutions and understanding.\nDuring Guided Practice, students are identifying greater and smaller\nfractions. The denominators can all be easily changed, but their placement\nvaries.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-green\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Coloured paper strips ● Fraction wall</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-gold\">Maths Language &amp; Variation</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Maths Language</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>It is important students are able to understand and use the following mathematical terms and phrases in this lesson:</li><li>common denominator</li><li>equal</li></ul></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Variation</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Example 1: Comparing mixed numbers using &gt; or &lt;. Example 2: Ordering three mixed numbers, from smallest to greatest.</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assess Learning</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-data\"><thead><tr><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Misconceptions</th><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Non-Negotiable</th><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Formative Assessment</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">Students believe that the bigger the denominator, the bigger the fraction (for example, sixths are bigger than fifths).</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can compare fractions in pictorial format from pre-designed pictures and their own creations.</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can read mixed numbers and improper fractions. Students can represent mixed numbers and improper fractions as a picture.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can represent mixed numbers and improper fractions as a bar model.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can compare fractions using equivalent fractions.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can arrange fractions from smallest to greatest and vice versa.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Check the Curriculum</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">National Curriculum</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>For fractions with denominators of 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 8, 10, 12, or 100:</li><li>compare and order the fractions ● identify when two fractions are equivalent.</li><li>Convert between mixed numbers and improper fractions with denominators of up to 10.</li></ul></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Content Priority</th><td class=\"lt-td\">This is a key lesson.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Outcomes</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>This is a key lesson which supports a number of learning outcomes, including: ● Students use representations to describe and compare two fractions. ● Students use their knowledge of equivalent fractions to solve problems.</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Lesson 7: Making Number Pairs":{"label":"Lesson 7: Making Number Pairs","content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson Type</th><td class=\"lt-td\">KEY LESSON</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Textbook Page(s)</th><td class=\"lt-td\">225 - 227</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Lesson Approach</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>To begin this lesson, show students the Explore task and allow them to discuss the important information in the question. Ask them if they are able to use bar models to show what the question is suggesting. How can we denominators. show 1 whole – 1/8? What do we need to do to the whole amount? Once\nstudents have been able to represent the lasagne appropriately, ask them\nto think about all the different ways that Tama and Ruby can share 7/8.\nAllow students some time to work this out, using pictures and concrete\nmaterials finding multiple suggestions.\nUse Master to show students some of the ways to form the number pairs\nof 7/8. Tell students that one way to share 7/8 is using sixteenths. Is this\npossible? Can eighths be converted into sixteenths? What is the maximum\nnumber of sixteenths that one person could get from 7/8? Allow students to\ndiscuss and then prove their answers. What about quarters? Give students\ntime to discuss the different ways that 7/8 can be shared between Tama\nand Ruby. How many number pairs can be made? In Master 3 students\ncan explore the numerous possible number pairs.\nDuring Guided Practice, students are using number pairs to create\nfractions.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-green\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Coloured paper strips</li><li>Fraction wall</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-gold\">Maths Language &amp; Variation</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Maths Language</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>fractions</li><li>share equally</li><li>numerator</li><li>denominator</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assess Learning</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-data\"><thead><tr><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Misconceptions</th><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Non-Negotiable</th><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Formative Assessment</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">Students believe the only way to make number pairs is by using fractions that have the same denominator.</td><td class=\"lt-td\">To make number pairs (number bonds) with fractions that have different denominators.</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can read mixed numbers and improper fractions. Students can represent mixed numbers and improper fractions as a picture.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">Check the curriculum</td><td class=\"lt-td\">denominators to make up to one whole (e.g.,</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can represent mixed numbers and improper fractions as a bar model.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">National Curriculum Add and subtract fractions with the same</td><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can compare fractions using equivalent fractions.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can arrange fractions from smallest to greatest and vice versa.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can use bar models to make pairs of fractions with different denominators from a given fraction.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\">3/8 + 3/8 + 2/8 = 8/8 = 1).</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Check the Curriculum</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">National Curriculum</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Add and subtract fractions with the same denominators to make up to one whole (e.g., 3/8 + 3/8 + 2/8 = 8/8 = 1).</li><li>Add and subtract fractions with the same or related denominators (e.g., 1/4 + 1/8).</li></ul></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Content Priority</th><td class=\"lt-td\">This is a key lesson.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Outcomes</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>This is a key lesson which supports this learning outcome:</li><li>● Students use their knowledge of equivalent fractions to solve problems.</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Lesson 8: Adding Fractions":{"label":"Lesson 8: Adding Fractions","content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson Type</th><td class=\"lt-td\">KEY LESSON</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Textbook Page(s)</th><td class=\"lt-td\">228 - 230</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson Objective</th><td class=\"lt-td\">To be able to add unlike fractions by finding a|To begin this lesson, show students the Explore task and ask them to&lt;br&gt;identify the important information.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Lesson Approach</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>To be able to add unlike fractions by finding a To begin this lesson, show students the Explore task and ask them to\nidentify the important information. Ask students if they can use counters or common denominator using pictorial methods. draw a picture to represent the box of doughnuts, and colour in what the\ntwo friends said they took. Would this be helpful? Would it allow us to see\nhow much was eaten exactly? Allow students to discuss and solve with the\nir partner.\nRefer to Master 1 &amp; 2 and discuss their methods, check students'\nunderstanding and clarify any misconceptions.\nRefer to Master 3. Ask the students if they can draw a box of doughnuts\nagain with 12 doughnuts in total. Tell them that three friends share it. The\nfirst friend eats 1/2 of the box, the second eats 1/6 and the third eats 3/12.\nIs this possible? Can we show this in our picture? Give them time to\ndiscuss and represent the amounts. Can we shade in sixths on a picture\nthat is divided into twelfths? What do we need to do?\nDuring Guided Practice, students are adding like and unlike fractions,\nwhere the common denominator is in the group of fractions.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-green\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Coloured paper strips</li><li>Fraction wall</li><li>Counters</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-gold\">Maths Language &amp; Variation</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Maths Language</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>It is important students are able to understand and use the following mathematical terms in this lesson:</li><li>denominator</li><li>common denominator</li><li>add</li></ul></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Variation</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Example 1: Adding two fractions with pictorial support; a) same denominator, b) one denominator is a multiple of the other</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Check the Curriculum</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">National Curriculum</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Add and subtract fractions with the same or related denominators (e.g., 1/4 + 1/8).</li></ul></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Content Priority</th><td class=\"lt-td\">This is a key lesson.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Outcomes</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>This is a key lesson which supports this learning outcome: ● Students use their knowledge of equivalent fractions to solve problems.</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Lesson 9: Adding Fractions":{"label":"Lesson 9: Adding Fractions","content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson Type</th><td class=\"lt-td\">KEY LESSON</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Textbook Page(s)</th><td class=\"lt-td\">231 - 232</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson Objective</th><td class=\"lt-td\">To be able to add unlike fractions by finding a|To begin this lesson, display the Explore task and ask students if they can&lt;br&gt;show how much Holly drinks in total.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Lesson Approach</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>To be able to add unlike fractions by finding a To begin this lesson, display the Explore task and ask students if they can\nshow how much Holly drinks in total. Allow students time to work on this common denominator using pictorial methods. and then discuss their methods as a class. Refer to Master 1: Is it possible to show 1/2 and 1/10 of two different jugs? How can we do this? Allow students time to determine what this might look like with their partner. Suggest to them that to add unlike fractions, the fractions must have the same name, so they need to turn tenths into halves. Is this correct? Is it possible to turn 1/10 into halves? Why, or why not? Which fraction should we change? Refer to Master 2: Next, tell students that one of your friends has said that Holly has drunk 6/10 l, but another friend says that she drank 3/5 l. Who is correct? Remind students that they should simplify fractions where possible. Can fifths become halves or tenths? Ask students to discuss how they will add the three fractions together and allow them time to work on it. During Guided Practice, students are adding like and unlike fractions. They are also exposed to the term 'sum'.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-green\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Coloured paper strips</li><li>Fraction wall</li><li>Maths Language Variation It is important students are able to understand and use the Example 1: Adding two fractions where both denominators are following mathematical terms in this lesson: the same ● simplify ● fraction Examples 2 &amp; 3: Adding two fractions where the first fraction</li><li>Examples 2 &amp; 3: Adding two fractions where the first fraction has the common denominator</li><li>denominator ● equal</li><li>common denominator</li><li>sum Example 4: Adding two fractions where the second fraction has</li><li>the common denominator</li><li>Example 5: Adding three fractions where one denominator is a</li><li>multiple of the other two denominators</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assess Learning</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-data\"><thead><tr><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Misconceptions</th><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Non-Negotiable</th><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Formative Assessment</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">Students add both the numerators and denominators to create a new fraction.</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can add fractions with the same denominator accurately.</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can divide and share concrete materials.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">Students do not find a common denominator when adding unlike fractions.</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can find a common denominator between two fractions when one of the fractions has the common denominator.</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can represent fractions as a bar model. Students can use the terms 'numerator' and 'denominator'. Students can read a fraction written in words and numbers. Students can recognise a fraction represented as a picture. Students can add fractions with the same denominator.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Check the Curriculum</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">National Curriculum</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Add and subtract fractions with the same or related denominators (e.g., 1/4 + 1/8).</li></ul></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Content Priority</th><td class=\"lt-td\">This is a key lesson.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Outcomes</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>This is a key lesson which supports this learning outcome:</li><li>● Students use their knowledge of equivalent fractions to solve problems.</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Lesson 10: Adding Fractions":{"label":"Lesson 10: Adding Fractions","content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson Type</th><td class=\"lt-td\">KEY LESSON</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Textbook Page(s)</th><td class=\"lt-td\">233 - 235</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Lesson Approach</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>To begin this lesson, show students the Explore task and ask them if they are able to identify the key information. What unit of measurement is being used? What solutions can we come up with? Give time for students to sum is greater than 1, creating mixed numbers think and work together. or improper fractions. Refer to Master 1: Ask students if they have represented both fractions using a bar model. Can you see 1/4 inside of 7/8 in the bar models you have drawn? How many eighths are equal to 1/4? Is that helpful when adding them together? Refer to Master 2: Ask students if they are able to show you the solution using an improper fraction and a mixed number. What is 9/8 as a mixed number? Allow them to discuss and solve this. Refer to Master 3: What method did Oak use During Guided Practice, students are using number pairs and addition to find the sum of two unlike fractions, where the sum is an improper fraction or mixed number.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-green\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Coloured paper strips ● Fraction wall</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-gold\">Maths Language &amp; Variation</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Maths Language</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>common denominator</li><li>add</li><li>sum</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assess Learning</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-data\"><thead><tr><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Misconceptions</th><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Non-Negotiable</th><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Formative Assessment</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">Students add both the numerators and denominators to create a new fraction. Students do not find a common denominator when adding unlike fractions.</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can find a common denominator between two fractions, when one of the fractions has the common denominator. Students can find an improper fraction when adding fractions.</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can divide and share concrete materials. Students can represent fractions as a bar model. Students can use the terms 'numerator' and 'denominator'. Students can read a fraction written in words and numbers. Students can recognise a fraction represented as a picture. Students can add fractions with the same denominator. Students can find common denominators.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Check the Curriculum</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">National Curriculum</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Add and subtract fractions with the same or related denominators (e.g., 1/4 + 1/8).</li></ul></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Content Priority</th><td class=\"lt-td\">This is a key lesson.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Outcomes</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>This is a key lesson which supports this learning outcome:</li><li>● Students use their knowledge of equivalent fractions to solve problems.</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Lesson 11: Adding Fractions":{"label":"Lesson 11: Adding Fractions","content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson Type</th><td class=\"lt-td\">KEY LESSON</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Textbook Page(s)</th><td class=\"lt-td\">236 - 238</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson Objective</th><td class=\"lt-td\">To be able to add unlike fractions, which create|To begin this lesson, show students the Explore task and ask them to&lt;br&gt;identify the important information.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Lesson Approach</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>To be able to add unlike fractions, which create To begin this lesson, show students the Explore task and ask them to\nidentify the important information. What do we know? What amount are we improper fractions, and mixed numbers that starting with? What amount are we adding? Allow students to discuss this give rise to simplification. and attempt to represent this information using bar models. Encourage multiple methods. Discuss their ideas as a class. Refer to Master 1: Is this problem like others we have seen? How can we add tenths and fifths? Which fraction should we change? Can we simplify our fraction. Refer to Master 2: Work through this question together. During Guided Practice, students are adding unlike fractions that give rise to simplification.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-green\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Coloured paper strips</li><li>Fraction wall</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-gold\">Maths Language &amp; Variation</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Maths Language</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Students have difficulties drawing bar models for 4/5 of a kilogram and 7/10 a kilogram.</li></ul></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Variation</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Students can find a common denominator between two fractions, when one of the fractions has the common denominator.</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assess Learning</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-data\"><thead><tr><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Misconceptions</th><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Non-Negotiable</th><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Formative Assessment</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">Students have difficulties drawing bar models for 4/5 of a kilogram and 7/10 a kilogram.</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can find a common denominator between two fractions, when one of the fractions has the common denominator.</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can divide and share concrete materials. Students can represent fractions as a bar model.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can use the terms 'numerator' and 'denominator'.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can read a fraction written in words and numbers. Students can recognise a fraction represented as a picture. Students can add fractions with the same denominator.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can find common denominators. Students can make equivalent fractions. Students can use a times table chart to find common multiples.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Check the Curriculum</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">National Curriculum</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Add and subtract fractions with the same or related denominators (e.g., 1/4 + 1/8).</li></ul></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Content Priority</th><td class=\"lt-td\">This is a key lesson.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Outcomes</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>This is a key lesson which supports this learning outcome: ● Students use their knowledge of equivalent fractions to solve problems.</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Lesson 12: Subtracting Fractions":{"label":"Lesson 12: Subtracting Fractions","content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson Type</th><td class=\"lt-td\">KEY LESSON</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Textbook Page(s)</th><td class=\"lt-td\">239 - 241</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson Objective</th><td class=\"lt-td\">To be able to subtract fractions with different denominators; to be able to subtract fractions from whole numbers.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Lesson Approach</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>To begin this lesson, show students the Explore task and ask them how we can represent the information. How many pieces of ribbon are there to cut? What is difficult about subtracting these two amounts? Allow students time to discuss this and attempt to solve the problem with their partner. Suggest to them that they can subtract 1/6 from 1 whole because 6 sixths and 1 whole are the same amount; but you are confused about what to do when you have 5/6 left over and need to subtract 5/12. Can they help you? What do we need to do? Give students time to solve this, discuss their ideas as a class and then work through Master 1. Refer to Master 2: Ask the class how we can solve a similar problem but starting with 2 as the whole number rather than 1. How can we subtract 2/3 from 2? Is it possible? How can we represent it using pictures? What about using numbers? Allow students time to discuss and represent this idea with their partner. Tell them that as they are taking away less than 1 from 2, they will have 1 and something left over. Is this true? What would this look like in pictures? Refer to Master 3: Ask students if they are able to find the difference between two fractions, such as 5/8 and 1/4. Is it possible to do this? What do we have to do first? Do they have the same denominator? Is there a way to solve this using pictures before we use numbers? What might that look like? Work through Master 3 to illustrate this and present the formal way of recording solutions. During Guided Practice, students are subtracting unlike fractions from one another, where only one fraction needs to change to have the same denominator as the other.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-green\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Coloured paper strips</li><li>Fraction wall</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-gold\">Maths Language &amp; Variation</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Maths Language</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>It is important students are able to understand and use the following mathematical terms in this lesson:</li><li>find the difference</li><li>subtract</li><li>simplify</li><li>fraction</li><li>improper fraction</li><li>mixed number</li><li>denominator</li><li>equal</li><li>common denominator</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Check the Curriculum</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">National Curriculum</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Add and subtract fractions with the same or related denominators (e.g., 1/4 + 1/8).</li></ul></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Content Priority</th><td class=\"lt-td\">This is a key lesson.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Outcomes</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>This is a key lesson which supports this learning outcome:</li><li>● Students use their knowledge of equivalent fractions to solve problems.</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Lesson 13: Subtracting Fractions":{"label":"Lesson 13: Subtracting Fractions","content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson Type</th><td class=\"lt-td\">KEY LESSON</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Textbook Page(s)</th><td class=\"lt-td\">242 - 244</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Lesson Approach</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>To begin this lesson, show students the Explore task and ask them to discuss the key information in the problem. Give them time to draw and label bars to represent the problem before working out the answer. use bar models as a key strategy for subtracting\nfractions. What do we know about the amount of pizza that Hannah and Ravi ate.\nWho ate more? By how much? Ask students what they should check next\nto find the difference in the amount of pizza the two friends ate. How can\nwe work with two different fractions on one bar? Is this possible? What\nwould be more helpful for us?\nUse Master 1 to work through, and solve, the problem, checking the 4\nsteps with them. Give students time to work on subtracting 5/12 from 3/4\nbefore working through Master 2 to show the solution.\nDuring Guided Practice, students are using bar model diagrams to help\nthem visually represent the subtraction of fractions with different\ndenominators. They simplify their answers where necessary.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-green\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Coloured paper strips</li><li>Fraction wall</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-gold\">Maths Language &amp; Variation</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Maths Language</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>It is important students are able to understand and use the</li><li>following mathematical terms in this lesson:</li><li>find the difference</li><li>subtract</li><li>simplify</li><li>fraction</li><li>improper fraction</li><li>mixed number</li></ul></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Variation</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>common denominator; reducing the fraction to its simplest</li><li>form.</li><li>Example 1: Supported pictorially with bar models.</li><li>Example 2: No pictorial support.</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assess Learning</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-data\"><thead><tr><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Misconceptions</th><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Non-Negotiable</th><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Formative Assessment</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">Students subtract both the numerators and denominators to create a new fraction. Students do not find a common denominator.</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can find a common denominator between two fractions, when one of the fractions has the common denominator. Students can subtract fractions when given two parts, or one part and the original amount.</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can represent fractions as a bar model. Students can recognise a fraction represented as a picture. Students can subtract fractions with the same denominator.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can find common denominators.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can make equivalent fractions. Students can use a times table chart to find common multiples.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Check the Curriculum</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">National Curriculum</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Add and subtract fractions with the same or related denominators (e.g., 1/4 + 1/8).</li></ul></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Content Priority</th><td class=\"lt-td\">This is a key lesson.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Outcomes</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>This is a key lesson which supports this learning outcome:</li><li>● Students use their knowledge of equivalent fractions to solve problems.</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Lesson 14: Subtracting Fractions":{"label":"Lesson 14: Subtracting Fractions","content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson Type</th><td class=\"lt-td\">KEY LESSON</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Textbook Page(s)</th><td class=\"lt-td\">245 - 248</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Lesson Approach</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>To begin this lesson, show students the Explore task and ask them how we can attempt to solve the problem. What information do we know? What's numbers from mixed numbers with different\ndenominators. different about this problem to ones you've approached previously? What\ndo you notice about the two masses of strawberries that the families eat?\nAllow students time to discuss and work on this with their partner,\nprompting them if necessary to talk about the mixed numbers.\nGive them time to discuss their approach and ask them if they are able to\nprove their answers by using a bar model or pictures. Is it possible?\nDiscuss their ideas as a class. Work through Master 1–4 to show students\nhow to work with mixed numbers and improper fractions. What is the\ndifference between these methods? What is the same about them?\nDuring Guided Practice, students are finding the difference between two\nmixed numbers.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-green\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Coloured paper strips</li><li>Fraction wall</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-gold\">Maths Language &amp; Variation</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Maths Language</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>It is important students are able to understand and use the</li><li>following mathematical terms in this lesson:</li><li>find the difference</li><li>subtract</li><li>simplify</li><li>fraction</li><li>improper fraction</li><li>mixed number</li><li>denominator</li></ul></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Variation</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Subtracting mixed numbers.</li><li>Example 1: Using two different methods; supported pictorially with bar models</li><li>Example 2: No pictorial support, numbers in abstract form</li><li>Example 3: No pictorial support, word problem</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assess Learning</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-data\"><thead><tr><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Misconceptions</th><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Non-Negotiable</th><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Formative Assessment</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">Students subtract both the numerators and denominators to create a new fraction.</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can find a common denominator between two mixed numbers when one of the mixed numbers has the common denominator.</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can represent fractions as a bar model. Students can recognise a fraction represented as a picture.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can subtract fractions with the same denominator.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can find common denominators.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can make equivalent fractions.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can use a times table chart to find common multiples.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can subtract mixed numbers when their denominator is the same.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Check the Curriculum</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">National Curriculum</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Add and subtract fractions with the same or related denominators (e.g., 1/4 + 1/8).</li></ul></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Content Priority</th><td class=\"lt-td\">This is a key lesson.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Outcomes</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>This is a key lesson which supports this learning outcome: ● Students use their knowledge of equivalent fractions to solve problems.</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Lesson 15: Multiplying Whole Numbers by Proper Fractions":{"label":"Lesson 15: Multiplying Whole Numbers by Proper Fractions","content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson Type</th><td class=\"lt-td\">KEY LESSON</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Textbook Page(s)</th><td class=\"lt-td\">249 - 251</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson Objective</th><td class=\"lt-td\">To begin this lesson, show students the Explore task and ask them to discuss the task with a partner.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Lesson Approach</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Allow students to explore the key To be able to multiply fractions by whole numbers creating other fractions, mixed\nnumbers or improper fractions. information from the problem. How could you represent the information to\nfind out what fraction of a sushi roll Jacob ate? Allow students time to work\non this task with their partner then share their thinking with the class.\nRefer to Master 1: Tell them that multiplication is the same operation as\nrepeated addition. If this is true, then how can we multiply 1/5? Can they\nshow you by using pictures? If we use pictures to find 1/5 times 3, we will\nget 3/15. Is this correct? What is wrong with this answer? Can we prove it\nis wrong?\nRefer to Master 2: Work on this question together and share methods.\nCan we get a mixed number after multiplying a fraction? Can we show that\nby using pictures? Use Master 3 to illustrate this.\nDuring Guided Practice, students are multiplying fractions by whole\nnumbers.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-green\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Coloured paper strips</li><li>Fraction wall</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-gold\">Maths Language &amp; Variation</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Maths Language</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>It is important students are able to understand and use the</li><li>following mathematical terms and phrases in this lesson:</li><li>proper fraction</li><li>improper fraction</li><li>mixed number</li><li>whole number</li></ul></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Variation</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Multiplying fractions by whole numbers.</li><li>Example 1: Supported pictorially with fraction squares. Example 2: Supported pictorially with a bar model.</li><li>Example 3: No pictorial support.</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Check the Curriculum</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">National Curriculum</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Use known multiplication facts to scale a quantity.</li><li>Convert between mixed numbers and improper fractions with denominators of up to 10.</li><li>Find a fraction of a whole number, using multiplication and division facts and where the answer is a whole number (e.g., 2/3 of 24).</li></ul></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Content Priority</th><td class=\"lt-td\">This is a key lesson.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Outcomes</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>This is a key lesson which supports a number of learning outcomes, including:</li><li>● Students explain the relationship between repeated addition of a proper fraction and multiplication of fractions.</li><li>● Students multiply a proper fraction by a whole number.</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Lesson 16: Multiplying Proper Fractions and Whole Numbers":{"label":"Lesson 16: Multiplying Proper Fractions and Whole Numbers","content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson Type</th><td class=\"lt-td\">KEY LESSON</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Textbook Page(s)</th><td class=\"lt-td\">252 - 255</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson Objective</th><td class=\"lt-td\">To begin this lesson, show students the Explore task and ask them how they would represent the word problem.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Lesson Approach</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Ask students to represent the To be able to multiply fractions by whole numbers, where the product is an improper\nfraction or mixed number. problem with a picture, and then allow them time to work out the answer to\nthe question with their partner.\nTell them that when multiplying a fraction, we only multiply the numerator.\nIs this true? Can they show this on their pictures?\nWork through Master 1 and 2, allowing the class to check and discuss their\nanswers. Emphasise to the children the commutative property here, so that\nthey understand that 3/4 × 6 = 6 × 3/4.\nAsk students to find the value of 2/3 × 12 and 2/3 × 13 (Master 3 and 5).\nGive them time to explore the numerous ways in which multiplication can\nbe done before showing them the answers. During Master 4, students will\nneed to combine their multiplication facts knowledge with their place value\nknowledge to support mental calculations.\nDuring Guided Practice, students are multiplying fractions by whole\nnumbers, giving rise to mixed numbers or improper fractions.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-green\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Coloured paper strips</li><li>Fraction wall</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-gold\">Maths Language &amp; Variation</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Maths Language</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>It is important students are able to understand and use the</li><li>following mathematical terms and phrases in this lesson:</li><li>proper fraction</li><li>improper fraction</li><li>mixed number</li></ul></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Variation</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Examples 1 &amp; 2: Multiplying the same fraction by different whole</li><li>numbers; looking for patterns. Example 3: Multiplying a fraction by a whole number to solve a word problem.</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assess Learning</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-data\"><thead><tr><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Misconceptions</th><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Non-Negotiable</th><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Formative Assessment</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">Students multiply the denominators when they are multiplying the fractions.</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can represent the multiplication of a fraction by a whole number in pictures to solve.</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can represent fractions as a bar model or picture. Students can recognise a fraction when it is represented as a picture.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can repeatedly add fractions with the same denominator.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can represent improper fractions and mixed numbers using bar model diagrams or other pictures.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Check the Curriculum</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">National Curriculum</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Find a fraction of a whole number, using multiplication and division facts and where the answer is a whole number (e.g., 2/3 of 24).</li></ul></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Content Priority</th><td class=\"lt-td\">This is a key lesson.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Outcomes</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>This is a key lesson which supports this learning outcome:</li><li>● Students multiply a proper fraction by a whole number.</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Lesson 17: Multiplying Mixed Numbers and Whole Numbers":{"label":"Lesson 17: Multiplying Mixed Numbers and Whole Numbers","content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson Type</th><td class=\"lt-td\">KEY LESSON</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Textbook Page(s)</th><td class=\"lt-td\">256 - 257</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson Objective</th><td class=\"lt-td\">To begin this lesson, show students the Explore task and ask if they are able to show you how to solve this problem using bar models.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Lesson Approach</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Allow them To be able to multiply mixed numbers by whole numbers, creating larger mixed numbers. time to work on this with their partner.\nIs it possible to show mixed numbers as a bar model? Tell them they can\nshow this in pictures, but some of the bars would need be cut into pieces,\nwhile others wouldn't need to be. Is this true? What would this look like?\nWhy would some of the bars not need to be divided? Work through Master\nwith the class to show the two methods. Ask them which method they\npreferred: turning the mixed number into an improper fraction or dealing\nwith the wholes separately giving an explanation for their preference.\nDuring Guided Practice, students are using both methods to solve\nmultiplication problems.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-green\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Coloured paper strips</li><li>Fraction wall</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-gold\">Maths Language &amp; Variation</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Maths Language</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>It is important students are able to understand and use the following mathematical terms in this lesson:</li><li>proper fraction</li><li>improper fraction</li><li>mixed number</li><li>whole number</li><li>numerator</li><li>denominator</li><li>simplify</li><li>multiply</li></ul></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Variation</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Examples 1 &amp; 3: The mixed number comes first in the calculation</li><li>Examples 2 &amp; 4: The whole number comes first in the calculation</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Check the Curriculum</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">National Curriculum</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Find a fraction of a whole number, using multiplication and division facts and where the answer is a whole number (e.g., 2/3 of 24).</li></ul></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Content Priority</th><td class=\"lt-td\">This is a key lesson.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Outcomes</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>This is a key lesson which supports a number of learning outcomes, including:</li><li>● Students multiply a mixed number by a whole number.</li><li>● Students multiply a proper fraction by a whole number.</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Lesson 18: Multiplying Mixed Numbers and Whole Numbers":{"label":"Lesson 18: Multiplying Mixed Numbers and Whole Numbers","content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson Type</th><td class=\"lt-td\">KEY LESSON</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Textbook Page(s)</th><td class=\"lt-td\">258 - 260</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson Objective</th><td class=\"lt-td\">To begin this lesson, show students the Explore task, and then ask them to write down all the information they have been given from the task.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Lesson Approach</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Ask To be able to multiply mixed numbers by whole numbers in multi-step word problems. students how they can show 3 2/3 as far as 4 kilometres. What would that\nlook like? What would each third be worth? Is it possible to draw this as a\nbar model? Allow students to work together to draw this diagram.\nAllow students time to solve this problem and then share their ideas before\nworking through Master 1 and 2 with the class.\nDuring Guided Practice, students are solving word problems involving\nmulti-step multiplication. They are asked if there is more than one way to\nsolve each of the problems.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-green\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Coloured paper strips</li><li>Fraction wall</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-gold\">Maths Language &amp; Variation</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Maths Language</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>It is important students are able to understand and use the following mathematical terms in this lesson:</li><li>proper fraction</li><li>improper fraction</li><li>mixed number</li><li>whole number</li><li>numerator</li><li>denominator</li><li>simplify</li><li>multiply</li></ul></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Variation</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Example 1: Solving a word problem involving kilometres. Example 2: Multiplying mixed numbers by a whole number; no given context.</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Check the Curriculum</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">National Curriculum</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Find a fraction of a whole number, using multiplication and division facts and where the answer is a whole number (e.g., 2/3 of 24).</li></ul></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Content Priority</th><td class=\"lt-td\">This is a key lesson.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Outcomes</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>This is a key lesson which supports a number of learning outcomes, including:</li><li>Students multiply a mixed number by a whole number.</li><li>Students multiply a proper fraction by a whole number.</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Lesson 19: Chapter Consolidation":{"label":"Lesson 19: Chapter Consolidation","content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Textbook Page(s)</th><td class=\"lt-td\">261 - 262</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson Objective</th><td class=\"lt-td\">To be able to apply knowledge of fractions to solve problems.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Lesson Approach</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students solve a multi-step word problem involving the addition of mixed numbers and the subtraction of that total from a whole number.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension &amp; Reflection</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Mind Challenge</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Students solve a multi-step word problem involving the addition of mixed numbers and the subtraction of that total from a whole number.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-amber\"><th class=\"lt-th\">My Maths Journal</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Students determine who — Meilani, Charles, Ravi or Hannah — has worked out the fraction addition problem correctly, and explain why the other answers are wrong.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"}}}}},"art":{"label":"Category","children":{"Maori Art":{"label":"Chapter","children":{}},"Art Concepts":{"label":"Chapter","children":{}}}},"pe":{"content":""}};
